\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for November 23, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Michelle Goldberg, Ben Collins, Charles Blow \par \uc2 8,241 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". Have a happy Thanksgiving. ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE-PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The President`s words were reckless. It`s clear he decided to be part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Prosecutors investigating the Trump coup are seeking testimony from the former vice president.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  does the right thing, we win the election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the new reporting that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is considering telling the DOJ what he wouldn`t say to the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 Then, another brutal mass shooting this time at a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  in Virginia Beach. Ben Collins on what we`re learning about the Club Q shooter in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a new runoff controversy for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Can a candidate hold a primary residence in Texas while running for Senate in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 And why tonight could be the most important Thanksgiving Eve for \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  in quite some time. When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. The Department of Justice wants to talk to former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . They want to talk to him about Donald Trump`s attempt to foment a violent coup against these United States to disrupt the peaceful transfer of power for the first time in modern history.\par \par \uc2 The reporting which comes to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  notes, "Pence was present for some of the critical moments in which Trump and his allies schemed to keep him in office." And that reasoning is why this development doesn`t really come as much of a surprise. We already know. There were many key moments when Pence was essentially the only person in the room with Trump, as the Ex-President attempted to pressure him to overturn the results of a free and fair election.\par \par \uc2 And we know this in part because Pence`s own White House Counsel, a guy named Greg Jacob, repeatedly told the country as much during his public testimony in front of the January 6 Committee about the days leading up to the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): Did the Vice President ever waver in his position that he could not unilaterally decide which electors to accept?\par \par \uc2 GREG JACOB, FORMER COUNSEL TO MIKE PENCE: The Vice President never budged from the position that I have described as his first instinct, which was that it just made no sense from everything that he knew and had studied about our Constitution, that one person would have that kind of authority.\par \par \uc2 AGUILAR: Mr. Jacob, the President and the Vice President meet again on that same topic the next day, January 5, correct?\par \par \uc2 JACOB: The Vice President was then asked down to the Oval Office, and he went down to the Oval Office. While Mark and I stayed back in the Vice President`s office.\par \par \uc2 AGUILAR: You weren`t in that meeting?\par \par \uc2 JACOB: I was not.\par \par \uc2 AGUILAR: And did the Vice President have a call with the President that morning?\par \par \uc2 JACOB: He did.\par \par \uc2 AGUILAR: Were you with the Vice President during the call?\par \par \uc2 JACOB: We were told that a call had come in from the President. The Vice President stepped out of the room to take that call and no staff went with him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A lot of one on one meetings, a lot of one on one conversations. Now, in those meetings, the days before January 6, as Trump was getting increasingly desperate and erratic, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was one of the only witnesses, maybe the best witness to testify to Trump`s state of mind as far as we know. So, again, it`s no wonder why DOJ would want to speak to Pence, the guy that was on the other end of those conversations about what he and Trump discussed in those meetings.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether or not Pence will actually testify is another question. The former vice president already firmly rejected any possibility of cooperating with the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: January 6 was a tragic day. I`ve been very clear as I am in my book that the President`s words and actions in and around January 6, were reckless.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you intend to ever sit and answer questions written in person for the January 6 Committee?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I never stood in the way of senior members of my team cooperating with the committee and testifying. But Congress has no right to my testimony.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You are closing the door on that entirely.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I`m closing the door on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. So, Pence`s answers there are not entirely surprising, right? I mean, after all, he wants to run for president himself almost certainly in 2024. He`s assembling a team. He`s doing the kinds of things that a candidate would do. And Republican primary voters are not exactly big fans of the January 6 Committee. But the DOJ is a different beast entirely, a beast that it`s a little harder to close the door on.\par \par \uc2 There is an argument to be made that it would serve Pence`s own political interests if, I don`t know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were to get indicted before the next election, maybe even have a trial. In fact, the Times reports that Pence might very well cooperate with the Department of Justice. "Pence, according to people familiar with his thinking, is open to considering the request, recognizing the Justice Department`s criminal investigation is different from the inquiry by the January 6 Committee."\par \par \uc2 [20:05:02]\par \par \uc2 But anonymous sources telling the Times that they`re open to considering testifying is not the same as confirming publicly he will testify if subpoenaed. And if Pence wanted to get out of participating were he to be subpoenaed, which is what would happen, his old boss could try to give him away out. Again from the Times, "complicating the situation is whether Trump would try to invoke executive privilege to stop him or to limit Pence`s testimony, a step he has taken with limited success so far with other former officials. Trump can certainly try to invoke executive privilege to prevent Pence from testifying. Whether or not he succeeds is a big, different story.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump already attempted to invoke privilege to stop Greg Jacob -- that`s the man you heard from a moment ago -- and Mark Short, Pence`s own Chief of Staff from participating in the DOJ investigation. In fact, from giving grand jury testimony in the January 6 investigation. But both men did ultimately testify before that grand jury is part of the inquiry.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the thing. Any attempt to invoke privilege would also be complicated by the fact that, as you heard Pence mentioned in that \uc2 CBS\uc2  interview, he`s currently on a press tour plugging his book where he himself writes about and has published, writing about his interactions with Trump before January 6, the interactions in question.\par \par \uc2 Now, privilege claims can be thorny and complicated. It`s true the Vice President is in a different position and his staffers, but it would certainly seem me that if Pence can discuss Trump and the insurrection in a hardcover book for 22 bucks, he can tell a grand jury about it as well.\par \par \uc2 Harry Litman is a former U.S. Attorney and a former Deputy Assistant Attorney General at the Department of Justice. \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2  is a civil rights attorney and former Assistant U.S. Attorney. And they join me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Harry, let me just start with you on how you think this goes. So, you know, the idea of the Department of Justice is interested in his testimony, right? So, the question is, well, what`s next? What is this interaction between Pence`s world and the DOJ probably look like based on your experience?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: So, the political leverage here, I think, is with the department because Pence doesn`t want to look too resistant. But more importantly, the legal leverage is with them. I know Mike`s reporting said it could take months, but there`s no reason that it should be harder to get Pence to testify than it was to get his counsel and chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 And remember, as you say, he may have waived it. And it may also be the case that Biden has waived it. And at the end of the day, you really need his testimony singularly in the whole investigation. However, it`s the kind of thing where both parties may prefer a deal. Pence may prefer to not be looked like he`s getting there at gunpoint. And the department as well for its case, even though I think it has the goods on him, might prefer for him to be testifying voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s what`s happening now. Emmet Flood, a sophisticated, good Washington lawyer, not like Trump lawyers, and the DOJ are discussing and circling. Flood might be probing, could we maybe do it in written questions? Can you not subpoena and he`ll be there voluntarily? Maybe it`s not under oath. Maybe it`s limited in some way to basically serve his interests to not have to be -- have to say everything. But he`s going to need to give them information. And at the end of the day, if the DOJ wants to force him, they can and they would.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Maya, there`s a fascinating political dynamic here, right? I mean, everyone looks at this field. And I think we look at Pence, particularly, but all the fields. You know, what exactly is the plan here to get around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I mean, particularly if you`re \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ? Like, you`re like Trump but without the insurrection is sort of the -- sort of the -- you know, sort of the shtick. But, you know, who is that being sold to?\par \par \uc2 And it is the case, I think that, you know, if the President were like, convicted of a felony, that would change the field. If you`re \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , you might be able to help bring that off.\par \par \uc2 MAYA WILEY\uc2 , FORMER ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: You know, look, I think you`re absolutely right, Chris, that anybody wants to seek the votes of the American people has to figure out, one, how are they running? Who are they? Who are they going to be? And who are they telling the American public they are and which of that public are they trying to get on their side?\par \par \uc2 You know, white suburban women are someone everybody is trying to get on their side. I say that explicitly if you`re thinking about politics, because you`re also thinking about white suburban women and something that Trump -- I mean, Pence actually said in his interview, right, which he said, you know, after Trump tried to pressure him and he saw the tweet that went out, that Pence do put him in danger, put him in harm`s way. In fact, as we saw, put them at risk of what literally was a lynching, that he turns to his daughter and he said it takes -- he said, it doesn`t take courage to violate the law, but it takes courage to stand up for it.\par \par \uc2 And the question now is, is Pence looking to be the candidate that says, I`m the Republican candidate that is the hero in the story because I stood up for the rule of law, or is he the person who wants to stand up and say, I stood up to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:36]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s such a good point too because it`s clearly the case that the thesis of the book is that, right? That we -- I`m MAGA through and through up until the coup. Then, I chopped off the train of the coup and I want -- tried to stop the train, not the coup, right? But again, if that`s your deal, Harry, to come back to you on the politics and optics here, right, it`s hard for that to be your deal and then try to get out of what is obviously germane legal testimony to a criminal investigation by the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: 100 percent. And it`s basically impossible for him politically, I think, to do what other Trump partisans have done when pushed to the wall, take the Fifth.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: (INAUDIBLE) constitution, Mr. Pious, etcetera. And -- but he would prefer not to look like he`s being forced. And the department, in a sophisticated way, understands that as well. So, as long as they`ll give the information for -- it will be sort of consistent with the dignity he wants to have if he`s walking in a voluntary way. But also, he has to worry about the right flank, as you said, and the MAGA voters. It`s just a delicate dance on both sides. But I think both sides see the virtue in not making it look like it`s at, you know, legal command, but rather voluntary.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what this Ned Flood and DOJ -- Emmet Flood, excuse me, and DOJ negotiations are about right now. Again, push comes to shove, the department will say look, we need the testimony. We have a legal right to the testimony.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. To that point on pleading the Fifth, which I think is well taken, Maya. And to your point about the story he`s telling, would you -- I think Harry is right there. It`s just as -- he`s not \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 . And he`s not \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 . And he can`t go in and do a bunch of pleading the Fifth, right?\par \par \uc2 WILEY: No, he can`t. Especially if he wants to run for office. There`s just no affinity. But the other thing is, he`s not the guy -- you know, this isn`t \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2  who wrote a kiss and tell book that said, hey, hey, don`t forget me and the conspiracy. I was in the conspiracy too.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: You know, this is the book and this is the person and the potential candidate. He wasn`t candidate yet, but the potential candidate that saying, hey, I`m the guy that did the right thing. You -- what`s the Fifth that he`s pleading for unless he wants to actually implicate himself in a conspiracy that right now he`s not implicated in? In fact, the opposite. He`s implicated as the guy that said, oh, I`m not joining that conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 And one other thing to remember here. I completely agree with Harry here. But you know, also, if you`re Mike Pence`s lawyer saying there is no executive privilege here. There`s none. There`s nothing that Trump is going to do and win on, by the way, legally, when the Supreme Court already said in January, hey, look, the district court said this is ridiculous even for getting presidential records for the committee.\par \par \uc2 This is a criminal investigation. Mike is a fact witness. And he will have to testify one way or the other. And pleading the fifth really isn`t an option.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s a good point. Finally, and quickly, Harry, on that point, that there`s not really a privilege case here. The precedent here is on the other side. You could try to kick the can but then you`re just kicking the can. Again, I think if you`re Pence, you actually want to do this sooner rather than later. Because the more drawn out it is, again, from that political standpoint, that`s not the story you want front of mind for your Republican primary voters.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: That`s exactly right. You know, it`s 100 percent. And even if you kick the can, I think its own -- you don`t kick it very far here. Just you know, maybe a month. Again, he wants to be, you know, looking like he`s doing it on his own terms. DOJ wants the information. There`s hopefully a way to do it where both parties are served. And that`s what they`re trying to negotiate over now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that grand jury would rather prepare itself for very, very high dosages of ponderousness when they -- when they get that testimony. Harry Litman and \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, is Alaskan politics about to be done with \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 ? Tonight is the night that Alaska is tabulating its rank choice vote rankings.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 We`re literally getting results in as I speak. Will Palin lose to the Democrat Mary Peltola for the second time in three months? The dramatic ranks choice finish -- has that phrase ever been uttered? I`m not sure. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s Thanksgiving Eve and we`re still doing election results because moments ago the Alaska division of elections finished tabulating the votes from earlier this month. And we now have two breaking new election calls.\par \par \uc2 NBC News just projected that Republican Senator Lisa -- there`s a music. Republican Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  has won reelection and Democrat Mary Peltola is the winner of the at-large congressional seat now. Congresswoman Peltola, the first indigenous Alaskan to serve in Congress, defeated former governor and Republican vice presidential nominee \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  with nearly 55 percent of the vote. That`s a live stream, I imagine, of the rank-choice voting that they beam out to the world as they tabulate there, the Alaska division elections.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:32]\par \par \uc2 Now, this has been another not-so-great runup to Thanksgiving for Palin. 14 years after her infamous turkey pardoning gaffe, if you have a weak stomach, you may want to look away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARAH PALIN\uc2  (R-AK), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Oh, well, this was this was meat. I was happy to get to be invited to participate in this. And you know, for one, you need a little bit of levity in this job, especially with so much that has gone on in the last couple of months that has been so political, obviously, that it`s nice to get outand do something to promote a local business and to just participate in something that isn`t so heavy- handed politics that invites criticism. Certainly, we`ll probably invite criticism for even doing this too, but at least this was fun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, on a serious note, this is all happening now two weeks after the election because Alaska has got this new voting system, Alaska voters now rank up to four candidates in each race, instead of choosing only one. If no candidate receives 50 percent plus one of the vote, as was the case in Alaska`s at-large congressional district, the division of elections move on -- moves on to count the voter`s second choices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The candidate who gets the least number of first- choice votes is eliminated. Then tabulation starts in rounds. If your first choice was not eliminated, your vote stays with them and gets counted in the next round. If your first choice was eliminated, your second choice is now counted. This keeps happening in rounds until two candidates are left and the one with the most votes wins.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A bunch of places use this. Maine uses it. New York City actually used it for its primary here. Tonight, that is what happened for Democratic Congresswoman Mary Peltola, who will serve in this next Congress in the seat formerly held by Don Young.\par \par \uc2 For more on this momentous night of rank-choice drama, I`m joined by Michelle Goldberg, opinion columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . It is an amazing trajectory for \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 . You and I are sort of in the same age cohort, right? We both -- we both covered that race in 2008. And she was like a shooting star that lit up the night sky. It was just -- she was a sensation, a star and an attentional sensation. Everyone was thinking about her, talking about her, SNL. She was everywhere and everyone had their eyes on her.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right. And she set the stage for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or at least in many ways was the model for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the fact that she`s been sort of losing her shine for years now is one reason why I`m a little bit skeptical of people who believe that Donald Trump`s hold on the Republican Party is eternal, right? I mean, I certainly think that we shouldn`t assume that he`s just going to go away, but this stick does get old. You know, people do lose their novelty and their allure and things are just always changing.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing I think is interesting about \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  is she could have had such a different career. I mean, as you said, when she was chosen, there was -- you know, Democrats were terrified. She seemed electric. The media was kind of in love with her until those very embarrassing interviews. And had she sort of pursued the path that she had pursued somewhat as -- I mean, she was very right-wing, but she wasn`t as much of a kind of full-time provocateur when she was governor of Alaska. She could have had a huge career on the national stage, right? I mean, the path that she went on was a choice.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, she could have probably had a good shot of being the nominee. But she stopped being the governor when she decided to be a kind of like a full-time sort of right-wing celebrity. I think a little earlier, earlier than that was a plausible path to presidency, which again, the proof of concept that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ends up proving. But she was very much, you know, this kind of troll politics.\par \par \uc2 I`ll never forget being in that room at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  in 2008 in the Twin Cities when she was giving her speech. At one point, she turned to talk about the -- I don`t -- she didn`t call it the fake news, but the media, you know. And everyone -- the place went nuts and everyone started pointing and cursing and giving the middle finger to bring up the -- you know, the camera booths. And it was -- it was very much Trump before Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I remember being in that moment. Like, ooh, something real dark and unsettling is being unleashed in front of me right now.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. And then we`ve seen the consequences over the last few years of, you know, as that being unleashed and kind of growing and growing and growing until it nearly brought the country to its knees. But I do think it`s interesting that the people who know her the most, right, the people who`ve watched her --\par \par \uc2 [20:25:05]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Incredible\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: -- ascend up close a very -- I mean, you know 55-45 is not a normal margin in a kind of swing congressional race, right? Like, you don`t see people in swing districts winning by 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: By the way, it was lamestream media. Thanking (INAUDIBLE) to my ear who has the scars to -- for all of Palin coverage he`s done his life. The lamestream media -- but yes, that`s a great point. And I remember when she said -- when Don Young died in office, and there`s going to be a special election, and there`s a whole bunch of candidates. And when she declared, I was like, oh, we`re going to have -- we`re just going to have \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  as a political figure. Like, I just -- in my head, I was like, I think she`ll probably win. Like, she won statewide. And we`re just going to now have \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: It`s a Republican seat.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s a Republican -- and we`re going to have \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 . And maybe we`ll have now -- is this like the \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  2.0. If she`s member of Congress, who knows what else? And for -- in two consecutive elections, for Alaskans to be like, no, we don`t want the stick. We want the woman who`s like really focused on like our fisheries and like, our healthy fish population, and someone who`s like the opposite of \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 , and seems very liked and respected on all sides, is not a polarizing character, is really like a complete different way of doing politics than her.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, I also think that you know, ranks choice voting is kind of depolarizing, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s a good point. That`s the idea behind it.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: I mean -- right, it`s deep polarizing both because it means that sort of the most pivotal thing isn`t who wins the primary, you know, so you don`t kind of outsource these decisions entirely to the rabid base in primary elections. But it`s also depolarizing because it gives you an incentive to try to win over the entire electorate, as opposed to just turning out your base, because even the people who will might not vote for you in the first round --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Could get you on the second.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And so -- right, exactly. And so, the idea of -- right, the political logic which propelled \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in that primary in 2016 which was, I`ve got this incredible Hold on 30 percent of the population. Everyone came around, but I can call this plurality. And it`s the same thing he has right now, larger than 30 percent, is the opposite math of like, can I be broadly appealing to a whole bunch of people, which again, used to be one of the key recipes of politics that has been kind of lost, but I think resurrected a little bit in these midterms.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. I mean, part -- I mean, part of rank choice voting is part of the story here. And then part of the broader story, I think, is a rejection of \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 -style politics, Trumpist politics, obviously, in the loss of all of the Stop the Steal candidates. And I think -- you know, and I also don`t think you can underestimate the impact of abortion and the Dobbs decision in this race. You know, I think -- you know, she ran -- Mary Peltola ran as the pro-choice, pro-fisheries candidate.\par \par 