nominee in Georgia \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been one of the worst political candidates we`ve ever seen run for Senate. Just yesterday, the second woman to say that Walker allegedly pressured her into having an abortion gave a press conference. She appeared on camera though she did not disclose her name.\par \par \uc2 At the presser she came forward for what she claims to be new evidence supporting the relationship. See that picture there? I should tell you, Walker says this woman is lying. NBC News has not independently verified her claims. But Walker has said that he supports a national ban on abortion, he has called abortion murder, and does not support exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 This is precisely the kind of vetting problem that everyone in Republican circles is warning about back when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  handpicked the former football star and Celebrity Apprentice contestant to be his chosen Senate candidate in Georgia. But it`s not the only big problem with Walker`s candidacy. There are many. But another one is that he doesn`t really live full-time in Georgia. Like failed Pennsylvania Republican Senate candidate Mehmet Oz, Walker is a carpetbagger.\par \par \uc2 Now it`s true Walker has strong roots in the state of Georgia. He played college football at University of Georgia, won the Heisman trophy. But today, a new report shows Walker is getting a tax break on his house in Dallas, Texas, one that is intended only for a primary residence. Walker`s campaign did not respond to a request for comment from \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . We`ve also reached out and had not heard back. The new information calls into question Walker`s standing as a real resident running for Senate in the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Charles Blow lives in Atlanta, Georgia. He`s a columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . He`s written quite a bit on Herschel Walker`s campaign. And he joins me now. You know, Charles, I remember when Walker was being floated very early on because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a relationship with him, had had him on his show, and he`s sort of like, you know, fixed idea away. He was like Walker, Walker, should run in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And at the time, I remember reading a bunch of articles being like, well, he doesn`t live in Georgia. He lives outside Dallas. Like, he probably should live in Georgia. And that issue is still there, even though it does seem like it went away. It`s not like Warnock tried to do to Walker what Fetterman did to Oz.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, you know, Warnock is just not that kind of an attack dog. He`s not your traditional politician. He`s a pastor of a church. You get that sensibility from him. People here in Georgia always knew that they were kind of bringing \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  back into Georgia to run. He was a carpetbagger from the beginning. That is the least of the problems, however, with the running of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He is completely, completely ill-equipped to be a senator or elected official of any sort, on any level. And everyone here knows that. Even the people who voted for him know that, but they are locked in. Now, they have no other candidate. When they had a chance to really challenge \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  with should be in the primaries, no one did it because he was Donald Trump`s pick.\par \par \uc2 All of this would have come out. They probably would have had a stronger candidate. There was a lot of resentment or fear of \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  which drove a lot of votes to Kemp. You know, I don`t know if people remember this, but \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  once said on -- I think it was on a podcast, that her voters were telling her that they would move out of the state rather than be governed by \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The idea of a Black person, a Black woman, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  governing the people of Georgia freaked people out. And so, Kemp had that advantage. Walker or the candidate who is in the Republican seat on the Senate side would have also had it, but he`s terrible. And so, he didn`t have it. And so, this is a big problem. This idea of him being a carpetbagger and getting a tax break in Texas only adds marginally to that because there`s so much other stuff that disqualifies him from being a senator from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I mean, the thing about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  -- and here`s where I do think there`s a sort of -- there`s a little bit of a similarity with Mehmet Oz. It`s like, when Mehmet Oz was running for Senator in Pennsylvania, I was like, what`s the argument, what`s the logic Mehmet Oz, Pennsylvania senator, right?\par \par \uc2 Like, \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , he was a Republican Congressman before. Like, you know, Katie Britt who just got elected in Alabama, worked in Alabama politics. I believe she`s -- she was a \uc2 Richard Shelby\uc2  staffer in Alabama, right? So, you know, there`s certain logic there. It`s like, why is this person going to be the senator for this place? There`s literally no reason other than name recognition and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  likes him. That is the sum total of the argument for why this individual should represent this state in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [20:55:24]\par \par \uc2 BLOW: I mean, I think they can make a slightly stronger case if in fact he had any other qualifications to be a senator, which he does not. But they could probably make a slightly stronger case because he is a son of Georgia, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: So, he is here, raised here. He goes to college -- he goes to high school to college here. So, there -- it`s not like he`s completely disconnected from the state of Georgia, he`s just disconnected from reality. Like, he`s disconnected from intellect. He`s disconnected from qualifications. That`s the bigger problem.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also -- I mean, one of the things that`s happened in the abortion discussion here where you know, there have been -- there was the first reporting in which the woman said that -- who actually had a son with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and said that in one case he pressure for an abortion, actually pressure for a second though she didn`t have it. She showed a get- well-soon card. There was a receipt from the abortion clinic where she received the abortion.\par \par \uc2 You know, a lot of this is sort of -- the way that it`s been kind of characterized as like, oh, these dark stories from his past. But to me, the key thing is, he would vote for national abortion ban. Like, that is -- that is the position that he is running on. That is the party that he wants to be a part of. And this demonstrates that a fundamental hypocrisy or on seriousness about that. I wonder what role you think given on this day when we have news the Georgia Supreme Court has now reinstated the state`s ban of abortion after six weeks. What role abortion is played in this race? What role you think it will play in the last few weeks of the runoff?\par \par \uc2 BLOW: I think it has -- it has played a role in this race in the same way that it has played in other races because it is not a separate line on the ballot. It plays a little bit less of a role. You have to kind of vote by proxy that this person is with the party that opposes the thing that I am in favor of. I think the big question though, the thing that we don`t talk enough about is that that child is a lie, right?\par \par \uc2 This is the party that talks incessantly about absent Black fathers, and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is by all measures an absent Black father to that style. How do you -- how do -- Republicans in this state have made -- twisting themselves into knots, to knots. To kind of put \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  into the basket, they put all other Black people in, right? If anybody -- any other Black person on the liberal side would show up not being able to make a sentence, they`d attack and lambaste that person. But not Hershel Walker because he`s saying what they want to hear.\par \par \uc2 Any other Black person who was an absentee father who was advocating for abortion for the people who we impregnated would be lambasted by these people. But because he`s saying the things that they want to hear, they excuse it. Here -- any other Black person who was doing -- who behaved in a way that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  behaves but happened to be liberal and actually supporting policies that help Black people would be lambasted by the Georgia Republicans who are now supporting and excusing and making excuses for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, the way that I`ve run the thought experiment in my head is just take 30 seconds to consider what would be the electoral results in the divided state of Georgia if the Black Democratic nominee for a statewide office had Herschel Walker`s package? I mean, it`s just like, obviously, it doesn`t -- you know, it`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 You`ve now got the full team trying to pull him over the finish line, including the Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  who I think helped him probably the first time around because he`s relatively popular was on the -- on the ballot. He`s not this time. He will be campaigning for him. I wonder if you think how much that matters. A, it`s a runoff without any other offices, or B, that Kemp is campaigning for him.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Listen, Kemp has to do this to stay in the good graces of the Republican Party because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  already hates him. And so, it`s the only part of the party that he can appeal to is this Senate Republicans, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and the like who need help. They want to have the extra seat even though it means -- it doesn`t mean they`re going to get control of the Senate, it does strengthen their hand in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 He -- Kemp is trying to stay in their good graces. Kemp wants nothing to do with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . And he proved that during his campaign for governor. And no one wants to touch \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  but they have to stay in the good graces of the Republican Party, and so they do their due diligence, they do what the party is asking them to do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it has been quite a spectacle this entire race. Charles Blow, great pleasure to have you on. Have great holiday. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: You too. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thanksgiving Eve. You know, I realized today that this is the 10th one of these we have done. To those of you who have been watching throughout, a hearty thank you. Have a great holiday, all of you. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now with Alicia Menendez who`s in for Alex. Good evening, Alex -- Alicia.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221124eibn00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for November 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell \par \uc2 6,958 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:41]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel. You know, I have never felt closer to you than --\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": We can`t show anybody the white spots, the wide shots, nobody knows!\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We can`t?\par \par \uc2 So, you know, for the first time ever, instead of listening to you in my left ear, which is where my ear pieces, I`m listening to you in my real ear --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Are they going to get this shot?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I am looking directly at you, Rachel, if you were to look to your right for a second. That`s me!\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Hello! That`s weird.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And the same -- yeah.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Are you wearing pants?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We should do this all the time.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I -- yeah.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You`re not wearing pants?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MADDOWE: Excellent. No comment.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Rachel, free up the camera, I`ll talk to you this way. It`s like talking to Kornacki, enough, the big boy, across the room.\par \par \uc2 So, Bradley Hart, your historian on the show tonight, made me feel great. When he said he has written a book about this. He studied the period very closely. He learned things from your podcast.\par \par \uc2 I love that part. I thought I knew a little something about the period, and I`ve learned everything from the podcast, but Bradley Hart learned something from the podcast.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: He wrote a book on it and he learns stuff. It was very, like, I was overwhelmed when he said that. He said he`d never before heard the sound of John Rogge`s voice, I mean, that`s one of the things we can do with NBC Archives, and help you got from the \uc2 Library of Congress\uc2 , and all this other stuff.\par \par \uc2 It`s so -- it`s so cool to be able to make a real contribution in terms of -- even for the people who know the stuff, and very few people know this story. It`s relevant to what we`re going through today. To be able to add something people couldn`t do because we were able to get that stuff on the air, I just --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I`ve got something I`d like you to spend Thanksgiving holiday thinking about.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Okay.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I think you are right that a lot of people on long drives over the course of the thanksgiving weekend are going to be listening to the podcast, final episode only buys you an hour of driving time. There is, what? A total of almost eight hours of material there?\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah, roughly, yeah.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Seven-ish, at least, right? So, you know, like, you know, Sacramento to L.A. kind of drive.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: With traffic, sure.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Right. So, there`s going to be a lot of listening. I -- think about this, maybe next week, after Thanksgiving, you -- you know I was going to say walk from their over to hear and talk about the podcast, or sit there and talk about the podcast.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You want to do an after Thanksgiving like review?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Like, what did we learn? I mean, especially the inspirational ending of it that I did not see coming --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: He did not see coming? That`s funny.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Did not see that coming.\par \par \uc2 It`s a podcast, right. It`s just so much, and I think by, as a podcast audience member, we will have many of the kinds of questions about it that your listeners have had, and think about, it don`t commit to it now --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`ll commit to it now. I say it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I don`t normally book you on TV, all right, so, okay, we`ll figure -- out\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We will figure out a time next week after Thanksgiving. I would love to.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We will have a camera that could actually track you walking across the room.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You know what? I`m going to put out a -- if \uc2 Twitter\uc2  still works, if twitter still exists, I`ll post something where people can reply if they have questions, they might want to answer about the podcast. It is really relevant to what`s going on right now, particularly with the new special counsel, and all this stuff. If people have current residents questions, or things they want answered, and then we can find out what people are missing.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know what I`m going to do tonight, which is not normal practice? I`m going to watch the re-run of the show. I`m going to see how they shot this thing we just did.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: This is not --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The strangest angles we`ve ever been involved in.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, thank you for being here in this room tonight.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And you too. Thanks, Lawrence, I`ll see you later.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, welcome to episode two of Mr. Smith goes to Washington. Friday night on this program, when the breaking news of the night was that attorney general`s appointment of a special counsel to oversee the investigation and possible prosecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the documents case and the general sixth case, I said it was another very bad day for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:05:04]\par \par \uc2 And by the time this program began at 10:00 p.m. on Friday night, what I had to say about it was actually a counterpoint to what by then was a few hours of critical comment about the appointment of Jack Smith as special counsel, with many commentators, not all, but many insisting that it would delay both cases, and it was somehow, this is the part I really didn`t get, it was somehow a win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I said the opposite and included the suggestion that it was very likely that the appointment of Jack Smith would actually speed up the cases. Because, before Jack Smith was appointed, no one was in charge of both of those possible prosecutions of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . No one.\par \par \uc2 All the important decisions of those investigations had to go all the way up the chain of command in the Justice Department to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  who, on a daily basis, is overseeing the work of a department of 115,000 people, who have nothing to do with the investigations of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Jack Smith has nothing else to do except work on the investigations and possible prosecutions of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Even though Jack Smith has not yet made his way back from Europe, where he has been prosecuting war crimes at the Hague, even though we are still waiting for that big, cinematic moment, when Mr. Smith goes to Washington, Jack Smith officially entered the case today in a filing by the United States attorney for the southern district of Florida, telling the 11th circuit court of appeals that Jack Smith is on the case.\par \par \uc2 United States hereby gives notice that John L. Smith has been appointed by the attorney general as special counsel. The appeals court was receiving that notice today, exactly one day before the scheduled oral arguments in the Justice Department`s appeal of the special master in that case.\par \par \uc2 Jack Smith did not ask the court for a delay in that hearing tomorrow, so that he could catch up the way many commentators suggested he might do. Instead, the court was notified, special counsel Smith has reviewed the filings in this litigation and approves all of the arguments that have been presented in the briefs, and will be discussed at the oral argument in this case.\par \par \uc2 So much for the delay caused by the appointment of Jack Smith. Jack Smith has already read all 1,200 pages of the filings in that case, which means he now agrees with every single assertion the Justice Department has made in this case, including the need for a search warrant, at Donald Trump`s Florida residents, to uncover possible evidence of obstruction of justice, and other felonies involving Donald Trump`s possible criminal handling of government documents, including classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Jack Smith agrees with every single word that prosecutors working on this case have already said in their filings about the case. By inference, that means that Jack Smith agrees with every single word they have not said publicly to support what they have said publicly. That also means that Jack Smith already knows much more about the case then \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  ever could.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general has not been sitting around reading every single line of every judicial opinion on every filing in this case, but Jack Smith has already done that. So, the evidence, as of tonight, supports what I suggested on Friday night, that the attorney general was telling the truth when he said there would be no delays because of Jack Smith`s appointment.\par \par \uc2 And because Jack Smith doesn`t have the entire Justice Department to manage all day long. Jack Smith will be able to make much faster prosecutorial decisions that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  could make.\par \par \uc2 We will have much more on the appointment of special counsel Jack Smith, a Harvard law professor, joins us later in this hour. Professor Tribe will also conserve the implications of the latest scandal at the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , and institution that has been scandal-free for most of its life, and has now been corrupted by the dark money, dark money influences, so masterfully identified and tracked by our first guest tonight, Senator Sheldon Whitehouse.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " wrote the story about the latest scandal at the Supreme Court this weekend, and identified Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and his wife as suspects number one and two in the possible leaking of a Supreme Court opinion eight years ago in 2014. That opinion, like the Supreme Court opinion leaked earlier this year, overturning Roe v. Wade, that is written by \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Evangelical minister Rob Schenck told "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " that he obtained advanced notice of the \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  opinion in the \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  case that overturned a provision of the Affordable Care Act, requiring employers to provide contraception coverage and health care plans. "The Times" reports that it is the dark money that Senator Whitehouse has been following, that created the Supreme Court leak.\par \par \uc2 "The Times" reports, quote, Mr. Schenck recruited wealthy donors like Gayle Wright and her husband, Donald, encouraging them to invite some of the justices to meals, to their vacation homes, or private clubs. He advised allies to contribute money to the Supreme Court Historical Society, then mingled with justices at its functions.\par \par \uc2 He ingratiated himself with court officials who could help give him access, records show. Mr. Schenck said his aim was not to change minds, but rather to stiffen the resolve of the courts conservatives and taking uncompromising stance that could eventually lead to a reversal of Roe. Mr. Schenck wanted the conservatives on the court to hear from people who would hail them as heroes if they seized the opportunity to strike down Roe one day.\par \par \uc2 Rob Schenck says he exploited the fact that Supreme Court justices are not bound by the same code of ethics as other federal judges. He said, I saw us as pushing the boundaries of appropriateness. Rob Schenck now says he regrets the actions he took then and felt compelled to come forward after the leak of the Alito draft opinions striking down Roe v. Wade in May, that Justice Alito called a brave betrayal of trust.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schenck says that it was Mrs. Wright who called him after a dinner she and her husband had at Samuel Alito`s home with Justice Alito and his wife in 2014. "The Times" reports, quote, Mr. Schenck said Mrs. Wright told him that the decision would be favorable to \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , and that Justice Alito had written the majority opinion.\par \par \uc2 He used that information to prepare a public relations push, records show, and he said that, at the last minute, he tipped off the president of \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , the craft store chain, owned by Christian evangelicals, that was the winning party in the case.\par \par \uc2 Justice Alito has now done something that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has never done, Justice Alito is publicly denying the allegation against him. In a written statement to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  said, the allegation that the Wrights were told the outcome of the decision in the \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  case, or the authorship of the opinion of the court, by me or my wife, is completely false.\par \par \uc2 In his written statement, Justice Alito admits that the Wrights bought their way into his home. My wife and I became acquainted with the Wrights some years ago because of their strong support for the Supreme Court Historical Society, and since then, we have had a casual and purely social relationship.\par \par \uc2 Strong support for the Supreme Court Historical Society, in other words money. Strong support is money. Massive amounts of money pumped into the Supreme Court Historical Society by Donald Wright and his wife, Gayle. That money is the only reason \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  knows those people. That money is the only reason those people got into Samuel Alito`s home, while he is writing or finished writing the \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  opinion but she now denies he or his wife leaked to those people who bought their way into his home.\par \par \uc2 After Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  announced in the greatest possible terms, he was launching an investigation of the leak of a \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  opinion this year of overturning Roe v. Wade, Rob Schenck sent a letter to Chief Justice Roberts, telling him the story of the league of the 2014 \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  decision, and said: Considering there may be a severe penalty to be paid by whoever is responsible for the initial leak of the recent draft opinion, I thought this previous incident might bear some consideration by you and others involved in the process.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:26]\par \par \uc2 Of course, I would be fully cooperate should you find any value and other detail surrounding what I have transmitted here.\par \par \uc2 Chief Justice Roberts never responded. Taking their place on the list of possible suspects for both leaks are Mr. and Mrs. \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Senator Sheldon Whitehouse of Rhode Island. He`s a member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . He co-wrote a letter Sunday to Chief Justice Roberts calling for the court to investigate these possible ethical breaches.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Senator.\par \par \uc2 And let`s begin with the leaking Supreme Court of the -- what we learned about the 2014 leak which was also the lead of an Alito opinion. That seems to be quite a resemblance to what we saw this year in the lead of the Alito opinion overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (D-RI): Yeah, a few things converged here, Lawrence. The first is organized plans and patterns to put people in touch with the justices, to lobby them off line, out of sight of other parties. You can only imagine the conversation that got a Supreme Court justice in a room with a wealthy individual in order to do that lobbying, who made that connection?\par \par \uc2 Of course, it appears many of whatever these events were at peoples clubs, at people`s homes, that were not reported, and that is all consistent with a lot of the stuff we have been tracking about this Supreme Court, failing to make proper disclosures like everybody else when they are recusal and other concerns failing to investigate the use of power of investigation. That, as you said, Chief Justice Roberts displayed in the Alito draft opinion lead, and this constant, constant engagement with very, very rich right wingers, as they go about doing the business of the rich white wing.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We think of the Supreme Court as removed from it all, and physically removed from it all. It`s not easy in Washington to think about, you know, if I wanted to run into a Supreme Court justice, where would I go? It just isn`t that simple.\par \par \uc2 Enter the Supreme Court Historical Society. Were you surprised the Supreme Court Historical Society is now on the map of the flow of dark money?\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: No, it`s been a gathering place for people to hobnob with Supreme Court justices, and the price of admission for that is to make significant contributions to a fund that helps maintain the Supreme Court and various artifacts of the Supreme Court. It is right for misuse, and it appears that, in this case, it has been misused.\par \par \uc2 We`ve actually been tracking this group`s fate and actions since before this broke because of this earlier pattern of whining and dining of the justices. We had written an earlier letter in September to Chief Justice Roberts, asking for an explanation, and some answers. All they got back from him was we have an ethics code.\par \par \uc2 And this seems to be a persistent pattern again with this court. It won`t answer specific questions. It won`t conduct inquiries into ethics matters. They don`t do the work of finding out if there`s been an investigation. They just point at the ethics code as if that makes all of the activity go away.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, can the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  become the ethics investigator of the Supreme Court? And, while you are at it, can you say the \uc2 United States government\uc2  will fund the Supreme Court Historical Society? It`s not allowed to take any more contributions, in that there`s any important historical artifacts to be maintained at the Supreme Court, in conjunction with those in the federal government who know how to do that, we at the federal government will take care of it?\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: Well, I think a good, hard look at the Supreme Court Historical Society is in order, and stand by for further information in that regard. I think that the important thing is the Supreme Court should have, not just a code of ethics that is wall hanging, but a code of ethics that works like a real code of ethics.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:07]\par \par \uc2 So, that if anybody has a complaint, somebody looks into the complaint. If there`s a question about what a justice knew, and when he knew it, and it bears on whether he should`ve recused himself, then a proper investigation is accomplished. But we`re not having to listen to Justice Alito answer by unsworn press releases, when in fact he goes through a regular, fair, honorable process of answering questions, which, if they were false, would- be false statements, so that the truth can be brought out, and the integrity of the proceedings of the court assured.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator Sheldon Whitehouse, the determined illuminator of dark money, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, we will get Harvard law professor Laurence Tribe`s view of the latest scandal at the Supreme Court, and his former law student, Merrick Garland`s, choice of Jack Smith as special counsel after Justice Department legal filing today said that Jack Smith is completely up to speed with, and fully approves of the Justice Department`s handling of the case involving Donald Trump`s possession of government records, including classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Professor Laurence Tribe joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:25:33]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: After discussing the attorney general`s appointment of Jack Smith as special counsel Friday night on this program, Andrew Weissmann, who served as a senior member on Special Counsel Robert Mueller`s team, said, on Saturday, for those concerned that the appointment of a special counsel will delay things, just the opposite. Jack is a superfast, no nonsense, and let`s cut to the chase kind of guy. And now, with less DOJ bureaucracy, and decision-making, the investigations can move faster. That is a version of what he said on this program Friday night.\par \par \uc2 And in a filing in the appropriately piled case of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  versus the United States of America, today, the U.S. attorney for the southern district of Florida said this about the appointment of special counsel to handle the documents case. Special Counsel Smith has reviewed the filings in this litigation and approves all of the arguments that have been presented in the briefs and will be discussed at the oral argument in this case.\par \par \uc2 That oral argument is tomorrow, before the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals, the Justice Department with Jack Smith`s complete support is appealing the appointment of the special master in that case.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is Professor Laurence Tribe, who has taught constitutional law at Harvard Law School for five decades.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Professor Tribe.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been eager to hear your reaction to the appointment of the special counsel.\par \par \uc2 LAURENCE TRIBE, HARVARD LAW PROFESSOR: I was delighted. I did think, as early as this March, the special counsel would be helpful. When many months passed, and none had been appointed, I was worried it might be too late.\par \par \uc2 But now, it`s very clear that by picking someone with a track record of Jack Smith who is faster than a speeding bullet, and very much like a bulldog in terms of what he gnaws on when he gets his teeth around it, I think that this is going to move very quickly. And as you pointed out, until this point, there was no one person who was in charge of the multitude of crimes that the former president appears to have committed, both in connection with the top secret documents in Mar-a-Lago, and in connection with trying to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 The attorney general and his deputy couldn`t possibly be in, day-to-day command of it, because as you pointed out, they have a sprawling over hundred thousand people to supervise hundreds of inquiries, hundreds of investigations and prosecutions. This way, it`s all construct in the office of one person who got completely up to speed and the person who got into tomorrow`s argument. I look forward to wrapping decisions and the need for rapidity is something that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  emphasized in announcing this selection.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, and just a note to the audience, I know you know this professor tribe, but the attorney general is under his jurisdiction as the Bureau of Prisons, 34,000 people working there alone, not to mention the \uc2 antitrust division of the Justice Department\uc2 , Civil Rights Division -- all these divisions that have nothing to do with this kind of work.\par \par \uc2 And so, the attorney general, to get a decision out of him, you have to get in line. Even if you are the top priority item, you still will have to get in line. And with Jack Smith there, it does seem to be it speeds up the process, which is what Andrew Weissmann suggested on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 And then, today, we didn`t have to wait long to find out, where is Jack Smith on this material? There is the Justice Department issuing a filing in court, saying he is completely up to speed. He read it all in the documents case. That is minimum 1,200 pages of pleadings, who knows what else he has read, or what else the department has given him they haven`t been public.\par \par \uc2 So, he is, right now, tonight, right up to speed with a case that`s going to be argued tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: It really doesn`t surprise me. I`ve known his work for a long time. I`ve admired him from afar. He was in charge of the political corruption unit of the Justice Department. He has been a career prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:46]\par \par \uc2 He`s prosecuted war crimes in Kosovo, he was working at the Hague, he certainly didn`t leave the Hague just to preside over a lazy, slow-moving, molasses-like investigation. He is there to wrap it up and decide exactly which indictments to bring, in what sequence.\par \par \uc2 There are all kinds of crimes, many that haven`t even been named publicly yet that the president appears to me, to be seriously in jeopardy of conviction under. And it has to happen quickly because the president`s whole strategy is delay, delay, delay. He`ll make motions. He`ll make arguments. He`s got the right to do it.\par \par \uc2 But because of that it is critical that the first indictments come down no later than early next year, because otherwise, there will be no final conviction, then we`ll get a new president, and even though under the current regulations, not even a new president or new attorney general could remove Jack Smith without good cause, there are people in this country who don`t pay attention to regulations or laws.\par \par \uc2 And some of them want to hold power in Washington, and they might just be there. So this has to move quickly. And that`s exactly what the attorney general indicated he would do.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Could we -- before we go to break -- get a quick word from you about this latest scandal at the Supreme Court. \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  has at least responded to the accusations about him, and denied them, which is something \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has never bother to do.\par \par \uc2 Is that in some tiny, microscopic marginal improvement in the way the Supreme Court responds to these accusations?\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Not really. I think it`s important, as I argued with Dennis (INAUDIBLE) in an opinion piece in the "Herald" this morning, important that the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  conduct hearings into these very serious accusations of deliberate leaks designed strategically to solidify the majority in support of taking women`s rights away from them.\par \par \uc2 You know, credibility is the lifeblood of the court. And I`m very glad that the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  appears ready to hold hearings. It`s not enough for any justice to simply toss off a letter not under oath. We really need to get to the bottom of this.\par \par \uc2 Lightning is unlikely to have struck twice in Justice Alito`s chambers. I think we need to get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Right. Harvard law professor, Laurence Tribe, thank you very much for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, democracy was on the ballot in Arizona, and because of our next guest, democracy won in Arizona. Governor-Elect \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  will join us next in the Winners` Circle.\par \par \uc2 [22:32:37]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In the Winners` Circle tonight, the governor-elect who saved democracy and saved the integrity of the next presidential election in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D-AZ), GOVERNOR-ELECT: In this election, Arizonans chose solving our problems over conspiracy theories. We chose a better Arizona, and we chose democracy. The system of government that made America the best and most prosperous country in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 But the attacks on democracy won`t end today with this victory. And so it is on all of us to continue to defend it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It has been exactly one week since NBC News called the Arizona governor`s election for Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . She defeated Trump`s chosen candidate election liar, Kari Lake, by more than 17,000 votes. Kari Lake is, of course, still refusing to admit that she lost. Both Kari Lake and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have suggested that a printer issue in Maricopa County that slowed the tabulation of some ballots and briefly lead to long lines at some polling precincts, disenfranchised and disproportionately affected Republican areas of the county.\par \par \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 , the Republican chair of the Maricopa County Board of Supervisors, says the printer issue had no effect.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES (R), CHAIRMAN, MARICOPA COUNTY BOARD OF SUPERVISORS: We`ve reviewed this issue. And we found that the 70 vote centers that were impacted by the printer issues were spread all across Maricopa County, and they did not impact one part of the valley more than the other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The Maricopa County Sheriff`s Office confirmed today that \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2  has been moved to an undisclosed location for his safety, and is now being accompanied by a security detail following threats on social media. The Maricopa County sheriff was asked about these threats today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Is the intensity of threats right now, you know, after this election more intense, less intense, about the same as it was in 2020?\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF PAUL PENZONE, MARICOPA COUNTY, ARIZONA: That`s a great question. I just think that there has been a natural escalation because the folks feel emboldened, and they have people in office, or running for office who are empowering them, and it needs to stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And joining us now, Arizona secretary of state, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , who in January will take the oath of office as Arizona`s 24th governor. Governor-Elect Hobbs, thank you very much for joining us tonight. It is great to have you back with us as a winner.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:52]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I just want to spend a moment on the campaign because I know, because you refused to debate Kari Lake, there were people who wanted you to win, who thought you made a mistake, who thought your campaign was making mistakes and that you were campaigning ineffectively. And they were preparing themselves for Kari Lake winning because of that.\par \par \uc2 What was your confidence level in those decisions as you made them, including the decision not to engage in a debate with her?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: I knew that we were running a solid campaign, a campaign that was doing all the things that we needed to do, and that the folks criticizing weren`t looking at the same data that we were looking at.\par \par \uc2 And so at the end of the day, I`m here as the governor-elect, and we ran a good campaign.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Do you agree with the sheriff`s assessment, that the threats to people like you and others are worse this time than they were two years ago, on the presidential election?\par \par \uc2 DOBBS: I certainly have experienced the same level of threat that I was under in 2020. It`s -- it probably has escalated. But you have folks like my opponent Kari Lake, the Republicans up and down the ticket who are refusing to accept the results. They`re calling for a redo of the election.\par \par \uc2 So of course, what are their followers going to do? They think this election was stolen just like they are led to believe that the 2020 election was stolen. It`s simply not the case. But they are being egged on by their political leaders, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Do you think it`s accurate to say, as I have, that democracy was on the ballot, in Arizona, because people like Kari Lake and others, the Republican candidate for secretary of state, were virtually pledged to deliver the Arizona`s electoral votes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or whoever the Republican presidential candidate was, no matter how many votes the Democrat got?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Absolutely. I mean it`s not an accident that the former president gave his full-throated endorsement to this entire slate of Republican candidates here in Arizona, because they pledged their loyalty to him versus the voters of Arizona that they were running to represent.\par \par \uc2 They`ve made it clear in their actions since this election, refusing to accept the results, that they would have done the same thing if they were in these offices of power in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, you take the oath of office as governor in January. What is Governor Hobbs first action on day one as governor?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, I said many times that I will call a special session to repeal Arizona`s pre Roe abortion ban. This is a ban that completely criminalizes abortion. It is far out of touch with the majority of Arizonans who support access to safe and legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 And we are under the threat of this being enacted into law depending upon what the courts do with the injunction that`s currently in place on it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Governor-Elect \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us as often as you did during the campaign to keep everyone updated on what was at stake in Arizona. We really appreciate you joining us during the campaign, and, of course, tonight as a winner.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  turned to the Pennsylvania legislator in 2020 to try to overturn the presidential election. He won`t be able to do that again thanks to our next guests joining us in the Winners` Circle tonight. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:43:36]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Also in the Winners` Circle tonight, the Pennsylvania House of Representatives. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost Pennsylvania to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he turned to the Pennsylvania legislature totally controlled by Republicans to try to get them to award Pennsylvania`s electoral votes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That was legally impossible, so it did not happen. Donald Trump`s point man in the Pennsylvania House of Representatives was \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who just lost his campaign for governor and conceded the election to Democratic Governor Elect \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the election this month for the first time in 12 years, Democrats won control of the Pennsylvania House of Representatives. So the Pennsylvania legislature will not be trying to change laws so that they can play around with Pennsylvania`s 20 electoral votes. And they won`t be continuing to try to pass a constitutional amendment to ban abortion in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOANNA MCCLINTON (D-PA), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: We are talking about women dying. We are talking about more than half the population not being able to make decisions when not even half of this body has a uterus. But here we are being silenced yet again.\par \par \uc2 And understand, when you`re silencing us, we are actually elected officials like you all. So you are silencing millions of voters from every corner of the commonwealth when you silence us and don`t allow us to amend bills that will let people vote. That will allow women to make their own decisions. You are silencing all of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:49:48]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: She will not be silenced. That is the Democratic leader of the Pennsylvania House of Representative, Joanna McClinton who will become speaker of the Pennsylvania House in January.\par \par \uc2 Mandy Steele, who will be joining us in a moment along with Joanna McClinton, will be joining the Pennsylvania House of Representatives in January after flipping a Pennsylvania House seat for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MANDY STEEL (D-PA), STATE REPRESENTATIVE ELECT: When I knock on doors, when I meet people in this district, Pennsylvanians want to see abortion protected in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 We believe that is a right we want to see women participating in as full citizens in our state. And we know that we need to have autonomy over our own bodies in order for that to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And joining us now are Pennsylvania House Democratic leader Joanna McClinton and State Representative-Elect Mandy Steele. And Presumptive Speaker McClinton, I know I should not pronounce you speaker quite yet, but I want to speak to the senior most -- we have the senior most member of the delegation and the junior most member tonight. This is a great way to start.\par \par \uc2 How did the Democrats do it? How did they pull off this victory that has taken 12 years for the Democrats to win the House of Representatives in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: So we were able to accomplish this big victory for working families all across Pennsylvania through hard working candidates like Mandy Steele. Our candidates knocked on more than 1 million doors. They went straight to voters to explain the horrific things that were happening in Harrisburg.\par \par \uc2 Because most of the time, people think, oh, that`s my Republican Rep. They`re doing their job. You know, they show up to my kids` baseball game and give out an award.\par \par \uc2 But the truth of the matter is, they had been undermining voters will for years. And unfortunately, they were led astray by our former president and they tried to silence all of us.\par \par \uc2 And most recently, with their majority, they moved forward a constitutional amendment, the first step to ban abortion here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Mandy Steele -- I had my eye on your race because I was told by someone who knows that area of Pennsylvania, if Mandy Steele can win in this district, that means that Democrats will probably win the House of Representatives in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And when did you feel the win was within reach?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: You know, I have to say, I felt it from the beginning because I spent a lot of time out on the doors talking to people and people did not want to see women`s rights overturned in the state and across all kinds of doors.\par \par \uc2 It was clear to me from the beginning that we were going to vote against this extremist minority. And vote for the people. And it sure played out that way in a big way for us in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joanna McClinton, what advice do you have to the incoming new members on the Democratic side like Mandy Steele?\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: So everything you heard from the voters at the door, everything you heard from your neighbors, it is a mandate. The voters were clear. They overwhelmingly rejected fear, hatred, division. So now, Mandy, as we go into 2023, and you become a representative, we are going to craft an agenda that defends workers` rights, that helps families who have been trying so hard to get ahead in Pennsylvania, whether you`re from the west where you are, or whether they`re from the eastern part of the state where I serve.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. And Mandy, I think people have their best understanding of politics before they become too deeply professional. I mean you`re going to be moving from your life out there as a citizen kind of watching this from the grandstand, to an actual participant down on the field.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I think people have a really valuable contribution to make to the elected officials who are already there. What insights do you think you can bring to the House of Representatives now that you are coming in from the outside?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Well, what is amazing to me is that I was so fortunate to be the first woman and first Democrat elected to Borough (ph) council in my community several years ago. I just had my final council meeting tonight.\par \par \uc2 And now I will be able to vote for future leader McClinton, who will be the first woman speaker -- future speaker McClinton who will be the first woman speaker in Pennsylvania. My heart is just overflowing in having the chance to participate in that.\par \par \uc2 And what I can bring to the table is a community feel. You know, I`ve spent years in the community, governing at the local level. I understand how Pennsylvanians want to see the state move forward. And I am so excited to bring that perspective to the state house.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Future speaker McClinton, what did it mean for the Democrats to be able to control the house in Pennsylvania, a legislature that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was turning to in 2020 to see if there was some way they could grab Pennsylvania`s electoral votes for him?\par \par \uc2 [22:55:02]\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: It means that we are turning the page to election deniers. When 60 of my colleagues, including our speaker of the House and all of the Republican leadership in the Senate and the House, signed a letter, urging our congressional delegation to overthrow the election, it was completely outrageous.\par \par \uc2 That was my introduction to being the minority leader. It`s like, are we going to start the session trying to throw out votes? But that`s what happened. But voters in this election, this November, they are turning the page to it and so are we.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joanna McClinton and Mandy Steele, thank you very much for joining us tonight in the Winners` Circle. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:42]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221122eibl00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for November 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,290 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: We can do a whole segment. I have rolled back on and talk about why there`s run-offs because that -- there`s a reason for that, you know, to make sure Black people couldn`t win. Charlie Sykes, Roland Martin, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The devastation this violent act has had on our community cannot be measured.\par \par \uc2 DANEYA ESGAR (D-CO), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: This club was a safe haven in our community.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Police identify the victims of a mass murderer at a Colorado drag show. Tonight, what we know about the shooter`s motive, and why it wasn`t hard to see this coming.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There was so much hate and visceral spread about our LGBTQ communities, especially our trans community that I can`t say that that didn`t have an impact on this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, what we`re learning about the man who just took over the Trump investigation. Congressman Adam Schiff, from the new special counsel and what he expects from Kevin McCarthy?\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I suspect he will do whatever \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  wants him to do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And as primary hopefuls gathered for their first unofficial campaign events, what could be the biggest flaw in their plan to stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PAUL RYAN\uc2 , FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER: I`m a never again Trumper. Why? Because I want to win.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Tonight, police have released the names of the five victims in Saturday shooting a Club Q, an LGBTQ nightclub in Colorado Springs. Daniel Aston, Ashley Paugh, Kelly Loving, Derrick Rump, and Raymond Green Vance. Officials say the tragedy could have been far, far worse if not for the heroic actions of a guy named Richard Fierro, an Army veteran who was at the club with his family taking him to the drag show at the time of the attack. Fierro who served in Iraq and Afghanistan says he went into combat mode as he rushed across the venue to successfully disarm the shooter.\par \par \uc2 This evening, the mayor of Colorado Springs praised Fierro for his heroism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN SUTHERS, MAYOR, COLORADO SPRINGS: I had the opportunity before I came here today to talk to Richard Fierro, identified as one of the two heroes that subdued the suspect in this case. And in my opinion and I think the opinion of everyone involved, saved a lot of lives.\par \par \uc2 I have never encountered a person who had engaged in such heroic actions that was so humble about it. He simply said to me, I was trying to protect my family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The shooter who was in custody has been charged with five counts of first-degree murder, five counts of bias-motivated crime causing bodily injury. The Department of Justice is also investigating whether federal charges are warranted. Now, this attack exists within a number of contexts, including clearly, the crisis of mass shootings in this country spurred on by easy access to weapons of war.\par \par \uc2 We know this shooter used an AR-15-style semi-automatic rifle, the same type of gun used in numerous other deadly incidents of mass violence and atrocities like this one. We also can`t ignore the fact that this attack comes during a particularly fraught and dangerous moment at the gay and trans community. Here`s the last post on Club Q`s \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page prior to the shooting. It was this advertisement for an upcoming all-ages drag brunch, which was scheduled for Sunday, the morning after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 Now, in recent months, drag events, particularly all ages events like that, like brunches, story hours of public libraries have become a point of obsession for right-wing forces seeking to demonize LGBTQ folks and falsely accused them of attempting to indoctrinate straight children. And this has led to an increase in intimidation and threats of violence.\par \par \uc2 Back in June, one example, members the far-right group known as the Proud Boys stormed a drag story hour in California where they have hurled homophobic and transphobic slurs at those in attendance. The sheriff`s office describe the right-wing agitators as "extremely aggressive with a threatening violent demeanor causing people to fear for their safety."\par \par \uc2 And just a few weeks ago, Proud Boys stalked a drag brunch at a North Carolina brewery with one of the event`s organizers saying, "The Proud Boys were very hostile, openly harassing patrons as they entered and exited the brewery. They were calling them groomers, pedophiles, and trying to intimidate everyone. The Proud Boys came to fight."\par \par \uc2 These are just two examples, absolutely the tip the iceberg. The right`s fixation with gay and trans communities is becoming increasingly reckless. Earlier this year, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  infamously signed into law what critics have called the don`t say gay bill which presents -- prevents some teachers from even discussing the existence of gay and trans folks, and in some cases could force school administrators to out trans students.\par \par \uc2 Last month, dozens of House Republicans introduced a national version that law, which is actually worse, if you believe it, manages to be even more draconian than the Florida law and more reckless and wide open. And it seems very likely Republicans will hold a vote on that bill when they formally retake the House in January.\par \par \uc2 And in the run up to the midterm elections, anti-trans rhetoric in particular was constantly used by right-wing politicians and Republicans for all kinds of different offices. Republicans even tried to unsuccessfully fearmonger against Michigan`s referendum enshrining abortion rights by falsely claiming the bill would lead to the forced sterilization of children.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:35]\par \par \uc2 The Michigan ballot proposal passed overwhelmingly. Republicans vastly underperformed in the Midterms after going all-in on anti-trans issues. That has not stopped them. They`re still at it, taking issue with trans people existing at all. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  dedicated a significant amount of time in his third presidential announcement speech to anti-trans broadsides. Trump handpick candidate for Senate in Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , trying to close out his race in the runoff, is making trans women participating in sports as one of his closing arguments, top issue for Georgia voters about who you want to send to the Senate ahead of next month`s runoff election.\par \par \uc2 And it`s important to note however, this is not just elected officials and political candidates, many cases these politicians are taking their cues from right-wing media. Those incidents that drag events I mentioned a few moments ago, they happened within the context of conservative media personalities fear-mongering against queer folks.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  noting, "Events featuring performers in drag have received disapproving coverage of some of the most prominent voices on the political right, including the Fox News host \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and Jesse Watters who frequently claimed the events are dangerous for children. Tucker, in particular, seems fixated on this anti-queer panic. In addition to his diatribes against drag shows, his platformed anti-trans activists use particularly vicious and extreme rhetoric to attack children`s hospitals for providing trans youth from medical care.\par \par \uc2 Just as an example, in one segment of his primetime show, he put up the faces of a Tennessee hospital`s board of directors and read their names aloud to his audience. Now, in this climate, you may not be surprised to hear that threats against these hospitals that have this kind of care on the rise. Boston Children`s, we reported on this, Boston Children`s Hospital receiving its third bomb threat just last week.\par \par \uc2 And again, to be clear, no one is responsible for this weekend`s horrific attack but the shooter himself. But as one Colorado State Legislator reminded my colleague Katie Tur, it happened within the context of this anti-LGBTQ hysteria.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ESGAR: The rhetoric, the awful, visceral rhetoric that has been spread for years, quite frankly, in this community, but also especially a lot lately in the past election that we had. Just a few weeks a few days ago, even, there was so much hate and visceral spread about our LGBTQ communities, especially our trans community that I can`t say that that didn`t have an impact on this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That (INAUDIBLE) is such tragedy feeling shocking, but sadly, not exactly surprising. Chase Strangio is the Deputy Director for Transgender Justice to the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 . \uc2 Sarah Kate Ellis\uc2  is President CEO of \uc2 GLAAD\uc2 , the world`s largest LGBT media advocacy organization. Both joins me now. It`s great to have you both here at the table, though under awful, awful circumstances.\par \par \uc2 First of all, that there`s this profound sense of mourning. I thought that the tributes we started to hear about the folks that work there and what an amazing welcoming safe space it was are sort of testament to what is taken away when violence like this happens.\par \par \uc2 CHASE STRANGIO, DEPUTY DIRECTOR FOR TRANSGENDER JUSTICE, \uc2 ACLU\uc2 : Yes, I mean, I think this occurred just after midnight on Trans Day of Remembrance. So, here we have a day dedicated to honoring the lives of trans people who are killed in acts of violence. Two of the five victims are openly trans individuals. One of the bartenders was a trans man who is being described by friends as this person who was irreverent in his joy.\par \par \uc2 And he has a picture on his \uc2 Instagram\uc2  of himself after he had top surgery celebrating the embodiment that he felt, the very thing that we are seeing being demonized not just in the right-wing media, by the way, but also in the center-left media. And that is contributing to this climate in which people are emboldened to enact violence on the trans community and on the entire LGBTQ community.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I was -- that bartender, I was reading his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed and he was talking about how the place saved his life, Club Q. Like, this place -- I found this place, I love these people here, this place saved my life. And he was one of the people that was killed.\par \par \uc2 SARAH KATE ELLIS\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, \uc2 GLAAD\uc2 : Yes, we have a team that`s on the ground actually in Colorado Springs, and what I`m hearing that is that the community is just so open and welcoming allies and the LGBTQ community. I want to add one more dot because I thought you did a beautiful job of connecting all these dots of the storyline of where this rhetoric is coming from and the impact that it`s having.\par \par \uc2 I think when you look at the latest study that says 51 percent of rise in anti-LGBTQ hate crimes this past year, a study that we do at \uc2 GLAAD\uc2  shows that 71 percent of the LGBTQ community in the past year feels discriminated against. And to the drag shows, we`re about to release a report that shows nearly 125 attacks or threats to drag shows across this country since June.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:26]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Of this year?\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Of this year.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I have to say, I mean, this is the -- this is the inescapable context, this in particular. So, I want to talk about politicians, but I want to talk about this for a second, Chase, because I think, honestly -- and maybe I`m naive or wasn`t paying attention. But if you went to me in 2020 and said, a year and a half from now, a big thing on the right is going to be like whipping up folks to go outside drag brunches and scream at people. And I would have been like, what?\par \par \uc2 It really -- I mean, it seems -- I don`t know, it seems so awful. But it also seems so strange to me. But I guess it`s not strange.\par \par \uc2 STRANGIO: I mean, I think it`s a both end situation. I think that if you trace the rhetoric going back to 2016, and you and I have talked about this back when we saw the rise in anti-trans bathroom bills, part of what we were seeing was this obsession with anything that was gender deviance at all.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 STRANGIO: And if you go back, you know, 50, 75 years, the origin of a lot of this was in the form of criminal cross-dressing laws. And now we`re sort of back full circle where we`re criminalizing the public expression of gender variants at all. And so, it went from bathrooms, to locker rooms, to sports, now to drag and medical care. And this idea of the sort of enemy converting people into these gender deviants, when of course, we`ve been here all along.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, drag is probably one of the oldest traditions in the (INAUDIBLE) that exist. And children have been exposed to it for literally millennia. So, I mean, just as a point of historical fact, it`s like a ludicrous obsession to be like, oh, the pre-modernity.\par \par \uc2 STRANGIO: But also too, going back to the sort of it`s been around forever, the laws that we`re now seeing that proposed to criminalize drag are so broadly written that they would criminalize Shakespeare.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 STRANGIO: You know, it`s -- that`s where we`re at.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also I think -- you know, Chase and I have talked about this before. You know, I think the sense of -- there`s obviously been this focus on trans folks and trans bodies and trans healthcare in the bathrooms, right? I do think there was a certain set of gay and lesbian folks, sis folks who are like, that`s awful, and I`m like with, you know, trans folks, but also like the we won on marriage, we sort of had this incredibly successful set of social and legal victories. And a lot of people I know feel like that is at risk right now.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Well, I want to -- I want to pull back on that a little. We won marriage equality through a Supreme Court that we know now is hostile, so that`s on the line. And then we`ve only have employment protections. They`re kind of pieced together state by state, and then at the federal or through the Supreme Court, as well. And that`s at risk as well.\par \par \uc2 So, I hesitate to say that we have a lot of protections as gay and lesbian, much -- and trans folks obviously have a lot less than that, even. So, we haven`t been able to pass the Equality Act, we haven`t been able yet to codify marriage equality. We have a long way to go. What we have had on our side in terms of gay and lesbian, it`s an accepting society. When nine out of 10 Americans say they know someone who`s gay or lesbian, and that they - - it`s a family member, friend, that makes a much more accepting society.\par \par \uc2 Three out of 10 Americans say they know someone who`s trans. So, that gap right there is where all these politicians are filling in with the rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And you -- Chase, you wrote something after because I had sort of written about it and said on election night, you know, one of the things I found sort of cheering, honestly, of that result was I had watched them obsess over trans folks during the campaign. I felt like there was somewhat of a repudiation of that at the polls. Like, Michigan, you know, that was - - this is -- this is going to carry us over.\par \par \uc2 But you made the point that look in the places where Republicans particularly hold power. Some of the most rapidly anti-trans politicians won reelection paving the way for another year of anti-trans bills and state legislatures and a national landscape of anti-trans rhetoric in the lead-up to 2024 presidential election. And it strikes me, one of the things that`s important to consider that your piece made me think of is, to the extent this is red meat for the base, it is, but they also believe it. Like, they`re pursuing a project which is that they think this is deviant and these people need to be marginalized.\par \par \uc2 STRANGIO: Yes, I mean, absolutely. We`ve seen hundreds of anti-trans bills introduced every year. And in fact, more and more are passing. We`re now in a situation where, you know, Alabama made it a felony to provide medically supported by every major medical association in the United States health care. We`re in a situation now where Texas Governor Abbott, who was reelected, had his executive order that mandated to the agency to investigate the provision of this medically accepted care as child abuse.\par \par \uc2 And now, coming into 2023, Texas is very likely to try to pass that statutorily. And we will see that across the country. So, we`re in a situation where these bills are passing. And of course, people do believe it. I am personally called a groomer every single day. This idea that just by existing and being visible that we are encouraging other people to be like us, when the reality is that our existence and our visible existence, just like the bartender at Club Q helps people feel like it`s okay to be who they are.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:32]\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Yes, for sure. And I would just add to that, the one thing that we haven`t talked about is social media`s play in all of this, because that`s an organizing tool. That`s where they`re finding out about the drag shows and organizing to show up. That`s where they`re building out extremism and grooming people actually to -- for extremism.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that we have to hold the social media platforms accountable. And we need government to also hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Chase Strangio, \uc2 Sarah Kate Ellis\uc2 , thank you so much for coming in. I really, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, he`s taking over the major investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Who is Jack Smith? An in-depth look at the new special counsel next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Throughout his career, Jack Smith has built a reputation as an impartial and determined prosecutor who leads teams with energy and focus to follow the facts wherever they lead. As Special Counsel, he will exercise independent prosecutorial judgment to decide whether charges should be brought. Mr. Smith is the right choice to complete these matters in an even-handed and urgent manner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The announcement of a special counsel overseeing multiple Trump investigations was an assignment editor`s dream, who is this largely unknown character entering this massively compelling and important story? Of course, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  knew that. And so, this weekend, we got our answer in the form of a Jack Smith profile reported by Charlie Savage and Alan Feuer.\par \par \uc2 In the first paragraph of the article, we have the story of Jack Smith`s 2010 appointment to lead the Public Integrity Unit of the Justice Department, and the uproar he caused when he made the decision to close a series of investigations into several members of Congress without charges.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to tell us about that story and what else he found in his profile, Washington Correspondent Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist Charlie Savage. Charlie, it`s great to have you. And this was exactly what I was looking for. Thank you for writing it. Tell us first that story about that decision that you -- that you use in the profile?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SAVAGE, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Sure. So, at the very beginning of the Obama administration, the Public Integrity section of the Justice Department was in turmoil. At the end of 2008, it had brought a prosecution against Ted Stevens, Republican senator of Alaska, who was convicted and lost his reelection.\par \par \uc2 And then during sentencing, it turned out, they had not given the evidence that would have helped his cause to the defense. The case collapsed. Attorney General Holder, throughout the case, the head of that section was under investigation, had to be reassigned. And it was a gigantic mess.\par \par \uc2 So, the head of the Criminal Division calls back from Europe, Jack Smith, who was there prosecuting war crimes in The Hague, and said I need you to take over this section which is in turmoil. And he comes back and one of the first things he does is he looks at all the cases that have been pending about -- against as many as eight members of Congress of both parties for various corruption allegations. And he recommends closing all of those investigations about charges in which makes everyone think this -- he missed this -- this section has lost its nerve after this disastrous Ted Stevens debacle.\par \par \uc2 I was then covering the Justice Department for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and I went in and interviewed him then not knowing, of course, he would now be in this role a decade later, and say, what are you doing? You closed all these investigations. People say you guys have lost your nerve. And he denied it.\par \par \uc2 He said, look, one of my first things I needed to do was look at everything that was pending that I inherited. If stuff was stale, the facts weren`t there, it was just lingering, it`s not fair to keep it going. You had to shut it down and make the tough decision. But it doesn`t mean that I don`t have nerve. No one who knows me knows that I do not have aggression when it comes to bringing cases. And I`m not going to be like well, I know this person did it, but I`m not going to bring the case anyway because we might lose and we might look bad. That`s not me, he said.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re stepping into this complete political cauldron that he is in as of Friday, when \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  makes him the Trump`s Special Counsel. We`ll see exactly what kind of fortitude he has because nothing is like what is about to happen to him.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, the first thought I had when I saw the announcement, right, was like, that`s quite a job. And part of the -- what makes Smith particularly interesting, I think, is his time abroad. If you can talk a little bit about that, because that`s -- you know, there are a lot of very decorated prosecutors that move in and out of government. That aspect of his work is quite distinct, jumps out in his bio, clearly something he loves, he`s done multiple times. How do you think that plays a role in where he`s coming from?\par \par \uc2 SAVAGE: Well, so what you`re referring to is he served two stints as a war crimes prosecutor before international courts in The Hague. Right before he took that job I just talked about in 2010, he spent several years at the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2 . And then more recently, from 2018 to now, he`s been at a different Court in the Hague, a special court that`s working on war crimes from the Kosovo War.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:10]\par \par \uc2 And so, this is not someone who wants to go to a fancy corporate law firm where he can command $1,000 an hour fees and get wealthy. He is someone who clearly wants the big cases. And war crimes cases are some of the most difficult there are because of the difficulty in gathering evidence in battlefield scenarios and witnesses and so forth. It`s not like just investigating a crime on the streets of New York or Chicago.\par \par \uc2 And it`s also a place where your own life could be threatened because you were dealing with global bad actors here, not just random criminals. But it`s something that he clearly felt a calling to do and has done twice. Again, showing this is someone who has perhaps more nerve than the average prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And quickly here. You note this case which he is particularly relevant. He helped oversee the prosecution of Jeffrey A. Sterling, a former CIA officer was convicted of mishandling national security secrets and obstruction of justice. Sterling was convicted in 2015 of the Espionage Act and obstruction statute, notably, both of these -- those are potential charges at the center of the investigation of Mr. Trump`s handling of government documents. That does seem quite directly relevant experience at least as pertains to Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 SAVAGE: That`s true. Both of those are charges that are raised by the holding of documents marked as classified and refusal to return them back even after being subpoenaed for them. One of the two major cases that he`s been handed that involve former President Trump along, of course, with events that culminated in the January 6 riot. So, that is a case. Because that`s the case where journalists are a little leery of Jack Smith, because as part of that case, he was subpoenaing a then-colleague of mine at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  to testify about his sourcing, and the Justice Department won a legal battle to establish that judges don`t have a right to quash subpoenas to reporters.\par \par \uc2 So, we -- that`s certainly not something that makes us stand up and cheer from a vantage point of press freedom rights, but it does show a certain aggression. And I would say the other case I would throw before readers here because I`m sure he`s -- readers -- viewers. I`m sure, he`s going to be attacked by Trump world as oh, you must be, you know, just a liberal ideologues or something if you`re investigating Trump. He is the guy who oversaw the prosecution of \uc2 John Edwards\uc2 , the former Democratic senator in 2004 or vice-presidential nominee.\par \par \uc2 That was the case, of course, that did not work, that collapsed. But it shows maybe, in his defense then, that he`s aggressive and is going to go after hard cases, even if they`re not 100 percent there. So, two cases to think about.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Charlie Savage, thank you so much. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, House Republicans say they will use their narrow lead to punish Democrats. What does that mean for the people who investigated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Former impeachment manager Adam Schiff joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s clear that House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy has made a whole lot of promises in his quest to become speaker, including to Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  of Georgia, who is now whipping votes for her. You`ll remember that Green was stripped of her two committees last year after it came to light she had endorsed violence against Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You see, back in 2019, Green had like a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  comment that suggested, "A bullet to the head would be quicker to remove Pelosi." She also trafficked in insane far-right lies including particularly an antisemitic one that, get this, the California wildfires were a false flag started by a space laser on behalf of the Rothschilds and then Governor Jerry Brown.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, the far right extremists in the House are now scheming for revenge, and Kevin McCarthy is pledging to give it to them by removing, tit for tat as he views it, Democratic -- three Democratic House members from their committees.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  cannot get a security clearance in the public sector. Why would we ever give him a security clearance in the secrets to America? So, I will not allow him to be on Intel. You have Adam Schiff who had lied to the American public time and again. We will not allow him to be on the Intel Committee either. And look, Congresswoman Omar, her antisemitic comments that have gone forward, we`re not going to allow her to be on Foreign Affairs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Joining me now, one of those people, Congressman Adam Schiff, Democrat of California who`s the chairman of the Intelligence Committee, a member of the January 6th Committee. Chairman, you`re accused of lying time and time against the American people, which is apparently disqualifying for being on a committee which I`m not sure about that. I`ve covered a lot of politicians. I don`t think that digs you, but I would like to get your response.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Well, by lying, what Kevin McCarthy is referring to is telling the truth about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and holding him to account, impeaching and leading that trial, being a member of the January 6th Committee. But look, you know, notwithstanding the bogus rationalization, this is about satisfying the lowest elements of his conference the Marjorie Taylor Greenes who want payback, the Gosars, that Matt Gaetzes.\par \par \uc2 And this is what it`s going to be over the next two years which is Kevin McCarthy can`t become speaker without the QAnon caucus in his conference. He will do it \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  tells him to do. And this is just the beginning. And it`s tragic for the American people. I mean, you know, whether I stay as chairman of Intel or I don`t, is not the tragedy. The tragedy is, there are some serious things we need to deal with as a country.\par \par \uc2 And in addition to these sort of petty attacks on people who have held Trump accountable, they`re threatening default on the nation`s credit. They`re threatening government shutdowns if we don`t cut Social Security and Medicare. They want to investigate \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 . I mean, it`s absurd, but we`re going to see that if the Republican Conference is in the same sign, and it looks a lot like one, lunatics are taken over.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:43]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You have this -- as I said last week, voters rejected extremist Republicans at the ballot box. Yet despite that clear repudiation, Republicans are plowing full steam ahead with their conspiracy theory agenda, a political Sideshow for the QAnon base, they are so dangerously out of touch. I want to -- I want to ask about what this -- what this means for governing.\par \par \uc2 There are things the House has to do to keep the government running, right? Raising the debt ceiling, which you mentioned, which they`re already saying, but also passing appropriations bills on a timely matter to keep the -- are you confident that McCarthy can wield this narrow majority to deliver at that threshold level or are those going to be tough big, you know, catastrophes?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: No, not at all confident that he can do even the bare minimum of the job. You know, it`s one thing if you have a large majority, and you can sort of say, well, I can afford to ignore the crazies like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . It`s another if you have just a handful that are keeping you in the Speaker`s chair, and they`re crazy.\par \par \uc2 And you have to go along with crazy in order to do that, to keep that job. And if the crazy wants you to shut down the government, then you have to shut down the government. It`s why \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  walked away from the job. It`s why \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  left the job. And, you know, Kevin McCarthy is nothing if not morally flexible, malleable.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, he will do whatever he`s told to do by Trump and by these extreme elements of this conference. But that will get in the way of just basic governance.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Schiff: And, you know, the American people want their government to work and I just don`t see their ability to even govern their own, let. alone govern the chamber.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to ask about the January 6th Committee and that speeding towards its conclusion. One of the big outstanding questions, of course, is about testimony from two individuals, Vice President and then-Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been subpoenaed and he is challenging that subpoena.\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who was invited and was in negotiations, as far as I understand it, just came out with a book. He`s trying to sell that book everywhere that he can go. And basically is saying, if you want my testimony, you got to pay the 25 bucks for it. But the committee doesn`t get it. Here`s how he described his decision that you don`t get to talk to him. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I never stood in the way of senior members of my team cooperating with the committee and testifying. But Congress has no right to my testimony.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re closing the door on that entirely.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I`m closing the door on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Why do you think he`s wrong when he says Congress has no right to his testimony?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Because it`s -- you know, at its base, the most basic, it`s not about what Congress has a right to -- indeed, we do have a right to his testimony. It`s what is his responsibility to the public and to the country. Former presidents have come and testified before Congress, certainly, a former vice president can.\par \par \uc2 But as you say, no, it`s more important for him to sell his book. It`s really basically his following the \uc2 John Bolton\uc2  model. I can`t come in to testify before Congress for some fictitious reason, but I can put it all in a book and make money from it. You know, look, we have to view everything \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says and does right now through the prism of two things, he wants to run for president. He doesn`t want to alienate the Trump base any more than he has to. And this is his way of walking that line.\par \par \uc2 But it really is a disservice to the country. And for him to parade this as some kind of a sanctimonious decision that they have no right to my testimony, doesn`t of course, answer the question, does he have a responsibility to the country? Certainly, if he wanted to testify, he could. And he is withholding that from the American people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And to the extent there`s any privilege that`s been punctured by the fact he wrote a book about it. He does seem to be planning a 2024 run on the platform of, with the exception of the whole hanging \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  bid, I`m with Trump all the way.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Adam Schiff, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, after the red wave failed to arrive, some Republicans are blaming the Supreme Court`s abortion decision. Why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won`t even say that word next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Five months since the conservative majority in the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade, ending the constitutional right to an abortion in this country, Republicans are facing the aftermath. They`re seeing their position which is that abortion should be outlawed or criminalized rejected over and over from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 The backlash began just weeks after that decision came down at the end of June when Kansas voters overwhelmingly rejected an amendment to their state constitution that would have allowed the legislature to ban abortion. That vote was 59 to 41 in a very red state, and it sent shockwaves through the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:20]\par \par \uc2 This month abortion rights are on the ballot in several more states, California, Montana, Michigan, Kentucky and Vermont, and it was a clean sweep for reproductive rights in all five states. Now, abortion rights activists and other states want to follow suit with their own ballot measures, including in Ohio where a current so-called heartbeat ban went into effect the same day Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 The executive director of Pro-Choice Ohio says of the proposed ballot initiative "It`s a win, it`s not an if." Now, Republicans in the state of Ohio again, conservative state, a state that Trump won by eight points, are obviously worried about putting their deeply unpopular position on abortion up for votes to the people. So, they`re trying to make it harder for the other side to win.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Secretary of State \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2  and Republican Representative Brian Stewart are proposing what they call the Ohio Constitution Protection Amendment. The amendment would make it so a citizen-initiated constitutional amendment on any ballot would have to pass by 60 percent of the vote, rather than the current simple majority.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE, OHIO: If you don`t think that your idea is broadly popular enough to muster 60 percent vote of the people, then then maybe you should not consider bringing it to the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, OK, you`re like, you`re tracking this, right? Pro-choice advocates say we want to put a ballot initiative constitutional amendment on the state. It`s happening in other states. The Republican Secretary of State \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2  says, you know what? I`ve been thinking, let`s make it harder to pass one of those. Now, LaRose claims that proposal has no connection at all to any potential ballot measure on abortion, but he wants to put the amendment up for a vote during the primary in May, likely before an abortion initiative would make it onto the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the best part of this. Their own plan to make it harder to pass future constitutional amendments would require just a simple majority pass. So, if you can get 50 plus one, maybe you can stop the people of Ohio from actually governing themselves where abortion is concerned, which is the point. It is all very transparent.\par \par \uc2 But Ohio Republicans are certainly not the only ones who understand they are on the wrong side of this issue, they will not say what they really think. The man who ultimately delivered that Supreme Court decision on abortion, the criminalization banning of abortion in 13 states of the possibility of a national abortion ban, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that man, will not say a word about it now that he`s back on the presidential campaign trail. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Over the weekend, we saw the first event, believe it or not, of the 2024 Republican presidential primary. It was the Republican Jewish Coalition annual meeting in Las Vegas. There were nearly a dozen speakers, all of whom are clearly interested in running for president including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who was there remotely.\par \par \uc2 There were, as far as I can tell, two big takeaways from that event. The first is that all the non-Trump folks do understand they eventually will have to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , many of them using the event to take some jabs at the ex-president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  turns 80 years old tomorrow. Happy Birthday, Mr. President, but it`s time for a younger generation to lead across the board.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: What is really required as we present the conservative case, as we make the argument which is what matters, we make the argument and win the argument. We don`t simply rail against the machine, that we don`t simply go on Fox News or send tweets.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE\uc2 , FORMER GOVERNOR, NEW JERSEY: We keep losing and losing and losing. And the fact of the matter is the reason we`re losing is because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has put himself before everybody else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  distinguishing himself there by at least summoning the courage to say the name of the man. The other big takeaway from that event, the one that I`m pretty obsessed with and everyone I think getting Republican politics is, is that the fields too big, which can mean a repeat of 2016. The Associated Press flashback to that election`s 17 candidate primary which "allowed Trump to become the party`s presidential nominee, despite winning just 35 percent or less of the vote in each of the three opening primary contests."\par \par \uc2 And if that does happen again, that means the more divided the field, the more Trump brand loyalty matters. And that is the one thing the ex- president has actually been good at. As Jelani Cobb writes in the New Yorker, "His talent lies not in organizational leadership or shepherding a novel concept from its inception to a place of prominence but, rather, in marketing.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now is Jelani Cobb, staff writer from The New Yorker and Dean of the Columbia University School of Journalism. Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 JELANI COBB, STAFF WRITER, THE NEW YORKER: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You wrote about the sort of enduring power of Trumpism. You write this. You said Trump did not single-handedly inject the strains of intolerance, racism, nativism, belligerence, and a durable sympathy for anti-democratic behavior into the Republican Party. There`s no reason to believe that his absence would cause them to evaporate.\par \par \uc2 It strikes me that part of his challenge is that because everyone is so much like him now, it`s harder for him to distinguish himself.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Sure, you know, although they have their own problems, because you remember when John Huntsman thought that he could just easily separate himself from \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and then run against Obama? Well, you know, the people who you`re talking about here have all been closely tied to Trump, if not in his cabinet. And how do you justify then going after him and how hollow does that sound?\par \par \uc2 You know, and at the same time, Trump is performing a trick that the audience has seen before. You know, magic tricks work once the first time the crowd sees them. And so, it places this kind of conundrum in terms of like how this law will shake out.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:26]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There is this this collective action problem which they had the first time around, and it`s wild to watch it again, right? Really, I was like, looking at the reports and \uc2 RNC\uc2 , I was like, hey, guys, can we get in a room here? Can we figure this out. But it really does feel -- it`s a real Groundhog`s Day sensation setting in right.\par \par \uc2 COBB: So, the smoke-filled rooms came about for a reason.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 COBB: It came about because people realized that, you know, conventions could get away from them. They could wind up with candidates that were completely unelectable. You could wind up with all kinds of political disasters in those ways. And if you recall, in 2016, the Republicans was selling this as a virtue. They were saying, look, our process is completely open. We`re not trying to shepherd any favorite son or favorite daughter candidates the way the Democrats are. And we see how that turned out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And the -- and the smoke-filled room also solved that coordination problem.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Sure.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is what political parties for years have done, which was to solve coordination problems.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Right. That`s what they do. I mean, the reasons that people have political bosses is that kind of needed somebody that you`re a boss. That to say that machine politics are great or any of those kinds of things, but I`m saying that there is a political --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Logic.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Right, there`s a logic to why that exists.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The other thing that I keep thinking about as I watched them plunge the country back into this, is how much this could have been avoided, which is, if \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  comes out in that second impeachment hearing and says I am voting to convict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I whipping for it, there would have been unbelievable histrionics and anger in the Republic base. But they could have convicted him and under the U.S. Constitution, stopped him from running again.\par \par \uc2 And whatever anger, it would have, I think, dissipated and they would just not have this problem now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Sure. But I think it`s all -- like, the entire Trump phenomenon has been the kind of short-term pain versus dealing with the kind of long-term prospects. There`s been not been very much long-term thinking associated with it. Especially if you look at, you know, the fact that they lost in the Midterms in 2018, lost in 2020, lost in 2022. It`s not until the third election that people began to actually say, OK, maybe the price for this is too high.\par \par \uc2 And so, the fact of it is like, you know, the reason it was so easy to get the predictions wrong in this midterm cycle, was that nobody was expecting people to actually feel that there were any consequences regardless of how the Republican Party fared in that election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You`re the head of a journalism school right now at a moment of -- crucial moment of crisis for American democracy or testing. And I think there`s this question right after the midterms where most of the candidates sort of slunk away. Some gave gracious concessions, some good passive- aggressive ones. Kari Lake is the exception in Arizona. She is essentially trying to rerun Trump. She`s -- videos, we`re going to fight, etcetera.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the status of democracy and Trumpism? And particularly lake as his sort of emissary here testing whether that has legs that`s in someone other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 COBB: Sure. So, I think the thing is that irrespective of what you might think about him, you know, politically what he stood for, Trump possessed a certain kind of charisma, malignant charisma, you know, as it may be, but a certain kind of --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Unquestionable.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Right. The kind of flair to be able to do the things that he did. He was a salesman for all those years. Simply being belligerent and simply being antagonistic toward various ethnic groups or various religions and whatever, that`s not the entirety of the Trump sauce. And so, I think that`s why people haven`t succeeded with it. People kept trying to replicate it, but no one has really done it the way that he`s done it.\par \par \uc2 And so that in and of itself doesn`t mean the threat to democracy is over, it just means you don`t get to replicate Trump in that way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s such an important point. It is also I think, what Kari Lake sticks out, right, because she does have this background as a TV presenter, because she is obviously an effective communicator, far more effective than many of the other replacement levels, Trump wannabes.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And why I think this term that she`s taken us is such an important test and so dangerous, right? Do people rally behind this because there`s an appetite for this or does she go down as a as a loser, you know. And I think that`s really an open question.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Sure. Well, here`s the thing, though, right? We talked about individual candidates. What happens if you have a rogue legislature?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COBB: That`s the more dangerous proposition. An individual candidate that doesn`t want to concede, that`s bad, you know, that`s bad form. You know, eventually there may be even a backlash for that person politically. But what do you do if a handful of people in a swing state decide that they don`t want the election to go the way they actually went?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Jelani Cobb of Columbia University School of Journalism and the New Yorker among other illustrious associations, great to have you.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221122eibl00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for November 16, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Michelle Goldberg, Carlos Curbelo, Ali Velshi \par \uc2 8,400 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT." ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I honestly believe that we`ll have better choices come 2024. You know, I don`t think anybody - -\par \par \uc2 STEVE DOOCY, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Better choices than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A low-energy rollout literally send supporters running for the exits.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KARL\uc2 , CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, ABC NEWS: Perhaps a little concerned that hallway -- the hall would empty out too much. They actually started preventing people from leaving. So, now they`re no longer allowing people to leave.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the diminishing returns of a disgraced ex-president and the coordinated movement to stop him.\par \par \uc2 Then, Marc Elias returns on the successful midterm defense of democracy and where the problems still exist.\par \par \uc2 And the voting isn`t over yet.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I don`t know if you know vampires are cool people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The genuinely high stakes of the Warnock-Walker run-off in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: A werewolf to kill a vampire. Do you know that? I never knew that, so I don`t want to be a vampire than I wanted to be a werewolf.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. I`m going to start tonight with a hot take. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not actually good at politics. In fact, you could argue there`s no bigger loser in American politics than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean, just look at his record, loss after loss after loss. Even the one win in 2016 when he won the presidency, remember, he lost the popular vote by nearly 2.9 million votes. It`s like two percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2018, the first Midterm Elections, his party lost control the House. Democrats gained a net of 40 seats that year. Of course, in 2020, he lost both the Electoral College, 306 to 232, and the popular vote with seven million more Americans choosing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then just last week, the de facto head of the Republican Party once again, for the third election in a row, presided over a wildly disappointing loss. This one in Midterm. Democrats will retain control of the Senate and Republicans will take the House by a tiny, tiny sliver of margin. So, whatever political poll \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have once had, it does seem clear it`s just no longer working.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that`s not stopping the ex-president from launching yet another bid for the White House from his exile at Mar-Lago last night. It was largely the same old nonsense, the same stick. There`s not a lot of new material, rigged election, fake news, making America great again. But the hour-long speech was a brutal slog. I mean, really, like really low energy, so much so that even Fox News at one point got bored and cut away for more than 10 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s very, very much hurting our country. Germany tried it. You know, Germany tried it. They were up for about a year. Remember, I sent to Angela. Remember, Angela? Do you remember Angela? Nobody`s remembering her now. \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: If you`re just joining us, President Trump at Mar-a-Lago announcing his 2024 presidential run. We`re going to get back to the speech in just a minute. Let`s get some quick snap reaction first from Pete Hegseth who`s been watching along with us. Pete, he went through every issue of success, every issue of failure as it relates to the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Sean, you`re killing me. The \uc2 Angela Merkel\uc2  crowd work was amazing. Why would you cut away from that? And we would play some of the highlights of the President`s speech but there`s really -- there`s honestly nothing to highlight. Like, you`ve seen this a million times. The most interesting part of the entire announcement was who did and did not attend. Trump`s eldest daughter Ivanka, a top adviser in his administration, along with her husband, was most notable absence.\par \par \uc2 In a statement that she gave basically, during this speech, I think, she said she`s quitting the game altogether. "I love my father very much. This time around, I am choosing to prioritize my young children, the private life we are creating as a family. I do not plan to be involved in politics."\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., probably his father`s biggest cheerleader, also missed the big announcement. Source tells The Daily Beast he was busy on a hunting trip in the Mountain West and couldn`t get a flight out to make it back to the speech because of bad weather. Tough break. In fact, 16-year-old Barron and \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  were the only two of Trump`s five children in attendance. And the ex-president actually highlighted that during the speech.\par \par \uc2 Trump did manage to gather a small crowd of fans in the ballroom at Mar-a- Lago, but as ABC \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  reported, even they couldn`t make it through the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARL: I actually saw people trying to leave -- and people leaving early even before he was done. He`s still speaking now. And then they, I think, perhaps a little concern that the hallway -- the hall would empty out too much, they actually started preventing people from leaving.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s not just the people in the ballroom that were running for the exits, the fawning right-wing media is doing the same, turning on Trump as well. You`ve seen it. This morning, the \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 -owned New York Post relegated the news of the announcement to a banner at the bottom of the front page, cheekily, "Florida man makes announcement." Buried on page 26 was a brief article headlined, "Been there done that." It reads in part, "If elected, Trump would tie \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as the oldest president to take office. His cholesterol levels are unknown, but his favorite food is charred steak with ketchup." Caddy.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:30]\par \par \uc2 Now, over on Fox News, another branch of the Murdoch empire, they have pretty clearly tried to move on from Trump. Again, Murdoch runs an empire that he controls and he tells them what to do and they`re doing it. Get this. They didn`t even include -- this is the funniest graphic I`ve seen in cable news in literally years. They did not include the former president in their list of 13 potential 2024 candidates. See they`re in the right corner? I think that`s Asa Hutchinson made the cut, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And they appear to already have a clear favorite among the field, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , of course, who they call the new Republican Party leader in this recent op-ed. Today, this was the headline in the conservative National Review, "Different year, same guy, same stuff." OK, now, I know you`re saying because I`ve been saying it too, right? I know. Everyone feels deja vu. Frankly, I can`t believe we`re all going to go through this again. I had -- it was seven years ago, I was sitting at the studio with down the escalator, the whole thing, the empty podiums. We have literally been here before.\par \par \uc2 And it`s also easy to forget this part of it because Republicans all jumped on the Trump train. They all humiliated themselves. They all prostrated themselves so intensely before him. But in the very beginning, that summer 2015 when he came down the escalator, the conservative media was broadly against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when he announced his first run for president.\par \par \uc2 Remember this? At the very first Republican debate, Rupert Murdoch`s Fox News was hosting it. And then \uc2 Fox\uc2  host Megyn Kelly grilled him about his misogynistic statements.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEGYN KELLY, FORMER HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: You`ve called women you don`t like fat pigs, dogs, slobs, and disgusting animals. Your \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Only Rosie O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: You once told a contestant on Celebrity Apprentice it would be a pretty picture to see her on her knees. Does that sound you like the temperament of a man we should elect as president?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: God, that seems like another lifetime, right? You can hear the crowd laughing. It wasn`t even going the way Murdoch and Kelly, I think, wanted to go in that moment in the moment. Because the Murdoch machine very clearly intended to take Trump down the last time, and it didn`t work. The crowd was like, yes, we love that, calling them fat pigs.\par \par \uc2 And once he started winning, the only calculation that \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and every other Republican leader was making was whether or not Trump would help them gain power. That`s it. They never had or cared about any moral red line he might cross, how it reflects on them, their eternal souls, the judgment of history, yadda, yadda, yadda.\par \par \uc2 That`s true, even after he tried to overthrow the government on January 6, led a violent mob to the Capitol that chanted hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . None of that mattered or matters. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said it very plainly in the interview earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SWAN, NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, AXIOS: You said Donald Trump`s actions preceding the January 6 insurrection were a "disgraceful dereliction of duty" and that he was practically and morally responsible.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Well, as the Republican Leader of the Senate, it should not be a front page headline that will support the Republican nominee for president.\par \par \uc2 SWAN: After you`ve said that about him, I think it`s astonishing.\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: I think I have an obligation to support the nominee of my -- of my party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. So, the only question about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that matters to Republicans, to \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 , and the conservative media and all that is whether he will hurt or help their quest for power. Does having Trump on their team helped them in their ability to cut corporate taxes or not. That`s it. Seven years ago, they thought he was going to hurt their chances to cut corporate taxes. But then he won in what was essentially a fluke election, where again, he lost by nearly three million votes. Republicans and arguably everyone overinterpreted that fluke and just hitch the ride with Trump. He`s the future. He`s our political Messiah.\par \par \uc2 Well, now they have basically lost three elections in a row. It`s like they -- sometimes they`ll call up a young rookie in the major leagues, at the first game they hit a homer, the first week they`re incredible. And then it`s like after awhile, it`s like, oh, they can`t hit a curveball. That person can`t play in the Major Leagues. They`re actually bad at baseball.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, in the interim, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also has failed miserably on every substantive level. He got a lot of people killed from COVID unnecessarily, broke the law left and right, mounted as we noted a violent insurrection against \uc2 United States government\uc2  for the first time ever. None of that was ever going to make them jump off the Trump train. What`s finally pushing them off now is the fact they are right back where they were in 2015 when he came down the golden escalator. Everyone thought he was a loser who would be bad for the Republican Party`s quest for power. Now, seven years later, the question is, can they do anything about it this time?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:26]\par \par \uc2 In 2016, the biggest challenge was the party was divided, couldn`t rally around any single Trump challenger. Now, they have \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a candidate they are already trying to get the whole party behind. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not doing himself any favors by running on the same old nonsense. Just listen to his former senior adviser, a guy who`s recently convicted on two counts of criminal contempt of Congress for refusing to comply with a subpoena from the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , TRUMP ADVISOR: Wait until the House of Representatives the next year when we have a real January 6 Committee, and we`re going to adjudicate --\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 BANNON: Oh, no, we`re going to adjudicate all of 3 November.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You don`t really believe (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 BANNON: 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Do you seriously think it was stolen still?\par \par \uc2 BANNON: 100 percent it was stolen.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: People stormed Capitol over the (BLEEP) dude. These are -- these are lies.\par \par \uc2 BANNON: That was not by Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That guy, there with his T-shirts, with his two convictions, two years later, they got the same tired schtick. It feels like it was already a washed-up campaign before it even began.\par \par \uc2 Tim Miller is a former spokesman for the \uc2 RNC\uc2 , writer-at-large for The Bulwark, and a guest host in the last season of The Circus -- latest season of The Circus on Showtime. Michelle Goldberg is an opinion columnist for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  where she just wrote a piece on how you should not buy into the Trump-DeSantis smackdown.\par \par \uc2 Tim, since we just played that great tape of you talking to \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , let me go to you first. I mean, we`re all, I think, creeped out by this deja vu feeling. I`m seeing people talk about how it`s making them feel nauseous or dizzy, or it`s like they`re stuck in some vortex. But there do seem to be crucial differences this time even if they don`t -- you don`t see them or hear them from like Bannon or Trump. Do you think there are crucial differences this time?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I think there`s one crucial difference that you talked about, which is that the off-ramp from Trump is one that Republicans can be comfortable with. And so, you know, going all the way back to, you know, I guess when Marco and Donald were comparing hand size on the debate stage, was kind of the last time that Republicans had an off-ramp for Trump that wasn`t Trump -- or that wasn`t a Democrat, rather.\par \par \uc2 And it`s hard for these folks because of their quest for power, and because their cynicism to just come to terms with the fact that they might have needed to be for a Democrat one time or two before going back. Very few are willing to do it. Now, they have an off-ramp that`s \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who gives them everything they want really. He gives them the tax cuts they want. He gives them the lib-trolling that they want.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that is, you know, created a -- to steal the David Axelrod term of permission structure for people -- for enough people to say, yes, OK, I`ve had enough of this guy. That said, so far all the people we`ve heard from, all the people that you`ve listed who have said that they`re taking that exit ramp where people that weren`t with him last time, which you pointed out in your -- in your intro.\par \par \uc2 You know, so the judgment for me is I want to wait to hear for people who were MAGA people last time saying they`ve had enough. Because if you were a \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  supporter last time or a Jeb or Ted Cruz supporter and you`ve had enough, well, obviously, OK. Those were going to be the first people off of -- off of the Trump train, right?\par \par \uc2 So, I think that the big question about whether we`re going into a total deja vu or not is whether your rank and file MAGA voter, the people that were there at that Kari Lake and \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  rally that I was at, whether those people are ready for the show to end. And I`m still skeptical that they are but I think we`ll learn more in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, I`m glad you said the show to end because that gets to what Michelle wrote -- what you wrote your column about him, which to me is the other big part of this, right, which is the attentional magnetism of the spectacle, the car wreck spectacle that was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2015-2016, has been all along. The fact that there`s a huge difference between you like someone and whether you like watch them do something insane.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sort of exploited that gap to a point that I think we all learn the wrong lesson about politics, which is like, oh, it doesn`t matter if someone`s like, a tremendous jerk. Like, that`s how politics works now. And we just saw that tested in the Midterms. It didn`t work. But the attentional draw is a real outstanding question to me. What do you think about that aspect of it this time around?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think in terms of the sixth sense of deja vu, I mean, this is a do-over in a lot of ways. And I think that those of us in the media have to think about our own role in 2015 and 2016 when the Republican primary was going on and it was this kind of, you know, car wreck spectacle of watching Trump bulldoze through the Republican Party and bestow insulting nicknames on his competitors.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the point -- it`s not that I don`t think -- you know, look, Trump is a petulant narcissist. I`m sure that his resentment of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Glenn Youngkin in Virginia is real. But I also think that we should not once again get sucked into covering politics like it is pro-wrestling and kind of letting Trump write the narrative in which his various interpersonal feuds are, you know, sort of the central story.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:15]\par \par \uc2 The central story, I think, even if it`s boring to repeat it, is that he is running for president in part to evade indictment in the numerous investigations into him, including for helping to incite and insurrection. His company is currently -- there`s currently a fraud trial about the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  going on in New York. And you know, there`s just this tsunami of corruption behind him.\par \par \uc2 And in some sense, everybody knows that. But I do think it was a mistake in 2015 and 2016 to focus so much on his, you know, kind of schoolyard insults, and maybe not enough on his various pyramid schemes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think that`s a great point. I also do think that -- and Tim live this because he was working on another campaign in that primary. You know, there`s always been the problem -- this is something that we`ve tried to get our arms around, right, the sheer avalanche of news and scandal, keeping track of them all the time.\par \par \uc2 I do think to your point there, and Tim, we`ve been a democracy for 230- plus years. There`s only one man who`s ever really tried to frontally assault and end constitutional governance in American democracy. His name is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now, the South seceding sort of the other big example, but like, that`s it. Like, that`s the -- so, in the old grand scheme of the things, the guy tried to end our Republic as we know it. He tried to murder constitutional governance. Like, that`s the top of the bio. That`s the first time in the open. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. And that has to be the headline every time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. But Tim, I guess, my --\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: That`s what -- and I hope we`ve learned our lesson that we make sure that that`s the emphasis and not -- you know, the spectacle last night, like you said, was you extremely low energy. But I think that, you know, I think Trump does have a feral instinct for media attention and will know that if he insults the right people or picks the right fights, he might get some eyeballs, but hopefully not as sort of unthinkingly as before.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Tim, I mean, that point about Trump trying to end constitutional governance, I know that`s not the point that`s going to make a difference in Republican primary, sadly. The question is whether the folks over there can coordinate effectively enough to stop him this time around.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, you hate to reward the cynical jerks who for seven years have gone around -- gone along with his attempts to end democracy and are now coming around now. And so, they certainly don`t get any awards from this. But I will say, in a weird way, Donald Trump`s anti-democratic attacks is part of the reason why these Republicans are able to move away from them. Not because of the morals and the ethics of it, but because people were so grossed out and turned off and offended by the fact that he was going to do -- that he actually tried this.\par \par \uc2 And the Republicans losses in the Midterm, the more you went along with the big lie, the more you went along with his frontal assault on democracy, the worst you did. And that is a lesson that the Republican strategists have learned. And so, it might be a cynical pivot, but there`s something to be said for. You know, maybe it`s not a direct correlation, but it definitely is an indirect correlation with why he`s vulnerable right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s a huge point and part of why that -- the election was so important and those defeats particularly of candidates like Joe Kent in Oregon that Michelle wrote a great dispatch from, that those defeats were so key to turning them towards that in an amoral sense, just pure politics about whether it helps or not.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tim Miller and Michelle Goldberg, I enjoyed that conversation. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, the makeup of the next House of Representatives now coming into focus and what will a narrow margin of victory means for Republicans, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Earlier tonight, we finally got a call on control of the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 . It comes just over a week after the Midterm Elections, which is a long period of time. The NBC News decision desk projects Republicans will control the House in the next Congress with at least 218 seats, a very, very slim majority. There are eight remaining uncalled seats that will determine just how narrow the margin will be for Republicans and potentially if House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy becomes speaker.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  is a former Republican congressman from Florida, and he joins me now. And Carlos, I really wanted to have you on tonight to talk about this news because there`s been so much discussion of the Freedom Caucus and the Gosars and the Biggs and the Marjorie Taylor Greenes. The majority making caucus here, of course, will be the 12 or so members representing Biden districts who are coming into Congress.\par \par \uc2 You are one of those, right? I mean, you were in a swing district. You are a frontline member who lost your seat in 2018. How do those folks figure in the calculations of how McCarthy and the caucus as a whole operate in the majority?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2 , MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Chris, those majority makers, for the time being, are supporting Kevin McCarthy. This tends to happen. The majority makers are the ones that tend to get the most attention from leadership, the most support in trying to win their elections. That means campaign contributions. So, you (AUDIO GAP) stay with McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 Where those members will be critical is if McCarthy fails to get the votes. And by the way, you have \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  calling members of the House, other Republicans saying that there`s no way McCarthy has the votes, that he can guarantee it. We`ll see if that`s true or not. But where those majority makers can make a big difference is if McCarthy doesn`t get the votes, what happens next?\par \par \uc2 A candidate to the right of McCarthy is probably not going to get the support of those majority makers. And that`s really where you can get into a situation where the House could fall into chaos. And it`s not necessarily a bad chaos, because what you could get is the first speaker at least ever, or not in a long time that`s elected with bipartisan support. And that would be a game changer in American politics.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:48]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, that would. I think that remains not a likely scenario, although a possible one. If -- let`s say McCarthy does win the speakership, right? So, look -- or whoever it is, right? Here`s my big question. When I -- when it looked possible that Republicans were going to have a 30-seat majority, it seemed essentially inevitable they would, for instance, impeach \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That the cries to do that from the base, the way that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be sort of urging them to do that from the sidelines, it would be impossible for McCarthy to hold them back even if he thought it was bad politics.\par \par \uc2 With a five-vote margin, that seems way dicier to me. I`m just speculating. But as someone who occupied precisely the kind of seats that would be imperiled by that vote, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: So, Chris, let me just give you some perspective on how difficult the situation in the House is going to be for Republicans. In 2015, when I started my service, \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  was a speaker. He had 248 Republicans, and that Republican conference was ungovernable. So, Boehner left and left it to \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and said, take this, I`m done.\par \par \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  started the next Congress with 240 Republicans and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  was a little more tactful and had more patience, though, he had an easier job. But still, there were government shutdowns, there were all sorts of difficult situations that \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  was put in. And that was a fairly healthy majority with 240 members. 220, 221, I mean, when you look back at the recent history of the House Republican Conference, it`s going to be a very difficult job for Kevin McCarthy or for whoever ends up getting it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But what does that cash out to in terms of what they can and can`t do? I mean, that`s my real question, right? Like, I was scared of the worst, like, they`re going to default on the debt ceiling, they`re going to impeach everyone, they can put -- you know, they`re going to have a special committee, special Hunter Biden laptop committee. Like, whatever -- you know, whatever that is, like, how much does that -- the narrowest majority shrink the realm of possible things they can undertake?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Yes, without a doubt, some of this hard right stuff is going to be out of bounds because those centrists members are going to have a lot of power themselves. And they are not going to allow this type of policy to move forward. I think what you can expect, a lot of which -- I think most Americans would appreciate, is the Republican governors group, which is the caucus for centrist Republicans in the House, to work a lot with the New Democrats, which is a caucus of centrist Democrats, to try to drive the chamber and to try to move policy that can get support from both sides, which of course, is compatible with the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, you might move into a situation where the House isn`t passing all these messaging bills that although most of them never become law, they`re still, you know, kind of destructive to the fabric of the country, divisive. And instead, you`ll get a house that passes bills that are built from the center out and that, you know, most Americans can get behind.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, I continue to think that is unlikely, but it is one possibility. The big test for me, Congressman, is abortion. Like, HR1 is basically -- like, a natural abortion ban of some kind is HR1 in every Republican Congress. Usually, HR1, 2 up there. Do we see that coming out of the gate here? That`s one thing to look for, as an early indicator.\par \par \uc2 Former Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the Republicans ran election deniers for important positions across the country and nearly all of them were defeated. So, what should we feel about the stability of American democracy in the aftermath of that? Election lawyer Marc Elias joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D-AZ), GOVERNOR-ELECT: Republicans and Democrats will have an open door to my office so we can get to work, find bipartisan compromise, and deliver for the people of Arizona. But for those of you who prefer to obstruct, spread misinformation, and continue to pursue an extreme agenda out of touch with this state, take note of the results of this election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s the next governor of Arizona, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . It`s been two days since NBC News called the Arizona governor`s race for the Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , the current Secretary of State. She defeated the Trump-endorsed election denier, former TV broadcaster Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:01]\par \par \uc2 Now, after the call, Lake who has yet to concede, tweeted, "Arizonans know BS when they see it." Maybe not the best thing to tweet after you lost but it is striking that this one snarky tweet from Lake is basically the most we have seen her deny the results of this election. In fact, it`s basically the most we`ve seen from just about any high-profile Big Lie candidate who lost the cycle.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the overarching fears of this election was that the candidates running as election deniers would attempt to undermine and subvert the results if they lost. But by and large the election was well- run, the final votes are being counted, and largely the losers have accepted their defeat. So, what does that mean about how the system performed?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the people who`s manning the barricades, Marc Elias, expert voting rights litigation, founder of the progressive media platform, Democracy Docket. He`s got a new piece on how election denyers lost, but will the GOP change.\par \par \uc2 Mark, as someone who we have been in constant contact with in the run-up to the election around election litigation around election deniers, what`s your takeaway about what this says about the stability, the robustness, the health of the system?\par \par \uc2 MARC ELIAS, FOUNDER, DEMOCRACY DOCKET: Well, Chris, it means that democracy won the Midterms. The system withstood the pressure put on it by election deniers, put on it by meritless litigation, put on it by people sticking out drop boxes in body armor. But we don`t know what the future is. So, while I am relieved by the outcome of this election, we have to stay vigilant and see whether this is going to cause Republicans take a step back and reevaluate their adherence to the Big Lie, or whether this is just a gathering and teaching moment for them to perpetrator for next election cycle.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Are you personally surprised that we haven`t seen more of the stuff like Kari Lake`s tweet that \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who basically kind of went into hiding for a few days, and then basically, you know, sort of conceded not in a particularly classy way, saying what (INAUDIBLE) but basically said, we lost. Are you surprised by that?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Yes, I was surprised by it, honestly. You know, the Republican establishment and their legal teams and the people, you know, who were paying attention to \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  were gathering and seem to be gaining a lot of energy. And then they seem to lose a lot of cases right before election day, had some setbacks on election day. And then it`s like they walked off the playing field.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s a good thing for democracy, so I`m not complaining. But it does seem like after they sort of took the first punch, they just sort of went and turned tail and left.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, I should say the normal way this works is when a political party gets punched as it were, particularly by the -- by the voters, right, and it happens to Democrats all the -- you know, same way to happen as Republicans. I`ve been through those elections where Democrats like, oh. They tend to not say, well, we need to change the rules, we need to change the voting machines.\par \par \uc2 The correct normal way the system is supposed to work is like, what was wrong with our message? What was wrong with our candidates? What was wrong with our strategy? It does seem like there`s much more of that discussion this time around than there was last time.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Absolutely. And look, that`s to be celebrated. You know, the piece that you allude to that I wrote today, begins by me pointing out that, you know, I was on the phone with \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  when he conceded in 2004. I was literally with Secretary Clinton the night of the election when she conceded and sent \uc2 John Podesta\uc2  to deliver the news to the faithful.\par \par \uc2 And I know what it`s like for candidates to face that moment and have to engage in what is a very difficult thing for them for the sake of democracy, and for the sake of the future of our country. And you`re right, that`s normal. That`s not abnormal. But we didn`t expect it because that isn`t what we`ve seen out of Republicans. That it`s not how they organize themselves for this election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s take --\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: It`s good that we saw, but it doesn`t mean it`s a guarantee for the future. And, Chris, I`d also be remiss if I didn`t point out that if you look at the House of Representatives, a lot of election deniers did win. In fact, the Republican conference right now is basically one big gathering of election deniers. So, it`s not all good news, at least at the congressional level.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about sort of outstanding questions, including in the runoff in Georgia. There`s that new Georgia law that was passed after the last election that changed election administration in that state. Lots of parts of that -- many activist lawyers and others have objected to including yourself who sued. Other parts of it seemed like more sort of common sense technocratic changes.\par \par \uc2 But there`s this controversy now about whether Saturday early voting is allowed or required by the law before the runoff. Explain to me the issue here.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Yes, this is really a brewing scandal, frankly, that is deserves a lot more attention. So, there is a law in Georgia that predates the new law, predates the changes that changed the runoff that talks about when you can and cannot have Saturday voting after a holiday. Well, it`s clear from the context of the new law when we set the runoff in December that that law was not intended to prevent Saturday voting after Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 In fact, if you -- Chris, if you go back to 2020 when that law was in effect, because that log took effect in 2016, there was Saturday voting in the -- on the Saturday after Christmas. So, the fact is, the state of Georgia has a choice. And they took the position in 2020 that voting after Christmas on Saturday was OK. Secretary Raffensperger and \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2  told the media, it`s on tape, after the -- after this November election, that there would be Saturday voting in many counties.\par \par \uc2 And then in sort of the dark of night, over the weekend, last weekend, for whatever reason, the Republican Secretary of State changed tunes and said no, no, there now can`t be Saturday voting. Well, that`s preposterous. And if the Republican Party wants to show itself as having turned a leaf and is no longer about voter suppression but it`s about empowering voters, this is their chance.\par \par \uc2 This is their chance to take the common sense interpretation of this law, the version -- the interpretation that was in place in 2020, and join with us in this lawsuit in insisting that counties can have voting on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It seems sensible to me. Let people vote whoever they are. Marc Elias, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: All right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, Republican star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is back on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: The other night, I watched this movie. I was watching that movie called Fright Night, Freak Night, some type of night, but it`s about vampires. I don`t know if you know vampires are cool people, are they not? But I`m going to tell you something that I found out. A werewolf could kill a vampire. Do you know that? I never knew that, so I didn`t want to be a vampire anymore. I wanted to be a werewolf.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I kid you not. He spent the next two minutes trying to explain the plot of that vampire movie. So, what`s next for the Georgia runoff? And why does \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  have a real shot at being a U.S. senator? Ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s been less than a month since Elon Musk, the world`s richest man, purchased \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And he`s gone about demoing the platform with remarkable speed and recklessness, firing about half the company`s employees in that time with just an email. Just today, Musk then sent the remaining workers a truly bizarre memo giving them an ultimatum. Click the box to "commit to being extremely hard, core work long grueling hours, or quit."\par \par \uc2 Now, this is not the first time an incredibly rich person has purchased a media platform and run it into the ground. Sadly, that`s a story of this time. But this is still one of the most bizarre and in many ways unnerving, impossibly important spectacles in American public life right now. So, for my podcast, Why Is This Happening, I wanted to talk to someone who knows Silicon Valley knows, Elon Musk to help make sense of what on earth is going on.\par \par \uc2 And Kara Swisher was kind enough to join me. She`s known Elon Musk for a while and says his brain has essentially been poisoned by right-wing media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER, PODCASTER: He was never -- he was never particularly political. He just didn`t trust anybody, I would say. He would be, you know, sort of a -- he definitely was an independent. But you know, when he was -- when he joined that Trump council, I wrote him. I`m like, don`t do it. You`re letting your credibility with this guy and he`s just bad on many accounts, including immigration, and you`re an immigrant.\par \par \uc2 And he was like, I can change his mind. I can be there. And you know, he had hope. And I was like, no, you can`t. You can`t. Not this guy. I would agree with you. You should try to do that with reasonable people. This guy isn`t reasonable. And so, he`s very thoughtful in that regard.\par \par \uc2 Honestly, this is not the person -- it`s -- a lot of people will tell you that. And I`m -- we`re not friends. I`ve covered him a lot. I`ve interviewed him half a dozen times. Probably the most extensive interviews have been with me that he`s done. I -- this is not the guy I know that I can see. It`s the bad -- I`ve seen glimpses of this, and now it`s taken over his brain. It`s sort of Invasion of the Body Snatchers in that regard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It was a really fascinating interview with Kara Swisher who is one of a kind. It`s available now wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In the state of Georgia, one man has received the most votes for U.S. senator in three consecutive elections in just the last two years. How is that possible? Well, that man is United States Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . He got the most votes when the seat came up in a 2020 Special Election. But since neither candidate hit 50 percent, Georgia requires a runoff. And so, Warnock had to win it the second time in January 2021. You see, he won it there.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, that seat was up because it was a special election, only a two-year term. And once again, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  got the most votes. But again, not 50 percent. And so, yet another runoff. On December 6, he will try to win for the fourth time against the same bad Republican candidate that he beat last week, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, for the runoff, Republicans are going to have to adjust their messaging a bit. You may recall the lead-up to the Midterm Election, this is how Republicans campaign for Walker.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): If we can win in Georgia, it`s over for them. Do you realize that, that the pathway to the majority in the Senate runs through the state of Georgia? Herschel beats Warnock, game set match.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that ship has sailed. Democrats already won the majority. So, the question for Georgia voters now isn`t which party do you want controlling \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , it`s whether this is the guy you want representing you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: And if we were ready for the green agenda, I raised my hand right now. But we`re not ready right now. So, don`t let them fool you like this is a new agenda. This is not a new agenda. We`re not prepared. We`re not ready right now. What we need to do is keep them those gas guzzling cars until we get emission on old cars. We`re doing the best thing that we can.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:19]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The thing is, this will probably still be a neck-and-neck race. This time around, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  will not have Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  on the ballot with him, which probably helped him out. Democrats are already mobilizing for Warnock who is a star in the party with a very bright future.\par \par \uc2 Fred Hicks is Atlanta based political strategist who`s worked on hundreds of campaigns in Georgia. And he joins me now. Fred, the big factor here that people are pointing to is this is a special, Kemp is not on the ballot. He ran several points ahead of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . He`ll be campaigning with Walker I think on Saturday for his seat. But how much do you think that will affect the turnout here?\par \par \uc2 FRED HICKS, POLITICAL STRATEGIST: Well, you know, Chris, you said it right there. This is all about turnout. And there`s some structural differences between this runoff and the last one that you were just talking about. You know, last time in 2020, going into 2021, we had two months. You have absentee ballot voting, which is very strong, and you got drop boxes everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Republicans changed those rules. And so, you have -- basically, you have very little absentee voting in this runoff. We only have four weeks, three weeks from yesterday. And drop boxes are located inside the facilities so you can only drop ballot off between the hours of nine to five in most cases.\par \par \uc2 So, while \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has a big obstacle -- a big number to overcome, Senator Warnock, Democrats, we have a lot that we have to do to overcome the structural issues created by SB202 that the Republicans passed last year.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I also wonder, how much do you think it does change the race on the ground? I mean, last time, it sort of felt like the race -- these races tend to feel like they just get rerun again in some ways. I guess the question is, does anything change in four weeks in that before, one of the big questions was, who will control the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ? Now, the question is squarely, which of these two individuals do you want representing you in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 HICKS: Absolutely. You know, Georgia has been the sort of the laughingstock since the peak of the country in many respects. My friends from all over in New York, California, and many places ask, how is \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  even competitive? And my response is, well, this is still Georgia. So, I think with Nevada coming in the way that it did for Democrats right now, it`s about securing majority.\par \par \uc2 Chris, if you remember, about 20 years ago, we had a 50-50 Senate, and Jim Jeffords, then a Republican, decided to switch over to be -- to be an independent, and that change control the Congress -- Senate. And so, for this election, I will say this is about securing the majority so that we can move -- talking about judicial appointments, when talking about legislation, we`re talking about someone like dealing with the \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  or \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , we actually have that additional vote that we need so that we can -- we can move legislation ahead.\par \par \uc2 But I think for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , for him and for the Republicans, I believe for them it`s about say, hey, look, we`ve been fairly successful in tie-up legislation amid the 50-50 Senate, so let`s try to do that again. And for Democrats, this is about securing a majority so that we don`t even have to deal with things like a national ban on abortion that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  support, anti-climate legislation, and all these other kinds of things that are out there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You just mentioned abortion, which was obviously a main issue. There was significant reporting about two different women who said that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  had paid for, pressure to them to have an abortion. One of them was able to provide the check signed by Walker and a get-well card. He denied those -- denied them, but then also kind of said, oh, I did different things in the past.\par \par \uc2 A judge has just struck down the abortion ban law in the state of Georgia. I wonder how much that focuses public attention on the two different positions between these two men on the question of abortion, which we`ve learned for the Midterms really is a massively important one to voters.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: And it`s massively important to voters in Georgia. Even here in Georgia, voters support reproductive health. We saw those bans fail in Kansas and Kentucky this year, and there`s really no different in Georgia. But I think that the state has already said they`re going to appeal to that ruling. And so, I think that abortion, reproductive health could become actually the central issue here next to, or in addition to, do you believe, do you like, do you trust the character of these two individuals.\par \par \uc2 And listen, character is something that`s extremely important. And in principle, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  character and abortion are linked hand in hand. He want to have a national ban, no exceptions, but apparently or seems like he have -- he had paid for multiple abortions or he`s been accused of such by people. And so, we were talking about character.\par \par \uc2 And in terms of issues, crime is not the big deal. We had a lot of noise going into the last election last week. But right now, when you`re looking for an issue, and one that can motivate people, I think as reproductive health and it`s abortion, especially if you -- if the judge decides to -- if the appeal is heard sometime soon.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Fred Hicks, thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now with Ali Velshi sitting in for Alex. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Chris, good to see you again, my friend. Thank you for a great show.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221117eibg00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for November 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Steve Kornacki, Dan De Luce, Alex Wagner \par \uc2 8,868 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: -- when they got the franchise, leaned into the idea of democracy and wholly, 100 percent, thoroughly believed it. They believed in it and they just weren`t allowed to keep it. It`s the -- if you can keep it, that`s the big deal. Nikole Hannah Jones, \uc2 Jon Meacham\uc2 , brilliant.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): If we don`t unify behind Kevin McCarthy, we`re opening up the door for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): At the first opportunity, he will zap her faster than you can see Jewish space laser.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The leadership fights turn the House and Senate upside down.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  BUSINESS: Are you yourself backing \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  would be much better than McConnell.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, a Republican Party in absolute chaos as a disgraced ex- President returns to lead them.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): This is certainly not the rollout, I`m sure, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted for his announcement tonight.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, Rick Hasen on how democracy prevailed in the face of MAGA.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): What happened last night with Kari Lake, that`s proof positive that this MAGA stuff doesn`t work.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And what the Pentagon, the Russians, and \uc2 NATO\uc2  are saying about an explosion that killed two people over the Ukraine border in Poland when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. There`s a lot going on right now, a lot swirling around us. And right now, we are waiting for our decision desk to potentially call control of the House of Representatives one week after Election Day. As of this moment, NBC is predicting a very narrow Republican majority of about 220 seats. Steve Kornacki joins me from the big board with the very latest. Steve, what do we got?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Chris, well, we`re getting to that time of day when we do start to get some of these releases of votes from California. And California is basically where all of the action is when it comes to the battle for control of the House Republicans at this hour with at least 216 seats, democrats with at least 207.\par \par \uc2 218 is your number you need for control. So, the magic number for Republicans is two. They need to pick up two from this list of seats. You see it right here, these are the uncalled races. And again, the vast majority of them are in California. So, what could come in the next few hours? What could come this evening?\par \par \uc2 One place to keep an eye on, the 41st District of California. We`re expecting \uc2 Riverside County\uc2  to release a new batch of votes tonight. This is with Republican incumbent \uc2 Ken Calvert\uc2 . And the last night`s release of votes extended his lead over Democrat Will Rollins. Rollins running out of votes here in this district. If there`s another favorable release for Calvert tonight, the math could be very daunting for Rollins there. Potentially that could be number 217 for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Also, the third district to California, kind of runs along in Nevada border, but the population is centered in the Sacramento suburbs going into the Sierra, and Nevada is here. Kevin Kiley, the Republican, leads here. We do think another update in this district is possible tonight as well. And again, see if it would be a favorable one for Kylie, the math could start to get daunting for Kermit Jones, the Democrat here.\par \par \uc2 So, potentially those two, if they were -- if there were enough reports that came in tonight and continued trends we`ve seen, that could be 218 for Republicans. I don`t know if it`ll be tonight, but the 27th district, Mike Garcia, the Republican, continues to lead there. So, there are certainly plenty of pathways here for Republicans to hit 218. And I think a lot in Washington now is thinking about, did they get to 220, did they get to 221? Did they get to a level where they`ve got at least a little bit of a pad there as opposed to that idea of like a one seat majority?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Steve Kornacki, thank you. We will check back with you if we have updates.\par \par \uc2 Now, to Steve`s point, the narrowest House majority of all time is one vote. It was last seen after the Midterm elections of 1930 into 1931. We don`t know as Steve just said the final margin for this next House. But as of this moment, NBC News is predicting a very narrow Republican majority, about 220 seats to 215. We`re still waiting on the official call.\par \par \uc2 But as we prepare for this, and as Washington does as well, now is the time when every Republican in Washington starts bullying Kevin McCarthy. McCarthy is currently the Republican leader, so he is all but guaranteed to become Speaker of the House come January when the new Congress is sworn in. Mostly because anyone else capable of doing the job is, well, smart enough not to want it at this point. As Republican Congressman \uc2 Dan Crenshaw\uc2  of Texas told HuffPost, "There`s no other alternative."\par \par \uc2 Today, the House had their preliminary secret leadership vote, the House Republican Caucus, and in that McCarthy seem to dispatch his only real rival, the far right election denier Andy Biggs of Arizona. In that preliminary speakership vote, this is just within the Republican caucus, 31 Republicans voted against McCarthy. That means he got 188 votes. He will need 218 to wield the Speaker`s gavel, which is a vote of the full House of Representatives. And he now has to work tirelessly to get his party unified behind him.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:06]\par \par \uc2 Now, this naturally gives the fringes of the Republican Party, the far- right Freedom Caucus, the unofficial MAGA troll caucus, an extraordinary amount of power. They can make or break McCarthy`s speakership. They are going to maximize their leverage, and they are going to make him sweat a lot. And here`s the thing, whether they`re doing it explicitly or implicitly, they are really wielding that power on behalf of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to essentially force Kevin McCarthy`s hand to do whatever the ex-President wants to make him the shadow speaker.\par \par \uc2 Trump is apparently already demanding McCarthy`s complete fealty, at least according to his close advisor \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON MILLER\uc2 , ADVISOR TO \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : What I would like to see, Steve, I do think that Kevin McCarthy needs to -- if he wants to have a chance of being speaker, he has to be much more declarative that he is supporting President Trump. I thought what \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  did yesterday was very smart. And the fact of the matter is, it`s going to be a MAGA-centric caucus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s probably true. Now, even if McCarthy does cave and get behind Trump for 2024, that MAGA caucus is still going to make his life a living nightmare. That`s not going to end it. One of the concessions that some of those folks, those far-right Republicans are demanding on condition of supporting McCarthy for speaker, would be the return of a procedural rule - - it`s sort of obscure, but it`s known as the motion to vacate the chair.\par \par \uc2 And the thing about that is it effectively allows any member to submit a vote of essentially no confidence in the speaker. Way back in July of 2015, then-Republican Congressman \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  filed a motion to vacate against then-Republicans Speaker \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 , which effectively forced Boehner into retirement. Boehner was like, screw this. I`m done. I`m going to go retire with my red wine and cigarettes. See you guys later.\par \par \uc2 The same threat also loomed over Boehner`s replacement, Republican Speaker of the House \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 . But unlike Boehner and McCarthy, it was obvious to anyone watching that Ryan did not actually want the job. So, after two years, he was like, OK, I`m out too. I`m going back to Wisconsin. Now, it is unclear if the far-right Freedom Caucus will succeed in bringing back the motion to vacate, but either way, MAGA diehards will absolutely be calling the shots here.\par \par \uc2 Again, NBC News projecting Republicans will hold 220 seats in the House. They need to 18 for the majority. That means with that projection, they can afford to lose just two votes. That is a very tight margin to work with. Those numbers are around what House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has been able to successfully navigate with the Democrats. She had 220 votes in this last Congress.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is an exceptionally gifted politician when it comes to wrangling votes, probably the best vote wrangler of her generation, and up there in the all-time list of us speakers. I doubt even Kevin McCarthy supporters believe he matches her ability in that regard. So, McCarthy starts out playing the game at the highest level of difficulty with not that much experience in a very precarious position.\par \par \uc2 But let`s assume Republicans overperform our expectations slate. Let`s say at the outside part of the window, they wind up with 225 seats. That`s a margin of just seven votes that McCarthy can afford to lose, right, 225 to 10. Now, that`s already a tall order for conferences divided as House Republicans. But keep this data point in mind. For this current Congress, the ones about the end, the one that we have right now, there was never a single day without at least one vacancy.\par \par \uc2 In fact, over the course of the two years, seven team members the House left their positions. Six members died, five resigned to work in the private sector, including, remember this guy, California Republican Devin Nunez who left to run Trump`s knockoff \uc2 Twitter\uc2  platform which has been beset with problems since he did that.\par \par \uc2 Then you got three members, \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2  of Louisiana, \uc2 Deb Haaland\uc2  of New Mexico, Marcia Fudge of Ohio. They all left to work in the President`s administration. You have Florida Democrat \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  who left to run for and lose yet again his State`s gubernatorial race. You have New York Democratic Congressman Antonio Delgado who left to become his state`s lieutenant governor. And then you had Republican \uc2 Jeff Fortenberry\uc2  of Nebraska who was indicted by a federal grand jury last year and then forced to resign in March after he was convicted of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OK, so, again, even if Republicans have a great run with the ballots that are outstanding in say, California, and they surpass expectations. Even if we give McCarthy that cushion. Let`s say he gets 225 votes. Suddenly, like a few people leave to take other jobs, someone gets indicted which, you know, you never know. Maybe a member of Congress dies. God forbid. That also happens several times in the last few years.\par \par \uc2 Suddenly that margin is slimmer and slimmer. And the narrower that margin becomes, the more powerful the MAGA troll caucus gets. If McCarthy is in a position where say he can now only lose say three votes, well then, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  of Georgia and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  of Florida and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  of Arizona, they could become the three most powerful people in the House, demanding that McCarthy drag his party as far as possible to the MAGA fringe.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:03]\par \par \uc2 Now, at this moment -- this is kind of interesting. Greene is actually supporting openly McCarthy. She voted for him and she`s kind of campaigning for him presumably because she knows how much power she will wield under his speakership. Gaetz, however, is not. Kind of a bad cop, good cop routine if you asked me. Gaetz, in fact, just made the rounds at the right- wing podcast circuit to lobby against McCarthy, essentially promising to make a life -- a nightmare for him if and when he takes control.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GAETZ: What I`m here to tell you is there are definitely at least five people, actually a lot more than that, who would rather be waterboarded by \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  than vote for Kevin McCarthy for speaker of the house, and I`m one of them.\par \par \uc2 We cannot trust Kevin McCarthy. Whatever Kevin has promised \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , I guarantee you this, at the first opportunity, he will zap her faster than you can say Jewish space laser. And I wish that wasn`t the case. But it is the conduct and it is the pattern and practice of Kevin McCarthy over years that showcase this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You can just tell how much he really wants to be a podcast host, not a member of Congress. Maybe he`ll be one of those retirements. Now, tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you may have heard, is expected to announce he is running for president again. He will, of course, have a vested interest in showing how much power he has over House Republicans through members like Congressman Gaetz. That will be the Trump arm of government in exile essentially. Anything Trump`s demands, votes, investigations, impeachments, Kevin McCarthy will be under immense pressure to support or risk his entire agenda being blocked by Trump`s acolytes or maybe a vote of no confidence and back to California. The result is a governing majority most likely that is about to become ungovernable.\par \par \uc2 Jake Sherman is the founder of Punchbowl News where he follows Congress more closely than just about anyone. And Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , Democrat of Washington, is chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus. She knows a thing or two about caucus dynamics. And both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, let me start with you. I wanted to get your perspective because you occupy an interesting position here. You`re the chair of the Progressive Caucus. So. you have a caucus inside the Democratic Caucus and the speaker at a very narrow House margin, and there`s back and forth, right? Your members want certain things, leadership might want other things, you kind of go back and forth. How do you see that dynamic playing out on the other side of the aisle as you look at this incoming possible majority of two, three, four votes, somewhere in there?\par \par \uc2 REP. PRAMILA JAYAPAL (D-WA): Well, Chris, it`s good to see you. You know, I always rejected the idea that there was any comparison between the Progressive Caucus and the Freedom Caucus, because the Progressive Caucus has always been a caucus of yes. We have been focused on how do we govern, but push the limits of what`s possible to the very end. You know, how do we make sure we land the plane, but get as much as we can for working people and folks of color, and poor people across this country that just need a leg up?\par \par \uc2 The Freedom Caucus has always been a caucus of No. They have been a caucus willing to just explode everything. They don`t really have a desire to govern. You saw it with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you saw it with, you know, the Freedom Caucus in the -- in the last couple of rounds of Republican leadership, as you mentioned, with \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 , with \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I`m not sure that there is any dynamic that changes that in this Congress. In fact, I think it makes it even worse, because the Republican Party as a party of values has imploded on itself. It is now a cult party of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is -- who has already lost the 2020 election, he lost a whole bunch of elections across the country, and has put this Republican Party in a place where it really has nothing to stand for.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is -- thank you for saying that. And I think this is, Jake, I think such a such an important in this two-way symmetry, right. So, on the -- on the left side, right, it was Pelosi and Congresswoman Jayapal are going back and forth, right? There are lots of substantive things those members want. You know, we want a big infrastructure investment in green energy, OK.\par \par \uc2 So, if you say, well, it started as a trillion now, it`s down to 500 billion, you can say, well, that`s half a loaf and we really care about getting that money out the door. We really care because it`s important for what we believe in. I`m not sure what those things are for the Paul Gosars or Marjorie Taylor Greenes and Matt Gaetzes. It`s like they`re going to, in their own weird way, because of that kind of nihilism, because of that party of no, Jake, it seems like they actually wield more leverage, because I don`t even know what the thing they want to do is in a substantive sense.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, FOUNDER, PUNCHBOWL NEWS: Yes, I think that`s right. The Congresswoman will not be surprised I disagree with her on a little bit of what she said and here`s why. This Republican majority is not only built in the conservative districts in America, it`s built-in blue states where Republicans won a bunch of seats in New York, and in other places around the country that Republicans are not really accustomed to, to having seats in.\par \par \uc2 So, I do agree on the -- on the core of the argument that the conservatives have more leverage perhaps than -- not even than they used to in the past but they do have a lot of leverage. But there -- it`s going to be a push and pull between the Conservatives and the moderates in this -- in this conference I think in a way that we`ve never seen before.\par \par \uc2 Remember, when \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  was elected speaker in 2015, I believe he had 247 people in his conference.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:23]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Most of whom were pretty conservative. I don`t think we`re going to get majorities that size anymore but that`s besides the point. I just think that everyone is a kingmaker here. And I know, the congresswoman had to deal a lot with Joe Manchin and people like that in the last -- in this Congress, earlier in this Congress. I think that that`s something that Kevin McCarthy is going to have to deal with in 2023 and 2024, is the Mike Lawler who just knocked off the DCCC chair in New York, and then also the Marjorie Taylor Greenes.\par \par \uc2 But at the core of your argument, whether there is a governing philosophy that`s overarching in the House Republican Conference, I would concede that point that there`s not kind of a cohesive governing philosophy that`s discernible.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, to that -- to that point, I think it`s an important point that there will be -- I don`t know, there`s going to be seven, eight, nine members in Biden districts. Some, you know, Biden plus seven, Biden plus eight, those New York members who are going to want to be reelected, right? So, things like I think impeaching \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  out of the gate is probably not your smartest vote, you know, like after -- a month after you got the Congress.\par \par \uc2 But then there`s the question of what you deliver. I mean, the big question to me, Congresswoman, is the debt ceiling, the fear of sort of shutdowns, destruction as being in the kind of political interest of that caucus as a whole, particularly with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  urging them on?\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Well, yes. And I would just say, you know, in response to Jake, I mean, look, I think that the thing is not that there`s push and pull between the Conservatives part of the Republican Party and the moderate part, but the fact that the -- that the country has already rejected MAGA extremism, and you do have the possibility of these MAGA extremists really being able to control everything on the agenda. The only way that Kevin McCarthy is going to get to 218 votes is if he convinces all those people to go along with them.\par \par \uc2 And so, he`s going to owe people things. And what I will tell you is, let me just say, there is not since 1934 has the president`s party actually been able to keep every state legislature in the country much less what a couple of legislatures, right? We have the first time in a very long time, the governor and both senators from Arizona as Democrats. We have in Pennsylvania recovered space from tough frontline districts that used to be Republican. We captured Washington 3 in southwest Washington with Marie Gluesenkamp Perez. We have a phenomenal class of progressives that are coming in.\par \par \uc2 All of that to say, Chris, that I think the country is grappling with what is this extreme MAGA Republican agenda. And Kevin McCarthy is going to end up having to play to that agenda. It will be a really important contrast for Democrats as we go into 2024. We are going to Preserve Social Security and Medicare, they want to cut it. They want to do more tax cuts for the wealthy, we don`t. We want to lift up working people. They want to take away abortion and have a national abortion right -- ban, we don`t. We want to protect those fundamental freedoms.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, Chris are the party of freedom, family, and faith. And that`s what we will have going into 2024 to bolster us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congresswoman \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , Jake Sherman, thank you both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JAYAPAL: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, after his party flops in historical fashion, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  plans to announce another run for president. Why it`s OK to start feeling better about the state of American democracy, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For a very long time in the past year heading into the midterm elections, we seem to be headed towards another crisis. Not unlike what happened after the election 2020 into the January 6 interaction, those weird few months there. Now, coming out of the aftermath of January 6, the failed violent coup, the way the Republican Party led by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  attempted to reassert itself was to run a slate of election deniers in the crucial swing states of Arizona, Michigan, Nevada, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, with the hope that, you know, at least a few of them would win.\par \par \uc2 And once they won, they would then control the levels of -- levers of power when it came to elections. Meaning, when it came time to certify the electoral votes of say, Arizona, to pick one example and 2024, and Trump had lost the state, well, then this time around, his handpicked pro-coup official would just refuse to do it, precipitating a constitutional crisis.\par \par \uc2 But then the midterm election happened, and I have to say, I am just amazed and relieved. Not one of them won, not one. All those people up there standing next to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , every single Trump-endorsed election denier in competitive swing states got defeated.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, some did get elected in those non-swing states like Indiana, for example. But on a night like tonight when Trump is about to announce that he is running in 2024, that he`s mounting another her attempt to take the American government under his own ownership, it is comforting to know that come 2024, if he loses in those swing states, we are just as of today as opposed to two weeks ago, far, far less likely to have basically a full-on constitutional crisis. Because those election-denier candidates, those candidates would all but pledge never to certify an election Trump lost will not be in position to install \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  against the will of the voters because they lost. We basically escaped that worst- case scenario because voters rally to beat them off. It is astonishing, improbable, joyous victory for liberty and for freedom and democracy.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:50]\par \par \uc2 Rick Hasen is one of the foremost experts in U.S. election law, professor of UCLA School of Law. And he recently published an article for Slate where he writes, "I`ve been way more worried about American democracy than I am right now." That`s in the aftermath of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Rick, tell me why you feel less worried, less acutely panicked about the state of a democracy in the wake of these results than and you did in the run up to these elections?\par \par \uc2 RICK HASEN, PROFESSOR, UCLA SCHOOL OF LAW: Sure. Well, the piece is actually a sequel to something I wrote in September of 2020 called, "I`ve never been more worried about the election than I am right now." And that was when I thought that Trump was going to try and mess with the results if he loss. And in fact, he did, and then things got worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 Trump claimed that the election was stolen. He did all of these things to get these election deniers in place. We learned from 2020 that it really takes people of good faith to make sure that our election results actually translate into the winner taking office. And so, I was extremely concerned that having these people in place would create the conditions where it would be far easier in 2024 for Trump to manipulate the results than not.\par \par \uc2 Now, not only did these elections deniers who were secretaries of state lose, some of the governors who gets assigned the certificates of electors who are election deniers, Kari Lake, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , they lost. The Pennsylvania legislature might flip one house. The Michigan Legislature flipped, so that route is gone. So, I think all in all, if we were two minutes to midnight, on the doomsday clock, we`re now 10 minutes to midnight.\par \par \uc2 We can`t let our guard down completely, but this isn`t much better shape than I expected we were going to be in a week after the 2022 Midterms.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, one of the scenarios I had thought a lot about was Arizona particularly because Arizona -- there`s two things going on in Arizona. One, you had a full kind of big lie slate, right? You had, you know, the person running for Secretary of State Mark Finchem, the Attorney General candidate, the Gubernatorial candidate.\par \par \uc2 And so, in Arizona, I believe, four statewide officials off to sign off on the elections. So, it seemed like one of this kind of, well, someone is going to get through. And it just seemed like obvious what the crisis was going to be, right? The state officials say, no, we reject. We reject this that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost. And then it`s like, well, it gets litigated, where are we? And remarkably, we still haven`t called the Attorney General race. But as of now, three of those four statewide races run by a big lie candidate, one of them being the Senate, the voters rejected them in a very tightly contested seat. And it looks like it cost them.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Yes, I think that -- yes, one of the things that`s the main lesson to take away here is that it`s possible to get sane Republican voters to vote against the election deniers. And, you know, if you look at the margins, you know, those who were election deniers did worse than other Republican candidates. And you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was pushing -- for example, in Pennsylvania, was pushing -- McCormick was running against Oz in the Republican primary to deny the results of the election. McCormick didn`t do it, he lost the primary. And then Oz, the weaker candidate, goes on to the general.\par \par \uc2 So, it really backfired. And I think it`s going to give Republicans pause about who they`re going to nominate in 2024 if they`re going to continue to have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pressuring these big lie candidates to try and run again for office.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is a really important point because it`s not just the victories. And tangibly, what that means, it`s the deterrent effect. Here`s Rupert Murdoch`s Wall Street Journal editorial page sending a very clear message where they are on this. And they`ll go either way. They don`t really have a substantive care. They just want to win.\par \par \uc2 They say about Kari Lake, they say that her loss destroys stop the steal. She`s a telegenic fraud theorist straight out of Mar-a-Lago casting running in historically red state in a year with an unpopular Democratic President with eight percent inflation. If Ms. Lake couldn`t win on stop this deal in 2022, it`s hard to see how anyone else can pull it off. Maybe at last the 2020 election is over.\par \par \uc2 The big question here is Trump, right, because he`s the one who`s been driving this the whole time. And as we know, he`s not going away.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: He`s not going away. And you know, he`s going to run on a grievance platform. I think there`s no question about it. The question is whether Republican voters will go along with him. He`s still got to have the inside track. He`s still very popular among the Republican base. But it`s a situation where just like in these other races, you could end up nominating someone who`s very popular with the base, but who can`t make the sale for in the general election.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I think some Democrats, despite the risks, are hoping that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the nominee because they think it`ll be easier to run against. I think that`s terrible thinking. I think it`s way too big a risk for the country. But certainly, the weaknesses of his position and his grievances were exposed by what happened last Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:37]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, for the record, to editorialize, I agree with you. It`s just too dangerous to have him anywhere near power given what he`s done and who he is. Rick Hasen, thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, Russia`s invasion of Ukraine spills into a \uc2 NATO\uc2  country with deadly results and the U.S. is responding. We`re going to go to the Pentagon for report next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the biggest Democratic upsets of this midterm cycle, probably the biggest upset of the cycle, full stop, came in a Washington State Third Congressional District southwest part of the state, where against all odds, Marie Gluesenkamp Perez defeated Republican Joe Kent. Now, this is a seat that was supposed to stay in Republican hands after being held by a Republican for more than a decade. It was held by sixth-term Congresswoman \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, you may remember, she gained national attention last year as one of just 10 House Republicans who voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for inciting the insurrection on January 6. And Congresswoman \uc2 Herrera Beutler\uc2  also revealed details of the phone call on the six between House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That was that infamous chilling call where Trump told McCarthy that members of the mob were ransacking the Capitol and hunting down \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and members of Congress were "more upset about the election than you are."\par \par \uc2 Of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was furious with \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2  after that. So, what did he do? Well, he backed her primary challenger Joe Kent, a 42- year-old former Green Beret. And to be clear here, Joe Kent is extreme even for a MAGA Republican. He has ties to white nationalists, including this man who called Adolf Hitler "A complicated historical figure which many people misunderstand." Ken`s campaign paid a member of the Proud Boys over $11,000 in consulting fees. The former chairman of the Washington State Republican Party called Kent and I quote, "the freakiest of the MAGA freaks."\par \par \uc2 But the woman who defeated Kent, Marie Gluesenkamp Perez, just about as normal as it gets, for lack of a better descriptor, working-class small business owner who has never held elected office before.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARIE GLUESENKAMP PEREZ (D-WA), REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT): I`m not your typical candidate for Congress. My name is Marie Gluesenkamp Perez. My husband Dean and I own an auto repair shop. I`m running to be a voice for workers who have a little grease under their fingernails, for those struggling running small businesses taking care of young kids and taking care of aging parents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They call that in business a bio ad and that is a very, very good one. And in a lot of ways, that one race in Washington, that race is the story of the Midterm elections. We saw Trump-backed extremist after extremists fail as voters chose normal relatable candidates like Gluesenkamp Perez. And on an even deeper level, these elections showed how the extreme wing of the MAGA movement of the Republican Party have become just trapped in their own ideological chamber.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Joe Kent is a perfect example. Look at this tweet. This is really amazing. This tweet is Joe Kent condemning probably the most popular pastime in America, some sort of, I don`t know, like wussy liberal distraction. "I don`t know how people watch pro-sports, major corporations spending woke propaganda while convincing men that the masculine thing to do is watch other men compete in a silly game."\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, there`s lots of legitimate criticism of professional sports and the sports leagues, some of which I have, but they`re wildly popular in this country. This is one of the most like, out-of-touch things a candidate can say particularly in a district that leans right. Again, it demonstrates exactly what has happened to Donald Trump`s Republican Party. The MAGA wing has become so obsessively wrapped up in their own little twisted ecochamber, their own neurotic obsessions about wokeness everywhere and trans youth. They have completely lost touch with what normal people think and want.\par \par \uc2 This year they nominated a whole bunch of people who just have no connection with the median voter. I mean, look at Kari Lake, who is probably the strongest candidate among all of them, who still lost her bid for governor of Arizona. Into being an election -- in addition to being election denier, anti-vaxxer, she called her imprisoning her opponents and journalists. Or look at Blake Masters on the ballot in that state as well, the failed Arizona candidate for Senate. A libertarian who thought the Unabomber manifesto was interesting. Who repeats far-right conspiracies and election lies.\par \par \uc2 His campaign ads varied from strange to downright creepy, like this two- and-a-half-minute video that reads more like a promotion for gun silencers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R-AZ), SENATE CANDIDATE: They`re made in Germany, 007 gun, why would you not want this? Let`s load it up and see how it shoots. Silencers sometimes get a bad rap. People want to pretend that it makes it easy to commit crime or something like that. But actually, shooting with a silencer just makes it a whole lot more pleasant to shoot because you don`t have to worry about giant ear protection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, the silencers get a bad rap constituency. If someone at a party like showed you that video who`s like, hey, you want to do something cool? You want to get the heck out of there. Who is the median Arizona voter that watches that and it`s like that, that`s what I want from my senator. Not a lot of them, it turns out.\par \par \uc2 All over the country, we saw this kind of out-of-touch extremism rejected over and over. In New Hampshire, it was Republican Senate nominee Don Bolduc who repeatedly falsely claimed that children are identifying as cats and using litter boxes in schools, a widely circulated myth in right-wing circles. He also called an aspect of in vitro fertilization, a fertility treatment "disgusting," indicated he was open to banning the procedure. I wonder how that went over with voters.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, there was J.R. Majewski, the MAGA-rapping, QAnon-believing Republican candidate for Congress who also lied about his military service. And North Carolina congressional candidate Bo Hines who thinks that community panel should decide if victims of rape or incest should be allowed to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 It is not an accident that all these people are the kinds of candidates who flourished in MAGA Republican primaries. The litmus test in Donald Trump`s party is do you believe the 2020 election was stolen by some fantastic conspiracy involving Italian satellites and the ghost of \uc2 Hugo Chavez\uc2 . If you believe something that obviously preposterously false, you`re going to be a weirdo. It`s not surprising more insanity would follow.\par \par \uc2 The real irony is Republicans are convinced they are the ones who represent real America. It`s those out-of-touch liberal coastal elites, they don`t get us. They`re the ones who have lost sight of the fact they are utterly out of touch with ordinary people. And they got a rude awakening last week.\par \par \uc2 There`s one reason we`re not talking about a battle for control of the Senate tonight. That`s because the line against the anti-democratic MAGA forces held in the state of Nevada. And joining me now is Nevada Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . Senator, it`s good to have you on. Congratulations on your victory. What do you chalk up your win to?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D-NV): Thank you. First of all, it`s great to join you. To Nevadans, every Nevadan that came out, those working -- hard- working families. It`s it was the kitchen table issues. It was the repeal of Roe vs. Wade in a state that`s a proud pro-choice state. And it is the big lie.\par \par \uc2 What happened on January 6 was fueled by the big lie. And my opponent who was peddling those conspiracy theories and lies in Nevada on the last election that was proven by the courts in Nevada that was not stolen. There was no wholesale fraud. And Republican Secretary of State in Nevada that stood up for our elections as well. So, I was hearing from so many events and all of those issues that really they cared about.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Your state strikes me as a perfect example of this. This is a state that is that had, in your same race, a Republican who won that gubernatorial race. You have had Republican governors before. In fact, very popular ones. It`s not a state that`s out of reach for the right statewide Republican candidate by any means. But the two big lie candidates, your opponent, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  and Jim Marchant who was running for Secretary State both loss and underperformed that gubernatorial candidate. Do you think there`s a lesson there?\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ MASTO: I do. You know, and I -- and the reason I say this is because I was hearing from Nevadans. And it wasn`t just my party. It wasn`t just Democrats. It was Republicans. It was nonpartisan. It was women. It was men. It was in urban areas. It was in rural areas. So many people were concerned about what they saw.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, this is what I learned. I lived what happened on January 6. But so many Nevadans I talked to watched it happen in front of their own eyes. And that was emotional for them. They were outraged. They lived it and had emotions just watching it. And so, I would hear from some many events who were concerned about protection of our democracy and wanting to come out and stand up for it and to stand up against those candidates, those far-right, extreme candidates that were continuing to peddle the Big Lie and continue to erode our rights, the rights of women across the country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In terms of that, and in terms of the agenda that Senate Democrats pursued, voted for, and was signed by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , how much were you running on that? I mean, when you were -- when you were doing focus groups, when you`re looking at polling, and when you were running your ads in your state, how much of it was, hey, here`s what I voted for and here`s what it did.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ MASTO: Well, I`ll tell you. I was sent to the Senate by Nevadans to do the work that I`ve done. Let me get -- just give you an example. You know, I was attorney general for eight years. And when I ran for the Senate, there were a number of seniors I was meeting with. And they said, we know you`re a prosecutor, we know you`re an attorney general. And we expect you to go to Washington and prosecute the case to lower prescription drug costs to take on big pharma and make sure that Medicare can negotiate to lower costs for seniors.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:26]\par \par \uc2 So, I was able to go back to the state and said, guess what, we did it working together -- and thanks to so many out there that were -- voices were being heard, we were able to not only negotiate prescription drug costs, to cap the cost of insulin, to lower out of pocket costs for seniors, to lower energy costs for so many families. And I was able to go back and talk about that.\par \par \uc2 But also, part of it was also, you know, this idea that inflation is out there and not impacting me or my family in Nevada is crazy. You know, I understood it. I was just as frustrated as the Nevadans about the high gas prices and why -- question why big oil wasn`t at the table working with us. I was just as frustrated about when you go grocery shopping. I go grocery shopping. My family does. My mother does. I hear it. I hear the high prices.\par \par \uc2 So, part of this is why aren`t we all working together to lower those costs. Why are we not just focused on helping working families, those families that I know in my state, address these issues, these cost issues. I was able to go talk to them about what we had done already with the bipartisan infrastructure package, the Inflation Reduction Act, the Chips and Science Act that was bringing all manufacturing and creating good paying jobs in Nevada, talking about those jobs and those good union jobs and how it is continuing to grow our economy.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Nevada`s economy during the height of the pandemic was devastated. We had 30 percent unemployment. We had -- the Las Vegas Strip was shut down. That`s our main revenue generator. So, we had to work to come out of that. And then also have a long-term plan to keep us going to make sure that we had good-paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think I said last week that I think the governor of your state, Governor Lombardo, who did lose that race, had arguably the hardest job in the whole country in terms of governors during COVID to shut down the Strip, to sort of implement those public health measures in a place that`s so dependent on exactly the kinds of businesses, travel, tourism, hospitality, that might be sort of on the side for other -- for other metro areas or for other regions or states. As Senator, for you -- for you yourself.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  who will be joining the Senate Democratic Majority returning to Washington, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ MASTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, we`ve been scrambling things around right now because there was a blast in Poland today, you might have seen. Poland is a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally. That blast killed two people. It came from something from the sky. We don`t quite actually know the details of it. It could have been a Russian rocket. It could have been the Ukrainians firing on a Russian missile.\par \par \uc2 But President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in Bali, where he`s already meeting with world leaders. He`s called an emergency meeting. He`s talked to the President of Poland. We expect to hear from the President directly momentarily. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We are expecting any moment now to hear from the President of the United States who is in Bali meeting with world leaders of the G20. There was an emergency meeting called with world leaders at the G20 today in the aftermath of the deadly explosion in Poland near the Ukraine -- near the border with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Senior U.S. intelligence official told \uc2 The Associated Press\uc2  today that a missile strike killed two people at a grain processing facility in a Polish town near the Ukrainian border. Now, this occurred amidst a Russian onslaught, barrage of missiles that had been targeting civilian targets including the Ukrainian power grid. They`ve fallen in Kyiv and other cities.\par \par \uc2 Poland, of course, which has been assisting Ukraine in its efforts to beat back the Russian invasion, is also a \uc2 NATO\uc2  member unlike Ukraine. \uc2 NATO\uc2  members have something called Article Five in which an attack on one is considered an attack on all. Poland -- Polish government said it increased the readiness of some military units following that explosion.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  again currently in Bali at the G20 Summit. He met with the President of China yesterday calling a gathering of world leaders in response to this incident today in Poland. And he`s walking out right now. Let`s hear from the President.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thanks for being here. I -- as you saw, I just met with the leaders of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and \uc2 G7\uc2  and talked about the latest events in Europe. And I briefed them on my discussions with President Duda of Poland, as well as \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary General Stoltenberg and we agreed to support Poland`s investigation into the explosion in rural Poland near the Ukrainian border.\par \par \uc2 And I`m going to make sure we figure out exactly what happened. Our empathy and sympathy goes out to the family of two people who were killed. And then we`re going to collectively determine our next step as we investigate and proceed. There was total unanimity among the folks at the table.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 We`ll also discuss the latest series of Russian missile attacks which are continuing to brutality and inhumanity that they`ve demonstrated throughout this war against Ukrainian cities and civilian infrastructures. And they`ve been totally unconscionable what they`re doing. Totally unconscionable. And the moment when the world that came together at the G20 to urge de- escalation, Russia continues to -- has chosen to escalate in Ukraine while we`re meeting.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there were scores and scores of attack, missile attacks, in western Ukraine. We support Ukraine fully in this moment and we have -- we have since the start is conflict. We`re going to continue do whatever it takes to give them the capacity to defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, is it too early to say whether this missile was fired from Russia?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There is preliminary information that contest that. I don`t want to say that until we completely investigate. But it is -- I found likely in the minds of the trajectory that it was fired from Russia. But we`ll see. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unlikely, sir?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. President, does Poland suggested invoking Article four or five from \uc2 NATO\uc2 , sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`re going to probably have a meeting of the -- of the ambassadors so that`s -- it looks like we`re going to go to the next. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK, thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, that was the president responding to the explosion, deadly explosion that happened in Poland today over the Ukraine border. Again, the actual details of what happened a bit unclear. And the President was asked a follow-up question about whether it originated in Russia and said they`re conducting an investigation. He also pledged a full U.S. support for that Polish investigation.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, he had a conversation with the President of Poland, President \uc2 Andrzej Duda\uc2 . For more on this developing news, Dan De Luce is a National Security and Global Affairs reporter for NBC News Investigative Unit. He joins me live at the Pentagon. Dan, do we have clarity on exactly what happened before we get to anything about what the recriminations will be?\par \par \uc2 DAN DE LUCE, NBC NEWS GLOBAL AFFAIRS REPORTER: We do not. It`s kind of extraordinary now since this news first broke. There is not a definitive explanation as to what was -- what landed on Polish territory, and who fired it. And you heard there just now, the President of the United States not ready to level an accusation against Russia in this instance.\par \par \uc2 So, the stakes are very high. No one at the White House or across the NATO Alliance wants to escalate the situation and make matters worse, especially when it`s just not clear if that projectile came from Russia. And then if so, was that intentional? And common sense would suggest that if Russia really was launching an attack on a \uc2 NATO\uc2  member, they probably wouldn`t just use two missiles landing in a farm field, there would be much more than that.\par \par \uc2 So, at the moment, I think there`s a lot of caution coming from the White House. They really don`t want to trigger anything worse. They`re offering help to Poland to investigate it. And the same message from Poland. You have the Polish president saying it was Russian made that projectile, but we -- but he did not say it was who actually fired that weapon.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s still unclear. Ukraine and Russia, of course, have been trading accusations. Russia denies that it fired anything into Polish territory. But it`s a reminder really, of all the concern all along that the whole NATO Alliance has had from the beginning since Russia invaded, Chris, right, which is that this conflict could spread. That it could develop into a war between \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Russia.\par \par \uc2 And even if this was not intentional, if it did come from Russia, this is another example of the concern about misunderstandings or miscalculations, that you actually -- an accident can trigger something much worse. So, a lot of anxiety right now. The whole schedule on G -- the G20 meeting there upended while Biden and the other leaders of democracies were discussing how to proceed.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we saw that picture sitting down \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2 , \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , of course, who`s the recently the new Prime Minister of the U.K. in the aftermath of this. The caution that you`re -- there`s a shot right there, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  as well. The caution you were telling -- talking about there really came through in the President`s very careful description of it. And then that question, I could, you know, see that he sort of wasn`t supposed to answer but he clearly doesn`t -- we`re not getting the Russians did this and we will respond in kind which you understand why.\par \par \uc2 Dan De Luce, thank you so much for that reporting. I really appreciate it. That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Thank you, Chris. It is -- what an evening it is.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221116eibf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for November 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Tim Miller, Garrett Haake, Steve Patterson, Ali  Vitali, Blayne Alexander, Carol Lam, James Stavridis, Marc Caputo \par \uc2 8,475 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Timing is everything in politics. So, what does it say about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that he would pick now tonight to announce his third presidential campaign at a time when congressional Republicans are in a mad scramble for power? His handpicked candidates have flamed out and his own inner circle has been telling him to put the launch on hold.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): This is certainly not the rollout I`m sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted for his announcement tonight. But, you know, it`s also not the first time he`s been totally detached from reality.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  went on to insist again that Trump is unfit for office. And in fact, a growing number of other Republicans are also saying it`s time for the party to find some new blood. And they`re not just talking about Trump. They`re talking about \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and Kevin McCarthy too. The House Republican Conference is meeting behind closed doors right now. We will have a live report in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 And the first lady of California, Jennifer Siebel Newsom, breaking down in tears while testifying against \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2 , the latest on her horrific account and the slur against her coming from Weinstein`s lawyer. We`ll go there live later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But we start with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , whose big plans to announce his third run for president have so far at least, been marked by bad optics, bad polling, and bad timing. In the past 48 hours, we learned of accusations he tried to sic the \uc2 IRS\uc2  on his political enemies. He dodged a deposition from the January 6 Committee and was called out by his former vice president for endangering his life. And the one candidate who embody Trump`s brand of politics more than any other, Kari Lake lost in Arizona. Now, instead of rallying behind Trump`s bid for the White House, more and more Republicans are running the other way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY, (R-UT): I think President Trump was an albatross on the electoral prospects of some of our candidates.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2 , (R-SD): It`s clear that you know, running on re-litigating the 2020 election is not a winning strategy.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CYNTHIA LUMMIS, (R-WY): Currently, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is the leader of the Republican Party, whether he wants to be or not.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ? Well, in fact, new polling from the Club for Growth may show exactly that. In hypothetical matchups in Iowa and New Hampshire, the polls show that Trump went from being tied or ahead of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in August to losing by double digits. In Florida and Georgia, modest leads for DeSantis ballooned to 20 points or more. And it`s not just Republican voters. As we saw, Republican lawmakers are also ready to turn the page.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2 , (R-MO): I think that this election was the funeral for the Republican Party as we know it. The Republican Party is, as we have noted, is dead. This party is going to have to -- actually, it`s going to have to be different. We`re not going to be a majority party in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`ve got the perfect people with me to discuss this. NBC`s Garrett Haake is in beautiful West Palm Beach. I`ve got two political veterans who helped run campaigns against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as well. Tim Miller, writer at large for the Bulwark and an MSNBC political analyst who worked for Jeb Bush in 2016. And Adrienne Elrod is a Democratic strategist, a former senior aide on the Biden-Harris campaign, and also worked for Hillary Clinton`s 2016 campaign. Great to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 So, Tim, we just heard from \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  a few minutes ago when he was asked about Trump`s big announcement and Trump going after him. And I want you to listen -- oh, we don`t have it. We don`t -- oh -- so, basically, he tried to kind of thread the needle. He didn`t go all in, but he suggested, you know, come on come at me. If he is going to use kind of that sly slow response to Trump, is he showing something different than what I`ve heard most Republicans say, which is, if you`re going to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you got to go all in?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Look, I saw that so I can speak to it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Good.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And, you know, he did -- can`t basically buck the question, right? And I think he implied that it was, you know, somebody`s criticizing him, it means I`m doing a good job, you know, one of these sorts of passive- aggressive pushbacks. And here`s the thing, I think that this works around DeSantis for now because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has really stepped in it in a way that he hasn`t in a long time with his base voters, right?\par \par \uc2 Obviously, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been traitorous and he`s been a liar and he`s gotten bad media coverage for seven years now but the base of what voters have all loved him for it. And he`s really made a mistake by costing the Republicans these midterms and by picking this fight with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  right before the midterms. And I think for the first time in a long time, you were saying, you know, even MAGA types start to get frustrated with him. So I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is happy to let them continue digging his own grave for a little while longer.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s not going to work forever though, so here`s the problem for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Eventually, you`re going to have to run through \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if you want to beat him. And we don`t know what -- \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has never disavowed January 6. We don`t exactly know what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  thinks about election denial. In fact, in November of 2020, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  dabbled in election denial himself and talks about alternate electors. So, he`s been saying all the stuff that cost Republicans elections in the midterms. So, eventually, he has to get to this place where if he`s going to take on Trump, he`s going to have to address that.\par \par \uc2 Is he going to agree with Trump on that, and anger the voters that just cost the Republicans the midterms, or is he going to disagree with Trump on that and become \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in the face of the MAGA voters? You know, these are challenges that are ahead for DeSantis. I think in the meantime, he`s happy to let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  flail.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Adrienne, if he does announce tonight and that is the expectation, but you never know, right, what do you see as the biggest difference in your mind between Trump the candidate in 2016, and Trump the candidate in 2022?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I think there`s a couple of things, Chris, you know. Number one, in 2016, I think even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was surprised at how well he did in the primary with his sort of bravado chest- thumping mannerisms that you know, where he bullied other candidates on stage and apparently base voters in the Republican Party really liked that. They also like, you know, divisive language and racist language and whatnot. I think this time around, it`s different because I think he knows inherently that he is losing support among those very base voters. That got him to become the nominee in 2016. He -- you know --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think he knows that and understands that and believes that, Adrienne?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: I think he -- I think that there`s a -- I don`t know how our -- Chris, he is going to be on this but I think that deep down he -- or maybe not even so deep down, he knows that that is the case. Because he`s seen the polling, he saw this club -- there`s no way that he hasn`t seen this Club for Growth. We know -- we know that he`s an avid connoisseur of cable news television so I`m sure he`s seen this today.\par \par \uc2 He also knows that you know, a lot of the candidates he supported, I think it was out of the 16 candidates that he endorsed both statewide and for Senate in the states that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won, only two of them actually won election in the general. So, he knows that he`s weakened and that his brand is not what it used to be. But I also think that he committed to right -- to announcing today, a week ago, or whatever it was, and feels that if he doesn`t go out there tonight and say something, his voters are going to see him as even more weak.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think he`s coming out this, you know, not in this strong position that he was in 2016 -- certainly not in 2016. And he`s had a pretty bad track record, Chris, ever since 2016. He lost seats in 2018. He lost reelection in 2022 so, you know, maybe we can actually call him a loser but he`s not in the position that he once was.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Garrett, we know there were advisors for some of the reasons that Adrienne just stated that wanted him to put this off, right? But assuming again and what we`re hearing is going to happen tonight, what`s the mood inside the Trump world as far as you can tell?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL correspondent: Well, there were some advisors who wanted to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  push this announcement back after the midterms in -- or the runoff rather in Georgia so that would not be a distraction in that race. But I think especially after it became clear, the Democrats would control the Senate anyway, regardless of the outcome in that race, it became impossible to restrain the former president from announcing something that he`s come so close to announcing, really almost since the minute he left the White House in 2021. This has always been the goal. It`s always been the plan. The only question has been the timeline.\par \par \uc2 And all these points about Trump being weaker now than he was in the past, notwithstanding even a weakened Trump is still the dominant force in the Republican Party. That`s certainly what he and his advisers believe. And by getting in now, they`ve potentially squashed other elements of the field and potentially keep the field clear from other challengers.\par \par \uc2 Even something like that Club for Growth poll, which, you know, shows DeSantis beating Trump one on one in states like Iowa and New Hampshire, he`s unlikely to face \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  one on one. You`re expected to see a very large, or at least a medium size primary field in this election, which is exactly the kind of circumstance that Trump did take advantage of in 2016 when the field was arrayed against him. And as Tim knows better than anybody else you`re liable to talk to you about this issue, being the anointed candidate two years before an election is not especially fortuitous for Republicans in recent history. And so, I think, taking all those factors into account, the Trump orbit feels pretty good about this. They`re getting get their guy out there and try to remind Republican voters why they liked him so much in the first place.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Adrienne, when Clinton went up against Trump, it was one-on- one and I wonder what you think about the advantage or disadvantage for him of a smaller field, a larger field, you know, and basically having the field to himself for a while?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes. I think -- I think, Chris, it`s -- time will tell. Again, I think Garrett`s right. I mean in many ways, he still does come in as the strongest candidate. You know, he`s got the highest name ID, but just seems it`s creeping in on him. And, you know, he was able to sort of barnstorm through that primary in 2016 by calling people names by, you know, bullying his way sort of the top of the ticket. And even we were surprised on the Clinton campaign that he was, you know, started winning all these states, and not just winning but winning these primary states by a very large margin.\par \par \uc2 This time around, again, Chris, I think a lot of it has to do with the fact that he is weakened. You know, I don`t think we`re going to see a giant primary like we saw in 2016. But it wouldn`t surprise me if we see people like Asa Hutchison, a -- you know, relatively moderate Republican from Arkansas get in. You know, there have been, you know, Reps \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  would get in. So, how does that match-up look? I mean, it could be one of those situations where he`s still able to garner you know, 20 to 25 percent of the vote, but some of the others are also equally competitive. So, I think time will tell. It`s too early to really make that determination at this time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, the bottom line, Tim, is that whether whoever gets in can actually win, right? You get the nomination, but then you have to win. And the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  put it this way. "It`s a painful reality for the cohort of Republicans hoping to move on from Mr. Trump." First, they have to find answers to two inevitable questions. How many Trump diehards are there, and can they be persuaded? What`s the answer in your mind?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, I think that there`s probably about a third of the party that is unpersuadable Trump diehards, but that still leads -- leaves about two- thirds, you can give or take, that are -- that are gettable. Here`s the problem is that middle third -- you know, there`s maybe another third of the party that liked \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , likes normal Republicans would be happy to go back to old school pre-Trump Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But that middle third is very conservative. And so in order to win them over, you know, let`s call them like the Trump navies, right? The people that really liked \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but might be willing to move forward, you are going to have to run very hard to the right. You`re going to have to do all the things that hurt Republicans in this midterm, you know, cruelty, live owning, you know, dabbling in conspiracies. And so, that damage is huge for the general election.\par \par \uc2 And I think this is the great conundrum that somebody like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is in, right? Like he`s in an OK place right now because it`s kind of the idea of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  versus Trump but once you`re in in a campaign, and look, I do experiences day in and day out the bright lights, people like Chris Jansing asking you what do you think about the latest crazy thing Trump said, you know, he`s got to decide every day whether he`s going to agree or disagree with the crazy thing Trump said and the base is going to want to do agree nine times out of 10, right? And so that gets you in trouble with the more mainstream voters that moved away from the Republican Party in the midterms. So, it`s not an easy tightrope to walk. It`s doable, but it`s not easy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I did cover the Jeb campaign, but you never held those questions against me. Did you, Tim?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I did it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tim, stay with me. Adrienne Elrod, thank you so much. So, 