 Weinstein\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, the numbers of accusers, and as you say, a high-profile person, the first lady of California. But you also have a famous Hollywood producer. And when you`re going into a trial like this, Carol, how does that fame, that notoriety play a role? Do you just sort of built into your strategy the idea that a lot of the people on the jury are going to know who they are and maybe even though they said they could be objective have some sort of preconceived notions about them?\par \par \uc2 LAM: Yes, there`s also a sort of star power that attaches to big names. I think in this case, we`ve heard the name \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2  in an unflattering light so much in the past few years that I think that may have worn away, wiped a bit, and I think that, again, the Me Too Movement has done a lot to sort of expose this power play that takes place in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 For the defense to argue, as it has, that this is just sort of par for the course casting couch type of behavior, I`m not sure that plays that well today, in today`s atmosphere, but the defense again doesn`t have a whole lot to work with. They`re just going for that acquittal. Once there`s an acquittal, double jeopardy attaches and that can`t be revisited again.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol Lam, always good to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And right now the man charged with attacking House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s husband Paul Pelosi is making his first court appearance on federal charges. David DePape faces multiple charges including attempted kidnapping of a U.S. official. The charges in all carry a sentence of up to 50 years in prison. The 42-year-old already pleaded not guilty to state charges. We`ll have updates from the courtroom as we get them.\par \par \uc2 And a spokesperson for Speaker Pelosi who has been inundated with questions about her future wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , "She is not even thinking about her futur