 the obvious. Where the heck is Putin? I mean, the Kremlin says there was a need for him to be in the Russian Federation, but why do you think he isn`t there?\par \par \uc2 ADM. \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS\uc2 , FORMER NATO SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER: Hard to say with certainly, but here`s three theories of the case and it`s probably a little bit of each of them. First is, he doesn`t want to let President Xi, you know, his best friend right before the war, he doesn`t want to look him in the eye knowing what a debacle he has created. He told Xi he would conquer Kyiv in a matter of days. Here we are eight months later and he`s the one being kicked out of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s number one. Number two is who wants to walk into a room, Chris, full of people that are just mad at you. Whether you`re kind of leaning toward Putin a little bit or you`re a staunch opponent as most of the G19 are, all of them despise the fact that he`s helping crater the global economy and bring on a recession.\par \par \uc2 And third and finally, I`d pay attention to that comment he needs to be in the Russian Federation. I think the forces are starting to circle in Russia, forces of opposition to Putin. He may not want to be out of the country right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, to your second point, central to the G20, G19 meetings have been these concerns over inflation, food and energy security, and some Republicans, we`ve talked about this before here in the U.S., are questioning the ongoing investment in a war that feels very far away at a time when we have our own economic concerns. But I guess the big question then becomes, given where we are, is there an off-ramp? Where do you see this going now?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Well, first, I think this, Chris, is money well spent for the United States of America and our European allies. It`s the Ukrainians who are fighting and dying. We`re putting tools in their hands, and, yes, there are costs involved in it but compared to Iraq or Afghanistan, these are much lower costs and it`s some treasure and no blood, and the Ukrainians are fighting the fight for all of us, for freedom, for democracy, for liberty.\par \par \uc2 To your second question, yes, I think there`ll ultimately be a diplomatic off-ramp here, probably not until we get through the winter. Putin wants to turn over a card that he thinks may help him, European response to a cold winter. Personally I don`t see the Europeans collapsing in the least, but Putin is going to want to see that. There`ll be some refreshing and refitting, but at the end of the day by the time we get through the winter, both the Russians and the Ukrainians are going to have a hard time simply resuming high levels of combat.\par \par \uc2 They burned through too many people, too much equipment. I think that`s the moment when you could see a move toward the negotiating table. Let`s hope so.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Admiral \uc2 James Stavridis\uc2 , always a pleasure, sir to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 With the high stakes Georgia runoffs still in limbo, Herschel Walker`s campaign has two words for the GOP. Pay up. We`ll explain that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:32]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Herschel Walker`s campaign has a big message for Republicans ahead of the Georgia runoff. Stop the deceptive fundraising. Because despite an uptick in e-mails trying to raise money for Walker`s ongoing Senate runoff, he`s not seeing all that cash.\par \par \uc2 I want the bring in NBC national political reporter Marc Caputo who has been covering this for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Marc, where was the money going and is the Walker camp seeing any changes after calling this out?\par \par \uc2 MARC CAPUTO, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, the money was going to the other candidates and political committees who are saying, hey, give money to help in the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  runoff. And they`re keeping 90 cents of every dollars. Now Herschel Walker`s campaign raised a stick about it, and now those various Republicans, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  among them, J.D. Vance in Ohio, maybe \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  in North Carolina, they`ve agreed to go to a 50-50 split. The problem is these committees keep cropping back up and all that money being raised in Herschel Walker`s name is not all going to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You also have a new story out today about the growing tensions between Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  over the leader`s support of Walker. Once Democrats took the Senate, did it cause some Republicans to lose a little bit of steam over the stakes in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Well, Democrats smelled blood and they`ve committed at least $4 million in TV ad spending and right now the National Republican Senatorial Committee headed by Governor \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  or Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  has pledged about $700,000 and they want the Senate Leadership Fund which is headed up by McConnell allies to kick in a whole bunch more money and match the political committee on the Democrat side and so far that`s not happening.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 So at the heart of these tensions there`s a long running feud between Scott and McConnell that`s kind of rooted in Scott`s support for Trump and McConnell`s relative lack of it, and an interest in Scott possibly running for leader one day. But what`s manifested in the meantime these two top level Republicans are feuding, and meanwhile \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is looking for extra money as he faces kind of an uphill climb in helping overtake Senate or better said take the Senate seat back from \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Marc Caputo, thank you so much. Appreciate your reporting.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of cash, if you`re still dreaming of how you`d spend a billion dollars if only you`d won the Powerball, how about this? A mere 37.1 million bucks was the winning bid to make your home more like a museum with this Pablo Picasso painting. "Guitar on a Table" previously hung just a few blocks from where I`m sitting right now. It was in \uc2 the Museum of Modern Art\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And if not a painting, how about some shoes? \uc2 Steve Jobs\uc2 , late founder of \uc2 Apple\uc2 , was widely known for his sleek black turtleneck, but also part of that iconic uniform, Birkenstocks, that an auction house billed as worn during many pivotal moments in Apple`s history. Acknowledging they were indeed well-worn, the obvious question was, do they smell? The auctioneer`s answer, they do smell -- of success. The auction price was $218,000.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. I`m sticking around for "KATY TUR REPORTS" and that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221116eibf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word With Lawrence O`Donnell for November 14, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell \par \uc2 7,422 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:01:06]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 You know, there are two kinds of people who listen to what you just said. And they are the lucky people who`ve already listened to episode seven today.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s the other lucky people that includes me who have not yet listened to episode seven, who -- who can look forward to it, and in my case, listen to it tomorrow. Those are the two groups out there, two very happy groups.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": Can I tell you one thing that I`m super proud of about this episode? Which is --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Please.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: -- like I know it`s terrible to break, but I`m excited. So, when we are tracking this episode seven, the one that went up today, we had an engineer who is listening to the initial track, like my first draft read of it. And he didn`t know that we can hear him, but the intercom, the system was on, so we could hear him in the studio, so he`s listening to it.\par \par \uc2 As he was listening to the track, and one point in the plot, he went, what? No way! No way!\par \par \uc2 He didn`t know that we could hear him. And it was like, after tell, you that moment, if I could bottle it, it would -- I could take -- I could take a dose of that once a year every year and live to be 350 years old.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. No, it`s when you know it`s working.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes. I was so happy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Rachel, before we go, I`m going to just say if I can say this correctly. We have the most successful White House chief of staff in the first midterm election of a Democratic presidency since 1962 joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Arguably since 1932, the pending on how you`re looking at it. But, I mean, yes, I know the man of whom you speak, and he has a place in the history books.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. And he will be joining us. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Excellent. Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 ODONNELL: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the breaking news of the night is enough their big win for the Democrats in Arizona, with Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  winning the governor`s election. That means Donald Trump`s Republican candidate for governor of Arizona who is an election liar -- let`s use the right term. Election denier is not quite specific enough. Election liar, about the last presidential election, will not be an opposition to the destroy democracy, and our next presidential election by trying to find a way to award Arizona `s 11 electoral votes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or whoever the Republican residential nominee turns that to be.\par \par \uc2 That was -- this was one of those races where as President Biden put it, democracy was on the ballot. And this time, and the person of \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , democracy won. Some Democrats in Arizona and elsewhere who mistakenly believed polls indicating that \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  was on her way to losing to Donald Trump`s candidate, Kari Lake, openly criticized \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  for being what they considered a weak candidate, and they criticized her for her decision to refuse to debate Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 Because Kari Lake is a relentless public liar, that`s why she refused to debate her.\par \par \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  explained her decision not to debate this way: Unfortunately, debating a conspiracy theorist like Kari Lake, whose entire campaign platform is to cause enormous chaos and make Arizona the subject of national ridicule, would only lead to costly interruptions, pointless distractions, and childish name-calling. Arizonans deserve so much better than Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 And that is what Arizonans shows. So much better than Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  had to say about her opponent in what turned out to be \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  last appearance on this program before the election.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D), ARIZONA GOVERNOR-ELECT: She`s not focused on what Arizonans need. She`s focused on what the former president wants. That`s exactly why he`s endorsed her in this race, and why we have to stop her. Because she`s made it clear she`s not interested in upholding the will of the factors, and she doesn`t agree with the will of the virus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, tonight, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  did stop her.\par \par \uc2 Friday night, when Steve Kornacki and I delivered the news from Arizona, that Democrat Mark Kelly won his reelection to a six-year term in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , the Democrats moved to within one seat of control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , and about 24 hours later, and Nevada, Steve Kornacki was at the big board in the moment when the NBC News decision desk projected Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  would be reelected to a six-year term in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 That was the most important election announcement of the week, since it ended the crushing suspense about which party was going to control the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . After the Nevada race was called, White House chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  who will join us tonight, tweeted Senator Schumer and Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , that`s the tweet.\par \par \uc2 You will hear from both of them tonight. You`ve already heard from Senator Schumer during Rachel`s hour, and you will hear from Senator Peters during this hour.\par \par \uc2 When Mark Kelly won his Senate reelection in Arizona on Friday night, the first gassed I wanted to hear from on this program was Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , who is the chair of the Democratic Senate campaign.\par \par \uc2 By Friday night, \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  was all ready the most successful chair of the Democratic Senate campaign committee enough first midterm election of a Democratic president in the history of the Democratic Senate campaign committee.\par \par \uc2 Now, he`s even more successful by having delivered Democratic control of the Senate to the Democrats once again. That tweet by \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  on Saturday night, after the Democrats reached the magic number of 50 in the Senate was the White House chief of staff`s highly experienced assessment of who gets credit for the Democrats holding the senate. Senator Schumer and Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , that`s the tweet.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  talked very Peters into becoming the chair of the Democratic Senate Campaign Committee, the COVID-19 pandemic was still raging through the United States. People weren`t yet vaccinated. And travel was still very risky in a job where Senator Peters was going to have to travel to dozens of states and support the Democratic candidates running for Senate.\par \par \uc2 In a private, off record chat with Senator Peters after he became the chair of the Democratic Senate Campaign Committee, I began by offering him my condolences for getting the worst job in the Senate. And that is the inside view in the Senate of running the Democratic Senate Campaign Committee, which is the single most difficult fundraising task any senator could ever take on. It is much more difficult than raising money for your own Senate reelection campaign. It is much more complex, much more unforgiving, much more relentless.\par \par \uc2 It`s the kind of job where you have to kiss your family goodbye for two years. And, it`s not easy to make friends while doing it. Because, the most common attitude Senate candidates have is that the Democratic Senate Campaign Committee is not giving them enough money. No matter how much money they are getting from the DSCC.\par \par \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  had the advantage of going into the job with no known enemies and the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , and being known as a relentlessly hard worker who was never trying to elbow others out of the way for his own public attention.\par \par \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  is now a hero, a hero on the majority side of the Senate. Unlike most chairs of the Democratic Senate Campaign Committee, \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  job is continuing on for four more weeks after election day to the next election day in Georgia on December 6th. When \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  has the possibility of leaving a record of no Democratic senator, losing a reelection campaign on his watch if Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is elected in charge of, reelected in Georgia on December 6th.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " points out that that would be the first time no Senate incumbent has lost in either party since 1914.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the story is the chair of the Republican Senate Campaign Committee, Florida Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who has done the worst job of any chair of the Republican Senate Campaign Committee in the first midterm election of a Democratic president. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  contributed personally and mightily that the disaster that Republicans have faced when he said that Social Security should be repealed, and that Social Security would then be placed with a temporary five-year program that could be reauthorized by Congress every five years if Congress passed a bill to do it, and if that bill wasn`t signed by the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 In other words, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  proposed turning social security into retirement insecurity in the form of a program subject to the whims of Congress and presidents.\par \par \uc2 Today, Senator Scott went to Georgia to help Donald Trump`s chosen candidate for senate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and ended up saying exactly, exactly what \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  would say about what`s at stake in the Georgia Senate race that could deliver 51 Democratic senators to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): We do committees based on how many senators you have. So, it`s 50/50 -- each of these committees, it`ll make a big difference in what type of legislation -- on top of that, it takes 60 votes to get through a filibuster. So, if we had 50, we`re in a better position to stop something we think is crazy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight, in the winners circle, Democratic Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  of Michigan. He`s the chair of the Democratic senatorial campaign committee, for just a few more weeks. You must be very happy to be coming to the end of this and actually be able to start your Christmas shopping on December 7th.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 GARY PETERS\uc2  (D-MI): Exactly, Lawrence. Thanks for having me on. Yes, I`ll be glad when it`s over, especially when we reelect \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  as the 51st Democratic senator. I`m confident we`re going to do that, that we`re going to continue to run a tough, hard campaign. We`ve got a clear contrast between these two candidates, and I`m confident the good folks in Georgia will want a senator like \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , a man with character, a man with impeccable qualifications and talent, the represent them in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, when we had that private discussion about this a while ago, before the campaign really heated up, I began the discussion feeling sorry for you, as I always do for whoever gets stuck with this job. But I have to say, more than anyone I`ve ever known who`s had the job, you convinced me that you actually had a plan about how to do this.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t believe the plan, mind you, I didn`t go that far. But I saw that you really had a determined approach to this, and you thought it was worth what this commitment had to be for you to do it. But that wasn`t the only reason you are doing it, that you have to plan. At the time, he also told me he believed the stakes for the country were so deeply important, and that I was convinced of.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, it was. There`s no question that we needed to maintain the majority, particularly when we see our democracy and a threat from what we`ve come to expect from the Democratic Party. It was important for us to hold that.\par \par \uc2 But you have to have a plan. You know, I`ve been in a lot of very tough races. When I was first elected to Congress, I was the first Democrat since 1893 to hold that district. In 2010, remember how difficult that was for Democrats? I won reelection even though I governor, Republican governor in my district, won by 26 points.\par \par \uc2 And then in 2014, when I was elected to the Senate, I was the only new Democrat elected that year in the country.\par \par \uc2 So I`m no stranger to tough races. And basically, the way you win as you have to have a plan, you have to stick with the plan, you have to be disciplined, you have to be focused, and you have to understand what your objective is. My objective always was, first and foremost, to bring everyone coming back. That`s my number one job. Based off on my, gosh when we did that, I knew we`d be a 50, and then we have a chance to pick up some seats as well but some very talented candidates that were running and our other states.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:11]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is the Peters` plan for Georgia?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, it`ll be a continuation of what we did to be successful on election day, and that is to continue to support candidates, to continue to basically show the very clear difference between these two candidates, as I mentioned already. You`ve got \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , a man of incredible character and qualifications, versus his opponent, who was seriously flawed, and it`s not just me saying he seriously flawed. It`s Republicans that say he is seriously flawed. So we`ll continue to make that distinction.\par \par \uc2 But bottom line, it`s going to be about turnout. It`s going to be about making sure our voters and that`s what we did in these elections. In fact, this election, we invested record amounts of money from the DSCC. It`s the first time in history that we spend more money on the ground campaign, giving voters to the polls than we did an independent expenditures and TV commercials and the like, because ultimately, we knew these would be close races, and the way you win a close race is to make sure that you`ve got a ground campaign able to deliver your voters to the voting booth.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , I`ve seen many people do this job that you`ve done, including \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , \uc2 Bob Kerrey\uc2 , many capable senators. I`ve never seen one do it and end with no -- I won`t say enemies, but with no animosity on his party`s side of the Senate at the end of doing this job.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight, and congratulations on the big win.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, thank you, and we`re not done. We still got one more big win to come.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Please come back in join us. Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  will join us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:21:07]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: At 7:32 p.m. on Saturday night, as we eagerly awaited the results of the Senate election in Nevada that could give control of the Senate to the Democrats, former White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Everybody in the White House right now is holding their breath, ready to issue a big sigh of relief if you get some results tonight, because it`s going to change the next two years for President Biden. I mean, it`ll change they got confirmed, it`ll change cutting judges confirmed, which is a huge part of his legacy, I complete obsession of Ron Klain`s, and they`re waiting for the results to come. In this changes the vibe for the next several months, and even years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, one hour and 45 minutes later, this happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: That is right on the numbers she`s been getting. That`s right what Democrats are expecting. You see it`s not been updated in our system. And so, statewide, there it is.\par \par \uc2 For the first time now since election night, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  has past \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , has moved into the lead. It`s essentially a 5,000-vote lead, 4,982 votes. That`s Catherine Cortez Masto`s advantage statewide, one half of 1 percent. She moves into the lead again.\par \par \uc2 This is it in terms of mail ballots in Clark County. They said tonight would be the last night. That`s the final batch. We got 22,000-plus right there. It has totally changed the leader board in this race. Masto ahead by about 5,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 And as they say, what is to come now? I believe -- I believe that maybe, with very -- there it is. That`s the check mark. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  declared by our decision desk the winner of the Nevada Senate race.\par \par \uc2 With Catherine Cortez Masto`s victory and then by that Senate race, Democrats now have control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in short. They have their 50 seats, with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  casting the tie breaking vote, the guarantees Democratic control of the Senate for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now in the winners circle is White House chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ron, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said that win would put a skip in your step. So, thank you for skipping over to our microphone this evening for this discussion.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF : And thank you for not showing me skipping. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We have that video for later. We`ll show that later.\par \par \uc2 Ron, what was that moment like for all of you in the White House for waiting for it?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Well, I mean, obviously, it`s enormously significant. Historically, this is something no president has done since \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2 , to hold Senate seats and potentially even go up one Senate seat, pending the outcome in Georgia. To keep control of the Senate, which will indeed allow the president to pass legislation in the Senate, and of course, get people confirmed, and in some ways, it`s perhaps -- historical purposes, the idea to get our judicial nominees confirmed by a Democratic Senate.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s one of the most important legacies of any president, and a very, very important legacy to this president. And so, winning the Senate, keeping control of the Senate is just an unbelievable moment.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You mentioned the candidate presidency. That midterm is 60 years ago. And we know that in the White House, the president, the vice president, and the White House chief of staff all participate in both governing and political campaigning issues and strategizing and considerations.\par \par \uc2 That means that \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is the most successful Democratic White House chief of staff in a midterm first midterm election since 1962.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:06]\par \par \uc2 The job that you held then was then held by Kenneth O`Donnell, not related to me in any way, from Massachusetts. What`s it like for you personally? Because a lot of people were watching the job you are doing over the last two years and over the last year, questioning how you are handling that shop. What does this victory mean for you?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: What it means is I work with an incredibly talented group of people, starting off with Jen O`Malley Dillon, who was the deputy chief of staff and oversees our political operation, the president`s chief strategist, Mike Donilon, his chief communication guru, Anita Dunn, \uc2 Steve Ricchetti\uc2 , our legislative team. You just on the line -- \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2  who came in to oversee our outreach in advance of this election year.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, I am lucky to work with an amazingly diverse team of people, and amazingly talented team of people, and amazingly dedicated team of people, and they certainly, you know, just work night and day to help deliver this result.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One thing the president said at the very end of the press conference today is that this win, apparently, does not mean codifying Roe versus Wade. Let`s listen to what exactly he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don`t think there`s enough votes to codify, unless something happens unusual in the House. I think we`ll get very close in the House, but I don`t -- I think it`s going to be very close. But I don`t think we`re going to make it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, Ron, does not mean that if the Democrats won`t actually end up winning the House, that there still won`t be an attempt to codify Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Look, I think we`re going to take a soft shot at. Certainly, absolutely, if the Democrats win the House, we`re going to take us -- a run at it.\par \par \uc2 I think what the president was reflecting there is just uncertainty about where the House is going to end up right now. We have an under dog in the House before the election, or an underdog now as the votes are being counted, but there still a path to 218 for the Democrats. So we`re going to keep our fingers and toes crossed here, to see if that`s how the count comes out.\par \par \uc2 The president made it clear throughout this campaign that we`re going to do everything in his power to protect women`s right to choose. We`re going to use our executive time powers to protect the woman`s right to choose. It`s already taken significant actions in that regard in terms of the right to travel, in terms of making sure the abortion pill, which is the way most women access their reproductive rights, is available in all 50 states, making sure the hospital performs urgency procedures.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of legislative action, that will obviously depend on where the vote count ends up, and we still just don`t know.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Elizabeth Warren today is saying that the Senate and House should come back into session before the end of the year. And among other things, pass some increase in the debt ceiling so that Republicans, if they control the house next year, can not hold the debt ceiling hostage.\par \par \uc2 What`s the president`s position on that?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Well, first of all, again, as I just explained, we`re not really sure who`s getting control of the house next year. But certainly, I think it`ll be wise of the Congress could take steps to raise the debt limit before the end of the year, the kind of take it off the table and not have anxiety in the economy about it next year.\par \par \uc2 But the House and Senate have a busy agenda in this lame duck session. We need to fund the government, of course. We just learned today there`s a bipartisan agreement to protect marriage equality. We`d obviously like to see that get passed and to the president`s desk and signed into law.\par \par \uc2 There`s work needing to be done on electoral reform, the Electoral Count Act needs to be updated so we don`t have a repeat of the insanity we saw on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And so, there`s a big agenda here. We`re going to see how things sorted out the next three weeks and try to get as much of that done as possible.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ron, you are now, I think, officially the only person on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  who is monitoring gasoline prices. Back in the middle of the summer, I think every other tweet I saw was the skyrocketing price of gasoline, and that obviously meant the collapse of any possibility of Democrats being successful in this last election.\par \par \uc2 Today, since we won`t give up, he said the grass prices fell last night -- down from last week, about half the states are now $3.59 or lower. What is -- and that is one of the major components of inflation that we`ve been dealing with. What is the president`s position going forward on what he thinks he can do, even if there is a Republican House of Representatives in terms of wrestling with inflation?\par \par \uc2 [22:30:00]\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, I think with inflation overall, you know, the top of our list obviously is continuing to untie supply chains. You`ve seen the price of goods starting to fall now, in the consumer price index, that`s the product of the work we`ve done already, and will continue to do to improve supply chain. So goods get to consumers easier and the cost of that comes down.\par \par \uc2 You started to see some break in inflation around food. That`s something we`re very focused on. And, of course, as you said, gasoline prices matter. I know what matters to people. I know it`s a very visible price. It really impacts your household budget, and your sense of your household budget.\par \par \uc2 And so we continue to process the sales. We`ve ordered from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. We`re going to continue to work with the oil industry to make sure that when the barrel price of oil comes down, the gallon price of gasoline falls as well. We`ve seen a larger gap than usual on that. That`s one reason why the President`s been pressing so hard to get that price of gasoline down.\par \par \uc2 And most importantly, or perhaps additionally, I should say, the Inflation Reduction Act passed by Congress in August doesn`t really take effect until January 1. We`re just a few weeks away from consumer seeing the price of insulin capped at $35 a month, from seeing over charges by drug companies getting rebated back to them, for seeing their Medicaid, Medicare premiums drop for the first time in a decade, for seeing lower costs on energy efficient appliances. So all these things start to take effect just a few weeks away on New Year`s Day.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Ron, you have your own experience working on the staff of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , especially on judicial confirmations when you`re on the staff --\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- of the Judiciary Committee, when Senator Biden was chairman. What does -- what is the difference for the Democrats and for the President`s agenda in the Senate, the difference between having 50 Democrats in the Senate or 51, depending on the outcome of the election in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Well, it is very significant. I think in terms of making the Senate work more efficiently. Look, we`ve been very fortunate. We`ve picked excellent people to serve on the federal bench. And the vast majority, the record setting 86 judges the President has gotten confirmed, have had bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 But in the few cases where it`s a party line vote only, you have to have an extra vote to get them out of committee when you`ve only got 50. And the committee hearings go along and all these things. So I think 51 will certainly make it easier to move things in an orderly way through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to continue. Whether we`re at 50 or 51, we`re going to continue to look for bipartisan support for our nominees, bipartisan support for our judges, bipartisan support for legislation. That was always Joe Biden`s goal when he`s a senator. It`s been his goal as President. And as I said, for the vast majority of our record setting 86 judicial nominees, they have had that bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , whose Wikipedia entry should now include the title most successful -- I got to make sure I get this exactly right -- most successful Democratic White House Chief of Staff in a first midterm presidential -- midterm election since 1962. Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Ron. That`s going to be an all of your introductions here when you come back.\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: I appreciate it. Thank you, Lawrence. Thanks for having me as always. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, one of the big Democratic winners of this weekend who flipped a Republican House seat in the state of Washington to Democrat will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:38:24]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks to our next guest, this man will not be a member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The State of Washington has laws that protect a right to an abortion. Would you want to see those overturned in Washington State?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I would. Yes. Life begins at conception. And I think that the federal government should outline in all the states. I don`t think states should be able to sanction killing the unborn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Representative-elect Marie Gluesenkamp Perez of the state of Washington, the 3rd Congressional District. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it. Was that the decisive issue in your election, abortion?\par \par \uc2 MARIE GLUESENKAMP PEREZ (D), WASHINGTON REPRESENTATIVE: It is, it is rights, it`s the economy and it`s working out in the middle. You know, we live in a moderate district. We elected Brian Baird, \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2 . Listen, we have no business running these kinds of MAGA extremists, people that want a federal ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 My opponents supported a 20-year ban on all immigration to reestablish a white majority, defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . I don`t know anybody in my community who supports those goals. So he`s way out of step with our district. And I feel so honored to have the opportunity to come here and work for the people of Southwest Washington.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And your district has been a Republican district for over 10 years now. So you, obviously, had to appeal to Republican voters to win. How did you do that?\par \par \uc2 GLUESENKAMP PEREZ: Well, you know, I`m a small business owner. I own an auto repair and a machine shop. I live in Rural Skamania County. I get my water from a well. I get my internet from a radio tower. I get my \uc2 Netflix\uc2  on DVDs.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:05]\par \par \uc2 And I think that that is -- listen people want a Congress that looks like America. We are really tired of clickbait politics and having \uc2 Twitter\uc2  control our policy. We want small business owners, working families, young parents. That`s what people are looking for in Congress today, I believe.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I get my \uc2 Netflix\uc2  on DVDs, is the line of the night on this hour. You know, in Washington, D.C., you can get \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , you know, the easy way when you get there. We just wanted to say --\par \par \uc2 GLUESENKAMP PEREZ: But there`s no trees here.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I know, I know. I know it`s a big difference. Thank you for joining us tonight. I wish we had more time. I just wanted you to get a victory moment in here and a greeting to our viewers.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Representative-elect Marie Gluesenkamp Perez. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GLUESENKAMP PEREZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, finally, the Democrats did well, very well. And this is really important in the elections for state legislatures. The person most responsible for that success at the all-important state legislature level will appear in the victory circle here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:45:52]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In Michigan, Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  was re-elected with 54 percent of the vote on 11 point margin over her Republican challenger and in Michigan Democrats flipped control of both chambers of the state legislature giving Democrats control of the state government for the first time in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Jessica Post, President of the Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. You were the leader of the Democratic Party`s national effort to win at the state legislature level. What are some of the other important winning stories of this campaign?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA POST, PRESIDENT OF THE DEMOCRATIC LEGISLATIVE CAMPAIGN COMMITTEE.: Well, certainly the Michigan State Legislature was a huge win. We were so happy to flip both chambers there. But I also think one stat you may have heard is that Democrats as the party in power, we lost fewer legislative chambers, we lost zero legislative chambers. And that`s the best Democrats have done at the state legislative level in a presidential midterm since 1934, since FDR.\par \par \uc2 So we`re very happy about that. And typically, in midterms, the President`s party loses seats or legislative chambers, and we were able to beat the odds in a huge way this cycle.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There`s always been an impression that the National Democratic Party ignores the state legislature level this year when we see that state legislatures are now in control of abortion laws in this country. Was it easier to focus people`s attention on the state legislature?\par \par \uc2 POST: I think it was still difficult, Lawrence. I wish I could say that it was an easy -- it was sort of an easy move. But I think people don`t necessarily understand the mechanics of government. Look, the Republicans really understand how power works in the states. And we as Democrats have been behind for a long time, like wishing that the federal government will come in to save us.\par \par \uc2 But if abortion rights is being resolved, state legislative level, that`s also true for voting rights and education and the quality of the public schools. So many things are resolved in state legislatures. And as you know, and many of your viewers know, after the Thomas concurrence on Dobbs, more things like Obergefell, birth control and the Griswold case, all of those things could head to the states. And that`s why it`s so important to -- for Democrats to win state legislatures across the country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It`s also the initial training grounds of some of the greatest leaders of the Democratic Party in history from Franklin Delano Roosevelt, to \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , to Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , who just started us off tonight, they began as state legislators.\par \par \uc2 POST: That`s 100 percent right. The new governor of Arizona, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , was in the State Senate when I started my job here at DLCC. So it`s been an incredible proving ground for folks from \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , Jon Tester, Claire McCaskill, all sorts of folks who have served in state legislatures all over the country.\par \par \uc2 It`s an incredible proving ground. But I think, folks, look, this isn`t the minor leagues getting to the major leagues, which I think is a common misconception. Most of the decisions that are affecting your day to day lives, those decisions are being made in state legislatures, and it`s past time for us to understand that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Jessica Post in the winner`s circle tonight, thank you very much for joining us. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 POST: Thank you, Lawrence. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I did not obsess on the polls during the campaign because I let our next guest do it for me. The brilliant Simon Rosenberg who was never fooled about a big red wave, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:53:38]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Great news, our next guest is going to give you your life back because after listening to him, if you`re wise, you will never obsess over polls again and you will never be trying to fall asleep in fear of a giant red wave that isn`t coming.\par \par \uc2 Simon Rosenberg writes, "Red wave became a collective delusion. It obscured what had been happening in the election since Dobbs elevated Democratic intensity, Republican struggling. Late promising Democratic data, good polls, strong early vote, was dismissed. Hopium it was called."\par \par \uc2 Once again, the pundit world showed that Oscar winning screenwriter William Goldman`s line about showbusiness applies to them, William Goldman said of show business quote, nobody knows anything.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN HOST: The politicos with whom I spoke today all agree, as of now a modest red wave at the very least seems to be building.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The short answer is it`s going to look good for Republicans. The longer answer is it`s going to look very good for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I for one, never obsessed over polls during the campaign. I never reported on the generic congressional ballot polling because I think those polls are mostly useless and because among many other things as legendary House Speaker Tip O`Neill famously put it, all politics is local. So I personally gave up trying to figure out what was going to happen in congressional elections a very long time ago.\par \par \uc2 But this year, whenever the latest poll showing some version of so-called bad news for Democrats came up I always noticed that on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Simon Rosenberg had a wise counterpoint or two or more to raise about those negative polls for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:15]\par \par \uc2 Simon Rosenberg is now widely credited as the person who wisely saw through the red wave delusion, when just about everyone else in the prediction game, fell for it.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now for a well-deserved victory lap is Simon Rosenberg, a veteran \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  successful first presidential campaign, and he has served tours of duty at the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee. Thank you very much for joining us, Simon.\par \par \uc2 I want to cover as much ground as I can for people just to convince them to stop obsessing over polls. And let me just begin with one thing that I`ve heard you explain brilliantly, and that is, what is a likely voter? What is a likely voter in polls and how that`s basically some form of a guess of who will likely voter is?\par \par \uc2 SIMON ROSENBERG, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yes, I mean, it is a guess. And it is -- particularly, it was particularly hard in this election, because in the last two elections, the electorate grew so much. And we had all these new voters in the electorate, it was going to be very, very hard to know who was really going to vote.\par \par \uc2 And so I do think this question about likely voter is gotten -- it was always hard, but it was much harder in this election. It`s one of the reasons why the polls were really all over the place over the last few months.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And I want to go to something you said about what we saw in Michigan, which we were just discussing, this win --\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- of both chambers in the state legislature --\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- and the governor, you`ve said that as the dust clears, that might be the most important thing that happened.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes, I think it`s the most important thing -- it may be the most important story of the election, because it shows the power and potency of the abortion issue. It should really worry the Republicans and on an issue that is just so important to so many people, they are so deeply out of position.\par \par \uc2 And it`s very hard to understand how they get back into position on this issue. Their base is not going to let them cut a deal with Democrats, not let them moderate in any way on this issue. They`ve made an historic error. And to me, it could be an error as big as what happened with the Republicans in the Great Depression, where an entire generation of people just look at that party as something that`s just not really available to them.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s a deeply consequential thing. It`s not just going to drive this election and could drive the next few elections. And it`s why I`m very optimistic about Democratic chances in the coming years.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, when you were in the Clinton war room, and I was hanging around my very first presidential campaign that I was working in the Senate, but --\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- adjacent to it. I remember being in New Hampshire, and for the primary for the first time in my life, and every -- you walk into one of the places where all the presses hanging out and everyone asked everyone else the same question, what do you know? It`s just that oh, no, hello, nothing, just what do you know.\par \par \uc2 And to be smart in that conversation, you were supposed to know what was going to happen which has something to do with what we were just seeing on video there with people pretending they know what`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes, listen, the big miss this cycle was on Democratic intensity. I mean, the polls weren`t wrong. The analysis was wrong. The analysis of the polls and the data were wrong. And let me explain really quickly why, right?\par \par \uc2 In June, the election changed. The combination of Uvalde, January 6 committee hearings, you know, ending of Roe, the abortion restrictions, and all of a sudden, what we were seeing, right, in these five House specials that took place, were consistent Democratic over performance, and Republicans struggling.\par \par \uc2 You saw it again, in Kansas, you saw Democrats over performing Republicans struggling. Didn`t really look like a red wave. And then you saw Democrats raising much more money into their campaigns than Republicans, another sign of Republican struggle. And then the voter reg numbers went way up for us, right? We saw a big surge of Democrats registering particularly women and young women.\par \par \uc2 So we were checking all of these intensity boxes, and the Republicans were checking none of them. And so the question was, would that same dynamic show up in election day? And then the early vote came, and we saw that same dynamic play out over the two weeks the early vote. And so what we posited was, you know, look, what we`re looking at here, and as you put out in the tweet earlier, the polling in the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 Also, if you stripped out the partisan polls, were pretty good for Democrats, including your final NBC poll, which may be the single best poll of the whole cycle, by the way, the NBC poll that came out the Sunday before the election. What we were looking at was high democratic intensity for months and months and months, Democratic over performance, a close competitive election, and polls that were unbalanced, actually pretty good for us at the end.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, we were optimistic that election day, the election that you saw was going to take place and we`re pleased that it did obviously.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Simon Rosenberg`s Twitter handle is up on the screen under his name so that you can follow him as I did during the campaign so that you can relax and have your life back and read a book or something like that.\par \par \uc2 Simon Rosenberg, thank you very much for guiding my thinking through this campaign --\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Lawrence, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221115eibe00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for November 13, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,819 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, in control.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the battle for at least one chamber of Congress has been settled nearly a week after election day. With Katherine Cortez Masto`s reelection victory last night in Nevada, Democrats have retained control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , and they still have a chance to pick up another seat. The pending a runoff in the state of Georgia, the Senate race there between Democratic incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his Republican Trump-backed challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , both candidates holding campaign events today in the Peach State ahead of next month`s runoff.\par \par \uc2 While the Senate majority will remain with Democrats regardless of the outcome in Georgia, the House is still in play. Republicans holding the slightest majority at present and expected to keep it when all is said and done. As the GOP tries to chart its way forward after and underwhelming midterm showing, it has to deal with the looming disruption of Donald Trump`s expected announcement this week that he may make a third bid for the White House in 2024. Later in the show, I wrote a memo to Trump with my advice for what I think he should do.\par \par \uc2 But control of the Senate is where we start today. Joining me now, Senator Bob Casey, Democrat of Pennsylvania. Senator, first, thank you for being with us tonight. With the dual victories of Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in his Senate race in your state and Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  in this race for governor last week in your state, is it a stretch to say that Pennsylvania, which was considered an electoral tossup just week, is now going to blue?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB CASEY (D-PA): There is no question that the margins that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  won by and \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  an even bigger margin indicate that Pennsylvania has got a strong preference for Democrats right now, and that is a good foundation. We have to continue to battle, though, in the years ahead to make sure that we maintain that edge.\par \par \uc2 But both candidates, I think, started with a strong foundation because people knew them. Josh is the attorney general, and John is a lieutenant governor. And that connection they have to voters was difficult to break, no matter what their opponents did. But both ran really strong campaigns, and we are very proud of the races that they won.\par \par \uc2 John`s race, of course, for the Senate, was especially personal to people like me because we are working so hard to make sure he can be elected. But he withstood a barrage of negative advertising the likes of which no Democratic candidate for the Senate has ever seen that, and he stood strong in the eye of that storm and he prevailed by more than most people would have predicted.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It`s absolutely true. I preached down in Philadelphia, moved around a little bit with GOTV, and it was very ugly, some of the stuff you would see that were said at Fetterman. But I`m glad you said that you have to keep it because people that came out and voted in big numbers are expecting Democrats to deliver. Your current and future majority leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , said this at a press conference earlier today coming off the results of last week and of Nevada, Senator Katherine Cortez Masto`s win last night in Nevada. Listen to what \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): This is a very happy day. We had great candidates. They had flawed candidates. We won because we had a really good agenda that we actually passed that help the American people on things that they cared about. We won because the American people rejected the kinds of autocracy that some -- the MAGA Republicans, a wing of that party, was talking about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, that is a pretty long list of reasons why we have Democrats being successful in this midterm cycle. What can you tell us about how Senate Democrats are now preparing to move forward with regards to everything that is laid out before us?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: Well, Reverend Al, I can tell you what I hope we do. We made a lot of progress in the first two years of President Biden`s administration.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:03]\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to helping people and helping communities, the child tax credit, for example, was a great benefit for people who had children, raising their children in the midst of the pandemic, whether it was in urban community like Philadelphia or a small rural, like Mifflin County or Green County, or so many others. So, it was a direct help to families at a time of crisis.\par \par \uc2 But we also were helping the community by investing in infrastructure so they can`t rebuild roads and bridges and invest in high-speed internet. I think they heard what we did this August, when we took action to lower the cost of prescription drugs for seniors. And I think more of that is going to become manifest in the next couple years.\par \par \uc2 But there are still some things we`ve got to do. I think we should try to continue the child tax credit, the generous version of what we passed in a rescue plan so we can continue to help millions of children across the country. We still, as you know, have to do more when it comes to criminal justice and voting rights and so much else. And with John Fetterman`s win, we have one more vote to get closer and closer to what I hope will be a rules change so we can pass these important matters that affect people`s lives with a majority vote instead of having it still be tied to a 60 vote rule, which has blocked so much progress.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And That`s exactly what I wanted raises, because, as you know, people like me are going to be saying, yes, we want to know about the child tax credit, yes, voting rights, as you mentioned, yes criminal justice reform. We cannot just have people vote for an exercise, they must vote for progress.\par \par \uc2 And saying that, as we await what might be Donald Trump`s expected announcement of a third White House bid on Tuesday, are you at all surprised by the criticisms of Trump and his impact on the midterms coming from his own party now? As dozens of the candidates he backed in the midterms were defeated, including one last night, of course, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , in the Nevada Senate race. Are you surprised that now it seems that some Republicans are criticizing Trump and actually moving away from him?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: Well, I guess, I`m a little surprised at the scope of it but we will see what they do. And I think they have to ask themselves a question, whether or not they want to be the party of extremism, and the party of kind of genuflecting to one person as opposed to being a party that is getting things done for the American people. So, that`s something they have got to ask themselves and answer that question.\par \par \uc2 But I think what we`ve got to do is make sure that we stay committed to the issues that helped us win this year and to get more done. I was impressed that in the Senate race, for example, not only did \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  do a little bit better in a lot of rural counties than President Biden had done in his one-point victory in 2020 in Pennsylvania, but he was also able to do a little better in urban and suburban communities. And this is true.\par \par \uc2 In order to win Pennsylvania, you have to be able to do both. And I was certainly proud of what John received in cities, the urban communities. I mean, for example, he was getting 83 percent of the black vote in our state, and that vote is only about 8 percent of the vote. So, he got a lot out of that 8 percent of the vote to help him in a tough race.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No doubt about it. Senator, quickly, because we are out of time, but we still have a Senate race in Georgia with the majority secured last night for the Democrats in the Senate. What does it mean for Senate Democrats` fortunes if Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  ultimately prevails in the December 6th runoff, his Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , both of them campaigning in Georgia today, what will the Democratic base -- how will the state energize to get one more victory when they`ve already secured the majority?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: Well, I think it will stay energized, and I think Senator Warnock has already won one race. When I called him the other day and said, congratulations on the first round, and he is determined to win the second round in the runoff. But I think a lot of people want him to win because of what he has stood for and who he is, his character, his commitment to public service, his commitment to the people of Georgia, it`s also helped us that we have one more vote.\par \par \uc2 But I want him to win mostly because of who he is and what he stands for, but it always helps to have an extra Democratic vote, and we are grateful that he has run such a strong campaign in a tough state like Georgia.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, it does help to have one more vote, when some you have one or two that you can`t always depend on, like on the voting rights bill, \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [17:10:08]\par \par \uc2 I said that. You didn`t, Senator, Thank you, Senator Casey.\par \par \uc2 CASEY: We need more votes, you are right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you, Senator Casey of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Let`s continue our discussion of the midterms. Joining me now is Representative-Elect Becca Balint of Vermont, who just became the first woman and openly gay person to be elected to Congress from Vermont. Thank you for joining me today. And, first of all, congratulations on your historic win, Congresswoman-elect.\par \par \uc2 Although the control of the House currently remains unresolved, it is likely the result will be a slim Republican majority. As an incoming Democratic freshman, what would be your approach? If your party is in the minority, how do you plan on working with Republicans across the aisle, if at all?\par \par \uc2 REP.-ELECT BECCA BALINT (D-VT): I really appreciate the question, Reverend. Thanks for having me on. I think the question is one that has to be put to Republicans, as well, because even if they eke out a slim majority, they are going to have to work with their counterparts in the Democratic caucus to get anything passed.\par \par \uc2 So, I am someone who has had a reputation in Vermont, working with people of all political stripes. I am the current president of the Senate in Vermont, and you have to find your allies where you can on issues that are going to impact working people. That`s what I ran on, is really running to support working people. And I`m sure there are members, backbenchers in the Republican Party, that also want to help their constituents. So, I will work with anyone who will work with me in good faith, but, of course, it`s going to be challenging.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You represent the first state in the union to recognize same-sex civil unions. Over the summer, the House voted to pass legislation to codify same-sex marriage nationwide and strengthen other marriage equality protections. The bill was a direct reaction to the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe versus Wade, as conservative Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  argued that the courts should reconsider other landmark cases, such as same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 However, the Senate delayed the same-sex marriage vote until after the midterms. And in this election cycle, more LGBTQ candidates such as yourself won more seats in Congress than ever before. Where do you see this fight going, Congresswoman-elect?\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Well, we`ve been saying this is the gayest Congress in American history. I`m very excited to be a part of that. Look, we have got to codify this right. I was somebody who was so honored to be able to marry my spouse in Vermont, and then had to serve with members of my Senate, who actually did not vote in support of my right to marry.\par \par \uc2 And so I know what it is like to feel like a second-class citizen when it comes to marriage equality. And so I think that we have to take this very seriously in this lame duck session and try to pass legislation that will codify that right. It means a lot to me, but it also means a lot to my constituents across Vermont, and not just the LGBTQ community, all Vermonters that I speak to want to make sure that this right is enshrined.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Your state, Vermont, also made a history of becoming the first state to enshrine abortion rights in its Constitution. And during your time as a Vermont State Senate majority leader, you ushered legal protections for abortions, including this newly-passed legislation. It`s been reported that some of your colleagues did not see the urgency for abortion legislation. But you correctly predicted that the end of federal abortion protections was near. How do you plan on fighting for abortion rights in your newly elected position in the U.S. House?\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Well, certainly, two things that are so important to me is making sure that we codify Roe, but also we have to deal with the Hyde Amendment, which has really been hampering our ability to make sure that everybody has access to reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 What is heartening to me is that we were able to pass this measure, this reproductive liberty constitutional amendment in Vermont, and other states are also looking to enshrine these rights. I think that voters spoke across the nation that they do not support the actions of the Supreme Court and they want women to be able to have control over their own bodies and their own medical decisions.\par \par \uc2 And so I feel like this is a moment that we really need to seize on the ways in which our electorate is energized on this issue.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:04]\par \par \uc2 And we have to make sure that regardless of where you live, you have access to abortion and other health care matters concerning reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, also in Vermont, this election cycle, voters overwhelmingly voted yes on a constitutional amendment to close an old loophole in the state constitution regarding slavery and indentured servitude. Some of your measures are passed in Alabama, Oregon and Tennessee. What are your thoughts on this historic yet overdue move?\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Yes, very overdue. And we always say in Vermont how proud we are that even before we were a state, when we were the Vermont republic, we had outlawed slavery. And it has always -- those of us -- I taught history for a long time, I was a public school teacher. I taught history and civics, and knew that actually we still allowed indentured servitude in our Constitution. And it just didn`t sit right with so many of us.\par \par \uc2 And I think that a Constitution, a state Constitution is a statement of your values. It is where you see your state in regards to human rights and civil rights. And we felt so strongly that that document needed to reflect our values. And I am so pleased that it passed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you Congresswoman-elect Becca Balint, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming, up former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a big announcement coming on Tuesday. But I`ve got a little bit of advice for the former guy before he makes any rash decision.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a good day to you. Some of the stories we`re (INAUDIBLE) right now. A deadly explosion went off at a tourist area in Turkey`s largest city of 15 million. The Istanbul street was crowded with tourists, shoppers and families at the time. Turkey`s president says the attack might be terrorist-related. 6 are dead, 81 injured.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  took over the investigation of a deadly mid airplane collision that killed six people. Multiple World War II planes were overhead during a Texas air show this weekend. One plane flying down, hitting another, both crashed to the ground. The CEO of the organization that owns both planes said the planes were well-maintained, also saying the volunteer pilots were well trained too.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden in Indonesia today for the G20 summit. He will meet tomorrow with Indonesia`s president, or rather today, as he prepares for tomorrow`s much anticipated meeting with China`s leader, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 . He also is squeezing in congratulatory phone calls to candidates, as Democrats secure control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It`s been almost two years since my last memo to Trump, and, truthfully, I`d hoped there would never be another. But I`ve heard you have a big announcement coming, Mr. Ex-President, and I`ve been a little worried about your decision-making recently. Your strategy to take over Congress, Senate, and the states with a motley crew of T.V. doctors, retired running backs and civil war re-enactors didn`t quite pan out the way you wanted. The red wave was barely even a ripple.\par \par \uc2 Now, many Republicans are trying to dodge your calls. They don`t want you campaigning for them no matter how many QAnon supporters you can pack into a regional airport hangar. Even your media friends are abandoning you. Rupert Murdoch`s New York Post called Florida`s governor Ron DeFuture and called you Trumpty Dumpty. I`ve even heard you blaming your wife, Melania, for your political woes. I don`t need to be a marriage counselor to tell you that won`t end well.\par \par \uc2 So, when you step to the podium, at you are gilded Mar-a-Lago ballroom on Tuesday night, I am urging you, do not run again. Sit this one out. I know you will never be a regular ex-president. You won`t start building houses, like \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  or take up painting like \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . But in a recent \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  article I`ve read, you`ve been spending your days playing 18 to 36 holes of golf, counting your money, and regaling visitors with tales of your supposedly stolen election, it doesn`t sound that bad.\par \par \uc2 A lot of Democrats might offer you differing advice. Some are even anxious to see you out there cheating headaches for another crop of Republican candidates. But I have seen the damage your career in public service has done to the country. And I can`t imagine how difficult it would be for you to balance a new campaign with your many, many, many, many legal liabilities.\par \par \uc2 So, take this bit of wisdom from a fellow New Yorker who knows a thing or two about perseverance and reinvention. Turn the page, start a new chapter. Because when it comes to politics, Mr. Ex-President, you built no wall and now you`ve taken a great fall.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Let`s now turn to my political panel for their insights on today`s big stories. Joining me now, former Republican Congressman David Jolly of Florida and former Democratic Congresswoman Donna Edwards of Maryland, both are MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with you, David. You`ve made it clear that Democrats need to control the agenda unapologetically for the next two years. Now, right now, it seems there will be a slim Democratic lead in the Senate and a slim GOP- led House. Let`s take a listen to what Leaders \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Kevin McCarthy had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: So, what does it mean for the future? What does it say to us as leaders? It says let`s get something done in the next two years.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The American people are ready for a majority that will offer a new direction that will put America back on track. Republicans are ready to deliver it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I want to get you both to weigh in on this.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:02]\par \par \uc2 What do you think the American people can expect to be accomplished if there`s a divided Congress? David, you first and then Donna?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. DAVID JOLLY, (R) FLORIDA: Yeah, the problem with Kevin McCarthy`s statement, right there is that is absolutely not what the American people said Tuesday night. What the American people said for the third straight election, is we want to keep going in the direction that Democrats are going.\par \par \uc2 Look, it does not feel like an ideological mandate. But it is a mandate for Democratic leadership. Without question, that performance that defied history is American voters saying, we do not trust where Republicans want to go. We want to stick with Democrats. So, what that means is, listen, Biden has successfully balanced and ambitious agenda with modest expectations. And it represents the general direction that main street America wants to go.\par \par \uc2 And so, my advice to Democrats would be, keep with it, stay with it, stay on those main street issues and education and healthcare and the economy and curbing inflation. And if Republicans don`t come along, that`s fine. Look, you always want to accomplish what you set out to accomplish. But in 24, America will once again look at the contrast and say, who was fighting for me? And who were the ones that were ignoring where I really want to go? And I think that contrast was affirmed on Tuesday, and would likely be reaffirmed in two years.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Donna?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. DONNA EDWARDS, (D) MARYLAND: Well, first of all, I think Kevin McCarthy has no more control over the Republican Conference than I do. It`s really clear that there are significant numbers of Republicans, particularly in the House who want to investigate. They have announced plans already. They want to impeach everybody from the Secretary of Homeland Security to the President in the United States. That is not an agenda that the American people are interested in.\par \par \uc2 And I think that David is right on this one that Democrats have gotten where they are right now, because they stayed focused, they stay focused on things that are important to the American people. I don`t think anything is going to be accomplished legislatively in a divided Congress, should that be where we end up. And I`m not really clear that`s going to happen yet. But should the Republicans take the majority, I think that they`re going to be very fractured and frayed. And they`re going to be proposing things that are never going to make their way into a democratically controlled Senate. And will never make their way to the desk of the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think Democrats are in the catbird seat here. Implement a lot of the aggressive and important legislative agenda that they passed over this last two years, stay focused on the things that are important to the American people. And let the Republicans have their sideshow. And it will be a sideshow.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Donna sticking with you, I want to mention your State of Maryland, which some has start first this election cycle, starting with Governor like Wes Moore, who we had on Politics Nation last night, he became the first black governor of the state. The Lieutenant Governor-elect is an Indian American woman, making her first immigrant to hold statewide office in Maryland. And Anthony Brown will become the State`s Attorney General, the first black person elected to that office. What do you make of the progress happening in your state and how well this election was handled under such intense pressure?\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: Well, I think Maryland is a classic example where at the top of the ticket Republicans nominated one of the most outrageous election deniers who couldn`t even get the endorsement of our current Republican governor here in Maryland. And so, as a result, Democrats in an overwhelmingly democratic state united to elect these first starting with the head of our party in the state, Governor-elect Wes Moore. And then on down we forgot to add to that list, Brooke Lierman, who was the first woman to be elected as our comptroller in a statewide office in Maryland, as well.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think Democrats are, you know, sort of very focused in Maryland and across the country, where you had people who were talking about what they want to do for the future, who got elected and the people who were litigating the past did not.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David, in two days, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was expected to announce his plans to run for president for a third time. Now, this comes as many in the GOP are blaming him for a weaker than expected showing in the midterms. What do you expect from Trump on Tuesday? And what`s your assessment as a Floridian of the GOP rush to embrace \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  as the future of the party?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yeah, Rev., I think we can expect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to either announce or to threatened to burn the whole party down if people don`t follow his lead. The truth is, in the modern Republican Party, you have the Reagan crafted party and you have the Trump crafted party. Trump remade the party in his image.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:09]\par \par \uc2 I have a problem with the analysis that blames Trump for this. That`s 2016 analysis. By 2022, the entire Republican Party has evolved to a place. And I don`t even like to call it Trumpism. They have adopted a platform that is anti-migrant, anti-LGBT, anti-science, anti-history, anti-communities of color, and that is their platform. It is a hateful populists platform. And so even if Trump exit stage left on Tuesday, this notion of embracing \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is the very profile that the rest of the nation rejected on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 And so, I actually think Republicans are about to learn all the wrong lessons from this past Tuesday. And the other thing that should anger people, as you will see the white washing of the Republican Party by the likes of Jeb Bush, and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 , and maybe even \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , Republicans who are otherwise respected are going to say, well, if Trump`s gone, I can go back to the full embrace of the party. And we are going to elevate \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has engaged in the same hateful politics that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did, not because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to do that. But because \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  chose to do that on his own volition. He should be held accountable, just as the nation held \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accountable.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You wouldn`t be talking about the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  that banning books and saying we shouldn`t talk about race and sending migrants to democratic states that have come to Florida. You wouldn`t be talking about that, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . But let me go on Donna, Democrats already have control of the Senate. But How crucial is winning Georgia to cutting the gridlock. Does Donald Trump`s announcement help or hurt the Senate prospects?\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: Well, look, I think that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is going to run a really strong and aggressive campaign on the ground in Georgia and just win that outright. He is by far the more competent and the better, the better candidate. And frankly, I think if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really wants to go into Georgia and take credit for Georgia and announced his campaign, you know, I can`t believe that Republicans would want that to happen, because I think that the state has already rejected \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and rejected \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and voting for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in Georgia. So, I think that there`s going to be a lot of work to get Democrats elected. And by doing it, by talking to voters and turning them out.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, former representatives, Donna Edwards and David Jolly, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 After the break, Pulitzer Prize Winning Journalist, Nikole Hannah-Jones, joins me to discuss affirmative action, democracy and the future of journalism. You`re watching Politics Nation.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:41:36]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. With most midterm races called, journalists have had nearly a week to examine the results for what they say about American politics right now. That of course, to say nothing of either races were covered by media, where messaging succeeded or failed. And now what impacts of Democrats success last week will be for the party, the President and the country forward.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to talk about it all, Nikole Hannah-Jones, professor and inaugural night chair in race and journalism, founder of the Center for Journalism and Democracy at \uc2 Howard University\uc2 . Professor, great to have you with us. Before I get to the launch of your new journalism center, what`s your overall impression of how this midterm cycle was covered in media before and in the five days since the election, the red wave didn`t -- that didn`t materialize in the polling data that in many cases suggested it was coming, but ultimately proved wrong. What`s your take on all of that?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE HANNAH-JONES, PULITZER PRIZE WINNING AUTHOR: Well, thank you so much for having me on today. And I think, you know, there was some great reporting on the ground reporting and very critical reporting about the election cycle. But I think too much of it was actually driven, as is often the case by Republican talking points. And that there was this sense, of course, you know, lots of reporting around a crime way, lots of reporting around the idea that voters weren`t that concerned, about democracy, that the most important thing to voters were pocketbook issue that pick a downplaying of the Dobbs decision and how that was going to impact voters.\par \par \uc2 And so, it really led to this Republican narrative that we were going to see this Republican takeover of Congress. We`re going to see this massive wave of Republicans being seated in Congress. And clearly that wasn`t true. And many of us were kind of disappointed at the way that these conservative talking points seem to continuously be mainstreamed by journalists, who should be much more skeptical of all of those narratives, who should be more skeptical of polling, which again and again, is proven to be an ineffective measure of where Americans stand in the political landscape.\par \par \uc2 I think we don`t often enough as journalists think about the way that our reporting is not driven by the facts on the ground, but the way that our reporting actually drives perceptions and drives the way that we think about what`s happening in our country, as opposed to just covering what actually is happening.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I understand that on Tuesday, you will host a democracy summit for journalists, the summit serving as an inaugural event for the Center for Journalism and Democracy, which you founded at \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , and by the way, congratulations on that. Howard is especially to me. I preached there every October on my birthday time, so dear to my heart.\par \par \uc2 HANNAH-JONES: Yes, you do.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So, congratulations on the launch as well. I`d be curious to hear in your words, what defines the Center`s specific mission? Because I`d imagine that this time and you`re concerned with democracy and journalism for reason. Am I right, Professor?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:05]\par \par \uc2 HANNAH-JONES: Yes, absolutely. As you very well know democracy is always been contested in the United States. It`s always been very fragile, especially then, after the 1965 Voting Rights Act meant we -- for the first time, had a semblance of a multiracial democracy. And so, I founded the Center because I was very concerned about the state of American democracy. But even more so the way journalists seem to be covering the fragility and the erosion of democracy under the Trump era, and even preceding the Trump era with what happened under the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 So, the charge of the Center is really to try to push our profession, to take a stance on democracy. We understand that you cannot have a free press without a democracy that these two things are dependent upon each other. And as, you know, journalists don`t like to take sides. But in a free society, we have to take the side when it comes to democracy. So, this center, the objective is to produce pro-democracy historically-informed investigative reporting. And to say that this is not one of those areas where we can be neutral.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to ask you about this new report from NBC News.com this week, as this conservative majority Supreme Court appears poised to rule against affirmative action. The leaders of several historically black colleges are bracing to absorb an influx of students, they feel will not feel safe at predominantly white institutions in this polarized environment, given your academic experience, and it has brought you through both HBCUs and PWIs. What is the impact on American higher education, HBCUs included? Should the court ultimately do away with affirmative action as a whole?\par \par \uc2 HANNAH-JONES: Yeah, so one, I think the problem with how we talk about affirmative action is we have a largely because of the way the courts have ruled on affirmative action, forgotten that the purpose of affirmative action was that we had 350 years of affirmative discrimination against black people. And so, the idea of it was we need to take affirmative action to undo that discrimination and the realities of that discrimination. So, of course, a ruling against discrimination is a ruling against us trying to move towards a more equitable society. So, I think the impact is going to be, yes. I think a lot more black students will choose to come to HBCUs. And as someone who made the choice to come to Howard, I think that that is a great thing. I think it`s important to build up black institutions and for our students of color, and particularly black students to have spaces where they feel respected, and treated with dignity.\par \par \uc2 But I also believe we deserve to be in any of the spaces that exist in this society. And that it is a terrible thing in our society to believe that universities where black students still are underrepresented with so poor affirmative action that we will see those numbers declined even further. So, there will always be a welcoming space for the students at historically black colleges. But it`s a sign of a society that`s regressing that we may lose this.\par \par \uc2 And what I hope PWIs will do is to start to think about affirmative action in different way that we can have affirmative action to me based on lineage that if you have formative action, basically for legacy admits, why can`t you have affirmative action for descendants of slavery for indigenous people? Because what happened with their stolen lands? So, we`re going to have to think more creatively about this. But we will certainly welcome students to Howard and other HBCUs and minority serving institutions across the country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And the premise, I`m glad you put it that way. Because I often say, when people question me at colleges and other places about affirmative action, and why the government should have to do it. The government is the one that put it in place, that we had to affirmatively go against an affirmative discrimination policy.\par \par \uc2 Rosa Parks was arrested. She didn`t break a customs. She broke the law. And people keep acting like the government had no involvement in those 350 years. So, I`m glad you put the context right. It -- and then people say why should it be based on race? Because slavery was based on race. Segregation was based on race. You`re correcting what was done.\par \par \uc2 Professor, one last midterm question while there`s time. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting this weekend that while more than 8 and 10 black voters supported Democrats in this election cycle, that support slipped between four and seven points in this past cycle versus the 2018 midterm according to exit polling and the A.P. What does that tell you about where Democrats need to give priority looking in the next two years?\par \par \uc2 [17:50:09]\par \par \uc2 HANNAH-JONES: Well, look, as you know, the black vote is always fluctuates between five and 10% for Republicans depending on who the candidates are. And you see a greater fluctuation in non-presidential races, so in your state races and your local races. So, I don`t know that we should read too much into that. But what I will say is that we know every national election, Democrats are calling on black people to save the party, right? To be that deciding vote. And then as soon as black people come out and vote, it seems like their needs, the reasons that they are supporting the Democratic Party were immediately dropped from the agenda. And I just think that that cannot continue, that Republicans understand there is a number of black people who can be shaved off from the Democratic electorate for a variety of reasons, and that Democrats need to take the black vote seriously to not make black people feel like they`re just being used every election but actually delivering on the promises and the reasons that black people have supported them in the first place.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Absolutely. Thank you, Nikole Hannah-Jones, as always for being with us. And good luck on your new center. We`ll certainly be checking in with you to see as it moves forward.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:33]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: There has been and will continue to be many analysis about what happened with the midterm elections. But let me invite you to one thing that I hope everyone thinks about and that is do not let the forecasters, the pollsters and the pundits get in the way of you voting your convictions. They told you it would be a red wave. They told you it was going to be the rise of the deniers and it didn`t happen. Why? Because Americans stood up for what they believed, rather than what they were told. And that is the most important thing that we can do in this country. The Senate is now exactly where it was. And the House is only a few votes away from maintaining democratic control.\par \par \uc2 You got to keep standing up. That`s one thing I`ve done all my life, talking about all my life. The documentary that John Legend`s film company`s done Loudmouth on me will be in theaters starting December 9. I`ll talk more about it in the coming weeks. I`m proud to be a loud mouth. Proud to try and make noise about democracy and rights. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez start right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221114eibd00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for November 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Steve Kornacki, Brahm Resnik, Garrett Haake, Jake  Ward \par \uc2 8,513 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the votes are still being counted with control of Congress still on the line. We`re getting numbers at this hour. Steve Kornacki, tracking it all at the big board. And control could come down to the State of California, where Congresswoman Katie Porter`s race still not called. She`ll be here live.\par \par \uc2 Plus, is this the end of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in charge? More signs tonight, the site could be bankrupt and broken. As THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening once again. I`m Stephanie Ruhle, 48 hours after the midterm elections and the vote count is still underway for key races in some battleground states. All eyes on those uncalled Senate races in Arizona and Nevada. And we received new batches of votes from both of those earlier tonight. So, let`s get right to Steve Kornacki. I don`t have to tell you where he is, you know, it`s at the big board. Steve, give us an update?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Stephanie. And in fact, in just the last two minutes, we received another update from Nevada where that close Senate race is playing out. This update was from Washoe County where Reno is, and \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  who has been trying to erase that lead that \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  has statewide netted about 4600 votes, it was a batch there over 10,000 votes. She got more than 60% of it. She next more than 4600 votes. And that brings as you can see, Adam Laxalt`s lead statewide now, sits at just 8054 votes. We are inside of 1% right now. That is the difference between these two candidates, 0.9%. So, we`re from here in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Well, again, there are still some more ballots to come. We believe out of Washoe County, they may be less favorable to Cortez Masto than what we just saw. Maybe based on some of the information about party registration. But there are still some more ballots to come out of Washoe County.\par \par \uc2 And then the big thing, the big deal, what it all may come down to is Clark County where Las Vegas is. We think there were about 56,000 votes here. These are ballots that would have been dropped off in drop boxes on election day that have yet to be counted. There`s also a small number of provisional ballots there as well.\par \par \uc2 So basically, it`s an opportunity for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , with what is still to come in Washoe County, although the possibility exists that the rest of that vote in Washoe County, won`t be as favorable to her. If it is show further erode into this lead. And then it wouldn`t take much for her to catch \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  with what`s in Clark County. If she doesn`t get much more to Washoe and she`s trying to erase that kind of deficit. You know, she just she`ll probably need to be in the high 50% range out of that remaining vote in Clark County. There was an update earlier tonight from Clark County. She got 61% of it to give you an idea of how things have been going, so the opportunity for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  to catch Laxalt certainly exists and certainly in the last few minutes, got a little bit more real. This would be a seat Democrats, if they could get this along with Arizona the Senate, race there.\par \par \uc2 Democrats would win control of the Senate, even without needing that Georgia run off. And in that Senate race in Arizona, you see Mark Kelly, earlier tonight, we got an update from Maricopa County. And again, these were early votes, votes that were received before Election Day. Expected to favor the Democrats. They did favor Kelly. And Kelly now leads statewide by nearly 115,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 The action is going to be shifting here in Maricopa County and in Arizona, but particularly in Maricopa, which is the biggest county in the state. The action is going to be shifting to about 290,000 votes dropped off in-person on Election Day. There`s a possibility here that this is a very Republican friendly group of votes. There`s also a possibility it`s been very Republican-friendly in 2020, wasn`t so much in 2018. We`ll see this time.\par \par \uc2 But what the Democrats are hoping is that Kelly is building such a big lead here that even if this is a Republican-friendly batch of votes, Masters won`t get enough out of it to erase that kind of lead. Masters is going to need to get something like 70% of it if he`s going to erase the lead that Mark Kelly is building statewide. So, Democrats I think feeling good about Mark Kelly`s chances. What they may be feeling not so good about in Arizona, though, is the governor`s race where \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , as you can see has been running consistently about 80,000 votes behind where Mark Kelly is in the Senate race. And that same big batch of votes I was just talking about that remains there. If it is a Republican-friendly batch of votes, that will be more than enough for Kari Lake to \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . So, we have to see what those votes look like but that could potentially be very significant one.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:04]\par \par \uc2 Other development just in the last few minutes as well. NBC News has changed, updated its House projection, remember 218 seats needed to take a majority. We called three more districts tonight for Democrats. And so now the Republicans are projected to land at 220 seats, just two more needed for than the minimum for a majority. And that is keep in mind, plus or minus seven seats. So here we are, 11 o`clock, several days after the election in the possibility that Democrats could still land at 218 or above and maintain control of the House. It still exists.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, I need you to educate us on something because tonight on Fox News, it`s not a big surprise. There are already sowing doubt around this. Why is it taking so long to count these votes? What`s with the delay, is something funny here? There`s nothing funny. This is how it works in those states. Can you explain that?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Well, yeah, I mean, I certainly understand the frustration. And the officials in Maricopa County, they had a press conference this afternoon. And there were some questions from the reporters about, you know, why does it take this long? Why are other states like Florida able to get so many votes counted so quickly, even though they have all these different voting methods to the answer that was given by the officials in Maricopa, County. Was they said the big difference between Florida and Arizona is that in Florida, you can`t take your mail ballot, your mail-in ballot and drive it into the polls in-person on Election Day and drop it off. And they`re saying the reason for the delay here in Maricopa, County. Again, that`s the biggest in Arizona. They`re saying the reason is just that that batch of votes that is describing. 290,000 people bringing their mail-in ballots to the polling place on Election Day. And when you bring the mail-in ballot, they say that requires all sorts of verification, all sorts of processing, all sorts of administrative steps before you can actually count the vote. And so, they say when they get 290,000 of them, it slows things down to a degree you seeing it slowed down.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But that doesn`t mean anything nefarious is happening, correct, Steve?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: No, that`s just -- that`s the, you know, the explanation is that that, you know, that`s why it would take so long there as opposed to a place like Florida where, you know, I think the difference that as they`re saying is in Florida, you know, the voter who has a mail-in ballot and hasn`t mailed it by election day, the procedure would be to drive to the polls and cast a ballot as opposed to drive to the polls and drop off a mail-in ballot that then requires all these administrative steps to delay the process. That seems to be the difference they`re saying between those two states.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s just how it works. All right, Steve Kornacki, stand by.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter and bring up our lead and panel tonight. MSNBC Senior Capitol Hill Correspondent who I have not seen in months, Garrett Haake, \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2 , Editor at Large at the Bulwark and an MSNBC Political Analyst. And Brahm Resnik, Arizona Royalty, the ACE Political Reporter who hosts the state`s leading political show on our NBC Affiliate there. He has covered Arizona for decades. And I will go to the king of Arizona right now. Brahm, what are the campaign saying tonight about this latest vote count?\par \par \uc2 BRAHM RESNIK, KPNX NBC 12 NEWS POLITICAL ANCHOR & REPORTER: They`re really ignoring it on the Republican side, at least. They`re looking at the big prize 292,000 ballots, early ballots dropped off on Election Day. They believe since most of the voters on election day appear to have been Republican, they believe most of the voters in that batch, that huge batch, were Republican and that will deliver victory.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you`re \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , you might be concerned about that since you`re so close to Kari Lake. Mark Kelly, who has a bit of a cushion might be able to withstand whatever happened in that batch of ballots. Democrats are remaining largely silent. That`s been a guessing game for a while here of again, exactly who those 292,000 voters are. I`ll add one data point is that there is some information that suggests they are more diverse than Republicans would have us believe. By one measure about more than half of them are 38%, Republican 31%, Democrat 30 or so percent independent. That`s not necessarily a mix that should scare Democrats right now, given the number of split tickets we`ve seen, you know, during this election.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That was my next question. Do you think we could see a lot of people voting Kelly-Hobbs? Excuse me, we could see a split?\par \par \uc2 RESNIK: Absolutely. Absolutely. We see the split tickets up and down the ticket. Look at the Secretary of State`s race between \uc2 Adrian Fontes\uc2 , the Democrat and Mark Finchem, the Republican the farthest right election denier on the ballot, on the Republican side by far. Finchem is way underperforming and what`s so interesting are the under votes in that case.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:08]\par \par \uc2 A lot of voters more so than in any other race aren`t casting a ballot in that race. They don`t want to touch it. You also see how Democrat like \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is falling well short. Democrat Mark Kelly`s total ballot count telling you some votes are going to Kari Lake, Arizonans are notorious ticket splitters, and you`re really seeing it in this race.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Notorious ticket splitters. All right, Garrett, take us to Capitol Hill, we still do not know which party controls Congress. So how are leaders they`re reacting to the fact that we might not know for days?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, on the Senate side, everybody`s keeping things relatively cool at this point. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  went back to New York today, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said yesterday to reporters, he doesn`t have feelings. He`s just counting votes there, waiting to see how this goes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We knew that already.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Not a surprise to people who`ve covered it. And I mean, this is a guy who looks at this in a very straightforward way. He`s bemoaned candidate quality from the start in this Republican class, and it may be candidate quality that keeps them from getting back the majority, when all is said and done in some of these battleground races.\par \par \uc2 You know, like the House side is frankly, much more interesting. At this point, something like 30 races still to be called. If our projections hold up, and Republicans do end up with a narrow majority, that means it Kevin McCarthy, as a leader as potentially Speaker has only two votes to play with on anything, whether it be on naming himself speaker on passing a simple rules package on any bill, that is almost non-functional majority in terms of actually governing the House, especially with the way the Republican Conference works. He has got his hands full, not just in making sure they get across the finish line and actually hit those projections and take control the majority. But making sure it`s a majority that can govern which is no simple task.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Non-functioning majority which, sadly, is exactly what Wall Street likes. One of the reasons besides inflation cooling, the markets did so well today. It`s actually sad.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, I want to share a bit of what we heard for some -- from some pretty prominent voices on the right about these races.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have a tremendous opportunity going forward. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should not be a part of that. I just wish he would ride off into the sunset.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I wrote an open letter to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this morning on my sub-stack that, sorry, Kayleigh (ph), it says please, Mr. President, don`t run again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think he needs to put on pause. We`ve got to make strategic calculations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know, what I call the my pillarization of the Republican Party is we`ve attracted these more cartoon characters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The voters have spoken. And they have said that they want a different leader. We have a clear mission, and it is time to move on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, going into 2024, the Republicans are going to be looking for candidates who are focused on winning, not just making a point, or settling a score. That`s got to be the goal for the next presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Trumpty Dumpty had a great fall, Charlie, are you buying this? Are Republicans finally leaving him?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES\uc2 , THE BULWARK FOUNDER AND EDITOR-AT-LARGE: Well, look, the Republicans bought the ticket, and they`re going to take this ride again. And what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, is signaling by his meltdown down in Mar-a-Lago over the last 12 hours is that he`s prepared to burn it all down, that they may be done with him, but he is not done with them. And he is declaring war against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2 : It basically means I`m not going quietly. I`m going to announce next week, I don`t care. I don`t care who I heard. If you do not nominate me, I will burn the House down, I will destroy and attack any other Republican that comes against me. And you know, think about how many decisions Republicans have made over the last six years that you and I have talked about, Stephanie, that brought us to this moment that they can`t -- they know that they need to move on against him, they know that he is a loser, they know that he is boat anchor. And yet how do you get rid of somebody that will not go, that will never acknowledge defeat? And so, you watch what he`s doing right now? How he is splitting even the Maga base.\par \par \uc2 Now, the one thing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is convinced of and he may not be wrong, is that he thinks this is going to be a replay of 2015 and 2016 where people say, well, this is outrageous. This is too far. How dare you say that about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And you have the donor class and the conservative media turned against him. And he will stare to them down. And he is confident that they will cave in to him again.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Charlie, they are turning on him. conservative media is turning on him. The donor class is welcoming \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in. It`s happening. So, where exactly does the big fear of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lie? He`s a senior citizen living in his private home in Florida and New Jersey. He`s unemployed, who isn`t going to heard? He can`t even run a business anymore. Tish James has a stiletto on his throat.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:01]\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Yes, well I have the scars to answer your question here because what he`s got is he`s got 30% to 40% of the Republican base that will go with him. And so, let`s say, you know, in some, you know, fantasy world that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  beats \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that he actually has the guts to go up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and, you know, face down these threats of destruction. And he beats him, let`s say 60, 40 of what`s going to happen to that 40%, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ever go away?\par \par \uc2 What Donald Trump`s thread is, is that I don`t care about the party. I don`t care about any of you. I don`t care about the country. I don`t care about the Constitution, or about the elections. I`m going to take my ball and go home. And so, you`re dealing with a very, very unusual character. Look, this is a party that stuck with him through Charlottesville that stuck with him through that shambolic for years that didn`t walk away when he was defeated, that didn`t walk away after he orchestrated an insurrection at the Capitol. They were all OK with that.\par \par \uc2 Up until 48 hours ago, he was still, yeah, you know, he`s still the leader of our party. And now because he is led them to this underperformance in the midterms, they`re going to do something that they`ve been incapable of doing for the last six years. He thinks -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looks at the Republican establishment and he says, these are weak people. I have crushed them in the past. They will cave into me. They have done it in the past, and they will do it again. That is what he`s calculating. And he doesn`t care whether or not he jeopardizes their Senate hopes in Georgia. And because he`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, I agree with everything you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: News flash.\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Except that, it has a lot of --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He never cared. There`s no surprise here, Garrett?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Steph, I just want to give some backup to Charlie here a little bit. I mean, I remember covering Trump in the conservative ecosystem back in 2012, when \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  felt like he had to hug Trump just to keep him from being this kind of free radical bouncing around and doing damage to the party. Trump was never particularly about the Republican Party. It was always about the Trump brand. And as we talk about the, you know, brewing feud between \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , make no mistake DeSantis had a huge night this week, Trump had objectively a terrible one. But I`m old enough to remember, you know, Scott Walker, and some of these other, you know, Republican candidates who were going to be the next big thing who stumbled out of the gate. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is not a declared candidate. He is not somebody who`s won outside the State of Florida, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you still have to treat like the 800-pound gorilla until proven otherwise. Maybe somebody can come out and punch him and take those 40% away, metaphorically speaking. But until that happens, he`s still the guy until he`s not.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I don`t know that he`s quite 800 pounds. That`s a little much.\par \par \uc2 SYKES: 700 pounds.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Charlie, let`s talk about Democrats for a second and who their stars are. When we look \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , huge win at a Michigan months ago, she was in terrible shape, less more replacing a Republican in Maryland as governor and what`s Wes` message? Unity, saying they need independence. They need Republicans. What does that tell you about the Democrats coming out of these midterms? Where are they moving?\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2 : This is an extremely interesting question because one of the raps on the Democrats was that after Obama, they didn`t have much of a bench. And look what we have right now. What Democrats have right now, they have Whitmer, out of Michigan. You have Shapiro in Pennsylvania. You have Wes Moore in Maryland. These are all rising stars.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, what is the Republican bench look like? Because, you know, going back to our previous conversation about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in the process of either destroying that bench or demanding that that bench, humiliate itself by bowing the knee to him. So, it is an interesting contrast. Democrats have to feel that they have a new generation waiting in the wings. Now, how long we`ll have to wait? We don`t know. But it is extraordinary. And I do hear the complaints. People say, you know, Whitmore`s accomplishment in Michigan is certainly as impressive as what DeSantis did in Florida. Why is she not getting as much love and as much buzz and I think that`s a legitimate question because she not only won by double digits, she also flipped the legislature there. In a very, very impressive way, she`s got a hell of a story.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Big blue. All right, Garrett Haake, Brahm Resnik, \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2 , great to see you all.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  is here on her race that has not yet been called. What it`s like running against big dark GOP money? The red wave that wasn`t. And it it`s time for her party to rebrand on the economy.\par \par \uc2 And later, the kids they`re all right. What we know about the all-important youth vote and why it doesn`t look good for Republicans. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on an important Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:24:03]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: You know, Tuesday was a good day for America. A good day for democracy. And it was a strong night for Democrats. First, national elections is January 6, and there were a lot of concerns about whether democracy would meet the test. It did, it did, it did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Some of what we heard from President Biden today after Democrats avoided that red wave key races are still undecided in several states, including California where our next guest is fighting to keep her seat in the House. Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, earlier tonight Kevin McCarthy was on Fox News. He says when all the votes are counted, he thinks you`ll be defeated. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: Well, all the votes are not counted respectfully, I would say, and I think that we`re seeing across the country in a lot of these states. My colleagues that are winning. I just had another colleague \uc2 Kim Schrier\uc2  win in Washington tonight. I think we`re seeing people win in Nevada. I think we`re going to see more victories come out of Arizona and California in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:09]\par \par \uc2 So, look, this is the same man who predicted a 60 vote Republican red wave. And this instead, you know, it`s really up to up to the voters here in the next few days, as we count all the ballots to see whether the Democrats or the Republicans have the majority.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, the big red wave certainly didn`t come. What is your biggest takeaway? I know, you don`t know your results yet. But as you look at the results across the country, what does that telling you?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I think it`s telling us that people can see that Democrats are standing up for their values, their ability to make their own decisions about their health care, including abortion. They`re standing up for our democracy for our Constitution, and protecting it against people who want to trample on it. And I think we`re standing up for consumers and workers in this economy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You didn`t just run against the Republican opponent, you really ran against big dark money, GOP Super PAC money at a time when many have said, well, maybe if Democrats put more money in Katie Porter`s race, but there was $17 billion total put into these midterms. What is that like?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I think that, you know, what we see with the dark money in the Super PACs is almost all negative messaging. It`s personal attacks, it`s potshots. And I think what we can see in some of these races is actually voters don`t want that. They actually want to know, what are you going to do. And in the case of Democrats and Republicans here in my race, I think I was able to show Orange County families, that what I do, when I go to Washington is fight for them.\par \par \uc2 I`m willing to stand up to corporations to big oil, when they pollute, to Big Pharma when they rip off patients. I`m willing to do that. And I`m willing to fight to protect our tax dollars. And I think when you put that kind of really, here`s where I am, I am side by side with you fighting for a better economy up against personal attacks. I think voters see through that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is this an opportunity for Democrats to look back on this and say, let`s talk more about the economy. Because every time you and I talk, you talk about what you`re fighting for in Washington, and Republicans have kind of been exposed here. They`re the ones that kind of get this credit for being good on the economy, while offering no policies. And many, many Democrats only talk about social issues. And, you know, social issues and economic issues are completely intertwined?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: No, absolutely. Look, a lot of the social problems and concerns come from not having a stable job, from not having housing that`s affordable, from not having opportunity for social mobility. So, I think that Democrats absolutely should talk about the economy all of the time, because we are the party that does a good job with the economy. We`re the party that brought down unemployment, we`re the party who`s paid down the deficit, just to mention two things we`ve done in the last few years. Not only that, but we`ve stood up to monopoly power, we`ve tried to create more competition in the economy, we`ve had to bring manufacturing back here, including in semiconductors. We have a great message on the economy, and we don`t have to choose between these issues. Americans want both. They want the ability to earn a living, raise their kids, take care of their family save, and they also want to have the rights and freedoms as Americans at the same time.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then what`s your message to leadership within your party? What`s next? Where do you all go from here? What did you learn from these midterms?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I think one of the things we saw is that candidate Quality Matters. My colleagues who ran in these districts are tough, they`re smart, they flipped seats in 2018. Many of them, they hung on in 2020. And so, I think we`ve got a great group of people who have learned how to win in tough races and in parts and pockets of this country, including Orange County that people sometimes haven`t thought we could do. That`s a real positive. And I think the next question facing Democrats are, how are we going to create that kind of multi-generational leadership? How are we going to lift up a mix of voices in our leadership that`s going to let us connect with these voters all across the country, and that includes connecting with them on economic issues?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And how are you feeling? This time tomorrow night? You`ll know where you stand?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: I probably will know tomorrow night, I don`t know. But listen, this is the best part of a campaign in some ways. Because this is the part where, you know, you left it all on the field. You worked hard. You`ve listened. You`ve gotten to every aspect of a community get to know voters, knock doors, had a great team of volunteers, spent the money that needed to be spent to win. So, I am very -- people say, aren`t you stressed out? I said, no. We ran a tough race. We did everything we could do and we feel confident it`s going to pay off. So, for me this is just about catching up on laundry and letting democracy do the rest.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then how do your kids feel tonight? You have three young kids that were knocking on doors with you, how are they feeling tonight?\par \par \uc2 [23:30:05]\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I think for kids it`s a mixed bag, having a mother who`s an elected official, they are definitely glad that the commercials are down. They`re like I hope they took those billboards down yesterday, mom. So, they`re definitely some notoriety and some attention with it that isn`t always welcomed for them. But I think they`re -- they also really care about what happens in this country. My children care a lot about things like climate change. They care a lot about things like abortion rights and LGBTQ equality. They care a lot about an economy that`s going to make sure they have a high paying job when they graduate. So, I think they`re glad to see Democrats winning and continuing to fight for a better future for them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , catching up on laundry, and maybe wiping off that whiteboard getting ready for work next week. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, every day more people are turning 18. And they want a country that is very different than Fox News male viewers. And it looks like it is already setting off the alarm bells for the GOP. We`ll dig into that when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:45]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When you think about all these new people, even in the last six months that registered to vote, it`s all young people. And last I checked young people they don`t even take calls from their mothers. I can`t imagine they take calls from pollsters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That is so damn true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So damn true. We have been talking about it for weeks and Tuesday, the results proved us right. Polls failed to factor in young people. And they turned out in record numbers, voting nearly two to one in favor of Democrats. These voters are among the most diverse ever, and they`re not buying the culture war controversies the Republicans are trying to sell. With every passing day, an old white man longing for an America of yesteryear dies, and a bunch of young, diverse people are turning 18. And they are sick of school shootings. They want to have sex and not get pregnant. And they damn well know there`s not a transgender basketball team plotting to win the big game.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s find out more and bring in three people that know all about this. John Della Volpe, Director of Polling at \uc2 Harvard Kennedy School\uc2 , Victor Shi, Co-host of the iGen Politics Podcast. He was also the youngest elected delegate for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020. And my friend Juanita Tolliver, a veteran Political Strategists to Progressive Candidates and Causes.\par \par \uc2 John, you know, I`m going to you first, you are the man who studies these voters most. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DELLA VOLPE, \uc2 HARVARD KENNEDY SCHOOL\uc2  DIRECTOR OF POLLING: Well, I think what happened is exactly what we said was going to happen on your show a week or so ago, Stephanie. If not for young people, we have a starkly different result. We saw not a red wave. We didn`t see a blue wave. But we certainly saw a young voter Gen Z wave.\par \par \uc2 Let me break it down are really, really simple, OK.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do it.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: If you look at voters over the age of 45, they supported Republicans by 10 percentage points. Well, more than half of the electorate is over the age of 45 closer to 60%. It is the 13-point margin among voters under 35. It is the 28-point margin among Gen Z and young millennials that turn red states into blue. And we have a different country because of Victor and 1000s of 1000s of young organizers around the country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Juanita, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I think they showed up and showed out at historic rates. And I also appreciate the demographic breakdown that you put on earlier with 89% of black youth voters supporting Democrats, 68% of Latino voters supporting Democrats, if nothing screams court Gen Z, court youth vote, loud as this, I don`t know what the other bat signal we could send Democrats because in addition to the praise that we`re hearing from the President and from Democratic leaders, they also needed to -- need to be courted with substantive investments and legislation, whether that`s related to climate change, abortion rights, LGBTQ rights, more student loan debt, really all of that needs to continue happening, because if nothing else, Democrats have to sustain this attraction with Gen Z voters and youth voters in order to have continued impact in politics going forward.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, none of that is on the Republican platform. So, Victor, Republicans are waking up and saying, gee, we got to reach out to those young people, but reach out with what offering?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR SHI, IGEN POLITICS PODCAST, CO-HOST: You`re so right, Stephanie. I mean, one of the things you`re seeing from Republicans right now is they`re basically freaking out over the fact that young people overwhelmingly voted for Democrats. And so right now you`re seeing a bunch of Republicans on the air basically trying to win over young people. But here`s the thing. I think that Republicans just don`t have anything to offer. And young people see this specifically, you know, every single time that a Republican cast their votes, it seems to be against the interests of young people. We saw this with Inflation Reduction Act, one of the biggest things that young people care about was climate change. The Inflation Reduction Act was something that had a historic investment, climate change. Every single Republican voted against that bill.\par \par \uc2 You also have gun reforms legislation. Every single Republican voted against that bill. And you also have Republicans doing everything that they are doing to really denigrate the lives of young people with overturning abortion, enacting more strict voter suppression laws. And so, all of these things, really just like you said, it makes it clear to young people that Republicans have nothing to offer and so all we can do right now is turn to Democrats as our next solution I think that`s what you`re seeing a lot of the data right now coming out of the states and early ballots and mail-in ballots and some of the exit polling showing.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:12]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: John, they`re not just offering young people, anything, they`re turning them off. Every night, they`re appealing to older white males in this country and preying on their fear. Given that where thought Republicans go, you could get your base totally fired up. But if your base is small and only shrinking, it doesn`t get you anything.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Stephanie, their base is a small melting iceberg, melting every single day, as you said at the open --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Allowed one.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Every single day an older conservative is passing away replaced by several well-educated, thoughtful, diverse young Americans. And worse, not only they`re turning them off, but they`re mocking them. They`re punching down. They are using the most vulnerable citizens among us to try for cheap writing points. They`re not doing damage, I think to their party, but they`re trying to do damage to this generation. And thank goodness, this generation is fighting. And we`re seeing the results of this. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  won 70% of the under 30 vote. He`s not Senator without young people. Same thing in New Hampshire, same thing in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Juanita, Republicans had another idea for how they can expand their base. I want to share what one Fox News host is suggesting. Let`s watch this.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Oh, gosh.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Single women are breaking for Democrats by 30 points. And this makes sense when you think about how Democrat policies are designed to keep women single. But once women get married, they vote Republican. Married women, married men, go for Republicans by double digits. But single women and voters under 40 had been captured by Democrats. So, we need these ladies to get married. And it`s time to fall in love and just settle down guys, go put a ring on it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Just put a ring on it. I`m pretty sure, Beyonce isn`t --\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: You cannot make up the stupidity. You cannot make this up, Steph. And it`s not -- it`s not just chauvinistic. It`s just plain ignorant. Like you think that`s the pitch that`s going to get young, independent women on your side, like and what kills me is that Republicans are so anti-woman. His response is get a man in your life to control them. That`s the rebuttal here. That`s the response here. It`s just disgusting. I expect nothing better from those folks over at that other network. And honestly, it just reaffirms how much they are anti-women, how much they hate women. And that fear of women who are independent, that fear of young people that`s only going to continue to grow, because you`re not getting anybody on your side with this argument. So, shout out to the independent women, shout out to the young people who are rejecting this disgusting argument from the right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You know, who doesn`t fear women? The state of Michigan, Victor, tell us about that state who won and who turned out for them?\par \par \uc2 SHI: So, Michigan is one of those states that I paid a lot of attention to. Because just like the other states that you`re seeing, like Pennsylvania, and other states around the country, your -- Michigan is a state that has a trifecta, basically. So, the governorship of the state legislature, all branches of government are controlled by Democrats. And that`s what you saw on Tuesday night, young people voted overwhelmingly for Democrats. And there really is no path, there was no path to victory without young people turning out in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that I was doing throughout the day on Tuesday was texting some of my friends in Michigan, and some of the videos that I was able to see from my friends was just stunning to me. I mean, it`s sad that young people have to vote in such long lines. But some of the lines that young people had to vote in, were up to five hours long and they stayed in line, they made sure that their voice was heard. And that to me was so inspiring. I was really a little bit overcome with emotion because all of these young people know the stakes of this election. And I think part of the reason is because you saw proposition three on the ballot, that was an amendment to make sure that abortion is enshrined in the Constitution, which means that young people turned out to vote young women, especially women, as well, they turned out to vote in historic numbers. And I think that`s what really allowed someone like \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  or something like proposition three to pass.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It was those videos that you posted, Victor, why I wanted you to join us tonight when I saw you post them on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , I was amazed. And I was guilty when I thought back to what I was doing when I was 18, 19, 20 and 21. And I wasn`t waiting on lines like that. That is inspirational. Those right there are patriots.\par \par \uc2 John Della Volpe, Victor Shi, Juanita Tolliver, great, great, great to see you all.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, he said it wasn`t going to be a free for all hell escape but Elon Musk`s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is looking more and more like one by the minute the new trouble tonight, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 [23:45:08]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Big news but no surprise, the chaos continues over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Two top executives resigned just hours before the new owner and CEO \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  sent his first email to the company titled, "Difficult Times Ahead."\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 Well, the top government regulator issues a warning of deep concern to the company. So, let`s break it down with NBC News Tech Correspondent, my dear friend, Jake Ward.\par \par \uc2 All right, Jake, let`s just go through this. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has lost their head of trust, their head of compliance, their head of H.R., their head of sales, they don`t seem to have any communications department at all anymore. What is left over there, besides \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and his avid tweeting?\par \par \uc2 JAKE WARD, NBC NEWS TECH CORRESPONDENT: I think there are a lot of very, very unhappy people working inside a company that at this point, as you pointed out, Steph, is suddenly without the executives in charge of things like trust and safety without a head of internal -- sorry, information security, without a head of compliance, the person that the FTC would come to and say, show us the documents to show that we are complying with this consent decree or we hit you with billions of dollars in fines, right?\par \par \uc2 You`ve got people literally without anybody at the top of the organization that has the long-term experience you need at a place like this, to keep the trains not just running on time but on the track. I mean, I`ve been trying to think these days about like what it is like to be in -- you know, to be \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  in a \uc2 Slack\uc2  channel or to be \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  looking at an inbox? You`ve got, you know, not to -- you know, putting aside all the other companies he runs, right? You`ve got now parody accounts popping up as of today. Now, that it is possible to pay $8 to be verified. We`ve been talking about this forever. And now it`s finally here. And you`ve got parody accounts like Chiquita: Banana, somebody claiming to be that company saying we have overthrown the government of Brazil.\par \par \uc2 You had someone pretending to be \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2 , saying insulin is now free, right? You have all of this stuff going on. And all of us at NBC were just thinking, what if this had happened two days ago? What if this had happened before the midterms? That`s how close we came to disaster in that case, but still the gist there, there is a tremendous amount of chaos going on inside that company right now. It`s happening so fast and furious. I find it totally overwhelming, Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Given what you`re seeing happen, right, to companies like \uc2 Eli Lilly\uc2  having to put out a statement, no insulin isn`t free. It`s no wonder advertisers are running for the hills. At this point, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and no one can possibly make the argument. If advertisers are leaving, it`s because they`re woke. It`s because it`s a disaster. Have we ever seen anything like this with any public company, any tech company? I mean, just basically the bottom fallout completely?\par \par \uc2 WARD: It is absolutely unbelievable. We are literally -- I keep checking out minute by minute just to make sure I`m not missing some new disaster. I mean, you know, the thing that you mentioned here with, you know, advertising advertisers backing out helps to explain in part I`m sure why \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  sent out his first communication to the employees directly on Wednesday. Is the first time that people who survived around a firings have heard from him. Those that -- those 1000s of people who got fired, they had never heard from him directly. And in that email, he said, as you said, dire road ahead. And he said I quote here, "without significant subscription revenue, there was a good chance \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will not survive the upcoming economic downturn."\par \par \uc2 He then reportedly went on to say in an all-hands meeting, that bankruptcy is not out of the question. We`re talking here about not just, you know, things getting a little out of control, right, or some difficulties around verifying who is who, we are talking about an existential threat to a company that has been the center of political and media life for the last decade. This thing is actually imploding in front of us unless this guy can turn it around.\par \par \uc2 And with the departure of these executives, that is such a big deal. I can`t stress it enough because at the very moment that he`s trying to get his hands around advertising. He`s trying to get his arm around the product. The FTC could come knocking at any time not to mention his creditors. We haven`t even gotten to that. So, there`s just a lot going on for this guy. It makes me sweat to think what it`s like to be him.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: $44 billion to potential bankruptcy. Redefining you break it, you bought it. He bought it. Then he broke it. Jake Ward, thank you so much. Jake Ward is sweating just thinking of that.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, before the cat is in the big board our one and only Steve Kornacki was predicting politics on public access TV when he was in high school. We`re going to take you back to where it all started when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:37]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go, childhood dreams unlocked. Steve Kornacki is beloved in this building and on your TV screens. And lately all of his TikTok groupies but before he had the big board 30 Rock, he had a very different studio back in the 90s. He shared it all with Seth Meyers last night. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS: You mentioned you were interested in this at a young age. And by the way, everybody laughed. I think they believe you. But I also want to point out you have brought video evidence. This is local access television?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Somebody dug this up. But, yeah, my high school in Massachusetts, they were two kids. They hosted a comedy show --\par \par \uc2 MEYERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: -- on the public access channel, which is in the basement of our high school. And they asked me to sit down and just do like a commentary on this. I think it was the `94 election. And I realized when they thought I just played a straight.\par \par \uc2 MEYERS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: They figured it was so boring. It`d be funny. That`s the --\par \par \uc2 MEYERS: I went like, when you go back and you watch people from high school, it`s always fascinating to see how they turned out.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MEYERS: You have been the straightest line of everybody I`ve ever seen from who you were last night to who you are here. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: As mandated by the Constitution, 33 seats or 1/3 of the Senate will be up for reelection in 1994 since it would be impractical for me to go over each race, I`ll only go over a few of the highlights this year. The Senate balance is 56/44 for the Democrats as of this moment it is highly plausible that the Republicans can take over the Senate for the first time since 1986. That`s all for now. There`s other races, perhaps another time, though, I`ll talk about more.\par \par \uc2 MEYERS: I just wish -- I wish it anyway, like one day I`m going to have a giant map.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is actually impossible to not love Steve Kornacki. A younger version of our dear friend Steve takes us off here tonight. And on that -- on that epic throwback note, I wish you all a very good and safe and healthy night. From all of our colleagues across the network. So, if NBC News thanks for staying up late. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221111eiba00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for November 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Steve Kornacki, Michelle Goldberg, Ben Collins \par \uc2 8,527 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes back at the election desk. Two days on, 48 hours since we were sitting here on election night, a few things are clear. One, a historic election, I mean, bucking historic trends going all the way back to 1934. And two, there was no red wave. In fact, Republicans severely underperformed for Tuesday`s midterms, Democrats severely overperformed.\par \par \uc2 And all kinds of people from all kinds of segments of the commentariat have their idea why. Here`s mine, all right. Think of it as a three-legged stool, three main components to Democrats improbable success on Tuesday night and Republicans` failure. The first and most obvious leg of the stool, the decision from the Supreme Court in Dobbs overturning Roe vs. Wade effectively turning abortion over to the states, many of which immediately made it illegal.\par \par \uc2 And immediately after that decision, we saw outrageous, heartbreaking stories. For instance, a 10-year-old survivor of child sexual abuse in the state of Ohio forced to flee her state in order to obtain an abortion. Real people affected immediately. And so, abortion consistently showed up on exit polls after that decision through the summer, into the fall, and then in the exit polls on Election Day as a top motivating factor for voters.\par \par \uc2 And get this. In all five states where the issue was put directly to voters on the ballot, California, Michigan, Vermont, Kentucky, and Montana, voters came out in favor of reproductive rights. That`s even in Kentucky, conservative state, Montana as well. The overturning of Roe, which was the results of course of a half-century-long political project by Republicans and conservatives, it was brought to fruition by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  essentially taking Scalia seat away from Democrats by refusing to confirm \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , installing three justices under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . All of that was a reminder as well of the extremism of the Republican Party and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A big part of elections is making a compelling case to voters about what you will do differently. Now, Republicans struggled to do that. They had a lot of critiques of the status quo, a lot of things they hated about what was going on. There`s crime, and there`s inflation, and the border. But what were they offering?\par \par \uc2 That brings us to the second leg of the stool, that the Biden the agenda, the domestic agenda of the Democratic Party, was pretty popular. Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , unified party control, the Democrats passed the Inflation Reduction Act, which among many other provisions, lowers prescription drug costs significantly and invest in clean energy. They passed the American Rescue Plan, right, which gave people checks and extended all kinds of relief for cities to hire more teachers and things like that. They passed the Chips Act which invests in domestic manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 The President, through executive action, relieved people have their student loan debt, $20,000 for some, as much as 40,000 for others. There was a historic bipartisan Infrastructure Bill. The President also put the first Black woman on the Supreme Court, which was confirmed by a Democratic Senate.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of those things that I listed pulled above water, majority approval for all of them, even some of the controversial ones like the student loan forgiveness. And then I think is the biggest difference between today and the last actual giant red wave in 2010. Back then, Democrats were defending what had become an unpopular agenda in Republicans run office.\par \par \uc2 Now, I covered that race very closely. And in key races all across the country, Democrats were running headlong from their vote for the Affordable Care Act or Obamacare. Those who voted against it were running headlong from their association with it. Now, Democrats took two years to pass this big, ambitious health care bill, the largest health care reform in the country since the introduction of Medicare, arguably longer. And by election day, that signature piece of extremely important, worthy legislation was polling anywhere from five to 12 points underwater.\par \par \uc2 So, you got Democrats pushing this legislation, passing it. that legislation being unpopular and polling and Republicans ran against it. Now, to be clear, they`re running against it included ludicrous fear- mongering about socialism. They went around saying that a payment reform system to make Medicare pay less to providers was cuts to Medicare. That was a big one. Billions of dollars in cuts to Medicare. There were lies about the so-called death panels that were coming to euthanize grandma. And all of that lying worked.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats passed this legislation, they lied about a lot of its details, and then they ran against it. Democrats lost 63 seats in the House, six in the Senate. And even then, President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  had to concede his party got walloped.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:32]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Sometimes we lose track of, you know, the ways that we connected with folks that got us here in the first place. And that`s something that -- now, I`m not recommending for every future president that they take a shellacking like the -- like I did last night. You know, I`m sure there are easier ways to learn these lessons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: President Biden did not take a shellacking like that. In fact, in some ways, maybe he learned the lesson. Because here`s the thing, fairly or unfairly, and I think in some ways unfairly, because the Affordable Care Act now pulls like 20 points above water, help the Democrats win in 2018. So, I think unfairly. But the truth is the Joe Biden Democratic Party domestic policy agenda of unified governance for 2021 to 2022 on the whole polled much higher. And it showed up in those contested races.\par \par \uc2 It showed up in incumbents like \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , and \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  who won their races in tight quarters, who didn`t have to run away from the vote, who could run on voting for the bipartisan partisan infrastructure bill, who could run on the American Rescue Plan or the Chips Act. It gave Democratic incumbents particularly defending against challengers a strong message to run off. That includes Mark Kelly, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . It gave Democrats a strong message.\par \par \uc2 Now, that brings us to the third leg of the stool which was a strong- spirited and consistent defense of American democracy, a rejection of what happened on January 6 and the big lie of a stolen election. That was, as you will recall, as I told you from those desks in the weeks leading up to the election, the closing argument among Democrats, and especially from the president himself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They tried everything last time to nullify the votes of 81 million people. This time, they`re determined to succeed in thwarting the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 It`s within our power, each and every one of us, to preserve our democracy. And I believe we will. I think I know this country. I know we will. You have the power. It`s your choice. It`s your decision. The fate of the nation, the fate of the soul of America lies where it always does, with the people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, after Biden gave that speech, there was all this sniping from reporters and pundits who said that optics were bad and that the -- it was weird, that there were red lights, Marines were there. It was a terrible closing argument. People care about the cost of milk, not this stuff. They were all wrong. They were just wrong. They got it wrong.\par \par \uc2 According to NBC News, exit poll data, nearly 70 percent of voters said democracy was under threat. And exit poll data before we verified voter files can be a little wobbly, but we saw consistently in the lead-up to the election, people telling pollsters how much they were thinking about democracy and its security. And we see in the places that Democrats were able to win, they won against anti-democratic candidates in places where they got Republican votes.\par \par \uc2 The democracy argument worked plainly. We saw punishment for extremist candidates who ran on the big lie of a stolen election. Here`s an example. In New Hampshire, OK. Nominee for the Senate there, Trump endorsed guy named Don Bolduc who said this back in August, tried to walk it back when he won the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R-NH), SENATE CANDIDATE: So, I signed a letter with 120 other generals and admirals saying that Trump won the election, and dammit, I stand by my word. I`m not switching horses, baby. This is it.\par \par \uc2 I`ve come to the conclusion, and I want to be definitive on this, the election was not stolen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think that was the day after he got the nomination. Now, voters did not buy his attempt to switch horses if you will. Bolduc lost his race for Senate by nine points to Democratic Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . In the same election, on the same election night with the same electorate, New Hampshire`s Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , a more traditional Republican who did not say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won, who said that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won, he won reelection by 16 points.\par \par \uc2 So just in the state of New Hampshire, and obviously Sununu is an incumbent, this is not a perfect experiment, we can`t devise it in a lab, but just look at that. That`s a seven-point pen guilty for the extremist anti-democracy candidate, seven-point penalty for MAGA, for Trump for supporting insurrection. We are seeing examples of this penalty, four points, five points, six points, seven points, 10 points all over the country.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:17]\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Republican voters ousted incumbent Republican Congressman Peter Meijer in the primary because \uc2 Meijer\uc2  supported American democracy as well as the second impeachment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after January 6. And \uc2 Meijer\uc2  was replaced in the ticket by John Gibbs, a Trump-backed election denying, anti-democracy candidate. And Gibbs lost to Democrat Hillary Scholten by 13 points, flipping a Republican district.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing something playing out similar in Washington State. Another pro-impeachment Republican member of Congress, \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2 , lost her primary to a Trump-back extremist, a guy named Joe Kent. Now, we don`t know who`s going to win that race. We have not called this race. But as of now, Kent is trailing his Democratic opponent by about four points with 70 percent of the vote in. Even if Kent pulls it out, it is clear he is paying that penalty. And we saw it in race after race after race. In fact, voters, Republican voters, independents, ticket splitting voting for a Republican who is not anti-democracy and for a Democrat against another candidate who was anti-democracy.\par \par \uc2 The central contention of Biden in this messaging was that yes, actually, people do actually care about American democracy, that it`s not weird, out of touch, elitist to talk about democracy because we all share in it together. Based on these numbers, based on what we see, and the ticket- splitting we`re seeing, it`s almost impossible to deny there are voters out there who don`t love \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who don`t like Democrats, who might even hate the libs and hate inflation, and they still identify as American patriots who believe in democracy and they think this crop of Republican candidates are a threat to it.\par \par \uc2 That`s a heartening message. It`s one of the main messages from Tuesday`s election, the united popular front of pro-democracy voters across the political spectrum is a majority coalition. And that majority coalition stood up to Republican extremism. It might have been enough for Democrats to hold the Senate. It might even be enough for them to keep the House. Because right now, control of Congress is really uncertain.\par \par \uc2 There are three outstanding Senate races in Arizona, Nevada, and Georgia. The Georgia race is going to run off, but if Democrats managed to win both Arizona and Nevada, they would not need the Georgia seat to retain control of the Senate. In the House, there are still a ton of outstanding races so the possibility remains Democrats could retain control of the House. We just really don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to go through all this is Steve Kornacki at the big board. Steve, it is great to see you. I have you all to myself tonight. I`m excited about this. Should we start? Can we start with the Senate with -- let`s -- can we do Nevada and Arizona? Maybe Nevada first?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, literally, you know -- well, how about Arizona first?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s do that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: In the last two minutes, we just got a vote update in Arizona is from Pima County where Tucson is second biggest county in the state. They released about 20,000 new votes. They were early votes. These are votes that arrive before election day. These have been Democratic-friendly. And you can see they`ve pushed Mark Kelly over a million votes and they`ve pushed his lead over 100,000.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen that grow now -- grow now over the last two nights as the outstanding early vote from Pima County and from Maricopa County, in particular, has continued to come in. So, Kelly`s lead over Masters now sits over 100,000. And the other major development we`re waiting on Arizona tonight is another release of the early vote from Maricopa County. That`s the biggie in the state.\par \par \uc2 They said there it`s probably going to be the size of last night`s release. Last night, they put out 62,000 new votes. So, expect something in that neighborhood. It`ll be between 10 and 11:00 East Coast time is when we`re expecting to get that. Again, it`s early vote. It`s vote that was received over the weekend or on Monday. That batch last night favored Kelly in Maricopa County by double digits.\par \par \uc2 The name of the game here for Democrats and for Kelly is they expect from that 62,000 to be able to grow that lead further. And they`re hoping to grow it big enough that when the final batch of votes, the final big batch of votes from Maricopa is 290,000 votes that were dropped off on election day by voters -- there`s some reason to think that might -- we don`t know but there is some reason to think that might be a pretty Republican batch of votes. So, the question is, can you build a big enough lead here where even if Masters is doing well, really well with that vote, Kelly wins anyway. And Kelly is building the kind of advantage here where Masters would have to put up a monster number.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: They have said they`re not going to -- we had some indication last night that they might release some of that tonight and give us a sense of it. But they did say at their press conference earlier tonight that none of that will be At least tonight. But that`s going to be important when we get a sense of that vote. And even if Kelly withstands it, one other thing to keep in mind there is it`s a different story in the governor`s race.\par \par \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , the Democrat, is running significantly behind Master. She`s ahead of Kari Lake. But if that last batch is heavily Republican, that may not be enough to save \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  in the governor`s race. You have the possibility of a split ticket.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:22]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and Finchem too, we should say, that Secretary of State race. The Republican Mark Finchem, big election denier, he is running more like Blake Masters, less like Kari Lake, right? So that --\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You can see right there? Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. There we go. So, that`s like five points. Right. So, just to go back to -- I just want to stay on Kelly for one more second just to do the math real quick, just so people get a sense.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, let`s say the next batch, very conservatively, we bump them up to a difference of 110,000 votes, right? I mean, that`s very conservative.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But if you had 290,000 outstanding, right? If Masters won that 200 to 90, right, that will get him even. So, that`s the kind of margins that you would have to be talking about for that final batch to get -- for him to get back. That`s along those lines. Again, this is back the envelope. But just to give people a sense of the level of landslide that would have to be happening in that last batch of votes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And I think you look at it this way, that batch of votes, and I say that was a Republican batch of votes in 2020, Trump was generally winning those updates with 58 or 59 percent of the vote over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: So, if Masters were getting 58 -- if he were getting 60 percent, something like that. If he got 60 percent of the 290, he would net about 58,000 votes, which would be probably half of what he needs. We have to be pretty --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, let`s go quickly now to Nevada. Cortez Masto is behind Laxalt. We got some rural voting for Laxalt today. It`s a 16,000 vote lead for Laxalt, but still lots of Clark out which there`s reasonably would favor the Democrat.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: There is -- there`s two pieces of suspense in Clark. And we also have -- there`s -- OK, there`s two places with a lot of votes still left here. 40,000 ballots out of Washoe County where Reno is, though election officials say not all of those ballots -- I think there may be a fair number of spoiled ballots in there, potentially. So, it`s not going to actually be 40,000.\par \par \uc2 There`s also reason to believe those ballots will break more evenly, potentially, between the candidates. So, yes, I think Democratic hopes really do rest on Clark County here. And what it is, we -- it`s 50,000- plus. They had an update today where the head of the elections there said it`s 50,000, a little bit more than 50,000. We think these are the ballots that were put into drop boxes on election day. And the question is, you know, do they break big enough for the Democrat to overtake, you know, to make up 16,000 votes?\par \par \uc2 The problem is there`s a little confusion. We were getting indications from officials in Clark County yesterday that this number was higher, that this would be closer to 70,000. Now, we`re getting indications around 50,000. So, we`re trying to sort that out because if it`s 50, that could be tough.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: If it`s 70 -- sorry to take a bit, but if it`s 70, it`s a different story.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s exactly right. And I actually -- thank you for clarifying that because I actually was going back and forth with Ralston and some others about this. And I thought I have the numbers wrong, but it actually turned out they moved around the estimates. That was the source of confusion. Steve Kornacki, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Republicans start to turn on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  essentially threatens to do to the GOP what he did to the Capitol on January 6. It`s a wild out there. We will explore the chaos and the recrimination tasteful next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In the wake of an epic disaster in the Midterms for the Republican Party, the knives are out. Growing number of conservative pundits, party leaders, professionals taking aim at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , some publicly, some privately. The Murdoch media empire appears to really just gone all in. The New York Post cover today mocks the ex-president as Trumpty Dumpty who had a great fall. Yesterday`s cover touted Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  as the future.\par \par \uc2 At Murdoch`s Wall Street Journal, the Editorial Board declares that Trump is the Republican Party`s biggest loser. Even on Murdoch`s Fox News, commentators from guests are openly blaming the ex-president for the losses.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Part of the problem -- I don`t think there`s any question in my mind that hurt Republicans all over is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MICHELE TAFOYA, SPORTS COMMENTATOR: I wrote an open letter to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this morning on my \uc2 Substack\uc2  that, sorry, Kayleigh, it says please, Mr. President, don`t run again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Even Michele Tafoya doesn`t want him to run again. All of this comes as Trump is expected to announce his candidacy for president in 2024 on Tuesday at a 9:00 p.m. like primetime thing, and as one of his handpicked candidates, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , faces a runoff in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race in Georgia next month. Even some of Trump`s current and former advisors are urging him to delay his announcement and not even visit Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Of course, President Trump had said that he would be making an announcement on November 15 next Tuesday. I`m advising the president to hold off until after the Georgia race.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Do you see the former president I need for him to go to Georgia?\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , CONTRIBUTOR, FOX NEWS: I think we`ve got to make strategic calculations. Governor DeSantis, I think he should be welcomed to the state given what happened last night.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, that`s interesting. Send DeSantis not Trump. Tonight, on his own fake \uc2 Twitter\uc2  platform, the ex-president is -- what`s the right word? I guess, responding. He`s losing it. I mean, he`s always losing it so it`s not something new. But he`s effectively declaring war on both the Murdoch media empire and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and it is a sight to behold.\par \par \uc2 Here to hold it with me, Tara Setmayer, former Republican Communications Director on Capitol Hill. Sarah Longwell publisher of The Bulwark, and host of the Focus Group podcast. Welcome to you both. I don`t have in front of me the long and, you know, typically deranged addled screed offered by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , but he wants to have it out now, Sarah, and is ready to have it out now.\par \par \uc2 And he`s mad at Murdoch, because you can see Murdoch and the -- and the bigwigs, the establishment, the donor class moving behind DeSantis. How does this play out?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:54]\par \par \uc2 SARAH LONGWELL, PUBLISHER, THE BULWARK: Well, look, in some ways, we`ve actually seen this movie before.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: I mean, look, as somebody who desperately wants to be rid of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I have waited with Access Hollywood, with Helsinki, with Charlottesville, with January 6, with the 2020 loss. At every turn -- you know, the Wall Street Journal came out and said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be impeached the second time. But you know who none of that makes a difference to? Donald Trump`s MAGA base voters. His power emanates from those base voters and their almost perverse sense of loyalty to him.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, is that that base is quite large. You know, it was large enough to essentially give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  every candidate that he wanted out of these Republican primaries, with the exception of Georgia. And of course, that`s the problem for the Republican Party because those kinds of candidates are then poison to swing voters.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, of course, the party bigwigs want to move on. This guy keeps costing them elections. But the question is, the question is, what are base voters going to do? Are they going to abandon Trump? Are they going to move on to DeSantis. And look, I talk about DeSantis in the Focus Group all the time. There is absolutely an audience there for him. That`s because they see him as very Trumpy. They see him as a fighter. The question is what happens when he`s head-to-head with Trump?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What happens when he`s with the guy that he basically is imitating? How does that play with these voters? I don`t know the answer to that question yet. But to me, that`s the thing. You know, DeSantis can win the Wall Street Journal. The question is, can he win over Donald Trump`s base voters?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, what do you think about that Tara?\par \par \uc2 TARA SETMAYER, FORMER GOP SPOKESPERSON: Well, you know, at the Lincoln Project, we`ve been prognosticating this internecine warfare for over a year. We put out two ads, one called Sad and one called Star, where we pointed out the fact that this was the way that the gentry Republicans were going because they were looking for an alternative to Trump because he has been a proverbial loser for them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should go off and be the star of the next Biggest Loser, the politics edition, because after this disaster for Republicans on Tuesday, it`s clear that when -- you know, politicians are single seekers of reelection. That is their goal. And will the only thing that makes them change is when they lose at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 And like to Sarah`s point, we`ve been waiting for this for several iterations over the last few years and several transgressions by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and nothing moved the Republican Party. If anything, they went more towards \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, is that going to -- is this it finally? I`m not so sure. And I don`t think that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is the heir apparent that they think he`s going to be because he will not hold up under the scrutiny of a full onslaught coming from Trump world. He will not.\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t have the political charisma that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has, and he does not have the base of support that Trump has to get through the primary right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that -- I mean, that point is important. I mean, I think one point we should enter in here is \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  could have whipped for an impeachment vote, impeach him and disqualified from office under the U.S. Constitution.\par \par \uc2 SETMAYER: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this wouldn`t be an issue as Robert De Niro says in Copland. I gave you a chance and you blew it. So, you blew it, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SETMAYER: More than once.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: More than once. But the other -- the other thing I think to think about here is when we say deja vu, we`ve all watched this, right? To me, this feels more like 2015-2016, Sarah. People forget Murdock wanted to knock him out early then too and it didn`t work. He stuck around. But they have to, at the very least, have the actual fight out now.\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: Yes. And look, I hope they do. I mean, I want -- I think that other -- I think people should run against Trump. My big fear is that actually what happens is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  declares -- and this is what he wants, right? He wants to freeze the field. He wants to get out early, and make sure that nobody runs against him. And I think that would be awful for the Republican Party and the country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For America too.\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: But there`s a real risk. Like, you just can`t analytically not understand that there`s a risk to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` career and to anybody`s career who goes toe to toe with this guy because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not out to beat you. He is out to scorched earth humiliate you and end your career. He will say that your dad, you know, murder JFK and call your wife ugly. Like, he is a -- he`s the worst person in politics.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, if you`re a young guy like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , do you just wait it out for four years or do you risk that. And a lot of that comes down to whether or not \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is a good enough politician and genuinely believes he`s a good enough politician to handle that.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:31]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Tara --\par \par \uc2 SETMAYER: He`s not.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. But he may -- but he may still get it. I mean, again, they got to have the fight. That`s the big thing I`m watching for. They`ve made noises about having the fight, and then they all shrink. And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  doesn`t vote for it. They do this over and over again. At least have the guts to have the fight now. If you don`t have it now, you are guaranteeing yourself from further losses.\par \par \uc2 SETMAYER: I mean, they`re clearly gluttons for punishment. They should have had that fight after January 6. If anything, there was a violent insurrection. I mean, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  still hasn`t come out and fully denounced \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  send a mob to hang him.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SETMAYER: So, I mean, it`s really quite remarkable that they`re such nihilists when it comes to this. But you know what the bigger issue is here, Chris? This is really about the future of democracy and what we want as a country. This is the choice that we have to make. The Republican Party of old that everyone is wishing to come back -- like, the George W. Bush Republicans and Reagan -- that party is dead and gone.\par \par \uc2 And what they -- they`ve gone too far here. They think they`re going to claw their way back into some type of respectable party again under the -- under the leadership of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the guy who sent a Gestapo voting squad down to, you know, to stop people from voting, the Don`t Say Gay stuff. Like, he wants to scale up Florida. That`s what the Republican Party wants now. I mean, they really need to think about that.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that for America, this is a very stark contrast as we go into 2024. Watching them fight it out, it shows you what the Republican Party is about, versus the adults in the room which had been the Democrats now who were actually interested in governing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s why they won the midterms. I mean, that`s why they do well. Tara Setmayer and Sarah Longwell, thank you both. Still ahead, how the Republican Party`s non-existent platform hurt its candidates in the Midterms. Democratic Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  of New Jersey joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:35:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, not enough time has passed yet for us to know exactly why what happened on Tuesday had happened and why Republicans did not win in the way that they kept forecast and they would, particularly Republicans kept forecasting. But here`s the theory that explains a bunch of it. As I said before, the domestic policy agenda of the Biden administration and the Democrats is popular and the Republican Party actually had nothing to offer voters.\par \par \uc2 A guest essay in New York Times from a conservative summarized it like this. "In an era when national politics exerts a strong gravitational pull in local elections, the most important question is what sort of national division did the Republican Party offer working Americans in 2022? It`s hard to say really. The best I can come up with is something like hand us the keys to government, but don`t expect us to give you anything in return. And in that indifference lies a central problem bedeviling Republicans up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , Democrat of New Jersey, who said ahead of Tuesday`s election, the Democrats had a very strong pathway to add seats in the Senate. Senator, it`s good -- it`s good to have you on. How conscious were you and your colleagues of the level of popularity on the votes you were taking from the American Rescue Plan, the Infrastructure Bill, Chips, and then the Inflation Reduction Act, whether that was heading into the headwinds of public opinion or had it behind you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CORY BOOKER (D-NJ): You know, the leadership circle I sat in with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and other Democrats ranging from Chuck -- from \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  all the way to friends of mine on the right -- on the left in our caucus, all of us knew that the agenda we should set, the policy program we should set should be the one that working class Americans will really appreciate.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we were excited and were able to get the votes to cap insulin costs, to get the votes to pass an infrastructure bill and put a heavy emphasis on union jobs, and get the votes to do things. Even in -- as much as people said we were -- had conflict in the Democratic caucus, we passed the American Recovery Act that had everything in it from an expansion of the Child Tax Credit, all the way to helping with eviction moratoriums.\par \par \uc2 So, it was easy to go to voters in this election. And I campaigned in 12 states and show the receipts. In a bad economy, who do you want representing you? The people that are going to watch out for everything from the rent you have to pay, to the small business rescue, to protecting Social Security, Medicare, or lowering prescription drug costs, or the party the last time they were in power, their biggest signature policy issue was a massive tax cut overwhelmingly that went to the wealthiest Americans and the wealthiest corporations who just didn`t need it? And it was an easy case to make.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think sometimes I`m tempted by this very nihilistic streak that none of this substance stuff matters, and voters don`t really pay attention, and it`s all just vibes or it`s all just culture war, it`s all just, you know, what they see in the local news. And I got to say, I feel like the results this week cut against that a little bit. Like. it does seem like it did turn out that it did matter a bit.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:13]\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Yes, it definitely mattered to working people who had a lot of real fears in this economy and the best they were being offered. You know, I had a conversation with a bunch of folks of all places in a barber shop. When I challenged them, what`s the Republican agenda? If they take control of the House, what are they going to do? And the conclusion was, well, their agenda is just to go after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It`s just to launch, you know, investigation. They didn`t have an economic plan.\par \par \uc2 And then you double down and add to the fact two other things. One is, there was a massive overreach as we saw in Kansas with trying to take away a woman`s right to control her body, even under the conditions of rape and incest. That insulted voters and sent a lot to the polls. And then the second thing, I don`t think we can underplay how much the bombastic rhetoric around our democracy was really aggravating and scaring people to have a Wisconsin gubernatorial candidate say, if I win, Republicans will never lose again in Wisconsin. That`s crazy.\par \par \uc2 I was sent by Mark Kelly out to a Republican fundraiser. The Republicans doing a fundraiser for Mark Kelly. And the conversation really was common sense, pragmatic, and the fact that this far-right swing of this sort of MAGA extremists was alienating centrist Republicans themselves, and leaving them with nowhere to go but to voter -- to candidates that were appealing to them. People like Mark Kelly, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , and even frankly, someone like \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I think that -- I mean, I think the data shows that there were enough ticket splitters, enough Republicans voting for Democrats to make the difference. And again, these are really narrow races, right? We`re talking a point or two. You know, it`s one or two people out of a room of 100. You can get over to the other side and can be the difference. And it looks like in a lot of places, that was the case. Senator \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  who has been out on the campaign trail, thank you for making a little time for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BOOKER: Thank you always. Good to be with you, Chris. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, coming up, if you missed it last night, get ready for an encore presentation of an ALL IN special investigation. Where did all this red wave talk come from anyway?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In between the commercials on Fox News from Mike Pillow and Mike Pillow bed sheets and Mike Pillow shower shoes, the cable news pundits there spent the last month projecting with unwavering certainty that the election day was going to be a disaster for Democrats. Some called it a red wave, others called it a tsunami, but they were all certain Republicans are going to enjoy a midterm blowout of epic proportions.\par \par \uc2 The thing is, all that expert analysis inside the right wing bubble, was clearly just a bunch of people talking to each other. Fox News viewers looking for some kind of refund today after being promised this for a month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Be an analyst for a second. Does it feel like a red wave?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It feels like a red wave, Brian.\par \par \uc2 STEVE DOOCY, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: You know, your predictions of a red wave are accurate.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, CONTRIBUTOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: So, I made a surfboard. They said the red wave is coming.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Red wave rising.\par \par \uc2 SARAH PALIN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: That red wave that I`m convinced is coming.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2  (R-CO): The reports I`m seeing show a big red wave coming.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sleepy Joe just guaranteed a red wave in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There`s a lot of energy on the ground. You probably hear the rally in the background right now. We think we`re going to have a big red wave in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Democrats are bracing for the worst-case scenario, a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, HOST, FOX NEWS: We are officially on a red tsunami watch.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Sean, we`re going to see a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, FOUNDER, OUTKICK:: Red tsunami grows.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That means red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: And we`re not just going to see a red wave, we`re going to see a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Poverty, joblessness, critical race theory, crazy gender ideology in our schools. We are going to see a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And lastly, your prediction for tonight.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we`re going to have a red wave. I think it`s going to be maybe bigger than anyone thought.\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: On Tuesday, we will be part of a big red wave that says enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 JOEY JONES, CONTRIBUTOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Up next, Elon Musk ready to ride the big red wave.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Elon Musk tweeted massive red wave.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Massive red wave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Massive red wave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Massive red wave.\par \par \uc2 BEN SHAPIRO, CONTRIBUTOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: You`re about to see a red wave that makes day after tomorrow look like nothing.\par \par \uc2 JOE ROGAN, RADIO HOST: That`s going to be responsible for the red wave. I think the red wave tha`s common is going to be like the elevator doors opening up in The Shining.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Rogan said that the red wave is going to look like the elevator doors opening and the blood pouring out of the elevator in The Shining.\par \par \uc2 ROGAN: Like the elevator doors opening up in The Shining.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: That is correct, except it`s not going to be an elevator, it`s going to look more like Deep Impact. A tsunami at the end but colored red.\par \par \uc2 DAN BONGINO, CONTRIBUTOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Remember that Tea Leoni Deep Impact disaster movie? That`s the red wave tsunami that will come ashore.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: It`s going to be a brutal week for the Democrats beginning on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: And frankly, I`ve already DVRed CNN and MSNBC for election night not because I`m going to watch. but just because I want to enjoy the tears post red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: What happened to the red wave, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What happened is the Republicans kind of outkicked their coverage. Ben Collins is a senior reporter for NBC News covering online disinformation and right-wing conspiracy theories. And Michelle Goldberg is an op-ed columnist from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  who just wrote a piece about how Republicans did not read the room. They both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, I want to start with your column because it`s the column that I would have wanted to write which is, you know, basically a lot of people criticize folks on the left for being in their own bubble, the coastal elite, you know, cosmopolitan metro areas. And some of that critique is really true. It`s easy to get embedded in your own sort of world that people that agree with you, agree with your politics. But I think that problem is worse on the right.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:21]\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right, it`s way worse on the right, because the right is not self-conscious about it. I mean, the left has that problem, but the left also knows it has that problem. I mean, one reason for the kind of often mocked trope of the Diner Safari where journalists, you know, kind of earnestly head into some rural part of the country to take the temperature is because they know -- they`re worried about being out of touch. Whereas Republicans have convinced themselves that they speak for the people. You know, I mean, Stop the Steal in a way is like the ultimate version of that, right? They don`t know anyone who voted for Biden. So, they assume that nobody did.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You hear this all the time from conservatives. Like, there`s no way you`re telling me that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got any 81 million votes. It`s preposterous on its face.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right, they`re completely out of touch with what`s happening in their own country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, exactly, because they don`t actually know anyone. And the other part of this too to me, Ben, is that there is such a pipeline -- you see it in, in some of the shows on Fox News -- between like, the most extreme corners of the internet, to the right wing media, such that they become fixated and obsessed with things they think are huge, big issues that are really like Nish obsession.\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR REPORTER: Yes, I mean, you know, it`s trans swimmers and trans kids in school, and we must do something about these books in our school. That`s really what they`re focused on most of the time. And look, there are people in this space who are aware now that they have a problem. You know, if you watched Fox News in the last couple of days, you would say -- you would see, they would say, look, we have a problem with young women.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: The problem is the solution for this, the remedy for this is, do we tighten the boot and make it bigger, right? Do we -- do we --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: How do we coerce more?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What power and leverage can we --\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Can we raise the voting age to 21?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: That was literally a suggestion from them. So, like, you know, Nick Fuentes, the white nationalist podcaster with young people, his solution wasn`t how do we reach out to them? It`s literally we need to call for dictatorship now because we need to get these people in line.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, we need a dictator to force people to adopt our ideas. I`m paraphrasing here. And we should note about Nick Fuentes, who was a fringe figure, who was at the Capitol on January 6, outside the capitol with a bullhorn. But \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , who is now, if the Republicans do take the House, which is not a sure thing, going to have an influential seat at the table in Republican -- in the Republican Party. She went to the guy`s conference.\par \par \uc2 So, again, we`re like it`s one skip from like, the craziest fringes to the mainstream. And there was a -- there was a backlash, there was a public opinion backlash at the polls today.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. And I think, again, if this had happened the other way, we would all sort of recognize it for what it is. So, Democrats went too far. They shouldn`t have talked about defund the police. They`re, you know, kind of culturally out of touch.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And we`ve read a million pieces about that.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: We`ve read a million pieces like that. And I hope to read now a million pieces about all the ways that various kinds of right-wing lunacy has alienated -- has alienated normal people. And that it turns out that that message that every -- that a lot of people made fun of Biden forgiving, you know, that second speech about democracy --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, you could -- people were breaking their skulls rolling their eyes so hard at it. I mean, you saw people like -- oh, he`s going to do -- when he announced another speech, it was like, well, I guess he would do another democracy speech. It was like this like, gross Mean Girls snicker?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right, because, you know, I think that the political media values like snark and sarcasm. But you know, I mean, as you wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , ordinary people are pretty earnestly patriotic. And the person who speaks to that the most, you know, not always, but often has an advantage.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You mentioned trans swimmers. This is one area that I really think that the bubble is most intense. They are obsessed over it.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, they are.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, it`s truly -- and I think you can speak to this. And I saw it in some -- there`s a rally in Michigan, the like closing rally for all the candidates where they were like talking about this, and they had a woman who was protesting trans girls participation in swimming as like their big hero. And I just -- what I found comforting about the results is that like, it turns out, most Americans don`t actually care that much. They`re not that hopped up on anger about trans people living in their lives.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: And also, the cruelty of this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: To people who are -- you know, these people are systematically targeted. They`re bullied in school, they are kids. They`re literal children. And you hear people like Don Bolduc, for example, in that audio that came out. That guy who ran against \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  in New Hampshire and lost, where people were just -- they were telling these mythical fables about trans people in schools, the kitty litter thing. They`re using kitty litter, because they identify as cats.\par \par \uc2 And it sounded like -- you know, it sounded like an old boys club. They`re just sitting around and making fun of children together. And maybe if they really take a step back here, maybe the thing that`s turning off suburban women is being cruel to children for no reason whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, and also, people know that the kitty -- right, the kitty litter thing, which you know, it was fascinating.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It was (INAUDIBLE) to quickly -- to quickly give our viewers. There was basically an urban legend that there were kitty litter being put in schools because children were coming in saying they identified as cats, which was to show the ludicrousness that we had taken this too.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:16]\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. But I think, you know, they`ve all convinced themselves that this is something that`s happening on a mass scale, but anyone who has a kid in school knows that this is absurd.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, really quickly. So, the moderate position on the Fox News, right, for the kitty litter and the trans stuff, is this is bad and we have to stop it. The far-right position is there`s this thing called transhumanism where they`re trying to put chips in your kid and turn the idea of gender into a mishmash because they want to indoctrinate your kids. So, like the moderate position right now on the Fox News far right is we -- like, we have to be cruel to kids. So, right -- they have to do some reflecting.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I think, again, there`s a majority in America to be decent to people.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think that`s like the Obama position. I still hold to it. And I think this week was a win for that thesis.\par \par \uc2 Ben Collins and Michelle Goldberg, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221111eiba00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC LIVE for November 09, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Steve Kornacki \par \uc2 7,665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:28]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: On night two of our election coverage, we still do not know the winner and three Senate elections, in Georgia, Arizona, and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  received the most votes in the election in Georgia but did not win 50 percent of the vote which is necessary to win. And so that race will decided in a runoff election in Georgia on December 6th. Election then will be between \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Donald Trump`s Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They`re still counting votes in the Senate elections in Arizona and Nevada where new vote totals have been reported tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go straight to Steve Kornacki at the big board with the latest numbers -- Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESOPNDENT: Yeah, Lawrence, let`s check. And we got some reports as you say from both states.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Arizona. Mark Kelly, the Democratic incumbent senator, he saw his league grow tonight. Why? Because he got some ballots in from two counties, Pima, which is where Tucson is. The ballots -- these are ballots that were dropped off just prior to election day. Kelly did very well with the votes came in, about 13,000 of them from Pima County. Also from Maricopa County which is the giant in the state. The majority of the vote from the state comes from Maricopa County.\par \par \uc2 Again, Kelly winning the votes, about 62,000 that were released here. He won them by about 12 points over Blake Masters his opponent. What that did was it brought Kelly advantage statewide to about 95,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 Now there are still a lot of votes to come in Arizona and mainly in Maricopa County which that is just a mother lode of votes in the state. What is still to come we think are 50,000 votes that voters returned or came into polling places on Monday. We probably have something in the order of 65,000 or so votes that came over in over the weekend. Those will still be tallied.\par \par \uc2 And based on what we saw from those votes that came in tonight, this pool of votes could help Kelly build an even bigger lead over Blake Masters. If this pool of votes looks like the pool of votes like we got out of Maricopa tonight, that will help Kelly pad his lead.\par \par \uc2 But the big number that looms over this race is 275,000. That is the number of ballots that were dropped off at polling places on election day by voters. The question is, are those going to be a Republican type of vote or Democratic type of vote. Because in the 2020 election, those types of ballots, the ones that voters just chose to bring in themselves on election day in person, they were heavily Republican. If they are this year, it would offer Masters an opportunity to knock down and a road that Kelley leads substantially.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen another elections like 2018 the same type of vote being Democratic. So it`s an X-factor, it`s a wildcard court, it`s a mystery, it could be decisive in this race.\par \par \uc2 One thing to note in Arizona. Look at that lead Kelly has in the Senate race in 95,000 votes. Now look at the governor`s race. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is running ahead but 82,000 votes behind Mark Kelly. Her lead over Kari Lake is much narrower over that there`s, vigor verdict there`s a split vote between the Senate and the governor`s race. We`ve seen that in a few other states.\par \par \uc2 And then up to Nevada, where we also got some more votes tonight. You`re looking at the -- that`s the governor`s race where \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent is in some trouble in the Senate race. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent, is also in trouble, but she`s doing better than Sisolak. And tonight, she knocked off about 5,000 votes from the lead that \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican challenger, has over her.\par \par \uc2 She did that because we get a batch for the 14,000, 15,000 votes from Clark County in Las Vegas. What is left in Nevada? They`re approximately 70,000 votes to be counted, 70,000 mail-in votes to be counted in Clark County, and in Washoe County where Reno is, there approximately 61,000 mail-in ballots to be counted there.\par \par \uc2 The vast majority of the vote in Clark County, these are ballots that people dropped off in the drop boxes on election day. And again, it`s sort of a wildcard. It`s a guessing game like in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 The type of person who on election day wants to go out and put the ball in the drop box, is that we know that Republicans tend to do better with election day vote than Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:01]\par \par \uc2 Is that disproportionally Republican? Is it disproportionately Democrat? Is it neutral?\par \par \uc2 That`s a huge x-factor here, and I think, obviously, the character that vote once it gets revealed, we will tell you whether \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  can catch Laxalt. On paper, the numbers are there. She needs them to break a certain way. It`s a bit of a mystery how they are to break.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Steve, what is the timetable for getting to a winner in Nevada, the timetable for getting to a winner in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yeah. In Clark County, the election officials later the press conference today later the plan that could potentially go into next week to get all the votes finalized. There could be a call before then, depending on how these votes break. If they break away it makes a winner clear, makes it mathematically impossible, there could be a call in this race before next week, but they`re talking about the results not being finalized until next week.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, we have heard from county officials in Maricopa some talk about numbers that will come out, and it releases a voice to not always live up to those numbers, but there`s a lot of talk about the vote being come out around 8:00, 9:00, 10:00 tomorrow night eastern time from Maricopa County, gets into those 275,000 votes, the mysterious 275.\par \par \uc2 There`s a chance tomorrow night we will get a real sense of what that looks like, and I think once we know what that vote looks like, potentially it`ll be very clear how this the race is going to turn out, even if it takes a few days to get a final official result.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Steve, will know exactly where you are going to be on December six, standing in front of that map with the map of Georgia for the runoff election.\par \par \uc2 Is there any indication that third party candidacy that we`ve seen the current returns in Georgia, is there any indication of how that faux will break when it`s just down to two candidates?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yeah, it`s an interesting question. And one of the questions about it is will that vote exist at all? Are there voters who are going to turnout for one-on-one race between Warnock and Walker? Because the real interesting dynamic here is Walker`s underperformance in the state relative to \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  with a Republican in the governor`s race, 47 percent Walker in the Senate race, 53.4 percent, basically Kemp outpacing Walker by five points here.\par \par \uc2 I think one thing you see here. Take a look at some of these core Republican counties. Cherokee County, this is one of the biggest Republican vote producing counties in the United States of America. So, Herschel Walker wins it by big margin. You say that`s great news for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But compare two things here. Compare Herschel Walker`s number, and compare the third parties number to what we see in the governor`s race. Look at that. Kemp amp got seven more points in the third party candidate got more than two points less. So it tells me than in a place like Cherokee County, a very Republican area, there`s a tip of Republican-leaning voter doesn`t like Biden, doesn`t like Democrats but also doesn`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And to that voter, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  was acceptable, that`s why Kemp gets a number like 74 here in the governor`s race. But that voter, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  so closely aligned with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it wasn`t acceptable. And instead, some voted third-party, some voted Warnock, and you wonder, the ones who opted for that third party, do they end up caring about this election? They end up voting for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Maybe if Senate control is on the line, they ended up viewing it as I want to vote for Republican control of the Senate. But they weren`t willing to do with potential control of the Senate on the line yesterday, so it`s possible they just stay home.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Steve Kornacki, thank you for the update.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You got it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And here`s what President Biden said this afternoon in a press conference about the election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: While the press and pundits are predicting a giant red wave, it didn`t happen. And I know you were somewhat miffed by my obsessive optimism, but I felt good during the whole process. I felt we were going to do fine. While any seat lost is painful, some good Democrats didn`t win last night, Democrats had a strong night.\par \par \uc2 We lost fewer seats in the House of Representatives than any Democratic president`s first midterm election last 40 years. We have the best midterms for governor since 1986.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now, \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 . She is the White House deputy chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. The news of the night is President Biden speaking to Kevin McCarthy. What can you tell us about their conversation?\par \par \uc2 JEN O`MALLEY DILLON\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, they had a conversation earlier this evening in the president as he has been, you know president focused on delivering for the American people and making sure that he continues to get things done.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I think you saw yesterday, I think you`ll continue to see him focused on getting to the work of the American people and that`s part of the call ahead tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Was the president initiating this call?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: I think as many of you know, the president has been making calls. Last night he had the opportunity to call a lot of people. I think he talked to 40 different people who are on the ballot that won last night. Today he`s been calling Democrats, Republicans, members of the House, Senate and governors throughout the day just to check in. Someone, some lost, he doing that all day long.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Does this mean that Kevin McCarthy believes he will be the next speaker of the House?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: Look, the president is very optimistic of what he`s seeing out there. Not all the ballots being counted. As he said, election day we need to have patience. He spoke to the speaker. He spoke to Leader Schumer as well.\par \par \uc2 So, he feels very good about what we are seeing. He also knows there are more votes to be counted and he`s going to wait for that to come in.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Did he tell Kevin McCarthy that any changes to Medicare and Social Security that were proposed by some Republicans during the campaign in effect putting them on a regular recurring budget appropriation, did he tell them that that`s just dead on arrival? Did he tell Kevin McCarthy there`s no chance of that?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: I think President Biden has been very clear that`s dead on arrival. Everywhere he`s traveled in this country he`s been talking about really setting up the choice that was before the American people on election day, and a big part of that choice was in past forward to make sure we are protecting Social Security and Medicare. And an alternative as you mentioned putting it on the chopping block.\par \par \uc2 Every five years or every one year in the instance of Senator Ron Johnson. He has been very clear, he`s going to continue to be very clear, there is no chance that we move forward with him as president.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: President Biden delivered a speech, a pre-election speech unlike any we have ever heard from an American president, saying democracy is on the ballot. Does he believe that speech helped turn the outcome of this election?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: Well, I think as you and I have talked about, President Biden was talking about democracy as something before even got into the race in the first time in 2019, and has continued to talk about issues of democracy throughout his time as president. But he clearly feels, and I think he has spoken about this very clearly that there is a lot at stake. Democracy was at stake in this election and that was critical for the American people to show up making sure that they understood that they had a role and making sure that we held on to the democracy that is fragile. It takes all of us.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we saw, what he saw, what he was talking about really show that when the American people turn out to vote, when poll workers and volunteers show up to man the polls, you can see a free and fair election in this country executed beautifully, and the American people having the patients to wait and see the results. We are still waiting to see over the next few hours and probably a few more days.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There`s been a lot of talk today about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being underestimated. It`s a version of talk we have heard that many points in his career, especially since he began running for president last time. And when he would have his big win suddenly that campaign, there was a top that he had been underestimated.\par \par \uc2 Is being underestimated the key to Joe Biden`s success?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: I think the key to Joe Biden`s success is that he understands the American people and he understands what they are going through, what they care about and what he can do to deliver for them. I think as you know, what you heard him talk about, he wasn`t looking at the polling, he was out there talking to real voters. He knew that people cared about costs in their lives, the prices were too high.\par \par \uc2 He also knew he had to tell the story about what his administration and Democrats were doing to bring those costs down. You heard him today saying that he knows that many people still have yet to feel the impacts of the Inflation Reduction Act that went forward earlier this year. We also heard him talk about reproductive choice in the fact that we are in a country where women`s roads rights have been rollback 50 years.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, you heard him talk about democracy. Those are things that he understood to be critical to the American people because he understands what they`re going through, he`s out there talking to them, he was hearing about it on the road and that`s what he was focused on. Delivering for them, making sure that we are making progress but thanking it clear that there`s more work to be done together, and I think that`s what the American people responded to.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Jen, we need one more piece of advice from me before you go, because you all guided us through the way we should watch the votes being counted during the last presidential election over that week that they were being counted and led to the Biden victory.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:04]\par \par \uc2 You taught us how to watch those returns and that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would win.\par \par \uc2 What`s your advice for people watching maybe weeks of vote counting until the vote final vote counts in California that could determine control of the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: Well, I think the American people should feel great that their democracy is working. It`s in action and the reason we need to have a little extra patients as we wait to see some of these results come through it`s because turnout was very high across the board. All ages, all parts of this country people turned out to vote.\par \par \uc2 So don`t fret. The more patients you have the better, because we feel confident in what we are seeing. Obviously there are more votes to be counted. We`re going to wait for those official results.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , thank you for joining us once again for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 DILLON: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, we`ll be joined by the Democrat to beat a Trump Republican who stated position that ten year old rape victims should be subjected to we called a community review in order to obtain abortion services.\par \par \uc2 Wiley Nickel is one of the congressional Democrat candidates who appeared on last word and he is now on his way to Congress. Wiley Nickel joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:21:07]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: North Carolina State Senator Wiley Nickel made his national television debut as a congressional debut on our program. Now he is a winner on his way to Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP.-ELECT WILEY NICKEL (D-NC): This is a win about sending a message. Folks are tired of far-right extremists. If you run as a hunter percent pro Trump in a district like this, you`re going to lose. The message we send with this, a far-right extremists are going to be rejected by voters because they know the difference, and we get to send that message with this win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is Democratic Representative elect now Wiley Nickel who`s celebrating a decisive victory in North Carolina`s 13th congressional district over Trump-backed Republican Bo Hines. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reported Bo Hines was, quote, seen as a rising star and by some in the Trump wing of the party. Bo Hines said access to abortion for rape and incest victims should be decided by what he called a community level review.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Are you saying that you would accept an exception for rape or incest in North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 BO HINES (R) NORTH CAROLINA CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: I think in certain cases yes in, a case like that were a ten year old girl I should say, obviously she should not be able to go through that pregnancy. That`s a horrific tragedy there has to be exceptions in place for.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: So is it more the rape and incest or her age?\par \par \uc2 HINES: I think it`s a combination of both, but you have to look at each individual case, look at each individual circumstance and what happens there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Representative-elect Wiley Nickel of North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for returning to us and joining us tonight. It`s a real pleasure to have you here.\par \par \uc2 Was that the decisive moment in your campaign when he said that about what the ten year old girl would have to go through after being a victim of rape or incest?\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: It was certainly an important moment for our campaign because it showed what far-right extremists we were up against. It`s an issue we keep continue to talk about, because women`s rights were on the ballot this election. Case by case committee level review for victims of rape was something that is far out of step with voters in this district.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It was a very close race that you won. You are a state senator, you could`ve stayed presumably in the state senate. What made you make the decision to take this risk and run for the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: I wanted to do all I could that we continue to make gains in congress. This race was really about sending a message. I`m glad to be going to Congress to represent the 13th district, but we were able to send a message for any swing districts, this was a 50/50 see, that if you stand 100 percent pro Trump, say you want to be a MAGA warrior, you are going to lose at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 For anyone else who stands with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Madison Cawthorn\uc2 , of the Republican Party, they made two okay in Republican plus 20 districts, but we are going to reject them at the ballot box in swing districts like this one.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Do you know if it was helpful in your race to have Cheri Beasley running as strong a campaign as she did statewide for senate?\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: I think absolutely. We won as you saw there just by little over two points. A lot of people came out to vote for Cheri Beasley and if she hadn`t run such a strong campaign, there`s a chance I might not have won this race. So in a close election like this, everything matters, and she did a really good job, making sure the people got out to vote.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:04]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Wiley Nickel, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Thank you for joining us during the campaign so that voters could actually hear from you during the campaign. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: Thank you so much for having.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Michigan voted 57 to 43 to make abortion a right by adding it to the state constitution. Michigan Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  was reelected with more than 54 percent of the vote and Democrats won control of the state legislature for the first time in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Michigan State Senator Mallory McMorrow who won reelection last night.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 What was the key -- let`s concentrate for the moment on the legislative victory. What was the key on winning that control of the legislature?\par \par \uc2 MALLORY MCMORROW (D), MICHIGAN STATE SENATOR: This is incredible. Last night sent an earthquake through Michigan and the entire country, and really it was about focusing on the state legislature which I have been stranded since April, making sure we have the resources early. We are able to recruit and credible candidates all across the state who fit their districts perfectly, we were able to get up on the TV and on the field with positive messages, that sent such a strong rebuke to this politics of divisiveness the politics that voters are tired of.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We had this discussion earlier tonight in our election coverage but the national Democratic Party not paying enough attention to what you achieved in Michigan. Not paying enough attention to state legislatures and the elections there and how important they are. What is your feeling about that the national party`s attention tonight?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: I hope that this victory and it`s a big one. Michigan is one of the most consequential battleground states. We now have a blue trifecta heading into 2024. I hope that this sends a message that with this is where we have to focus our time, effort, energy and resources, where these are races that are run with thousands of dollars, not millions of dollars. We just need our candidates to have the right resources.\par \par \uc2 And with the issue of abortion, we know that Roe fell because of a down ballot collapse in state legislatures during the Obama administration that led to these extreme anti-abortion laws being passed out of state after state after state. So if we really want to build power back, we have to start on the ground in state legislatures and we did here in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There is another point about state legislatures that I`ve always wondered why it doesn`t get more attention, and that that is that Barack Obama who is a former president tonight because he ran a successful campaign to become part of the Illinois state legislature. That`s how he got started and actually winning elected office.\par \par \uc2 We just had a member of the state legislature in North Carolina who was our guest who is now going on to congress. Would he be going on to Congress if you had me to successful state center like yourself? It`s more than a training ground for these other offices, but it is also a good training ground for other offices?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: It absolutely is and beyond that, this is where we build actual relationships with voters. You may not ever see your member of Congress or U.S. senator in your community except for maybe at a big event. But you will see your state legislator, at the grocery store, at a bar, at a restaurant and, a coffee hour almost regularly in our district.\par \par \uc2 This is where we as a party can build real meaningful relationships with voters that`s also exactly what we need to be building so we have people in touch with what voters are really feeling, and that we are set up to have a really successful election moving forward.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s consider the governor`s campaign in your state. We have seen the governor going through so much from the plot to kidnap and murder her and this incredible threat that she`s been under while in office. Not only has she dealt with that, but she also had to deal with the pandemic interstates response to the pandemic. And we see that she also had to run a reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 Talk about the way your governor achieved this decisive victory. What was the key to her decisive victory?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Number one, she is an incredibly relatable representative. She`s gone to hell and back. And she talks about it on the campaign trail. She`s just going to get up and dust herself off and keep ongoing.\par \par \uc2 People her coined her -- actually \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  coined her that woman from Michigan. And she took it as a badge of honor. If you go across the state, every woman in the state of Michigan feels like that woman from Michigan.\par \par \uc2 And this election felt like a challenge. You don`t mess with those women from Michigan. We stood behind her. She stood strong. She really plants the flag early with us in the legislature and in the courts that abortion is a right that we will protect long before Dobbs came down, long before we lost a right.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:46]\par \par \uc2 And it sent this message that I think people are really hungry for that we need regular, relatable people who talk about their families and their values, and we need people who are going to fight for you every single day, and she does that better than anybody else out there.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What achievements are you hoping that the Democratic -controlled legislature will be able to bring to voters when the next reelection campaign occurs?\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: We`ve got 40 years of an agenda in the making. And number one, abortion is really the issue, reproductive rights that got us across the finish line. So even though we passed Prop 3 and it`s a right in our state constitution now, we have to repeal our 1931 abortion ban to send the message to voters, we hear you, we`re listening and we`re going to make sure our laws align with you on the books.\par \par \uc2 Beyond that it`s protecting voting rights. It`s making sure we amend our state Civil Rights Act to protect the LGBTQ community. And then it`s protecting public education. This is the home of \uc2 Betsy DeVos\uc2 . We have to fight back against vouchers and privatization, protecting clean water, this is a union state, making it easier for workers to organize and so much more and we are really excited to get it and then put that in the agenda together.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Michigan state senator Mallory McMorrow, thank you very much for joining us. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MCMORROW: Thanks Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up. We will be joined by an Ohio Democrat who beta a Republican member of Congress. Our list of winners continues here. Representative-elect Greg Landsman will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:36:43]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Last night and today President Biden had a lot of phone calls to make to winners.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hey buddy, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Congratulations man.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How are you Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m better now that you won, I`ll tell you.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations, I`m so happy. I really, really am.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations. I`m so excited for you.\par \par \uc2 I think with the grace of God and the goodwill of the neighbors, all the stuff we did is only going to come to fruition next year. Because they heard about it but they haven`t seen it. I just think there`s a lot we can do. It`s really, really going to matter.\par \par \uc2 I`m so excited. God love you. Well, I`ll sleep better knowing you won. Keep the faith, man. Talk to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is one of the Democrats who flipped a Republican congressional seat representative-elect Greg Landsman, won Ohio`s first congressional district defeating Republican \uc2 Steve Chabot\uc2  who was first elected in 1994.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Did you get a call from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 REP-ELECT GREG LANDSMAN (D-OH): I did. You know, he couldn`t have been kinder, more excited, and obviously it was a -- it was a really exciting moment. You know, winning was the most important thing but getting a call from the president of the United States is a big deal.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Did he get into the details with you of how you won? Did he want to know how you pulled that off in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 LANDSMAN: I think he was paying very close attention to what was happening here and in other races. So we talked a little bit about it. I mean the fact was that it was a race where you had, you know, Democrats and Independents and a lot of Republicans coming together to, you know, beat back the kind of chaos and extremism that folks are just frustrated with.\par \par \uc2 And Chabot who you know has been there for almost three decades was sort of a do-nothing or you know, innocuous backbencher. But when Trump got on the scene, he really, you know, dove all in and was, you know, part of, you know, the group that try to overturn the election on January 6th. And that was just too much for a lot of Independents and, you know, a surprising number of Republicans who just, you know, want that to end.\par \par \uc2 And then he`s also, you know, my opponent was very well known as an anti choice extremist. And that caught up to him with the Dobbs decision. You know, folks just don`t want what he wants which is, you know, a full ban on abortions with no exceptions. Rape, incest, life of the mother -- it doesn`t matter to him.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, they sent -- I think a strong very important message to the rest of the country that if you`re going to be in a competitive district like this one and it`s very competitive, you know, the big lie, anti-choice extremism is just not going to fly.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:56]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is going to be your number one priority in your first year in the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 LANDSMAN: Yes. I think it`s got to be all of our, you know, number one priority and that is the economy. And it`s not just dealing with the issue of inflation, but really fixing this economy which has been broken for a long time.\par \par \uc2 And wage stagnation is not new. It`s been going on for decades. We`ve got to get wages up. We`ve got to get costs down across the board, not just prescription drugs but housing and childcare.\par \par \uc2 You know, I hope there is bipartisan support for the child tax credit which has been -- was incredibly effective in helping millions of families pay their bills.\par \par \uc2 I also think that there`s got to be consensus on public safety, and investing in police and fire, and helping communities with budget shortfalls so that we have enough resources to deal with crime.\par \par \uc2 And then for us locally, you know, we`ve got to get this -- you know, We have a big bridge that needs to be fixed. And the money is there but we need leadership to ensure that we get the bridge done and done well.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Representative-elect Greg Landsman, who in the House of Representatives will be Mr. Landsman of Ohio, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 LANDSMAN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is representative-elect Emilia Sykes who was elected to Tim Ryan`s House seat in Ohio. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. You were running in a seat that was already -- had been occupied by a Democrat but it was a big move for you to go from the state legislature, House of Representatives now to the House in Washington. What made you decide to make the run for Congress?\par \par \uc2 REP.-ELECT EMILIA SYKES (D-OH): I made the decision to run for Congress because of my community. We desperately need a voice that is going to stand up for us, fight for us, to ensure that we are able to afford the every day expenses in this community -- gas, groceries, mortgage and health care, protecting our public safety as well as making sure we have access to quality affordable health care including abortion here in Ohio 13.\par \par \uc2 And when this district was redrawn, which was far more Republican than the one that \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  currently holds, I knew that it was important for this community and for this country to ensure that there was a fighter for democracy as well.\par \par \uc2 And so I was excited to be able to be the Democratic nominee and even more excited that I`ll be representing my hometown, my community in Congress next year.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now you have an unusual element of your bio which is that both your mother and your father served in the Ohio state legislature. What did they teach you about what you are now doing for a living?\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Well, they taught me that first of all, my job is to serve the public. And I come from a long line of public servants including my sister who is in the public school system, my brother-in-law who is a retired police officer. My husband who is in public service.\par \par \uc2 We have made it our mission to live our lives to give back to others. We have received so much from these communities that we live in, and the least that we can do is give back. And so originally I wasn`t sure if elected office was going to be my path but certainly I am so grateful for the opportunity because it`s really important for this community to have someone who`s fighting for them, thinking of those everyday issues that continue to plague them.\par \par \uc2 And I do encourage anyone who`s interested in this race and this community to visit my Web site. It`s EmiliaSykesforCongress.com, and learn more about why it was so important for me to run for this seat and protect our community as well as this country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Did you get a winner`s phone call from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Yes I did get a phone call. He was very gracious and complimentary. But I think that the one thing I got from that phone call was that we have a lot of work to do. There are people all across this country who are counting on us to put aside our political and partisan differences, to make sure that we are working for the people who elected us, and ensuring that this is the best country to live in.\par \par \uc2 I know that northeast Ohio is the best place to live in the country and the work that I will do as a member of Congress will be reflective of them and I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 We were originally counted out in this race, very much so. I think that Republicans thought that they had, it was the most expensive race in the state, one of the most expensive in the country.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately our community said, we want a fighter. We want someone who`s going to fight for us and that`s why they elected me. I look forward to serving them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is your top priority in your first year in the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 [22:44:50]\par \par \uc2 SYKES: My top priority is making sure that my community is taken care of. I heard on the campaign trail that the economy was their number one, sometimes number two and number three issue and wanting just to be able to afford those everyday expenses and be safe and secure in their communities.\par \par \uc2 Their public safety was very important. And I had done a lot of work around victims` assistance and protecting our community which people really resonated with. I was a champion for reproductive rights, it was something that really helped resonate with the folks on the campaign trail. And mostly I spent a lot of time just listening and I will continue to do that as a member of Congress because obviously the people in the district know what`s best for them.\par \par \uc2 And I will make sure that I`m visiting all parts of the community, all parts of Ohio 13 and making sure that they know that they have a fighter in me and someone who`s ready to work for them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, we will want to be listening to you here on this program so please come back next year when you are member of the House. Representative-elect Emilia Sykes, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. And coming up, Maxwell Alejandro Frost made his national television debut as a congressional candidate on our program and he will now be the youngest member of Congress. Maxwell Frost will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:46:05]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Last night I was pleased to call Maxwell Frost the 25-year-old who got elected, I guess the youngest man ever elected to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 . But I`ve no doubt he`s off to an incredible start in what I`m sure will be a long and distinguished career.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Maxwell Frost made his national television debut as a congressional candidate on our program. At age 22, he became the national organizing director for the gun violence prevention group, March For Our Lives.\par \par \uc2 Now at the age of 25, Maxwell Frost when he is sworn in on January 3rd will become the youngest member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Representative-elect Maxwell Frost of Florida. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. This really is a special feeling for me certainly to have you joining us now when I`m looking back at when you first joined us.\par \par \uc2 What was it like when you picked up the phone and there was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 REP.-ELECT MAXWELL ALEJANDRO FROST (D-FL): Well, thanks for having me on tonight. An incredible feeling. I remember I was in the middle of having a conversation to area code answer and I heard someone say "please hold for the president".\par \par \uc2 I put it on speaker and everyone in the room and I started doing some silent jumping and cheering. So it was an incredible experience. Especially to hear from him and hear him talking about the fact that he was elected to the Senate at 29. So he knows what it`s like to be the youngest person in the room.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So basically, your first phone call as a representative-elect. First phone call for part of the governing body in Washington is from the President. Do you feel, did you feel at the end of that phone call that you are part of this team that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the leader of?\par \par \uc2 FROST: I did, I did. And you know, I`ve gotten calls from not just in the president but folks all across the spectrum of Congress in our caucus. And I`ve really felt a part of the team even before the election was called.\par \par \uc2 Many members reaching out to me offering advice, offering different ways to help out. And so I know I`m not going in alone. And I`m really excited to serve my community in Congress.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now, how many of the current members of Congress are smart enough to be asking you for advice?\par \par \uc2 FROST: Well, I`ve had some people ask me for advice on how to get out the youth vote. And really looking at how do we get young people excited about voting. And I think a lot of folks use that advice. As we see we had record turnout yesterday with the young voters across the country.\par \par \uc2 And that`s really exciting for me. We`re going to continue to see that trend go up over the coming years.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. On the age demographics, the 18 to 29 group, 63 percent voted Democrat. That was the highest vote for Democrat in any age group that we`ve seen. So it seems to me that when you get to Washington, and before that, everybody there in the party should be talking to you about that 18 to 29 vote and how to get it.\par \par \uc2 FROST: Yes. I mean what I found when I worked at March For Our Lives is young people really want somebody to vote for and not against. So my advice was always telling for talk about the world that you want to build and the world that`s going to be better for future generations. Talking about canceling student debt, combatting the climate crisis, ending gun violence and building a world that works for every single person.\par \par \uc2 And when do that you don`t really have to persuade someone to vote. You just got to remind them when election day is.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So what is your top priority in the first year in Congress?\par \par \uc2 FROST: There`s so much we have to tackle. The economy is top of mind for me. We had just had a devastating Hurricane Ian in Orlando, Florida, which left people with nothing. They are outside of their homes. They don`t have anything anymore.\par \par \uc2 We have stagnated wages, we need to ensure that we bring up wages. We need to make sure that our people have health care. And that they have the ability to be healthy. And that they have the resources to tap into the opportunity that this country has. So I`m really thinking about the economy, and how we can make it work for every single person.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And going forward, as you move into the next year, are their committee assignments that you are thinking about already that would be important to you?\par \par \uc2 [22:54:49]\par \par \uc2 FROST: Yes, we`re thinking through what makes sense. But at the end of the day I really want to be put where I can best serve my community and best serve central Florida. And so we`ll see what that ends up being. But I know wherever it is, I`m going to be working hard for the people of District 10 and for the people of Florida.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, I know the viewers of the 10:00 p.m. hour on this network feel like they`ve been on this journey with you from the start of the campaign. Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Representative-Elect Maxwell Frost. Really appreciate you being here.\par \par \uc2 FROST: Of course, thank you so much for having me on.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Chris Hayes will continue MSNBC`s coverage of the 2022 election right after this.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:31]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221110eib9000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 DECISION 2022 for November 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Joy Reid, Alex Wagner, Steve Kornacki, Chris Hayes,  Claire McCaskill, Jen Psaki, Ari Melber, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,898 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [21:00:01]\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Technocratic Steve is my favorite Steve actually, I have to say. I have no complaints about explaining what might be rational in terms of that kind of progress.\par \par \uc2 I should tell you, we`re just coming up at 9:00 on the East Coast right now, and we`re watching these results come in Arizona and in Nevada. Obviously, the Georgia race, the Georgia Senate race we know is going to be a runoff. And for the purposes of us having a digestible amount of information to handle, that`s sort of handy, because we sort of put Georgia aside, that`s going to be a runoff, we can think of that after.\par \par \uc2 But just as a reminder, I think there are three Senate races that can`t be called. One of them is in Alaska, the reason it does not being described as part of the partisan discussion here is because the likely winner in Alaska is either going to be a Republican or a different Republican. It`s either going to be an incumbent Republican \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , or her Republican challenger. And so whatever happens in the Alaska race, however interesting it is, it will affect the overall balance of power in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  between the two parties.\par \par \uc2 The question then really for us now comes down to Arizona and Nevada. If \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  or \uc2 Kelly Tshibaka\uc2  wins, we`ll be looking at 49 Republican seats, and a question is whether they got 49 seats, 50 seats, or 51 seats. And that`s going to make a huge difference in terms of what the agenda is for the president in Washington, whether or not we spent two years talking about.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, Arizona, Nevada, all of the vagaries of the election systems now become profoundly interesting to us as we watch these things come in. It`s just incredibly consequential how these two sort out.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST, THE REIDIOUT: It`s ironic that the thing that is probably the most immediate change there would be if somehow Republicans were to eke it out and get control of the Senate is the same thing that they got us here. Roe got us here, it got Democrats here, and there will probably be never be another confirmed Biden federal judge if \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  gets in. All he really does is pass tax cuts for the superrich, and stop Democrats from naming judges.\par \par \uc2 So, I think for the Biden administration, what they would have to be thinking about the fact that they will never be able to get another judge through, that`s the end of that.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": It`s also worth noting that Alaska is a R versus R. But \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  is not \uc2 Kelly Tshibaka\uc2 , very much in the mold of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  is a Republican , which is better than everyone else, can sometimes be counted on for reality, and sometimes work in a semi-bipartisan fashion, and losing her from the Republican Party that`s increasingly radical, I think that`s a loss for democracy in some ways.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Particularly if the Senate is close to 50/50, she`s one of the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: She is a normie. She is not one of the crazies. What you`re really seeing in Alaska is what is going on in the Republican Party in full blown fight fashion for the next two years, and that`s going to be the establishment Republicans trying to claw their way out of the Trump ditch, back to a place of the normalcy where they are not going to be offending voters with extremism, the virulence, the lies, the horrible meanness that that part of Republican Party has to represent that. We`ve seen that in these results. Not really palatable to most Americans.\par \par \uc2 Lisa is somebody who is anxious to get back to, okay let`s get some Democrats and Republicans in a room and see what we can agree on, you know? Put aside what we can agree on , let`s get something done.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I mean, she`s not a swing voter. She`s a Republican, but occasionally agree to govern.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: But she`s also pro-choice Republican, proudly pro-choice Republican. It`s my recollection that she voted against Kavanaugh.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST, "ALL IN": She did. So she`s somebody who is capable of standing up to pressure for people in the Republican Party to vote for something she truly believes in. And there`s short of supply in the Republican Party. So, since it`s crazy Republican versus Lisa, my friend --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: She was also one of three Republican votes to vote against ACA repeal, which preserved Obamacare, and crucially so. I mean, they need every last one of those.\par \par \uc2 I also think in terms of what it means to be -- I think Joy`s point is really important, that the biggest, most tangible in the same way, when you look to the Pennsylvania governor`s race, whether they get a abortion ban or not, the most tangible thing is judges, judges and appointments. Not just judges or appointments.\par \par \uc2 You got to fill the federal government. People start to leave. There`s only so long they will serve in the cabinet, like the government needs to be staffed, and having control of the Senate means that you can do that smoothly, and it also means that you burn less time. It also means that the Biden administration has had a remarkably successful record for the first two years, along with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , confirming judges.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:06]\par \par \uc2 It`s been record setting. There are I think about three dozen vacancies which \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , if you`re watching, probably want to get in on that either way. Seriously, by the end of the year. But that is a huge, tangible, immediate, no question stake for this control.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Claire, let me also ask you. A little later in the night last night, you said it was not impossible that Democrats could be heading into the Georgia runoff on December 6th with \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . They could be heading into that with control of the Senate already in hand, because you thought they could win both Arizona and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Do you still believe that?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: I do. Nevada, I hate even talk about this with Steve Kornacki within the sound of my voice because he knows so much more. He knows so much more about it better than I do.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: But based on the nerds that I`ve talked to today, and we`ve got some nerds in --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You have other nerds?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: There are nerds in the Democratic Party, they wanted the drop box number in Maricopa to be a little bit higher. They wanted it to be closer to 100 as opposed to the 56,000, 57,000 it was. But they still feel pretty good about it.\par \par \uc2 What`s the unknown here is the same day drop box, what are they? Are they Republicans who don`t trust the mail? But don`t want to wait in line? Or are they Democrats who completely trust the process, and just want to mail in their ballot, they want to go drop it off.\par \par \uc2 We don`t really know that. It`s the only question. But in Nevada, -- excuse me, I said Maricopa, I meant Clark County. They wanted more drop box votes , more than 57. There are still enough there that it would be very close.\par \par \uc2 The interesting thing that happened today, \uc2 Brian Schatz\uc2 , the senator from Hawaii, he is busy helping locate people that were remotely help people cure the ballots in Clark County.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So, what cure the ballot means?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, cure the ballot is when they have discovered something where signatures don`t match, where they cannot count the ballot. But you get an opportunity to fix it, come in and show your ID, re-sign the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And so what the Democrats are doing right now is trying to mobilize a bunch of people to reach out to all 5,000 of those people, in Clark County, to help make sure that they know that they can cure their ballot, and they know how to cure their ballot.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Look, how close it is in Nevada , both Nevada and Arizona, these races. I would tell you they`re going to get called tonight, and we should stay with us, but that would be me lying. We have no idea when they`re going to get caught, but we`re still watching as new votes come in.\par \par \uc2 You know, it wasn`t just the historical trends that made so many people that so many people think that Republicans would do better last night. It was also, and I think this is important, it was also because Republicans said they were going to do great last night. Republicans explicitly set expectations high about how they`re going to blow Democrats out in this election.\par \par \uc2 And not just recently, this time last year, Kevin McCarthy, the top Republican in the House, got up in a formal press conference in the Capitol, and told reporters that Republicans were expecting to take more than 60 seats in the House. If things go great from them, from here on out, it will look more like six, than 60.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who is in charge of the Republican campaign for the senate, he said Republicans were going to get minimum 52 seats in the Senate. He said Republicans were going to get minimum, 52 without even trying. But he said he expected more than that. He expected 53, 54, 55 Republican seats.\par \par \uc2 At this hour, is not even clear that the Republicans will get 50, although they might. At 8:15 Eastern last night, after the first big round of polls closing, are you okay, Chris?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I don`t know --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It is 8:15 p.m. Eastern Time last night, after the first big round of polls closing, the eldest son of former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Mr. Junior, posted this on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Just the word "bloodbath" with three exclamation points.\par \par \uc2 I should tell you, he hasn`t been heard from since. It`s possible that that was a cry for help, but a bloodbath is the one thing that this election was not.\par \par \uc2 Expectations about the election results went all over the country. But there are also defied in a lot of different directions. I`ll tell you one of the heartening surprises, Joy, you and I were just talking about this moment ago, was that some election deniers, not all, but some election deniers, people that I had refused to accept the late last election being real, people who refused to commit to accepting the results of their lost this year, a bunch of them did actually, in fact, concede.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:05]\par \par \uc2 Republican Senate candidate Mehmet Oz, for example, in Pennsylvania, he conceded to Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Michigan Republican candidate for governor, Tudor Dixon, she conceded to \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , who lost to \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  in the governor`s race, did not concede last night, but he did concede to \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Even Matt DePerno, the Republican attorney general candidate in Michigan, one of the most vituperative election deniers running anywhere, Matt DePerno, conceded to Democratic Attorney General Dana Nessel in Michigan, to whom he lost.\par \par \uc2 I should also tell you, in New Hampshire, in the very hard-fought Senate race there, a Republican Don Bolduc had said that his the Trump won in 2020, he actually said it was mathematically impossible for Trump not to have won in 2020, Don Bolduc who has praised even after his defeat last night, praised by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as an election denier, even Don Bolduc in New Hampshire last night, conceded. He went out of his way to tell his supporters that he did not concede to his opponent, Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , he was only conceding to them. But still, he, in fact, acknowledged reality.\par \par \uc2 REID: We`ll take it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R), FORMER NH SENATE CANDIDATE: \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  has won. I was told the protocol was to call her first, but I`m not one for protocol.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Protocol, schmotocol, what matters is that he acknowledged it. That was not a given.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  of New Hampshire who won reelection last night against Republican Don Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 Senator, congratulations and thank you for being here with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): Well, thanks for having me on, and it was a good night.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It was a good night, and I have to tell you, it feels weird that part of it is a sigh of relief, that your opponent who you defeated is acknowledging that the election was real, that it had a result, that he came out on the losing side of it, and that there isn`t going to be at least that kind of a fight over this kind of race. I have to imagine it was not a given for you heading into this.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: It was not a given, but here`s the thing. The people of New Hampshire in a really resounding way stood up for common sense, for reproductive freedom, and for democracy. At the end of the day, the work that we did talking to voters, putting together a really robust, grassroots effort starting over a year ago, really resulted in Granite Staters understanding what the stakes were. And it really -- my opponent and the Republican super PACs that supported him had really tried to mislead Granite Staters about who he was, and Granite Staters saw through that, and really rejected somebody who is way too extreme and out of step with where our state is.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: One of the things that we talked about heading into this election, when you`re really in the middle of the fight was the resonance, the comparable resonance of different issues that so many Democrats and observers around the country were debating in terms of what people want to hear about. Did they only want to hear messaging on the economy? Did they only want to talk about reproductive rights, that they only want to talk about trust to democracy?\par \par \uc2 I wonder now that you`ve gone through this, we have seen what was in effect a resounding strategy for you, if you have any more clarity on that, in terms of what worked and what Democrats -- excuse, me what voters in New Hampshire took as a motivating force to get them not just to vote for you, but to vote at all?\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Look, what I heard recently that we heard from Granite Staters was that they have a lot of things on their mind, just the way that all of us to. Really worried about inflation. But, you know, I`ll tell you that canvassers came from doing their door to door shifts, and I still remember one person telling me that a grandfather said that he was voting for his granddaughter as he pulled his eight-year-old granddaughter next to him.\par \par \uc2 So, reproductive rights mattered, inflation mattered, public safety matters, democracy matters, and so the thing about people in New Hampshire and all across the country is that they`re dealing with all of these things at once, and they expect their elected officials, their public servants to address all of these things, all at once.\par \par \uc2 So, for us as a campaign, this was about making sure that voters understood that I was working to deliver for them on all of these things, the priorities that they identified for me. I really made a point, as I always do, going to work where I was working. Weather is the factory floor or small business, if change, or just a coffee shop, and really listened to them, and then talking about the way that I have worked in the Senate to develop priorities, and make differences for them.\par \par \uc2 [21:15:03]\par \par \uc2 You know, whether it`s banning surprise medical billings, working with the Republicans to do it, or negotiating the CHIPS and Science Act, or helping our veterans. Granite Staters expect people to exercise how nonsense just the way that they knew when they put aside their differences and solve problems.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Practical, practical politics. I have to ask, you and this is a little bit route of me to ask, and you cannot answer. I know the president Biden called a number of Democrats who won the races last night, and we think that you are on that call list.\par \par \uc2 If that`s true, can you confirm that for us, can you tell us anything about the conversation?\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Oh sure. The president was gracious enough to call, and it was very nice to hear his voice, and it is nice to touch base and talk about the things that are important to people all across the country.\par \par \uc2 I did use the opportunity to talk with him about the work that we still need to do, particularly on lowering home heating costs in the Northeast as winter approaches. But it is a good conversation.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , Democrat of New Hampshire, as always, practical, nuts and bolts, getting the work done. It`s always a really big pleasure to talk with you, thank you and congratulations on your big election victory last night.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Thanks so much. Be safe everybody.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Of course. All right. Up next, we`re going to turn our eyes to Nevada. The big outstanding Senate race there, obviously the governor`s race there, let`s to talk about in Nevada. Jon Ralston, who is the dean of Nevada political reporters and a very blunt spoken man, he will join us from Nevada, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:21:22]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I love that Vegas shot. It makes me feel like I`m in all those places at once.\par \par \uc2 It`s been nearly 24 hours since the polls closed and the state of Nevada is still -- yeah, we don`t know. Nevada Senate, too early to call. Nevada governor, too early to call. Nevada secretary of state, where the Republican vying to run Nevada`s elections is himself in an election denier, that`s too early to call. Nevada attorney general race, also still not called at this point.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, as long as your ballot was postmarked on or before election day, it has until Saturday to arrive and still get counted.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the state`s biggest county, Clark County, which is home to Las Vegas, just stated 60,000 mail-in ballots were retrieved from drop boxes on election day. So, that`s not in the mail, it is actually dropped in, and thereby election day.\par \par \uc2 The question is, what is outstanding is Nevada, is it going to be enough? Is that vote of whatever character that it`s going to be enough to push Democrats over the edge, which is what they`re hoping? When do we expect we`re going to have final results? I`ve been talking with Steve Kornacki about this all evening already.\par \par \uc2 But joining us now from Nevada is the man himself, Jon Ralston, CEO of the Nevada Independent, and our longtime Nevada quarterback.\