ing to get all of that ballot, and I don`t know how these are going to go. But Nevada and Arizona are really going to be down to the wire.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Nevada has this almost Wisconsin -like quality, where you just know it is going to be extremely close, extremely fingernail, and it`s really interesting to look at Cortez Masto and Sisolak, right, because we know, you look at Bolduc and Sununu, you`ve got these statewide races, and these could be two incumbents, they`re only running 18,000 votes apart, so we should be clear about that.\par \par \uc2 I remember having Governor Sisolak on our show, and I thought during the pandemic, hit one of the hardest jobs in the entire country, which is shutting down Las Vegas. Truly one of the -- in he had to do it, and you can tell he was tortured, and you can tell people were ticked off about it. He tried to navigate an incredibly difficult situation, I think arguably the most difficult in the nation.\par \par \uc2 I think to the extent that you see that 18,000 vote difference there, in particular them nominating a Clark County politician to run against him, which is very crucial, that explains some of that.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: But there are a bunch of good pieces of news for Democrats, and it`s always a relief when Jon Ralston says something align with what I`m hearing. But the Democrats I`ve talked to who are watching this very, very closely say that one of the more ballots that come in, the better it is for \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , because the Democrats from the beginning have been running their strategy focused on these mail- in ballots, and also on these drop boxes.\par \par \uc2 They`ve been working with the culinary union as he said. They have been instilling confidence in people, telling people this is how they should vote. And to that margin, he tells you that. So, the more that comes in, the more space.\par \par \uc2 They have a dose of confidence, not going to over 