eople who want a functioning government, that`s not really what the markets like.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Right, and the idea of what counts is a functioning government has changed so much in the terms of our lifetimes, as to what a governing coalition is. The idea of split government right now being able to do anything is something that, as you say, allows you to equate corporate irresponsibility with underage drinking, which I think was very subtle the way you did that, Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But it`s exactly how it works, but it`s also this reminder. When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  just laid out all those economic accomplishments, Democrats can look at these midterms and say, we need to lean into this, right? This notion that, Democrats are the party about social issues, while Republicans they are the ones who care about the economy.\par \par \uc2 That`s not true, right? If we don`t have a functioning society, we can`t have a functioning economy, and the reverse is true. So this is a moment where Democrats can lean in.\par \par \uc2 It`s not like, oh, well, the exit poll said inflation was the number one issue. I guess people didn`t care about it, they only cared about abortion. Both things can be cared about and voters who care about the economy don`t automatically vote for Republicans. Remember Republicans didn`t offer any policies.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Right, when it`s the Democrats who are doing the Inflation Reduction Act and the Republicans are burning books. And you end up in a different valance there, you know?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So one of the things, Stephanie, that I`ve been most since fixated on the upcoming debt ceiling and that very clearly Republicans in the making all these noises about what they`re going to do is they are going to hold it hostage like they did back in 2011 to get the cuts they want, maybe cut Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 My read of the election results is the narrow or the Republican House margin is, the less likely they can make that threat, because they will have frontline members who know what to do it. But what is your read about what the odds are for big, nasty debt deficit fight are, given the results?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I think you`re 100 percent spot on, right? What do we see? I`ve talked to donors all day, sort of centrist donors on both sides who are saying that what these elections should have shown, let`s get to a more practical place. You are not going to see the far crazy super right play with the debt ceiling cause that`s not what corporate America likes, it`s not what Wall Street likes, they`re not going to want them to play that game.\par \par \uc2 But the thing you really think about, Chris, everybody`s say they`re voting because they`re unhappy with the economy. What every one of those voters to look at that today, what are they going to get different with inflation? Nothing. It doesn`t matter who we voted for yesterday.\par \par \uc2 There`s no lawmaker, there`s no president who controls the economy and controls inflation. It`s a global issue, it`s coming out of the pandemic, it`s tied to the war. There is no one today that`s going to walk in and say, we`ve got inflation covered, you`re all set. Voters should be aware of that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , talking about what`s going to happen in Washington.\par \par \uc2 [21:55:02]\par \par \uc2 We heard \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  saying the Washington White House will do anything \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  wants to help in that.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You want a car.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We just have a few seconds here but in terms of what`s happening in congress, are the things a Democrats can do in Washington that will set up \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  better toward -- for that runoff?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yeah, I mean, it seems like a decade ago, but in 2020 remember the Democrats push to get these checks that they could go down to Georgia and say, look, we are giving you checks, we`re giving you money, Georgia. That`s what Democrats can do if you get these two through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Right now, there are corporate tax cuts that run out. The government funding runs out middle of December. There`s an opportunity for Democrats to get through Medicaid expansion through Georgia, which is a huge issue for Warnock as an option to help him in this runoff.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Our coverage of election 2022 continues tonight with our dear friend Lawrence O`Donnell who`s up next.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221110eib9000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC LIVE for November 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Steve Kornacki, Chris Hayes, Alex Wagner, Vaughn  Hillyard, Joy Reid, Lawrence O`Donnell, Symone Sanders \par \uc2 10,368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From MSNBC, Decision 2022 Live from Democracy Plaza.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Back together again over, caffeinated, underslept, plus all the weird eating you do when you`ve had too much caffeine and no sleep. We are ready. We are here. We are not at all a mess. None of us, right? We`re all fine? Welcome back to our special 2022 election coverage night two. I`m Rachel Maddow joined once more by my colleagues. Honestly, it feels like we never left. I`m not sure that all of us did.\par \par \uc2 Chris Hayes, Alex Wagner, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , Lawrence O`Donnell, great to be with you, guys. Our friend, Steve Kornacki, is of course at the big board. You know, guys, I can tell you, this is what he trains for. He`s fine. You can stop sending us care packages for him and inquiries about his well-being. He`s great. Look at him. He was better than he was yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Any minute we`re expecting a big chunk of new results out of Maricopa County in Arizona, home to one of the two outstanding \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  races we are still waiting on. We`re going to be going to Steve when we get that data in. But of course, here`s the bottom line at this hour. We still do not know which party will control the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , nor do we know which party will control the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2 . Just the fact that there`s even a chance that Democrats could hold the House is so ahistorical, it almost doesn`t compute.\par \par \uc2 The President`s party always gets shellacked in the first midterm after he takes office. The President`s party`s losses are usually measured in the dozens of seats. That is not what has happened here to Joe Biden`s party. He and the Democrats have defied history. This afternoon. A noticeably buoyant President Biden held a press conference. He called yesterday a good day for democracy.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now at the great big board is Steve Kornacki. Steve, am I right that we`ve just got some new data in from Maricopa County or was that a promise left unfulfilled?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): It is an unfulfilled promise, but it could change any minute. That`s why I`m eyeing this right here because what we`re expecting any second now is from Maricopa County, the largest county in the state by far. 60 percent of all the vote in Arizona comes out of Maricopa County. This is what happens basically around this time every night in the days following the election. They report out big chunks of vote.\par \par \uc2 So, what`s left to come here in Maricopa County, what we`re expecting in the next few minutes, it`s votes that were basically returned, ballots that were returned Friday, Saturday, and Sunday, right? So, basically, over the weekend. Ballots that were returned over the weekend, plus some ballots that were returned on Monday of this week, OK. They were all brought in, they have not been added into the count yet. This total pool the weekend ballots, plus the Monday ballots, is 136,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 Now, the hope of Democrats in the expectation of Democrats, frankly, is that this pool of votes -- and we don`t expect that all of this 136,000 is going to be reported out, but we do expect that a significant chunk of it is going to be reported out. The hope of Democrats is that this is a Democratic-friendly batch of votes. They hope that because generally, you know, it`s early vote. It came in a few days before the election or a day before the election.\par \par \uc2 And certainly, we saw that trend last night in Arizona. If you were watching last night, you saw the big initial reports put Kelly ahead by like 20 points in the Senate race. That was early vote all getting reported out quickly. Then what happened in the overnight hours is they added up all of the same day vote. And the same-day vote is what brought masters closer in the Senate race. It`s what brought Kari Lake very close to the governor`s race. She`s only 3000 votes behind statewide in the governor`s race right now.\par \par \uc2 So, Democrats are hoping that this batch of votes, the sort of late- arriving early votes, that it favors them and that it gives Kelly a pad in the Senate race and also that it boosts the Hobbs, their candidate, in the governor`s race as well. Because again, I mentioned that governor`s race is close. So, we`re going to get -- we`re expecting to get a big chunk of this any minute now. Then once all of that is added in, the one other major outstanding piece of business in Maricopa County, and this is a biggie, is going to be 275,000 votes that were delivered by voters to the polls on Election Day in person.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:14]\par \par \uc2 And this is the wildcard and it may end up being the ultimate wildcard because there`s good theories from each party about how to interpret how this votes going to look. Republicans look at that and they say, well, that vote, the vote that was delivered in person on election day in 2020, that was a pro-Trump vote. That`s why if you remember, in 2020, as the week went along, Biden`s lead got shorter and shorter and shorter, and he ended up winning the state by less than 9700 votes. That was these ballots being counted up election week 2020. Democrats countered that, hey, in the last midterm election of 2018, it was this same group of votes, the votes delivered on election day, that lifted the Democrat \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  to victory in the race for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, we have seen this batch of votes behave two different ways in the past two elections. So, it looms is sort of the ultimate wildcard. But I think what Democrats are hoping here is, you know, on the assumption or the possibility that this is a Republican-friendly group of votes, they see in the votes that you`re going to get reported out. We think tonight -- we think in the next few minutes, I keep looking over here, they see an opportunity to try to pad Mark Kelly`s lead. I`m showing you Maricopa County but --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`m just going to tell you that I`m going to give you a second to wait for those votes that you are expecting any minute to come in. What we`re going to do is we`re going to pause you for a second, if you don`t mind. We`re going to speak with White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , and then we`re going to come back to you expecting that those votes are going to be here.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now live is White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , Mr. Klain, it`s a pleasure to have you with us tonight. Thanks for making time.\par \par \uc2 RON KLAIN, WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: First, let me just start by apologizing for treating you like an interruption to Steve Kornacki as White House Chief of Staff. I know that this is sort of a pay grade conflict here and I apologize. But I also just want to --\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: No, look, it`s a -- it`s an honor to become -- go on before or after Steve Kornacki as he counts votes. Let me tell you, it`s a rare, rare honor either way.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We all feel the exact same way. But we don`t work in the White House. I wanted to ask you just what it has been like today at the White House, how the President views these results. Obviously, we saw him speak on his own behalf today at today`s press conference, but I wanted to ask how you are feeling, what the mood is in the White House, and whether or not this was as much a surprise to you as it was to so many of us in the media worlds and in the public sphere?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Well, look, we entered the election with confidence. We thought the President and the Vice President had gone around the country and laid down a very strong message about what we had done the past two years, about what was at stake in the election, and we felt very confident in that. But of course, you never know till you know. And the results certainly have been very, very strong.\par \par \uc2 We are within reach of holding the Senate, and possibly even expanding our majority in the Senate. The House -- the President -- we already know the President achieved a result in the House that hadn`t been matched in 40 years, a result in the governorships that hadn`t been matched in almost 50 years. So, when you have one of these kinds of historic triumphs, even though again, you know, we certainly lost some races, we wanted to win, but overall, a very, very, very strong outcome for the president and his agenda.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It is as we`ve been describing, as we`ve all been talking about, it isn`t ahistorical outcome to have the president`s party do so well in a first midterm election. The practical impact of that, and we`ll see it in stark relief as we start to get the results of these last remaining results. But the practical impact is likely to be very, very closely divided power inside the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This President and you in the White House, you are not strangers to how to deal with that. That has been the circumstance for these first two years of your term. Do you feel like you have had -- you have learned lessons that you have tried things that didn`t work that you wouldn`t try again, or that there have things that you`ve learned might work with closely divided power in the Congress in terms of how you will treat the next two years for governing?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Well, look, I think the President laid that out today, Rachel. I think he made it very clear that he has tried to and has successfully on occasion, worked with Republicans to reform our infrastructure to get roads and bridges built, to pass the Chips Bill which is going to make America the world leader in advanced manufacturing and create so many jobs and spur our high-tech manufacturing industries, to do things for veterans. The Pact Act which took care of veterans who`ve been a victim of burn pits.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve proven we can make this work with Republicans in the House and the Senate. Obviously, we`re going to have to find ways to do the same. But the President has also proven that he is willing to stand his ground and he was very clear on that today. If we wind up with a Republican majority in the House, we`re going to try to find areas of common ground to work with them on. But the President`s not going to agree to cut Social Security, cut Medicare. He obviously would veto any effort to ban abortion or anything like that.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to Try to work with us where we can. We will draw the lines where the lines need to be drawn.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:05]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Ron, it`s Chris Hayes here. And I wanted to ask you a question specifically about the overperformance of Democratic incumbent members of the House in frontline districts. If you look at how Democrats overperform the fundamentals, that was really where they were very strong, a bunch of them. And it struck me that they were not getting hammered over voting for key items of the President`s agenda in the way that you`d saw frontline members getting hammered over the vote for the ACA in 2010.\par \par \uc2 How much did that go into the thinking around the legislative agenda? How central was that you think to last night`s results?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Chris, I think you`re spot on. And perhaps more importantly, is the President called winners last night. One by one, they said exactly that to him. They said, Mr. President, I was able to win because I ran on your infrastructure bill. Mr. President, I was able to win because I talked about what the rescue plan had delivered for my district. Mr. President, I was able to win because of what the Chips Bills could deliver for my district.\par \par \uc2 So, we put together a historic agenda to recover from the pandemic, to recover our economy from the pandemic, to make the economy strong and grow and to create jobs. And the Democrats in the House and Senate voted for that agenda and they ran on that agenda. This was not a midterm where endangered members of the House and Senate ran away from what the President had done. This was a midterm where they ran on what the President had done. We gave them a substantial record. They use that record. I think that`s why we won a lot of these close incumbent races in both the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Ron, it`s Alex Wagner. Congratulations on what is seemingly a victory for this President, even though the outcome is not yet -- it`s not finalized, but I know the feeling is good. I do wonder -- the President said today that after he gets back from his Asia trip, he`ll be inviting Republican and Democratic leaders to the White House to talk about the road ahead. Who specifically in the Republican Party does the President feel he can speak with and work with on an agenda?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Well, look, he has worked with Senator McConnell on issues. Obviously, we have wide differences. There`s no question. Senator McConnell has worked very hard against us on some things. But Senator McConnell did help us pass the Infrastructure Bill. He did provide his vote for the Chips Act. He provided Republican votes for the Veterans legislation.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to try to find more partners in the House. We did have a number of House Republicans vote with us on some key legislation. So, you know, we`ve been through this for two years. There`s no one here who is naive about the challenges of divided government, of closely divided government, or 50-50 government in the Senate. We`ve been through it. But we have been able to deliver results. Those results are making the country and the economy stronger. We`re going to try to keep that up in the next two years.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Ron, are there plans for the White House to try to help \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  over the finish line in what we now know will be a December 6 runoff between him and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: You know, Rachel, we`re going to do whatever Senator Warnock wants us to do in terms of, you know, events or fundraising. We`re going to deploy all kinds of resources down there. I feel very optimistic about that run-off. I think Senator Warnock has done an incredible job in his start in the Senate. I think he`s got a great record to talk about. I think the comparison with Herschel Walker is just a stark comparison. And I think that, you know -- so I believe, you know, the Senator Warnock has done a great job as a senator and look forward to that run off.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , White House Chief of Staff, we really appreciate you being with us tonight. We know you guys haven`t slept either. Thanks very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 KLAIN: Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right, while we have been speaking with the White House Chief of Staff -- again, I don`t mean to characterize it as playing a placeholder function, but Steve cranachan was working in that time. And Steve, I understand we`ve got a little more vote in in the interim.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes. Now, we`ve been teeing up here, Maricopa County, which is the biggie here. I`m being told there`s a slight delay in getting the Maricopa County number. But if you watch closely here, in the time you were doing that interview, Mark Kelly`s lead statewide did take up over Blake Masters. That`s because the other big County in Arizona, nowhere near the size of Maricopa, but Pima County where Tucson is in, it`s a similar situation to what I was describing about those early votes that kind of came in late in the process, you know, right before election day. They kind of put them aside and they`re counting them out and they`re releasing them out tonight.\par \par \uc2 They released 13,440 of them just a few minutes ago, and Kelly won them by a margin of 4937 votes. Basically, Kelly beat Masters a little bit better than two to one with the new batch of votes that was reported out of Pima. So, what that did again, is it extended Kelly`s lead statewide that had been sitting at about 83,000, now sitting up close to 88,000. And again, there are more votes like that. That`s not all of the late-arriving early from Pima. There are about 30,000 more to come from that county that may well look just like that.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:13]\par \par \uc2 So, you can see for Democrats, it`s an opportunity to pad the lead statewide that Kelly has. And again, just when I was saying, their hope for Maricopa with whatever comes out tonight is that it`s similar to what just happened in Pima County, that the sort of character of that late-arriving early vote is more democratic friendly. So, it`s on a much bigger scale potentially, you`ll be getting a report out from Maricopa and Democrats are hoping that could push Kelly`s number up there significantly.\par \par \uc2 And also, to keep in mind here at this governor`s race. The gap between Kelly and Hobbs is about 80,000 votes here. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , the Democrat is barely ahead of Kari Lake. In fact, that update from Pima County more than doubled Katy Hobbs` advantage over Kari Lake this governor`s race is razor- thin right now. So, you talk about control of the Senate, for Democrats, from their standpoint, it`s crucial to get Mark Kelly the victory here and to pat his lead as much as they can. Because again, that last big batch from Maricopa could be Republican-friendly.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of this governor`s race for Democrats, it`s even more important they get big numbers out of Maricopa tonight, because Hobbs needs all the patches he can get given how close Kari Lake is in this race. So, again, just -- I`m going to hit the refresh here just to make sure. We were told maybe about a 10-minute delay. Still, nothing new there from Maricopa. I`ll shout as soon as we haven`t.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing we are waiting on at some point tonight, and maybe later in the night, is an update from Nevada. The outstanding vote -- you see \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is trailing by 23,000 votes. The core of the outstanding vote is in the two big counties, Washoe and Clark where Las Vegas is, combined there 90 percent of the state. Really, the biggie is Clark County.\par \par \uc2 Basically, in Clark County, there`s about 15,000 votes we might get reported out tonight. And then on top of that, there`s still another 70,000 or so yet to be reported. There`s probably a total of about 85,000 outstanding votes in Clark County with a mainly ballots that were put in drop boxes on Election Day. Nearly 60,000 of that total were votes -- were ballots taken from drop boxes on election day.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get an update we expect tonight. But again, it won`t be anything near that 85,000. And then the number in Washoe County is 61,000. And about two-thirds of that -- again, this is mail vote basically -- about two thirds that arrived before Election Day, and about one-third of that came in on Election Day. And we do know just based on the partisan composition of the ballots, the vote that came in before -- the vote that came on election day is there are more Republican ballots there than democratic ballots.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a bit of a mix here in terms of what to expect with these votes. But between what`s left in Washoe and what`s left in Clark County, we`re going to see how those numbers start breaking. There certainly are enough ballots just you know, theoretically, for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  to catch Laxalt statewide, but she`s got to win them by some big margin. So, when we do get updates, really, really crucial to see with a mark -- Cortez Masto has to be leading them and it`s crucial to see what the margin is because you`ve got to be leading them by a significant amount to overtake Laxalt statewide.\par \par \uc2 So, that I think may come later tonight. Again, I don`t mean to just tease this and not follow through, but usually my experience with Maricopa has been, they have these updates, and they`re usually pretty quick. It`s 8:18 and we still don`t have this a vote tally, this release. But as soon as we get it, I`ll let you know.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve, can I ask you one just clarifying question.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You have explained this. I just want to make sure that I`m getting it because I`m -- I feel like I`m fuzzy on this. It seems to me the way that you`re describing the outstanding votes, the votes that are still to be counted in Nevada and Arizona is that in Nevada, they may be more predictable in terms of their partisan camps. Whereas in Arizona, it`s a little bit more of a jump ball in terms of what the likely partisan character of those votes is. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Well, the reason is that in Washoe County, you know, we`re Reno is, they actually gave us a breakout of those votes that were -- votes that arrived on Election Day. And they actually told us how many of them were by Republicans, how many were by Democrats. And the number who were Republican outnumber the Democrats. And we know that overall, in the early vote in Washoe County, there were more Democrats than Republicans who were voting, so it`s a reversal from that trend.\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, and in Maricopa, we don`t know. So, it`s -- what we know is that in 2020, that big, big final batch was Republican, and in 2018, it was Democratic. So, there`s real suspense around it.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes, legit. All right. We`ve got much more to come tonight. We`re going to go back to Steve as soon as we get those new votes in. We`re going to go live to Arizona. We`ve got a lot to get to. This election in still going with all the most important questions still unanswered. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: In Pennsylvania where Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  has won the governor`s race by a double-digit margin, his Republican opponent, fervent election denier \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  has still not conceded the race. In fact, since Mr. Mastriano danced a little bit to the rocky song and made a brief statement to supporters last night, he hasn`t said anything since.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:12]\par \par \uc2 Over in Michigan, the losing Republican Secretary of State candidate there has not been shy. Kristina Karamo accused one city clerk in the city of Ann Arbor in Michigan of engaging in mass election crimes. Just like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , Kristina Karamo has not conceded the race that she lost to incumbent Democrat \uc2 Jocelyn Benson\uc2 , despite the fact that as you can see here, she trails by 14 points.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s Arizona. In Arizona, the race is -- the big-ticket races remain too close to -- excuse me, too early to call as Steve Kornacki has just been telling us. Votes are still being counted. We`re watching for votes to come in tonight that could potentially be determined to live. We shall see. The races very close. And that of course is why late last night, Republican candidate Kari Lake told her supporters to be calm and patience and wait for democracy to run its course.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, I`m kidding. Of course, she didn`t say anything like that at all.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-AZ), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We had a big day today and don`t let those cheaters and crooks think anything different. And I want you to know we are going to monitor the ballots. The system we have right now does not work. When we win, and I think it will be within hours, I think it will be within hours, we will declare victory and we will get to work turning this around. No more incompetency and no more corruption in Arizona elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Those cheaters and crooks. That speech came after a whole day of Kari Lake and the whole online MAGA universe promoting conspiracy theories about ballot counting machines in Maricopa County -- Maricopa County stealing the votes or something. Arizona is a place where the far-right pro-Trump movement went through a very recent period of radicalization around the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 It wasn`t just the cyber ninjas forensic audit fiasco, there was the armed protests around the Maricopa County ballot counting center just after election day in 2020. There was another armed protest at the state capitol on January sixth at the same time the U.S. Capitol was being attacked. This year, people with guns, armed vigilantes stocked ballot drop boxes in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Now, law enforcement officials have erected barriers around the Maricopa County Center -- Maricopa Counting Center this week. Sheriff`s deputies are stationed on the other side of the big fence they put up around that center. Maricopa County police know exactly how bad things can get when election deniers start whipping up their supporters. But frankly, so does Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 As the Arizona election stretches into what may be an extended period of counting in these close races, is there a reason to worry about what might be being stirred up in that state? Let`s bring into the conversation NBC News Correspondent Vaughn Hillyard. He`s standing by at the Maricopa tabulation center in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn, thanks very much for being with us tonight. I know you`re able to hear me setting the groundwork there for this discussion a little bit. First, let me just ask you if any of that strikes you is as wrong or sort of not the right context to be looking at this in given what you`re seeing there now.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: No. In fact, Rachel, we could have had this conversation a year and a half ago. I remember the first rally that I went of hers. It was in a church down the road in Glendale, Arizona. And she was talking for the arrest, the prosecution for perp walks of elections officials.\par \par \uc2 We should note that Kari Lake to this day has still never named who these elections officials are. You heard in her primary when she would suggest that there was irregularities and fraud, that she never provided any actual evidence of any allegations of evidence or fraud. But yet she continued to perpetuate that. And that`s what you heard last night, the crooks in the cheaters.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake has never provided any evidence of who these individuals are. Instead, she has directed ire, directed -- even called for essentially retribution against individuals that do not take action, whether it be Arizona governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 , \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2 , the attorney general who worked his tail off to try to find some sort of fraud here in the state of Arizona, and yet failed to. And it wasn`t until a 60 Minutes interview earlier this month in which he used an expletive to describe Kari Lake and the allegations of fraud that they suggest.\par \par \uc2 But it gets down to an even more baseline level when we`re talking about these Maricopa County folks here. You`re talking about Clint Hickman who is on the Maricopa County Board of Supervisors. Just earlier this month, an Iowa man was arrested for threatening to hang Clint Hickman. Clint Hickman owns the local ag industry here before he ran as a Conservative Republican who voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to serve on the Maricopa County Board of Supervisors.\par \par \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , she faced this last year, a threats of being blown up. A Massachusetts man arrested here. And yet this has not stopped these individuals. Mark Fincham the Secretary of State candidate, Republican, he was at the Capitol on January 6 on the premise there. Anthony Curran, an individual who took part in that audit, the so-called audit here. He was at the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:21]\par \par \uc2 And the last thing, when we`re talking about what to control the power, Rachel, of what a potential Governor Kari Lake with supermajorities in the House and the Senate here could mean. It was just a few months ago that the Arizona Republican legislature came one vote shy of voting to arrest the Maricopa County Board of Supervisors for refusing to turn over the Maricopa County ballots from the 2020 election for their so-called audit. One vote away from calling for the arrest of these individuals.\par \par \uc2 These individuals have their targets on their back. And that is why here you see tonight, not only barbed wire, but fencing, deputies, drones up in the air to ensure that this area is protected. As you can see, we`re talking about thousands of votes separating these highly contested races.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard for us in Phoenix. Vaughn, thank you very, very much for that. It`s really -- I mean, it`s really helpful to have you there. But it`s also really helpful to have that context. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 You know, Alex, you were talking about this last night that was presented - - we`re entering into an interregnum. That could be a very dangerous period. And with so much attention focused on Arizona with so many important races, the Senate race, obviously, the governor`s race, that Secretary of State race.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I mean, Mark Finchem, who was there at the attack on the Capitol on January 6, couldn`t be the new Secretary of State. I mean, look, those are the results right now in that race. It`s too early to call. Arizona becoming the crucible here. It feels like a place that`s particularly ill- suited to handle the pressure well.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes. I mean, I think we also need to listen to what Kari Lake is saying, right? This is a woman who knows she well may be installed as the governor. She may actually win this thing. And she`s focusing her commentary right now on the system, that the system, the corrupt system needs to be fixed. And we should pay attention to that. Because if she wins, that`s what she`s going to do.\par \par \uc2 She`s going to make sure at least if we`re, you know, listening to what she`s saying, she`s not going to say the governor`s race was fraudulent because she won it. But as governor, you can bet that she will want to install loyalists or some kind of operatives that she feels will be malleable to Republican wins. At least that`s what I`m gleaning from. The fact that she is focusing on the corruption of the system and not the vote in and of itself when it comes to the gubernatorial race.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And I think we need to -- we need to think about -- the reason we have to talk about Kari Lake -- we used to have this conversation in our team. I don`t know. You guys probably do it in your team meetings. Why do we have to keep talking about Trump? Like, can`t we just let it go, right? We don`t want to talk about it anymore.\par \par \uc2 And the reason we have to talk about people who are actually dangerous to the system is their core danger in their ability to gather around them as sort of cult following. That`s why Trump is dangerous, right? He wasn`t particularly good at anything president -- being president, running casinos, anything. But he gathered around him a cult.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake is dangerous because she`s actually really good as a presenter. You know, she sort of looks like she always has Snapchat filter on. She never seen without, you know, complete filtration. But she also knows how to communicate. She`s -- she used to be a journalist. She was a local news anchor. And so, she actually knows how to present the information that she`s giving in a telegenic sort of pro -- you know, sort of communicative way that actually I think makes her pretty dangerous.\par \par \uc2 If she were to become the governor of that state -- Arizona is already the heart of crazytown when it comes to these, you know, lack of belief in the system, not believing elections should matter, all the conspiracies. Remember the cyber ninjas? That`s where they were. If she was actually leading that state, I think that would be incredibly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I mean, there`s a number of different levels here that we have to think about this. One is what she`d be like as governor. One is what happens if she loses the governor`s race. And one is the very immediate concern about what happens right now while we`re waiting will this -- with this kind of rhetoric basically holding the day in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Kari Lake was one of the people \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had in mind when he said democracy is on the ballot. He meant in that particular ballot choice of that name, and Mastriano, and many others. But if these -- in the key races where there -- where it`s on the ballot are governor and secretary of state, and in some cases attorney general, but certainly governor.\par \par \uc2 And so, that is exactly what he was worried about in that speech. And you know, what`s so interesting about the moment we`re in now, which is the day after, is that Kari Lake is one of those people who has been suggesting for a while that you really shouldn`t be counting any ballots after midnight on election day. You shouldn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Stop the count.\par \par \uc2 REID: Stop the count.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, those are all the fake ballots. Don`t count those.\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: She said it as soon as yesterday. She said as soon as yesterday. It upset the Maricopa County Recorder so much so that the Republican Maricopa County Recorder put out a statement explaining the process to people working with the Supervisor of Elections talking about this is how the votes will be counted.\par \par \uc2 I think we have to give some credit to the local election officials who are Republicans who have stood up in the face of what is just truly --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Oh, yes. They set in the fire.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: -- nasty business happening in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I think actually one of the things that -- one of the heartening things of last night as we look at these races and particularly the ones that are the hardest core election deniers, the Michigan Secretary of State candidate, right, who loses by 14 points. Finchem now running quite far behind. We`ll see. That race is early to call. But a bunch of them going down in those contested races.\par \par \uc2 Again, is it like, the play from Biden is that there is a pro-democracy majority in America. And it includes members of the Republican Party as you see in the testimony of \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  when he came before the January 6 Committee and said I refuse to defy my oath to my God and the Constitution to hand over the election against the will of the people. And then the -- and the genuinely courageous work being done by the Republican officials in the Maricopa County Board who have stood up against this and have done the right thing. And like that has been the bulwark in many cases.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:46]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And it is -- you know, we honor their heroism and their bravery in standing up against it and also rue the fact that it should take physical courage to work in an American election.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And barriers and panic buttons.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That`s right. That`s right. All right, we`re still waiting on that new vote from Maricopa County that Steve has been setting us up for. That`s Maricopa County, Arizona where we`ve got outstanding races for Senate and Governor and Secretary of State. Lots to come. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Welcome back to day two of our coverage of this wild election with so many races still undecided, with a lot of vote expected to come in over the course of this next hour. While we`ve been waiting to find out who ultimately will control Congress and the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , one of the things that`s flown more under the radar is that Democrats are having a phenomenally good election at the state level in state legislatures around the country.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania Democrats think they have done it there. They think they have gained control of the House in Pennsylvania for the first time in more than a decade. If that holds up, Pennsylvania Democrats will have much more leverage when it comes to enacting the policy agenda of the incoming Democratic governor there, \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, it`s more stark than that. This was the first election in Michigan since the districts there were drawn up by a bipartisan citizens commission instead of just in a partisan scrum. Look what happened. Michigan Democrats flipped the state house and they flipped the state senate for the first time in 40 years. And because Michigan Democrats also kept the governorship when \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  was reelected, that means they`re going to have a governing trifecta. Democrats will be in charge in Michigan for a change. They`ll have all major statewide offices and the state legislature both houses.\par \par \uc2 Also in Minnesota, Democrats in Minnesota kept their majority in the state house. And they flipped the state senate away from the Republicans. Because in Minnesota, they`ve also kept the governorship, Democrats will now be in charge in Minnesota as well, fully in charge of state government. When I say Democrats at the state level had been having a really good election, I don`t just mean where they have been on offense like in those states I just described. Also, it`s true on for them in places where they were playing defense, frankly.\par \par \uc2 Check out what happened in Wisconsin where Republican lawmakers have been kept in check just barely. This time around Wisconsin Republicans set their sights on trying to get a veto-proof supermajority in the state legislature, which is -- which would essentially render the Democratic governor there irrelevant. They did not get their supermajority in Wisconsin despite the fact that that state is gerrymandered beyond the point of living democracy.\par \par \uc2 They fell short by two seats, thanks to excellent defense played by the Democrats in Wisconsin. It was even closer in North Carolina. Republican lawmakers there thought they would also get a veto-proof supermajority, which again, would render the Democratic governor there effectively irrelevant. North Carolina Republicans did get that in the state senate but they were stopped in the state house.\par \par \uc2 Republicans fell short in the State House by one vote by the slimmest of margins. North Carolina Democrats were able to hold the line there. That is why when you say -- if we say you got to watch every race, we really do mean every single race.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to talk about Michigan for a second just because last night the first thing that happened -- and I think this -- I think Florida`s early count East Coast thing has this weird effect on election night perception.\par \par \uc2 REID: It`s like a drug.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It comes back so early. You know, we get the results early and it`s been trending Republican. Last night, if there was a Florida of the Democrats, it was Michigan.\par \par \uc2 REID: 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And if there`s a governor like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for Democrats in terms of political acumen, it`s \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . Now, there are a lot of things that went into that happening. It`s not just Whitmer. This is a -- this is a tight state. This is a swing state. Whitmer wins by 10, 11 points, it looks like. They sweep the statewide offices. They win both houses.\par \par \uc2 And this is a governor in \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  who was targeted by the right from day one. She had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looking at her. She had an -- she had a plot busted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to literally kidnap her. They had armed people showing up at the state house.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And at her -- and at her --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: At her house when they were -- and she stuck to her guns. She never lost sight of where the electorate was. She has governed in a progressive fashion but has also been a broadly appealing figure despite how much they have tried to polarize her. And I just think the performance of Michigan Democrats and Whitmer in particular last night securing that trifecta was one of the most impressive overnight.\par \par \uc2 REID: Thank you for saying that. I mean, I -- we said this in our little call today on the -- on the -- for the show that I agree with you. I think that she has been eclipsed. People want to talk about DeSantis but I consider her to be the big winner last night. She now should be in the presidential conversation if Biden doesn`t run.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:15]\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: You know, Big Gretch as they call her down there. She even had like a rap at the end. That was like really pretty good. I don`t know why Ari Melber is not here to talk about it with me. But I mean, she`s emerged as a really popular governor, you know, as a as a woman, as somebody who I think should be a national figure who should be much more in the conversation that we`re generally tending to have about --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Symone, I saw you nodding there. Obviously, as somebody who`s very recently been playing a role in national politics, national Democratic messaging on these things, what`s the view of Democrats running the table in a state like Michigan? What`s the view of the importance not just of the policy stuff that can happen with those legislative controls, but what that means in terms of politics?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, I think it`s -- the investment is key here. And you cannot underestimate the effect of Roe. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  did a number of events. She had these T-shirts that said Roevember. She did a debate wearing a Roevember T-shirt. She was a governor that ran on the issue of abortion. The \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2  will tell you that they spent upwards of more than $20 million on abortion this cycle which made a difference.\par \par \uc2 I also think we got to get some credit to the Democratic legislative campaign committee. They usually get no money and they didn`t get any in this cycle from the DNC.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Even though Jamie Harrison gave money elsewhere, they didn`t get any from the DNC, but they raised their own funds. They were facing headwinds in Colorado, Maine, Vermont, like you name it, and they held the line. And that is a testament to organizing into a laser focus from Jessica post and her team on the state house.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We should also say, just on that point, in Michigan specifically, there was a ballot measure on abortion which won in a -- which was to enshrine abortion rights in the state constitution. While Democrats ran the table in Michigan, that abortion measure also won in a landslide. Like, it won by 14 points in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: We also need to know -- like, understanding the big picture of what`s happening. After 2010, Republicans set their sights on state legislatures. They focus on down ballot races because they understand that states are the laboratories of democracy. We are finally at the point where Democrats equally understand that lesson. And the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is writing cover stories about the attempt in Wisconsin to gain a supermajority in the state legislature. We all are now very awoke to the idea.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: But Democrats still have not put money behind it.\par \par \uc2 REID: No. That`s what I say we have -- to that point --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I think voters understand it in a way that they have -- they have not. And it`s talked about in the national media and it isn`t important part of election day.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I think the voters understand. I think the Democratic Party apparatus has yet to wake up to figure out how, like what -- you have to do more than just talk about it, you have to put money in infrastructure around it. And you have to knock doors specifically for say, legislative races.\par \par \uc2 REID: And by the way, ironically, the Republicans like culture wars over things like critical race theory, over things like what kids could be taught in school about LGBT. You know, all of that culture war stuff has hyper-focused a lot of Democrats on states and on how important it is they vote all the way down the ballot. I think the messaging on that -- and honestly, to come back to Roe again, women really came to understand and learn this very simple civic lesson that now your state essentially controls whether you have freedom. Whether you have bodily autonomy depends on who runs your state, that means your governor, that means your, you know, your lawmakers in your office, it means prosecutors in your office. It means the state legislatures and the state representatives. You need to control who`s controlling.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Unless Republicans get total control in Washington again, in which case we will have --\par \par \uc2 REID: Then, it will be national.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We`ll have the national federal ban.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One last point. Just whenever we --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Which by constitutional thing that happened in California makes a difference.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The last point about just since we raised Wisconsin, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  is going to win that race by about two and a half points. The state voted for the Democratic governor by two and a half points. They almost got supermajorities in both houses. That`s -- think about that gerrymander. They were too --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Slow down on that though. So, what I`m saying is the Democratic governor --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The people of Wisconsin came out and said, who do we want to govern this state? And by two points, the people of Wisconsin said we would like the Democrat to govern our state. And when those votes were fed into the maps, the Republicans themselves had written what it spat out was near supermajorities in both the house and the senate.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: For the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For the Republicans with the same votes that got \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  elected by a good margin.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: They have rigged the maps in Wisconsin. It is the most gerrymandered state in the country.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes, it is. And in states where the gerrymandering process has been undone so that it does not advantage either party, that is what happened in Michigan. And what happened, Democrats ran the table with ungerrymandered maps with --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: The map --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes, it turns out that people in Michigan wanted Democrat to be in charge. And when asked to do so in a fair process, a transparent nonpartisan process, that was the result. That is what Republicans are so afraid of when it comes to giving up on gerrymandering.\par \par \uc2 Now, I have to tell you -- I don`t have anything definitive to tell you but I do have to tell you a bunch of votes just came in. At least that`s what I have been advised. You see how Steve has hunched over.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That`s Steve receiving numbers.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:06]\par \par \uc2 REID: How much do you love the Kornacki camp. We can just feed them all the time.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You see his tail feathers go up like that. And then you know, he`s going to hop into the hollow of that tree. And what`s going to pop out is going to be information.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: So, it`s -- you`re not -- any second now you`re going to see this populate in the border. You certainly should. But this Maricopa County vote batch that I`ve been talking about, we did just get 62,000 new votes out of Maricopa County. And I can tell you how they broke.\par \par \uc2 Mark Kelly, the Democrat gets 33,842 of them and Blake Masters, the Republican, gets 26,521 of them. So, it breaks. This update here is -- that is 55 percent of this new batch is Democratic vote. And 43 percent of this new batch is Republican vote. So, again, I`m just waiting to see this populate in our screen. But what that will essentially do as you can see is that will add to Mark Kelly`s lead, you know, about 7,321 votes. That will add to his weed in Maricopa County and statewide. I`m just waiting to see if it if it takes in here.\par \par \uc2 This is from the Maricopa County Recorders site. That`s the source I`m using for this, sO they reported directly up on there. It should come into our system. But the significance of the --here it is. OK. So, now you see the new total and now it`s a 92,000 vote. Plus, we add a Maricopa County. What does that do to the statewide number? It brings it up.\par \par \uc2 Remember where it was at this -- when we came on the air an hour ago. Now it`s back into the mid-90s, 95,000 for Mark Kelly. Take a look at what it does in the governor`s race as well because it`s a similar -- we`re waiting on the update there actually. But it in the Senate race, 95,318 votes. That is now Mark Kelly`s lead. So, remember we said the significance of this vote was the Democrats were hoping this would be a Democratic vote that would allow Kelly to pad his lead.\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, the total number from this kind of pool that we just got votes from, there`s 136,000. This is what county officials told us. They said there`s 136,000 ballots that were returned Friday, Saturday, Sunday, or Monday. And again, what you`re seeing reported out here is about 62,000. So, it would appear that it is not the entirety of that late, you know, weekend, slash Monday vote. It would appear there`s more from that bucket to go. And if the rest of that bucket breaks the same way, you know, again, that`s, you know, more ground that Kelly can gain there in terms of a padding lead.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve updated the governor`s numbers too. We can show you what that looks like with this new update. So, again, statewide, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  I think at the start of the hour, if it was this hour we started, was leading by about 3000 statewide. Between the Pima update in between what we just got in Maricopa County, that goes up to 13,000.\par \par \uc2 And so again, from Hobbs` standpoint, she would love to get more from this same bucket. As I said -- as I said, there could be 60,000-plus from the same bucket just based on what county officials have told us. And I think the name of the game for Democrats is just to get as much as they can out of that bucket, the votes that came in Friday, Saturday, Sunday, Monday. And then once all of those are added in, and we just got a huge chunk of them, probably about half of them, once all of those are added in, the big number in Maricopa County is going to be 275,000. And again, that`s 275,000 votes that were brought in on election day.\par \par \uc2 And if they`re Republican, as they were in 2020, then the Democrats are hoping that Mark Kelly`s lead is big enough to withstand the Republican, you know, sort of bent of those votes. And if they`re Democratic, like they were in 2018, then Kelly would be fine. And in the governor`s race, certainly \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  clinging to that narrow lead right now, that would obviously be key to her as well. She In fact, just given that she`s running 80,000 votes behind Mark Kelly. She probably needs this last batch of votes to be a lot more like 2018 when it was Democratic-friendly than 2020 when it was Republican-friendly.\par \par \uc2 So, Democrats do add now -- Kelly has added about 10,000 votes to his margin statewide over the last hour, again, thanks to that Pima update, and now that Maricopa update. And this is probably -- I`ll see if I can confirm this, but the way they usually do this is one update a night. This may be all we get from Maricopa County until tomorrow night. But let me see if we can get that track down for sure.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve, let me just ask you. In terms of what you did just see come in -- and we understand that it`s only a portion of what remains -- was the partisan split on that vote that we did just get the kind of partisan split that Democrats were hoping for or was it an underwhelming number in terms of it being 55 percent roughly a Democratic vote?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, I think it`s helpful then because if you`ve use as a point of reference in 2020 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  used that final batch of votes that he`s talking about to nearly erased Joe Biden`s lead, that was basically bringing it down from 90,000 to about 9700. So, that`s the ground Republicans were able to make up with that in 2020.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:23]\par \par \uc2 So, I think if the Democrats can get the lead, you know, in the Senate raise over 100,000 votes, you know, again, it`s a real wild card here because we`ve seen that late arriving that final batch have two completely different characteristics from a partisan standpoint in the past two elections. So, I mean, if it ends up being -- sorry, what`s that? We may have some new Nevada vote. We can -- we can take a look at here too. So, really, it`s a big variable what that final vote is going to look like. But certainly, Kelly with much more of a pad that you could see withstanding a late Republican advance than Hobbs has in the governor`s race.\par \par \uc2 And in Nevada, we do have new vote that just came in. And I think this was from Clark County. Let me just check. It was from Clark. Yes, OK, it was from Clark County. So, we said we were expecting about 14-15,000 votes, something along those lines at Clark County. And that`s what we just got reported out here. Cortez Masto picks up 9158 votes. It looks like she adds 9158 from where she was. And Laxalt adds 4269 votes from where he was.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, a two to one -- better than two-to-one advantage in these votes that were just reported. Again, this I think -- this was a combination of mail that had arrived Monday, and that drop box ballots in Clark County that it arrived Monday. Democrats -- this is an encouraging sign I think for Democrats in that they`re counting on specially drop box ballots being, you know, disproportionately Democratic.\par \par \uc2 From this pool here for about a little under 14,000 votes, you`re getting a better than two-to-one advantage for the Democrats. So, again, what that does in Clark County, what that does statewide, call that up, would have been about a 22-23,000 vote deficit for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 . Now, it cuts down to 17,800. As we said, the number of outstanding votes in Clark County at the start of the evening was approximately 85,000. Now, with this new update in there, it`s closer -- it`s closer to 70,000 that are left -- great handwriting, Steve. It`s close to 70,000 that are left.\par \par \uc2 And we still have 61,000 mail ballots in Washoe County. They`ve said there`ll be an update. They expect an update tonight, but they don`t expect a bigger significant update tonight. But what Democrats are hoping for again, is there were 56,000 ballots in Clark County that were dropped off in the drop boxes on election day that are uncounted. And certainly, they`re hoping they look -- if they look anything like we just showed you in that sample of 15,000 or so, that`s a very -- it could be a very significant gain for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  in terms of trying to catch Laxalt statewide.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve, I`m going to ask you a process question because I know you`re also in touch with elections officials in terms of when votes are expected and from where and what kind. In your experience doing this, is there anything that seems to you strange or off or atypical or worrying at all in terms of the way votes are being processed, the pace the pace at which the vote is happening? I only ask this because there is still conspiracy theorizing particularly on the right that there may be something wrong with this vote coming in in the way that it is. Does it seem like a fairly typical process to you in these two states?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: I mean, yes, in these two states -- and I know it`s frustrating, believe me, it`s frustrating for me that it comes in so slowly. And it may be there`s -- I know this gets all twisted up in politics and in conspiracy theories part. I do wonder if there`s a discussion to be had if both parties could put aside -- this is an ideal world I`m describing here where they could just kind of put aside all their -- all their differences and talk just kind of rationally about what are common sense election procedures that could kind of be done state to state.\par \par \uc2 Because we do see states like Florida, for instance, where you know, last night -- it`s a huge state. They got tons of early vote, they get tons of mail vote, they get tons of same-day vote and they managed to get it all reported out in the same night. And there are some things that are done in Florida, and it`s been done under Democrats, Republicans in that state to create what -- you know, they had an election crisis in Florida in 2000. That presidential election was such a disaster in terms of election administration.\par \par \uc2 And I think what Florida as a state has done in the two decades since then has produced -- you know, we watch it every two years. We watched, you know, the votes come through in Florida in a very efficient, very digestible way. And maybe there are some lessons there that could be learned by other states, but I`m probably just thinking in too idealistic - -\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Technocratic Steve is my favorite Steve, actually, I have to say.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221110eib90008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC LIVE for November 09, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Joy Reid, Jacob Soboroff, Vaughn Hillyard, Michael Steele \par \uc2 8,755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [18:00:22]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good evening, friends. We begin this hour with the Republican and pollster promised red wave that turned out to be more of a red wheeze. Voters across the country rejected election denial and Donald Trump`s handpicked candidates, giving Democrats an historic showing for the party that holds the White House, something President Biden addressed a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Democrats had a strong night, and we lost fewer seats in the House of Representatives than any Democratic president`s first midterm election in the last 40 years. And we had the best midterm for governors since 1986.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REID: It was an especially strong night for Democrats in governor`s races, retaining seats in three critical battlegrounds ahead of 2024, with \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  winning in Pennsylvania and voters reelecting \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in Michigan and \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  in Wisconsin. Democrats also made history by flipping two additional gubernatorial seats that were held by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Wes Moore elected as Maryland`s first black governor. And in Massachusetts, \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  will become the nation`s first lesbian governor, an historical fact that Florida kids probably can`t legally learn in school.\par \par \uc2 One result was absolutely definitive in three states with referendums on abortion, voters chose to enshrine abortion rights. And Kentucky rejected an anti-abortion amendment with an outright rebuke of Republicans` forced birth extremism.\par \par \uc2 As foresaid Republicans, it was a night of shock and tears of election parties that ended early and with the crudites going cold. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his celebrity candidates wondering where it all went wrong. Well, today a major upset is brewing in Colorado as MAGA`s second famous congresswoman, \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , trails in her race for a second term.\par \par \uc2 And we still don`t know for sure who will control the House of Representatives. It`s down to a handful of seats, and it will be a while before we know about control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  which hangs on three states.\par \par \uc2 NBC News projects Georgia`s race for the Senate is headed to a runoff. Democratic incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  will face off again in December after neither candidate secured 50 percent for an outright victory. In Nevada the contest between Democratic incumbent \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is still too early to call. And the count also continues in Arizona where the race between Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly and Trump picked Blake Masters also remains too close to call.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now from Las Vegas, Nevada, is NBC News correspondent Jacob Soboroff.\par \par \uc2 We enjoyed you in the mall last night where you were there. I think you might have gotten a few treats.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Joy.\par \par \uc2 REID: While you were there and some free food, but we`re not going to talk about that right now. Talk about where we stand as far as this vote count is concerned.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I`m dieting this morning.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes, in all seriousness I mean I think it`s fair to say that given the race and the circumstances, that control of the Senate could be decided in this building behind me. This is the election headquarters of the Clark County Registrar of Voters. His name is Joe Gloria. And all the ballots from the Las Vegas area, all throughout Clark County which is the biggest election jurisdiction in the state, coming to this building and they`re all going to be tabulated.\par \par \uc2 At the bottom line, Joy, I think everybody needs to know is that we`re not going to know what`s happening in Nevada, at least from Clark County tomorrow, nor the next day, nor the next day. He said it could be as long as Friday November 18th. And that`s not because they`re slow counters. That`s not because they`re bad at their job. Statutorily state law dictates that they have a process to ensure a fair and accurate and reliable and safe vote.\par \par \uc2 And so that the process they`re going through right now. All the in-person election day votes have been counted. All the early in-person votes have been counted. But those mail-in ballots have not all been counted yet, provisional ballots have not been counted yet. And some of the ballots that are questionable, like the ones where the signatures don`t match or provisional ballots, those ones haven`t been counted yet. So it`s going to be a bit of a process but they`re going step by step. And he guarantees everybody that`s going to get done is going to get done accurately.\par \par \uc2 REID: I feel like I have to ask you this in the era that we live in Jacob. What are the atmospherics around that building where you`re standing, security measures? Is it tense? Have you seen anything because, you know, we know that these election deniers don`t just talk a good game, they sometimes show up armed.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes, you know what, Joy, it`s a wonderful question because two years ago you remember \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  was out here having that press conference with \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  and Ric Grenell alleging that thousands of illegitimate ballots were cast in the presidential election here.\par \par \uc2 [18:05:03]\par \par \uc2 And I chased all those guys to their van and they sped away without answering any questions. There were a lot of -- I don`t want to call it dangerous necessarily but antagonistic forces out here in 2020 after the election. Because of that there`s an additional security presence here. There`s a police watch tower with a camera that goes back to Clark County Sheriff`s Department. They`re taking it very seriously out here.\par \par \uc2 And I`m happy to say it is certainly not as antagonistic as it was. We haven`t had any challenging of the votes even though Laxalt was an election denier-in-chief out here in Nevada last time around. But there`s a lot of time yet they have to count the votes and for people to come out here and challenge them, Laxalt in particular.\par \par \uc2 REID: One of the best in the business, Jacob Soboroff, thank you very much. Keep us posted. Raise your hand if anything develops while you`re there. Thank you, man.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Thanks, my friend.\par \par \uc2 REID: Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 So joining us now from Phoenix, Arizona, another one of our great colleagues, NBC News correspondent Vaughn Hillyard.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn, what are you seeing behind you in the very active building?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right. Joy, we`re here at the Maricopa County tabulation center here where these county officials are actively processing and tabulating these mail-in votes as we speak here. In less than two hours from now we`re going to get the latest batch drop. When you look at this governor`s race there`s the reality that \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is up by just about over 4,000 votes over Kari Lake where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 So what do we expect from this batch just two hours from now? We are expecting the mail votes that came into county offices on Saturday, Sunday, and even Monday. Now what Democrats want to see in these results is \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  frankly expand her lead, but also Mark Kelly expand his lead over Blake Masters. That will be important for them here because you have seen early votes mailed in continually over the past weeks favored Democrats. That`s why you saw in those initial results yesterday Mark Kelly and \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  had significant leads.\par \par \uc2 So that needs to take place because tomorrow night is going to be the real big question, Joy. Those ballots that are going to be released are going to be mail ballots that voters didn`t mail back, many of them because they`re skeptical of the mail-in voting system, but chose to hand deliver them to polling locations instead. That is expected to vote more Republican. That is what happened in 2020 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  nearly was able to eclipse \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  here in the state.\par \par \uc2 So that is why for Democrats tonight`s ballot drop is going to be so significant because it`ll raise the stakes of what is to come here tomorrow, Joy.\par \par \uc2 REID: And Vaughn, I have to ask you the same question. Thank you for that detail. What are the atmospherics in the state where you are? Because we know Arizona was home to the Cyber Ninjas. Election denialism is kind of based in Arizona in a lot of ways. What are the atmospherics there? What are the security measures being taken to keep the people who are counting those ballots safe?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: I mean, this office where we are right now, this tabulation center has been at the forefront of the conspiracy theories and the so- called audits over the last two years. You know, it was just a couple of blocks down the road where the folks who did the geolocation data that ultimately turned into "2000 Mules," held a hearing and calling into question this county`s ballot drop box operations here.\par \par \uc2 So what you have seen over the course of the last 48 hours was the county essentially set up fencing, barbed wires and other barriers here. They also have deputies. They have drones in use around here. To note, we have not seen any indications of violence or individuals who have been stalking the premises. At the same time we should note here there`s a lot of questions about where this election can go in these days ahead.\par \par \uc2 And it was Kari Lake last night when she was still down by multiple percentage points who was calling out these so-called, what her own words were the cheaters and the crooks. Of course she made no names or no explicit suggestion of who those individuals were, but she has targeted over the last 24 hours the Maricopa County officials, suggesting wrongdoing and a lack of honest elections.\par \par \uc2 Of course the issues at the polling locations yesterday, there is no evidence that any of it was nefarious or intentional. Yet you saw \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and other statewide officials here and of course Kari Lake and Mark Finchem really take a hard line against these Republicans who run these county operations and from all accounts run an honest and fair election here. Joy?\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes, indeed. Thank you for all of that detail. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard, thank you very much. Really appreciate all of that detail and great reporting.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Michael Steele, former chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  and my friend.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: And thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Can I just say?\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Whew.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Can we just take a what, what?\par \par \uc2 REID: Dude, dude, what, what, I mean, listen, listen. I think last night really I think was a good night for America in the world.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: It was. Yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: As the world looks at us we conducted an election that was free and fair. There was no violence. I`m very thankful there was no violence around the country, which is sad that we have to say that about our country, right?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: I know. Well, look, you know, as Vaughn was just mentioning, you know, Kari Lake is out there spewing bile and recriminations before the first tally comes in the door.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [18:10:07]\par \par \uc2 STEELE: So you still have to be on guard against these soldiers of disruption and soldiers of insurrection. And, you know, the people, I think, reached a point, Joy, where they said, you know what, I just can`t go there.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: I can`t go there.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: And that`s a good thing for now.\par \par \uc2 REID: And I have to say that, you know, among young white voters under 30 apparently now can come to the cookout because they actually voted with the majority of the country to say, you know, we don`t want to do this. They actually voted.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Well, look, let`s give them props because they did. You know, we extolled them to come and produce.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes. Young voters came out.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: And they did.\par \par \uc2 REID: You know, not just young white voters, young voters, period.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: They did.\par \par \uc2 REID: Voters under 30 really showed out, and that was one of the things that helped Democrats. What we just heard in the reporting, though, from -- both from Vaugh Hillyard and from Jacob, let`s start with Nevada. We`re in a situation right now where \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  has to make up votes. Laxalt is leading her by about 22,000 votes. Here`s where the risky bit comes in. As those votes come in, if those votes flip this from being a Laxalt lead -- it`s not a Laxalt victory. It`s a lead at the moment when they haven`t counted all the votes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 REID: That`s just normal.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: It`s normal. Yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: And then if \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  winds up pulling this off that`s where we have one bucket of potential problems from people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and election deniers, and you just mentioned the same thing holds true in Arizona, even worse because that`s like election denial central. What risk do you feel we have of potential, you know, I hate to say it, but violence?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: No, I think there is a real risk there. And there is -- there are a number of groups that couldn`t quite figure out what to do with last night, you know.\par \par \uc2 REID: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: And they were like, do we go out and do we protest, do we stir up trouble or -- because they were planning for one thing and the American people did something very different.\par \par \uc2 REID: That`s correct. Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: So here`s I think part of the secret sauce for this period until next Friday when presumably all of the numbers will have been processed through between those two states.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Just keep talking about it and telling people this is part of the process, this is normal.\par \par \uc2 REID: Normal. Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Don`t get upset, don`t get alarmed, the numbers can go back and forth because between now and Friday or now and Saturday.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: You know, Maricopa County`s going to be producing these numbers. But between now, you know, and next Monday or next Tuesday, you`re going to see these kind of results coming out of Nevada.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: The more we inform people, and I think this was very important, you were among many of the hosts on our network making sure that people understood as we get ready for the election on Tuesday you all need to be aware of what this --\par \par \uc2 REID: Calm (INAUDIBLE). It`s good. It`s fine.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right. This is what this looks like. And I think it`s a matter of informing and re-educating people of what happens in elections.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes. Yes. Just explaining elections. It`s so important.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Explaining it without secrets, you know, crazy.\par \par \uc2 REID: And it`s not a partisan thing.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: It`s not.\par \par \uc2 REID: It`s literally a thing just that this is just how votes are counted. I wanted to ask you about the incentive system. Chris Hayes, you know, made a really good point last night that one of the things that we learned last night is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he ain`t really great at picking candidates.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: No.\par \par \uc2 REID: And he actually might be -- there might be a tax on you for being associated with Trump. Right? I mean, you could argue, OK, JD Vance, but JD Vance was calling him maybe Hitler and then he ran to him and sucked up to him as his opponent Tim Ryan said at the last minute.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right.\par \par \uc2 REID: But he was really Peter Thiel`s candidate.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Not Trump`s, right.\par \par \uc2 REID: Donald Trump`s candidates didn`t do very well, OK, let`s just be clear.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right.\par \par \uc2 REID: Do you think -- you know these people. These Republicans like the Lindsey Grahams who have insisted Trump is the future, we have to follow Trump into the gates of hell, we have to keep following him. Do you see any movement in that incentive structure to stick with him and that he`s letting him run them?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Honestly no. Until -- unless and until someone whether it`s DeSantis or someone else --\par \par \uc2 REID: Trump will destroy -- Trump versus DeSantis, (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 STEELE: I 