ale" fantasies of making the \uc2 Gilead\uc2  real. Even in heavily Republican Kentucky, voters rejected a ballot measure aimed at denying any constitutional protections for abortion.\par \par \uc2 California, Vermont and battleground Michigan enshrined abortion rights in their state constitutions while in Montana voters were asked whether to require medical care for infants born alive after an attempted abortion, even though that`s not how abortion works and not a thing that ever happens. NBC News projects the referendum will not pass.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 , Democratic political strategist and CEO of TargetSmart, and \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I`m going to have \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2  pause on yelling I told you so for the next six minutes, and I`m going to go to you first, Alexis. What does this mean for the protection of abortion rights, last night`s vote?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 , PLANNED PARENTHOOD PRESIDENT AND CEO: Look, we told you so, too.\par \par \uc2 REID: You did. Actually you did.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MCGILL JOHNSON: We did. We told you that when abortion was on the ballot people will vote for freedom, and that`s what we saw again and again not only on the ballot measures, but also, you know, we recognize that abortion also made these midterms more competitive. And I think it`s really competitive. I cross the country along with Laphonza Butler from Emily`s List and Mini Timmaraju from Naral, as a collective of movement leaders making this case.\par \par \uc2 And everywhere we went we saw the energy. We heard the stories. We knew that people were going to show up because this was the first time other than Kansas that they had an opportunity to really demonstrate their outrage, their frustration, quite frankly their betrayal from the Supreme Court overturning Roe. And that`s what we saw last night up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes, and Tom, you know you saw something in the data that not a lot of people I guess saw or maybe didn`t want to see. Can you just talk about that just for a moment? Because you were on our show a few times and said look, nothing has changed, the data hasn`t changed over time. What were you seeing in the data that some of the polling averages were missing?\par \par \uc2 Yes, Joy, I don`t know how they missed it. You have been reporting on this from the beginning but going back to the Dobbs decision. You know, Justice Alito said if women don`t like this, they should vote. Right?\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TOM BONIER\uc2 , TARGETSMART CEO: And that`s what they did. We saw it in Kansas. As you reported. We saw it in these surges in voter registration, women registering at record setting rates, big gender gaps. And then we saw in the early vote, and there was only one place where we weren`t seeing it, and it was these polls, these absurd polls that were suggesting somehow that women have forgotten when in the end the data was showing otherwise, and frankly and not coincidently, the states where we saw the biggest gender gaps in registration, the states where Democrats have had the biggest victories. We talk about Michigan and Pennsylvania, those are two big ones.\par \par \uc2 REID: Well, and, you know, you and Simon Rosenberg, I have to say, we had you guys on. Obviously we`re ping-ponging the two of you because you were saying the same things and I just found it really interesting. You know, and, you know, Alexis, one of the things we talked about last night, and, you know, it was a point that Nicolle made, Nicolle Wallace made, that I think is really smart is that one of the things that you can`t see in polls is when women are deleting the app they used to the watch their periods.\par \par \uc2 And I know a lot of women who are doing that. You don`t see it in the polls if women are hesitating on encouraging their daughters to go to school in Texas, to go to college in Texas or in, you know, South Carolina because they`re worried that if they`re in a state like Georgia, these states with these mass abortion bans, and God forbid something happened to them, they will then be trapped or, you know, or pregnant women thinking I need to move out of Texas.\par \par \uc2 [18:25:15]\par \par \uc2 That`s the kind of stuff that`s not pollable. So do you think -- give us some advice, or give some advice to people who really still want to do these data kind of before these elections. How can they be talking to women differently so they understand the issues that are important?\par \par \uc2 MCGILL JOHNSON: Well, you know, one of the things that we saw on the ground in Michigan in particular when we went and sat in round tables of, you know, in Detroit and in, you know, suburban independent, you know, largely Republican communities was how consistent the fear and the stories were, that people were expressing, you know, that their pregnancies had all been different and unique as they all are.\par \par \uc2 And their concerns about what would happen if they went back to 1931 law and the stories that were coming out of Texas where patients were literally being sent almost into sepsis in order to get abortion care. So all of those stories I think have been incredibly real. And what we saw, you know, yesterday, Joy, there was a story of a young Michigan woman who literally voted on her hospital bed while she was eight centimeters dilated because she wanted to make sure her voice was heard.\par \par \uc2 They had an emergency ballot rushed into her. But she wanted to get her vote in for Governor Whitmer and for Proposition 3. It was just that important. And so I think you`re right, we don`t see that in the polling, and I think we defied the polls with the actual abortion voters but at the same time I think there`s an opportunity to really sit down with people. That`s actually how I think all of these ballot measures past.\par