pendents who are more likely to cross over and vote pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 REID: You know, and Alexis, it is kind of frightening. I felt when I was in Texas before the election in Fort Worth, when I left it really hit me that there are free states and there are sort of looming slave states now. Right? That there are two Americas for women. And you know, those of us who live in states that are modern and think women should have rights over their bodies, it`s easy to take it for granted that so many of our sisters in east states, mainly in the south or in Missouri and these other places, they`re in trouble. What do we do?\par \par \uc2 MCGILL JOHNSON: You`re absolutely right. And I actually think that that is, you know, the contrast that we were able to draw so clearly during this election cycle, right, to be able to -- you know, because not only were many of these states, these 18 states also banning access to abortion so quickly post-Dobbs, they`ve also introduced other extreme measures like, you know, right to travel across state boundaries.\par \par \uc2 So literally saying a state could hold you hostage in your state so that you couldn`t get the health care that you need. And I think that actually really made the difference. So even in a state whereas as heartbreaking as a loss in North Carolina to not have Cheri Beasley coming into the Senate, I do think that her ability to raise this issue and have the conversation is what made that race as close as it was and made it competitive, and I think we need to continue to demonstrate and draw these contrasts with these candidates who have been very clear that they`re going to sign up for a national ban and do things that are even more extreme, and people just do not want government making these decisions for them.\par \par \uc2 [18:30:00]\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes, and I think that`s one of the reasons Democrats are in good position to hold the Senate. A lot of women woke up and said wait a minute, you don`t -- you want to put them in control. The Senate then a national enforcement would come immediately and don`t think they`re not coming for contraception to because they`ve already said they are, and they are.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2  and \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 . Up next, voters in Pennsylvania, they said no to Christian nationalism and to Trump`s hand- picked quack T.V. doctor. Quack, quack, quack. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:35:10]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I`m proud of what we ran on. Protecting a woman`s right to choose, raising our minimum wage, fighting the union way of life. Health care is a fundamental human right. It saved my life, and it should all be there for you when you ever should need it.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Tonight, you the good people of Pennsylvania, you one, opportunity one, a woman`s right to choose one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REID: Well, the red trickle looked more like a drought in Pennsylvania where Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  defeated celebrity, Dr. Mehmet Oz, in a pivotal Senate race flipping a key seat previously held by a Republican. Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  defeated far-right Christian nationalists and insurrection-curious Republican \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in the state`s governor`s race.\par \par \uc2 Both winners put abortion rights and democracy front and center, defeating candidates like Mastriano, who pledged to enact voting restrictions, and Oz whose campaign mocked the health of his opponent. Its bad news for Trump, who backed both Republicans in the battleground state that`s often decisive in choosing presidents.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Representative-elect Summer Lee winner of Pennsylvania`s congressional district 12 race, and Will Bunch Philadelphia Inquirer syndicated columnist and author of After the Ivory Tower Falls. Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Representative elects Lee, I want to congratulate you on your victory. I was just reading a bit about you and how -- this is that weird history is not only are you the first black woman elected to the Penn -- to the United States House of Representatives of Pennsylvania, but four years ago, you were the first black woman elected to Pennsylvania`s State House.\par \par \uc2 That seems like that`s very belated. But tell us what went into your victory. You beat a guy named Mike Doyle, who was replacing -- trying to replace a guy named Mike Doyle from the other party. Tricky, tricky on their part.\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LEE (D-PA), REPRESENTATIVE-ELECT: Yes, that -- that`s their ploy. Thank you so much for having me. First of all, I was the first black woman from Southwest P.A., which is a large portion of P.A., but I don`t want to take credit when we have a lot of sisters doing things at the statehouse.\par \par \uc2 But you know, our victory really was grassroots effort, our movement spaces that has just worked for years to really center, you know, working-class people and common sense, pieces of policy that folks care about that expands the electorate, be it movement, criminal justice reform, housing, environmental justice, especially.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve built and we`ve organized, and we`ve brought in more people. And at the end of the day, those people came on vote it.\par \par \uc2 REID: And what is your -- what do you want to do in Congress? What is your -- what are your goals?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes, I mean, I think that right now, we have such an urgent moment, right? There`s a reason why black women have never been represented from Pennsylvania. And that is such a lacking perspective in not just Congress, is lacking perspective everywhere. But when we have that urgency of lived experience, right?\par \par \uc2 Folks who actually have experienced what it`s been to be working poor, what experience -- it has experienced being in housing insecure. You know, which is bringing a different urgency. So those are the issues that I want to work on. I want to work on the issues that impact my community, first and foremost.\par \par \uc2 But be it housing justice, be really environmental justice, here, we have some of the worst air quality in the nation, I want to talk about environmental racism. I want to talk about racial justice. I want to talk about a living wage and make sure that we`re passing the ProAct so that working-class folks have rights on the job.\par \par \uc2 This is work that a lot of folks are doing. I`m here kind of as a part of the calvary but to bring in some reinforcements because we need louder voices. And we need people who are going to fight with every tool we have these days.\par \par \uc2 REID: Will Bunch you`ve been writing a lot about where we are in this country in terms of this deterioration is sort of, you know, openness to fascism and the Republican Party. Pennsylvania is such a key state, you know, because had Mastriano in particular one and Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 We would be then having a conversation about whether Pennsylvania would affirm its own state`s voters or just wipe them out and just put in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or whoever Republicans put up in 2024. What`s the significance of the Shapiro victory and the Fetterman victory?\par \par \uc2 WILL BUNCH, SYNDICATED COLUMNIST, PHILADELPHIA INQUIRER: It`s very significant Joy, you know, and it`s fitting that, you know, in the state where the Declaration of Independence and the constitution, were given birth, that this battle to defend democracy 240 years later, centered here. And I think -- I think voters really rose to the occasion, you know?\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, living here in Pennsylvania, it was palpable in the sense that democracy was on the line. It wasn`t just something that you know that you guys in D.C. in New York were talking about in the studio voters on the street, or aware, and 