 had a chance to talk to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 ? Because you guys are going to be heading to Washington together. And you both have a very similar, you know, pro the regular person for real people.\par \par \uc2 You know, somebody has said he looks like if the word union was a human, that`s what he looks like, you know, and I feel like the two of you really could do some great things together. You guys had a conversation?\par \par \uc2 LEE: I haven`t -- I haven`t yet. I`m sure that he`s probably hopefully getting some rest. But yes, we do have a lot in common, right? We actually come from representing the same small town, Braddock P.A., punching above our weight class. But yes, we`re here to bring these progressive values.\par \par \uc2 We share so many of them, we share really -- the way that we excite and bring in new people, people who hadn`t seen themselves reflected in this process, who now feel like they have a voice. So, I`m really excited to work with them. I`m excited for Western Pennsylvania, for the many folks who came out.\par \par \uc2 Some for the first time to vote for us, who are now going to see that, you know, money is not going to stop us. The fearmongering, the racist ads that were playing. You know, they threw everything they had at us. And I think that just goes to show that we`re onto something, right?\par \par \uc2 That when you come, and you are going to take on corporate interests when you`re going to work for working-class people, when you look like the textbook definition of union of working class. You know, that`s something new here.\par \par \uc2 So, we know we`re going to get the attacks, but I hope that folks watching us now know that we want to keep on fighting because we made it through this, so you can count on it.\par \par \uc2 REID: All right. Well, Representative-elect Summer Lee who I think I might see it delta days at the next -- OK, sir. Yes. Listen. You can`t stop at delta. Listen, we`d be doing this. We do this. We do this. Thank you very much. Congratulations, Summer Lee. I`ll see what delta days. And Will Bunch, thank you, as always. Always appreciate you being here.\par \par \uc2 OK, coming up next. There were historic victories all across the country last night. Maryland`s Governor-elect Wes Moore joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:48:35]\par \par \uc2 REID: History was made last night with trailblazing victories and historic firsts. In Florida, 25-year-old Democrat Maxwell Frost is the first Gen. Z. candidate elected to Congress. Massachusetts elected Democrat \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2 , the nation`s first openly lesbian governor. She`s also the state`s first female governor. A flip for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 In Vermont, Becca -- Becca Balint becomes the first woman ever to represent the state in Congress. We didn`t believe that. Democrats Summer Lee, who we just spoke with becomes the first black woman elected to Congress from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And in Maryland, it`s Governor-elect Wes Moore. Democrats other big flip last night. He`s become just the third black elected governor in U.S. history and the fourth overall and the first black governor of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WES MOORE (D-MD), GOVERNOR-ELECT: What an amazing night and what an improbable journey but you believed. You believe that in this moment our state could be bolder. It is not lost on me that I`ve made a little history might tonight myself as well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 REID: And Maryland`s Governor-elect Wes Moore joins me now. Governor-elect Moore, it is good to see you. and I -- congratulations. Let me just start by saying congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Thank you. Thank you so much. It is great to be with you. It really is.\par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 REID: I don`t know if you`re going to get to keep doing them dancing videos on your \uc2 Instagram\uc2  because you may have a lot of fun on your \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . You might have to celebrate up and do like serious things now on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  like you`re now a governor.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I`m -- I`m -- I`m not -- I`m not stopping. I`m not stopping. I`m not stopping.\par \par \uc2 REID: Let me put up for you because it is kind of wild. Like we think about American history, this country has been, you know, around for quite a while, like 246 years of history, there have only been this many black governors. We go back to Oscar Dunn, and PBS Pinchback during reconstruction. You`ve got David Paterson, who served in New York, but he wasn`t elected. He actually just served in New York.\par \par \uc2 And then in the modern era, there`s just Doug Wilder in Virginia. And \uc2 Deval Patrick\uc2  in Massachusetts. It`s a very -- you could literally count them on one hand. What does it mean to you to be the third black elected governor of -- in the United States ever?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: You know, it means a lot also, just in terms of -- in terms of my family`s history. You know, my grandfather was the first one on my mom`s side of family born in the United States. And when he was just a toddler, it was the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  that ran them out, where my great-grandfather picked up the family in the middle of night and moved them.\par \par \uc2 And much of my family always pledge to never come back to this country, and much of them did not. But my grandfather did. And he came back, he attended an HBCU. And he became the first black minister in the history of the Dutch Reformed Church. And I think about him a lot, actually, in this moment because, you know, I would ask him about what it was like being the first.\par \par \uc2 And he always was telling me, he was proud of it. But that wasn`t the assignment. That he said, you know, my job was to follow the will of God and follow the word of the Lord. And I think about in this moment, I am very proud to be the first black governor in the history of the state of Maryland. But I come in, and I say, but that`s not the assignment.\par \par \uc2 The assignment is that we actually have a unique chance to not just make history, we have a unique chance to make child poverty history, a unique chance to make the racial wealth gap history, to make environmental injustice history. And if I can do those things, then I think it truly it underscores the fact that I understood the assignment.\par \par \uc2 REID: And you know, Maryland also legalized marijuana. Is one of the things that you are thinking about doing, you know, there are a lot of people who are in jail for doing what a lot of folks in Colorado and other states are getting rich doing. People who are not look like us are getting real rich selling marijuana now. Would you consider wiping out those sentences of people who were convicted of state marijuana crimes in Maryland?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: 100 percent. We are going to focus on making sure that there is automatic record expungement. Because, you know, there`s two things that we have to think about when it comes to the fact that the state of Maryland has now moved to legalize cannabis. You know, the first is we have to make sure that there`s an equitable rollout for what this is going to look like.\par \par \uc2 That we have to ensure that -- that this is used as an opportunity to not increase a racial wealth gap, but actually decrease and close a racial wealth gap. But you`re absolutely right, Joy, I mean, the other thing that we have to remember too, is you cannot talk about the benefits of legalization. If you do not address the consequences of criminalization.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that we have people who continue to walk around with asterisks next to their name for things that is now going to be a multi-billion- dollar industry. So, we have to be serious and sincere about using our office to be able to address things like automatic record expungement for individuals.\par \par \uc2 And also -- and also being able to -- to raid our prisons and think about how we`re using this as a moment to think differently about parole probation for people who are still have cannabis convictions on their record.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes. And you defeated a election denier Mr. Cox, who was so extreme that the sitting -- the current Republican governor wouldn`t even endorse him. Did he call you to concede?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: He did. Actually a few hours ago, he -- he called, and he conceded. It was -- it was a -- it was a brief but actually a very kind call. And where he, you know, just he thanked me for the race. He congratulated me on the victory.\par \par \uc2 And we actually talked about his son, who was a -- who was a member of the 82nd Airborne Division, which is actually my former unit in the army. But I`m excited for the fact that now we are -- we are moving forward. And we`re moving forward with an intensity that I think this moment deserves.\par \par \uc2 REID: Well, and I`m sure you`re going to have great conversations with my friend, Michael Steele, who was the first black lieutenant governor of Maryland, and he`s a good guy, so hopefully you guys can chill. But I got to ask you about your mom.\par \par \uc2 I know you`re Jamerican. You got some Jamaican people in your family. But your mom`s name is Joy, which is, I mean, clearly the best name. How is how`s Miss Joy? I mean, how excited is your family? I got to ask about your family and how they`re taking your win.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I mean, my family is aesthetic. And I always say that the greatest gift that God gave me was the day that he has Joy more to give birth to me. You know, that was the greatest gift that I could have ever gotten from God. But, you know, I think about it where, you know, in this moment, you know, I wanted to remind people that for all of our children, we are never in a room that we don`t belong in.\par \par \uc2 [18:55:00]\par \par \uc2 And last night, I accepted, you know, not just a nomination, but was announced as a 63rd, governor of a state. Where I was 11 years old, and I had handcuffs, my wrists. You know, my mother didn`t get her first job that gave her benefits until I was 14 years old. And if someone would have said to me when I was 11, sitting there with handcuffs, my wrist that you know, what, you are one day going to be the governor of this state.\par \par \uc2 I would have never believed it. And the reason I think our family is so exciting is this is the culmination of a lot of people pouring a lot into me and seeing something in me before I could see it in myself. And so, we`re -- we`re excited, we`re thrilled, and we`re humbled. And we just want to make sure that we can build a society that is not creating exceptions but actually changing the rules.\par \par \uc2 REID: Amen. And I hope at some point, you live close enough that Vice President Harris and you guys can sit down and talk about some cook-up -- she loves to cook. You know, and you got to talk to the -- talk about some of Jamaican stuff. You guys ever have a chance because she got --\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Bring the ideas together.\par \par \uc2 REID: Listen, listen, listen. Well, you know, we had a guy and he`s a guy and he`s confirmed to the cookout too. So, let`s just make it -- will make it a diaspora kind of party. Maryland Governor-elect Wes Moore, thank you very much. We got much more -- and congratulations.\par \par \uc2 We got so much more in our next hour, including that much talked about red wave that turned out to be more like a red wheeze. Steve Kornacki will be on the bid board. He`s wheezing as he`s at the big board. Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  di