.\par \par \uc2 Now that`s a projection with give or take seven many races have not yet been called for their part, democrats beat election deniers for governors races in key states like Michigan, Wisconsin and Pennsylvania where Josh Shapiro absolutely crushed the extreme candidacy of \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 . He was, of course at the Capitol on January 6. And \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  beating out a Trump style T.V. candidate and Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 And that puts more heat on the republicans to try to win the outstanding and close senate races if they have any hope of flipping the whole senate. Today, both parties are tracking Arizona and Nevada where every statewide race is still too close to call. The senate hanging in the balance because of these open states. The big answers will take more time and more counting. This year saw a spike in conspiracy theories and threats.\par \par \uc2 But election workers are doing their jobs, stacking up every ballot. So we get a correct count. And we all need patience for that, as Jermaine Cole famously said, I know that it`s difficult I`m stacking this paper, it`s sort of habitual. Count it up, count it up, count it up. Count it. So the counting continues. And when the president does speak, and what we believe will be just a few moments, he will be able to invoke some of the numbers that have been counted.\par \par \uc2 That show he performed better than most recent presidents, as mentioned. Better than \uc2 Obama\uc2  losing 60 plus or \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  losing 50 plus. And that Biden basically held much of his coalition together with many independents in the places where we do have results against what were of course, the challenges of inflation and economic headwinds, how? Well, let`s get into that with our panel as we await the president`s remarks.\par \par \uc2 And we will go to it of course when we get it. But right now we are joined by former republican congressman and MSNBC contributor David Jolly. Alicia Menendez, host of MSNBC`s "AMERICAN VOICES," democratic strategist Aisha Mills. And Jim Messina, the former White House deputy chief of staff for operations under the aforementioned former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  as well as campaign manager for \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s 2012 reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 A wonderful panel here. We`re awaiting the president`s remarks. I could start anywhere. But I will start with you, Jim. It`s not a knock on \uc2 Obama\uc2  to recall those headwinds, the Tea Party, all of that as we await this president`s remarks. What do you think of how this early indication stacks up against past losses for incumbents?\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, OBAMA`S 20212RE-ELECTION CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Way better than expected, I was just done a call just now with the largest donors in the Democratic Party. And to say there was a sense of relief and surprise, yes, we`re going t lose the house, although very, very close. And a bunch of races are outstanding. History matters here. The president`s party in the last 40 years has lost an average of 28 House seats and four senate seats.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are just overperforming and once again, it`s a women who saved us the abortion issue and the democracy issue both combined. If you look at some of the exit polling and you look at why we did so much better than expected, as you know, discussed many times the polls are wrong again for the fourth cycle. And the reason why they were wrong is they underestimated democratic enthusiasm in some of these key races, especially in the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 AISHA MILLS, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yes, the democrats are doing well. But I would like to think that actually decency is what prevailed last night. Sure the democrats are going to lose a few seats. But what I`m excited about is that we are seeing a resistance in a lot of places to Trumpism, the vile, nasty, hostile evil tones that republican extremists have campaigned on seem to be falling flat.\par \par \uc2 And so, what I`m going to be watching for is that when they do have an opportunity to lead in the house, what`s going to be the tone and tenor of that. And are they still going to succumb to that radical wing of the party or are they going to get back into normalcy with the rest of us?\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: What`s going to be the tone and tenor and also what is their governing agenda actually going to look like, right? That has been sort of a big question I think we were all prepared to ask the question. What happens if voters don`t punish republicans for the overturning of Roe?\par \par \uc2 [16:05:08]\par \par \uc2 They have now seen how this has played out with Americans? Do they continue this nonsense about a federal ban? Do they -- what do they do about inflation? It is the issue they ran on that they have no solution for. What do they do about crime? They also don`t have an answer for that. So this is where all of their chickens come home to roost.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Yes. And Alicia, to build on that -- and then I want to go to David, as we wait the president. We`re seeing in the exit polls and some of the indications that again, we don`t have all yet. We`re going to keep counting what we don`t know. But in many places where the coalition for the president held independence didn`t break with republicans. Women did appear to turn out.\par \par \uc2 Democrats spent a lot more time and money messaging and ads on women`s rights and abortion. How much of that is the story of this turnout?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It`s a lot of the story. You know, there was sort of this freakout at some point that there have been too much emphasis on democracy and that the other issues hadn`t gotten enough attention. I got to tell you when I was out there speaking with candidates, I heard extreme message discipline. I heard them talking about the economy. I heard them talking about the summer of action. Yes.\par \par \uc2 When you talk to voters, they may not say, wow, I loved the IRA, right? They may not talk about the chipset, but they had a sense that the government was working and so much of that language that democrats wanted voters out there thinking about who`s got your back? Who cares about you? When I was standing in parking lots outside of grocery stores talking to voters in Arizona and Nevada, that was being repeated back.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Yes. And so, David, you look at the president here is going to be coming out with much, much better scenario.\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Absolutely. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Then most of Washington was bracing for. Jim mentioned the polls. A note about that and then I want to be reaction. The polls are not a measurement of the actual turnout, right? The polls are a prediction about who will turn out. They tend to be more accurate when you get the turnout model right and worthless at the other end of the spectrum. What does it tell you that this turnout model did something different than most incumbent presidents?\par \par \uc2 And then second, as someone who`s been around that town, how is the Republican Party that you were a member of waking up (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Well, let`s just lay out there that the Republican Party is really good at learning all the wrong lessons. And I think they`re going to learn all the wrong lessons coming out of this. I don`t think you will see a course correction that Alicia is referring to. I think we`re going to see from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , a very confident president, maybe a little bit of swagger. And he should show it because there is no other way to look at what happened last night then a historic night for democrats.\par \par \uc2 And very importantly, consistent with 2018 and 2020. This is now a pattern and this is why this could be a complete shifting of the ground of American politics. What you saw emerge in 2018, in the face of Trumpism. Is this coalition, a strong resilient coalition, led by democrats together with disaffected republicans and independents who said we want to put a stop to the type of republicanism we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 They turned out in `18 and they gave republicans control -- or democrats control of the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Let me -- let me jump in there and then --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Turned out on `20 And they turned out last night.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: I`ll let -- I`ll now let you build on this point. You almost get the feeling at this juncture that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might be unpopular.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: What?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Listen, yes, you`re --\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Just go with me, you know, just --\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: I`m going with you. I`m going with you. But --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Just consider it. Is it possible because he got fewer votes in `16 and he got fewer votes and `20 and everyone seems to agree, including \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  that these low quality as \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  put it, MAGA-style candidates who are in the Trump echo are underperforming for a public. I know, it`s a crazy thought, but I`m just putting it out there for you.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: No, no, no. I know what you`re meaning. But I think a lot of pundits are too quick today to dismiss \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and write his epitaph because he has a stranglehold on about 30 percent of the country.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Sure. But I`m talking about what -- wait, let me go to you and then Alicia wants to get in.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: But if you don`t have Trump and MAGA candidates and big lie deniers, I mean, nobody went around after Romney lost and said, oh, it was a big lie. Do republicans do better today than they -- than they did?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Of course, the biggest losers last night, Kevin McCarthy and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But to think that this means \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  exit stage, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELBER: No, no. I didn`t say. I said unpopular. I didn`t say gone.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: But this is also why this anointment of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is also premature.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn`t going anywhere. He`s not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I would take a slightly different tack on that and you`ll tell me where I`m wrong because --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: No. You tell me where I`m wrong.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: What I think is there`s Trump and there`s Trumpism. And Trumpism is still deeply entrenched in the Republican Party. So I think to distill Trumpism solely to election denialism is a mistake. I mean, you look at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He is putting migrants on buses and planes and shipping them to Blue cities, a political stunt, nothing more the same type of cruelty. We saw \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lay the foundation for.\par \par \uc2 You had the Miami Herald saying this is supposed to be a disqualifier for election. Instead you have Florida voters coming out and actually supporting DeSantis because of it. That is because the ground was laid for that type of behavior.\par \par \uc2 [16:10:01]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: You have his election police task force, right? Where all they have discovered is voters caught in the act of voting. There is more than one way to disrupt a fair and free election. And then you have the 15-week abortion man out of Florida. So, yes, he may not be carrying the water explicitly on the big lie. But there`s still so much of Trumpism that he is bringing into his own count.\par \par \uc2 MILLS: Including the bombastic attitude in the way that people are showing up. So, I keep looking at Georgia right now. And it is shocking to me that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  who can barely put two sentences together sometimes says things that don`t make any kind of sense asserts them with a lot of bravado, tells lies, is rude to people is doing so well. And so, I`m with you on this. Trumpism may not, you know, go anywhere, anytime soon. We just have to keep beating (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Yes. I mean, Jim, let`s weight in on this. I`m not talking about predictions. That`s because so many people in Washington in trouble. I`m not saying that, because he`s unpopular. XYZ will happen. Right? I mean, you could point to a lot of unpopular items that persist. But if they did worse yesterday because of Trumpism and Trump, then where does that put the Republican Party? Because democrats, Biden might love it if they don`t course correct. That might be good for the White House.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Two things. One, there`s a fundamental split between the modern Republican Party and Trumpism, right? You`re exactly right. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and Kevin McCarthy would jump him tomorrow if they could. But his approval rating in the Republican Party grassroots is over 80 percent. He leaves the primary against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  70 to 11 in the last thing. I mean, that was exactly right. They are not going to get rid of this guy until this guy decides to get rid of himself.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: One more point I want agree with David on. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should take a victory lap here. He was largely and people routed him in D.C. for his democracy speech saying it was stupid. They hammered him for the democrats moving to abortion. And both those decisions by the White House were spot on and they had message discipline, and they close better than I`ve seen the democrats close in a very long time.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: All right. So you talked about that close as we keep an eye right here on the White House where we have the president prepared to give these post-midterm remarks. The senate and the House are still not technically decided. The senate might be a long time, it might even go to a special election Georgia to decide it. And yet, as Jim Messina was just reminding us and we`re about 12 minutes past when the president said they come out. So we expect this soon.\par \par \uc2 Jim, you`re saying that the president has a lot of opportunity open lane here. I mean, we`re used to hearing people spin a loss. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  being folksy about the thumping. What exactly do you want to hear from the president right now?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I want him to say the America had a very clear vote last night about working together and getting things done and about the democracy stuff that she was talking about and about choice. And I want him to steer right into it. The other thing I want to have very, very clear, is that the American public has been clear that we don`t want extremism and we want this democracy to work together.\par \par \uc2 And one more thing, we are gone. And we`re now no longer discussing a primary for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That`s the other thing last night, he is absolutely going to be the democratic nominee for president and everyone has just done talking about it.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Was that the perception on the -- you said you were on a call with the party`s biggest donor.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELBER: The feeling that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is now the presumptive nominee. In other words, he`s an incumbent president who doesn`t tend to truly run again. That was not just telegraphing, you`re saying that?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: And democrats have to have it right. Except for 1988, parties when the president gives it up or is term limited. The other party usually wins the White House. If democrats want to hold the White House especially against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is our candidate.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: So you think we`re going to -- we`re about to hear from someone who in a very real way outperformed and is planning to run again?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: You could tell -- I want to tell folks, we think we`re -- within two minutes, one minute here, you can see from sort of the -- some of the reporters there who are -- who are in position to give their final remarks and sit down. We think the president`s coming out any moment, we will jump to it immediately. Alicia your --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Which I also appreciate as your cue to me and David to keep it short.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: It could be. It could be. It could have been what they sometimes call double discoursing. It was more than one purpose. But go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And I think the speech writes itself, right? I mean, one of the things that this president has always been very good at is being empathetic. So I don`t -- yes, it is a victory lap. But it also is a victory lap that is couched and I know that people are still out there hurting.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Yes. Sure.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And I know that things may not be where you want them to be. But what I need you to do is trust that me and my allies in congress have already delivered and we`re going to continue to deliver on the part of you and your family. And then he acts like this one is not his. This win belongs to the American people. There were a lot of people who doubted you. There were a lot of people who didn`t think that you understood the stakes, that you didn`t know what was on the line. And you prove them wrong, right?\par \par \uc2 People want to be the heroes of their own story. And he -- if he now gets up there, he puts that power back in the hands of people. That`s something that has a long tail.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: I think there`s a big question here. I mean, look, if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  takes the approach, there`s a split decision. I think that`s the wrong approach. I think he needs to say America chose the direction of democrats last night and here`s why.\par \par \uc2 [16:15:06]\par \par \uc2 It was in that three or four that democrats had to win. And the truth is through gerrymandering republicans were gifted five or six. And then beyond that there were a dozen seats held by democrats that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had won. So the fact is, this was a layup for republicans should have been. Historically and by redistricting and in fact, democrats have really crushed republicans in this moment.\par \par \uc2 And I -- look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not an arrogant actor, right? He will naturally be humble, he will be a servant leader, but I think he can declare victory and still keep the Biden tone.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: We`re watching the room. I`m giving folks the play by play as we are waiting the president coming out. We were told the First Lady has entered the room, you can see other reporters in the room take their seat. Jim Messina, you`re saying we`re going -- to let`s go right to it. We have the president of the United States here as -- after outperforming the midterms. Let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, we had an election yesterday. And it was a good day, I think, for democracy. And I think it was a good day for America. Excuse me, I`m a little hoarse.\par \par \uc2 Our democracy has been tested in recent years. But with their votes, the American people have spoken and proven once again that democracy is who we are.\par \par \uc2 The states across the country saw record voter turnout. And the heart and soul of our democracy, the voters, the poll workers, the election officials, they did their job and they fulfilled their duty, and apparently without much interference at all without any interference, it looks like. And that`s a testament, I think, to the American people.\par \par \uc2 While we don`t know all of the results yet at least, I don`t know them all yet. Here`s what we do know. While the press and the pundits are predicting a giant red wave, it didn`t happen. And I know you were somewhat miffed by my obsessant optimism, but I felt good during the whole process. I thought we were going to do fine.\par \par \uc2 While any seat lost is painful, some good Democrats didn`t win the last night. Democrats had a strong night. And we lost fewer seats in the House of Representatives than any democratic president`s first midterm election in the last 40 years. And we had the best midterms for governors since 1986.\par \par \uc2 And another thing that we know is that voters spoke clearly about their concerns, about raising costs the rising costs and the need to get inflation down. There are still a lot of people hurting that are very concerned. And it`s about crime and public safety. And they sent a clear and unmistakable message that they want to preserve our democracy and protect the right to choose in this country.\par \par \uc2 And I especially want to thank the young people of this nation, who I`m told, I haven`t seen the numbers, voted in historic numbers again. And just as they did two years ago. They voted to continue addressing the climate crisis, gun violence, their personal rights and freedoms, and the student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 Last night, I was pleased to call Maxwell Frost, the 25-year-old who got elected. I guess the youngest man ever elected to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 . And I told him that he -- I told him that I was the first elected -- the second-youngest person ever elected to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  at 29. That I have no doubt he`s off to an incredible start in what, I`m sure, will be a long, distinguished career.\par \par \uc2 And when he`s president and they say, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is out in the outer office, I don`t want him to say, Joe who?\par \par \uc2 But the voters were also clear that they`re still frustrated. I get it. I understand it`s been a really tough few years in this country for so many people.\par \par \uc2 When I came to office, we inherited a nation with a pandemic raging and an economy that was reeling. And we acted quickly and boldly to vaccinate the country and to create a stable and sustained growth in our economy. Long- term investment to rebuild America itself and our roads, our bridges, our ports, our airports, clean water systems, high-speed internet.\par \par \uc2 And we`re just getting started. The interesting thing is that this is all going to really come into clear view for people in the months -- in the months of January, February, March of next year. It`s just getting underway. So, I`m optimistic about how the public is going to even be more embracive of what we`ve done.\par \par \uc2 Historic investments that are leading companies to invest literally hundreds of billions of dollars combined to build semiconductor factories and other advanced manufacturing here in America. It`s going to create tens of thousands of good-paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, a significant number of those jobs are going to be jobs that pay an average of $126, $127,000. And you don`t need a college degree to get those jobs.\par \par \uc2 We`re dealing with global inflation as a result of the pandemic and Putin`s war in Ukraine. We`re also handling it better than most other advanced nations in the world.\par \par \uc2 [16:20:06]\par \par \uc2 We`re lowering gas prices. We looking -- we`re taking on powerful interests to lower prescription drug costs and health insurance premiums and energy bills.\par \par \uc2 After 20 months of hard work, the pandemic no longer controls our lives. It`s still a concern, but it no longer controls our lives.\par \par \uc2 Our economic policies have created a record 10 million new jobs since I came into office. The unemployment rate is down from 6.4 when I was sworn in to 3.7 percent near a 50-year low. And we`ve done all this while lowering the federal deficit in the two years by $1.7 trillion. Let me say it again. $1.7 trillion. No administration has ever cut the deficit that much.\par \par \uc2 And reducing the federal deficit is one of the best things we can do to lower inflation. But while we`ve made real progress as a nation, I know it`s hard for folks to see that project that progress in their everyday lives.\par \par \uc2 And it`s hard to see the results from actions that we took while that we have to implement what we`ve done. But I believe we took the right steps for the country and for the American people.\par \par \uc2 In fact, if you look at the polls, an overwhelming majority, I don`t look at them much anymore, because I`m not sure how to read them anymore. I hope you are uncertain as well.\par \par \uc2 But the overwhelming majority of the American people support the elements of my economic agenda from rebuilding America`s roads and bridges, to lowering prescription drug costs, to a historic investment in tackling the climate crisis. To making sure that large corporations begin to pay their fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 And I`m confident these policies are working and that we`re on the right path, and we need to stick with them.\par \par \uc2 All these initiatives take hold as they do, from lead pipes being removed from schools and homes, to new factories being built in communities with a resurgence of American manufacturing. It`s already created, by the way, 700,000 brand new manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 You`ve heard me say it ad nauseam. I don`t know where it`s written it says we can`t be the manufacturing capital of the world. We are now exporting product, not jobs, around the world.\par \par \uc2 People across the country are going to see even more clearly the positive effects on their day-to-day lives. But I still understand why they`re hurting right now and so many people are concerned.\par \par \uc2 As I have throughout my career, I`m going to continue to work across the aisle to deliver for the American people. And it`s not always easy, but we did it the first term. And I`ll be surprised lot of people that we signed over 210 bipartisan laws since I`ve become president. And we`re revitalizing American manufacturing, gun safety, we did it together and dozens of laws positively impacting on our veterans.\par \par \uc2 And let me say this. Regardless of what the final tally in these elections show and there`s still some counting going on I`m prepared to work with my Republican colleagues. The American people have made clear, I think, that they expect Republicans to be prepared to work with me as well.\par \par \uc2 In the area of foreign policy, I hope we`ll continue this bipartisan approach of confronting Russia`s aggression in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 When I return from the G20 meetings in Indonesia with other world leaders, I`m going to invite the leaders of both political parties, as I`ve done in the past on my foreign trips, to the White House to discuss how we can work together for the remainder of this year and into the next congress to advance the economic and national security priorities of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And I`m open to any good ideas. I want to be very clear. I`m not going to support any Republican proposal that`s going to make inflation worse. For example, the voters don`t want to pay higher prescription costs for drugs. We`ve cut that now. We`re going to kick into gear next year the next calendar year. And I`m not going to walk away from the historic commitments we just made to take on the climate crisis. They`re not compromiseable issues to me, and I won`t let it happen.\par \par \uc2 The voters don`t want more taxes for the super we -- tax cuts for the super wealthy and biggest corporations. And I`m going to continue to focus on cost-cutting for working and middle-class families, and building an economy from the bottom up in the middle out.\par \par \uc2 I know you`re tired of hearing me say that, but I genuinely mean it. That`s what makes America grow. The wealthy do very well when the middle class is doing well, and the poor have a way up.\par \par \uc2 And while continuing to bring down the federal deficit.\par \par \uc2 You know, as we look at tax cuts, we should be looking at tax cuts for working people and middle-class people, not the very wealthy. They`re fine.\par \par \uc2 Look, I -- if you can go out and be a multimillionaire, that`s great. Just pay your fair share. That`s all. That`s all. Just pay your fair share. It`s like those 55 corporations in 2000 that made $40 billion and didn`t pay a penny in federal taxes.\par \par \uc2 [16:25:01]\par \par \uc2 It`s not right. Everybody has an obligation. So now they have to pay a staggering 15 percent. And you all pay more than that in your taxes.\par \par \uc2 So I`m going to keep my commitment that no one -- no one earning less than $400,000 a year and that`s a lot of money, where I come from are going to see their federal taxes go up.\par \par \uc2 And I want to be very clear. Under no circumstances will I support the proposal put forward by Senator Johnson and the senator from down in Florida to cut or make fundamental changes in Social Security and Medicare. That`s not on the table. I will not do that.\par \par \uc2 I will veto any attempt to pass a national ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 But I`m ready to compromise with the Republicans where it makes sense on many other issues. And I`ll always put the needs and interests of the American people first.\par \par \uc2 So let me close with this. On this election season, the American people made it clear. They don`t want every day going forward to be a constant political battle. There`s too much that -- of that going on. And there`s too much that we have to do.\par \par \uc2 The future of America is too promising. Too promising to be trapped in an endless political warfare.\par \par \uc2 And I really mean it. You`ve heard me say it time and again for the last 20 months or so. I am so optimistic about the prospects for America. We need to be looking to the future, not fixated on the past. And that future is bright as can be.\par \par \uc2 We we`re the only nation in the world that`s come out of every crisis stronger than we went into the crisis. And that`s a fact. I mean -- I mean, I literally mean that. We`ve come out stronger than we`ve gone in.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve never been more optimistic about America`s future than I am today. You know, I particularly because of all those young people I`ve talked about, 18 to 30. They`re showing up. They`re the best-educated generation in American history. They`re the least prejudiced generation in American history, the most engaged generation in American history, and the most involved.\par \par \uc2 Look, after a long campaign season, I still believe what I always have. This is a great nation, and we`re a great people. And it`s never been a good bet to bet against America. Never been a good bet to bet against America.\par \par \uc2 There`s nothing, nothing beyond our capacity if we work together. We just need to remember who the hell we are. We`re the United States of America. The United States of America. There`s nothing beyond our capacity.\par \par \uc2 And I`m pretty well convinced that we`re going to be able to get a lot done. Now, I`ve been given a list of 10 people that I`m supposed to call on. And you`re all supposed to ask me one question, but I`m sure you`ll ask me more.\par \par \uc2 And so let me start off with a list I`ve been given. Zeke Miller, Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 ZEKE MILLER, ASSOCIATED PRESS WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Thank you, Mr. President. I have two questions for you. As you mentioned -- as you mentioned --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: How come we never hold you guys to the same standards you hold us to? But, anyway, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m teasing. I`m teasing. I`m teasing. I`m teasing.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You mentioned that Americans are frustrated. And, in fact, 75 percent of voters say the country is heading in the wrong direction, despite the results of last night. What in the next two years do you intend to do differently to change people`s opinion of the direction of the country, particularly as you contemplate a run for president in 2024?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Nothing, because they`re just finding out what we`re doing. The more they know about what we`re doing, the more support there is.\par \par \uc2 Do you know anybody who wants us to get rid of the change we made on prescription drug prices and raise prices again? Do you know anybody who wants us to walk away from building those roads and bridges and the internet and so on? I don`t -- I don`t know any. I think that the problem is the major piece of legislation we passed and some of it bipartisan takes time to be recognized.\par \par \uc2 For example, you got -- you got over a trillion dollars` worth of infrastructure money, but not that many spades have been put in the ground. It`s taking time.\par \par \uc2 For example, I was on the phone congratulating a Californian recently and then someone in up in Scranton, Pennsylvania the congressman who got elected. And he said, can you help us make sure we`re able to have high- speed rail service from Scranton to New York? New York City? I said, yes, we can. We can.\par \par \uc2 First of all, it`ll make it a lot easier, take a lot of vehicles off the road. And we have more money in the -- in the pot now already out there we voted for than the entire money we spent on \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  to begin with.\par \par \uc2 It`s the same way for example, I talked about, through the campaign, that we`re going to limit the cost of insulin for seniors to $35.00 a month instead of $400 a month. Well, it doesn`t take effect until next year.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a lot of things that are just starting to kick in. And the same way with what we`ve done in terms of environmental stuff. It takes time to get it moving.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m not going to change. As a matter of fact, you know there`s some things I want to change and add too.\par \par \uc2 [16:30:03]\par \par \uc2 For example, we`ve had passed the most bipartisan, we passed the most extensive gun legislation and, you know, rational gun policy in 30 years. And -- but we didn`t ban assault weapons. I`m going to ban assault weapons. They`re going to try like the devil. So I`m not going to change the direction. I said, I ran for three reasons. I`m going to continue to stay where am I? No. I fully understand the legitimate concern that what I`m saying is wrong. OK. One is that I said, we`re going to restore the soul of the country, began to treat each other with decency, honor and integrity.\par \par \uc2 And it`s starting to happen, people are -- the conversations are becoming more normal, becoming more, how can I say it? Decent. Second thing I said was, I want to build a country from the middle out and the bottom up. And that way, everybody does fine. I`m tired of trickle down. Not a whole lot trickles down when we trickle down to hard working folks. The third thing I know is still very hard. I would do everything in my power to see through to reignite the country. It`s hard to sustain yourself as a leading democracy in the world, if you can`t -- you can`t generate some unity. So I`m not going to change anything in any fundamental way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just on a different topic, Mr. President. Russia today claimed that it had evacuated the Kherson region and the Kherson City, do you believe that this is potentially an inflection point in that conflict? And do you believe that Ukraine now has the leverage it needs to begin peace negotiations with Moscow?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: First of all, I found this and they waited till after the election to make that judgment, which we knew for some time that they were going to be doing. And it`s evidence of the fact that they have some real problems, Russian -- the Russian military, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, whether or not that leads to at a minimum, it will lead to time for everyone to recalibrate their positions over the winters period. And that remains to be seen whether or not there`ll be a judgment made as to whether or not Ukraine is prepared to compromise with Russia.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to be going to the G20. I`m told that President Putin is not likely to be there. But other world leaders are going to be there in Indonesia. And we`re going to have an opportunity to see what the next steps may be.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, \uc2 CBS\uc2 . Nancy Cordes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDER, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Mr. President, I have a few questions.\par \par \uc2 Been saving them up. First of all, Republican Leader, Kevin McCarthy, said last night that it is clear we are going to take the House back, do you think he`s probably right about that?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, based on what we know, as of today, we`ve lost very few seats for certain, we still have a possibility of keeping the House, but it`s going to be close. And, for example, in Nevada, we won all three of those seats, contested seats. I went off free to I spoke with each -- for each of those folks, but we won them all. I didn`t know that last night. So it`s a moving target right now. But it`s going to be very close.\par \par \uc2 CORDES: Can you -- can you describe your relationship with Mr. McCarthy? How often do you speak to him? What do you think of him?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think he`s the Republican leader. And I haven`t had much of occasion to talk to him. But I will be talking to him. I think -- I think I`m talking to him later today.\par \par \uc2 CORDES: When it comes to your legislative agenda -- when you were vice president, your legislative agenda basically ran into a brick wall, two years in when Republicans took control of the House and that lasted for the rest of the \uc2 Obama\uc2  presidency. Is there any way for you to prevent that same fate from happening this time around?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CORDES: If Republicans take control of the House.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes. Because it`s going to be much closer if they take control. Look, the predictions were, and again, I`m not being critical of anybody made the prediction I got, OK? This is supposed to be a red wave, you guys, you were talking about us losing 30 to 50 seats, and this is going to -- you know what, that`s not going to happen. And so there`s always enough people in the -- on the other team, whether it`s Democrat or Republican, that the opposite party can make an appeal to and maybe pick them off to get the help.\par \par \uc2 And so it remains to be seen. But look, I doubt whether or not, for example, all the talk -- I`d ask the retort -- I don`t expect you to answer, but a rhetorical question. Do you think that, you know, Senator Johnson is going to move to cut Medicare and Social Security? And if he does, how many Republicans are going to vote for it? So it depends.\par \par \uc2 CORDES: And then my final question. Republicans have made it clear that if they do take control of the House that they want to launch a raft of investigations on day one into your handling of Afghanistan, the border, they want to look into some of your cabinet officials, they want to investigate you, they may even want to investigate your son. What`s your message to Republicans who are considering investigating your family, and particularly your son Hunter`s business dealings?\par \par \uc2 [16:35:20]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Lots of luck in your senior year as my coach used to say. Look, I think the American public wants to move on and get things done for them. And, you know, I heard that there were -- it was reported whether it`s accurate or not, I`m not sure. But it reported many times that Republicans were saying and the former president said, how many times you`re going to impeach Biden? Impeachment proceedings against Biden.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think the -- I think the American people will look at all of that for what it is. It`s just almost comedy. I mean, it`s a -- but, you know, look, I can`t control what they`re going to do. All I can do is continue to try to make life better for the American people.\par \par \uc2 OK. Phil. Phil Mattingly, CNN.\par \par \uc2 PHIL MATTINGLY, CNN SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Mr. President, I have 37. OK. Sir, at a fundraiser last month, you said, quote, the rest of the world is looking at the selection, both the good guys and the bad guys. You noted you`re going to the G20 in a couple of days. You`ll come face to face with many of those leaders. The same moment that your predecessor is considering launching his reelection effort. How should those world leaders, both good guys and bad guys, view this moment both for America and for your presidency?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, first of all, these world leaders know we`re doing better than anybody else in the world is a practical matter. Notwithstanding the difficulties we have our economy is growing. You saw the last report, we`re still growing at 2.6 percent. We`re creating jobs, we`re still in a solid position. And there`s not many other countries in the world are in that position.\par \par \uc2 And I promise you from the telephone calls I still have and from the meetings I have other heads of state, they look into the United States, and saying, how are you doing? What are you doing? What can we do together? How -- so I think that the vast majority of my colleagues, at least those colleagues who are \uc2 NATO\uc2  members, \uc2 European Union\uc2 , Japan, South Korea, et cetera, I think they`re looking to cooperate and wanting to know how we can help one another. And what was the other question?\par \par \uc2 MATTINGLY: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 MATTINGLY: No, no, no. So I think the one way I would follow up on that is you noted that you felt like there was a shift in terms of people being willing to show more decency in this moment. You`ve often talked about breaking the fever or kind of a transition from this moment that we faced over the last several years. Do you feel like the election is what represents that? Do you feel like the fever is broken, I guess?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I`m not -- I don`t think we`re going to break the fever for the super mega MAGA Republicans. I mean -- but I think they`re a minority of the Republican Party. I think the vast majority of the members of the Republican Party, we disagree strongly on issues, but they`re decent, honorable people. We have different -- differences of agreement on issues.\par \par \uc2 But they -- you know, I work with a lot of these folks in the Senate and the House for a long time. And, you know, they -- they`re honest and they`re -- and they`re straightforward. They`re different than mine. But they`re -- you know, they`re decent folks.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that the rest of the world, and a lot of you have covered other parts of the world, and you know, the rest of the world looking at the United States -- I guess the best way to say this is to -- is to repeat what you`ve -- some of you heard me say before, the first \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting for the public, that`s the seven largest democracies, when I went to and right after we got elected in February, after I got sworn in, in January.\par \par \uc2 And I sat down at a table, a roundtable with the six other world leaders from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  and candidate, et cetera, and said, America is back. And one turn to me and said, for how long? For how long? It was a deadly earnest question, for how long? And I looked at them, and then another one on to say, and I`m not going to name them, went on to say, what would you say, Joe, if in fact, you went -- we went to bed tonight here in England, woke up the next morning and found out that thousand people had stormed the \uc2 Parliament of Great Britain\uc2 , gone down the hall, broken down the doors, two cops ended up dying, number of people injured, and they tried to stop the confirmation of election. It`s not the same situation obviously as we have.\par \par \uc2 And he said, what would you think? And what -- I ask a rhetorical question, what would you all think? You`re thinking it was really in trouble. You think democracy was on the edge if that happened in Great Britain. And so that`s the way people were looking at us, like, when is this going to stop? Nothing like this has happened since the Civil War. I don`t want to exaggerate. But literally nothing like this has happened since the Civil War.\par \par \uc2 [16:40:19]\par \par \uc2 And so what I find is that they want to know, is United States stable? Do we know we`re about? Are we the same democracy we`ve always been? Because, look, the rest of the world looks to us, I don`t mean that we`re always -- like we`re always right. But if the United States tomorrow were to, quote, withdraw from the world, a lot of things would change around the world, a whole lot would change.\par \par \uc2 And so they`re very concerned that we are still the open democracy we`ve been, and that we have rules in the institutions matter. And that`s the context in which I think that they`re looking at, are we back to a place where we are going to accept decisions made by the court, by the Congress, by the government, et cetera?\par \par \uc2 MATTINGLY: So the entire genesis of that \uc2 G7\uc2  conversation was tied to your predecessor, who is about to launch another campaign. So how do you reassure them if that is the reason for their questioning that the former president will not return, that his political movement, which is still very strong --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, yes?\par \par \uc2 MATTINGLY: -- will now yet again take power in the United States?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, we just have to demonstrate that he will not take power by -- if we -- if he does run. I`m making sure he, under legitimate efforts of our Constitution, does not become the next president again.\par \par \uc2 Steve? Reuters. I`m sorry, Steve Holland.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HOLLAND, REUTERS SPECIAL CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, sir. How do you interpret last night`s results in terms of deciding whether you want to seek another term? Isn`t now more likely that you will run? And what`s going to be your timeline for consideration?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, first of all, Jill and I have -- and by the way, this is my wife Jill, who`s held a lot more popular than I, I`m in the Democratic Party, too. But anyway, all kidding aside, our intention is to run again, that`s been our intention, regardless of what the outcome of this election was.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that we won, we -- I didn`t -- I`m going to run -- the fact that the Democratic Party outperform anything anyone expected and did better than any off your presidencies since John Kennedy, is one that gives everybody, like, sigh of relief, that the mega Republicans are not taking over the government again, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And so my judgment of running when I announce, if I and I -- my intention is that I will run again, but I`m a great respecter of fate. And this is ultimately a family decision. I think everybody wants me to run, but they`re going to -- we`re going to have discussions about it. And I don`t feel any hurry one way or another what -- to make that judgment. What today, tomorrow, whenever, no matter what my predecessor does.\par \par \uc2 HOLLAND: Early next year or what`s your -- what`s your thinking?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I think my guess is I hope Jill and I get a little time to actually sneak away for a week around between Christmas and Thanksgiving. And my guess is it`ll be early next year we make that judgment. But my plan to do it now, I mean, but, you know. OK. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 Karen. Karen Travers of ABC Radio.\par \par \uc2 KAREN TRAVERS, ABC RADIO: Thank you, Mr. President. \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star, Brittney Griner, today was moved to a Russian penal colony to serve out her nine- year sentence. Do you have an update right now on her condition? What do you know about that? And does this mark a new phase in negotiations with the Russians to secure her release? Can the U.S. now fully engage in talks on a prisoner swap? A follow-up if I can.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, we`ve been -- we`ve been engaging on a regular basis. I`ve been -- I`ve been spending a fair amount of time with her wife about what`s going on with her. And my guess is -- my hope is that now that the election is over, that Mr. Putin will be able to discuss with us and be willing to talk more seriously about prisoner exchange. That is my intention. My intention is to get her home. And we`ve had a number of discussions so far. And I am hopeful that now that our election is over, there is a willingness to negotiate more specifically with us. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [16:45:10]\par \par \uc2 TRAVERS: And if I can, your press secretary had said that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  has continued to follow up on that significant offer, but also had proposed alternative potential ways forward with the Russians. Can you tell us what those alternative ways forward are and how Russia has responded to those?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, I can, but I won`t. OK. I can`t. I mean, you know, it would -- it would not be a wise thing to do in order to see if they would move forward. But it is my -- I`m telling you, I am determined to get her home and get her home safely, along with others, I might add.\par \par \uc2 April Ryan.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN, THEGRIO SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Of TheGrio.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Of TheGrio, excuse me. I`m -- I beg your pardon.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I got it right last time we did this.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes, you did. Yes, you did. Mr. President, I have a couple of questions on several issues. One, the Supreme Court. As you know, the Supreme Court has before the issue of college admissions and affirmative action, what can -- and are you planning in case of a rollback that is expected? There are legal analysts that say that there will be drastic implications, their tentacles from this, and they even say that this could impact Brown v. Board, the decision from Brown v. Board.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, you know, first of all, I asked our Justice Department to defend the present policy before the Supreme Court. And like a lot of pundits, I`m not prepared to believe that Supreme Court is going to overrule the previously existing decision. That`s far from certain, and I don`t -- I don`t believe that. But number one. So number one what I did to try to change it is object to it before the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 , our administration.\par \par \uc2 Number two, I -- there are a number of things that we can and must do to make it -- and by the way, this is a case involving an Asian-American in terms of getting into school, and whether there`s an affirmative action makes sense at all from the standpoint of those who are arguing against it.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the fact is that we`re also in a circumstance where there`s a lot that we can do in the meantime to make sure that there`s an access to good education across the board. And that is by doing things that relate to starting education at age 3, formal schooling at age 3, which increases not daycare, but school.\par \par \uc2 And all the studies over 10 years show that that increases the prospect of someone making it through 12 years without any difficulty, no matter what the background they come from by 56 percent. And I also think that we should be making sure that we have the ability to provide for two years of education beyond that, whether it`s apprenticeships or community colleges.\par \par \uc2 And we also are in a situation where I think that, for example, I want to make sure we -- a lot of it has to do with finances as well, that we make sure that we have help for people come from modest means to be able to get to school. You know, the cost of college education is increased fourfold. And it used to be that a Pell grant would cover something like 70 percent of the college tuition, now it cover significantly less than that. So I want to increase the Pell grants as well. But let`s see what the Supreme Court decides and I`m -- I am hopeful, and our team and our -- the lawyers argued for us are not nearly as certain as the people you quoted as saying it`s going to be overruled.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Next question, sir. The issue is inflation. TheGrio and KFF conducted a study of black voters that said inflation was the number one issue, and we saw it in this midterm election. What can you promise concretely in this next two years that will help turn the pocketbook for the better in the midst of staving off a recession?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, a number of things. First of all, on black unemployment is almost cut in half under my administration just since I began. More black businesses have opened up, small businesses never before. We`re now in a situation where we`re providing through the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2 , down payments for people buying home because most people accumulate wealth in the value of their home. Most middle class families like mine, my dad bought a home didn`t just scraped together get home. By the time he was able to retire, he was -- he had built up equity in the home. That`s how most people do that.\par \par \uc2 And so -- but what I can`t do is I can`t guarantee that we`re going to be able to get rid of inflation, but I do think we can. We brought -- we`ve already brought down the price of gasoline about $1.20 a gallon across the board. And I think that the oil companies are really doing the nation a real disservice. They`ve made six have made over $100 billion in the last quarter and profit $100 billion.\par \par \uc2 [16:50:33]\par \par \uc2 In the past, if they had done the two things that they have done before, one, invest in more refineries and producing more product and or passing on the rebates to the gas stations that, you know, they sell the oil at a cheaper rate than they have to -- than they are selling it now and not taking advantage. And that lowers the price of the total gallon of gas because that gets passed on. So there`s a whole lot of things we can do that are -- that are difficult to do. But we`re going to continue to push to do them.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is that one of the things that makes a gigantic difference is, what are the costs that exists in the average family and average black community? One, prescription drug cost. Well, we`re driving those down precipitously beginning next year.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I`ll bet you know a lot of people in African-American and Caucasian community that need to take insulin for diabetes, well, we`re going to reduce that cost and I can`t pay more than $35 for the insulin instead of four -- average of $400.\par \par \uc2 And I can go down the list of the things that my dad used to say to different way. At the end of the month, the things you have to pay for, from your mortgage to food on the table, a gasoline in the automobile, do you have enough money to do it? And when it`s done, do you have anything left over? And medical bills are a big piece of that, particularly in the African-American community and the poor -- and poorer communities, they need help.\par \par \uc2 And so we`ve driving down all of those costs, and we`ve already passed the legislation to do that. It`s just taking effect. So there`s a lot of things we can do to affect the things that people need on a monthly basis to reduce their inflation, their cost of living.\par \par \uc2 And so -- but I am optimistic because we continue to grow and at a rational pace. We are not anywhere near a recession right now in terms of the growth. But I think we can have what the most economists call a soft landing. I`m convinced that we`re going to be able to gradually bring down prices, so that they, in fact, end up with us not having to move into recession to be able to get control inflation.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Mr. President, last question on humanity. Everybody else got some. Last -- well, you`re --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: OK. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Last question on humanity. Sir, you can`t legislate and you can`t executive order out the issue of empathy or the lack thereof in the midst of this rhetoric. This heated political rhetoric. What`s next?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Part of what I think leadership requires, and I hope I meet the standard, is letting people know you understand their problem. Again, my dad used to have an expression, he said, I don`t expect the government to solve my problems, but I expect them least know what they are. Understand them.\par \par \uc2 And like a lot of you, we`ve been very fortunate as a family, but we`ve also been through a lot of fairly tough times. And it`s not -- and I`ve had the great advantage of having a family to get through them. When my first wife and daughter were killed in a tractor trailer broadsided them and kill my wife and kill my first wife and kill my daughter. And my two boys are expected to die they were in a -- they took the jaws to life three hours to get them out there on top of their dead mother and dead sister.\par \par \uc2 I understand what that pain is like. And when Jill and I lost Beau after a year in Iraq, winning the bronze star speaker service medal a major in the United States military came home a stage four glioblastoma because he lived about 205 -- between 200 and 500 yards from the burn pit, just 10 feet deep and big as a football field burning every toxic waste you could find.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think that we understand what it`s like to lose family members, mothers, fathers that can`t -- all of you have been through that kind of thing. We`ve been fortunate though. We`ve had each other. We`ve had strong families, Jill`s sisters, my brothers, my sister. And so what we can do to deal with that empathy is make sure there`s help available. Make sure there`s people who were there to help whether they are a psychologist or whether they`re medical doctors or whether they`re social workers to be there to help, to help just hold a hand.\par \par \uc2 [16:55:12]\par \par \uc2 And for example, we can do an awful lot for a lot of families, the families you`re talking about if we really reinstate this child tax credit, it cuts child poverty by 40 percent. And when it was in place, I couldn`t get a pass the second time around. So there`s a lot we can do. And the empathy is not just talking about us, communicating to people you genuinely understand. And I hope a lot of people don`t understand because they -- I don`t want people having to know the pain.\par \par \uc2 But the second piece of that is, let them know that you are there to help. You`re there to help. And one of the things I`ve talked with Vivek Murthy about and a lot of you have written about it, and you`ve written it well about it, is the need for mental health care in America. You know, when we got elected, there were something like, I don`t know, two, three, five million people have gotten their COVID shots.\par \par \uc2 Well, in the meantime, I`ve got over 220 million people with all three shots. But in the meantime, what happened? We lost over a million dead. A million dead. I read one study that for those million people, they had nine people who were each one, on average, nine people close to them, a relative, someone they`re married to, a child, someone close. The impact has been profound and profound.\par \par \uc2 Think of all the people, think of all your children and your grandchildren who didn`t have that senior prom. Who didn`t have that graduation party, who didn`t have all the things we had that we took for granted that impact on the psyche. So there`s a lot we have to do.\par \par \uc2 And empathy reflects itself not just on what a person demonstrates they understand of knowing what people need and helping them make it happen. And we`re trying to do that. And a lot of Republicans trying to do it too. I don`t mean this is a partisan thing. A lot of people are trying to do it, because they know we got a problem.\par \par \uc2 OK. Excuse me. These 10 questions are really going quickly. Well, I`ve got to meet with some of my folks and some of the Republican leadership soon. But anyway, Jenny Leonard, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JENNY LEONARD, WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Thank you, Mr. President. Two questions. One shifting back to your trip to the -- to Asia. When you meet with President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  of China, will you tell him that you`re committed to defending Taiwan militarily? And what are you hoping to get out of this meeting that will make it a success? Are you willing to make any concessions to him?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, look, I`m not making -- I`m not willing to make any fundamental concessions, because what I am -- what I`ve told him in the beginning, and this is -- we`ve -- I`ve spent over 78, I think they told me hours with him so far, 67 in-person when I was vice president. President \uc2 Obama\uc2  knew he couldn`t spend time with the vice president of the country. So I traveled 70,000 miles with him in China and around and the United States. I`ve met with him many times.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve told him, I`m looking for competition not conflict. And so what I want to do with him when we talk is lay out what the -- what kind of what each of our red lines are, understand what he believes to be in the critical national interest of China, what I know to be the critical interest of the United States and to determine whether or not they conflict with one another. And if they do, how do we resolve and how to work it out?\par \par \uc2 And so -- and Taiwan doctrine has not changed at all, from the very beginning. The very beginning. So I`m sure we`ll discuss China and the -- excuse me, Taiwan. I`m sure we`ll discuss a number of other issues, including fair trade and rate -- and relationships relating to his relationship with other countries in the region. And so anyway, so there`s a lot we`re going to have to discuss.\par \par \uc2 You want other questions? Everybody else got one, two or three.\par \par \uc2 LEONARD: You didn`t say if you will tell \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  personally that you are committed to defending Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m going to have that conversation with him.\par \par \uc2 LEONARD: That wasn`t my second one. Sorry. Sorry. I actually have an unrelated question too. Mr. President, do you think Elon Musk is a threat to U.S. national security? And should the U.S. and with the tools you have investigate his joint acquisition of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with foreign governments which include the Saudis?\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221109eib9000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC LIVE for November 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrea Mitchell, Hallie Jackson, Katy Tur, Michael Steele, Jake  Sherman, Kristen Welker, Robert Gibbs, Ellison Barber, Vaughn Hillyard,  Guad Venegas, Ali Vitali, Maria Teresa Kumar, Halle Jackson, Ryan Nobles,  Philip Rucker, Michael Steele, Ben Collins \par \uc2 10,698 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC ANCHOR: We are back now heading up hour two of our special elections coverage right here on MSNBC. Thank you for being with us. I`m Hallie Jackson at our election headquarters here in New York along with my colleagues, Andrea Mitchell and Katy Tur as well.\par \par \uc2 Quick reset top of the hour. Let`s talk about what has happened, what has developed in the last 60 minutes or so, right? Big picture here, House projection is pretty much where it`s been for most of the morning here, kind of an open question, you see plus-minus 10 seats with dozens of races yet to be called across the country. In the last maybe seven minutes, we saw from our friend in the -- friend of the show, Jake Sherman, Kevin McCarthy announcing his bid for the speakership.\par \par \uc2 The Senate as we take a look at that board -- listen, we don`t know, right, Senator Ron Johnson keeping Republicans in the running for maybe the majority with the projected win today over his Democratic opponent, Mandela Barnes. And then, of course, that big news in just the last hour, the Georgia race now advancing to a runoff between incumbent Democrats \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as well. And Arizona is still too early to call with Democratic Senator Mark Kelly holding the lead over Blake Masters. In Nevada, a potential bright spot for Senate Republicans, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is leading incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . But there`s still a chunk left to be counted out there.\par \par \uc2 The big news in the House, earlier today Democratic campaign committee chairman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , he conceded his race in a big pickup for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: And this hour, we`ll also have reports from key battleground states that still big uncalled races. And the latest from the White House ahead of President Biden`s remarks at 4:00 this afternoon - - 4:00 Eastern. And we will look ahead to a potential 2024 primary matchup between former President Trump and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a matchup that`s already dividing the party.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: All right, so let`s get into it here. We are joined by our panel now. Michael Steele, former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair. Robert Gibbs, former press secretary in the Obama White House, as well Former Maryland Congresswoman Donna Edwards. Plus Brendan Buck who has advised Republican House speakers, \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 . And Brendan used to work for Kevin McCarthy as well. I think we`re going to talk about that in a second. Maria Teresa Kumar, president, and CEO of Voto Latino is here with us, and then, of course, NBC`s Ellison Barber, who was on the ground in Georgia for us.\par \par \uc2 Brendan, let me start with you because of the news that we are getting in that -- wait, I don`t see Brendan Buck, do we have him?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I can`t see --\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: The ghost of Brendan -- there he is.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER CHIEF COMMUNICATION ADVISER TO FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER PAUL RYAN: Hi, guys.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: This is really going off the rails. We`re still on track. Thank goodness. Brendan, the news in just the last couple of minutes here that, you know, as expected, Kevin McCarthy is going to be announcing -- is his now, according to Jake Sherman, announced his bid for the House speakership. You also have as we talked about, right before the break, you know, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  talking about how he`s not dealing in feelings, he`s going to wait and see how the vote count comes in, and he`s several outstanding Senate races that are still to come. Talk us through how you`re seeing the House landscape, as it stands here, just after one o`clock Eastern.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes. The vibe, Hallie, is not great, a lot of frustration, a lot of anxiety, a lot of shocks. Frankly, members, really trying to understand what went wrong, the competence that they had, how it slipped away. But Kevin McCarthy doesn`t have any room to sit around and wait so he`s moving forward. And I get why he`s doing it. They`re going to have leadership elections next week. That`s going to be interesting to see how it goes.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, a leadership election inside the conference doesn`t matter a whole lot. The way it works is he just needs 50 percent of his colleagues to nominate him to be a speaker. But ultimately, he has to get 218 votes on the House floor in January when they do that. So, he could easily slide through an election inside the conference, but two months from now, when they actually go to the floor, that`s an eternity.\par \par \uc2 And so, what I`m going to be looking for is, are there -- the Freedom Caucus members have the sort of far right of the conference, are they going to try to extort anything out of Kevin McCarthy for their vote to the speaker? I still think that he has the inside track to be the next speaker. I think members want him to be the next speaker, but it only takes -- if you`re talking about a margin of three, four, or five seats, it only takes that many folks willing to say I`m not going to get my vote to you unless. And what those lessons are, we`re going to have to figure out but I expect there`s going to be a little bit of conflict from now until January when the real vote is.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And I know, Brendan, that you had experience -- painful experience at times working with speakers who were really frustrated by the Freedom Caucus and others, and it`s now become far more aggressive and there are the extreme, you know, members, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and others that didn`t even crop up at that time. But talk to me about the effect of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Because hasn`t done from being discredited to a certain degree by last night`s results and it was McCarthy`s embrace of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that brought him to this leadership position in the first place.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Sure, it goes cuts both ways. Obviously, Kevin McCarthy has had a close relationship with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that`s, I think, what has strengthened him within the conference, and it`s why Kevin McCarthy has been able to keep the Freedom Caucus at bay. But yes, look, I think we all agree that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, at least has some blame that to take for this election. His brand is hurting the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 The problem is we`re not a very introspective party. I don`t think there`s going to be a lot of deep reflection among Republicans right now. I`m sure they`re trying to figure out exactly what happened. But what they won`t say out loud is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the problem. And until someone is willing to say it out loud, I don`t think much is going to change.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Michael Steele.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIRMAN: I couldn`t agree Brendan more.\par \par \uc2 TUR: You should see the faces he`s making\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Oh, man, you shouldn`t see my face because you nailed it. There is no like, OK, geez, what do we do? How did this happen? That`s not how we look at these things. This is -- was -- it`s cold critical calculations that are made along a political scale. And Kevin McCarthy -- and Brendan is right in making the move he`s making now irrespective of where the vote is in the House, is sending the signal that he`s going to put things in play. He wants to put things in motion to set the table before it`s set by \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and others. But the backside of this, are two pieces.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: One is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I still don`t believe that this is going to be a clean, smooth transition for Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Do you think?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Because when you get to late December, early January, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have a thing or two to say about who the next speaker is going to say.\par \par \uc2 TUR: He`s going to be breathing down his neck if the past is precedent.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Exactly, right?\par \par \uc2 TUR: He will be using social media, certainly.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: How many -- how many years we`ve been talking about Kevin McCarthy who wants this right and how he worked with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the White House. And I mean, this is like, for him, years in the making.\par \par \uc2 TUR: And all the arrows he`s taken.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: All the arrows he`s taken.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: But the second piece is, you`ve already heard some of the tales from the Freedom Caucus folks who like a Jim Jordan or like someone else, to Brendan`s point, how do they leverage that to get an extract something? Keep in mind when we had a more significant majority in the House, Republicans took out not one but two Republican speakers and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 . So when you have --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I think Brendan remembers that.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: You have a very -- I know he does. You have a very narrow majority here. Oh, the power shifts inside that caucus tremendously.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Let`s go to Jake Sherman, co-founder of Punchbowl News and an MSNBC political contributor. And perhaps no one better to ask this question about Kevin McCarthy and perhaps his next very slim majority.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, God, I have a lot of thoughts. Do we have the whole hour? But listen, I think -- I think there`s a --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: We have a couple of hours today.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: There is -- there`s a lot to unpack here. Number one, the question I told this to Katy earlier when we spoke, not on television, but the question isn`t whether Kevin McCarthy is able to become the speaker. There are -- the House is an institution in which there`s a tremendous amount of leeway to do things to make people happy. The question is, what does he have to give up to become a speaker? \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 , both of whom -- both who Brendan work for both of those men have had to go through this at various times in their careers as well. The demands that the right is putting on Kevin McCarthy are ridiculous and he should -- and he would be crazy to accept them.\par \par \uc2 Why would he be crazy? Because once he starts giving into the right on any issue, he`s going to have to give in to them on every issue. And it`s not a precedent he wants to set. I spoke to him about this earlier this fall when he thought he was going to win 25 to 30 seats. And he said I don`t deal well with leverage. So I agree with Brendan, he has the inside track. But it isn`t an eternity until January.\par \par \uc2 And I think he will become a speaker. I mean, I don`t -- Jim Jordan cannot get 218 votes, I don`t think, and nor does he want to. I don`t know how McCarthy`s going to get there. That doesn`t mean I don`t think he will. I think he will. I think it`s going to be extremely bumpy and there are just tons of speed bumps in the way between now and January 3.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TUR: There`s also a question of how do you run that conference with the knowledge that you`ve got to try to win again in `24, and what do you go after, what do you pursue, and how much gridlock do you allow that might end up hurting you? And what sort of, I don`t know hearings, you might allow Jim Jordan to conduct. But I want to ask you a bit of a curveball. And this is going to be about Georgia. Say it comes down to Georgia controlling the Senate, which likely could, and there`s a month now before we`re going to find out what happens in that runoff. How does a lame-duck Congress function with that hanging over its head?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: This is just the most important question, Katy, that we have in front of us in Washington for the next eight or so weeks because the government runs out of money on December 16. That -- the Georgia office right then. So, you`re going to have a matter of days to fund the government potentially raise the debt limit, and do a whole host of serious heavy-lift legislating with very little time on the clock.\par \par \uc2 And furthermore, these deals are typically conducted between the four corners, the speaker, Minority Leader, Senate Majority Leader, Senate Minority Leader, there is no chance. And I think Brendan would agree with me here, there is virtually no chance Kevin McCarthy could be part of any of those discussions, even if he tacitly supports them, no way because his conference is going to be vehemently opposed. So this puts just a massive, massive amount of pressure on the rest of the institution going into the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Jake Sherman, thanks so much, who`s all over this leadership struggle and Congress, of course, thank you. And I want to bring in NBC`s chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker from the outside the White House. So, Kristen, Georgia, you covered \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and you`ve interviewed him in his hometown, what`s your take on how this runoff will shape up?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Andrea, first of all, I think that all sides were anticipating this. I just heard from someone within the Walker campaign who said that as soon as this seemed