 it`s 2022. You know don`t everybody yell at once? Because of Donald Trump`s, you know, anticipated announcement and Ron DeSantis`s hugely successful Florida victory, and the rivalry already brewing on the Republican side. And now, what does President Biden do? How does he calculate it? Two-thirds of those in the exit poll said that they did not want him to seek re-election. And that -- you know, there was a rejection of his policies overall. And people think that his policies are taking us in the wrong direction. Well, how does he frame this decision?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is going to be the key question, Andrea, and there is no doubt that those numbers are staggering and that the president has to be taking a very hard look at that. I spoke with one of his top advisors today. He said, look, he`s always said that he intends to run for reelection. That has not changed as of today, and yet he has not made a final decision. We know that it`ll huddle with family members to reach that final decision ultimately. But the pressure, Andrea, is going to be on him to make that final decision because if he decides to bail out which is at this point in time no signs he`s going to do that, he`s going to want to make way for someone else. So that, of course, is on the table as well today as we prepare to address the president in just a few hours from now.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Kristen Welker. Kristen, thank you very much. I want to ask about something we saw in the NBC News exit polls. We put it up on screen a little while ago, and it showed the number of people that want to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run for president. And again, an overwhelming number of people said, no. You looked into the cross tabs, what do you -- what do you do if you`re the White House?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, two things. I mean, I think it`s important to look, as we were talking about this, the crosstabs 83 percent of Democrats say they do want to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run. I think what that does is gives him the political space. That`s important to make a very personal decision.\par \par \uc2 I -- you know, I think what Kristen said, I think what we`ve heard, even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in interviews, he intends to run. Every president intends to run. Now they have to make this intensely personal decision about what they`re going to do. And I -- and I think that`s going to be a meeting there, we`ll see what the family decides we`ll see when they set up a legal apparatus, when do they start hiring people. I think this number, though, the exit poll, gives them a little bit of space to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Does it matter if it`s Trump running or if it`s DeSantis running? Does that make the decision for him?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well, probably hard, given what I think the Republican primary will be like to understand who that person is going to be when he has to make that decision. I think the one thing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can take out of this exit poll of all voters is I`m -- I don`t have the number in front of me but my guess is that number is lower for people that want to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  run. And so if you -- if you`re a Democrat, you`re thinking if I get the nomination, I`m a 50-50 shot, right? If I`m running against Trump, I`m probably better than a 50-50 shot. That`s what`s going to animate part of that decision-making.\par \par \uc2 And I would say too, one of the things is it`s a very easy decision about whether he goes to Georgia or not. Yes, the Warnock campaign, am I helpful? If the answer is yes, you gas up Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: If the answer is no, you call \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  or somebody else. And, look, I remember being in the White House when we had to make some of these decisions. And we had to go out and talk about it. I`m happy to take a couple of bad questions in the briefing room about why my boss isn`t going to go. And then six weeks later win that race. That`s far more important than the spin of that day.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, on Georgia, let`s go to Ellison Barber, who can give us the view from -- on the ground in Georgia. She is joining us now. Ellison, talk to us about this last hour and a half or so, I mean, not even since we`ve seen this projection now for the Georgia runoff. What you`re hearing?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, a lot changing -- a lot very quickly changing but not unexpected based on what we`ve seen in the polling. Kristen Welker was reporting Herschel Walker`s campaign. They have told her, they have told us in the past that they very much knew this could be a possibility, so did Warnock`s campaign. But it was a possibility both campaigns were hoping they could avoid. We do expect to hear from the Georgia Secretary of State on the election results in about an hour.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`ve already seen the two campaign managers going back and forth on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with Warnock`s campaign manager tweeting at \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  that he underperformed Trump`s vote share in rural suburban and urban counties. He ended up calling last night a good night for his candidate, incumbent Senator Warnock, and a bad one for Walker. Walker`s campaign manager replying to that saying more than 50 percent of Georgians voted against the incumbent that spent more than $100 million.\par \par \uc2 This has been a fascinating race because, at the beginning, you had two candidates who had very well likability, a lot of name recognition in this state, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  because he is a legendary football player in this state, Senator Warnock, someone who is well-known given his position at Ebenezer Baptist Church and generally speaking, also well-liked. I think back to one of the very first campaign ads we saw hear from national Republicans that essentially started by saying, hey, we know that you think Senator Warnock is a good guy but he votes with President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  X amount of time, and this is why we think you should not vote for him.\par \par \uc2 So we started on a platform where both of these candidates had high name recognition, both of them relatively well-liked as people, then we saw this race just get more and more intense, more and more messy. You had a lot of allegations and questions about Republican Herschel Walker`s personal life in this race. He was accused by two women of paying for and encouraging them to have an abortion. He is a staunch anti-abortion candidate. He denied those allegations, but from some Republican0leaning voters who I spoke to, that gave them a bit of pause here.\par \par \uc2 And you do have the fact that Warnock spent a ton of money in this race. He outspent \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  by about $78 million, but they are still going to a runoff. This is a state that seems incredibly polarized. More polarized perhaps than it is purple. Back to you, guys.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ellison Barber, live for us there in Atlanta. Ellison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Buck, talk to us a little bit about how you see Georgia, given the numbers that we`ve been seeing in the exit polling and where this race is headed here.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  not being on the ballot is going to be a huge deal in Georgia. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  was a very popular governor but he also doesn`t have a great relationship with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Obviously, we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Kemp had a bit of a fight. And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was Donald Trump`s candidacy. And in the primary when Donald -- when Brian walked to the -- \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  was facing a challenge, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  didn`t do anything to help him. And \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  remembers that. And so while \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was struggling throughout this general election to get to that 50 percent, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  didn`t do anything to lift the finger for him.\par \par \uc2 So, I`ll be very curious to see whether the Kemp apparatus is very popular, Georgia comes out to try to save \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  at this point. But I got to think with just the two of them head to head with so much at stake, and a lot of frankly, momentum for Democrats right now that the runoff is going to favor Warnock. And we saw this two years ago, we saw a lot of democratic energy come in and steal away a Republican seat in the deep south. So, if I`m the Walker campaign, I`m nervous going into a runoff right now.\par \par \uc2 TUR: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  lost the governorship but there`s no doubt that her Get Out the Vote campaign or effort down there has helped to transform Georgia. Brendan, thank you very much. Let`s go out west and get an update from our reporters who are still in those not yet called races. Joining me from Phoenix is NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard, and in Las Vegas is Guad Venegas. So, Vaughn, give us an update on what is happening there in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: I think that we just got some new numbers here from the Maricopa County Recorder`s Office. And what these numbers are ultimately going to be telling us is the fact that we will not likely know the results out of Arizona until Thursday night. You can see the governor`s race. You see a 12,000 gap now separating \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  from Kari Lake. You see Blake Masters`s race. He is a larger gap. He has more room than he has to make up here.\par \par \uc2 But there is a particular batch of about 275,000 ballots that we frankly do not know the trend lines whether they will favor \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  or Kari Lake. These are mail ballots, the folks received in their mailboxes, but instead of mailing them back, they walked into their polling places yesterday and hand-delivered them. Maricopa county officials say that they will not tabulate those ballots until tomorrow, and will