f whether Kari Lake is ultimately able to eclipse \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . Obviously, for Blake Masters, it`s going to be even tougher, guys.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Vaughn Hillyard, you`ve got a lot of waiting to do as they count those ballots. Thank you so much. And Guad Venegas -- Guad Venegas is in Nevada, the other state that`s still too close to call. Republicans are up in the Senate and governor`s races though. Guad, when we get to know more there?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Andrea, there`s more waiting happening here. We were told it could be a few days until they count all of these mail-in ballots. I was listening to Vaughn describe what`s happening in Arizona, you know, a lot of the same is happening here. Except here, we`re paying attention to two counties obviously, Washoe County, the second largest.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 We have an idea of how many mail-in ballots came in on Election Day there, it`s less than 20,000 so that`s good just to get an understanding when you see how close these races are, right? So, the other county is, of course, here Clark County, where the vast majority of voters are in Nevada. We still don`t know how many mail0in votes came in on Election Day. So, you look at the two big races.\par \par \uc2 First, the race for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the incumbent, and \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the challenger who is up by 23,000 votes, that`s the difference. It is very narrow. So, we need to have an understanding of how many mail-in votes came in, how many ballots came in here to Clark County to see what kind of a leader -- what this lead means for \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 . He`s up by 22,000 so if say, Clark County has about 100,000 votes or ballots that is that they still have to count, then \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  has a real chance of catching up.\par \par \uc2 But we won`t find out until a little maybe an hour and a half from now when they`re we`re going to be speaking 