 today for that news conference out of Clark County. Guad Venegas, thank you very much. Let`s bring in Maria Teresa Kumar now to get further into this. Maria, what are you watching for?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, PRESIDENT AND CEO, VOTO LATINO: Right now in Nevada, specifically in Arizona, we -- what we`ve been looking for specifically is what is the turnout of young Latinos? Young Latinos, for folks may not realize, they`re the larger share of the Latino voting population in both the states. And they have been lagging behind in both -- it since the last results we got on Monday. So that I think is going to help determine who`s going to control the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And then in Georgia -- one of the things that folks didn`t realize, but in Georgia between the last -- between Election Day and the special runoff day, back when Warnock won, there were 50,000 youth that turns 18 during that four-week, six-week period. Voto Latino ended up registering 15 percent of those folks, and I think that is a silver lining for the Democrats if they are able to create and run a robust voter registration effort on top of their mobilization effort.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Maria Teresa Kumar, thank you so much. It is good to see you as always. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 So, listen, there is a question today. I know we`re talking 2022. I know we are, right? But we`re also simultaneously talking 2024 because there are implications, what happens this week, etcetera, for what`s going to happen in a couple of years. Is that like -- are you -- is that annoying to you, maybe? But let`s be real. We got to talk about it right? Specifically, what happens with the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  factor, right? When you look at what has happened in Florida and we -- I think can now call the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  factor there.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about it with Ali Vitali, who is posted up live in Tampa, Florida. Ryan Nobles is joining us from West Palm Beach. And we`re also joined by a friend of the show, Phil Rucker, deputy national editor for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . It`s good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 Ali, let me start with you because if -- you know if you look at who have a pretty strong night last night Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with reportedly people at his party chanting two more years instead of four because of this speculation around 2024. Ron -- check out the front page of this morning`s New York Post. I think we have that graphic. Not DeSantis but the future right there. Ali, talk us through.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hallie, this is a conversation though that Republicans clever, yes, but have been having over the course of the last year and a half. Every time I have gone to a CPAC gathering or other kind of conservative conference, there have been straw polls done that showed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leading the PAC but \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  right there after him and seen as the heir apparent to the Trump mantle if you take Trump out of those straw polls.\par \par \uc2 Now everything is unofficial at this point but at the same time it`s a good gauge of how the Republican Party is thinking about what its future might look like. Clearly still in the Trump image, but maybe not with Trump himself as the actual standard bearer. We also know that that`s probably what the former president`s mindset is too because he`s out there giving nicknames, giving voice to the schism that has now been in place between DeSantis, a former ally and acolyte of his, and Trump, this is something that I`ve been hearing about from sources for months, but the fact that it`s now being litigated, he put that nickname out there for DeSantis just a few days before DeSantis` own reelection here, a day before Trump actually came to the state and campaigned, not with DeSantis, but with Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , it`s clear that there`s some bad blood that`s brewing here already.\par \par \uc2 And yes, it`s annoying to talk about presidential politics when it`s still squarely calendar year 2022, but first of all, we love this stuff, we do it so that no one else has to, but also this is what the reality is. The day after the midterms are over, it`s when we immediately pivot to what`s next because all the candidates are doing that. Operatives I`m talking to are doing that and donors I`m talking to are doing that. They`re ready to start seeing what this field might look like.\par \par \uc2 And again, it`s not just \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who is probably thinking about this, it`s Trump but it`s also people like Nikki Haley, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . We know the names of people who are thinking about it. Now is just the time that we get to start talking about it.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Ali, and consider how many Taylor Swift lyrics you can work into discussing these races.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: So many.\par \par \uc2 TUR: And the standoff between these two men. The bad blood.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ryan Nobles -- glad you explained it. Like I`m not a Taylor Swift person.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You didn`t get it? Come on.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yes. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Bad blood.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I get it now.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That was brilliant, Katy.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Trump was a drag on lots of candidates last night. But he`s still claiming victory. His last post on Truth Social says, quote, "174 wins and 9 losses. A great evening. Amazing job by some really fantastic candidates." But he also had sine seriously high profile defeats like Dr. Oz, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania, Tudor Nixon in Michigan, I think of Bolduc in New Hampshire. So we`re hearing the public messaging but what are the private whispers in Trump world right now?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: First of all, thanks for not putting me on the spot with any Taylor Swift lyrics. I could have gone there if you asked me. But I`m glad I didn`t to take that challenge.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You`re a better man, Ryan Nobles. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TUR: I`m going to get some fresh lyrics in and I`m going to throw you all over the bridge.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Yes. But to get back to the thoughts in Trump world this morning, obviously this is not the narrative that they were expecting when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  called all of this closest advisers to Mar-a-Lago last night for what he expected to be the type of party where he could take credit for everything and the fact that now they`re being forced to deal with this narrative that perhaps he was a drag on some of these candidates that he had hoped would win, and maybe could be preventing the Republicans from taking control of the Senate, and a much narrow, much more narrow majority in the House, is a big problem for Trump as he makes the case that he is the most dominant candidate in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 But Trump is already doing what Trump does, right. He`s finding a way to spin these results in his -- to his benefit. He`s focusing on the fact that he did put out a ton of endorsements over the course of the 2022 campaign and just focusing on those largely noncompetitive races where his candidates were successful and he`s either ignoring the results in places like Pennsylvania or finding blame.\par \par \uc2 For instance, in the race in New Hampshire, where he claimed that Don Bolduc, the Republican nominee there, didn`t focus enough on the 2020 election in his race against the incumbent \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  which Hassan won easily. So this is all against the backdrop of what Ali was talking about. He`s already announced plans for a major announcement next week here in Palm Beach and whether or not that is still going to be to announce a race for president we`re going to have to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 But this is obviously not the narrative that Trump and his team were expecting to come out of the results from last night.\par \par \uc2 TUR: So, Phil, if Trump does announce, which I think it looks like he`s going to, and if DeSantis says, hey, listen, look what I`ve been able to do and look what Trump is not able to do, I`m also going to get in this race, my question is, how ugly does it get and how damaging does it get? We already had Trump yesterday saying that he knows secrets about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and only his wife knows. It reminded me of the stuff that he went after, you remember this, Halle, Ted Cruz with in 2016.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, THE WASHINGTON POST DEPUTY NATIONAL EDITOR: Yes. Katy, it could certainly get ugly. I mean, we know how nasty these campaigns can be especially when Trump is one of the candidates.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 But we should keep in mind that the attacks can go each way, and we`ve seen Trump for the last several years be this dominant force in the Republican Party, but he`s been unchallenged largely within the Republican Party. He`s never had a Florida governor coming off a massive reelection campaign the way \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is as a potential rival really take it to him and go to the voters and say look, I know how to win, I know how to broaden the coalition of voters to get back the White House and I`m conservative, too, but I can get things done and govern.\par \par \uc2 That`s the kind of message I think you could see a \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  take to Trump, and I think we`d be fooling ourselves if we`re to just assume that Trump runs through this primary as the favorite. I think it could be difficult for him to navigate this environment and last night showed that there are real limitations to the breath of appeal that Trump has across the broad electorate.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And as -- I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Well, let me ask Michael Steele about that -- I`m sorry. If DeSantis and Trump and they`re firing back at each other, how damaging is that for the party in `24?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIR: Yes, I think in one respect you could say it`s hugely damaging because you`ve got this dynamic where clearly DeSantis is generating a lot of energy around his efforts in Florida that have expanded across the political universe inside the GOP but then you`ve also got the Trump factor, which is an unknown.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been saying for some time now, I think a lot of folks in the press and certainly a lot of people in politics aren`t really catching on is, Trump hasn`t engaged yet. Trump is just sitting there saying I`m very, very, very much considering possibly doing.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Probably.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Probably. When he decides to do it, it changes the way people look at him and respond to him real time inside the party. That`s what we`ve seen play out in the past.\par \par \uc2 TUR: So you think DeSantis does not do it?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: I`m not 100 percent convinced. I`ve talked to some DeSantis people who are like, they`re looking to `28 because they think this is going to be a little bit more of a hot mess.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Is that a \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  mistake, though?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: It could be a \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  mistake, but you also have some of the DeSantis people who are like he`s ready to play depending on how -- what I just said -- plays out.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: But he can`t jump in too fast. He`s just been re-elected and he could take credit arguably, Chuck Todd was pointing this out last night, for swinging several new congressional House seats because of the way he gerrymandered and forced through that redistricting in Florida, you know, single handedly really, and added to the list of House seats.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: But he can`t take credit for anything beyond Florida. And that`s the important metric here. Trump arguably can. You know, if -- you know, we have a governorship in Arizona, he can take credit for that. Yes, he lost Pennsylvania, but you look at where else Trump has influenced and has some impact and so that`s sort of the competing interest right now.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I just think it comes back to, Katy, something you said at the top of the show, right, it`s voters, what are voters saying.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ali Vitali knows it, Ryan knows it, they`re talking to people out there. There are people who are like, hey, I dig \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I love something fresh.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Well, let`s talk about some numbers now because as we`ve been discussing control of the Senate still hangs in the balance with key races in Arizona and Nevada. We`ve talked to reporters in both places. They`re still to be called. Georgia Senate race now officially headed for a runoff. That`s still weeks away.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 . He is the CEO of Target Smart, a political data analytics company.\par \par \uc2 So, Tom, looking at what`s out there in Maricopa County, what`s out there in Clark County, how are you reading those races and potentially what happens with the third party libertarian votes going one way or the other or dividing between Walker and -- between Walker, you know, and Warnock or staying home?\par \par \uc2 TOM BONIER\uc2 , TARGETSMART: Yes. You know, these are the key questions we`re expecting here. And as you know, these things change on a moment by moment basis. We`re waiting to hear from Clark County, Nevada, obviously where, you know, almost 75 percent of the votes in the states are coming from -- the state is coming from about how many votes. But right now, Democrats believe there are about 100,000 votes still to be counted from Clark County, Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Those are overwhelmingly mail votes which we know were overwhelmingly Democratic votes. If those numbers bear out, that`s a very good sign for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , and the other thing we have to keep in mind is ballot carrying. It`s something we`ve seen in other states but Nevada is a state that allows that and mail ballots can keep coming in. So this is one we`re going to have to keep watching for a while here.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 Similarly, in Arizona, Maricopa County, as you know, there are hundreds of thousands of votes still out in Arizona. What we`ve seen so far in Arizona is that Mark Kelly -- it`s what we`ve been reporting through the early voting period is that Mark Kelly was over performing with those early votes. Again this is one that`s looking to be very close at this point. There are certainly a lot of things that the Kelly campaign has to feel good about.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, in that runoff, another big question here is just the time period. We have to remember it`s only a four-week runoff. That means mail ballots which were a big advantage for Democrats not just in Georgia but around the country won`t be as big of a factor in Georgia in the runoff. That said, Senator Warnock and his team know how to win in these runoff situations. They did it two years ago. They`ll be looking to do it again next month.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And Tom, what are the possible Senate scenarios you`re looking out for in terms of which one goes which way and Georgia becomes the -- all-in race?\par \par \uc2 BONIER: Yes. Well, I think we`ll know a lot more just in terms of the landscape, right, and that Republicans need to win at this point two of those three remaining seats to be called. Arizona is one that I don`t think they feel as good about right now. If that goes then they have to run the table and they would have to then win Nevada and then win Georgia in the runoff. That`s certainly something that`s difficult.\par \par \uc2 And again, if these numbers we are hearing out of Clark County that we`re seeing potentially another 100,000 votes coming in, and again we should be finding out about that momentarily, that makes Nevada look difficult for them, too. So they`re facing an uphill battle. It`s not that it can`t be done but they are facing a scenario where if they don`t win one of Arizona or Nevada, then this runoff isn`t for control anymore.\par \par \uc2 TUR: We`ll see what happens. \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 , thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We have some news from Steve Scalise making it official, not going to be challenging Kevin McCarthy, will be running for majority leader, though, officially. That is if the Republicans take back the House.\par \par \uc2 And over to Colorado, it was a good night for Democrats. They took the governor`s mansion, the Senate, the AG`s office and four out of six House races that are called so far. Two House races including that surprising race that has incumbent Republican \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  trailing, are still uncalled at this hour. You can see how close the margin is right there.\par \par \uc2 Let us bring in Colorado Democratic Senator Michael Bennet who won again last night, won his re-election.\par \par \uc2 Michael -- Senator, excuse me, always good to have you. What do you chalk this up to?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): I chalk this up to a relentless economic message, Katy, about how 40 years of Washington consensus, about outsourcing and about trickledown economics just isn`t working for the American people and it was a clear distinction between me and my opponent on that. I think that it`s a repudiation of Donald Trump`s chaos and a recognition that we actually made progress capping drug prices for seniors, for example, requiring Medicare to negotiate on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, you know, the overturning of Roe versus Wade was something that was -- made people furious. So that combination of those three things made it a very good night in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator Bennett, it`s Halle. I wonder how much you see the Trump factor is at play in your race. I spoke with your opponent just a couple of days before the election and asked him about that. He was very clear as he said before, he is his own man. We know that the former president went after him in Colorado. How much do you see that as being a factor in your victory and your return to the Senate?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Yes. I don`t think it was a huge factor because I don`t think my opponent was ever very persuasive on that subject. I mean, he had voted twice for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and he got religion three weeks before the election day, but I do think it`s fascinating to see counties, and you know these counties well, like Douglas County, where I only lost by six points last night, El Paso County where I only lost by six points last night, those are places Democrats historically, including myself, you know, expect to lose by as much as 20 points. And what we saw in red and rural Colorado was something very different this year.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Senator, looking ahead to 2024, I know that`s painful, we`re barely surviving after being up all night on 2022, but our exit polling does show that more than two-thirds of voters do not want President Biden to run again. You align with the president certainly before the midterms. Where do you stand on whether Biden 2024 should be the candidate, the standard bearer, after seeing these results?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Yes. First let me say I do think we`re going to win in Nevada and in Arizona, and that`s going to give us a real shot at Georgia as well, which I`m really excited about before we go to `24.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 And then I`m going to say what you would probably expect, which is this is up to President Biden to decide. You know, he`s going to have to consider a lot of different factors in making this decision. But it is true that he was the one person out of 330 million people that beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I think that if there were a rematch based on the results we just saw last night, I don`t think there`s any doubt that he would beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 TUR: What do you think of the \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  race?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: It`s amazing. I talked to her opponent today. The race is very, very close and I think she`s going to have a hard time making up her deficit, and I think this is what happens when you treat your constituents with contempt and you spend all of your time on national television instead of doing the hard work that people of the western slope of Colorado deserve, and in Pueblo, Colorado, deserve out of their congressional representative. You know, we should know something I think later today.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Is Colorado a purple state or would you say Colorado is now a blue state?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I would say it`s a purple state. We -- Katy, we have the largest registration by far in Colorado of unaffiliated voters and I think that`s why anybody who`s in one of these jobs is smart to run like you`re 20 points behind, which is how I treated this race and I`ve treated every one of my races. I don`t -- I think Colorado will be a purple state for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Let`s talk if the Senate is as tight as we think it`s going to be, we know it is obviously going to be tight, right. How do you see the role of one of your colleagues, for example, and listen, this is all hypothetical until we know more results here. Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , right? What are your governing priorities in the Senate especially if the House does end up with a House Speaker Kevin McCarthy, are you looking to reconcile bills on that front from a governing perspective?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I mean, I think it`s going to be honestly very difficult with the House that`s led by Kevin McCarthy. I have not had trouble finding Republicans in the Senate to work with, but I mean, McCarthy is so beholden to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Maybe that will change. I doubt it will, which means that it`s going to be, you know, there`s going to be a lot of friction in the air. The Senate can continue to approve judges and I think we should pass bills that show what we stand for compared to what McCarthy and the Republicans stand for.\par \par \uc2 And that needs to be, as I said, a very explicit economic message that is going to resonate with working people in red and blue parts of America. The Democrat -- long overdue from the Democratic Party. I think we`ve begun turning the page, bringing the semiconductor industry back, fighting and winning those wins over pharma that we`ve been fighting for for generation, but there`s more that we can do.\par \par \uc2 We can pass bills to reverse Trump tax cuts for the rich and extend my tax cuts for working people that I wrote with \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 . I mean, I think there`s ways of creating distinction and looking for opportunities to work together if we can find places where we can get stuff done on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Senator Bennet, thank you so much for being with us and congratulations on your election victory.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Thank you for having me today. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: It`s great to have you. See you. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the misinformation flooding social media, as the right`s hopes of a red wave falls short. Ben Collins joins us to break it all down on our continuing coverage of the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. This is MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:32]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And Republicans are not seeing the red wave they`ve hoped for, and in fact, we`ve just gotten another concession, this is from election denying \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is the Republican who really tried and competed very hard to defeat \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  in her first actual competition because she had not actually run before for governor. But Zeldin`s concession had not come last night, but he did concede today. Of course many are hoping that the base will accept these results. But for more on this and on the misinformation that comes from election denial, which was spreading last night, NBC News senior reporter Ben Collins.\par \par \uc2 So, Ben -- where`s Ben? It`s a big day.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ben is usually right here. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It is a 900-foot table.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Special coverage.\par \par \uc2 Ben, let`s talk about what you were seeing last night because there were a number of election deniers who were trying to, you know, repeat the Trump memes and in the middle of a race that was not even that close.\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: They would be in denial.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: That`s exactly right. And I do think there was a moment in the evening, I was watching all of the shows, I was watching Infowars and \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , and there was a moment where they just realized, like, wait a second, we don`t have a plan for this. They were all red wave all the time. They were high on their own supply all evening. And then there was a moment where they looked up at the actual results and realized that red wave was not there.\par \par \uc2 And it was fascinating watching it happen because they live in -- they are so sectioned off from reality in these spaces, where the idea that things aren`t going right, that things aren`t going to work out at the end of the day for them, it`s just not possible, and then when it becomes reality, they have to really adjust. And I think they did have a backup plan really quickly, where they thought like, you know, worse comes to worse, it will be 50-49. We`ll go to Arizona, we`ll contest everything. We`ll make it hell for everybody.\par \par \uc2 But that might not actually matter. If Democrats win Nevada, you know, contesting that state is not going to matter for the Senate. So they are on their back foot right now. And I think they were really -- look, I`m not saying, you know, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is looking inward, but I`m saying for the first time he might actually have to reflect.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: One of the things that you were talking about during the commercial break, Ben, is that you think that what the lawsuits, Dominion, Alex Jones, maybe mattered.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:00]\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. I think so. I think if you consistently, over and over again, realize that this might have consequences to lie about private figures, it`s money. To lie about election officials, to lie about a company that will sue you for billions of dollars, which is what places like FOX News and \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  are facing, that stuff can`t siphon all the way up to the top anymore. That`s stuff that may have ended up on FOX News at the end of the day, that started on a blog or started on 4Chan, now that stuff just ends up stuck.\par \par \uc2 It gets stuck in these zones where, you know, only the people who are already pot committed, people like Alex Jones, people like the Gateway pundit, who are already being sued by these people, or Mike Lindell, those are the only people still talking about the fraud, whereas on FOX News, they have to face reality.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Going for broke, as they might end up being broke regardless.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUR: What about the rest of the dark inner Webs that you look through? Anything concerning that you`re seeing?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, look, there is a large portion, so like the people who planned the insurrection still have their forum and they gave up on democracy a long time ago. And by the way, we should have factored this in a little bit more before people started voting, I think. There is a large section of the Trump base that simply does not want to vote anymore. They just want to get violent, and maybe those people didn`t show up.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ben Collins, it`s good to have you on, the expert on all of this. Thank you, sir. It`s good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot more coming up on our next hour of special coverage right here on MSNBC. We`ll hear from Georgia`s secretary of state. We`re going to hear the latest on the vote count over in Nevada in Clark County there. I`m going to say a quick goodbye and sneak out to my other job over on the NBC News side, but you will be in very capable hands with Andrea and Katy. See you in a bit.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221109eib9000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for November 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Andrea Mitchell, Hallie Jackson, Katy Tur, Michael Steele, Jake  Sherman, Kristen Welker, Robert Gibbs, Ellison Barber, Vaughn Hillyard,  Guad Venegas, Ali Vitali, Maria Teresa Kumar, Halle Jackson, Ryan Nobles,  Philip Rucker, Michael Steele, Ben Collins \par \uc2 10,699 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC ANCHOR: We are back now heading up hour two of our special elections coverage right here on MSNBC. Thank you for being with us. I`m Hallie Jackson at our election headquarters here in New York along with my colleagues, Andrea Mitchell and Katy Tur as well.\par \par \uc2 Quick reset top of the hour. Let`s talk about what has happened, what has developed in the last 60 minutes or so, right? Big picture here, House projection is pretty much where it`s been for most of the morning here, kind of an open question, you see plus-minus 10 seats with dozens of races yet to be called across the country. In the last maybe seven minutes, we saw from our friend in the -- friend of the show, Jake Sherman, Kevin McCarthy announcing his bid for the speakership.\par \par \uc2 The Senate as we take a look at that board -- listen, we don`t know, right, Senator Ron Johnson keeping Republicans in the running for maybe the majority with the projected win today over his Democratic opponent, Mandela Barnes. And then, of course, that big news in just the last hour, the Georgia race now advancing to a runoff between incumbent Democrats \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as well. And Arizona is still too early to call with Democratic Senator Mark Kelly holding the lead over Blake Masters. In Nevada, a potential bright spot for Senate Republicans, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is leading incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . But there`s still a chunk left to be counted out there.\par \par \uc2 The big news in the House, earlier today Democratic campaign committee chairman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , he conceded his race in a big pickup for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: And this hour, we`ll also have reports from key battleground states that still big uncalled races. And the latest from the White House ahead of President Biden`s remarks at 4:00 this afternoon - - 4:00 Eastern. And we will look ahead to a potential 2024 primary matchup between former President Trump and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a matchup that`s already dividing the party.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: All right, so let`s get into it here. We are joined by our panel now. Michael Steele, former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair. Robert Gibbs, former press secretary in the Obama White House, as well Former Maryland Congresswoman Donna Edwards. Plus Brendan Buck who has advised Republican House speakers, \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 . And Brendan used to work for Kevin McCarthy as well. I think we`re going to talk about that in a second. Maria Teresa Kumar, president, and CEO of Voto Latino is here with us, and then, of course, NBC`s Ellison Barber, who was on the ground in Georgia for us.\par \par \uc2 Brendan, let me start with you because of the news that we are getting in that -- wait, I don`t see Brendan Buck, do we have him?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I can`t see --\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: The ghost of Brendan -- there he is.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER CHIEF COMMUNICATION ADVISER TO FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER PAUL RYAN: Hi, guys.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: This is really going off the rails. We`re still on track. Thank goodness. Brendan, the news in just the last couple of minutes here that, you know, as expected, Kevin McCarthy is going to be announcing -- is his now, according to Jake Sherman, announced his bid for the House speakership. You also have as we talked about, right before the break, you know, Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  talking about how he`s not dealing in feelings, he`s going to wait and see how the vote count comes in, and he`s several outstanding Senate races that are still to come. Talk us through how you`re seeing the House landscape, as it stands here, just after one o`clock Eastern.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes. The vibe, Hallie, is not great, a lot of frustration, a lot of anxiety, a lot of shocks. Frankly, members, really trying to understand what went wrong, the competence that they had, how it slipped away. But Kevin McCarthy doesn`t have any room to sit around and wait so he`s moving forward. And I get why he`s doing it. They`re going to have leadership elections next week. That`s going to be interesting to see how it goes.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, a leadership election inside the conference doesn`t matter a whole lot. The way it works is he just needs 50 percent of his colleagues to nominate him to be a speaker. But ultimately, he has to get 218 votes on the House floor in January when they do that. So, he could easily slide through an election inside the conference, but two months from now, when they actually go to the floor, that`s an eternity.\par \par \uc2 And so, what I`m going to be looking for is, are there -- the Freedom Caucus members have the sort of far right of the conference, are they going to try to extort anything out of Kevin McCarthy for their vote to the speaker? I still think that he has the inside track to be the next speaker. I think members want him to be the next speaker, but it only takes -- if you`re talking about a margin of three, four, or five seats, it only takes that many folks willing to say I`m not going to get my vote to you unless. And what those lessons are, we`re going to have to figure out but I expect there`s going to be a little bit of conflict from now until January when the real vote is.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And I know, Brendan, that you had experience -- painful experience at times working with speakers who were really frustrated by the Freedom Caucus and others, and it`s now become far more aggressive and there are the extreme, you know, members, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and others that didn`t even crop up at that time. But talk to me about the effect of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Because hasn`t done from being discredited to a certain degree by last night`s results and it was McCarthy`s embrace of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that brought him to this leadership position in the first place.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Sure, it goes cuts both ways. Obviously, Kevin McCarthy has had a close relationship with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that`s, I think, what has strengthened him within the conference, and it`s why Kevin McCarthy has been able to keep the Freedom Caucus at bay. But yes, look, I think we all agree that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, at least has some blame that to take for this election. His brand is hurting the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 The problem is we`re not a very introspective party. I don`t think there`s going to be a lot of deep reflection among Republicans right now. I`m sure they`re trying to figure out exactly what happened. But what they won`t say out loud is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the problem. And until someone is willing to say it out loud, I don`t think much is going to change.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Michael Steele.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIRMAN: I couldn`t agree Brendan more.\par \par \uc2 TUR: You should see the faces he`s making\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Oh, man, you shouldn`t see my face because you nailed it. There is no like, OK, geez, what do we do? How did this happen? That`s not how we look at these things. This is -- was -- it`s cold critical calculations that are made along a political scale. And Kevin McCarthy -- and Brendan is right in making the move he`s making now irrespective of where the vote is in the House, is sending the signal that he`s going to put things in play. He wants to put things in motion to set the table before it`s set by \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and others. But the backside of this, are two pieces.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: One is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I still don`t believe that this is going to be a clean, smooth transition for Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Do you think?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Because when you get to late December, early January, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have a thing or two to say about who the next speaker is going to say.\par \par \uc2 TUR: He`s going to be breathing down his neck if the past is precedent.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Exactly, right?\par \par \uc2 TUR: He will be using social media, certainly.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: How many -- how many years we`ve been talking about Kevin McCarthy who wants this right and how he worked with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the White House. And I mean, this is like, for him, years in the making.\par \par \uc2 TUR: And all the arrows he`s taken.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: All the arrows he`s taken.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: But the second piece is, you`ve already heard some of the tales from the Freedom Caucus folks who like a Jim Jordan or like someone else, to Brendan`s point, how do they leverage that to get an extract something? Keep in mind when we had a more significant majority in the House, Republicans took out not one but two Republican speakers and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 . So when you have --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I think Brendan remembers that.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: You have a very -- I know he does. You have a very narrow majority here. Oh, the power shifts inside that caucus tremendously.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Let`s go to Jake Sherman, co-founder of Punchbowl News and an MSNBC political contributor. And perhaps no one better to ask this question about Kevin McCarthy and perhaps his next very slim majority.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, God, I have a lot of thoughts. Do we have the whole hour? But listen, I think -- I think there`s a --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: We have a couple of hours today.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: There is -- there`s a lot to unpack here. Number one, the question I told this to Katy earlier when we spoke, not on television, but the question isn`t whether Kevin McCarthy is able to become the speaker. There are -- the House is an institution in which there`s a tremendous amount of leeway to do things to make people happy. The question is, what does he have to give up to become a speaker? \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 , both of whom -- both who Brendan work for both of those men have had to go through this at various times in their careers as well. The demands that the right is putting on Kevin McCarthy are ridiculous and he should -- and he would be crazy to accept them.\par \par \uc2 Why would he be crazy? Because once he starts giving into the right on any issue, he`s going to have to give in to them on every issue. And it`s not a precedent he wants to set. I spoke to him about this earlier this fall when he thought he was going to win 25 to 30 seats. And he said I don`t deal well with leverage. So I agree with Brendan, he has the inside track. But it isn`t an eternity until January.\par \par \uc2 And I think he will become a speaker. I mean, I don`t -- Jim Jordan cannot get 218 votes, I don`t think, and nor does he want to. I don`t know how McCarthy`s going to get there. That doesn`t mean I don`t think he will. I think he will. I think it`s going to be extremely bumpy and there are just tons of speed bumps in the way between now and January 3.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TUR: There`s also a question of how do you run that conference with the knowledge that you`ve got to try to win again in `24, and what do you go after, what do you pursue, and how much gridlock do you allow that might end up hurting you? And what sort of, I don`t know hearings, you might allow Jim Jordan to conduct. But I want to ask you a bit of a curveball. And this is going to be about Georgia. Say it comes down to Georgia controlling the Senate, which likely could, and there`s a month now before we`re going to find out what happens in that runoff. How does a lame-duck Congress function with that hanging over its head?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: This is just the most important question, Katy, that we have in front of us in Washington for the next eight or so weeks because the government runs out of money on December 16. That -- the Georgia office right then. So, you`re going to have a matter of days to fund the government potentially raise the debt limit, and do a whole host of serious heavy-lift legislating with very little time on the clock.\par \par \uc2 And furthermore, these deals are typically conducted between the four corners, the speaker, Minority Leader, Senate Majority Leader, Senate Minority Leader, there is no chance. And I think Brendan would agree with me here, there is virtually no chance Kevin McCarthy could be part of any of those discussions, even if he tacitly supports them, no way because his conference is going to be vehemently opposed. So this puts just a massive, massive amount of pressure on the rest of the institution going into the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Jake Sherman, thanks so much, who`s all over this leadership struggle and Congress, of course, thank you. And I want to bring in NBC`s chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker from the outside the White House. So, Kristen, Georgia, you covered \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and you`ve interviewed him in his hometown, what`s your take on how this runoff will shape up?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Andrea, first of all, I think that all sides were anticipating this. I just heard from someone within the Walker campaign who said that as soon as this seemed to be where things were heading overnight that they started to launch the runoff campaign. And so all sides are bracing for this. This is a senior Biden aide, Andrea, who said this. This is what we expected. We are filling up a bit of a runoff without \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  on the ticket. Helping Walker is a great shot for Warnock.\par \par \uc2 There`s no doubt though that control of the Senate could come down to Georgia, as it did two years ago. So this is certainly familiar terrain for all sides. The question I think, Andrea, that`s going to be interesting, will we see President Biden campaign for Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 ? He was not there during the first part of this race. And, of course, you saw Warnock kind of trying to distance himself.\par \par \uc2 What we saw overnight were some victories for some candidates who embraced President Biden, including \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who, of course, was locked in that very tough race in Pennsylvania. So that remains an open question because we`ve been reporting though, President Biden is expected to speak today in just a few hours from now and expected to take questions as well. So those will be I think, among the types of questions that we will be looking to get answers to.\par \par \uc2 And then more broadly speaking, Andrea, the White House really relieved feeling somewhat emboldened today because they were afraid that there would be that red wave, that did not materialize. But the question becomes where can they find common ground with Republicans if in fact, Republicans do take control of the House? And what happens next? How would they respond to those investigations that are potentially looming? We are told that the president will talk about the issue of abortion, the importance of that in these midterm elections. So a lot at stake, particularly when we expect to hear from President Biden in just a few hours from now, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And, Kristen, 2024 is already happening, even though it`s 2022. You know don`t everybody yell at once? Because of Donald Trump`s, you know, anticipated announcement and Ron DeSantis`s hugely successful Florida victory, and the rivalry already brewing on the Republican side. And now, what does President Biden do? How does he calculate it? Two-thirds of those in the exit poll said that they did not want him to seek re-election. And that -- you know, there was a rejection of his policies overall. And people think that his policies are taking us in the wrong direction. Well, how does he frame this decision?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is going to be the key question, Andrea, and there is no doubt that those numbers are staggering and that the president has to be taking a very hard look at that. I spoke with one of his top advisors today. He said, look, he`s always said that he intends to run for reelection. That has not changed as of today, and yet he has not made a final decision. We know that it`ll huddle with family members to reach that final decision ultimately. But the pressure, Andrea, is going to be on him to make that final decision because if he decides to bail out which is at this point in time no signs he`s going to do that, he`s going to want to make way for someone else. So that, of course, is on the table as well today as we prepare to address the president in just a few hours from now.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Kristen Welker. Kristen, thank you very much. I want to ask about something we saw in the NBC News exit polls. We put it up on screen a little while ago, and it showed the number of people that want to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run for president. And again, an overwhelming number of people said, no. You looked into the cross tabs, what do you -- what do you do if you`re the White House?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, two things. I mean, I think it`s important to look, as we were talking about this, the crosstabs 83 percent of Democrats say they do want to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run. I think what that does is gives him the political space. That`s important to make a very personal decision.\par \par \uc2 I -- you know, I think what Kristen said, I think what we`ve heard, even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said in interviews, he intends to run. Every president intends to run. Now they have to make this intensely personal decision about what they`re going to do. And I -- and I think that`s going to be a meeting there, we`ll see what the family decides we`ll see when they set up a legal apparatus, when do they start hiring people. I think this number, though, the exit poll, gives them a little bit of space to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Does it matter if it`s Trump running or if it`s DeSantis running? Does that make the decision for him?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well, probably hard, given what I think the Republican primary will be like to understand who that person is going to be when he has to make that decision. I think the one thing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can take out of this exit poll of all voters is I`m -- I don`t have the number in front of me but my guess is that number is lower for people that want to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  run. And so if you -- if you`re a Democrat, you`re thinking if I get the nomination, I`m a 50-50 shot, right? If I`m running against Trump, I`m probably better than a 50-50 shot. That`s what`s going to animate part of that decision-making.\par \par \uc2 And I would say too, one of the things is it`s a very easy decision about whether he goes to Georgia or not. Yes, the Warnock campaign, am I helpful? If the answer is yes, you gas up Air Force One.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: If the answer is no, you call \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  or somebody else. And, look, I remember being in the White House when we had to make some of these decisions. And we had to go out and talk about it. I`m happy to take a couple of bad questions in the briefing room about why my boss isn`t going to go. And then six weeks later win that race. That`s far more important than the spin of that day.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So, on Georgia, let`s go to Ellison Barber, who can give us the view from -- on the ground in Georgia. She is joining us now. Ellison, talk to us about this last hour and a half or so, I mean, not even since we`ve seen this projection now for the Georgia runoff. What you`re hearing?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, a lot changing -- a lot very quickly changing but not unexpected based on what we`ve seen in the polling. Kristen Welker was reporting Herschel Walker`s campaign. They have told her, they have told us in the past that they very much knew this could be a possibility, so did Warnock`s campaign. But it was a possibility both campaigns were hoping they could avoid. We do expect to hear from the Georgia Secretary of State on the election results in about an hour.\par \par \uc2 This morning, we`ve already seen the two campaign managers going back and forth on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with Warnock`s campaign manager tweeting at \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  that he underperformed Trump`s vote share in rural suburban and urban counties. He ended up calling last night a good night for his candidate, incumbent Senator Warnock, and a bad one for Walker. Walker`s campaign manager replying to that saying more than 50 percent of Georgians voted against the incumbent that spent more than $100 million.\par \par \uc2 This has been a fascinating race because, at the beginning, you had two candidates who had very well likability, a lot of name recognition in this state, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  because he is a legendary football player in this state, Senator Warnock, someone who is well-known given his position at Ebenezer Baptist Church and generally speaking, also well-liked. I think back to one of the very first campaign ads we saw hear from national Republicans that essentially started by saying, hey, we know that you think Senator Warnock is a good guy but he votes with President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  X amount of time, and this is why we think you should not vote for him.\par \par \uc2 So we started on a platform where both of these candidates had high name recognition, both of them relatively well-liked as people, then we saw this race just get more and more intense, more and more messy. You had a lot of allegations and questions about Republican Herschel Walker`s personal life in this race. He was accused by two women of paying for and encouraging them to have an abortion. He is a staunch anti-abortion candidate. He denied those allegations, but from some Republican0leaning voters who I spoke to, that gave them a bit of pause here.\par \par \uc2 And you do have the fact that Warnock spent a ton of money in this race. He outspent \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  by about $78 million, but they are still going to a runoff. This is a state that seems incredibly polarized. More polarized perhaps than it is purple. Back to you, guys.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ellison Barber, live for us there in Atlanta. Ellison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Buck, talk to us a little bit about how you see Georgia, given the numbers that we`ve been seeing in the exit polling and where this race is headed here.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  not being on the ballot is going to be a huge deal in Georgia. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  was a very popular governor but he also doesn`t have a great relationship with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Obviously, we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Kemp had a bit of a fight. And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was Donald Trump`s candidacy. And in the primary when Donald -- when Brian walked to the -- \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  was facing a challenge, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  didn`t do anything to help him. And \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  remembers that. And so while \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was struggling throughout this general election to get to that 50 percent, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  didn`t do anything to lift the finger for him.\par \par \uc2 So, I`ll be very curious to see whether the Kemp apparatus is very popular, Georgia comes out to try to save \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  at this point. But I got to think with just the two of them head to head with so much at stake, and a lot of frankly, momentum for Democrats right now that the runoff is going to favor Warnock. And we saw this two years ago, we saw a lot of democratic energy come in and steal away a Republican seat in the deep south. So, if I`m the Walker campaign, I`m nervous going into a runoff right now.\par \par \uc2 TUR: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  lost the governorship but there`s no doubt that her Get Out the Vote campaign or effort down there has helped to transform Georgia. Brendan, thank you very much. Let`s go out west and get an update from our reporters who are still in those not yet called races. Joining me from Phoenix is NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard, and in Las Vegas is Guad Venegas. So, Vaughn, give us an update on what is happening there in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: I think that we just got some new numbers here from the Maricopa County Recorder`s Office. And what these numbers are ultimately going to be telling us is the fact that we will not likely know the results out of Arizona until Thursday night. You can see the governor`s race. You see a 12,000 gap now separating \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  from Kari Lake. You see Blake Masters`s race. He is a larger gap. He has more room than he has to make up here.\par \par \uc2 But there is a particular batch of about 275,000 ballots that we frankly do not know the trend lines whether they will favor \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  or Kari Lake. These are mail ballots, the folks received in their mailboxes, but instead of mailing them back, they walked into their polling places yesterday and hand-delivered them. Maricopa county officials say that they will not tabulate those ballots until tomorrow, and will not release the results of several tens of thousands of them until tomorrow night, probably around 9:00 p.m. Eastern. So everybody should take a pause here over the next 36 hours because there`s a lot more tabulating votes to do. We`re talking about in Maricopa County alone, still 400,000 ballots here at hand. So we`ve got a few days ahead of us looks like. Back to you, guys.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Take a breath is the -- is the lesson here. Vaughn, just from what you know about that state, do you have an idea of who`s more likely what political affiliation is more likely to get a ballot in the mail and then walk to drop it off?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right. This is the difficult aspect of it. And in 2018, I`m going to take you back here. On election night, Republican Martha McSalley had the lead. And then it was this batch of votes, these mail ballots that actually surged \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , the Democrat, to ultimately serving a term here in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . Then go to 2020, it was those ballots that actually benefited \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And what we saw his -- was a large gap between him and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ultimately come down to just 10,500 votes. He won those ballots by about 55 percent to 45 percent. And so that is where there`s a lot of question marks.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s -- we should note also if I may, tonight, we`re going to be getting more results here out of Maricopa County. And yes, over the course of the night we saw Kari Lake actually shrink the margin. We may and could be anticipating \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  to actually re-expand her lead here tonight. I know this is very complicated, but the balance that you`re going to be seeing dropped tonight, those were mail ballots that were received by the county on Saturday, Sunday, Monday, and \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is overwhelmingly won votes that were mailed back. So, you should see that 12,000 margin balloon backup before tomorrow, likely shrinking again. It`s a matter of whether Kari Lake is ultimately able to eclipse \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . Obviously, for Blake Masters, it`s going to be even tougher, guys.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Vaughn Hillyard, you`ve got a lot of waiting to do as they count those ballots. Thank you so much. And Guad Venegas -- Guad Venegas is in Nevada, the other state that`s still too close to call. Republicans are up in the Senate and governor`s races though. Guad, when we get to know more there?\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Andrea, there`s more waiting happening here. We were told it could be a few days until they count all of these mail-in ballots. I was listening to Vaughn describe what`s happening in Arizona, you know, a lot of the same is happening here. Except here, we`re paying attention to two counties obviously, Washoe County, the second largest.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 We have an idea of how many mail-in ballots came in on Election Day there, it`s less than 20,000 so that`s good just to get an understanding when you see how close these races are, right? So, the other county is, of course, here Clark County, where the vast majority of voters are in Nevada. We still don`t know how many mail0in votes came in on Election Day. So, you look at the two big races.\par \par \uc2 First, the race for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the incumbent, and \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the challenger who is up by 23,000 votes, that`s the difference. It is very narrow. So, we need to have an understanding of how many mail-in votes came in, how many ballots came in here to Clark County to see what kind of a leader -- what this lead means for \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 . He`s up by 22,000 so if say, Clark County has about 100,000 votes or ballots that is that they still have to count, then \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  has a real chance of catching up.\par \par \uc2 But we won`t find out until a little maybe an hour and a half from now when they`re we`re going to be speaking to the press show. That`s when we`re going to get some answers from Clark County. They`re going to tell us how many mail-in ballots they have. That`s what we`re expecting. And more details to see how long it will take to have some more information here, Andrea.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It`s not just, Guad, the Nevada Senate race where we still don`t have a projection right as we`re waiting for this vote count to come in, but the Nevada governor`s race too which is interesting, especially when you look at some of the issues that have been in play there big time. Crime, as you will know, former Sheriff, etcetera, up against the incumbent Democrat, the economy, when you look at the inflation numbers and the data is seen.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Right, Hallie. So, in that race, you look at the difference. Lombardo, the challenger, is about 40,000 votes up from Governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 . So, you know, we -- talking to nonpartisan voters over the last few months and we think that what could happen here from what they told us -- so you could see some split tickets from these nonpartisan. A lot of them told us look, when I go vote -- and I mentioned the non-partisan is because there`s a large number of non-partisan voters here in Nevada, they -- a lot of them told us I might be voting for \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , and for Joe Lombardi, so we could see some split tickets. For now, things do look better for Joe Lombardo with those 40,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 Again, both for Lombardo and Laxalt, the two Republicans who are up, they`re waiting to see how many votes still have to get counted here in Clark County to have an understanding of what it means to have that type of lead. And only then will we have an idea. What officials have told us is that it could be a few days until they are done counting all of these votes, but we`ll just have to wait and see how many votes they still have inside this processing center.\par \par \uc2 TUR: We will set our alarms for 2:30 today for that news conference out of Clark County. Guad Venegas, thank you very much. Let`s bring in Maria Teresa Kumar now to get further into this. Maria, what are you watching for?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, PRESIDENT AND CEO, VOTO LATINO: Right now in Nevada, specifically in Arizona, we -- what we`ve been looking for specifically is what is the turnout of young Latinos? Young Latinos, for folks may not realize, they`re the larger share of the Latino voting population in both the states. And they have been lagging behind in both -- it since the last results we got on Monday. So that I think is going to help determine who`s going to control the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And then in Georgia -- one of the things that folks didn`t 