 have gone to a CPAC gathering or other kind of conservative conference, there have been straw polls done that showed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leading the PAC but \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  right there after him and seen as the heir apparent to the Trump mantle if you take Trump out of those straw polls.\par \par \uc2 Now everything is unofficial at this point but at the same time it`s a good gauge of how the Republican Party is thinking about what its future might look like. Clearly still in the Trump image, but maybe not with Trump himself as the actual standard bearer. We also know that that`s probably what the former president`s mindset is too because he`s out there giving nicknames, giving voice to the schism that has now been in place between DeSantis, a former ally and acolyte of his, and Trump, this is something that I`ve been hearing about from sources for months, but the fact that it`s now being litigated, he put that nickname out there for DeSantis just a few days before DeSantis` own reelection here, a day before Trump actually came to the state and campaigned, not with DeSantis, but with Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , it`s clear that there`s some bad blood that`s brewing here already.\par \par \uc2 And yes, it`s annoying to talk about presidential politics when it`s still squarely calendar year 2022, but first of all, we love this stuff, we do it so that no one else has to, but also this is what the reality is. The day after the midterms are over, it`s when we immediately pivot to what`s next because all the candidates are doing that. Operatives I`m talking to are doing that and donors I`m talking to are doing that. They`re ready to start seeing what this field might look like.\par \par \uc2 And again, it`s not just \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who is probably thinking about this, it`s Trump but it`s also people like Nikki Haley, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 . We know the names of people who are thinking about it. Now is just the time that we get to start talking about it.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Ali, and consider how many Taylor Swift lyrics you can work into discussing these races.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: So many.\par \par \uc2 TUR: And the standoff between these two men. The bad blood.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ryan Nobles -- glad you explained it. Like I`m not a Taylor Swift person.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You didn`t get it? Come on.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yes. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Bad blood.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I get it now.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That was brilliant, Katy.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Trump was a drag on lots of candidates last night. But he`s still claiming victory. His last post on Truth Social says, quote, "174 wins and 9 losses. A great evening. Amazing job by some really fantastic candidates." But he also had sine seriously high profile defeats like Dr. Oz, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania, Tudor Nixon in Michigan, I think of Bolduc in New Hampshire. So we`re hearing the public messaging but what are the private whispers in Trump world right now?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: First of all, thanks for not putting me on the spot with any Taylor Swift lyrics. I could have gone there if you asked me. But I`m glad I didn`t to take that challenge.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You`re a better man, Ryan Nobles. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TUR: I`m going to get some fresh lyrics in and I`m going to throw you all over the bridge.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Yes. But to get back to the thoughts in Trump world this morning, obviously this is not the narrative that they were expecting when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  called all of this closest advisers to Mar-a-Lago last night for what he expected to be the type of party where he could take credit for everything and the fact that now they`re being forced to deal with this narrative that perhaps he was a drag on some of these candidates that he had hoped would win, and maybe could be preventing the Republicans from taking control of the Senate, and a much narrow, much more narrow majority in the House, is a big problem for Trump as he makes the case that he is the most dominant candidate in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 But Trump is already doing what Trump does, right. He`s finding a way to spin these results in his -- to his benefit. He`s focusing on the fact that he did put out a ton of endorsements over the course of the 2022 campaign and just focusing on those largely noncompetitive races where his candidates were successful and he`s either ignoring the results in places like Pennsylvania or finding blame.\par \par \uc2 For instance, in the race in New Hampshire, where he claimed that Don Bolduc, the Republican nominee there, didn`t focus enough on the 2020 election in his race against the incumbent \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  which Hassan won easily. So this is all against the backdrop of what Ali was talking about. He`s already announced plans for a major announcement next week here in Palm Beach and whether or not that is still going to be to announce a race for president we`re going to have to wait and see.\par \par \uc2 But this is obviously not the narrative that Trump and his team were expecting to come out of the results from last night.\par \par \uc2 TUR: So, Phil, if Trump does announce, which I think it looks like he`s going to, and if DeSantis says, hey, listen, look what I`ve been able to do and look what Trump is not able to do, I`m also going to get in this race, my question is, how ugly does it get and how damaging does it get? We already had Trump yesterday saying that he knows secrets about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and only his wife knows. It reminded me of the stuff that he went after, you remember this, Halle, Ted Cruz with in 2016.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, THE WASHINGTON POST DEPUTY NATIONAL EDITOR: Yes. Katy, it could certainly get ugly. I mean, we know how nasty these campaigns can be especially when Trump is one of the candidates.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 But