ly a four-week runoff. That means mail ballots which were a big advantage for Democrats not just in Georgia but around the country won`t be as big of a factor in Georgia in the runoff. That said, Senator Warnock and his team know how to win in these runoff situations. They did it two years ago. They`ll be looking to do it again next month.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And Tom, what are the possible Senate scenarios you`re looking out for in terms of which one goes which way and Georgia becomes the -- all-in race?\par \par \uc2 BONIER: Yes. Well, I think we`ll know a lot more just in terms of the landscape, right, and that Republicans need to win at this point two of those three remaining seats to be called. Arizona is one that I don`t think they feel as good about right now. If that goes then they have to run the table and they would have to then win Nevada and then win Georgia in the runoff. That`s certainly something that`s difficult.\par \par \uc2 And again, if these numbers we are hearing out of Clark County that we`re seeing potentially another 100,000 votes coming in, and again we should be finding out about that momentarily, that makes Nevada look difficult for them, too. So they`re facing an uphill battle. It`s not that it can`t be done but they are facing a scenario where if they don`t win one of Arizona or Nevada, then this runoff isn`t for control anymore.\par \par \uc2 TUR: We`ll see what happens. \uc2 Tom Bonier\uc2 , thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We have some news from Steve Scalise making it official, not going to be challenging Kevin McCarthy, will be running for majority leader, though, officially. That is if the Republicans take back the House.\par \par \uc2 And over to Colorado, it was a good night for Democrats. They took the governor`s mansion, the Senate, the AG`s office and four out of six House races that are called so far. Two House races including that surprising race that has incumbent Republican \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  trailing, are still uncalled at this hour. You can see how close the margin is right there.\par \par \uc2 Let us bring in Colorado Democratic Senator Michael Bennet who won again last night, won his re-election.\par \par \uc2 Michael -- Senator, excuse me, always good to have you. What do you chalk this up to?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): I chalk this up to a relentless economic message, Katy, about how 40 years of Washington consensus, about outsourcing and about trickledown economics just isn`t working for the American people and it was a clear distinction between me and my opponent on that. I think that it`s a repudiation of Donald Trump`s chaos and a recognition that we actually made progress capping drug prices for seniors, for example, requiring Medicare to negotiate on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, you know, the overturning of Roe versus Wade was something that was -- made people furious. So that combination of those three things made it a very good night in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator Bennett, it`s Halle. I wonder how much you see the Trump factor is at play in your race. I spoke with your opponent just a couple of days before the election and asked him about that. He was very clear as he said before, he is his own man. We know that the former president went after him in Colorado. How much do you see that as being a factor in your victory and your return to the Senate?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Yes. I don`t think it was a huge factor because I don`t think my opponent was ever very persuasive on that subject. I mean, he had voted twice for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and he got religion three weeks before the election day, but I do think it`s fascinating to see counties, and you know these counties well, like Douglas County, where I only lost by six points last night, El Paso County where I only lost by six points last night, those are places Democrats historically, including myself, you know, expect to lose by as much as 20 points. And what we saw in red and rural Colorado was something very different this year.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Senator, looking ahead to 2024, I know that`s painful, we`re barely surviving after being up all night on 2022, but our exit polling does show that more than two-thirds of voters do not want President Biden to run again. You align with the president certainly before the midterms. Where do you stand on whether Biden 2024 should be the candidate, the standard bearer, after seeing these results?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Yes. First let me say I do think we`re going to win in Nevada and in Arizona, and that`s going to give us a real shot at Georgia as well, which I`m really excited about before we go to `24.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 And then I`m going to say what you would probably expect, which is this is up to President Biden to decide. You know, he`s going to have to consider a lot of different factors in making this decision. But it is true that he was the one person out of 330 million people that beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and I think that if there were a rematch based on the results we just saw last night, I don`t think there`s any doubt that he would beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  again.\par \par \uc2 TUR: What do you think of the \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  race?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: It`s amazing. I talked to her opponent today. The race is very, very close and I think she`s going to have a hard time making up her deficit, and I think this is what happens when you treat your constituents with contempt and you spend all of your time on national television instead of doing the hard work that people of the western slope of Colorado deserve, and in Pueblo, Colorado, deserve out of their congressional representative. You know, we should know something I think later today.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Is Colorado a purple state or would you say Colorado is now a blue state?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I would say it`s a purple state. We -- Katy, we have the largest registration by far in Colorado of unaffiliated voters and I think that`s why anybody who`s in one of these jobs is smart to run like you`re 20 points behind, which is how I treated this race and I`ve treated every one of my races. I don`t -- I think Colorado will be a purple state for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Let`s talk if the Senate is as tight as we think it`s going to be, we know it is obviously going to be tight, right. How do you see the role of one of your colleagues, for example, and listen, this is all hypothetical until we know more results here. Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , right? What are your governing priorities in the Senate especially if the House does end up with a House Speaker Kevin McCarthy, are you looking to reconcile bills on that front from a governing perspective?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I mean, I think it`s going to be honestly very difficult with the House that`s led by Kevin McCarthy. I have not had trouble finding Republicans in the Senate to work with, but I mean, McCarthy is so beholden to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Maybe that will change. I doubt it will, which means that it`s going to be, you know, there`s going to be a lot of friction in the air. The Senate can continue to approve judges and I think we should pass bills that show what we stand for compared to what McCarthy and the Republicans stand for.\par \par \uc2 And that needs to be, as I said, a very explicit economic message that is going to resonate with working people in red and blue parts of America. The Democrat -- long overdue from the Democratic Party. I think we`ve begun turning the page, bringing the semiconductor industry back, fighting and winning those wins over pharma that we`ve been fighting for for generation, but there`s more that we can do.\par \par \uc2 We can pass bills to reverse Trump tax cuts for the rich and extend my tax cuts for working people that I wrote with \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 . I mean, I think there`s ways of creating distinction and looking for opportunities to work together if we can find places where we can get stuff done on behalf of the American people.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Senator Bennet, thank you so much for being with us and congratulations on your election victory.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Thank you for having me today. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: It`s great to have you. See you. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the misinformation flooding social media, as the right`s hopes of a red wave falls short. Ben Collins joins us to break it all down on our continuing coverage of the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. This is MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:32]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And Republicans are not seeing the red wave they`ve hoped for, and in fact, we`ve just gotten another concession, this is from election denying \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is the Republican who really tried and competed very hard to defeat \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  in her first actual competition because she had not actually run before for governor. But Zeldin`s concession had not come last night, but he did concede today. Of course many are hoping that the base will accept these results. But for more on this and on the misinformation that comes from election denial, which was spreading last night, NBC News senior reporter Ben Collins.\par \par \uc2 So, Ben -- where`s Ben? It`s a big day.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Ben is usually right here. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It is a 900-foot table.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Special coverage.\par \par \uc2 Ben, let`s talk about what you were seeing last night because there were a number of election deniers who were trying to, you know, repeat the Trump memes and in the middle of a race that was not even that close.\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: They would be in denial.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: That`s exactly right. And I do think there was a moment in the evening, I was watching all of the shows, I was watching Infowars and \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , and there was a moment where they just realized, like, wait a second, we don`t have a plan for this. They were all red wave all the time. They were high on their own supply all evening. And then there was a moment where they looked up at the actual results and realized that red wave was not there.\par \par \uc2 And it was fascinating watching it happen because they live in -- they are so sectioned off from reality in these spaces, where the idea that things aren`t going right, that things aren`t going to work out at the end of the day for them, it`s just not possible, and then when it becomes reality, they have to really adjust. And I think they did have a backup plan really quickly, where they thought like, you know, worse comes to worse, it will be 50-49. We`ll go to Arizona, we`ll contest everything. We`ll make it hell for everybody.\par \par \uc2 But that might not actually matter. If Democrats win Nevada, you know, contesting that state is not going to matter for the Senate. So they are on their back foot right now. And I think they were really -- look, I`m not saying, you know, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is looking inward, but I`m saying for the first time he might actually have to reflect.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: One of the things that you were talking about during the commercial break, Ben, is that you think that what the lawsuits, Dominion, Alex Jones, maybe mattered.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:00]\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. I think so. I think if you consistently, over and over again, realize that this might have consequences to lie about private figures, it`s money. To lie about election officials, to lie about a company that will sue you for billions of dollars, which is what places like FOX News and \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  are facing, that stuff can`t siphon all the way up to the top anymore. That`s stuff that may have ended up on FOX News at the end of the day, that started on a blog or started on 4Chan, now that stuff just ends up stuck.\par \par \uc2 It gets stuck in these zones where, you know, only the people who are already pot committed, people like Alex Jones, people like the Gateway pundit, who are already being sued by these people, or Mike Lindell, those are the only people still talking about the fraud, whereas on FOX News, they have to face reality.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Going for broke, as they might end up being broke regardless.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUR: What about the rest of the dark inner Webs that you look through? Anything concerning that you`re seeing?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, look, there is a large portion, so like the people who planned the insurrection still have their forum and they gave up on democracy a long time ago. And by the way, we should have factored this in a little bit more before people started voting, I think. There is a large section of the Trump base that simply does not want to vote anymore. They just want to get violent, and maybe those people didn`t show up.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Ben Collins, it`s good to have you on, the expert on all of this. Thank you, sir. It`s good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot more coming up on our next hour of special coverage right here on MSNBC. We`ll hear from Georgia`s secretary of state. We`re going to hear the latest on the vote count over in Nevada in Clark County there. I`m going to say a quick goodbye and sneak out to my other job over on the NBC News side, but you will be in very capable hands with Andrea and Katy. See you in a bit.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be re