nnouncement.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC ANCHOR: And let`s be real about what we`re all doing right now, right? This and this and looking and seeing what we got, and looking at the board.\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: I`m just waiting for Steve Kornacki.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I mean, when is he going to walk? He`s in the room. We`re getting over. Because we have races out, right. We have vote out and potential projections that we could make in some of these key Senate races. And let`s not overstate it, right. We think it`s going to be a while before we`re seeing anything in Nevada or in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the big news, just as we`re coming on the air here is this projection that NBC News has made in Wisconsin, right, where we are projecting Republican Senator Ron Johnson, the incumbent, of course, as the winner with a third term by defeating the lieutenant governor, the Democrat in that race, Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, keep in mind, it is still too close to call. You see Ron Johnson there, the projected winner. Let`s talk about Georgia where both Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his Republican challenger former football star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Listen, neither of them have hit that 50 percent threshold to avoid a runoff. If we get to a runoff, do I have to tell you when it is, December 6. We`re in election month, we know it potentially, right, if this does go to a runoff, people are bracing for it.\par \par \uc2 There are also two battleground Senate races that are too early to call out in Arizona. Now look, look at the numbers here. You have Senator Mark Kelly, who is leading the Republican Blake Masters with just over two thirds of the vote in, but NBC again too early to call. And then you have perhaps the most vulnerable incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  up against \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  in Nevada with a lot of votes to be counted in two critical counties, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TUR: So let us take a look at the big picture as well. Those big questions are still outstanding. The Senate is still a jump ball with four calls to go counting the race in Alaska that will be a Republican hold, over in the House. It is the clearest visual of that Republican wave that did not materialize and the projection of a slim GOP majority that could cause massive political headaches for, I don`t know, someone like Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 And for the House Democrats, a big embarrassment. Campaign Committee Chairman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  conceded just over an hour ago losing his reelection bid after he was redrawn out of his own district. And governor`s races, a crisis averted for Democrats in New York, historic gains in Massachusetts and Maryland. A big win this morning in Kansas and a national rejection of Republican candidates who refuse to say they would not have certified President Biden`s victory in 2020.\par \par \uc2 All right, everyone, let us get right to the conversations. I know we want to answer a lot of these big questions. We got our panel here. We have Michael Steele joining us in person, good to see you. Our friend, the former \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair Mark Murray as well, also great to see you. NBC News Political Director Robert Gibbs, former Press Secretary in the Obama White House, and Donna Edwards, a former Maryland Congresswoman. Donna, we miss you here in the studio.\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to start with you, Mark Murray. Steve Kornacki is going to join us in a little while. But let`s talk about these outstanding races. What`s happening in Arizona? What`s happening in Nevada? And we`re probably going to see a run off in Georgia, but is it tightening even more so there?\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY\uc2 , NBC NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL EDITOR: So let`s start with Nevada, which actually think is going to be the most fascinating state for us to follow over the next 24, 48, 72 hours whenever we get the vote. And right now, you have \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  with about a lead of about less than three percentage points. But we know that there is significant outstanding votes, particularly in Clark County, that`s Las Vegas, as well as in Washoe.\par \par \uc2 And the question, there`s a lot of mail-in ballots that disproportionately have ended up benefiting Democrats. So we just have to count these outstanding votes. And it is possible that Laxalt holds on his lead, it`s also possible that \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  winds up -- will going to pulling ahead. And if that happens, then all of a sudden we actually might know the balance of power in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  before we even get to Georgia.\par \par \uc2 [12:05:13]\par \par \uc2 TUR: It would be at least a 50-50 at that point, right?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Before we get the Georgia run off. Now back up, we still need to get through Arizona Senate as well, too. And right now, you have Mark Kelly with the lead, as we have seen from previous cycles, whether in 2018, or 2020. Arizona also takes a while too and it also has this, like the Democrat takes the lead, and then the Republican catches up, it goes back and forth, back and forth.\par \par \uc2 So I think the only guidance I have is it`s going to take a little bit of time, but hopefully, we`ll have some clarity maybe tomorrow, maybe the day after, but it`s coming soon.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That`s optimistic.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Let me follow up on that just a second on Arizona, because we saw the bouncing around last time and that is what fed a lot of the misinformation that false Trump claims all kinds of accusations and no one, you know, accepting the result. So from what you see now in Arizona, do you think Kelly`s lead is going to shrink?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Andrea, I just don`t -- we`re going to have to see the vote come in. But as you ended up mentioning, you know, about the doubts to how Arizona is conducting their elections, I will say covering how Arizona conducts their elections, particularly gigantic Maricopa County, most people are professionals. They do great work there.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, what I`m actually focused on is Kari Lake in that governor`s race, because we talked about Mark Kelly in the balance power, that governor`s race is a lot closer. And as our colleague Vaughn Hilliard was reporting yesterday, you already had Kari Lake doubting the -- if she -- if I`m losing, it`s because I`m being cheated. She said the exact same stuff in her primary that she ended up winning.\par \par \uc2 So I do think, you know, let`s count all the vote, let`s let our colleagues at the decision desk do their great job. Let`s have it Kornacki break it down on the board. But I do think we`ll have some more clarity in the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: OK, so we`re talking Senate, let`s talk about House for a second. Because did you have on your bingo card that at noon on Wednesday, wouldn`t have balanced --\par \par \uc2 TUR: I did.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: -- power in the House?\par \par \uc2 TUR: I did.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: (INAUDIBLE), I mean, the Powerball.\par \par \uc2 TUR: I did.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: OK, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: She said it.\par \par \uc2 TUR: I think the polling has been -- you know, the polling -- and Mark, you can talk more about this and not to steal your thunder here at least.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Steal away.\par \par \uc2 TUR: But the polling has been -- I mean, we`re hearing from pollsters that they`re just not sure about the data that we have. And we`re living in such a wild moment with such crazy policies at stake, such crazy divergent issues, answers to questions that might sound the same, but have totally different motivations and reasoning behind them. It`s just so hard to predict.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And by the way, do you pick up an unknown number when it calls you, like if a pollster is going to --\par \par \uc2 TUR: Never.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right? Like who 