st. Actually Kornacki call that on MJ yesterday morning on Morning Joe, we --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You talked about it --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: We talked about it at noon, that this could be a case where \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  did well enough against Hochul to pull his supporters in those districts and there were five of those districts, and two in Long Island, three in upstate New York. That the five seats could be decided right there that the Republicans needed. But -- so that said, that`s partly what happened. Three out of those five.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Undoubtedly. If you look at -- I mean, I forget how I wouldn`t even know how much Biden won New York by in 2020 because I wouldn`t have paid attention to it, right?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: But the idea that you`ve got Hochul maybe --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Then Hochul was such a weak candidate.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: -- plus five, plus six means probably a 14 or 15-point shift in this sort of atmospherics of the electorate and that`s going to swap some people and it did -- particularly for somebody who faced a lot of money, but was also in a different district, right? He`s spent -- there are a lot of news --\par \par \uc2 [12:10:12]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: By his choice, though.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Kind of it. Well, and some challenges probably with Democrats, none too happy about how --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Out \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  in that district. I spent some time in that district speaking to voters, and a lot of them would bring up crime. I mean, that`s currently the headline of every New York Post paper that we see here in New York --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: But not the headline of the exit poll.\par \par \uc2 TUR: No, no. But if you turn on the television here, the amount of advertising the Republicans had on the air was astounding.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUR: And the pushback the Democrats had, especially with the \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  race against Mike Lawler, they were trying to tie him to extremists, but they could only do so one step removed, which was not as effective for them. Look who Mike Lawler stands next to and then they talked about a firm that he had worked that -- worked with that had supported somebody who stormed the Capitol. That was as best that they could get to try and to tie him to the extremism of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 So when you`re looking at what worked, Michael Steele, across the country here in New York, across the country, I think it`s interesting that Democrats had a much easier time against the people that they were actually able to paint as extremists. The ones who were not in favor of democracy, the ones who said 2020 was stolen, the ones who were more supportive of what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Although the exit polls did show that in places like New Hampshire, the voters said that both sides were extreme, Republicans thought Democrats were extremely --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: But \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  still won by a mile.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I think to your point, Katy, it didn`t start out that way. And, you know, in the afterglow, of seemingly important victory for Democrats, because it is a victory to do what they did last night and achieve, you know, sort of force of keeping repelling the wave, it wasn`t always that way.\par \par \uc2 If you go back six weeks, you know, everybody had their head in their soup and walking around like little zombies, you know, thinking the sky is falling, but not -- but there were those who saw something and it goes to how you started the conversation, who saw something different because they were listening to what voters actually tell them.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: And as Robert and I know very well, voters will always tell you what they think. They just can`t help but they will. You ask them a question, they`re going to tell you, no I`m not happy.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Or they`re going to tell you, I don`t think anything. Don`t ask me this question.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Exactly. You always have an understanding of where they are. And I think that they were tells along the way. And one of the pollsters that I latched on to because I had an outlier view at the beginning of the summer, but how this would play out where I was on Velshi show on a Saturday morning and I said, Democrats, my estimation, looking at everything and talking to people, they`re going to hold the House. And if Republicans win, they`ll probably get somewhere no more than 18 seats.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Have you bought a Powerball ticket?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: No, I did not.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 STEELE: But the (INAUDIBLE), I have it on TV and I have it on my podcast because --\par \par \uc2 TUR: You have a date, you know who you are with.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Oh no. I remember because I was laughed out of the studio.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: And I was vilified by people. And I was reinforced by Simon Rosenberg. Noted democratic pollster, had him on my podcast back in early October and we were talking about this very thing that you just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 TUR: But it`s talking to voters.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: It`s talking to voters.\par \par \uc2 TUR: It`s not necessarily -- I`m sorry, Mark, relying on the poll thing all the time. And I know the pollster --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And let me just say, the NBC poll mark, let`s defend the NBC poll mark.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: We acknowledge when it`s wrong, but this time we were doing close.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Our poll ended up finding that Democrats had that enthusiasm in that --\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: -- winning weak and that actually it means so much in the midterm cycle. You know, the big reason why the party in power takes a shellacking usually is that your side just isn`t as fired up as the opposition. You know very well.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: When you were at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  in the 2010 cycle, that getting that opposition, you know, the opposition`s already fired up.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: It`s getting the people in power, who`s -- like that`s the work. And the -- I think the power of the Dobbs decision in first really kind of igniting that Democratic enthusiasm that we ended up seeing in the summer was really important and our polling actually picked up on that. I think were a lot of people kind of ended up saying, well, it looks like it`s going to be headed for a really big Republican night is the humongous confidence and overconfidence from a lot of Republicans the last two weeks of the election where it`s like, well, you know, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  was on Meet the Press, hey, I think we`re going to have 52,53 Senate seats.\par \par \uc2 There was a lot of confidence in a game in which usually, even if you`re showing some really good cards or you have some good cards, you try to temper your expectations. You`re playing the expectation games like, oh, we just want to get Senate majority or House majority. That`s all we care about. But our poll did pick up that enthusiasm from Democrats and, you know, I think the kind of question going forward, you know, is, you know, are these kind of normal midterm dynamics that we ended up saying?\par \par \uc2 [12:15:09]\par \par \uc2 Because even in 2018, you know, it was a good democratic cycle, but it wasn`t great. We have big Republican wins in Florida and Georgia, and a lot of that Cavanaugh factors where Republicans were equally fired up headed that election even up blunting some Democratic games that cycle.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And let me just say to everyone, the President will be responding. We expected this to happen. The President is going to be responding at 4:00 p.m. in the White House. So we`re, of course, going to be live on that. Robert Gibbs?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I was going to say they just extend your time because -- no, just one point on a polling, look, every poll assumes some educated hopefully, guess on what the electorate looks like.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Right? And I think it`s pretty clear Republicans expected a far different electorate. A good friend of mine, a pollster Danny Franklin that I work with, had a good sort of thinking about this this morning. Voters are good at telling you, to Michael`s point, how they feel, what they feel.\par \par \uc2 They may not be as good at predicting --\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: -- in six weeks what they`re going to do, right?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: And so, I think we had a sense that abortion was going to be big. I think we had a sense that inflation was going to be big. But how all of that wound through six, eight -- the last six, eight weeks of this race. And what it spits out at the end can be very different. It could be a confluence of a lot of things, seeing more election deniers, watching more stories on that, seeing extremist positions on abortion.\par \par \uc2 I think that sort of denialism, Trump popping back up, I think that may have had people thinking not differently than what they told a pollster, but the outcome of what they did clearly was different. I mean, I will say that one of the numbers that really stood out to me, Mark and I were talking about a second ago, I don`t think anybody would have given me -- would have not taken the bet if I said, I think Democrats are going to win independence on this election -- in this election.\par \par \uc2 49 to 48, according to the exit polls last night. So that -- it even -- we were talking about this, you had Democratic and Republican enthusiasm. Everyone`s worried about independence. Well, you will see. And I think it was -- it`s pretty remarkable there was a rejection of that extremism, that rejection of that denialism, that really had accumulated in a way that Republicans never really dealt with.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: But you know what the turning point was, it was that little thing that no one seemingly cared about. It was the January 6 committee. You go back to the beginning of the January 6 hearings, and the national mood is certainly, you know, projected by Republicans was, no one`s going to care about this. This is like, you know, who cares?\par \par \uc2 This is beginning of the summer, I`m going to go to the beach, I`m going to hang out, I`m going to work on the tan, I`m going to be beautiful. The reality of it was 22 million people tuned in on the first night.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: And over the course of the summer and into the fall, they stay.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: And they listened, and they learned and they connected dots. And I think the one thread which the great polling by NBC News brought out right before the weekend before this election, was when it said that the number one issue was what?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Threats to democracy.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Democracy. And that was not on your bingo card at the beginning of this poll.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Nor was the attack on Paul Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right. So the combination to both those points, the combination of forces really rounded out the picture, I think, for voters in a way that Republicans just didn`t bank on when it came to your point, how they would vote six weeks later.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Should we sneak in what Hallie flagged this earlier, what Glenn Youngkin send to Pelosi today, an apology. The governor of Virginia sending an apology.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUR: A written apology to Pelosi about joking about the Paul Pelosi`s attack.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I think apology going to catch up with the slam before the election?\par \par \uc2 TUR: Well, let me ask you why did he do that today? Anyone have an idea?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I think in a -- let`s assume he runs for president. I think there is a lane for a hopeful, optimistic, forward-looking Republican candidate. That`s the model of a Reagan. I think that`s the lane Glenn Youngkin wants to occupy. Glenn Youngkin, he`s not going to get into his sleeveless fleece and out yell everybody else. He`s got to occupy a distinct more hopeful lane, and I think that`s probably a big part of it.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I will tell you, I mean, there are some Republican donors who are hoping that a Youngkin 2024 thing happens. Donna Edwards, I know you are eager to jump into the conversation here, please do.\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. DONNA EDWARDS (D), MARYLAND: Well, I mean, there are a couple of things that I think accumulated over this spring and into the summer. One is that all along I had been saying that Republicans were nominating some of the most extreme candidates in House seat and in Senate seat. And that independence and moderates, centrist Democrats we`re not going to stomach those candidates.\par \par \uc2 [12:20:06]\par \par \uc2 Then the Dobbs decision happens and there`s a huge uptick of young people registering to vote, all through the summer. And those are people who would never be registered in any polling that was going on. And I think that that had a huge factor. If you look at the long lines on college campuses, and in communities where there are colleges of young people who were showing up to vote.\par \par \uc2 And then you add to that, some people question whether President Biden should have given that democracy speech at the close of the campaign, it turns out that, of course, that was really smart to do because these races were always going to be decided on the margin. Some people were going in on abortion, politics, others on democracy.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day for independence and for young voters that those are things that we`re going to move the races in the margins. And so, I remember yesterday, early in the day, I said, don`t believe conventional wisdom, because conventional wisdom only plays out if it`s not -- if it`s a normal midterm. This was anything but normal.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: And joining us now is Republican Congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , a member, of course of the January 6 committee where the two Republicans on the committee, who has had death threats and all kinds of abuse for being on that committee.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if you were listening, but we were just talking with Michael Steele about how he thought the January 6 committee hearings were a turning point in terms of changing the ideas of the electorate about what democracy means and what a threat means. And perhaps also, we should add the President`s closing arguments that people derided.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R), ILLINOIS: Yes, and I think he`s right, you know, and he`s actually the first person I`ve heard say this, because, you know, I guess the conventional wisdom, and the thing and I was been kind of, I`ve been holding on to is, look, the Committee will make an impact in future history. It will tell the truth and schoolbooks that the kids are going to learn about, but I don`t know if it`ll have an impact on this race.\par \par \uc2 I think it did. And it may not be the thing that people voted on. But I think it was cumulative. So whether it was abortion, whether it was January 6, whether it was Paul Pelosi, these things add up to an uncomfortable feeling for people. This is -- I mean, if you`re Kevin McCarthy right now, you`re in a really bad place. He`s got to be feeling terrible, which doesn`t bother me in any way.\par \par \uc2 So I look at that and say, it`s -- now a quick thing to Democrats, real quick. Candidate quality matters. I think you could have beaten Ron Johnson, in Wisconsin with a more centrist candidate. But that said, Democrats had much better candidates and Republicans did and that`s obvious.\par \par \uc2 TUR: What`s next for the committee, since we have you here. You got a few months before the House does or does not turn over, but certainly only a few months left in your term. What are you guys doing?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Well, look, it`s interesting. So we`re continuing to chase down leads, everything kind of pops up. Every day, we get a new lead or a new interview, we have to do. At the same time, we`re putting our report together. So it`s kind of like walking and chewing gum. It`s recognizing that we`re coming to the end of, of course, our mandate, which is the end of the year, the end of this Congress, but we`re not giving up in terms of when we have stuff come forward.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, there`s going to be some unanswered questions I think, ultimately, because, you know, investigations can take years to get done. And we had some people that just didn`t bother to come in. And so that`s where it`s not our committee doing it, but it seems like the Department of Justice has kind of picked up the torch. They have more time, more resources. And I think any unanswered questions that we may have run into DOJ will get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Congressman, we understand that you have had obviously a different perspective than many members of your Republican Caucus, but give us some insight into what it would look like from a governing perspective, right? Because that`s why the margin matters here, right, of what is a potential House Speaker Kevin McCarthy do, how does he wrangle his members from just like the policy perspective? What does that look like? Help us understand how that works?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: It`s going to be chaotic. So the first thing Kevin McCarthy has to do is actually get elected speaker. So --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: -- if it`s a tiny Republican majority, he doesn`t just have to win a majority of the caucus, of the Republicans. He has to actually get 218 votes, which means it can only maybe lose a couple Republicans. Let`s assume it`s going to be a tight a tight margin. That gives people like Jim Jordan, like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , I would say Boebert but thankfully, it looks like she`s not going to make it through this race.\par \par \uc2 You know, real power to either extract concessions for their votes, or to just simply pick another candidate. This is where you have a real opportunity. I`ll say this to my Democratic colleagues, if they`re in the minority, and it`s close, maybe to look for a moderate Republican that you can stomach and get Democrats to vote for them that person with concessions. See if you can peel off a couple of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 [12:25:03]\par \par \uc2 You have a real opportunity for an interesting dynamic never occurs. I don`t expect that to happen, I`m just putting that bug in people`s ear. And so, but look, on terms of things like the debt limit, Kevin McCarthy, assuming he`s speaker, will have to reach out to Democrats or he will not be able to pass anything. I`ve lived through this, I`ve lived through when you have the Freedom Caucus decided to extract more and more from the governing majority. And it`s not going to be fun to be in that position for him.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Well, let`s see if the debt limit is raised while the Democrats still have power. And that is if Republicans are going to retake the House. That`s still an if at this point.\par \par \uc2 You talked about candidate quality, Congressman. First read today talked about how this is another one of those moments where the Republican Party is being shown that the extremism, the denialism that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  maybe is not the best leader of the Republican Party, isn`t delivering the Republican Party wins. And that this is one of those moments where they have a chance to dump him, to throw him aside, to go for, I don`t know, someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . What`s your sense of what might happen?\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Well, I think it is that moment. The question is, can they, you know --\par \par \uc2 TUR: Congressman, hold on one second. We do have a little bit of news.\par \par \uc2 KINZINGER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TUR: The Georgia Senate race is now going officially we thought it would, officially to a runoff --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So that`s it.\par \par \uc2 TUR: -- in \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So we know, December 6.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So Nevada what`s up, you know?\par \par \uc2 TUR: That`s unless the Democrats win Nevada and Arizona, right?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Right. And so Happy Thanksgiving, everybody.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Steve Kornacki is sprinting to the (INAUDIBLE). He`s racing in here. OK, so listen, here`s what this means, right, because if you are looking at some of the numbers here, you see just the slimmest of margins, which is why this run off December 6, is going to be. We will see.\par \par \uc2 Katy as you point out, not unexpected, but still significant. We are still waiting, of course, on projections in Arizona and Nevada. Keep in mind that in Arizona, you have at last count, if we can get the board, there`s incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly, who is leading Blake masters. In Nevada, it is the opposite.\par \par \uc2 Mark Murray has talked about how -- in Nevada, we know that our colleague, Jacob Soboroff, has been talking about a slow count. We could get it -- Mark said 72 hours, maybe that`s optimistic, but we`ll probably see Nevada vote before we see Georgia and that`s going to be an interesting tale.\par \par \uc2 Ellison Barber is live for us in Georgia. And Ellison, you know, I was out on the campaign trail not too long ago in Savannah. And one of the things that came up was voter fatigue, because, boy, these voters in Georgia had been through big elections again. Here we are 2022, we`re talking about Georgia yet again, right?\par \par \uc2 I had one -- I had talked to one voter on Monday and he said to me, he ended up voting Republican. But he said I hope there`s not a runoff just because I don`t want to see any more ads. I`m sick of them on both sides, right? There is the fatigue factor as it relates to a run off. Talk us through how this goes. I imagine no incident reaction from the campaign`s just yet though, I bet that`s coming.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: No, I -- you will probably see me glancing at my phones --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Please do.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: -- because I am texting some campaign sources now to try and see and get an initial reaction. Look, you mentioned voter fatigue, Georgia voters are so sick of the ads here. There`s also, I think, a little bit of fatigue, or at least there was a hope from both campaigns that they wouldn`t have to deal with a runoff again, right? This has happened before in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And even, you know, weeks ago, month and a half, two months ago when we were here talking to campaign sources, regardless of which campaign they were with, they were saying, we really hope this does not go to a runoff. I`m not sure Georgia voters can take it. Now we know that this is going to head to a December runoff.\par \par \uc2 Look, I ended last night with both campaigns telling me that they felt good, both campaigns saying they thought they were going to be able to eke out a win. At the time I woke up this morning, both campaigns seem to already be hinting at the possibility of the run of -- of a runoff. And these tweets I want to read to you these are from prior to this announcement, but I still think it`s a little bit of telling of just in terms of how kind of the perspective seem to be shifting amongst these campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Walker`s campaign manager tweeting this morning, time to go to work. Warnock`s campaign manager tweeting, "\uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  underperformed Trump`s vote share in rural, suburban and urban counties. Last night was a good night for Warnock and a bad night for Walker."\par \par \uc2 So both campaigns they have consistently said that this race is going to be close. The polls showed us that this race was going to be closed last night. When I was talking to a source close to Warnock`s campaign, he said that they thought whether they eked out an outright win or it went to a runoff. They felt like in this political climate, that was still going to be a win for them.\par \par \uc2 In our reporting, we`ve been talking for the last week and a half, two weeks about this possibility of split ticket voters. I`d spoken to voters here in Georgia, a small group of them but small is significant in races this close who said they were Republican leaning and they plan to vote for Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . But in the Senate race, they were either going to split their ticket and vote for Warnock or the Libertarian candidate.\par \par \uc2 [12:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Or they just weren`t quite sure what they were going to do. It was nearly impossible to get any of those voters to say that to me on camera. But you look at the numbers here, the Libertarian candidate pulling 2 percent of the vote in this race.\par \par \uc2 When you go through county by county and compare Walker`s returns in each county to Kemp, there is a clear difference. I mean, Walker underperformed in a lot of the Republican strongholds by at times 2 to 3 percent. I want to point out just two counties to you that we spent a lot of time in Chautauqua County, Georgia, that is a rural Republican stronghold in the northwest part of the state.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won that in 2020, with 80 percent of the vote. Walker, he`s behind that by about 2 percent pulling 78 percent in that county, another county. On the other side of the state, furthest north east county, a Republican stronghold, Raven County, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won that with 78 percent in the vote, Walker at 75 percent. So those are small differences. But again, in a race, this tight, small is significant.\par \par \uc2 And now it looks like Georgia voters will have to gear up, they will have to gear up for more ads --\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC ANCHOR: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: -- more weeks of this. Warnock had run a bunch of ads in the final push saying don`t have me interrupt your Thanksgiving, show up, vote. But this race was one that divided a lot of people here and we see that reflected in the numbers and it looks like they both will be interrupting everyone`s Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: And you got 2 percent for that third party candidate, the libertarian candidate. So --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: 81, 000 votes right now,\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: -- where does that 2 percent go? Ordinarily, you would think that the libertarian would side with the Republican but \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not your typical Republican.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, FMR. WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No. And clearly the libertarian was a receptacle for people that wanted to vote on the more conservative side but didn`t want to register the vote for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . I think the Walker campaign has a significant challenge going into this runoff. It is clear, had Georgia Republicans nominated sort of a kind of a typical Republican congressman in Georgia. I`m not sure we`re having this conversation, right?\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Well, considering how well Kemp did.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Right. And it`s clear how much further Walker ran behind Kemp. And that is not something that you can -- that`s not something you`re going to be able to change with a few weeks of ads. People have heard the argument.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Let me ask you about the variable of -- when in Nevada, or when in Arizona, one of those, and what might Georgia voters do if they`re confronted with the -- well, it`s either a Republican Congress or a Democratic Congress. Does that motivate Democrats to go out? Does it motivate more Republicans to go out?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: My guess is it motivates each side. And we saw this, quite frankly, in these run offs in January of 2021, right? Remember, Democrats were at 48 seats and needed to win both to be at 50-50. So Georgia has got a lot of practice. I`m sure, they`re not exactly excited about that.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: They`ve got a lot of practice and understanding just how much they`re going to be the focus, not just in the run up to this election, but now for an extra amount of weeks. I mean, it`s going to be remarkable. I think each campaign -- I know the Warnock campaign spent more than $100 million, which is a fantastic amount of money for a Senate race.\par \par \uc2 TUR: And even more, it`s going to pour in now.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: It`s going to be even greater.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Well, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  have the experience and he`s just been reelected.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So --\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: It`ll be interesting too to see what is the effect, you know, Trump`s tease -- or more than tease that he`s going to announce for president on the 15th. No candidate in Georgia history has ever lost more races than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TUR: Yes. Well, (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well --\par \par \uc2 STEELE: I don`t think he did.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: And also --\par \par \uc2 STEELE: I think there`s going to be an enormous amount of pressure on him to hold the top.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I have to tell you, I have heard this morning from people who are saying something similar. I mean, who knows? That`s probably -- I mean --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: How rational and reasonable he is outside opinion.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Well, and, you know, Republicans is looking at this and said, well, why are we giving Democrats additional incentives to turn out and make Trump the focus of this battle between now and December?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I can assure you there`s a lot of Democrats right now. This is like pickup basketball, right? Best two out of three.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: They would love in this -- in Nevada, in Arizona --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: So --\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: -- and have Georgia be important, but not the balance of power, yes.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Listen, you`re talking about Nevada, can we talk to somebody who knows Nevada inside and out and that`s Jon Ralston, because listen, with this Georgia race going to December 6, Jon, you`re the guy, give it to us, what do you know, what do you got. Bring us up to speed.\par \par \uc2 JON RALSTON, CEO, THE NEVADA INDEPENDENT: Well, I hate to tell you about how many and not to sound too much like \uc2 Donald Rumsfeld\uc2  known unknowns there are up here in Nevada right now. Listen, here`s the situation. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is a head by 23,000 votes or so right now. But what we don`t know is how many mail ballots remained to be counted in Clark County which all of you know is 70 percent of the vote and that`s where the Democrats always do well and they especially do well in mail ballots.\par \par \uc2 [12:35:06]\par \par \uc2 And so, if there are enough mail ballots there, and some people think this is coming from both sides, and if you look at the turnout, it`s probably true that there may be as many as 100,000 mail ballots to be counted. The Democrats generally have won the mail ballots in Clark by about two to one. So it`s not over yet here in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 But I know what the next question is, so let me help you guys out. When will we know? And the answer to that question is --\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Anchor yourself right through this segment, Mr. Ralston. Take it.\par \par \uc2 RALSTON: I repeat, right. And so, listen, we may not have any numbers today, the county election department here in Clark County, which is Las Vegas is supposed to update us later this morning, maybe closer to noon, this time. And maybe they`ll tell us how many ballots there are, but they`re not going to be done counting them today.\par \par \uc2 They may post an update later. But I would guess tomorrow, we`ll have a much better sense. Don`t forget, Friday`s a holiday. And the law here says they can count those mail ballots, as long as they`re postmarked before or on election day through Saturday. So it may be Saturday before we know anything. But if -- and if you remember back to 2020, that`s when Nevada was called for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as well.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Let me ask you a variation of the question of when will they know, what exactly are they doing behind the scenes? Why does it take so long, Jon?\par \par \uc2 RALSTON: Do you know how many times I have been asked this question, Katy, over the years? And the answer that is too many times. Listen, there`s no logical explanation for what the Registrar of Voters in Clark County says is that he only has so many hands to put the ballots through the signature verification and processing.\par \par \uc2 The question is, why didn`t his bosses, the Clark County Commissioners, make sure that he had the resources, so people on MSNBC would not be asking me how long is it?\par \par \uc2 TUR: Well, I think he`s listening. I just got a text message from Jacob Soboroff, who`s down there in Nevada. Joe Gloria, the Clark County Registrar of Voters will hold a press conference today at 2:30 p.m. Eastern time. By the way, we`ll still be on the air, 11:30 a.m. Pacific, is going to update the vote count there.\par \par \uc2 So maybe Jon, we might get -- what do you think, are we going to get the vote count itself updated or an update on when we`re going to get more information?\par \par \uc2 RALSTON: The number everyone`s looking for, Katy, is how many ballots there really are. If there are 100,000 ballots or so, this race is going to end up being really close. But say there`s only 50,000 or 60,000, her path, Catherine Cortez Masto`s path to catch up, it`s going to be very, very difficult.\par \par \uc2 And they`re not just -- remember, they`re not just going to count, eventually the mail ballots that were dropped off yesterday or received yesterday. That`s what we`re talking about in that count. Mail ballots can still arrive today and tomorrow and Saturday and be counted in that total. And we just don`t know what that number is.\par \par \uc2 We do know this, the culinary union, which all of you are familiar with is the big Democratic turnout operation instructed its members to drop off mail at that -- at those drop boxes yesterday, and they`re running a mostly mail campaign. So, if \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  ends up coming back, she will have the culinary union to thank for that.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Jon Ralston, well, I know what you`re going to be doing at 2:30 Eastern, so we will be checking back with you later. Thank you so much. And we`ll ask you more questions that you`ve heard before. You always got the best answers for me.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Be nice, Jon. Be nice.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That`s because you`re the expert. You`re mister Nevada for all of us.\par \par \uc2 And as we mentioned, we now expect to hear from President Biden, someone who has more than a passing interest in what happens in Nevada, and Arizona and Georgia, at 4:00 Eastern this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Monica Alba is with us now from outside the White House. Monica, what do we expect to hear from President Biden after a long night?\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, there had been a big question, Andrea, about when the President was going to come out and react. We knew to expect to hear from him in some form today. We now know also he is expected to take questions from reporters. But I think you can anticipate based on conversations I`ve had all morning with Biden advisers, that the President feels buoyed so far by the results for Democrats and his party.\par \par \uc2 He was, after all, be incurable and eternal optimist throughout all of this in the last couple of days saying things publicly about how he thought Democrats would fare that privately some of his own aides would say behind the scenes likely wouldn`t happen. They were bracing for a tougher night following what would be a traditional midterm pattern. And instead, overnight, I`m told as the President was doing all of those congratulatory calls, he was feeling very upbeat. He was feeling that the issues that he had spent all of this time talking about did resonate namely something like abortion.\par \par \uc2 [12:40:09]\par \par \uc2 I`m told by a Biden adviser, they feel vindicated that that strategy seems to have paid off in terms of how many people cited that as a main decision and factor in their ballot box rationale. So later this afternoon, you can expect to hear the President talking about some of those key issues. The other main thing I expect to hear from him is that argument he made in the closing weeks about democracy being on the ballot. He gave those high- profile speeches, laying out the threats to democracy and what he called these ultra MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And there was some criticism at the time saying maybe that is too much attention spent on that. Maybe you should be talking about pocketbook issues. I think you`re going to see the White House come out and say we feel good about that. Because again, that was cited by a lot of people and some election deniers and some Trump-backed candidates that loss last night, they`re going to point to as evidence for perhaps that strategy working.\par \par \uc2 So again, the President is set to face some tough questions. We still don`t know, obviously, control of House or Senate, but in terms of those GOP investigations, they are ready for that as well. There will be certainly plenty of questions that could go into these probes about Hunter Biden`s business dealings, the origins of COVID, the Afghanistan withdrawal.\par \par \uc2 So they know that that is also a more difficult area here that they`re going to have to field a lot of inquiries on, but I think you can expect to see the President come out and say, I`ve been underestimated before, I was underestimated last night, and now I am feeling a little bit more optimistic about where things went and where they still may go here, Andrea, Hallie, and Katy.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Monica Alba, thank you very much, outside the White House for us.\par \par \uc2 4:00 Eastern, you will watch it here live on MSNBC. We`re going to try to sneak in a quick break on the heels of that important projection, not unexpected projection, but an important one that the Georgia Senate race is going to a run off in early December. We also know there have been a bunch of firsts in these midterm election races here.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the first woman elected to Congress from the state of Vermont, she will join us as you watch continuing coverage right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [12:46:10]\par \par \uc2 TUR: All right, ladies. History was made last night.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TUR: The first woman to represent Vermont in the House was elected last night. Believe it or not, Becca Balint is going to be the first woman, also the first openly LGBTQ person to represent Vermont and she joins us now. Becca, or Congresswoman-elect, I should say, how are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 REP.-ELECT BECCA BALINT (D), VERMONT: I am feeling tremendous. I feel so honored that I`m going to get to represent this state that I love so much. It is really a dream come true for me.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Also, your state voted to enshrine protections for abortion as well. What happened last night?\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Oh, so much good news here in Vermont. Yes, we`ve been working on our Reproductive Liberty Amendment for five years really, and to have Vermont come out so strongly in support of our reproductive freedoms was such a confirmation of the work that I did for so many years in the statehouse.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it was an amazing race because a lot of people said we couldn`t do what we did. I am the child of an immigrant, child of a working-class mom. I`m, as you said, I`m openly gay woman and former school teacher, there were so many reasons why people didn`t think we`d be able to pull this off. But we really tapped into this feeling that Vermonters had that they really wanted a message of courage and kindness and really tried to pull our communities back together. And it was a broad coalition that we put together. I couldn`t be more proud of the race that we ran.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: What are your thoughts about the House you`re likely to enter, which is likely to be read, we don`t know, by what margins, but there`s going to be an outsized influence by, you know, all of the election deniers in that group, some very, you know, conservative and extreme members of the House right now in the Republican majority, possibly led by Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Absolutely. So that`s been on my mind in earnest for the last few weeks, of course. But I`m waking up to a much better map than I thought I`d wake up to. I am feeling quite hopeful, much like, I think many people within the House right now on the Democratic side. It did not end up being a repudiation of the Biden presidency at all, there was not a red wave.\par \par \uc2 And so, I actually see many signs of hope. Looks like \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  lost her seats, some other extremists that went down. I think Trump did not perform as well with his candidate than, yes, as people thought he would. And I`m feeling like there`s an opportunity here for us to actually get some work done, because the issues that impact rural America are very similar, whether you`re in Vermont, or whether you`re in, you know, the middle of the country.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve really got to get back to thinking about what working people in this country need. And so, I`m going to be looking for those partners wherever I can. But I know it`s going to be tough. It is.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Well, congratulations, and we look forward to meeting you when you become a new member of the House. Becca Balint, thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 And it was a tough night for some Republican candidates who were denied the 2020 election results. Take a look at this. Just some of the marquee candidates who refuse to accept President Biden`s win and lost their gubernatorial races last night.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Jeh Johnson, former \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , former Head of the Secretary of Homeland Security during the Obama administration and before that, top official at the Pentagon. Jeh, first of all, what message does it send that so many of these prominent election deniers running for governor lost?\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER DHS SECRETARY, OBAMA ADMINISTRATION: Well, first, Andrea, Katy, Hallie, I`m sorry, I`m not there in person. I`m in COVID quarantine here in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Oh Jeh.\par \par \uc2 [12:50:03]\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I`m sorry, I`m not there particularly because you`re -- pretty rock right across the street from my office, but I`m feeling fine. I`ll be out in about -- in a couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Andrea, in answer to your question, if history is a guide, what happened yesterday falls far short of Republican expectations and whether the Republicans eke out a majority in the House and Senate. My hope is that what comes out of this Republican officeholders, Republican politicians, those who are running on a Republican line, conclude that the Trump, \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  style of being outrageously offensive in politics is going out of style, is running out of steam, is something maybe we need to rethink. Mocking victims of violence, remaining silent in the face of an attack like the one on Speaker Pelosi`s husband.\par \par \uc2 My hope is that, collectively, Republicans begin to think that this style of politics really isn`t working very well, and that we need to move on to basic common decency in our political dialogue.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Secretary Johnson, it`s Hallie. So, you know, one of the things that we`ve been looking at, one of the storylines is what happens with people who have questioned the 2020 election or outright denied it, right? We`ve been tracking this year at NBC News. And I want to share some of the numbers because I think they`re interesting here.\par \par \uc2 In 150 House races, election deniers are skeptics won, OK. 60 lost. We saw something different, though, in some of these other races. In the Senate, by the way, eight election deniers winning their races on the Republican side, five losing. But look of governor and Secretary of State because these are arguably as you will know, among the most important because these statewide races for governor, for secretary of state, I would add for attorney general as well, are so critical in determining the mechanisms by how people vote, right? Like they have a say in your state and how you go and cast your ballot.\par \par \uc2 Ten of the governors -- we showed that graphic on screen -- have lost their race -- are projected to lose. And on the Secretary of State side, five of them have lost, 3-1, and we have a number of several outstanding projections that are yet to come in here. When you look at the landscape here, when you look ahead to what this means for 2024, when you look at a state like Arizona, right, or Michigan, where all three of those statewide races have election deniers on the ballot, cleans -- almost a clean sweep in Michigan, right?\par \par \uc2 We know that the gubernatorial candidate, the secretary of state candidate on the Republican side who are election deniers lost. In Arizona, it is still an open question. How much does that concern you as you`re looking ahead to 2024 and faith from American voters on the legitimacy of our elections?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Hallie, one of the last things I did when I was in office as Secretary of DHS in January 2017 was to declare that our election infrastructure is critical infrastructure. The good news is that a lot more attention is now being paid to election integrity, ballot integrity. The other good news is -- examples like you`ve cited a number of election deniers.\par \par \uc2 But among Republicans, you`ve got the Georgia`s Secretary of State who stood up for that election result. You`ve got \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  in Arizona, Republican, who stood up for the election result in Arizona. He was censored by the local Republican Party as a result. But there are a number of Republicans who took their office seriously, took their oath of office seriously.\par \par \uc2 But we`re right to focus on this particular point of concern. State level secretaries of state, in some cases, attorneys general may try to cast doubt on future elections. But, you know, the reality is that this election cycle in 2020 turnout is growing. This may well be the largest midterm turnout in history.\par \par \uc2 2018 at that point certainly was. And in the face of COVID, we`ve done a remarkable job at having remarkably safe and secure elections with the vast number of mail-ins. The nearest round number of percentage in terms of voter fraud is zero. And so, the facts speak for themselves here.\par \par \uc2 TUR: Jeh, I want to correct a couple things. We`re not correct, just to clarify for everybody. \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , we have not called that race yet. It`s still considered too close to call though, the fact that it is too close to call is pretty surprising considering that she was safe.\par \par \uc2 [12:55:04]\par \par \uc2 There`s also news from our friend Jake Sherman of Punchbowl, Kevin McCarthy has officially announced that will be running for Speaker. No surprise there. He released a letter talking about how the Republicans he expects are going to flip the House. He says a while, "A number of races remain outstanding. I can confidently report that we will join that list of flipping the House building on our significant gains from last cycle to achieve our goal of taking back the House." Very carefully worded.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why he`s running. He`s going to be in charge of a conference full of election deniers. A lot of the key ones didn`t win, as Hallie pointed out, but a lot of election deniers weren`t safe races. And they did win. And they are going to Congress. Some of them were already there.\par \par \uc2 How -- I mean, this is not -- not that he`s going to listen to you. But if you`re going to give Kevin McCarthy advice, I mean for the country, what would you advise him to do?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, Kevin McCarthy is good -- he`s about to find out, it`s much more difficult to be the majority leader, the speaker than it is to be the minority leader. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , in my judgment, has been the most phenomenal speaker in modern times, simply because of her ability to hold her caucus together, whether it`s in the minority or the majority.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy, if he is elected speaker is about to inherit a very thin majority with a very, very cranky majority, with some real outliers within his caucus. And I know from personal conversations with past Republican speakers while I was in office, that is an extremely frustrating, difficult job. To the extent, he listens to me, my advice is to try to, you know, govern from a center point of responsibility, and not just play to the extreme base of your caucus.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Secretary Jeh Johnson, good to have you with us. We are sorry, you cannot be here on set, obviously, all hoping for a quick COVID recovery. And out of quarantine for you, sir. Thank you very much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Get well soon.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes, for sure. We`ve talked about some of the new developments that have happened, Katy mentioned. Officially now in the running for Kevin McCarthy to be the House Speaker. We`re also hearing now from Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  asked about how he`s feeling about things that I don`t deal in feelings. He said let`s look at the vote count. We`re going to talk more about that in just a minute after the break.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That indicates a lot of self-knowledge.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Well, listen, we`re going to talk more about all of that next. Stay with us. And just a couple of minutes, we`ll be back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221109eib90008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Decision 2022 for November 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Nicolle Wallace, Lawrence O`Donnell, Joy Reid, Alex  Wagner, Steve Kornacki, Claire McCaskill, Chris Hayes, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 11,221 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:09]\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Shapiro a few minutes after a few moments after the top of the hour, to the extent that we are able -- we will try to bring you some of that \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  victory speech in Pennsylvania and that Pennsylvania governor`s race right after these polls close.\par \par \uc2 At 11 o`clock on the East Coast, we`ve got polls closing in four states. NBC News` election desk can project at this hour, these races as follows. In the California Senate race, the projected winner is Democratic incumbent \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2 . \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2  will be returning to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . In the Idaho Senate race, the projected winner is the incumbent Republican \uc2 Mike Crapo\uc2 . In the Oregon Senate race, it is right now too early to call between incumbent Democrat \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  and his Republican challenger Jo Rae Perkins. In the Washington Senate race. We`ve got incumbent Democrat \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  facing Republican challenge from Tiffany Smiley, that race is also too early to call.\par \par \uc2 Looking now at some of the governor`s races with the California Governor`s race, incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is projected to have won that race. He will be reelected as California governor and the Idaho governor`s race. The incumbent Republican is \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 . He is also projected as the winner. He will be projected to be reelected to that race.\par \par \uc2 And in the Oregon governor`s race, which is a very interesting one. It is too early to call between Democrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2  and the Republican Christine Drazan. Now, again in that Oregon race it`s worth watching a third-party candidate who may be determinative in that fact, but we shall see.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin Senate race, recapping some of our previous races here, but actually this one is a change in characterization. This one previously had been too early to call. It is now too close to call in Wisconsin between incumbent Republican Ron Johnson and his Democratic challenger Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 In the Georgia Senate race, it is too close to call between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with about 86% of the vote in.\par \par \uc2 In the North Carolina Senate race, with just over 90% of the vote in, it is too close to call between Republican \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  and Democrat, Cheri Beasley.\par \par \uc2 In the Pennsylvania Senate race, we`ve got about 71% of the vote in. It is too early to call between \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Democrat and Republican Mehmet Oz. And in the Arizona Senate race, the Arizona Senate race we`ve got 51% of the vote in. It is too early to call between Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly and Republican challenger Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 At this hour, this is the control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . Republicans holding 45 seats. The Democrats holding 44 with that dwindling number of undecided seats. 11 Senate seats still to be called.\par \par \uc2 There is one House race that we want to point out here. This is the Vermont at large House race. And the reason that we are highlighting this race in particular between Becca Balint and Liam Madden. Becca Balint, the Democrat and Liam Madden, the Republican is that in this race, if Becca Balint wins this, she will be the first person ever elected to Vermont, while being female. We have never had a woman elected either to the House or the Senate from the great state of Vermont, if Becca Balint wins this race. She will make history in that way in Vermont.\par \par \uc2 Do we also have a characterization on the Vermont abortion race? We`ll get back to that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 We have a call in a governor`s race in Georgia. Incumbent Republican \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is projected to be reelected as Georgia Governor. Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  was his challenger but \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  will return to the governor`s mansion in the great State of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And as I just mentioned just a moment ago, we`re keeping an eye on some of these abortion measures around the country. Vermont is one of three states where voters were asked to enshrine reproductive rights, reproductive liberty in the state constitution that has won in Vermont. The yes vote is a vote to enshrine abortion rights in the state constitution in Vermont. That measure has won in Vermont. Lots going on there.\par \par \uc2 In terms of -- I mean, at this point, the fast and furious nature of this is starting to feel slightly overwhelming. Although we`re getting -- I think we`re getting a stronger sense of some of these contours.\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: Yeah, well, and you mentioned \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  will give a victory speech. It`ll be interesting to hear if he mentions a call for \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 . He was one of the election deniers and MAGA candidates who had said he may not accept -- he said he would not accept the results election. So, usually the first thing that Victor says is I got a nice call from my opponent, who will be waiting to see if he says that.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Number one of the candidates used to wait - -\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: For them to call, right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It would be rude of me to give my victory speech if I haven`t gotten my caught my concession phone call yet. We`re past that I guess.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  still waiting for --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I want to just reiterate -- we had the J.D. Vance call in Ohio, which will be Trump will claim as a victory but beyond that, Dan Cox, Maryland, Don Bolduc, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:03]\par \par \uc2 Mehmet Oz is facing a tight race in Pennsylvania. This is the candidate that Trump went to the mat four after David McCormack effectively begged Trump for the endorsement in Pennsylvania, might have run a better race than Mehmet Oz has run. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , we`re talking about, you know, what is the difference? I can`t -- I don`t have that, I`m not Steve Kornacki --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Very tight race in Georgie.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: These are Trump`s --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Blake Masters?\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Blake Masters, these are people, these are Trump. This is Trump`s team.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And they are not performing to the degree that I believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had helps this --\par \par \uc2 REID: Michael Steele did mention, like where you spend your money and how this is important. I mean, the DNC threw $90 million into just ground this time, that`s three times more than they spent in 2018. And so, if you look at what`s happening today, everyone`s waiting for the same day votes, which are not as Republican as before. Because what Democrats have been doing throughout the country in the states that they`ve spent money in is they`ve been driving out same day vote and also obviously, pushing for early vote as well.\par \par \uc2 So, I think Democrats, if Cheri Beasley doesn`t pull it off, there`s going to be a question of whether they should have put some of that ground money there. Because it`s obvious that she has momentum, there`s going to be questioning whether they should have put money in Ohio, because they didn`t fund that candidacy, either. Because it looks like what`s helping Democrats. One of the things that`s helping them, one of them, I still say is abortion and people`s passion on that to young voters. Younger voters who are passionate about abortion of young voters are coming through for them, but ground.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REID: Ground game and where they spent their money and where Democrats invested., it definitely appears in House races, and in some of the statewide races --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: On the House races on that point. I`m still a little shocked by an interaction I had a few moments ago with Steve, in which I said is it even possible that Democrats might hold control of the House. And Steve seemed to suggest -- I think, I wasn`t dreaming, right? That actually happened where Steve was like, yeah, that`s within the realm of possibility. Steve, can you do that again? Can you just tell me what you meant?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, the answer is yes. They have a chance. And I mean, we could just take you through it again. I`ve been stressing this whole night, we rank these districts, by vulnerability for Democrats, OK. Democrats -- the Republicans net gain of five seats. And we said at the start of the night, Republicans had basically drawn themselves five seats. They won those five seats relatively early in the night and you still haven`t seen any other calls in this category.\par \par \uc2 So, for Democrats give you a couple examples here, the ninth district of Ohio, \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2 , you can see there, three quarters of the vote. You can see how she`s doing. Second District of Maine 40% of the vote, Jared Golden. Again, this is an instant runoff district so Golden`s got to finish it -- wants to finish ahead of Poliquin. He also wants to break 50 to win it outright tonight. We`ll see. There`s still a lot of vote to come there.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania`s Eighth District. We showed you how \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was over performing in Lackawanna County. \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2 , the Democratic congressman here again, these are all Trump districts, I`m showing you. Trump carried them all. Democrats represent them in Congress right now. There are a couple places here. The 15th District of Texas, this is South Texas. This is that part of Texas, heavily Hispanic that really moved dramatically away from the Democrats. And you could see this was the district of these three deep south Texas districts. This is the one Republicans on paper had the best chance and this may well end up being a Republican pick up here. But you see Democrats holding their own, defending or at least potentially defending a lot of this turf in here. So, they got to limit.\par \par \uc2 First step for Democrats here is limit the number of losses here. They got five, you know, try to limit it to a couple more, maybe two more there. If they want to have a shot at the House. Then take a look at this thing. I`m sorry, he`s a little funky here. But now let me show you the vulnerable democratic seats. And again, what should be striking given that it`s past 11 o`clock on the East Coast right now is these are districts that Democrats represent that were carried by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that Republicans were targeting and, in many cases, feeling very good about.\par \par \uc2 We haven`t called a single one of these districts for a Republican now at 11 o`clock. We in fact, we call you mentioned it before Kansas three, \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 . This is heavily based in Johnson County, Kansas. This district was actually redrawn in redistricting to be more favorable to the Republican.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve, I`m sorry to interrupt you. We have a projection in an important race. This is a governor`s race in New York. NBC News at this hour can project that Democratic incumbent \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is elected as New York Governor. It may not seem from 30,000 feet like a Democrat winning a New York governor`s race is a big deal, but there was a scare for Democrats here with an unexpectedly strong challenge from Republican \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . But \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  has come up short in that challenge. And \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  will remain as New York`s governor. Sorry, back to you, Steve.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, no. New York is a big part of this story when we`re talking about the battle for House control here. And again, just showing you some of these vulnerable democratic seats here. Republicans had been thinking they`d be, you know, I`m just showing you where they stand right here. Illinois, sixth district, Illinois` 14th district, here 75. You`re 80% of the vote in. We`ve been talking about the first district of Indiana around Gary and Hammond all night. Now, getting over 80% of the vote. Frank Mrvan is still at 53%.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:09]\par \par \uc2 So, again, the name of the game here for Democrats, they haven`t had a single one call for Republicans yet. Democrats want to defend as many of these as possible. They have to defend the lion`s share of the seat in that column. And then this is where New York comes into it.\par \par \uc2 Look at all of these vulnerable democratic seats in New York. So that was one of the -- that was one of the aspects of the governor`s race that made it so crucial. If Hochul was losing, or even if the, you know, Hochul only wins by a small margin, all of these democratic held House seats in New York, does it jeopardize them. So, you could just take a look at some of these right here, democratic Pat Ryan, he won that special election over the summer in New York. This is what his race is looking like right now.\par \par \uc2 This is a big one. \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , the Chair of the DCCC is in some trouble with two-thirds of the vote counted in his district, although the biggest outstanding vote in this district is from the biggest -- this is from Westchester County, which is the most democratic friendly part of it. So, we`ll see with the remaining votes, what happens there.\par \par \uc2 We showed you, Rhode Island, two Republicans were very excited about this 92% of the vote. And you could see \uc2 Allan Fung\uc2  still running three points behind right there. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , conservative Democrat from Deep South Texas. Again, this is one of those districts that really moved heavily toward Trump, but Cuellar getting some good numbers, encouraging numbers for him out of there.\par \par \uc2 We do have one call now here of these vulnerable democratic seats. It just came in the last few minutes. This is the Virginia Beach area, the second district, this goes to Republicans. So that`s a six gain for Republicans that we`ve shown you. But keep this in mind. At the start of the night, I said there were three tiers to Virginia. We say that the second district was kind of the baseline. Republicans aren`t just the kind of like, oh, OK night. We get Virginia 2 and nothing else. On a good night, they get Virginia 2, Virginia 7. On a great night, they`d get Virginia 2, Virginia 7, Virginia 10. They`re going to get Virginia 2. So that tells you the kind of night overall it`s been.\par \par \uc2 And again, as I say, the question becomes now, could it be so positive for Democrats that they actually are in the game for controlling the House? And that`s where this set of districts comes into play. This is where Democrats are on offense. There`s a couple others, too, that were our surprises that we should add to this list. And we`ll add to this list. But this is where Democrats are on offense. And where they can have the opportunity to offset those gains that I just showed you or some of those gains that I just showed you.\par \par \uc2 So, for instance, take a look at Illinois 13th District. This one was redrawn, used to be a Republican health district. Illinois Democrats were aggressive with the redistricting and you can see the Democrat with more than 60% of the vote is leading here. This district as it was redrawn is a Biden plus 11 District if this hold is a democratic, is a Democratic win, it`s a pickup. It offsets one of those Republican gains I showed you.\par \par \uc2 This if it holds and it`s now a 14-point advantage for Hillary Scholten over John Gibbs, Michigan`s third district would be a democratic pickup remember. This is the district where Republicans ousted Peter Meijer, the pro-impeachment Republican in the primary replaced him with the pro term Gibbs. This would be a pickup for Democrats, would offset what I was just showing you earlier.\par \par \uc2 Texas 34, this is where over the summer, Mayra Flores, they get deep south Texas trended away from the Democrats in 2020. This summer, Mayra Flores won a special election. It is -- so she is the incumbent in this district. \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2  actually is a member of Congress who was redrawn and moved into this district. So, this is a Republican seat right now that has two Republicans running in it. So, this would be if Gonzalez holds on here, this would be a game for Democrats. That would potentially offset a loss we were just showing you in the 15th District.\par \par \uc2 Look at Ohio`s 1st District. We`re getting up to 90% of the vote in here. Cincinnati City Councilman Greg Landsman continues to lead \uc2 Steve Chabot\uc2 . Again, district redrawn here. Cincinnati based. We could take a look at what`s still to come in this district. The Republican area basically all in. Cincinnati still votes to come and Landsman is ahead. These -- everything I`m showing you here would be a democratic game. We haven`t even -- North Carolina 13 is coming right down to the wire but the Democrat ahead by two points with 90% counted.\par \par \uc2 We have, you know, New Mexico 2, 77% of the vote. This was the redraw in New Mexico was also very aggressive. This was a very Republican district that was redrawn to become a Biden plus six district. \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2  is the Republican incumbent. And look at this with more than three quarters of the vote in, \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2  is trailing in the tally right now.\par \par \uc2 Again, these would all be democratic pickups. If anything that I`m showing you holds here, these would all be democratic pickups. And what I`m showing you so far, if they hold huge if there would offset those Republican gains that we already showed you on the first page. You So what becomes key at this point and what`s starting to come into focus I think is first of all how important in the next couple of hours those races in New York are?\par \par \uc2 [23:15:11]\par \par \uc2 Five democratic held seats, where I think we`re going to get a lot of information in the next couple of hours. Can Democrats hold those seats. And the other major piece of this, if Democrats can emerge from that without suffering too much damage, California is a place where Democrats, you can see it right here, 1, 2, 3, 4, 4 Republican districts in California, won by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So, if the Democrats can get through the next couple of hours, and have those Republican gains, you know, in single digits, they would have a change that control of the House. And you really would look at an election that`s coming down, can they pick off these Democrats -- these Republican held seats out in California. And I`d warn you ahead of time, if it does come down to that, the count in California really is a slow one. It will take weeks. And if we`re in a situation where this is just a couple of seats, these are the districts that are going to decide it. And it would come down two weeks.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve, I have to ask you about one -- other one just because I`m desperately curious before we go, can I ask you about Colorado 3?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Let`s -- I am curious myself.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Let`s -- and you notice, what a surprise that was this. We didn`t have it on that list there. But take a look. Almost nothing has happened since we checked in. I think they were at 72%. The last time we checked in, but it remains basically a four-point advantage for the Democrat Adam Frisch over \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 . And we just break the -- look at the counties here.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`m just going to say, we don`t know anything about the -- still outstanding?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yeah, no, I mean, we have two -- you know, as I could show you, Rio Grande here, this is trumped by 18. You know, this is tight -- and what we`ve been seeing is that Frisch has been running, you know, about 5, 6, 7 points ahead of Biden`s number. So potentially, this could be a good county for him. If he runs 5, 6, 7 points ahead or something, this could -- he could limit the losses here, you know, that Biden suffered. And again, you just look at these counties that have been coming in, you know, there he is running for, a core democratic county. You`re getting into. There are some Democratic areas in this district. Look, we`re Grand Junction is, you know, Mesa County, a Trump got 63%, Boebert sitting down at 55. Almost a 10-point improvement for Adam Frisch here. It`s a big part of the districts here. So, he seems very much in the game. And again, all those seats I just took you through, if he pulls it out, that`s democratic game.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We`ve got a projection that we need to make and an important and closely watched \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race. This is the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race in the great state of North Carolina. This is an open Senate seat, made open by a Republican retirement but the projected winner is the Republican candidate, Republican Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  had a hard-fought race against Democrats Cheri Beasley, who`s the former State Supreme Court Chief Justice. But \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is the projected winner in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in our friend Claire McCaskill, actually, in response to some of the stuff that we have just been hearing. And in response to this, this North Carolina race that we`ve just had the projection in. Claire?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, (D) MISSOURI FORMER U.S. SENATOR: Yeah. Listen, it`s always hard when you go, gosh, if we just could have put more money in there. But let`s keep in mind