`t competitive. Again, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  won that state in 2008 by 14,000 votes. He only lost it by 90,000 votes. The governor is a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have to start rethinking what they think are winnable states. They`re so fixated on states like Florida, North Carolina is the state where they should be investing. The $90 million they spent on ground. This was a state that didn`t get the attention and the investment. I think, toward the end, Cheri Beasley was basically out of money. And so, the reality is if you think about how the ground game has worked for Democrats, and how spending on ground has really done wonders for them in all of these states, where they, you know, the sort of narrative was that they were going to get washed out in some red way, which I think was BS, honestly. In this state, I think this was a winnable state. This one is sad.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I have to interrupt with a characterization, NBC news at this hour can now project in the Senate race in the great state of Oregon, that incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  has been reelected to the Senate in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 We also have an interesting projection in the governor`s race in Oregon. And this is one that everybody`s been talking about as the sleeper race of the year, and with that many people calling it that, it`s no longer that much of a sleeper. But in this case, we`ve got the Democrat \uc2 Tina Kotek\uc2 , the Republican is Drazan and the Independent candidate, Ms. Johnson. And in this case, this is now considered to be not too early to call, but too close to call. It is a change in the characterization of the race. This is a very unusual set of dynamics in this race. Republicans had been hoping that this dynamic might result in Oregon picking a Republican governor, which seems strange given the overall blue cast of the state. But right now, this is too close to call with half of the vote in. In the state of Oregon.\par \par \uc2 We also have an interesting House race in which we can now make a projection. Steve, has been talking about this all night. This is the Ninth Congressional District in the great state of Ohio. Veteran Democratic incumbent \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  facing a Republican challenge from J.R. Majewski, who made a number of statements during the course of the campaign that caused even the House Republican campaign committee to abandon him. That may or may not have made the difference. But \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  is going back to the House.\par \par \uc2 We have one other characterization that we can now make just coming in right now, in the Washington, \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race, Democratic incumbent \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 . This was not a shoo-in race, but Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  will be returning to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is the new characterization in the Pennsylvania Senate race. It is too early to call. But I want to make sure that I get this right. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is now described by NBC News as being in the lead in this race.\par \par \uc2 I want to go back to Claire McCaskill on this, given what we were just describing. Claire?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Yes. So, it`s really interesting, isn`t it that we had the Republican scare right around Halloween, where all these Republican polls came out, and really shifted national media narrative to this, oh, you know, the sky is falling and, you know, Patty Murray`s in trouble. And, you know, they could even pick up Connecticut and blah, blah, blah, blah, blah. Well, as it turned out, those Republican polls remodeling wrong. \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  is going to win comfortably. We did not have the scares that we thought we`d have. And we`re talking about beating \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is very, very good. And abortion was on women`s minds in this election. People tried to say, oh, it`s crime. It`s the border. It`s not abortion. No, you`ve got Kentucky. I mean, the last time I looked, I don`t know when you guys look last. But the last time I looked 53% of the voters in Kentucky are saying to protect women`s freedoms when it comes to the reproductive health and abortion. This is a problem that the Republicans are going to have going forward because the extreme elements in their party aren`t going to give up on this. They`re not going to say, oh, yeah, we were wrong about this issue. They`re going to keep driving to make all the laws in the country as bad as they are in my state. This is not a winner for the Republican Party. And I think it was one of the big motivators for the young voters that turned out for us like they never have before, it appears right now. That is the case.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: You know, it`s interesting, Claire, I`m glad you brought that up about that abortion ban. We`ve got Kentucky and Montana votes -- they`re not similar legislation, but they`re both anti-abortion legislation that voters are going to be looking at tonight and then 33 states where there`s pro reproductive rights measures. We`ve already seen one call on one of those measures in Vermont.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:12]\par \par \uc2 But there will be a lot to parse here. This is the status right now. Montana, again, only 16% in here. But this is a vote, a yes vote is to vote for an abortion ban. And a no vote is to vote against it. And so, you see the no vote, again, very early days in Montana but leading. There`s going to be a lot to parse. But what Claire is saying in terms of how abortion may be playing, not just among women voters, but among all voters, and whether that might have screwed up some of the modeling and some of the polling plans on both sides.\par \par \uc2 REID: You said it. It wasn`t showing up. It wasn`t showing up. It wasn`t showing up in the air. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Let`s just be clear. There`s the reality of what`s happening tonight. And then there`s reality of the where the Republican Party is and who the stakeholders are. \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , who just won the North Carolina Senate race was the co-sponsor in the House of the 15-week abortion ban. This person is now in the Senate. The GOP is going to have to reconcile what they have been on record, the legislation that they have proposed with the political reality that is emerging tonight, which is that the voting public and the Republican Party are not on the same page when it comes to reproductive.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: There has never stop or slow the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Well, but at the level of extremism that we are now seeing, the power of them to actually enact some of this stuff at the state level, I mean, you got to put them the rubber is meeting the road, so.\par \par \uc2 REID: And their base is going to demand more because the other co-sponsor of that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  bill is named \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , who is going back in the Senate, and they`re going to be politically incentivized to go forward. Because the people in the anti-abortion movement, they don`t want to just have a few states that have abortion ban, they want it banned period. They are abolitionist in their minds. That`s what they call themselves. And they are going to push and push and push. They`re not going to be satisfied for people to think the steam is now out of the kettle, because they finally got Roe overturned. You`re wrong, because think about what they`ve said, they`ve said that before to patients include birth control, IUDs are abortifacients.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: IVF.\par \par \uc2 REID: IVF is abortifacients. You think they`re going to stop just because they`ve gotten the Supreme Court? No. And \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is the person who has no filter. So, he just said it out loud, and then put a bill together. And a lot of the Republicans to your point to a winning are co-sponsors of that bill.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The woman who got more votes than any other woman running tonight is now the elected Governor of New York. And her challenge in this campaign was getting voters in New York City to get out and go vote for Governor. Because it`s something, because it all -- it happens in the off- year election, doesn`t happen in presidential elections. It`s one of the challenges.\par \par \uc2 The media, especially the New York media, was able to imagine that she was somehow being challenged, especially on this issue of crime. In this race, it`s not going to be close. She`s crushing the Republican candidate. And one of the ways she did it was make it very, very clear that what the abortion rights mean in New York State and what it means to have her as governor instead of this Republican governor. And so, the issue played all over this map in ways that won`t leap out at you. Like really New York, why would they care about that? Well, there were voters who went to the polls today in New York City who only went because of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yeah, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Kathy Hochul`s numbers, she was going to win, nothing was going to prevent it from winning. But the margin might have been somewhat more narrow. The crime thing, by the way, let`s note was a complete mirage, created by $11 million thrown in there by Ron Lauder, who was very positively profiled in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  the other day, just before the election. In an article in which he actually said, he actually said, I`m going to read the, "You couldn`t pay me to get on the subway."\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I got on the subway, right -\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It turns out, you can`t pay him to do anything, because his mother made so much money. He has never had to work a day in his life. But this -- Ron Lauder`s imagination of what happens in the subway is different from the 3 million New Yorkers every day who use it every single day. And they showed up at the polls today and said, this is who the governor is. And that crime issue just evaporated.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Let`s go now to Pennsylvania where \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  is giving his victory speech. Let`s listen to a little bit of this. We`re going to have to take a break on the other side of it, but we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA GOVERNOR-ELECT: They have sacrificed so much to give me this opportunity to serve. And we have a family that has been with us all together making that sacrifice possible. I`m so great. My mom and dad, Judy and Steve, I`m so grateful to Ellie (ph), Saul (ph) and John and I am so profoundly grateful to my brother and sister, Adam and Rebecca, and their wonderful families. We are all about family. And they have always been there for me. And I love you all so very much.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:00]\par \par \uc2 I`m also profoundly grateful to have a partner in this process, a history making partner in this process. Our next Lieutenant Governor, Austin Davis. Austin and Blair have become friends to me and Lori, and they will soon be friends to all Pennsylvanians. And Austin will redefine the role of Lieutenant Governor, mark my words.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: So, these last 13 months of this campaign, I spent a whole lot of time crisscrossing the Commonwealth, showing up at your hunt clubs, your union halls, having an opportunity to talk to you on your street corners, and in front of our schools. And I want you to know, I`ve done a whole lot of listening, and a whole lot of learning, and a whole lot of growing as a result of those conversations.\par \par \uc2 You`ve helped me by sharing your hopes and fears, your dreams and your worries, you have helped me become a better man. And you have humbled me along the way. Rural, urban, suburban folks across this commonwealth, well, I`ve had the opportunity to talk with, you know, that basically all want the same thing. They want a real opportunity for good schools, safe communities, and an economy that just gives everybody a shot. No matter how different our Commonwealth looks, no matter how different the terrain is, those are the basic values that every single Pennsylvanian has a right to, and that I`m going to fight to deliver for.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:26]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: NBC News has a projection on the question related to abortion that was put to California voters tonight. A yes vote was to enshrine reproductive rights, reproductive liberties in the state constitution in California, NBC News projects that that abortion proposition in California succeeds. It is yes, it is yes to add reproductive rights to the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Similarly in Vermont, NBC News projects that Vermont voters have also chosen to enshrine reproductive rights in the state constitution. There are three other states in which this is being considered one way or another tonight on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, voters were asked to vote for an abortion ban. Right now, with 77% of the vote in, Kentucky voters seem to be voting no but it is close. Again, a no vote here is no. It`s a vote against a new abortion ban in Kentucky. A yes vote is for abortion to remain legal.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, we`ve got less than 20% of the vote in but this is similar. You can think of this similarly to what we`re seeing in Kentucky, a no vote here is a no vote against a new abortion ban in Montana. A yes vote is a vote for the ban.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, this is a very closely watched one in Michigan. Michigan like California, like Vermont, a yes vote would enshrine reproductive rights in the state constitution. Right now, with just over a third of the vote in this proposal appears to be leading again with only a third of the vote and we can`t really say but these are the results thus far in Michigan, on enshrining 