ers of the vote here. Chairman of the DCCC haven`t seen the chair of a party`s campaign committee go down in a general election in 42 years. That`s what he`s facing there.\par \par \uc2 Pat Ryan is has gotten very close here. Pat Ryan, the Democrat who won that special election this summer, Democrats badly need to hang on to this district. And in the 19th District next door here. Again, look at this 2000 votes and change almost all the voting. \uc2 Marc Molinaro\uc2 , if that name sounds familiar to you. He`s the Republican who lost to Pat Ryan, in that special election over the summer. Now running in the 19th District. If Riley hangs on here, that would count -- that would off -- that would prevent a Republican gain. So, Democrats are trying to limit as much as they can what Republicans squeeze out of those five New York districts and I could add a New York area district to this, the Seventh District of New Jersey. This one row, Democrats are in some troubling right now, some real trouble and right now.\par \par \uc2 Tom Kean Jr\uc2 ., the son of the former governor, running in a rematch against \uc2 Tom Malinowski\uc2 . And you can see here without more than a 10,000-vote cushion right now for \uc2 Tom Kean\uc2  with most of the voting right there. So, you can see some potential losses here for Democrats. I showed you the places that are sort of in the non-California places where they can get some gains. But ultimately, it then comes down potentially to California, where are those four Republican held seats. The Democrats are targeting there in -- the six Republican held seats for that were won by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the Democrats are targeting. So that`s ultimately, I think, you know, potentially going to be the opportunity for Democrats here.\par \par \uc2 If they can make gains in the places I`m showing you, if they can limit losses in some of those outstanding places, they`ll end up with an opportunity in California, an opportunity to make a significant numbe