g weird jerky. We have 30 different kinds of chocolate. We`re going to be fine.\par \par \uc2 Steve Kornacki is at the big board. He is contractually obligated to avoid sleep for the foreseeable future. So please spare some kind of thoughts for our beloved Steve. He can`t even hear us right now. The roaring in his ears is too amazing.\par \par \uc2 The first polling places have just close in the parts of Kentucky and Indiana. We`ll be getting our first results in any minute. The rest of the polls in those states will close in one hour along at 7:00 p.m. Eastern, along with polls in a whole bunch of states, Vermont, South Carolina, Virginia, parts of Florida, also the all-important state of Georgia, where one of the tightest and hardest fought Senate races in the country could determine which party controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Over 44 million Americans voted early this year. And I know big numbers mean nothing in the abstract, but that means that the record-setting early vote from the last midterm elections in 2018, which everybody thought was unbeatable, those numbers have already been beaten in the early vote this time around.\par \par \uc2 Now people who didn`t vote early have been lining up all across the country today to cast ballots. There are many hours of voting left to go in many states. On the line tonight, as you know, control of both chambers of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have an uphill battle to keep control of the House, where they have a very narrow majority right now, and history says the president`s party always loses a significant number of seats, and particularly in a first-term midterm election like President Biden`s party is facing now. In the Senate, it is Democratically controlled, but it`s 50-50 and it absolutely appears to be a jump ball tonight, which means it could hinge on any one of a number of extremely close races.\par \par \uc2 Also on the line tonight, three dozen governors races. Hotly contested races for attorney general and secretary of state. In some states tonight, the vote could determine whether abortion is banned. Democrats, including President Biden and former President Obama have been warning heading into today that democracy itself is on the ballot. And even though that sounds like a slogan with a lot after happy American patriotic sounding words, it means something very specific this year.\par \par \uc2 Something like 300 election deniers are running for office as Republicans across the country tonight. If they win, some of them could be in charge of how the next elections play out, whether vote totals are certified, whether winners are allowed to win, whether losers will have to acknowledge that they lost. It`s that fundamental.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to tonight`s vote, Republicans are telling their supporters they ought to be suspicious of what`s expected to be a long ballot counting process in lots of different states in particular we`ve seen Republican legal maneuvers in the state of Pennsylvania. Today, even today make it more likely that the vote counting in Pennsylvania will last through much of this week with a super tight Senate race in Pennsylvania, which could very well decide control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . We of course expect that Republicans will use the drawn-out process which they caused in Pennsylvania as supposed evidence of something being wrong with the vote there.\par \par \uc2 Republican former President Trump is already telling his supporters to protest today`s vote, already saying there`s so much wrong with today`s vote. We`ll have more on that later on and over the course of the evening. But for now, voting continues across the country. We`ve got a big, big long night ahead which we mean in the best possible way. We are just getting started here.\par \par \uc2 Now, my compatriots, you all were tired last night.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC ANCHOR: I wasn`t tired.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: There was discussion of (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HAYES: How dare you?\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And intermittent fasting and stamina already. But I mean, I feel like it is actually a healthy thing for the country that we are inoculated to expect a long night. We are inoculated to expect not only a long evening but long week of counting.\par \par \uc2 [18:05:03]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And I think one thing I want to say at the outset here before we get into the returns. People made this point and I want to make it up front. Our beloved Steve Kornacki at the board. He will be reporting the data as he gets it. This data does not reflect actual changes in the state of affairs. The state of affairs exist. The votes are cast. We`re getting them in different orders.\par \par \uc2 It`s like if someone reported to Steve Kornacki a basketball game with all the buckets out of order and he just posts the buckets, like all the votes are cast, right. I think that this sense that gets transmitted in Republican circles, there`s something going on in Philly, or there`s something going on here, it`s like --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: They found more votes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s just all the votes, it`s all the people that voted. They vote in different methods. They`re being counted in different ways. But in the end, that will sum to an outcome. But there`s no actual movement happening behind that. We`re just learning it in real time and processing it in real time, and I really do think -- we saw this in 2020, leaping on that process as a means of sort of trying to create suspicion, right?\par \par \uc2 That like there`s something suspicious about this county reporting later or that county reporting later. There are just the votes. So we`re just going take as long as it takes to count the votes and then when you add them up, then you know who won the election.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I think it`s also, I mean, to that point I think one thing that we haven`t talked enough about is the disingenuous nature of the critique, right? The critique of the slow voting and of when votes come in and of what types of voting are supposed to count and what they`re not. The reason, you know, it`s disingenuous is because none of the people making criticism apply that criticism to any races that Republicans won.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We never saw Republicans in Pennsylvania, Republicans in Arizona say, my race doesn`t look right either. I mean, if they won, they consider it to be fine. And so it is a pretextual criticism designed to make election results seem optional rather than being something that`s actually nitpick or pulled apart on a factual basis.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC ANCHOR: And the thing that`s unfortunate, too, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know, I think people who think that he`s some sort of genius are just off on, right? I mean, you know, on "The Apprentice" he was a decent actor, whatever. He`s not a genius. But he did innovate a couple of things that I think we`re stuck with, is that he did demonize one particular way of voting, which is of course absentee voting, which used to be a staple of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 He made that an anathema to his voters so he siphoned that piece of voting off so that you could then do what you`re talking about and demonize it and say, well, there`s something funny in that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So that means once the ballots are cast that particular type of ballot --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You could go after them.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Can be problematized.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. Yes. Right, right.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Because it will be mostly Democratic.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 REID: And if you want to do, let`s say, do a lawsuit, which he`s already sort of starting to talk about doing in certain places where he thinks he`s not going to like the outcome, you could say, I`m just going sue over these votes. Because he siphoned them off 