ight.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: It`s his line of work in Republican politics. The mail. Literally things that went through the mail, ads and the mail-in vote. I think the important thing to remember is that the disenfranchisement is the bug. The feature is the red mirage. We know.;\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Because all of Trump`s campaign officials testified under oath before the January 6th Committee that the reason they demonized every vote other than same day, which they`ve never relied upon but they do now is to create a Trumpian mirage, a red mirage, so that the litigation and perhaps even the unrest takes place while people are deluded into thinking the stuff that gets -- to your point, the baskets that get count first tell a lie.\par \par \uc2 And it really, if you sit and think about it, it signals extraordinary political weakness, which may not even be the case tonight. They may be politically strong.\par \par \uc2 [18:10:02]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s what`s so weird. That is what`s so weird.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARI MELBER, MSNBC ANCHOR: And then, Rachel, you know, you talk about this record breaking turnout already early. This is a really interesting election. And I`m excited for it and you think about what we`re up against and also why people are turning out. This is the first election since Trump was booted out of office, and the first election since an insurrection and failed coup, and since the pandemic transition and since the inflation crisis, which many countries are facing, and since the war in Ukraine. So you know that feeling --\par \par \uc2 REID: And Roe. And Roe.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: And then Roe being overturned. So you know that feeling you had in `16 like there`s a lot going on and then those four years was like a lot going on. There was like, he lost, then he`s going to leave, but not without a militant coup attempt, and then everything else. And so I think high participation is really exciting. It may be across the spectrum we`re going to have to, as everyone was saying, you see what voters say tonight and beyond, and what Steve counts up. But people are participating in democracy, many of them with the expectation that their vote will count and the results will be adhered to, and I think that`s a positive.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And one thing that`s amazing is that, you know, the partisan valence of voting method which is like mind blowing, it`s like the Dr. Seuss, the two warring tribes, which side they butter their bread on. Like there`s no partisan content to whether you vote by mail or -- there`s really none. There`s no partisan content. It has been polarized by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right. So now you have this thing, I`m seeing Republicans being like, stay in line, stay in line, which we`re used to do all the day.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REID: That`s what would Democrats say.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But the one thing I will say about that is like, this more I think it really is important, right, this sort of small D democratic principles, like I want -- like I want everyone to vote. Like I don`t -- like I literally really do to the core of my cells want everyone to vote. I want everyone to be able to have their vote counted. Like I just do not -- that has to be the first principle.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Technocratically sound good democracy where it`s not hard for anybody to vote.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Everything gets counted. Is that so much to ask?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the great Steve Kornacki at the big board.\par \par \uc2 Steve, I feel like we`re cracking the seal on you right now. When are we going to start do you think getting meaningful insight into how the night is going to unfold?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICS CORRESPONDENT: In 49 minutes, I would say. That`s at 7:00, we`re going to have a bunch of polls closing in some of the states and districts where the polls close at 7:00. I think we`re going to get a lot of vote quickly and potentially a lot of clarity quickly.\par \par \uc2 Right now technically in Eastern Time Zone portions of Indiana, polls are now close. Same in Kentucky. No numbers yet. There is a House race, a competitive House race, and potentially a bellwether House race in northwest Indiana in the Gary area, that`s in the Central Time Zone. So actually it`s not going to be until 7:00 that we start getting numbers there.\par \par \uc2 But there is a congressional district in Indiana starting at 7:00 Eastern Time we`re going to be looking at. But let me give you a sense here of where we`ll be looking in the early hour, hour or two of election night here where I think most the action is going to be, again, starting at about 7:00 Eastern Time. So start in Florida here, and I think Florida is the most efficient sort of big state, maybe the most efficient state period when it comes to counting up its votes.\par \par \uc2 Everything except the Panhandle, so that is basically 90 percent of all the vote in Florida is going to start getting reported at 7:00. And essentially the rule in the state is every county within 30 minutes is supposed to report out all of its early and all of its mail vote, and what that will be is about two-thirds of the vote give or take in every county. So in that 7:00 to 8:00 hour, you`re going get the majority of the vote in Florida.\par \par \uc2 And then as soon as that`s reported out by the counties they then begin reporting out their same-day. Typically you`re talking about an imbalance here in how, you know, members of different parties vote. In Florida, there`s a bit of a difference. It`s not been hugely pronounced in the past but the Democrats tend to do a little better with that early mail vote that you`ll see first. The Republicans do better with the same-day.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not -- we don`t see huge splits, but typically expect the first number you see in these counties in Florida to be the high water mark for Democrats and to come down from there. One of the places we`re going to be paying very close attention to in Florida is Miami-Dade County. Obviously one of the biggest counties in the country here.\par \par \uc2 I`ll just show you right now. This was the shift that we saw in 2020 in Miami-Dade. Look at this, in 2016, Hillary Clinton won Miami-Dade by almost 30 points. In 2020, Trump brought that down to a single digit loss for Republicans. Again this is 2.75 million people. This is 70 percent Hispanic. This has a lot to do with that national story line that we`ve been following the last couple of years about are Hispanic voters moving the bid away from the Democrats toward Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Want to see what Miami-Dade looks like tonight. Republicans have been talking optimistically about outright winning Miami-Dade County in the Senate and governor`s race tonight. There`s also a competitive House race there tonight. So we`ll be keeping an eye on that at 7:00. Also at 7:00, critically closing in Georgia. And that`s where we`ve got obviously the governor`s race and then this, the Senate race everybody is talking about here.\par \par \uc2 Now in Georgia, one thing to keep in mind, again, related to that conversation you`re having about the order that votes are released. Under the new voting law in Georgia, it`s explicitly in the law right now they encourage the counties to begin tabulating -- this is the keyword here, tabulating instead of processing the early and the mail vote an election day.\par \par \uc2 [18:15:06]\par \par \uc2 And the secretary of state`s office in Georgia has been working to encourage the counties to do this. Meaning they get this flood of votes that are cast either early or by mail. And in the past a lot of counties have waited until, you know, the end of the night to count it up and it`s been -- you know, we saw in 2020 how that could result in some the long delays. There`s the possibility here that it`s going to be a lot like Florida in Georgia tonight where you get the mail and you get early vote especially in the big counties early.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see if that happens, but that possibility exists, and if that`s the case, you could see especially in this Atlanta area, that`s the core Democratic area of the state, you could see the Democrats jump out to a quick early lead here in the statewide vote, and then it could be same-day vote coming after that, the Republicans catch them. So that`s one thing to keep an eye on there.\par \par \uc2 Another place we`re going to be looking at 7:00 that I think is going to be key is New Hampshire. The official poll closing time in New Hampshire is 8:00. It is done by city and town. These are not counties. New Hampshire doesn`t have this many counties but this jigsaw puzzle here you see is every city and town in New Hampshire and they each set their own poll closing time. Most of them have chosen 7:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And what I think is going to be so significant here is with this many cities and towns, we`re going to get some complete results, I think. You know, it will be scattering, but a lot of places will be able to report out their complete results. And once we have complete results from a city or town, we can compare it to the last election. And so the interesting thing here in this New Hampshire Senate race, which has emerged as an unexpectedly close race here is, look it`s a state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost by seven points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So Bolduc, the Republican, basically he wants to be running in any given city or town five points better than Trump. So we`re going to begin to see as these cities and towns report, maybe in that 7:00 hour, is he doing that? Is he falling short of that? It could tell us how New Hampshire`s going. That could offer us a clue to about perhaps what`s happening nationally. So those are some of the big things we`re going to follow at 7:00.\par \par \uc2 At 7:30 we`re going to add in votes. North Carolina, the polls will close there. Again early and mail will probably come first in North Carolina. Also at 7:30, the polls are going to close in Ohio. That Senate race between JD Vance and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . Again, I would expect the Democrats the early and the mail to be predominantly the vote that`s reported out first, so, again, I would expect that to be the high watermark for Ryan.\par \par \uc2 And then the question of the night to be as the same day is counted, is Vance able to overtake him in Ohio? And of course all sorts of House seats here. In Virginia, New Hampshire, in Ohio, North Carolina, Georgia, Florida, all sorts of House seats where we could be getting results in that 7:00, 7:30, 8:00 window, that I think could tell us a lot about that Republican quest to flip five seats and win control of the House.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve, I have one thing to confirm with you, one thing to ask you, and one thing to prompt to you talk about. The thing that I want to confirm is that you`re saying that while we have been talking about the so-called red mirage and how in a lot of places the first vote that gets counted will tilt toward Republicans and Democrats has been trying to prepare people for that. You`re reminding us that actually in Florida and Georgia specifically, not only will we get votes soon, but that`s likely to be high watermarks for Democrat. It`s likely to be the most Democratic leaning vote totals that we`re going to get in one big dump.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Definitely in Florida. In Georgia, that is the theory.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: OK.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: This is the first time they`re running the election with this encouragement to the counties to do this, so we`re going to see if they do that. In Ohio, yes, that was the case in 2020. You saw \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  actually had a lead in Ohio until about more than 50 percent of the vote was counted and the same day came last.\par \par \uc2 What you`ll see this idea of the red mirage, as it`s been called, where Republicans get out to the big lead and then the Democrats, you know, try to play catchup, I think the states where that`s more likely to happen are Pennsylvania, Michigan, and potentially Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: OK.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Wisconsin because the biggest city in the state, Milwaukee, takes the absentee vote, the vote by mail, and they bring it to a central processing facility and they count it at the very end of the night. So we`ve seen twice now in Wisconsin, in 2018 and in 2020, the election was decided, that`s razor thin margins, it always is in Wisconsin, in both times, the governor`s race in 2018 when Scott Walker lost, in 2020 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the state by 20,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 You literally saw that the state shift from red to blue with that Milwaukee absentee that was counted very, very late at night. So that`s typically how it goes in Wisconsin. So there are states where I think that pattern will obtain this year.\par \par \uc2 One of the key differences, like I say is states that have early voting, in person early voting like in North Carolina, like Florida. Republicans seem OK with doing that. You don`t see a huge partisan divide with in-person early. Where you see the huge partisan divide is mail.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: That`s just overwhelmingly Democratic. And Pennsylvania offers no in-person early voting. So you`re either voting by mail or you`re voting same day. And so that`s where you really get those dramatic splits in a state like Pennsylvania, and so it really depends. And you could see those, the numbers move all around.\par \par \uc2 [18:20:03]\par \par \uc2 But I think Pennsylvania has the potential, again, to be one of those states where the same-day gets tallied faster perhaps than the mail, although again they are saying it`s going to be a little bit different this year in terms of incentivizing the counties to do the mail faster. But we shall see.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: OK. Steve, we`re going to take a quick break. I know that we are starting to get some of the first real vote from Indiana. We`re going take a quick break and we`re going to come back to you for those first votes again. Some of Indiana is closed but not all of it. So we`re looking at that right after the break.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to be live to Nevada, where Democrats are hoping to hold on to a Senate seat, and there`s a hotly contested governor`s race there, too. It`s all ahead. First vote is coming. We`ll be right back with Steve after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:25:22]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It wasn`t Paris. Do you know where that was? There`s an Eiffel Tower but it was a little one. That was Nevada. Let`s go live to Jacob Soboroff in Henderson, Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Jacob, everybody has been telling me that you are at a polling place that is having unexpectedly big turnout. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, they say maybe as many as 2,000 people. One of the election observers, Rachel, said to me, I`m not in the Paris hotel but I`m at a mall in Henderson, Nevada. It`s called the Galleria. And it`s amazing actually to see, as my favorite of this job, seeing democracy in action in real time.\par \par \uc2 And I think what we can do actually is give you a shot from up above to show you what`s happening here at what`s called a voting center. I don`t know if you can see me right now, but I`m waving to you next to the American flag down. And I think what should be the bottom right hand corner.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: This is a vote center. There`s 30 polling places here. The green lights are open polling places or people who are just showing up. The red lights are people who are actually actively voting right now. And this is a unique and novel concept because voting centers are centralized voting locations. There`s 128 of them in Clark County, Nevada. Usually there`d be 914 different election precincts but people can vote at any of the 128 vote centers here in Clark County, in Nevada. And they are turning out.\par \par \uc2 People were worried about this. Five, 10 years ago this was not a concept that used in election administration. I was an election reform advocate. We talked about ways to get people to turn out. Ft. Collins, Colorado, pioneered this concept, and with COVID you now see this all over the country.\par \par \uc2 The turnout here is massive. I know we`ve got to go, but I just want to show you real quick. Come with me.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: And by the way, Merry Christmas at the mall. And Happy Hanukkah to my people, as a matter of fact. It`s early yet in the season, but you can see over here, Rachel and everybody, in that room, the line snakes all the way around it, and then when you come over here, we got some inclement weather right now in the Las Vegas area. The line stretches all the way to the door, and then this is the very back of the line right here.\par \par \uc2 So if anybody was worried about turnout, Joe Gloria I talked to, the county registrar here, has said he was worried that people talking about the integrity of the election would scare people away. That`s not happening at all at least here at the Galleria Mall in Henderson. And we`re going to stay here as long as we can.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Jacob, let me just ask you about the vibe and how people are doing. Obviously that`s obviously a very convenient and comfortable place to be queued up, but people are still lined up. Have spirits been high? Has anything been tense? Has it been OK?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: No. People have been terrific. In fact there was a guy Dave I was talking to. I`m just trying to see if he`s still here. He left. It was about an hour he said from the back to the front of the line. That`s when the line started at the door, now the line starts -- let me just see if I can ask this nice person.\par \par \uc2 You guys are at the very back of the line. How are you guys feeling? Are you worried?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: How long you ready to wait for?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As long as it takes to make things right.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: That`s democracy right there. As long as it takes to make things great. What`s your name? Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nick.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Nick, Jacob. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 So as long as it takes to make things great in Henderson, Nevada, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Right on. Jacob Soboroff, live in Henderson, Nevada. We`ll be back to you certainly so we can get you in front of that Christmas tree again so we can just blackmail you with the photo later.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go back to Steve Kornacki at the big board. Steve, I said just before we had to take a quick break we`ve got the first real results from Indiana where some polls have closed. What have we got?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes. Election night has now officially begun because we really are getting returns and the colors are starting to light up. This is the Indiana Senate race. Now this one not expected to be too competitive. This is a state \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  carried overwhelmingly. You see the Republican here \uc2 Todd Young\uc2  early on obviously off to a good start for Young here in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 What we are going to be looking for in Indiana, and again this is a split poll close state here between Central and the Eastern Time Zone. This is the congressional district that I alluded to a minute ago. This is in, you know, Gary-Hammond area. Democrat Frank Mrvan running for reelection getting a challenge from Jennifer Ruth Green, the Republican. The backdrop here is this is a district that voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by eight points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 It changed very little bit in redistricting. A little bit more Republican friendly. This is one of those targets Republicans have. They`ve invested heavily here. They think they`ve got a very strong candidate. 7:00 p.m. Eastern we`re going to start getting numbers here and this could be a bellwether congressional district. But right now we`re just getting numbers in Indiana from the Eastern Time Zone.\par \par \uc2 We`re also starting to get numbers from the other state with 6:00 p.m. Eastern Time Zone closing. That is Kentucky. You see just a very small, really one county from Eastern Kentucky coming in here. \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  again running for re-election, the heavy favorite to get re-elected. But what we`re also following in Kentucky tonight is if you remember over the summer that abortion referendum in Kansas, that was a constitutional amendment in Kansas that would have said, would have put into the state constitution that there is no right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Went down to big defeat in Kansas, as you remember this summer. Essentially that identical proposal is on the ballot in Kentucky tonight to put into the state constitution that there is no right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 [18:30:00]\par \par \uc2 So, one county -- I`m just going to give you a sense of what we`re following tonight. But this is an overwhelmingly Republican county in Eastern Kentucky, \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  is winning by 33 points. How`s that ballot proposition doing here? It`s basically 50-50. Yes, would enshrine in the state constitution language that says there`s no right to abortion. No, would not do that.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the anti-abortion side is yes, the pro-choice side is no, essentially on this. And you`re seeing a significant split between the Republican-Democrat vote in this county and how that ballot proposition is doing.\par \par \uc2 So, get one county in Kentucky, but we`re going to be keeping an eye on that proposition all night, certainly. And the rest of this hour, this board could light up and we get a lot more clarity potentially, on that ballot proposition.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Excellent. Steve, thank you very much. And on those -- on the issue of abortion, obviously, there`s a couple of different ways in which that plays out tonight. One is how it affects people`s overall propensity to vote and who they vote for. It`s a nationally motivating thing for a lot of voters.\par \par \uc2 And there`s been some interesting questions I think raised about whether or not polling accurately captures people`s feelings about abortion, both Nicolle Wallace and \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  were talking about that during our special election eve coverage here last night.\par \par \uc2 But then there are five states in which abortion is literally on the ballot, Michigan, and California, and Vermont there are propositions on the ballot that would enshrine reproductive rights, reproductive liberty in the state constitution. But then in Kentucky, and in Montana, we`re seeing anti-abortion measures as well.\par \par \uc2 So, it`d be interesting to watch those in the abstract, I mean, are in -- in themselves and also how they match to the votes in the rest of the states and everything else that is on the ballot. All right, we got much more to come here this election night. I do believe we`ve got a shot here of McAllen, Texas. Big lines tonight in McAllen in South Texas.\par \par \uc2 Texas is one of the only states in the country where early voting numbers actually didn`t keep pace. Didn`t set a record compared with the last midterm in 2018 whether or not that maps itself to there being larger number of -- larger numbers of people who turn out today on election day, we shall see. But this is the line tonight in McAllen. Much more to come tonight. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:36:57]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Welcome back, it is 6:36 on the East Coast, which means that in about 23 minutes we are looking at poll closings for a bunch of big states. We are looking at poll closings at the top of this hour, in Georgia, in the rest of Indiana, some of Indiana has already closed but the rest of Indiana will close.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky, South Carolina, Vermont, Virginia, almost all of Florida. That`s going to be happening tonight within the next 23 minutes. (INAUDIBLE) I`m going to turn things over now to my colleague Alex Wagner. She`s got her team of political insiders who are surveying the landscape for tonight. Alex, over to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Thanks, Rachel. I`m here with former White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , former Missouri Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill, and former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair Michael Steele, here in the clubhouse guys. OK, you are connected to important networks. What are you guys hearing at this moment in time when so much is on the line? Jen?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER BIDEN WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I`m hearing a lot from Democrats about their concern that because they`ve run such a big push on mail-in ballots and early voting across the country, Republicans are going to try to claim victory early. Because in a lot of these key states, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and others, they count the early votes and the vote by mails later.\par \par \uc2 And sometimes that takes hours, or it takes days. So, this is really an area that Democrat, after Democrat has brought up with me, is when they want to watch, when they want to call out as they start to see that on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and other places.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Part of the reason that those ballots are counted late is because Republican-led legislators have made sure that the processing of those mail in ballots and absentee ballots cannot happen until --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That is actually the case in Pennsylvania, which is such a key state tonight that we`re watching. If they are not counted today because of the Republican legislature there. And that is also a place where this type of game playing could happen.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And we`re already seeing cries of fraud from the former president and other Republicans across the country. Senator McCaskill.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL (D-MO), FORMER SENATOR: Well, people feel pretty good about Georgia, the turnout. I`ve been talking to the back room and the Democratic senatorial campaign headquarters. They feel really good about turnout of young people.\par \par \uc2 They feel very good about the turnout of African Americans, especially in Georgia, and Pennsylvania. Little worried about Nevada, but what`s weird in Nevada is it`s hard to tell how many people are showing up that are dropping a ballot in a ballot box, as opposed to waiting to vote in person.\par \par \uc2 So, what you`re seeing in terms of early numbers are those people who voted in person, it`s very hard to tell what that turnout actually is. And when you see Jacob`s reports, it looks like they`re lined up for miles and everything`s fine. But there`s a little bit of worry about the turnout in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: And the demo -- oh, I was going to say, and the Democrats had been really focused on getting people to turn in those ballots by just dropping them off the culinary workers which is such a huge important union, especially in Clark County, Vegas, baby, the big place where Democrats are relying on.\par \par \uc2 They`re doing that. Their pick -- they`re picking them up legally, everyone, legally. They`re picking them up, dropping them off. Those also we don`t know the numbers on so there`s a lot of early numbers and Democrat numbers that we don`t know it (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 [18:40:00]\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Just keep in mind the people that we are expecting are dropping those ballots off may be disproportionately voting for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Yes, because MAGA has been trained not to use a drop box, right? I mean, they`re out there with guns and camo, right? I mean, they`re not going to be doing that against their own people. So, they`ve been trained to not use the mail and not use drop boxes, but to show up in person. So, they`re really going to be cynical about trying to claim victory before they can or hopefully never.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIR: Meanwhile! Meanwhile! Meanwhile! On the other side of town --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes, on the other side of the town.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Folks are fat and happy. I mean, it is -- I mean, it is -- I`m hearing and seeing a lot of big wave, big night enthusiasm, from Republicans right now. And it`s interesting, it`s on two fronts. Trump is kind of pushing a separate narrative that you see playing out with folks like Kari Lake who are, you know, nitpicking every little detail claiming or setting up a narrative for fraud.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the mainstream guys are really kind of not paying attention to that, but you don`t want to get distracted by that. But that`s going to come back later on. Should we go into day two, day three. So, they`re setting that emotion.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: (INAUDIBLE) what if you win outright tonight and you have been claiming fraud all along?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: So, I`m glad you said that.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So awkward than for Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Is that fraudulent, Kari Lake?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Because that Kari -- Kari Lake is interesting because she`s saying that this system, all these little issues that she`s had all day long are problematic. So, when she was asked just recently, well, should we then if you win question whether or not you won?\par \par \uc2 She`s said, well, it`s not that, I`m just concerned about the people who are administering -- administrating the election. So, she`s trying to cut it both ways, and probably will be successful in doing that. Because if she wins, then she gets hit by a, great election everybody, go home, right?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: And remember, in Maricopa County, that`s administered by Republicans. It`s not Democrats are administering their election.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Exactly. But that`s lost in a lot of voters. The other side of that same block, though, is you`re now seeing the jacking beginning for leadership. So, Claire and I had a little conversation last night off-air about the future Republican leadership in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  tonight comes out and declares I`m not supporting \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  for speaker. So, you`re beginning to see that setup. Now I talk to some folks about that. Mitch isn`t worried, but you know, it`s got to go through the motions.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Told you.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: No, no. You say he`s going to be a speaker. I didn`t disagree with (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: No, I said there`s going to be a fight, for who is the leader of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: I don`t think there`s going to be a fight.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: That they have to win.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: (INAUDIBLE) Scott who wants to cut Social Security be the leader Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  in a fight. We saw him run, already. So, we`re not worried about \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: First they have to win.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: They have to win the Senate.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: We have the videotape of \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  running away.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: But there`s excitement -- there`s excitement. There is a little bit of measuring of drapes right now. A little bit of preparation. And so, you`re going to see this, I think, by late evening, certainly, tomorrow begin to shift into how we govern now, what we do, and what`s the next step.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know how much governing we`re going to hear since, you know, top of mind is impeaching \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , for many in the House. But you -- there`s felt uncomfortable, let`s just put it that way, right at this hour to eve.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Cautiously optimistic and fat and comfortable. Somehow those two realities are going to intersect in not only a matter of hours. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  - -\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Don`t forget, we still have the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: That is true. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , Claire McCaskill, Chairman Steele. Thank you all for your insights and insider information. Rachel, back over to you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We will be checking in with the political insiders, those political professionals who have been there, done that, and escaped with their lives. Over the course of the evening tonight at the top of this hour. We`re going to start seeing the first results coming in from one of the tightest races in the country, which could determine of course who controls the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . These are the poll closings that we are heading quickly toward. We`ll be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:48:44]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Just moments ago, we had a live look at a big healthy-looking line of voters in McAllen, Texas. We`re going now there to -- with Priscilla Thompson, who is live for us there at that polling place. Priscilla, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, MSNBC HOST: Yes, Rachel, that line has only gotten longer since we last showed that shot. And I`ll give you a look at it. We`re at the end here and we`ll just walk all the way up. All of these folks coming to get in line to vote after getting off work wanting to make their elections heard -- their voices heard on election day.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a high-profile governor`s race here, and three congressional seats that are very competitive here in South Texas. And this line has been out the door throughout the entire day since we`ve been here. This is about as far as we can go. But you`ll see the lines still curving around with folks trying to get inside the door.\par \par \uc2 I have been talking to voters here all day today about what`s on their mind as they`re heading to the polls. And for the people who I`ve spoken to that have talked about the economy. They`ve talked about immigration and crime. Many of those people tell me that they`re supporting Governor Abbott and Republicans this time around.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, I`ll swing the camera around to show you there are a whole host of supporters of these various candidates who have been out here all day just shouting at this line of voters. A lot of Governor Abbott supporters saying vote your pocketbook, look at the gas prices, and trying to make that last ditch effort to voters.\par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 But I`ve also been speaking to Democrats here, and especially a lot of women who are saying that abortion is the number one issue on their mind. And they`re going to be supporting Democrats. And I was struck by one conversation I had with a voter. Her name is Marissa (PH), she is a teacher here. And she told me about that she`s supporting Beto.\par \par \uc2 And she is for a woman`s right to choose even though she`s very religious. She feels like that`s a decision between a woman and God, not a woman and the government. And I was very struck by how she was thinking through that decision. But this is what things are looking like here with just an hour to go until polls close. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Priscilla Thompson in McAllen, Texas. Priscilla, thank you for that. Fascinating. It`s nice to see -- I have to say it`s seen the lines of people, and people who`ve been there all day, she said those lines have been long. And to see all the electioneering going on, but everybody`s still in good spirits. Everybody, you know, not being tense, it just being a lot of enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 That`s exactly what we`re looking for. But I also want to talk about some of that data that comports basically with what Priscilla was just describing there anecdotally. Some of the most interesting Exit Poll data that we`ve seen today is on the issue of abortion. I want to show you guys this and get your take on it.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the NBC News Exit Polls today, voters were asked what is the most important issue to you. And overall, the number one answer was inflation at 32 percent. But look how close abortion is, abortion at 27 percent. And then everything else much lower, crime, gun policy, immigration, down at 10 and 11 percent.\par \par \uc2 But abortion right up there with inflation. And then there`s a -- there`s a qualitative question -- follow up question on this subject, which I think is also interesting, which is how do you feel about the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe versus Wade and the Exit Poll on that is striking.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the largest response there, 39 percent of voters saying they`re angry with the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe, another 21 percent saying they are dissatisfied with it. Put those together that`s 60 percent in terms of the people who are enthusiastic or satisfied with it, that comes to less than 40 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, these Exit Polls are suggesting as Priscilla was just saying, what she`s hearing from people in line who were there to vote Democrat in McAllen, that abortion is motivating and emotionally so.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We saw this, you know, we live in an era in which it`s hard to know how accurate polls are, but we do know or when there are special elections, that`s hard data, right? We saw it this summer with the special elections, right? The Kansas abortion vote. We saw a number of elections here in New York State, right? In New York 19 special election that was run largely on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Again, not polling, not error, the actual votes of the actual voters. And I think you know, one of the things that jumps out in that Exit Poll is if you`re a political practitioner -- Nicolle, you could tell me if I`m wrong here. You want to talk about issues, you`re winning 60-40. And what you don`t want to do is talk about issues, they`re winning 60-40, and much of a campaign has battlespace between them trying to get everyone to talk about the issue.\par \par \uc2 They`re winning 60-40. And you trying to get everyone to talk about the issue, you`re winning 60-40. There was a little bit of second-guessing down the stretch about like Dems focusing too much on abortion, it always seemed to me like they`re winning that issue 60-40, of course, they want to -- they want to center it in voters` minds.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REID: I mean, just real quick. I mean, it`s not even just centering in voters` minds. Anecdotally and I don`t know if you heard you talk to yesterday about people deleting their apps to follow their periods. What I`m hearing just anecdotally are people who were angry. And the thing is, it`s not just a matter of what issue you think is most important, it`s what issue will actually make you vote.\par \par \uc2 What are you voting on? Are you voting on the thing that you`re the most passionate about? That is what I think campaigns are caring about. I mean, I`m just -- was just furiously texting with people, long lines in Dane County in Wisconsin, they`re expecting potentially 85 percent turnout.\par \par \uc2 Campuses is where they`re seeing a lot of energy. They`re having to actually add poll workers in some Wiscon -- areas of Wisconsin because you know, they have same-day voting registration in Wisconsin. Now, we don`t know what that is. But all I know is its passion that makes you actually register to vote on election day.\par \par \uc2 And it`s so -- it`s what you`re most passionate about. And I think for women, for a lot of women, this is a foundational issue of whether you own yourself whether you`re religious or not.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I totally agree. And the seismic thing that we`ll be talking about for a long time is that 60 percent of voters view the Supreme Court as a dead political weight. That is a total -- that might be the most seismic shift and issue in my political career. Republicans used to vote on Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 You don`t have 60 percent of voters saying we don`t like it. It is now a liability against Republicans. And you know, Karma is a you know what, but this Supreme Court was made by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , it was made by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and they pay now are sort of choking on it. They deserve everything they get.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: We talked about the democracy crisis. It`s also a crisis of legitimacy for the Supreme Court. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  stole a seat. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got fewer votes and put three people on it. People went before the Senate and said under testimonial oath of perjury they wouldn`t change Roe.\par \par \uc2 [18:55:00]\par \par \uc2 First thing they did was going to do that. You see a wave here. Inflation is huge. We have an inflation problem in this and many countries. We`ll see whether that predominant for some, but 60 percent say legal, 60 percent is satisfied or angry. It`s a huge issue. It`s not going away.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And it plays directly into a lot of the states for governor`s races are here because it`s going to be up to individual state policies whether or not abortion is banned. And so, it puts the abortion issue front and center in abort -- in every governor`s race where this is potentially in play.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to take a quick break. When we come back on the other side of it we`re going to have polls closing in a bunch of big states. You`ll want to be here for this. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We are just moments away from polls closing in Georgia and Florida, New Hampshire, Vermont, South Carolina, Virginia. Steve, what are we looking for?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, we`re it`s two minutes away. That`s when it`s really going to start getting fast and furious. But right now, just a few more votes we can show you`re coming out of Kentucky. Again, right now disproportionally where the vote is coming from is Louisville, one of the very few Democratic areas in the state. Jefferson County it`s about 60 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 Charles Booker the Democrat that puts him ahead in the tally over \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 . But just again to compare the Senate race, wherewith numbers we have reported right now, that`s what you`re seeing to that abortion proposition. So, you see that the no side is running about 10 points right now, ahead of the Democrat in the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 Again, this is disproportionately Democratic vote we`re getting in Kentucky, mainly from Louisville. But we`re going to keep an eye obviously on this one, as this fills up over the next hour, but I`m looking at the clock in less than 90 seconds, Florida, Virginia, New Hampshire, we`re going to start getting Georgia, we`re going to start getting a lot of numbers and a lot of very important places.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All right, as we head toward those 7:00 poll closings, again, keep in mind here that what we are getting is results as they come in. Some states give you a lot of results at the very beginning, some states they will trickle in and in state by state, there will be a partisan skew depending on the state as to whether or not Democratic-leaning votes come in first, or Republican-leaning votes come in first.\par \par \uc2 Now you can try to be your own mini Steve Kornacki and memorize these things in 50 states across the country, so that you know exactly what color glasses to put on when looking at what percent -- percentage of what precinct is coming in at what time, or you can take the hopefully more relaxing course of -- course of -- of looking at these things which we would recommend, which is be patient.\par \par \uc2 Wait till a lot of the result is in. And by the time you get an NBC News election desk characterization of a race, you can be confident that the election desk is more than 99 percent certain that that is how the race is going to turn out. So again, patience is a virtue in all circumstances. Patience is probably a health necessity on a night like tonight, as we are starting to get our first poll closings.\par \par \uc2 At 7:00 we`re looking at Georgia, Indiana, Kentucky, South Carolina, Vermont, and Virginia. And we will give you these results as they come in. The NBC News election desk feeds these to us just as we get there. But at 7:00 on the East Coast, as polls close in those states, these are the results as we can see them now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221109eib800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for November 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Stephanie Gosk, Vaughn Hillyard, Trymaine Lee, Guad  Venegas, Jesse Kirsch, Lindsey Reiser, Ali Vitali, Elise Jordan, Adrienne  Elrod, Doug Heye \par \uc2 8,409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: The culmination of the most expensive, most inflammatory, most contentious, and the tumultuous midterm battle we`ve ever seen. The stakes are unbelievably high. Control of Congress, control of three dozen governorships across the country, and control over the direction of this country for years to come. That means the question of how this nation handles the issues you care most about, the economy, crime, abortion, democracy, will all be decided today. The candidates have made their final closing arguments to rally an American public that is at once anxious, angry, and exhausted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: Are you ready to win this election?\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, OHIO: I`m sick of talking about American decline. I think it`s time we started to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATE CANDIDATE, PENNSYLVANIA: Please send me to Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: We win in this. We didn`t talk about no run-on. We`re winning this.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY, DEMOCRATIC SENATE CANDIDATE, NORTH CAROLINA: I believe in democracy. I certainly believe in a free and fair election.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, PENNSYLVANIA: This is a transformative election. It`s going to send a loud message to Washington.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATE CANDIDATE, NEW HAMPSHIRE: In New Hampshire, we have always done democracy better anywhere and we are not going to stop now.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2 , REPUBLICAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: Are you all ready to close this thing out?\par \par \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, NEW YORK: Today, we stand united as one voice. And I feel the power of that voice.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, REPUBLICAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, ARIZONA: We don`t want a small win. We want a massive victory that shocks the world. I`m ready for it, are you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, now it`s up to the voters with millions heading out to cast their ballots today. We`re keeping an extraordinarily close eye on the voting process nationwide, and so far, so good. That`s despite a lot of concern, even fear regarding the security and safety of today`s vote. Former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson joins me live in just a minute to tell us what he`s watching for.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re tired of elections and campaigns and politics, brace yourself, we may be a week away from the first chapter in the presidential campaign of 2024. What today`s vote means for the long campaign road ahead. That`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the polls open across the country. And for all the divisions among the candidates and two parties, there`s one thing both sides seem to agree on. Some results could take a while. Here are some of the states we`re watching and when we might start to get an idea of where things stand.\par \par \uc2 At 7:00 Eastern, polls close in Florida, New Hampshire, and Georgia. Georgia, of course, is home to that bitter fight between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . 7:30 polls close in North Carolina and Ohio, two Senate races where Democrats are trying to flip the seats from red to blue. At 8:00, we`ve got maybe the tightest Senate race in the country, Pennsylvania wrapping up, along with polls closing in Michigan, Oklahoma, and Kansas, all of them home to super tight governor`s races.\par \par \uc2 At 9:00, polls close in New York, where we`ll see if \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  can hang on. Plus, the polls close for the key Senate and governor`s races in Arizona, Wisconsin, and Colorado. Finally, at 10:00 Eastern Time, polls close in Nevada and Oregon where Republicans are trying to pull off upsets in the Senate and governor`s races.\par \par \uc2 So, after the polls close tonight, we could start getting races called in the 7:00 p.m. Eastern hour. But remember, in 2020, Election Day came and went and eight states still hadn`t called their races, and in three of them, it took more than a week to get the results. Of course, MSNBC will be here all day and all night long, all week, frankly, if that`s what it takes. We`ve got reporters spread out across the country right now watching all of the most competitive races, the ones that will ultimately decide control of Congress and what comes next for this country.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also got two veterans in the campaign wars with me on set, Democratic strategist Adrienne Elrod and political analyst Elise Jordan, so great to have you both here. I want to start though by heading out to Pennsylvania, where NBC`s Stephanie Gosk is in Montgomery County. Stephanie, what have you seen? What is the mood there?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Chris. Well, you know, the lunch hour rush seems to be over. But we can have lines out here just about half an hour ago. And there were lines at 7:00 a.m. when these polls opened up.\par \par \uc2 And let`s talk a little bit about enthusiasm because enthusiasm is that gauge for turnout and turnout is so important, especially here in Montgomery County. We talked to the chair of the board of elections here in Montgomery County and asked him specifically about mail-in ballots. And he said that the mail-in ballots, they`re seeing more mail-in ballots than they did in 2018. They`re not quite at the levels that they were at the presidential election two years ago, but they are seeing more than that 2018 elections.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`ve been chatting with people all day here today, and I`ll tell you some things that we`ve been hearing. Well, importantly, I`ll tell you what I`ve not been hearing and that`s not anyone talking about abortion right now. We`re talking to people they say that this is an incredibly important election. One guy even told me that our lives depend on this election. Some people looking for a change.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And another thing that I`ve been looking for and this is very specific to the situation right now in Pennsylvania, I`ve been looking for that split- ticket voter. The voter that is going to potentially vote for \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , who`s the Democrat running for governor here who has a significant lead against his Republican opponent, and then the voter that votes for him and then switches and votes Republican potentially, for Mehmet Oz. The Senate race here in this state is so tight, and that is an indication that there are people that are split in these very partisan times. Having that kind of split is certainly unique, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: One of the most fascinating places in the country. Stephanie, thank you. I want to go to Arizona now where both the Senate and governor`s races have essentially been tied for weeks now. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is there. Vaughn, I think Arizona, frankly, is seen as ground zero for election-denying candidates. And at the top of the ballot, of course, is Kari Lake. Talk to us about what`s happening there.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right. Kari Lake is front and center of the entire GOP apparatus here because of not only the name recognition, but the fact is that over the course of the last month, she has been outpolling the other Republicans running down ballot. That includes the likes of \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  candidate Blake Masters. And campaign adviser to him even acknowledged to me that Kari Lake has been fundamental to his poll numbers rising here over the course of these last weeks.\par \par \uc2 And when you are looking at the states here, it takes not only the governor to certify an election, but it also takes the Secretary of State and Attorney General here in Arizona. In all three of those individuals have called for the decertification of the 2020 election. They`ve even called for the arrest of elections officials. We are also looking at the potential of Republican legislatures next session. So you can construct in your own imagination, what type of legislation could be sent to potential Governor Kari Lake`s desk.\par \par \uc2 Now, what do we expect tonight here, Chris? We`re going to expect the initial batch of ballots when they drop one hour after polls closed. That`s at 10:00 p.m. Eastern time, 8:00 p.m. Local, to favor the Democrats here. You`ll recall this happened in 2020. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a significant deficit. But then the ballots that were counted, those the -- of the individuals that voted in-person, which are overwhelmingly Republicans here in the state of Arizona, and those that are dropping off their mail ballots and handing them in person. Those also in 2020 win overwhelmingly for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, when you see that initial batch, you`re likely going to see even the Democrats in the lead. The question is, to what extent do the Republicans close the gap or overcome those democratic leads here? That is why we are waiting to see what the turnout here in person is today. And how many votes -- folks are turning in their ballots because those numbers are going to skew quite heavily Republican is our expectation right now, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vaughn Hillyard, thank you for that. In Georgia, the wild contest between Walker and Warnock, by the way, the country`s most expensive Senate race, could very well drag on for weeks potentially going to a runoff in December. NBC`s Trymaine Lee is in Atlanta. And, Trymaine, if one of those candidates manages -- candidates manages to get 50 percent and avoid a runoff that a lot of people expect, what factors will have played into that? Tell us what you`re looking at and why.\par \par \uc2 TRYMAINE LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, let`s see what if, is the big word here, if someone gets to 50. This race has been neck and neck. They`ve been trading a few points here and there. But folks in this community here in Georgia, they`ve been resilient and steady. So, when we look at what might push one over the line is how can -- who has been best able to organize around that kind of that steady connection, right?\par \par \uc2 And so with Walker as Republicans have in more recent weeks coalesced around their candidate, even though he`s been plagued with allegations around providing abortions for his girlfriends. When it comes to Reverend Walker -- \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , forgive me, how they`ve been able to organize not just black voters who came out at record numbers in for early voting, but also black men. That`s been the conversation. Have folks been able to organize and engage and get those folks enthusiastic about supporting \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 I`m here in southeast Atlanta, where folks all day long had been trickling in. And the one thing that has stood out among many other feelings and you know effort from these folks is that it`s more than just voting for them. I spoke to a woman who said she got COVID with her entire family, five people in her family, all but one of them came out alive. Her sister died. So, she`s here to raise their voices around health care.\par \par \uc2 I talked to another man who said the economy, health care is important because he is formerly homeless. And so there was another man, a reverend said he`s standing on his Christian faith, but also this idea of integrity. When you think about all those ideas, the economy, healthcare, but also integrity, who can organize those folks? And right now it`s too close to call. But we`ll see how things shake out, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Trymaine, thank you so much for that. Let`s go to Nevada where Jon Ralston, dean of the Nevada political reporters predicted the Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , who has looked vulnerable will keep her seat. NBC`s Guad Venegas is in the Las Vegas area. Well, predictions are one thing, voters sometimes have different ideas. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  herself is Latina but she`s losing, from what I`ve seen the Latino vote. How`s the race looking to play out there today?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, some of the polls said that Democrats in general, were losing some of this Latino vote. I spoke to \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  months ago about the Latino vote and she says she`s not losing it. She says she`s going to keep that Latino vote, that Latinos, let`s just say they supported Democrats for decades. So, the Latinos are there for the Democrats to lose that vote.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s other factors here in Nevada. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  needs three things, right? She needs to keep that Latino vote. It was projected that one in five voters in these midterms in Nevada would be Latinos. But then also there`s the Democratic base that is so important. It`s going to be all about turnout in this election. Now, so far before Election Day, the mail-in ballots in the early voting numbers did show a very narrow lead from the Democrats of those that have come out to vote. But then we did expect a lot of Republicans to come out and vote today.\par \par \uc2 Now you look at this center behind me, this is one of the larger ones in the Las Vegas area, 10 computers checking people in, 30 machines -- voting machines that are being used. I spoke to officials, they told me they`re seeing about the same numbers they usually see during the midterm elections. There was a lot of talk about the weather here in Nevada, there are so many factors. We had this storm that`s coming into the northwest part of the state that would bring snow to Washoe County, the second largest county, and a lot of rain to Las Vegas with the wind.\par \par \uc2 Now so far this morning, we have not seen that rain. The weather is much better than we expected, which means people will come out and line up. Because the question was are people going to line up if it`s snowing in parts of the state or raining? At least here in Clark County, the largest county, we have seen the numbers so it`s going to be about turnout. And one last thing, those nonpartisans. Before Election Day one-fifth of the voters that have casted their votes were the nonpartisan so Republicans and Democrats are hoping they can sway some of those votes their way, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Guad, thank you so much. As we`ve watched that line continuing to make its way into vote. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 As promised, I want to bring in Democratic strategist Adrienne Elrod, a former senior aide to the Biden-Harris campaign, and Elise Jordan, a political analyst and former White House aide in the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  administration.\par \par \uc2 You know, we said at the top, how consequential this is. But we heard a couple of things that strike me. You know, you have Stephanie Gosk talking about a man who says to her lives depend on this that`s why he came out to vote today, a homeless man who went out to vote in Atlanta. The stakes, I know we talk about them. But when you hear from people what they`re thinking when they go into that voting booth, it really hits you. Republicans, the folks you`re talking to, are they feeling really confident? What are you watching for them? What are you thinking about today?\par \par \uc2 ELISE JORDAN, FORMER AIDE TO THE GEORGE W. BUSH WHITE HOUSE & STATE DEPARTMENTS: You know, based on the last past couple of cycles, I`ve never seen as much uncertainty about what the outcome might be tonight and some key races among strategists, both Democratic and Republican. They`re just really, really tight races this year. And there are some places where Republicans are very confident, but then there are places where Democrats think that they have it. Pretty much in the bag, but they also don`t want to say that just because the margins are so close. And you look at the polling, and it`s all subject to error charts. Really just so close this year.\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yes, really.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What are you hearing from your side, Adrienne?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes. Look, I mean, I think we are side feels very good about the early vote, Chris. We believe --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thought?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes, exactly. And it`s sort of hard to tabulate, like, you know, which side like, obviously, there`s enthusiasm on both sides that our side believes that early vote has been very good for us in some of these key states. We also do believe that Latinos -- I mean, there is a narrative out there that we are losing some of the Latinos, but according to our early vote tabulations that, you know, a lot of us are sort of looking at on the D side, we believe that three to -- every three out of every four Latinos voted for Democrats and that early vote. We will see how that actually plays out.\par \par \uc2 But look, in terms of like, what are the like signs to look forward tonight and looking at number one in New Hampshire, if you know --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Exactly -- yes, it`s either -- it`s either Hassan or her competitor does exceptionally well or not as well, that`s going I think, dictate a lot of what we`re going to see today across the country. Because we have to remember in New Hampshire, there`s no early vote. You vote on Election Day. And those results tend to be tabulated very quickly.\par \par \uc2 So, I think in 2020, Biden was -- New Hampshire was called for Biden like 10:57 p.m. So, we should know a lot about what`s happening for the whole country when we look at New Hampshire. Also, some of those key states in Virginia, Virginia 2, Virginia seven --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Congressional races.\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Congressional races. These are also two, you know, states that have changed over redistricting become more Republican, but are also led by \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , two very, you know, strong members of Congress with a -- with a pulse on their district that are sort of seen as, you know, being able to like hold on because of who they are and the profile that fits their district.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, you`re going to stay with me throughout the hour, so we`re going to hear a lot more from them. Thank you, both Adrienne and Elise. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And a red wave possibly coming ashore, how confident are Democrats feeling in this moment? I`ll be talking with DNC chair Jamie Harrison ahead. The election security fear factor at an all-time high, how rising false claims of voter fraud could disrupt races across the country? Former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson is also here next. And the defense is facing the voters and major implications for 2024. So much to talk about, keep it here on "CHRIS JANSING REPORTS" only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Never before has American democracy and one of its pillars, the sanctity of the vote, been under such broad attack. Even with fears and false claims being ratcheted up, election security has been an issue without a problem. Despite a rise in threats both cyber and physical, American elections have been historically safe. And a record number of legal challenges to the 2020 outcome, of course, found no evidence of widespread fraud. Still, the rise in threats has election officials across the country adding security and training poll workers. Legions of lawyers are on call on both sides of the aisle ready to litigate a growing number of challenges that could extend Election Day 2022 into 2023.\par \par \uc2 Today, officials including Wisconsin`s Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  are urging the public to ignore false claims of fraud.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TONY EVERS, (D-WI): Don`t let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  talk about the idea that if it`s going late in night that somehow somebody`s in the backroom of Lockyer or Crandon screwing with the -- screwing with the deal, it is not -- it is not the case, it just takes longer to do absentee ballots period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve got the person to talk about this with Jeh Johnson, former Secretary of Homeland Security. It`s always good to see you, Mr. Secretary. You know, there are I think 8800 election jurisdictions across the country, and there`s always going to be a hiccup here or there, right? But I want to ask you about the big picture. How confident are you that today`s elections will go smoothly? And is there anything that does worry you?\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER SECRETARY, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Chris, thanks for having me on. If history is a guide, and it should be, today`s election will be remarkably secure. If you look at the 2020 election, in the face of the challenges presented by COVID, in the face of the political attacks on the integrity of that election, having to count a lot of mail-in ballots and the largest levels of voter participation in history, we pulled off a remarkably secure election. The incidents of fraud, of mischief, were so minor that the nearest round percentage is zero. I believe that the same is true for 2022. This will be one of the highest if not the highest voter turnout level for midterm elections in history.\par \par \uc2 But at this stage, election officials, secretaries of state around this country know how to do this. One of the things that I`m glad I did before I left office, Chris -- just before I left office in January 2017, was to declare election infrastructure, critical infrastructure in this country. DHS and a lot of state and local election officials have been working overtime to ensure the security of this election. No American should be discouraged about going to vote today because of false claims about our election security.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We do know, though that many poll workers are nervous. I think about my parents. They both were poll workers and they were proud children of immigrants. They were thrilled to be able to serve in that way. Now, you hear from poll workers, they`re afraid. If a poll worker walked up to you and said what to be looking for, what would you say to them?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I`d say that -- I`d be realistic about the current climate. We live in a very fractured political climate right now where the false claims about the integrity of our elections or election infrastructure are rampant. But on the other hand, people like your parents, people I know were right up the street right now monitoring working elections take a lot of pride in their work and they should not be discouraged by these -- by these false claims.\par \par \uc2 If they are, if people who are out there doing their patriotic duty are discouraged, then the detractors win. And so let`s keep our eye on the ball. And for election workers today, do the job that you`ve been doing for years and years and years to make sure our elections are fair and safe.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you about outside influences because just yesterday, a Russian businessman who`s a close ally of Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said that Putin would continue to mess in U.S. elections in the future. And he put it this way "surgically, and in our own way, as we know how to do." How concerning is that threat?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Chris, I think we`re a lot better prepared now than we were in 2016. 2016 was an unprecedented attack on our democracy by the Russian government. Our government has become a lot smarter. And at this point, I think the principal concern that I`d have about our democracy is domestic face. A lot of the false claims about our election integrity, originating, frankly, from within this country, many of whom are originated by people running for office.\par \par \uc2 There was a graphic that was on this network a few minutes ago about how in 45 states there are election deniers on the ballot today. So, I believe the principal concern -- the principal source of concerns about attacks on the integrity of our election, our domestic base. We always have to keep our eye on the ball when it comes to attempts at foreign interference in our -- in our democracy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jeh Johnson, it is a pleasure to have you on the program. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the things that I think about when he talks about election deniers and he`s right about the graphic on election deniers is that I always joked around election time, it`s the election lawyer Full Employment Act.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But we have never seen anything like what the potential is, do you think here?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: I mean, you have campaigns who are lawyering up preemptively, just so that they are ready because they want to be ready to fight.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Campaigns that maybe never would have had the money before to even think about such a thing?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, the onslaught of money this year, especially when you look at just how much money has been spent there in Texas.\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: I did love the point you made about your parents being poll workers because to me, that`s what is most disturbing about the cycle that so many good Americans who are choosing to go and volunteer and to go out and serve, have to feel threatened. And we`re just --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: They were so proud of it, honestly.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Mine were too. And I`m just -- I feel so lucky that we have people who are still willing to volunteer and to help democracy on days like this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Indeed. OK. So now also I want to talk about this. We have for the first time an account on the attack of Paul Pelosi from his wife and I have to say it`s chilling. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  told CNN she was asleep in DC when she heard the doorbell ring, then a banging on the front door. Here`s what happened when she opened it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA): I was very scared -- I see this \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  and they said we have to come in to talk to and I`m thinking my children, my grandchildren. I never thought it would be Paul. We didn`t even know where he was or what his condition was. We just knew there was an assault on him in our home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Pelosi reaffirmed what she had said which is that her husband does have a long road ahead but doctors do expect him to make a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 The Republican state turned battleground Ohio, will Trump`s pick prevail on that contentious Senate fight there? And Democrats bracing for a potential New York upset, will the crime spike catapult Republican governor to victory for the first time in 15 years? We`ve got it all next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:49]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve got some live pictures for you. This is from Los Angeles, California. There`s a big mayor`s race there. Voters filling out ballots. We are in fact watching folks across the country right now, and we have our reporters spread out across the country as well. We`ll be hearing from a lot more of them.\par \par \uc2 Something is likely going to surprise you when the results are in tonight because even if predictions are consistent there is a feeling in the political world right now that races that once seemed predictable definitely are not.\par \par \uc2 Case in point, the governor`s race in New York. Major Democrats coming out for Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who is facing an unexpectedly tough challenge from Republican \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  in a state that hasn`t seen a Republican governor in 15 years. And Ohio, where Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has kept the Senate fight against Trump`s pick JD Vance surprisingly competitive in a state Trump won by eight points back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Jesse Kirsch is on the ground in Youngstown, Ohio. NBC`s Lindsey Reiser in Summers, New York.\par \par \uc2 So, Jesse, you`ve said all this comes down to moderate Republican voters there. What are they telling you about which candidate moved the needle in the final days?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You know, Chris, that`s because we`ve seen Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the Democrat, repeatedly in his advertisements and on the campaign trail try to make that appeal beyond his own party, bragging about agreeing with former President Trump. When you talk about people who might be persuadable in the final push, I can tell you there are a lot of people who didn`t even wait for the last couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Early voting in this state according to the secretary of state`s office, early in-person voting is up by more than 100,000 compared to 2018. So a lot of enthusiasm clearly here in Ohio, which is something we`re seeing reflected across the country.\par \par \uc2 And something that stood out to me, we are in Youngstown right now. This is at the heart of the congressional district represented by \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . So take, you know, take what we`re hearing from voters here with a grain of salt. They obviously would be more likely to know him better here than in other parts of the state. But something that really struck me. I just talked with a 21-year-old nursing student who told me that he sees \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  as someone who is backing up workers but also someone who has flip-flopped. And those are both perceptions we`ve heard throughout this race. So an interesting dichotomy there, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right. Lindsey, you`re now at Republican House candidate Mike Lawler`s polling place. And I know earlier you saw \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  cast his ballot this morning in his unexpectedly close re-election bid. I`m wondering if early returns in House races there are going to provide us some clues into how the governor`s race might be going.\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY REISER, MSNBC ANCHOR: Chris, they should. In fact, this district, District 17, is really important for Zeldin. If things are looking really good for Lawler, things could be looking also very good for Zeldin. So how did we get here? \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  was enjoying wide margins but recently those margins have really tightened. A mid-October Quinnipiac poll only put her four points above Trump-backed Zeldin.\par \par \uc2 But just like where Jesse is, where the polling locations we`ve been at, you ask three different people what drove to the polls you`ll get three different answers. One told me reproductive rights, another told me election security, and another actually told me the war in Ukraine. So even though polling is showing crime, inflation, election security are really important, there are a diverse amount of issues here in New York driving people.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned also the governor`s race, District 17 itself, \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  in charge of protecting the House for Democrats and the campaign arm for the Democratic Party, finding his own seat to be quite vulnerable. We`ve talked to both candidates. Both of course feeling very confident going into this but Maloney does say that the last few years, these last several elections it has been tight. He says he is no stranger to trying to earn this seat, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lindsey, Jesse, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 And candidates across the country are hoping for this kind of luck today. Yes, it`s happened. There`s been a winner in California who can almost call themselves a billionaire after matching all six numbers in yesterday`s Powerball drawing, winning a whopping $2.04 billion jackpot. According to the Lottery Commission, that`s a cash value of $997.6 million.\par \par \uc2 I could live on that.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, meantime, Republicans will not just be watching who wins the race for governor. The clue they think lies in one specific county there that could chart out their strategy to take back the White House. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:39:15]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s a look right now. Voters casting their ballots in Dallas. Texas of course is the place where the marquee race is for governor between the incumbent Greg Abbott and \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 . Millions of others doing the same thing across the country. We`re keeping our eye on polling places.\par \par \uc2 And right now the ballot in Florida says DeSantis or Crist for governor. But the shadow ballot, the one with real national implications for the Republican Party, is the potential 2024 DeSantis-Trump match-up. What the GOP will watch now is how close or how far away Democrat \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  ends the night.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Vitali is covering this race from Tampa.\par \par \uc2 I think, Ali, the biggest clue of all for 2024 may come out of Miami-Dade. But you tell me. Walk us through it.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:00]\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Well, the biggest clue for 2024 for national implications may come out of Miami-Dade because for over a decade that has been the area in South Florida that Democrats have been able to run up big margins that have helped them offset the way that the rest of this state except for the urban centers has really gone red. That`s one of the signs of shifting Florida, one of the swingiest swing states on the map.\par \par \uc2 But as we`ve seen over the course of the last few presidential elections it has been tilting more solidly red and most Republicans and Democrats alike that I talked to here say that this is really the cycle that could see this state solidify in the red column. But look, 2024 is also on the lips of voters here because of who`s on the ballot. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  really on a glide path to re-election here, but many are looking at this as a potential springboard to what`s next.\par \par \uc2 The 2024 shadow primary has been happening over the course of the last few months but we`re now watching it spill into public view. You`ve got Trump clearly emphasizing the schism that`s happening between him and DeSantis. That`s something that I`ve been hearing whispers about for months. But now the fact that he`s given DeSantis a nickname, clearly coming out here to Florida and not campaigning with the sitting governor, it definitely has voters` ears perking up.\par \par \uc2 Many of them that I spoke to both at the Trump rally over the weekend and the DeSantis rally yesterday say they don`t want to have to make a choice but they`re aware this is likely the collision course that these two men are on for 2024, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, always great to talk to you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So election results tonight will intensify questions tomorrow about the future of both parties` standard bearers, whether they embolden or weaken \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Donald Trump`s 2024 prospects. So I want to bring back Democratic strategist Adrienne Elrod, political analyst Elise Jordan. Also Doug Heye, a Republican strategist and former communications director for the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to see all of you. Adrienne, look, no matter what the Biden camp might want us to believe, midterms are a referendum on the party in power, right? So if Democrats do suffer worst case scenario in their mind tonight, if it`s a Democratic blowout. does it hurt \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his prospects for 2024?\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: No, not necessarily, Chris, because I think you have to take a step back and look at what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done as president. He`s passed four major economic bills, two of those bipartisan. Those bills are extremely popular.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But wouldn`t a blowout say that Americans don`t buy that that`s enough?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Well, look what happened to Obama in 2010. That was a blowout during the midterms and he got re-elected in 2012, and went on and now everybody loves the Affordable Care Act. So I think so at all. I think you`re going to see a lot of pundits saying that but I think what you have to do is look at what President Biden has done, focus on his policies and let that be the driving force going into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Of course -- I mean into the 2024 presidential run. And of course with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  very likely on the ballot that, you know, also emboldens Biden among a lot of independent voters.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s talk, Elise, about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because no matter what happens tonight he`s going to declare it a win. Right? He`s going to say if the Republicans do well I did it. If not, well, I wasn`t on the ballot. But having said that, if the Republicans don`t do as well as expected, does it hurt him? If his candidates that he endorsed and went to the mat for don`t win, does it hurt his prospects?\par \par \uc2 ELISE JORDAN, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it`s not good for him reputationally if candidates that he crowned and he put in place, bad candidates I might add who are struggling, if they can`t pull it over the finish line, it`s not a good thing. But does it hurt his actual brand? No. He still is a deadlock right now for the Republican nomination any way you look at it. Really the only way that I see that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would not be the Republican nominee is if there are reasons that take him out that are not of his own volition, meaning --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Legal?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Legal reasons. Or if his health, you know, simply -- right now he has it in the bag if it`s a crowded field. If it`s four more people, which it looks like it would be.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right. So, Doug, it`s important to remember that while Trump is not on the ballot today, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is, and I want to play for you what former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair Michael Steele said kind of colorfully. His take this morning when DeSantis was called the party`s rising star.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIR: He`s not the rising star in the party. He is the star in the party.\par \par \uc2 MIKA BRZEZINSKI\uc2 , MSNBC ANCHOR, MORNING JOE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: He`s eclipsed Trump in a way that has Trump right now sitting on that golden toilet banging his head up against the wall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Doug, if DeSantis does win big tonight, I mean, really big, how might it affect Trump`s prospects? Or does it?\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Yes, you know, I used to work for Michael Steele twice, at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and in Maryland, and I forgot how colorful his language could be sometimes, and imagery that I didn`t need. Look, obviously DeSantis is going to have a good night. We`ll see how big of a night that is. But regardless of whether or not he wins by, say, seven points or 17 points, he`s poised right now as the number one challenger to Trump.\par \par \uc2 But here`s also the reality. The person who are important right now. The person who leads two years out doesn`t necessarily become that party`s nominee whether they`re Republican or Democrat.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 You have to have primaries. You have to have this process. And fortunately we`re nowhere near there yet.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So much discussion is centered on Trump-DeSantis. But we just heard Elise say four candidates. How many Republicans out there do you think, whatever happens tonight, are poised to get in?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: You know, I really don`t know. And I think we saw earlier, you know, with Tom Cotton saying that he`s not going to run where a lot of people thought he might, shows that some of these candidates are going to decide not to run. Maybe they`re operating out of fear. Maybe they just don`t think they can get there. And so some of these candidates are going to have to make a real decision.\par \par \uc2 Do you essentially pull a Ted Cruz and let Trump attack you and say, eh, it`s all fine, or do you step in and say, I`m going to be the person to challenge this one? Because as we know, quite often candidates have been told wait your turn. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was told this is Hillary Clinton`s turn, she`s inevitable. She wasn`t. We`ve seen that time and time again. So who`s going to be bold enough to make that move, and then will there be too many of them to have any of them be effective?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Doug Heye, thank you so much. Adrienne, Elise, stay with me. We have much more coming up. But we can show you a picture right now from Jackson, Mississippi, as we`re keeping our eye on places all across the country where millions of people are showing up today to cast their ballots.\par \par \uc2 Also all across the country what it means to the future of the Senate, how it could end up in either party`s hands. Where do Democrats think they can actually win? And did they spend their war chest in the right way leading up to the election?\par \par \uc2 I`ll ask DNC chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:27]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Here`s the bottom line. All it takes for Democrats to lose the majority in the Senate is one seat. For Republicans to flip the House, five seats. Really low numbers with incredibly high stakes. From the balance of power to deciding the policies that affect everyday Americans, let me bring in the chairman of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for being with us. Look, you`ve done the deep dive on so many levels, as chair, when you run for Senate yourself. When you look at the Senate races that we`ve all been watching so closely and talking about consistently, Georgia, Ohio, Nevada, Pennsylvania, where are Democrats` best chance?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , DNC CHAIR: Well, listen, Chris, I`ve been in 13 states over the course of 14 days and with many of our battleground states and candidates for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . I can tell you there`s a lot of energy on the ground. I feel good about our candidates because our candidate quality is much better than the candidate quality on the other side.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But just to be fair, can I ask you? Are you preaching to the converted? I mean, are you surrounding yourself at rallies with people who of course are energized and that`s why they`re there to see \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: No. Well, listen, I was with President Obama in Pittsburgh. We had 7500 people at a rally. The same day as a football game on the college campus. And so not only were there Democrats there, but there were a lot of voters there as well. That was followed up with another event. 7,000 in Philadelphia. So there`s a lot of energy on the ground and we`re talking to our organizers, we`re talking to our canvassers, we`re talking to voters, who are interested to be there for these great candidates that we have.\par \par \uc2 And so I feel good about that. And just understand this, Chris. You know, I was a candidate in 2020. And many of us had tons of money to go up on TV, but the difference was that we didn`t really have a ground game because of COVID. So one of the things that we decided to do at the DNC this year because, you know, the DNC normally play a backstop in these midterm elections, we decided to invest heavily on the ground.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, the DNC put $30 million on the ground. We have spent three times that. $90 million on the ground. And we didn`t just start in August. We started this last year. On top of that, $25 million for voter protection programs. So close races, you win those not by more TV ads, but you win them with very, very strong get-out-the-vote operations on the ground and that`s what we have in many of these battleground states.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: When you look at Cheri Beasley in North Carolina, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in Ohio, are your critics right? Should you have taken more of that cash and put it behind those candidates early on?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, just understand that the DNC invests in state parties and coordinated campaigns. We are the largest contributor to the North Carolina Coordinating Campaign. We`re one of the largest contributors to the Ohio Coordinating Campaign. Our other committees work with individual candidates but we work on building that operation on the ground because nobody else other than the state parties focuses on those.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the role of the DNC. So it`s very important to understand that and we have played that role across the country. And so I`m very, very proud of it. Just yesterday, our volunteers made a million calls into just four of our battleground states and that`s the type of get-out-the-vote operation that is so crucial and key when you have a very close election like we have this year.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I understand you had lunch with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  today. First and foremost, how is she doing? And do you think she`ll still be speaker in January?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, the speaker is doing well and she, like everybody else, is focused on the election today. We`re not thinking about what, you know, tomorrow or anything else. We are trying to make sure that we`re doing every single thing, putting everything that we have on the ground, to do every single thing to win these elections tonight.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , it`s good to see you. Thank you so much for taking the time on what is a fairly busy day for you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We should also note we invited \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  to join us as well. She declined saying she is campaigning this hour.\par \par \uc2 Adrienne and Elise are back with me. Let`s just look at big picture. I mean, look, there are always people who say the party could have done more, Republican or Democratic side, but when all is said and done, did they do enough?\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes, look, I think it`s too early to tell right now, Chris. I mean, if we lose North Carolina, if Cheri Beasley loses by one or two points, there will be a lot of people who are very frustrated with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and the DSCC for not putting more money, and the Senate Leadership PAC for not putting more money into that race.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 Same with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . You know, but look, I am not looking at some of the same data, internal data that they are. I have to believe that they are making the best decisions with the information that they have. But if there are some of these close races, and look, we need to win North Carolina in 2024. So that`s a state that regardless we need to invest in. As Jaime said, the DNC has made a lot of investments in there. But it`s a different ballgame when you`re looking at the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Less than a minute, but if Republicans end up with a huge night, is the obvious answer usually the right one, which is simply kitchen table issues and maybe the Republicans focused on it earlier?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, yes, and crime. I would also say crime if we count that as a kitchen table issue. Democrats just allowed that to be the conversation and dominate and didn`t answer to it until maybe they were out of time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Talk about a power panel. Adrianne, Elise Jordan, thank you guys for being here. Much appreciated.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. I`ll be back tomorrow with my friend and colleague, Jose Diaz-Balart, for special election coverage. Do you want to know what`s going on from 10:00 a.m. Eastern to noon, we will be here and be sure to tune into MSNBC tonight for election night results all night long, starting at 6:00 Eastern, but right now, "KATY TUR REPORTS" is up next.\par \par \uc2 (