`s four living former governors, we share much in common as regards our beloved Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 They go on to say more about that and then they say we are asking you, as the leaders of the Pennsylvania Republican and Democratic parties, to pledge to honor the process, respect the law, abide the people`s will, and support a peaceful transfer of power. In doing so, you will demonstrate to all Pennsylvania candidates, who will be looking to for leadership, that love of commonwealth and country, must come above all.\par \par \uc2 They say that you must -- they`re asking them to, it`s astonishing that there`s a letter saying this, but they`re asking these two candidates to accept the results, of the November 8th election.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: To accept the results, and also ensure a peaceful transition of power, having to say that the transition needs to be peaceful, makes your heart leap into your throat that is where we are.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And there`s also the point, Rachel, that they`re not concerned with just a governor`s race, they`re concerned with Republicans, surely, in other offices around the state, not conceding and not accepting the results of the election, so they`re asking these candidates to be the leaders on it.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It`s an open question around the country, and this is the election for which there actually has to be small lead Democratic innovation. We saw in 2020 what happens when a candidate for president doesn`t accept the results of an election. We sort of now know in retrospect maybe how we should handle that.\par \par \uc2 But this will be the first election, where a majority of Republican candidates so they don`t believe in the last election result, and every Democrat who`s running against the mask come up with a plan. Every state in the country has to come up with the plan, for how they`re going to deal with Republican candidates, rejecting election results, that show that they lost.\par \par \uc2 We have never had to deal with this in a wholesale level before. But because of what the Republican Party has become in the Trump era, we now need to innovate how we do that is a country, this will be the first time we ever face it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And this is what will be covering, starting late tomorrow night through the rest of the week.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That`s right. Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Rachel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, in his closing argument this weekend to voters, who have not yet voted, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Tuning out is not an option. Sulking, and moping, that`s not an option. The only way to make this economy fair who, is if we, all of, us fight for it. The only way to save democracy is if we together, fight for it.\par \par \uc2 And it starts with electing people who know you, who see you, who care about you who stood in your shoes, they did that two years ago when you sent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to the White House, he`s fighting for you every day, he`s got your back, doing everything you can`t put more money in your pockets, to make our streets safer to bring more good paying jobs here to Pennsylvania, now you need to do it again.\par \par \uc2 Because while there may be a lot of issues at stake in this election, there is one basic question, that you should be asking yourself right now, and that, is who will fight for you. Who will fight for you? Who will fight for working people who are struggling to pay their bills?\par \par \uc2 Listen, inflation is real problem right now, it`s not just here, it`s for Americans worldwide. It has to do with the legacy of the pandemic. It wrecks supply chains, disrupted the economy, then you`ve got to war in Ukraine that sent energy prices through the roof, so yeah folksy grocery prices going up, gas prices going up, it takes real bite of the out of the paychecks, it hurts, I get that.\par \par \uc2 But, the question you`ve got to ask yourself is, who`s actually going to do something about it? The Republicans like to talk about it, but what`s their answer? What`s their economic policy?\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 They want to gut Social Security, they want to get Medicare, they want to give rich folks and big corporations more tax cuts. By the way, don`t boo. Vote. They can`t hear you boo, but they`ll hear you vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: On this, the final night of the campaign, tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  decided that his final campaign appearance will be at a rally trying to stop our first guest tonight, from being elected to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , stopping \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is Donald Trump`s top priority tonight as his appearance in Ohio proves \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who won the state of Ohio by eight points in the last election said this about the Republican Senate candidate he is now supporting in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: JD is kissing my asking, he wants my support so much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our first guest tonight, Ohio Senate candidate \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , reminded Ohio Republicans, what his opponent JD Vance, has said about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): You were calling Trump America`s Hitler. Then you kissed his ass --\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: It`s not true.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: It is true. And then you kissed his ass, and then he endorsed you, and you said he`s the greatest president of all-time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And in another debate, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think the problem is, when you have guys like JD Vance, who can`t stand up to anybody, it just a few weeks ago in Youngstown, on the stage, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said to JD Vance, all you do is kiss my ass to get my support, he said that, and that`s bad because that means JD Vance going to do whatever he wants. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  given a 40, he`s going to do what he wants, and \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  give 50 million, here`s the thing that`s most troubling about this, a lack of courage, is that after Trump took J.D. Vance`s dignity from, him onstage in Youngstown. J.D. Vance got up on stage and said, started taking his hand take a picture saying, hey, are we having a great time here tonight?\par \par \uc2 I don`t know anybody I grew up with, I don`t know anybody I went to high school with, they would allow somebody to take their dignity like that, and then get back up on stage. We need leaders who have courage to take on their own party, and I`ve proven that, and he was called and asked for by the former president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In the first year of his presidency, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised to save the \uc2 General Motors\uc2  plant in \uc2 Lordstown\uc2 , Ohio, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told people, quote, don`t move, don`t sell your house, end quote.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Lordstown\uc2  plant closed two years later in 2019, leaving 1,600 people without the jobs \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised he would say. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is back in Ohio tonight trying to win the job for JD Vance, in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And joining us now from his campaign bus in Ohio, is Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the Democratic Senate candidate in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. What are you telling Ohio voters in these closing moments tonight, in your closing argument against JD Vance? Who had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come in to help support him tonight?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: You`ve got a vote for who is most Ohio, Lawrence, and I`m the Ohio guy here. JD Vance gave up on Ohio, he left Ohio. He went to Silicon Valley, he`s a millionaire funded by billionaires who have shipped our jobs overseas, and the $40 million that he got from \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , this guy is bought and paid for, before he even steps into the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t get Ohio, he said itself he`s not comfortable in Ohio. He said if you`re a 55-year-old worker in Ohio, might as well just come to grips with the facts, that you will never have a good paint job again. And I wholly reject that, if that goes against every fiber of my being, that goes against my DNA.\par \par \uc2 We are going to fight to bring jobs by to Ohio dominate the industries of the future. That`s why he`s going to lose, because as you rightly pointed out, he`s an ass kisser and people in Ohio want to a leader.\par \par \uc2 And I`m a leader. I`ve taken on my own party. I`ve taken on Republicans. I`ve agreed with Republicans, if that`s best for Ohio, and he`s just going to follow the herd, and follow the extremists, and that`s not going to work and that`s why we`re going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We heard President \uc2 Obama\uc2  just a moment ago, talk about people worried about the economy, and how it`s affecting them, and how inflation is affecting them. But then, making the point, who do you think is going to do something about it? What have you heard from the Republicans, about what they will do about it?\par \par \uc2 [22:10:06]\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, they`ve got nothing. And first, I just want to note, Lawrence, for the record. I had a rally in Youngstown Ohio tonight, and I had more people in my rally than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did, OK?\par \par \uc2 This thing is going in the wrong direction for him, and in the right direction for us. And what we`re going to do, is we`re going to continue to invest into America, and outcompete China, rebuild the middle class. That`s what we`re doing here, we`re starting to big buildings again in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 That is 