 up to big pharma, something I was committed to doing, I did that. We finally got this Medicare price negotiation issue fixed, and seniors will be paying less for their prescription medication across the country.\par \par \uc2 And I committed to working with Republicans to do this. That's what I've done. My opponent Blake Masters, he says that he will not even work with moderate Republicans, and calls Democrats psychopaths. That's not what we need in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: I want to list what President \uc2 Obama\uc2  said in Arizona about economic policy. Let's listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: What's their economic policy, they do have one. They want to gut Social Security, and Medicare, and give rich folks and big corporations more tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, the reason we know that's their economic policy, is because they're policy for everything. If inflation is low, like it was when I was president, what we need to cut taxes. If unemployment is high, they want to cut taxes. When it's the reverse, what do they want to deal? They want to cut taxes for their friends.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Senator, you've got a significant retirement community in Arizona. President \uc2 Obama\uc2  talking about Republican Senate plans that really exist to cut Social Security, basically to create a new version of the program, which would be subject to finding every year. Do Arizona voters understand what the Republican plans for social security are?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I hope so. I've been talking to them about it. My opponent, Blake Masters, I mean, he said on the debate stage that he wants to privatized Social Security, not too long ago. He said he wants to cut the not, and that's not good for seniors. He wants to send their hard earned benefits to Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 And on the tax issue mentioned, he says he wants to double down on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's 1.9 trillion dollar tax cut, to the biggest corporations, and the wealthiest Americans. That's his economic, plan those two things.\par \par \uc2 He also said he wouldn't ever stand up to big pharma, he didn't say like that, but he said the legislation that we got across the finish line, that I negotiated, within our caucus, he said he wouldn't support that.\par \par \uc2 And he would allow seniors to continue paying more for the prescription drugs. So he's not on the side of Arizonans, especially Arizona seniors.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Let's listen to what President \uc2 Obama\uc2  said in Arizona, about the crime issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: Who will fight to keep you in your family safe? Is it the Republicans who want to flood our streets with more guns? Who actually voted against more resources for police departments? Or, is at the Democratic leaders, like Mark Kelly, who worked with President Biden to past the first major gun safety legislation in 30 years? That's the choice in this election. That's what's at stake right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Senator, what can you do for people in Arizona who are concerned about crime, if you return to the Senate?\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so I'm a son of two police officers. My mom was a first woman to be a cop in our hometown, my entire family, cops and firefighters. I drove an ambulance myself. So we come from a family of public servants. I did that when I was much younger.\par \par \uc2 There's certainly more we can do. We did pass bipartisan gun safety and mental health legislation that will have an impact, it's not everything. We have to do better. We have to continue to focus on this.\par \par \uc2 I've appropriated money for new police stations, in Evansville, coaches county for the sheriff's department, digital safety equipment, Glendale, the command center, constantly looking for opportunities to support law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 I think we have stronger and safer communities, and law enforcement has the tools, has the resources they need to do a tough job, get that and I'll continue what I'm in, Washington continue to fight for cops and sheriffs here in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Arizona Senator Mark Kelly, thank you very much for joining us. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, thank you, Lawrence. You know, we got -- you know, this election could be close, we are very confident here. I just want to make a pitch for any Arizonan out there to get out and vote for stronger democracy, with more people vote, regardless of who they're voting for. If you want to chip in and help, go to markkelly.com.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Senator, thank you very much. And you have heard Senator Kelly's closing argument here, at THE LAST WORD. Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, we`ll meet a Democrat who is going to be the next governor of Maryland. Wes Moore will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:32:28]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We will probably have an early call in the Maryland governor`s race tomorrow night because polling shows Donald Trump`s chosen candidate for governor of Maryland is running about 30 points -- yes, 30 points -- behind President \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s choice of candidate for governor of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You`re about to elect a new governor, and the choice couldn`t be clearer. My friend Wes Moore, is the leader Maryland needs. Now at a time when so much of our politics this about tearing people down, Wes Moore is working to bring people together and lift them up. He wants to build an economy that works for everyone and schools that are second to none. Because Wes knows that Maryland is strongest when no one is left behind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The Democratic Party`s best chance to flip a Republican governorship to a Democratic one is a Maryland, where outgoing Republican Governor Larry hogan has called the Republican candidate, Dan Cox, who is supported by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a quote "QAnon whack job".\par \par \uc2 Wes Moore is a Rhodes scholar and a combat veteran, who was joined on the campaign trail in Maryland tonight by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For Wes Moore, patriotism meant leaving his family and putting on the country`s uniform, and leading his fellow soldiers in combat.\par \par \uc2 For his opponent, patriotism means putting on a baseball cap, inviting people to attack the Capitol. You can`t be pro American and pro insurrection. It`s real simple.\par \par \uc2 WES MOORE (D), MARYLAND CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR: I love this state.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And I love this country. And I am a true and a proud patriot -- where I probably laid my life on the line to defend this country and I would do it over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:41]\par \par \uc2 And I`m proud of the fact that I am in a room full of patriots. Patriots who understand that loving your country does not mean lying about it. And who understand that fighting for democracy does not mean working to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 Patriotism isn`t simply waving a flag, patriotism is giving that flag breath and life. And to show people that the flag represents so much more than just simply an ideal.\par \par \uc2 It represents a promise, and a hope, the same promise and the same hope that my immigrant single mother latched onto after my father died in front of me when I was only three years old.\par \par \uc2 We refuse, and we will not allow one group to lay claim to the notion of patriotism, especially when that entails denying elections and storming the capital.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Wes Moore, Democratic candidate for governor of Maryland. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 What is your closing argument to the people of Maryland, on the issue of crime that Republicans have been running on?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, the closing argument is that we have to be able to have a society where we can get violent offenders off our streets, to get illegal guns off of our streets, and frankly, that is something that our party has been pushing, on and actually making happen.\par \par \uc2 We all understand the issue of public safety. I had to get off the campaign trail early this year, because a grandmother, a 69-year-old grandmother, who is living in east Baltimore, and was a member of my home church, was killed in the church bathroom.\par \par \uc2 So I understand this and I`ve seen this firsthand, and I understand the need to be able to address it. I also know that we need a police force that`s going to move with appropriate intensity and absolute integrity and full accountability.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re looking at the coalition that we have built, that our campaign has built to be able to address it there`s a reason why everyone from progressive Maryland, to the police union have actually supported our campaign. Because we understand that we`ve got to find ways of being able to work together. We`ve got to find ways of being able to be collaborative. If we`re going to get violent offenders off of our streets, illegal guns of out of neighborhoods, and making sure that children never have to grow up in a community that they do not feel safe in.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Your current Republican governor has called the Republican candidate a QAnon whack job. Do you expect your opponent to concede this election, and accept the results of this election, if you win?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I mean my opponent has said, he has not decided if he`ll accept the elections for tomorrow night. But the reality is that I`m not holding my breath, because he still has not accepted the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 So the thing that we`re doing is, we`re going to control what we can control which is we plan on winning so overwhelmingly. We plan on not just winning tomorrow night, but also sending a message, that whether or not we get a concession from my opponent or not, is frankly irrelevant to me.\par \par \uc2 We plan on winning by such a margin, and such a number, that we are going to send a message to the state, that we are prepared to lead, and we are going to be moving forward.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: With inflation as a global phenomenon, inflation in the United States actually a bit lower than it is in many other industrialized countries. What can you do, and what can you tell voters of Maryland you can do, about what they are suffering in the current economy, with inflation, that is beyond the reach of a governor.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes. I mean the issue of rising cost is real, Lawrence, and people say around the state of Maryland whether talking about Westminster or West Baltimore, when they say things just feel like they`re more expensive the answer is it`s because they are.\par \par \uc2 And so there`s a few things that the governor can uniquely do to be able to address it. One is we`ve got to get people back to work. And that means doing things like fixing a broken childcare system. Since COVID began, we`ve had over 750 childcare centers in the state of Maryland closed. If you have childcare centers that are not open, you`re not going to get parents, particularly parents of young children back into the workforce.\par \par \uc2 We have to invest in job retraining and job reskilling. Right now in the state of Maryland we have two available jobs for every single person filing for unemployment, because there`s a disconnect between the skill sets and the dynamic economy that Maryland presents.\par \par \uc2 And the third thing is we`ve got to increase wages. You cannot have situations where people are working jobs, in some cases multiple jobs, and still living above below poverty line. That`s the Alice (ph) population. And so we`ve got to make sure we`re focusing on pathways for work, wages, and wealth for all Marylanders.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:52]\par \par \uc2 That`s the way we cannot just deal with the issue of short term rising costs, but the issue of long term economic stability and economic growth.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You have not held elective office before. To start off in a governorship, voters are going to have to trust you with an ability they have not seen. They have not seen you hold an elected office before at some lower level, or some other level that we give them an indicator of how you would work as a governor.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell voters about what to expect of you as a possible governor?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, I tell people that, I`ve been a public servant for much of my life, I just haven`t been a politician. But what voters are going to vote for and hire for their next governor is the state`s next chief executive, and I would put my executive experience against anybody, whether it was leading soldiers in combat where I led paratroopers with the 82nd Airborne Division in Afghanistan, or when I came back and started a successful small business in Maryland helping students go to and through college, or when I lead one of the largest poverty fighting organizations in America.\par \par \uc2 I know what it means to lead. I know what it means to work across sectors. And I know what it means to work on these issues whether you`re but they`re talking about economic growth or folks in early childhood or criminal justice reform or housing. These have been -- this has been the work of my life.\par \par \uc2 And so when you look at so many other states who actually for their governor to include Maryland in Virginia and Connecticut and Pennsylvania where the first elected office that those governors held was governor. Because when people are looking for a governor, you`re looking for an executive who understands how to work across sectors and get big things done. And that`s what I`ve done for my entire life.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Wes Moore, you have now heard his closing argument in this campaign, and you might be hearing from him very early tomorrow night in one of the first victory speeches of the night.\par \par \uc2 Wes Moore thank you very much for joining us tonight, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: It`s great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. And coming up, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  has joined Beto O`Rourke`s campaign for governor of Texas. \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:42:01]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE (D), TEXAS CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR: Your friends, your family members, your classmates, your colleagues, your neighbors, the folks who follow you on social media. They know and trust you. They believe in you, and if you say that you`re going to vote, they are more likely to vote as well.\par \par \uc2 So I am asking you to respond to this message with your own, from your own heart in your own words, why this election is so important to you, and why you have either already voted or are planning to vote on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 I bet if you do that, it will break through to the people in your lives, the folks who have the power to decide the outcome of this election, and together, we will win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  is using his final days in the campaign to turn out as many voters as possible, visiting college campuses, knocking on doors, and speaking to crowds at campaign stops across Texas.\par \par \uc2 Some of those voters are also receiving this call from President \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: On reproductive rights, Greg Abbott has signed one of the most extreme abortion laws in America. \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  will protect a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 On gun safety, Greg Abbott made it easier to buy guns just before 19 kids and their teachers were murdered in Uvalde. \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  will work to restore common sense.\par \par \uc2 On health care, Greg Abbott refused to bring home billions of dollars in Medicaid money to give more Texans access to health care. Beto will do what`s right.\par \par \uc2 On issue after issue, \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  will restore common sense and decency to Texas state government.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I`m asking you to vote for Beto, and help build the America we deserve.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , Democratic candidate for governor of Texas. Thank you very much for joining us on what will be your final appearance at this hour as a candidate in Texas. What have you learned in this long campaign that you bring to the final day of the campaign, that you want to leave with Texans before they vote tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: We, the people of Texas, are absolutely extraordinary. You have everything in the world that has been thrown at us in recent years. The power grid failure that killed 700 people in the energy capital of the world. The most extreme abortion ban in a state that is at the epicenter of a maternal mortality crisis. We lead the nation in school shootings, gun violence the leading cause of death for children and teenagers. We have seen property taxes go up, electricity bills go up.\par \par \uc2 And yet instead of succumbing to the temptation to despair, Texans have stood up to overcome this and in record numbers. It`s folks from across the state, including those young people that I saw today at Texas State and San Marcus (ph) and Texas A&M and College States -- voters who will be casting their first ballots in their first elections. They`re not showing up in anyone`s polls, they`re not in anyone`s forecast or model. Their voices have never been heard and their votes have never been counted.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:50]\par \par \uc2 And they represent the margin of victory in this to bring us through not just to stop the bad stuff from happening. Although that is urgent. But to make sure that we can do truly great things for one another like focusing on world-class public schools and paying those educators enough not to work a second and a third job, expanding Medicaid, as the former president described in his message which would bring billions of dollars back to our state and connect millions of Texans with care that will literally save and improve their lives.\par \par \uc2 These are the things that we want to do. Democrats, Republicans, Independents alike, we are so much better than those who represent or serve us in office right now. and we`re going to be able to show the world that tomorrow night, 7:00 p.m. Mountain Standard Time is when polls close in my hometown of El Paso.\par \par \uc2 After that, we will be watching the returns, and we hope celebrating together with the people of Texas.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to go back to that point you just made about the Medicaid expansion and what that could mean for Texas. Especially at a time of inflation where obviously health care costs are always part of inflation. They go up higher than the normal inflation rate. This is an opportunity if Texas were to expand Medicaid under the Affordable Care Act. to help people with the cost of something as crucial as health care.\par \par \uc2 What is the Republican reason for refusing to do that as they have repeatedly refused to do it Texas?\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: There really isn`t one. In fact, a lot of Republicans agree with Medicaid expansion. Our four neighboring states -- Louisiana, Arkansas, Oklahoma, New Mexico, three with Republican governors -- have all expanded Medicaid because it brings back your federal income tax dollars that you`ve already paid up to D.C. to connect to people in your state with care.\par \par \uc2 It creates hundreds of thousands of jobs annually. And the kicker for us in Texas where we have seen our property taxes go up $20 billion under Greg Abbott, is it will bring those property taxes down, because we`re no longer on the hook for uncompensated indigent care our county hospitals.\par \par \uc2 And in a state that now leads the nation in childhood diabetes deaths, a state that is 51st behind every other and the district of Columbia in mental health care access, to the point that our jails are the largest provider of mental health care services in the state today. This is going to help a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 There`s no good reason not to do it. In fact, the only person standing in the way right now is Greg Abbott. That`s why we have to turn the page on him, vote for change, and move forward with great, big ideas that enjoy bipartisan support, like expanding Medicaid.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Governor Abbott seems to have admitted that it is unclear now in Texas when doctors can act to save the life of the mother in a possible abortion procedure that could be legal to save the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 How did it come to this? And what is it that Texas needs to do?\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: This is the most extreme abortion ban in America. So no exceptions for rape, incest, and to your point, there is really effectively, functionally, no exception for the life of the woman.\par \par \uc2 And I know this from having listened to so many women across the state of Texas who described stories of intended pregnancies. Ones where, as the fetus develops, they`d already chosen a name for what they hoped would be their child, only to learn that the complications were so severe that in some cases, the fetus was not viable or even dead. And yet doctors could not perform the surgeries or provide the Medicare help that they needed because they`re also unclear about this law.\par \par \uc2 And they`ve asked these women to come back when they are literally on deaths door. Luckily, the women I met were able to survive, some by going to other states, some by being able to prevail upon these doctors that they were about to die. But in a state that leads the country in this epidemic of maternal mortality, three times as deadly for black women, this is going to consign more women to suffering and death, another reason to vote for change tomorrow in Texas.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , Democratic candidate for governor of Texas, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Thank you for giving us your closing argument.\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:21]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here is President \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s closing argument to voters this weekend in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: The kind of slash and burn politics that we`re seeing right now, that doesn`t have to be who we are. We can be better. And it has nothing to do by the way, with political correctness, or being too woke.\par \par \uc2 It`s about fundamental values, that my grandparents from Kansas taught me. Values I grew up with. Values you grew up with. Values we try to teach our kids. Values we learn in churches and mosques and synagogues and temples. Honesty, fairness, opportunity, hard work, values that \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  stand for, values that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  stands for. Values that were enshrined in our founding documents, a few miles from here. A clarion call for freedom and equality that Philly`s own Liberty Bell represents. That`s what America stands for. That`s who we are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:43]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: President \uc2 Obama\uc2  gets tonight`s LAST WORD and the campaign`s LAST WORD on this program.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow is election day in America, as you know. And tomorrow night I`ll join Rachel Maddow for a special election night coverage, which begins at 6:00 p.m. eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221108eib700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MSNBC DECISION 2022 for November 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Steve Kornacki, Chris Hayes, Joy Reid, Nicolle  Wallace, Lawrence O`Donnell, Alex Wagner, Jen Psaki, Claire McCaskill,  Michael Steele, Jacob Soboroff \par \uc2 8,503 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): This is by the way, the first time I have ever campaigned for a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): It`s the first nationwide vote since an American insurrection.\par \par \uc2 MEMET OZ (R-PA): We need to get back to the policies of the real \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TIM RYAN (D-OH): Ohio needs an ass-kicker, not an ass-kisser.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Now, with 36 states voting for governor.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): So, you put the Second Amendment before second graders?\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R-MI), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Republicans will never lose another election in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: 35 Senate seats up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): This cannot continue.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): You actually have to know stuff to do this job.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: All 435 House seats in play.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): If that means the government shutdown, then I`ll be calling for a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And fear that this free and fair election could be the last.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Make no mistake, Democracy is in the ballot for all of us.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Tonight, Special Election Eve coverage with Nicolle Wallace, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , Chris Hayes, Alex Wagner, Lawrence O`Donnell, Ari Melber, Stephanie Ruhle, Steve Kornacki at the big board, and the MSNBC Political Insiders. Special coverage of the 2022 Midterm Elections begins now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Good evening from MSNBC headquarters. I`m Rachel Maddow joined here in our brand spanking new election studio. I brought my colleagues Nicolle Wallace and \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  and Chris Hayes and Lawrence O`Donnell. Tomorrow is the final day that we can all vote in this year`s big Midterm Elections. Of course, many, many, many Americans have already cast their votes on this Election Day Eve.\par \par \uc2 We can now report that over 42 million Americans have already cast a ballot. That is significantly more early votes that were cast in the last midterm election in 2018. And that one overall had record turnout. Americans are turning out in record numbers.\par \par \uc2 Now, for folks who have put it off to the very end, and you have not yet voted, candidates and tight races across the country, tonight, they`re making a frantic final push to try to get voters out to the polls. At this hour. President Biden has just wrapped up a rally in Maryland, a rally for Westmore, the Democratic candidate for governor there, and for other Maryland Democrats.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is about to take the stage in Ohio where the tight race for an open Senate seat there may decide who controls the Senate overall. Trump has previously insulted and tried to humiliate the Republican contender for that Senate race in Ohio, J.D. Vance, but tonight he`s in Ohio with Vance to support his campaign.\par \par \uc2 Today`s new NBC News poll, the last one before the election, finds that likely voters are almost exactly evenly split on which party they want to control Congress. 48 say that -- 48 percent say they prefer Democratic control. 47 percent say they prefer Republican control. That`s a tie. But that same poll also had some unexpected and heartening news for Democrats. Since last month, Democratic enthusiasm levels have gone up while Republicans have decreased.\par \par \uc2 Just a month ago, Republican voters held a nine-point edge on enthusiasm about the election. Now as of today, Democrats have caught up and it is actually even between the two parties. That enthusiasm measures considered an important factor in terms of who will turn out at the polls tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But if you have to pick just one thing to learn one thing to be cognizant of this election eve, it`s this. Democrats and Republicans are voting differently, meaning they are voting by different methods. NBC`s new poll today shows that voters who want Democrats to control Congress voted early by a two-to-one margin. Voters who want Republicans to control Congress are not doing that by a two-to-one margin. Pro-Republican voters plan to vote tomorrow on election day itself.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, the vote from people who vote on Election Day tomorrow will lean Republican, the early vote will lean Democrats. And that is the one thing to keep in mind as we all head into the incredible circus enjoy that is election results tomorrow. That is why Republicans all over the country have been laying the groundwork for early votes and absentee votes and mail-in votes to be seen as suspect or somehow less legitimate.\par \par \uc2 Already Republicans have filed lawsuits that have gotten certain early vote ballots tossed out in states like Pennsylvania and Wisconsin. Republicans from former President Trump on down have been telling their supporters that they ought to be suspicious of any vote counts that take a long time. Trump lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  today saying that only the ballots that are tallied in the first few hours should count and any ballots counted later than that should be seen as suspect.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:04]\par \par \uc2 The reason they are doing this is because in many states, the same day Election Day votes, the ones that are disproportionately Republican, those votes are counted first. Only after that that they start tallying up the early votes which lean Democratic. So, tomorrow, we should all expect that in many states, the first tallies will look relatively good for Republicans, and then Republican leads will begin to shrink as the night goes on and as the vote counting stretches into the late evening, overnight into Wednesday, and even longer.\par \par \uc2 We know now to expect howls of protests from Republicans who will insist that that`s somehow suspect, that there`s vote fraud and election rigging going on. They`ll demand that the vote counting stop. They`ll file even more lawsuits on top of the dozens, they already have to try to disqualify early, largely Democratic votes.\par \par \uc2 But knowing it`s coming doesn`t stop it from happening. It does help us know what it means when it does come. But setting those expectations is probably the best thing that we can all do for ourselves, for our family members, for our friends, in terms of being real, being patient, and not jumping to conclusions about tomorrow night`s results.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now for the first time is the great Steve Kornacki at the big board. Steve, tell us what`s important from this big final NBC News poll that`s out today.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, some sort of conflicting signals I think you could say. You had the headline there. That was the likely voter model. There`s also registered voters basically the same here though, the generic ballot asking folks which party do you want to have control of Congress. Registered voters dead even 47-47. If you`re a Democrat, you spent the year here and about an impending red wave, obviously, this is welcome news for you in the final NBC News poll before the election.\par \par \uc2 Underneath these numbers, though, there are some pretty strong headwinds that are working against Democrats here instead of sort of an interesting test between this number and what`s underneath the number. And it starts with this. It starts with the President`s job approval rating. We have seen -- it`s not common, but we`ve seen in history some parties defy the trend where the White House Party suffers losses, generally big losses in Midterm Elections. They don`t tend to do it when the President`s approval rating is where Joe Biden`s is right now, 44 percent in our final poll. A majority disapproving we`ve seen Biden`s number hovering in this area for a long time in our polling.\par \par \uc2 To put this in some context, final NBC News poll for recent presidents before their first midterm, you can see it right here, Biden`s number is right in that range. Look, it`s -- Trump was 46 in 2018, Obama was 45 in 2010. You know, in 2018, democrat -- Republicans lost the House 40 seats. In 2010, Democrats lost the house 63 seats. You see the outlier. This is the kind of approval rating. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  in 2002, 63 percent. That`s the kind of approval rating it typically takes for a White House Party to defy history and have a good midterm. In this case, the Republicans picked up a handful of seats in 2002.\par \par \uc2 So, just on the approval rating which historically has driven midterm results in a lot of ways, Biden is right in that danger zone and Democrats are right in that danger zone. You layer on top of that the feelings, the environment, the attitude when it comes to the economy, our poll finding that more than eight in 10. voters say they are dissatisfied with the current state of the economy. We haven`t seen a number that high on this question since `09, `10, around the time of the crash, the Great Recession, that period of time.\par \par \uc2 And then when you ask people straight up, what`s the most important issue as you make up your mind, economy and cost of living, inflation, those two combined for 37 percent here in terms of being the top issue. And the folks who cite that at their top -- as their top issue are breaking in our poll for Republicans by a 43-point margin.\par \par \uc2 The headwinds that Democrats have against them here is sort of historically speaking, and in terms of the climate we`re in right now. What are Democrats counting on to try to counter all of that? Well, you could see this issue of threats to democracy which comes in as the second most important issue on people`s mind. Democrats on this have an issue -- have an advantage of 68 points on the generic ballot.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s also the issue of abortion obviously brought to the floor by the Supreme Court decision. Just under one in 10 voters citing that as their most important issue. And again, it`s a tremendous Democratic advantage there of 45 points. So, there are some issues besides the economy and inflation where Democrats have pronounced advantages.\par \par \uc2 And then as you said at the beginning, there is simply that shift that our poll finds. We`ll see what tomorrow brings, but our poll says that on enthusiasm, there`s been a change in the last several weeks where Democrats have drawn even. And I think that is the ultimate wildcard in this election tomorrow. We are looking at probable turnout when all is said and done of somewhere in the neighborhood of 120 to 130 million voters for a midterm election. Just eight years ago in the 2014 midterm, 85 million people voted in it. That`s how much interest in politics has risen over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 To be at a level that high, it would have been unthinkable before. And so, it sort of is the ultimate variable. Are we at a point in these midterm elections where Democrats may get voters motivated who in the past wouldn`t have been? Maybe they`re not wild about Biden, maybe they`re not wild about the economy, but maybe there`s an idea, they`re voting against Republicans, voting against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , may be that kind of thing, or an environment now where it can drive turnout in a way it hasn`t been past midterms.\par \par \uc2 Basically, if Democrats walk away with a good night tomorrow and our poll does give them a couple of things to be hopeful about. If they walk away with a good night, we`re kind of seeing the rules of politics get rewritten a little bit.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:54]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Steve Kornacki, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I will say that last point that Steve was making about quantitative analysis versus qualitative analysis, I don`t feel all that surprised at seeing what people are saying they care about and how those numbers break down. But thinking about 120 to 130 million people turning out already breaking midterm turnout records. I mean, that`s the part -- to me, that`s surprising and that makes it feel unpredictable.\par \par \uc2 And I remember 2014 very well because we -- you know, started the show in 2013 and it was the first big election I covered at ALL IN. And there was not a lot of interest. Like, believe me, like people were tuned in.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We arranged that in the country just to mess with you.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Yes, well, it was -- no, and it was really -- I mean, you could feel it, right? It was hard to get people motivated. It was the sort of final act of the Obama presidency, so I think people were sort of a little bit checked out. We kind of recovered from the Great Recession. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not come on the scene yet. So, just looking at that 40 million vote difference, right, between 85 and 125, the only thing I will say is this.\par \par \uc2 I do worry about expectations for tomorrow on the right for this reason. Conservative media has told its base, there`s no question what`s happening tomorrow, it is a red wave, it is going to be a landslide. And it may be. It may be one of those elections where you see some random Democratic governor go down who no one thought was going to go down, right? Early in the night, some Congress -- members of Congress who no one thought had a contested race loses to an unknown. That`s when you know --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And then it ends up being 60 seats.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and then -- exactly. That`s when you know you`re in that kind of like huge landslide. It may be that. I don`t know. The polling data is not that clear. It could also be a polling miss in the other direction such that Democrats have a fairly decent night. I don`t know if they hold on the House, but they -- and I do worry the expectation has been so set in Republican conservative voters` minds this is going to be an enormous red wave landslide, that should that not come, we know what the answer will be provided to them about why -- not a polling miss like Democrats had to deal with in 2016 when they`re were like, what happened? Or in 2020 when they lost a lot of seats, they should have. Not oh, the polling was wrong, the measurement was wrong, turnout was different than we thought, look in the data, there was all these confounding signals. And it turned out actually Democrats overperform. No, we know what the answer will be and I`m very worried about that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And we`ve already got 40-plus percent of Republican voters saying they don`t trust this year`s midterm election results even before we`ve got them.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And I think we have to think about as media in general, not just conservative media. You know, I think in general, the media has created an expectation where Republicans -- I think it would be bad enough if they could just say Fox News said it. But I think in general, the general zeitgeist of the media coverage is that the red wave is coming, because a lot of the polling averages are saying that, but a lot of the polling averages are filled with a lot of junky polls.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think one of the issues --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Also, there`s just uncertainties.\par \par \uc2 REID: And there`s just uncertainties, and no one knows. And so, I think there`s a risk. And I think we as the media have to start really thinking about what are the incentives to presuppose anything? The only vote that really counts -- the only poll that really counts is the vote, right? That`s the one that`s real. And I -- you know, I think what you said earlier is really important. The way that campaigns are thinking about this tonight, is the Democratic campaigns writ large, they`re saying, OK, we have about a -- we`re nursing about a four million vote lead in terms of what the electorate looks like that`s voting early.\par \par \uc2 The real data, not in the polling, but the data, Democrats or the way that the electorate looks is it`s about 53 percent women. That says to me that there`s a huge amount of energy that is being driven by a thing that women care about? What is that thing? It`s Roe. I feel like we`ve been undercounting it and undercutting it since the summer. Women just don`t get over the idea that they no longer own their bodies. That`s not something that they say, you know, if milk was a little cheaper, I`d get over it. That isn`t something that happens.\par \par \uc2 So, what I look at the electorate, and the way that campaigns are looking at it, are saying can Republicans catch up to that four million vote lead on election day because that`s when they`re voting. And it used to be back in the day when I used to be on the other side of a campaign with that pretty lady over there is that we would say, OK, we`ve got an early vote lead because young voters, Black voters in particular, college students people who have to work hourly jobs, that`s your Democratic electorate. They vote early.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:11]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And they lead high election day turnout.\par \par \uc2 REID: Souls to the polls, right? And then on election day, it used to be a wash. You know what the difference used to be? Absentee. Because in `04, we really thought, oh, Democrats going to do OK. And then the absentee from Republicans wiped out the Democrats, OK. And then that 2014 thing too.\par \par \uc2 Now that Trump has turned Republicans against absentee, we know absentee is probably leading D and now the only question --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s the election day.\par \par \uc2 REID: -- is can they get it done that one day?\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: I think too, there`s something that polls don`t pick up when women are deleting their ovulation trackers. So, you think they`re going to be like, oh, delete, delete. Yes, this is what I`m voting on. I don`t -- you`re going to blow a gasket when I quote Don Rumsfeld, but it`s a known unknown.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Who knows? I mean, to your point, who knows? I think --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So, you both -- just to be clear, you both think that the importance of abortion as a driver for Democratic voters, that abortion is being under -- it`s being under --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It`s not showing up in the polls. Do you think it`s stronger than it looks?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I think it`s unknowable, because I think if you don`t trust your \uc2 Apple\uc2  watch to track your period, you`re not responding to a polls about how much you care, whether you care a lot or not at all. I don`t think you`re answering the question. I don`t even know if you`re taking those calls anymore if you`re that voter.\par \par \uc2 I also think that this idea that the dog`s vote peaked early, it`s 50 years of precedent was overturned. You don`t get over it in like 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 REID: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: It`s not like, that was then, this is now. You know, I also think that the trap that the right set is that if you care about democracy, you`re indifferent to economic angst. You are not. I have watched all these Democratic candidates. To a tee they all understand, and I bet you, they all know the price of milk, meat, and bread in all of their grocery stores in their -- in their districts or in their states. So, I think that Republicans set this trap and I think that`s another flaw of the media.\par \par \uc2 The third thing is something has changed in the last seven days. I talked to candidates in Pennsylvania and strategists in Michigan, and they all said that this weekend felt totally different than last week.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Why?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I`m not sure. I mean, I`m not sure if it`s that last jobs number. I mean, I think because I`m sort of a political romantic that Obama got out there and was like, this is how you do it. And you know, he tied together democracy and economy, and you know, started sort of -- you look at the Josh Shapiro speech from Saturday night, and this tells you too much about my Saturday night. I have seen -- I have seen the numbers and I`m like, oh, my God, who is that?\par \par \uc2 I mean, Shapiro is channeling Obama. Obama is channeling the majority of Americans who don`t want to think get to choose between the price of milk and living in a democracy. You can have both. And thank God, he finally said it.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: But, you know, I think if that sunk in. And I`m not saying there won`t be a red wave. I`m just saying, we won`t know until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: I am going to take my place in the audience for the rest of this program. I have no idea what`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I would like to sit next to you in the audience. I have no idea either.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I have no idea. And I`ll just give one footnote about why I have no idea. I`ve given up thinking about this -- these kinds of elections, congressional elections, literally for decades for the following reason. You got to be 40 years old in this country when I was about 40 years old without ever having seen the Republicans control the House of Representatives, ever right in my lifetime, OK.\par \par \uc2 And then in 1994, the Republicans win the House and they win the Senate in what is Bill Clinton`s midterm -- first midterm election. Here`s who predicted that, not a living soul. No one predicted that the Republicans is going to win the House for the first time in 40 years, and no one predicted that the 57 Democrats in the Senate were the next day going to become the minority. No one predicted it.\par \par \uc2 And I was working in the Senate at the time. I had to move from a big office in the majority side to a cubicle on the minority side of the building, and I knew then what William Goldman, the great screenwriter, had said about showbusiness, years before that, no one knows anything. And that`s -- so a predictive -- the predictive elements of tonight`s discussion I will listen to. I have nothing to contribute.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: No. And I mean, what we are all predicting is that what`s important is what happens tomorrow. And the polls thus far have been interesting but nothing -- as Joy put it, the only poll that matters is the vote. A man who knows that better than all of us put together joins us now. He is Mandela Barnes, the lieutenant governor of the great state of Wisconsin. He is the Democratic candidate challenging Republican incumbent Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  in a race that is absolutely crucial for both sides.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Governor Barnes, it`s a real pleasure to have you with us tonight. Thank you so much for making time.\par \par \uc2 MANDELA BARNES (D-WI), SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, thank you so much for having me. I`m really excited about this campaign. I`m excited about the position we`re in and about the momentum we`ve been seeing. I`m really glad to have the conversation with you all.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:07]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Well, tell us about that momentum. We`ve all been discussing here how much we feel like tomorrow is a black box and that anything seems possible. Nicolle has been saying that she feels like particularly in the last week, and some of the polls indicate this, it does feel like there`s some Democratic momentum and the Democrats in places where they`ve been behind feel like they`re catching up.\par \par \uc2 When you say that you`re -- you`ve got momentum heading into tomorrow, what does that look like in practical terms from your campaign?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, I`ll tell you. I`m actually on my RV right now, if this looks a little bit rumbly, from the middle of our Wind for Wisconsin RV. We just hit our 100 stop and 15 days. And I can tell you, the crowds that have been showing up are all across Wisconsin, the enthusiasm is very real. And the best thing about it is these are folks that I haven`t seen before ever.\par \par \uc2 These aren`t the same people that showed up four years ago. They aren`t the people who have been coming to campaign events throughout this campaign. That`s the most exciting part about it. And the energy is, you know, is felt from the older generation, people were frustrated about the fact that \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  wants take away Social Security and Medicare. Its young people who were frustrated about the fact that they have a Senator who call climate change BS, and also just women in general all across Wisconsin who were frustrated that one of their senators told them that they can move if they don`t like the laws in their state like our 1849 criminal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 But as you know, races in Wisconsin are notoriously close. Our last three November elections were decided about 30,000 votes or fewer. We need all the help we can get to get over the hump. If folks can go to mandelabarnes.com to help us out in this last minute, everything goes a long way to help us beat \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Lieutenant Governor, Chris Hayes here. I have a very clear memory of about two or three in the morning the day after the election in 2020 when there was big votes -- vote tallies happening in the state of Wisconsin, particularly around Milwaukee that showed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  polling into a mathematically plausible situation that he might win your state and that might mean the election.\par \par \uc2 What is your expectations for yourself and setting in terms of how long it`s going to take to count these votes? Because Wisconsin, like Pennsylvania, is a state where the Republican legislator has not allowed state officials to pre-count those early votes. So, how are you just thinking about the timeline of tomorrow night?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, I`ll tell you. I`m prepared to talk to as many people as I can personally all the way until 8:00 p.m. tomorrow night when polls close. And we`re ready to be awake as long as it takes until the last ballot is counted. You know, we have -- we were up late at night four years ago in 2018. And we`ve been through this before. We`re not afraid to go through it again.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is you got people like \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  who won`t even commit to accepting the election results. I`m committed to accepting electoral results because I value democracy even when \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  doesn`t. And with that being said, there are a lot of folks who have been putting in countless hours whether it`s the boots on the ground or the people who are, you know, work in the polls, making sure that democracy functions properly and keeping people`s ballots safe and secure and protecting the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Wisconsin Democratic Senate candidate Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes, sir, we know this is the busiest of all nights for you. Thank you for joining us tonight and good luck to you.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Of course. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: By NBC`s counts, there are at least 276 Republican candidates running for office tomorrow, including Mandela Barnes` opponent, \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 , who refused to accept the results of the last election at one level or another. What will that mean for whether they accept the results of this next one tomorrow? That`s next stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The majority of Republican candidates on the ballot for House, Senate, and statewide races tomorrow, the majority of them are election deniers. More than half of those election deniers are expected to win their races. This is a really unprecedented phenomenon. And in covering this election, we are having to treat it as such.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow night for the first time ever, we here at MSNBC are going to have a team of 15 people just following the election deniers, keeping track of who among them wins, who among them concedes when they lose, and who among them refuses to concede despite the results.\par \par \uc2 One of the races we`re going to be watching closely is one in New Hampshire between Democratic U.S. Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  and her Republican challenger, a retired general and election denier named Don Bolduc. Last week, Mr. Bolduc got an endorsement from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And interestingly, in that endorsement, I kid you not, Trump overtly described Bolduc as "a strong and proud election denier."\par \par \uc2 He wants people to know he`s an election denier and he`s proud of it. That`s why you should support him. For whatever reason, Mr. Bolduc has decided to not mention that Trump endorsement publicly, not even once since it happened. But the latest polling in this New Hampshire race is close. It is within the margin of error close.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Joining us now live is \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , Democratic senator for New Hampshire. Senator Hassan, it`s nice to see you. We know you`re very, very busy tonight. Thank you for taking a few minutes to be here with us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): It`s great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Senator Hassan, I wanted to ask you, as somebody who`s an incumbent senator, as somebody who is very much moderate and has that reputation, somebody who has a reputation both as a -- as a workhorse, not a show horse, somebody who works at both sides of the aisle, I wanted to ask, given the challenge that you`re facing from sort of the far-right with your -- with your competitor, has anything surprised you in terms of what New Hampshire residents want to talk to you about, what they`re saying they care about, what they want to hear from you about?\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Look, I am all over the state right now talking to Granite Staters where they are. And what I am hearing about is of course inflation. And we`re talking about the work I`ve done across party lines to do things like ban surprise medical bills and lower people`s health care costs, or the Chips and Science Act, which will bring supply chains back to the United States and help lower inflation that way. People are very, very concerned about home heating costs here in New Hampshire as we face wintertime. And we`re talking about the steps we`ve taken to help with that too.\par \par \uc2 But I am also hearing about concerns about extremism and my opponent`s extremism. You just talked about it. Don Bolduc is an election denier who spent over a year stoking the big lie about 2020 in our state. He is actively casting doubt on tomorrow`s results in New Hampshire, a state that has some of the best elections in the country. And has said that if in 2024, if his preferred presidential candidate doesn`t win, he as a United States Senator, if he were to be in the Senate, would vote to overturn that election.\par \par \uc2 People are concerned about that because of its extreme nature and how undemocratic small D that is. But they are also really concerned about the combination of an election denier, like my opponent, with his extreme agenda that would raise their costs and eviscerate their personal rights. So, you know, if you have an election denier in office, what it ultimately means is that they can impose an extreme agenda. Don Bolduc would eliminate Social Security and be a yes vote for a nationwide abortion ban, because they don`t think they`re accountable to you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator, it`s Lawrence O`Donnell. I want to go to that point you just mentioned about Social Security. What are New Hampshire voters telling you about that? How -- because issue like Social Security has been -- has been part of these kinds of campaigns for decades now. You now have the highest cost of living adjustment in Social Security in many years, in fact, in decades, because inflation is so high, and the Social Security payments are pegged to that inflation to keep pace with it.\par \par \uc2 So many Republicans have worked for many years to try to cut that cost-of- living increase and to cut Social Security itself. But it doesn`t seem to penetrate in these campaigns.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Well, so, one of the real issues in my campaign is that my opponent keeps trying to conceal his position. So, he is on record, on video saying he would eliminate Social Security. That position has been confirmed by independent fact-checker checkers. But he denies it when asked about it. And he`s being backed up by over $60 million in money from National Republican super PACs who are trying to buy an election-denier \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat.\par \par \uc2 And so, part of our job is to make sure that people understand how extreme his position is, that he is trying to conceal it from people. People want us to protect their social security. They want us to protect Medicare. He also would privatize Medicare and make over $1 trillion in cuts to it, but he`s also trying to conceal that. And so, that`s the real rub here.\par \par \uc2 You know, in a recent debate, Don Bullock said that he was sick of talking about election denial. He wasn`t going to answer any more questions about it. This is somebody who believes he can refuse to accept results of an election, so he really doesn`t think he has to engage with Granite State voters or be honest with them. And voters do care about this. I`m hearing a lot about it. But they also care about other things like inflation and like a woman`s fundamental freedoms.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , Democratic senator for New Hampshire, up for reelection against Don Bolduc, the Republican candidate. Senator Hassan, we know this is a really busy night. Thank you for making time tonight and good luck to you.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: And thank you. And Rachel, just one last thing I will say. We also really encouraged people to still get engaged in the campaign at maggiehassan.com. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Well done. You`d be -- you`d be remiss if you didn`t mention that we know. It is amazing to be talking to Senate candidates. Wisconsin and New Hampshire, we just spoke to those two candidates to know that one of the things they have to be contending with is that their Republican opponent, if the Republican loses, will likely not acknowledge the result of the election. And that`s one of the things that these campaigns have to contend with. We`ve all covered a lot of elections. We`ve never had to -- had to contend with this at a systemic level.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:22]\par \par \uc2 All right, when we come back, we`re going to go to our panel of political insiders on something that Nicolle and Joy both say may be being misstated by the polls right now, the importance of abortion rights on the ballot tomorrow, whether the polls accurately capture it. That`s next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The last NBC News poll before tomorrow`s election shows that when you ask Democratic likely voters what their top concern is heading into the election, their number one answer is threats to democracy. But right after that, is abortion rights. For more on that, we turn now to our colleague Alex Wagner and the all-star team of political insider she is with. Alex, over to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Thanks, Rachel. In five states, Michigan, Kentucky, Montana, California and Vermont, abortion is literally on the ballot tomorrow. And depending on what happens in races elsewhere, we could see entire regions of the country becoming deserts for reproductive freedom. This is a map of the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 Illinois is the only state there where abortion is actively protected. Michigan`s 1931 trigger law that would ban all abortions has been blocked in courts and tomorrow, Michigan voters will decide on an amendment that would enshrine abortion rights in the state`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 The stakes in the Southeast are even higher. It pretty much all comes down to North Carolina and whether the state legislature welcomes a Republican supermajority tomorrow. If that happens, it could effectively cut off abortion access for an entire region of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s in a ballot measure or in the hands of state lawmakers, the matter of choice is being voted on across the United States tomorrow. Is that reality enough to energize Democratic voters to turn out. We will ask our insiders right here.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now are former White House Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , former Missouri Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill, and former \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair Michael Steele. Thank you, my team of insiders. Jen and Claire, let me start with you in terms of that map. It`s confusing, right? I think a lot of people don`t fully understand the reality of the attacks on reproductive freedom and the -- and what American women could be waking up to after November 8.\par \par \uc2 Has the confusion around what`s prohibited, what`s allowed, what`s on the ballot, do you think that has anyway confused in turn voters and undermine the sense of urgency that might otherwise be felt among independents or moderate Republicans on this issue?\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Look, I think that it could, and we don`t know yet. The fact is that this is motivating women across the country, no question, but a lot of people it`s motivating our progressive white women, and the people who will be most impacted our lower income communities and communities of color.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think when the dust clears, whatever happens in the election, this is going to be one of those issues that people are going to wake up Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, whatever daily no more, and they may be confused about what the outcome is they may not thought they should have turned out and voted. And they may not know what access they have, because it does go back to the states.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Claire?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: This may be the first chapter. I mean, this is going to be an evolving situation as more and more women confront the reality of their states. In my state, the government is going to force birth on a rape victim, end of story. And now, they`re talking about ways to get at the abortion pill, which has become the method of choice now and a lot of that is going on online. So they`re going to try to do that. And that would have to be done nationally.\par \par \uc2 So, hopefully, we have a good day tomorrow and we slow this thing down. But if we don`t, the Republicans will continue to go too far and this will continue to be a growing issue for our party on the positive side.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: As more women face this reality, right, as the laws take effect. Michael --\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: When it`s your daughter.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Exactly, or your sister.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Or your neighbor, or your aunt. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Do you think Republicans are, pardon the metaphor, but prepared to live in the house that they are effectively building at this stage?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: No, they`re not. I don`t think they are. I don`t think they understand the full extent of what we`ve just heard here, the impact that it can have on and will have, and it`s already having in many respects on women across the country. A lot of the Republican women I know, many of whom are pro-life, in every sense of that word, are appalled by what they`re seeing happening in the states in which they live in and watching what types of legislation are being proposed, and the impact is having on women who don`t share their views, but it`s happening to women.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t think they`re fully prepared for what comes next. It`s always that thing in politics. It`s you can beat your chest about a lot of stuff until you actually have to act on it and follow up. And they don`t know what to do in this space, because as we`ve already seen, we`ve gone from, oh, the locals -- the states can decide to oh, national ban.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEELE: \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 . So, the men don`t know quite yet what to do for the women.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:05]\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: The men don`t -- the men don`t know could begin a lot of sentences. We`re going to leave it there for the moment. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , Claire McCaskill, Michael Steele, thank you all, for your wisdom and insights. Rachel, back over to you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 The great State of Nevada is one of those states where the experts and much of the polling are all basically saying that tomorrow is a tie. It looks like a tie in all the important races. There`s no way to tell what we`re going to happen. Well, we`re going to go live to Nevada for closer look next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So, the last midterm election was 2018. That Midterms set a record for voter turnout, which is great. But as of today, the early vote this year has far surpassed what we had in 2018. The early vote then was 39 million votes. As of today, we`re closer to 43 million votes. So, it`s a record number of people who have already voted this year.\par \par \uc2 Given those huge early voting numbers, one candidate in one of the most closely watched Senate races in the country, sounded the alarm today that the first returns, the initial vote count that will first get reported tomorrow will likely have a partisan skew that won`t reflect the final tally. Democratic U.S. Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the lieutenant governor in Pennsylvania, today, he sent out basically a warning flare about what to expect tomorrow, why you shouldn`t jump to conclusions when you see the first returns.\par \par \uc2 He said in part, "Republicans are already laying the groundwork to potentially spread false conspiracy theories about ballot processing and Pennsylvania. The reality is Pennsylvania law means in person votes that skew Republican tend to disproportionately be counted and reported before the Democratic-leaning mail-in votes. Ballots cast by mail and early in- person votes can`t be counted in Pennsylvania until election day thanks to the Republican-controlled legislature, which he calls an intentional move to help Republicans basis baselessly sow doubt about the election results when it suits them."\par \par \uc2 He says, "we should expect one of the most dramatic shifts in the country from initial Republican support in early results to stronger Democratic gains as more votes are processed." So, Pennsylvania, we are likely to get results quite late. Same goes for Arizona, same goes for maybe New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 One other state where things look like they`ll be incredibly closed and where we almost certainly will not know results tomorrow night is Nevada where incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is in a tough battle for reelection to his second term as his Democratic Governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from the Clark County Elections Department in Las Vegas, Nevada, by far the largest county in the state, is our beloved Jacob Soboroff, MSNBC Correspondent. Jacob, it`s great to see you. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Rachel. It`s great to see you. And you`re 100 percent right. It is not going to be an early night here in Nevada tomorrow night. I`ve been -- I`ve been inside this building, as a matter of fact, over the course the last couple of hours with Joe Gloria, the man in-charge of elections here in the largest election jurisdiction in the state. He was kind enough to invite us inside to show us a little bit of the machinery of democracy.\par \par \uc2 They have been doing mail-in voting here and in-person early voting. And unlike you mentioned in Pennsylvania, those votes are actually counted in advance, processed in advance of tomorrow. They won`t be tabulated until tomorrow, but they`ll be some of the first votes that come out of this building tomorrow night. But he`s the one that told me, I have -- I`m going to throw it. I`m not just making this up. That is not going to be an early night here in Nevada tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Inside this building, the vote could go on -- the counting, I should say, could go on for days and days. He explained to me why. Take a look at what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: So, if it`s close, there`s a chance we might not know the results in Clark County until around 10 days from election day.\par \par \uc2 JOE GLORIA, REGISTRAR OF VOTERS, CLARK COUNTY, NEVADA: That`s correct because we have statutory deadlines that we must abide by. We can receive mail ballots up until Saturday, the curious on Monday. I can`t even read a provisional ballot until the secretary sends me a report. That could be Tuesday afternoon or Wednesday morning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: So, you heard that right. You heard that right, 10 days from election day. But Joe Gloria insists that`s not because they`re slow counters or bad at their job. That`s what the law says here. And he in fact says that`s not a reason for people to doubt the integrity of the vote -- of the vote. On the contrary, people should have great faith in the integrity of the vote here.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , Senator Cortes Masto`s opponent here in Nevada, stood outside this very building the day after Election Day, alleged thousands of illegal votes were cast and counted inside that building. It turned out to be a totally bogus of course accusation, including the Republican secretary of state saying this after an independent investigation. All of that count will go on inside this building. Joe Gloria insists the process they use here, while it might not be fast, is the best process for getting a fair and accurate counts so everyone`s votes are not only are cast but counted inside here, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And Jacob, given what we saw there in 2020, given that kind of kind of scene that you just described of what happened in the parking lot outside that building and the kind of wild claims that were made by Arizona -- excuse me by Nevada Conservatives and Republicans, have they had to make sort of contingency plans for that kind of pressure, that kind of criticism, potentially, that kind of physical pressure on their counting site?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes. It was announced today actually that the Department of Justice is sending election monitors to 64 different election jurisdictions all across the country to monitor potential federal civil rights violations when it comes to the vote. And I asked Joe Gloria about that as well. And he said, I just want everybody to be clear. It`s not because we have had violations, but it`s in order to watch and prevent those types of violations from happening.\par \par \uc2 There are other jurisdictions that are not welcoming to the federal -- the department of justice folks coming in. He says, I welcomed them with open arms. There`s a lot of security here. You could probably see the police lights flashing on my face. There were not those kinds of police presence here two years ago when we`re here for the presidential election. They have boned up security here for that very reason, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:39]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Jacob Soboroff, MSNBC Correspondent, thank you so much for your time tonight. It`s going to be fascinating to watch Nevada tomorrow with all those close races.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have much more to talk about this election eve including what Jacob just mentioned there. DOJ announcing today that they will be sending election monitors to 24 different states. We are seeing at least one Republican-controlled state saying no, they reject the Justice Department monitors. We`ve also going to be taking a look at the tight Senate race in North Carolina. That`s all coming up after this. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221108eib700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for November 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Mike Memoli \par \uc2 8,982 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:36]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST, POLITICSNATION: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: America decides.\par \par \uc2 Right now, with three days left until Midterm Elections, a deeply divided America is preparing to cast final ballots, and the distance between parties and voters is vast.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was in Illinois earlier today warning older voters about the future of entitlements if the GOP regains the majority in Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Social Security and Medicare are more than government programs. They`re a promise. They`re a promise. Work hard and contribute, and when the time comes to retire, things can be a little easier for you.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been paying for this your whole life. Now, these guys want to take it away. Who in the hell did they think they are? I really mean it. Think about it.\par \par \uc2 [APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: NBC News is reporting that nearly 39 million Americans have already voted early and despite concerns and reports of voter intimidation at polling sites, early voting is at a pace to break Midterm records in several States.\par \par \uc2 But a bigger concern for Democrats on the defensive is whether it includes in the turnout, who it includes, who it does not include. Are the specific voters the party needs going to be enough to prevail? Because the future of the Biden administration is hanging in the balance along, of course, with Democrats congressional majorities.\par \par \uc2 But it`s in the Senate particularly that Democratic leaders are trying to stave off losses. And President Biden is back in Philadelphia tonight along former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  both slated to take the rally stage shortly in support of Democratic senator hopeful, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in his tight race against Dr. Mehmet Oz who will be joined by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for a campaign rally in a few hours.\par \par \uc2 We have a busy full hour of news and analysis ahead and we start in Pennsylvania, where NBC News reporter Mike Memoli is standing ahead of the Biden and \uc2 Obama\uc2  party taking the stage together.\par \par \uc2 Mike, set the scene for us, please?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS REPORTER: Well Rev, you really just nailed it because we know that there are still a lot of undecided voters not just here in the critical battleground State of Pennsylvania, but across the country.\par \par \uc2 But the number one concern that I`m hearing, as I talk to White House officials, Democratic strategist is whether the Democratic enthusiasm levels are matching what we`re seeing on the Republican side, and whether the Democratic turnout operation will deliver what it needs to on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we`re seeing here in Philadelphia tonight, something we haven`t seen throughout this entire campaign. Really, we haven`t seen it in two years. It`s the former President of the United States, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  joined by his former running mate, the current President of the United States, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the two leading Democratic candidates on the ballot here, Pennsylvania, Josh Shapiro and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , and their message I am told is going to be twofold.\par \par \uc2 One, acknowledging that this country has been through an awful lot over the last five years, that the Democratic control of Congress and the White House, the Biden administration has tried to do everything they can to deal with those challenges facing the country, but they know they have a lot more to do. But that they know Democrat voters can`t be complacent.\par \par \uc2 So, as I looked out at this rally tonight, Rev, I am seeing a lot of exactly the kind of voters Democrats seem to turnout on election day.\par \par \uc2 We`re here at \uc2 Temple University\uc2 , a lot of young voters, a lot of African- American voters as well in this city that are going to be critical. Their turnout could be the margin of victory here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the seventh time we`re going to see President \uc2 Obama\uc2  on the campaign trail for Democrats. Biden obviously has had a busy schedule himself, but never together. We`ll see that shortly -- Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, well, thank you, NBC`s Mike Memoli and we will be back to you once the President is taking the stage.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , Democrat of Michigan. He is the chair of the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee and Chair of the Senate, Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, thank you, first of all, for joining us tonight. Democrats are doing what they can to hold on to the Congressional majorities, particularly the Senate where that razor-thin majority is there and several key Senate races are neck and neck right now making control a pure tossup.\par \par \uc2 What are the consequences for the country if the Democrats were to lose control of the Senate? And what`s the strategy with three days just left to prevent that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 GARY PETERS\uc2  (D-MI): Well, Reverend, thank you for having me on your program. And certainly, the consequences are significant. We`ve heard the President in the segment earlier here on the program, talking about how Social Security and Medicare could be reduced if the Republicans take the majority. They`re already saying that.\par \par \uc2 One of the significant accomplishments that we were able to pass in Congress, which would require Medicare to negotiate with drug companies to bring down the price of prescription drugs, we`ve got Republicans already saying they`re introducing legislation to unwind that.\par \par \uc2 Basically, they want to stand up for the big drug companies, whereas Democrats, we passed comprehensive legislation to help everyday Americans lower the cost to their families, and lower the cost of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 And then you`ll look at the judiciary as well and how important we need to make sure in the Senate that we`re confirming Judges who believe in the rule of law, who believe in upholding democracy, if we lose control of the Senate, that will be difficult.\par \par \uc2 And I will say over the last two years, we`ve been able to confirm 85-plus Judges, which is critically important.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Senator, let`s think about outcomes on Tuesday. A recent AXIOS/Ipsos Poll found that nearly 40 percent of Republican voters are likely to blame, put the blame of fraud on the GOP election losses, nearly 20 percent are highly likely to do so.\par \par \uc2 I think about that in the context of alleged voter intimidation and harassment we`ve seen from North Carolina, Arizona and other states, and given your work with the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee on voter protections and as chair of Senate Homeland Security. What are you worried about a headed into this election in three days?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, certainly we are worried about the continued Big Lie of the election denial, you know, the numbers you just put on the screen are very disturbing, and it shows what happens when you have elected officials starting with the former President lying about the previous election, and trying to undermine the elections. It has serious consequences for the future of this Constitutional Republic of ours and our democracy, and it also threatens people.\par \par \uc2 We know that the rhetoric, the harmful rhetoric that has inspired violence, unfortunately, political violence and threats, death threats against Members of Congress at an unprecedented right is all a result of this Big Lie.\par \par \uc2 And now we`re seeing election deniers running, a large number of the candidates running on the Republican ticket are election deniers. God help us if they get elected. What will they do to our election system and undermine democracy? It is a very frightening prospect.\par \par \uc2 At the DSCC, we have invested unprecedented amounts of money into election protection. We started this last year, not just recently. We understood that Republicans were going to try to disrupt this democratic process if they can. We`re doing everything we can to thwart that.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got folks on staff, we`ve got lawyers, we`ve got teams in all of our key battleground States to make sure that we can ensure that the election process goes smoothly with complete integrity, and that includes the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2 , which is also certainly putting out warnings now that there could be violence during this election season.\par \par \uc2 So, we have to be focused, and if anything else, it means we have to make sure we`re electing Democrats in these key battleground States. Folks have to get out to vote, and if they can volunteer over the next two days, it`ll make a difference.\par \par \uc2 Get out the vote efforts will make the difference in these key battleground States.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, we`ve talked about the consequences for the nation if Democrats lose control of the Senate, but what kind of picture are Senate Democrats looking at if they lose them and become the minority come January? The impact of the party`s legislative priorities, how does that look ahead if you were to be the minority party in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 [17:10:06]\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, those priorities would come to an end. So, the work that we have done to pass gun safety legislation, the work that we have done to increase investments in infrastructure and bring down the cost of families like lowering prescription drugs, our efforts to make sure that we diversify our energy sector and putting in renewable energy to bring down the cost of energy and make sure that we`re not dependent on people like Putin and other autocrats around the world, drive up energy prices, the ability for us to fight against oil companies now that are racking up massive profits as a result of the gouging that they`re doing at the gas pumps -- all of that changes.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got Republicans who have already said that they`re going to roll back the drug law that we passed to bring down the price of prescription drugs. They`re siding with big pharmaceutical companies. They`ve always sided with big oil companies. They are not going to be standing with the American people, and they will do everything in their power to try to stop the Biden agenda from moving forward.\par \par \uc2 The stakes could not be higher in this election. We know all of these races are razor thin. I`m confident we can win because we have put together a great ground operations in all of our key States, but that also means that people have to make sure they`re getting out to vote and if you`ve already voted, if you can put additional help, reach out to the local campaigns, volunteer these next two days.\par \par \uc2 When you`re in these razor thin elections that we`re in, every vote matters. It could come down to a very small number of votes. We need people out.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Valerie Jarrett, former senior adviser to the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration. Miss Jarrett, we always appreciate when you join us here on POLITICSNATION. I don`t have to tell you the value of your being here, and I don`t have to tell you the value of the Black vote for Democrats, especially in this Midterm cycle.\par \par \uc2 The headline from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " this weekend, asking the question: "Democrats count on huge Black turnout, but has the party delivered in return?" Adding, "Given that the political fundamentals of the midterm election favor Republicans, Black voters are more crucial than ever for the Democrats. One overriding question is whether they will turn out in numbers big enough to offset those GOP advantages. There are questions about whether the Democratic Party speaks to Black voters as effectively as needed."\par \par \uc2 Now Black voters are expected to figure significantly in Michigan, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and of course, Georgia. Are you concerned that Democrats have not delivered for Black voters and that it will haunt them on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 VALERIE JARRETT, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO THE OBAMA ADMINISTRATION: I think that the Democrats who are in power right now have most certainly delivered for the Black community, and I think we`re seeing record turnout and that is encouraging, but we cannot rest on our laurels.\par \par \uc2 I was happy to see Senator Peters emphasize how important these next few days are. Every single vote matters. So yes, I think the Democrats have definitely delivered and look at the contrast, when Republicans are threatening to take away our Medicare, our Medicaid, Social Security. You`ve worked your whole life, you should be able to count on that. A woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 We see all across the country, and Reverend Al, you and I have talked about this many times, laws that are being proposed by Republican legislators to try to make it more difficult for people to vote and who are those laws directed at? They are directed at Black people, they are directed at young people, making it more challenging.\par \par \uc2 And what I am encouraged by, one of my best friends right this minute is down knocking on doors in Atlanta, and she said people are standing in lines for hours determined to vote. So the efforts that they went through to try to make it harder to vote will be proven to be ineffective.\par \par \uc2 But we can`t take anything for granted, we have to turn out and we have to vote. And it isn`t just healthcare, the economy, all of the different rights -- our voting rights, our rights, our Civil Rights that are all at stake. But Reverend, the underlying foundation of our democracy is being challenged.\par \par \uc2 And so we have been the beacon of hope to the world, the democracy that believes in the rule of law, and what we are seeing around the country, our attempts to pull the fabric out from underneath that foundation and allow it to tumble and we can`t let that happen, because if it does, the people who are going to suffer most are the people who do not have the strong voices.\par \par \uc2 And so we have to be those strong voices and make sure that every human rights are protected, and that`s not going to happened if the Republicans win, we know that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I need to quickly get your opinion here on something. Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  publicly addressed the recent assault on her husband for the first time yesterday, on a viral call with Democratic activists one day after Paul Pelosi was released from the hospital after suffering a fractured skull in the attack.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Most top Republicans have condemned the attack, but without connecting it to a climate of increased political violence. And then, of course, you`ve had some Republicans make light of the incident, turning it into a punch line or echoing debunked conspiracy theories about what happened that night in the Pelosi home.\par \par \uc2 What`s your reaction to the GOP reaction?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, first of all, my heart goes out to the entire Pelosi family. Speaker Pelosi has served our country admirably over her tenure. Her family should not come under attack, ever. Nobody`s family should come under attack.\par \par \uc2 And I do think that there`s a direct line between this dark undercurrent that has been bubbling up in our country, and the violence that we`re seeing spreading around and that elected officials are being targeted -- and we shouldn`t stand for that, that`s not who we are.\par \par \uc2 And so Reverend, I am very concerned that not just Speaker Pelosi, but that any member who would be attacked or whose family would be attacked, that, again, is inconsistent with the pillars of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 We believe in the rule of law, and we should not have to be come under threat because we simply try to serve our country, and that should apply to members of both parties.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, let`s stand by a minute. I want to take you live to Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, with President Biden and former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  are campaigning for Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who was running for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and for Josh Shapiro who is running for Governor of the State.\par \par \uc2 The event is at \uc2 Temple University\uc2 . Let`s listen in for a moment.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Hello, Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Hello, hello, hello. Don`t jump.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This crowd is so loud I think they can hear us in Latrobe.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They are going to hear us. They are going to hear us on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 Folks, I`m Jill Biden`s husband, a Philly girl.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: She`s ready for Game Six, like all of you.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s good to be home. It`s good to be with family, and that includes someone we all love, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: A great President, a historic President. And I`m proud to say, a dear friend.\par \par \uc2 Well, folks, three days, three days until one of the most important elections, one of the most important elections in our lifetime. It`s going to shape -- the outcome is going to shape our country for decades to come, and the power to shape that outcome is in your hands.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Two years ago -- two years ago you used that power to make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not only a former President, but you made him a defeated President.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And this year, you have the power to make \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  your next United States senator.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Josh Shapiro your next Governor.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, folks, I want to be very clear and I mean this sincerely about what`s on the ballot this year. Your right to choose is on the ballot. Your right to vote is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Social Security and Medicare is in the ballot.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There is something else on the ballot -- character. Character is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When I think of character, I think of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 [17:20:06]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You know, as an endorsement of him, "The Philadelphia Inquirer" today said, "John knows what his values are." Well, from my view, I`ve been saying for a long time, I`m a Pennsylvania boy born and raised in Scranton, and --\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But for the longest time, Delaware, like many other small places did not have -- did not have a television station. So Philadelphia TV had a cover me the same as they covered all of the Pennsylvania senators. I was known as Pennsylvania`s third senator.\par \par \uc2 My point is, I know Pennsylvania well and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is Pennsylvania. He is Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Oz and Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, I lived in Pennsylvania longer than Oz has lived in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And I moved away when I was 10 years old. Look, like a lot of us, John`s gotten knocked down, but he gets back up. He`s gotten up quickly. John has character, integrity, and he`s going to be a hell of a good senator -- a United States Senator from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The other thing is, don`t worry, we`re going to talk about Iran - - courage. Courage is also on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 When I think of courage, I think of Josh Shapiro. This guy will take on anyone and back down to no one and we saw that in 2020.\par \par \uc2 He stood up for the Constitution. He stood up for the rule of law. He stood up for the people of this State and he is going to be one hell of a fantastic Governor. He really is.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But it`s not just John and Josh, we`ve got a great Democratic lineup on the ballot here in this State. You heard from a lot of them today. You heard from a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 We have one of the best delegation in Congress -- we -- I keep making myself a Pennsylvanian again.\par \par \uc2 But really Democrats, up and down the ballot, we need to elect them all. We need it badly.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania, this isn`t a referendum this year. It`s a choice. A choice between two vastly different visions of America, vastly different. Maybe it`s in our blood, but John and I believe it all, it`s all about fighting for working and middle class people.\par \par \uc2 The way I`ve said from the beginning of my career -- my objective when I ran for President was to build an economy from bottom up -- bottom up and the middle out. It`s a fundamental shift compared to the Oz and the mega MAGA Republican trickledown economics.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, really. This ain`t your father`s Republican Party. This is a different breed of cat. I really mean it.\par \par \uc2 Look, they`re all about the wealthier getting wealthy and the wealthier staying wealthy. The middle class gets stiffed, the poor get poor under their policy.\par \par \uc2 You saw what happened last time under my predecessor. The economy was in ruins. He was the first President since \uc2 Herbert Hoover\uc2  to lose jobs in the course of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment rate -- the unemployment rate was 6.4 percent. He lost manufacturing jobs and hundreds of small business here in this State and 100,000 nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Look folks, the country is in a pandemic with no plan how to get out. The day I was sworn in, only two million people have been vaccinated. Now more than (220) Americans are fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So we moved. And our approach is working.\par \par \uc2 Since I came to office, we`ve created 10 million new good paying jobs.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ten million, because of you all. Ten million. Now unemployment rate is 3.7 percent near record 50-year low.\par \par \uc2 John and I understand we need to make things in America the first time in a long time and we are, and that means instead of shipping jobs overseas, we`re shipping product overseas.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m serious. We`ve created so far 700,000 manufacturing jobs just in the last 20 months, 700,000.\par \par \uc2 But for all our progress -- and by the way where in the hell -- heck is it written that we can`t be the manufacturing capital of the world again? Because we will be. We will be.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 [17:25:07]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And folks look -- but a lot of families are still struggling, a lot of families are struggling. That`s why I signed into law the Inflation Reduction Act that is going to reduce the cost of everyday things for families in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Things you have to pay for every month and you have to have enough in your paycheck to do it.\par \par \uc2 We gave Medicare the power to negotiate to lower prescription drug costs.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The same power the Veterans Administration has to cut drug prices in half. We tried to get that done for years when I was in the Senate and Vice President, but Big Pharma spent a lot of money and folks -- and supported a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? Not this year. We beat Big Pharma.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And the American people won. We put a cap of $2,000.00 for your prescription drugs for seniors on Medicare, no matter what their drug costs were, and some of them pay ten, twelve, fourteen thousand dollars a year for cancer drugs.\par \par \uc2 But now, beginning January 1, they will not have to pay more than $2,000.00 a year. Period. Period.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We put a cap on insulin to deal with their diabetes. Instead of paying $400.00 a month like they`re paying now, they`re going to pay no more than $35.00 a month.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in the Senate, with that one more vote I need, we`re going to make sure we cap prices for every single person, every life -- because they blocked -- we had it in the measure bill, but only stayed in for Medicare recipients. But everyone, how many of you know somebody who has to take insulin for diabetes?\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look around, and it is incredibly costly, no matter what your age, it is badly needed. And folks, look, we made the biggest investment to deal with the climate crisis, ever.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`re lowering energy costs for families.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Josh and John know that we can cap oil and gas price as well as abandon mines, we can do that in Western Pennsylvania, we can create thousands of good paying jobs for the same people who dug those mines in the first place.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That`s why we`re investing $350 million here in this part of the country to do just that.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`re not just talking it, we`re doing it. We`re going to do this while reducing the Federal deficit.\par \par \uc2 You know, because of the help I have from the senators in the Democratic Congress, we literally cut the Federal debt in half by $1.4 trillion --\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: $1.4 trillion just this year, and last year by $350 billion.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And another $250 billion over the next decade.\par \par \uc2 Look, I don`t want to hear from the MAGA Republicans who blew up the debt, we are the ones bringing it down, they shot it up.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And by such a massive reduction in debt, we`re able to help working and middle class families in America.\par \par \uc2 Look, we`re finally making sure the biggest corporations begin to pay their fair share for God`s sake.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: One of the reasons they`re so upset with me is we just passed a minimum 15 percent tax. Look, folks, if you`re a school teacher, you pay more than that. If you`re a cop, you pay more than 15 percent. But the days are over where corporations are paying zero Federal tax.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: By the way, in 2020, fifty-five of the largest corporations in America made $40 billion and they paid not one single penny in Federal tax, not one.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because we`ve reduced the deficit so much, we`re in a position to help millions of working and middle class folks caught in the crossfire of the pandemic and what has happened with the Russians moving into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Because I acted, we provided $10,000.00 in student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And another -- another 10,000 bucks if you got a Pell Grant. No one under 20 -- you have to be making less than a hundred twenty five a year and 90 percent of that money goes to people earning less than $75,000.00 a year.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And look, it is an incredible burden to carry. So now they`re going to be able to buy a home, start a business, just get a little bit of breathing room.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And what do MAGA Republicans like Oz and Mastriano talking about? They`re trying to stop me. Their hypocrisy is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 [17:30:07]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The same MAGA Republicans who took hundreds of thousands of dollars, even millions of dollars in the pandemic relief loans, had their loans forgiven are trying now to block student loans.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , God bless her soul. She and her husband had $183,000.00 in PPP loans forgiven. Congressman \uc2 Vern Buchanan\uc2  of Florida, he got over $2 million forgiven.\par \par \uc2 Who the hell have they think they are complaining about helping out students? Who do they think?\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I really mean it. What the students are going -- the people getting debt relief are going to be spending the money on growing the economy. For God`s sake, Oz and MAGA Republicans don`t think we should give working and middle class Americans a break.\par \par \uc2 But here`s what they do think instead: They want to make permanent the $2 trillion tax cut that they passed when Trump was President.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That tax cut is not -- not a penny was paid for and it goes to the biggest corporations, and it will explode the deficit by trillions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 They think -- they think the biggest oil companies, that just the six largest oil companies in the middle of this crisis we are in, you know how much money they made in the third and fourth quarter? I mean, this second and third quarter? $100 billion. One hundred billion.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No. It`s outrageous. Look, they should have used that money with what they were supposed to do, to lower gas prices at the pump for the American people.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If they did, the price at the pump nationally would be 50 cents lower than it is today per gallon.\par \par \uc2 It gets worse. Do you know what their number one priority is if they win? They`ve made it -- look, they are saying it out loud, they want to get rid of everything we just did, the power we just gave Medicare to negotiate drug prices gone. The $2,000.00 cap on prescription drugs gone. The $35.00 cap on insulin gone. The tax credit for lower energy costs gone. Fifteen percent corporate tax is gone, and the $800.00 savings in healthcare premiums we just got for families in the Affordable Care Act gone.\par \par \uc2 In fact, they want to do away with the Affordable Care Act and that means manage the people with preexisting condition would lose their insurance completely.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No matter how hard they try to get rid of Obamacare, I`m never going to let it happen.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  will not let it happen either.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But, folks, these guys never cease to amaze me, man. They never cease to amaze me.\par \par \uc2 Now, it gets worse. They`re literally coming after Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, no, no. Look, I know -- I know, the only thing you`ve ever -- all you younger people in here and thank God, there are so many of you, I know the thing you know is from the time you`re 16 you have money taken out to pay for Social Security. But guess what? If you`re somebody out there busting their neck, you just lost your husband or your wife, you`re sixty- six or sixty-eight years old and they want to take away your Medicare and your health and your social security.\par \par \uc2 I think, I`m exaggerating. Front page in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " talk about it. I want to show you what -- there is a guy out there, you know, they`re telling us what they`re going to do. They`re saying it straight out loud, at least, I give them credit for that.\par \par \uc2 And look, here`s what they`re going to do -- they want to do.\par \par \uc2 There is a guy named Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Who is in charge of electing the Republicans in the Senate. He`s the guy pushing Oz.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Okay. Well, let me show you. I`ll read from -- they listed in a program -- listed all their proposals, it is too small to be able to read.\par \par \uc2 I just circled the one and brought it out here and I`m going to quote what they say about Social Security. It says all Federal legislation sunsets in five years. If the law is worth keeping, Congress can pass it again. It means that the law literally goes out of existence and let us just affirmatively vote it back into existence.\par \par \uc2 And then along came Ron Johnson of Wisconsin, a Senator.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: By the way, he thinks five years is too long. Not a joke. I mean, this almost is so surreal sounding. He thinks Social Security and Medicare should be on the chopping block every single year. If Congress doesn`t vote to keep it for the first time, it goes away, not a joke. It goes away.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:20]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s not just Social Security and Medicare, he also wants to put veterans benefits on the line.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, to put everything at risk.\par \par \uc2 Elect \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to the Senate, please.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He will protect and strengthen Social Security and Medicare, and will guarantee that veterans are always cared for, always, always, always.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I also got criticized when I first got elected years ago, I`d say we have a lot of obligation, there is only one truly sacred obligation, to prepare those we send a war and to care for them and their families when they come home. That`s a sacred obligation. A sacred obligation.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, folks, that`s why I pushed and signed a thing called the PACT Act. The PACT Act guarantees healthcare for service members and veterans exposed to toxic burn pits.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When they go home -- these burn pits are literally the size of a football field, and range and depth from eight to ten feet. There`s everything in them from jet fuel and human -- just everything is burned in there.\par \par \uc2 You know, remember all the fight about the cancer and the brain cancer so many firefighters are getting from the 9/11, the toxic exposure? Well, it is the same kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been in and out of Iraq and Afghanistan over 30 times. I had a son who spent a year in Iraq. His hooch was literally, literally less than 200 yards from one of these burn pits.\par \par \uc2 Well, this new proposal guarantees healthcare for the service members and veterans exposed to toxic burn pits.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because here is what it does. Like my son, so many went incredibly fit, and came home dizzy and having trouble and so many have brain cancer.\par \par \uc2 My son came home with stage 4 glioblastoma, and he passed away. But here is what we did, up to now, we don`t pay any attention to this. We haven`t done anything for it and we had to fight to get it done.\par \par \uc2 Now for thousands and thousands of people coming home, more people coming home from these wars with brain cancer than any other war in American history and other cancers.\par \par \uc2 But guess what? Now, if they come home and you`re still alive, you get full benefits with healthcare to try to fight it.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And the families and the children are getting monthly stipends for their families and tuition help for the children.\par \par \uc2 Look, folks, it is not just the about the economy. Let`s talk a little bit about crime and keeping communities safe. I signed the most significant gun safety law in nearly 30 years.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I proposed over a hundred thousand new police officers. But here`s the deal, public safety is why John got into public service in the first place. Oz won`t do a thing about guns.\par \par \uc2 [BOOING]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  veto -- with your votes, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  will be in the Senate and be able to help me add one more thing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`ve been watching President Biden campaigning in Pennsylvania together with former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  for Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who is running for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and for Josh Shapiro, who is running for Governor.\par \par \uc2 We`ll go back there in a moment once former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  takes the stage.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, early voting is wrapping up tomorrow in many counties of Florida. There is still time to go online and plan your vote.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, President Biden held a rally in Miami in support of Democratic hopefuls, including Senate candidate Val Demings.\par \par \uc2 Representative Demings joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, you`ve been listening to President Biden speak just now. What are your thoughts about what he had to say?\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): Well, Reverend Sharpton, it is great to be with you. And look, we are excited about what is happening on the ground here in Florida.\par \par \uc2 As you`ve indicated, the President was with us on Tuesday night. We had a great get out to vote rally, if you will. But I think the President has laid out very clearly that there`s really only one party and that`s the Democratic Party that is working to lower costs, addressing everything from the cost of prescription drugs, to the lack of affordable housing and the cost of college debt, holding the largest and richest of corporations accountable.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:10]\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: And I really do believe that sending our message to the voters so they can clearly understand that there is a clear choice in my race for the Senate, there is a clear choice in \uc2 US Senate\uc2  races all over the country, that there is only one party working for the everyday working class, including our seniors.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, Republicans have spent a lot of time painting their Democratic opponents as soft on crime, but as the President said this week, that won`t work with you.\par \par \uc2 You served 27 years in the Orlando Police Department, including four years as the first female Chief. Talk to me about how that experience has shaped your politics.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: You know, I don`t know how gullible \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  really think Florida voters are. You`re right, Reverend. I spent 27 years, had the honor of serving as Chief of Police by bringing the community and the Police Department together. We were able to reduce violent crime by over 40 percent, which is still the most dramatic reduction in the history of our city.\par \par \uc2 You know, when you have on the ground experience, I`ve seen people at their best and seeing people at their worst. I`ve heard their stories, and we have to hire the brightest and the best to do the job of policing. We have to give them the best training and best equipment.\par \par \uc2 But what I also know is that we`ve got to deal with some of the social ills that cause decay in communities in the first place like poverty, low wages, unemployment, substandard education, substandard housing, by addressing too, we can have some of the safest communities not just in Orlando, but across the nation.\par \par \uc2 There is no substitute for real life experience, and believe me, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  doesn`t have that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, there are currently no Black women serving in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and only two black women have served in the nation`s history.\par \par \uc2 If elected, you become Florida`s first Black senator and there is a chance that you may be the only Black woman representing in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  in total.\par \par \uc2 Talk to me about why these representations are so important?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Well, Reverend I`m certainly cheering my colleague on the campaign trail, Cheri Beasley from North Carolina on as well. But let me say this, the Senate not only needs to reflect the diversity that we see across our nation, but a diversity of perspectives at the table.\par \par \uc2 You better believe growing up poor, Black, and female in the South, I not only bring a different perspective, but the diversity that we should see within the Senate, just as we all celebrated Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , not only was she could be -- I`m going to say it, probably the most experienced Justice on the bench, but she also brings a diversity of perspectives with her public defendant experience.\par \par \uc2 And so believe me, when the Senate reflects America and looks like America, everything gets better.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as Democrats like to keep control of the Senate, abortion rights have become the bedding issue of this Midterm season.\par \par \uc2 Your opponent, Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is co-sponsoring a bill that would ban abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy nationwide, embracing Senator Lindsey Graham`s Federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been critical of his stance on abortion rights all throughout your campaign, and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  vowed to back in October that if voters elect more Democratic senators and keep the House, he will send the bill to Congress to codify abortion protections into law.\par \par \uc2 If elected to the Senate, what would you do to protect the abortion rights on a Federal level in contrast to your opponent?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Reverend Sharpton, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  has made clear for years that he wants a total national ban on abortion with zero exceptions, including rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Sitting in the Senate right now is the Women`s Health Protection Act that would codify Roe versus Wade. Now, you know, shame on us that we did not codify this law before now, but we can do it. If we can get two more seats in the Senate, we will have the numbers that we need to break the filibuster and move this much needed legislation forward.\par \par \uc2 People may say, well, look, abortion is not my issue. Anytime we send a message that it is okay to take away a constitutional right over here, we are sending a message that is okay to take away a constitutional right everywhere. We cannot allow that to happen.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, Justice Thomas clearly communicated that okay, we got Roe versus Wade, let`s look at some other constitutional protections. So, the Women`s Health Protection Act is there, we will vote for it.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:16]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Democrats are increasingly worried that Florida, once considered the nation`s largest swing State, may slip away to Republicans after this year`s Midterms.\par \par \uc2 In the most recent presidential elections, Florida went twice for former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and then twice for Trump and Florida hasn`t had a Democratic US Senator since 2018 when \uc2 Bill Nelson\uc2  was in office.\par \par \uc2 Now, this week in Florida, Republicans reportedly out registered Democrats, hundreds of thousands of people. With three days to go, what do you want Floridians to know as you make your final pitch ahead of the Election Day?\par \par \uc2 What is at stake in your race?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: You know what, Reverend? Our democracy is at stake. And I know that there are people, Republicans, Independents, and Democrats who care about protecting our democracy, it is the foundation on which we build a great nation.\par \par \uc2 If you want to lower the costs for people who have to go to work every day, protect Social Security and Medicare, make sure we have safe communities, make sure our children are not saddled with college debt, make sure we reduce gun violence and keep dangerous weapons out of the hands of dangerous people and protect voting rights so that all people can exercise their right to vote, there is only one person in Florida that is prepared and has the courage to do that and that`s Val Demings.\par \par \uc2 If you want to learn more, go to ValDemings.com.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now in the wake of Hurricane Ian that left devastating damage throughout Florida`s southwest coast, you`ve extensively criticized Republican Florida Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  for missing the Senate vote on legislation that would allow FEMA to access funding for disaster relief.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Rubio spokespeople defended that move by claiming the senator missed the vote because he was touring hurricane damage. Has Senator Rubio done enough for Floridians following the disaster that the hurricane -- Hurricane Ian left behind?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Well, Reverend Sharpton, doing enough starts with being there to vote. And look, Senator Rubio has to be on the Senate floor to cast his vote. But we all know that he has a record that he doesn`t show up for work, and when he shows up, he votes usually to hurt Florida.\par \par \uc2 Look, I chair the Subcommittee on Emergency Preparedness Response and Recovery. I choose to tour the damage the flooded areas in Central Florida and tour the unbelievable devastated areas in southwest Florida.\par \par \uc2 Senator Rubio should have been on the House floor to vote. Senator Scott got there and voted. Senator Rubio once again neglected his duties and I`m sure hoping that Florida is paying attention.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, we need to deal with climate change. Climate change is real. It`s something else Florida`s senior senator has played around with. If we want real representation, we have an election in three days, vote for Val Demings.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman Val Demings, candidate for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  in the State of Florida, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to my political panel now.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Susan del Percio, political analyst and Republican strategist and Juanita Tolliver, political analyst and Democratic strategist, both are MSNBC contributors.\par \par \uc2 Juanita, you`ve been following along with the rally in Philadelphia right now, Democrats have been going all out to win these very close races in an extremely important swing State.\par \par \uc2 Do you think \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Biden can seal the deal with Pennsylvania voters?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Look, Rev, I sure hope so. They`re bringing a lot of energy here, and let`s keep it real. Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is still one of the most popular Democratic figures in the nation and he is driving energy and enthusiasm and ultimately getting out the vote for Democrats in Pennsylvania. And we know this race is going to be tight, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Remember, in 2020, Biden only won Pennsylvania by 1.2 points. So, it is going to be a tight race and this type of rally, this type of engagement where the former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  has been not only right now in Philadelphia, but earlier today in Pittsburgh is going to have an impact on how voters show up, how many how many more people pick up volunteer shifts, and help get out the vote, because it all comes down to who shows up and vote.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now Susan, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is also in Pennsylvania tonight. He will be holding a rally in Latrobe outside of Pittsburgh alongside Republican Senate candidate, Dr. Oz and gubernatorial candidate, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [17:50:12]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But the big question will be, what if anything Trump will say about his own potential run for the White House?\par \par \uc2 Two sources familiar with his thinking tell NBC News, he will likely officially announce a third presidential run just weeks from now, but caution the date could slide.\par \par \uc2 Trump has been teasing another presidential run for a while now. Do you think this current speculation is helping or hurting Republican candidates in the Midterms?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think it`s actually hurting Trump-backed Republican candidates, and if you`re Dr. Oz, the last thing you want is to have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the State the weekend before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz has been trying not successfully, in my opinion, to be a little more moderate. Having \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come for -- and let`s be clear about it -- for a rally for himself. I don`t think you`ll hear the candidates` names more than two or three times maybe, but you will certainly hear a lot of I, I, I and whether or not he decides to announce that he is running, I still think it will not be the kind of run where he puts an announcement -- that he puts the paperwork in.\par \par \uc2 So he can talk, he wants to still raise money. He could say I`m definitely, definitely really thinking about it, and it`s looking good or something like that, but I don`t think we`re going to see his name on a ballot, but he`ll hurt a lot of people along the way.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Susan, let me stay with you a minute, let`s turn to the New York gubernatorial election.\par \par \uc2 The race between Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and Republican Representative Zeldin has tightened in the final weeks of the campaign. What do you think this race has become? Why do you think I should ask you - - why do you think this race has become so close in reliably Democratic State? Is this similar to the scare Governor \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  got last year in New Jersey?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: It`s a combination of the scare \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  got in New Jersey in the sense of the economic scare, which is what we saw, not just in New Jersey, but in New York on Long Island last year.\par \par \uc2 But now you add the issue of crime and we saw Eric Adams run a very successful mayoral campaign being a crime fighter, being a former cop, understanding the issues. But the problem is right now for \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , those headlines are still there.\par \par \uc2 We still are seeing people on the Subway platforms and other places, feeling an intense fear and you and I have talked about this, Rev. If you`re afraid to go to work, that is not a place where the city or State is coming back. It is the definitely in the wrong direction, if you will.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s just one kind of campaign factor in that is that \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  was running for election not reelection. She has run statewide as a lieutenant governor, but never got much attention. She doesn`t have an operation, and \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  has not let go of campaigning in New York City, which is shocking, and that`s where this race is close, in New York City.\par \par \uc2 He hits 35 to 36 percent in New York City, we are talking that it could be a Governor \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . I don`t think we`ll see it happen. But boy, this shows just how important it is to keep up your campaign network.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, switching gears now to a new report by "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ," which explores \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` one-year of teaching experience at a private school in Georgia after he graduated \uc2 Yale University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The piece explains how a former Black student recalls DeSantis` teaching the Civil War in a way that sounded to her like an attempt to justify slavery. Twenty years later, he is the Governor of Florida, which is the State that has the second largest amount of banned books, including math books, he said, contains critical race theory.\par \par \uc2 What makes this report so significant? What can people glean from his teaching past and how that translate into his policies?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Well, first, Rev, I cannot pretend to be shocked that someone who is trying to ban basic teaching of basic history of our country tried to teach the Civil War as though he was justifying slavery. That should not come as a surprise to anyone.\par \par \uc2 And honestly, it should present a roadmap for how far he is going to continue to go to keep the truth away from young people and students and out of schools in basic education, so all of the talk, all of the laws, everything that he`s done to date to try to again create barriers between basic educational elements of history is only going to continue and it is only going to get worse and he`s going to continue to run on it because he knows he has an audience within the Republican Party who believes what he believes.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:19]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right Susan del Percio and Juanita Tolliver, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: . that have been threatened by one of the Justices may be on the block. The only defense that most Americans will have to preserve their rights will be to have a Senate and a Congress that will codify law and protect the rights earned by Civil Rights activists, women`s rights activist, LGBTQ rights activists and others.\par \par \uc2 With hate now being normalized from cultural figures to athletes, we can`t act like this is just some Midterm exercise. It will decide the future of this country and the present conditions that we will live under.\par \par \uc2 If you don`t do anything else, whether you like Republicans or Democrats, don`t vote for who you like, vote for yourself and who is in your best interest.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:06]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221106eib500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Kornacki Countdown for November 4, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Steve Kornacki, Dasha Burns, Rich Lowry, Jesse Kirsch, Shaquille  Brewster, Sahil Kapur, Antonia Hylton \par \uc2 8,644 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: People are out there doing the right thing, which is voting, and that is great for democracy, undeniably. That is the only way this process works and stays working. So, keep doing it. If you haven't already, go cast that ballot. We'll see you on the other side. Now it's time for the KORNACKI COUNTDOWN WITH STEVE KORNACKI.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC HOST: Three days, 19 hours, 59 minutes 55 seconds and counting until the first polls close on election night. I'm Steve Kornacki coming to you from, well, not from 3A in Rockefeller Center, our usual location for our Friday night election special. Instead, I'm at the historic, world famous Keeneland Racecourse in Lexington, Kentucky, heart of the sixth congressional district, Lexington, Kentucky is.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow is going to be the biggest day in horse racing. The Breeders Cup Championship races featuring some of the best horses from all around the world. But tonight, we are here to talk about the election, the midterms. Now, just days away. The final pace is being put in by candidates all across the country here, building towards Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Already, tens of millions of votes have been cast in this election when all is said and done, we're anticipating the turnout level for this midterm election is going to shatter all existing records. We could be north of 120 million people who cast votes. So, the interest level is extremely high. The stakes are high.\par \par \uc2 Let's take you through what that looks like again. If you need a reminder here, right now, the battle for the House, the Democrats with a narrow majority here. Republican needing a gain of just five seats and they would get control of the House. And in the Senate 50/50 right now. Democrats with power there. Only thanks to the tie breaking vote of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Republicans needing a net gain of one seat, they would take over the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Here is the generic ballot average here we've been following, asking folks, Democrats or Republicans, who would you vote for, for Congress? You see, the RealClear Politics average, we've been following them this fall. They have a Republican advantage of about three points. It's been at that level in the RealClear Politics average for a couple of weeks now.\par \par \uc2 The 538 average also favoring the Republicans, though not by as much. Just over a point in that average. So, generally when you look at these poll averages, you're seeing some kind of Republican edge. Unclear exactly how much that edge is. And again, there is the x-factor of exactly what turnout is going to look like when you're talking about the kind of huge numbers that we are looking at.\par \par \uc2 This, the Senate battleground map in blue, the five most vulnerable Democratic held seats in red, the five most vulnerable Republican held seats. One of the things that we've been talking about on this Friday night shows in general is the importance, the central importance of Pennsylvania when it comes to the battle for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because Pennsylvania, as you see, is a Republican held seat right now. Pat Toomy, the incumbent not running for re-election. But it's a state that also \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carried in 2020. Remember, it's the state that made him president. The importance of Pennsylvania for Democrats, it means if they can carry Pennsylvania, huge if there, but if Democrats can flip Pennsylvania, and if all these red states here, it's probably their best flip opportunity.\par \par \uc2 If they can, then they can absorb a loss in one of these blue states. In one of their seats right now, if Democrats were to lose, they would be okay. Their majority would remain intact if they could flip Pennsylvania or if they could flip any other red seat on this map. But again, Pennsylvania has consistently been the most promising for them.\par \par \uc2 The poll numbers, we've seen the number come out tin the state this week. One tied, one putting Mehmet Oz, the Republican, ahead. Three showing \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Democrat, continuing to lead. The significance, most of these polls, just about all of these polls conducted entirely after that debate between Mehmet Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . There was a lot of discussion about whether there'd be a huge fallout from that.\par \par \uc2 The poll numbers not looking hugely different than they did before that debate. And Pennsylvania, going to take center stage in a whole new way tomorrow because you're going to have not one, not two, but three people who either are or were president of the president of the United States in Pennsylvania tomorrow campaigning for the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 You're going to have first, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , the former president. He is going to be there at noon in Pittsburgh in Allegheny County with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . This is obviously a blue county. The name of the game there for Democrats, drive up turnout as much as they can. Later in the day, interestingly, and Westmoreland County, Latrobe, Pennsylvania, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be campaigning with Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 Western Pennsylvania, in general, when you get out of Allegheny County, Republican territory, obviously trying to drive up Republican support there.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But the risk if you're Mehmet Oz of appearing with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , of making him such a big part of your closing message in Pennsylvania, is that Trump lost Pennsylvania. And one of the reasons that Trump lost Pennsylvania is because of the ground he lost in suburbs particularly the suburbs right outside of Philadelphia. Four suburban counties there, Bucks, Chester, Montgomery, Delaware, if you look at the margin that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost Pennsylvania by in 2020, it can be accounted for almost entirely by the ground that he lost in those four suburban counties.\par \par \uc2 So, the risk for Mehmet Oz, you bring \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in, you try to fire up the Republican base. Are you simultaneously turning off voters? I know it's across the state, but they are certainly going to hear about it. Are you simultaneously turning off voters in those suburbs who you desperately need? Oh, by the, way, at the end of the night, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , they are all going to be in Philadelphia holding their final rally.\par \par \uc2 So, all sorts of political power going to be in the Keystone State tomorrow. Who's already in the Keystone State? Our own Dasha Burns. She has been covering this campaign. She joins us now from Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania. Dasha, tell us right now, three plus days from election day, how each campaign is feeling about where this race sits?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Steve, you set it up brilliantly there. You hit on so many of the factors that we're hearing constantly on the ground here. Look, I'm in Luzerne County. This is an area that used to be, you well know, a Democratic stronghold until it went for Trump by double digits in 2016 and 2020. This is exactly the kind of area, the kind of blue-collar county that Democrats have lost because of that.\par \par \uc2 They've got to win big in those collar counties around Philadelphia and Montgomery County, and those suburbs. But that's also where Oz has tried to chip away at Fetterman's lead there. He's been targeting suburban women in particular with a lot of messaging, Steve, around crime, around inflation, around public safety. And as we've been talking to voters, you know, that message has been resonating.\par \par \uc2 But you're absolutely right that there is a risk in the rally he will be attending this weekend. Not only because he'll be there on stage with Trump who lost Pennsylvania, but also because the other name at that rally is Republican candidate for governor, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who many Republicans we've talked to here, feel is too far-right, is too extreme.\par \par \uc2 And in that race while the Senate race is neck and neck, in the governor's race, Democrat \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  has a huge lead, which, Steve you know very well, we've been talking to experts about this, but tells you that are some voters out there who are splitting their tickets because, especially Republicans just feel like they can't cast their ballots for Mastriano.\par \par \uc2 While we are at a rally for Shapiro just a little while ago here and I had a chance to speak to him one-on-one. And he's really trying to make the case not only to Democrats but to Republicans as well that there is a space here to vote for a more moderate candidate like himself. Take a listen to what he told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC GUBERNATURIAL CANDIDATE FOR PENNSYLVANIA: \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  has shown us who he is. He was part of the violent mob that stormed up to the capitol on January the 6th. He was part of the violent mob that disobeyed police orders. Police died that day and then the days there after. And instead of understanding the consequences of his actions, he's at it again. He continues with the lies and he's already told us that in 2024 he will decertify certain voting machines here in Pennsylvania so he can pick the winner. He is a clear and present danger to our democracy he has to be defeated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And Steve, Shapiro and the Democrats, they spent a lot of money putting that message on the air. Oz has spent a lot of money putting the message about crime on the air, and we are running into so many voters here who are splitting their tickets who are going Shapiro, Oz. Now, we don't know what's going to happen here, but it's very possible that ticket splitters are going to be a big part of the story here in Pennsylvania and potentially other states as well, Steve.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Alright, Dasha Burns in Pennsylvania. I am sure we'll be seeing a lot of you in the next couple of days. Thank you for that report. Appreciate it. And joining us now, David Plouffe, the former campaign manager, White House senior adviser to President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 . He's an MSNBC political analyst. And Rich Lowry, the editor-in-chief of "National Review," also and NBC News political analyst. Thanks to both of you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Rich, let me start with you because I think this is a question that has kind of hovered over a lot of these Senate races this year where atmospherically big picture, there are a lot of things working in Republicans favor this midterm election year. And yet, you look at some of the candidates who are being put up in some of these swing states, you look at Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania. He has very high negative numbers when you poll it.\par \par \uc2 Now you add in the fact that a couple days before the election he's going to be appearing with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who lost the state in 2020 with \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  who, by all accounts, is doing significantly worse than Oz in the poll.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:06]\par \par \uc2 Is Pennsylvania a state where Republicans may be squandering what is otherwise a good political environment for them, and potentially losing a seat in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think Oz, given his constraints as a candidate, he is not a natural fit in Pennsylvania -- for Pennsylvania in all sorts of ways, has run quite an effective campaign, but there's this balance here. There's not a natural connection that even Republicans developed for Oz, not a lot of enthusiasm there.\par \par \uc2 I was talking to Republican operative a couple of weeks ago who was going door to door with activists in Pennsylvania and said all the activists are Mastriano people, right. They're into him and they'll give out the Oz material and speak the Oz lines at the door but they're not there for him.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this is the balance he's looking at here at the final weekend. He wants Trump and Mastriano to get some of that energy and enthusiasm to drive up the turnout in the western part of the state and then hope, you know, Trump's not on the ballot so, and Oz has done a lot to try to soften his image and, you know, he's got a very non-confrontational message in Philadelphia in the suburbs and hope that's enough to thread the needle.\par \par \uc2 But this -- this is perhaps a close Senate race in the country and it wouldn't surprise me if we don't know on election night and we're still counting a couple of days later.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yeah. It took us to 11:30 a.m. the Saturday after the election to call Pennsylvania in 2020, point of reference for some folks there. David, I want to ask you, you heard Dasha in that report talk about how much the Oz campaign has been focusing on the issue of crime. We see that in Pennsylvania. We see that elsewhere, an attempt by Republican candidates they feel the wind back in part, a lot of those suburban voters who they lost ground within 2020.\par \par \uc2 There is a piece in the "American Prospect" from Stan Greenberg, longtime Democratic pollster, he just put this up. He says, "How Democrats mishandled crime" is the headline, in his view. He says that he had conducted research quoting from the piece here, "in the African American, Hispanic, and Asian American communities. All of those communities pointed to the rising worry about crime and they were worried more about the rising crime than the rise in police abuse, yet Democrats throughout 2021 focused almost exclusively on the latter."\par \par \uc2 He's making the case in this piece that the Democrats created in the way they've addressed crime in the last couple of years, they've created a political opportunity that Republicans like Mehmet Oz can take advantage of and are taking advantage of. Is he right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, first of all, I tend to like those pieces that come out after the election. I find them to be most helpful then if you're going to navigate (ph) a little bit. Well, first of all, Steve, listen, you know, I ran races for a very long time as you know. And in every race, this isn't new. This is core to the Republican argument. No matter what's going on in terms of crime rates, up, down it doesn't matter. They try to turn you into soft on crime.\par \par \uc2 I think that there are some things that probably you're starting to see more Democrats talk about crime rates in the red states, murder rates in red states. You know, probably would've been useful to be talking about that three or four months ago, but you also have to just guarantee people you're going to look out for them from a public safety standpoint.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is trying to talk about that. So, you know, there's no doubt I think that the crime issue has wounded Democrats, maybe more than it needed to. And listen, my view and politics is, where you've got some weakness, whether it exists, you know, sort of neutrally or your opponent is going to go there, you know, you've got a fight that campaign within the campaign.\par \par \uc2 That's the thing about campaigns I've run, you kind of have to do it all. You have to push where you have offensive advantages and then, you know, make sure you're building them out where you've got concerns. I agree with Rich, I mean, you know, Trump was what 44,000 votes in '16, his Pennsylvania margin. Biden under 100,000. Hard to believe this thing won't be that close.\par \par \uc2 So, everyone watching this should understand, you know, it is going to be Friday, Saturday before we know because this looks like it's going to be really, really tight, and there's two things. Dasha talked about the ticket splitters, they clearly exist in Pennsylvania. So, the Shapiro margin is going to be important. If that gets high enough, there's not going to be enough ticket splitters probably to Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, Rich mentioned the activist loving Mastriano. I'm going to be looking very carefully. I don't think it's going to be a lot of voters, but there may be some Republicans who vote for Mastriano and don't vote for Oz. And you know, if it's 10,000 or 20,000 people, that can make a difference. So, in a race like this, you know, those nuances are going to be really important.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Let me ask you each just a quick question here kind of big picture thinking about the map here when it comes to the Senate, and Rich I'll start with you. A state -- if you could give me a state where Republicans are feeling generally good right now where you think maybe they ought to be a little bit more worried, and a state where Republicans you think might be poised to do well, that we're not talking about right now.\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Well, I'm not -- they're being talked about, but Arizona and New Hampshire have really come back on to the board. I mean, we've talked about Arizona sort of all along as a swing state, but Masters has tightened that up, I think that's a toss-up.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:01]\par \par \uc2 I think New Hampshire is a toss-up, and if I'm not mistaken, they're not early vote in New Hampshire. So, this kind of Bolduc surge could put them over the top on election day. I'm not sure what, you know, maybe Pennsylvania is the one most to worry about if you're a Republican because there was a surge of optimism around the debate and Fetterman didn't do well, but it's, you know, he's still leading in some polls. But I think the map is such now that Republicans can drop Pennsylvania and still have really plausible paths to a majority.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: David, same question to your different twist, where are Democrats feeling good now that you're a little worried? And is there a state there that not being talked about where you think Democrats are poised for a good night?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Well, I think both of the Democratic candidates, Beasley a Ryan, have run really strong races in North Carolina and Ohio. The question is, can they get to 50? I think both going to be close. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in particular I think is going to get every vote a Democrat can get. He's not (inaudible) Brown in the year 2022. So, you know, there was, you know, maybe somehow you pull one of those off.\par \par \uc2 And then I think I agree. New Hampshire is tightening and that's concerning. But of course, you showed, if Fetterman wins, you know, that's enormously strategically important because it just ups the degree of difficulty for Republicans. But you know, Nevada, Arizona, Georgia, all of those could go either side, and so if you're a Democrat those are the ones you have to be, those are existential.\par \par \uc2 And obviously if you win Pennsylvania, it gives you a tiny bit of margin for (inaudible) baseball. You head in and you're above the ninth, maybe down a few of the Republicans, two instead of one. That's an enormously big difference even though it may not seem like it on the face. But that would be my sense of it.\par \par \uc2 I think Ohio and North Carolina are both going to be close. It's just hard for a Democrat to get to 50 in those states. We'll see. You know, do they get the 47 or 48 or can they pull something up? And so much of this as you pointed out comes down to turnout. With all the polling and all the early vote, we still don't really have the beginning idea about what that turnout is going to look like as we begin to really count votes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Alright, David Plouffe and Rich Lowry, thank you for joining us tonight. And many of these Friday nights that last month or so I've enjoyed and appreciate it very much. Thank you. And we are just getting started breaking down the battle for the Senate. You just heard the latest from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Next, we're going to dive into two other crucial states, one where Democrats came into this year thinking they had one of their best shots at a pick up, but now new numbers suggest trouble for them. And another state where their candidate has seemingly defied political gravity all year. David Plouffe was just talking about it. Does \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  have a real chance at carrying Ohio? We're on the ground live and next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Clock continues to tick down toward election day toward the first poll closings on election night. So, we're talking about the battle for control of the Senate again. Republicans needing that gain of one seat if they're going to flip it if they're going to get control, Democrats trying to keep them from doing that. We were talking in the last block about how important Pennsylvania is to Democrats, because it's a red seat right now.\par \par \uc2 Because Biden won the state, such a golden opportunity for Democrats to get a flip and then if you get a flip if you're a Democrat you can afford a loss. Okay, what happens let's say if \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  the Democrat does not come through for Democrats in Pennsylvania, if instead Mehmet Oz wins that race? Then, Democrats are left scrambling. Well, what other red state could they get?\par \par \uc2 And that's where -- this came up a little bit at the end of the last block there. That's where for instance, Ohio, comes in. And the polling in Ohio has been pretty close. You got J.D. Vance, the Republican, you got \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the Democratic congressman, on average right now. Vance a couple of points ahead. We've seen some very close, consistently close polls in the state.\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks think that Ryan has been running a very strong campaign here. Here's the x-factor in Ohio, it's how badly the polls have missed in Ohio the last couple of elections. You know, we talk about polling misses all the time. They're not evenly spread out. In some states the polling is actually been pretty good the last couple of elections. Not in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 In 2016 and in 2020, you could see it right here, this was the final poll average, Trump was barely ahead in Ohio, Democrats came in to both election night thinking they had a shot in Ohio. Trump ended up winning the state in a runaway. So, that is one of the questions here, is the polling missing in Ohio again or is this for real? Does Ryan have a shot at this?\par \par \uc2 Our own Jesse Kirsch is on the ground. He is in Glendale, Ohio near Cincinnati. Jesse, you've been following this race closely. Tell me, what is your sense of it? How do Republicans, how do Democrats feel about this race? Did they feel this is legitimately a one, two-point race right now.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, and I can tell you, Steve, that we've heard from J.D. Vance himself pointing to polling inaccuracies. Obviously, we were just looking at what we've seen history has shown us in the past in the state, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  on the other side of this race. We'll tell you that he is trusting what he sees on the ground compared to the polling.\par \par \uc2 But money doesn't lie, right, Steve? And we have seen a huge infusion of cash from national Republicans, a group along with Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  coming to help boost J.D. Vance in recent months. So that tells you that at minimum they are focusing on this race trying to defend the seat for Republicans. And what has really stood out to us about covering this back and forth between Ryan and Vance in recent months is that unlike other races where you're seeing Democratic candidates stressing issues like election integrity and abortion.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , congressman right now, he's really zeroed in on the economy, specifically buzzwords of jobs, workers, China. Those are probably three of the words we hear the most from him. He calls his campaign bus "the workers first express." And on the other side of this race, we see J.D. Vance talking about inflation, tying that to Democratic policy and tying Ryan to those policies as \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  tries to keep the Democratic Party at arm's length.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:01]\par \par \uc2 We caught up with both candidates today, asked them specifically what their message would be for undecided workers working in manufacturing. Here's part of what they told us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, OHIO: For a factory worker, the thing that I'd say is, we're going to lose every factory job in this country unless we have energy independence. Energy independence is the core thing that you need to bring manufacturing back to this country.\par \par \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATUC SENATE CANDIDATE, OHIO: I would want them to know that J.D. Vance has two donors worth $55 million, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Peter Till, bought and paid for. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  425,000 donors, 95 percent of contributions are under 100 bucks. Who's for who?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: And for what it's worth, Steve, the Vance campaign tells me that they have hundreds of thousands of donors as well. But you can see the posturing there for them in the final days. And again, this is right in the heartland, obviously, where manufacturing is key, and we're seeing more jobs coming back here. So, it'll be interesting to see who people give credit for the good that's done economically here when you see thousands of jobs coming here in the days and weeks and years ahead. But obviously also there are the inflationary woes as well and who will take responsibility for that, we'll find out on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Alright, Jesse Kirsch in Ohio, thank you for that. And a 7:30 poll closing time in Ohio. So, one of those states we could be getting a pretty early readout maybe and how the night is going like portending at least elsewhere. So, from Ohio, we're talking again here about the Senate map. How about Wisconsin? Another one of those vulnerable red seats. Ron Johnson, the Republican, running for a third term.\par \par \uc2 And this is a state, you know, Democrats came into the year thinking of all these red states, Wisconsin might be their best chance at a flip. Ron Johnson has always been a polarizing figure in Wisconsin, barely won re- election in 2016. It was a big surprise that he did. There's been a change in the polling in Wisconsin. You're seeing the newest poll out of Wisconsin that has Johnson ahead by two points. But this poll, I want to show you inside of it. It reflects a shift because over the summer in September, this is the Spectrum News/Sienna College poll, look how unpopular Johnson was in Wisconsin over the summer, 37 favorable, 50 unfavorable as Democratic opponent, Mandela Barnes, was actually above water.\par \par \uc2 Well, now, with Johnson leading in the polls, he's ticked up by six points, his favorable rating. And Mandela Barnes is unfavorable has gone up nearly 10 points. So, you know, we've talked about the Johnson campaign spending heavily, really emphasizing the issue of crime. And it really seems to have perhaps changed perceptions of these candidates, and no longer is Wisconsin the Democrat's top target in terms of flipping a seat. They're still in the game, but it doesn't look necessarily as rosy for them as it did a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 Shaq Brewster is on the ground for us in Milwaukee. And Shaq, I wonder if you could talk about those dynamics. This is still very much a close game, but it appears that Ron Johnson has moved this campaign, the discussion to some issues where he has some strengths over Barnes, the Democrats might not have been expecting?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right. No, that's exactly the case, Steve. And you know, it's really been a busy week here in Wisconsin. It started with the release of those polls that you talked about. Those are the gold standard polls. Both campaigns, I tell you, we're paying attention to them. At the governor's level we saw things were tied and then it what you saw there with the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 Where -- while Senator Ron Johnson has that two-point lead, it's well within the margin of error, and it's tighter than what we saw even just last month. And because of that, on the campaign trail, we've been seeing the attacks continue to escalate. Senator Ron Johnson getting more personal, saying that Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes has disdain and contempt for his country and for Wisconsinites.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Governor Barnes firing back and saying that Senator Ron Johnson is in it for himself, that he changes tax policy for the simple benefit of himself and his donors. You see those attacks flying and you're also starting to see some doubt, at least being raised by one of the candidates, Senator Ron Johnson, about election results.\par \par \uc2 I asked him after an event earlier this week whether or not he would accept the election results on Tuesday. He essentially told me, it depends. He pointed to a story out of Green Bay with the Republican lawsuit against election workers there and then the firing of an election worker here in Milwaukee to say that there is some doubt there, that Democrats in his words, are cheating in this election. That's something that Democrats in the state have already been pushing back on. So, it's been a busy week and we know it's only going to get more intense as we get closer to election day, Steve.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Alright, Shaq Brewster in Wisconsin, the always swing state of Wisconsin. Shaq, thanks for that. We got more, North Carolina, Nevada, the state that could keep us waiting all week. More from the battleground Senate states right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:34:36]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. We were talking about some of the states where Democrats are trying to play offense again, looking at the Senate battleground map here. States where Democrats are trying to flip Republican seats here.\par \par \uc2 Let's look at it from the other angle, what about where Democrats are most vulnerable here. Georgia, that race in Georgia has been tight all year.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:54]\par \par \uc2 Take a look though, the dynamic in Georgia is the dynamic right now of potential split ticket voting. Something that was supposed to be extinct in our politics.\par \par \uc2 But in the race for governor, you see \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  the Republican incumbent. He's leading in the poll average by eight points over \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . He's over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in the Senate race running nearly 5.5 points behind \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and actually behind in the poll average \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent. It's an interesting dynamic here.\par \par \uc2 Trump of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  went to war politically with \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  earlier this year, in a Republican primary, Kemp survived. Did that win him over some of those Trump skeptical suburban voters outside Atlanta? \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  may be having some trouble with.\par \par \uc2 Could split ticket voting save a Senate seat for Democrats, our own Sahil Kapur is covering the Georgia race, joins us now from Georgia. And Sahil, we have this x factor of a potential runoff here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But putting that aside for a minute, if you could just talk about the big picture dynamic of there is this group of voters who are clearly ready to vote Republican in the governor's race but so far, the weekend before the election, have not been persuaded by \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Who are we talking about here? And what do you think is going to decide how they ultimately cast their votes?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Well Steve, this race is dead even. You showed one polling average that shows the contest within a point. There's another polling average by 5:38 that shows the contest at one-tenth of one percentage point between the two. That's how close it is.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a possibility that neither candidate gets to 50 percent required by state law to avoid a runoff, in which case, it would be on December 6th would be when that runoff is held, and if Democrats hold their own elsewhere, Georgia's runoff could once again decide which party controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, there's no question, Steve, that down the stretch \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has the momentum. He's got political gravity on his side. He's in a state where Republicans have structural advantages. It's a Republican stronghold up until just two years ago and the president's low approval rating concerns about the economy are propelling him.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand you have \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  who's who has sharpened his attacks lately, portraying \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as someone who is unfit to serve in the Senate, given his lack of knowledge about the issues, given his turbulent past. Allegations of violence.\par \par \uc2 And there are ultimately three questions, Steve, that will decide the outcome of this race. And how voters come down. The first is rural areas. Are they going to show a big heavy turnout, that's MEGA country, Republicans need that.\par \par \uc2 The second question, are Democrats going to show the same kind of firepower that they showed in recent cycles where they really surged their own turnout. They're going to need that to have a shot.\par \par \uc2 And if both those things happen. And to your question, it's going to be the suburban moderate college-educated voters in places like where I am right now, Atlanta metro area, who flip from Republican to Democrat who are crucial to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carrying the state in 2020. And \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  as well as Senator \uc2 John Ossoff\uc2  winning Georgia at that point.\par \par \uc2 The single biggest question here could be whether \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  can hold on to that suburban moderate maybe center-right swing voter who doesn't like the direction of the Republican party. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  has tried to appeal to them in ads where he tells testimonials from those types of voters saying they're going to support him. Not only is that going to be important in Georgia Steve, it could decide Arizona in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Fascinating split ticket voting, we thought it was a thing of the past, could decide this election.\par \par \uc2 Sahil Kapur in Atlanta, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And from Georgia, now we take a look at North Carolina, what you're seeing here is the presidential result in 2020 from North Carolina. Remember this one kept us waiting for about a week before we declared that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did carry North Carolina here. The margin though was barely a point. And what's interesting is that Trump margin was down, it was more than a 3.5 points in 2016. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats were able to eat away at a lot of that Trump advantage though he still did end up prevailing by 75,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 There were suburbs outside of Charlotte, outside of Raleigh in particular where Democrats saw movement. And a tough midterm environment for them but a state where there is growth potential potentially in the suburbs. It's given them hope.\par \par \uc2 It's maybe this is a Republican-held Senate seat \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2  retiring that they could pick up. Antonia Hylton, our reporter is on the ground in North Carolina. And Antonia, I know you caught up now with both Cheri Beasley the Democrat and \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  the Republican. A race that's flown a little bit under the radar nationally, but again, in a state that was very tight in 2020.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That's right, Steve. And I've spent some time with both of them today in the western part of the state. For Representative Budd, that meant being in friendlier territory, some rural communities that overwhelmingly went for Trump. He was talking to voters, who in most cases had already voted for him. and really message to them was to try to get other people in these rural areas who may still be on the sidelines or forgotten to participate in the midterms, to the polls as fast as they can.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:59]\par \par \uc2 Cheri Beasley was here in these same areas, again these, you know, plus 36 Trump counties for example. And she has really taken a unique strategy for Democrats, who normally in North Carolina spent almost all their time in urban centers, just trying to drive their numbers up there.\par \par \uc2 But she's kind of got a different tactic going on here. She's trying to get some of these voters, some of these Democrats and some of these black voters who live in these rural counties often get ignored, and drive them to the polls. That's what she thinks is going to help her kind of get this winning coalition that people in her view, may be sleeping on right now.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to my conversation with both candidates, I had a wide-ranging conversation with both of them from everything from abortion and the economy to, you know, the threats of democracy and how voters are feeling about both of these candidates right now. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: What about abortion restrictions? This is probably the number two question I get. What would the ideal abortion policy or restriction look like? And that's really for you, but also for you. The two of you will be working together.\par \par \uc2 REP. TED BUDD (R-NC), SENATE CANDIDATE: It's really not what North Carolinians are talking about.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: I talked North Carolinians worried about women's access to abortions all the time.\par \par \uc2 BUDD: So what we're talking -- I mean how extreme they are. This is what Democrats want. Abortion at anytime for any reason all the way up to the moment of birth. And they want that at taxpayer expense, and that is wildly out of step with North Carolinians.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D), NC SENATE CANDIDATE: \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  and his national Republican allies have been spending millions of dollars against me to distort my judicial record because they know we really can win this race. They don't spend that kind of money unless they know.\par \par \uc2 And while \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  has said a whole lot of things, what he hasn't talked about is what he's done. He's the one who's been in Congress for six years. He's had every opportunity to work hard to lower costs for folks here in North Carolina which is a huge issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  right now is just a bit ahead of Cheri Beasley but the reality is this race is closer than many Republicans wanted it to be or thought that it would be. And so neither of these candidates are resting on their laurels at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Both of them are circling around all 100 counties trying to get as many people, you know, in this large group of unaffiliated voters in the state, undecided voters in the state ,and try to get them to get them, their friends, their mom, the grandmas to the polls right now.\par \par \uc2 And it's really, you know, again people say this is \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 's race to lose. I try to tell people not to sleep on North Carolina. Because of just all of the sort of unknown factors here.\par \par \uc2 This is (INAUDIBLE), you know, Steve a really interesting state and with all the new voters and undecided and unaffiliated people here I think there's going to be some interesting information we get, when we get closer to Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Antonia Hylton in North Carolina. It will also be interesting just because the poll closing time, 7:30 and they report out a ton of votes very quickly usually in North Carolina. Again it could be one of those early indicators.\par \par \uc2 So from North Carolina we head west to the Silver State, Nevada. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , Democratic incumbent, trying to hold on against Republican \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The backdrop here, in the Trump era, the state of Nevada moved in the Republican direction. You see Biden won it, blue state in 2020 about 2.5 points. Pretty much the same as it was in 2016 but look before Trump came along, we were starting to think of Nevada as something getting close to a safely blue state Barack. Obama won it by seven points in his reelection in 2012.\par \par \uc2 All the way down to about two and a half Republicans have felt for a few years now, that they are on the cusp of getting over the top of Nevada. Is this is the year they could finally do it. And is this the Senate race that could potentially, if they get it, potentially get them control of the chamber.\par \par \uc2 No one better to bring in here than from Nevada -- sorry, there he is. John Ralston from Nevada. Sorry, I thought we didn't have you there for a second. Mr. Nevada politics, John, you know Nevada elections. You've seen many of them. What is your sense, the weekend before this campaign, where the Senate race stands.\par \par \uc2 JOHN RALSTON, CEO, THE NEVADA INDEPENDENT: Well, Steve, as you know we've had some really close Senate races here in Nevada. In fact Harry Reid, who held \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 's seat before she did, had some very close races. This is a very, very close race. It's a mid term in a purple state. Yes, you're right about the slight rightward turn that the Republicans hope to capitalize on. They thought they could in 2020. Now, they think they can. There's a lower turnout. The Democrats have not done what they did during the Reid era, Steve, which is build up this large bank of early votes. You know, people deride trying to make projections of early votes, but here in Nevada, as you know, so much of the vote is in before election day, often at least two thirds.\par \par \uc2 And the Democrats only have a 1 percent lead statewide and the early voting ends today. Mail ballots can come in for the next week.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:49]\par \par \uc2 And this race is about as close as anyone as I've ever seen. I think Democrats are optimistic that she can hang on, but Republicans are almost equally optimistic that \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is going to pull this one out.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. John Ralston in Nevada, I'm sure we'll be checking in with you a lot over the next week too as that Nevada race gets decided. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the battle for control of the House. We're going to talk to somebody whose job it is to know the polls politically in all 435 districts. What does Amy Walter of the Cook Political Report think about where the battle for the House stands on this weekend before the election, that's next.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:27]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Let's revisit some numbers we showed you at the top of the show. Again, this is generic ballot averages they ask in the polls. Do you want the Democrats or the Republicans to be controlling Congress.\par \par \uc2 You got two different averages here -- RealClearPolitics, 538 -- bit of a difference between the two of them. Each showing the Republicans with an edge. Almost three points in RealClearPolitics, just over a point in the 538 average.\par \par \uc2 And as I said, this number's been about where it is right now for a few weeks now. It's caused some I think a lot of folks to not be sure what to think about this upcoming midterm election.\par \par \uc2 There's been a lot of discussion about the quality of the polls that go into these poll averages about the differences you can see from average to average and about the fact that hey, there are some past midterm elections where when you look at the generic ballot average the weekend before the election. It was very clear which party was poised to have a huge night.\par \par \uc2 In 2018 the Democrats were up 7-8 points in the generic ballot. In 2010 the Republicans when they won 63 seats in \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's first midterm had giant leads in the generic ballots.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes you look at the generic ballot, and it's very clear the weekend before where it's going. You look at the generic ballot this year you might scratch your head a little. And you might scratch your head a little when you look at some of the polls that we've seen from some individual house races too.\par \par \uc2 Environment that seems to favor Republicans, but some numbers that make you think, is there anything else going on here? Is there a little more complicated.\par \par \uc2 So to try to untangle this, the weekend before the election, we thought there'd be nobody better to talk to about the battle for the House than the editor of the Cook Political Report Amy Walter.\par \par \uc2 Amy, I'm curious. I mean all the midterm elections I'm talking about here, 2018, 2010, 2006. You know them you remember them.\par \par \uc2 I'm curious, do you think that your sense, our sense the weekend before this election, of what this environment is really like. Is it as clear as it's been in the past? Is it fuzzier? Are there things that are giving you pause? Are there things you feel confident in? What's your sense of the atmosphere overall?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, COOK POLITICAL REPORTER: Sure. I think you put it really well, Steve. The environment, clearly, benefiting Republicans. Just a national narrative, where the focus is right now. On the economy, the focus on inflation. That's definitely helping Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The other thing we forget, Steve, it's a redistricting year. And I don't know that we've paid enough attention or given enough credit just how important that issue is, especially in some of these races where incumbent Democrats like the chair of the Democratic Congressional Committee running in a district that 75 percent new to him many of these members are running in districts that on paper are competitive.\par \par \uc2 But, where they don't know their constituents very well because they quite frankly haven't been on the ballot before.\par \par \uc2 There are so many cross currents going on here Steve that it's interesting to watch some of these Democrats who were in tight races right now tough races in states and in districts that on paper looks safe.\par \par \uc2 But because of redistricting as well as the fact that in many of these states, you have either an unpopular governor or issues going on in those blue states that are also causing people to look for change.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: The other question here is turn out. When we're talk about potentially 120 to 130 million people. I mean that used to be high for a presidential election now we're talking about that many voting in a midterm election.\par \par \uc2 Is that a variable that you consider too just in terms of creating more uncertainty heading into election day?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Absolutely. you know, the interesting thing that's giving me a lot of uncertainty is where independent voters are going. Usually, at this point in the election, and that generic ballot, it's pretty clear where independent voters are leaning.\par \par \uc2 But in the most recent polls we've seen Independent voters again depending on the model whether it's a likely voter or registered voter model, anywhere from a low of three points separating Democrats and Republicans among Independents to a high of 15 points.\par \par \uc2 What I'm wondering is whether these independent voters, many of them still saying they're undecided, this close to the election maybe they just don't show up at all. And what does that do to our turnout? And what does that mean for the final results?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You've given me yet another possibility to think about there. As we head into the final weekend here.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: For sure.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Amy Walter, Cook Political Report, thank you and good luck to you the next few days. It will be a very busy time.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Thanks. You too.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. THE COUNTDOWN continues right after this.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:43]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And that does it for THE COUNTDOWN here at Keeneland Race Course in Lexington, Kentucky. If you need a quick break from politics this weekend, the Breeders Cup is right here tomorrow. This is the world championship of horse racing.\par \par \uc2 Nine races with the top horses from all over the globe, including one named Flight Line -- remember that name. Some say he is the best horse since Secretariat. He'll run in the $6 million Breeders Cup Classic, that's the biggest race on horse racing's biggest day. And also, by the way, it's the best betting day if you're into that at all. Coverage starts tomorrow. 1:00 p.m. eastern on USA, and then 3:30 p.m. Eastern on NBC.\par \par \uc2 Of course, I will see you on Tuesday, Election Day and election night for live coverage analysis starting at 6 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:54]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Thanks for watching THE COUNTDOWN.\par \par \uc2 And "THE 11TH HOUR" starts right now with Stephanie Ruhle who has New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  with her.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law