ttle secret you won`t hear in any other ads.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will very very very probably do it again. OK?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the return of chaos in American government that`s on the ballot in four days.\par \par \uc2 Then, Cornell Belcher on reading the wild variations in last minute election polls.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Republican candidate who wants a community level review process to decide whether rape victims can get abortions.\par \par \uc2 BO HINES, CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE OF NORTH CAROLINA: There are certainly legal mechanisms you could put in place legislatively that would create an individual basis reviews.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And direct from the campaign trail with \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , the one and only John Legend on his midterm message to voters.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEGEND, SINGER: The reason why we`re here is because we need to show up for our democracy. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of Tuesday`s midterm elections, Democrats are bringing out all of their heavy hitters to mobilize people and get them out to vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Congressional Republicans, eyeing a midterm election victory that could hand them control the House and Senate have embraced plans to reduce federal spending on Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 They`re just saying, they`re not even hiding it. They`re not even hiding it.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Ads about crime every 30 seconds, right? No solutions, but just a lot of really fearful, scary pictures and scary music. They don`t care about keeping you safe. They want to keep you scared.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They have said there will be a national ban on abortion. And we`re going to need people in the state House and at a local level who have the courage to stand up and push back against what is happening in our country.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: These days are just about every Republican politician seems obsessed with two things, owning the libs and getting Donald Trump`s approval. That`s their agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The sitting president and vice president, a former president and a former Secretary of State. Not to be outdone, the Republicans have trotted out their biggest star for a rally in Iowa last night, the undisputed leader of the party, the twice impeached ex-president.\par \par \uc2 I should tell you before we get to what happened at that rally, we are expecting a statement from the January 6 committee about the Trump subpoena deadline. He of course subpoenaed by the January 6 committee was supposed to start giving over documents today. If and when we get it, we will let you know about that.\par \par \uc2 But during a predictably rambling rally, Trump teased his forthcoming presidential run, walking up as far to the line as possible without an actual formal announcement, which brings with it a number of legal and financial hurdles.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s comments are notable despite the fact that he has done little to hide his intentions to run again, because there has been lots of reporting, indicating that Republicans do not want Trump to announce before the midterms, it`s a consensus position between both Republican moderates and strategists and the MAGA days because anyone in the Republican Party with at least half a functioning brain cell understands why Trump announcing early is bad for them.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is supremely polarizing. In fact, one of the most unpopular figures in modern political history.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have a lot of momentum heading into Tuesday`s midterms. The last thing they want is for Trump to remind everyone that if Republicans win, he will effectively be the shadow speaker of the House in charge of all major party decisions.\par \par \uc2 But to be clear, even though they don`t want you to know that, that is of course exactly what will happen. We know this.\par \par \uc2 If you`re a generic Republican House candidate running in a swing state, state like Rhode Island or you`re running an Oregon blue state, right and you`re in a swing seat. You`re not saying I will be an agent of Donald Trump`s political whims when in office, but that is precisely what you are running to be. That is what effectively will happen.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:03]\par \par \uc2 If Republicans retake the House, the entire conference, or at least the overwhelming majority of it will take its marching orders from Trump.\par \par \uc2 He will bark at them to impeach President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  probably more than once and they`re very likely to try, then he will bark at them to impeach Biden`s cabinet members too, especially Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , who Republicans have already said they will try to impeach.\par \par \uc2 And it just so happens to oversee the Department of Justice, which is currently investigating Trump`s gross mishandling of some of our nation`s most sensitive secrets. And they`re almost certainly the listen to that too.\par \par \uc2 He will bark at them to shut down the government to default on the debt limit which would plunge our country into economic catastrophe. We`ll see what they do with that.\par \par \uc2 And the so called moderate Republican candidates and swing districts, they can sell themselves however they want, on crime or inflation or gas prices. I assure you, like I`m with you on abortion, I`m not like one of those crazies, doesn`t matter -- doesn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, their platform will do nothing to solve any of those problems. Instead, they will be effectively fuel in the engine that is the Republican Party under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Whatever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  posts online that day, that will be what the Republican Party sets their mind to. That is the dynamic at play here.\par \par \uc2 There`s this old cliche in politics campaigns, elections come down to change or more of the same. Republicans as the out of power party, they want it to be a change election, they`re running against the incumbent party. And they have a pretty easy time making that case, because it is very clear that voters feel unsettled.\par \par \uc2 Heck, I feel unsettled. I think almost everyone feels unsettled. Three out of four Americans think the country is headed on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 We`re in the aftermath of a once in a generation pandemic, which is still going, has killed more than a million Americans and counting. It shut down the country -- shutdown the world in a totally unprecedented way, had never happened before in human history, basically.\par \par \uc2 The after effects of that have been really hard on people. And there`s a tremendous feeling of enduring instability, disruption, disorder, and understanding, you can see the fraying of the social fabric right before our eyes, feels bad.\par \par \uc2 Republicans want you to think they`re the solution to all those problems. If you see disorder, they will give you order. If you see instability, they will give you stability. That`s even though many of them started under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of course, Republicans are not the solution to any of these problems, they will not usher in a way stability or lower prices. That`s for a bunch of reasons.\par \par \uc2 But the prime obvious one staring us all in the face, the guy at the podium last night being like I`m going to run for president, they are enthralled by an under the command of an agent of pure chaos, whose only goal will be to disrupt things even more.\par \par \uc2 Again, think about the incentives of the two parties as you head into this election. If you are one of those voters who`s seeking stability and order.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are the ones far more likely to develop -- to deliver on that. Because if for literally no other reason, not even an ideological reason, just the sheer political incentives, their political incentives over the next few years are to make American life and the economy as great as possible heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have the opposite incentive structure. If disorder and disruption and chaos make people feel unsettled and make them vote for Republicans, they have an incentive to create more of those things.\par \par \uc2 To make things as bad as possible, to get those wrong track numbers up, to kneecap Democrats running against them two years from now. And they`re already trying to sell voters a preposterous vision in retrospect to the Trump years. Just listen to Republican Senator Tom Cotton of Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): The Democrats are always saying that never forget January 6. Yes, remember that on January 6, gas was at $2.40 a gallon. It was at $2.40 a gallon because for four years, we had an administration that understood that American energy production and specifically fossil fuels, natural gas, oil, and coal, literally power our economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What? That`s what you remember at January 6? First of all, gas was cheap because the global pandemic crushed demand, it -- barrel of oil went negative at some point.\par \par \uc2 But when you think back to 2020 2021 January, you think, man, gas was cheap. Because that was -- at that very moment he`s talking about was COVID was killing more people every day than literally any other cause of death. 20,000 people dying every week. Almost 10 times more people dying a day than now.\par \par \uc2 So yes, I mean, I guess if you just like don`t care about that, you`re super focused on gas. Better times then.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:06]\par \par \uc2 But again, 2020 is not that long ago, OK, we were there. We lived through it. We live through Trump, we lived through COVID.\par \par \uc2 You do remember what it was actually like under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? The chaos, the disruption, the complete negligence from the White House, the feeling that everything was sort of teetering on the edge.\par \par \uc2 And again, that was before COVID hit when he had inherited a very smooth set of circumstances in a macro economy that was growing. He did everything he could to screw it up.\par \par \uc2 That sense of disorder, disruption, instability, chaos. That is what Trump carries with him everywhere he goes. And I`m telling you, I assure you, that is what Trump will bring back if Republicans take power next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Robert Draper, writer from \uc2 New York Times Magazine\uc2  and the author of the new book Weapons of Mass Delusion: When the Republican Party Lost its Mind.\par \par \uc2 And, Robert, I do think this is under appreciated. I think you`re one of the few people focused on this appropriately. We`ve watched what happened in the U.K. with the craziness and leadership, turning over and proposing things that causes financial markets to go.\par \par \uc2 The level of chaos that a House Republicans party is going to bring I think is really under appreciated by most people. But I think you have a -- have a beat into the fact that this is going to be utterly unwieldy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DRAPER, WRITER, NEW YORK TIMES MAGAZINE: Yes, unwieldy on multiple levels, Chris. I mean, for one thing, the chaos that will ensue will be intramural, I think. I mean, it`s -- you do have plenty of Republicans who are eager to seize power because they want to have certain classically Republican things done, more tax cuts, stripping away Obamacare protections, things like that.\par \par \uc2 Then you have the Marjorie Taylor Greenes of the world who want a far right agenda, and will push at every turn against something that looks milk toast to them.\par \par \uc2 Finally, you have, as you put it very eloquently, the ghost speaker of the House, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and Trump once failed to Trump. I mean, Trump is not going to be pushing an agenda, you know, like so \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , for example, has actually been saying you know, that there needs to be justice reforms and that all these January 6 people are being unfairly persecuted.\par \par \uc2 Who knows what that will look like legislatively, what we do know is that Trump doesn`t give a damn about that, that Trump will care about only -- and he`s already said this, you know, constantly about who right now has pledged, basically, a failed t to the Trump vision, which starts with his notion that the 2020 election was stolen, and he wants everybody in line on that.\par \par \uc2 And but the -- you know, the real chaos will ensue when actually really important issues relating to the debt ceiling, relating to fund Ukraine will come into play.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And here`s the thing that I think people don`t appreciate, which would be novel, right? So, obviously, there was a Republican House with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president, but again, think about incentives here, right?\par \par \uc2 The incentive when the president was not to just break the country. In fact, his incentives were like lots of simulative spending, right? He actually increased the deficit, right? Wanted money going on the pockets, wanted the Fed to cut rates, so he had an incentive to not break the country.\par \par \uc2 This will be all of the control of the House, but none of the responsibility of being president and wanting a record to run on which means what he says to do they will listen to him and what he says to do, because he`s just thinking about one thing is likely to be quite destructive.\par \par \uc2 DRAPER: Yes, I mean, he`s thinking most of all, you know, first and foremost, Chris about retribution. All right, so retribution against Democrats, retribution in the press, also retribution against RINOs.\par \par \uc2 And so, there will be endless investigations, there will almost certainly be an impeachment inquiry on using precisely what rationale we have yet to know.\par \par \uc2 But what we also know is that Kevin McCarthy, though he`s already kind of flicked at his reservations about things like an impeachment inquiry, because he knows that`s not the world`s greatest midterm message to swing voters doesn`t have the power to go up against the MAGA core.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  is in this conversation for that reason and that reason only because she is the proximate warrior to Trump and in the MAGA constituents who are looking for a brawler, they`re looking for a fighter. And McCarthy knows that he`s got to have their support from him to get anything done for him to hold the gavel for that matter.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me ask you about -- we just got this statement from the committee. Obviously, one of the things that will continue to sort of loom over all this are the continued after effects of January 6, possible prosecutions the Department of Justice might undertake and the committee`s work, which likely if the Republicans take over will suspend.\par \par \uc2 So, that will likely not continue. But today was the deadline for the subpoena of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which he has just blown off. The committee just issuing a statement saying we`ve received correspondence from former president`s counsel in connection with select committee subpoena. We`ve informed the former president`s counsel that he must begin producing records no later than next week. And he remains under subpoena for deposition testimony starting on November 14th.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:21]\par \par \uc2 I wonder what you anticipate will be the move from Trump here at or his sort of protectors in Congress?\par \par \uc2 DRAPER: Well, more foot dragging for sure. And they`ll find whatever legal channel that they have to achieve that goal.\par \par \uc2 As I think I`ve said to you before, Chris, I do believe that the devil on Trump`s shoulder, which is Trump himself, you know, kind of has an itch for testifying in primetime television him versus \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so, I don`t expect him to defy this outright, I imagine that there are backstage conversations taking place between him and his lawyers, in which they`re saying, please, sir, under no circumstances, should you, you know, go down this path.\par \par \uc2 But I`m sure that you know, at any given moment when you talk to him about this, he`s receptive to it. So, I think that there will be a lot of, you know, push me, pull you even within his own ranks. But I think the one thing we can be certain of is there`s not going to be any complying anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and they also have -- I mean, obviously, they`re hoping for a Republican victory that would time limit all this, which has been what he`s on before.\par \par \uc2 I think that temptation to do some big show stopping exchange with the committee is high. But what you`re -- what you`re saying, and what I think is likely is that the cooler heads will prevail here and stop him from doing that. Although, you would think thought they did that with the documents at Mar-a-Lago and they didn`t.\par \par \uc2 DRAPER: That`s right. Yes. I mean, it`s -- you know, even in the MAGA world that`s inhabited by say \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  and \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  and the rest of them, they just think the January 6 committee is a farce and no one should pay any attention to it and they pay no attention to it.\par \par \uc2 Trump is different in that regard. I mean, Trump pays attention to anything that is getting views and he sees this as an opportunity and he never underestimate the longing that a man like him who no longer has the center stage that he wants commanded to getting back on it.\par \par \uc2 So, I still wouldn`t count out the possibility that sometime if indeed, the Republicans regained control of the House, the Trump rises to the bait on this. I don`t think the odds are great, but I think it`s possible.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Robert Draper, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DRAPER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, Election Day is next week, it seemed like nobody has a good handle on what`s actually going to happen. The problems with polling in 2022 and how to think about what could happen Tuesday, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:22:19]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`re heading into the final weekend before Tuesday`s midterms, many people are looking to the polls because they`re anxious and they want to know the future as humans always do.\par \par \uc2 The thing is, polls can be all over the place. Now this is partly because over the past 25 years, people are responding less and less to pollsters.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2019, \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  released this chart showing how telephone response rates, typically the most reliable polling method fell from 36 percent in 1997, to just six percent in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Then last month, the pollster for the New York Times Nate Cohn said "Only 0.4 percent of dials in their poll at the time yielded a completed interview". Adding, if you were employed as one of our interviewers at a call center, you would have to dial numbers for two hours to get a single completed interview.\par \par \uc2 So, given all that, the best estimate we have is that Republicans are favored to win, but not overwhelmingly so. And there`s a huge range of possible outcomes.\par \par \uc2 That`s not the message on Fox News, which has been touting surging Republicans and days until retaking our country, laying the groundwork for Republican candidates to claim fraud if they lose next week.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Cornell Belcher, a pollster and Democratic strategist talking about what his expectations are for where the midterms stand right now.\par \par \uc2 First, Cornell, let`s start about -- start on just like the response rate issue and the -- and the general reliability of polling right now. You`re sort of takeaway as a practitioner.\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: This is an important conversation, by the way, very dirty conversations, but I love that about your show.\par \par \uc2 But look, you know, \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  has been doing this, going back to 1997 1996, I believe, studying this relationship between response rates, and quite frankly, they found that thus far, the response rates in survey accuracy, you know, very little relationship in the fall of response rates and survey accuracy.\par \par \uc2 Now, that said, at some point, do you go off a cliff? Right? When you get down to two or less percentage sort of response rate, does it go off a cliff?\par \par \uc2 Now, I will say that I have not in our sort of internal polling got down to the point where it`s -- where it`s less than one percent response rate.\par \par \uc2 But it is -- but it is an issue. Although thus far, it has not been a huge issue. I think the larger issue, Chris is, quite frankly, the art of polling, not the science of polling.\par \par \uc2 I think the science of polling is still fundamentally sound. But the science -- but you know, getting the science right does not make you a good pollster, it doesn`t make a good poll. It is the art of polling.\par \par \uc2 Take 2012, for example, in the last week, and I was working on the Obama campaign. Within the last week of that campaign or so, the Romney folks were doing really interesting things. They were going into Pennsylvania and spending money and doing all these other things in blue areas that was very curious.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:14]\par \par \uc2 Now, after the fact, it comes to find out that a lot of Republicans in fact thought that they were going to have an electorate that look more like the Bush Kerry electorate than the 08 electorate.\par \par \uc2 And so, when you get -- when polls are wrong, Chris, it is in fact happening with what their expectations of the electorate are, what`s their likely voter.\par \par \uc2 I want to say this, and this is important, Chris, there`s been a -- Republicans have flooded the zone with polls after poll after poll.\par \par \uc2 Chris, that internally don`t look anything like the electorate that we`ve seen over the last two set cycles. And I think there`s more here than meets the eye.\par \par \uc2 Like, why are you doing this? It`s diabolical. And we in the news media are beginning to follow that narrative about a Republican surge, and how sort of, you know, how everything`s leaning Republican.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you right now, when you look at early vote, and you look at the percentage of people, especially young people who are participating early vote, this doesn`t look anything like three of the last four midterm elections, it looks like the outlier of 2018.\par \par \uc2 And when the outlier of 20 -- given the outlier of 2018, a lot of these polls are going to be quite frankly off because it looks nothing like what a traditional midterm electorate looks like.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we should say that that`s the big variance, right? What the electorate looks like, we should also say that voting choice has become so polarized, it`s hard to know what early voting pretends for same day.\par \par \uc2 But generally, what I`m getting from you, as a takeaway here in the last 40 seconds is just that, you think that this is not -- you think there`s a wide range of outcomes possible on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes, I do. And by the way, Chris, I`m going to -- I`m going to go -- I`m going to go on a little deeper. We are bastardizing the science of polling for