ut I also see some pretty meaningful differences.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: You know, that`s a great point, Maria, because when you look at -- when you look at 2010, you look at 2018 too, you have this big signature vote that was so present in the election, right?\par \par \uc2 In 2010, it was -- it was the Affordable Care Act, and it might have been Waxman-Markey, which is the climate bill, right?\par \par \uc2 2018 Republicans voted to repeal Obamacare, unsuccessfully, and every swing district ad talked about it. I mean, it was -- it was -- it was present.\par \par \uc2 There`s -- the legislation that Democrats have passed in these two years polls pretty well, and really has not been the subject of attacks in the -- in these richer swing races, except when they make up things like they defunded the police, which they did the opposite.\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, PRESIDENT AND CEO, VOTO LATINO: Right. Well, and this is the thing, is that these individuals right now on the Republican side, many of them voted against the signature of legislation that you speak of right now, Chris. Everything from $35.00 on insulin caps, everything from Medicare prescription drugs, being able to actually, you know, come in and negotiate those prices.\par \par \uc2 The $500 billion that they`re expecting in infrastructure through the environment and chips and manufacturing, and so forth, and the list goes on. They didn`t vote for them, yet, they want to own it.\par \par \uc2 And the most important thing right now is that floating statement from the Congressman went from the ad that you just saw, because at the end of the day, what we are fighting for just four days from now, Chris, is our democracy.\par \par \uc2 In the 18 years in which I have headed up Voto Latino, the largest voter registration often in the Latino community, I have yet to see us president take an appeal to the American people regardless of party and sound the alarm the way President Biden did just a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 And he was clear, this what is on that -- what is on the ballot on Tuesday is democracy and for individuals that believe in democracy, don`t take my word for it. Don`t even take the president`s word for it. Take Liz Cheney`s word for it, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and I we may not be aligned on many issues and values. But we do believe deeply that democracy is what keeps us as a country together.\par \par \uc2 And the more that we can make sure that that is the litmus test, at the end of the day, when it is who we`re voting for, then the country will continue in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and we played that \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  ad to bump in because she`s one of the swing street, frontline members who was making that pitch quite directly. She`s on the January 6 committee, and she`s also making it a central theme, others have gone in different directions.\par \par \uc2 I want to play this Tom, which I think you`d appreciate from \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  is in Michigan, she`s in a very tough race. And this is an ad that`s a very like, bread and butter. Here`s what we`ve accomplished. Here`s what I want to do for the middle class in Michigan. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISSA SLOTKIN (D-MI): I`m \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  and I approve this message. Let me tell you a story about Michigan manufacturing. We have powered this country for 100 years but now we face a choice. Do we want to build the next generation of cars in Michigan? Are we OK with them going out of state and overseas?\par \par \uc2 If Tom Barrett got his way, we`d have no GM battery plant, no thousands of jobs that come with it. And there`d be zero chips made in America.\par \par \uc2 Tom Barrett says he doesn`t want to pick sides. If it`s Team USA over team China, it`s a no brainer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So Tom, I thought this was interesting, because it`s, you know, it`s populace, it`s also very brass tacks. It`s like, here`s some actual legislation we passed. Here`s the result we`ve seen, here`s what I`ve delivered for you. What do you think of that message?\par \par \uc2 PERRIELLO: I think it`s a great message. And you know, she and \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  are both great patriots who really stood up for principle at a -- at a tough time. And I think it`s really a choice. One side is really focused on taking away our rights and freedoms. And the other side is trying to bring back our jobs.\par \par \uc2 And I think you`ve seen that with Tim Ryan`s race in Ohio, you`ve seen that in other places, that idea of wanting to build making grow things in America again. And I think that`s something that resonates with people across demographic groups, including in the Latino community where people want to believe in the American dream. And I think you see in the Biden agenda, trying to make that American dream a little bit more affordable again.\par \par \uc2 And I think, frankly, if the Republicans had given any care about the economy, they would have run away with this election if they had been putting forward plans on inflation and affordability, but they weren`t. They were focused on destroying our democracy and taking away women`s rights and voting rights. And they`ve shown their true colors. That`s what they care about. That`s what they`re going to focus on.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Finally, quickly for you, Maria, you`ve done this for a while. This is not going to be a low turnout election. 2014 Democrats had a bad election. It was because of low turnout. Whatever happens on Tuesday, do you -- it seems pretty clear it`s going to be a high turnout election, right?\par \par \uc2 [20:40:06]\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Right now, we`re seeing great strides in high turnout in Pennsylvania, in Georgia. I`m very interested in where Tom is right now, Nevada. We need Nevada and Arizona to get those numbers up, because they`re not quite where they were in 2018.\par \par \uc2 If they are, then this is going to be much closer. And we`re going to actually see a Senate that is going to be favorable to the president. And Congress is going to be much closer and I believe in limiting the losses for the president if that is the case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Maria Teresa Kumar and \uc2 Tom Perriello\uc2 , thank you both, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, as the biggest names in democratic politics hit the road for one final push, they`re getting help from one of the biggest names in music, singer John Legend joins me ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:26]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: As a physician, I`ve been in the room when there`s some difficult conversations happening. I don`t want the federal government involved with that at all. I want women, doctors, local political leaders.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Women, doctors, local political leaders. We`ll remember that moment of unintentional honesty from T.V. Dr. Mehmet Oz, the Trump backed candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in Pennsylvania during the debate last week.\par \par \uc2 His answer expose the absurdity of the position of the many Republicans who say they support abortion bans with exceptions.\par \par \uc2 You can hear the same thing from another Trump backed candidate Bo Hines running for Congress in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 You see, after interviewing the Republican candidate, a local T.V. station described his position this way, "he wants victims of rape and incest to be allowed to get an abortion on a case-by-case basis through a community level review process."\par \par \uc2 OK, surely something got lost in translation. They got that wrong. That can`t be this guy`s real position, can it?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HINES: I think we have to look at each case individually.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As you know, legislation can`t be written on an individual basis. So, if it comes before Congress, Lindsey Graham`s for example. His proposal is to have exceptions for rape, incest in the life of the mother, do you support that?\par \par \uc2 HINES: I`d have to look at the bill to be honest with you, I`d have to see how it`s structured. But there are certainly legal mechanisms you could put in place legislatively that would create an individual basis review. So, I reject the notion that you couldn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Legislative mechanisms for individual basis review, community to review to decide if a pregnant woman or a girl meets the criteria of an exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 You know, whenever one of these candidates is pressed for even 30 seconds on how these exceptions would work, this is all they come up with.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the fact is, most rape and sexual assault victims do not even report it to police. So, are they going to submit to a community review process?\par \par \uc2 I mean, think about it, again, think about it for more than 30 seconds. Imagine a girl having to go down to town hall for a hearing standing at a microphone to explain to a panel of community members how she got pregnant from incest.\par \par \uc2 This is the reality. This is what it would look like, these calls for exceptions to make abortion bans seem less invasive than they really are.\par \par \uc2 And some Democrats are effectively conveying that message like this ad for \uc2 Joe Cunningham\uc2  running for governor in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When I was 12 in 1972, I was babysitting for a neighboring family. The father came home with his friend early from work, locked their children outside and commenced to take turns raping me, I had to have an abortion at that time with no anesthesia.\par \par \uc2 I did what was best for an 88 pound 12-year-old with no other