 of the few people whose won an Emmy, Grammy, Oscar and Tony Ward, has worked relentlessly in his spare time to advocate for equal voting rights across the country.\par \par \uc2 John, it`s great to have you on. You`ve been a bit on the trail. And I understand that you`ve actually been doing some door knocking, you`ve been doing some canvassing?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:02]\par \par \uc2 LEGEND: Yes, we`re actually going to do some canvassing tomorrow morning here in Atlanta. I`m in my hotel room in Atlanta. I spent the afternoon and evening here speaking with folks working on housing policy.\par \par \uc2 But in the morning, we`re going to work with the Warnock and Abrams campaigns, and try to get folks out to vote here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, I always tell people who are interested in politics who have not knocked on doors to go do it, because it`s a really remarkable experience. And it can be difficult and weird, but it`s incredibly rewarding I`ve found. What has your experience been?\par \par \uc2 LEGEND: I love talking to voters. I love connecting with them. And we have to remember that our democracy is powered by the people.\par \par \uc2 And as long as we stay in touch with the people and hear what their concerns are and what their priorities are, we can have a healthy democracy, have leaders who are responsive to the people, we can do better together, but we`ve got to get out there and talk to them and hear from them and press the flesh. And I think it`s important. And I believe \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  believe it`s very important as well.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You obviously you care deeply about issues of social justice and politics. And I want to talk a little bit about criminal justice reform, which you and I`ve spoken about before.\par \par \uc2 What do you view -- what do you view as the stakes here? You talked about - - I like that line about people picking their politicians, not politicians picking their voters, which is what happens in some states or gerrymandering like in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 What do you see as the -- as the main stakes that have you out on the road talking to people this time?\par \par \uc2 LEGEND: Well, I think this election is a defining moment for democracy, we see so many election deniers running for state office where they will actually control elections.\par \par \uc2 So, you`re seeing it with governor elections, you`re seeing it with secretary of state elections, you`re seeing it with local election boards. We have so many people who don`t even believe in, you know, free and fair elections who are running to administer our elections.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is an inflection point for our democracy because if we allow so many of these people who are anti-democratic, who are fascist -- flirting with fascist tendencies to be running our elections, we may not have an actual democracy the next time we have an election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You have campaigned on and organized around criminal justice reform. I spoke to you a few years ago back in Florida with that remarkable ballot initiative to restore voting rights for people who had put felony records that passed by over 60 percent needed, it`s subsequently been kind of, you know, pared back a bit or made more complicated by \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 LEGEND: Well, DeSantis is a cruel and small individual, and he`s found as many cruel ways he can to undermine the progress that the voters of Florida overwhelmingly voted for.\par \par \uc2 You see his cruelty when it comes to arresting people who were told that they were allowed to vote, you see his cruelty when it comes to him flying refugees and asylum seekers around the country based on deception and lies.\par \par \uc2 He`s a cruel, small person. And you`re seeing that in his resistance to the democratic reform that the voters of Florida overwhelmingly voted for.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I got to say that we`ve covered that quite a bit. I mean, the arrests were some of the most shameful stunts that I`ve seen, I really, truly --\par \par \uc2 LEGEND: And it frustrates me -- and Chris, it frustrates me because it`s your job as a journalist to show that, but you`re also doing the work of DeSantis. Because what he really wants to do is make people afraid to vote. He wants to make people who have felonies on their records, who most of them are eligible to vote, he wants them to be afraid to exercise that right.\par \par \uc2 Even if they were told by the local administrator of the election that they were eligible. They`re being arrested. And so, we`re doing whatever we can to help fund the legal defensive folks who are being unjustly charged. We`re fighting for folks. We want people in Florida to make sure they go out and vote, make sure they check their status and do what they need to do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and we should note that one of the first cases has already had the charges dropped of the 20 plus and talking to legal experts.\par \par \uc2 I suspect that will not be the last of those because these are -- these are pretty paper thin.\par \par \uc2 John Legend who is going to (INAUDIBLE) hit the doors tomorrow. I think hopefully someone`s recording when they open the door and John Legend is there. So, we can see that later. Thank you so much for making time for us tonight, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LEGEND: Thank you, Chris. Hi, Chrissy. Hi, Luna. Hi, Miles.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:05]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right. Be well.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN for this week. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221105eib400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for November 02, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Charles Blow, Glenn Kirschner \par \uc2 6,665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:27]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex. I`m glad you reported on that settlement today, especially including the famous Donald Trump`s 2016 quote vote of how he never settles, when in fact, as litigation history has a lot of settlements, even before Trump University, where he just kind of gave up and paid people off, rather than go to --\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": It`s what he does.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: He settles. It`s also a great window into his adherence to the truth.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, we have breaking news tonight in the federal criminal investigation of Donald Trump`s possession of government documents, including classified documents at his Florida residence. "The Wall Street Journal" is reporting tonight that Kash Patel, a close associate of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , has reached an immunity agreement with the Department of Justice, and will soon be forced to testify under oath to a federal grand jury investigating the case.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors obtained the immunity agreement with Patel after he appeared before the grand jury on October and refused to answer questions by invoking the Fifth Amendment, on the grounds that his answers could incriminate him. Kash Patel was the last person \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put in charge of his dealings with the National Archives about the documents that the Archives was trying to retrieve from Trump`s Florida home.\par \par \uc2 Before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  obtained a search warrant to search the residence in August, Kash Patel said publicly, Trump declassified whole sets of materials and anticipation of leaving government that he thought the American public should have the right to read themselves. Never mind that he hasn`t allowed the American public to read those documents. Patel went on to say, I was there with president Trump when he said, we are declassifying this information. We will see if he`s now willing to say that under oath.\par \par \uc2 Kash Patel served in the Trump administration until the day President Biden was inaugurated. And the speech tonight in Washington about next week`s election, President Biden began by describing what happened to Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s husband when he was attacked with a hammer that produced a skull fracture. That happened in his home, in a case prosecutors call attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 After referring to that, President Biden said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We don`t settle our differences in America where the riot, a mob, or a bullet, or a hammer. We settle them peaceably, at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our first guest, Glenn Kirschner, was in the courtroom today in Washington when we learned that some Trump supporters wanted to settle at the last election with rifles. And the federal criminal trial of the so- called Oath Keepers for seditious conspiracy, a secretly recorded audio revealed the leader of the group, Elmer Stewart Rhodes, saying this after, after the January 6th attack on democracy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEWART RHODES, OATH KEEPER FOUNDER: We should have brought rifles. We could have fixed it right then and there. I`d hang (EXPLETIVE DELETED) Pelosi from the lamppost.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We should`ve brought rifles. We could`ve fixed it right then and there. I`d hang effing Pelosi from the lamppost.\par \par \uc2 A message that all members wrote to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the last days of his presidency was also introduced in court today. The message urged \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to use the military to hold on to power and refused to leave the White House.\par \par \uc2 The message said: If you don`t, then Biden, Kamala will turn all that power on you, your family, and all of us. You and your family will be imprisoned and killed. You and your children will die in prison.\par \par \uc2 Elmer Rhodes wrote the message to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after being told by Jason Alpers that he could get that message to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . After seeing the message to Trump and becoming disturbed by it, Jason Alpers contacted the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and provided them with the written message and the audio recording of Elmer Rhodes saying we should`ve brought rifles.\par \par \uc2 The Rhodes message to Trump also said: I am here for you, and so are all my men.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:04]\par \par \uc2 We will come help you if you need us, military and police, and so will your millions of supporters.\par \par \uc2 That was, of course, a very insulting lie about Donald Trump`s unions of supporters. A couple thousand of whom actually showed up to fight for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on January 6th. That`s a couple thousand out of 74 million law abiding Trump voters who never have joined and never would truly a violent political crusade.\par \par \uc2 A court filing has revealed some of the emails that the California judge ordered turned over to the January 6 committee by one of Donald Trump`s election lawyers, \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 . In an email dated December 31st, 2020, one of the lawyers on the Trump team, Kenneth Chesebro, wrote this to \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nearly having this case pending in the Supreme Court, not ruled on, might be enough to delay consideration of Georgia, particularly if Pence has the legal ability and well to insert himself at least enough to win delay. Possibly Thomas would end up being the key here -- circuit justice, right? We want to frame things so that Thomas could be the one to issue some sort of stay or other circus justice opinion saying Georgia is in legitimate doubt.\par \par \uc2 Realistically, our only chance to get a favorable judicial opinion by January 6th, which might hold up the Georgia`s account in Congress is from Thomas. Do you agree, professor Eastman and \uc2 John Eastman\uc2  replied, I think I agree with this.\par \par \uc2 So there`s \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 , who served as a Supreme Court clerk for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , and who has remained close to him, and who has remained in lifelong close touch with Clarence Thomas`s wife, Virginia Thomas. There he is, saying that knowing -- as he does, he agrees that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was their only hope of getting a delay from the Supreme Court involving the Georgia presidential election result.\par \par \uc2 The friends of \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  know that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  would not recuse himself from that case, even though the law requires him to recuse himself from a case in which his spouse has an active interest. The members of the conspiracy who were friends with \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  -- the conspiracy to overthrow the presidential election, had their eye on the most publicly compromised member of the Supreme Court in the court`s history, as their last best hope, because they could not use rifles.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Glenn Kirschner, a former prosecutor and NBC legal analyst, who`s been following the Oath Keepers trial from inside the courtroom. Also with us, Bradley Moss, national security attorney.\par \par \uc2 And, Glenn, I want to begin with immunity. This situation now where Kash Patel has immunity, has to go back to the grand jury, he does not have Fifth Amendment protection anymore, of course. He doesn`t have immunity from perjury. If he commits perjury in his testimony, then he can be charged with crime.\par \par \uc2 But what else does it mean to this investigation that someone who was personally involved with the documents once they arrived in Florida now has immunity to testify?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, it suggests to me, Lawrence, that the Department of Justice is focusing on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s why they`re willing to immunize Kash Patel. You know, it`s interesting, because immunity is not ordinarily the prosecutor`s friend, because when you immunize somebody, you`re essentially giving them a pass for their own crimes. And then, they`re going to ask a jury in the future to credit them, believe them, when they testify about the crimes of others.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell, you that makes for some robust defense cross examination of an immunized witness. But the Department of Justice, first of all, litigated the issue of whether Kash Patel`s invocation of his Fifth Amendment right was legitimate or not. We can tease something out of it. What we can tease as it was probably a relatively minor crime that they were dealing with, one where there was an open question as to whether Kash Patel should be allowed to evoke his fifth.\par \par \uc2 But once a judge ruled he has a viable fit, the department of justice had to make the decision. But we have enough to charge him and tried to develop him as a Cooperating witness with a guilty plea and cooperation agreement?\par \par \uc2 [22:10:08]\par \par \uc2 Or should we immunities him so we can work our way closer to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? It looks like they want the immunity back. That might be good for Kash Patel, but, boy, is it bad for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Bradley Moss, your reaction to this immunity deal?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY MOSS, NATIONAL SECURITY ATTORNEY: Yeah, if I`m \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , this is the last thing I want to see, because now, Kash Patel has no way to avoid testifying. He invoked his Fifth Amendment right to avoid testifying the first time. Now he`s been immunized. He`s going to have to talk to the grand jury. He`s going to have to describes what he saw, what he knew about Donald Trump`s awareness of the documents in existence, what he actually saw and witnessed in terms of any declassification efforts, and what he knew about efforts to relocate or conceal the boxes and the records within the boxes once that investigation started, once the issue had been subpoenaed.\par \par \uc2 This is one more material fact witnesses that the government has lined up as part of this grand jury presentation to bring what I expect at some point in the next few months to be an indictment against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for Espionage Act and for obstruction charges.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn, I imagine in your experience as a federal prosecutor with immunity deals that there are witnesses like Kash Patel, who get immunity and then go into the grand jury and still, somehow, try to cling to some kind of way of protecting their principal. Some kind of way of trying to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in this case.\par \par \uc2 What do prosecutors do when they sense that is going on?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: We breathe a sigh of relief, because we`re now reluctant to grant immunity in the first place. As I say, that`s giving someone who committed crimes potentially a pass for the criminal conduct. If we go into the grand jury and they lie, for openers, Lawrence, that`s perjury. They may be an accessory after the fact trying to help their criminal associate. And maybe obstruction of justice, maybe evidence of a conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 The reason I say we may breathe a sigh of relief is because now, we have so much evidence that we can use to charge them, and then flip them and develop them as a cooperating testifying witness.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Bradley Moss, as this case goes forward, they`ve got immunity for Kash Patel, this testimony, when they get, it brings them as close to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in terms of testimony as they have come so far.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Correct. We know they`ve also spoken with Christina Bobb, the lawyer who from what I understand sanitation back in June saying there were no more records when of course there where. We`ve spoken with at least one if not two different staffers, who were insisting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was part of the effort to move the boxes, move the records after the subpoena was issued. They`re building the entirety of the case.\par \par \uc2 If you`re going to bring this kind of case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you want to be ironclad. You want to dot every I, crossed every T, getting all the way to the inner choir, and making a decision that I don`t think they`ve made yet. But I assume they`re getting ready to, which is, are we going to indict the former president?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn Kirschner, what was it like in the courtroom today when the jury heard on audiotape they are Elmer Stewart Rhodes say they should`ve brought rifles on January 6th?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You could`ve heard a pin drop. You saw the juries riveted to the under covered recording that was made by a civilian, who was a former military member himself. Circumstances were left a little murky, not fully explained by either the prosecutors or the defense. But you know, Lawrence, we`ve been listening to a lot of experts and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  reference, custodians, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, reading incriminating signal chance between and among Oath Keepers, and it kind of tends to lull you into comfort.\par \par \uc2 And of a sudden, you hear the marquee defendant, Elmer Stewart Rhodes, head of the Oath Keepers and his own voice, saying what happened on January 6th was good. But, my only regret was we should`ve brought rifles. We could`ve fixed it than in their. I would`ve hung effing Pelosi from the lamppost.\par \par \uc2 And all of the jurors, I think, reacted to that. I would say it`s all over, but the verdict, of course, no verdict is a sure thing, and the defense has promised that Elmer Rhodes will testify. So I think we`re all looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn Kirschner, Bradley Moss, thank you for starting off our discussion tonight. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Coming up, joining us next is Cheri Beasley, the Democratic candidate for Senate in North Carolina, where one candidate says I`m community level review process should decide who, if anyone, can have an abortion, including a ten-year-old rape victim who he referred to as a ten- year-old woman.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:14]\par \par \uc2 You will hear that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I don`t want the federal government involved with that at all. I want women, doctors, local political leaders --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Local political leaders should decide who should have an abortion. That line by the Republican candidate for Senate in Pennsylvania was used by his Democratic opponent, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , and campaign ads stressing that Donald Trump`s candidate for Senate in Pennsylvania says that he wants local political leaders to decide who, if anyone at all, can have an abortion in Pennsylvania. Donald Trump`s candidate for the House of Representatives in North Carolina is now saying, essentially, the same thing.\par \par \uc2 The 27-year-old Republican candidate Bo Hines running in the newly registered 13th congressional district in North Carolina has said, quote, abortion should be made illegal throughout the United States, no exceptions.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:14]\par \par \uc2 Now, he`s saying something else.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: Are you saying that you would accept an exception for rape or incest in North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 BO HINES (R), NORTH CAROLINA U.S. HOUSE CANDIDATE: I think it`s certain cases, yes. Obviously, a case like that, where you have a ten-year-old woman -- ten year old girl, I should say, obviously she shouldn`t have to go through with the pregnancy. That`s something that`s a horrific tragedy that has to be exceptions in place for it.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: Okay, is it more the rape and incest, or her age?\par \par \uc2 HINES: I think it`s a combination of both. But you have to look at each individual case and look at each individual circumstances and what happens there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: WRAL of Raleigh, North Carolina, then summarized Bo Hines` new position on abortion this way, he wants victims of rape and incest to be allowed to get an abortion on a case by case basis through a community level review process outside the jurisdiction of the federal government. So, the ten year old girl who Bo Hines first referred to as a ten-year-old woman and that answer would have to go through how many interviews in the community review process after being raped?\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who is always been in favor of banning all abortions in all 50 states, with no exceptions at all, campaigned in North Carolina for a candidate who supported the crowd, they wanted to hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  on January 6th. Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  voted to overturn the presidential election on January 6th after -- after the Trump mob attacked the Capitol and tried to kill \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is desperate and hopeful pursuit of the presidency now has him pursuing the political support of the people, who wanted to kill him on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Cheri Beasley. She`s a former chief justice of the North Carolina Supreme Court. She`s now the Democratic candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 What`s it like for you, campaigning in North Carolina, when a former Republican vice president comes into campaign against you?\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D), NORTH CAROLINA SENATE CANDIDATE: You know, this extremism is not what folks here in North Carolina want. And Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  has been very clearly that he wants an absolute ban on abortion without exceptions for rape or incest or risk to a mother`s health. And these extreme positions really aren`t tantamount to a violation of the constitution.\par \par \uc2 We know that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  says we shouldn`t make abortion illegal. We should make it unthinkable. You know, nobody gave any forethought, clearly, to the fact that women sometimes are sexually assaulted and it means she will be forced to carry a pregnancy to term. One woman who has an ectopic presidency, septate uterus or miscarriage that are body won`t release, will not be able to get the lifesaving treatment that she needs, which is an abortion. And it means that women will die if \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  get their way.\par \par \uc2 I will always fight when our freedoms are online. And I will fight hard to make sure that Roe versus Wade is the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What are you saying to North Carolina voters about the issue that shows up at the top of the list of concerns for voters, and that is inflation?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: You know, I`ve been all over our 100 counties, and I`m talking with people all over the state. They are concerned about rising costs. They`re feeling everything from pain at the pump, for the cost of prescription drugs, and everything in between. And then in the greatest country in the world, folks did not have to make decisions about buying groceries or school supplies or high-priced medications.\par \par \uc2 They want the next senator to fight hard to lower costs, that`s exactly what I`m going to do. Congressman Budd however has been in Congress for six years. He`s had every opportunity to vote to lower costs. But instead, he`s been thinking about himself in corporations.\par \par \uc2 He voted against lowering the cost of gas while taking tens of thousands of dollars a big oil money. He voted against lowering the cost of prescription drugs and camping insulin while taking thousands of dollars from big pharma.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Congressman Budd has showed us that he`s not going to work hard for the interests of people here in North Carolina, and the reality is, when people show you who they are, you need to believe them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Cheri Beasley, Democratic candidate for Senate in North Carolina, thank you for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: CheriBeasley.com for more information. Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is Wiley Nickel, the Democratic candidate for the U.S. House of Representatives of North Carolina, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 We just saw what your opponent is now saying about abortion after saying that all abortion should be banned.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:04]\par \par \uc2 Now, it should be up to some kind of community group, I guess, to what, interrogate rape victims?\par \par \uc2 WILEY NICKEL (D-NC), U.S. HOUSE CANDIDATE: I mean, Lawrence, it`s absolutely cruel, it`s disgusting, and it just shows the stakes that are, you know, at play in this race for congress. You know, we get a chance to reject this far-right extremism, and you heard it. This is a candidate for Congress echoing the comments that Dr. Oz made about local politicians making these decisions.\par \par \uc2 It`s frankly just unimaginable. A woman who`s been traumatized and raped would have to go in front of a panel of strangers to beg for health care? You know, we get to reject that in this race.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: As President Biden has pointed out, very many Democrats in the Senate, almost all of them, who have publicly, at some point, been in favor of cutting Social Security. Some of them like \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  of Utah actually eliminating it, repealing it, getting rid of it, having absolutely no Social Security program.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to the voters in your district about the future of Social Security?\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: You know, I say when I say on the campaign trail every day. Social Security and Medicare are on the ballot in this election. All I`ve heard from Republicans nationwide are attacks on the economy. There`s no solutions other than cutting Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 From my constituents, those are benefits that they`ve earned and worked for. I`m going to do everything I can to preserve those programs.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Wiley Nickel, a candidate for the -- it`s the 13th congressional district, right, in North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 NICKEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, coming up, Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  is on video, saying he wants to get rid of Social Security, to uproot it. Independent Senate candidate Evan McMullin, who has the support of Utah`s Democratic Party is running to uproot \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  from the senate, and he will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:31:50]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: This is a live look at Cesar Chavez High School in suburban Phoenix, where former President Obama is scheduled to speak very soon at a rally for Arizona Democrats, including Senator Mark Kelly and the Democratic nominee for governor \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator Kelly`s wife, \uc2 Gabby Giffords\uc2 , is also expected to speak. We will be covering that.\par \par \uc2 Here is Republican Senator Mike Lee`s plan for Social Security.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): It will be my objective to phase out Social Security. To pull it by the roots and get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hear, hear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Utah Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , who wants to uproot Social Security is now running for reelection in opposition to \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 . Last week, \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  said this. "I don`t remember ever, in any time since I first became a candidate for Senate ever saying, no, we just have to end Social Security and uproot all those expectations of those who`ve paid into it -- quite to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 And of course, of course, there`s video of him saying exactly that, the first time he ran for senate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: It will be my objective to phase out Social Security.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nice.\par \par \uc2 LEE\uc2 : To pull it by the roots and get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hear, hear.\par \par \uc2 LEE: People who advise me politically always tell me that`s dangerous and I tell them in any case, it`s not worth my running. That`s why I`m doing it, to get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 Medicare and Medicaid are of the same sort, they need to be pulled up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Evan McMullin, the Independent Senate candidate in Utah. He`s a former CIA officer and former House Republican policy director. He is supported by the Democratic Party of Utah in his campaign.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 You know, I am struck not just by your opponent`s declaration that he wanted to repeal, uproot Social Security, get rid of it, along with Medicare and Medicaid, he throws those in at the end.\par \par \uc2 But it`s that bravery, that bravery that he proclaims upon himself in the middle of that statement, when he says that his advisers tell him that it`s politically not safe to say that. Said it`s dangerous to say that. And he says, he tells them, well, in that case, it`s not worth my running.\par \par \uc2 Well, it turns out -- it turns out that he is not saying that anymore at all, is he?\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN (I) UTAH SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, great to be with you, Lawrence. I mean look, this is the same kind of extremism that would lead Senator Lee, and did lead Senator Lee to try to overturn our election in 2020 by recruiting fake electors to overturn the will of the people and to dismantle our Democratic republic. I mean, Senator Lee is an extremist. And that is what we are fighting against here in Utah.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:51]\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I`m running as an Independent so that I can build a cross partisan coalition of pro democracy Democrats, Republicans, and Independents. And that`s what we`ve done here and that`s why this is now a competitive race.\par \par \uc2 That`s why Senator Lee and the far-right are frightened that we are going to unseat him and that we`re going to challenge their power.\par \par \uc2 And this is something that should not be partisan. Most Republicans, Democrats, and Independents want to protect Social Security. Most of us want to still have a democracy in America.\par \par \uc2 But the far-right is opposed to those things. And that`s why we`re building this coalition to replace them. and so we are inviting people from across the state and across the country to join us at EvanMcMullin.com. This is how we can save Social Security and how we can save our democracy.\par \par \uc2 It`s by coming together. Those of us who still believe in these things, still believe in truth, that`s the coalition we are building here in Utah.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In his speech tonight about next week`s election, President Biden said democracy is on the ballot. Given that you are running against someone who tried to overturn the last presidential election, do you believe that democracy is on the ballot in Utah?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: It is on the ballot in Utah. I consider this to be a race that is sort of ground zero for the defense of American democracy Because you`ve got in Senator Lee someone who literally tweets, literally says we are not a democracy. And then works by his own account 14 hours a day to make it so.\par \par \uc2 And he told the president, the former president, that he was trying to unravel the results of the election in 2020 to keep President Trump in power, despite having been rejected by the people.\par \par \uc2 That is what we are up against. And so my belief, Lawrence, that I think I`ve probably said before, even on this show that the primary dividing line in American politics today is not whether you`re a Republican or a Democrat, or a conservative or a progressive.\par \par \uc2 It`s whether you`re committed to our founding ideals that we`re created free and equal. And therefore, we have a democracy and we have free and fair elections and the constitution and we accept the reality of it objective truth. You know, that is the dividing line of in American politics -- either you believe in those things still or you do not. And thankfully, here in Utah, a majority of Utahns still do believe in those things and that`s why we are poised to replace \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  and have an excellent opportunity to help defend our democracy from the far right that wants to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll add, Lawrence that, you know, they are so worried that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just endorsed \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  for the third time today. Now, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is getting involved. \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  has been spending I think upwards of $1 million here, as well, to hold onto the seat.\par \par \uc2 And the reason why they are afraid is that the rest of us are uniting to stand up for our democracy. And that`s, you know, we`re inviting everyone who still believes in those things to join us.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Evan McMullin, running for Senate in Utah as a defender of democracy. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Democrats in Wisconsin are facing Republicans who are so extreme that the "Milwaukee Journal Sentinel" did something that it never does -- endorsed candidates for senate and governor, both Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Charles Clow will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:38:19]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The remarkable thing about American democracy is this. Just enough of us, on just enough occasions have chosen not to dismantle democracy, but to preserve democracy. We must choose that path again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was President Biden earlier tonight. Today, the "Milwaukee Journal Sentinel`s" editorial board does something it really does, endorsed Wisconsin`s candidates before the election.\par \par \uc2 "Normally, the USA Today Network-Wisconsin editorial board does not recommend candidates in elections. But because of the stakes in this election and our deep concerns with these candidates, we have decided to do so."\par \par \uc2 The editorial board endorsed Democrats Mandela Barnes for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  for governor over their Republican opponents Senator Ron Johnson and businessman \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 . The editorial board cited the Republican candidates` unwillingness to affirm the results of the 2020 election as a reason not to endorse them.\par \par \uc2 In the race for Senate, the editorial board emphasized the Republicans` focus on crime prevention is dangerous, saying "Crime is a local issue handled by mayors, city councils, and police departments, not senators. Its emergence in the senate race this fall is simple fearmongering on the part of Republicans who are hoping to scare voters that the black man running for senate is too different and too dangerous to serve, actual words used in commercials supporting Johnson. One of the ads even intentionally darkened Barnes`s skin. The ads are racist and disgraceful."\par \par \uc2 [22:44:41]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Republicans are also using racist ads in Georgia, where an ad from American -- America First Legal, a group founded by former Trump policy advisor \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  accuses the Biden administration of what it calls anti-white bigotry and promotes a rhetoric of discrimination and racism against white Americans.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Charles Blow, columnist for the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Charles, we are seeing this extraordinary editorial coming up and saying that the Ron Johnson campaign is basically running a racist campaign against Mandela Barnes. What is your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, they are saying he`s running a racist campaign, and also this guy could destroy, help to destroy our democracy. But those things are not able to be disentangled at this point.\par \par \uc2 Part of the reason that Trump was on the rampage was that he lost. And part of the reason he lost was because enough people, a coalition of people -- white, but also black, brown, and Asian -- banded together and defeated him. If it was just left up to the white people of America, majority of them would have voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he would still be the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Part of what he was doing was attacking people, attacking areas and districts that were majority black and saying, look there, look there, there must be something nefarious happening in Fulton County in Georgia. There must be something nefarious happening in other parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 And that is what was the basis of, you know, his big lie. And that fueled the insurrection. And that has fueled all of these elected officials across the country jumping in line behind Trump to say they too do not accept the results of the 2020 election. And may not accept the results of their own elections if they lose. And if they are elected, may do things to undermine our democracy in furtherance of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 And so that is what the editorial board is looking at. And anyone who looks at this soberly is just as frightened as they are. There`s some people who look at it and say it cannot be as bad as you guys say it is. Actually, it can be worse.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, and President Biden`s speech tonight saying that democracy is on the ballot struck a new note for him, a new note of realism. There`s a passage in the speech that we just used at the beginning of this segment that I don`t think is the kind of thing he would`ve said a couple of years ago.\par \par \uc2 He said just enough of us on just enough occasions have chosen not to dismantle democracy but to preserve democracy. I think the way he would have said that in the past is America has always chosen to preserve democracy. Instead, he`s identifying in the way he`s saying it, that there is a huge segment of the American electorate, close to half, that are willing to junk democracy.\par \par \uc2 He said just enough of us on just enough occasions have chosen not to dismantle democracy. And that is the reality. And if it happens this time, it will be because just enough of us have made that choice.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: And that is what has been happening. This point of realism, we`re not seeing the elections of, you know, half a century ago, where people could win in landslides. People are winning with 3 percent, 5 percent, maybe 10 percent of the vote. It just looks like they are landslides because that tips the electoral college in ways that make it look, you know, the winner has a lot more electoral college votes.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the actual vote, it`s not large percentages. And that is the scary part. It only takes a little bit of voter suppression to tip it in the other direction. It only takes a little bit of voter intimidation, as we`re seeing in some cases, to tip it the other way. It only takes a few elected officials willing to say that they do not accept the results of free and fair elections to change those results in ways that would benefit someone who also does not believe in democracy.\par \par \uc2 That is just a realistic way of talking about what is happening in this country. We have to stop talking about, like, these victories, that when we do get them, America has chosen, America has turned away from.\par \par \uc2 No. Just a tiny majority of Americans turn away from this horrible thing. And in the next cycle, they may in fact turn back to it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We are going to take a break here. As we go out, we are continuing to watch the rally in Arizona where Senator Mark Kelly`s wife \uc2 Gabby Giffords\uc2  is now speaking. President Obama will be speaking soon.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:36]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here`s a live look at Cesar Chavez High School in suburban Phoenix, where you see Senator Mark Kelly speaking there. He is running for reelection in Arizona. He is the Democratic candidate for Senate.\par \par \uc2 He will be introducing President Obama at this rally. Let`s listen to what Senator Kelly has to say.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:35]\par \par \uc2 [LIVE EVENT]\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): -- jobs back to America. But better than that, to Arizona. Back to America to lower costs and create good paying jobs that do not require a four year degree.\par \par \uc2 Now, as our state faces more intense heat and drought and wildfires, we are now boosting renewable energy. This is going to help us -- help us tackle climate change and bring even more jobs to our state.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know that there is more work to do. Families -- families are struggling with the high cost of gas, groceries, and housing. And Arizona women? Arizona women have lost a right they`ve had for decades.\par \par \uc2 Arizona women have lost the right to make their own health care decisions about abortion. So there is so much at stake in this election in six days.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is the important part. Are you listening?\par \par \uc2 So I have an opponent, Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 Blake Masters has some beliefs that are just dangerous for Arizonans.\par \par \uc2 That`s very funny. He wants to cut taxes. He wants to cut taxes for big corporations that are hiking up prices and has said that he would privatize Social Security.\par \par \uc2 I understand the boos. We won`t need the boos if we all vote.\par \par \uc2 He wants to send seniors` hard earned benefits to Wall Street. You know who makes money when money goes to Wall Street? Wall Street. That was an easy one.\par \par \uc2 And now this is serious, all of this is. Blake Masters, my opponent, he calls abortion "demonic". And he said, he said he wants to punish the doctors. And he wants a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 He wants a national abortion ban that`s so strict that even when a woman is raped she will not be able to make this decision herself.\par \par \uc2 Blake Masters wants to make this decision for Arizona women. You don`t want that.\par \par \uc2 He thinks that he gets to make this choice instead of you.\par \par \uc2 So what are we going to do? We are going to vote, in big numbers.\par \par \uc2 So, I`ve got some new info for you. This is also about Blake Masters. So after getting a phone call from a guy in Florida a few weeks ago, Blake Masters is again saying that the 2020 election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 But worse than that -- worse than that he is now questioning the results of an election that is still six days away.\par \par \uc2 This is dangerous, folks. Now, we all know guys like Blake Masters. He thinks that he knows better then everyone about everything. Don`t you know somebody like that?\par \par \uc2 Well let me tell you what`s -- here`s the problem. Somebody who thinks they know better than everyone about everything, letting them make decisions for you is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 So in just six days, let`s make sure that we beat him.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:56]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is Senator Mark Kelly campaigning in Arizona. He`s about to introduce President Obama. That will happen after the end of this hour.\par \par \uc2 Senator Mark Kelly gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221103eib200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for November 01, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Andrew Weissmann, Joyce Vance \par \uc2 6,456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:35]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 We have \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  joining us later in the hour, because the January 6 Committee did an interview with Secret Service official about exactly what was going on over there, on January 6th. We`ll see what she can tell us about how that went.\par \par \uc2 And we are also going to be joined by Senator Michael Bennet, running for reelection in Colorado. We will see how comfortable he`s feeling tonight.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGT": The Secret Service piece of January 6th is the biggest mystery I think there is. So, I`m eager to hear that interview. And Michael Bennet, always a good talker.\par \par \uc2 Have a great show, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have two important confessions tonight, two historic confessions, historic confessions about the Trumpian madness that consumes millions of people in this country. One of the confessions is by a Trump supporter who confessed to attempting to kidnap and possibly murder the speaker of the House and to actually attempting to murder the speaker`s husband.\par \par \uc2 We have another confession from another Trump supporter who has not yet committed the crime he has confessed to, the crime that he has confessed he is planning to commit. Here is that confession.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R), WISCONSIN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Republicans will never lose another election in Wisconsin after I`m elected governor.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You just heard Trump supporter \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  say, Republicans will never lose another election in Wisconsin after I am elected governor. He did not know he was being recorded at that campaign event. That is the Republican candidate for governor in the state of Wisconsin, with a poll say the Republican candidate is tied -- tied now with Democratic Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , who is running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 The Republican is campaigning for governor of Wisconsin by telling his supporters that he will and democracy in Wisconsin, end it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Republicans will never lose another election in Wisconsin after I`m elected governor.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That means if a Republican is elected governor in Wisconsin, he will deliver Wisconsin`s ten electoral votes for president to Republicans no matter what. He is promising that the next presidential election will not be fair. And he`s promising that no election in the state of Wisconsin will ever be fair again if he is elected governor.\par \par \uc2 That is what is at stake in this election. To say that Republicans, like the Republican candidate for governor and Wisconsin, do not believe in democracy is to understate their feelings. They are actively opposed to democracy. They are enemies of democracy. They are trying to kill democracy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Republicans will never lose another election in Wisconsin after I`m elected governor.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now, his press people are claiming that he simply meant that he will do such a great job as the Republican governor of the state that voters will only want Republicans in office, in all offices, in Wisconsin, for the rest of their lives. That is their defense for that statement.\par \par \uc2 But that same candidate, that very same candidate, has promised that he will decertify the 2020 presidential election result in Wisconsin, which gave Wisconsin`s electoral votes to President Biden. That is his word. Decertify. Now there is no such process. But that is how much of an enemy of democracy the Republican candidate for governor in Wisconsin is. And that is why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  so fully and energetically supports his candidacy.\par \par \uc2 We have the confession of a Republican candidate for governor planning to kill democracy at the same time that we have more details of the confession of the Trump supporter who tried to kill Nancy Pelosi`s husband, just as he would have tried to kill Speaker Pelosi if she had been home when he broke into her San Francisco home.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:03]\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  added more lying poison to the cognitive systems of his followers, whose cognition is already overloaded with Donald Trump`s lying poison.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appeared on a podcast today, where he said that there were, quote, weird things going on in that household for the last couple of weeks. Those were his exact words. Now \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is obviously just making that up. He has no idea what has ever been going on in Nancy Pelosi`s household at any point in her life.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that most Trump supporters have no ability left to separate fact from fiction, none. And that he can therefore say anything to them. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was speaking on that podcast to the kind of Trump supporter who broke into Nancy Pelosi`s home and confessed to police that he was on a suicide mission.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in the same podcast, said, quote, the glass, it seems, was broken from the inside to the out. So, it wasn`t a break in. It was a break out.\par \par \uc2 Now that lie by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  provoked San Francisco`s police chief tonight to publicly repeat on television that it was a break-in. The man who committed the break-in has confessed that it was a break-in. Millions of Trump supporters are much smarter than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and know that it was a break-in.\par \par \uc2 But most Trump supporters are not smarter than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Like the Trump supporter who was arraigned in state court today in San Francisco on charges of attempted murder, filed by San Francisco District Attorney Brooke Jenkins.\par \par \uc2 In outlining the charges against 42-year-old David DePape, the district attorney added new details to the confession that San Francisco police obtained from David DePape and information that was obtained from Paul Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pelosi, quote, asked defended why he waited to see or not talk to Nancy. Well, she`s number two in line for the presidency, right? When Mr. Pelosi agreed, defendants responded that they are all corrupt and we`ve got to take them all out. When Mr. Pelosi asked if he could call anyone for defendant, defendant ominously responded that it was the end of the road for Mr. Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pelosi grabbed his phone, turned it on, called 911, and put the phone on speaker. Watching Mr. Pelosi, defendant stood about three feet away, still holding the large hammer and the zip ties. During the 911 call itself, Mr. Pelosi said there was a gentleman there waiting for his wife -- \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  -- to come back.\par \par \uc2 To defuse the situation, Mr. Pelosi told the dispatcher that he did not need police, fire or medical assistance. Trying to be calm and discreet while also trying to help dispatch to understand the situation, Mr. Pelosi then asked for the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  because they are usually at the House protecting his wife. The dispatcher than asked Mr. Pelosi if he knew the man and Mr. Pelosi said that he did not.\par \par \uc2 The dispatcher then asked for the man`s name in the man responded, my name is David. When the dispatcher asked to David`s, Mr. Pelosi said, I don`t know. But David said, I`m a friend of theirs. Mr. Pelosi then confirmed with the dispatcher that he did not know the man.\par \par \uc2 After the 911 call ended, David DePape said he wanted to go downstairs to get his backpack. They proceeded downstairs with defendant walking behind Mr. Pelosi, still holding the large hammering the zip ties. Turning on the lights, Mr. Pelosi could see where the defendant entered the house. Defendant commented that he had to bash the window several times to breakthrough and enter. The defendant also said that the police would be there any minute.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pelosi tried to calm the defendant by saying they would not. But defendant responded, I can take you out. When the doorbell rang, the defendant directed Mr. Pelosi not to open the door. But Mr. Pelosi opened the door with his left hand. At the door open, the two men stood in the dimly lit foyer, facing the officers. Mr. Pelosi nervously become we greeted them. When the officer asked what was going on, defendants smiled and said, everything is good, and pulled his hands toward his body. When an officer turned on his flashlight, the defendant could be seen holding the bottom handle of the hammer with one hand and Mr. Pelosi`s right on with the other. Mr. Pelosi had his hand on the top of the handle near the hammer itself.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 One officer ordered, drop the hammer. At the same time, the defendant raised the hammer and said, nope. The defendant tried to pull the hammer away from Mr. Pelosi, which twisted Mr. Pelosi`s arm back. Simultaneously, Mr. Pelosi pleaded, hey, hey, hey. The officer asked again, what is going on here?\par \par \uc2 The officer asked again, what is going on here? But Mr. Pelosi could not maintain his grip on the hammer. A second later, the defendant rift wrenched his hammer away from Mr. Pelosi, immediately stepped back and lunged at Mr. Pelosi, striking Mr. Pelosi in the head at full force with the hammer, which knocked Mr. Pelosi unconscious. The officers rushed into the house, tackled the defendant and disarmed him.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Pelosi remained unresponsive for about three minutes, waking up in a pool of his own blood. The police say that DePape showed them exactly where he had broken into the House. The police did not ask him questions. But he did spontaneously make statements. I`m sick of the insane effing level of lies coming out of Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 I came here to have a little chat with his wife. The defendant added, I didn`t really want to hurt him, but you know, this was a suicide mission. I`m not going to stand here I do nothing, even if it cost me my life. Hurting him was not my goal. I told him before I attacked him that he`s escalating things and I will go through him if I have to.\par \par \uc2 A highly experienced public defender named Adam Lipson has been assigned to this case. His constitutional duty is to represent the legal interest of this client and we should all be grateful that he is doing that, because the system of jurisprudence could not work without dedicated defense council. Adam Lipson knows that his client has confessed to the federal and state crimes he is now charged with. Adam Lipson said that he`s concerned about his clients, quote, vulnerability to misinformation -- vulnerability to misinformation.\par \par \uc2 How many future criminal defendants are out there tonight, who is vulnerability to misinformation has them believing the lies that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is telling about the attempted murder at Nancy Pelosi`s home, which would have been an attempted an assassination of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , if she had been at home?\par \par \uc2 Listen carefully now to what this public defender had to say today after the arraignment. And keep in mind that Adam Lipson maybe a constituent of Nancy Pelosi`s or may have been in the past. He may have voted for her, possibly many times. And notice that he had the basic human decency to begin his comments, unlike \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and so many Republicans, with a full expression of sympathy for the victim in this case, Paul Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADAM LIPSON, PUBLIC DEFENDER FOR PELOSI ATTACK SUSPECT DAVID DEPAPE: Good afternoon. I will make every statement. My name is Adam Lipson. I`m a deputy public defender who is just appointed on this case.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I want to say that I am pleased to hear that Mr. Pelosi is progressing well in his recovery. And we certainly wish him the best. As you have heard, there has been -- we have very little information about this case at this time. There has been a lot of speculation, a lot of rumor, simply based on the nature of this case.\par \par \uc2 But as of right now, I have not even seen the police reports. I met my client last night for a brief meeting for the first time.\par \par \uc2 And so, I am not going to add to all the speculation by talking about the facts of this case right now. What I will say, is that there has also been a lot of speculation regarding Mr. Depape`s vulnerability to misinformation. And that is certainly something we are going to look into, that we are going to delve into as his defense team. But again, it would be premature to talk about that at this time.\par \par \uc2 I just want to end by saying that I have been a public defender for 20 years. I cherish my role as public defender. As most of you know, we represent people who cannot afford to hire a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:03]\par \par \uc2 I look forward to representing Mr. Depape, to providing a vigorous defense for him, and to getting to an equitable and just resolution of this matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight is, Andrew Weissmann, former White House counsel and former chief of the criminal division in Eastern District in New York. He`s a professor of practice at NYU Law School.\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Joyce Vance, former U.S. attorney. She`s professor at the University of Alabama School of Law, and co-host of the podcast "Sisters in Law". They are both MSNBC legal analyst. And we are lucky that they are.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Weissmann, that final note by the public defender, a just and equitable outcome. What I was hearing and I hope it is what we are hearing, and what we will continue to hear, is a responsible public defender, who is staying within his, so far, limited knowledge of the case. Everything he said is completely accurate.\par \par \uc2 He`s not protesting his client innocence, not throwing around lies like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , about, this was someone trying to break out of the house. And a just and equitable outcome could eventually involve a guilty plea, could involve, ultimately, and agreement here, between defense and prosecution on this case.\par \par \uc2 It is so striking, to compare what he had to say today, beginning with that decency, about his expression of sympathy for Paul Pelosi, to what we heard from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is out there actually trying to pollute the information, basically, we have on this case.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: The word decency, Lawrence, it is hard not to think about the McCarthy hearings and the famous line from Joseph Welch chastising Senator McCarthy -- at long last, have you no sense of decency? And that very much could be said about the former president, Ted Cruz, Elon Musk, Kari Lake, a slew of people who, I think, by any stretch of the imagination, have had a completely in human response to the truly harrowing situation that Paul Pelosi found himself in.\par \par \uc2 I was really struck by his wherewithal and having his wits about him. And it reminded me so much of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  having her wits about her on January 6th, both of them having this incredible sense, and you can see it`s something that probably bound them together. And one word, which I think Joyce would agree with, which is, when I was a prosecutor, we had a truly terrific relationship with the defenders in our district. They do just incredible work.\par \par \uc2 They are -- I found, completely ethical in terms of how they handle themselves, and just a truly wonderful relationship with prosecutors, being tenacious, as they have to be, but always playing within the rules. And I think what you played tonight is an example of that, somebody who is exactly what you want, and a system that has the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joyce, I`m struck by so many comparisons tonight between, say, that public defender and the Trump lawyers who throw around the craziest legal arguments in writing, all the way to the Supreme Court on the documents case, and in other cases involving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself, who is just making up evidence on podcasts today that does not exist anywhere near the case, such that the San Francisco police chief had to go on TV tonight and say, no, no, no, that is wrong. He did not specify that he was contradicting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But there will probably have to be more of that as this case comes on, more corrections of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or other leading Republicans tell as lies about this case.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: We live in a moment where we all crave a little bit of simple human decency. And we saw that tonight from a public defender who was thrust into the situation, where he had to speak to the press not knowing all of the facts, but knowing that the crimes committed by his client are largely captured on police body cameras. It will be a tough case to defend.\par \par \uc2 And he did the right thing here. He committed to not speaking out of turn until he knew what the facts were. And he committed to doing what the rule of law demands that he do, giving his client the best offense that he could give him.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:06]\par \par \uc2 And that stands in such a stark contrast to what we see from the former president, who never understood his constitutional role in government and who, today, appeal to the worst sorts of demons that people who listen to him may have. It`s exactly what you pointed out, Lawrence, in your opening, when you said that there are other people out there who have the same level of vulnerability that Mr. DePape has, people who can listen to falsehood and be motivated to violence.\par \par \uc2 And so, when you have a former president who declined to speak the truth, all that does is fuel additional political violence. That is the risk that we live in. It sounds alarmist to say it. I think people hesitate to say it in some ways.\par \par \uc2 But the risk we are taking as a country, if the Republican Party refuses to rein in their former leader, these incidents will become more -- across the country as we head into the more closing moments of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, what is going on tonight between federal prosecutors and the district attorney`s office in their air traffic control of this, of who goes first? And how does that work?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Yeah, that`s a great question. It is very unclear when it comes to the Georgia part of this sort of overall scheme of overthrowing the will of the people. What the federal role in that is going to be.\par \par \uc2 It appears that the feds are really leaving that to \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 . I`m sure they are monitoring that closely. But I`m sure you get the sense of true coordination in terms of overlapping witnesses.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  had, day today, a good ruling from the Supreme Court that denied Senator Graham`s application to delay even further his grand jury testimony. So, he`s going to be going into the grand jury. So, the state part of the case will be going forward.\par \par \uc2 Let me just remind people one more thing about that state case is, regardless of what happens with federal elections and presidential elections in a couple of years, there is no federal pardon -- if there were to be a stakes charge -- and conviction.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann and Joyce Vance, thank you very much for beginning our discussion tonight. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Welcome.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, right now, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is on the campaign trail for Democrats in Nevada. That is where he is tonight, where there is a must win Senate campaign for the Democrats there. The campaign for control of the Senate and the future of democracy is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:27:25]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is in Las Vegas tonight. There is the location right there on your screen. He`s there to rally for Democratic candidates in Nevada, as he did over the weekend, in key battleground states, it`s preciously for the Senate races.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: This current crop of Republican politicians, they are not interested in solving problems! They are interested in making you angry and then finding somebody to blame. And they`re hoping that that will distract you from the fact that they don`t have any answers of their own.\par \par \uc2 So, inflation is a problem. What is the Republican answer? Look, if they had a great answer, that would be one thing.\par \par \uc2 But you know what they are big economic policy is? They want to gut Social Security and Medicare and then give big tax cuts to the wealthy and some of the most successful corporations in the world. And -- pick up a few more seats in both chambers, they will be able to force the extremely unpopular laws from everything on guns to education and abortion, and there won`t be anything Democrats can do about it.\par \par \uc2 There maybe a lot of issues at stake in this election, but the basic question, the fundamental question that you should be asking yourself right now is, who will fight for you? Who cares about you? Who sees you? Who believes in you? That`s the choice in this election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our next guest, Democratic Senator Michael Bennet, of Colorado, is running for reelection. Here is some of his debate Friday night with Republican candidates Joseph O`Dea.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE O`DEA (R-CO), SENATE CANDIDATE: We have plenty of laws on the books. Every time we turn around and have an event, we put another law on the book. We need to enforce the laws that are on the book now.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): Joe O`Dea has said he supports no gun laws beyond what we already have a society. He`s against the red flag law we have in Colorado. And he said that he would have voted against a bipartisan gun bill that is the first time in a generation that Republicans and Democrats have --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado. He`s a member of the Senate finance committee and intelligence committee.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Senator. What do you expect to be the decisive closing issue in this campaign?\par \par \uc2 [22:30:00]\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I think President \uc2 Obama\uc2  had it right. Who`s side are you on, Lawrence? I think that we are at a moment in the country`s history when we`ve had 50 years of an economy that has worked really well for the top 10 percent and hasn`t worked well for anybody else.\par \par \uc2 And I believe we are turning the page on the Reagan era, the trickle-down economics, the supply-side economics that has created such massive income inequality, made it so hard for people in Colorado to afford housing or health care or higher education or childcare. And you know, Democrats have actually passed three important pieces of legislation turning that page. One was the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 One was bringing back the first -- industry from Southeast Asia that we brought back, the semiconductor industry, since Reagan decided to privilege people in our economy he wanted to make stuff as cheaply as possible in Southeast Asia and China over lots of other choices that we could have made.\par \par \uc2 And then an energy bill with important health care provisions that my opponent opposes but that caps drug costs for seniors at $2,000, requires Medicare to negotiate finally. Finally we overcame pharma to require Medicare to negotiate prices on behalf of the American people, insulin at $35 a month. And instead of the energy and climate policies that are going to be good on cars, are going to be good for our national security, and keeping Europe in the fight against Putin, and driving emissions down.\par \par \uc2 Those are popular bills in Colorado and my opponent has said that had he been in the Senate, he would have voted against every single one of them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, it has been difficult to track your opponent`s position on abortion. Two years ago, he apparently signed a petition trying to ban abortion in Colorado. Let`s listen to what he said in your debate about where he seems to be trying to move in your direction on this question. Let`s listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: I would support a woman`s right to choose up to and including five months. That decision should be between her and her mother. After that, for the exceptions of rape, incest, life of the mother, medical exception -- that too should be between a woman and her doctor, that decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He wants it to be between her and her mother. Ok. I guess that depends on the age of the person. What is his actual history on this subject?\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I mean he has no credibility here, Lawrence. He not only signed a petition. He voted for a referendum in Colorado to ban abortion with no exceptions for rape or incest. He said that he is opposed to Colorado`s law codifying a woman`s right to choice. I`m proud to live in a state that was one of the first states to do that.\par \par \uc2 And he says he would have voted for the three Supreme Court justices that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put on the court, who stripped us of the first fundamental constitutional freedom we have lost since Reconstruction.\par \par \uc2 So, he has no credibility at all. And now he tries to say ok, all I want to do is make sure that there is an abortion on demand for late term abortion. Then he`s talking about a universe of people who, 1 percent of abortions -- the most tragic cases of women that had planned to carry their child to term, and some devastating thing happens to their health and they have to make a decision to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And that is the point at which Joe O`Dea thinks he should be in the hospital room with that woman and her doctor or, that she ought to lawyer up or demonstrate that she was actually raped or a victim of incest.\par \par \uc2 It is -- it is -- it is -- let me say this, Lawrence -- it is a position well outside the mainstream of where Colorado is on that issue.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Colorado Senator Michael Bennet, running for reelection, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Thanks for having me, Lawrence. I really appreciate -- we`re working hard to hold on to this majority and I think we will.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Kansas shocked American politics in August when nearly a million voters turned out to vote against banning abortion in the state. Kansas` only Democratic member of the House of Representatives, \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:27]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our next guest, Democratic Representative \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , is running for reelection in Kansas. Yes, Kansas. She is the only Democratic member of the House of Representatives from Kansas. She won her seat in the big blue wave election at the midterm of Donald Trump`s presidency in 2018.\par \par \uc2 In August, Kansas voted overwhelmingly 59 to 41 to reject a constitutional amendment that would have allowed state legislators to ban abortion in the state.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:37]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: A Republican voter and resident of Kansas` 3rd Congressional District told the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", "Even if a candidate now is not promising me low taxes or financial incentive, the social issues right now are just too big," said Debra Hoffman, a 56 year old Republican and database administrator who lives in Shawnee, Kansas. This year she is planning to vote for the Democrat Representative \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Representative \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2  of Kansas. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. How important is abortion in your reelection campaign this year?\par \par \uc2 SHARICE DAVIDS\uc2  (D-KS), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Well it`s -- I mean it continues to be something that is really impacting people. I mean when Roe was overturned, you know, I came home to Kansas that day and was met with people who were upset, who were scared and having anxiety, you know.\par \par \uc2 And we are right next door to Missouri, where they passed a total ban. And we saw lives being upended. So it continues to be a really important issue even if, you know, in August, I know Kansas got a lot of attention because we were able to push back in a really resounding way against what was an extreme attempt to change our state constitution.\par \par \uc2 But you know, the next day, the anti choice groups said they were going to double down and that they would be back. And so I think that folks know that it`s just as important for us to get out and stay engaged and show up in November because our rights are on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 And I have an opponent that would absolutely support a federal ban. And you know, I think that -- I think that people recognize that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Polling indicates that in your district in Kansas that 61 percent would prefer to vote for a candidate who supports legal abortions services. Only 25 percent would vote for a candidate who wants to make that illegal.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell Democrats outside of your district about running in a place where you have to find a way to appeal to Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 DAVIDS: Well, I think one of the most important things that we can do is -- you know, I`m always making sure that people I`m here to serve. I`m here to serve the folks in the Kansas 3rd. And I`m here to serves the state of Kansas.\par \par \uc2 And you know, in August, we pulled together. There was a broad coalition of people across political ideologies and beliefs that wanted to push back against these extreme attempts for total bans, no exceptions, attempts to criminalize doctors or patients.\par \par \uc2 And I think that it is really important that folks know that we do not want the government making these very personal decisions for us, you know. And I hear that -- I do hear that from across the political spectrum, whether we are here in Johnson County or out in -- which is a suburban county -- or out in the more rural areas like Anderson or Franklin County, where I have farmers asked me where I am at before the amendment. Farmers were asking me where I was at on this, because they don`t want the government getting involved in their family and medical decisions.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congresswoman \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , thank you very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVIDS: Yes, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. And coming up, Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  will join us on the breaking news that the January 6th Committee interviewed a top Secret Service official about Donald Trump`s actions on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [22:43:45]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, NBC News reports that the January 6th Committee interviewed Secret Service press secretary Anthony Guglielmi yesterday. NBC News reports that the committee focused on Secret Service response to Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony to the committee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : The president had gotten into the vehicle with Bobby, he thought that they were going up to the Capitol. And when Bobby had relayed to him, we`re not, we don`t have the assets to do it, it`s not secure, we`re going back to the West Wing.\par \par \uc2 The president had a very strong, very angry response to that. Tony described him as being irate. The president said something to the effect of, "I am the effing president, take me up to the Capitol now." To which Bobby responded, "Sir, we have to go back to the West Wing."\par \par \uc2 The president reached up towards the front of the vehicle to grab at the steering wheel, Mr. Engel (ph) grabbed his arm and said "Sir, you need to take your hand off the steering wheel. We are going back to the West Wing. We are not going to the Capitol."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump then used his free hand to lunge towards Bobby Engel. when Mr. Ornato had recounted the story to me he motioned towards his clavicles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:49:50]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " which was the first to report the story today says quote, "The committee repeatedly on Monday asked Guglielmi to walk through how he chose to describe Tony Ornato and Bobby Engel`s accounts of events on January 6th 2021 and the agency`s rebuttal of Hutchinson`s account of the story provided by Ornato about Trump`s behavior in the heavily fortified SUV.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  of California. She`s chair of the committee on House Administration, and she`s a member of the January 6th Committee. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 What can you confirm for us about this testimony and the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting that the committee reviewed closely how the Secret Service made public comments about Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): Well let me just say, generally, as you know, we can`t discuss under our rules the details of the testimony. But let me just say generally, that a lot of what has been publicly said by the agency doesn`t square with the documents we`ve received in testimony form other sources, including not just Miss Hutchinson, but other witnesses.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, we`re methodically going to go through, and we`re going to try and square up, you know, all of the facts. We got a million documents from the -- finally from the Secret Service. It took a lot of pushing to get that done.\par \par \uc2 We`ve gone through it and we need to find out what happened. Not only to find out the truth, but also to make recommendations for what, if anything, needs to be changed so we can be safer as a country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Can you say that any doubts were cast on Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony by this new testimony?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, you will recall that Miss Hutchinson only relayed what she was told by Mr. Ornato. She didn`t say it happened, she said he told her that it happened.\par \par \uc2 Now, we know from other testimony that has been presented in public from other sources, that there was an angry exchange at least. We know that for the metropolitan police and several other witnesses.\par \par \uc2 We also know, and this has been revealed publicly that there was a plan to go to the White House, contrary to some of the public statements made by the agency.\par \par \uc2 So we just need to get to the bottom of this and find out what the truth is. And I`m not making any allegations, but we need to find out everything about it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Are you ever going to be able to find out what the truth is about Secret Service communications, text messages, and other communications on January 5th and January 6th?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, the text messages are gone, and cannot be recovered. However, we did receive, as I say, a million documents -- a little over a million documents of radio traffic, emails, team meeting recordings. So we do have a lot of documentary evidence. There was a time when the radios were interfered with.\par \par \uc2 And the agents, who generally don`t like to use text messages, had to resort to that, according to some of the documents we received. So we we`re not going to recover back, but texting was not a method primarily a method used by the Secret Service, we`ve learned.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When you say the radios were interfered with, was that a willful act? Or just a technological accident?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: I think it was technological. I don`t think it was willful.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:53:52]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, President Biden campaigned for Democrats in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Thank you. And we are under siege by our Republican friends. And by the way, this isn`t your father`s Republican Party. This is a different deal right now. There`s a lot of good Republicans out there, but they`re under a lot of pressure.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been paying social security your whole life, you earned it, now these guys want to take it away. Who in the hell do they think they are? Excuse my language.\par \par \uc2 Some of you are on social security, or your parents or your grandparents are. They earned it, they earned every single paycheck, they put money in since they are teenagers to pay for Social Security.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:47]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And those are more than government programs. They`re a promise, a promise we made as a country, to work hard and contribute, and when it comes time to retire, we`re going to be there for you, we`re going be there for the basic needs.\par \par \uc2 That`s been a rock-solid guarantee, an ironclad commitment. Generations of Americans, have counted on it and it works.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: President Biden gets tonight LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221102eib100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for November 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 8,488 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: That`s been a rock solid guarantee, an ironclad commitment. Generations, generations of Americans have counted on it. And it works.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: President Biden gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:20]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Tonight, the suspect in the attack on Paul Pelosi makes his first court appearance and we get disturbing new details on what he called a suicide mission as the vicious misinformation about the attack spreads even further.\par \par \uc2 Then exactly one week before the votes are counted, will Republican members of Congress help move the midterm needle in favor of Democrats and the critical role young people could have in his crucial election or polls missing the newest voters as the 11th hour gets underway on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. And tonight chilling new details are surfacing about the suspect accused of breaking into Nancy Pelosi`s San Francisco home, beating her husband Paul with a hammer and plotting to kidnap her and possibly break her knees.\par \par \uc2 David DePape appeared in state court for the first time since Friday`s attack to face attempted murder and other charges. Today in court filings, prosecutors revealed that he was also targeting other political leaders. They said he told police he was on a suicide mission. And they stress there is no question the attack on the Pelosi was politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BROOKE JENKINS, SAN FRANCISCO DISTRICT ATTORNEY: He specifically sought out their home he sought out the speaker and in turn when he could not locate her at her home, turned his violence towards her husband.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: DePape has pleaded not guilty. He is now being held without bail. Tonight, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting cameras at the command center for the \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  in Washington DC actually captured the breaking of the Pelosi`s home 3, 000 miles away. Here`s the problem. The Post says the video was not reviewed until later.\par \par \uc2 Despite all of that we are learning from law enforcement, conspiracy theories and falsehoods about the attack and its motives continue to spread. Many conservatives are now publicizing these baseless theories. And today, not surprisingly, the former President joined in on a podcast \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  suggested without any evidence whatsoever, there might be, quote unquote, more to the story than what officials are saying.\par \par \uc2 And now finally, lawmakers on both sides of the aisle are calling out fellow political leaders who are allowing these lies to spread.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  (R-IL): I`d like to see every Republican candidate an elected official say that you condemn this attack. That`s really basic just say you condemn the attack. But secondarily, recognizing the fact that when you convince people that an election was stolen, when you convince people that there are lizard people that drink baby`s blood, I`m not even joking. That`s actually what a lot of folks believe. Eventually you`re going to find somebody that acts on that/\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): There are noticeable things in the last 72 hours that I find deeply disturbing in the way that people are framing. What happened to Paul Pelosi we are normalizing unacceptable behavior.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Of course, check your calendar, these developments are happening exactly one week before the midterm elections. President Biden`s spend today in Florida campaigning for two Democratic hopefuls. \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , who`s running for governor against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Val Demings, who wants to take Marco Rubio Senate seat and a message tailored two Florida senior citizens. Biden warned a Republican controlled Congress could threaten government benefits.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You would pay the Social Security your whole life you earned. Now these guys want to take it away. Who in the hell do they think they are? Excuse my language. Just imagine what that would mean.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  also out on the campaign trail, the departing Republican representatives speaking out for Democratic candidates for the first time. She`s also talking about her January 6 committee`s effort to get Trump to respond to their subpoena.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): Committees and discussions with President Trump`s attorneys. This is not a situation where, you know, the committee is going to put itself at the mercy of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in terms of you know his efforts to create a circus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Hence, speaking of a circus there`s also news tonight concerning Elon Musk`s controversial takeover of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting the company is now facing an exodus of advertisers and top executives. And according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , the Treasury Department now exploring whether it has the authority to review the deal.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The paper says the terms of that agreement give major foreign investors, including Saudi Arabia access to confidential information about the company and wait for it, potentially \uc2 Twitter\uc2  users.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter tonight with the help of our leadoff panel. Alayna Treene, congressional reporter for Axios joins us. Renato Mariotti is here, former federal prosecutor and current legal affairs columnist for POLITICO magazine. And Kyle Spencer, journalist and author of the new book "Raising Them Right: The Untold Story of America`s Ultraconservative Youth Movement and its Plot for Power."\par \par \uc2 Renato, I turn to you first, let`s talk about the suspect accused of having additional targets beyond Paul Pelosi. Given that can we expect additional charges beyond what he`s facing with Paul?\par \par \uc2 RENATO MARIOTTI, FMR. FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Possibly, although I have to say he is in a world of hurt already in a difficult spot facing state and federal charges at the same time. And frankly, I think that there`s a potential that we`re going to learn about a broader body area, you know, one thing that is eerily mentioned in the federal complaint is that he was actually hoping to use Pelosi, and as a way of luring another individual and just clutches.\par \par \uc2 And so I think, you know, the evidence there could potentially suggest a broader conspiracy, and we can all speculate as to who that other target might be.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Alayna, the idea that the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  cameras caught this break in at the Pelosi home, but nobody was actually watching them is incredible. What in the world is going on?\par \par \uc2 ALAYNA TREENE, AXIOS CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Oh, it`s totally remarkable, Stephanie. And I think, no, it looks very badly on \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2 , which is already navigating a very, very tough time with what happened to Paul Pelosi and trying to assure members that they and their families are safe, and that they`re going to be putting in new and implementing new security measures to protect them.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Capitol police chief Tom Manger said today that he wants to put in place more things that ensure redundancies of protection. And this is exactly what happened with Speaker Pelosi`s house. I mean, the fact that no one was there in the capitol who was able to see these videos from that the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , very well done reporting there, that they no one saw the footage there is incredible to me.\par \par \uc2 And I think also it shows, you know, yes, when Speaker Pelosi is not in her California residents, there is less security around her residence, of course and around the speaker. And that`s, I think, why obviously, this was able to happen.\par \par \uc2 But still, I mean, the thing that I had heard from so many members, when I spoke to them in the aftermath of this attack is that this can happen to the Speaker of the House, the woman who is third in line to the presidency. It this could happen to her what could happen to us. We need more protections and as the political environment has become far more polarized, far more violent in recent years. It`s really scary. And then people are wondering whether or not there are enough protections in place to keep up with the environment as it`s continuing to change in turn more violent.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kyle, you want to talk about crime in this country? Fine. Let`s put that over here. But On what planet is it a political win for some Republicans to spread twisted lies and attack Paul Pelosi, an 82-year-old man who`s lying in a hospital bed? Who are the voters who are saying yes, amen to this.\par \par \uc2 KYLE SPENCER, AUTHOR "RAISING THEM RIGHT": It`s the voters who really hate the Democrats and who really hate Pelosi and hate commanded and hate humanity. I mean, these are people Pelosi has been a target for Republicans, particularly young Republicans, it`s hard for Democrats, particularly for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi is one of these women that they have targeted as part of the kind of evil left wing empire. And so immediately anything that happens to her if there`s an opportunity to take her down on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , they`re going to do it. And what you saw with this was an immediate takedown of this kind of yellow journalism rolled out little crumbs. You had Charlie Kirk, you had Ted Cruz, you had Dinesh D`Souza. Well, this is fishy. I don`t know what`s going on. They started a narrative right away to learn people into that this idea that there`s something very nefarious that was happening here.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Very twisted, very awful. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  also is reporting that the suspects former boss said this guy bought the MAGA all the conspiracy theories hook line and sinker, Pizzagate, stolen election, the whole thing down the line, he bought it. Are we at the point where these sick lies and conspiracy theories are just a permanent part of American politics, which by the way, are about to get supercharged on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: I think we are at a point where those who don`t want these types of lies to be spread need to figure out a better way to combat them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Which is?\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: Which is really getting out the store what really happened and not necessarily fighting all the time the lies. I mean, what happens with these lies is that they started out and the story about this guy quickly, quickly in the right wing. You know, Twittersphere became a story about somebody who was mentally ill, somebody who the Democrats want to now, you know, lock up even though they don`t want to lock anyone else.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:09]\par \par \uc2 You know, the story immediately became something that was completely fabricated and a story about the how bad Democrats were. And that`s just what`s going to happen. And if Democrats -- the problem that Democrats made in this particular instance was just start talking about these conspiracy theories right away and trying to refute them.\par \par \uc2 And I think, yes, I think the Democrats really needed in this instance to talk immediately about what we are sad, this is horrible. This is a terrible thing for our nation to counter the hate and the rage, to counter these crazy voters who are thinking I`m going to jump on this horrible bandwagon work and attack someone I don`t like in their husband just got hit by a hammer in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 You got -- I think the what the Democrats needed to do was come in there with -- this is a tragedy when one of us is down, we`re all down. When some public official or his spouse is hurt, we are all hurt. We`re a country, we`re together, we are together. We, you know, keep, keep that message. I think a lot of us spent way too much time refuting some of the conspiracy theories which got more play.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK. But we always hear the reverse In other instances, right? When Democrats ignore it, then we say, Oh, well, they really screwed up, they should attack this head on. So they`re kind of screwed either way in this.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: No, it`s -- I think they can ignore it. And they can`t attack it. They need to start their own narrative. They need to create their own narrative. I think that`s what Democrats are really struggling with, even with an instance like this. Everybody on the Democratic side needs to be -- needed to be voicing the exact same thing. This is a tragedy for our country. This is a sad thing. We are behind the speaker. We are always as Americans behind our speaker, and that wasn`t really didn`t come through as clearly as it should have. And that`s the -- it`s the strong counter narrative that needs to happen.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: People shouldn`t have to create a narrative, the truth should just be the truth. Alayna, Axios is now reporting on this new effort to track incidents of threats and harassment against government officials. What are you finding?\par \par \uc2 TREENE: Well, this report that we have is very timely, obviously, because of the aftermath of the attack on Paul Pelosi. But this is something that \uc2 Princeton University\uc2  and the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2  have been working on for several years now. They`ve been pulling together a database to track a lot of these harassment and threats toward elected officials.\par \par \uc2 And what they`re found -- what they found is that, more often than not this that the statute more than three times women are targeted more than men, women in Congress, election workers, candidates are targeted more often. They`ve also found that death threats and gun violence are the most common threats that are being made. I mean, really scary stuff here. And they`re hoping that this tracker that they`ve pulled together can start to predict some of these patterns more frequently and be something that the public uses more often moving forward.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s not a surprise, things are getting more dangerous. The question is, what are we going to do about it? Renato, let`s switch gears and talk about the Supreme Court saying \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  must testify in the Georgia investigation, is that it? Is this thing over no more appeals? This guy`s going to have to show up.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: He`s going to have to show up. Now, the question is, is he going to answer questions, or is he going to take the tip? I think that`s really interesting. I know, it`s frustrating Stephanie. But I will say, I think a lot of what this was is he`s been trying to run out the clock. He`s been trying to show Trump that he`s not doing this willingly, that he really doesn`t want to be cooperative. I think that`s what a lot of this is. I think he`s getting answered the questions. But obviously, taking the fifth, this is some questions is also possibility.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Alayna, at the January 6 Committee negotiating with Trump and his team over their subpoena. I mean, I know they`re already getting testimony from some members of the Secret Service. At this point, what is left to get, Trump isn`t going to show up, even if they get stuff from the Secret Service. What is that going to turn into? Like who is going to be held accountable? What does that look like?\par \par \uc2 TREENE: Right. Well, we are in this weird phase, Stephanie, where the committee is wrapping up its work. They`re supposed to disband by the end of the year, so they only have so much time to wrap up their investigation and file that report.\par \par \uc2 And I think the thing about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  testifying, it`s just, I find it so unlikely. I know that the committee does not want to give him a platform, a public platform for him to come in and spew lies, spread more disinformation and make it the Trump show. I think that`s a huge fear that they have.\par \par \uc2 But I also think having him testify behind closed doors and under oath, while it would be very compelling, and I think, you know, the committee could learn a lot from that, very unclear whether Donald Trump`s lawyers would do that. I don`t think it`d be a good idea for Trump`s lawyers to do that. We know that he`s misled people behind closed doors and lied under oath in the past.\par \par \uc2 And so it seems like the subpoena is becoming more and more unlikely to be fulfilled. And they really like I said, they just don`t have a lot of time left to wrap this up. I think really, the big question now is what will the Department of Justice do all of the attention is going to very quickly turn particularly after the midterm elections to the Justice Department, are they going to issue any charges, criminal charges against many of these people tied to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 [23:15:09]\par \par \uc2 Will there be any fallout and legal fallout really of this, and I think it`s going to be a very, very difficult road for attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to navigate once the committee wraps up their thing and the attention turns to the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kyle, let`s go back to talking about knocking down conspiracy theories, you know, who doesn`t like to do that? Elon Musk, he has now taken over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And we know what he likes to do. Talk to us about what this takeover has been like so far, and what we`re in for.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: So, I think that Elon Musk is hedging his bets right now. He went in there. He told all of his right wing followers and friends that what he was going to do was liberate them. And a lot of them then went on to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and said they had been liberated, that they were going to be able to say whatever they wanted on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  now.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s not so clear, that`s going to happen. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  already has a problem, right? \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has a yellow journalism problem. It is a home for people to spread disinformation, racial epithets, lots of nasty stuff. And so that is going to -- that`s going to continue to be a problem, I think.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that corporate America, it is not in their best interest to make \uc2 Twitter\uc2  a worse place than it already is. And so they are, I think, at this point, going to be the check for him. And I really don`t think that he is going to defy them because they`re going to take off. They`re going to - - they`re not going to advertise. And I think it says in some crazy instance, they may save us.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Without those advertising dollars it won`t work. Quickly Renato, the Treasury Department now potentially reviewing the deal. From a legal perspective, isn`t it too late? The deals close. It`s done. He in the sink showed up last week.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Yes, I don`t see the deal actually being undone. I think it`s interesting that they`re reviewing it. It`s possible that they might impose some additional requirement in the company, particularly given foreign ownership. But I don`t see a world in which the government at this point is going to undo Mr. Musk take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But are there conflicts of interest, especially with potential foreign investors? Sure, as heck there are. Alayna, Renato, Kyle, thank you so much for starting us off tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back. The ace election forecaster whom was perfectly predicted the 2018 blue wave is here on what she`s seeing, as both parties cram for the midterms and pull up all the stops. We`ll also talk with a popular Democrat battling a late conservative challenge.\par \par \uc2 And later, the unexpected. A week from tonight might just come from political newcomers. The pollster tracking the youth vote reveals what he`s watching for. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a busy Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:22:10]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Exactly one week from Election Day and Democrats are hoping the former president can get them across the finish line have to race and stops in Georgia, Michigan and Wisconsin. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is in Nevada tonight. He just got on the stage moments ago. Let`s listen in.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: People that they love. Maybe some people in this room while some people that they love. And the pandemic also highlighted in some cases made worse problems we`ve been struggling with for years. An economy that works real well for folks right at the top, but not so well for ordinary people. Housing -- I love you back but I`m going to make --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming after tonight, Obama will be headlining a rally in Arizona tomorrow and join President Biden in Pennsylvania over the weekend. Let`s talk about this final push the Tim Miller, contributor to The Bulwark and former communications director for Jeb Bush. He`s the author of the book "Why We Did It," and political strategist and ace election forecaster Rachel Bitecofer. She almost perfectly predicted the 2018 blue wave. And her upcoming book is "Hidden Where It Hurts: How to Save Democracy by Beating Republicans at Their Own Game."\par \par \uc2 Rachel, let`s start with your data. What are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL BITECOFER, POLITICAL STRATEGIST: Yes, so we`re really talking about midterm fundamentals. Here, the Democrats are the in party. The party is defined by who controls the presidency. And so they`ve always been up against a real strong headwind. And that was before inflation became an issue from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But what we`re really looking at is Democrats in a surprisingly strong position, a lot of that has to do with Roe. I think Democrats could have made the strategic push that they`ve made and not be where they are without that Roe of inflaming their own coalition.\par \par \uc2 So now that we have energy on both sides, we`re going to see an unprecedented midterm here. We`re going to see two really enthusiastic bases, clashing collide, and then we`re going to see who wins this conversion pool argument, will it be crime and inflation? Or will it be democracy and freedom?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And that`s where the middle sits?\par \par \uc2 BITECOFER: So we`re going to find out. One thing that`s really nice this time is at least in some of these contests for them democratic strategy, they`ve made it really a very clear referendum choice, right? So they`re not just trying to tell voters who done this and that for you, which is an important component of politics.\par \par \uc2 But what they`re really doing is painting a picture of change. Midterm effects are powered by the status quo bias. When you`re in power, you get blamed for everything, whether you did or not like inflation and gas. And you know, in this case, what is happening is we`re seeing that midterm bias kind of mitigated both in the in the conversion pool and on the base.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:00]\par \par \uc2 As and we don`t know really what`s going to happen. This is the first election that I`ve watched in a long time I felt so uncertain about and we know the polling has just been really difficult the last few cycles, and it`s because we`re living through unprecedented times, it`s really important to recognize that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tim, why does it have to be though a referendum on either or, right, Roe and democracy or inflation and crime? Democrats have lots of strong arguments to make on both crime and inflation. Why can`t it be both? Why can`t they say we`ve got all of this covered?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: Well, two things one, I think is Rachel pointed out correctly. Some of this is just the natural gravity of politics. Look, we obsess about what`s happening in America. But if you look around the world, you have far right party in Italy, and a far left party in the UK, both doing well by making the same arguments that the Republican Party is here, right. I just -- voters get frustrated when things when they`re -- things like inflation, we talked about how annoying that is in past episodes, Steph. So I think that`s part of it, the Democrats are up against.\par \par \uc2 But then I think that there`s another part where to Rachel`s point, they`ve made strong arguments on Roe. I`m not sure how strong of arguments the Democrats have made on crime and inflation. And I think if you look back at this, and they have a bad night next Tuesday, that is going to be the thing that they have regrets over.\par \par \uc2 I think you could go on offense against Republicans on crime. I have an article in The Bulwark on this tomorrow. The most popular position on crime is more cops on the street and fewer guns on the street. That`s Joe Biden`s position. The Democrats are letting the Republicans define this debate on crime instead of going on offense about how it`s the Republicans who want to have more guns on the street and how it`s not and how \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has funded more police for local communities. That`s gotten lost a bit.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that, you know, there`s a week to still make that case and President Obama has been making it on the stump, but I think there`s been a bit of a miss there for some of the Democratic campaign.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let me ask you about two people on the stump \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  campaigning for Democrats. Tim, this question is for you. Who are the voters they`re targeting?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Suburban swing voters, i former Republican voters I like to call Red Dog Democrats. They voted for \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , probably in 2012, maybe 2016. They sat it out or voted for Evan McMullen or \uc2 Gary Johnson\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So it`s you. You`re their target audience.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, but there are a lot of people -- there a lot of means it`s actually my friends, mostly political strategists, my friends and their wives, you know, from Colorado and Arizona, outside of Phoenix, outside Denver, outside Atlanta, you know, these people made that change. And I think that some of them are at risk of sliding back because they`re unhappy with the economy. And some of them are unhappy with what`s happening around crime.\par \par \uc2 And so hearing a Republican, somebody they trust, make the case to them and say, no, it`s actually Kari Lake and Blake Masters that are too dangerous. And you have to hold the line and vote Democrat at least one more election, maybe two more if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs again in 2024.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think those voters do matter. It`s not a huge part of the electorate, but it could be 2, 3 percent and that can be decisive, particularly in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Take us to this blue wall, Rachel. You`re watching these statewide races Michigan, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, what`s at stake?\par \par \uc2 BITECOFER: Yes, so lots at stake. And you know, when we talk about securing democracy, because, you know, Republicans are making no, you know, bones about it, they`re going to come after election administration, that leaves the blue wall, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, especially those governor races and