particularly Pennsylvania, in part because Republicans made a law that doesn`t allow you to count early votes before election day. And so I think those are the two states to look at. And there`s a good reason to be concerned about what could happen in the week that follows Election Day.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For those of you watching, tell your neighbors, tell your friends, it could likely take days especially in those states where they cannot count early votes. That`s the way it works. Tim, Rachel, thank you both for being here. You always make us smarter.\par \par \uc2 When we come back. She`s gone from her whiteboard warnings to knocking on doors. California Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  on the Democrats final days before the midterms when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:22]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: California Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  is in an extremely close race for reelection. The Cook Political Report has moved her district from leaning D to a toss-up. Now, Orange County has deep Republican roots dating all the way back to the Reagan era. \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  flipped traditionally red area to blue back in 2018. And it is now at risk of flipping back.\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight, Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 . Katie talked to us, you have gone from big television ads to you`re going street to street knocking on doors. What are people telling you?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2  (D-CA): People are talking a lot about wanting to have an economy that works for them. They`re very concerned that if Republicans take power, we`ll go back to what we`ve seen and what Republicans have told us they`ll do, which is an economy that works for global corporations, that giving away subsidies to special interests that`s giving tax breaks to huge corporations, instead of doing what Democrats have done, which is move us toward an economy that puts workers and puts families first.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You talk about this all the time. Do you think it hasn`t resonated with enough voters? Why do you think your race is suddenly getting so close?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, look, I think this is always has been a competitive race. I have a lot of new voters to introduce myself to following redistricting. And we have equal numbers of Democrats and Republicans in this district. So we`ve always expected it to be a tough race.\par \par \uc2 But I also have a track record of standing up to corporate power, standing up to big pharma, who`s cheating us with prescription costs, making Medicare costs more, standing up to monopolies that are driving up inflation with their price gouging, standing up to big oil and their greed at the pump. So I think people understand that I am a fighter, and they know who I fight for. I fight for families.\par \par \uc2 And I think the contrast here could not be more clear with my opponent, \uc2 Scott Baugh\uc2 , who is a professional lobbyist, and who himself has been involved in multiple campaign elections scandals, one of which resulted in four felony indictments.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Four felony indictments, and yet the polls are getting closer. You just laid it out. Democrats have done a lot with this very complicated economy. But here`s the thing, and it has to be very frustrating for you. Republicans win on messaging around the economy without offering any real solutions. Why are you one of the only Democrats that actively talks about the economy and explains all of your successes?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, look, I talk about it all the time. And I invite everyone to listen, and to repeat this messaging into every pocket and part of this country, because there is no voter not urban, not rural, independent, Republican, no voter who wants a weak economy. What they want is a strong, stable, globally competitive economy. And that`s what democratic policies have done.\par \par \uc2 They have been about standing up to monopoly power. They have been about making sure that we`re putting money in worker`s pockets and paychecks. But making sure that we`re cracking down on things that are unfair, like the inability for us to negotiate drug prices, that`s been the Democratic agenda. It is working and workers are going to continue to see the benefits if they keep us in power. If they don`t, it`ll be right back to Republican giveaways for special interests and for big corporations. Count on it, because they`ve said that`s exactly what they`ll do.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Your messaging is practical and crystal clear. Then I have to ask you, why is it that Republicans outspend by a multiple Democrats when it comes to messaging around the economy. If you the winning message why don`t more Democrats use it?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, we`re certainly using this message for Orange County, both on TV and mail. And when I`m talking to voters that doors. Well, tell you Republican super PAC spending in my race is approaching $9 million. That has not been answered with Democratic spending, which is down around $300,000. That is a huge gap in spending in an investment in this race. So I think we`re seeing some of the results of that.\par \par \uc2 But look, I mean, people need to understand \uc2 Scott Baugh\uc2  is good at crime. He has four felony indictments. He has 18 misdemeanors. This is somebody who`s going to put more weapons on our streets. This is somebody who`s not going to stand up to special interest because he spent his whole career working for them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow. Before we go, I do want to ask you about the attack on Paul Pelosi. \uc2 The Capitol Police\uc2  Chief is now calling for more resources to protect members of Congress. Katie, you`re a mother of three kids. I know you are passionate about your work. You`re proud to do it. Are you nervous about your safety?\par \par \uc2 [23:40:04]\par \par \uc2 PORTER: I think we all have to be worried about the increase in political violence and it hurts everybody. Most of all, it hurts everyday Americans who want to make their voices heard in our democracy. It discourages people from participating in town halls or coming to political events, getting engaged in campaigns door knocking, because they`re worried about the rise of political violence.\par \par \uc2 Violence is a crime, period, full stop. It is not a weapon of political disagreement. And Democrats understand that. And what we`ve seen is the extreme Republicans, folks like \uc2 Scott Baugh\uc2  and Stephen Miller, he was just on the air with Stephen Miller. These are folks who have been willing to take extreme strategies and encourage violence to try to win when they couldn`t win by simply winning the hearts and minds of the American people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What is your closing mind to the -- your closing message to the American people with one week to go?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: My closing message to them is they deserve an economy that works for them. And that is what I promised to fight for. And that is what I have done, whether it`s standing up to big oil companies, standing up to big pharma, standing up to corporate monopolies, standing up to big banks or Wall Street, including standing up to my own party when they have not done right by voters, not doing things like banning congressional stock trading. I am on the side of the American people. And that`s where I`m going to stay and they can count on it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, you can always come over here if you want to talk about congressional stock trading ban, because you know I have time for that. Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , thank you for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, the TikTok. They`ve got Spinsters (ph) but are they answering pollster`s calls? I`ve got a feeling no but we`re going to take a close look at the youth vote and if it`s being accurately counted in the polls with just one week to go when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:24]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: According to the Harvard youth poll, 40 percent of 18 to 29-year-old say they will definitely vote in the midterms that is on track to possibly exceed the record breaking youth turnout in the 2018 midterms. The poll also finds 73 percent of young Americans believe the rights of others are under attack and 59 percent believe their own rights are.\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight to discuss the man who knows best John Della Volpe, Director of Polling at the \uc2 Harvard Kennedy School\uc2  and author of fight -- how -- "Fight: How Gen Z is Channeling Their Fear and Passion to Save America."\par \par \uc2 John, you`re the number one person I want to ask this all these young people registering to vote. Do we even know today answer pollsters calls because they don`t answer our calls.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DELLA VOLPE, \uc2 HARVARD KENNEDY SCHOOL\uc2 , DIRECTOR OF POLLING: Right. It`s tough to get a response back to texts. But Stephanie, we`ve been at this now for 22 years. And every semester, we conduct a couple of 1,000 in depth interviews with young people across the country in a very robust way. Most polls that we`re seeing in the news and the aggregation sites might do a couple 100 at most. We`re doing a couple 1,000, 4,000 or 5000 a year.\par \par \uc2 And what we found, which is different than the better part of the last four years, when Baby Boomers and Genxers and Millennials were eligible to vote midterm elections, roughly four and five stay home.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I believe that.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: On a good year.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I was not very responsible about voting when I was in college.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: On a good year. In 2000 -- in 2014, 16, or 17 percent turned out. The difference is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comes into office, Parkland students inspire generation, Gen Z votes for the first time at record setting levels twice as many participated, then in the last 40 years. The same wave continued in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The only way within polling to identify this kind of precise level of interest is these large robust academic like surveys. And that`s why we`re pretty confident that we`re going to see this. I`m not sure if we see a red wave. I`m not sure we see a blue wave. I`m confident we`ll see a Gen Z wave next week.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A Gen Z wave but will that wave skew Democrat how it traditionally does. When you said they`re motivated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning in Parkland To me that`s Democrat, Democrat?\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Yes. And in 2018, for perspective, seven out of 10 voted Democrat. So 2018, 10 seats went from red to blue, because of the increased turnout and the increased partisanship, which was relatively new in terms of that margin. We`re tracking perhaps not 70 percent, but our poll showed that in the high 50s, 57 percent of young likely voters preferred Democrats. That`s up five points since the spring. There`s still some undecided (ph).\par \par \uc2 The thing about young voters, which is different than a lot of the other subgroups is you have young voters in Pennsylvania. We know where they are. We have young voters in Georgia, you have young voters everywhere. It`s not the case with a lot of the other subgroups that were focused on.\par \par \uc2 So, in many ways, this country`s divided 50-50 yes. But it`s really better to think about this country`s being divided by based upon your age, if you`re under 45, two-thirds of voting Democrat.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What`s driving them to the polls? Is it abortion and if it is abortion is that men and women or just women?\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Well, you talked about -- well, men and women are just as likely if you`re in a battleground state you`re more likely than everyone else to turn out to participate by the way.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But what issue is pushing young voters?\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: I think this, two things, right, one is for the -- for conservative conservatives and Republicans and men clearly inflation, cost of living in the economy just like everybody else. That`s clear.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:08]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Young ones.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Young ones. OK. But there`s the second, third, fourth and fifth most important issues, our Democratic leaning issues, they include abortion, they include protect our democracy, climate change, as well as gun violence prevention and gun control.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Biden`s moved to forgive student debt was meant to help get out that young vote, isn`t working?\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Well, it`s hard to say one effort necessarily is driving this. But I think not all partisanship is negative, right? There`s positive partnership as well. What I mean by that is young people vote when they see the tangible difference that their participation can make. Young voters vote in 2020 for three or four things, one of which was to relieve student debt $10,000, the President delivered that. So without question that brings more faith in this --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, he delivered it.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are they saying like, Yes, thanks onto the next or are they saying you delivered it? Now I`m going to show up and vote, like did it have an impact that will push them to the polls?\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: This generation sees the distinct difference that their participation can make. It`s different than other generations. So I think the combination, again, it`s hard to disaggregate these things, but the combination of like the significant fear, fear not just for themselves, but fear for other members of the community. We talked about this. You talked about this in the Open (ph), seven out of 10 people feel like their rights -- the rights of other Americans are under attack, that`s clearly driving people as well. So it`s not only themselves but they`re voting to protect other people more vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But that goes back to my abortion question, is it only women that are motivated by the overturning of Roe versus Wade are men too, because last I checked, men are 100 percent responsible for unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: No disagreement here. Clearly, it`s -- the data shows that`s a more important driver for women. I`d be lying if I said otherwise. However, there`s a 15-point gap in the difference between how many people -- how many men think they`re under attack versus others. So men see this. I`m not saying it`s a significant driver that is for women, specifically college women on campuses.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re out of time, but fair to say you`re predicting a Gen Z voter turnout wave next week.\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: Yes, we`re beginning to see that already.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A Gen Z voter turnout wave and many of those people are probably not accounted for in all the polls we hear about?\par \par \uc2 DELLA VOLPE: It`s a very difficult subgroup to reach. I think they`re not accounted for.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Not accounted for and they`re coming out in droves. You heard it here. John Della Volpe, thank you so much. Coming up. The very personal message from our friend Politics Girl tonight when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:56:51]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN, ACTRESS AND ACTIVIST: I think we`re forgetting that that abortion and this kind of access is an economic issue. Sure. It`s a huge economic issue. It`s just not a front and center in the moment economic issue. It`s not --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is for a women who`s -- who has an unwanted pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 TAMBLYN: It is --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, abortion and the economy. You heard it during our Friday night cap last week, access to abortion is an economic issue. Our friend Leigh McGowan, aka PoliticsGirl understands this connection in a very personal way. She shares her story and takes a deep dive into the issue in her new video. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEIGH MCGOWAN, POLITICSGIRL PODCAST: ABC just released a poll that said although the majority of Americans agree with Democrats on abortion, the number one issue is the economy. So they`re going to vote on that. And I just have to say that I am so deeply sick and tired of this framing. It`s not abortion or the economy. Abortion is part of the economy.\par \par \uc2 What do you think happens to the economy when women are forced to carry babies they can`t afford or aren`t ready for? What do you think happens to the economy when women have to drop out of school or leave their jobs to care for a child? You know, what`s the kitchen table issue? How many people are sitting at your kitchen table?\par \par \uc2 We live in a country where it can cost over $20,000 just to have a baby. We have no paid family leave, no affordable childcare. We are constantly defunding public schools. We don`t have affordable health care or college, adding to your family is 100 percent an economic issue in this country. I only have one child. I got very sick after he was born. And I couldn`t have any more. I have to take birth control because with my disease that pregnancy would kill me.\par \par \uc2 But when my son was very young, a good friend of ours offered to surrogate for us. She knew we wanted to have more children, and she wanted to help us and my husband and I sat down and we had a really serious discussion about it. And what we concluded was that we couldn`t afford to have another child.\par \par \uc2 Being very sick in America is extraordinarily expensive. We live in Los Angeles, which is astronomically expensive. We were both freelance workers who didn`t have a set salary or job security. We loved being parents. But we knew that adding to our family would ultimately take away from what we`d be able to give the child we already had.\par \par \uc2 So we said no, it broke our hearts. But we said no. And it was the right decision for our family. We have struggled over the years. I have spent months at a time in the hospital fighting to stay alive. Life is difficult enough. And it would have been irresponsible to add another child to the mix.\par \par \uc2 Family Planning is a personal issue. It`s a humanity issue. But never forget that it is an economic issue. If you`re voting on the economy, then vote for the party that allows Americans to decide what is best for their own lives. A party that keeps women in the workplace if that is where they want to be. The party that is working to keep costs manageable for families on a budget. The party that voted to raise the minimum wage and tax major corporations to lower prescription drug prices. The party that wants to shore up social security not take it away.\par \par \uc2 It`s not abortion or the economy. Choosing to bring a child into the world is an economic issue. So stop separating them because they are inextricably linked.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [00:00:00]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They are indeed inextricably linked. It is abortion and the economy. And on that very serious note, I wish you a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221102eib100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 31, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Michael Steele \par \uc2 8,530 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: I hope to see you all there. I will not be in costume. This is as much as I`m getting for Halloween. This is all you get. I hope you guys were paying attention to the lipstick changes. Thank you, guys, for watching. ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE JENKINS, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, SAN FRANCISCO: It appears as though this was based on his statements that this wasn`t politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Pelosi attacker reportedly confesses.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC HOST: He was going to hold Nancy hostage and talk to her and if she lied he was going to break her kneecaps.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the explicitly political motive, the Pelosi attack, in the suspect`s own words. And the nonstop lies and conspiracy theories from the right.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-AZ) GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: She`s in D.C. Apparently, her house doesn`t have a lot of protection.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, can you support an insurrection and get elected Governor of New York? New alarm over the Republican candidate there.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what may be promising new polling numbers for Democrats in the Senate. And it made a record high profits for fossil fuel companies, the new White House effort to lower the cost of gas.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Give me a break. Enough isn`t enough. It`s time for these companies to stop for profiteering.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Tonight, Paul Pelosi, husband of the Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  remains in the intensive care unit at a San Francisco hospital. He is recovering from surgery to repair a skull fracture as well as other serious injuries he suffered when he was violently assaulted in his own home in the middle of the night by a man who was looking for the speaker.\par \par \uc2 And today, we now know exactly what his assailant wanted to do when he broke into the Pelosi residence early Friday morning. He intended to take Speaker Pelosi hostage, interrogate her, and if she did not tell him what he wanted to hear, assault her. This new information comes from a criminal complaint filed in federal court today, bringing charges against 42-year- old David DePape of assault on the immediately family member of a federal official and attempted kidnapping of a federal official.\par \par \uc2 The complaint also includes revelations from an interview DePape gave to police officers describing his intentions in his own words. He told the officers that he was going to hold Nancy hostage and talk to her. If Nancy were to tell Depop the truth, you would let her go. And if she lied, he was going to break her kneecaps. Pelosi is attacker was certain that Nancy would not have told the truth.\par \par \uc2 In the course of the interview, he articulated that he viewed Nancy as the leader of the pack of lies told by the Democratic Party. He later explained to officers that by breaking Nancy`s kneecaps, she would then have to be wheeled into Congress, which would show other members of Congress there were consequences to their actions.\par \par \uc2 DePape also explained generally, he wanted to use Nancy to lure another individual to him. Later on the interview, he explained that he did not leave the house after Paul Pelosi managed to call 911 because much like the American founding fathers with the British, he was fighting against tyranny without the option of surrender.\par \par \uc2 We should note, DePape brought zip ties, tape, rope, at least one hammer with him into Pelosi home that morning. And he subscribed to a series of dangerous paranoid delusions that have become incredibly mainstreamed in the modern MAGA, right? You read a blog touching on almost all manner of modern conspiracy thinking aliens, Jewish people, communism, vaccines, voter fraud, and many other topics.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon, the San Francisco District Attorney also announced the state charges being brought against DePape which include attempted murder, assault with a deadly weapon, and threats to a public official and their family. District Attorney Brooke Jenkins also confirmed the motive of the attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can you definitively say now this was obviously politically motivated?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Yes, it appears as though this was based on his statements and comments that were made in that house during his encounter with Mr. Pelosi that this was politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, we don`t have a full picture of this individual what may or may not have been going on inside their head but it`s quite clear what this was regardless, right? This is a man who hates \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , hates what she stands for and the political coalition she represents, who wanted to attack her because of it according to him.\par \par \uc2 He was driven by vile false conspiracies promoted and adopted by influential right-wing figures. Most prominent among them, of course, is the leader of the American right \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who finally commented on the attack today, telling a right-wing Spanish language network, the attack was "A terrible thing, but claimed it was just an example of crime in democratically-led cities."\par \par \uc2 Look at what`s happened in San Francisco generally, he said. I think we have to give the police back their authority and their power and the respect because this country is out of control. Faced with his obvious example of political violence, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and many of his fellow Republicans wave their hands, spread even more conspiracy theories, truly insidious ones.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:02]\par \par \uc2 But again, the plain basic facts, the basic facts of the matter are staring us in the face. This was plainly a political attack on a political figure based on her political views. This is now also the second time in a row that we`ve seen a grisly example of political violence in the run up to the Midterm Elections directed at Democrats or people perceived to be allied with them.\par \par \uc2 Remember, the last Midterms was 2018. And just a few weeks before Election Day in that election, a far-right conspiracy theorist named Cesar Sayoc mailed more than a dozen bombs to liberal figures and members of the media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: A fingerprint cracked the case leading to today`s dramatic arrest of the prime suspect in the mailing of at least 14 pipe bombs that targeted prominent critics of President Trump, including two former presidents. Today, just hours after the discovery of for more suspicious devices, officials confirmed the arrest of a 56-year-old Florida man who they say was behind the mailings.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And to be clear, these were actual bombs filled with explosive powder. Thankfully, they lacked a triggering device. They didn`t detonate. Cesar Sayoc was not just some crazy guy. He was a Trump superfan posting pro-Trump messages on social media, tempting rallies, wearing a MAGA hat. His van was covered in images of the ex-president, as well as a kind of enemies list, a picture of Democrats including \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  with targets over them.\par \par \uc2 Cesar Sayoc`s intent was clear as day. And people barely even remember that this happened. Just four years later, we are again nearing a Midterm Election seeing another example of political violence along very similar lines with the nearly identical target. And of course, in the intervening years between 2018 and 2022, we saw the largest act of organized political violence in this country in recent memory, the mass violence undertaken by the mob on January 6 urged on by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself. The ex-president is still defending himself and his mob and vowing to pardon his supporters. It really makes you wonder the man who wanted to assassinate the Speaker of the House in her own home and violently attacked Paul Pelosi thinks there may be a pardon in his future.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by someone who has also been the target of politically motivated violent threats. On Friday, a Pennsylvania man pleaded guilty to making threats to kill Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  of California. And Congressman Swalwell joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, I want to talk about that case, but first, just your reaction to hearing again, according to the federal complaint, what appears to be the words of the suspect himself explaining what his own motivation was for what he did?\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): Well, Chris, Paul Pelosi, thankfully, is lucky to be alive right now. This was an assassination attempt on his wife, and it looks like the assailant was ready to harm anyone who stood in his way of getting to Speaker Pelosi. This feels tragically inevitable after all the threats that Speaker Pelosi has faced, the demonization that she`s had aimed at her by Republicans for decades that, of course, it would end this way.\par \par \uc2 Now, hopefully, going forward, we recognize that this cannot be the cost of being in politics in America. But my fear is that if we don`t get this right, this will not be something that shocks the conscience but it`ll just be the cost of being an enemy of the mega state. And that`s why it`s so important that Republican leaders across the board, join Democrats in condemning this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We saw Kevin McCarthy and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and others come out and condemn it and condemn it unequivocally very quickly. But then we also saw the evolution of among prominent figures in the right, the ex-president`s sons sort of joking about it, making this like, snide joke in reference to this completely ludicrous invented fabrication about what happened, which is completely belied by the facts.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s this sort of like, yes, the most prominent people establishment figures saying no, this is -- you know, saying the right things, and then around that a lot of wink, wink, nudge, nudge and joking. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Well, I think that if Kevin McCarthy is going to denounce it -- and actually, I don`t think he`s really denounced it too publicly. Nothing on his, you know, social media platforms which have the widest audience. But it`s not just denouncing the violence, whereas Kevin McCarthy to denounce the president`s son and what he is doing to mock the violence, because the president`s son has a large megaphone in this?\par \par \uc2 Where`s Kevin McCarthy, you know, to denounce what Elon Musk has suggested. And McCarthy and Musk are friends. Musk attended a recent retreat of Kevin McCarthy`s. And so, it really takes going that extra mile. Because when you don`t do that, you just give a green light or a permissive lane, you know, for you know, this sort of glorification of violence to continue in.\par \par \uc2 And, Chris, if there`s any doubt that the leaders of this country have weighed in whether they condemn or condone, go back to January 6. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  incited and flamed and aimed at mob at the Capitol, but when you saw \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say it`s time to go home to a tee, people on site interviewed by journalist said, we`re leaving because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said it`s time to go home.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:30]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And there`s also to your point, it seems to me, different forms of condemnation. You can say quite explicitly, not only do we condemn this, if you want-- if you contemplate this, or advocate this or think this is good idea, we don`t want you as part of our movement. I mean, there are ways to be as explicit and denunciatory as possible that again, I think, to your point about getting this right -- let me ask you this, when you say get this right, and then I want to turn to this individual who pleaded guilty.\par \par \uc2 You know, when he`s talking about demonizing the Speaker of the House, it does seem to me that it`s a fine line, right? The opposition party is going to, at some level, demonize members of the other party. You know, some could say that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has been demonized by Democrats and on and on. Like, what specifically is that line for you as a participant in politics who understands that isn`t beanbag, and people aren`t going to be very critical of other folks?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Chris, I get fired up. And if you were to go through my \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed, he`d say, well, he feels very strongly about some of these things. But the line to me is violence. And the difference is you`ve seen consistently from Boebert, to \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , to \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , they are calling for and glorifying violence against their political opponents. And I think that`s what this upcoming election is about.\par \par \uc2 Will we be a country that decides its future through violence or well we decided through voting? And one side seems more comfortable with violence than they do with voting right now. And I think that`s on the line in eight days.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The individual who pleaded guilty to issuing death threats towards you -- his name was Joshua Hall. During the calls, he claimed he had AR-15 rifle so that you wanted to shoot the Congressman. He intended to come to Swalwell`s office with firearms, that if he saw the congressman, he would kill him per the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Do you view this as part of the job? Do you view this as like, particularly unnerving? Do you view this as something that`s new to politics? How do you view it?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Yes, it`s unnerving, Chris. You know, I`ve got three kids five and under. And just taking them trick or treating earlier this evening, you know, was a little unnerving. It`s not going to shake me. I`m not going to change, you know, what I say or how I speak out. But the people around us, they sit on the axe. They`re the ones who are at the House all the time, your staffer at the office all the time. I`m the one on the move. It`s much harder to find me than it is to find your family. So, of course, you worry about what it could mean for your family.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I just believe the antidote to this is unity among Democrats and Republicans to condemn violence every time it happens, and also to condemn those who glorify the violence that is taking place.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I want to ask about a detail here because it does seem to me that we know this individual. Again, he has this blog and it`s sort of -- I think, the NBC article about it sort of captured it. Well, like, you know, aliens, communism, antivax, January 6, just sort of whatever paranoid conspiracy is in the -- in the ether he seemed to sort of cottoned on to. But in this report from the San Francisco Police Department, the criminal complaint that the SFPD recovered zip ties and Pelosi, his bedroom, in the hallway, and the front door to the closer residents. In addition, law enforcement searched DePape`s backpack. Found, among other things, a roll of tape, white rope, one hammer, one pair of rubber and cloth gloves, and a journal.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, the zip ties to me echo January 6 as did this idea of him invoking being like as the revolutionaries were under British tyranny.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Yes, he went there to kill. And speaking of that revolutionary talk, we heard that from Ted Cruz, we heard that from \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  leading up to January 6. Most Americans, if you go to their \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page, they`ve got pictures of their family, their dogs, their favorite sports teams, maybe hunting with their kids. This guy had conspiracy theories about January 6, COVID, and other nonsense that looks just like the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  pages of Ted Cruz, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And the problem is when they conduct themselves by espousing violent rhetoric, people like this guy take it is the gospel and they carry out the violence.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: My pleasure. Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As we noted, Paul Pelosi is still in the hospital tonight recovering from his horrific injuries. But that hasn`t stopped some truly appalling reaction from the right-wing mainstream and a rebuke from the former president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This habit of saying the worst about other people, demonizing people, that creates a dangerous climate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That dangerous climate boosted by many conservative politicians and the richest man in the world next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just hours after the attack on the husband of the Speaker of the House by a suspect to, by his own account, according to federal court filings, believed in a bunch of right-wing conspiracy theories, the usual suspects are already spreading spin, misinformation, and cover for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, CORRESPONDENT, FOX BUSINESS: If we have a situation where this is politically motivated, you have to step back and say, why is our country so divided? President Biden, you got up to the podium and said you were going to bring us together.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:09]\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, HOST, FOX NEWS: People may be quick to make leaps to certain events in the past, certain political ideologies, what may have driven this person, we can`t make those leaps. And we shouldn`t make those leaps because we just don`t know at this point that this person could have been motivated by just about anything. We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We do not know all the information. I mean, we obviously hope Paul Pelosi is OK. That`s the bottom line of the whole thing. But this doesn`t add up. Something doesn`t make sense.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`ll take a while to figure it out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, so there`s a few things going on there, right. You can say, well, we don`t have the information when it`s early enough, fine. That doesn`t add up is a little questionable. What do you mean by that? But then that doesn`t add up very quickly becomes and became is completely disgusting responses to the attack on Paul Pelosi. Some of which were retweeted by sitting United States Senator and podcaster Ted Cruz, some were said out loud by Arizona`s Republican gubernatorial nominee Kari Lake to get a big laugh on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAKE: It is not impossible to protect our kids at school. They act like it is. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , well, she`s got protection when she`s in D.C. Apparently, her house doesn`t have a lot of protection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The guy interviewing her had to cover his face with the notes because he thought that was so funny. Meanwhile, if you`re worried about the worst kind of misinformation on twitter.com under new management, Elon Musk is doing great so far because the new owner is spreading this stuff himself. Elon Musk tweeted to more than 100 million people a completely fabricated, unhinged conspiracy lie about Paul Pelosi source from a bottom- of-the-barrel website already, infamous for its QAnon-level content.\par \par \uc2 Now, he later deleted the tweet but you know, when 100 million people may have seen a thing, it`s a bit late for take backs. So, people like Donald Jun Trump Jr. feel unable to get on the action making posts like this, snorting with delight the man broke the home to violently assault the Speaker of the House of Representatives. And \uc2 Clay Higgins\uc2 , a Republican congressman from Louisiana who shared them deleted a similar meme.\par \par \uc2 Again, this kind of delight in other people falling prey to violence and the conspiratorial worldview that twist and distorts any development to its own ends, it`s very much the mainstream of microworld right now.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Weill is a reporter at The Daily Beast, co-host the Fever Dreams Podcast, which covers right-wing groups, movements, and conspiracy theories. She`s also author the book Off The Edge: Flat Earthers Conspiracy Culture And Why People Will Believe Anything. And to that point, I have to say, Kelly, I think of myself as not easy to shock and yet, Elon Musk tweeting a link to a again, entirely fabricated, despicable lie about the events that he knew his assailant, they had had some relationship. I was shocked by that A, and B, shocked by how quickly that just became the kind of like standard story for a huge who`s who have conservative.\par \par \uc2 KELLY WEILL, REPORTER, THE DAILY BEAST: You know, it should be shocking, but we`ve seen this behavior played out over years now on the right-wing of the Republican Party. And certainly, I think we have to include Elon Musk there. He`s someone who`s been an edge lord on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  for years. He delights in hosting things that stir the pot, and things that are not necessarily grounded in reality.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, what would have been years ago a very fringe website with a very limited readership now reaches hundreds of millions of people. You know, Elon tweeted that link. It was a frankly, homophobic article. It was completely baseless. But it received so much traffic after that tweet from Elon that the site crash. That`s the kind of media ecosystem we`re in right now where these people have massive megaphones and they can really use them to build serious effect.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s also sort of a perfect kind of synthesis here between this individual who clearly had kind of fallen down a rabbit hole of kind of paranoid conspiracy theories. Every single one of them, you know, as we said, referenced and then == and then precisely the kind of thing he would subscribe to popping up and advocated by folks in the right in the immediate aftermath of him being driven by the same conspiracy theories to this violent action.\par \par \uc2 WEILL: Yes, you know, I think it`s really striking Chris. There`s this cycle really of Republicans instigating this kind of language winking and nodding at the fringes of their party, and then not taking any responsibility when people inevitably act on those claims. You know, if you truly believe the QAnon claims that people are drinking baby`s blood in a basement, I think it`s very natural to want to take action on that.\par \par \uc2 Of course, however, this is an easily debunked claim and Republicans have a responsibility to say that`s not true. They have a responsibility to safeguard their peers against these violent and bloodthirsty claims. But that`s not what was happening. And when people again, do act on those claims, Republicans will either say, I`m not really any of arguing or really laugh at it, encourage the next guy.\par \par \uc2 [2:25:09]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, laugh at it. I mean, that`s the other thing I found. You know, that Kari Lake moment I thought was so revealing. You can see it in her sort of deadpan. You could see it in the crowd`s reaction. Like, again, they`re laughing at an 82-year-old man being slugged with a hammer in his head so hard that his skull fractured, and he needed emergency surgery and is currently in the ICU. That`s the joke. Like, there`s no joke other than that.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re laughing at that, what you`re -- what you`re doing is you`re sort of, it seems to me, kind of collaborating with the audience in producing a sense that this kind of thing is OK.\par \par \uc2 WEILL: Absolutely. And I think Republicans sort of jumped the shark nearly two years ago now when they failed to hold most people accountable for the January 6 riot. You know, there were a couple of moments after the fact where it did seem like some Republicans were going to get on board with widespread condemnation of this attack. And unfortunately, that hasn`t held out. We`ve seen Republicans either brush off the event or honestly treat these defendants as martyrs.\par \par \uc2 We have folks like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  talking about January 6 defendants as political prisoners. And these are again, people who broke into the Capitol, attempted to attack their peers. And the valorization of this movement is really, I think, quite worrying. I think we`re going to see more of these kinds of incidents.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And also, I think this defense, the sort of half defense, half trolling, oh, well, it`s just trolling, it`s just trolling. I mean, here`s Greg Gutfeld on Fox doing exactly that in defending the Musk tweet again along the same lines. Like, it doesn`t matter because it`s just trolling. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CONTRIBUTOR, FOX NEWS: Elon Musk replied, I believe, to a Hillary Clinton tweet about this promoting complete lies about what had happened --\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, HOST, FOX NEWS: So did Hillary. That was the joke. That was his joke. He was trolling her.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: No, no, he --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He was trolling her because she came out with their own conspiracy that this was obviously a right-wing -- a right-wing conspiracy:\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, the guy -- excuse me, Elon Musk is the CEO of that company. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , you can say whatever you want about her. It has nothing to do with this.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He wasn`t -- he was responding to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . He was trolling her.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He was spreading lies.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, CONTRIBUTOR, FOX NEWS: Actually --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He was trolling her then -- I mean, this sort of I think is -- gets at the kind of like, Trump, Kanye, Elon Musk to name three people that the House Judiciary Committee Republican staff were sort of celebrating as the kind of Mount Rushmore of current conservative politics. If everything is trolling, then all is permitted.\par \par \uc2 WEILL: Absolutely. You know, there`s this weird juvenilia on the right where they can really say anything they want. It`s all a joke. And if somebody takes action on it, well, what`s the big deal? They`re misread the joke. It`s a -- you know, it`s a huge handwave of any responsibility that they might have in spreading these conspiracy theories and in driving people to violence.\par \par \uc2 And they know exactly what they`re doing when they say that Elon is just trolling. It`s obviously playing into a larger ecosystem of conspiracy theories. And he doesn`t have to be holding the bag when again, somebody acts on that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Kelly Weill, thanks so much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 WEILL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, how a trumped back insurrection supporter could become the next Governor of New York. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, there are at least 10 people who are at or near the Capitol on January 6 who were running for national statewide office right now. Then, there are also about 300 candidates on the ballot next week who have supported Trump`s big lie of election fraud. They`re raising doubts about the results of the 2020 election or just outright rejecting Biden`s win.\par \par \uc2 One such candidate is \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , the Trump-backed New York congressman who is currently the Republican Party nominee for governor in the empire state. In the run up to January 6, Zeldin texted Trump Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  offering advice on how to spread Trump`s big lie, including a pitch to "Upload vetted voting irregularities onto one narrowly focused, credible microsite with a donation link to the president`s legal fund for us to reference.\par \par \uc2 In just hours after the deadly insurrection, before the debris had been fully cleared from the Capitol`s, Zeldin went into that chamber where he voted to reject the official certification of the 2020 results and invalidate the votes of New York`s voters. And yet in a state \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by 23 points, Zeldin is pulling within single digits of incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, if a Republican Congressman who`s voted to support the aims of the insurrection, right, to overturn the election, to invalidate the votes of New York voters wins a statewide race in New York, it means, I think, one message, that there`s literally no penalty, none whatsoever for endorsing Trump`s attacks on American democracy. You can basically get away with anything. And it`s up to voters in New York to decide if that`s a message they want to send nationally.\par \par \uc2 Alyssa Katz is the deputy editor at the city where she covers all things New York politics. And she joins me now. It`s great to have you here. This is -- looks like a very close race. Zeldin isn`t -- you know, you`ve got these sort of like \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  types, \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2 , right, Republican governors in blue states who run on this very kind of like old line, the establishment team, \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  moderate profile. Zeldin is not that.\par \par \uc2 ALYSSA KATZ, DEPUTY EDITOR, THE CITY: No, Zeldin is not that, and that he is polling so close to \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  at this point. The Democrat is pretty stunning, and I think it`s attributable to a couple of things. I mean, one, I think that Zeldin has been pretty masterful at manipulating voter amnesia. Really, you know, he`s -- you`ve mentioned a couple of things that he had done. Let`s remember also that not only did he vote not to certify Arizona and Pennsylvania as votes, but then he gave a speech on January 6, right, when you know, the Capitol is in wreck and people are injured, and he`s sitting there talking about voting irregularities.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:33]\par \par \uc2 And he has continued to maintain on saying, you know, that the issues remain. It`s what he said last week in the debate with \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . So, he`s trying to kind of play both sides of the fence. He`s saying that, yes, irregularities are still a concern, but also says that Biden was legitimately elected now. And he`s trying to just sort of be this very kind of amiable figure who`s just doing real talk about New York`s issues and what we need to do here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I think that this sort of amnesia is a key part of this. He`s also -- I mean, he`s also focused relentlessly on