ggle out from your skin into anything that`s sort of on the ballot for New Yorkers.\par \par \uc2 KATZ: No, I mean, he`s for instance, wants to criminalize traveling between states for abortions, including to New York, right, where people could get abortions right now if they -- if they can travel. No, absolutely. The issue is I think that New Yorkers feel, you know, accurately that abortion is not under threat here, at least not from a governor, perhaps from Congress, but it`s just not a salient issue and not something that Hochul can get traction on.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And I think there`s this real question about who someone like \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  would answer to whether they become New York Governor. I think this is really true in Arizona with Kari Lake. If he were to become a governor, he would immediately be a huge national conservative figure. Everything about his political future would depend on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and on that base, much more than it would the voters of New York.\par \par \uc2 KATZ: Yes. I mean, I think he`s going to do something -- I think you`re right that he will become a national figure. I do think -- and like DeSantis in Florida, it`s very similar there, right, where you know, I do think that if he wants to get reelected, he is going to have to govern somewhere from the middle. You know, and I say if, right? I mean, we really don`t know how this is going to go. I think the odds are still in Hochul`s favor but, you know, I think that he will be susceptible to the laws of gravity here in New York.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He will, although again, I think that one of the things that happens here is the laws of gravity tend not to apply to a lot of Republicans who view their political future based less than their ability to get elected or most than to stay in the good graces in the favor of the most powerful elements of their political coalition who is unquestionably \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KATZ: That`s right. And Zeldin has just prove this again and again and again with a \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  text and with, you know, the Trump`s endorsement of him, his complete and total endorsement of Zeldin. No, absolutely. I mean, I think he is working for Trump ultimately.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Alyssa Katz who`s at the city which is a fantastic publication, deputy editor there. I can`t recommend it highly enough for those of you interested in New York. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 KATZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Even as things look rocky for the Democratic governor of New York, there`s some good polling news for Democrats in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . We`ll get into that ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Gas prices are continuing to drift down the U.S. hitting an average of $3.76 today which is way, way down, of course, from the peak over $5.00 a gallon in June. Now, that`s significant, not just for Americans who drive, but also for the potential impact on the Midterm Elections next week. Because as we`ve noted on the show, there`s this really strong correlation between gas prices and voters view on whether the country is on the right track, as you can see on this \uc2 New York Times\uc2  chart.\par \par \uc2 When gas prices shot up this spring, confidence in the direction of the country plunged. So, Democrats were fighting to retain control of Congress have a political as well as civic, economic governmental interest in kind of keeping those gas prices low. Now, President Biden clearly knows this, has done a lot to try to lower the cost of gas, including releasing a total of 180 million barrels of oil from the special reserve this year.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of this, most of it, is out of the government`s hands, particularly as Russia`s invasion of Ukraine sparked an energy crisis around the world. But it`s important to keep in mind that oil companies aren`t exactly hurting. In fact, the opposite. \uc2 Exxon\uc2  just posted its highest profits ever $19.7 billion for just one quarter. \uc2 Chevron\uc2  reached record profits in the prior quarter over $12 billion. And today, President Biden slammed what he called war profiteering.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:49]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: In the last six months, six of the largest oil companies have made more than $100 billion, $100 billion. Look, I`m a capitalist. You`ve heard me say this before. I have no problem with corporations turning a fair profit and getting a return on their investment and innovation. But this is not remotely what`s happening. All companies record profits today are not because they`re doing something new or innovative. Their profits are a windfall of war. The windfall from the brutal conflict that`s ravaging Ukraine and hurting 10s of millions of people around the globe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The President went on to warn that if oil companies don`t reduce their profits to cut prices to consumers, they could face taxes on excess profits. That would be what`s called a windfall tax. And it works something like a tax on lottery winnings, a tax on lump sums, and in this case would be the extra billions of dollars in oil company profits.\par \par \uc2 For those companies, the record profits have been a lot like winning a lottery. In some sense, It`s a pretty perfect analogy. I mean, they did kind of win it even if everyone else lost, because it`s not like the oil companies did, as a present note anything, particularly innovative for those record profits. They were in the right place the right time for them, the wrong place and the wrong time for everyone else. There`s no reason why they should be able to squeeze every last cent from consumers while ordinary Americans just sit back and suffer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R-AZ), SENATE CANDIDATE: Mr. President, how are you, sir? I`m doing great.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don`t know what you did with the debate but I heard that you did good at the debate. But that bad election answer, you got a lot of support. You got to stay with those people.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Absolutely we stay with those people.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You want to get across the line. You got to go stronger in that one thing because that was the one thing there`s a lot of complaints about. Look at Kari. Kari is winning with very little money. And if they say, how is your family, she says the election was rigged and stolen. You`ll lose if you go soft. You`re going to lose that base.\par \par \uc2 MASTER: I`m not going soft.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The race between Republican Blake Master is seen they`re taking election-dying advice from the disgraced ex-president. And the incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly of Arizona is one of the key races that could decide the balance of power of the Senate. Some brand-new polling from \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College on that race and three others that look pretty good for Democrats. Kelly is up six points on Masters. In Pennsylvania, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  leads Republican Mehmet Oz by nearly six points. We should note that survey was conducted before their debate. In Georgia, Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  has a slim lead over Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . In Nevada, Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  are dead even.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by David Plouffe, former senior adviser of President Obama and campaign manager for his 2008 presidential campaign, and Michael Steele, former chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 . David, let me start with you on these Times polling. Again, there`s a ton of polls. The polling average has definitely been moving, I think, even Republicans favor over the last several weeks, although I think it`s sort of steadied a little bit in the last few weeks.\par \par \uc2 These jumped out to me because this is a fairly high-quality polling operation as the Times notes. Most of the polling over the last few weeks is coming from partisan outfits, usually Republican or auto dial firms. And polls are cheap enough to flood the zone. Many of them were emboldened by the 2020 election when their final results came close to the election results even as other pollsters struggled. What do you make of where these marquee races are and whether they`ve sort of tightened to 50-50 and that`s your assumption, or where do you think folks like Kelly, are still holding a little bit of an edge?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, Chris, I think there was tightening across the country. That was natural because there was a period of time, you know, let`s say 45, 60 days ago, you know, Republicans were basically at their bottom. And a lot of the folks who were saying they were undecided in polls were Republicans or Republican-leaning independents. They`ve come home.\par \par \uc2 I agree with your assessment. There are a lot of garbage Republican polls for the most part that are polluting these poll averages. You know, some of them have Republican Senate candidates getting 25, 30, 35 percent of the African American vote. So, either -- maybe, if they are, the Republicans are heading to a landslide win. But if it`s anywhere around, let`s say 90- 10, things look better.\par \par \uc2 So, the important thing about these polls, and that is a high-quality pollster is you have Kelly above 50 and you have Fetterman and Warnock, basically almost at 50. Now, Cortez Masto is not there, but in Nevada, her win -- I think she won her first race at 46 or 47. So, there`s going to be a bunch of none of the above, which is an option in Nevada and third party votes.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s the most important thing right now, if you`re even looking at your own data in a campaign is, are you getting closer at your win number? Because again, after poll shows you went up 44 or 43 but there`s a bunch of undecided, there`s a bunch of people undecided about voting, and you don`t like who those people are, you know, you`re heading to a rough outcome. So yes, these are very solid results for the Democrats. And of course, if this result were to carry forward, you know, eight days from now, they would retain the Senate.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:23]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And that`s such an important point. I keep reminding myself, Michael, about that 50 number. And as someone pointed out today, you know, if you look at the Ohio race, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , and J.D. Vance, I mean, Vance is clearly underperforming in the polling. It`s possible the polling is off. The polling has been off in Ohio, so I don`t know what the reality is.\par \par \uc2 But it keeps being this sort of 45-46 race. And, you know, there`s a great dispatch from Ben Mathis-Lilley at the Slate today that people should read about the J.D. Vance campaign which seems underwhelming and lackluster though I think he`s still favored. But a number of people have pointed out. It`s like, look, you can get to 45 or 46 in a state like Ohio if you`re the Democrat and you`re running a good race like \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 The question is the next five, right? And you see that in North Carolina. You see that in all these places where, you know, if there`s lack of enthusiasm for your opponent, fine, but you got to get that the next five after the 45 is the difference between winning or losing, even if you`re pulling at a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: It is as. It always is. But here`s the rub in a place like Ohio. J.D. Vance shouldn`t be where he is.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, right.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: He should be above 50 percent in Ohio, and he isn`t. And so, the fact that the Democratic candidate is at 45, 46, 47, in that range, depending on the polling, tells you how competitive the race is. And it tells you also very much as we saw in Kansas, there are Republicans who are not getting counted or being assumed to vote or lean on a particular way who aren`t sold on Vance`s campaign who may just in fact say, you know what, I`m going to do it. OK, grandma, don`t look --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: -- and I`m going to vote for the Democrat. So, that -- and that`s good for Democrats at this stage, particularly given how we saw, as David knows, the trendline coming off of that crestfallen moment for Republicans as they began to bounce back, it began to hit Democrats. So, they stabilize the races where they need to. Now, they`ve layered Obama on top of these races at a critical time. And so, it`s going to create a lot of energy and synergy in a way that the polling I don`t think is necessarily going to capture.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, it`s interesting. I think, you know, my assumption is these are basically all essentially 50-50 races, right? Like there`d be nothing surprising about any of these races going either way. It does strike me when you look at -- when you look at these ones, and this is not really true of Laxalt who is sort of a kind of both hybrid kind of Nevada Republican and Trump guy. But you know, Oz, Walker, Masters, Vance, those are all just -- those are Trump people.\par \par \uc2 And I do think -- I mean, when you look at Ohio, I keep coming back to this, David, you know, DeWine is polling 12, 13 points better than J.D. Vance. Like, the Republican Party could just run a bunch of like, people that didn`t try to be jerks to everyone, people that weren`t \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and I think they would be in much better shape right now. I mean, I really does think that the Trump endorsement candidate quality nexus is probably costing a few points in each race at this point. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: There`s no question. I mean, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  spoke to this a couple of months ago where he said candidate quality could cost us the Senate. He`s seen that movie before, by the way, with witches (INAUDIBLE) - -\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, Christine O`Donnell. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: -- and other things.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: There`s no question about that. And so, I think that, yes, all of those races, you know, have Trump candidates supported by Trump. You showed a clip of Trump lecturing Masters that he wasn`t going far enough. You know, don`t talk about the economy, don`t talk about health care, just talk about rigged elections. And that`s for the most part what a lot of these folks are doing.\par \par \uc2 And there`s no question that it`s a rogue`s gallery of Senate candidates. And on the flip side, I think a lot of these Democratic candidates are running tough races. It does tell you though, what an overall tough environment it is, you know, with inflation the way it is, with wrong direction the way it is, with the President`s party facing its first election. We know that history.\par \par \uc2 So -- but despite that, Democrats see there are eight days out from an election, which I think at the beginning of the cycle, most people probably thought it was going to be a Republican, if not landslide strong election, would have a chance to hold the Senate, fighting chance to hold the House, good chance to win important Secretary of State`s races, good chance to hold major governor`s races.\par \par \uc2 So, this really -- the one thing that concerns me in some of the early voting, and we should not divine too much from early voting, is you know, the younger electorate has not shown up to the (INAUDIBLE) in a bunch of places, and that`s what if you`re a campaign you`re trying to rectify over these last eight days.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. The youth turnout key and you see it in the modeling in terms of how they weighed them in polls. It basically changes outcomes. David Plouffe, Michael Steele, thank you, gentlemen both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221101eiav00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Kornacki Countdown for October 28, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Steve Kornacki, Ellison Barber, Jen Psaki, David Plouffe, Rich  Lowry, Vaughn Hillyard, Ali Velshi, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 8,602 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: That does it for us tonight. Now with 10 days to go until election day it is time for The Kornacki Countdown with Steve Kornacki.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC HOST: And we are exactly 10 days, 19 hours, 59 minutes, and 41 seconds and counting until the first poll closing on election night. We are picking up our countdown to election night right where we left off last Friday night, and where we will be every Friday night between now and the midterms.\par \par \uc2 That is after tonight, only one more Friday night between now and the midterms. It is getting really close. We are inside of two weeks right now to go. And not only is it getting closer on the calendar, but voting is underway in all sorts of states. Early voting nearly 20 million, they have already been cast in this midterm election, in the 2018 midterm election, all sorts of records were shattered when it came to turnout.\par \par \uc2 About 116 million votes were cast in that midterm election when all was said and done. We may be over 120 million when this one is over. Numbers that would have been unthinkable years ago, that is of course where politics are right now.\par \par \uc2 So, the biggest campaign news tonight, the Democrats, they are bringing out their biggest star. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who within just the last few hours began a battleground state tour in Georgia. We are going to get a live report from the peach state in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 Also, did that Pennsylvania Senate debate this week move that key race toward Republican Mehmet Oz? We are going to talk about that. And what`s happening on the ground in Arizona, and also, a deep dive on the battle for the House.\par \par \uc2 All of that is ahead tonight, but let`s start with our big picture view. We have been checking in every week on some of the numbers here. This, the generic ballot, we check it every week, the average of the generic ballot, when you ask folks do you want Democrats or Republicans to control Congress.\par \par \uc2 Now a week ago, we were showing you these numbers. We were showing you a Republican advantage, on average of just over three points. That a week ago, it represented a significant shift in the Republicans favor. And we were asking the question at this time last week, was this the start of a bigger shift, where we were going to be here a week later and saying, wow, this thing has really broke for the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 Well, where do thing stand exactly seven days later. It`s actually come down just a bit. The Republicans still lead the general ballot average. But you can see it now, it`s about two and a half points, 2.6. So, again, we say, where does it compare to other recent midterm elections at this point?\par \par \uc2 So that 2.6 advantage, that Republicans have right now, you can take a look 18, 14, 10, 2006, every one of these midterm elections ended up being a wave election for the opposition party. You can see here that 2.6 is the smallest one here, although it is kind of in the ballpark still of 2014.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of Republicans have been saying, and hoping that this midterm ends up like 2014, which was that was Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s second midterm -- it`s kind of a late forming, late breaking wave. A lot of it really only became apparent on election night.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans are open that this is kind of lining up for a repeat of 2014. Democrats obviously are hoping, hey, you, know it`s a closest one of these that you see, and maybe it could get closer in these final 10 days. Maybe this could be a little bit different than what we`ve become accustomed to in recent midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s where things stand. And as we mentioned, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  hitting the campaign trail. This is something Democrats hope will help them in some of these key races in the home stretch. So, we started tonight in Georgia, just outside of Atlanta, it`s the beginning of a tour that`s going to take him tomorrow to Michigan.\par \par \uc2 The governor`s race in Michigan also a couple of significant House races, it is going to take them to Wisconsin, governor Senate race, how about this? An election is close in Wisconsin, who`s ever heard of that before? The Senate and governor`s race in Wisconsin both razor-thin. \uc2 Obama\uc2  will be there heading out to Nevada, next Tuesday, a week from the election, obviously that Senate race is key.\par \par \uc2 Some house races in Nevada, we are going to have some polling to tell you about from Nevada on the House side leader, too, but \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  will be there. And then he`s going to finish up the Saturday before the election, you see both sides of Pennsylvania. Pennsylvania as I say, we are going to talk about the Senate race there in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 But in Georgia tonight is where things began. That Senate race, let`s just show you where it stands right now. The polling average, this is something else it changed this week.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:01]\par \par \uc2 In the polling average in Georgia right now, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the Republican, it is very slight but has taken a lead in the polling average over \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now remember, in Georgia it`s a runoff state if no one gets to 50 percent on November 8th, they will move it to December 6th. The top two, that would be Warnock and Walker and they will run that again on December 6th.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen Senate control come down to Georgia before two years ago, not impossible that the same thing happens this time. Of course, that`s president -- former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  hoping that he`d get Warnock to a victory before December 6th.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a closer look at that first stop on the former president`s campaign tour. As we said was in College Park Georgia, that`s just outside Atlanta. Only a few hours ago he began tonight by addressing the news story today, and of course that was a violent attack on House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s husband Paul at the couple`s home in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 The speaker herself was in D.C. at the time. Paul Pelosi is expected to make a full recovery after undergoing surgery for a skull fracture. A 42- year-old suspect has been arrested.\par \par \uc2 At a press conference a short while ago, the police said the attack was, quote, "intentional." The motive, though still being investigated. Here is what the former president said about all of that tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The pandemic highlighted, and in some case -- cases, made worse, problems that we have been struggling with for years.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes, it leads them to violence and despair. An erosion of basic civility, and Democratic norms in our politics. Not just here, but around the world. I want to take a moment just to say a prayer for a friend of mine, Mr. Paul Pelosi who was attacked.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And then he turned to the state`s key races, and here is the message that \uc2 Obama\uc2  hopes will ultimately convince voters in Georgia and in other swing states to vote Democratic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA\uc2 : The fundamental question that you should be asking yourself right now is who will fight for you? Who cares about you? Who sees you? Who believes in you? That`s a choice in this election. Who -- who will fight.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD CHEERING)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA\uc2 : Who will fight for working people who are struggling to pay their bills? Listen, listen, inflation is a real problem right now. And by the way, it is not just here in America, it`s worldwide. The question you should be asking is, who is actually going to do something about it?\par \par \uc2 The Republicans talk a lot about it. But what is their answer? What is their economic policy? I will tell you what it is. Because it`s the same answer that they have got for everything. They want to gut social program. They want to cut social security, Medicare, and then they want to give their rich friends a tax cut.\par \par \uc2 Who will fight to keep you and your family safe? The Republican politicians who want to flood our streets with more guns, who actually voted against more resources for our police departments? Is it somebody who carries around a phony badge and says he is a law enforcement?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA\uc2 : Like he is a kid playing cops and robbers? If a candidate`s main qualification is that he is going to be loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it means that he is not really going to be thinking about you, and your needs. And you deserve better! Georgia deserves better!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Well, that was Barack Obama`s message tonight. And joining us now from College Park, Georgia is NBC News correspondent Ellison Barber. Ellison, thank you for taking a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Look, two weeks ago tonight at this hour we were talking to you, the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 /\uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  debate had just wrapped up. And at that point, we were looking at polling averages in Georgia that showed Warnock with a slight lead over Walker.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, there were questions about those allegations about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paying for an abortion, which he denied, but questions about how that was going to affect this race.\par \par \uc2 I`m curious, obviously when Democrats bring in \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , it`s an indication they take the race very seriously. It`s no surprise Georgia is a close race, but two weeks after that debate, I`m curious in your reporting how you would characterize how Democrats in Georgia are looking at this Senate race?\par \par \uc2 [22:09:53]\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You know, it`s interesting, because if you are in this arena tonight, we were told there were about 7,000 people here. He would be forgiven for looking at it, hearing the sounds, and thinking the momentum in key races here is on the side of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 But you look at the polls, and that`s not the reality at all. The reason why former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s here is because Democrats need the help. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is behind in a gubernatorial race.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the poll average in the Senate race, which is what I focused on covering, and essentially \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  are in statistical tie and neither of them are in that 50 percent, ar or above the 50 percent threshold that is necessary in order to avoid a runoff election.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is here, is Democrats in this state are hoping that he will energize their base and they`ll show up to vote. The timing of his visit here is strategic. Early voting is already underway. It ends on Friday this weekend is the last weekend of early voting in this state.\par \par \uc2 And Souls to the Polls, which is a very big get out to vote effort for Democrats, that`s going to take place on Sunday. Big picture, we are 11 days out. So, they are bringing him in at a very critical time when we see in the polls, just how close this is.\par \par \uc2 And one thing that was noticeable, and notable to me on the stage tonight listening to former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , is how much time he spent specifically talking about the Georgia Senate race. I think that gives you an indication, perhaps, of where Democrats are thinking maybe they can nudge the poll numbers. But it also gives you a sense of how close that race is.\par \par \uc2 I was talking to Chuck Todd the other day and he brought up the question, the idea of whether or not Georgia is really a swing state, or if it is just a reflection of how polarized this country is. And I have to tell you, in the last week and a half, it has been interesting to be on the campaign trail with the Senate candidates because both Walker and Warnock have been campaigning in districts that will absolutely hold on election day.\par \par \uc2 It seems like they`re focused for both Democrats and Republicans, at least in the Georgia Senate race, it`s not on convincing an independent or a swing voter to vote for them, but convincing their most loyal bases to get out, and to get their friends, and to take them out to vote as well. Steve?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, Ellison Barber on the ground there in Georgia just outside Atlanta. Thank you, Ellison, for joining us. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 And now for more, I want to bring in \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , former White House press secretary and now MSNBC host. Jen, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good to be. I`m so excited to see the board in person. It`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: I`m excited too. I`m excited to show it off to anybody who is excited to.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Good.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: But to check out this board, and it worked which is always a bonus too.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It`s a good step.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: So, thank you for joining us. I`m curious. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , in terms of national Democrats has proven himself, obviously, winning elections twice. He`s extremely good at motivating voters to get out and vote for him. His track record of going out on the campaign trail in transferring that energy to other Democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 I`m thinking of, I think the same week last year he was in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  was in trouble, and Democrats were excitedly talking about \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  coming in and of course \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  lost. I`m remembering the closing days of `16 campaign --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Twenty sixteen, yes, the night before.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: -- and he was going to help \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Is there a challenge here? Can you diagnose it why he has struggled to transfer that support to other Democrats, and is there any reason to think it would be different this year?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Look, I think it`s really hard for any surrogate, whether it is President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , or a celebrity, or a rock star to transfer their support, and love to another candidate. But it`s still worth trying, which is why he is out there.\par \par \uc2 Because as Ellison was saying, this is the pivotal, final stretch here in Georgia. There is only a couple more days of early voting. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is still very popular. I think his popularity is almost 60 percent in the country. And he knows how to send a direct message with a little humor. He brought the jokes tonight to voters. And that`s exactly what you heard him do tonight and what he`ll do in a couple of other states.\par \par \uc2 He does like to go out and campaign when people are already voting, because he likes to light that fire. And this is kind of the light the fire season for Democrats. So, we`ll see. Even if it`s 20 people go out and vote tonight or tomorrow or the next day, that`s still a victory. But it is hard to transfer and I think that`s hard for any surrogate elected official campaign for people.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And on the subject of Georgia, this got some attention yesterday. \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the Democratic leader in the Senate --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: -- encountered the President Biden on the tarmac. Let`s play that exchange --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: -- because it got picked up here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [inaudible].\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): It looks like the debate didn`t hurt us too much in Pennsylvania as of today. The state where we`re going downhill is Georgia. It`s hard to believe that they will go for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And by the way, Barack is down there tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Tomorrow, yes. But our vote, our early turnout in Georgia is huge, huge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:15:01]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: I`m -- here`s what curious. I`ve heard from folks that speculating, that Schumer has been in politics forever, he must have known that that might be picked up. And he is trying to send some kind of message.\par \par \uc2 I guess the first -- my first question is, is that at all possible? Because I can`t imagine what the message would be. And if not, he is expressing a real concern there about Georgia. What do you think is happening in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Look, I think it`s always, it was always going to be a very close race. I think if you look at the numbers, it looks very likely it`s headed to a runoff in December, we are going to have a lot of political conversations over Thanksgiving, that`s what that means.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of what he was saying in that hot mic moment, which I do not believe was an intentional, there`s lots of ways to get information out, he also showed him socks, that was a funny moment, was -- it`s consistent with what I`ve heard from other Democrats.\par \par \uc2 A couple of weeks ago in Nevada it was feeling concerning, it`s now feeling a little bit better over the last couple of days. In Georgia, they`ve clarified a little bit that his comments were taken a little bit out of context by what he was saying to the president. But there is no doubt that it`s close, it is going to be close. And yes, it defies logic that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is where he is in the polls, and I think that you heard him saying that as well.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: We showed where \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is going to be over the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: In terms of the current president we do have, he is in Pennsylvania tonight.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: This is his native state.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: He is going to be there with \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  at that Saturday before the election. But otherwise, I think here is a Time magazine piece on this. This captures, it says Biden prefers Delaware to the campaign trail, and Democrats aren`t exactly complaining. Welcome to 2022 when a compromised candidate from 2020 is now seen as an albatross almost everywhere, except his home states of Delaware and Pennsylvania. And even there, he is still underwater.\par \par \uc2 I did see, he is also going to Maryland. Maryland not exactly a swing state. What does that say that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is out there, this sort of expansive and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  isn`t?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, look, I think \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  would say that when you are the former president, you are a little less under the spotlight in terms of people criticizing you, and his popularity is higher than it was when he was president.\par \par \uc2 I think for President Biden, his objective is keeping the Senate, and fighting like hell for the House. I don`t think that he is worried about how many campaign trips he is checking off a box. What he has done is raise a lot of money for candidates, he`s gone into fundraising events. He`s done a lot of those privately. That`s true.\par \par \uc2 But his objective, and the objective of the White House is to do everything that can be -- that they can do to be helpful. That also includes talking about a lot of these accomplishments that are helpful to Democrats who are trying to show their voters, look, we are doing something about inflation, we are trying to bring your cost down. We are trying to lower the cost of prescription drugs. And he`s been doing that a lot as well.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , thank you for taking a few minutes joining us.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: I appreciate that. And coming up, the debate in Pennsylvania. You might have watched it, you definitely heard about it. Will it affect the tight Fetterman/Oz race. Has it raise the Democrats badly need if they are going to hang on to the Senate?\par \par \uc2 I`m going to talk to David Plouffe and Rich Lowry about that and more.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And continuing our countdown to the first poll closing on election night 10 days and change away. So, we showed you the big picture national scene there with the generic ballot. Now let`s check in. We`ve been doing this every week, too. Let`s check in on the battle for control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Of course, 50/50 right now. Republicans in need of a net gain of one seat. So, we`ve been showing you this chart every week, too. First you start with the five most vulnerable Democratic seats, and again, a little bit of movement here where Georgia now, is the state, the Democratic seat where Republicans lead in the polls.\par \par \uc2 We just mentioned this last block, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the Republican, very, very slight lead on average here. But \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  leading Warnock in the poll there right now, potentially. And again, there is that whole issue, the 50 percent in Georgia that we talked in the potential runoff, but potentially Georgia maybe representing the Republicans best chance at a pick up right now.\par \par \uc2 Right behind that, you got Arizona, Mark Kelly, the Democrat continuing to lead. It`s down to about a point and a half. Nevada, \uc2 Catherine Cortez- Masto\uc2 , actually was a favorable poll. She got it from the University of Nevada, Reno, let`s move the average there a little bit. New Hampshire, Colorado sitting there as well.\par \par \uc2 The whole key to this, though, is for every seat from this column that Republicans win, obviously Democrats would love to run the table, protect all their seats, they`d keep the Senate. But for every win that Republicans get in this column, it must be matched by Democrats by a win in this column if they are going to keep the Senate to keep it at 50-50 to keep that tiebreaker.\par \par \uc2 So, if right now, let`s just say Republicans were to succeed in winning Georgia, get \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  over the top there, and Democrats were to ang on you see everything in this list, then Democrats would have to win one of these Republican held seats.\par \par \uc2 And you can see there is one, this has been the story, as long as we have been doing these Friday night shows, it`s been the story all year. There`s one Republican held seat that looms above all others as a right target for Democrats. It`s Pennsylvania, it`s a state \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won in 2020. It`s the state where \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , the Democratic nominee this summer have led in the poll average by nearly nine points, that number for Fetterman has come all the way down to just over a point.\par \par \uc2 We are now only starting to get polls after that debate that was held this week, Oz and Fetterman squaring off, the only time they did so, that debate has sparked a lot of discussion and that debate is also playing out on the airwaves now in Pennsylvania in the final weeks of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 The Fetterman campaign, for instance, seized on this answer from the debate, on the subject of abortion from Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: This is who Dr. Oz wants in charge of women`s health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I want women, doctors, local political leaders, local political leaders, Local, political leaders.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:03]\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Oz would let politicians like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  ban abortion without exceptions. Even in cases of rape, incest, or life of the mother. Oz is too extreme for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I`m \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , and I approve this message.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Meanwhile, on the other side, the Trump super PAC went out with this new ad the day after the debate called just not right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: The worst border crisis in American history, the worst crime wave in decades, the threat of nuclear annihilation, our economy collapsing. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  aren`t up to these challenges. Biden is stumbling around, and Fetterman just isn`t right.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our economy is as strong as hell.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: The eagles are so much better than the eagles.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Biden and Fetterman, liberal, clueless, weak. Make America great again is not responsible for the content of this advertising.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And joining us now, David Plouffe, the former campaign manager and White House senior adviser to president \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , he is an MSNBC political analyst, and Rich Lowry, the editor-in-chief of National Review and an NBC News political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to both of you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 David, let me start with you. So, that ad that we just played there from the Trump aligned super PAC, it did not actually feature any of the clips from the debate, but it is certainly playing on something that you`ve heard in the wake of that debate, concerns about \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , his recovery from the stroke, and the performance that he delivered in that debate.\par \par \uc2 I`m curious how you look at that as a factor in this race. Has it -- we say over the summer it was nine points was the Fetterman lead, now it`s one. Do you think that slippage had something to do with his performance on the campaign trail? And how do you think what voters saw in the debate this week, and what they`ll see in an ad like this, do you think that will affect how they vote in this race?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: My sense, Steve, is that the tightening that we`ve seen in Pennsylvania, and I would say the same thing in Georgia, is both Walker and Oz were well below what they were ever going to get, you know, once people voted.\par \par \uc2 So, I think they were both performing poorly. You have a lot of Republicans, Republicans leaning independents in polls saying they were undecided. But they have come home and I think that is why these races is (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 Now, I think Fetterman has taken a lot of, I mean, I thought that that just shows a terrible, stereotypically terrible bad ad, but there have been some other ads that have been more affective, hitting him on crime and other issues. That I think that`s probably been more of the issue, I think than his performance on the campaign trail, or the debate.\par \par \uc2 You know, my guess is that this is a very close race, there is not that many undecideds left, so I don`t think you are going to see any sea change because of the debate. Now, Fetterman campaign is obviously trying to have Oz pay a price for his really, you know, deeply unpopular comment that local, political leader should be involved in women`s health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the Fetter -- the Oz side trying to hit him on his performance. I don`t think so. I think we are headed to a super close race in Pennsylvania. One question there will be, if Shapiro does not win by some margin, you know, that is going to be really important.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to look at that same factor in Georgia if Kemp is heading to a victory of some margin. So, I think that could end up being a crucial factor. But I understand why the debate got so much attention. But I really doubt at the end of the day that`s going to be the driver of the outcome here.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Rich, I`m curious from your standpoint, do you think that debate is going to affect the outcome?\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE NATIONAL REVIEW: Yes, I do. You know, Oz got this thing down from nine points double digit, whatever it was, negative advertising, largely on crime, and that has added about one or two point Oz deficit for a while there. And republicans were kind of frustrated. What can we do to get his over the top.\par \par \uc2 And it seems they now believe Oz is narrowly ahead. This is going to be a close race. It can be razor-thin one way or the other. But I don`t think people are prepared to see what they saw from Fetterman, the spin from Fetterman, his campaign was just, you know, he mishes a word together here or there. It was much, it was much worse than that during that debate.\par \par \uc2 And it`s the kind of thing that people talk about, and they wonder about, and you know, - a big part of being a senator is talking, and listening, and arguing, and debating, and his recovery is just not what it was portrayed us.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Let me ask you about a point that David was making to, though, about look at that governor`s race in Pennsylvania, and the polling there shows \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  the Democrat, well ahead. And I look at Pennsylvania, and I look at why did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  carry it in 2016, and then lose it in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And the answer, when you look at the map here, it just comes screaming at you, is the suburbs right outside of Philadelphia where you got these voters who did not like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and right now, by all indications in the polling, aren`t too fond of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , aren`t too fond of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:03]\par \par \uc2 In that debate --\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: -- Oz seemed -- when he was pressed on it, seemed to go out of his way to endorse Trump for 2024. And I see he`s going to do a rally with Trump in Pennsylvania on a Saturday before the election. Is he risking the very voters that he needs those suburban voters who -- they may not want to vote for Democrats, but they don`t want to vote for Trump again. He`s -- is he aligning himself too closely with Trump?\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Yes, there`s no doubt. I mean, Trump is a double-edged sword. You see, Mastriano not particularly skilled politician and just way over identified with Stop the Steal stuff and has no hope. Now Oz`s closing message in the -- in Philadelphia and the surroundings has been a moderate one. You know, it`s -- surgeons, you have to work with others. And we all have our part to play. And, you know, Trump obviously cuts against that message.\par \par \uc2 I`ll say, Oz, you know, he`s not a natural politician. And he`s not a natural choice for Pennsylvania. But he`s been a hard worker. He`s been a learner. He has pretty good campaign staff, and that`s why he`s close and probably a little bit ahead at the moment, I think.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: David, if Democrats do end up needing to flip a Republican seat and they fall short in Pennsylvania, where`s their next best bet? And is it a big drop off from Pennsylvania to the next one?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Well, first, Steve, on the question you asked Rich. Let`s look at what Youngkin did with Trump, you know, in his race a year ago, very careful. And you can think about what Youngkin was trying to do in those suburbs outside of Washington, very similar to what you just talked about what Oz needs to do in suburbs. Not just outside Philadelphia, that`s the big place, but also outside of Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m very puzzled by that decision. I understand Trump may help with turnout a little bit, but I think it`s risky. So Pennsylvania is clearly at the top of the list. I mean, I think you look at Ohio, that`s remained closed. But again, being 46, 45 is great in a poll, but that`s not at 50. So, you know, North Carolina has been closed.\par \par \uc2 But I think the one that you say of that list you showed on the right side of your chart, clearly, Pennsylvania is, I mean, it`s a must win state. Maybe the Democrats are going to run the table on all of the races on the left. But you can`t count on that. So, you know, it could not be more important.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t mean we could be surprised. Vance is obviously going to underperform what a Republican would normally get in a race like that. But will it be enough? I`m doubtful about that, despite the campaign that Ryan has run.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, I`ve noticed that Democrats and Republicans -- a lot of folks remember where the polls landed in 16 and 20 in Ohio and where the actual result ended up. David Plouffe, Rich Lowry, thank you for joining us appreciate it, both of you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a state that is also crucial to the battle for the Senate this year. And for the White House two years from now in one where Republicans are fielding a slate of candidates more closely tethered to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in any other battleground. We are taking a close look at what`s going on in Arizona, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:37:51]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And our countdown to the first poll closing on election night continues. OK, last block, we were talking about that dynamic in Pennsylvania where there`s a split right now potentially forming where the governor`s race \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the Democrat, comfortably in front. In that race, the Senate race pretty much dead even in Pennsylvania, the possibility of a split ticket result of a Democrat winning the governor`s race of a Republican winning the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 From Pennsylvania, let`s go to another state where there is another potentially surprising split ticket development. Let`s go out to Arizona. And here`s how it works in Arizona. Take a look at the Senate race here. This is where the Democrats are defending a seat. Mark Kelly, the Democratic incumbent being challenged by Republican Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 Now the polling is tightened a bit here. It`s very close. The average is only about a 1.5. But Mark Kelly, the Democrat, continues to lead in the polling here in Arizona overall, in the polling average over Blake Masters. That`s the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s the race for governor. Closely watched race. Kari Lake, the Republican, is actually leading by about four points on average in this race. So you got the Democrat leading in the Senate race. The Republican leading in the gubernatorial race. And of course, the extra layer of this is Kari Lake closely, closely has tethered herself to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- to Donald Trump`s rhetoric about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 She ran in the primary with a lot of frankly, members of her party at the national level thinking that she represented the weaker potential nominee, that this would be giving the governor`s race to the Democrats. And here is Lake outperforming Blake Masters. Blake Masters also ran with Trump`s endorsement in the primary.\par \par \uc2 But Lake is outperforming her Democratic opponent. Meanwhile, Masters is trying to play catch up in the Senate race. So you`ve got a bit of a potential split ticket here in Arizona to very close races.\par \par \uc2 So what is going on there in Arizona? Joining us now from Phoenix, NBC News Correspondent Vaughn Hillyard. And Vaughan, I wonder if you could talk a little bit about that dynamic. I think it`s been surprising to a lot of people who watched Kari Lake in the primary and have watched her in this general election campaign, that she has taken this lead in the polling. What accounts for that and what accounts for that split we`re seeing here where Blake Masters is not kind of tracking with Kari Lake?\par \par \uc2 [22:40:12]\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Steve. If we could start on the split component of this, nobody here in this state on the Republican side blinks at the suggestion that Kari Lake is doing whatever she can to lift up not only down ballot candidates like the Secretary of State candidate, Attorney General candidate, but also lift up the Senate candidate, Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 She has been holding campaign rallies every single night over the last couple 