rizona, staying in Maricopa County, staying in Phoenix, MSNBC Anchor Ali Velshi in Phoenix. He has spent the day talking to Arizona voters. Ali, thank you for joining us. Appreciate it. So, Vaughn was just kind of given us the lay of the land there in these -- the governor and the Senate race there. I`m curious, what are you picking up on the ground from folks in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST, "VELSHI": So Vaughn is there at a rally with people who are, you know, highly politically motivated. I spent my day with six Arizonans, people from right around here from across the political spectrum. There were three who identified as Republicans, three who identified as Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And I will say, you know, that you know this very well, Steve, there`s a long history of republicanism and conservatism in the state. In fact, the three conservatives I`ve worked for had -- I spoke to, had all in their time at some point worked with or for \uc2 John McCain\uc2 . Here`s what one of them told me about election denialism and how it`s influencing him -- his own vote as a Republican.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANNY MAZZA (R), ARIZONA VOTER: Most important issue in Arizona, when I`m voting is protecting the essence of our democracy. Where, for worse, ground zero of this election denier stuff, the fraud, the audit that they did with the ballot in the 2020 elections.\par \par \uc2 I -- when I`m deciding who to vote for, it`s a disqualifier, if the person isn`t 100% honest of the fact that we had an open, honest, fair and free election.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right. And you`re speaking as a Republican?\par \par \uc2 MAZZA: I`m speaking as -- yes, I`m a, technically, a registered Republican.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So there you go. That`s a Republican. By the way, all the Republicans on my panel have said they will not vote for election deniers. On the other hand, as you said, they got a 15-week abortion ban that goes back to a very, very old time in this state like in Wisconsin and like in Michigan, there isn`t a positive ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 There`s a woman I spoke to who had Native and Latina background. She was queer. She`s a person of color, and she says that there`s just a lot in this election that she feels outside of and attacked by. She`s, by the way, she`s quite young. She`s 21 years old. Here`s what she told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ADALILA MACHIC-HUITZIL (D), ARIZONA VOTER: I think what`s motivating me right now, especially with going to the election and going, you know, voting, this is my first midterm election. And --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACHIC-HUITZIL: -- the big thing that I didn`t think would have to be an issue is misinformation, and denying something that is so simple and basic. At the end of the day, I am what, most people target us, I am what is commonly being harmed. I am a queer person with the uterus. It`s like I`m of color and it`s just constantly my rights are being pushed away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:45:06]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, you would appreciate this a lot, Steve. I had people from across the political spectrum, but they were united in this concept of what citizenship looks like, what pluralism looks like, what civic engagement looks like. The fact that down ballot races are really, really important and that Arizona has got a proud political history of representing people across the political spectrum that they think is at risk of being lost right now.\par \par \uc2 All of their messages to everyone else is, please, go vote. Don`t get up the next morning and be fascinated by the fact that some of these people want election in Arizona if you didn`t vote. It was a very eye-opening conversation that I`m going to have a lot of on TV this weekend and into Monday.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, Ali Velshi in 