use on election night, where are we going to look first, 7:00 p.m. Our first read out in the House is going to be in Virginia. The polls closed at 7:00 in two key districts. The 2nd and the 7th, in particular, two Democratic incumbents elected in 2018, facing challenging reelection.\par \par \uc2 I think these could be bellwethers the start of election night, that tell us how it`s going. And someone who has spent some time in both districts recently, NBC News Capitol Hill Correspondent, Ali Vitaly. Ali, the 2nd District and near Virginia Beach, the 7th District getting into northern Virginia, what did you find out there?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Steve, great minds think alike. Because I thought the same thing, which is why I`ve spent time in these two districts, because we`re going to see these results early in election night. And they are bellwethers, which, frankly, when I talked with both Spanberger and Luria, yes, they`re aware that that`s how we`re looking at them. But they would argue they have always been running in tight sweeney districts.\par \par \uc2 Spanberger argues that her district has been that, even back to 2014 when it was held by \uc2 Eric Cantor\uc2 . And, of course, Luria saying that although the Contors of her district have changed, the issues that she`s running on are different now and shaped heavily by 2020. But at the same time, she`s used to running in a tight race.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:06]\par \par \uc2 I think the fascinating thing for me as I was there in Luria`s district is, yes, we talk about a lot of these issues that are top of our polling, crime, inflation, abortion, the economy, all of those are important. But what Luria says is a hinge issue in her district is the January 6th Committee and the work that she has done to protect democracy after what we saw during the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 But when I talk to voters, they have two very different opinions of this. When you talk to most Republicans, I`ll show you one of them. And then on the back end, what a Democrat was saying. Listen to how this went.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on-camera): How do you feel about the work that Luria has done in the January 6th Committee?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it`s atrocious. I have family that went up to January 6th, I have friends that went up there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can understand that people are standing up for their rights and everything like that. But to have an assault on our democracy is ridiculous. And to think that the election was stolen, absolutely ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Steve, I think the larger question that I`m looking at -- and I listened to Ali Velshi`s reporting Vaughn`s reporting -- the election denialism thread is going to be really fascinating for us to watch on election night, because the big question that I`ve been asking is, if \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  is right, and people who don`t believe that the election was stolen don`t -- and who -- people who believe the election was stolen, don`t vote for her, what does that say about where the country is right now and we`re going to find out in just about 10 days. Steve?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Ali Vitali, who was just in these two key districts. Thank you, Ali, for that. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221029eias00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 28, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Jacob Ward, Ben Collins \par \uc2 8,975 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Please come and hang out with us, President Obama. My guests will be the Democratic candidate for Senate, Congresswoman Val Demings and the man who`s challenging \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Former Governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , and all of our friends. More details to come.\par \par \uc2 Y`all, that`s tonight`s REIDOUT. We had a really great week. Thank you, Texas once again. We had a wonderful time there. And we expect to have a good time in Florida. But Florida can`t give us barbecue. I`m expected to see what kind of food they`re going to give us there. We`ll see. See you guys later. Special edition of ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SCOTT, CHIEF, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE: At approximately 2:27 this morning, San Francisco police officers were dispatched to the residence of Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: An attempted murder at the Speaker`s house.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This individual says where is Nancy, where is Nancy?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the violent attack on Nancy Pelosi`s husband. What do we know about the attacker`s motive, and the speaker for self as the intended target.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio Cortez\uc2  and Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  are both the specter of political violence in the aftermath of insurrection. And the Democratic case for control of Congress 11 days out. And a viewers` guide for how to think about all of the different ways election night could unfold with the one and only Steve Kornacki.\par \par \uc2 Live from Studio 6A in Rockefeller Plaza, ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes and welcome. I am here in Studio 6A at Rockefeller Center with a live audience. It`s something we have not done in nearly three years. Last time I was standing here was March 6, 2020 right before the entire world shut down. And of course, COVID still not gone. But we are back. We`re taking precautions so we can gather here safely. Everyone here is vaccinated. I`m vaccinated and boosted. You`ll see people wearing masks.\par \par \uc2 And we`re here on a night a very serious breaking news out of San Francisco. I`m sure that you`ve seen it. Nearly hours in the morning, there was a violent invasion at the home of the Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , one of the most powerful people the \uc2 United States Government\uc2 , second in line to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we know so far. The suspect is a 42-year-old man with a long online footprint. His social media and blog posts have espoused right-wing extremist ideas, conspiracy theories, including about the 2020 election, COVID vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Now, shortly before 2:30 a.m., he broke into Pelosi`s San Francisco home through a window in the back of the house. And the speaker was not there. She was in Washington D.C. with the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  security detail that always travels with her. And her husband was alone, 82-year-old Paul Pelosi at home.\par \par \uc2 According to law enforcement sources, the intruder confronted Mr. Pelosi shouting, where is Nancy? Where`s Nancy? He also told Paul Pelosi he was going to tie him up and wait for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  get home. And somehow, Paul Pelosi was able to surreptitiously dial 911 when the attacker wasn`t looking. San Francisco`s Chief of Police Bill Scott described what officers saw when they arrived on the scene.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Our officers observed Mr. Pelosi and the suspect both holding a hammer. The suspect pulled the hammer away from Miss Pelosi and violently assaulted him with it. Our officers immediately tackled the suspect, disarmed him, took him into custody, requested emergency backup, and rendered medical aid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Paul Pelosi is an 82-year-old man at home that night was hit in the head multiple times of that hammer. He remains in the hospital as we speak after undergoing surgery this afternoon to repair a skull fracture, as well as serious injuries to his right arm and hands. Now, we`re told, thankfully, he`s expected to make a full recovery. His attacker was also transported to the hospital for treatment. And he is being charged with attempted homicide, assault with a deadly weapon, elder abuse, and several other felonies according to Chief Scott, who also said the motive is still being determined.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re seeing, thankfully, near-universal condemnation of this horrific attack across the political spectrum, including from Republicans, leader the Senate, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , leader of the House, Kevin McCarthy. We do have to note, as of this hour, we still have not heard anything from the actual leader of the Republican Party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And of course, this is the context that looms over all this. And political violence, and intimidation, menace has been cultivated and encouraged by the ex-president for years to his followers. And that threat of violence has been specifically directed at \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  for years now. She has been the target of the most radicalized members of the Trump movement who stalked the halls of the Capitol looking for her on January 6, banging on our office doors, yelling out the very same phrase the intruder in Pelosi has said this morning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Where are you, Nancy. We are looking for you. Nancy! Oh, Nancy! Nancy! Where are you, Nancy?\par \par \uc2 [20:05:07]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Bring her out. Bring her out here. We`re coming in if you don`t bring her out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They`re with -- we need \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  come into the hallway. They`re pounding on doors trying to find her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I figured well, I`m in her office. I got blood in her office. I put a quarter on her desk even if she (BLEEP) worth it. And I left her a not on her desk. It says, Nancy, Big-O was here, you (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, to be clear and to be fair about all of this right, we have seen all sorts of examples of political violence and threats in this country. In 2017, a gunman attacked Congressional Baseball practice shooting Republican Congressman Steve Scalise. Earlier this year, a man with a gun was arrested near Supreme Court Justice Brett Kavanaugh`s home. In July, an armed man showed up outside Democratic Congressman Pramila Jayapal`s house. They were all terrifying and completely, obviously, unacceptable incidents.\par \par \uc2 What we`re talking about here is in a somewhat different category, I think, because there is a very specific form of murderous rage coming from Donald Trump`s most violent and militant devotees directed at \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . And on January 6, on the day of the largest instance of political violence in this country in recent memory, a mob of people directed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rampage through the Capitol wanting to find and hurt Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One rioter said he wanted to drag the speaker from the building. And I`m quoting here, "I just want to see Pelosi`s head hit every effing stair. Another member of the mob said she was looking for Nancy to shoot her in the frigging brain. A third said, Tell Pelosi we are coming for that B."\par \par \uc2 Now, these are just a few examples. And over the course of hundreds of cases, there are many, many more. It`s no surprise that the violent threats and attacks on the speaker continued. I mean, just listen to what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself was saying the week after his failed coup as the House prepared to impeach him for the second time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This impeachment is causing tremendous anger and you`re doing it and it`s really a terrible thing that they`re doing for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  to continue on this path. I think it`s causing tremendous danger to our country and it`s causing tremendous anger. I want no violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Causing tremendous anger, right? You`re doing this thing, people getting riled up. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says that, and he says things like that quite a bit, he`s painting a target, right? It`s clear and direct, in no uncertain terms. Everyone needs to understand what he is doing.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Jacob Ward reporting for NBC News in San Francisco. And Leigh Ann Caldwell reporting for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  in DC. Jacob, let me start with you. Just on the latest on the condition of Paul Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, that has been the question all across the San Francisco neighborhood, the one I`m standing in, how is Paul Pelosi? Earlier in the day, it was not at all clear whether he might even survive these injuries. He was taken to Zuckerberg General Hospital. That is the big level-one trauma facility here in San Francisco. It`s where you go when you have a big bad injury.\par \par \uc2 But statements from Senator Nancy Pelosi`s office have shown us that he is in fact going to make it. He had surgery to repair a skull fracture and those injuries to his right hand and arm, ones that we have to assume he sustained as a sort of defensive wound against the hammer. And the fact that he is now out is a very, very happy thing. But the sort of the details of it are adding this extra sort of grisly shadow to it, and one that neighbors all across this neighborhood, I think, are having a very emotional reaction too, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we just received word the speaker has actually made it to the hospital where her husband is. There are some details about how he managed to call 911 that have come out. If you could tell us -- what do we know about how he was able to actually get help?\par \par \uc2 WARD: Well, what is so extraordinary about it is to imagine that this grandfather in his 80s, you know, sleeping alone in his house would somehow have the presence of mind at two in the morning to in fact not only somehow make conversation with this man who was broken into his home, brought a hammer, smashed his way in through the backyard window, but can also somehow, you kn0w, managed to actually during a moment of distraction, when David DePape was not looking, somehow managed to dial 911.\par \par \uc2 It`s not clear at this point whether he actually managed to speak into the phone or simply dial 911 and left it perhaps in his pocket or on the table, but somehow he got word out. And an emergency dispatcher, one who was called out by name and thanked by the mayor here, London Breed, that the presence of mind of that dispatcher, to get a couple of officers out here for what they call a wellbeing-check. All of that is a miracle.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, when they arrived, they found David DePape standing over Paul Pelosi, you know, halfway tied up and explained, we`re here waiting for Nancy. It`s at that point that somehow the scuffle broke out. That and the presence of police officers was when Paul Pelosi was struck and why he was so grievously injured and had to be taken to the hospital, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:28]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I can imagine the reverberations throughout Washington in terms of federal law enforcement authorities, as well as everyone in Congress and on the Hill and their own calculations of security.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, POLITICAL REPORTER, WASHINGTON POST: Yes, absolutely. Obviously, everyone I`ve talked to is horrified. But Chris, this is people`s worst nightmare that has borne fruit. People are not only -- members of Congress are not only concerned about their own safety, because there are many threats against many members, but also the safety of their families. A lot of members of Congress not just have spouses, but they have children, as well.\par \par \uc2 And so, the fact that this -- it was not just a threat, that this attacker was successful and injuring and harming very critically, Speaker Pelosi`s husband has led to a lot of questions. Now, publicly, Republicans and Democrats are saying that they are horrified by this, that violence has no place in American politics, but the big question is what`s going to happen in the days to come?\par \par \uc2 We are 10 days from a midterm election. Remember, there are still magnetometers outside of the House floor to get on to the House of Representatives floor to vote. And are these members of Congress going to retreat into their corners, say things that they should say publicly to a general audience, but to a base audience, to a base of the -- audience of their supporters, are they going to have a different tone? And that is what is going to matter.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I also wonder if you`ve heard any discussions or reporting indicates just, you know, concrete questions about security, obviously, after this. It`s one of the great things about -- had been a great thing about the Capitol. You can walk in. It`s the people`s house. The great thing about covering particularly members of the House who were very accessible. Of course, you know, we saw what happened to \uc2 Gabby Giffords\uc2 . We saw what happened in Steve Scalise`s case at that Congressional softball practice. But I wonder if you`ve heard anything about how those security protocols might be thought about after this.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yes, there`s a lot of conversations from -- by members of Congress that I`m talking to on what needs to happen next. But this has been a conversation that`s been happening for a long time ever since January 6 this really came into focus. They`ve put more money towards security because there have been reports that since 2016, there`s been 107 percent increase in threats against members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 So, they`ve tried to better fund it. They`ve tried to hire more Capital Police officers. But there`s a shortage. There`s a shortage of manpower, of woman power. And does this security extend to the families? That is something that`s not currently available? Of course, the conversation is happening, but nothing concrete yet, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Leigh Ann Caldwell, Jacob Ward, thank you both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Joining me here on set, NBC News Senior Reporter Ben Collins who`s been doing some reporting on the extensive online footprint of the individual who`s been apprehended. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR REPORTER: So, this was a person who had a blog. At least that`s the blog that the police are looking at right now. And in that blog, you saw constant conspiracy basically, and the conspiracy that you and I probably know very much about, the QAnon stuff, the pizzagate stuff, basically this idea that a cabal runs everything, a lot of stuff about pedophilia and stuff like that as well.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. It also seems, from what I`ve been able to see, and I`ve seen some of the posts itself, this is someone who went through a kind of ideological journey that is actually quite common, which is people who sort of have this kind of anti-establishment distrust as their sort of core belief. And I`ve watched people, including people that I know or in my social circle, and I imagine people watching this show have watched this happen, of going through that and then moving to pizzagate and then to anti-vax stuff, and to QAnon. And the next thing you know, to the sort of like very MAGA aligned sort of authoritarian views.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Right. And we don`t want to get out ahead of what, you know, the police know and the facts here. We absolutely do not. But we do know that. We know this kind of person, right? We know people from California who wind up at this very MAGA worldview and start in natural health spaces or start in -- you know, a thing that you would start in Berkeley, California, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Natural food, yoga, crystals. I mean, this is something that I have seen over and over from Brandi Zadrozny who you do great reporting with, has documented a lot of folks, particularly in the QAnon movement sort of coming from that place.\par \par \uc2 [20:15: 01]\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. And that is a -- like you said, it`s not unique. But it`s also a -- it`s something that the far right is trying to absorb recently. You see this a lot like with the \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  types and things like that in the non-extremist spaces. They`re trying to bring in people from that natural health space. You see it a lot on Tik Tok, too, right? You see, constantly people talking about not just crystals, but how to eat only paleo stuff or things like that, not to trust GMO stuff. Things that used to be associated with the American left are now associated with the TikTok right, basically.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And again, to the extent that this is the blog that this individual held, we know that police are looking at the same blog that you and I have been looking at images from. This is pretty clear as someone who`s just very immersed in like the most sort of violent, seething, paranoid worldview of that, you know, QAnon, antisemitic, kind of right.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Right. And I think the important thing here when we`re talking about that cabal that, you know, is very pervasive right now on the --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In the imagination of these people, not in reality.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Not in reality because they have to do -- because that explains everything. That explains why they feel like they`re losing power, why the vaccine stuff is happening, you know, there`s this thing with transhumanism that secretly they`re trying to take away your power by putting trans people more into your life and, you know, taking -- absolutely absurd, like robotic conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: That is very big in these spaces with these specific people. And it was all there. It was all -- it was all exactly in those -- in those blogs that he wrote.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, I`m reminded of individual Cesar Sayoc, who`s I think we don`t think about a lot, but if people remember, it was exactly four years ago, right around now, that he sent bombs to a bunch of prominent folks. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was one of them. \uc2 George Soros\uc2 , if I`m not mistaken, right? The bombs didn`t go off, but he had actually created them.\par \par \uc2 And he was a guy who had sort of gotten radicalized. He was much more squarely a Trump guy. He had been to a bunch of rallies. He had a truck. But this was someone who sort of went down a rabbit hole and the rabbit hole ended at political violence.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. And by the way, the synagogue shooter as well on Gab was radicalized in the same two-week span, right before Midterms. This might just be something that happens at Midterms while we`re at this fever pitch where people are constantly told you might lose all of your rights in two weeks, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: When it becomes really readily apparent to people that the enemy is right there. You can look up their address. And if you -- that`s what they`ve decided to do is to preempt it with violence instead of going through democracy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I do wonder about what the reverberations will be like. This -- we should say that this -- he was -- he was again, as far as we know, keeping a very old-school blog. This was actually not on -- this was on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . It wasn`t on the social media networks. It was in the kind of forum that I frankly came up using, right? Just like a random person puts up a blog on the internet and has been doing that for many years.\par \par \uc2 But of course, like the question of this kind of trajectory is front of mind for basically anyone that`s in any position of power at any tech company right now dealing with content moderation.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Oh, yes, absolutely. Probably not quite so much Elon Musk --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Based on what he said recently. But it is. And that`s the thing is \uc2 Facebook\uc2  has whole teams dealing with this explicit kind of thing. Like what crosses the line, how do we -- how do we identify what exactly the line is, so people don`t get sort of shifted into that direction. You know, they realized they had -- they had a funneling problem with QAnon people and they tried -- they tried to cut it off at the stem. Elon Musk sounds like he doesn`t really care about that funnel at best, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Although, we should also say, look, people are capable radicalizing through all different kinds of means regardless of what social media platform they`re on.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Ben Collins, thank you so much for being with us and for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 Right now, almost everyone who works in government is living in the shadow of January 6 and the specter of this kind of physical intimidation, violence, menace, but my next two guests have more experience with it the most. Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  sits on the January 6 Committee. Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio Cortez\uc2  has been a right-wing target since she took office. Both join me right here on set next. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As the news first broke this morning that the husband of the Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was assaulted, I was reminded in one of the most indelible moments to have emerged from January 6 and the hearings into them. During the attack on the Capitol, Speaker Pelosi was ushered to an undisclosed secure location away from the Capitol, close but away, because her safety was threatened. And in that secure room she huddled with congressional leadership. Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  was also there, though she`s not in leadership, but she was brought to that room because of the specific and acute threats against her. They were worried for her safety.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi spent much of her time in that room on the phone with top government officials including with then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who himself was sheltering at a secure location inside the Capitol as the mob chanted it wanted to hang him. And as she spoke to him, you could just hear the concern in Speaker Pelosi is voice. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Mr. Vice President? Hi, yes, we`re OK. We`re here with Mr. Schumer, Mr. McConnell, the leadership House and Senate. And how are you? Oh, my goodness, where are you? God bless you. But are you in a very safe --it`s still not safe enough for us to go back. We were being told it could take days to clear the Capitol and that we should be moving everyone here to get the job done.\par \par \uc2 OK, that call is bad. OK, I worry about you being in that Capitol building. Don`t let anybody know where you are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Don`t let anybody know where you are. I think about that line a lot. January 6 is one of the worst examples of this trend of violence against members of Congress and the vice president. It`s hardly the first. Threats against member of Congress have more than doubled from the year \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took office. Look at that chart. Nearly 400o in 2017, nearly 10,000 threats just last year.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, two people who understand that threat more than perhaps anyone else, Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio Cortez\uc2  of New York who has been a very frequent target of right-wing threats since she was first elected nearly four years ago. And Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland who sits on the January 6 Committee. It`s great to have you guys here.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:18]\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just obviously, profoundly upsetting news. First, just your reaction to hearing this, particularly given your position.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: With tremendous concern for the Pelosi family, love to Paul Pelosi who is a very popular, beloved figure in our caucus, and the whole Plessy family. You know, I think everybody feels like it`s an assault on all of us. And, you know, these kinds of threats have been going up in the political violence. It obviously hit very close to home for the speaker`s family now.\par \par \uc2 And it`s terrifying in terms of what it bodes for America if we continue to go down this direction. So, I hope it will be an opportunity for both major political parties and people across the spectrum to denounce and renounce political violence clearly, because my fear is that the response to January 6 has been so ambivalent and muted in some quarters where there`s been attempts to minimize and trivialize and whitewash it, that it essentially condones political violence going forward.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Absolutely. And, you know, I just want to echo Representative Raskin`s just thoughts towards the Pelosi family. This is an extremely egregious, and just horrifying attack. But it also speaks to the level of vitriol that we are seeing, and that this is really reflective of an overall highly unstable, volatile political environment.\par \par \uc2 And it`s very connected and tracks very much with the rise in this violence that we`re seeing on the ground, everyday people, people who sit on school boards, people who want to serve on their PTAs, people who want to serve as you know, poll workers are also really experiencing these elevated threats of violence. And this is going and being reflected at the very, very top of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Can I -- I`m going to ask you a question, Congressman Cortez and -- Ocasio-Cortez, and you cannot answer it because sometimes security questions can be a little thorny, right, because you don`t want to give too much information. But I imagine that you in particular have a little bit of a hard road to walk here, which is at one level, like I said before, I don`t know if you saw the previous break, like it`s the great thing about American democracy. It`s like Congresspeople were just people.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And I remember like, the first time that I covered a member of Congress running and we were like in a bingo hall, you know, at 8:00 in the morning with a bunch of people who didn`t want a congressperson shaking their hand. They`re like, yes, yes, whatever. I`m looking for numbers, you know. I was like, yeah, this is what a democracy is. So, at the same time, you got to be accessible.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But then there`s also like, real security issues to deal with. And I`m just curious how you deal with that.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes, I mean, I think there is a perception that politics is like in this similar world of celebrity. And I think we have folks like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and things like that, that have certainly blurred those lines. But really, folks in politics are everyday people, and especially lately where, thankfully, in the Democratic Party, we`ve had a lot more working class. People actually be able to be elected in a system that has historically not been able to be accessible in that way.\par \par \uc2 But that also means that you`re a normal person and you come back to, you know, in my case, in New York City, I go back to my apartment in the Bronx. And there`s --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think you`re safe in the Bronx and someplace. I mean, that`s sort of the -- right, yes.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes, I feel that way too. I do. I feel that way too. And so, there is -- there are real concerns here, especially if we achieve what we want to achieve, which is more everyday people being elected to the \uc2 United States House of Representatives\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I -- we were wrestling of course with -- we were thinking about the monologue, right, and how we`re going to cover this today. And, you know, I`ll just disclose there`s sort of this tension because at one level, it`s like, I don`t want to do this thing where it`s like, they`re -- there are people who are terrible, you know, exclusively and, you know, it`s like, a guy took a gun to the Republican softball team and shut up -- like shot Steve Scalise, almost killed him.\par \par \uc2 There was a guy who said he turned himself in because he`s going to assassinate \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 . So, it`s not like there`s some ideological monopoly on this. And yet, but --\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It also feels like the difference is that on that side, there -- because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  chiefly, there is more tolerance for excuses for apologies, exhortations to threaten menace. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: You know, what we want to avoid is a situation where people are afraid to go into politics.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Because of threats to the themselves or their families, their friends, their neighbors. And you know, as Alex says, it`s especially dire at the local level with school board and city council and town council and so on.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:14]\par \par \uc2 And then generally, all of this engender is a climate of fear, which is not conducive to us engaging in the kind of political thinking and imagination about how to move America forward on everything from lowering prescription drug prices to addressing climate change to, you know, promoting the wonderful young generation that`s out there that`s so afraid about, you know, gun violence and COVID-19 and climate change. And this just deepens that climate of fear. So, we got to break that cycle, you know, the best that we can.\par \par \uc2 And you know, at this point, I don`t know that it helps for us to diagnose what the political or ideological provenance of it is. I mean, you can certainly look at it historically and there`s no doubt that political violence has a connection to the fascism and authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 But in America today, the important thing is for people all across the spectrum to denounce political violence and renounce political violence so we can have something like ordinary civil democracy again.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, the ordinary civil democracy is what I`m thinking of when I`m seeing these images out of Arizona, where these folks are. Standing outside these ballot drop boxes, it was just a state judge in Arizona, you know, sort of issued a ruling today saying it was OK that they`re doing this, they`re sort of outside the ballot drop boxes.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, they`re not doing anything violent. But they`re there watching some of them -- as you can see there, they`ve got their tactical vests on. And it`s like, that`s the perfect example. It`s like there`s the line of liberal democracy, in which we are a free and open society, we argue, and then like the people in the tactical vest by the ballot drop box field that are armed. That doesn`t feel like on the right side of that line.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: No.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: I mean, there`s a history of voter intimidation and voter discrimination and obstacles to the ballot, especially in African American and Latino communities.\par \par \uc2 And so, you`ve got a view the guy is showing up in, you know, camo gear, and masks as part of that history. This is an attempt to discourage people from going out to vote.\par \par \uc2 And it`s really critical in this election that every voter understand all of the different ways they can vote in their state, and that they do vote and they show no fear.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes, and I think, you know, I think it`s important to acknowledge the point that you said that political violence can come from all different parts of the political spectrum, while also really having to acknowledge a very central fact that reporting from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and from -- and even in terms of Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 Jamie and I sit and we -- he is the chair, and I`m the vice chair of the House Oversight Subcommittee on Civil Rights and Civil Liberties. And we`ve held hearings on this. And there is absolutely no doubt that the data shows that the vast majority of incidents of domestic terror come from white nationalism, and that we are really, truly facing an environment of fascism.\par \par \uc2 And in the United States of America, this type of intimidation at the polls brings us to Jim Crow, it brings us back and harkens back to a very unique form of American apartheid. That is not that long past ago.\par \par \uc2 And we have never fully healed from it. And those wounds threatened to rip right back open if we do not strongly defend democracy in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, the plaintiffs in that lawsuit cite, the 1871 \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  Act, which of course, is passed precisely because of voter intimidation, precisely of this type.\par \par \uc2 Now, the judge in that ruling very sort of fascinatingly, who knocks down and says it`s fine says, well, there`s no example of their targeting black or Latino minority voters here, which is true.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also like, I don`t know if like equal opportunity ballot intimidation passes constitutional muster where specified ballot intimidation doesn`t, which seems to be sort of the ruling here.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also like, it just feels there`s a level that we`ve walked off some platform of liberal democracy, free and open debate into this kind of menace and berserk.\par \par \uc2 And I guess the question is, like, how do you think about getting back onto the platform? Like how do you wrench that back in a society that has taken some real steps towards something other?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, I mean, what are the big political tectonic plates here? You know, there`s the central rhetoric on the right beyond big lie and election denialism is replacement theory, which reflects the anxiety that America sometime later on this century will be a society where whites are the biggest racial bloc, and yet, are not a majority in the country. And they`re playing on that fear.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:17]\par \par \uc2 And so, all of the electoral paranoia is really about the fact that the white block vote is not going to be able to win elections whenever deliberate white blocks form.\par \par \uc2 And the way to address that is through the recognition that the vast majority of Americans have already accepted the fact that we are the world`s greatest multiracial, multi-ethnic, multi-religious, constitutional democracy. And we know how to live together. And that`s what makes America great. That`s what makes us truly exceptional.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And I think -- I think the other thing, and I`ve watched Democrats struggle I think to find this message, and some do it better than others is, you know, this question of weaving together, this coalition of sort of solidarity across these lines of difference, which is that like, it doesn`t have to be zero-sum, like, I get the piece of pie, so you don`t, right?\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s particularly relevant it seems to me in the case of this these midterms, right? Because at one level, it`s like, it feels existential from the level of democracy. And I think it is, in some ways, but it`s also like, I was just talking to someone who works on a political campaign, he`s talking about being in \uc2 NYCHA\uc2  houses, which is public housing here.\par \par \uc2 And it`s like, he`s talking to people that can`t feed their kids. Right? That`s what comes to mind for them. And I guess the question to you is like, how do you message around this stuff in an environment where people are focused on real important material, immediate concerns, and also those concerns are being used to kind of pry people in such a way that they tipped the scales towards this political party that increasingly doesn`t believe in democracy?\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Well, I mean, I think what`s really important in the way that we message about this is talking about the racial realities of our economic issues, and the economic realities of racial issues. That these are things that are not -- are not distinct, or siloed.\par \par \uc2 If you are a Black American, if you are Black or Latino in the last 20, 30, 40, 50 years, you were disproportionately targeted for unjust incarceration.\par \par \uc2 Then, once that happened, particularly around the war on drugs, then once you have that on your record, you cannot access housing in the same way, you cannot get employed in the same way. You have all these structural barriers that really funnel you into being trapped in poverty.\par \par \uc2 When it talks about inflation. When we talk about redline, we talk about who can get a mortgage and who can`t, how it`s 10 times harder to be poor in America, or to be working class in America. And that -- and how that divide is growing.\par \par \uc2 All of these things are very interconnected. And they want us to think that they`re very separate that it`s either or.\par \par \uc2 And even when we talk about issues like inflation, a lot of this has to do with the massive consolidation of our markets and corporate greed. Our inflation is not going up due to government policies, inflation is going up due to Wall Street decisions.\par \par \uc2 And the idea that they can just squeeze us for every penny that we`re worth. And we can also say that and acknowledge the fact that that`s impacting some communities more than others. And that I think is how we can really emphasize this message.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I think it`s just really important to again center this fact that this idea that it`s either or is very dangerous. And the idea that emphasizing social equity is somehow a detriment to our democracies, playing into the hands of the folks that don`t want us to talk about either.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Look, we need a functioning democracy in order to deliver the economic goods to the people. I`m proud of the fact that it`s our party.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I don`t know if people believe that though.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, but it`s true.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Like, I think that -- I honestly think that people`s belief in that as a first principle --\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: I`ll give you a great example of it. I`ll give you two great examples of it.\par \par \uc2 OK, one, we just dramatically lowered prescription drug costs out of pocket prescription drug costs for millions of Americans in the Medicare program by saying that you`re -- that you`re limited to $2,000, to what you`re going to have to pay in a year.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re a diabetic on insulin shots to $35.00 a month, that`s going to save a lot of my constituents thousands and thousands of dollars a year, and we were able to save billions of dollars while we did it.\par \par \uc2 How did we do that? Well, we repealed a GOP special interest provision that Billy Tauzin, as chair of the Energy and Commerce had slipped in, which said, the government in the Medicare program cannot negotiate with big pharma for lower drug prices.\par \par \uc2 In other words, it was corporate socialism, where they got to dictate to the government and to the people what the prices would be.\par \par \uc2 We just finally after 10 years of struggle overturn that and we got Republicans campaigning all over America to repeal what we just did in order to save billions of dollars into lower everybody`s prescription drug costs. It`s the same thing with infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:05]\par \par \uc2 You know, I sat there for four years under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They had an infrastructure day, they had infrastructure week, they had infrastructure month. They just didn`t have an infrastructure bill, you know, and we got the bill done in President Biden`s first year in office.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s a $1.2 trillion investment in the roads and the highways in the bridges and the ports and airports and expanding broadband access in rural areas. We did that, we didn`t just talk about it, we delivered.\par \par \uc2 And so, look, does anybody think that it`s the autocrats of the world, like \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  or the kleptocrats like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who are going to deliver to the middle class. And they get in and what they do immediately is they cut taxes for the wealthiest people in society. That`s their agenda. And they do it every single time.\par \par \uc2 And you can look at it historically, the middle class in America prospers when Democrats are in control, not Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s pretty wild. They have -- I mean, they definitely have been -- they haven`t sort of led with this, but in recent last few weeks, you`ve heard Republicans try and talk about if they win the House, what they`ll do, and it`s like, hold the debt ceiling hostage, cut social spending, maybe cut Medicare and Social Security if we can and make the tax cuts permanent is like the guiding north star.\par \par \uc2 Like, when everything is wiped away in the end, that`s in the dollars and cents, that`s what they`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Yes. And they want to criminalize abortion at 15 weeks nationally. Kevin McCarthy, the House -- the House minority leader, who wants to become speaker, has come out in favor of criminalizing abortion at 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: So, they`re going to pay for all those babies?\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Exactly. And they know they won`t. No, they won`t. And they want to ensure that -- and not only did they say that they want to cut programs like Medicare, but that they want to -- and social security, but they want to hold the entire United States economy hostage by threatening to not raise the debt limit in order to force President Biden to cut Medicare.\par \par \uc2 And so, we want to talk about class issues. You will -- I mean, this will be so destructive for all of old -- all older adults in this country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The one thing I will say as a final point is that we`ll have you back on the show after the election. We`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 But if they --\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: We`re going to win, by the way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, I would -- that prediction is worth what he`s charging you for it. For the record, but we will have you -- we will -- we will have you back afterwards and talk about how to deal with the debt ceiling issue if that comes to that.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2 , Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , it`s so wonderful to have you both here. I really do appreciate it. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 OCASIO-CORTEZ: Of course.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, still to come, with the midterms, rapidly approaching, Steve Kornacki will join me with a big board right here in studio, that`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:46:44]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: At this exact time in just 11 days, I`m going to be right back here in this studio. Studio 6A for our big election night coverage with Rachel Maddow and the whole MSNBC team.\par \par \uc2 And as we watch the midterm results come in from across the country determine which party will end up in control of both Houses of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Now, everything is really tight right now. A lot of conflicting evidence about which way things are going particularly in certain states, certain races, so we`re going to try to process it all with the best person to process it all. Steve Kornacki joins me right here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:52]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right. We`re just 11 days into the midterms. What can we expect on election night? Steve Kornacki is here with his big, beautiful board to tell us what to look for, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, yes, with 10 days out, why don`t we start on the House side here? Republicans needing a gain of five seats to get control of the House. To give you a sense of what we have here is early in the night. Where should we be looking?\par \par \uc2 This is some of the earliest indicators we`re going to get an election night. So, we`ve got a couple of districts here. These are going to be early closing polls, we got some numbers to give you a sense.\par \par \uc2 One place to look. You can see it right here, Virginia, three districts in Virginia, the poll closing time in Virginia 7:00 p.m. So, this is one of the first readouts we`re going to get. And you can see the second district here. This is the Virginia Beach area. The Democrats, \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , she was part of that class of 2018 for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is the kind of district we`ll show you. In the presidential election, Biden won it under these new lines by two points. This is the kind of district that if Republicans are having anything like the kind of night that they`re expecting. This is a district they should win.\par \par \uc2 Biden only carried it by two in the governor`s race last year, Glenn Youngkin, the Republican won this district by 11 points.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans almost need to be winning a district like this I think to be having the kind of night that they`re hoping to have.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s take the seventh district. This is \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , another Democrat elected in 2018. This is a very different district geographically for her, used to be Richmond suburbs base. Now you can see it`s more into northern Virginia, getting into those D.C. excerpts. It`s more democratic. Biden won this thing by six points. Glenn Youngkin win this thing by five points.\par \par \uc2 If the Republicans are winning here, now you`re starting to see this isn`t just an average night for Republicans. This is starting to become a better night for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And then you can go one level further, the 10th district. This is right outside Washington, D.C. here. \uc2 Jennifer Wexton\uc2  the Democrat, you can see Biden won this by almost 20 points. This is -- you`re getting into the part of Virginia here that`s really trended towards the Democrats. This though went last year, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  in the governor`s race, less than two points was his margin.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He won it by less than two.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Won it, but yes, if this is close, and if the Republican is ever in contention in this district, this is a republic -- this signals Republican wave nationally.\par \par \uc2 So, Virginia is a place I think you could look early. There are a couple of others here I think to give you a sense. In Ohio, for instance, there`s a 7:30 poll closing time in Ohio, the first district of Ohio. Here`s a place --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is a fascinating one.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Democrats are playing offense here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They are.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: If Democrats are going to have any chance here to hold on to the House, this is exactly the kind of district they got to win this one.\par \par \uc2 So, to give you a sense. \uc2 Steve Chabot\uc2 , Republican incumbent and look at that. Whoa, Biden won the district by eight points. How did \uc2 Steve Chabot\uc2  ever get elected in a district Biden won by eight points? It was not the district he got elected in.\par \par \uc2 The redistricting in Ohio generally favored Republicans except for \uc2 Steve Chabot\uc2  because --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In fact, he was the sort of sacrificial lamb of their plan to maximize Republican seats in that state.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Correct. And because part of the rules they have to deal with in Ohio, Cincinnati has to be kept intact here. So, he`s -- that`s the more democratic part of it. There`s a Republican suburban county in here.\par \par \uc2 It adds up to Biden plus eight, so Chabot, this is the kind of see, if Democrats can pick this up, it`s a net gain for them. There`s a few like this on the map, they got to win all the seats like this, among other things to have a chance, but a 7:30 poll closing time in Ohio, so again, could get an early readout.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And we should say that Chabot is a long-serving incumbent, right? So, he`s got some -- part of the reason I think they put him in that seat was because they thought he could withstand this.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about the Senate races, which of course, are front of mind for a lot of us and what we`re going to be seeing on election night there.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes. So, I mean, look, we know the bottom line 50-50, Republicans need a net gain of one seat to get control of what we got here. We split it into two. Here`s your five most vulnerable Democratic-held seats right now. And here`s what the poll average looks like in those seats.\par \par \uc2 And the movement really, this week has been here in Georgia. You see \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in this poll average leading by a point right now over \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and remember in Georgia, it`s the runoff state. So you know, you got to get to 50 percent plus one on election night or it goes to a runoff, December 6th.\par \par \uc2 But basically, if the -- if the Republicans succeed in winning Georgia, or you know, maybe Warnock hangs on and wins, any seat here that Republicans pick up, the Democrats have to --\par \par \uc2 [20:55:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And so, all year Pennsylvania has loomed as the most logical place for that and Biden carried the state, Fetterman this summer was leading in the polls by nine points. We have seen that Fetterman number come down lower and lower and lower sitting there just over a point right no.\par \par \uc2 And I just think the big X factor -- the big X factor when it comes to control of the entire \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  may well be what`s the reaction in Pennsylvania to that debate this week.\par \par \uc2 Is it something, you know, that ends up costing Fetterman? Does it end up reaction being more positive to him?\par \par \uc2 Whatever it is, the Democrats, unless they run the table and all their seats, they`re going to need to win one and Pennsylvania looms as the most win.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And all the money is pouring in there. And I think the odds of a run-off here are also fairly high given that there`s some other folks on that ballot. Last time we all remember of course, both those seats, both to the run-off, both are up on January 5th 2021.\par \par \uc2 Steve Kornacki, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in just over an hour for even more in-depth over the midterms. "THE KORNACKI COUNTDOWN" tonight 10:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC stream -- MSNBC streaming on Peacock. Do not miss that. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In just the last few minutes while we were talking to Steve Kornacki, the San Francisco police started another press conference with an update in the details of the attack of Speaker Pelosi`s husband. There will of course be continuing coverage of that throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:07]\par \par \uc2 That is all for us tonight. Thank you so much for joining us here in studio 6A in New York.\par \par \uc2 "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221029eias00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for October 27, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell \par \uc2 6,753 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:36]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 It is candidate night here at THE LAST WORD. We have Senator Mark Kelly from Arizona, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  running for governor of Arizona, Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York running in New York, \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  running for governor in Texas. They all have one thing in common, one very important thing in common. Every one of them is running against an election denier.\par \par \uc2 The Republican candidate for governor in New York voted to overturn the presidential election as a Republican member of the House of Representatives. That is how pervasive this is on the Republican Party. You cannot escape it.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": Greg Abbott, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Kari Lake, these are important names to remember as you talk to the candidates that he will have tonight. Lawrence, that is a great group of people, and a very important time.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a good show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The first time that I heard that this was the most important election of our lives, it was in 1968, when I was way too young to vote and the Vietnam War was raging and there was a chance to stop that war. In the election of 1968. But Republican \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  won the presidency, and the war raged on, and many times since then we have been told that we are facing the most important election of our lives, and sometimes it felt that way.\par \par \uc2 Now we are being told that again. But this time, this time it is for a reason that we have never faced before in this country. It is the most important election of our lives because the losers of this election might not just be candidates. We might actually lose democracy in this election.\par \par \uc2 And then maybe you will be relieved of ever hearing again that this was the most important election of your life because the election will not matter in the future because America lost democracy in 2022.\par \par \uc2 In the 2020 election, we did not know before we voted that democracy itself was at stake. There was some rumblings about Republicans in some states thinking about how to deliver their electoral votes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  even if he came in second in that state. But there were no legal mechanisms for them to do that. Those scenarios were always legally impossible in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But the Republicans running for office now want to create legal processes for stealing elections. Let me say that again, because that sentence has never been an element of American politics before in our history. Republicans running for office now I want to create legal processes for stealing future elections. I know that I am stating the obvious for most of you when I say that!\par \par \uc2 We have all internalized the Republican campaign created by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to end democracy in America as a political fact of our lives. We have grown used to it. We may have internalized it, but we should never, ever get used to it.\par \par \uc2 We should always feel the shock when we say, when we hear it, and we need voters to experience that shock as a call to action, the action of voting. Democracy can be saved in this country by voting, and democracy can be lost in this country by not voting. It is as agonizingly simple as that.\par \par \uc2 If we lose democracy, it will be because people who believe in democracy choose not to vote. There are more people in this country who believe in democracy than the people who are trying to destroy democracy. But people who vote on the Democratic side of our ballots are