r \uc2 As we get closer to Election Day, we see that in the polling, the voters of Arizona believe that you did an overwhelming majority, they believe you did a very fair job of counting votes. They believe you are going to do a very fair job of making sure the votes are counted this year.\par \par \uc2 And yet, Republicans are running against -- very specifically against that majority view in Arizona. What is their strategy, how do they get to 51 percent of the vote and defined the way most voters think about these elections in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D), ARIZONA SECRETARY OF STATE: Well, Arizona instilling state, that is why you are seeing these races very close. Democracy is indeed on the ballot. And we have a lot of other issues on the ballot as well, reproductive health care, making Arizona more affordable for Arizonans, addressing our water crisis.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:06]\par \par \uc2 And my opponent has no plans to address any of these issues.\par \par \uc2 For folks can find our plans at katiehobbs.org and look at comprehensive ways to bring Arizonans together and address these issues. So, the polls are close, we are running a front campaign, and I believe at the end of the day, we are going to win this race. And at the same time, we are doing our jobs in the secretaries of state`s office to make sure that every single voter who is eligible has access to their voting rights.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the vigilantes hanging out outside the ballot boxes. We are making it loud and clear to voters that they have every right to cast their ballot free from interference and we are taking these reports seriously, and making it clear that voter intimidation is illegal.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I have never before told New York voters or, you know, Illinois voters of Massachusetts, that they have a strong stake in the outcome of a governor`s election in Arizona. It should not have come to this, but it has.\par \par \uc2 What might happen with a Republican governor of Arizona as we approach the next election and the next counting of electoral votes?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, you laid out the case very clearly of how dangerous it is if election deniers are elected in a lot of races, but particularly in the governor`s race in Arizona. The governor participates and certification of the election. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is supporting candidates like my opponent, Kari Lake, because he wants them to have those hands on the levels of power, be able to change the rules, change how electors are selected, or tossed out the electors of the voters choose, if they do not like the outcome.\par \par \uc2 He wants people who are going to do his will, and disregard the will of the voters. And that is what is at stake, and that is why these folks in Arizona, if they have the chance, could go against the will of the voters and affect the presidential election results in 2024.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I know there is a lot of other issues in this race, including the unique water issues that a state like Arizona has. But to pretend that this election is about something other than the future of democracy itself, it`s just simply deny the truth that we are seeing out there every day.\par \par \uc2 Arizona Secretary of State \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , candidate for governor, thank you so much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And today, President Biden was in Syracuse, New York, with my next guest, New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who is running against a Republican candidate for governor who voted to overturn the presidential election as a member of the House of Representatives. Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:32:56]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Democracy is also on the ballot in New York state where the Republican candidate for governor voted to overthrow the 