ment, and actually sent text messages to help outline and develop the origins of the big lie.\par \par \uc2 So that is what is on the ballot in New York as well. But I`m going to out with my record and talk about the jobs we just created. 50,000 jobs, that is extraordinary, it is a very exciting day here in New York.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know that region of New York, upstate New York has always been an economic policy challenge for the New York senators like \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , and yourself as governor, and governors before you. And so when you see the president actually showing up there, with a delivery of not just jobs, but high paying jobs, that kind of high paying job has historically been difficult to attract there.\par \par \uc2 What more can you do in that arena, especially in the upstate region?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: This is transformative for upstate. I am the first upstate governor in over 100 years. I am from Buffalo. When I was growing up, the unemployment rate for my dad -- he worked at a steel plant, the grandpa worked at the steel plant -- it was about 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 You know, when people grew up, they lost -- they didn`t have any opportunity for a single job. So for me personally, and the people who never give up on upstate New York, and stayed, this is a whole new day for our state. Because when a Micron selects New York state because of what President Biden, and Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  did in Washington, we would not have had this happen if \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  was not the majority leader. But they did in Washington meant we had incentives to come to America to build these semiconductor chips.\par \par \uc2 But I had to make sure that they came to New York. And it is one of the last things I did in our budget was put in a $10 billion benefits incentive package for all semiconductor companies to come here.\par \par \uc2 But today, it was not just about that announcement, the largest private sector investment in our state`s history, it is about the fact that this company is now under our law to be the greenest semiconductor factory in the world following our sustainability rules, and also giving money to the community for education, childcare, affordable housing, creating a pipeline for workforce development.\par \par \uc2 So our criteria says that we are not just going to give you money. No, no, you`re going to give -- we`ll give you incentives for every job you create. But you are going to also build up the whole ecosystem. And now more companies are looking at us already.\par \par \uc2 So what the president delivered today is extraordinary, and I can see this happening all over our country as we work our way through the 0vestiges of the pandemic, which was hard, but we are getting there, we are making real progress.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In the polling of issues of concern to New York voters, at the bottom of the list, at 6 percent now is abortion. And I am wondering if you think that is because New Yorkers take for granted that you will be able to hold on to the current abortion law in the state of New York, especially people in New York City, I think who can`t conceive of any of these things changing for them.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:50]\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Well none of us conceived that justices on the Supreme Court would overturn the law of the land as they stated in their confirmation hearings, that this was precedent, we would not do anything and all of a sudden, guess what? They overturned Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 So my opponent is fine just saying after the day after Dobbs, the decision that came down, nothing changed. I said no it didn`t change, because I am the governor and you are not.\par \par \uc2 Here are our opportunities for governor. In the budget, we have tremendous power to be able to influence policy or put in something that is anti choice, you can put it in a budget, and people do not want to shut down the government.\par \par \uc2 He has a lot of opportunity, so no one should believe that these are safe, if a Republican gets into this position. So that is on the ballot as far as I am concerned. But so is public safety, letting people know we are out there making sure that they are safe in our streets and subways.\par \par \uc2 As well as the economy, Letting people know that we know how to create jobs, we just did it today. We are doing it all over the state. So this is an exciting new era. And I think Democrats across the country just have to lean in to the accomplishments from Washington what we as elected officials in our states are doing, and all the rights to democracy, and reproductive freedom, and gun safety legislation are all on the ballot this year, and don`t anybody forget that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  of New York, thank you very much for joining us again tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. And coming up, one of the other champions of democracy and his campaign, \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  will join us from the campaign trail for governor of Texas, he will be joining us from San Antonio, next.\par \par \uc2 [22:41:24]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If Republicans win control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  they will not allow the confirmation of another federal judge appointed by President Biden. We will go at least two years with no federal judges being appointed and this has never happened before in our history but that is what they will do. They will not confirm another federal judge until the Republican Senate can confirm federal judges appointed by the next Republican president, however long it takes to get one.\par \par \uc2 And of course, all Republican appointed and confirmed federal judges will be in favor of banning all abortions with no exceptions for rape, or incest, or the life of the mother, just like all of the Republican appointed justices of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And those Republican appointed judges along with the Republican Senate will do everything within their power to make us all live the way the people of Texas are forced to live now under Republican governance.\par \par \uc2 A woman in Austin, Texas, nearly died from a bacterial infection because she was not sick enough for doctors to legally perform an abortion under Texas law. It took three days until Amanda Zurawski got sick enough to be admitted to the ICU with a life-threatening infection, as doctors, friends and family feared she might die.\par \par \uc2 In a magazine essay, she writes, "We still don`t know the extent of damage the wait or the infection had on my body. We don`t know yet whether the baby we want more than anything will ever be possible. Everything that happened after my cervix dilated was avoidable, and it never should have happened.\par \par \uc2 What`s worse is I`m not the only one. This will happen to many women, of all races, all ethnicities, all ages, all across the country if we don`t fight back."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, someone who is fighting back, Democratic nominee for governor, \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , he is on the campaign trail tonight in San Antonio. Thank you very much for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 And I want to begin with the theme of these candidates discussions tonight, and that is the fight to preserve democracy in America. And what Republican governance in Texas has done to try to limit democracy, to try to limit access to the vote, to limit the capacity to register to vote, and to cast your vote in Texas, and what that means for democracy in America.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE, DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE FOR TEXAS GOVERNOR: That is right. You don`t get the most extreme abortion ban in America, you don`t have permit- less carry, which has increased homicides and gun violence in and the state of Texas, and has made us the leader in school shootings. You don`t get a total collapse of our electricity grid, or teachers being hounded on their classroom, if you have a fully, functioning democracy.\par \par \uc2 Because these things are not what most of us in Texas want. But when you have attacks on the right to vote, and what Texas has now become, the hardest state in which to cast a ballot, or to be registered to participate in an election, these are the outcomes.\par \par \uc2 So, the only way to overcome this is to make sure that we reach those who are the targets of suppression and intimidation, bring them into this election and make sure that they help decide the outcome, and actually form the margin of victory when we win this.\par \par \uc2 Early voting is taking place now in Texas. It lasts through the 4th of November, and of course election day on the 8th. This is when we need people to come out and make this important decision to change Texas for the better.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:48]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Republicans are trying to make what they call crime, just this word crime, an issue in the campaign, they are trying to make it the top issue in all campaigns. The one crime that absolutely none of them ever mean when they are talking about crime, there are many crimes that they don`t mean, but the most prominent one they don`t mean is the kind of crime that happened in Uvalde, where someone walked into a school, and shot and killed as many people as the assault weapon would allow, and to kill as many children as possible.\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t count in the Republican accounting of crime, and the Republican Party has done everything they possibly can to make sure that those mass murderers are the best equipped mass murderers in the world. What can be changed about that in Texas?\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: It has been 22 weeks since those 19 kids and their two teachers were slaughtered in that classroom in Uvalde and in those 22 weeks, this governor has done absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 When we win, I`m going to bring Republicans and Democrats around the table, to at a minimum, raise the age of purchase for an AR 15 to 21 to ensure that we have universal background checks, and red flag laws so that someone has a firearm, and is threatening to use it as the shooter in Uvalde threatened to do before he ever walked into Robb Elementary, law enforcement will be able to intervene before it is too late.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, Democrats, gun owners, non gun owners, Texans, all agree on this. The only thing standing in the way is Greg Abbott, and that is why we have to replace him on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The economy, of course, is the other issue that is high in voters` minds. Inflation being something that governors can do literally nothing about in the effects of it, especially global inflation that we are in right now.\par \par \uc2 But what are the ways in which governance in Texas can improve the economy of Texas?\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: We are paying among the highest property taxes in America, in fact, the tax burden for the average Texan is greater than the tax burden for the average Californian. You have some of the highest electricity prices in the country. Greg Abbott literally is the greatest driver of inflation in the state.\par \par \uc2 So things like expanding Medicaid that bring $10 billion back of our federal income taxes, that will reduce the burden on the local property tax payer. Upping the share of state spending for public ad means that we pay teachers more and also reduce property taxes for homeowners and in doing common sense things like raising the minimum wage which is now $7.25 in Texas to something more livable like $15 an hour, investing in childcare, in universal pre-k. These are things that are not just great for families, and for our fellow Texans. They will supercharge this economy, and allow more people to live to their full potential, and make sure that this state realizes its promise.\par \par \uc2 There is so much more that we can do but again, we`re going to need change after eight years of Greg Abbott to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If you can get every Texan registered to vote who wants you to win, to actually show up and vote, you would win. Is there a strategy, a new strategy for getting those people who do not show up when you need them, to make the margin of difference here? Is there a new strategy for getting them to show up this time?\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: Absolutely. We had more than 95,000 people sign up at Beto4Texas.com and take on shifts to knock on the doors of those who are the very targets of suppression and intimidation.\par \par \uc2 It is literally the only way we are going to do this because if the same people, and only the same people keep showing up every election, including this one, we will get the same outcome. We have to bring in those who are the target of this suppression. Very often young voters, black and brown voters, and voters in communities of color. The very old and increasingly voters with disabilities. These are the voters that we have to reach.\par \par \uc2 And our volunteers are at their doors sharing this message about the great things that we can do on public education and creating better jobs in Texas, expanding Medicaid, restoring a woman`s right to make her own decisions about her own body, bringing those Texans in who want us to do better, but need a little help making sure that they actually vote in this election.\par \par \uc2 If we get that done, we win, and so we are asking for the help of every Texan out there, and everyone who cares about Texas to make sure that we turn out that winning majority and make those who`ve been drawn out of this democracy the very margin of victory that helps us to win it back on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , I know your voice is strained from the campaign trail, and I hope you can get some rest tonight, especially vocal rest.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight in this important discussion. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:58]\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Remember, democracy is on the ballot everywhere. That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:18]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221028eiar00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 27, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,258 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And that my friends is tonight`s "REIDOUT". Thank you all for joining us tonight. ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m just going to ask you straight up. Is the fix in? Is this a sign that the fix is in?\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R-PA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: That`s an attempt to have the fix in.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The pushers of the big lie reach full throat.\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , TRUMP ADVISER: You`re not going to have 2000 mules situation in these boxes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, Marc Elias on the multi-front fight against the anti- democratic threats across the country. Then, how the Georgia Senate race got somehow crazier.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I want the biggest night for the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  campaign to be tonight.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, did \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  just endorse her opponent in Alaska?\par \par \uc2 SARAH PALIN\uc2  (R-AK), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Maybe it`s kind of that Alaskan chick thing, you know.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And David Wallace Wells on the new climate reality coming into view and why there may actually be a case for optimism, when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Today, a January 6 insurrectionist, violent one, was sentenced to one of the longest prison sentences yet for someone who did what he did, for his role in the violent attack on the Capitol. His name is Albuquerque Head. He`s a Tennessee resident. And he was sentenced to seven and a half years in prison after pleading guilty to a felony charge of assaulting, resisting, or impeding an officer.\par \par \uc2 He assaulted police officers with a police shield before dragging DC police officer \uc2 Michael Fanone\uc2  into the crowd shouting I got one, a chilling disturbing moment that was captured by Fanone`s body camera.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ALBUQUERQUE HEAD, CAPITOL RIOTER: I`ve got one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Fanone urged the judge presiding over the case, Amy Berman Jackson, to sentence Head to the maximum possible, saying, "Show him the same mercy he showed me on January 6, which is none." During the sentencing, the judge talked about the larger threat to American democracy. "People need to understand that they can`t do this or anything like this again. They can`t try to force their will on the American people once the American people have already spoken at the ballot box. That`s the opposite of democracy. It`s tyranny. And the threat to democracy, the dark shadow of tyranny, unfortunately, has not gone away."\par \par \uc2 The dark shadow of tyranny, the threat still looms over all of us. Since 2016, the Republican Party has increasingly adopted Donald Trump`s vision of democracy, which only allows for two options in any given election, either Trump, fellow Republican wins, or the election was rigged.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Just make sure your vote gets counted. Make sure because the only way we`re going to lose this election is if the election is rigged. Remember that. It`s the only way we`re going to lose this election. So, we have to be very careful. Look, we have -- this is more than this election. That`s a big statement. The only way they`re gonna win is that way, and we can`t let that happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was August, OK. So, we all saw this front. He was doing this for years, right? We win or it`s rigged. That`s it. That lie is now the orthodoxy in most of the Republican Party. This time, they`re running that playbook without Trump on the ballot and it`s happening all over the country.\par \par \uc2 Today, for instance, a debate between the Trump-backed New Hampshire nominee for Senate, a guy named Don Bolduc, a guy who is a big lie adherent and proponent, push ludicrous conspiracies about people being bused in from out of state to vote for his Democratic opponent. That`s one that Trump himself was trumpeting back in 2016. Now, the audio is a little messed up, but you can still hear Buldoc`s nonsense.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R-NH), SENATE CANDIDATE: You have to make sure that school buses loaded with people at the polls don`t come in and vote.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just to be clear, you`re saying -- you`re claiming that buses full of voters who are not permitted to vote, you`re claiming that happens in New Hampshire?\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: I am claiming that is what Granite Staters telling me. And I`m saying we need to respond to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The perception totality. I`m not saying it happens. It`s just that other people say it happened and so we should respond to that, which is the fallback of every Republican across the country. But of course, people say that happened because people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tell them it happened. It`s just a nice little circle.\par \par \uc2 And of course, it`s not true but that`s the kind of rhetoric Republicans are pushing relentlessly in races across all 50 states, although particular attention is now focused on the Senate race between Trump-backed Republican TV doctor and New Jersey resident Mehmet Oz and Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who is currently the state`s lieutenant governor.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:08]\par \par \uc2 Now Republicans and right-wing media are already right now trying to sow doubt about the legitimacy of that race, including this Rolling Stones reports, perhaps not surprisingly, the ex-President himself. "In recent months, Trump has convened a series of in-person meetings and conference calls to discuss laying the groundwork to challenge the 2022 Midterm Election results. As one source who`s spoken to Trump about a legal battle over the Oz-Fetterman race puts it, Trump views a potential Midterm challenge is a dress rehearsal for 2024."\par \par \uc2 And the propaganda arm of the Republican Party is taking note with just a little under two weeks before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , HOST, FOX NEWS: Fetterman`s candidacy, like Joe Biden`s candidacy two years ago, tells us something pretty dark about the Democratic Party. What it tells us is the Democratic Party has such contempt for voters and for democracy itself, and so much confidence in its ownership of the media and of big tech, that it no longer has to try to win your votes.\par \par \uc2 And to prove it, they can even run mentally defective candidates who can barely speak and not only expect them to win but expect you to accept the outcome no matter how transparently absurd it is. On November 9th, they`ll be telling you that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  that 81 million votes in Pennsylvania and they`ll threaten to put you in jail if you don`t believe it. Why wouldn`t they do that? They work with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, if you could follow that right through the rhetorical knots and flourishes, first, it`s the exact same formulation as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , identical, we win or it`s rigged. Tucker is also saying that Trump won 2020. And that rant, all of them are precisely as unhinged as The Big Lie itself. The pro-Trump activist Tom Fitton and Republican podcaster Ted Cruz, who also happens to be a sitting senator, are both already joining the course baselessly casting aspersions on the early ballots in Pennsylvania. As is \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , another big lie proponent, a person who was at the Capitol on January 6, though didn`t go into it and also Pennsylvania`s Republican nominee for governor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your state`s Acting Secretary of State Lee Chapman just came out yesterday saying that she expects the vote count to last for several days, maybe even as long as a week because they can`t count some of those mail-in ballots early and that kind of thing. I`m just going to ask you straight up. Is the fix in? Is this a sign that the fix is in your mind?\par \par \uc2 MASTRIANO: That`s an attempt to have the fix in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, what`s the deal?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How is that even a conversation? And again, they call us election deniers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, and we just had -- we had this --\par \par \uc2 MASTRIANO: Right, they`re denying it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s true. Pennsylvania can sometimes take longer than other states to count its early mail-in ballots. But that`s because, stay with me now, the state is not allowed to start counting them until election day. If Republicans really thought that was a problem, the Republican-controlled Pennsylvania legislatures, which include \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  as a member, could change that. They could let the state start counting those early votes as they come in.\par \par \uc2 But they don`t want to do that, because they want to sow doubts in the results by seeing the influx of early votes, which are expected to favor the Democrats are some outlets illegitimate. The exact same playbook Trump has set up in front of all of us and ran all in front of us leading to January 6 back in 20 and 2021.\par \par \uc2 And Trump has made his voters so paranoid and delusional about the vote tallies, they are going to extreme measures to stop the fake threat of voter fraud. As the Associated Press reports in a truly remarkable bit of reporting, there`s one county in rural Nevada which is now hand-counting ballots, because they do not trust the voting machines after MAGA worlds spread possibly defamatory conspiracy theories about the machines switching votes to Biden in 2020, which of course were false.\par \par \uc2 But as the residents of Nye County are learning, there`s a reason we automate this task and use those machines. I quote here. Two groups of five spent about three hours each counting 50 ballots. Mismatch tallies led to recounts and occasionally, more recounts. Several noted how arduous the process was with one volunteer lamenting I can`t believe it`s two hours to get through 25 ballots.\par \par \uc2 A county New Mexico was also forced to recount primary ballots by hand after conspiracy-addled voters refuse to accept the machine tabulations. What these people will discover is the same people -- the same thing that people who ran that deranged audit in Arizona in 2020 discovered, which is there`s no meaningful instances of fraud and the machine calculations are incredibly accurate.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s be clear, election and election administration in this country can be unwieldy. I`m a voter in a state that takes a really long time to vote and it`s really annoying. It can be kind of a bumpy ride. But we do have free and fair elections for now. Those elections, though, and the people who administer them are explicitly under attack.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:11]\par \par \uc2 For example, the AP reports there have already been more than 100 lawsuits filed related the Midterm Elections, which has not even happened yet. The majority filed by Republicans targeting everything from mail-in ballots, to rules making easier to register to vote and, of course, the voting machines themselves. But there are ways to fight back.\par \par \uc2 Marc Elias is an attorney on the frontlines of that fight for American democracy. He`s an expert on voting rights litigation, the founder of The Democracy Docket. It`s great to have you here. I want to talk, Marc, first about a story that we`ve been covering a lot, which is these folks dressed up sometimes in tactical gear or sometimes with their cameras outside ballot drop boxes in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Federal lawsuit filed against one of the groups for doing that. The Arizona right-wing group abandoned it. In turn, I want to read from their -- what they said to their members. The hammer has unfortunately fallen. We the Lions of liberty have been named in a federal lawsuit. Due to this fact and possible restraining order, we are stepping down our sponsorship of Operation \uc2 Dropbox\uc2 . This seems like a welcome development amidst a lot of leaked news.\par \par \uc2 MARC ELIAS, FOUNDER, DEMOCRACY DOCKET: It is. But I think we need to keep in mind that this is one group. It`s great that they are now stopping violating federal voting rights laws, but there are others. And there are other law lawsuits going on and this lawsuit continuing in Arizona against other groups. And it`s not just Arizona, we`re seeing this as you point out in state after state after state.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing Republican and Republican allied organizations challenge absentee ballot voting, vote by mail, machine counting of ballots, voters ability to vote at all. And of course, the staking out of drop box. So. it`s it`s a little bit of happiness but there`s a lot to be worried about.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, what are -- where are you focused right now? I mean, the ballot drop box thing is just it`s sort of an outward sign, right, of the paranoia and the intimidation. That to me is very potent. But where are you focused your energies right now?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: So, right now, I`d love to say it`s focused in one or two places, but to put a little more meat on the bones of what AP reported, Democracy Docket tracks every piece of election litigation that goes on in the country, no matter -- whether it`s filed on the left or filed on the right, they track it. And there have been 156 separate pieces of litigation filed in this calendar year alone. That is across 34 states and 96 of them are still going on.\par \par \uc2 About 40 percent of all the litigation that is still pending in courts are actually brought by Republicans or right-wing organizations because they`re now turning to the courts to try to limit voting. So, it`s nationwide. It`s tactic, non-discriminatory in how they try to limit voting. And it even includes bringing litigation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, one of the things that we learned from January sixth from the Trump big lie in the run up, and then the election, that election night, and then the aftermath is there`s the law of elections, right, there`s democratic institutions, there are courts. Ultimately, the concession of the loser, or the agreement of both parties to accept an election as fair is the kind of binding institutional bedrock more than anything else. The other stuff really helps. And I just wonder how you think about that where these people are very clearly saying like, we`re not going to do that. We will say it`s rigged if we lose.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Yes, I always say that the most American of all times of year is when we vote. Election Day I remember as a child being a, you know, a great day. It was a day where you got to go to vote with your parents. And, you know, even in recent years, people wearing their I voted sticker. But it was following the elections that we saw the pageantry of democracy. And the pageantry of democracy began with those gracious concessions.\par \par \uc2 Then we saw in counties around America, the canvas board meetings that produce certificates, fancy certificates. Those fancy certificates would go to the state and the states would create even more fancy certificates saying who won the election with the vote tallies. And finally, there would be those big certificates of election or ascertainment that would go to the house and, and be in those wooden boxes and presidential elections or are hanging in senators offices signed by the Secretary of State and by the governor with calligraphy.\par \par \uc2 The pageantry of democracy is what makes America special. And it is the pageantry of democracy that Republicans are tarnishing, are trashing, and are trying to exploit and make it a vulnerability. And we`re going to need a system that relies less on that sort of goodwill that it builds through that system, but there will be a real law, a real loss of civic virtue when that happens.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:19]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And it also strikes me that trust is the key here, right? They`ve sow distrust and all these different institutions, are controlled by the left and media and big tech. The courts are everyone is out to get you. It`s all rigged. Such that there`s no -- there`s no final arbiter, right, who will be trusted. Like, for the folks in Knight County who are like we have to hand-count the ballots even if it takes us 90 hours to count three ballots. There`s no proclamation by any judge or justice appointed by whoever that is going to satisfy the people that have inspired the movement to do that. And that really is a problem, even though the courts are an important remedy here.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Right, but that`s the plan. That`s the -- that`s what the Republicans want. I mean, if you -- if you think about where we are today, you see the echo of 2020. You know, first you had the election-denying rhetoric before the election, the trashing of vote by mail, the sowing the seeds of distrust. Then you had to after that, you had the litigation that was further aimed at perpetuating what we then became all the big lie. And then to have the violence.\par \par \uc2 You have the violence on January 6 where failing all else, you saw Republicans and Donald Trump`s supporters stormed the nation`s capitol. Well, what are we seeing now, we`re already seeing the undermining of trust in elections, the bad-mouthing of democracy, the bad-mouthing of voting. We`re already seeing the frivolous litigation and the litigation aimed at creating the groundwork for the next big one.\par \par \uc2 And what you see in fortunately, in places like Arizona, with people armed standing at drop box and following people through parking lots, you`re seeing the beginnings of the echoes of January 6. So, I think it`s really important that we keep focused on just how little this country has healed since then.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, coming up -- thank you, Marc Elias. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Republicans attempt to consolidate total power in a state \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won just two years ago. Why it might not matter who wins or Wisconsin governor`s race next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You understand what the future could look like if Republicans control the levels of political power. Take a look at what`s happened in Wisconsin. So, Wisconsin is a quintessential purple state. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won it by about 20,000 votes and 2020. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won by less than 30,000 votes in 2016. What`s on -- Wisconsin currently has a Democratic governor, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , one Democratic senator, and one Republican senator. So, it`s about as closely split as a state can be.\par \par \uc2 But despite the state being basically a coin flip, Republicans have already locked up control of the state legislature, and they are very close to controlling super majorities in both chambers after Election Day. And that is thanks to one of the most aggressive partisan gerrymanders in the entire nation, maps that were written by, you guessed it, the same Republican legislature. Neat little trick.\par \par \uc2 As the Associated Press explains, "If Republicans can flip five seats in the assembly, and just won in the Senate, they`ll have two thirds majority they need. This projection of the upcoming assembly election shows Republicans winning 64 seats with two toss ups. If they win both toss ups, they get the supermajority. This projection of the Wisconsin Senate shows Republicans winning 23 seats, more than they need for a supermajority.\par \par \uc2 And if Wisconsin Republicans get their super majorities, it will not matter who the governor is, or at least not that much, because Republicans will be able to override any veto and pass whatever legislation they choose. Democrats will not have a recourse even if the state were to elect a Democratic governor statewide.\par \par \uc2 How did Wisconsin get this way? How did it become essentially a GOP laboratory for the dismantling of democracy? That`s exactly what Ari Berman just explored for Mother Jones. And Ari joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Ari, I just want to linger on the math here because I think it could take a second to sink in for people. So, let`s just go through it again. Like, if people show up this election day, and the votes -- people voting for Democrats or voting for Republicans at the state legislative level are basically 50-50. Like, the same amount of votes for each in the state in the aggregate. That 50-50 voting could still result in supermajorities in both houses.\par \par \uc2 ARI BERMAN, SENIOR REPORTER, MOTHER JONES: Yeah, that`s what`s so crazy, Chris, is basically, Wisconsin has created a system that is voter proof for Republicans, because just think about this stat. Democrats have to win a statewide vote by 12 points just to get a majority, a bare majority in the legislature. Republicans can get a majority in the legislature with just 44 percent of votes.\par \par \uc2 So, what we`ve seen over the last decade is that Democrats are getting a majority of votes or close to it in Wisconsin, but Republicans are getting huge majorities in the legislature. And now they`re on the verge of a supermajority, which would basically allowed them to totally nullify democracy in that state. So, there`s a lot of states where gerrymandering has skewed politics, but I think there`s no state where gerrymandering has skewed politics more than in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:11]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That number is crazy. So, they can win 44 percent of the vote statewide, and win a majority in the statehouse. And a 44 percent is a bad showing. Like, 44 percent, you get 44 percent election night, you`ve probably lost the election. But the way they`ve drawn the maps, the way they`ve packed Democrats into districts that are big -- you know, districts, they can win by huge margins, they`ve been able to pull this off so that even a relatively good night for Democrats where they win by plus two or plus three, they`re not going to win back the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: No. And we`ve already seen this. In 2018, Democrats won all five statewide races in Wisconsin, including for governor. They got 53 percent of the vote for the State Assembly, but they only got 36 percent of seats in the chamber. So that`s a huge discrepancy between votes. So, you basically have -- Democrats are right now the majority party in Wisconsin, but Republicans are about to be potentially the super-majority party in Wisconsin based on how the maps are drawn.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that led me to write this article is when -- there`s been all this talk about how gerrymandering hasn`t really screwed American politics, that doesn`t really have that big of an impact. And maybe in the U.S. House maps, that`s true. It has less of an impact than it has in the past. But gerrymandering is absolutely having an impact in Wisconsin, not just in terms of rigging the politics of the state but rigging the policies of the state, right, Chris? Because that`s why it really matters.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin can`t expand Medicaid. They can`t have background checks for guns. They have an 1849 abortion ban from when Wisconsin -- a year after Wisconsin became a state that makes no exceptions for rape or incest. They have all these policies because Republicans can do whatever they want despite 80 percent of voters believing otherwise.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this is why, as I understand it from the perspective of Wisconsin Democrats, that these state legislative races really matter, because one, stopping them from getting a super majority, alongside with the statewide races. And two, state Supreme Court races, there`s going to be a big one next year, to basically kind of get enough voters and win enough victories that you could start to dismantle this structure.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: That`s absolutely right. I mean, in a 5050 year, you`d assume that they could potentially get a majority in the legislature. That`s completely off the table. The only thing they could do now is stop Republicans from getting a supermajority by trying to win a few of these targeted races. There`s not that many, but winning the few that there are competitive.\par \par \uc2 And then you`re right, it`s all about the courts. Because remember, Republicans couldn`t do this alone. They have these maps in place because of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , and the conservative majority on the Wisconsin Supreme Court. Changing the structure of the courts and the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  is very difficult. Changing the structure of the Wisconsin Supreme Court is not nearly as hard. The election in 2023 will determine whether or not Wisconsin has a conservative or progressive majority on the Supreme Court. That`s going to go a long way to determining the policies of that state.\par \par \uc2 So, I know we`re all focused on 2022 right now for obvious reasons, but in Wisconsin, it`s not just 2022, it`s also 2023. It`s going to shape the politics of 2024.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, Wisconsin is a real like, flashing red light about the nature of American democracy. Ari Berman, who has been on the democracy beat longer than most, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Thanks so much, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, another woman accuses Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  of pushing her to get an abortion. Will Republicans continue to defend Walker with less than two weeks until election day? You probably know the answer, but that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the most astonishing developments in the run-up to the Midterms is that the Trump-backed candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  and Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , a guy who supports the national abortion ban, with no exceptions. He`s on the record saying that. He now faces allegations by two different women that he pressured them to get abortions, the very procedure that of course, he would have made illegal.\par \par \uc2 The latest allegations surfaced yesterday when an anonymous woman came forward claiming that Walker drove her to a clinic for an abortion in 1993. The woman`s lawyer, Gloria Allred, presented evidence that they said support her claim of a relationship with Walker, including an apparent voice message from him and a photograph of him clothed on a bed. She told reporters on Zoom that her allegation is not politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am a registered Independent, and I voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in both elections. I do not believe that Herschel is morally fit to be a U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, I should tell you we hear at NBC News have not independently verified this woman`s account. And all the material she presented, it didn`t provide specific evidence of the actual abortion, unlike the other woman, remember, who showed up check and also a receipt from the place where she got the abortion, right, as close as you can get to corroborating documentary evidence.\par \par \uc2 But we should also note that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not exactly disputing the facts here. His response is just more of the rote response he had last time. This is a lie and I`ve moved on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, ANCHOR, FOX NEWS: Another accuser has come forward. Anonymous, although she spoke to reporters today, saying that she -- that you pressured her to have an abortion and that you paid for it. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, that`s a lie. And I`ve said that`s a lie. And I hope people can see right now that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and the left will do whatever they can to win this seat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You`re saying you don`t know this person.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: What I`m saying this is a lot, and I have said it once and I`ve moved on, my campaign and moved on.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:09]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, of course, he`s moved on. He doesn`t want to talk about this. It`s of course not the first claim against Walker because just a few weeks ago, another woman alleged Walker paid for her abortion in 2009. Now, that woman`s allegation included an actual receipt from the abortion clinic, a $700 check dated a few days later, a card -- a get well soon card signed by \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  apparently.\par \par \uc2 Walker has acknowledged the check was his but denies it was for an abortion. He gives lots of people`s money. And so that`s the strategy this apparently not so pro-life candidate for Senate, deny move on. And here`s the thing, I don`t know, maybe none of this really matters. I mean, I think it matters at the margins. But what I think is really clear is that Republicans don`t care. Of course, they don`t care. They don`t actually believe what they say they believe about abortion being murder, or even if they do, they don`t care enough about that to hold to it.\par \par \uc2 Just today, Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, a guy who himself proposing a federal ban on abortions so that a woman at 17 weeks who has a fetus that is non-viable, is going to have to sit around and go septic before she can get a procedure. That same guy who wants that woman to go through that sheer horror was in Georgia campaigning with Walker. Listen to what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Graham: All this crap they`re throwing at you coming from Los Angeles 13 days before an election. All you got going for you is the people of Georgia. So, I`ve seen this movie before. Remember, Kevin, all right. Right at the end, all this stuff comes out. There`s another one and another one and another one.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I seen a movie before with \uc2 Roy Moore\uc2 . That was another one, and another one, and another one. But again, this is where the Republican Party is right now. They have created this hermetically steel universe where you can`t trust anyone that says anything negative about the guy you like, so they can throw their support behind a guy who say he`s pro-life who`s been accused now of pressuring two women to have an abortion and I don`t think it keeps anyone up at night. But it should keep the rest of us up at night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: With all of the 435 House seats up for reelection in less than two weeks, you can`t take any seats for granted. In fact, seats that were thought to be safely Democratic are suddenly hotly contested in a bunch of states. There`s also some surprises in the other direction. The biggest one probably comes from Alaska, pretty red state with one single at-large district for the whole state that has existed for 63 years.\par \par \uc2 And for nearly 50 of those years, this seat was held by one man, Republican \uc2 Don Young\uc2 , a legendary figure. When he died earlier this year, something shocking happened. A Democrat, a Mary Peltola ran for his seat in a special election held this summer, and she won. Now, there were two major factors that helped her victory. One was rank choice voting, which allows people to rank who their preferences are in lieu of a primary Republican-Democrat, and that whittle the field down to two candidates. This allows voters more nuanced choices.\par \par \uc2 Peltola ran an incredibly strong race. She was also helped by the other factor, the fact that she was running against two Republicans who absolutely despise each other, one is \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 , the former governor of Alaska and vice presidential nominee, the other is Nick Begich who worked on Young`s 2020 campaign and has a famous name in Alaska politics.\par \par \uc2 Now, the feud between Palin and Begich has been intense and very public. Last month, Palin called on Begich drop out, calling him delusional. This summer before the special election, Jim and Faye Palin, the father and stepmother of Sarah Palin`s ex-husband, Todd, remember him? They hosted an event at their home for Nick Begich.\par \par \uc2 And now these same three people, two Republicans and a Democrats are running for the same seat this fall. This is the normal election. They`re also running with libertarian newcomer. Now, if the Republican Party could squash the beef between Palin and Begich, they`d have a pretty good shot at winning the seat back, but it`s not looking likely. And Palin has not been shy about showing her adoration for Peltola, who`s won all kinds of fans across Alaska politics.\par \par \uc2 Listen to how Palin talked about Peltola, her Democratic rival, during their debate last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALIN: You know, it`s so funny that even in this race, people actually hold our friendship against us. So, it is heartening to get to stand beside somebody who gets it. Maybe it`s kind of that Alaskan chick thing, you know, tough Alaskans, commercial fish, were pregnant at the same time in office. I took some heat for that too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It almost sounds like an endorsement at the campaign event. Peltola has also scored some other major endorsements like from Alaska Republican Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , who said she will rank Peltola first and the late Congressman Young`s daughters. Some of his staff have also come out in support of Peltola. So, while Palin and Begich continue to beef, they have created the conditions where the Democratic candidate and the current Congresswoman, Mary Peltola, running on her pro-jobs, pro-choice, pro-fish and pro-family platform as a real shot at staying on as a Democratic Congresswoman from Alaska.\par \par \uc2 And Congresswoman Mary Peltola joins me now. Congressman, it`s great to have you on the -- on the program. I was describing earlier today in our -- in our meeting your agenda, the pro-choice, pro-jobs, pro-fishing, and pro- family, and someone said, well, what`s pro-fish? I was like, well, I think I know, but why don`t I ask you. What`s the pro-fish in your pro-fish agenda?\par \par \uc2 [20:45:10]\par \par \uc2 REP. MARY PELTOLA (D-AK): Well, the Alaskan economy is really built in part on the fishing industry. And my former job was with the Kuskokwim River Inter-Tribal Fish Commission. And unfortunately, we`ve had 13 years of serious declines of Chinook salmon. And we`ve had three years of very precipitous declines in chum salmon and silver salmon. So, reds are doing really well in Bristol Bay, but some of our other species are really in declined to the point where we`re not -- we`re catching maybe one percent of our need. And then the crab industry is now in the tank.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s what it`s really about, about low ocean productivity and how management is responding.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, it`s striking to hear you talk about that, because one of the things that`s happened to American politics just in the time that I`ve covered it is that it used to be much more regional, and much more local, the old Tip O`Neill, all politics is local, and it`s gotten much more nationalized, right? Everyone fights about big national fights. But it seems like you found a way to resist that. Both to resist the kind of national polarization and to talk about issues that are very Alaska- specific. Has that been intentional?\par \par \uc2 PELTOLA: Oh, absolutely. I don`t pretend to be a national politician or really invested in any platforms of any political parties. I`m an Alaskan, born and raised, lifelong Alaskan. And I think Alaskans really appreciate hearing from someone who doesn`t have a canned message.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s pretty striking. It`s also striking to see these cross- party endorsements. \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , of course, who is the Republican senator, she`s in the center of that caucus, meaning she`s sort of one of the most moderate members of the Republican caucus. How does that come about? Is that a product of the unique politics in your state or of the rank-choice voting system?\par \par \uc2 PELTOLA: I think it`s not really anything to do with rank-choice voting. I think that it really is a product of Alaska in terms of numbers of people. We`re a very small state. Leaders know that it only harms us and our state when we`re making enemies. We all -- the three of us are all lifelong Alaskans. We`ve been in leadership positions for over 20 years. And we just recognize that memories are long and friends come and go, but enemies are yours to keep and there`s just no sense in making enemies.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s so weird because I feel like I`m through the looking glass in way -- most politics really -- I mean, what you`re saying is just like politics 101. Like, try to be affable, try to create friendships, try to get people to like you, whatever their politics are. But it does seem like our politics is the opposite of reserve right now. So, it doesn`t seem like your state, there`s something different going on there.\par \par \uc2 PELTOLA: Well, and like you said, it didn`t used to be like this. When I was elected in 1998 and when I first was introduced to politics firsthand in 1996, there was so much more bipartisanship. At that time, we had Senator Ted Stevens who had been working with for Alaskans for decades. And he had such a close partnership and friendship with Senator Daniel in a way from Hawaii. And just on and on examples like that.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Young, my predecessor, was one of fewer than 50 people to lay in state. And \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was the person who made that happen. He had welcomed every single member of Congress as a freshman. And he had friendships that spanned across all party lines and all of the range of parties.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PELTOLA: I think we can -- it`s fair to say there are parties within parties now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. \uc2 Don Young\uc2  was absolutely one of a kind that anyone who ever interacted him will tell you. And so, it`s only fitting for Alaska to have a one-of-a-kind member representing them. Congresswoman Mary Peltola, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PELTOLA: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, there are many reasons to be concerned about climate change. We talked about it all the time on the show. But I`m happy to say they`re also like, genuine, concrete, tangible real reasons for hope. Things are moving in the right direction, an optimistic block about the environment next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Back in 2017, writer journalist named David Wallace Wells wrote this fantastic magazine piece, one of those magazine pieces that people just noticed and read and passed around. It was called The Uninhabitable Earth. And the piece later became a book. And it was a warning about the possibilities of our apocalyptic future, like what it would look like if things got as bad as they could, worsening wildfires, devastating floods, mass extinctions, and it was a call to action.\par \par \uc2 And now it`s five years later. And things are actually not quite as bad, looking quite as bad. And I`ll say this personally, for the first time in the 20 years that I`ve covered the climate catastrophe, there really, truly, actually is some relatively good news, thanks to the measures that have been taken in the last three to five years. And those have been reported on by the very same guy who wrote the Uninhabitable Earth.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is David Wallace-Wells whose new magazine piece in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is titled Beyond Catastrophe: A New Climate Reality is Coming Into View. Great to see you, David. You know, I`m a huge fan of your work. I was psyched to see this piece. What is the new reality coming into view?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WALLACE-WELLS, AUTHOR, UNINHABITABLE EARTH: Well, there are two stories happening at once and that`s a little tricky to keep in mind. The first one, the good news is that we used to think four or five degrees of warming was what was called business as usual this century. That was really, really quite that. We`re at about 1.2 now. So, three, four times as much warming as we have now.\par \par \uc2 Now, because of action, political mobilization, investments, we`re thinking business as usual is somewhere between two and three, so only about half as bad as we thought a few years ago. And there are chances if we move really quickly and really aggressively, we could even get below two. The bad news is the other part of the story is that we`ve lost the window of opportunity to keep temperature levels below 1.5 degrees, which is the global goal. So, the worst=case scenario is vanishingly unlikely now, but the best case outcomes are also impossible.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:42]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. So, you talk about it as sort of squeezing it, right? So, we -- and this makes sense, right? When you`re starting out the trip, there`s a wider array. And the further you go on this trajectory, right, the more you get hemmed in. We now -- the worst-case scenario, business as usual means no one does anything. There was a world -- I remember reading pieces, writing pieces where I was saying business as usual could be seven degrees.\par \par \uc2 And seven degrees is like that really is when people talk in apocalyptic terms. Like, seven degrees is probably not a habitable planet.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE-WELLS: At least around the equator.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, just -- you know. So, that`s like, really -- what has changed to get us from business as usual being apocalyptically bad to be as usual being very, very bad but half as bad as it was?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE-WELLS: There a bunch of different things. One is just that we`re no longer thinking we`re going to be burning much coal this century, like, especially in rich countries in the world. You know, maybe there`ll be a little bit more in China, in India, maybe the Ukraine war is going to produce a little bit more in Europe. But if we`re like imagining 10, 20 years from now, this is not a coal-based economy anymore.\par \par \uc2 Renewable technology has gotten so much cheaper. It`s fallen depending on what you want to measure, 90 percent in the last decade, that anybody making any long-term plans now is going to be making long term investments in renewables. And in part because of that, in part because of political mobilization the world over. We have global leaders who are really committed to this. They talk about climate now as a top shelf political priority, and they`re really making legislative policy as a result.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is the thing about the progress here. And I`ve used this metaphor before that people want progress to be like painting a fence where like, you know, at every moment how far you`ve gone. And it`s like getting a stuck jar lid off where like you press, you press, you press and you pass it around and it pops off, right? We`re not quite it pops off like everything`s great, but I do think that people haven`t internalized -- people don`t pay as careful attention as I think you do and I think as I do that, all this work, all this effort, all this mobilization, all these credits that are in past, Paris, all this stuff, really has moved the world energy mix in a better direction like appreciably and an unprecedented fashion.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE-WELLS: Yes, it`s really interesting. Five years ago, you would say nobody really appreciates the scale of this threat because the impacts hadn`t yet started. So, every time they looked out their window, they saw the world as it normally was and thought that would continue forever. And now, we`re seeing those impacts a lot more. And as a result, it`s sort of clouding what should be a pretty optimistic story, because we`re like, well, what about those wildfires? What about that drought in China? What about those heat waves? All of those things are unprecedented. And by the way, they`re gonna get considerably worse from here. But the end game looks still much better than we thought just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I want to, again, keep those both in the head because I don`t want to like -- it will get worse. There is much more warning already baked in. And we still need to make even faster changes, right? I`ve talked to people who are like, they temper that opportunity to be like, we`ve already snatched a bunch of the low-hanging fruit. So, it`s like, you know, solar power turned out to be get really cheap really fast and electric vehicles, but industrial processes flying, there`s all this stuff, hard problems to solve technically. Are we throwing the right amount of resources and brains at those hard problems?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE-WELLS: I think probably not yet. We`re starting to make investments in a lot of those areas, do a lot of R and D. But the thing I would keep in mind is that in order to keep temperatures to 1.5 degrees, which is I think, vanishingly unlikely, maybe even not possible at this point, means getting to zero emissions by 2050. Staying below two degrees, which will be a lot worse, but you know, still a better outcome than three or four. Staying below two degrees only requires us to get there by 2070 or 2080.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot more that we can do in the next 50 or 60 years than we can imagine doing in the next 28 years. And so, we need to be making those investments much more aggressively. For sure, we can`t just be doing the build out of renewables. We need to be developing new tech as well. But we have more time than we might have thought a few years ago, in part because we`re moving so quickly on solar and wind and E.V. technology, which is, you know, moving much faster than anybody really believe just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No one did. I mean, people in the space -- in the investment space, didn`t think so. I mean, the solar -- the solar cost, drop, and curve would have looked insane to almost anyone if you went back in time 15 years ago.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE-WELLS: I mean, and the \uc2 IEA\uc2  now says that solar power is the cheapest electricity in history. Other reports say that 90 percent of the world lives in places where solar power is cheaper than any other dirty power. This is like a whole new landscape. We`re going to be living in a whole new world. We already are. And it`s going to be a little bit better than the one we feared.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: David Wallace-Wells, thank you. I really, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 I`m thrilled to say that tomorrow, for the first time since 2020, I`m going to host a special edition of ALL IN with a live studio audience. We have some very special guest. Don`t forget to tune in tomorrow at 8:00 p.m. Eastern. I`m incredibly excited about that.\par \par \uc2 And that is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221028eiar00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 27, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ellison Barber, Tim Miller, Jonathan Allen, Vaughn  Hillyard, Tom Winter, Ryan Reilly, Keir Simmons, Morgan Radford \par \uc2 8,175 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 It`s all about the midterm mindset, both parties making critical decisions about deploying money and star power in these final few days. Right now, Democrats appear to be on defense, buying new ads and sending tough surrogates to blue states, and worrying about races they thought they had in the bag just months ago. Republicans meanwhile are trying to expand their territory, but they`ve got their own problems to deal with, starting with new allegations against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . We`ll go to Georgia live in just a minute where a Walker event just wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, President Biden trying to sell pessimistic Americans on his economic message pointing to new GDP numbers that are stronger than forecast as proof of things getting better. But with food and gas prices up 10 percent in a year, does anyone care about the GDP? I`ll ask White House Deputy Chief of Staff \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  when she joins me later in the show. And the mass exodus of healthcare workers just ahead of what`s expected to be a brutal COVID and flu season, the alarming statistics and what they mean for you, coming up.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the midterms and a deep sense of late campaign uncertainty gripping some of the closest races in the country. In Pennsylvania, some top Democrats are second-guessing John Fetterman`s decision to debate. Appearing in Pittsburgh yesterday, Fetterman admitted it wasn`t easy, but he didn`t apologize either.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATE CANDIDATE, PENNSYLVANIA: By January, I`m going to be feeling even better but he will still be a fraud.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is facing allegations from a second woman accusing him of paying for an abortion. Walker, who`s in a dead heat with \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  did exactly what he`s been doing and denied it all.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: Now we`ll just say right now, you know, guys, I`m done with this foolishness. I`ve already told people this is a lie. And I`m not going to entertain because you continue to carry a lie alone. And I also want to let you know I didn`t kill JFK either.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Ellison Barber who is on the road in Georgia, traveling from one Walker event to the next. Jonathan Allen is NBC`s senior national politics reporter. And Tim Miller is an MSNBC political analyst and writer at large for the Bulwark. It`s great to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 Ellison, I do want to hear your reaction from the event that you went to on his first one today. But you ask the one question I think everybody wants answered both to the candidate. You also asked it to the campaign. So tell us what that question was, and what was the response.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right. So, we were at that campaign stop in Diller, Georgia yesterday where \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  came over to cameras and made a statement about these latest allegations. After he finished speaking, denying the allegation, saying I`m done with this foolishness, I asked him, I said, can you unequivocally deny and say that you have never paid for anyone to have an abortion? He did not answer that question.\par \par \uc2 Now, the campaign had told us prior to Walker coming to make those statements that he wouldn`t take any questions after speaking with us. I asked the question anyway because this is in a public person running for public office at a public event, and it is our job to ask those questions, so we asked it. Walker didn`t answer. I then followed up with the campaign afterwards, asking them that same question.\par \par \uc2 I did not get a direct answer from the campaign either. Instead, what I got was a copy of the press release that they sent out with a transcript of what Walker had said at that event, the sound that you just played. It`s been interesting to see how some of this is playing out amongst Walker supporters.\par \par \uc2 The event we were at earlier this morning in Cumming, Georgia. It`s a weekday. Usually, you don`t have very large crowds at political events during the week. We`re about 12 days from the election if I`m keeping my days straight right now. And there was a fairly large crowd there were at least 200 people there to see \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and, in a dress, -- in an energetic crowd, and they seemed largely unfazed by these latest allegations, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you so much. I`ll let you go back and have your conversations with your producer who I assume is driving but I`m sure we`ll check in with you later on in the afternoon from that next event. Thank you so much, and hi to Dan.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Tim, I want to play something \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said during this morning`s Walker rally. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): Why are they so focused on Herschel? They`re afraid of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Because if \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wins, that means we`re not racist. And if you`re a Republican, aren`t you being tired of being called a racist all the time by everybody?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That does seem, Tim, to echo a playbook from one \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but I wonder what you make of that statement?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. I`m not sure that Lindsey understands the definition of what it means to be a racist.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 You can vote for a black person and still have some racist views. Those two things are not incompatible. But, yes, look this is something that -- this is why I think \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  won the -- or was unchallenged rather in the Republican primary. I think that let`s just be honest about this or that Republican strategist, Republican voters like the fact that he brings diversity to the ticket running for the Senate. I think they liked the fact that there are people who rightly called the Republican Party racist and bigoted for their corroborate -- or collaboration with the Trump administration. And this is a finger in the eye of those critics.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, that is not really a winning message for the suburban swing voters in Atlanta, Georgia that put \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , and \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2  over the top in 2020. And so, you know, I think that`s clearly just a base message, isn`t going to get him anywhere. Just one other thing having been a flak on the -- on Ellison`s report there about the abortion, one thing -- one thing that stands out to me is that the campaign isn`t answering the question about Herschel Walker`s abortions with anything beyond his own words because they don`t trust their own candidate.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`ve been in a situation before advising. He lied to them about their secret families. They don`t know what is true. They don`t know what else might come out. And so that`s why they`re not being clear in debunking or denying the allegations because they don`t know you know, what tomorrow might break on that front. And I just think that`s really telling about, you know, where the Republican Party is and having to cover for this guy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So that`s Georgia. Let me ask you, Tim, about Pennsylvania. There`s been lots of hand-wringing surrounding John Fetterman`s debate from some Democrats. But his campaign says he raised $2 million post-debate. And I want to play what \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  said on this network last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD): I was glad to see him get out there yesterday and put his positions on the table. I don`t think voters were looking for poetry, as you said. I think they just want to know what the positions of the candidates are on a range of issues. And he was very clear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So do you think, Tim, that the Democrats who are worried should be worried or hey, there`s nothing about you can do -- you can do about it now so all Democrats can and should do is support him that which is what I`m hearing from the other side of the Democrats I`m in touch with.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes. \uc2 Van Hollen\uc2  has a job to do so he`s putting a -- you know lipstick on a pig there. I think with that debate -- look, this is the reality. I -- like politics is not -- you know you can`t just care about people`s feelings in the sensitive thing. Whoever wins this race in Pennsylvania will be Senator for six years. I mean Dr. Oz is a puppet for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It`s a very important race. And I think that some Democrats are right to be frustrated, you know, by the fact that after \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  had this medical event, obviously, it`s not his fault. But you have to make a judgment for what`s right for the party, what`s right for the country, I think there would have been an opportunity, you know, maybe to go another direction if they were worried he wasn`t able to campaign.\par \par \uc2 And I think just kind of saying that, oh, it`s ableist or whatever to insult him, to criticize debate performance like that might be true. But you know, this is -- this -- the stakes are pretty high here. And so I do understand the frustration of Democrats who feel like they`re stuck between a rock and a hard place here. And, you know, maybe you know, the decision should have been different a few months back, but we certainly are we are right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Jonathan, we just talked about two races that are too close to call. And when we talk about late campaign uncertainty, it`s pretty much of a shocker from Arizona because Politico and Cook Political Report just turned. They say the Senate race there is now a tossup. What do you think is going on? Is it local to Arizona? Is it the national mood sweeping in? What`s happening?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, MSNBC SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER: It`s a great question, Chris. I think it`s probably a combination of those factors. Look, in Arizona, just like in many of the other western states, people are looking at gas pumps, and they`re looking at the price read out there and they`re not happy with it. They`re not happy about the prices at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 But in addition to that, you`ve got a situation here where Blake Masters, the senate Republican candidate, was not particularly well known before you`ve seen him debate. Now, you`ve seen his campaign commercials if you`re living in Arizona. You`re going to see consolidation of the Republican Party behind a candidate they didn`t really know before that they now have a better feel for. And of course, you know, part of this is dependent on Mark Kelly, the sitting United States Senator, the Democrat there, and you know, what his ability is going to be to fight those things.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan, thank you so much. Tim, you`re going to stick around.\par \par \uc2 Just minutes ago, we got some breaking news out of Arizona by the way. Police say an arrest has been made in connection with a break-in at the campaign headquarters of Democratic gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard joins me now from Phoenix. Vaughn, what can you tell us both about the break-in and about this arrest?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right, Chris, this breaking took place that we`re told on Monday night at the campaign headquarters in the heart of Central Phoenix of democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 The Phoenix PD responded to a call yesterday afternoon from the Hobbs campaign reporting this burglary. There were items we are told that were taken. It is not clear whether this was politically motivated or not. Neither the Hobbs` campaign nor the Phoenix PD are providing details about exactly what was taken from the campaign headquarters.\par \par \uc2 There were a couple of footage -- a couple of photographs that were taken from surveillance footage inside of this office that the Hobbs campaign believes is the individual here who has ultimately arrested, the person of interest in this -- in this case. At the same time, this individual was arrested and we are waiting for more details from the Phoenix PD, again, as to what exactly was stolen from the campaign headquarters and whether this was politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake pushed back explicitly last night, suggesting that anybody who would try to make the case that this was somehow politically connected or politically motivated, would essentially amounting to an unjust campaign that had no evidence or no actual substantive backing to it. Of course, that is why we are waiting and pressing the Phoenix PD to provide exactly what their investigation is led to at this point because we are just 12 days from this midterm election. And of course, when you hear a campaign headquarters being broken into, of course, there`s reason to have heightened attention and heightened concern as to what led that individual into that specific building, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, without a doubt. So, if you get any updates in this hour, please come back to us, Vaughn and we`ll get you on. Thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 For the second time in just seven years, one of the most powerful Democrats on Capitol Hill, New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  is under federal investigation. NBC`s Tom Winter is following this for us. Look, we know Menendez was investigated and indicted right on corruption and bribery charges, what several years ago. Never convicted --\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, MSNBC INVESTIGATION CORRESPONDENT: Correct.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Never convicted. What`s this about?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Right. His trial there ended in a mistrial with a hung jury. This investigation, we`re still trying to get our hands around, specifically, what it`s about, what it centers around, what types of things they might be looking into. You know, got a number of interesting questions on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as far as this idea that there`s these Justice Department guidelines, and how could this information be coming out so close to the election? Isn`t this prohibited the type of information that could come out?\par \par \uc2 So, a couple of things on that point. First off, it`s becoming clear to myself and my colleague, Jonathan Dietz, that this investigation is not something that started yesterday, not something that happened last week, this has been ongoing for some time, which means it would fall outside of the so-called 60-day window or a 90-day window, whatever it might be, where specific steps that would need to be taken, need to be run up the flagpole at the Department of Justice and improved at some of its very highest levels. But the most important part of this is it doesn`t apply to Senator Menendez. He`s not running for election. So, under the spirit in the -- in the rule of this so-called guideline, and it`s just a guideline, any information that would come out about this or announcement of an investigation rather, would not be something that would necessarily pertain to the senator and typical Justice Department guidelines.\par \par \uc2 Either way, it appears to us that this is -- this investigation has been going on for some time and that this is not something that was -- that came out specifically to try to jam up somebody who`s running for reelection because he`s not. As far as the Senator`s background, initially under \uc2 FBI\uc2  scrutiny starting in 2013, our colleague, Jonathan Dietz, broke the story in 2014 that he was under federal criminal investigation, and a whole host of different things he was ultimately charged in indicted in 2015 with Dr. Salomon Melgen, who is accused of being involved in a massive Medicare Medicaid fraud scheme. He was convicted of that, sentence to 17 years in jail. His prison sentence was commuted by former President Trump on his way outside of office. Who proposed that commutation? Senator \uc2 Robert Menendez\uc2 , he pushed for it.\par \par \uc2 So, we have no indication at this point that the doctor is involved in this at all but as we`ve said and we`ve talked about, this is somebody who is no stranger to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  into federal investigators. Where this case goes, whether or not there`s any charges or not, is something that is certainly going to take place in the future, they will know about it. And of course, Senator Menendez was steadfastly said that he was innocent of his charges last time. His spokesperson yesterday said look, we`re not aware of what the scope of this is. But if he`s contact by -- contacted by authorities, excuse me, he`s certainly happy to answer some questions.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Much more to come. Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Appreciate that. Now to a breaking news out of DC where a Tennessee man named Albuquerque Head has been sentenced to seven and a half years in prison for brutally assaulting DC police officer \uc2 Michael Fanone\uc2  during the January 6 riots. Here`s part of what Fanone experienced from his own bodycam on that day.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Before the sentencing, Fanone, who was seen here entering the courtroom, asked the judge to show Head the same mercy that he showed him on January 6, which he said was none. NBC justice reporter Ryan Reilly is live at the DC court. What more can you tell us, Ryan?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, MSNBC JUSTICE REPORTER: So, Albuquerque Head was the individual who actually dragged Mike Fanone into the crowd on January 6. And initially, he did it under the guise of telling Fanone that he was helping him. He said I`ll help you out. And then as he dragged him out, he said, I got one, I got one to the mob that eventually dragged him down the rest of the stairs. Another rioter, DJ Rodriguez drove a stun gun into Mike Fanone`s neck up, it triggered a heart attack for him, he lost consciousness, and it ultimately ended up in him leaving the police department.\par \par \uc2 Now, what Fanone said in court today is that you know why a lot of folks realize that he`s gotten a lot of notoriety now as a result of the January 6 attack and you know, the near murder that he suffered on January 6, he exchanged all that in exchange for going back -- being able to go back to policing. He`d much rather be in policing than be in the position that he`s in today. So, he did go into the court and encourage the judge to sentence this individual with the longest sentence available at the top end of the guidelines, which was 96 months, eight years, the judge ended up going just short of that, and sentencing Albuquerque Head to seven and a half years.\par \par \uc2 And has some very strong words about the lies that continue to be perpetuated by individuals about the 2020 election and said that the opposite of democracy is tyranny, and the threat -- the threat of tyranny is still very much alive today because of the -- these lies about the 2020 election. And encourage people who are spreading these lies about the 2020 election to think about the consequences of their actions, think about all the lives they`ve ruined, including the lives of the family members of many of the supporters who stormed the U.S. Capitol. There are some kids at home right now, because of what Albuquerque Head did, who aren`t going to have a dad for eight years. And if you want it -- she wants to emphasize just the consequences that have come about as a result of the lies that have been spread about the election in 2020, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan Reilly, thank you for that update from the court.\par \par \uc2 Pack of children`s hospitals dealing with an alarming surge in respiratory illness among kids. And now, a new report finds a startling number of healthcare workers are leaving their jobs. Plus, a promising new sign for the economy as big-name Democrats hit the campaign trail. I`ll talk to the White House deputy chief of staff about what they see as their winning economic message. And the announcement from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that Ron DeSantis`s ally calls "an elbow to Ron`s throat." You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Even as alarm bells are ringing over hospital overcrowding because of the tridemic, the surge of flu, COVID, and RSV, a new report exacerbates the bad news because it shows an estimated 333,000 healthcare providers left their jobs last year alone, couldn`t come at a worse time for the hospital industry. A number of pediatric beds filling up, it`s alarming. 87 percent full in Arizona, 89 percent full in Missouri, 91 in Texas, 99 percent full in Rhode Island. Here`s a parent who`s two year -- two-month- old newborn is struggling with RSV right now in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHEYANNE CHILDERS, MOTHER: Terrifying, terrifying, it`s a serious illness, you know, with him -- with him being this young. He doesn`t have an immune system to fight it off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in Dr. Ebony Hilton, who is an associate professor at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  focusing on anesthesiology and Critical Care Medicine. Doctor, a parent as you heard there, they`re terrified. So what can and should they be doing right now?\par \par \uc2 DR. EBONY HILTON, UNIVERSITY OF VIRGINIA ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR, ANESTHESIOLOGY & CRITICAL CARE MEDICINE: Right. You know, I -- my heart breaks for the mother. I would assume any parent can kind of feel that angst of what do I do? We know these children have very small narrow airways and it makes it difficult for even the slightest of holes for them to be able to breathe and it troubles you.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the signs that parents need to look out for and seek treatment is if your child has any different coloration, if their skin was more pale and or bluish, and or if you see they`re doing nasal flaring. So if your child is flaring out their nails -- their nails like this, that is suggesting that they cannot get air in right and they`re having difficulty in breathing. You definitely at that point need to take your child and to seek help.\par \par \uc2 But it`s one of those things that RSV is the most common cause of hospitalizations for infants. And in fact, each year, about 58,000 children are hospitalized for RSV, and unfortunately, 100 to 500 children die. And this is something that we as adults, we need to do our part in reducing the risk of these children getting infected by taking those precautions, wearing our mask, you know, keeping our hands clean because it`s not only just airborne, but it`s also droplets that we think contributes to the spread of this virus.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK, important information there. Now, I want to ask you about this massive churn in the healthcare industry that`s already exhausted. Some people who left during COVID did come back, some new people are coming in, obviously, as they graduate but these numbers are huge. What`s the real- world impact on the healthcare system? And do you think this slide can be stopped?\par \par \uc2 HILTON: Right. I -- we definitely are in a crisis. And this is not a crisis that`s necessarily new at the pandemic. We`ve known that we`ve had a shortage of physicians. And they`re entering into this era of where, by 2033, we`re estimating we`ll have a deficit of 139,000 physicians, 139,000. Now it took me 13 years after high school to get to the point of being a double board-certified anesthesiologist, so you can`t just create a person to replace us overnight.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 But at this stage, what we`re seeing is that two in every five physicians will be 65 years and older by the next decade, and they are going to retire, and we don`t have necessarily demand force to come and take their place. But we have an aging population and we also have a more sickly population. So, the things that we have to do now is to create those pathways, encourage kids to be interested in the STEM fields, but also increase our size of our medical schools, increase the size of our residencies in order to keep the population and match the population that we have to serve.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s so important. Dr. Ebony Hilton, it`s always great to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HILTON\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New GDP numbers show the U.S. economy could be headed in the right direction. The Biden administration already calling it a big win, but isn`t too late to impact voters? White House Deputy Chief of Staff \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  is here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some long-awaited good news on the economy coming in new today. The new GDP report that shows a 2.6 percent increase in all the goods and services produced over the last three months. That`s the first positive growth this year, and Wall Street is surging on the news.\par \par \uc2 For months Republican attacks on the deteriorating economy have tanked Democrats` poll numbers. Now the president and his Cabinet have some new messaging ammunition as they fan out across the country.\par \par \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  is the White House deputy chief of staff and she joins me now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you, Jen. Look, those GDP numbers are undeniably better than expected. But for the average American, if I can put it bluntly, do they care when their gas prices, their grocery prices, their housing prices, I guess what I`m saying is their everyday reality including worries about how much it`s going to cost to heat their homes are what they`re dealing with on a day-to-day basis?\par \par \uc2 JEN O`MALLEY DILLON\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Hey, Chris, thanks so much for having me. And look, I think that`s the exact right question. And the president has been talking about that this entire year. He has been fighting for and extremely focused on how to ensure that we`re making this economy work better for working families and middle class families. And there`s several parts to that. Certainly we`re very encouraged by the economic growth from today`s report.\par \par \uc2 He is out in Syracuse, New York, today talking about historic announcement from Micron, $100 billion of investment in Syracuse, the hundreds of thousands of jobs that are $100,000, good-paying wages, you know, looking at the investment in the community. And that`s part of what has come out of the CHIPS and Science Act that went forward and was passed earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 And then on top of that, as you`re saying, how do we ensure that we`re focusing on lowering costs to the American people? That was the signature part of the Inflation Reduction Act that went through this summer that the president has been talking about all 