l have a Democratic House and Senate. But he is talking about not just what he`s gotten done for the American people, but more than that. There`s more progress to be made.\par \par \uc2 He understands what it`s like to be in the situation they`re in. He knows it`s no solace to be having a lower inflation in the United States than other countries because they`re feeling it every day. And that`s why we`re continuing to focus on those impacts that they have around their table.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He also knows that -- and that`s why he`s been going out and going to continue to go out during this election season, that in order to deliver on many of the things that he`s talked about, he`s probably going to need Congress on his side, right? So he`s leading this massive surrogate push in the closing days of the campaign, stopping as you said in Syracuse. His Cabinet members are out in Maryland, North Carolina, Wisconsin, I think they`re in Michigan, even Texas.\par \par \uc2 But how are these visits going to change the trajectory of the race which is not looking good for Democrats right now?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: So, Chris, you know I`m limited in what I can say about politics. So what I can say is you`re exactly right, that his Cabinet is traveling all across the country, as he is as well, really bringing home the key elements of the bills and the legislation that have been passed, the executive orders that have been passed throughout the course of this year and really telling the story about what that means for them.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 And I think that has significant impact on how the American people and American families feel about that. You know, whether it is a groundbreaking or -- you know, President Biden and Lieutenant Governor Fetterman were in Pittsburgh I think just a week ago at the Fern Hollow Bridge, one that happened to have awfully collapsed on a day the president was traveling there. Less than a year later people and cars are going to be on that road in December because of the impact and the funds coming into the state from the Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill.\par \par \uc2 At the same time President Biden has been clear his entire career that you can get stuff done that help the American people. And that`s what he`s done. That`s what the Cabinet is out there talking about, about the economy, but also about the things that people are most worried about. His executive order on student debt in continuing to make sure that people have the information on how they can apply.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to continue to do that. The Cabinet is going to continue to do that. There`s no part of this country where that message and that storytelling will not be valuable because that`s what most impacts the American people.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: White House Deputy Chief of Staff \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , reminding what it was like to stand right where you`re standing with all of that equipment going on in the background, all the lawn care equipment.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Jen. Appreciate you taking the time to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 DILLON: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s continue this conversation with Tim Miller, who is back, former communications director for Jeb Bush`s 2016 campaign, writer-at- large at "The Bulwark," and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Tim, Republicans have a different view than we just heard from Jen, on those GDP numbers. They call it a ghost report. They warn any positive growth is a phantom, claiming the majority of Americans are bracing for what they call a cruel Biden recession. They can call it that. The numbers support Americans are worried about a recession.\par \par \uc2 At this late stage, can you sort of flood the campaign trail with a lot of surrogates and change enough minds, especially in these races where a fairly small number of votes per precinct could make a difference?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think that you can make a difference. I think that some of these races are going to be very close. You`re looking at the polls, and I think the question is, what is the most effective message for Democrats in order to bring that home?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And I just think listening to \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , everything she said was true, but it`s complicated, right? It`s complicated. It`s a hard message to explain to people that some of the macroeconomic factors are pretty good, you know, while their day-to-day bills are going up. It`s a lot easier to say inflation bad which is what the Republicans are doing as you see in that press release.\par \par \uc2 And so my recommendation to Democrats the last 12 days is go on offense against the Republicans who actually don`t want to fix inflation. The Republicans have no plans to try to fix the economy. In fact, I think that the Republicans would probably try to harm the economy because they would think it would help them in 2024 if they get in. They`ve already said that about the debt ceiling brinksmanship.\par \par \uc2 And so what you would see with a Republican Congress is someone that`s actively trying to sabotage the Biden economy while investigating \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And is that what voters really want? I don`t think so, but Republicans have done a good job of kind of not framing this choice like that. And I think that is the reframe I`d be focusing on if I was the Democrats in the last 12 days.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And of course Democrats aren`t alone in flooding the field. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has an incredible schedule coming up, lots and lots and lots of appearances. Most notably, can we talk about Florida? Because he`s going to appear at a rally for Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  on November 6th. And guess who wasn`t invited? The state`s governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , apparently.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" quotes a Republican consultant close to DeSantis saying the Sunday before the election day will be, quote, "totally hijacked by Trump parachuting in on Trump Force One, taking up the whole day. No Republican could go to a DeSantis event that day, none, and DeSantis won`t be here. This is big."\par \par \uc2 Is it big?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I think it`s noteworthy. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is nothing if not petty, Chris. We certainly have learned that over the last seven years. And I think that he`s concerned about the fact that DeSantis has not bent the knee to the same degree that some of the other potential contenders would for 2024. I think that, you know, there is some interesting parlor game analysis that we can do about what that means and how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sees him as a real threat for 2024.\par \par \uc2 I think the more relevant thing for 2022 is actually Trump having a very busy schedule is probably the best thing that could happen for Democrats over the last week and a half. I think the more voters are reminded of him and of the role that he might play in returning in 2024, the more helpful that will be for Democrats, particularly swing voters, if you look at states like Georgia and Arizona which could decide the Senate, and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 These are all states that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won narrowly. So I think getting Trump out there is probably a good thing for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tim Miller, always good to see you. Thank you for sticking around.\par \par \uc2 Massive protests in Iran showing no sign of slowing down.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:03]\par \par \uc2 The tactics that security forces are using to stop them.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: With shouts of freedom, thousands of Iranians are continuing the stand up to their government today. Many of them showing up at the grave of a woman who died after being arrested for allegedly violating the nation`s strict dress codes.\par \par \uc2 According to reports from an independent organization that covers human rights violations, security forces used teargas and live rounds to crack down on those protests.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Keir Simmons has more.\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, there. Good day to you. And we`re just getting more incredibly impactful images out of Iran today in the city of Mahabad in northwestern Iran. Protesters apparently targeted buildings, throwing stones. There seems to be teargas once again on those streets. Those videos posted by a Kurdish rights group that says that a number of government buildings have been taken over and that all of this today surrounds the funeral of a man who was shot dead during the protests yesterday.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:10]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): A powerful image seen around the world, a young woman not wearing a head scarf, stands on the roof of a car, hands in the air as crowds attempt to reach the grave of Mahsa Amini, and near the cemetery, this video from an independent Kurdish human rights organization captioned "The Resistance is Glorious."\par \par \uc2 Huge numbers across Iran determined to mourn and to protest the death of the 22-year-old, marking 40 days after she died in the hospital, the morality police targeting her for allegedly violating the country`s strict clothing code.\par \par \uc2 In the capital of Tehran, clashes with security forces and the sound of gunfire. In Mahsa`s hometown, they shouted women, life, freedom.\par \par \uc2 And these demonstrators daring to chant freedom, freedom, enough of the despotism.\par \par \uc2 Then images like this one, smoke rising, told of another violent crackdown there. Reports say live rounds were used and teargas.\par \par \uc2 The Iranian authorities switching off the internet in the city, but a semi- official news agency reporting at least 10,000 demonstrated. Thousands of miles away, the White House announcing sanctions targeting more than a dozen Iranian officials.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our message to Iran is very, very clear. Stop killing your people and stop sending weapons to Russia to kill Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : A dramatic change from earlier this year when the Biden administration was trying to negotiate a nuclear deal with Iran. But this morning there is no sign of compromising Tehran, and no backing down by these young female protesters, and the many men out on the streets in support.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : And just underscoring how incredibly tense it is in Iran now, 15 worshippers were killed at a mosque, a shooting completely unrelated to the protests. ISIS claiming responsibility for that. The government of Iran blaming foreign terrorists. But it just emphasizes how extraordinarily brave these protesters are, going out onto the streets day after day in support of Mahsa Amini.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Does it ever. Thank you so much, Keir Simmons, for that report.\par \par \uc2 Despite talks from world leaders and despite big announcements about sweeping change, a new report finds the climate crisis is getting much worse. Greenhouse gases are at their highest levels ever. And three gases responsible for climate change hit record highs in 2021. The levels of one of them, methane gas, is at its highest rate since the measuring started 40 years ago. The report comes just two weeks before world leaders are set to meet at the United Nations Climate Change Conference in Egypt.\par \par \uc2 Republicans making a big push with minority voters. How they`re spending millions in an attempt to move the needle in elections across the country. Fascinating story and it`s coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:58]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In a move that the Democrats have not matched, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  is putting millions of dollars into a strategy they hope will tip the scales just enough in some key places in November. Their target? Latinos. The fastest- growing racial and ethnic group of voters since the last midterms.\par \par \uc2 Here`s NBC`s Morgan Radford.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RADFORD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Here in Gwinnet County, just outside of Atlanta, David Martinez is all dressed up for a neighborhood event. But the organizer for this local get-together isn`t a person. It`s actually the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): So were you always involved in politics?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTINEZ, RNC OUTREACH CENTER VOLUNTEER, FORMER POLICE OFFICER: No. Actually I was never involved in politics. Until recently.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD: So are you saying this center, the creation of this center, sort of inspired you to get more involved in politics?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Yes. Yes, it did.\par \par \uc2 RADFORD (voice-over): This is the Hispanic Outreach Center, one of 37 minority outreach centers that the \uc2 RNC\uc2  has opened across the country in the last year and a half, focused on black, Asian and Native American voters along with 21 locations geared toward Hispanic voters alone.\par \par \uc2 It`s a plan the \uc2 RNC\uc2  says is working. While Democrats still have an advantage among Hispanic voters recent polls found a 14 percentage points swing towards Republicans since the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK ROCHA, SOLIDARITY STRATEGIES PRESIDENT, HOST OF "THE LATINO VOTE" PODCAST: Does it make me feel good that they`re doing it? No. Is it going to be super effective? I hope not. But it`s still worrisome for Democrats to see Republicans showing up in neighborhoods that used to be safely ours.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in Cristobal Alex, former president of the Latino Victory Project, also a former senior adviser to the Biden campaign.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you. So, look, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  says this is working. They say they`ve seen the movement. Do you think this kind of outreach can have an impact in these tight races?\par \par \uc2 CRISTOBAL ALEX, FORMER SENIOR ADVISER, BIDEN CAMPAIGN: Chris, it`s so great to see you. Thanks for having me on today. Look, this Republican strategy is twofold. As you just heard from Chuck Rocha, part of it is to just peel off enough voters for in a close election you can win and there are a number of close races right now, tossup races through Congress, even at the Senate level where you need about two-thirds of the Democrat, about two- thirds of the Latino vote to win in Arizona and Nevada and a number of other places.\par \par \uc2 The other part of the strategy here by Republicans isn`t just a short-term strategy to peel off voters but it`s a longer term strategy. They know the math and they see the demographics in this country are rapidly changing and they`re making investments. A good friend of mine has a saying, no beso, no say-so, and they want a say-so not just now but in 2024 and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Will it work? It might in a couple of places. But it doesn`t take away from the fact that a number of these Republican candidates are flawed and the policies that they are pushing would be devastating to the Latino community.\par \par \uc2 The last thing I`ll say about this, Chris, is the flip side is positive for Democrats. Number one, they`ve got an administration that I think is the most favorable administration we`ve ever seen towards Latinos, with very strong policies coming from the president and backed up by their greatest ambassador, Jill Biden, the first lady, who is in the Latino community all the time. And what they`re doing is talking about some of the big strides that we`ve made.\par \par \uc2 Latino unemployment is at the lowest rate it`s been in 50 years.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 As you heard earlier in the day from the jobs reports, from the Q3 reports, the economy is humming almost at full employment --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But let me ask you this, Cristobal, because I want to look at the facts on the ground because of the importance that you point out with the Latino voters, a lot of research has been going on just in the last couple of weeks and months. And it shows this. Latinos associate more strongly with Democratic positions, OK? But also, that support from Republican congressional candidates is up 10 percent. 10 percent over 2018.\par \par \uc2 Are Republicans just out-messaging the Democrats? Because they have a message to tell. Latinos believe in their policies more.\par \par \uc2 ALEX: Yes, it`s a great question. I think what we`re seeing is what you typically would see at this stage in the midterms cycle, where there`s a shift toward the party that`s not in power and voters are moving in that direction. Latinos aren`t that different in that sense than many others but you`re right, the Democratic policies are the ones that are better for the community which is why they favor those.\par \par \uc2 And again, speaking as a Latino father, I know when the schools reopened during COVID, that was a big deal. A quarter of all students in public schools are Latinos. If you look at what the administration has done with the record four Latino Cabinet members out there on the most important issues of our day, fighting for Latinos and fighting for all Americans every day, that makes a big difference in the minds of Latinos.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of work has to be done. And some of these races are getting closer and closer. It`s just a normal shift, I think, what we`re seeing in terms of these races being more competitive because both sides are putting a lot of money into it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cristobal Alex, great to see you. Thank you so much for coming on the program.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. Peter Alexander will pick up our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (C