 tonight, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Good show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 I could not live without champagne, in victory I deserve it, in defeat I need it.\par \par \uc2 That is a quote from \uc2 Winston Churchill\uc2 . And it`s from this 522 page book about Winston Churchill`s illnesses. Nothing but his illnesses. The name of the book is "Winston Churchill`s Illnesses", written by two British physicians.\par \par \uc2 The quotes about champagne comes from the chapter titled, was Churchill an alcoholic? The book was published in 2020 and using all of the latest analysis about alcohol addiction, the authors conclude that \uc2 Winston Churchill\uc2  exhibited none of the traits of alcoholism and that the image of Churchill as a very heavy drinker was much exaggerated including by himself, a note many close observers of the British prime minister, the book notes many close observers of the British prime minister, saying things like this:\par \par \uc2 Personally, throughout the time I knew him, I never saw him the worse for drink. The glass of weak whisky, like the cigars, was more a symbol than anything else, one glass lasted him for hours.\par \par \uc2 Churchill himself once wrote: I have been brought up and train to have the utmost contempt for people who got drunk.\par \par \uc2 So it turns out alcoholism was not one of Winston Churchill`s many, many health problems. It contributed to this book -- this book is filled with all of those health problems. And each chapter title describes a separate a major health event in Winston Churchill`s life.\par \par \uc2 Chapter titles like, chest pain during Christmas 1941 at the White House. Pneumonia in atrial fibrillation in December 1943 in Carthage. And acute stroke in June 1953 in London.\par \par \uc2 During all of those illnesses, including the stroke, \uc2 Winston Churchill\uc2  was serving as the now is still widely regarded greatest British prime minister in history. A British prime minister who rallied his country to continue to fight Hitler when it seems that all was lost.\par \par \uc2 When he suffered an acute stroke, Prime Minister Churchill simply retreated to his country house for a few months. When he made his first public speeches after his stroke, Churchill worried that he wouldn`t be able to stay standing through the speeches, but he did! And no one, no one, except his doctors and family, and very closest staff, knew that Churchill had a stroke. His cabinet did not know that the prime minister had a stroke.\par \par \uc2 When Churchill first mentioned the stroke publicly a year after he had that stroke, there was no controversy about it at all. \uc2 Winston Churchill\uc2  won World War II by strategizing with sometimes side-by-side the president of the United States who served longer than any other president and whose health was never good.\par \par \uc2 Franklin Delano Roosevelt once fell to the floor of his private railroad car, clutching his chest and agony in the company of only his son, James, who he told he didn`t think he was going to make it. He suffered unreported episodes but seemed like a minor strokes. He was ill for a long period of time and left the White House for long periods of time to recuperate.\par \par \uc2 Franklin Roosevelt\uc2  lost the ability to walk or even stand up at the age of 39 when he was struck with polio. He had been a political rising star in New York and many expected him to retire to the life of a country gentleman on this Hyde Park estate, paid for entirely by his mother and never work again. But Franklin Roosevelt recovered to the point where he won two elections for governor of New York and then won four presidential elections in a row. And he did all of that in a wheelchair.\par \par \uc2 He fakes the ability to stand for the occasionally big speech. But he would be clinging to the podium with the arm strength that he worked so hard to build up so that he could hold himself up.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 And he would be pouring with sweat as he held himself up with all of his arm strength. And he worked his way through those rare standing speeches that way. Pouring the kind of sweat that today`s HD television cameras would pick up and dwell on and perhaps make some observers say, it was painful to watch.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Franklin Roosevelt\uc2  and \uc2 Winston Churchill\uc2  had the good fortune to serve as the highest elected officials in their countries, long before television news to cover their every move and long before \uc2 Twitter\uc2  could have people pumping out of their instincts reactions to their public appearances.\par \par \uc2 Franklin Delano Roosevelt believed that it would be painful to watch him, being lifted in and out of a car by Secret Service agents who so he never allowed cameras to capture that. Elaborate steps were taken when his train would arrive or depart so that he could be transferred from the car to the train, lifted in and out, without anybody seeing and without anyone thinking that it was painful to watch.\par \par \uc2 Kids grew up in America during the FDR presidency not knowing that the president could not walk.\par \par \uc2 Caroline Morrell (ph) was an 11 year old growing up in Washington D.C. when her father, a lawyer with connections to the Roosevelt administration, brought her to a reception at the White House where, with no cameras present, President Roosevelt was rolled into the east room in his wheelchair. Caroline Morrell eyes widened, she talked her father`s sleep and looked up and whispered, I won`t tell anyone.\par \par \uc2 She felt she was in the presence about the president`s condition and she felt as most Americans did and as the press corps did way back then, that the president`s condition should be respected.\par \par \uc2 Eleven year old Caroline Morrell did not think that`s in her one lucky moment of being in the room of President Franklin Delano Roosevelt that he was painful to watch.\par \par \uc2 Painful to watch quickly became an off repeated phrase on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  last night during the Pennsylvania Senate campaign debate because Democratic candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  suffered a stroke this spring and has not fully recovered 100 percent of his verbal fluency.\par \par \uc2 Here is the first thing that he said in his debate with Republican Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I`m running to serve Pennsylvania. He`s running to use Pennsylvania. Here`s a man who spends more than $20 million of his own money to try to buy that seat.\par \par \uc2 I`m also having to talk about something called the Oz rule, that if he`s on TV, he`s lying. He did that during his career, on his TV show, he`s done that during his campaign about lying about our record here. And he`s also lying probably during this debate.\par \par \uc2 And let`s also talk about the elephant in the room. I had a stroke. He`s never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate, mush two words together, but it`ll knock me down, I`ll keep coming back up. And this campaign is all about, to me, is about fighting for everyone in Pennsylvania, that ever got knocked down. That needs to get back up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: While he was brilliantly winning World War II, Franklin Roosevelt once fell asleep in the oval office in the middle of a signing his name to a letter. That was not painful to watch because no one got to see that.\par \par \uc2 The painful to watch reviews last night seemed oddly disconnected to this country`s own experience with illness and elected office, including the experience of two members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  who had strokes this year. Democratic Senator \uc2 Ben Ray Lujan\uc2  of New Mexico suffered a stroke in January, he took time off and eventually came back to work.\par \par \uc2 Our first guest tonight, Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  of Maryland, suffered a stroke in May. He took time off, and he came back to work.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bernie Sanders had a heart attack three years ago when he was running for president and he took time off and went back to work not just as the United States senator, but he went back to running for president. And people continue to vote for Bernie Sanders for president after he had a heart attack running for president.\par \par \uc2 You don`t have to reach all the way back to Franklin Delano Roosevelt to find serious health problems in the White House, in the 21st century, we had a the vice president who had four heart attacks before he became vice president.\par \par \uc2 Dick Cheney`s first heart attack was when he was 37 years old! Vice President Cheney had a lifesaving heart surgery in 2005 that if it failed, would have made Republican House Speaker Dennis Hastert the next in line for the presidency, and years later, Dennis Hastert went to prison after confessing to the sexual abuse of high school kids when he was a high school wrestling coach.\par \par \uc2 The Senate is a deliberate of body. Senators never have to make quick irreversible decisions. They can take their time and think about each vote they cast in the Senate, they usually cast their votes in accordance with the positions they have taken in their campaigns. And so as with all such debates, the only thing that really matters and what was said last night is how we can expect the candidates to vote in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And so far, only one campaign ad has been produced, using the words of one of the candidates in last night`s debate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AD ANNOUNCER: This is who doctor oz wants and charge of women`s health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: I want women, doctors, local political leaders -- local political leaders -- local political leaders.\par \par \uc2 AD ANNOUNCER: Oz would let politicians like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  ban abortion without exceptions, even in cases of rape, incest, or life of the mother. Oz is too extreme for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: How painful was it to watch a physician who used to fully support every woman`s right to make their own reproductive choices, say last night in the hope of winning Donald Trump`s voters in Pennsylvania, that`s what he called local political leaders, should be making that choice for women. How painful was that to watch?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2  of Maryland. He`s a member of the \uc2 Senate Budget Committee\uc2  and the Appropriations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much for joining us tonight. You are something that very few senators have never been. You a member of a 50/50 Senate, and evenly split senate, the longest one that we have ever had.\par \par \uc2 What does it -- what would it mean for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  for the Democrats to flip that Senate seat in Pennsylvania that is now held by Republican, and have \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  join you in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS VAN HOLLEN\uc2  (D-MD): Well, Lawrence, it`s great to be with you, and thank you for playing that clip where Dr. Oz said that when it comes to reproductive freedom and choice, women should make up their own minds with their doctors and with those local officials. It was a very telling moment.\par \par \uc2 Look, it would be -- it would be great to have \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  as a member of the United States senate. As you know, we have a 50/50 split right now. We have a lot of contested seats where we have our own incumbents and we are fighting hard to keep those.\par \par \uc2 But what it would mean is another vote to work to try to codify Roe v. Wade nationally. It would be another vote to pass the John Lewis Voting Rights Act. It would be another bout to make sure that we have an economy to work for everybody instead of more tax cuts for the very rich and big corporations.\par \par \uc2 If the Republicans wanted to take the Senate and the House, we are going to see a reversal of the important gains we made including the reversal of the bill that we passed, to lower the cost of prescription drug drugs for seniors. They want to get rid of that.\par \par \uc2 They want to get rid of the minimum 15 percent tax on corporations, making sure that corporations pay their fair share, instead of zero, like many billion dollar corporations.\par \par \uc2 So, it would be great that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  will move forward a positive agenda. Also, we need to make sure that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is not in charge and not driving the country backwards.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Another issue that wasn`t a big issue in last night`s debate, which is so important to the every Senate race is the confirmation of federal judges. This has been going with you as part of the Democratic majority, the majority of one with the vice president`s vote in the Senate. And President Biden have set a record in the confirmation of a federal judges including one Supreme Court justice.\par \par \uc2 Do you think \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  would allow any, any more confirmations of any federal judges at the district court level, or the appeals court level, if the Republicans control the Senate?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:12]\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Lawrence, it`s very possible that process would come to a screeching halt. We saw what he did to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , reversing his previous position the position that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  had taken. And I certainly wouldn`t discount the very real possibility that it would put a break on Biden`s judicial appointments, which is another reason that we need to make sure that we have \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , because we have been able to appoint and approve a lot of judges, including Judge Jackson to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, look, with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , we know what his track record is. He says one thing when it`s in his interest to do it, and then he flip-flops. So, I think there`s a real danger that he could put the brakes on most if not all of those judicial appointments.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know having mentioned President Franklin Delano Roosevelt in the introduction of these subjects tonight. I want to refer back to him as also possibly the most brilliant legislative strategy certainly the most accomplished legislative strategist in the history of the presidency, created Social Security. And he talked about the design for Social Security was such that he believed as he put it, no down politician would be able to take it away I don`t think that he anticipated what the Republican Party would be in the 21st century where they seem determined with Ron Johnson and other Republican senators to take it away.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s exactly right. We are getting plenty of clues, in fact they are sort of screaming at us as to what they will do. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , of course, instructed his caucus there was no agenda, but he couldn`t control some of his senators letting the cat out of the bag.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  has said that he all federal programs that would include Social Security, Medicare. Every five years, Ron Johnson tried to do one better, and said he would do it every one year. The other thing, Lawrence, that we are seeing now is Kevin McCarthy, the would-be speaker, talking about playing games with the debt ceiling if they were to take power again -- in other words, preventing the \uc2 United States government\uc2  paying their bills on time, wreaking chaos in the economy, and threatening that they are going to create that chaos if we don`t make deep cuts in things like Social Security and Medicare. We`ve seen this movie before and when I heard Kevin McCarthy make that statement the other day, my ears went straight up, my antenna said this is going to be a replay of what they tried in 2011.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And you were there fighting that every day.\par \par \uc2 Senator, before you go quickly, any lingering affects of these drugs that you suffered earlier this year. How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : I`m feeling great. I`m back 100 percent. I`ve been lucky. Some strokes take longer to recover from than others. I`m really pleased that John Fetterman`s doctors say his prognosis is a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 And I was glad to see him get out there yesterday and put his positions on the table. I don`t think voters were looking for poetry as you said. I just think they want to know what the positions of the candidates are on a range of issues and he was very clear.\par \par \uc2 I`m looking forward to serving with him and we can compare notes on our experiences with the strokes. \uc2 Ben Ray Lujan\uc2 , as you said, another of our colleagues who also experience a stroke this year. And he`s doing fine.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Chris Van Hollen\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it!\par \par \uc2 VAN HOLLEN\uc2 : Good to be with you. Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Coming up, the latest on the criminal investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are next with former federal prosecutors Andrew Weissmann and Paul Butler.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:39]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There is a new reporting tonight on the Justice Department investigation of Donald Trump`s attempts to overturn the results of the 2020 election. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that Justice Department prosecutors have filed a motion that the federal judge to force former White House counsel, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , and his deputy, White House counsel Patrick Philbin, to answer grand jury questions.\par \par \uc2 "The Times" reports that prosecutors told the judge, quote, that their need for the evidence the mandate could provide should overcome Mr. Trump`s claims that the information is protected by attorney client and executive privilege, the people said. Mr. Cipollone, Mr. Trump`s former White House counsel, and Mr. Feldman, who served as this deputy initially appeared before the grand jury last month after receiving subpoenas, but declined to answer some of the questions prosecutors had about advice they gave to Mr. Trump or interactions they had with him in the chaotic post-election period, one of the people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Today, a South Carolina judge ruled that \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , Donald Trump`s final chief of staff must testify to the Fulton County special purpose gradually investigating the 2020 election. \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 ` lawyer said that he will appeal that decision.\par \par \uc2 Also today, attorneys for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accepted service of the subpoena that was issued by the January 6th select committee last week.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:01]\par \par \uc2 The subpoena sets a deadline for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to handle over documents to the committee by November 4th, and to appear for an interview with the committee on November 14th.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion of these points tonight is Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former chief of the criminal division in the Eastern District of New York. He`s a professor of practice at NYU Law School.\par \par \uc2 And MSNBC legal analyst Paul Butler is with us. He`s a professor of law at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , former federal prosecutor and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Weissmann, let`s go first to the question of getting the former White House counsel and deputy White House counsel to answer more questions under oath that they have already answered. And this is now going to require a ruling by a judge because it apparently touches on attorney- client privilege issues of some kind.\par \par \uc2 What is your reaction to what we know so far?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So I think that there are a number of things for people to keep an eye on. One, unlike \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  where you are going to see what you are seeing with \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , which is up a long drawn out fight that the prosecutors are going to have to engage and, to get the person into the grand jury. That is not what we are seeing with responsible lawyers like \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  and Patrick Philbin.\par \par \uc2 With the lawyers, what they really need is they can`t on their own wave executive privilege or wave attorney client privilege. So they needed a judge to say yes you can and must answer these questions. And so, I don`t think you are going to see these long appeals. They are just seeking court permission so that they don`t get an ethical trouble for answering questions.\par \par \uc2 And then far be it for me to correct "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", but the question of need is something that can overcome executive privilege. The prosecutors need for information, would not overcome attorney client privilege if it`s applied. But here, there is no attorney client privilege because these lawyers did not represent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  personally. They represented the office of the White House. And so they really shouldn`t be an attorney client issue.\par \par \uc2 And I fully expected the chief judge, \uc2 Beryl Howell\uc2 , has made pretty short work of this, because these are very straightforward issues.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Paul Butler, what do you see as the value that you can get from these witnesses?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So these two lawyers were in the room when the fake elector scheme was hatched up. They had conversations with people who are clearly targets like \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The D.C. Appeals Court has a case pending right now about Trump`s ability to make attorney-client arguments -- privilege arguments and executive privilege arguments, and Lawrence, the legal precedents is on the side of prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 President Nixon and Clinton both claims attorney-client privilege for the White House counsel and they lost. None of these privileges are absolute. Attorney-client privilege is the most protected, but none are supposed to be used to cover up crimes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Andrew Weissmann, they -- \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  is getting closer to that testimony in Georgia, where he`s under subpoena, that he has been fighting. But it looks like we are going to see an appeal in that.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Absolutely! \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  falls into a very different category and you can understand why the Georgia district attorney and why the federal government wants to speak to him because he basically is the man in the room for basically every possible facet of this game, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  seems to be a witness. He is making the phone call to \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  and to \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  on January 5th at the direction of the former president.\par \par \uc2 He`s on the call with Georgia, I mean, he is a wealth of information as we know from the testimony of Cassidy Hutchinson. So you could understand why the prosecutors are going to be like a dog with a bone in terms of litigating this, and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  as is his right, is going to be contesting the authority, both the state and the federal level.\par \par \uc2 But I think that somebody is really going to be looking to make a case on him and to just try and flip him. That really is the way forward on a case of the January 6th investigation.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Paul Butler, on the \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  subpoena, the district attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has time. In fact, she is in fact prevented from doing anything significant publicly as long as we are still within this window of the election.\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Yes, so she doesn`t have the deadline, the kind of loose deadline that the House Panel has if the House does go to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And Lawrence, remember, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  also refused to testify before that house January 6th panel, and Congress held him in contempt. But Meadows then turned over documents that were helpful to the committee and that`s when the Department of Justice declined to prosecute him under federal law.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann and Paul Butler, thank you both very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up early voting is underway in Wisconsin where the polls show a statistical tie, a tie in the Senate race between Ron Johnson and our next guest, Lieutenant Governor Mandala Barnes. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:45]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today was the second day of early voting in Wisconsin where incumbent Republican Senator Ron Johnson and his Democratic challenger, Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes, are running in a tie in that state`s Senate race. According to the polls both Senator Johnson and Mandela Barnes held campaign events after polls opened on Tuesday. During one stop, Senator Johnson who has been implicated in Donald Trump`s plot to overturn the presidential election touted a new Web site allowing people to report suspected voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 As voters head to polls, Democrats are reminding them of what a Republican controlled Congress could look like. That includes a proposal by some Republican senators, including Ron Johnson, to cut Social Security and Medicare, and cut them repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 Deputy White House chief of staff \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  appeared on this program last night to respond to that position.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN O`MALLEY DILLON\uc2 , DEPUTY WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: You hear from Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . He`s going to put Social Security and Medicare on the chopping block every five years and veterans benefits and more. Senator Ron Johnson says you know what five years isn`t enough, we are going to do it every year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: This week, Wisconsin TV station WKOW delivered this report about Senator Johnson`s tax compliance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: "Senator Ron Johnson has never been bashful about his support for tax cuts or his disagreement with the idea they`re skewed for the rich.\par \par \uc2 Democrats feel differently. They see something deeply unfair when they look at the state tax records showing the trust fund Johnson set up for his kids, paid zero dollars in state taxes going back to 2016.\par \par \uc2 Between 2011 and 2015, the trust fund had state tax bills ranging between from $400,000 and $920,000. Then it went to zero.\par \par \uc2 The Johnson campaign says it`s because of Wisconsin`s manufacturing and Ag tax credit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Wisconsin Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes, who`s the Democratic candidate -- for Senate in Wisconsin. And you are running now in a statistical tie in the closing days. This race could not be tighter.\par \par \uc2 What do you think -- what do you believe will be the decisive issue for voters in these closing days?\par \par \uc2 MANDELA BARNES (D-WI), WISCONSIN SENATE CANDIDATE: I`ll tell you there`s so many to pick from, especially given the fact that for the last 12 years, Ron Johnson has left everybody behind. We started off this campaign with no amount of (INAUDIBLE) in the middle class, we need to get rid of out of touch politicians like Ron Johnson who are making life harder for everybody else, but easier for themselves and other folks in the tax bracket.\par \par \uc2 People are tired of politicians like Ron Johnson getting over and using every trick in the book, to lower his own tax to zero dollars for his multi million dollar company while working families continue to struggle with rising cost of the gas pumping at the grocery stores.\par \par \uc2 He`s been against relief for working families like making the child tax credit permanent. He`s a person who is so out of touch (INAUDIBLE). The reality is that Ron Johnson`s position on abortion is too out of touch and extreme for the 70 percent of people in this state that made Roe versus Wade should be the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 So it comes down to a whole lot. And we keep telling them this final stretch. If your viewers will go to MandelaBarnes.com to help us out, we can send Ron Johnson back.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Social Security administration sends out in effect statements to everyone participating in Social Security, long before they receive retirement benefits, projecting for them exactly what their retirement benefit will be if their income continues at the level that it is at.\par \par \uc2 What would it mean to people in Wisconsin to end up with Social Security benefits, dramatically less, than the Social Security administration currently projects for them because Ron Johnson and other Republicans want to cut those benefits?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Not only will Ron Johnson put Social Security and Medicare on the chopping block that benefits people their entire lives` work, he refuses to tax ultra (INAUDIBLE) he refused to make them pay their fair share to help these beneficial programs stay afloat.\par \par \uc2 Ron Johnson could not care less about working people. His contempt for the rest of us is showing every step of the way. And groups like the Alliance for Retired (INAUDIBLE) that endorsed his campaign because my plan makes sure that folks can retire with some comfort and with some stability.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:47]\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Now if you are an ultra wealthy person in this country, sure you have a Senator Ron Johnson. Working people are not so fortunate right now. That`s what this campaign is about. It`s about applying the opportunity for the rest of us -- the rest of us that Ron Johnson never care about because he`s had 12 years of proving it, he certainly hasn`t.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Would you try to explain to Wisconsin voters what`s the difference will be in having Democrats in control of the Senate or having Republicans in control of the Senate. What is the number one distinction that you draw for them?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well right now the contrast couldn`t be more clear. They`re a person who spent his entire career looking out for himself, and a person who comes from the working class background and have been my whole life working to a good quality of life. Taken all our biggest challenges, recognizes our biggest challenges, also give us our greatest opportunities.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s creating tens of thousands of good paying jobs (INAUDIBLE) by addressing the climate crisis. Whether it`s making sure that every single person has health care.\par \par \uc2 But Ron Johnson still wants to repeal the Affordable Care Act, making life tougher for people. He`s against Medicare, (INAUDIBLE) prescription drug costs. He`s also against capping the cost of insulin.\par \par \uc2 I am for making sure that people have a decent quality of life. Folks have experienced some of the worst over the last couple of years. Ron Johnson is nowhere to be found. Over these last few years, we`ve been doing the work that he refuses to do. And if he refuses to do that work, I`ll be more than happy to be in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  to (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Mandela Barnes, Democratic candidate for Senate in Wisconsin, where the race is currently tied and where Mandela Barnes won`t really be getting any real night sleep until the election. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up California Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  is running for reelection in a reconfigured congressional district and she will tell us what she thinks is at stake in which party will control the House of Representatives after this election. Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:41:50]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Early voting is officially underway and 40 states. In California, over 1.5 million mail-in ballots have already been sent. Our next guest, Democratic Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , is seeking reelection to Congress this time from California`s newly-drawn 47th district.\par \par \uc2 Last week Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  showed how easy it is to vote in California.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2  (D-CA): I`m \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  and I represent Orange County in Congress. There`s an election on November 8th. Let me show you how simple it is to vote here in California.\par \par \uc2 All registered voters were sent a ballot through the mail. It is so important we make our voices heard in this election. Control of the House and Senate are at stake and that means abortion rights, climate action, gun violence prevention and accountability are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Once you cast your vote, seal and sign the envelop. I`m headed to my nearest drop box which is about five minutes from my house to drop off my ballot. Orange County voters can find drop box locations at OCVote.gov. You can also send your ballot back through the mail. No stamps needed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  of California. She`s a member of the House Oversight Committee and the deputy chair of the House Progressive Caucus. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I want to begin with the bigger question of what does it mean to the country on which party controls the House of Representatives next year after this election?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: The stakes are incredibly high. They are high for women`s reproductive freedom, for abortion rights, they are high for our climate crisis. But they`re also high for issues that maybe aren`t getting as much attention including the future of Social Security and Medicare. Programs that all working Americans rely on.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I have the ballot that you just showed. I have it at home on the kitchen table, it has been there for almost a week now. I know what I`m going to do on the big offices. I always know what I`m going to do in the big offices. But that ballot, as you know, goes down into the state legislative offices.\par \par \uc2 In California it has judges, not all states elect judges, but California does. And many of us get a little lost the farther down that ballot we go.\par \par \uc2 How important is it when we are reaching down there below the federal offices to vote for those state legislative offices?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: It`s incredibly important to vote down the ballot because to solve most of the problems that Americans care about, including problems like the climate crisis, health care, housing affordability, we have to have the right leaders at the federal, state, county, and local level.\par \par \uc2 And I think there were a lot of moments during the pandemic that really showed that where we provided funds to states and counties, they could invest those resources in helping to address things like hunger, making sure we can keep firefighters and law enforcement working.\par \par \uc2 So we have to have the right partners, climate change is for example not something you can solve in any one level of government.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:37]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Talk about inflation which all polls are showing is the number one issue that is on swing voters` minds. I mean one thing we have to always stress about this is inflation doesn`t matter at all to people whose mind was made up on how they were going to vote before the inflation happened which is most voters.\par \par \uc2 But those swing voters who will end up deciding this seem to be saying that this is their number one issue. What do you say to them when you knock on doors?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, voters don`t like getting cheated and they don`t like getting lied to. And that`s what`s happening right now. They are getting price gouge, getting overcharged at the grocery store, at the pump for so many goods and services, and then corporations are telling them, oh, it`s a supply chain issue. Oh, it is not our fault.\par \par \uc2 But the proof is in the pudding. About half of what we are seeing in corporate profit margins right now is coming from those boost to the bottom line. So about half of the inflationary effect is coming from corporate profit margins. And the other half is the added cost of input or the added cost of labor. And so I think Americans sense this. They are filling up at the pump and seeing prices go up and down. We have CEOs saying on earnings calls, CEOs of multinational global corporations, saying on earnings calls things like, quote, "a little bit of inflation is good because it lets them raise their prices."\par \par \uc2 And so I think consumers feel that and they are frustrated about it and they want to have the party, they want to have leaders who are straight shooters about what is going on and have the courage to stand up to powerful corporate interests to fight back.\par \par \uc2 I`ve had a lot of conversations like that at the doors in the last couple of days, I was out knocking today.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to take a look at the conversation you had in committee about this very subject with an economist as a witness. Let`s listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PORTER: According to this chart what is the biggest driver of inflation during the pandemic? The blue is the -- the dark blue is the recent period.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It would be corporate profits.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: And what is that percentage?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is 54 percent and that number does stay that level of high if you update that number to more recent numbers as well.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: So over half of the increased prices people are paying are coming from increases in corporate profits?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, the unit price index is reflected in corporate profits as opposed to other costs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What do your colleagues say when you make these points? You make them in a way that it seems translatable to the campaign arena. Do your colleagues use some of these points the way you described them?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I certainly hope so because look, voters want to hear about it. They are going to the grocery store. I`m going to the grocery store. We are filling up our gas tanks, we`re trying to purchase things.\par \par \uc2 So it`s on voters minds and you just need to tackle it straight up and have the courage to stand up to big corporations and tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 They are cheating us and we are all paying the price, our overall economy is paying the price. Nobody more so, by the way, than our seniors who are on fixed incomes and retirees and that`s why I was canvassing out today, about one in four of the voters in my district is over the age of 65. And they want to see someone who will protect Social Security and Medicare and stand up to corporations who are making it hard for them to take care of themselves in their later years.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Republicans, you know, look, if they had said something that in any way is related to inflation as a point they are making on the campaign trail, I would raise it to you now and I would challenge you with it now.\par \par \uc2 But they have not. There is not one anti-inflation proposal that Republicans have. They have a giant tax cut proposal which is not anti inflationary in any way. They seem to be interested in going back to a world in which there is absolutely no inhibitions on prescription drug prices which is in fact inflationary.\par \par \uc2 What do you say when voters say to you we want to switch to Republicans because of inflation?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: They get that Republicans are playing the blame game. But what they want to hear from Democrats is that we are on top of this and we are willing to fight to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 So Republicans are simply pointing fingers at Democrats but Democrats need to come back to voters and say they are just pointing fingers. We have solutions. We see the issues, we see the challenges, here is what we`ve done and we know there is more to do. You can trust us to do it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: California Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us once again.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The latest on the Trump jury pool is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:15]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight`s report on the jury selection process in the criminal fraud trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  in Manhattan is from "Business Insider". Another juror prospect, a woman in her 50s conceded on Tuesday that she despised Trump and was not sure she could be fair to his business, the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I`ll definitely pledge to be fair if I am on the jury. I mean I can`t deny it, I really, really don`t like Trump at all," The woman, a native of Ireland, told Trump Organizations lawyer, William Brennan.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:57]\par \par \uc2 When Brennan asked the woman whether she hated Trump, she admitted, "Yes, I hate him. If he was here on trial I couldn`t be fair."\par \par \uc2 The honesty of the Trump jury pool is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 26, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,150 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: But for now, that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I don`t want the federal government involved with that at all. I want women, doctors, local political leaders.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A TV doctor from Jersey gets hammered for debate stage honesty.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oz would let politicians like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  ban abortion without exceptions.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, renewed outrage over the radical Republican position on abortion they have been trying to hide.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN CURTIS (R-UT): I wish as a man I didn`t have to make this decision. I wish women could make this decision.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, new evidence that our disgraced ex-president will pull the strings if Republicans take over.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R-AZ): Mr. President, how are you, sir?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You did good at the debate but that bad election answer.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, do Republicans campaigning on inflation to have any real plan to do anything about it? And what we know about the effort to force this guy to spill the beans about the Mar-a-Lago documents.\par \par \uc2 KASH PATEL, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO THE ACTING UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: In December and January in the way out, I witnessed him declassify a whole sets of documents.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Pennsylvania Republican Senate candidate Mehmet Oz of New Jersey, more honestly, kind of gave away the game on his views on abortion last night. It happened during that debate with Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . The two men, of course, squaring off for that Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Now, before we play the tape, it`s important to know that generally, the way Republicans have dealt with this issue since the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade is to lie or change the subject or dodge or obfuscate. But in this case, Oz was onstage during debate and he had to answer the question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OZ: As a physician, I`ve been in the room when there`s some difficult conversations happening. I don`t want the federal government involved with that at all. I want women doctors, local political leaders letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: To me, what really nails that is the in the room part. Like, he starts with you`re in the room having a difficult decision and then invokes the people who would be making that decision on abortion, a woman, her doctor, and local political leader. So, we`re all in the room together. You got Dr. Oz, you got a woman who`s pregnant, and then you`ve got, I don`t know, what would inevitably be the most extreme right-wing legislature -- legislator telling her what to do. Someone like say, Pennsylvania State Senator and gubernatorial nominee, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  or a fringe Republican state legislator in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Those are the kinds of people who inevitably under this regime would be the ones looming over your personal decision according to Dr. Oz. And in fact, this is a reality. We`ve already seen this position unfold in real-time. Here`s example in New Mexico. New Mexico has got a Democratic governor and Democratic legislature. Some local conservative lawmakers are looking to ban abortion at the town level to skirt state laws which allow it.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of the town of Clovis, New Mexico, which is debating a ban said, "We hope this sends the message our state legislature that there are pro- life cities out there and we want to determine -- self-determine on this issue." That`s who would be in the room, the mayor of Clovis with you, with your doctor. These are the new frontline for the battle for reproductive rights, either a full-on ban or the sort of thing where the most right-wing legislator in your area tells you and your doctor that you can`t have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the really important thing to understand about this to be clear. That`s what Mehmet Oz believes should happen. And by Republican standards, Oz really actually does have their most moderate position. That`s why he was saying in the debate. He was in that moment trying to distinguish himself from the even more radical position, which is one step up, which is a national abortion ban at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s a position supported by many Republicans, including South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , who has introduced legislation that would do just that. That position is endorsed by Trump-backed Georgia Senate nominee \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who of course has been accused of paying for woman`s abortion and trying to pressure her into a second. An accusation that is backed up with some documentary evidence.\par \par \uc2 He`s also a man who just tonight had another abortion-related allegation pop up, one that he reportedly pressured another woman he had a long relationship with into having an abortion in 1993. Something Walker denies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, ANCHOR, FOX NEWS: Obviously, we have to address the elephant in the room today and that is another accuser has come forward, Anonymous, although she spoke to reporters today, saying that she -- that you pressured her to have an abortion and that you paid for it. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 [20:05:09]\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, that`s a lie. And I`d say that`s a lie. And I hope people can see right now that Raphael Warnock and the left would do whatever they can to win this seat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, well, this was Gloria Allred who came forward with this client. You`re saying you don`t know this person?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: What I`m saying this is a lie. And I`ve said it once and I`ve moved on. My campaign had moved on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`ve reached the stage where \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  denying having a woman have an abortion that he paid for is becoming a recurring bit on Fox News. That guy, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is of course, running on a ban abortion platform. And again, he`s not alone. It is also the position of the Senate nominee in Ohio, J.D. Vance, another Trump-backed Republican.\par \par \uc2 Now, the tricky thing about all this is that Republicans do know full well their ideas about banning abortion, criminalizing doctors are unpopular, and so they avoid getting into specifics at all costs. Just take J.D. Vance, for example, who today struggled to articulate any exceptions he would support for that national abortion ban to a sympathetic Fox News audience.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, ANCHOR, FOX NEWS: Where do you come down on the issue of exceptions to that if -- you know, in the case of rape, incest, or other particular exceptions?\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: Yes, John, so I`m very pro-life and not ashamed of it. Of course, you have to have some reasonable exceptions here. But I just want to save as many lives as possible. I think that`s the goal of a pro-life policy.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So, what`s reasonable for you?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Make it easier for young moms to afford healthcar, make it easier for people to afford, you know, afford adoption services if they need to.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I`m sorry, let me just drill down on that. You said reasonable exceptions. What exceptions are reasonable to you?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, look, I think it`s always hard to say every single possible exception. My goal, like I said, John, is to save as many lives as possible to try to protect as many unborn babies as possible.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, ANCHOR, FOX NEWS: I`m going to move on, but one last chance for voters to hear what those exceptions might be.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Guys, I think that I -- like I said, one obvious exception, of course, is you got to make sure that you know, you have life of the mother protected. There are other exceptions as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s on Fox. That is on Fox. They`re throwing him softballs. Like, just give us the exceptions then -- so we can move on because that`s the most politically expedient thing. By the way, as we`ve discussed before, on the show, when Republicans say life of the mother, we know what that looks like in reality because it exists in certain states what it means.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen this happen to actual women that if a fetus is non-viable at 16 or 17 weeks, then that woman is going to have to sit around while doctors call -- talk to lawyers, and wait until her body basically starts to shut down or she goes septic and she`s then in imminent medical danger before she`s allowed to get an abortion. But even that position is not the most extreme one Republicans hold on abortion. The most extreme position is one that seeks to ultimately avoid legislating it entirely by getting the Supreme Court to rule that life begins at conception, that a fetus is a person under the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Now, we already saw dozens of Republicans in the House and Senate endorsed this last year through legislation. They sought "to implement equal protection under the 14th Amendment to the Constitution the right to life of each born and preborn human person." This would effectively make abortion under any circumstances at any stage of pregnancy, two weeks, legally tantamount to murder, among many other just almost incomprehensibly extreme consequences.\par \par \uc2 Now, the ultimate goal of folks in the anti-abortion movement again, who weren`t committed to this vision that they really believe in it, is to get this in front of a sympathetic Supreme Court. And just to have it be the official interpretation of the Constitution, they tried, they got knocked down for this term, but it`s not going to go away. And if they could get that, it could not be legislated away by blue states or Democratic Congress or Democratic president.\par \par \uc2 Now, one justice who would be on the top of the list of most likely justices to be open to supporting this wildly nutty interpretation is, of course, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 . He`s the one who wrote the decision that struck down Roe. Alito is now whining about the draft of the decision which leaked before it became public, saying the real victims here are the conservative justices who voted to eliminate abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL ALITO\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: It was a grave betrayal of trust by somebody. The leak also made those of us who were thought to be in the majority and support of overruling Roe and Casey targets for assassination because gave people a rational reason to think they could prevent that from happening by killing one of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, obviously anyone considering that are threatening that is despicable. And no Supreme Court Justice, just to be clear, were assassinated because of the leak, thank God. What Alito`s decision did do is imperil the actual lives of women who lack access to basic reproductive rights. We`ve told their stories on the show. It has forced a 10-year-old victim of child rape to flee her state to access an abortion so she doesn`t have to carry her rapist child to term.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:28]\par \par \uc2 And of course, that is why the court is so useful to Republicans because it saves them from having to fight unpopular political battles which are often destined to fail. And when they do fight those battles, one way they`re dealing with the issue is just to outright lie as the only way to gain support for their cause. Like in Michigan, there`s another battle there. That`s where right-wing groups are running this truly dystopian ad. It falsely claims the state`s ballot proposal that would enshrine abortion rights would essentially lead to the forced sterilization of children without their parents` consent.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be absolutely clear, it`s just an outright lie. It`s completely untrue, completely just, you know, made up from an alternate reality by abortion opponents who don`t really want to defend their actual position that abortion should be banned on its merits. So, I got to say this. Give TV Dr. Mehmet Oz this much credit. What he said yesterday is debates is bad. But it`s as close as you`re going to get to an honest answer for a Republican in this cycle.\par \par \uc2 Tim Miller is a former \uc2 RNC\uc2  Spokesman and author of Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell. And Leah Litman is a professor at the University of Michigan Law School, co-host of the strict scrutiny podcast along with Melissa Murray, and full disclosure, my wife, Kate Shaw.\par \par \uc2 Leah, me start with you on the Michigan question, because I do think it`s a fascinating snapshot of the politics of this. And it first came to my attention through -- you calling my attention to it. What is going on with the ads there that abortion opponents are running in that state against this state referendum?\par \par \uc2 LEAH LITMAN, PROFESSOR OF LAW, UNIVERSITY OF MICHIGAN LAW: I mean, the bottom line is they don`t want to have to defend their actual position on proposal three and abortion because their actual positions are deeply unpopular. So, without proposal three, there`s a criminal abortion ban in Michigan that prohibits abortions at all stages of pregnancy. And having that law go into effect would be devastating for people in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 So -- because they don`t want to state their position and say, yes, we are advocating for a world where abortions are banned in all cases and all stages of pregnancy, they are just trying to shift the topic of conversation to their preferred scapegoat for moral panic today, which is trans-children. And so, they`re insisting that proposal three would somehow grant minors the ability to obtain gender-affirming care, and maybe to sterilize themselves without their parents` consent, when the language of proposal three is specifically about reproductive freedom and matters related to pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t know if they need a lesson on the birds and the bees, but gender-affirming care isn`t a decision about how you get pregnant or stop being pregnant.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tim, you worked -- you`ve worked in conservative and Republican politics for a while. And I wanted to have you on tonight because there`s a really interesting thing that`s happened, and I wanted you to shine some light on it. It`s clear to me that at some point in your -- in your business, a memo went out to all Republican politicians to say the following line, I`m pro-life, I make no apologies for it, or I`m pro-life and I don`t apologize for it. Like they all say it almost robotically.\par \par \uc2 In the pre-Roe world, you could say that, and that just meant I support overturning Roe. But now, you say that, you got to grapple with the details. And it just seems like they don`t really know what the next sentence is after that which is how you get Oz saying what he said.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, FORMER SPOKESMAN, \uc2 RNC\uc2 : Yes, well, that`s exactly right. And in Mehmet Oz`s case, he`s obviously just a charlatan on this front, right? I don`t know what his deepest views are about abortion --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: -- but he clearly is not like a radical pro-life activist, right? And so, you know, this is kind of like it was reminiscent of when Trump was asked by Chris Matthews, right? Like, should women be punished for abortions? And he gave the answer that -- he kind of didn`t know what to say. It was -- it was one of the times that Trump got caught off guard, and he`s like, yes, I guess they should be punished, then he need to walk that back, right?\par \par \uc2 I think that is the situation. Oz has gotten in it. And the reality is, I think that most of these people, you know, if you gave them a few beers or truth serum, like they`re -- you know, to the ones that are genuinely pro- life hold, you know, what was kind of the reasonable pro-life position, even if people disagree with it just, you know, three years ago or you know, before Roe got overturned which was, you know, some kind of second, third-trimester ban with exceptions. You know, something it looks like the European abortion regime, right?\par \par \uc2 But they can`t say that now that Roe has been overturned because that gets them in trouble with base voters, that`s going to get them in trouble on television. And so now, you know, on Fox -- you know, on conservative media that is. And so, now, they`re kind of grappling around in the air for what to say. And you get, you know, either very extreme answers or kind of the accidental admission that Oz gave last night or the dodges you were talking about at the top.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:04]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that point is, is particularly I think astute about J.D. Vance who could have just said, like, you know, the thing that would make them be the most politically expedient would be like, yes, these reasonable exceptions, you know, and then you could move on. But because he doesn`t want to anchor those base voters, right, he can`t just come out and say that. And you can always tell as a journalist when a politician enters a zone they don`t want to talk about. And I could just physically feel it from every Republican candidate I watch on this.\par \par \uc2 I mean, every one of them just get in this kind of tense sort of posture every time. And that`s why, Leah, I do think it comes back to the court a little bit, because the kind of hail marry idea is, if we can get enough justices to find the 14th Amendment protects fetal personhood, we don`t have to deal with the politics of this.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: I think that`s exactly right. And the court has shown that it is willing to kind of adopt fringe legal positions that are being advanced by the conservative legal movement and the Republican Party. And so, of course, people are going to start trying for fetal personhood. And if you look at what is happening in the lower courts right now, some of the judges who were appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the federal courts of appeals have already cited scholarship that suggests fetuses are or could be people under the equal protection clause and constitutional provisions.\par \par \uc2 So, these ideas are already getting some traction among the judges that were nominated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And it`s possible they will get continued traction at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  one day.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And the division of labor, Tim, I mean, interestingly, I think, to the extent there was a liberal criticism of the Roe regime, it was about the degree to which it was out of the political -- you know, not in the political realm, right?\par \par \uc2 It was not a critique I agree with, but some people had that view. It is now squarely in the political realm. And it is interesting to me how little Republicans want to engage in the thing that they brought about through the conservative legal movement which is we`re now going to have public debates about this.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes. It`s really kind of similar to the immigration issue too when Trump came in and sort of forced that, right? Like, you have this small minority, whatever it is, maybe 10 percent, 20 percent of Republican elected officials who really do care about this issue and are passionate about it, and genuinely want to personhood amendment, you know, consequences be damned, right?\par \par \uc2 And then everybody else is kind of notionally pro-life, right, in the way that I was talking about before that would like to not deal with this politically. But they`re -- they`ve been -- they`ve been forced into dealing with it. And I think that`s why you see them struggling with how to navigate that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tim Miller and Leah Litman, thank you both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the disgraced ex-president gives some advice to Republican Arizona Senate candidate Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Look at Kari. Kari is winning with very little money. And if they say, how is your family, she says the election was rigged and stolen. We lose if you go soft. You`re going to lose that the base.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: I`m not going soft.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s the plaintive guitar scoring for me. How Republicans are still letting a one-term loser dictate how the party is run next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There should be no doubt that if the Republican Party wins control of either or both houses of Congress, it won`t really be Kevin McCarthy who wields power as Speaker of the House. It wouldn`t really be \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  calling the shots in the Senate though a little more so than McCarthy. But in the end, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be pulling the strings of Republicans in Congress like a puppeteer.\par \par \uc2 The disgraced ex-President remains the party`s leader. His endorsements this year help extremists across the country win their primaries. Candidates who just like many existing members of Congress, virtually bend the knee to Trump. Just listen to Blake Masters, Republican candidate for Senate in Arizona groveling in a phone call with Trump that was recorded for a documentary on Fox.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Mr. President, how are you, sir. I`m doing great.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You had a lot of good points about that. I don`t know what you did with the debate but I heard you did good on the debate, but that`s a bad election answer. You got a lot of support. You got to stay with those people.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: Absolutely, we stay with those people.\par \par \uc2 If you want to go across the line, you got to go stronger in that one thing. That was the one thing you got a lot of complains about. Look at Kari. Kari is winning with very little money. And if they say, how is your family, she says the election was rigged and stolen. We lose if you go soft. You`re going to lose that the base.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: I`m not going soft.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s this diluted dude calling up to be like the one thing you need to do is say the election was rigged. And here`s Blake Master then oh, yes, yes, I won`t go soft. Candidates like Masters use Trump and the big lie to get elected and then Trump uses those candidates for power. That`s sort of the way this works. He also use them for money.\par \par \uc2 Check out this fundraising email for Blake Masters` Politico poster. For every $5.00 donated, $4.95 goes to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and five cents goes to Masters. All that groveling for one shiny nickel. Thankfully, not all Republicans in Arizona have been Trump-loving sycophants.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Heather Carter, former Republican state senator in Arizona who has endorsed Blake Masters` opponent, Democrat Mark Kelly. This year, Carter changed her party registration to Independent. It`s great to have you on the program. I`m curious as an Arizonan, as an elected official in the Republican Party previously, what you think when you see that clip of the party`s nominee for Senate sort of groveling before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  specifically on this ludicrous contention that we all know is not true.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:18]\par \par \uc2 HEATHER CARTER, FORMER ARIZONA REPUBLICAN STATE SENATOR: Well, thanks for having me on your show today. And I will just start right at the heart of it. That is absolutely unacceptable. Arizona voters want elected officials to focus on Arizona problems. And to be beholden to a former president who lost the election in Arizona is absolutely unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And it also strikes me -- I mean, so there`s, there`s this sort of threat to democracy, right? There`s also just the responsiveness. It`s like who is Blake Masters going to listen to as U.S. senator? Is he going to listen to the folks in this state have got whatever issues they have, water problems, want to talk about security, or wanting to talk about development, or is it going to talk -- listen to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  whenever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  calls?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, that`s the big question. We know that Senator Mark Kelly has done tremendous work for the state of Arizona. He has addressed the priorities that we face in the state. When you`re looking at somebody running against him like Blake Masters who knows who he`s beholden to, when you look at all the dark money flooding into his campaign. Is they beholden to former President Trump? Is he beholden to his former employer \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2 ? We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 This election is too high of stakes to hand there is on his senatorial seat over to somebody with an unproven record in office, and who does exactly what you just saw on the recording, says that he is beholden to President Trump.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You`ve got Kari Lake who has similarly said that the election is rigged. She even suggested that her opponent, I think, last year should be locked up. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , the Secretary of State. You`re a Republican in that state for a long time. \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  another one. We`ve seen him. It does seem to me Maricopa County officials -- it does seem to me that there is some small but significant critical mass of Republicans in your state who consider themselves conservatives are also anti-coup, pro-vaccine, and that you guys are on the losing part of the factional dispute within the party there.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Well, we saw this play out in the Republican primaries across Arizona but specifically in Maricopa. As you mentioned, \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2  did lose his primary election to a far-right extremist supported by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But we`re headed into a general election in Arizona. And the general electorate in Arizona in 2020 rejected \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And we are counting on them to do the same in 2022.\par \par \uc2 I believe that the future of our representative democracy runs through Arizona and even more specifically through Maricopa County. So, now, it`s up to the voters here in our county and our state to make a choice.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, I`m a -- I`m a liberal, New York City lifelong resident cable TV host. So, I get if your media and Arizona voter thinks Chris Hayes, I don`t know. But you -- you`re not that. And I`m curious what your conversations are like with fellow conservatives, well-intentioned people, people of, you know, good faith about this, this issue about the fact that there`s just this looming disaster hanging over all this and this just, you know, really scary attacks on sort of our fundamental values happening in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Look, we can debate the important issues we face in the state. You mentioned some of them, the water crisis, making sure we have a quality education system, making sure healthcare is accessible to the citizens of Arizona. But at the core of our representative democracy is this peaceful transfer of power and a belief in the process that our elections are fair, and they are accurate.\par \par \uc2 And anybody continuing to peddle lies about it otherwise is downright dangerous. And there are enough Republicans, I believe, who will not fall prey to the lies being spread by the MAGA candidates. And at this point in time, we still have two weeks left in Arizona to vote. And it`s important for those Republicans to talk to their friends, their neighbors, and their families, and make sure people turn out to vote.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Heather Carter, I really appreciate you coming on the show tonight. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, after spending months attacking Democrats, Republicans finally revealed their plan to fight inflation. Just kidding. There`s not really a plan. And that problem is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Inflation is the number one issue that Republicans are running on ahead of the Midterm Elections. And they have been driving that message home in all of their debates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If elected, what is one idea, one idea that you would champion to reduce inflation. We ask you to be specific. You have 60 seconds?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, first of all, you have to blame this administration and Senator Warnock because within two years, this inflation has gotten worse.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): The problem people are having is the out-of- control inflation caused by out-of-control deficit spending, our growing debt and Democrats` war on fossil fuel.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:03]\par \par \uc2 OZ: We should be talking about crime and inflation, issues that are hurting Pennsylvanians that they`re talking about at the kitchen table.\par \par \uc2 MASTERS: It`s the massive, crushing inflation that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Mark Kelly caused. And it`s their fault. They caused it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, this is just politics 101. It`s very easy for Republicans to talk about inflation. And if I were advising Republican candidates, I`d tell them to do the same thing. But here`s the thing. There`s almost zero coverage of what Republicans` plan to do to fight inflation. Like, do Republicans -- does \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  or Blake Masters have even a minimally plausible agenda to address the number one economic issue they are running on? And I think the answer is no.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Michael Strain\uc2 . He previously worked for the Center for Economic Studies, the \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2 , and in the Macro-Economic Research Group at the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank in New York\uc2 , now the Director of Economic Policy at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 , which is a conservative think tank. And it`s good to have you here, Michael.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to be here.\par \par \uc2 You and I, I think, probably have different views on the economy in some ways, and politics and different traditions we come out of. But it was interesting to me to see your quote in that \uc2 New York Times\uc2  article today on this. Do you think a Republican House has a plan to combat inflation?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAIN\uc2 , CONSERVATIVE ECONOMIST: I think that the Republican candidates are right to point out that the American Rescue Plan contributed a significant amount to the inflation we`ve experienced. I think that`s a reasonable valid concern in an election. But I don`t think that there`s a whole lot that Republicans, should they take the House -- or even if they took both the House and the Senate, could do to really affect inflation in 2023 when it`s still going to be higher than the Feds` target, when it`s still going to be causing problems for American households and for American families.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, there`s not even like a plausible lever to me.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Like, there`s not even a story they`re telling that you could see, I guess, we`re going to reduce deficits or austerity.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: Yes. I mean, I think one plausible lever -- so, a step back. Who`s responsible for fixing inflation? The Fed. What is the role of Congress? What`s the role of the President in a situation like this? I think rule number one is do no harm.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: Rule number two is pick low-hanging fruit.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: And so, I think President Biden has violated both rules. Student debt forgiveness is going to add to inflation. Pick low hanging fruit, I think the president should be rolling back the Trump-era tariffs on Chinese imports will probably do a little help.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That one I think we I agree on.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: Yes. So, a Republican Congress might be able to help enforce rule number one, might be able to influence rule number two, but in terms of --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But it`s not going to cut prices.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: It would -- it would -- if there were another American Rescue -- so, if we were in recession in 2023, or a mild recession, if the President wanted to pass a big stimulus --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: -- kind of similar to the American Rescue Plan, would be half the size, something like that, driven by the progressive wing of the party, I think that Republicans could plausibly argue, if we were in charge, that wouldn`t happen, and therefore inflation wouldn`t get worse.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: But you`re right, that not making your problem worse is different than making a problem better.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and also, let`s show the global comparative situation here, because I do think that like, we`ve gotten a little attached to this. And I think this actually bears a bit on this question of the American Rescue Plan, which again, Republicans tell this story American Rescue Plan was inflationary. Larry Summers made the argument.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think it`s a ludicrous story at all, and you can tell a plausible story about it. But when we look, and we see like, where we are, where we stack up, you know, the U.K. has higher inflation. Germany is not exactly like hammer on the printing press. They`re one of the most fiscally conservative nations on Earth, right, 10 percent, inflation. Spain and Italy, much higher. So, like, what is happening here isn`t -- is being driven largely by factors outside of whatever \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 STRAIN: Yes, it`s complicated. So, I think it`s helpful to kind of break 