he Republican Mike Doyle says he`s not intentionally trying to confuse anyone. But as Summer Lee`s campaign manager told the \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2 , "He could identify as a Republican on his website or his literature, he could use his full name Michael Doyle, he could use a middle initial. There`s so many ways he could clarify who he is and who he isn`t if he was honest about his intentions.\par \par \uc2 So, Republican Mike Doyle doesn`t seem to be doing anything to help the issue because as he obviously knows, the only way Republican Mike Doyle can win is if people think he`s a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Once again, ladies and gentlemen, I am duty-bound to come before you and report that the legal issues for disgraced former-President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continued to escalate. Trump`s attorneys have officially accepted a subpoena issued by the January 6 Committee. The Committee wants the ex- president`s documents and his testimony.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, his chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  was just today ordered to testify before a Georgia Grand Jury investigating efforts by Trump and folks like Meadows to rig the 2020 election results in the State. And the Department of Justice reportedly asked a federal judge to force former Trump White House lawyers to answer more questions about Trump`s attempts to steal the entirety of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Well, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports that federal prosecutors have increased their pressure on key Trump aides to get their testimony about the other case the DOJ is pursuing. That`s the trove of classified documents stashed at Trump`s Florida retirement home.\par \par \uc2 One of the reporters who broke that last story, Michael Schmidt, joins me now. Michael, it`s great to have you. Tell us a little bit about what has changed or what this new round of developments look like in the DOJ case over the documents at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SCHMIDT, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: So, basically, we`ve gotten to the part of the investigation where the Justice Department is sort of squeezing those witnesses that it believes have not been fully cooperative with the ongoing inquiry. In this case, they are dissatisfied with the fact that one witness, a junior aide who started out in the White House bringing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Diet Cokes and followed him to Mar-a-Lago was not forthcoming and was not candid with them in the first interview that he had. This is based on the Justice Department`s account, and is using the specter of criminality to try and get him to come back in.\par \par \uc2 This is someone who moved the boxes. This is the question of whether the boxes were moved in Mar-a-Lago. And the footage, the surveillance footage that the Justice Department obtained showed this individual moving these boxes when he initially said he had not. The second person is Kash Patel. This is a person who has not everyone in Trump`s circle has trusted. Bill Barr didn`t think that Kash Patel should have a senior job at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He is someone that has become an increasingly popular figure in Trump`s inner circle and someone Trump is listening more and more to. Kash Patel took the Fifth Amendment before a grand jury in Washington in regards to the documents investigation. He wouldn`t answer investigators questions. They want to ask him about his claim and Trump`s claim that Trump says that they declassified all of these documents before they left the White House, a claim which nothing has come out to substantiate it in any of the proceedings or documents since.\par \par \uc2 And it`s part of this larger effort to gain the testimony of these individuals because to complete an investigation, you want to know what those individuals who were behind closed doors and could potentially speak to Trump`s intentions know and can tell you. And that`s where we`re at.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That last issue on Kash Patel is an interesting one. So, he -- there was reporting he came before that grand jury. We knew he came before the grand jury, then subsequent reporting that he invoked his Fifth Amendment right to avoid self-incrimination. They apparently -- and I guess I knew this but forgotten you could do this. Their -- the Department of Justice is now trying to basically say, we will grant you immunity and therefore compel your testimony because there`s no self-incrimination to worry about basically.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Well, we don`t know if they`ve given him immunity yet. What we do know is that the Justice Department went before a federal judge in a sealed proceeding and tried to get the judge to compel him to testify, despite the fact that he`d invoked his Fifth Amendment rights, and basically saying that, you know, there`s no reason for this individual to assert their Fifth Amendment right.\par \par \uc2 And to gain the testimony of Patel, if the judge does not go along with what the government wants, what will have to happen is they will have to give him immunity.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I see. I see.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: But the government is also allowed to do that. So, we`re not there yet. The government tried to compel him to basically say, you`re a witness, we`re not investigating you, but his lawyers have taken the position that this is someone who was the representative of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the National Archives. He`s someone that is very close to the President. The Justice Department has said they`re investigating not just mishandling of documents, but also obstruction of justice. And because of that, he has a right to his Fifth Amendment right.\par \par \uc2 So, courts usually go along with individuals who want to assert their Fifth Amendment rights. But if the -- if the government were to give him immunity, he would have to testify.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And finally, there`s the question of the lawyers. I mean, one of the striking elements of the reporting that you and your colleagues have done about this is there does seem to be quite a few moments at which different lawyers basically said, don`t do this. Don`t turn everything over, stop -- you know, other lawyers who refuse to attest to the truthfulness of a statement that all the documents have been turned over, those lawyers might be key witnesses here. But that seems like legally murky territory as well.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: A couple of months back or maybe it was a couple of weeks back, we wrote a story about the lawyers who work for Trump. And we put a bit of a tongue-in-cheek lead on the story, but it truly articulated the problem that there is for lawyers who work for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And we said in our story, in the -- in the beginning of our story, that does MAGA really stand for making attorneys get attorneys, and that this was sort of a dark joke that was going around in Trump`s inner circle.\par \par \uc2 And it is true that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tries to use his lawyers as tools to do the things that he doesn`t want to do. He often does not listen to them. And when he gets into trouble, he wants them to do the things again that he won`t do. And this is a story that has repeated itself throughout the Trump administration, now the post-presidency.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Michael Schmidt, thanks so much for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for October 25, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell \par \uc2 7,087 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:28]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 We are, of course, going to discuss the Pennsylvania Senate debate tonight. We are also going to consider a local race in Pennsylvania, a state in legislature race, as an example of how important all of those other offices are on those big long ballots that we are all going to fill out that we have governor and senator and all of that. But we have to keep going down and concentrate on all the rest of them.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of this hour, we are going to be joined by a guest I have been trying to get for a full three years now. The three-year campaign to get \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  succeeded, succeeded -- she was, of course, Joe Biden`s presidential campaign manager. I will never forget that it was \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  who gave press briefings the day after election night, saying, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has this won, which I then communicated to everyone any with complete confidence. And then on Saturday, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was officially called by the networks, oh my friends thought I was wicked smart because --\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": I just listen to \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , I remember that moment too, it was the opposite of what triggering is. I guess reassuring is the world.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, exactly!\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: She spoke with such confidence, such normalcy, such intelligence. It was actually an incredibly important role she played in what was a chaotic moment, as someone who basically lived in the Keystone State in the closing weeks of that election. I can attest to, A, how important it was to have \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  on the airwaves, but also, B, how critical those down ballot seats are, not just to Democratic control method to the preservation of democracy, because that this what is on the line this midterm season.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We can go through how Pennsylvania changes constitutional amendments, how the legislature, what their role is in constitutional amendments in Pennsylvania. It is kind of scary. So, people need to pay attention to, as you, sale all these races. And this is a good example of the way we`re going to teach that lesson tonight.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: It sounds like a great lineup, Lawrence, I will be tuning in. See you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has love letters -- that`s what he calls, them love letters -- from North Korea`s murderous dictator Kim Jong-un, they are apparently the most precious love letters he has received, in his estimation. Not letters from his kids. That is from a murderous dictator.\par \par \uc2 And they are the letters that might get him charged with federal crimes. The reason the National Archives knew that they did not have all of the presidential papers of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in his possession, after he left, office as the law requires, is that the archivists knew they did not have the level letters that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made world famous.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: I got a very beautiful letter from Kim Jong-un. He really wrote a beautiful, three page -- I mean, right from top to bottom -- a really beautiful letter.\par \par \uc2 We fell in love, okay? No, really. He wrote me beautiful letters. And they are great letters. We fell in love.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Those love letters are what started a criminal investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that led to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- where they found thousands of government documents, including over 100 classified documents. Some of those classified documents were found in his desk. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had classified documents at home in a drawer in his desk.\par \par \uc2 And we know all of that because of the Kim Jong-un letters. The Kim Jong-un letters were the first batch of material that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  finally sent to the National Archives, a year after he left the presidency, when the National Archives demanded the return of all the government documents in Trump`s possession. There`s new evidence tonight that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew that the Kim Jong-un letters were not supposed to be in his possession.\par \par \uc2 That same evidence also revealed how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  handles those letters when he was in the White House. Here is a tape of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the oval office discussing the Kim Jong-un letters with our first guest tonight, Bob Woodward.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Nobody else has them. But I want you to treat them with respect.\par \par \uc2 BOB WOODWARD, JOURNALIST: Understand. Understand.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And don`t say I gave them to you, okay?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think it`s okay. Normally I -- I wasn`t going to give them to Bob. What did you make a Photostat of them or something?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: No, I dictated them into a tape recorder.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Really?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Yes. And my assistant --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But you were surprised by them, right?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Yes, because --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I never saw these pictures.\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Yes, there were 11 of them, and it shows an evolving relationship of being tough --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Yeah, he wants to get along with me.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That recording is included in an astonishing new audio book by Bob Woodward called "The Trump Tapes: Bob Woodward`s 20 Interviews with President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 In the audio book, Bob Woodward describes what happened when he asked to see the love letters that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wrote to Kim Jong-un.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: I asked him for the letters he wrote to Kim Jong-un because I had only the letters Kim had returned to him. Oh, those are so top secret, Trump said. But I will let you see them. I don`t want you to have them all. I said, well, I want to quote from some of them.\par \par \uc2 Recent investigations revealed that Trump kept these letters, along with other sensitive and top secret documents at Mar-a-Lago after he left office. The National Archives retrieved the letters from Mar-a-Lago in January 2022.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Oh, those are so top secret, but I will let you see them. That was Donald Trump`s attitude about classified documents while he was still in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Bob Woodward, two-time Pulitzer Prize winner, bestselling author and associate editor of "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 His new audio book is, "The Trump Tapes", and it is available now.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, working backwards here, did you get to see the letters that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wrote and sent to Kim Jong-un?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Oh, yes. I have them all. I think it adds up to 27 letters. They really are kind of the fondness is that teenagers might exchange. There is nothing substantive in them. The idea that Trump said -- told me, they are so top secret, on the face, Lawrence, I will send you a set after I mentioned them in the book. I did "Rage" -- a number of academics contacted me and said, can we have copies? And so I have given them out to academics.\par \par \uc2 And if you read through them, you kind of laugh because Kim is wooing Trump in a very, very unsophisticated way and says, well, if we meet again, it will be out of a fantasy film which, I guess, the way Kim thought of it, and maybe, to a certain extent, Trump did also.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What did you -- what was your feeling, after all that experience with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , when he would hand you different experience documents? You are feeling about his understanding of his own obligation with classified documents?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: He is very careless. It does not see a line between what he liked to dish out or show people and what probably should not -- know, I kind of hate to puncture this. But I want to be realistic about it, because the theme in all of these interviews is how unrealistic Trump`s. He does not understand the presidency, does not understand his obligations to people.\par \par \uc2 But these letters, I thought, and there`s a lot of evidence early in 2020, that North Korea was going to be the big story, that maybe there would be a war and the ultimate corridors an exchange of nuclear weapons. And the core finding I have from all of this is that Trump had -- when I asked him, what are you doing? Are you trying to drive came to the negotiating table?\par \par \uc2 [22:10:06]\par \par \uc2 He said, oh, no, no, no, I`m -- you know, let`s talk about -- who knows, instinct. This is one of the most delicate foreign diplomatic relationships that he is just decided, oh, I am going to do it on instinct. I`m going to meet with him. We are going to exchange these letters.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at it, it is almost more comic than serious diplomacy. But it traumatized national security team because Kim Jong-un -- one of the most awful dictators on the globe had these nuclear weapons, he had missile launchers he got from China. He actually really knew how -- or somebody did -- to conceal them. And protect them -- so, they were a real threat and still are a threat to the United States.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You repeatedly tried to get him to focus on this -- the murders aspect of this North Korean dictatorship. Here`s just one example, you told him about President Bush`s attitude toward -- toward Kim. Let`s listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: President Bush once told me about Kim`s father Kim Jong Il. He said, I loathe Kim Jong Il because of what he`s doing for his people. And for --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And you know what? That attitude got him nothing.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Bob, he wouldn`t even let you finish with that thought. He absolutely at no point cares at all what this murderous dictator is doing to his people.\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Yes. I mean, that`s what is so -- that is the ugly dimension of this, the idea of, as he says in these interviews to me about Kim Jong-un, oh, he likes me. We get along. There was a chemistry.\par \par \uc2 He even says, at one point, it is like you know in one second when you meet a woman whether it is going to happen. And again, the defense department, the intelligence people were just, oh my god, he is going to get us in a war. He is going to do something that will set Kim Jong-un off and in this relationship, like all of Trump`s relationship, the they go up and there`s great chemistry. And all of a sudden, the chemistry evaporates.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Bob, I spent the day in the room today with you and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  listening and listening and listening. And the struggle that you had, the endless struggle that you had to keep his mind on the point that you raised -- no matter what you were talking about. If you are talking about the letters from Kim, he would show you the photographs, he would show, you oh, look, he smiling, he never smiles and photographs. And then you make the point that, oh, he smiles and photographs with other world leaders that he meets with.\par \par \uc2 But it`s just this constant kind of chaotic thing about talking to him, where we can just feel the wrestling match that you are in trying to move forward. Was it as exhausting as it is, in its way, to listen to?\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Well, no. It is a very unusual nine months to be in a situation where he can call me at any time of day or night and he did. He wanted to check in. I could call him with very specific questions. I`ve done a book earlier on him. And so I knew the modus operandi and that you were -- and it is hard to get him focused.\par \par \uc2 What is the most revealing part of all of this is, any time I want to ask a series of questions about race relations, or the virus, he does not want to pay attention. And I got from Dr. Fauci and other experts what needed to be done to mobilize the country.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:04]\par \par \uc2 This is in early April and went through the list of things, coordinate with the intelligence community and so forth.\par \par \uc2 And Trump kind of didn`t want to listen to all of them. Give them to me again, as I did, and I thought, maybe he is going to do something. Did absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Bob Woodward, historians will be listening to these tapes 100 years from now and beyond. Bob Woodward, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 WOODWARD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Bob Woodward`s audio book is "The Trump Tapes". Thank you, Bob.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the Senate campaign debate in Pennsylvania tonight revealed that Trump`s candidate, Mehmet Oz, believes the question of abortion should be decided by women, her doctor, and local elected officials. That`s what he actually said. While \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  said he would vote to legislate Roe versus Wade in the United States. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Let`s also talk about the elephant in a room. I had a stroke. He has never let me forget that.\par \par \uc2 And I might miss some words during this debate, mosh two words together. It may knock me down, but I`m going to keep coming back up.\par \par \uc2 And this campaign is all about, to me, is about fighting for everyone in Pennsylvania that ever got knocked down that needs to get back up and fighting for all forgotten communities across Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Before tonight`s debate, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was polling two points debate ahead of Mehmet Oz, which is a statistical tie within the margin of error of the polls.\par \par \uc2 To become a Republican candidate Senate candidate in Pennsylvania, Mehmet Oz had to not just changes address, but change the position he always held as a physician on the subject of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Mehmet Oz said, abortion is a matter to be decided by women, their doctors and local political leaders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA: I don`t want the federal government involved without that at all. I want women, doctors, local political leaders --\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: But if you believe the choice for abortion belongs between you and a doctor, that is what I fight for. Roe v. Wade, for me, should be the law.\par \par \uc2 He celebrated when Roe v. Wade went down. And my campaign would fight for Roe v. Wade, and, if given the opportunity, to codify it into law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Brendan Boyle\uc2 , of Pennsylvania. He`s a member the House and Ways Committee and the Budget Committee. He attended a fundraiser of President Biden and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Boyle, what was your reading of that debate tonight?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRENDAN BOYLE\uc2  (D-PA): Well, look, I think John did pretty well. I hope that people really focused on the substance of the debate and not just the recovery issues that John is gravely continuing to have in front of the public. He has been pretty honest about that. He still has some speech challenges. But he has reassured us, as well as his doctors, that he will make a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Dr. Oz`s condition of being a fraud is terminal. What you saw tonight was one candidate in John answering the questions honestly, where`s Dr. Oz, whether it was abortion or guns or education, just completely refused to answer question after question.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, there`s an independent candidate named Everett Stern who dropped out today, and threw his support to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . He says that he was polling at around 3 percent in the race. Will that make a difference?\par \par \uc2 BOYLE: Well, just to be clear, I don`t believe he had qualified for the ballot. So, I`m a little skeptical that he was at 3 percent. I believe he went on to say that he endorses \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . This is an important election.\par \par \uc2 I still remember, by the way, Pennsylvania in 2016, 44,000 votes for the difference in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  beating Hillary Clinton. There were more than 44,000 votes that went to third party and independent candidates. So, in a very close race, we have painful experience in them making the ultimate difference.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Brendan Boyle\uc2 , thank you very much for joining our coverage tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BOYLE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 All the political media tension now in Pennsylvania`s aimed at the Senate race. But in Pennsylvania, every vote for every office on that ballot is important because nothing less than the future of democracy is at stake. The Pennsylvania state legislature is currently controlled by Republicans, who could try to change the way electoral votes are awarded to the winning presidential candidates, and because of the peculiar way that the Pennsylvania legislature can pass constitutional amendments.\par \par \uc2 This year, the legislature passed a constitutional amendment that could ban all abortions in Pennsylvania, including cases of rape and incest and protect the life of the mother. The way the amendment procedure in Pennsylvania works is that, eight consecutive legislatures pass an amendment by a simple majority vote, that amendment will appear on the ballot for voters to approve. If Republicans control the Pennsylvania legislature next year, they will pass that antiabortion amendment again and that could -- they couldn`t put it on the ballot for voters in an off year low turnout, low visibility election, and hope to make it the law of the land in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Mandy Steele, Democratic candidate for Pennsylvania House of Representatives in the 33rd district.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. This is one of those districts that, if the Democrats are going to control Pennsylvania, they have got to win districts like this.\par \par \uc2 What, for you, is at stake in this election?\par \par \uc2 MANDY STEELE (D), PENNSYLVANIA STATE HOUSE CANDIDATE: Well, I will tell you that we are very, very close to losing abortion access in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:04]\par \par \uc2 I personally have knocked 10,000 doors in this district since the primary. And it is very clear to me that this is the work of a vocal group of extremists, minority extremists the work of a vocal group of extremists, minority extremists, who are trying to force this issue on us.\par \par \uc2 When I knock on doors, when I meet people in this district, Pennsylvanians want to see abortion protection in Pennsylvania. We believe that is a right. We want to see women participating as full citizens in our state. And we know that we need to have autonomy over our own bodies in order for that to happen. So, there is a lot o for some Pennsylvania this year.\par \par \uc2 For the first time, because of redistricting, we have a chance to potentially flip the Pennsylvania House to the Democrats. We could stop this madness from happening.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Your district stretches from Democratic areas of Pittsburgh, north, as I understand, into more conservative, more Republican areas. What do you tell those Republican voters up on that end of your district?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: The district is interesting in that it stretches in almost a linear way along our Allegheny River. So, the implications with a river district like this or extraordinary, as far as riverfront development and manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 This part of southwestern Pennsylvania was once the epicenter of energy manufacturing, of the world. And we now have a chance to restore that economy, restore that vibrancy, when we elect candidates, Democrat candidates, who are going to go out there and pursue that opportunity for us to drive investment in clean energy, manufacturing -- equally or even more importantly, protect women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 So, we see a lot of support for that message in southwestern Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Mandy Steele, Democratic candidate for the Pennsylvania state house, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Thank you, Lawrence. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, it`s a tie in the Ohio Senate race now in the latest polls. Ohio Senate candidate, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , who is trying to turn a Republican Senate seat into a Democratic Senate seat in Ohio will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:31:56]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It`s a tie. The Ohio Senate race is a tie in the polling. And here is why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: You were calling Trump America`s Hitler. Then you kissed his ass.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: Not true.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: It is true. Then you kissed his ass and then he endorsed you. And you said he`s the greatest president of all time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Polls taken after that debate show Democratic candidate \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in a tie with Donald Trump`s Republican candidate J.D. Vance. One poll shows both candidates at 47. Another poll shows both candidates at 46.\par \par \uc2 A new NBC poll shows that the most unpopular position a candidate can take in this election is denying that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: He talked about he thinks that the election was stolen, which was one of the reasons Trump agreed to support him.\par \par \uc2 He has called into question the presidential election. That is a threat to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 This is the crowd that J.D. is running around with -- the election deniers, the extremists. That is not Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What you are seeing, in the strength of Tim Ryan`s campaign in Ohio, is the value of experience. He won his first elected office as a state senator at the age of 27 then he went on to win ten congressional elections in Ohio. Now he`s on the verge of possibly winning the Senate seat currently occupied by a Republican in a state \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won by eight points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Ohio Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . He`s the Democratic candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in Ohio. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I want to extend the theme from the previous segment we just did with Mandy Steele, who is a candidate for the house in Pennsylvania, the state legislature in Pennsylvania because you started in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 And one of the lessons I want the audience to derive from this tonight is the way you get a strong statewide candidate like \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  running in Ohio is you start by paying attention to those state legislature races, those races down the ballot, that the Tim Ryans start their careers on.\par \par \uc2 Tell us what you think it means to your campaign, as you stand up there with J.D. Vance, who has never run for office before, that you have that experience, that you earned that experience beginning in a state legislature.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, you know, I remember the black hair that I used to have, Lawrence. Those pictures were bringing me back to those days. But you don`t want to go to a heart surgeon who has never performed heart surgery, you know. You don`t want a teacher teaching your kid who does not have any experience.\par \par \uc2 And I think we need to appreciate some level of statecraft where you can help bring people together, you realize that the bomb throwing, the extremism, sometimes on both sides, is something that just does not get it done.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:56]\par \par \uc2 RYAN: And so you have to figure out how to get along with people, how to make arguments, how to advocate and bring people together for something bigger than yourself and something on behalf of the country. And that experience along the way, in a state legislature, where you are really working in a hands on way, and then Congress and the committee process, you know, this is how you -- this is how you figure out how to get things done.\par \par \uc2 And if people are frustrated with nothing getting done you need to elect people who actually know how to do it. And we have had some success here in the last few months that I have been a part of. And now we just have to keep moving in that direction.\par \par \uc2 You don`t want an extremist who is just throwing bombs, which is what J.D. Vance has done throughout this whole campaign.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When you are filling your tank at a gas station, you know you have, if you talk to a voter here, you have the time for one issue -- you talk about one issue with that voter in those last couple of weeks. What is that issue?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Who is most Ohio? I think that Ohio is going to wholly reject J.D. Vance. He is not an Ohio guy. He came in from California. He wants to cruise. He`s not working hard. Even his own staff says he likes to keep a light schedule. We have had conservative radio hosts say they can`t find J.D. Vance with a search warrant.\par \par \uc2 And Ohioans have great determination. If there is one value we have, it`s to work hard. You may not be the smartest, you know, but if you are out there and you are working hard, you are contributing.\par \par \uc2 And J.D. Vance is lazy. He does not work hard. I`m not even sure he wants the job. He just wants to be called senator. I am the most Ohio guy here, Lawrence. I grew up outside of Youngstown, Ohio in the old Steel Valley. I understand what is going on all along Ohio River in Marietta and Portsmouth and Lyman and Toledo like get it. I understand what people have been through because my community has been through it too.\par \par \uc2 And so I want to earn their vote. We`re traveling everywhere. We`ve been in all 88 counties. We have small dollar donations from all 88 counties as well. And people understand that I know them best and I`m going to fight like hell for them.\par \par \uc2 And that is what your senator supposed to do, not mail it in, not just parachute in from California and come in with a bunch of criticisms of what we have not done right in the last 20 years while you were in eating, wining and drinking cheese in California. Like they want a strapper and that is me, from outside of Youngstown.\par \par \uc2 We know how to fight. We need help, you know. We have 350,000 donors from across the country. We need people to help us out, go to TimForOH.com -- chip in a few bucks here. We are taking them all on, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 We got \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  gave them $40 million. \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  gave them $15 million. You know, the Democratic Party nationally is not helping us, but we`ve got this powerful machine of grassroots people in Ohio, labor unions coming in strong for us, working class people coming in strong for us, Republicans coming to our events like crazy. We are going to do this. We just need a few extra bucks.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , I don`t know who is going to win this race, but you are running the race I expected you to run from the start. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thanks, Lawrence. We are having fun and we are going to win this thing. You heard it here on Lawrence O`Donnell -- we`re going to shock the world.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: All right, we are saving that videotape right there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Ok.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, in the last election no one was more clear and correct about the outcome than \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , Joe Biden`s campaign manager. She is now deputy White House chief of staff and \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:38:35]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If you agonized over the last presidential election during that first week of vote counting in November, you wasted your emotional energy especially after our next guest told you to stop worrying. On election night, November 3rd the presidential election was too close to call but it looked good for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with one network calling Arizona for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I told my very worried friends in phone calls and texts that it looked good for Biden.\par \par \uc2 Then, the next day, Wednesday, November 4th, I told them with confidence that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was going to win because his brilliant campaign manager who rarely spoke to the press, \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  said this in a Zoom press conference.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN O`MALLEY DILLON\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Good morning, everyone. Thanks so much for joining us after this long night. This is \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , I`m here with Bob Bauer and we wanted to take a few minutes to go over with you what we are seeing across the key states that are left outstanding.\par \par \uc2 Most importantly, I want to share with all of you that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is on track to win this election. And he will be the next president of the United States. So, we believe we`re in a clear path to victory.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And because this was \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  talking, I did not have to hear another word. But she had more details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DILLON: By this afternoon, we expect that the vice president will have leads in states that put him over 270 electoral vote. Today, the vice president will garner more vote than any presidential candidate in history. And we are still counting.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:58]\par \par \uc2 DILLON: Over 135 million votes have been counted so far. And the vice president has received 69 million votes so far. He has won over 50 percent of the popular vote.\par \par \uc2 We are on track to win in Michigan by more than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did in 2016; to win in Wisconsin by more than Trump did in 2016; to win in Pennsylvania by more than Trump did in 2016.\par \par \uc2 And we flipped one of his states Arizona, and congressional district, Nebraska, too, last night to add on top of that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And after, that I told my worried friends that it was all over. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won. But none of them believed it because they had never heard of \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2  because she prefers to do her work quietly backstage.\par \par \uc2 On our coverage on this program each night that week we stressed, as strongly as possible, every update delivered by \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 . Here is what she had to say on Thursday, November 5th.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DILLON: Good morning, everyone. It`s good to be with you today. This is \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , vice president Joe Biden`s campaign manager and I`m joined with Bob Bauer, senior adviser on our campaign. Hopefully, folks had a chance to get a little bit more sleep last night. I certainly did since the last time I`ve spoken to all of you.\par \par \uc2 And our goal for today is to give you an update on how we think things are going in the race, and where we are headed.\par \par \uc2 So let me be very clear. Our data shows that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will be the next president of the United States. And that the counting is happening now in the states. And they are moving to us as we see the counting throughout this morning, yesterday and into today that that counting is going to continue to show our path to victory.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And so it was with no surprise at all to me that two days later on Saturday, November 7th, 2020 I got to say this --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It is my honor to announce to you that NBC News is projecting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as the winner of the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Dancing in the streets broke out after that. Literally dancing in the streets. They could have gotten that dancing started on Wednesday if they had been paying attention to \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so to address the anxiety that I know millions of you are feeling tonight about the upcoming election, it is my honor to introduce you to the wizard of 2020 who manage Joe Biden`s winning presidential campaign from her attic after the coronavirus closed down the campaign headquarters. And while she was managing the campaign, she was somehow keeping an eye on her then twin eight-year-old daughter and three year old son.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is White House deputy chief of staff, \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 . And I wish we had an applause track here for you, Jen because you kept allowed of people sane -- on that election week in 2020.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us tonight about what you are looking at as you -- as these elections approach?\par \par \uc2 Lawrence, it`s so wonderful to be with you and to take me down that memory lane of that special time and the start of this administration. And you know, you talked about the confidence that I conveyed then. That confidence was built off of Joe Biden`s confidence. And you know, he really has focused on that and stayed focused on the people that we were campaigning for back then and the people he`s been fighting for his whole life.\par \par \uc2 In doing that, returning time and time again over the course of the last two years to get the job done for the American people. And changing the path of, you know, a bipartisan perspective here in the country that you can get things done, that you can do things to make people`s lives better.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, as I think about where we are, and I think about where we are headed, I really think about what President Biden has been saying all along. There is a clear contrast between his vision for the path forward and the extremist, he would say, MAGA agenda on the other side.\par \par \uc2 And that we have to be steady in our course to continue to focus on delivering for the American people, understanding what it is like to be a middle class family, a working family in this country. Doing all we can to lower prices. And you know, he would say, give them the breathing room that they have in front of us.\par \par \uc2 And if we do that -- if we continue to do stay focused on that and tell that story, we are going to continue to be able to deliver in ways that will have impact today and for the future generations like my kids, as well.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:44]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What numbers are you looking at do you take seriously in these final weeks? I mean for example, one that`s thrown around a lot is this congressional preference poll, the latest one from NBC shows Democrat control of Congress at 47; Republican control at 46. Does that have any meaning to you?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: You know, I will just say, Lawrence. Standing in front of the White House, I am limited in what I can talk about today. And the political context from the Hatch Act, that you know, what I know the president is focused on and what we are focused on are the numbers of how we are delivering for the American people.\par \par \uc2 So we are focused on the 3.5 percent unemployment, the best of the last 50 years. The 10 million jobs, the 700,000 of those new jobs coming out of manufacturing. You heard the president say yesterday, he said I`ve changing what made in America means in this country by creating new opportunities in communities that have been long forgotten.\par \par \uc2 So you know, those are the numbers that we are focused on here, you know -- $1.20 decrease in gas prices since June, ten cents over this past week.\par \par \uc2 But also we are incredibly encouraged to see American people voting and turning out, and the benefit of not having to worry so much about polling, but being able to see people all across this country express their voice by showing up and really making clear what they understand is at stake. And their role in the path forward.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So what is your personal anxiety level at this point in the final days leading up to the election? And how do you manage that anxiety?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: I will say, as the president has said that we are quite confident that we will continue to have a Democratic House and a Democratic Senate as we move forward. And you know, we are just so focused on doing the work. And you know, that is what President Biden has built in this administration.\par \par \uc2 Just an extraordinary group of people following his lead of how we can make sure that we are not ever losing sight of what the American people are looking for and they need and how we can go tell that story. He`s going to continue to cross the country speaking about that, speaking about the accomplishments. Talking about the progress that we`ve made but that we have so much further to go.\par \par \uc2 And that really just gives us a sense of purpose. And that`s something we talk about often. Purpose and progress and knowing that there is a real contrast here in this country where that progress could be at risk. And so we`re going to continue to stay focused on that and quite confident that we can tell that story and make sure that the American people understand the real impact.\par \par \uc2 You know what it`s like from being in the Senate, talking about legislative bills and how important it is to break that down and make it clear to people what`s in it for them. And why that really matters to them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is the number one item you want to leave voters with in what will be the president`s speeches, what will be Democratic candidate speeches, their messages in the final weeks?\par \par \uc2 DILLON: So, you know, Lawrence, I think you`ve heard him speak to this honestly. All the way back in the campaign and really why he decided to run to begin with.\par \par \uc2 But he`s going to continue to talk about that real contrast that we see in this country. That there is a vision and path moving forward where we can continue to deliver for the American people by lowering costs in their life. By making sure that we understand that their prescription drugs are too high. So he`s lowered them. That health care costs are too high, so he has lowered them.\par \par \uc2 You know, focus on gas prices, of course those are still too high. But continuing to use every tool he has. And that that is in stark contrast to that MAGA Republican elected officials who are not only siding with special interests that don`t want your prices to be lowered that the president was able to push forward in the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 But their number one priority, as you hear from Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , he`s going to put Social Security and Medicare on the chopping block every five years, and veterans benefits and more.\par \par \uc2 And Senator Ron Johnson says, you know what, five years isn`t enough. We`re going to do it every year.\par \par \uc2 You have Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  talking about a national ban on abortion. Something that is so unacceptable to the millions of people in this country that have had their rights taken away. A law that`s been with us for 50 years. The president has been so clear that we need to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 So there`s so many elements like that that the president is going to continue to really highlight his vision versus this extreme MAGA agenda and be clear about what that contrast is and make sure that people are able to see and feel the progress we`ve made and that there is more progress to be done together.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: White House deputy chief of staff, \uc2 Jen O`Malley Dillon\uc2 , thank you very much for giving us a big win tonight in what is our three-year campaign to get you to join us here on this program. Thank you very, very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DILLON: It`s been a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:54]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Jury selection continues in the criminal fraud case against the Trump organization in Manhattan. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporter Jonah Bromwich shared this update from the jury selection session this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:52]\par \par \uc2 A potential juror, quote, "Mr. Trump has no morals. He thinks only of himself. I think he is a criminal. I think he has done irreparable damage to this country. And that`s exactly why I think I can be impartial in this case."\par \par \uc2 The Trump jury pool gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221026eiap00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for October 25, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Tom Llamas \par \uc2 7,989 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: I think he has done irreparable damage to this country. And that`s exactly why I think I can be impartial in this case. The Trump jury pool gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie rule starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, two weeks to go, millions have already cast their ballots with a showdown in battleground PA. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Oz squaring off in their first and only debate.\par \par \uc2 Plus, more pressure from the DOJ on two of the former president`s ex- lawyers as the January 6 committee sending signs it`s not done, interviewing one of Trump`s closest confidants today.\par \par \uc2 And Kanye West anti-Semitic comments costing him big time. Meanwhile, hate speech is only getting louder across the country as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. It is the final phase of the midterm campaigns, with the election now exactly 14 days away. This was debate night for key governor`s races in New York and Michigan. But the spotlight was on the state of Pennsylvania with a contest that could decide control of the Senate. Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz faced off for their first and only debate. Still showing signs of his recent stroke, which Fetterman called the elephant in the room. He tried to paint his opponent as out of touch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: We need to fight about inflation, you know, right now because it`s a tax on working families, you know, and Dr. Oz can`t possibly understand what that is like, you know, he has 10 Gigantic mansions, you know, he -- we must push back against corporate greed. We must make sure that we`re also pushing back against price gouging, as well too.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Oz repeatedly tried to attack veterans record, but it was his response to a question about a federal ban on abortion that has gone absolutely viral tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I want women, doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And here`s the kicker, when pressed Oz said he would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024 if Trump decides to run.\par \par \uc2 As Democrats are fighting to defend and expand their majorities in Congress, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports some in the party are bracing for a Republican wave in the House. The head of one progressive polling firm told The Times this, "We thought for a little bit that we could defy gravity. But the reality is setting in."\par \par \uc2 Maybe not so fast because the number of people who have voted early by mail or in-person has now reached more than 10 million and that could be a very good sign for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  says, meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is supposedly already on the hunt to hire a senior staffer for a possible 2024 presidential campaign. The Post reports, a Trump presidential run could be announced shortly after the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Even as Trump supposedly plans a political comeback, a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report also says the Justice Department is focusing on getting more information from his former aides for the January 6 investigation.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reports DOJ is asking a judge to force Trump White House lawyers \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  and Pat Philbin to provide additional testimony. The Time says, it is part of an effort by prosecutors to punch through Trump`s privilege claims. And A source tells NBC News that \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2 , remember her, who was once one of Trump`s closest aides is being interviewed by the House January 6 committee today.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight, Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News Correspondent and Moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2 , Jacqueline Alemany, Congressional Investigations Reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC Contributor, and Criminal Defense Attorney Barry Berke, he served as counsel to the Democrats during both of the Trump impeachments and it is his first visit to our 11th Hour.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche, let`s start on the debate in Pennsylvania tonight. What are you hearing about it?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, PBS WASHINGTON WEEK MODERATOR: What I`m hearing is that Democrats feel comfortable with the idea that they were able to get \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  on that stage, talking about the issues that they think are important. But it`s clear that he still has communication issues. Even though his campaign has been very clear that he doesn`t have cognitive issues and that his doctor says he`s fit to serve. Their campaign has also put out a statement saying that they`re going to turn that statement about Dr. Oz, saying that local politicians along with a woman`s doctor should be making choices when it comes to reproductive health into a campaign. So, you already see Fetterman`s campaign saying this debate was good for them in some ways and they`re going to put out an attack ad. But I think it`s making a lot of Democrats that I`m talking to you very nervous because, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is clearly still recovering from his stroke.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:03]\par \par \uc2 Some people told me that they`re proud of him for getting up on that stage, that it`s clearly not easy. But some people also said that it`s going to make some voters weary about him serving in office because he did seem to struggle on number of times.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But of course, in is a recovery process. He`s still recovering.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche, I also want to ask you about this latest Cook Political Report, it offered some encouraging news for the GOP, saying some of Democrats biggest headaches are actually in blue states like New York, Oregon, Connecticut, and even Rhode Island. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 It`s interesting thing, because I think when you look at, when I talked to Democrats, they are nervous about places that they didn`t think that they were going to be nervous about. The New York governor`s race, one of them, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , looking like he could be getting closer and closer to defeating the current governor there.\par \par \uc2 This has to do with a couple of things. One that this was a year where there was redistricting. So, in some places, you have districts that were drawn, who have that have different sorts of populations, including different racial populations, that at times impacts the way that people vote. I think also, when you think about what`s top of mind for voters, its inflation, its crime. These are issues that even if you`re Democrat or Republican, often seem to lean toward people`s trusting the Republicans with those issues. So, there`s a lot of sort of tense and worry about that. And you hear Democrats pivoting toward the economy, leaning into it and having that conversation with voters, but it is something that I think is impacting the way that Republicans are running, and it`s giving them in some places an edge.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: 14 days to go, Jackie, what are you hearing on the Hill?\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ALEMANY, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, Stephanie, right now, you know, I think that the Democrats are realizing that some of this optimism that they might have held over the summer in the wake of overturning the Supreme Court, overturning Roe v Wade, is dissipating as the numbers are sort of becoming clearer and House Republicans in particular are setting the stage to take back the majority in terms of aligning what is next going to be necessary to sort of launch a full frontal of GOP investigations into Democrats after six long years in the minority and for four long years with the former president being attacked by Democrats, impeach twice, all of these things very fresh in their memories with the GOP base, very hungry for, as one GOP operative put it, to me some scalps here.\par \par \uc2 And with, you know, these ideas of trying to impeach Secretary of Homeland Security Mayorkas, potentially \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , a bunch of high-profile targets that have -- that, you know, Republicans have been whipping up on Fox News and conservative media outlets. So, Republicans, despite potentially the lack of quality amongst some of their candidates do feel that they have the wind at their backs and historic advantage in this homestretch.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Barry, let`s turn to the Justice Department`s January 6 investigation. Reportedly, they want to hear more from Trump`s White House lawyers. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 BARRY BERKE, CHIEF LEGAL COUNSEL, TRUMP`S SECOND IMPEACHMENT TRIAL: I think it`s a great sign because it reveals what`s really going on. It`s ironic that during election season, the big news is the former president`s attempts to interfere with the election. And what the department is saying is we`re going all in. Because they`re not stopping just showing the acts. They`re now focused on showing his intent.\par \par \uc2 And remember, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  was as close to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as anyone. I battled him in the first impeachment when he represented the former president. But he gave testimony already. It`s not a surprise, the former president wants to block him because he`s harmful and gives testimony that helps to show the former President knew he lost the election and tried to interfere it with it, which would violate a number of criminal statutes.\par \par \uc2 But remember, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  went before the January 6 committee. He said he knew the election was lost. He said that he took steps and told the President a variety of things. So, what we`re seeing now is the Department of Justice, using that evidence to build their case. And while there will be a side battle on legal issues, the former president`s going to lose those issues as he keeps losing them and other cases like the \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 , where a judge found there was a crime fraud exception and strong evidence of crimes. So, the illegal issues are a sideshow. I think the real main event is the efforts by the Department of Justice to go full steam ahead and proving the crimes that appear to have been committed by the former president.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The DOJ has also said they`re going to need more money to continue this. If Republicans take control of Congress two weeks from now, what happens to all of these investigations?\par \par \uc2 BERKE: The department will go forward. Obviously, money is important, and the legislature has the ability to control that to some extent.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And they`re going to roadblock him as much as they can?\par \par \uc2 BERKE: Well, we have hundreds of years of an independent department of justice. And I guarantee the people who are working there, if they believe there`s a crime that was committed as it appears they do. They`re not going to let budgetary issues prevent it. And I also believe that it`d be very hard for the House or the Senate controlled by whichever party to say that we are going to cut off the purse strings because we want to prevent a member of our party from being investigated. And you see all these people voting in this election.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:08]\par \par \uc2 And you see all these people voting in this election. I`d like to think that these investigations are reminder how sacred our right to vote is, and that it really is the centerpiece of our democracy. So, at the end of the day, Stephanie, I would bet you that ultimately, democracy and the integrity of our elections will prevail over politics.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Amen to you. The House optimist tonight. Yamiche, you haven`t covered \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for years. Now, this story leaking, breaking that Trump is looking to hire a senior staffer for potential run in 2024. Do you buy this? Or is this just a classic Trump flex? Never look weak, always punch, right? This is coming on a day when we hear \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2 , one of his closest allies. She`s talking to the committee. Maybe the DOJ wants to hear from his lawyers again. Do you really buy this? He`s readying to run? Or is he just putting on a show, because that`s all he can do?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Well, I think actually, both things can be true, Stephanie. He is someone who is clearly always wanting to put on a show. He`s someone who understands the media who wants to continuously be at the top of people`s minds. He`s also someone who it seems like once you run again, for president, now, he has been having this drip of information, where he`s been on stage, on across the country say, you know, it`s a matter of when, not if. I`ve already made up my mind in some interviews, he said. In some ways it`s really -- it`s -- he`s been signaling for a while, but the -- that he is very interested in running again. And that people around him have been saying that he`s interested in running again, whether or not he actually does that, or he just wants to sort of create this cloud of everyone talking about him so that people like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or Glenn Youngkin, or Kari Lake, or the number of Republicans who are eager to take the Trump mantle and run in 2024, that they`re eager to understand that he`s sort of still this chess maker and that they have to come and bow to him and figure out what he`s doing. He likes being the center of attention in that way.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s hard to understand whether or not this is actually true. And he`s really assembling a staff or whether or not that`s someone who`s just sort of saying all these things and leaking on purpose, just so that we can be talking about him, and of course, he has these mounting legal challenges, which is really the sort of hottest news and the most relevant news about former President Trump right now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jackie, \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2 , what on earth took so long? Why is the January 6 committee talking to her now? She`s known him, all about him for years and years, could they`ve been talking to her months ago?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: Yeah, you know, this committee is extremely deliberate every step along the way. And I think there`s a reason why they kept \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2  until the very end. And if you don`t think you can`t find a person closer to the former president than Hope. What some sources have suggested to me is that the committee deliberately waited until they had as much information as possible to throw at her during a more formal interview, in order to sort of dot their I`s and cross their T`s as they wrap up the -- their investigation.\par \par \uc2 As we all know, their final hearing focus, in large part on the former president`s frame of my state of mind what he was thinking during the months leading up to January 6, and his efforts to overturn the results of the election. So obviously, Hicks` testimony would have been extremely crucial and sort of painting that picture and rounding that out. But, you know, having her testimony going into this final stretch, where they`re writing the final report now, and we`ll probably be putting on one last hearing in order to present this report, they might be able to glean something new, and really, again, sort of corroborate and tie up any loose ends that they might have after this year and a half long investigation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, Barry, given their timeframe and their scope of power, which is limited. What should the January 6 Committee be focused on right now?\par \par \uc2 BERKE: You know, the January 6 committee has done such a wonderful job of using the subpoena power and the threat of holding people in contempt who don`t show up to really lay out for the American people and the Department of Justice, where the evidence is. You know, \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2  is a great example of someone close to Trump. She was featured in a number of books as saying that she thought the election was lost and said it and left shortly after January 6. So, what they`re doing now is they`re focusing on the most important issue, the former President`s intent. They`re doing it for the American people so they can learn more in the report and any further steps they take. And they`re also doing it for the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 So, while they may come to an end, depending on what happens in this election, they may not. They have created a tremendous body of evidence that can`t be ignored. I think both by the American people but importantly, by the investigators who are clearly following up on what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What happens if the American people ignore it?\par \par \uc2 BERKE: If the American people ignore it, it`s a continuum. Again, I do believe that the American people believe in our democracy, you see them voting in record numbers. And it may take the department to make the decision to indict the president. What I will tell you obviously that`s a big deal to indict a former president. But you know what a bigger deal, to not indict someone for whom there`s overwhelming evidence solely because of the position they held.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:07]\par \par \uc2 And I believe that if the department makes the determination that the facts are what they appear to be, and an overwhelming case that the former President tried to interfere with the most sacred right, we have in our country, the right to vote and the peaceful transfer power, they have a duty and obligation to bring that case. And if they don`t, then we`re really in trouble.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What Barry Berke is saying is, you can`t just shoot someone on Fifth Avenue and get away with it. Barry Berke, thank you for joining us tonight. Jackie Alemany, Yamiche Alcindor, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the highest stakes race that could determine Senate control more of tonight`s one and only debate between Pennsylvania Senate candidates \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 And later, more companies dropping Kanye West after his anti-Semitic remarks. The recent rise in rhetoric like that, and how to stop it. After Kanye`s deeply hateful history. It didn`t just start last week. So why is he losing contracts now? THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:20:43]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Let`s also talk about the elephant in the room. I had a stroke. He`s never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate, miss two words together. But it knocked me down but I`m going to keep coming back up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Pennsylvania is among the most closely watched Senate races in the country. And tonight, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz faced off in the only debate of the contest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: We need to fight about inflation. You know right now because it`s a tax on working families, you know, and Dr. Oz can`t possibly understand what that is like, you know, he has 10 Gigantic mansions.\par \par \uc2 OZ: The irony is he didn`t pay for his own house. He got it for $1 from his sister, and he hasn`t been able to earn a living on his own. He`s lived off his parents. He embraced us with Bernie Sanders who he endorsed. The two candidates call themselves the two most progressive people in America.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: You know what? Why don`t you pretend that you live in Vermont instead of Pennsylvania and run against Bernie Sanders because all you can do is talk about Bernie Sanders.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Oz, would you support a Trump 2024 run and why? 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I`ll support whoever the Republican Party puts up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has supported you. He has endorsed you. Why won`t you fully commit to supporting him in 2024?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I do, I would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he decided to run for president.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you support a Biden run in 2024, why? In 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: That`s obviously it`s up to his choice whether he wants. And if he does choose to run, I would absolutely support him. But ultimately, that`s ultimately only his choice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joining us now to discuss, John Micek, Editor-in-Chief at the Pennsylvania Capitol Star and former Philadelphia Mayor, Michael Nutter.\par \par \uc2 John, this debate is going to get spoken about a lot in the news in the next 24 hours. But is it going to matter to voters in P.A.?\par \par \uc2 JOHN MICEK, PENNSYLVANIA CAPITAL-STAR EDITOR IN CHIEF: My social media is already blowing up with people, meaning this one, Stephanie, the conversation is very much alive. And these are among some of the most engaged voters. But you`re correct. As to whether this is going to really move the needle in the race, which is now, if you dependent -- looking on what polls you look at, easy there will be between five points and two points. I`ll be skeptical to see. We had \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Mehmet Oz out there this evening.\par \par \uc2 You know, and what`s got to be a pretty chippy exchange pretty quickly, you know, I don`t know if that tone necessarily is going to appeal to a, you know, that small segment Pennsylvania voters who might still be on the fence here. Certainly, the partisans for both candidates who came in tonight came away energized by their candidate`s performance.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael, take a listen to this particular moment from the debate. We`ll talk about it on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OZ: There should not be involvement from the federal government and how states decide their abortion decisions. As a physician, I`ve been in the room when there`s some difficult conversations happening. I don`t want the federal government involved with that at all. I want women, doctors, local, political leaders, letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Walk me through what that supposed to look like. You`re a young, vulnerable, 16-year-old girl dealing with an unwanted pregnancy. And you`re supposed to address that with your parents, your doctor, and then what happens? You call a mayor?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL NUTTER, FORMER PHILADELPHIA MAYOR: Stephanie, that`s bullshit. I`m just calling it for what it is. Mr. Oz is avoiding the discussion about \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  trying to ban abortion nationally. He didn`t want to answer the question. He avoided it as best as possible. You know, by the way, as a black American, you know, oftentimes, states` rights have actually just not been good for many of us. So that that he wants to go back to the states` rights argument and avoid the question that was asked which was about Nancy -- \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , trying to put a national ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Oz is a fraud. It`s got 10, 11 whatever the number is, houses across the United States of America none of which are actually in Pennsylvania. He literally parachuted in to the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania and is trying to steal a seat in our state. He is a complete fraud in this race.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:21]\par \par \uc2 I think under the circumstances, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , having had a stroke, actually did very well. He took his time. He made his points, obviously, this debate format, under the best of circumstances, I`ve seen a few debates in my time. That was not a great format for anyone. But certainly not good for someone who had literally just suffered a stroke. But John was valiant in his effort to try to respond and if you really listen to what he actually said he, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , actually tried to answer every question that was asked him. Mr. Oz was on a campaign rhetoric and actually didn`t really say anything that made any difference to the voters of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, given how he performed recovering from his stroke, Michael, some Republicans are out there tonight, saying that Fetterman is unfit, he should withdraw. But do they not have a race in the State of Georgia, someone running for the very same position in \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who`s facing family scandals lies about his past jobs and in his last debate, pulled out a prop badge and somehow believes he has some sort of adjunct role with the local police force?\par \par \uc2 NUTTER: Clearly, Mr. Walker is delusional. But I mean, I mean that that`s just a sideshow. When we talk about Pennsylvania, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  tried his best, under difficult circumstances. Mr. Oz as a doctor, I think literally violated the Hippocratic Oath. He would never have advised any patient of his, I think, I hope, would have never lost any patient of his in the aftermath of a stroke to even be on a debate stage. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was valiant in his effort. He tried his best to answer the questions that were asked. Mr. Oz did not.\par \par \uc2 And I want to give that credit to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  for trying to do his best under a certain set of circumstances that were not favorable for him. But he gave a valiant effort. I admire what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  tried to do tonight. And Mr. Oz should be ashamed of himself for the things that he said. The positions that he has taken. He has no legitimacy in this race. He parachuted into Pennsylvania. He`s trying to steal a seat. And I mean, it was really despicable what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, not only did he parachute in, he left God`s country, New Jersey who would do that?\par \par \uc2 NUTTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: John Micek, Michael Nutter.\par \par \uc2 NUTTER: Come one.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Thank you both for joining me tonight. We`re going to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 NUTTER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, we`re talking Kanye West, finally facing new consequences for his anti-Semitism. But he has a long history of hate. Why face consequences now? When THE 11TH HOUR continues?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:33:06]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KANYE WEST, MUSICIAN: The thing about me and Adidas is like I could literally say anti-Sematic (bleep) and they can`t drop me. I can say anti- Semitic things and Adidas can`t drop me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, no, you can`t, I guess. That didn`t age well. About a week after Kanye West said that and other anti-Semitic things. Adidas did finally drop him today. Hours later Gap said, they are pulling all of the rappers Yeezy merchandise from their store shelves immediately. But his supporters they are still out there. Over the weekend, an antisemitic hate group hung a banner supporting his rhetoric over Los Angeles freeway.\par \par \uc2 Let`s discuss, joining us now former Democratic Senator from Minnesota my friend, Al Franken, who hosts a podcast bearing his name. And George M. Johnson, new friend of mine and activist and author of, "All Boys Aren`t Blue," which has become one of the most banned books in the United States. Gentlemen, I welcome you both. George wins, better dressed just so you know. Al, you`ve already lost this segment. I want to get both of your takes. George to you first, your take on what`s happened to Kanye?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE M. JOHNSON, AUTHOR, ALL BOYS AREN`T BLUE: Yeah, like --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With Kanye, not to Kanye.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: -- with Kanye, right? Like what took so long? I was around for, you know, slavery was a choice. And as I gave my speech tonight, you know, honoring the ancestors at the TIME 100 Next Gala, I think about that when I think about what`s happening now, right? It`s like, like what took so long for everybody else to understand that this person`s rhetoric was this dangerous, was this problematic, was this harmful to not just the people who lived in our past, but to all of us who have to exist now? Underneath the oppressions and the new laws and all of these things that are coming against black people, queer people, trans people as we watch these bills roll out Roe v. Wade being overturned, right? Like, this is not something brand new. And we watched his rhetoric for years. And he allowed it until it got to being antisemitic. And now all of a sudden, everybody`s like, now the line has been crossed.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:20]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What do you think about that Al? Because he makes the point, right? In 2013. Kanye put the Confederate flag on his merchandise. In 2015, he said racism is a dated concept. In 2020, he said that slavery was a choice. During that time period, what happened to Kanye? He got bigger and bigger contracts. And he got richer and richer. What does it say about us as a culture and a society, that we were OK with that racism? And we`ve rightfully so drawn a line today? We`ve drawn a line now. But what did take so long?\par \par \uc2 AL FRANKEN, (D) MINNESOTA FORMER U.S. SENATOR: I don`t know. I mean, he`s black. And --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, you can be racist if you`re black. We`re down with that?\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: And you can`t be antisemitic if you`re Jewish, either. I mean, there`s been the history of all of this, and I can`t believe it`s taken this long. And, you know, I`m Jewish. There`s bad history here, as there is about his terrible history in this country, slavery. But antisemitism is just on the rise. Here, the \uc2 ADL\uc2  takes these instances and statistics, they`ve just been going up and up and up. You saw this thing today. And Republicans aren`t condemning it. They haven`t condemned the other stuff either. And Trump has been leading the way in this from Charlottesville. Jews will not replace us. The people on both sides when he started doing stuff at the caravan and 18. And there was all the old Soros is paying for the caravan. When you saw the Tree of Life murders. This is a very dangerous. Man, and I can`t -- I don`t have an answer for you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We shouldn`t stand for any hate speech.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We should not stand for any racism, any antisemitism. But what does this say to you when you look at this, and you say, hold on a minute. So, everybody was OK with racism, all this time buying your Yeezy sneakers, getting your gap jacket. But now you draw the line. It says though, what does that say about our country?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah, I also want to, like clarify a few things like, you know, it`s you cannot be racist as a black person, you`d be prejudiced as a black person. You`d be prejudiced against many systems, you`d be prejudiced against -- this isn`t for oppressions that are against you. But like racism is a system that exists for a certain person, which is a white person who already has the power, to condemn, to oppress, to literally affect your livelihood. So, it`s like, I understand where we`re going with this, but it`s like, a black person can`t be racist, right? Like a black person does not have that type of power to will that type of thing.\par \par \uc2 Now, what Kanye West was doing, though was, was he being antisemitic? Yes. Was he participating in anti-blackness? Yes. And those are things that we can address. And those are things that we can call out. And what I would have liked to have happened was those things should have been called out when he said that, you know, slavery was a choice. When he was making --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: 2013, 2015, 2018 when he became a billionaire?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Right, when he -- when those things were happening, and he was still climbing up using that rhetoric, that was when those things should have been addressed. Many of us --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When truthfully people like me allowed their children to get Yeezy sneakers, right? That is an ugly truth, right?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I didn`t do anything. But what did I do in the last few days, spoke out against antisemitism. So, what does that say about all of us? What have we been desensitized to?\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: Maybe George`s point speaks to what I just kind of instinctually came up with, was when I said, well, he`s black. And therefore, and you said he can`t be racist because he`s black. I`m, I`m just trying to figure this out. I don`t know the answer to this.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We are together.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: And so maybe that`s --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s a progress happening.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: That`s an odd piece of it. That`s where I went kind of instinctually. And so maybe that`s an odd piece of it when you say a black person can`t be racist. Maybe there`s something in in people`s mind that somehow that explains some of this.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: The honest truth is, a black person can be prejudiced because of the experiences that they`ve had throughout their life, right? Like I grew up as a black person in this country and so like I can be prejudiced and like have like a really disdain and hatred even towards white people. Because if my experience was that, that was my direct oppressor for so many years and for so long, then I can have that.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:18]\par \par \uc2 But racism is not just about like I hate you. It`s a system and anti- blackness is a system and is the system that throughout education, throughout health care, it oppresses us all. So, or oppresses black people as a whole. So, yeah.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK. This is a healthy and constructive conversation.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Brittany Packnett Cunningham put something out on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  today that I thought was really profound.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Where she said, white supremacists -- white supremacist, love seeing the black and Jewish community fight with one another. White supremacist do not like blacks or Jewish people. So right now, are they actually getting their dream come true?\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: Well, yeah. Well, you see us fighting.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Are we fighting?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: No.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: By the way -- No, I was --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He`s joking.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: By the way, I want to say something, which I`m one of the only two people here was one of the TIME 100.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Oh, yeah.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: And you two met, like an hour ago.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We met we met an hour ago. We did. And I was worried that you weren`t going to be that impressive of a guest tonight. And so, I said, God, I met a guy tonight. Great, let`s bring Georgia in. I`m kidding. He doesn`t think my joke as funny. This isn`t a funny segment.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: It was funny. I was --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This isn`t a funny segment.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I understand.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How do we address this stuff, right? What if this is true? This is what white supremacists want?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FRANKEN: Sure, they want this. Then we can`t let them -- I mean, we have to, we`re going to fight him. And this is scary. This is a scary time. Our democracy is at stake. And this is we`re going in a direction that is very bad. And we`ve seen this in history before. And part and parcel is racism. Part and parcel is antisemitism. And it`s