d my guess is that the President himself would agree that we need to have this kind of diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, let`s talk about the next two weeks, what the Democrats need to focus on, it`s go time?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: The economy. Look, we`re winning on reproductive rights as a human right. Oz made a terrible gift today that`s going to cost that. We`re winning on voting rights. But we`ve got to win the economy. And we`ve got such a simple case to make. We`re bringing manufacturing home. We`re actually helping the working class. They`re running on Trussonomics. They`re literally running on what bankrupted the British. They`re saying that they want tax cuts. They`re saying they want to cut Social Security. How are we not winning the economic argument when they`re running on exactly what Britain did?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You make the best point, how are we not winning on the economic argument? You`re the ones who have taken us from the depths of the pandemic, then what is it about messaging and strategy that Republicans seem to win on this when they`re not even offering anything?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: You know, bluntly, Stephanie, it`s the consultant class.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What is that mean?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I`ll tell you what, they look at the polling. They say, Democrats you`re up on reproductive rights, you`re up on voting rights. Talk about that. And don`t talk about the economy. That may be a tough issue for us. Give me a break. I mean, look, I`m for codifying Roe vs. Wade as our first vote. Second vote, let`s have voting rights. But why not on third, fourth, fifth sixth vote to the economy that we need to talk about it and make the choice.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the things that`s a problem and the same thing with the with Russia, we have no sense of nuance anymore in our politics. No -- just the sense of, you do one thing, and you have to stick to that. We can talk about abortion, voting rights and the economy. And we need to speak with our hearts as opposed to poll tested messages.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right then. Congressman, thank you so much for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Really good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Great to have you here at 30 Rock. Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When we come back, so much kindness from one of college basketball`s fiercest competitors toward a hard-working Kentucky dad. When THE 11TH HOUR continues. You want to stay up for this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:56:58]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, a little bit of Kentucky kindness. College Basketball is a huge deal, especially in places like Kentucky. So maybe it`s not surprising that a picture of a father quickly went viral this week after he showed up with his son at a \uc2 University of Kentucky\uc2  pre-season game. In his uniform and covered straight from the coal mine. That father`s dedication to the Wildcats and to his boy touch the hearts of people across the country. It also got the attention of some very important people on the sidelines. My friend and colleague, Tom Llamas has this really sweet story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: For coal miner, Michael McGuire, cheering on Kentucky Wildcats basketball is a family tradition.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCGUIRE: I was a little boy growing up watching with my dad. We never did miss a game, watching on TV.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: So, when the Wildcat men`s basketball team announced a blue and white scrimmage match in the Maguire`s East Kentucky town, wife Mollie, was first in line to buy tickets.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE MCGUIRE: I hopped on and grabbed some tickets because I know it was important to him and his dad and I thought it would be a great experience for him to share with our son.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Working in a coal mine as a roof bolter. Michael often works long weekend shifts.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCGUIRE: I started about a year ago and it kind of blew my mind. It`s literally just like a big giant cave.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: The day of the game, Michael, ran straight from the mind to the game, still covered, head to toe in cold dust, but dedicated to have this moment with his three-year-old son, Easton.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pretty much anybody that football was his favorite.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Another Kentucky fan snap this photo of Easton and Michael at the game, that image going viral and resonating deeply with someone else on the court.\par \par \uc2 JOHN CALIPARI, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF KENTUCKY\uc2  MEN`S BASKETBALL COACH: He wanted to be there so bad that he was willing to leave without showering, without changing, just get in his car and go because he got out of the mind late and I appreciate it because it`s how my family got to start in this country.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Head Kentucky basketball Coach, John Calipari is the grandson of a coal miner.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE MCGUIRE: My phone rang and I had no clue who it was and I answered and he was like, hey, it`s Coach Cal. And I was like I got you sit there. I was like, it was a complete shock.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Coach Cal inviting the whole family to any U.K. home game this season. Adding on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , the family will be treated like VIPs.\par \par \uc2 CALIPARI: You`re bringing light to a good man, hard working Kentucky in a coal miner who does everything he can to make time for his family and his son, and his daughter.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: From one coal mining family to another, the chance to carry on a tradition.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Special thanks to my dear friend Tom Llamas for sharing that story. And I really hope my friend Rex Chapman is watching tonight. That`s invest to Kentucky right there.\par \par \uc2 And as we like to say here at THE 11TH HOUR, in a world where you can be 