 and there`s also -- I mean, the other thing that I think about is we -- it does seem like the most sort of unhinged, conspiratorial thinking, which is always been there probably since humans existed, and antisemitic conspiracy theory, which is at the heart of Western culture for literally centuries, right? That all manner of conspiracy thinking now seems closer to the mainstream than it used to.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And so, you can watch people go down rabbit holes. It might be about, you know, dropboxes. Or in the case of Kanye, you could just watch what sites he`s reading, where it`s like the Jews control the world. And that now feels more proximate in the informational environment we live in than perhaps it did previously.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: I think that one of the things we`re seeing is that the more conspiratorial a society is, the more susceptible it is to antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Because the more you`re convinced that there`s an invisible hand behind your problems, the fewer \uc2 Google\uc2  searches away you are from discovering that that hand belongs to an invisible Jew. And so, as our society starts to lose its ideas of what is true and everyone has these alternate realities where they literally can`t see eye to eye with each other, it becomes a lot easier for people to become conspiracy theorists, and then eventually to become antisemitic conspiracy theorists.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, because it converge like enough -- I mean, I`ve been in enough -- in enough spaces of paranoid conspiracy thinking and reported on them. It always is not a few -- more than a few clicks to -- it`s like, well, I don`t like the Federal Reserve. It`s like, well, who runs the Federal Reserve, you know.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: it always just a few hop, skip, and a jump to the ultimate conspiracy theory which is Jews around the world.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: And it 