oth parties believing their political opposition poses an existential threat to the country.\par \par \uc2 What will that divide mean for the country after November 8th? I will ask George candidate for governor \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in just a moment. You absolutely don`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 But, first, joining me now, Senator Kirsten Gillibrand, Democrat of New York. Senator, 16 days until midterms, more than half of the country is engaged in early voting by the end of the week, a lot of issues on the table, along with some of those new polling numbers, which I will get to in a bit. But broadly, why do you think Democrats will be able to hold on to the Senate just about two weeks out from Election Day?\par \par \uc2 SEN. KIRSTEN GILLIBRAND (D-NY): I think Democrats can hold on to the Senate because of the quality of our candidates. We`ve extremely strong candidates who are doing well in their states. And despite states being red or purple, we have Democrats who are leading in the polls today. So, I am optimistic. You look at races like Mark Kelly in Arizona, he is ahead, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  in New Hampshire, Reverend Warnock in Georgia, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  in Nevada, these are incumbents who are doing very well in their states. And on top of that, you have about five open seats, including Fetterman in Pennsylvania and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in Ohio. We are doing very well neck and neck. We are up in many polls.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Why is it so important that Democrats retain control of the Senate at this time, in your words, Senator?\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: Everything is at stake that you care about. Right now, if Democrats can hold the Senate, it means we can stop more right-wing judges from being appointed and populating our judiciary across the board. If another Supreme Court seat becomes available, Democrats can make sure there is a wise, thoughtful addition to the Supreme Court to counter the radical right of the current that Trump has created and stacked. Right now, women`s reproductive freedoms are basic civil rights and civil liberties, our equality and citizens is at risk nationally if we don`t hold the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And right now, President Biden and Democrats are doing good things to rebuild the economy. We`ve already passed the bipartisan infrastructure bill. We`ve already passed the bipartisanship chips bill, to do advanced manufacturing, semiconductor manufacturing, creating jobs. And we did a lot to help the cost of things, getting the cost of medicines down, getting the cost of energy down. Those are things that are really vital to American families. So, we are just doing a good job of moving this legislation to help people forward and I think we are just getting started.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been campaigning for Republicans and there are rumblings he may also insert himself into the midterms, even after the votes are cast.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:10]\par \par \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2  reports today that Trump wants to aggressively challenge close races that aren`t declared on election night. He is particularly fixated on the Pennsylvania Senate race, where he and other Republicans are already preparing to wage legal and activist crusades against the election integrity of Democratic strongholds, such as the Philly area.\par \par \uc2 The story cites sources familiar with Trump`s conversations, although NBC has not independently verified their accounts. How worried are you about Trump, once again, throwing our democratic system into chaos?\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: Well, President Trump has not stopped trying to throw our democracy into chaos. He contested the last election. He is going to contest the next election. And this is what President Trump does. He does not fight fair. He is dishonest and he lies and he continues to lie.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats are prepared. We have our own lawyers. We have our own arguments. We will make sure that we win these elections by a strong enough margin that there is no room for President Trump to contest the outcome. I think that is essential in the four incumbent races that I mentioned, as well as some of these open seats, like in Pennsylvania and Wisconsin and Ohio and North Carolina and Florida.\par \par \uc2 We have to work very hard to get every vote out, to not give an inch, and to make sure people understand what is at stake in this election. It`s the future of the country, whether we have equality, whether we have voting rights, whether we have reproductive freedom, whether we have a growing economy, whether we get people back to work, focusing on education and health care and jobs. It was President Biden, who got us out of this pandemic and got the economy moving again and that is what Americans want.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, going back to one of the NBC News polls I mentioned earlier, 81 percent of Democrats think the Republican Party`s agenda is an existential threat to the country. 79 percent of Republicans think it is the Democrats` agenda that is the threat. I can`t help but wonder what it means that voters are so polarized going into November and what that means for either party, should they succeed.\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: Well, I think it`s vital that Democrats correct the record and talk about all the strong bipartisan work we are doing, things that we have come together to pass common sense reforms, like investing in infrastructure, common sense investments, like mental health care, to make sure we can end gun violence, common sense bipartisan work, like making sure America can be at the forefront of advanced manufacturing, A.I., quantum, all these things that create jobs and make us more nationally secure. We also put in place a lot of reforms that will get our prices for medicines down as well as the cost for energy down, and as well as advancing new technologies, like green energy, to create those green jobs.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there`s a lot of misinformation out there and I can tell you that both \uc2 CISA\uc2  and \uc2 FBI\uc2  did release a statement, a warning, that, unfortunately, adversaries worldwide are trying to attack people in America, to make them really be divisive and to be angry with one another, purposefully, to undermine our democracy and undermine our confidence in it. So, voters should be aware that when you are having a fight online with someone, you may be having a fight with some bot that has nothing to do with the American electorate.\par \par \uc2 So, I would just really talk about what is getting done, why it helps people, why getting prescription cost down for our seniors matter so much, getting the price of insulin cap so people can afford it, putting a cap on overall medical costs for our seniors for their medicines. That matters. And so really correcting the record that Democrats are working hard on a bipartisan basis to get things done for the American people, to help jobs and the economy and health care, to improve our education system. Those are things that matter to every American family. And we are getting things done and we are doing it together.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you for being with us, New York Senator Kirsten Gillibrand.\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: Thanks, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Transitioning now to a very important gubernatorial race here in Georgia. Joining me now, Georgia Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , a Democrat. Ms. Abrams, first of all, thank you for being with us tonight and we truly appreciate, ahead of your midterm contest.\par \par \uc2 According to the Georgia secretary of state this weekend, more than 660,000 people have voted early in person in your state as of the end of Friday.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 I spoke at three church services. I preached, like your parents, at three services this morning here in Atlanta and 90 percent of all three congregations have already voted. Total turnout is near 730, 000, including absentee ballots. Looking at this kind of early turnout, where does your campaign see the momentum going with two weeks to go?\par \par \uc2 GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE STACE ABRAMS (D-GA): Thank you so much for have thing me, Reverend Al. We believe that the momentum demonstrates what we`ve been arguing for the last few months that there`s deep energy among voters who are too often not reflected in the polls, and that the most important poll is the one that you show up to, to cast your ballot. We are excited and enthusiastic about the turnout, but we want to encourage everyone to make sure we don`t let the momentum drag. Week two is just as important as week one, and we know for all of those who showed up in week one, we now need you to call three of your friends and make sure they show up this week.\par \par \uc2 Voter suppression is alive and well in Georgia and they are pushing this argument that says that because we have record turnout, there is no suppression. That is completely wrong. That is like saying because more people get in the water, there are fewer sharks. The sharks are still out there, but the best way to overcome them is to have more people participate in the process. Voter turnout is the antidote to voter suppression. It is not proof that there is no suppression. We believe that as voters understand, the barriers are rayed against them, they will show up and they will turn out in record numbers.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Ms. Abrams, Georgia has obviously emerged as a southern battleground. Its role in making \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  president two years ago, built on a multiracial cross-section of voters. There was, of course, the effort by President Trump to affect the outcome of that election in your state. And following all of that into 2021, the passage of the so-called Election Integrity Act, restricting drop boxes, giving more power to the state legislature over elections, triggering a Justice Department lawsuit over allegations that the law violates the Voting Rights Act.\par \par \uc2 With all that, with that scene set, for our viewers in other states watching this race, why has Georgia become this bellwether and what does its issues mean for the rest of the nation?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: So, let`s begin with the composition of Georgia. We are the most diverse state in the south. We are 48 percent African-American, Latino, AAPI and Native American, 50 percent white. That means that you have a very racially balanced population. But what we also know is that Georgia unfortunately lags behind on a number of key metrics. This is the most dangerous state in the nation for women who get pregnant, the highest maternal mortality rate. Georgia has the second highest rate of people without health insurance cards, which means when you get sick, you might get help, you might not. We are the eighth largest state in the country but we have the fourth highest eviction rate for housing, and so all of those issues are front and center.\par \par \uc2 This is a conversation in this election cycle about the economy. We know that inflation is a global phenomenon, but the state of Georgia, like the governors across the country, can do something to influence pricing at home. And, unfortunately, we have a governor who has refused to do his part. He has refused to expand Medicaid to reduce the cost of health care. He has refused to address the affordable housing crisis, sitting on $400 million in funds that were intended for the use of people to keep them in their housing. He is refusing to do real pay raises for our teachers. And he is refusing to invest in our small businesses to the extent that only even though we`re half the population, minority businesses only get 1.5 percent of state contracts. He said he used to study the issue.\par \par \uc2 But what this signals is that this election is a referendum on a kind of state we want to be, not only in the south but as a bellwether for the nation. And if we do what I know we can, if voters show up, look, what we can demonstrate is that we want someone who not only cares but is willing to help and is not going to attack our freedoms.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and his cohort, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , have spent the last four years attacking our right to vote, making it more difficult to participate in our elections. They have gotten credit for simply not committing treason. But that is the lowest bar in America. We need leaders who actually believe in our access to democracy.\par \par \uc2 And the last thing I will say on this, Reverend Al, is that we keep focusing on election denial about the outcome, but the more pernicious version is denying access, and that is what \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  specialized in, denying access to the right to vote, keeping people away from their participation in our democracy.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:14]\par \par \uc2 And that is more dangerous because you don`t even have to manipulate the outcome if you can stop people from even getting into the process.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I don`t have to remind you that your opponent, Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , who you just mentioned, has campaign for reelection against you in large part on the theme that you`re soft on crime. It was repeated in your first debate with Kemp last week, his questioning of your law enforcement relationships and it comes at a time critics are arguing some Republican candidates are intentionally linking race and crime as a tactic to undermine the black opponents. What`s your take, Ms. Abrams? Do you agree with those critics?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Well, I`ve noticed that Mr. Kemp has been willing to use almost every racial trope in politics. He called me an angry black woman on the stage. He darkened my skin in his ads. He has tried to link me to false allegations that I don`t support law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Now, unlike Mr. Kemp, I don`t feel compelled to list every man who supports me. I`m not a member of an all-boys network, so I don`t have the club roster. What I can tell you is that I`m supported by law enforcement but I`m also supported by returning citizens who want an opportunity to participate in our society again. I`m supported by those victims of gun violence who watched our streets be flooded by weapons because of Brian Kemp`s weakening of gun laws.\par \par \uc2 They know that because of his decision to eliminate a background check, Georgia has become a hotbed of gun violence just in the last two days. The number of violent incidences with guns have been across the headlines across the state. And so what we know is that \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is falling back on the same racist tropes that have too often been a part of how they win elections.\par \par \uc2 But what we know is that Georgia is different today than it was 50 years ago. We`re not going backwards. And we`re not looking backwards. People understand in Georgia, we have to have a complicated understanding of the need for both public safety and accountability and justice, that all three pieces are the way you move this state forward. And that is why we`re seeing so many people energized, enthusiastic and pushing back against \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and his attempts to scare us into submission. We`re going to fight our way to progress and I look forward to being part of that progress.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Ms. Abrams, I have to ask you this, because some political observers have raised the concern that in the midst of your rematch with Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , you haven`t garnered the same kind of fervor from black voters that you did during the first contest with him four years ago based on exit polls. Now, obviously, the black vote for Democrats seeking statewide office is critical in Georgia. Will those voters show up in the numbers that you campaigned for and campaigned with and that your campaign needs to be successful in two weeks?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I will tell you that if you look at the turnout that we are seeing, not only is that record turnout overall, it is record turnout amongst black voters. I have been traveling the state for the past week in our Let`s Get It Done tour, encouraging people to turn out to vote. And like you saw this morning in the churches, people are enthusiastic, they are ready to go.\par \par \uc2 What has been wrong about these reports is that these reports have been looking at a snapshot. The question is who are you taking a picture of? Are you taking a picture of those families who cannot send their children to college because the Hope Scholarship doesn`t go far enough, and black children are the most likely to lose hope, assuming they even get it. Are you taking a picture of those students who are trapped and underfunded schools where \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  slashed the budget in 2019 until federal Democrats stepped in and paid for education in the state of Georgia? Are you taking a picture of the 40 percent of those who would get access to health care if we expanded Medicaid knowing that 40 percent of those people are black?\par \par \uc2 And so what we know is that black voters are not only enthusiastic, they are showing up in record numbers. They`ve already exceeded their turnout in 2018. They`ve exceeded their percentage of the population, and that to me is a strong sign that the reports of our unrest, the reports were greatly exaggerated. What we are seeing instead is not only enthusiasm but a determination to navigate Brian Kemp`s voter suppression, to navigate the challenges and barriers because they understand what`s at stake, more money in their schools, more money in their pockets, more opportunity in our communities and more freedom in our lives.\par \par \uc2 Because the effect of his anti-abortion ban, that six-week abortion ban, is going to be incredibly harmful to black women in the state of Georgia. Because let`s remember, the south is home to 56 percent of black people. And unless I become the governor of Georgia, we will be in a wasteland, a desert, that has no access to reproductive rights from Texas to South Carolina, from Tennessee to Florida.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:05]\par \par \uc2 Unless I become the governor of Georgia, repeal that law and make Georgia an oasis for black women and for women across the country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , and you seem fired up, and the voters here I talked to seem fired up as well. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, recent polling reveals Americans favor Republicans when it comes to major midterm issues. How is the DNC going to respond, after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION from Atlanta, Georgia, tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let`s continue to dig into the numbers from NBC`s News poll. 46 percent of voters prefer Republicans to control congress compared to 47 percent for Democrats, leaving 7 percent still not sure.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:06]\par \par \uc2 And Republicans are leading Democrats in their enthusiasm to vote ahead of the midterms, 78 to 69.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 , Chair of the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 . Let`s stay on the topic of enthusiasm. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  writes, Democrats are holding out hope that the issue of abortion can raise turnout, overcoming a slight voting enthusiasm advantage that Republicans have held in national polls all year. In the summer, the Dobbs decision seems to give Dems a bump. But now, the GOP seems resurgent on the topics of crime and the economy.\par \par \uc2 With all the negative stuff put out there by Republicans about Democrats on these issues, what positive signs, Mr. Chairman, do you see for Democrats and the chances of keeping control of Congress?\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , CHAIR, DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Well, Rev, I know people like polls, but the best poll for me is being on the ground. And I`m right now in the midst of a bus tour. We are going from Florida to Pennsylvania. But even prior to that, I`ve been in Arizona, I`ve been Nevada, I`ve been in Ohio, Florida, Georgia, Kentucky. When I was in Kentucky, I went in a rally, we had 400 people jam-packed and ready for change.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of energy on the ground right now. And so a lot of people are saying, you can bet against us if you want but we know that Democrats are fighting for us and delivering for us while Republicans are trying to take us back, trying to take us back on abortion, trying to take us back in terms of the economy, destroying social security and Medicare. And that`s rallying the troops and getting people fired up.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why you see the record turnout you see in a place like Georgia. I was just in Georgia on Friday. And I can tell you, the people that I met, they`re fired up and they`re ready to go.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, you mentioned Arizona. Let`s turn to Arizona`s midterm races, where Kari Lake seems to be catching fire as a national Trumpy figure. Meanwhile, Democrats are losing ground not only in their governor`s race but in the Senate race too with Blake Masters. What`s behind this recent surge in Republican support in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, I just don`t believe, Rev. Again, I was on the ground there in Arizona. I met with African-Americans, Latino leaders and Native American leaders. And I can tell you they have organized on the ground there. The DNC has invested. We`ve almost tripled what we have invested in the 2018 cycle. And what we`re doing now in Arizona is one of our top battleground states. And Mark Kelly has put together one heck of a campaign and he`s working very closely -- his campaign is working closely with \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I feel good about the operations on the ground. We`ve just got to continue to go out there, push and make sure that we let them know what we are going to do, but reframe the Republicans as the extremists. These are the people who want these national abortion bans. They want to wreck the American economy. They want to suppress voters. And we cannot allow that to happen.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let`s switch gears to Pennsylvania and the Senate race there. In two days, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is squaring off with his Republican opponent, Mehmet Oz, Dr. Oz, during a make-or-break debate. Fetterman`s health has become a significant issue in the campaign. Do you think that`s fair that that has become such a big issue?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , I`ve known him for a while, he`s a good guy and he`s fighting hard for the people of Pennsylvania because he`s from Pennsylvania. He knows Pennsylvania. Dr. Oz a scam artist, has always been a scam artist. We see all of the lawsuits, we see the things that he`s even done to dogs. I mean, it`s shameful who this guy is. And he doesn`t live there. He`s from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 So, in the end of the day, I think the people of Pennsylvania are going to double down and vote for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , they`re going to vote for \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , because those two candidates are the best candidates to be in the governor`s mansion and also to represent Pennsylvania, the commonwealth in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right now to the race between \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  right here in Georgia. This week, Senator Warnock released to new attack ads going after Republican Challenger Walker over abortion and abuse allegations. Recent polling has the race as a toss-up. What do you think Warnock hasn`t been able to pull away -- why he hasn`t been able to pull away, I should say, from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 [17:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, we`ve got to continue to put out there, that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is -- he doesn`t know the truth, that the truth came up and tapped him on the shoulder. This guy lies about everything, about his jobs, about his family relationships. He is just a lie. And we got to continue to make sure that that message is out there. In all the polls over the past few weeks, Reverend Warnock has been ahead. We know it`s going to be a tight race because Republicans, they -- you know, they say they`re for something red, but we know they`re hypocrites, and they really on.\par \par \uc2 All there for is power. And we know that the person who`s been fighting for Georgia is Reverend Warnock. From the student loan efforts to the infrastructure, he`s been on the ground fighting. Almost came very close to getting Medicaid expansion in Georgia for federal but if we get two more votes in the Senate, we can even work to make sure that gets done. But Reverend Warnock has been fighting for the people of Georgia, and as we say, in the south, and as it relates to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , bless his heart.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And if we get some more votes in the Senate, on the right side of the aisle, maybe we can get voting rights in the name of great Georgian, \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Amen for that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: DNC Chair Jamie Harrison, thank you for being with us. After the break, we`ll head out west where the first African American Filipino Attorney General of California is working to keep his seat. Yes, we had his opponent on yesterday and today, he`ll get a chance to respond. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:35:46]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation, with 60 days until the midterms, we`re highlighting the race for California Attorney General, it`s the highest law enforcement position in the country`s most populous state, a position once held by Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . We heard from Republican challenger or Nathan Hochman on yesterday. Today, we`re joined by his Democratic opponent, the current Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Bonta, first of all, thanks for joining us. When we spoke to your opponent on Saturday last night, he cited his justice experience as a major qualification for the job. But you are the incumbent Attorney General, you`ve been in office for a year and a half since the governor appointed you to succeed, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , who is now the Health Secretary. And you had a long career in state politics before that. Talk to me about what you`ve been doing in office and what you`d like to do in the next term?\par \par \uc2 ATTY. GEN. ROB BONTA, (D-CA), INCUMBENT CANDIDATE FOR ATTORNEY GENERAL: Thank you for having me, Reverend. It`s an honor to be here and appreciate the opportunity to join you. And thank you for that question. I think one very important point to be made is, my opponent has never been elected to any office by any set of constituents ever, in his lifetime, never had any State experience. This is a statewide elected position. And I have vast experience in statewide office, in elected office and working in issues related to criminal justice and public safety.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, my job is to be the people`s attorney, to fight for everyday people, with their -- whatever their challenges might be in the State of California. They very, certainly Public Safety is job number one, number two, and number three. And we`ve been on the case, active, effective, strong, stopping mass shootings in progress, stopping murders in progress, cracking cold cases, arresting suspects on the run, taking down criminal organizations involved in human trafficking, drug trafficking, including fentanyl, gun trafficking, including ghost guns, are taking an organized retail crime, including having a felony sentence in the largest organized retail crime takedown in the history of California. We`re taking on hate crimes, we`re taking guns out of the hands of those who are prohibited from having them because they present a risk. And we`ve been involved in many spaces in places.\par \par \uc2 We`re also protecting rights and freedoms, like the right to reproductive health care. We`re taking on gun violence, making sure that we defend our common-sense gun laws in court. We`re holding corporations accountable when they violate the law. We`re making sure there`s no antitrust violations. We`re taking on our housing crisis as well, making sure that we build more consistent with California law.\par \par \uc2 And in the end, our job is to defend, protect, preserve the constitutional rights, the civil rights and the consumer rights of the people of California. That`s what we`ve done over the last year and a half. That`s what I would do over the next four years.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Your opponent says criminality is spiraling out of control in California. But the statistics show that while violent crime went up 7% last year compared to 2020 when most residents were in COVID lock down, overall rates have remained steady in the past decade and well below their peak in the 90s. However, a Berkeley study conducted at the beginning of the year found crime was a top issue for California voters along with the closely related issue of homelessness. The same survey found respondents wanted tougher penalties for some kind of crimes. How are you responding to these concerns over public safety that you mentioned?\par \par \uc2 BONTA: We`re proceeding with the data, leading with the data. And the data is exactly what you said, overall, crime rates are remain at a relatively, a relative historic low. There are some rises in some crimes in some places so we`re addressing those.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:04]\par \par \uc2 And taking on those rises in crimes and also the way people feel is very important, a feeling of safety, including a reality of safety are very important. So, we`ve been active and been involved in in all of the places and spaces where there have been rises in crimes, organized retail crime, human trafficking, gun violence, which 75% of violent crime is fueled by gun violence. I am the candidate who was willing to take on the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and address gun violence. So, we`ve been leading with evidence doing what`s effective. The data shows the evidence shows, the studies show that the best way to deter crime, stop an individual from committing a crime in the first place is for that person to believe they`re going to be cut if they commit the crime. So, we need to arrest people. Stop them from harming others, stop them from harming property when it`s occurring, and then make sure that there is accountability. And it`s appropriate proportionate form consistent with and in relation to the crime that`s been committed.\par \par \uc2 So, I am the public safety candidate in this race. We`re getting the job done every day and all the ways that I`ve mentioned, that`s why I have the support and endorsement of statewide law enforcement groups and national law enforcement groups. And we`re also, while we pursue safety for Californians, we`re also pursuing fairness for our system. And for Californians as well. We need to be ambitious enough to pursue and seek both safety and justice.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now last week, you announced your office would be investigating the Los Angeles City Council redistricting process, after leaked audio emerged of city council members making highly racist remarks, while discussing the issue. The city council president resigned over the incident and two other members of the City Council are under intense pressure to follow suit. A concern to me as head of National Action Network is not only the racist language, but they were talking about redistricting. And really shaping in a way that would rob many of the citizens of California of their impact in voting. What`s your takeaway from this incident?\par \par \uc2 BONTA: Well, I`ll first say that as a human being, as a father, as a voter, the words that were said, the expressions that were made were inexcusable, unacceptable, painful, hurtful, abhorrent, reprehensible, inhumane. And there has been a deficit of trust created in the community because of it. And that`s why we -- my office, and I moved in a week and a half ago, to announce that we will be investigating this incident and any impact on redistricting to make sure that the voting rights, the constitutional rights, the civil rights of the people of Los Angeles have not been violated. And we will uncover the truth, we will shine a light. We will make sure to dig and find and pursue until the truth is revealed. And we will go wherever the facts and the law takes us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before I let you go, if elected another term as Attorney General, you will be faced with a host of issues on which states are increasingly pitted against one another. Since the supreme court overturn of Roe versus Wade, there`s some -- some states are banning abortions, while others are protecting reproductive rights. Many Americans are now traveling across state lines to get the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, as the son of Philippinian emigrant, you no doubt have been disgusted by Republican governors shipping migrants and asylum seekers across the country to score political points. Talk about the role you play as California`s AG in confronting these hot button national issues and protecting the interests of the people in your state?\par \par \uc2 BONTA: I have a very leading role as the Attorney General for the largest state in the nation, the most diverse state in the nation, leading the largest state department of justice in the nation, with a leading a state where the phrase has been coined as California goes, so goes the nation. We need to lead in this nation as California always has and will continue to do and whether it be on reproductive freedom, protecting a woman`s right to choose bodily, autonomy, or any other issue protecting our immigrants as one, as someone whose parents brought me here as an infant because they wanted me to grow up in a place with human rights and civil rights with due process, the rule of law, they want to be broken and democracy when that wasn`t in my future in the country I was born in, I want that for everyone here as well. And I will stand up and defend everyone`s rights, freedoms, their right to a democracy, whether it be here in California or through throughout the nation. We care about all Americans. And we will fight for the principles that make us the great country that we are. That is freedom, rights, safety, democracy.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:11]\par \par \uc2 And my opponent unfortunately wants to lay down and be timid and be passive. I want to be a champion lead from the front. Be loud on all of these issues that matter so much to Californians and to Americans. We will be bold. We will be will be courageous.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: California Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, abortion remains a top issue for some American voters, but not the top issue. Can Democrats still use it to get the vote out? We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation, with the Democrats congressional majorities at risk and just 16 days from today, this past Tuesday, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  vowed that the first bill he would send to Capitol Hill would codify Roe versus Wade into law. But only if Americans vote to keep Democrats in power in both the House and the Senate this midterm election and with wide enough margins to be able to pass abortion protections.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Laphonza Butler. She is the President of EMILY`s List, and was a former Advisor for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` 2020 presidential campaign. I know when she was a labor organizer. She`s come a long way. Thank you for joining me today, Laphonza Butler.\par \par \uc2 LAPHONZA BUTLER, EMILY`S LIST PRESIDENT: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me start with this. The stakes are high for Democrats this election season, and President Biden is doubling down on making abortion. The centerpiece of the midterms. However, recent polling conducted by \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  shows that voters see the economy and high inflation as their top issues over abortion rights. We`re just roughly two weeks left until Election Day. Is abortion still the mobilizing issue for Democratic voters that it seemed it was going to be the summer after the repeal of Roe vs. Wade?\par \par \uc2 [17:50:19]\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Well, thank you again, Reverend. It`s good to see you again. And I appreciate the question. I think we won`t let one poll distract (inaudible) fundamentally at risk this election cycle. Look, we know that families are still recovering from a global pandemic. We know that our children are still recovering from missing so much in person school. We know that our nation is continuing to deal with the global issues of inflation and supply chain challenges and backup, and people, we will not stand for our government to 10 or 50 years. And that`s exactly what this Supreme Court just did. I think that the American people since then have not only heard had their voices heard and poll after poll saying that this is a motivating issue for them. But we`ve seen that forever now all over the country. Let`s remember not too distant the reaction to voters in Kansas. Let`s remember and not to pass the special elections in New York and Alaska. Let`s take a look at what the young people all over this country are doing and 60 high schools and universities, fuck out to have their voices heard and their demands, be listened to, as we head into this election cycle. And let`s take a look at the data. Let`s take a look at the data about the tens of thousands and hundreds of thousands of women and young women who have registered to vote since the Supreme Court announced the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 And so yes, it is and will continue to be a motivating issue and go into those ballot boxes knowing that it is the Democratic Party proposing solutions on inflation. It`s the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me go right there where you just went because the Dobbs decision by the Supreme Court in June, inspired a surge of new voter registrations, who are more likely to be female, young and democratic. Data from states including those with highly contested elections coming up show Pennsylvania, Ohio and Florida, indicating evidence of a big leap in enthusiasm and political interest among potential voters, supportive of abortion rights. How do you think Democrats can transfer that surge of enthusiasm into wins with the little time they have left?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: We got granted (ph). We`ve got to make sure that the message that we posing this midterm election out is a message that speaks to what it is that is important to the American people. And it is (technical difficulty) and reproductive freedom. I think that (technical difficulty) that is most about freedom than any I`ve ever seen in my working career. It is a reproductive freedom. And it`s also economic freedom. It`s the ability to have one job and raise your family and have lower prescription drug costs. It is the work to make sure that we are engaging voters in their communities and are presenting candidates who speak directly to them.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not what we see the Republican Party doing, which is scrubbing their websites and frankly doing backflips to try to manipulate the American people as to where they stand on issues of reproductive freedom and what their positions might be on how to help move the economy forward. And so, we`ve got to make sure that we are engaging every single voter, not taking a single voter for granted and communicating that message like EMILY`S List is doing right now. In terms of who -- which party is standing with the will of the American people and which one is not.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Laphonza Butler, thank you for being with us this evening. Thank you, again.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a very important court appearance on this coming Tuesday. I`ll explain after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:58:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As I said I delivered three sermons this morning here in Atlanta and talked to many, many people and urging them, praying that they will come out and vote for whoever they vote for, because I really believe the direction of this country is at stake. And then as I was coming to the studio, with no real plan, I happen to look out the window and was passing where Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  and his wife, Coretta Scott King are buried here in Atlanta. And I thought about how people liked that King gave their lives to make this a better country.\par \par \uc2 I also prayed because in just two days, the Russian government will hear the appeal of imprisoned \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner. She was arrested in February and accused of carrying hashish oil while boarding a domestic flight. Griner who recently turned 32 years old, pleaded guilty to the charges and the judge sentenced her to nine years in prison. I`m going to pray for her release. And I`m going to pray because I think that we cannot have any situation where around the world people are used as political pawns. And that is what I believe she is to make points. So, whether it`s voting and protecting voting rights domestically and women`s right to choose, whether it`s dealing with political prisoners, in my judgment abroad, we must uphold the things that made America great because great people like \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , and Fannie Lou Hamer and others gave their lives to make this a better nation and make it live up to its principle and be a shining light for the whole world, including Russia, where Brittney is being held.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez, my colleague, starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221024eian00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for October 22, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Jessica Layton \par \uc2 6,259 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:02:04]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST, POLITICSNATION: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Hanging in the Balance.\par \par \uc2 . has opened in 17 states including various battleground states where both parties have called in their heavyweights to campaign two-and-a-half weeks left to the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Top-tier Democrats have been on the campaign trail courtesy of President Biden has been there who was in Pennsylvania, going into this weekend campaigning for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  as Democrats try to hold on to their razor thin majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is stumping for MAGA candidates in Texas tonight, and he faces a November 14th deadline to be deposed before the January 6 Committee. The congressional subpoena issue coming yesterday just hours after Trump`s former campaign manager, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  was sentenced to four months in prison for defying his congressional subpoena.\par \par \uc2 This, amid new revelations about what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  may have found in its August raid of Trump`s Mar-a-Lago estate. NBC News confirming that among the dozens of classified documents retrieved as highly sensitive US Intelligence on both China and Iran.\par \par \uc2 There are 17 days left to the midterms, America and we are counting with you down from 17 to the actual Midterm Day. That`s where we`re going to start tonight, and joining me now is Senator \uc2 Bob Casey\uc2 , Democrat of Pennsylvania, member of the Senate Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for joining us tonight, 17 days left, Senator, before all of America votes in the upcoming midterm. Are you confident that Democrats will prevail, keeping their majorities in Congress and in down ballot races?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB CASEY (D-PA): Reverend Al, great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I am confident. Of course, I think I probably know more about what is happening in Pennsylvania, but I think we stand a good chance to retain majorities. But this is a very close race, as you know, and your viewers know.\par \par \uc2 Every Senate election, for example, that`s contested, they all seem to be one or two-point races, so it`s going to be close. So, we have a lot more work to do to get the vote out, but I am confident in Pennsylvania that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  will be elected to the Senate in a close race and that \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  will be elected Governor.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, Senator, President Biden was in your State of Pennsylvania, going into this weekend stumping for Democratic Senate hopeful, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and his contest against Republican Senate candidate Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 The President at least so far has not participated in big campaign rallies this election cycle, keeping his appearance relatively low key. Some observers suggesting that his low approval ratings have made him a liability in some congressional races. Apart from fundraising, you think Biden`s visit to your State this week was helpful for Democrats?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: No question about it. I think it was, for a couple of reasons. We were with President Biden on Thursday. We were -- I should say on Friday -- we were going first and foremost to a bridge in Pittsburgh that had fallen down in January, a huge bridge, a hundred feet off the ground that just collapsed in January and the President made a promise to come back when that bridge was almost completed. And here we are, only 10 months later and the bridges is about a month or so from opening.\par \par \uc2 And the only reason that bridge could be opened was not just because of the good work of Governor Wolf and folks on the ground in Pittsburgh and in Pennsylvania, but because there was $25 billion available from the Infrastructure Bill.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it was an example of the President being able to say, this is what happens when you elect the Democratic majority, you`re able to move forward on big infrastructure projects and get them done. In this case, for this bridge, it was record time probably record -- a State record, if not a national record.\par \par \uc2 But I think everywhere you look, the President has been able to demonstrate that he has worked to lower costs for families, that he has helped us begin to rebuild and repair our broken infrastructure, and I think people are hearing that, especially as we get closer to the end and they need -- he obviously needs \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in the Senate to provide not just the 50th vote to maintain a majority, but what I would hope would be the 51st or 52nd vote.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And 10 months of bridge built, these kinds of things out of a Democratic majority now in the Senate. What are the consequences if America fails to keep Democrats in control of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: Reverend, as you know, it`s a long, long list, but just consider Voting Rights for example. We know that we had a big debate in January, we had 50 Democrats vote in favor of the Voting Rights Bill that bears the name of \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , your friend, and such an inspiration to so many of us. But in order to get that bill done, we`ve got to do one of two things: Change the Senate rules which I favor to pass bills like -- something as consequential as voting rights or to be able to get 10 Republicans to go along with this.\par \par \uc2 I think the likelihood of that is low, so voting rights is on the ballot, in a sense in this election when it comes to voting for the Senate or the House. So, we`ve got to work night and day to make sure that we win these races.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, you know, I couldn`t agree more. Voting rights is on the ballot since we couldn`t deal with the filibuster, and lost the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Bill in the Senate after it passed the House.\par \par \uc2 Senator quickly, while there is time. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has now been formally subpoenaed by the House Select Committee investigating January 6. This deposition is set a week after the midterms in November 14. The Select Committee describing its case in a letter issued to Trump yesterday alleging that, "He personally orchestrated and oversaw a multi-part effort to overturn the 2020 presidential election and to obstruct the peaceful transition of power."\par \par \uc2 That`s some damning language, Senator.\par \par \uc2 The question of course, will Trump show up for any deposition? And what will happen if he does not is the question?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: I think the likelihood of him showing up seems to be rather low, but when you consider what they are investigating Reverend Al, the deliberative nature of this Committee month after month, gathering evidence that formed the basis for their justification for that subpoena, so it`s not as if they just decided off the top of their head to do this. They built a strong record that showed the President`s involvement and those around him.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:07]\par \par \uc2 CASEY: And when you consider the action by the House, on that subpoena the same week when we`re learning more and more about those documents that the President had at his Country Club, where he had documents that pertained to Iran and other highly sensitive national security issues, it shows you what is at stake in an election like this, when you can have a public official act in a lawless manner.\par \par \uc2 So much is at stake in the election. I think this week`s developments around the President just demonstrate that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you for joining us, Senator \uc2 Bob Casey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let`s go from Pennsylvania to Nevada. Joining me now is Representative Steve Horsford, Democrat of Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for being with us. Let me start with early voting that is underway in your State, it started today. Right now, where are new concerns swirling following the resignation of eight of 12 employees of the election staff in Nevada, who say they were being harassed.\par \par \uc2 An op-ed for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reads, "Officials expect the eventual losers of several contests, perhaps a dozen to contest the results and they are bracing for the possibility that conspiracy-minded County Commissioners might refuse to certify the results as several threatened in 2020."\par \par \uc2 How do you ensure voters their votes there count -- are counted properly, when some elected officials could fight to install their candidate of their choice.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE HORSFORD (D-NV): Reverend Al, it is great to be on your show. It is the first day of early voting here in Nevada. I`m in Las Vegas. I`m actually at the Broadacres Marketplace, which is an outdoor location, which kind of -- constituents are out. We`re talking about the importance of getting out the vote.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to protect this vote here in Nevada. Fortunately, we have the right person overseeing our elections, currently, \uc2 Barbara Cegavske\uc2 . She is not an election denier. She stood up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and those who tried to sue the State during the 2020 election, sadly, one of those guys, Jim Marchant is one of the election deniers who is actually running to be the next Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I`m trying to support people like Cisco Aguilar and others, to make sure that we have people who are going to protect everyone`s right to vote. But today, it`s about getting out the vote.\par \par \uc2 We`re out here, doing Latino outreach. I`ve been in the African-American community. Later today, we`ll be out in the Asian Pacific Islander community. We`ve got Souls to the Polls tomorrow, where churches throughout our community are going to be coming out to vote in droves.\par \par \uc2 As long as we vote who will win, and then we will protect our rights.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me go right there. Black and Latino voters are going to be crucial in determining several close races.\par \par \uc2 AXIOS reports, Democrats are growing in their concern with Black and Latino men who may lean toward the GOP or not vote at all, which may cost elections. We must note that Black men are among the most loyal backers of the Democratic Party, upwards of 80 percent.\par \par \uc2 Are Democrats doing enough to reach out to these communities and how is their message being received?\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: Well, as one Democrat, I`m doing everything I can to reach Black men, Latino men, men in general and it is about an agenda that includes Black men, to make sure that they know that they have opportunities for good paying jobs, for entrepreneurship, that we want to live in safe communities. We want to create wealth, we want to leave inheritance behind to our kids.\par \par \uc2 So we need an agenda that includes the Black community, the Latino community, and men specifically. You know, as a man myself, it`s about making sure that we`re able to provide for ourselves, for our families, our children; to make sure that we are leaving something behind based on the hard work that we provide.\par \par \uc2 And so as one Democrat, I am out here talking to men, Latino men. I`ve been talking to Black men, and I`ll continue to do so and I will make sure that they know how important their voices.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to the Black women who have held down this party, but we need every man -- Black, Latino, White or otherwise -- to also vote our interest and the Republican Party aren`t voting -- they are not fighting for our interests. They are not fighting to create opportunity for our communities and that`s why I`m running for reelection, StevenHorsford.com. Check us out. Make sure that we win so that we can continue to stand for the people over politics.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me go to another op-ed, this one published by "The Nevada Independent" entitled "Abortion in Nevada Post Dobbs: Moving beyond the political talking points of abortion advocates." So there is a concern in Nevada, around post Dobbs, what happens to the rights of women to choose. How important is that in an election? How important is that for the midterms there in Nevada and in your district?\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: It`s incredibly important, and this fundamentally comes down to women`s freedom to make their own healthcare decisions, not some politician in Washington.\par \par \uc2 My extreme opponent wants to support a Federal ban on abortion, including for no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 But they also go further than that. They want to eliminate access to contraception. They want to come after marriage equality. They want to come after some of the most fundamental rights. Well, guess what, Reverend Al? We`re not going back.\par \par \uc2 We fought too hard to come this far, and we`re not going to allow others to take us back. That`s why this election is so consequential.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re fighting to protect women`s freedom. Yes, we have certain protections in our State Laws here in Nevada. But unfortunately, if Republicans have their way, Federal Law could supersede State Law and we can`t leave this up to the Supreme Court, which is already ideologically driven, and had said what they will do, and we cannot leave it up to extremist ideologues who are trying to take over the Congress and that is why we have to get out and vote like these issues depend on it.\par \par \uc2 Voting is on the ballot. Social Security is on the ballot. Protecting women`s freedom is on the ballot. And I`m asking everyone to come out and vote here in Nevada and all across this country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I`m out of time, but I must ask you this. And answer me briefly, please.\par \par \uc2 The issue of crime has been an attack line for many Republicans against Democrats this election cycle. You are incoming leader of the Congressional Black Caucus.\par \par \uc2 A month ago today, the House passed the Break the Cycle of Violence Act, which establishes community violence as a public health issue, and investing in evidence-based programs that are proven to break the cycles of gun violence and save lives. This bill now goes over to the Senate for consideration.\par \par \uc2 You authored that bill, only one Republican voted for the bill that would curb violence. What do you make of them accusing Democrats of not doing enough on crime when they overwhelmingly voted against the solution?\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: Yes, Reverend Al, look, I`ll be brief.\par \par \uc2 My father was shot and killed when I was a freshman in college. I was 19 years old and like so many of my other constituents, I know the pain of losing a loved one to senseless gun violence, that`s why I authored the Break the Cycle of Violence Bill.\par \par \uc2 We did get $250 million in the Safer Communities Act as its initial down payment. Our bill would invest $6.5 billion over eight years. It will save lives, it will reduce crime, and it will break the cycle of violence.\par \par \uc2 I believe that we can both support law enforcement while also supporting community violence intervention. I want to commend groups like Greg Jackson and Community Justice, as well as Moms Demand and other groups who will put pressure and Republicans should join us in this efforts.\par \par \uc2 If they really want to keep our communities safe, then work with us to pass the Break the Cycle of Violence Act.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Congressman Steven Horsford of Nevada, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 After the break, Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin was supposed to be the rising Republican star who would lead his party past the Trump era. It hasn`t quite turned out that way.\par \par \uc2 I`ll explain in this week`s :"Gotcha."\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague Jessica Layton with today`s top news stories -- Jessica.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LAYTON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Thank you, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 Stories were watching for all of you at this hour: Russian installed authorities in the Kherson region in Southern Ukraine have ordered evacuations in the face of a looming counteroffensive by Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukrainian officials say Russia launched another massive wave of rockets overnight once again aimed at critical civilian infrastructure. The country is dealing with widespread blackouts after Russia knocked out a third of Ukraine`s power stations.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian Air Command said they shot down 10 more of those Iranian supplied kamikaze drones over the Mykolaiv region overnight.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:08]\par \par \uc2 LAYTON: Well, Mexico has issued hurricane warnings for a 260-mile stretch along the country`s West Coast in anticipation of Hurricane Roslyn, which is now a massive Category 4 Storm. It is taking aim at the resort city of Puerto Vallarta with landfall expected tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 The National Hurricane Center said Roslyn had maximum sustained winds of 130 miles an hour and warned it could lead to dangerous storm surge.\par \par \uc2 And nearly three quarters of the nation`s pediatric hospital beds are full right now as cases of the respiratory illness, RSV, as the virus are reportedly spreading quickly among young children. The cold-like virus can cause severe infections in young and elderly patients.\par \par \uc2 I`m Jessica Layton in New York. More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: While most Republicans have fallen in line behind former President Trump, a few have tried to chart their own course, portraying themselves as principled conservatives who refuse to engage in the conspiracy mongering and extremism that defines the MAGA Movement, but during these midterms, their true colors have come out.\par \par \uc2 When Glenn Youngkin ran for Virginia Governor, he won over suburban voters by clinging tightly to his fleece vest and business background, and he kept the Trump show at arm`s length. Now, he is out campaigning for Kari Lake in Arizona, a gubernatorial candidate who pushed for decertification of the 2020 presidential election results and called Democratic agenda demonic.\par \par \uc2 When supporters suggested Youngkin might someday go from sharing a stage with Lake to splitting a presidential ticket, this is what the genteel Governor had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: Youngkin-Lake in `24.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): That`s your call.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Nikki Haley tried to change the Trump administration from the inside as Ambassador to the United Nations. But after January 6th, she said Trump had led her party down the wrong path, she quickly reversed course. And now, he is stumping for candidates like Don Buldoc it in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Buldoc is a former Army General running for Senate despite wanting to repeal the 17th Amendment, giving Americans the right to vote for their senators in the first place.\par \par \uc2 He once signed a letter with dozens of other military leaders saying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the election and only recanted his election denialism after he won the GOP nomination. So perhaps he and Haley have more in common than we thought.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve seen no bigger heel turn than that of former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Pence did his job on January 6th and appeared to have paid the price for what he did with the MAGA faithful. Not only did rioters call for his hanging as they stormed the Capitol, they even had gallows waiting for him outside.\par \par \uc2 But none of this has shaken the former Veep`s faithfulness to the far-right fringe of his party, as he campaigns for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  nominee, Blake Masters in Arizona, who wrote on his website in 2020 election that it was a rotten mess and is already claiming his election will be stolen this year. Let`s see how this turns out.\par \par \uc2 The hypocrisy of these high-ranking Republicans is real in my opinion, but also inevitable. As long as the GOP is led by a demagogue hostile to basic democratic principles, it is nearly impossible for individual Republicans not to find themselves compromised by the mania gripping the party as a whole.\par \par \uc2 The only way to rise above as a Republican would be to fight the forces that are dragging the party down and that requires a level of courage these principal politicians seem to lack.\par \par \uc2 I Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:32:53]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 The midterms are drawing closer and we are highlighting key races here on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, we are focusing on arguably the second most powerful law enforcement job in the country. California`s District Attorney serves as a top prosecutor in the nation`s most populous State, and spearheads legal policy initiatives that impact the entire nation.\par \par \uc2 The post has been a springboard for many political careers, including that of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ll be hearing from both major party candidates in this race starting today with the Republican nominee, Nathan Hochman.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining me.\par \par \uc2 You have made fighting crime a major theme in your campaign. You`ve also recently said Californians are experiencing "spiral of lawlessness."\par \par \uc2 According to data from the \uc2 Public Policy Institute of California\uc2 , violent crime in your State did go up by six percent in 2021, compared to 2020, when many residents were staying inside due to COVID lockdowns. However, violent crime rates have been relatively stable over the past decade and remain well below their peak in the early 1990s.\par \par \uc2 What`s your response to critics who say Republicans, such as yourself, are exaggerating the severity of the post pandemic uptick in crime?\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HOCHMAN (R), CALIFORNIA CANDIDATE FOR STATE ATTORNEY GENERAL: It`s very -- thank you very much, Reverend Al for having me on your show.\par \par \uc2 What I rely on are the \uc2 California Department of Justice\uc2 `s own statistics that show a double digit rise for crimes like robberies and assaults that show for instance for Los Angeles, they just had a 15-year high in homicides this past year.\par \par \uc2 I also rely, if you`re going back to polls, on the fact that for the first time in 20 years, safety and security is a top three issue for all Californians -- Republicans Democrats and Independents alike -- and towards that end, I bring 30 years of criminal justice experience as a Federal prosecutor, a US Assistant Attorney General, and a defense attorney against literally zero years of law enforcement experience that the \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  appointed Democrat, \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  has, 30 versus zero, and maybe I`m old fashioned, but I think people want someone who is experienced for the position, not someone who is winging it for the first time by getting the job.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:34 ]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, let me say this, and I was not going to pose, I was going to actual data, but as head of National Action Network, I`m constantly in contact with community leaders in your State. We have an office in LA and chapters throughout the State, and no one objected to fighting crime.\par \par \uc2 But there is a major concern surrounding the racist rhetoric that often goes hand in glove with law and order messages, as well as some of the proposed solutions including more aggressive policing and harsher sentencing guidelines that historically hit Black Americans and other people of color the hardest.\par \par \uc2 How do you avoid these outcomes and address the problem of the more than 200 Californians who die each year with interactions with California Police?\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: So Reverend Al, I`m running as a moderate Republican. By the way, someone who believes that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the election, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is our president, and January 6 was a political insurrection, not legitimate political discourse.\par \par \uc2 The way I made the argument that you pose is that right now, in California, we have a situation where again, we have seen this spiral of lawlessness where one person walks out of a store with just under $950.00 and doesn`t get prosecuted, leading to a hundred people committing a flash mob in an LA 7-Eleven to this double digit rise of homicides.\par \par \uc2 I would meet that that issue by saying I work in the hard middle. I reject the blanket policies of the right putting everyone in jail for a hundred years, but I reject the blanket policies of \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  and the progressive left that basically are incarcerating no one for any time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, but I`m not asking you about incarceration. I`m asking you how you would deal with policing and the people dying at the hands of police. I preach funerals out there, well, two this year in California, about police shootings. I`m asking you about policing.\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: Absolutely. So in the mid-1990s, Reverend Al, I went after the police. I was a Federal prosecutor at that time and I actually brought a case as part of 15 different cases against an elite Major Deputy Sheriff Unit in the LA Sheriff`s Department and convicted them for crossing the line.\par \par \uc2 I have absolutely no tolerance for any law enforcement officer who crosses the line, or for that matter, anyone else. I believe that proportionate consequences are required and that does not mean you put people in jail for a hundred years, but you have to make sure that people understand there is a line and is consistently and fairly enforced, and that`s what I would do as opposed to move the line constantly, which is what our current Attorney General aligned with George Gascon of LA have done.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to get your take on something else, a political scandal in California`s most populous city. The Democratic President of the Los Angeles City Council resigned last week after leaked audio emerged in which she made a number of racist remarks.\par \par \uc2 But two other Council members involved in the conversation have refused to step down even in the face of criticism from fellow lawmakers and community leaders, including the last few days from basketball star, Magic Johnson.\par \par \uc2 How should this situation be resolved in your judgment?\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: Well, one of the three leaders were \uc2 Nury Martinez\uc2 , the Council President who has resigned, Gil Cedillo, a Councilman who is termed out in about two months, and then the third guy \uc2 Kevin De Leon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What I think is that the California Attorney General should bring an investigation, but \uc2 Kevin De Leon\uc2  has bought and paid for \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , literally bought and paid for him and that`s why he`s not resigning. He thinks there`s no teeth to a California Attorney General investigation because he gave him -- \uc2 Kevin de Leon\uc2  gave \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  and his wife $4,500.00 of campaign contributions that \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  failed to disclose when he initiated an investigation, didn`t return the money and is completely conflicted out of actually running that investigation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Are you saying that Bonta is conflicted in dealing with this issue that is basically clearly racist and clearly against the will of the people of the city that is calling for all involved in that conversation to resign?\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: Well, on the racist issue, of course, he`s not conflicted out. He should he should have asked, as I did, for Kevin De Leon`s resignation, but there is a potential criminal investigation dealing with the underlying problem of the racist discourse, which had to do with redistricting in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:13]\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: And on that issue, \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  has a raging conflict of interest that he refused to reveal, he refused to return the $4,500.00 that he and his wife received from \uc2 Kevin De Leon\uc2  and I would be an independent voice. That`s the advantage of bringing one Republican to a State government that has a monopoly control by Democrats, every statewide positions a Democrat, the State Legislature is a super majority of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 We need an independent experience moderate Republican to go toe-to-toe with the corruption and the raging conflict of interest in Sacramento.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Alright, let me ask you one last question, because we`re out of time. I want to ask you about the role of California Attorney General.\par \par \uc2 Attorney Generals representing your State filed more than 100 lawsuits against the Trump administration on a variety of issues, including the environment, immigration, and education. It`s a big way California protects its interests in a Federal system that under represents more urban populous states.\par \par \uc2 I know you aren`t a Trump supporter, but you are a Republican, as you reminded us. Can Democrats in your State count on you to represent them in these larger national issues Californians care about?\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: One hundred percent. I am pro-choice and have been my entire life and will enforce the laws on the books of the State of California. Full stop. That`s the job of a chief law enforcement officer as opposed to a politician like \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  who wants to make policy from the law enforcement standpoint.\par \par \uc2 What Californians can absolutely count on is I will follow the evidence. I will follow the law and I will protect their interests inside California and outside California.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Nathan Hochman, thank you for joining me.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, POLITICSNATION, we will have the Democratic nominee, the incumbent Attorney General of California, \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still to come though tonight, my next guest is running for office and if elected, would become the first Black female veteran in Congress.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:29]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We`re staying with the midterm elections and joining me now is Jackie Gordon, who is running for Congress to represent parts of Long Island, New York. Thank you for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 Miss Gordon, the Democrats are trying to hold on to a slim eight-seat majority in the midterm cycle that would normally favor of Republicans. You`re running as a Democrat in New York`s newly redrawn Second Congressional District, trying to flip a seat, which has been held by Republicans for a decade against incumbent Congressman \uc2 Andrew Garbarino\uc2  who you also challenged by the way in 2020, but lost by seven percentage points. What makes you optimistic that this race will be different.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE GORDON (D), NEW YORK CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Thank you for having me, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 I am super optimistic that we will win this cycle with the Dobbs decision that just came down and the climate in the air, folks in the district are really motivated to make sure that they get a representative who will carry their voice to Washington. This decision has really motivated voters to get out and make sure that their voice is heard.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Garbarino has not been a representative who have shown up. Just recently, there was a meeting amongst veterans regarding the burn pits from overseas deployments. They invited Mr. Garbarino, he responded. He said he would be there. He didn`t show up. He didn`t send a rep. He didn`t even call.\par \par \uc2 And I know that this happened, because the veterans called me. And of course, I responded to them, I always will, whether I`m in office or not. I am here to serve. That`s what I`ve been doing for the last 30 years and I will continue to do.\par \par \uc2 And so voters in this district realize that they need someone who will hear their voice. They need someone who understand the struggles that they face. I was an educator, a veteran. I was a council woman, a --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wait, let me put you right there. If elected, you would become the first Black female veteran in Congress.\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Correct.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`ve spent 29 years serving in the Army Reserve and retired at the rank of Lieutenant Colonel. You are proposing to ensure that veterans and families have access to resources through the VA and have promised to strengthen education, career training programs, health and housing assistance for veterans.\par \par \uc2 Why are these proposals so important? And how has your time in the military shaped your approach to politics?\par \par \uc2 GORDON: They are very important, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 In Long Island, we have the largest concentration of veterans in all of New York State. So having a representative who understands the needs of veterans, especially when they come home is so, so important. We have a VA Hospital, right here on Long Island. We`re fortunate to have a VA Hospital in our region. It`s not in the district, but it`s in the region and it serves our veterans.\par \par \uc2 And so, we need someone who understands that veterans need to be able to -- the support that they need to get back to the life that they once knew or as close to it as possible.\par \par \uc2 My military service helps me to get to this point, helps me to understand the struggles that folks face. It helps me to learn to work with others.\par \par \uc2 You know, in the 29 years, I was in the military, I never looked to my left or my right and asked anyone if they were a Democrat or Republican. The focus was getting the job done and accomplishing the mission, and that`s the mindset we need in Washington right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me go back to you talking about the Dobb`s decision.\par \par \uc2 In a recent interview, you said that codifying Roe versus Wade into law is your biggest issue in your district. What have you been hearing from voters about the issue and how does your stance contrast with your opponents?\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Well, voters in the district are upset that after 50 years, this right has been taken away from them. I believe that abortion is a healthcare decision. I think it should be between a woman and her healthcare provider in the privacy of the practitioner`s office, and clearly there`s not enough room in that office for the American government.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:17]\par \par \uc2 GORDON: And so the government does not belong in that decision.\par \par \uc2 And so, I will fight to make sure that women across this nation have a right to make a decision about their healthcare, along with the advice of their practitioners.\par \par \uc2 My opponent signed on to the amicus brief. He supports banning abortion across the nation without regard to incest, rape, or the health of a mother. It`s too extreme for our district. It is too extreme for the land, and we cannot have a Congress member like that representing us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Jackie Gordon, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 After the break, a baseless claim against an honest family that has already paid a heavy price after the death of their loved one.\par \par \uc2 I`ll explain in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This week, I learned that the family of George Floyd announced they are considering a $250 million lawsuit against rapper, Kanye West, after he claimed that George Floyd`s death was caused by a drug overdose instead of police brutality, an allegation that was ruled false by the Hennepin County Coroner`s Office and also by medical experts at the murder trial for Minneapolis Police Officer Derek Chauvin, which I attended.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:16]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This, while some other fellow conspiracy theorists also suggested the media is working with Floyd`s family to bury evidence.\par \par \uc2 Let me be very clear of what I think about all of these baseless claims against an honest family who already paid a heavy price with the death of their loved one.\par \par \uc2 Terrence Floyd was at National Action Network`s rally this morning. I preached to of George Floyd`s funeral. This family is still suffering from the loss of their father, their brother, their uncle, their cousin, and to go as Alex Jones did, creating more pain with baseless conspiracy theories is something that must be addressed and people must be held accounted for.\par \par \uc2 I`ve stayed out of getting into a back and forth with the rapper. I was asked in Washington by a reporter about the conduct of Kanye West, and in spite of some of the conduct that I might consider bizarre, I told them to quote another rapper. "I have 99 problems, but Kanye is not one of them."\par \par \uc2 But I must speak out when a bizarre conspiracy theory adds injury and antisemitism is uttered.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 A big show tomorrow: Senator Kirsten Gillibrand; Georgia gubernatorial candidate, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 ; and DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2  will all stop by on this show tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We will talk midterms, fresh new polls and the latest with the January 6th investigation.\par \par \uc2 Don`t miss it tomorrow night right here on POLITICSNATION at 5:00 PM Eastern.\par \par \uc2 But right now, "American Voices" with my colleague, Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [16:00:48]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221023eiam00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE KORNACKI COUNTDOWN for October 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Steve Kornacki, Garrett Haake, Ali Velshi \par \uc2 8,625 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: -- to voter fraud. But that vindication comes at a price. The governor`s message to ex-felons has been delivered namely, vote at your peril. And in the end, that`s the point.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us tonight. Rachel will be back on Monday. I`ll see you again on Tuesday, and now with just 18 days to go to the election, it is time for the Kornacki count down with Steve Kornacki.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and our countdown clock continues to tick away, 17 days, 19 hours, 59 minutes and 39 seconds and counting until the first polls close on election night. And here we are back where we were last Friday night and where we`re going to be every Friday night at this time, counting down to election day.\par \par \uc2 An early voting election day is pretty much already underway in more than a dozen states where early voting has already begun. That includes Georgia, North Carolina, both starting up this week. Each of those states home to a key Senate race, both of which we`re going to be talking about this hour. And tomorrow, early voting beginning in Nevada, and that is where Democrats may face what is their toughest individual battle to hold the Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 We have reporters on the ground there and in those two other battlegrounds states we mentioned, we`ll be joined by them shortly. Plus, we are going to hear from voters in Michigan. That is where the governor`s race has heated things up a bit this week and where there is a referendum to enshrine abortion rights in the state constitution. That`s going to be on the ballot in a few weeks as well.\par \par \uc2 All of that is ahead this hour. We`re going to start though with the question that everybody seems to be asking this week, and that is are we witnessing a shift in the national political climate in just 17 days out from election day. So, here`s why that question is being asked. Let`s take a look here at the latest numbers.\par \par \uc2 This is the generic ballot, this is that poll question they asked, do you want the Democrats or the Republicans to control Congress? When we came on the air at this exact time last week, this is where it stood. Remember, Republicans had the lead, it was a narrow lead. It was inside of a point. It was nine-tenths of one point. Now, just a week later, here is where the generic ballot sits tonight at this hour.\par \par \uc2 There`s been movement. There`s been a change. Republicans now have opened up a lead in excess of three points, 3.1 points. That`s the average, the real clear average. Republicans with that advantage now. So, we start to see here, if you take the trend line for the year, it really does start to look like Republicans are creating some distance here. Let`s just remind you, go back to May, go back to the spring, Republicans had a pretty solid advantage on the generic ballot.\par \par \uc2 Remember in 2021, those off year elections, they won the governorship in Virginia. They nearly pulled a big upset in New Jersey. They came into 2022 with a head of steam and then, at the end of June, you got that Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe v. Wade. You had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  kind of front and center in the news this summer, the Mar-a-Lago raid. And lo and behold, by August, Democrats actually caught Republicans in the generic ballot.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at that blue line, it was a above the red line for much of August into September into the middle of September. And then the Republicans did move back ahead, but here it is, this is the last week you`re looking at here. This is the real divergence. We`ve seen it over the last week where Republicans have opened up a lead, the size of which they have not had in months.\par \par \uc2 And again, this is with just a little bit more than two weeks between now and election day. So, a question of, have they opened up a lead for good here? Could it get wider? Or are we in for another change in these final days, in these final weeks of this campaign? Could Democrats tighten that still once more as they did over the summer?\par \par \uc2 But certainly, a significant week just in terms of the coal (ph) movement that we have seen here. And also, I think notably, putting this in some context, these are the most recent wave elections we`ve seen in midterms. This is where the opposition party, the party who doesn`t have the White House has done really well in midterms.\par \par \uc2 And you see again, Republicans leading the generic ballot right now at 3.1. Where was the generic ballot at the same point in these recent wave elections, where you can see, this Republican number is not where it was in 2010 when Republicans had that huge midterm and won 63 seats in the House. Barack Obama`s first midterm. It`s not where it was for Democrats four years ago when Democrats had that blue wave, took back the House in Trump`s first midterm.\par \par \uc2 But that 3.1 lead, it`s pretty comparable to where things were at this point in 2014. That was Barack Obama`s second midterm as president. Now it`s kind of a late building, late forming wave. It took a lot of people by surprise, really, right up until election day, but Republicans took back the Senate in 2014 and they got to their highest level of House seats since 1928 in that election.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And you start to see that movement this week. It`s brought the current picture kind of in line with what we were looking at in 2014. So that could be significant as well. What has driven this shift here? One thing is certain, there was some bad inflation news in the last week or so. Just looking at here, this is the long-term trend line on inflation since the start of the Biden presidency, just within the last 10 days, some bad news with the most recent inflation report.\par \par \uc2 And here by the way, new numbers from the University of Pennsylvania Public Opinion Program just out today, asking voters in their survey, what did they consider the most important issue? We have seen this in a lot now, we`re seeing this in a lot of polls, 41 percent citing inflation as their top concern.\par \par \uc2 There`s a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll earlier this week, if you combine the inflation and economy, it was at 44 percent in that issue. And look what`s number two here as well, crime and safety, 22 percent citing that in this Penn poll. Again, this just out today, and we have seen, we`ll talk about this a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to zoom in on crime a little bit later. Bt on both of these issues, we have seen in polls, when you ask which party has the advantage, which party is getting more support from the public, the Republicans have been doing well on these issues. The Democrats have been doing better on abortion, climate change and guns. And you just see in terms of the resonance of the issues, inflation in particular, the runaway leader here in terms of the issue that voters say is on their minds.\par \par \uc2 So, again, the numbers moved this week. What does that mean? What could Democrats do to counter that? Let`s break things down here. Joining me now, Jim Messina, who is President Obama`s 2012 campaign manager. He`s also deputy chief of staff in the Obama White House and CEO now of the Messina Group. And also with us, Rich Lowry, editor-in-chief at "The National Review" and an NBC News political analyst.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you both for joining us. And -- well, Jim, let me start with you because I mentioned -- I don`t mean to bring up bad memories from the past for Democrats here, but I mentioned the 2014 midterm and that Obama`s second midterm where the numbers right now on paper, when you look at the generic ballot right now, look somewhat like they did in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Are we looking at 2014? Do you think we`re looking at 2014 playing out again here, or is there something that Democrats can do or that could happen in the next 17 days that could change the trend we have seen this week?\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, FORMER OBAMA CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Steve, I`m wondering whether or not it`s more like 2018, right? And you showed those numbers earlier, which I thought wasn`t really interesting. Dems had a seven-point lead now (inaudible) took over the house, but in the same election, the Republicans have picked up seats in the Senate because their math was better.\par \par \uc2 And so, as you look at this election, I`m wondering whether Democrats have some strength in the Senate races or as the House races, you know, start to look tougher up. History matters. You and I are data geeks and if you look at the past 40 years of elections and midterms, the president`s party loses (inaudible) three Senate seats in 28 House seats. The more interesting question is where that doesn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 And the two times in the last 40 years where it didn`t happen is when you had national issues surging at the end. The question for Democrats is abortion, that issue to swing voters in some of these battleground states, or is it inflation. And that`s going to be the question that you`re asking on election night that really shows who`s going to win these races.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Again, just a follow-up on that, is there -- when you look at that trend line we showed, where over the summer when abortion was getting a ton of attention, obviously, the ruling had just come down, everybody was digesting the implications, and you saw the Democrats actually take the lead in the generic ballot, and now you see them falling behind by three points. Has the resonance of that issue lessened with the types of voters or the swing voters?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I don`t think so. And, you know, I tweeted out and made a bunch of my party mad when I said that those midsummer numbers right after abortion were our sugar high and I think they were a sugar high. Elections are always hard for the president`s party, but you know, what I think matters more is the actual voter numbers.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at early votes, 6 million people have early voted already. Democrats are outpacing Republicans, which just shows me enthusiasm. And so, Democrats have got to turn out their voters. And I think, you know, they`ve got a tough battleground with these independent voters where, you know, they`ve got inflation, they`ve got all these other things.\par \par \uc2 But what I really care about a midterm election, what you and I really work at, are these turnout numbers. Which party has the advantage in turnout? And those are the numbers that I`m going to be really looking at over these next few days?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yeah, Rich, I pick up on that actually because it is -- it`s staggering if you take a step back and look at the turnout from midterm elections. It was about 150 million in 2018. If you went back to 2014, I think it was 86 million. So, there`s just been this explosion of interest in politics where you could tie it certainly to the rise of Trump and everything around him.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And an expectation I think with that midterm turnout is going to be even higher this year. Does that add a level of mystery? You can see Republicans taking the lead in the generic ballot, but do you feel -- how confident do you feel when you see a number like that when you`re looking at the prospect of maybe 120, 130 million people voting in a midterm? We`ve never seen that before.\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE NATIONAL REVIEW: Yeah. This is one thing I talked to a lot of Republicans today, and the one thing they kind of -- they are wondering about is that early vote in Georgia which is (inaudible) number, who are those people? Where are they from? So, there`s a little concern there, but otherwise, Steve, they see everything break in the right way right now.\par \par \uc2 They`re not giddy, you know. It`s still 18 days or whatever. The momentum can stall out, something could change, but you see it in the public polling, they see it the internal polling, they see it in the general issue, environment. Jim races a question of what would be the most important issue; the economy, you know, inflation or abortion? I don`t think it`s close.\par \par \uc2 You know, it wasn`t close a couple of months ago in a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " Sienna poll that had three or 36 people saying that -- 36 percent of people saying the economy. It`s up from that now. It`s 44 percent. Abortion is clearly a second-tier issue and now a second-tier issue that`s been eclipsed by crime and immigration.\par \par \uc2 So, at the moment, Republicans are feeling very good. They only needed a typical midterm. They don`t need a blockbuster. They`re going to pick up the House regardless and just a typical midterm will get them the Senate as well. They only need one seat. And they are optimistic they will do much better than that.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Let me just -- one other question is, if it is the kind of night that Republicans are now anticipating and hoping for, that will almost necessarily mean that a lot of these candidates, who have really tethered themselves to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be successful, like a JD Vance in Ohio, a Blake Masters in Arizona, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia, the list could go on. If Republicans have a big night in 2022, is a consequence of that that Donald Trump`s grip on the party is strengthened?\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Yeah. It will help them at the margins, but I think that is kind of exaggerated. I mean, Trump basically deliberately threw away two Senate seats in Georgia, the special elections, right after the 2020 election in November and paid almost zero price for it in the party. So, even if every one of his hand-picked candidates went down, I`m not sure it would affect him much.\par \par \uc2 But yeah, you`re looking -- Kari Lake is very likely to win the Arizona gubernatorial race. It`s not done. You know, she has a narrow lead, but very likely to win. And she was, you know, like one of the -- a lot of people thought, I thought it was just committing suicide nominating a stop the steal fervent believer like her. It turns out, she`s a really practice performer, has been on TV for more than 20 years. And she might actually help Blake Masters get over the top.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s -- the Senate, a lot of these races are still really close, but I think the overtone window stretched out some where I would have said last week, if you`d ask me, 50, 52 seats for Republicans. Now, you know, you can squint the right way and say it could get up to 53 or 54, plausibly. I`m not predicting that, but I think it`s possible.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Alright, Rich Lowry, Jim Messina, thanks, and standby. We`re going to come back to you a little bit later this hour. And coming up, the battle for the Senate, Democrats trying to hang on to control that they have right now. We`re going to show you all the latest numbers and give a live report from the state where they may be the most vulnerable. And also, from two states where it`s -- two states where it`s the Republicans who are playing defense.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And that clock continuing to tick down until that first poll close on election night. It`s going to be in Indiana, by the way, 6:00 eastern time, cannot wait to be here with all the results. So, welcome back. And at this hour last week, the first debate between Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  had just ended in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And in that debate, Warnock notably did not engage with Walker on those reports that in 2009, Walker had paid for his then girlfriend to have an abortion. That`s something that Walker has denied, but today, notably, the Warnock campaign did decide to engage directly on this topic. They went up with this ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): I`m \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and I approve this message.\par \par \uc2 For you, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wants to ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA: There`s no exception in my mind because I believe in life.\par \par \uc2 There`s not a national ban on abortion right now, and I think that`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: But for himself --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for an abortion for his then girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 UKNOWN: She supported her claims with a $575 receipt from the abortion clinic.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Even his own son is saying Walker is lying.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS HOST: Is that your signature on the check though?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, that`s my --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And if you are watching this show right now, I don`t need to tell you the significance of that Georgia Senate race. Warnock holding it right now just in terms of the overall battle for control of the Senate. If you take a look here, we`ve been monitoring this of the Democratic held seats, the seats Republicans are targeting and hoping to make their gains in.\par \par \uc2 Republicans actually have an advantage in one, and the next best place for Republicans right now is in Georgia, in terms of playing offense. Warnock leading in the polls there. His lead about two and a half points on average over \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 And remember, in Georgia, they got that runoff rule. If nobody gets 50 percent in November 8th, December 6th, they`ll have a runoff there. So, let`s talk a little bit more now about the Warnock campaign`s decision to put this ad up, what it could mean for the race. Joining us is Greg Bluestein. He`s a political reporter for the Atlanta Journal-Constitution and the author of "Flipped: How Georgia Turned Purple and Broke the Monopoly on Republican Power." He`s also an MSNBC political contributor.\par \par \uc2 Greg, thank you for taking a few minutes in joining us tonight. I appreciate it. I wonder if you could tell us just the fact that a week ago, I remember, we were standing here remarking that it was seemed interesting that in the debate, the topic of those allegations against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was raised and Warnock didn`t seem to want to go anywhere near it. Now his campaign does. What should be read into that?\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, not only during the debate but in the days before the debate. You know, when I had asked him and other reporters would ask him directly on the campaign trail about these allegations, he would say either as a part of a disturbing pattern of behavior or avoid talking about them altogether.\par \par \uc2 Now we`re seeing this clear tactical shift. He`s leaning into this argument that it portrays \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as a hypocrite who would impose restrictions on women except for in the case of his girlfriend back in 2009, which he has denied those allegations. But I think for Warnock`s camp, this is a reflection in fact that the polls here are really tight. He is under 50 percent in most of these polls, and they don`t want to leave anything on the table that could affect that.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Did they feel, I wonder, a week now to process kind of the aftermath of this debate? Did they feel that Walker may have done better in that, gotten more out of that than they expected him to?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yeah, I mean, clearly, whether or not his campaign feels that there are Democrats here on the ground who feel that, that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  kind of blended some of the criticism by coming out swinging, by having a solid or at least a seemingly solid performance and by doing what \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  needed to do, which was really shore up -- he wasn`t trying to go, you know, draw in new voters really, but he wanted to do is shore up the base because you`re continuing to see poll after poll after poll that showed the split ticket trend in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Republicans were voting for Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and other statewide Republicans who were indicating that they weren`t going to vote for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . So, his job at that debate was to, you know, give them some confidence in this campaign and there was this sense down here in Georgia that he did that.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Alright, Greg Bluestein in Atlanta, thank you for joining us. Really appreciate that. And from one battleground state to another, let`s go back to the board here. We were just talking about what`s going on in Georgia with Walker -- with Warnock, excuse me, leading in the polls there. Let`s go to the other side here, the Republican held seats that Democrats are targeting.\par \par \uc2 Again, Democrats are trying to prevent Republicans from getting a net gain of one seat here. And you see we`ve talked a lot about Pennsylvania on paper. That`s where Democrats have their best shot. This one has been sitting here at the entire election, very close. We think of North Carolina as a red state. \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , the Republican nominee there against Democrat Cheri Beasley does lead, but that margin for Budd is close. It`s basically, it`s 2.8. It`s pretty close to what we were just talking about in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Take a closer look here at North Carolina, by the way here. We`re back here in, you know, the matchup again is Beasley versus Budd, but let`s show you just the 2020 presidential election in North Carolina. Yeah, it was a Trump state, it was close. Remember, we didn`t call this thing for a long time in 2020. Donald Trump`s margin was about 75,000 votes here, 1.3 points. And I think notably, that was down from 2016.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, Trump won the state by 175,000 votes, about three and a half points. So, Democrats didn`t win North Carolina in 2020, but they made progress, they got it very close, and they made Republican sweat. So, I think that`s the backdrop in a way for the Senate race here in 2022, that you know, from the Republican standpoint, they expect to hold the seat. This has been stubbornly close so far. So, for more on what is playing out in North Carolina, NBC`s Antonia Hylton joins us now from Raleigh.\par \par \uc2 And Antonia, a little bit more than two weeks to go to election day. We keep seeing polls that show a close race there in North Carolina. What are some of the dynamics shaping this race? What should we be looking for in the closing weeks?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Steve. You know, North Carolina is going to be really fascinating and it`s interesting to me just how much I think people have slept on this race a bit. I think that`s in part because the candidates here have been running less flashy or controversial campaigns here. But, you know, a lot of the same core issues that we`re seeing battled across the country right now are at the forefront here.\par \par \uc2 But then there are other, you know, some unknown factors that make North Carolina really interesting. I mean, first, this is a state that has more unaffiliated voters than almost virtually any other state in the country. They are in the midst of a massive and historic voting rights expansion -- 56,000 former felons have just regained the right to vote since a court ruling this summer.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And that may not sound like a lot of people, but in a state where people lose or win races by a matter of a couple hundred or just a couple thousand votes, that actually has a drastic effect on the electorate here, and the voters who fit that group, again, it kind of goes back to this unaffiliated voter camp. They don`t really fit into clean boxes.\par \par \uc2 They are people who are adversely affected by economic issues, the rising cost of living. They`re worried about inflation, but they`ve also had a civil right taken away from them, and so they tend to have a lot of sympathy when it comes to conversations about women`s reproductive rights and abortion access.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to a conversation I had with one second chance voter who`s doing everything he can to get a lot of his folks to the polls right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAQUAN PETERS, UNLOCK OUR VOTE CAMPAIGN COORDINATOR: You hear a lot of individuals that`s in this base, say that justice is on the ballot, and the reason why they say justice is on the ballot is because you have a lot of Supreme Court seats open. You have a lot of district attorney`s running for office, the sheriff running. All of those individuals now have to see us.\par \par \uc2 Now, our voice is powerful. If I get 500 of those individuals, then that`s a political power that we hold. Then therefore, anytime somebody in this town and this county run for something, they got to come see us. So, just imagine if I got a thousand votes directly impact the voters, what is that -- it`s not what does it mean for us, is what does it mean for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: For a lot of voters that we talk to, it`s abortion versus the economy, is inflation as the cost of living the biggest issue to you or is it women`s reproductive access? But then sometimes those issues are combined. So, one might say that the economy is number one for them, but they are so concerned about access to abortion, that that might really be the motivating factor that has made them interested in the midterms during a cycle they might not have otherwise glued in on.\par \par \uc2 And so, all of these factors here in North Carolina make it so that while this maybe is Ted Budd`s race to lose, certainly, people in Cheri Beasley`s camp feel confident that they can still pull this off and, I mean. \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  was here, Democrats are still spending millions of dollars here. This is still really up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Alright, Antonia Hylton in North Carolina`s capital city. Thank you for taking a few minutes joining us tonight. Appreciate it. And again, our tour here of battleground states, we`re going to continue it now back into the column of Republic -- excuse me -- of a Democratic held seats that Republicans are targeting.\par \par \uc2 And this is the one, the only one you see that`s covered in red right now. This is the only Democratic seat, Nevada, where Republicans currently lead in the polling average. That`s \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican challenger to Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . He leads in the polling average.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans could make this actually happen in the election, and then if, big if here, they could defend all the seats that they already hold, that would be enough to give Republicans control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . It could come down to this race in Nevada. So, in early voting by the way, starting in Nevada tomorrow. The election pretty much begins ion Nevada tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Garrett Haake is live in Las Vegas where that early voting is going to begin tomorrow. Garrett, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the polls say she is in trouble. Is she and why?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, she`s largely in trouble because of the economy. And in Nevada, that`s mainly an issue of inflation. Nevada has some of the worst inflation in the country, gas prices around $5 a gallon here in the Las Vegas area. And polling has shown that Republicans generally, and Laxalt specifically, have had the advantage on that issue so far.\par \par \uc2 So, her challenge is twofold. First, she`s got to confront the economic issues, the kitchen table issues that she knows Nevadans care about. We talked about that in our interview yesterday. She had an event with Latina business owners yesterday in which that was a significant focus. That`s issue number one.\par \par \uc2 Her second challenge is to try to expand the number of issues, kind of the slate of issues that voters here are considering as they start to fill up their mail ballots. They vote early. To do that, she`s been talking about abortion rights access. She`s been spending a lot of money on television, on abortion related advertisements. That`s obviously a big issue in what has been a state that has traditionally protected abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 And she`s also been trying to neutralize Laxalt on the issue of crime. And she`s been doing that by linking him to the efforts to overturn the 2020 election results. Remember, he was a Trump co-chair here in Nevada. He was involved in some of the lawsuits to overturn those results. And today, that paid some dividends for her when she secured the endorsement of the Reno police chief, the second largest city in Nevada, who tied Laxalt support for the stop the steal effort directly to is endorsement of Cortez Masto, the first time this chief had endorsed a Democrat. Here`s what he had to say on the stump today.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:45]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON SOTO, RENO POLICE DEPARTMENT CHIEF: And Laxalt and his allies fuel lies and support the mob of insurrectionists, storming our nation`s capital on January 6. Our senator was defending the vote, supporting our constitutional process to secure that vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Steve, I could tell you, I talked to a lot of Republicans working on this race. They believe that they are ahead, but they are wary because the Democratic turnout machine in Nevada has traditionally been excellent. The culinary union, the sort of machine that Harry Reid helped build has been good for points in the past. If it`s close, it could be a very late night here in Las Vegas come election day.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NEWS HOST: All right. Gary Haake. Yes, it could be a late few nights there for if 2020 is any indication but thank you for that report, really appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, there`s an issue besides inflation and the economy that Republican candidates are spending big to play up in their ads, and that is crime. We`re going to show you what voters are seeing on their TVs in competitive races across the country. And we`re going to look at the effect it is having on voters. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:52]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Our countdown continues to election night. It`s getting closer and closer by the second. Well, we have talked tonight, we`ve talked last week about the issues of the economy and inflation, about how Republicans have enjoyed an advantage on that, might explain why they`ve taken a lead in the generic ballot.\par \par \uc2 We`ve certainly been talking about how Democrats hope the issue of abortion will help them to counter that, will help them to bring out some new voters, some extra voters this fall, bring that turnout level to new heights.\par \par \uc2 There`s another issue though that Republicans have been emphasizing and maybe gaining some traction. We thought we would take a look at that and that is the issue of crime here.\par \par \uc2 So this is an interesting way of looking at -- this is Pew poll that came out this week, and it`s taking a look here at that issues where Republican seem to have their biggest strengths here. Either issues that voters say are, in their words, very important to them, in terms of making up their minds in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And again, not surprisingly, you see the economy is very high. Look what is second though on this list, it`s violent crime.\par \par \uc2 All of these are issues where Republicans have the advantage. How big of an advantage, in the Pew Poll, here we go. On the economy, it`s 13 points; on crime, it`s 17 points; and then foreign policy and immigration you see here as well.\par \par \uc2 So this is an issue. Again, more than six in ten voters saying it`s very important to them, and they are giving Republicans a 17-point advantage.\par \par \uc2 This is an issue that Republicans have been running heavily on. You see it in ads for house races, governors races, senate races.\par \par \uc2 We`ll give you an example here. Here`s New York state -- New York state, you don`t think of it necessarily as the most politically competitive place, but when it comes to the battle for the house here, it certainly is. There are potentially competitive house races all over the state. And you look at Republican candidates in these races, they are playing on the issue of crime heavily in their ads. You look at some of the key senate races here, suburbs of Philadelphia, suburbs of Milwaukee, again the issue of crime featuring prominently in Republican messaging.\par \par \uc2 Take a look here. Here`s a look at some of the ads voters in places like I`m describing are seeing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mandela Barnes stands with defund the police and supports no cash bail that releases dangerous criminals back into our communities.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They want to defund the police even as crime rages out of control across the state.\par \par \uc2 Josh Riley, extreme liberal, dangerous.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  called police -- defund. Now he wants to end cash bail and putting criminals back on the street.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why is radical \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  dodging debates? Because he cannot defend freeing convicted murderers even over Josh Shapiro`s objection.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Beto stands with groups that support defunding the police, It`s dangerous to everyone.\par \par \uc2 And it`s why \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  should never be governor of Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Governor Luhan Grisham -- has made it easier to be a criminal than a cop. She`s released criminals from prison early and appointed soft on crime judges.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Streets are exploding with drugs and violence, while liberals like Tim Ryan attack and defund our police. Tim, fight the criminals, not the cops.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And joining us now is Kyle Kondik. He is the managing editor of "Sabato`s Crystal Ball" newsletter at the Virginia Center of Politics. And he has watched more than 330 campaign as this season.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations on that, Kyle or my condolences. I`m not sure which one applies, but we`re giving folks a taste of these Republican ads playing up crime. I`m serious, since you`ve watched all of these, what`s your sense of the economy and inflation versus crime, in terms of the emphasis Republicans are placing in their ads? Which one outweighs the other?\par \par \uc2 KYLE KONDIK, MANAGING EDITOR OF "SABATO`S CRYSTAL BALL": I mean look, I think you show a pretty representative sort of cross section that we are seeing from Republicans on crime. And you know, it`s just sort of more vivid, I guess, sort of you get more of a visceral reaction, which is probably what the ad makers are going for. Compared to a lot of the other Republican messaging, which a lot of it is you know, candidate x votes with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  98 percent of the time. And that causes inflation, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 The crime stuff it just -- it kind of hits you in the gut a little bit more, you know, when Democrats see a lot of these ads, they think hey, you know, the Republicans are just basically going back to race baiting, you know, the Republicans I think point to a lot of criminal justice reform efforts in blue states. that they will argue, you know, haven`t work and it have led to spikes in crime. But again it`s all in the eye of the beholder, I guess.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:59]\par \par \uc2 KONDIK: But you know, you just this crime messaging a lot, and you mentioned New York. One interesting piece of the New York congressional races is that it`s pretty common to see like Biden and Pelosi in these ads. But you also see \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , the Democratic governor, in a lot of the swing state, you know, Republican -- or swing district Republican attack ads, which is also kind of interesting.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: So we see this polling, and I`ve seen some other numbers like this. We saw this Pew polling where Republicans have that advantage on the issue of crime. You`re watching all these ads, you`re seeing the Republicans play it up. Are you noticing Democrats countering it in any way? Has that -- how are they doing it? Are they doing it more than they were before? What does it look from their perspective?\par \par \uc2 KONDIK: Absolutely, I mean Democrats talk about this issue a lot too. And what Democrats often do is they put law enforcement in their own ads. One of this congressional candidate out in California, Adam Gray, trying to (INAUDIBLE) seat out there and he put a bunch of Republican county sheriffs who are, you know, he had worked with in his ads.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, Democrats feel like they can, you know, put law enforcement officers in their own ads as sort of validators for themselves. But I think what ends up happening too is that you almost hear sort of the same thing from both Republicans and Democrats, a sort of tough on crime message, which when you think back like two years ago, you know, summer of 2020 and the reaction to the murder of George Floyd and all of that and the sort of idea of sort of reform movement, I think sort of the pendulum has swung back to where you got kind of both sides kind of competing in what you think to be sort of the Republican law enforcement territory, essentially being kind of like really pro law enforcement and really tough on crime.\par \par \uc2 And again, that`s neither good nor bad, it`s just interesting that that is where we are at right now.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Kyle Kondik from UVA, thank you for joining us, sir. We appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 And our panel is going to rejoin us right now. Jim Messina and Rich Lowry. Welcome back to both of you.\par \par \uc2 And Jim, I want to start with you and actually play a clip of a panel that was put together for "MORNING JOE" here of some Democratic voters in Philadelphia, obviously part of Pennsylvania, one of the key Senate races.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a panel with Democratic voters in Philadelphia asking about the presence of crime in the city and its impact in their lives. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have you seen a rise specifically in your neighborhoods?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s a rise everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Everywhere. All around the city.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s carjackings crazy, there are robberies, it just doesn`t make any sense.\par \par \uc2 It seems to be like more than ever.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s not just the city, it`s even into the suburbs, so it`s obviously more heavily concentrated in the urban areas. But absolutely, we don`t even know going home today, how we`re going to feel, you know, whether you`re driving or on public transportation, what`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Jim, when you see a panel like that and you see a number like we showed you in Pew where Republicans have that advantage, what can and what should Democrats be saying and doing in the next 17 days when it comes to this issue?\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, FORMER OBAMA DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Yes. See, this is another issue that drives me crazy. Democrats can`t seem to message their way out of a paper bag on this one.\par \par \uc2 First of all, facts matter here. Crime rates in red states are higher than crime rates in blue states. You don`t hear Democrats talking about this.\par \par \uc2 Second of all, you`ve got to do what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and go right at defunding the police and saying absolutely not. I`ll fight that over my dead body.\par \par \uc2 Third, you need to do what \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  did and take the fight to the Republicans and use this as a law and order issue, do exactly what Kyle was talking about earlier, put a bunch of cops, especially Republican cops in their ads and don`t cede this issue.\par \par \uc2 But let`s go back to why Republicans are doing this. They`re doing this because they`re bleeding suburban women votes all over the place on abortion, and so they`re trying to change the subject. This worked in 2020, crime was a big issue. The difference is Dobbs didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 And so the question is for these suburban voters, is it going to be about abortion or is it going to be about crime? And it will be about crime if Democrats don`t push back on this.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: You know, let me ask you about that, Rich, because it was the biggest shift we saw, one of the critical shifts we saw in 2020 against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came in the suburbs. And one of the biggest challenges for a Republican in 2022 has been trying to win back those voters.\par \par \uc2 And as Jim is saying, they clearly see crime as a way to do that. What -- you see the advantage they have on that, I am curious though, where do you think Republicans with those suburban voters who defected from Trump in 2020, where are Republicans still vulnerable with then?\par \par \uc2 RICH LOWRY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "NATIONAL REVIEW": Well, they`ve made gains. I mean it just helps not having Trump on the ballot, because a lot of the reaction just had to do with personal revulsion over Trump and the way he conducted himself.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:57]\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: But the crime issue, it`s real. I mean you look at the numbers, the panel is talking about it anecdotally but something like robberies with guns or something like up 60 percent in Philadelphia, just shocking. You`ve seen that in major urban areas around the country.\par \par \uc2 And I was talking to a Republican operative the other day, he said he has never seen candidates images fall as sharply as Mandela Barnes and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , when the barrage started on crime. Now Barnes is narrowly behind, Fetterman perhaps is narrowly ahead, but this is a real powerful issue. And it`s not one that`s fabricated. It`s something that people care about, but Republicans basically bootstrapped it up above abortion I think largely just by talking about it and making it an issue.\par \par \uc2 So this is something -- you know, this is what I said about Republicans in the summer, when they are getting hit on abortion, find defensible ground, a compromise position, where you can at least neutralize a vulnerability. A lot of Republicans have done that. Democrats need to find a way to do the same thing on crime.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, Rich Lowry and Jim Messina, thanks to you both for being with us tonight. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, talking about that issue of abortion. It is on the ballot this November in Michigan, literally, and that is where MSNBC`s Ali Velshi is live on the ground, and he`s going to join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:46:11]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And as time continues to tick down toward that first poll closing, let us talk about a big state. It doesn`t have a marquee senate race, but does have some important and consequential statewide races to talk about here. And that is the state of Michigan. Michigan won by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016, flipped by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020. And now, in 2022, here`s the big race in the state, in terms of elected office.\par \par \uc2 It`s the race for Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  the Democratic incumbent being challenged by Republican Tudor Dixon, and the polling, some of the polling we`ve been getting out of Michigan has been closer than many expected.\par \par \uc2 I think when Dixon won this primary, there was an expectation perhaps that Whitmer was in a strong position for the fall. Whitmer is leading in the polling, but this is the RealClear average here, and you could see Dixon in this averages is within striking distance. So, you`ve got a close election there for governor in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 And the other major race you have got in Michigan is there is a constitutional amendment on the ballot. It would say that abortion is a right under the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 Remember, we saw this in Kansas, obviously, we saw in a differently worded referendum that attracted massive interest over the summer. Well, from Kansas now to Michigan, that issues taking center stage in Michigan, and that ballot referendum and in this governor`s race.\par \par \uc2 And on the ground in Detroit is MSNBC`s Ali Velshi. He has been out there covering this race, covering this referendum. Ali, thank you for joining us first of all.\par \par \uc2 And why don`t you tell us first, what are you hearing, when it comes to abortion on the ground there in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, it`s probably the issue that is most driving voter registration and turnout, particularly among women in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 I have heard it on both sides, though. I`ve heard women who are fighting for this Proposal 3 that you just talked about, to enshrine constitutional rights into the constitution, saying that women are signing up at great rates. They`re going to show up at rates that are much higher than would typically be seen in both the midterm election and the midterm election in which there is not a big senate race.\par \par \uc2 I also heard from someone today who said it`s activating conservatives, who want to go out there, and vote on the other side. Bottom line is unlike Kansas, Kansas was a question to take away existing protections in the Kansas constitution.\par \par \uc2 This is to fix the fact that Michigan`s abortion law is now a 1931 law that pretty much bans abortion across the board. It`s been deemed unconstitutional at the moment, so it is in a ban. But the bottom line is, this is a measure to try and make abortion constitutional in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Here is the woman I spoke to today, one of the panel of six people I spoke to, who said she is working hard to register and motivate women to vote on this particular issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY DILLAHA, RED WINE AND BLUE, MICHIGAN PROGRAM DIRECTOR: I think the biggest issue in Michigan is abortion. I am hearing that from, like I said, other parents, from the women that I work with through my profession, and families in general.\par \par \uc2 The people want to know that they have reproductive rights. And that they have access to health care when they need it, not just abortion. It is lots of other services that are related.\par \par \uc2 WAYHNE BRADLEY, FORMER STATE DIRECTOR OF AFRICAN-AMERICAN ENGAGEMENT: When I`m dealing with a lot of different community groups and folks. That is what`s the most important right now. Can I get a job? Can I pay for my home? Can I pay my rent?\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, know, I am not picking on anybody on this abortion issue, that is a luxury for some people to be that to be their number one concern.\par \par \uc2 For suburban moms, and for folks outside of inner cities, that might be the biggest issue. But in the inner cities, it`s about jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The second gentleman was a Republican, and he was saying that, like the other Republican on my panel, they were saying that the economy is the most important issue. But for most of the Democrats on the panel, abortion was issue number one here in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Well again, that governor`s race, that abortion referendum in Michigan is certainly going to be one of those states we`re following closely. 17 days and change from now, and those results coming.\par \par \uc2 Ali Velshi in Detroit, thank you so much for joining us. Ali will have much more live from Michigan tomorrow, and you can catch him starting at 8:00 a.m. right here on MSNBC. And we will be back with more right after this.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:57]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, we`ve got a big week ahead on the campaign trail. That long awaited Pennsylvania Senate debate between Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Mehmet Oz, is going to happen this coming Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is going to debate former governor, now, his Democratic opponent \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:59:59]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is going to hit the campaign trail, starting Friday in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 We are going to have complete coverage of that next Friday night on THE COUNTDOWN.\par \par \uc2 And big polling news, the NBC News poll is coming out on Sunday morning. You`re going to get brand-new numbers less than 48 hours from now. We will see right here next Friday night, 10 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And "THE 11TH HOUR" is next.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221022eial00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Dasha Burns, Michael Steele, Garrett Haake, Ken  Dilanian, Joyce Vance, Nicholas Confessore, Cal Perry, Ali Vitali, Danny  Cevallos, Ken Dilanian \par \uc2 8,793 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:13]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City. Today, new evidence that Democrats are struggling to connect on issues average Americans care about most. Look, we know the economy is number one, but fears about crime are up there too. And Republicans are winning the issue by double digits. But should they be?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY HOFMEISTER (D-OK), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The fact is the rates of violent crime are higher in Oklahoma to your watch than in New York and California. That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KEVIN STITT\uc2  (R-OK): Oklahomans, do you believe we have higher crime than New York or California? That`s what she just said.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, here`s a fact check. She`s right. So should more Democrats take Joy Hofmeister`s lead and talk more on the campaign trail about what voters are talking about around the kitchen table?\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  sentenced to four months behind bars for thumbing his nose at a congressional subpoena. But for now, he`s headed home instead of heading to jail. We`ll explain that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 And finally, if you`re joining us from the West Coast, look up numerous UFO sightings reported over the Pacific Ocean in recent weeks by more than a dozen commercial pilots. Check this out mysterious lights caught on camera, some of them 10,000 feet above where the planes were flying. What the heck is going on? We`ll have more on that later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the midterms and Democratic candidates running out of time to address the Americans top issues, the economy, inflation, some including North Carolina Cheri Beasley and Ohio`s \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  are trying to take these issues directly to voters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D-NC), SENATE NOMINEE: You know, folks are failing everything from pain at the pump to the cost of prescription drugs and everything in between. And Congress can address these really important issues and shouldn`t run away from them.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH), SENATE NOMINEE: I want to talk about jobs. I want to talk about wages. I want to talk about pensions. I want to talk about making sure that people have, you know, dignity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But sometimes they can seem like outliers. The message from national democrats has often been to focus on other issues, including abortion. And now this new Pew poll shows voters overwhelmingly trust Republicans to deal with the economy and crime. It may be partly the function of ads like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 2020 murder in Cincinnati at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Homicides are up 91 percent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And Greg Landsman`s response, defund the police.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mandela Barnes dangerously liberal on crime.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is dangerous for police.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And dangerous for Nevada.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So there may be a lesson for the midterms from the unceremoniously deposed \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  in the U.K. And here`s what it is. If people feel uneasy about their everyday lives, you better convince them that you`re the one to turn things around. I want to bring in NBC`s Garrett Haake following the Senate race in Nevada. David Drucker is senior political correspondent for The Washington Examiner and is also reporting on the ground in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Michael Steele is former \uc2 RNC\uc2  chairman and an MSNBC political analyst. And with me here on set, Dasha Burns, who is covering the race in Pennsylvania. So let`s dig into crime, Dasha. I know you`ve been talking to the candidates there. You`ve been talking to voters. What are they telling you?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, you mentioned it there, Democrats have been trying to drill down on abortion, but Republicans, they are really trying to ride that red wave on crime and it seems to be working. Look, you spend time in Pennsylvania, the televisions are absolutely full of the ads like you just played. And when I talk to voters, especially those key voters in the suburbs that both candidates are trying to win.\par \par \uc2 They are concerned about crime. They`re concerned about public safety. And I`m not talking about Republicans. I`m talking about republicans and democrats and independents who are watching this play out and are seeing Oz hammering, Fetterman on this issue.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m curious, do those concerns always align with where they`re actually are rising crime statistics.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: No?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No. So that`s the thing. I mean, look at Pennsylvania overall, just a quick reality check on crime. Crime rates are down in Pennsylvania overall, but they are on the rise in places like Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. We`re talking violent crimes and burglaries. But when I talk to voters in Philly, they feel this, but also suburban women, hundreds of miles away from Philadelphia or Pittsburgh that are seeing these ads on television.\par \par \uc2 And their neighborhoods might be perfectly safe. But that doesn`t mean they`re not, you know, getting that sense from what they`re seeing on television and the rhetoric that they`re hearing from the right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, my takeaway from that, Michael, is that sometimes we think that voters get a lot of nuance, but their lives are pretty busy. And if you see a -- an ad and it talks about crime and maybe it really doesn`t impact you on a day-to-day basis, that doesn`t mean it doesn`t hit home, right?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: No, that`s exactly right. It`s -- you create the environment and the environment sometimes is created for the voters and sometimes it`s created by the voters.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:08]\par \par \uc2 And I think in this instance what you have is looking at statistics, particularly as Dasha just noted in those -- in those states where crime is not on the rise and where may be an uptick in their more urban communities, it`s not an aggressive increase. That still does it allay the concerns that voters have about crime period.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, can I ask you this so, Michael, because I was looking at the statistics, out of the states with the top 10 violent crime rates from 2020, seven are run by Republicans. Those are the ones that were mentioned in that exchange from Oklahoma. We know that there`s a message there that Democrats have but they seem to be shying away from it. How are Republicans turning around? What would seem on their face to be the facts that are against them?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Because they`re playing in an old notion that crime is committed by people of color. Crime occurs in areas where people of color live, crime doesn`t occur in white suburban America, crime occurs in the inner city. So when you have, you know, what you saw the governor in the clip coming in basically saying Oklahomans, well, you know, you think we have crime like California? Of course, we don`t, because hey, there -- we don`t have those kinds of people living here.\par \par \uc2 So there`s this subtext that you have to be cognizant of as well. And what`s still amazingly stunning to me is that the Democrats didn`t see this coming. I mean, this is -- this is page two on the -- in the playbook. After the economy, page one, is crime. Page two and you couldn`t figure out that that`s the sandwich they were going to make the selection about not abortion because they don`t win on abortion.\par \par \uc2 You`re not going to find a Republicans engaging on a board trying to defend their position on abortion. No, I`m going to get you to talk about something else, something that is arguably a lot less scarier but it actually is more scary to voters. And that is the crime in the neighborhood in which they live even though the crime is literally 300 miles away in an urban center from them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So Garrett, that brings me to Nevada, one of the tightest Senate races in the country. You spoke with the candidates. How are the economy inflation, crime, top three issues almost everywhere playing out in that race?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, it`s interesting in a race with two former attorneys general on the ballot, as is this senate race here that crime has taken a secondary position that endorsements from various police groups are split between the two parties on that issue. \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  has spent some money on television and his allies have tried to make crime an issue.\par \par \uc2 But look 30 years on, nobody`s said it better than James Carville. It`s the economy, stupid. And that is the case here in Nevada. It`s the state with the highest inflation in the country. Gas prices in the Las Vegas metro area, pretty comfortably around $5.00 a gallon. And Senator \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  is in a tough place here. She in my interview yesterday, defended her vote for the American rescue plan, pointing out that Nevada`s really needed it.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment was sky high here. They needed the money. They needed the infusion of resources. She`s been defending the bipartisan infrastructure bills, some of these other bills that Republicans have pointed to as inflationary, but she doesn`t want to get punished for the National Party, the perception that Democrats are somehow behind inflation or these high prices. And so she`s trying to keep her distance from her own party.\par \par \uc2 As you can see here when I asked her what she`d like from the president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in terms of helping her out in this race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But you have a party that has your back here, you have a president from your party. What would you like to see from him? Would you like to see him campaign for you? Was he talking about abortion the other day? Is that helpful? What can he do to help you?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D-NV), SENATE NOMINEE: Well, I can tell you what I hear from Nevadans, and that`s my priority. And it`s not what you just asked me. It is about everyday issues, kitchen table issues, how we do this together. That`s my priority.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Important fact, Chris, the Cortez Mastro campaign has not invited President Biden to come campaign here with her. President Biden has only been to Nevada once as president, that for Harry Reid`s funeral. So for \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , she`s going to try to run on her accomplishments, but keep some distance from the National Party. That`s a very difficult needle to thread on these economic issues that are front of mind for everyone. And not just in Nevada, but around the country.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let me to that point, David, play what President Biden said just an hour or so ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think that we`re going to see one more shift back to our side, the closing days. And let me tell you why I think that. We`re starting to see some of the good news on the economy. Gas prices are down sharply in 46 of the 50 states. State unemployment today, state unemployment was all-time lows in the low 11 states and 17 states have unemployment rates under three percent.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:04]\par \par \uc2 The election is not a referendum, it`s a choice, it`s a choice . And the Republicans criticize my economic record. But look at what I`ve inherited, and what I`ve done, and look at what they`re offering.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So my question for you, David, is that wishful thinking? Or is there time for messaging and circumstances to turn momentum around?\par \par \uc2 DAVID DRUCKER, WASHINGTON EXAMINER SENIOR POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, there`s a little time but there`s not much. As you know, early voting has already begun cross country. There`s a big kickoff here in Nevada in the coming days. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  is coming to town, prominent Republicans are coming to town. They`re going to be active in Las Vegas and Reno. And look, one of the things that Republicans have been able to do in this election, in this campaign is make a big push for the Latino vote.\par \par \uc2 That really hasn`t existed before. The party has targeted Latinos in the past. But I`m right now on the east side of Las Vegas in a heavily Latino area, much of the signage on the storefronts is in Spanish. And I`m steps away from a \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  field office dedicated solely to reaching the Latino vote. And so, Democrats have a lot more competition for these demographics and voting blocks that have been solidly democratic, if you will, for several years.\par \par \uc2 And in talking to Latino voters here in Nevada, as Garrett mentioned, they are as concerned about the same issues Republicans are concerned about as any -- as anything. They`re concerned about the economy, they`re concerned about inflation. They`re concerned about what they believe to be rising crime. We talked to some of them yesterday, at a little bakery where Senator \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  spoke to a lot of her supporters.\par \par \uc2 They are her supporters but they too are concerned about the issues Republican voters are concerned about. So Democrats have a tall order, they have to push back against President Joe Biden`s low approval ratings, they have to push back against the national issue set that is moving against them at the moment. And with early voting and mail-in voting, they don`t have a lot of time to do it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So Michael, I guess that brings us all the way around to what we sort of premised at the beginning which is that hey, look at \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  she barely lasted long enough for you to blink. And next thing you know, she`s gone. Is the message from that, that if you don`t answer what people`s concerns are, there is no patience.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes. That`s the -- the lesson for Democrats today and it will be the lesson for Republicans tomorrow, meaning January. When they take the house. And if they take the Senate, they`re going t have to govern over 18 months and what do they do? And I think if nothing else, what the voters are saying is, you know, we`re kind of sick and tired of being sick and tired about being sick and tired about you doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 And so, as this economy tightens and it will, as the pressure grows on the political parties to respond and these electeds to govern, I think as we step into 24, this window that we now see from Truss and the Tories in London, it`s going to be more -- it`s going to be widened for the political parties here in America because it`ll be there but for the grace of governing well, and attaching ourselves to the concern of the people go us.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that`s going to be interesting to see how the parties pivot off this election. And actually getting something done because at the end of the day, voters still want that to happen.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Garrett, Dasha, David and Michael, thank you all so much. So, here in the U.S. we can channel dissatisfaction right by voting but, but take a look at the front page of the Daily Mirror because if it`s right, that`s what folks in Britain want. Do we have that graphic, but they`re not -- there it is. General Election Now, but they`re not going to get it. Instead, with \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  out, party leaders get to decide who will be the next prime minister with the possibility of bringing back \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  very much a part of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Molly Hunter is in London for us. So Molly, the number of conservatives who actually will pick the next prime minister represent a fraction of one percent of the overall population. The same folks who choose Truss. Do people you talk to see a larger failure of leadership or this is just one bad politician in their eyes?\par \par \uc2 MOLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris, without a doubt, not just one bad politician, but a failure of leadership, a failure of a