NG: Joyce, do you see grounds for appeal?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, Bannon is going to make this argument but -- and it should be vetted out on appeal, frankly. We give criminal defendants the opportunity post conviction to test whether there was air that might mean that their conviction was wrong. That`s an issue for the court of appeals to determine. But in terms of this release that the judge ordered for Bannon while that appeal is pending, that seems to violate the federal standards, which say that you can only release a depend -- a defendant while an appeal is pending if there`s a substantial issue on trial.\par \par \uc2 Here, there`s no substantial issue because the law in the circuit is very clear. There`s no reason to believe that it will be altered on appeal. And so, it`s rather unusual, this notion that Bannon unlike other similarly situated defendants can remain free on bond while his appeal when`s its way through the courts.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Nicholas, there`s a federal appeals court denial yesterday. Senator Lindsey Graham`s request to block a subpoena. That one was from the Fulton County, Georgia grand jury. Do you look at today`s sentencing and say, well, maybe that changes the equation for Graham and his lawyers or for that matter, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as we wait for the J6 Committee to deliver subpoenas to him?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS CONFESSORE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, look, I think it`d be pullback we see that there is gravity still, right? As a force in the world in politics and in the courts. You can`t just wave a magic wand, you can`t just defy subpoenas, you can`t just make up spurious claims.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:08]\par \par \uc2 What we saw on the Graham ruling was essentially the judge saying, look, you can use the speech and debate clause of the constitution to avoid being hauled into court for a normal debate for exercising your powers. You can`t use that clause to get out of testifying about an 