re is a difference between sending equipment and sending troops to help Russia. What`s your take on what we see unfolding now with Iran?\par \par \uc2 NAYYERA HAQ, FORMER WHITE HOUSE SENIOR DIRECTOR: Well, as we see the Ukrainians being bombed fighting for their country, we also are seeing a delicate playing out between the different countries involved. The United States as you mentioned has been providing military equipment, has been providing funding, but has not put any boots on the ground in Ukraine because the United States and its \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies are not looking to make this about the U.S. versus Russia.\par \par \uc2 Now we see that Iran is doing the same thing but on the Russian side of the equation. They are providing trainers in Crimea. That is, unfortunately, a part of Ukraine that Russia took over more than 10 years ago. The world effectively sat by and let Russia take over it. So the Iranians can say they are in Russian territory right now. They`ve also provided drones that are part of the attacks on Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 One of the things the State Department also said is they are tracking to see if Iran provides surface-to-air missiles. That would be a different level of equipment and support but at this moment we`re not seeing anything on a diplomatic side that shows that Russia has violated any new international laws.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, you mentioned U.S. support, Nayyera, and House minority leader Kevin McCarthy said this week that his party members are not going to write a blank check to Ukraine but now, today, Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  put out a statement calling for more aid to Ukraine. How critical could a potential lame duck aid package be especially if Republicans do win control of Congress?\par \par \uc2 HAQ: Absolutely critical. Let`s be clear. The reason Ukrainians are still functioning as an insurgency and able to protect the land they have is because of \uc2 NATO\uc2  and U.S. support to the tune of billions and billions of dollars. The United States is also training the Ukrainians in Poland again, no boots on the Ukraine, and that needs to continue if that is going to be a support for democracy in Europe and to make sure that no country, especially Russia, gets away with just invading and taking over another.\par \par \uc2 Congress is also right now trying to speed up the provision of drones, specific types of drones to the Ukrainian military, as well, and that`s part of why congressional support is so important right now in a lame duck session. And I will note, though, Chris, while on the political side there is rhetoric to attack any of Biden`s plans coming up in the midterm election, by and large, we have seen unanimous if not bipartisan support for the U.S. engagement with the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Nayyera Haq, it`s always great to have you on the program. Thank you so much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon will pay to help service members seeking an abortion to get one even if it is illegal where they serve. In a memo by Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  the Pentagon announced it will provide travel funds and support for troops and their families who want to get an abortion. The military will also increase privacy protections for members seeking care including extending the amount of time in which a service member has to report a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Kids coughing, having trouble breathing and being rushed to the hospital. The mysterious respiratory illness surging in dozens of states and it isn`t COVID. I`ll ask one of the country`s top pediatricians what needs to be done, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:51]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Breaking news. The long-awaited subpoena for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is ready to go. Let`s go to Capitol Hill and NBC`s Ali Vitali.\par \par \uc2 What`s the latest, Ali?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris. This subpoena officially being issued from the committee to the former president. This comes a week after the committee voted during a public hearing to actually move forward with this step but they take great pains in the letter, which is roughly 10 pages, and also includes the subpoena itself. That`s rare. We haven`t seen the actual subpoenas included in these filings in the past when they`ve issued them.\par \par \uc2 But what they try to do here is make clear that while this is clearly a ratcheting up in terms of their goal of speaking to the former president it`s not unprecedented for former presidents or even sitting presidents to come before congressional committees and either submit documents or to testify.\par \par \uc2 They lay out two important dates here, Chris. One before the election and one after. Certainly there will be many in the political sphere who point to that immediately but they have November 4th on the calendar at this point for the day that they want Trump to comply for their request for records. Some on the committee have said that they think those could even be more illuminating than any kind of deposition with the former president itself.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 But they also ask Trump to come in and talk to them himself in person saying that November 14th is going to be the deadline for that.\par \par \uc2 And there`s some really striking language in this letter Chris. They`re not shying away from using the word you when leveling allegations at the former president`s feet. Of course this comes after all of those multiple hearing and they point to that in part using language saying, in short you were at the center of the first and only effort by any U.S. president to overturn an election and obstruct the peaceful transfer of power, ultimately culminating in a bloody attack on our own Capitol Hill and on the Congress itself.\par \par \uc2 That is part of the letter that`s been issued from the committee, from Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  and Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  to the former president now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I think it`s not insignificant when you look at this one-page release that they do name, as you say, former presidents. Former presidents \uc2 John Quincy Adams\uc2 , John Tyler, Theodore Roosevelt, \uc2 William Howard Taft\uc2 , \uc2 Herbert Hoover\uc2 , \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2 , Gerald Ford, each testified before Congress after they left office. President Roosevelt explained during his congressional testimony an ex-president is merely a citizen of the United States like any other citizen essentially preemptively answering what they know is likely at least is going to be one of his arguments against.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes. That`s exactly right. And look, we often talk in this Trump era over the last five years about how unprecedented things are. In fact, in this case what the committee is trying to point out is that despite the fact that January 6th was an unprecedented and historic day it is not unprecedented for a former president to be asked to come in and talk about it. And so they`re trying to level the fact that if Trump were to not comply he would be out of step with other people who had previously held the Oval Office and still complied with Congress as former presidents, and even in the case of sitting presidents who have provided documents when asked for them by Congress.\par \par \uc2 So they`re trying to take all the novelty out of this despite the fact that they`re a committee investigating an unprecedented day. This request itself is not that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tell me, Ali, the latest you`re hearing out of Trump world. So there was conversations, we understood. Oh, yes, I want to -- you know, I want to answer questions and maybe I`ll answer questions if I can be live. Where are we with that? What`s the latest you`ve heard? Not, and just to put a big asterisk on that, not that the last thing we`ve heard might actually end up being what the decision finally is.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, look, because this is very much up in the air. We have seen in the past Trump tried to say that he wants to talk to the people who are investigating him, right, whether it was Mueller many years or other congressional committees that have investigated him on various fronts. We also know that those never came to fruition. So whether or not this is a conversation effectively going on in Trump world, what it doesn`t include is the fact that many lawyers probably wouldn`t want him to be opened up to that kind of a wide-ranging Q&A from this committee that has so much that they say evidence around what the former president did on that day, what he may have known and been told by advisers and then ultimately the actions that he himself took.\par \par \uc2 And you`ve got to imagine that the way that Trump had talked about the committee publicly on the campaign trail also plays a role here. He has said many times as he has about every other investigation about him that it is a witch-hunt and that it`s not steeped in fact. But, nevertheless, the committee has presented a compelling case. I would also add that when you look at just the public sentiment around this, while the hearings themselves haven`t necessarily changed people`s minds about what Trump did, how responsible he may be, those opinions are baked.\par \par \uc2 But the nevertheless I think about that new Monmouth poll that we saw just a few days ago where it says that 6 in 10 Americans do think that the former president should go before Congress and testify, so there is an overwhelming majority at least in the American public who want him to comply with the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali, stick with us because I want to bring in NBC News and MSNBC legal analyst Danny Cevallos.\par \par \uc2 Danny, it raises the question obviously former presidents are protected by the Secret Service logistically. How do you serve a subpoena to a former president?\par \par \uc2 DANNY CEVALLOS, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: I am fascinated by this issue because it doesn`t come up all that often. Yes, the Secret Service protects the president and typically when you use a process server it might be a private investigator, it might just be a regular civilian. You get all kinds of folks for a process servers. And it might be the kind of person that Secret Service is going to see approaching the president or the former president and they might have a problem with that.\par \par \uc2 So I expect there`s going to be a lot of, I hope, cooperation between Secret Service, former president, former president`s lawyers and committee lawyers to make sure that this is done in a convenient fashion to a lot of times for lawyers will just call up and say, hey, we`ll accept service and process. But I expect everyone wants to make sure that proper is affected properly in this case.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:04]\par \par \uc2 So hopefully committee and Trump`s lawyers, his legal team, both open lines of teams, both open lines of communication to make sure that the former president is served safely and correctly.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let me read a little bit more about this, Ali. This, as you say, is a very pointed statement. They are not pulling any punches here but they say as demonstrated in our hearings, we have assembled overwhelming evidence including from dozens of your former appointees and staff that you personally orchestrated and oversaw a multi-part effort to overturn the 2020 presidential election and to obstruct the people`s transition of power.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that was pointed out at the last committee hearing when they were essentially making what seemed to be closing arguments was that most of the people they showed us, many of the people they interviewed were family members or Republicans, or people who worked for the president.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: That`s exactly right. Those are the people that the committee has gotten to tell the story of what was happening in the White House. Many of the people who were in the White House, who were in the conversations with outside allies, who are Republicans, those have been the main people testifying and telling the story for the American public through the January 6th Committee by way of subpoena or voluntary testimony.\par \par \uc2 But I also think that one of the things that may have held up this process, because again, a week had lapsed between the time that the committee said they were going to do this and the actual issuance of this subpoena. Yes, there were conversations about making sure that the language was final, there were conversations about what the dates for compliance should be, but there were also conversations about the way that they could best serve the subpoena, the process piece that Danny was talking about.\par \par \uc2 Last night on MSNBC, for example, Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  sort of admitted the fact that that was one of the things that they were dealing with but that they did have a name of someone at that point who was willing to receive the subpoena. So look, they were trying to do this in a way that dotted all the I`s and crossed the T`s because they knew it would be a heavy lift to get Trump come in and comply.\par \par \uc2 That`s the mindset going into this, though the former president has yet to indicate either way whether or not he is going to end up complying with the subpoena but there`s really no mindset shift in terms of the committee. They`ve always known that this was going to be an uphill climb for them to get Trump to actually come in front of them, but again there are two requests here, right, for him to come and do the interview but also for him to do documents and there is a sense that the documents could be the more important and helpful piece of that puzzle for the economy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But in the meantime, as you well know, and we`ve talked about this before, Ali, the clock is ticking. This committee, members of it, will no longer be members of it in the new Congress. So we know what Donald Trump`s tactics have been previously, three parts, delay, delay, delay, right?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So what are you hearing from committee members about any concerns they have about the timing right now?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: They`re not being forthcoming. And this is not necessarily surprising, on what they`re willing to do in terms of enforcement. But the fact that this comes on a day that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  receives his conviction for thumbing his nose at the January 6th Committee subpoena is not necessarily indicative of how it will go for Trump because Bannon is certainly in a much different position than a former president, but it`s instructive in terms of just how long it takes to go from the committee, having someone defy their subpoena then move forward with criminal charges, through the House, then referring them to DOJ. It`s a lengthy process.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen how long it takes once the gears of the justice system have to start working through this process. And so they don`t have time on their hands in a way that they did with Bannon or Meadows or Navarro or any of the other people that they put through criminal contempt charges out of Congress and over into DOJ. This is a subpoena that expires at the end of the year. And look, when we reported that they were even going to move forward on the subpoena in the first place, again, this has been an awareness that almost every member that I`ve talked to has.\par \par \uc2 They knew it wasn`t going to be an easy road, that it wasn`t just going to be a quick ask or a quick subpoena and Trump would comply with it, but they thought it was important to get it in the record that they were willing to do this after establishing over nine hearings this year, 10 hearings overall, the role of the former president in what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring back justice and intelligence correspondent Ken Dilanian.\par \par \uc2 Ken, you only had a few minutes to look at this, but what stands out to you?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Chris, the first thing that jumped out at me is that the committee apparently has reason to believe that former President Trump is using an encrypted messaging app called Signal. If you look at the first demands for documents, the numbered part of the subpoena, they`re looking for any records of telephone calls, text messages or communications sent through Signal or any other means, placed or received by you or at your direction.\par \par \uc2 So that`s just interesting because, look, many reporters use signal to communicate with sources, if both ends are on Signal, then that means the transmission is encrypted and can`t be intercepted.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:00]\par \par \uc2 You can also set Signal to have your messages disappear so they`re not stored. But it doesn`t have to be set that way. So the messages can be stored. And Signal messages have been obtained by the government in various investigations, if you get somebody`s phone and they`ve stored the messages, you can get the Signal messages.\par \par \uc2 But remember, this is a man who famously does not use e-mail, we`re told, does not write many things down, so if there is a trove of Signal messages either by him or by aides about his communications, and apparently the January 6th Committee believes there may well be, that jumped out at me as something very, very interesting.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So just to reiterate the breaking news, if you`re just joining us, the select committee, the January 6th Committee has issued a subpoena to former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for testimony under oath and records relevant to their cause.\par \par \uc2 Ken, Ali, Danny, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. And this weekend, MSNBC Films presents "SHOUTING DOWN MIDNIGHT," the first installment of a new documentary series "The Turning Point," executive produced by Trevor Noah. It shines a light on former Texas State Senator Wendy Davis who stood up against her state`s 2013 restricted anti-abortion bill. That`s Sunday, 10:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC and streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead this afternoon, President Biden will talk student debt relief. Yasmin Vossoughian picks up our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221022eial00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for October 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Molly Hunter \par \uc2 8,810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Second Gentleman Doug Emhoff and the staff of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  get tonight`s Last Words and those words are happy birthday. THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:14]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Tonight, all the former president`s attorneys Trump`s growing roster of lawyers set for a showdown with the January 6 committee, as one of his closest allies in the Senate is forced to testify in the Georgia grand jury investigation.\par \par \uc2 Then it`s her party, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  no longer fringe inside her district as Georgia early voting rakes records and the scramble for a new leader in the UK after \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  stepped down after just 44 days and the warning signs flashing here at home as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. The twice impeached ex- president is bracing for what could be an explosive confrontation with lawmakers investigating January 6. The House Select Committee is said to be on the verge of sending \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  that subpoena for records and testimony.\par \par \uc2 The vote to take that step was exactly one week ago tonight. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting this. Lawmakers and committee lawyers spent the last days since last week`s hearing carefully crafting the subpoena language closely studying case law about subpoenas to current and former presidents. Tonight, committee members \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  confirm that saying the subpoena would be issued very soon. And she says much of the focus is on the paperwork.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): The documents may be even more important than his live testimony. We do know that the former president has a veracity problem. We`ve gotten a lot of documents from the archives but there -- there`s more that we like -- like -- we`d like to have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: POLITICO reporting Trump is lawyering up yet again. He has hired another lawyer conservative attorney Harmeet Dhillon to negotiate with the panel. She`s represented several witnesses who`ve already appeared before the committee, including Trump`s former National Security Adviser \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, one time Trump strategist and longtime friend \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , who refused to comply with a subpoena will learn his punishment for contempt of Congress. Prosecutors asking for the maximum sentence of six months in prison and a $200,000 fine. According to the Guardian, Bannon plans to ask a federal appeals court to overturn the conviction.\par \par \uc2 And tonight there are new developments in the Mar-a0Lago documents case. According to a new letter sent to the special master, Trump is now claiming at least nine documents seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or his personal property. But the Justice Department argues these documents involve pardons and immigration policies, and they are covered by the Presidential Records Act, and they belong in the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 There`s also major news in Georgia and their investigation into Trump`s attempts to overturn the 2020 election results in that state. NBC News confirming Trump`s former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  has testified to the grand jury in that case, and today a federal appeals court said South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  must testify before that very same grand jury.\par \par \uc2 Graham filed a lawsuit to try to avoid the subpoena. But two separate courts have now rejected his arguments.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors are focusing on Graham`s phone call with Georgia`s top election officials just days after the 2020 vote.\par \par \uc2 With that let`s get smarter tonight with the help of our leadoff panel. Yamiche Alcindor joins us, NBC News correspondent and moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . Katie Benner is here Pulitzer Prize winning Justice Department reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , and \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , Department of Justice veteran and former acting Solicitor General during the Obama administration. He has argued dozens of cases before the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Katie, let`s turn to you this subpoena. The anticipation for it grows by the day, it`s like the rose ceremony in the Bachelor at this point, how close are we to get in this thing?\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, THE NEW YORK TIMES JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER: I think that \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  was telling the truth. Of course when she said they are very close. They needed to be extremely careful with the subpoena, as you can imagine, and they also needed to weigh the risks about the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 I mean, soon after they said that they were going to ask \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for testimony and documents, you saw that he put out a 14-page screed filled with lies about the election. And it`s really illustrated the risk there is in getting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to testify, which is one of the reasons why as Congressman -- Congresswoman Lofgren said, they`re very, very focused on documents in addition to his testimony.\par \par \uc2 This is a high risk move that the committee has taken so they`re being as careful as possible, as they craft the subpoena and then we`ll wait and see what happens with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, let`s talk about language in the subpoena and why it`s so important because given history as a guide whatever it says Trump`s going to blow his nose with it and throw it in the garbage.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:09]\par \par \uc2 NEAK KAYTAL, FORMER ACTING SOLICITOR GENERAL: That`s 100 percent right. And so here he`s got all sorts of claims, we`ve started to see about attorney client privilege, executive privilege. They`ve always been bogus. But if you`re a member of Congress, trying to craft a subpoena, you got to be very careful.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look at just what Trump`s doing in Mar-a-Lago before Judge Dearie, the Special Master, he`s arguing that that documents are personal to him, and therefore, the investigators can`t have them. And at the same time, he`s claiming executive privilege over those documents, which means that they`re, of course not personal to him.\par \par \uc2 So Trump`s legal team has now invented I guess, the legal equivalent of Schrodinger as documents, so long as the papers are hidden in Donald Trump`s Mar-a-Lago desk, they`re simultaneously government documents and non-government documents.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this kind of inanity is what Congress has to deal with, is they craft this subpoena, and I hope one of the things, Steph, was they craft the subpoena for Trump. You know, and Trump`s obviously important, he`s the key figure, but a second finger is Vice President Pence and it is, you know, still to me baffling and inexcusable, that this January 6 committee which has done such a phenomenal job, marshaling the evidence telling the story of what actually happened to the American people, but there`s a huge piece missing, which is Pence. His aides have testified. He`s let them testify. But he`s so far has been afraid to, and I think Congress should call his bluff.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, you mentioned the Mar-a-Lago documents case and Trump`s new claims no, these documents are mine. How`s this special master going to rule on this? if Trump`s arguments nonsense, is he just trying to run the clock? What`s he doing?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Yes, I mean, nonsense is actually putting it charitably. These arguments are going nowhere. He doesn`t have any valid claim for at least the documents that the Justice Department really cares about. Now, I suppose in the search, they probably did take some personal documents, because one of the things you try and do as an investigator is show that that Trump actually knew that these documents were there.\par \par \uc2 And so if it`s, you know, if the classified and sensitive documents are with some of his other papers that he accesses, that`s a way of demonstrating that. But of course, there`s filter teams and all sorts of ways to protect privacy in that circumstance.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t think the Special Master is going anywhere, which is why, you know, nobody ever else gets a special master really mean, it`s an incredibly rare thing. But Trump found a judge who will gave him one. But be careful what you wish for, because this special master so far has been just, you know, pretty hostile to Trump`s claims.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yamiche, let`s talk about Trump`s new lawyer, well known conservative lawyer, you may have seen her on Fox News. She specializes in defending a lot of these people who claim election fraud, falsely claiming, what can we expect here?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, PBS WASHINGTON WEEK MODERATOR: What we can expect is the lawyer who`s going to try to do a good job on behalf of former President Trump, who is going to continue to have a client who is trying to dictate for himself with a legal strategy should be Katie, you know, Katie just talked about this idea of President Trump -- former President Trump sending this 14 page memo.\par \par \uc2 I was watched -- I read that and I literally was like, Who in the world allowed him to clearly dictate all of this by -- to send to the committee. The first line was literally an all caps, Stephanie. And I think in some ways it reminds us that while President Trump can have the means to hire -- high powered lawyers and have the means to hire lawyers that might be able to come up with a strategy that could win, he`s still himself going to have a really troubled relationship with the truth.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, you`re going to have people who are trying to steer him in one direction, and he`s going to go in another and that, of course, is the story of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the White House, outside of the White House, before the White House. So I think yes, we can see a lawyer who`s going to try to do her best here, but it`s still going to be a very, very challenging case.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, let`s talk about Kash Patel, because when you look at the cadre of Trump allies who are willing to say anything, do anything perform as a human shield for him. Kash Patel is high on the list. And now it has been reported former Trump Pentagon aid. He`s now testified to the grand jury in the classified documents case. And I want to share with you Andrew Weissmann`s take on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER SENIOR MEMBER OF MUELLER PROBE: The fact that he`s being put in to me signals that the prosecutors definitely have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a target. And that is where they`re going. Kash Patel was the person who in May and June had made statements saying that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had done this sort of spontaneous, miraculous declassification of documents when it was things were moved from the Oval Office to the residence, they spontaneously just became --\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC ANCHOR: Declassification.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Exactly. So you can be sure they`re going to ask about that, which is, you know, we want to know, is that a possible defense?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What do you think?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: So two things. One Andrew is absolutely right. This is further evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the target of the federal investigation.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:05]\par \par \uc2 I don`t think we need to Kash Patel basically to tell us that I mean, after all, Trump`s Mar-a-Lago property has been, you know, searched pursuant to a federal warrant, and a federal judge has signed off saying there`s probable cause to believe that evidence at Mar-a-Lago shows crimes will be around whether crimes were committed. So, you know, I -- this is not surprising news.\par \par \uc2 The part that I think should cause Trump some worry is this, it`s one thing for Kash Patel to go mouth off on Fox News about, you know, someone declassifying documents in their head or whatever other nonsense, but it`s a very different thing to make those claims under penalty of perjury before a federal grand jury. And it wouldn`t shock me if Kash Patel all of a sudden today, before the grand jury started recalling a very different version of events than what he`s going around and telling Fox News.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But isn`t that distressing a grand jury, a small group of people, they get to hear the truth. But when you sit on set at Fox News, you can just use their megaphone to amplify lies to millions and millions of viewers with no consequences.\par \par \uc2 Katie, here`s something else concerning for the Justice Department. They`re now saying they need more cash to continue the Jan. 6 investigation. That ain`t happening if Republicans win in November. So if that ends up being the case, what happens to all the evidence they`ve gathered so far?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: What will happen is the Justice Court will have to try to move money around within the department. And this does not mean that the investigation will end simply because Congress has not appropriate as much money as the Justice Department would like, it just simply means that the Justice Department overall will not have the money at wants to operate at the capacity that wants everywhere.\par \par \uc2 So you can see cuts being made in other places. You can see people -- you can see resources being moved around at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and other investigative bodies in order to accommodate needs for something as important as the January 6 investigation.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and Lisa Monaco, the top two officials are going to say we`re going to simply let this go because of appropriations. But to your point, Congress does have a lot of power. It has the purse strings and that is how it can control what the agencies are doing or how it can curb what agencies are doing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It all depends on who has control and Congress. Neal, let`s talk \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , he tried. He tried again. But he has now lost in his repeated attempts to avoid testifying in Georgia. What happens next? Will he continue to fight or Lindsay got to show up and talk?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: You know, he can try and take this to the Supreme Court, but it`s a loser. So the court of appeals basically said to \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , you got to play by the same rules that every other American does that if, you know, if you have a subpoena to testify before a grand jury, you can`t just blow it off. You got to actually do it.\par \par \uc2 And this has become in Georgia, a very serious criminal investigation maybe even more serious yesterday, because a federal judge in California, David Carter released some emails showing that Trump looked at sure looks like these emails show that Trump intentionally lied about the votes and vote counts in Georgia to a court under penalty of perjury.\par \par \uc2 So, the investigation serious and what \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has been trying to do is block and he`s afraid to go testify and tell the truth before a grand jury, and he`s asserting a constitutional privilege.\par \par \uc2 Now, I once wrote a Harvard Law Review article about that privilege it`s called the Speech or Debate Privilege. And I can tell you that, you know, it hasn`t been 200 years since the constitution of 1789 that has never been used once to say that an official act, which is what you need for speech or debate immunity is helping someone plot a coup, that`s just not within the constitutional protections.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s why he keeps losing and losing and losing. Again, he can try and bring this to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , but I don`t think it`s going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yamiche, every night we talk about January 6, we talk about the Mar- a-Lago investigation, and we do it from New York. You do it from Washington. But you`ve been talking to voters in Florida, where Mar-a-Lago is actually located. Are people in the state of Florida locally, are they talking about these investigations?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE: It`s an interesting question. I think if you`re a Democrat, who was already someone who was not going to be supporting Republicans, who are very frustrated with former President Trump, who, frankly, some voters tell me want to see him go to jail then you are paying attention to this.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re a Republican who`s looking at this, and sort of flustered, even if you`re frustrated with your party, they`re not really talking about these investigations at the top thing. They`re talking about inflation or abortion. I know some young Republican women have brought up abortion to me a number of times saying that they think their party has gone a step too far on that issue.\par \par \uc2 Of course, polls have shown that inflation is still the top concern for voters when you think about pocketbook issues. And people just surviving as someone who`s from Florida has a lot of family there. That is still I think the top issue for people, it`s how do I feed my family? How do I put food on the table? And also sort of what are the politics in DC and all of these places including (INAUDIBLE) how are they bettering my lives and for a lot of Floridian Democrats and Republicans they just don`t know what`s how their lives are being bettered by politics.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:07]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, there is the inflation reduction law, which is going to take time for people to see the results as well as the infrastructure law. They`re just not doing it overnight. Things don`t work that way.\par \par \uc2 Neal, new government evidence has surfaced today at the Oath Keepers trial. I know there are so many things going on at once. Text from a very frustrated Oath Keepers leader Stewart Rhoades urging his members to get violent because he thought Trump himself wasn`t doing enough to claim to power. At this point, how much more does the DOJ need to make their case?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: I haven`t been following too closely. But everything I`ve seen so far suggests they don`t need much, Steph. I think this prosecution has been going incredibly well, unlike say the prosecution was brought by \uc2 John Durham\uc2  which, you know, have fizzled, you know, tremendously but here I think it looks like a very serious well done prosecution.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, one more, one that surprised me today. And we have to ask you. Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , she rejected a request that would block the Biden college debt relief plan. That was a surprise to me, you?\par \par \uc2 KAYTAL: Not a surprise at all. This was a bogus lawsuit, Steph, every day of the week. I`m not surprised that Justice Barrett didn`t even bother showing it to her colleagues and just deciding it herself. And the reason is that this lawsuit was just a political grievance. It had no special legal interest, no legal claim.\par \par \uc2 The basic lawsuit was, hey, I`m a taxpayer and I don`t like how this government money is being spent, which is not a legal claim. I think it was the comedian Demetri Martin, who said and I`ll do this in a sanitized way, when someone begins a sentence as a taxpayer, I believe, you know, that person is going to be someone that you`re generally not going to like.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, there you go. Yamiche Alcndor, Katie Benner, \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 . I wanted to talk longer but in 40 minutes, Taylor Swift`s new album drops and I know Neal is positioned to download it, to listen to it, to dance to it, so we need to give him time to prepare. Ladies, thank you all. Neal, enjoy T. Swift.\par \par \uc2 And before we go to break, we`ve got to update you on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  saga. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  got a hold of internal documents outlining what Musk says he will do if and when he his takeover of the company goes through. And \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees cannot be sleeping well tonight.\par \par \uc2 The billionaire says he will fire almost 75 percent of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s workforce that is more than 5,000 jobs. The Post says that could potentially cripple \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s ability to combat misinformation, hate speech and spam.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reporting tonight that the Biden administration and its officials are considering national security reviews for some of Musk`s ventures including the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  deal and his Starlink satellite network now being used in Ukraine. So stay tuned. You know we`ll be covering it all.\par \par \uc2 When we come back from far-right conspiracy theorist without one single committee assignment to the nation`s top law enforcement official, some inner party are all for it. If things go their way, November 8. We`ve got two terrific political insiders standing by and we`re talking about somebody we don`t cover often here MTG herself.\par \par \uc2 And later, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  barely had time to unpack what the epic fail at 10 Downing Street means for Wall Street, Main Street and everything else here at home. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a very important Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:21]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCUS FLOWERS, GEORGIA DEMOCRAT RUNNING FOR CONGRESS: Did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  win the election, Congresswoman Greene?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 FLOWERS: Absolutely. But you pushed a big lie that said he did not win the election.\par \par \uc2 GREENE: There was election fraud proven.\par \par \uc2 FLOWERS: And you drove those people to the Capitol on January 6 with your lie.\par \par \uc2 GREENE: There was election fraud. There`s election fraud in 2022 election.\par \par \uc2 FLOWERS: We`re going to move on. Josh Roe (ph) it`s your turn to ask a question to Marjorie --\par \par \uc2 GREENE: And my husband has proof of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: She hadn`t seen frozen because Marjorie Taylor Greener -- Greene won`t let it go. And what`s more concerning neither will plenty other people in her party. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  points out in a lengthy profile of the Georgia Congresswoman writing this, every Republican candidate in her state and more across the country seem to be mimicking her, not mocking her, mimicking her.\par \par \uc2 With that in mind, we welcome back David Plouffe, former Obama campaign manager and senior adviser to the former president and Mark McKinnon, former adviser to both \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and \uc2 John McCain\uc2 . He`s one of the co- host of Showtime`s The Circus, which has him in Marjorie Taylor Green`s district this very week. So Mark, I must start with you. Spill it, what are people they`re telling you?\par \par \uc2 MARK MCKINNON, FORMER ADVISER TO \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  AND \uc2 JOHN MCCAIN\uc2 : They love \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  here. They -- Everybody you`ve talked to just, I mean, the more she is contentious, the more she gets in fights in Congress. I mean, that was one of the really interesting facts we found out is that when she got thrown off her committees, people saw that as affirmation of why they liked her.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s what they said. In their view they want her to fight, they wanted to break stuff. And they wanted to basically throw a big middle finger to Washington and that`s exactly what they`re doing. So, I think the interesting thing -- yes.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:02[\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Fight for what? You just said that she doesn`t have a single committee assignment. We`ve seen her on TV all day every day. I never talked about her on the show until now, because she has absolutely no policy influence. So what is it that people are backing? She does nothing that improves their lives?\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: Well, Stephanie, that`s about to change. And if the Republicans take over Congress, which is likely in the profile that you mentioned, the Robert Draper wrote, which is terrific, it`s very clear that that that she`s talking about leadership positions in the Republican Congress. She`s talking about getting an oversight position on the committee. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is talking about putting her into administration, if he`s elected.\par \par \uc2 The whole point of this is that people -- when she was knocked off the committee`s, you`re right. I mean, she wasn`t even taken seriously by most of her own party, but the party has moved to her now. And the party has become more like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and less like Kevin McCarthy. So the point is, the next Congress is going to be look a lot like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , and she`s got it -- she`s ascendant, and she is going to have a lot of power and influence. And she`s got to legislate -- she has a very clear legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, that`s the next Congress if Republicans take power. David, what does that tell you about where America is? Because these lawmakers just follow what voters want?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, FORMER OBAMA CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Well, Stephanie, it should gravely concern us because I don`t think we`re going to be out of the woods in terms of whether we`re going to remain a democracy until the Marjorie Taylor Greenes of the world, you know, begin to recede in the Republican Party to Mark`s point, I think they`re growing.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, a healthy percentage of the people who are going to vote in Republican primaries who donate, who volunteer are going to support people like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . And so that`s what should scare us, which is until Republicans running for House, Senate, governor,