sed to betting on markets and where markets move and they see voter markets moving towards very fringe part of the Republican Party. I mean, they`re looking at -- looking what`s happening with Keri Lake in Arizona, they`re looking at what happened with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And they`re -- these are people who rely on their business, and they make a lot of money off of power and influence. That`s what they care about at the end of the day. That`s how they make money. And so they`re looking to see - - who`s going to be in power, and they`re betting on.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But here`s why that doesn`t make sense. Because people who`ve spent their careers betting on markets, you have to bet on a market where you understand and you trust the framework and the rules. If you`re voting against the preservation of democracy, you could turn around and live in a world where there is no rule of law to these business people not realize that, none of them invest in Russia for that very reason.\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: Well, I`d love for you to get \uc2 Ron Lauder\uc2  on the show and ask him that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Me too. All right, David, we keep -- we`re keeping an eye on all these numbers all week. And these early voting numbers in Georgia are really big. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Well, it all depends on who they are, Stephanie. We`ll learn more about that as the experts dig into it. What you want to see in early but I`ll just as a Democrat. You want to see a lot of people that you weren`t sure were going to vote. Because if it`s just people who were going to vote on election day, but they vote early, it`s great. You get the vote in the bank in case something happens.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:00]\par \par \uc2 You want to see first time voters, younger voters, voters that don`t have a lot of history, voting in midterm elections. So, it`s the composition of early votes that matter. But my suspicion is I don`t know if we`re going to