r \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People complain about the gas. That`s the least of it. I mean, I can go two weeks without buying gas, but I can`t go two weeks without buying milk, bread. You know, things to eat at home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Feels like a recession to me. I remember the great recession. And that was a horrible time and I`m really dreading seeing that again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I agree. Everything costs more, ridiculously more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So here`s the thing, everything does cost more and when people go to the store, they feel it every day, but the buy administration has done a lot, right?\par \par \uc2 [23:40:00]\par \par \uc2 When you think about the Inflation Reduction Act, it`s going to lower health care costs, energy costs, it`s going to address climate change, it`s going to create better long term jobs and make the tax system more fair. We know the infrastructure bill is going to create good jobs, the big problem, none of these things happen overnight, and certainly not in the next 20 days. So how problematic is that?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: I think it`s very problematic for Biden`s political fortunes in these upcoming midterms just because they`re feeling the immediate hit. And they aren`t looking long term what he did today, going to Pennsylvania doing an event and touting rebuilding a bridge that was destroyed, that literally collapsed last year. He needs to be touting the work he`s doing and reminding people that this is a job, this is rebuilding, otherwise, you`re going to hear from voters who just simply are fed up, and they`re going to vote accordingly.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but what are they voting for? Because I haven`t heard one single policy proposal from any Republicans that are going to address any of those issues that those voters talked about. What are you going to do vote for a Republican and get social security reform? What are you going to get corporate tax cuts again? I mean, senior citizens who are facing higher prices, they`re going to get a Social Security adjustment next year up over 8 percent to account for inflation. Republicans love to cut that.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Something I heard this go round was so reminiscent of 2016. But you weren`t just hearing it from Democrats or Republicans. You heard it from both groups, to choice to the lesser of two evils. That was exactly what people said about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ad nauseam over and over and over, and it`s how they were describing their Senate choices.\par \par \uc2 Maybe they weren`t that happy with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . But they couldn`t see themselves voting for Dr. Oz. At the end of the day, what am I going to do? Maybe I`ll just -- I lean (ph) a little more Republican, and maybe I`ll go with them. Maybe I`ll decide that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has recovered accordingly. And I`m going to go ahead and vote that direction. It really is the sliver of just the 25 percent that are deciding the fate of democracy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But did it seem like voters who want to vote Republican know what they`re voting for? Or it`s just voting against what they don`t like what they have right now.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: A lot of voting against the current -- what they see is the current status quo, what they see as Biden policies that put a lot of money into the economy and cause the inflation. They see that. They see that and they see the spending the COVID releasing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did too, as coupling. The inflation message has been turned and pushed completely against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, then, Elise Jordan, thank you so much for joining us. Thank you for that important focus group. We always say we`re going to get out of New York and out of DC and get on the ground. And that is exactly what she did.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. She is now the shortest serving Prime Minister in British history by her resignation and her clearly unfavorable policies matter here at home. I`m talking about the UK and why it matters here. Republicans, they like a lot of those policies. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:39]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  announced her resignation today after just 44 days in office even her own party had lost confidence in her our own. Our Molly Hunter has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Truss`s downfall was swift after the markets rejected her sweeping tax cuts tanking the British pound, spiking mortgage rates. Inflation is now over 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We just can`t have this amount of turmoil.\par \par \uc2 UNIDETNIFIED MALE: The country is really, really, you know, the shambles at the moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m actually embarrassed to be British at the moment to be a conservative voter.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: And with a cold winter looming, the British people face a cost of living crisis many just worried about heating their homes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ve seen the effects of cost of living, you know, firsthand prices going up every day. It is incredibly unattainable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With us for more, Peter Spiegel, U.S. managing editor for The Financial Times. Peter, get us up to speed because \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  isn`t someone we talk about often here.\par \par \uc2 PETER SPIEGEL, THE FINANCIAL TIMES U.S. MANAGING EDITOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Just yesterday she said she was in it. She was fighting. And then today she said I`m out.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Yes, I mean, literally, you could see the meltdown happening minute by minute, hour by hour. But she was -- she seemed to what she said that I`m not quitting in the commons during the Prime Minister`s questions. She kind of had saved the ship and then one by one. All her senior aides, minister of the Home Secretary announced her resignation. She couldn`t get the votes in parliament for competence vote. She just from day one, and that`s his economic plan, that really saw the British economy melt down.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This is why people here should care.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s her economic plan that sent to everyone running for the hills. And why that should matter here is there`s a whole lot of Republicans who love that economic plan. I want to share it. It was just a couple of weeks ago, \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  was applauding her plans on Fox News. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FORMER DIRECTOR OF THE NATIONAL ECONOMIC COUNCIL: The new British Prime Minister Liz truss has laid out a terrific supply side economic growth plan, which looks a lot like the basic thrust of Kevin McCarthy`s commitment to America plan. Let`s start with trust. She is slashing tax rates and deregulating energy. I just love it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He just loves it. That`s Trump`s former economic adviser saying her plans a lot like Kevin McCarthy`s. Should we be worried?\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Yes, look, they`re two very different economies. Right? I mean, The U.S. is an economy that relies very much on a domestic market.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:04]\par \par \uc2 UK is a trading nation, it needs the world to -- particularly debt, it needs finances debt. That said, well we know about the global economy right now is there`s never been higher debt levels in corporations and governments. Once you start seeing interest rates rise, and people can`t pay off the debt.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: And that`s what we`re running into, suddenly, things begin to collapse. And that`s what happened to the years of crisis, that when the great financial crisis is over leveraged in the global economy. Now, the problem in the UK, particularly --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let me just tell the audience --\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: -- when it`s cheap to borrow, when rates are at zero, it`s what everybody does. It`s why we buy houses. It`s why we start businesses. And when they raise rates to slow the economy, it makes it a hell of a lot more expensive for these companies to operate.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: And as that famous Warren Buffet line that no one knows who`s swimming naked until the water goes out. And so what happened in Britain is suddenly --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And it`s never anyone with a good body.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: It`s true.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Just to tell you that. Never. Never is.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Absolutely true. But what happened in Britain is suddenly the race start raising and oh my gosh, there`s these over leveraged pockets and pension funds that we didn`t know existed before. And the markets begin to freak out. Because where else are these unknown bits of leverage. And even in the U.S., people started looking, can we see similar things happening in the U.S. in terms of little pockets of danger that we don`t know about. Because that`s what caused the great financial crisis, right? These leverage, these, you know, collateralized debt obligations they didn`t know they were in the system. And that`s what the danger is.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s go back to the UK because people are applauding this saying they made a swift move. She was terrible. She`s out. But we don`t know what comes next.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So what chaos can ensue? And why is it so important to us? I mean, the UK they`re very important ally.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: This is the issue which I think Americans need to pay attention to. Britain`s -- Brits had been really distracted for almost a decade because of Brexit. And I remember I was in Brussels at the time and a very senior American official came (ph). The first Ukraine crisis 2014, and almost was said the Brits are irrelevant. They`re so distracted. An American said is about our most important ally. They look to Merkel, they looked at Germany in the first Ukraine crisis.\par \par \uc2 What was happening in the last six months or so is (INAUDIBLE) the Brits were back, right. \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  started. \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  took it on. They were really the backbone for the Europeans on Ukraine. Now you have the Europeans beginning to waver a bit. You have a new Italian government that is quasi pro Putin. You have the French and the Germans fighting. The Brits were the ones that were backing the Americans on. And suddenly, we are going to have weeks if not months of inward looking chaos.\par \par \uc2 So when the Americans go and try to rally the Europeans to standby Ukraine, their most important ally in our tradition ally for a century is now distracted and irrelevant. And that`s very dangerous for the Biden administration. They`re losing allies in this fight. And I think that the coalition that`s held together so well since February. We got to watch that --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It has been extraordinary. Before we go, I do want to ask you, the State Department says that the Iranian military was on the ground in Crimea, helping the Russian military conduct strikes with Iranian drones. How concerned should we be?\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Well, very concerned. I mean, for the first (INAUDIBLE) with you the fact the Russians have to rely on the Iranians for drones tells you all you need to know about the state the Russian military right now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Bingo. And their economy.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: And their economy. I mean, they don`t have the munitions anymore. But it`s interesting, it has begun to change the calculus, you know, in Israel in particular. The Israelis have been very standoffish with Ukrainians. They were catering for the Russians a big Russian population in Israel, suddenly the Israelis have said, we have defensive systems, air systems, we are not giving to the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 So I think a lot of the calculations with American allies who may have been less prone to take a stand against the Russians now when they see the Iranians coming in, they`re going to be much more pro-American in this regard I think.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Peter Spiegel, I love it when you come on the show.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He`s so smart and dynamic and entertaining. Great to see you. Thank you for being here. Coming up. The power of purple, not just because it`s a royal color but the very important reason to wear it. Tonight, the support of the LGBTQ youth today and always. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:23]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARAH KATE ELLIS\uc2 , GLAAD PRESIDENT AND CEO: Thank you for wearing purple today. I always say it`s a symbol with significance for LGBTQ youth. When you think about where youth are today, they`re online. So they go there to find community and what they find is harassment. And so today we wear purple to let them know that we love them. We see them and that we`re out here fighting for them and for a better world for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, Spirit Day, many Americans, myself included are wearing purple this evening to show support for LGBTQ youth and to take a stand against bullying. Even the \uc2 Empire State Building\uc2  was lit up in purple last night to send this very important message. And that message needs to get out there because look at these troubling statistics the \uc2 GLAAD\uc2  organization collected about what LGBTQ students are facing right now.\par \par \uc2 About 76 percent report being verbally harassed. 72 percent report hearing homophobic remarks from teachers and or school staff because of their gender expression. And 86 percent said that recent politics have negatively impacted their overall well-being.\par \par \uc2 How could it not? \uc2 GLAAD\uc2  points out that since the start of 2022, think about all the problems we have in this country, but since the start of 2022, lawmakers have proposed 325 separate anti-LGBTQ bills. 130 of them target the rights of transgender people, especially youth.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:00]\par \par \uc2 This map on your screen from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  shows the states that have passed anti-trans laws since 2018. Tennessee has the most with a total of seven laws passed. Tennessee which ranks 41st in terms of poverty in this country. What are they addressing? Anti-trans bills, that`s their priority. And what is the most distressing is that more than half of transgender youth in this country have seriously considered suicide?\par \par \uc2 That right there is why showing up for these vulnerable young people is so important. I want them to know, I want you to know that you are not alone. We love you. And we will always support you. Remember, if you need help, ask for it. And if you`re in a position to give help, please give it.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us and I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221021eiak00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Wilfred Frost, Peter Baker, Ellison Barber, Ali  Vitali, Glenn Kirschner, Carlos Curbelo, Josh Lederman \par \uc2 9,001 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the question is what`s next? After a turbulent six weeks for British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  to resign, making her the shortest-serving PM in history. With one of our closest allies facing a new level of political chaos on top of the worst economic disaster in a generation, what`s at stake for a new prime minister, and what about Britain`s impact on the global economy or its support for Ukraine? We`ll go live to London and check in on reaction here in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a federal judge reveals that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have provided a paper trail leading to crimes of obstruction and conspiracy. Coming up, the lies Trump swore were true even when his attorney John Eastman told him that wasn`t the case, and whether that could prove once and for all that the Big Lie was a crime. And finally, if you`ve stopped worrying about COVID, this winter could come as a rude awakening. We`ll tell you about the new variants out there and whether so-called COVID immunity can stop them.\par \par \uc2 But we start overseas were \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  came into office just 44 days ago, promising to help turn the British economy around and provide some relief for millions who were struggling. That didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ TRUSS, OUTGOING UK PRIME MINISTER: I came into office at a time of great economic and international instability. Our country has been held back for too long by low economic growth. I was elected by the Conservative Party with a mandate to change this. I recognize though, given this situation, I cannot deliver the mandate on which I was elected by the Conservative Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In fact, her new economic policies backfire. The economic reality is going from bad to worse. Inflation in the UK is at a 40-year high. Food prices alone are up 14 percent. Now a new level of global unease about the world economy is raising questions about what it could mean for the U.S. and maybe even the midterms next month.\par \par \uc2 In a minute, we`ll talk to the New York Times Peter Baker about that. But first, let`s go to London and Sky News anchor and MSNBC contributor Wilfred Frost. Wilf, Truss is out, \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  apparently wants his job back. I mean, it`s wild but also incredibly consequential. So, what`s next?\par \par \uc2 WILFRED FROST, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, Chris, we did just learn the process of how the next prime minister will be chosen, and three key points to it. The first is that it will be a quick process. A new prime minister in place by next Friday, that`s because the country needs stability, but also because the Conservative Party hopes that MPs will fall behind a single unity candidate, wishful thinking there, of course. The second key point is that the threshold to get on the shortlist is very high. You need one hundred nominations from MPS. There`s only about 370 in total by Monday at 2:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 And the final key point, once down to a final two, it will be the party members across the country, about 200,000 people that will make the final choice. They have to do so online, given the short timescale. The fact that there`s a high initial threshold is bad for \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . The fact that the final choice is amongst that 200,000 membership base is good for \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . We will see over the course of the weekend who are those final two or three candidates. Nominations close by Monday at 2:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 I think the key point here is that it`s not going to the public. There won`t be a general election. And that is perhaps the only thing delivering unity amongst the Conservative MPs at the moment. They desperately don`t want to go to a general election whilst they are so unpopular in the polls. The bottom line, by next Friday, we will have three prime ministers in less than two months, the latest one with even less of a mandate than her -- his predecessor -- his or her predecessor, excuse me, and with it a diminishing image of the country on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wilfred Frost, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Peter Baker, so the president was asked about this a couple of minutes ago actually, as he was heading out on Air Force One. He said he doesn`t think the political and economic turmoil in the UK will have a big impact on us. But if it adds to global unease, it certainly can help. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it certainly can`t help. And obviously, the United States right now is depending on Britain as an ally in this war in Ukraine that we`re in the middle of against Russia. You know Britain has been a key ally of course in helping to support the Ukrainians, supply the Ukrainians, stand up against the Russians in terms of economic sanctions, they`re about to impose strict -- stricter sanctions on Russian oil in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s something that`s going to be difficult for all of Europe and in which Britain needs to play an important role. So it`s certainly not something welcome at the White House this is uncertainty and instability in Britain, even if \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  would have necessarily been Biden`s personal choice for prime minister there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Peter, stick with me. I mean, Britain is far from alone in its worries about economic uncertainty. And here that feeling may be having a real impact on Democrats` midterm prospects. Here`s how the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  put it. Democrats feared Red October has arrived. Read of it, obviously, for Republicans, candidates who once led in places like Nevada and Wisconsin are now trailing. And of course, there`s this tight race in Georgia for the Senate. That`s where NBC`s Ellison Barber is. She joins us along with Adrienne Elrod, a Democratic strategist and former senior aide with the Biden-Harris campaign.\par \par \uc2 So, Peter, I want to go to that Red October story in your paper where John Halpin of the \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2  ties what we`re seeing in the UK to hear. Let me quote him. "Inflation is a political wrecking ball for incumbent governments around the world. Why should the United States be different?" I mean, look, Democrats are certainly working it in the closing days of the campaign, President Biden heading to Pennsylvania for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , \uc2 Oprah Winfrey\uc2  for Georgia gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , but how much has the momentum shifted?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, I should say rather strikingly, of course, the Democrats had seemed a little bit wind in their back, as of just a few weeks ago, gas prices were coming down, the energy over the abortion rights issue seemed to be you know, lifting a number of democratic votes. Unemployment remains really low, and that`s something that traditionally has been helpful to an incumbent party. But at this stage, inflation is stubborn. High numbers are really, really biting into a lot of pocketbooks, a lot of American families, and while they may be concerned about other issues, like abortion rights, like immigration, like crime, inflation seems to be number one on the -- on the minds of many voters.\par \par \uc2 And you`re right that there are races where Democrats thought they were comfortably ahead that are suddenly looking more competitive, not just in Nevada, but even in Colorado, even Washington State, a traditionally very safe Democratic seat, you`re hearing some noises there. And so the momentum a few weeks ago is gone and seems to be, you know, heading into a tough, tough slog in these final few weeks, which many, many races are tightening. And really, we don`t know where it`s going to end up, but certainly not looking as good for the Democrats as they had hoped.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which brings us all of those concerns to Ellison This is obviously the first week of early voting in Georgia. Talking to voters, are they telling you that inflation, rising gas prices, those things are impacting the way they`re voting?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, they aren`t. I mean, it`s interesting because we`ve been talking to voters in Georgia on and off for the last year. And in recent weeks, the last month -- month and a half, we`ve increasingly heard voters regardless of their political affiliation, talk about inflation, talk about their concerns as it relates to gas prices. It`s not the only issue that they`re focused on, but it is a very big one. Here`s some of what we`ve heard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: An issue because since I live in Guyana and drive all the way to Decatur, you know, it`s a lot of gas, a lot of sitting in traffic, and I think it affects, especially teachers because we really haven`t seen that price adjustment for our cost of living. So it just makes it a little bit more difficult.\par \par \uc2 KAREN GILCHRIST, WALKER SUPPORTER: I`ve already voted, and it did affect the way that I`ve cast my ballot. And it`s very, very important that gas prices along with the inflation and securing the border, those are all very hot topics right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And the candidates know this is an issue on voters` minds, at least in the Georgia Senate race. We`re hearing them talk about it a lot on the campaign trail. We heard incumbent Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  talking about it at an event in Atlanta last night. He talked about it in the context of the work he has done in Washington. He emphasized the need for Washington and his efforts around this to do more to hold oil companies responsible. He said to supporters at that event last night, that those companies are seeing record profits while people are seeing record prices and that something needs to change and that he`s working on it in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 We heard Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  talk about it at an event here in Macon this morning. He says that the cost of gas, the rising prices that people are seeing at the pump and the grocery store are solely because of the policies of the Biden administration, an administration, and a president that he has closely tried to connect Senator Warnock to. And that`s the message that he is trying to deliver home to voters here. Again, both candidates seem to know that this really is something impacting Georgia voters. And we keep hearing them say that it is impacting in factoring into the way they vote, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Adrienne, it`s a harder argument to sell, isn`t it? Well, we`re working on it. Here`s all the stuff that we`ve done. It`s going to get better than -- look, the folks who are in there haven`t done a very good job, oh, and by the way, what you`re going to hear on all the talk radio, all the TV stations is what`s happening in Britain. There`s a sense of global economic unease, which can add to people feeling uneasy, generally. So, what do the Democrats do with the clock ticking?\par \par \uc2 (AUDIO GAP)\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Oh, sorry. Sorry about that. I think I was on mute. I think it`s important to run an offensive and defensive policy on this. You know, for one, yes, Democrats have a lot to talk about when it comes to what we`ve done for the economy. The Inflation Reduction Act putting money in people`s pockets. Also, of course, stressing the fact that this has been a once-in-a-lifetime pandemic that we`ve been has caused inflationary numbers, not just to rise here in the United States, but throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, though, Democrats have a lot to talk about when it comes to economic accomplishments. You all just talked about those record-low unemployment rates -- record job growth under President Biden. Again, I realize that those are not always the things that people are feeling at their kitchen table at night. But we have - but we have to, as Democrats stress what we`ve done, the positive accomplishments we -- accomplishments we made.\par \par \uc2 You also, Chris, will look at the closing ads that Democrats are making across the country, a lot of those closing ads are on abortion, which means it`s still an issue. That`s incredibly important to voters, especially those suburban swing voters who will ultimately decide what happens. So I think, again, you kind of sort of run an offensive and defensive strategy on the economy if you`re a Democrat. But you also have to draw the contrast between what life would be like with Republicans having full control of the House and Senate, and where we are now with Democrats having control.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, I don`t think there`s any question about two things. One is that the importance of abortion if you look at the polls is less than it was in the immediate aftermath of Dobbs, right? On the other hand, if you look at the breakdown, it remains very important to Democratic voters, particularly remains very important to women voters.\par \par \uc2 So, I guess it becomes a question of, can that sort of message mobilize voters who might otherwise be on the fence? I know you`ve got your finger on the pulse of a lot of races around the country, what are you hearing about organizing on the ground, especially in these tight races? I know we say it all the time. It`s all about turnout, turnout, turnout. But when you`re looking at polls that were within the margin of error, it`s about turnout.\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes. That`s right, Chris. And I think we`re going to see a high turnout on both sides. And it used to be that we lagged turnout in the off- year of "midterm elections." That has changed. That certainly changed in 2018. I think it`s going to change in 2022.\par \par \uc2 And again, yes, the economy is an issue when we talk to most voters. That is something that they cite as a top one or two issues. But the wildcard card here that we have is abortion. And the fact that if you are a swing voter, even a moderate Democrat -- I mean, a moderate Republican, looking at the construct of the Supreme Court, what we have to look forward to in Washington, all you have to look forward to is a six-three Supreme Court for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 So you know that the only way that you can actually drive some sort of change in impact is to vote Democratic in your state. And I do think it`s hard to sort of see that in the polling right now. But I do think ultimately, that motivation is going to be in favor of the Democrats. I don`t know if that means we`re going to hold on to the House and Senate, but I do think it means we`re going to mitigate some of the losses that we might normally have in this type of cycle.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Adrienne Elrod, Peter Baker, Ellison Barber, good to have all of you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  will sit down exclusively with President Biden to talk about the midterm elections, to talk about the economy, much more. That`s Friday on "THE REIDOUT" at 7:00 p.m. Eastern. More of that interview will then air on the Sunday show, Sunday at 10:00 a.m. Eastern only here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 So just how devastating is new evidence against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Evidence in writing, the legal implications of what a judge is now saying. And how could the former president`s legal issues influence potential GOP presidential candidates, as \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  gives new clues about whether he might run in 2024?\par \par \uc2 And the new variants of COVID worrying U.S. and international health immunity experts, it`s all evading these -- all these immunity evading variants that have led to a surge in cases in New York. Might that spread to the rest of the country? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Newly revealed e-mails today show a paper trail that appears to prove \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew he was lying when he spread claims of voter fraud in Georgia just days before the January 6 attacks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: 10,315 ballots were cast by individuals whose name and date of birth matches a Georgia resident who died in 2020 prior to the election, and your wacky Secretary of State said two people -- two people. Now I don`t know how many people are on that list, but it`s a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So here`s the problem. Those e-mails from former Trump Attorney John Eastman show Trump was informed weeks before that those numbers you just heard him put out there were wrong. And yet, Trump signed legal papers swearing that they were true. Now a federal judge says those e-mails could end up being key evidence that Trump knowingly committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, and former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner. Glenn, we know Trump is famous for not leaving a paper trail, putting his names on things, he`s not an e-mail guy, this looks like an exception, how big a deal could it be?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, it`s a pretty big deal, Chris. Because you have sort of questions about the quantity of evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed crimes, I would argue as a former career prosecutor, there is an abundance of evidence that we`ve seen that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed any number of crimes in his determination to retain the office of the presidency. Then you have the quality of evidence. And I don`t think we have seen evidence of this quality before.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  signed a certificate and filed it with the court and Judge David Carter found, he ruled that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew it contained false information about allegations of election fraud when he filed it. I can`t say it any better than judge Carter said it. He said that the court finds these e-mails are sufficiently related to and in furtherance of a conspiracy to defraud the United States. That is basically conclusive evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ali, the reason these documents came up was because John Eastman, the lawyer, didn`t want to hand them over to the January 6 committee. Now he`s going to have to. So how does this fit into their work?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, you`ve got to imagine that they want to get their hands on these e-mails as quickly as they can because the committee has been clear about the fact that they`re on the clock. They always have been. But especially now that they`re done with the public phase of their investigative hearings, they`ve turned their attention squarely to writing that final report that they need to put out before the last day of this year.\par \par \uc2 These e-mails, especially for all the reasons that you and Glenn have laid out, speak not just to what Eastman was doing. And of course, they`ve had a lot of focus on him as one of the key lawyers in the false elector schemes, and in other prongs of their investigation into what led to January 6, but also because they speak to things that the former president may have known. Things like the defrauding that he was trying to do, despite the fact that he knew the fraud claims in Georgia that he was touting were fake, that he was doing them anyway. All of those things speak to the mindset of the former president, something that the committee has worked really hard over the course of many hearings, to try to establish publicly his intent, his motive, what he knew, and what he did in spite of what he knew.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In the meantime, Glenn, there`s this article in \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  that says there is a team of prosecutors at the DOJ who believe there is enough evidence to charge Trump but they haven`t made a formal recommendation yet. We`ve speculated a lot that they`ve been waiting for the midterms, but what else might they be waiting for?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, I wouldn`t read too much into this, Chris. I mean, it`s nice to know that prosecutors who are hard at work investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , part of the belief that there is enough evidence to charge him, but you know what, there`s this -- there`s still a long way to go because that will only be a recommendation from the prosecutors, and it will have to go through the Deputy Attorney General Lisa Monaco, and it will ultimately be made that decision by Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . And we know he has sort of famously tried to rebuild the credibility and the legitimacy of the Department of Justice. So the one thing I think we can be sure of is we will see no charges before the midterms, but I do believe shortly after the midterms if we don`t begin to see indictments, dropping, that may be an indication that it wasn`t just about the midterms that was causing a delay. It`s more about the quality or quantity of the evidence that they have implicating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in crimes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Interesting. OK, Ali, meantime, we`ve been waiting for the January 6 committee to serve \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a subpoena. And I know you`ve got new reporting on the potential timing of that. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, look, it could be any moment now or the timing could continue to slide. Initially, our expectation was that they were going to issue this subpoena in the early half of this week, obviously, here we are on Thursday, the committee huddling at noon today to try to talk about what the steps forward would be, including trying to hammer out some last details on the date that these records in a deposition could be due. We think that it`s likely to be after the midterms but that`s something that they`re still trying to dot the I`s and cross the T`s on, and actively talking about as we wait for this subpoena to come.\par \par \uc2 But look, there`s also a view by some of the people that I talked to that it doesn`t necessarily even matter because the expectation level that Trump won`t try to draw this out is so low. We`ve often talked about the former president as someone who knows that he has to run out the clock and who is deft at doing that. Certainly, in this case, it`s a moment where if you just look at the weeks on the calendar, it`s not that hard to do.\par \par \uc2 The fact that we`re in mid to late October right now and this committee subpoena runs out at the end of the year, is the fact that we know that Trump could tie this up, could draw this out, but nevertheless, the committee still felt that subpoena hanging it -- subpoenaing him and putting that on the record was important enough that they are moving forward with that. It`s just a matter of them actually officially issuing it at this point, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, Glenn Kirschner, great to have both of you here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Department of Justice says it needs millions of dollars to keep the January 6 probe going. And now new NBC News reporting on whether they`ll get the funds they need to "bring the January 6 rioters to justice." Let me bring in NBC`s Sahil Kapur, co-author of that report. And you`re right, Sahil, that that next congressional spending bill might be the last chance for this investigation to continue. What are you hearing on the Hill?\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, Chris, it`s not exactly a priority for appropriators at the moment but that seems largely because the issue has not broken through on Capitol Hill. Yes, the Justice Department has said $34 million are critically needed to continue its criminal investigation of January 6 to bring the perpetrators to justice, but a lot of appropriators -- for a lot of them it hasn`t really made an impression.\par \par \uc2 Several senior appropriators in the House in particular have said that funding remains up in the air. Some of them were even surprised when I mentioned that the Justice Department says they need this money to continue the investigation. Some of them didn`t seem to know that the Justice Department was strapped for cash on this.\par \par \uc2 And all eyes at the moment are on that big government funding bill that is expected to pass by the December 16 deadline. Negotiators are expected to return to the drawing board after the election on November 8. And this could be a do-or-die moment for DOJ to get that money because currently, Democrats control the House and the Senate. They are more sympathetic to this. But if Republicans capture control of the House of Representatives, as they are currently favored to do, the chamber would be run by a staunch Trump allies who have been less sympathetic to this investigation. So that adds this element of urgency, you know, to include the funding in this bill.\par \par \uc2 And it`s also worth being clear, Chris, that DOJ is not saying that if it doesn`t get this money, they will have to end the investigation entirely and all the -- you know, the rioters go free. What they`re saying is that this has forced, you know, resources to be moved off of other vital law enforcement activities from U.S. Attorney`s offices all over the country. And what the DOJ is saying to Congress is please don`t put us in that position, don`t force us to choose whether to continue the January 6 investigation or other law enforcement matters, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Sahil Kapur, thank you for that renewed reporting. Trump`s former VP opening up about a potential 2024 presidential run. Is all the legal pressure on the former president forcing other GOP people to consider running? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:15]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As Donald Trump`s mounting legal pressure potentially threatens a 2024 comeback, the clock is ticking on any ambitious Republicans who might be waiting in the wings.\par \par \uc2 Will increasing legal jeopardy for Trump mean more GOP presidential candidates?\par \par \uc2 Well, here`s an amused \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  yesterday when asked whether he would support a Trump 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: Well, there might be somebody else I`d prefer more.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: You know, what I can tell you is, I have every confidence that the Republican Party is going to sort out leadership. All my focus has been on the midterm elections, and it will stay that way for the next 20 days. But after that, we`ll be thinking about the future, ours and the nation`s, and I`ll keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For more, let`s bring in \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , a former Republican congressman and an NBC News political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you. So, look, if you`re a Republican who`s thinking about running for president, even if Trump is never charged with anything, does it make him weaker and political opponents more likely to get in the more we hear about these unfolding investigations?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2 , NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Chris, that`s the interesting thing because as strong as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  remains in the Republican Party, he`s still the dominant force, the most popular figure, a lot of these presidential hopefuls are starting to look past \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And you have people like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who`s clearly positioning himself to run or at least to be a relevant figure in the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 You have \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  here in Florida, who is campaigning for re-election without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by his side. So, people are really looking past \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , even though he still gets all the attention in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, you make a good point because on one hand you see some of those potential candidates, like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , trying to distance themselves from Trump, at least like physically, right? But then they go out and they campaign for election deniers. Is this a kind of embrace his base but not him strategy?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Yes, this is the careful balance, let`s call it that way to be kind, that a lot of Republicans try to reach these days. And that`s why some people think that we might even see someone who doesn`t come from this paradigm, right, this pro-Trump versus anti-Trump world emerge and become a leading figure. A lot of people think we`ll actually see that in both parties, that the two nominees in 2024 will be people who aren`t even in the headlines today.\par \par \uc2 Another Floridian down here, Miami Mayor \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2 , who`s a Republican, a conservative, but doesn`t fall neatly into this pro-Trump, anti-Trump world. He`s starting to make some noise. So someone like that who can run on a record and isn`t tied to all the noise and the mess of the last three, four, five years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, that leave me to the question, could it be somebody who is not political? I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never ran for anything and he became president. We`ve seen other folks -- well, I mean, obviously you had \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  became governor of California before becoming president. You saw Arnold Schwarzenegger. Could we see a non-politician mount a serious campaign? And if they are thinking about it, do they got to start soon?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Certainly we`re living in times, Chris, where the traditional paths to the presidency really don`t exist anymore. And if they do, there are certainly other paths. And we can certainly see celebrity types like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  try to run for president. We can see business people. You know, obviously \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2  tried to do this in the Democratic Party last time.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 But certainly we could see that dynamic in the Republican lane as well, someone from the business world who says none of these politicians know what they`re doing. We have to reset. There`s really a fertile environment, fertile ground for all these new, different, and creative pathways to the presidency. And I think there`s a good chance something like that could happen.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Former Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , always good to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re also watching live pictures out of Pittsburgh. President Biden is set to speak there next hour. He`s going to be talking about rebuilding America`s infrastructure. He`s there for Democratic Senate candidate, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . At least that he`s among those at the event.\par \par \uc2 Wow, look at that. This is a guy we`re used to seeing in a hoody but obviously the president shows up and he put on a jacket and a tie. So he`s going to have a reception (INAUDIBLE) for Fetterman later tonight in Philadelphia. And we`ll have more on this coming up as we keep our eye on what is happening in Pittsburgh. Obviously Fetterman hoping Biden can help push him over the top there.\par \par \uc2 Overseas, thousands of Ukrainians forced to flee a key region in the south that could very soon become that war`s next battleground. A former U.S. ambassador to Russia joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:22]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thousands of Ukrainians are being forced to flee the Kherson region in southern Ukraine, amid growing fears it will be the next battleground in Russia`s brutal war in Ukraine. People there being ordered to leave by Russian installed leaders.\par \par \uc2 Check out this Telegram post from a pro-Russian leader warning the battle will begin in the very near future.\par \par \uc2 Those evacuations are an apparent reaction to Ukraine`s successful recapturing of villages and farmland in that region. And it comes just hours after Russian President Putin once again tightened his grip, declaring martial law in four illegally annexed regions of Ukraine, including Kherson.\par \par \uc2 Michael McFaul is the former U.S. ambassador to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Always good to have you here. So martial law has now been declared in those regions. You`ve negotiated face to face with Putin. I`m sorry, I apologize. We have to go to Pennsylvania. President Biden, who is there, let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Gov, since you don`t have a coat on, this is going to be painfully short. I`m not going to keep you too long.\par \par \uc2 It`s great -- thanks for that introduction. I really appreciate it, Greg. And, you know -- and thanks to the elected officials here today. Governor Wolf, one of the best governors I`ve ever served with in my life.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: One of my best buddies from Scranton, as we say. Bobby Casey, I know his dad and his mom is still around. And Representative Doyle, which -- how about this one? Is this working?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, that`s working.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I tell you what, I knew he`d come here and do this.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, I`m joking. Can we get -- there you go. All right.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And Lieutenant Governor --\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE DOYLE (D-PA): Just as you were saying something nice about me.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I was saying something nice about you. Jay, that`s why I went out. But I`m saying we`re going to try like the devil to keep you from having to -- well, not having to, deciding to leave. I wish you didn`t. And then, John, thank you very much for running. I really do appreciate it. And, Giselle, you`re going to be a great lady in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As I`ve said before that America is the only country in the world that comes out of crises stronger than we went into the crisis. And that`s the story I want to tell here today, briefly. Just nine months ago, January 28th in 2022, I was about to leave the White House on my way to Mill 19 here in Pittsburgh to talk about the resurgence of American manufacturing. And by the way, we`ve created 700,000 manufacturing jobs since then.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Before I left Washington, I got word about what happened earlier in that day. At 6:45 on a snowy day, the bridge behind me collapsed, 100 feet straight down to Fern Hollow. And five cars and a bus were crossing the bridge at the time. Several people were injured. But by the grace of God, the school was delayed that day because of snow and it was just before rush hour, so there was less traffic than usual. It had been a normal -- had it been a normal day, it would`ve been much, much worse.\par \par \uc2 I decided still to come to Pittsburgh. I came straight here from the airport. When I arrived, Mayor Gainey, who is not here right now, was here. And Bobby and Conor and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  were all here, along with the responders. You all told me about the emergency workers who had pulled the survivors to safety. They were heroes that day, and a complete catastrophe was avoided. But it never should`ve come to this.\par \par \uc2 For too long, we talked about building the best economy in the world and the best infrastructure in the world. We talked about asserting America`s leadership. We (INAUDIBLE) about the best and the safest roads, railroads, ports, airports, and so much more. But now, now, we didn`t do it, but we`re finally getting to it. We`re getting it done. We`re finally deciding that instead of being ranked number 13 in the world, 13 in the world -- the United States, 13 in the world in infrastructure, we should be ranked number one.\par \par \uc2 So, instead, instead of Infrastructure Week, which was a punchline for four years under my predecessor, it`s Infrastructure Decade, a headline on my watch. And with the help of your members of Congress here today, I signed into law a once-in-a-generation investment in roads, highways, bridges, railroads, ports, airports, and so much more.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 Over a billion two hundred -- a trillion and two hundred billion dollar. It`s called the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, and it`s the most significant investment in American infrastructure in roads, bridges, et cetera, than Eisenhower`s Interstate Highway system.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania alone has already received $5.2 billion just this year for hundreds of projects across the commonwealth. And that`s just announced another $2.5 billion to fix and upgrade Pennsylvania`s roads and bridges, and there`ll be billions more for other projects.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you why this matters. When I was here in January, I told the governor that we`d help and we`d be help rebuild this bridge behind us as fast as we possibly could. That day, the governor moved and signed an emergency order that let us move separate federal funding as quickly as possible to the project. The reason we could do that is because I had just signed the Infrastructure Law a few months earlier. But had we waited for that money, it would have taken longer.\par \par \uc2 The governor knew he could count on the new law to fund other projects to ensure the projects he already had started for infrastructure in the state would not be slowed down in the process. The result, Pennsylvania has been able to repair Fern Hollow Bridge in less than a year. And by Christmas, God willing, we`ll be walking -- I`m coming back to walk over this sucker. Although my staff said to me, "Do you realize how many times you`ve been to Pittsburgh?" I said, "No." He said, "Nineteen."\par \par \uc2 But, Bobby, don`t tell them in Scranton. Don`t tell them in Scranton. I -- anyway. But we -- it`s being done in record time. Normally you`d be looking at two to five years to build a bridge like this, literally. And the total project costs $25 million. Fully paid for by the federal government. And, by the way, and, by the way, in the process of that, not a single penny of that money has cost anybody making under 400 grand in the nation to pay a penny more in taxes.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, in the process, we also cut the deficit in this process nationally by a trillion four hundred billion dollars just this year.\par \par \uc2 So, Gov, the fact is it wouldn`t have happened without you, Gov. It wouldn`t have -- no, it wouldn`t have happened in this town. That`s not hyperbole. That`s a fact. And you`re working so hard to get it done. I want to thank you. This really matters.\par \par \uc2 Folks, folks in the neighborhood relied on this bridge and the walking path underneath to commute to work, to take their kids to school, to run errands, to stay connected to other neighborhoods like Squirrel Hill and Waterfront and Oakland. And below the bridge, Frick Park is going to be fixed up when this is done. It`s better than it was, hiking paths, new seating areas.\par \par \uc2 This bridge was built initially in 1901. It was rebuilt in 1970. These guys behind me know that. None of them were here then, I don`t think. But the fact of the matter is now it`s a modern bridge of the 21st century with four lanes for vehicle traffic, a path for cyclists and pedestrians. This project has supported over a hundred jobs, good-paying construction, union jobs. Laborers, carpenters, cement mason. So many more.\par \par \uc2 The contractor delivering this project, by the way, has been a company that`s been owned by the same family for four generations, a union company since 1930s that built our roads and our bridges, building during the New Deal, during Eisenhower`s Interstate Highway System, and now the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law. But, you know, this is -- one bridge also tells a broader story. There are nearly 45,000 -- say it again, 45,000 bridges across this country in poor condition. In Pennsylvania alone, there are 3,100 bridges and 7,500 miles of highway in poor condition.\par \par \uc2 And as I was talking to your person, Gov, also, we`re going to get the railroad going, too. I`m a rail guy. Anyway. Pittsburgh is the city of bridges, but too many of them are in poor condition like this bridge behind me before it collapsed. But with the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, we`re doing something about it. This is just one of 2400 bridges across the country that are being repaired just this year because of this law.\par \par \uc2 For example, the Allegheny County work is underway to repair the bridges of the Boulevard of the Allies. Not just bridges, though.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:00]\par \par \uc2 Just outside of Pittsburgh, we`re investing $850 million to replace and expand a 100-year-old Montgomery Locks and Dam that`s critical to the nation`s economy. And I`m -- we`re investing, that investment is going to increase the capacity of this system, the Locks, over 50 percent at a critical choke point in the river.\par \par \uc2 That means we`re going to be able to move an additional 14,000 barges every year, saving the equivalent of having to have one million hauls on tractor trailers to get the same material to where it has to go. These savings are going to allow the locks to be more efficient and move 10 billion tons of goods like coal, petroleum, and steel. As a consequence, we`re lowering the cost of transporting these materials, but just as important, just as important, we`re going to reduce emissions that can come from the trucks and trains hauling it over -- by reducing it by 3.5 million tons of CO2 a year.\par \par \uc2 Three point -- cutting pollution in the air we breathe. This is good for the economy and the environment and public health.\par \par \uc2 And, Bobby, I promised you, he been on my back to get this done for -- since I got elected President. Don`t forget the Locks, Joe. So I just think -- I`m not sure how we`re going to do this, but so long as I -- I couldn`t say no so if we get the money for the Locks projects done, and I think we should call it Casey`s Lock. I don`t know. But, Bobby, you have been -- for a Scranton guy to worry about the Locks in this side -- anyway.\par \par \uc2 In the Hill District of Pittsburgh, we`re providing funding to revitalize the area by making sidewalks and streets safer for pedestrians and drivers, and connecting communities that suffered for decades for lack of investment. And thanks to the Infrastructure Law, Pennsylvanians can get $170 million over five years to build out the electric charging stations that are going to be available to all the electric vehicles that are going to be on the road.\par \par \uc2 And that`s just what we need because half of the new car sales in America are going to be electric by the end of this decade. My administration has recently approved Pennsylvania`s plan so the funds are available now, so the IBEW can start constructing these facilities. We`re putting $20 million into a new 700,000-square-foot terminal at the Pittsburgh Airport to include upgrades to the security screening, baggage check system, so it`s easier and more efficient to catch your flight.\par \par \uc2 No one knows better than the people of Pittsburgh that there are thousands of uncapped orphan wells, oil and gas wells, spewing methane into the air. These abandoned mines scattered throughout this area. It`s a legacy left behind by the mining and oil and gas companies. When the wells and fields went dry, the companies left, and people -- and left it to the people of Pennsylvania to clean it up. Well, guess what? We`re going to clean it up.\par \par \uc2 Now, my Department of Interior is sending Pennsylvania close to -- Conor, I know you`ve worked on this -- close to $350 million this year to cap the wells and reclaim the abandoned mine lands and polluted waterways, creating good-paying jobs in the process for the folks in the same communities that dug the wells in the first place.\par \par \uc2 In addition, we`re investing at least $100 million in Pennsylvania to get high-speed Internet across the state in every part of the state -- urban, suburban, and rural. And no one is going to be left behind. Twenty-one percent of Pennsylvania families don`t have home Internet connections. But never again should a parent have to drive their kid to a McDonald`s parking lot, and sit there to connect to a McDonald`s Internet so they could do their homework. Not a joke. You saw it happening.\par \par \uc2 In addition, there are over 150,000 lead service lines carrying water to homes and schools in Pennsylvania and over 7,000 in Pittsburgh. This impacts children`s brain development. I mean, for real. It`s a hazard to their health. You can`t have this in Pennsylvania. We can`t have this anywhere in America.\par \par \uc2 Now Pennsylvania is going to get $87 million to replace these lead pipes, because every person, every child in this country should have access to clean, safe drinking water. And, by the