20 days from November 8, President Biden releases the oil.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`ve been doing everything in my power to reduce gas prices.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, why the cost of gasoline could decide the fate of American democracy. Then, why a federal court is calling John Eastman`s emails potential evidence of a conspiracy to defraud the United States?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I only need 11,000 votes. Fellas, I need 11,000 votes. Give me a break.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And today`s Trump deposition in a defamation lawsuit stemming from rape allegations. Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I want to pick the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): There`ll be exceptions for life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R-MS): There are exceptions for the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The horrifying reality of what has become a Republican talking point on abortion. And new depths of despair and deception from new reporting on the DeSantis migrant stunt, when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. You know, there is not a lot that American presidents can do to control gas prices, even though their political fate often depends on whether they`re going up or down. They do have the one trick up their sleeve. There`s an enormous stockpile of actual barrels of oil along the coastlines of Louisiana and Texas where there are hundreds of millions of barrels stored underground. It`s known as the Strategic Petroleum Reserves.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of people have heard that and think it`s some accounting thing. It`s not. No, it`s literally a reserve of oil that we keep in caves. We started stockpiling barrels of oil after the Arab oil embargo in the early 70s, a kind of emergency source to protect the U.S. from having to deal with a sudden supply crunch, which is basically what we`re dealing with now.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the Saudi-run oil cartel known as \uc2 OPEC\uc2  announced that it would slash production by two million barrels a day. Gas prices are already pretty high but that would result in further skyrocketing of prices, like the ones we saw right after the Russian invasion of Ukraine. And with 20 days before the Midterm Election, that of course can make a huge difference in the outcome.\par \par \uc2 The fate of American democracy hangs largely on these little digits you see at gas stations across the country, as wild as it is to say that, it`s basically true. And so, today, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  decided to pull a lever. He announced he will release 15 million barrels of oil from the strategic reserves to bring down gas prices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Gas prices have fallen every day in the last week. Let me repeat, gas prices come down and they continue to come down again. They`re now down more than $0.27 a gallon in Wisconsin this past week, $0.27 in Oregon, $0.16 in Ohio, $0.25 in Nevada, $0.17 in Indiana in just the last 10 days and that`s progress, but they`re not falling fast enough. When the price of gas goes up, other expenses get cut. That`s why I have been doing everything in my power to reduce gas prices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And yes, this is a pretty transparently political move on behalf of the President. That is because gas prices are and have historically been a driving factor in U.S. politics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IRVING LEVINE, FORMER NBC NEWS REPORTER: Senator Kennedy has said that the state of the economy will be a main factor in his decision on whether to challenge President Carter. Democratic officials say that today`s inflation figures may play into Senator Kennedy`s hand. Those figures include gasoline up $3.7 a gallon on average last month, up $28.2 a gallon so far this year.\par \par \uc2 Politician smelled not fumes but opportunity. Republicans called for repeal of the $4.3 a gallon federal tax that the Democratic Congress imposed in 1993.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We need to find some way to help the driving public.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For Democrats a coordinated attack on high gas prices and the President`s response.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Highest prices that we`ve had in this country on average ever and where`s the president?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MCAULIFFE\uc2 , FORMER CHAIRMAN, DEMOCRATIC COMMITTEE: It is now time to can Bush.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: July 2001, buck 33. It`s more than doubled in the five years that President Bush and \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  have been in government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:05:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Republicans for their part are pretty mad about Biden`s move because of course, they don`t want lower gas prices, they want to be able to attack Democrats on the price of the pump. And again, there`s good reason for that. There is really robust evidence the price of gas is one of the -- if not the most important factors in a President`s approval rating.\par \par \uc2 One study from 2016 looked at data from 1976, through mid-2007, found "a 0.10 increase in gas prices lead to a 0.60 percent drop in approval for the incumbent president. So, in other words, if gas prices go up by $0.50, a President loses three points in approval. If gas prices go up, $1.oo, that`s six points. That`s enormous in our polarized electorate, particularly elections are lost by six approval points. That`s the difference between, you know, 10 seats and 30, a bunch of Senate seats.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a bunch of tangible evidence now that this is a major issue for President Biden. We`ve got analysis in New York Times, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , left-leaning pollster Data for Progress all finding a correlation between Biden`s approval rating in gas prices. And while Presidents of both parties have struggled with this problem, right, you`re held responsible for this thing you can`t control, it is much harder for Democrats to tackle it than Republicans. Because, well, for starters, the big oil companies love Republicans.\par \par \uc2 According to the organization open secrets which tracks money in politics, "since the 1990 election cycle, more than two-thirds of the energy sector`s contribution to candidates and party committees has gone to Republicans." And that makes sense. Republicans are squarely the party of fossil fuels of oil companies and a drill baby drill. They`re also the party of burning the planet to a crisp long with all the coal gas and oil underneath the ground. They don`t care about climate. They deny it. They hand waved away.\par \par \uc2 It`s also why Donald Trump`s Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  was perfectly fine traveling to Saudi Arabia to clad hand and smile and joke with Crown Prince Mohammed bin Salman, MBS, two weeks after MBS ordered the murder of American newspaper columnist Jamal Khashoggi and his goons hacked up his body with a bone saw.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHAMMED BIN SALMAN\uc2 , CROWN PRINCE, SAUDI ARABIA: How was your trip?\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: It was good. Uneventful.\par \par \uc2 BIN SALMAN: Good. I hope you don`t have jet lag.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: In a little while, but so far, so good. And thank you for hosting me on such short notice.\par \par \uc2 BIN SALMAN: We are really strong and old allies. So, we face our challenges together. The past, the day of, and tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just sitting here with a guy that just ordered him bloody murder because we got to get the oil flowing. The Saudis have a lot of control over gas prices, the biggest oil-producing nation. And for Republicans, that`s really all that matters. That means \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and the big oil companies, well, they`re inclined towards the Republican Party over the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Democratic Party is the party that wants to fight climate change, which means the ultimate abolition ultimately of fossil fuel burning. So, this is not a tough case, right? Oil companies, \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , Saudi is not exactly eager to make sure prices are low before an election where Democratic candidates are vulnerable. It`s also why Saudi Arabia might want to suddenly announce a cut in oil production right before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Democrats can`t just throw their hands up and say, oh, well, because the future of American democracy is in as we report night in night out on this program, real peril, existential peril. One of the two major parties is currently under the thrall of the guy who tried a violent coup unsuccessfully but has basically announced he`d do it all over again.\par \par \uc2 By one analysis, 60 percent of Americans will have an election denier on their ballot this November. The stakes in the upcoming elections are highest they`ve been in a very long time. This is the first post-coup election in America. We cannot expect threats to democracy to be the only salient issue for voters even if it is salient for a lot.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, there`s millions of voters and people have complicated and busy lives, and they`ve got things they`re thinking about when they go to the polls, like the cost of living, inflation, and yes, gas price, the one price in America that is posted on every street corner, those digits, they are going to be front of mind for voters.\par \par \uc2 And so, we find ourselves in a situation where keeping gas prices low is key to preserving and strengthening the future of our democracy. And so, here we are, hence Biden releasing oil from the reserves today. And all this speaks to what the future of our elections look like because it`s -- you can`t just count on one thing to preserve American democracy. You can`t just count on the voters to vote for it and you can`t just count on the courts and you can`t just count on civil society. It`s going to require a broad coalition of factors, people being smart and tactically astute all working together across the spectrum of civil society to preserve the American experiment.\par \par \uc2 Rick Hasen knows a thing or two about the fight for democracy, professor of law at the UCLA Law School, director of Safeguarding Democracy Project. I can announce today, he just joined MSNBC as an Election Law Analyst, which is great.\par \par \uc2 Rick, welcome aboard. Great to have you on.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:10]\par \par \uc2 RICK HASEN, MSNBC ELECTION LAW ANALYST: Oh, it`s so great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, this is something I think a lot which is that public opinion alone and democratic publics cannot be relied upon solely to be the thing that safeguards a democratic system from continuing. Because voters are going to have a lot of things on their mind, there`s going to be thermostatic public opinion, they`re going to vote against the in-power party generally. If you have a system in which one of the two parties just cannot be trusted with power, that`s not -- that`s not going to work unless you`ve got a whole bunch of other safeguards.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Oh, sure. And, you know, we have a situation where the few Republicans who stood up to this election denialism have been pushed out of office or have decided not to run again. So, you`ve got one party that is in thrall to this election denialism and supporting this idea that the 2020 election was stolen, which creates conditions where if they`re in power, they might be trying to steal it back in 2024.\par \par \uc2 But then you`ve got the Democratic Party. And, you know, Democratic Party played with fire a little bit. First, they didn`t really prioritize pushing the fixes. We may get them after the election to the Electoral Count Act and all these other problems that came up in 2020. But also, you may remember they backed some election denial lists in Republican primaries in the hopes that that would, you know, create weaker candidates.\par \par \uc2 And maybe that`ll work in places like Pennsylvania, but it`s not going to work everywhere. And so, it neither party has had the electoral incentive to make this existential issue a focus of the Midterm Elections.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, even if they did, though, right, there`s also the problem of what voters are tuned into. I mean, you can talk about democracy all the time, and there`s going to be some slice of voters, and we`ve seen interesting polling -- \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  just did polling on this who might be interested in it. But ultimately, it`s not, it`s going to be one of many issues that people are going to vote on or think about in the context of, you know, employment being high or abortion being outlawed.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Right. So, you know, you think about an issue like climate change which poses an existential problem. It doesn`t rank number one or number two among voters even though, you know, it`s kind of like that movie don`t look up. I think we have the same thing going on with our elections now.\par \par \uc2 You go back -- and I was just looking at some tape recently of the insurrection itself. I mean, that was -- those were such dramatic moments, but they`ve been memory hold for so many people. The Trump years were tiring, people are exhausted, and they kind of forgot about it. But it was a really traumatic moment. And I guess I was a little naive in thinking that it would resonate across party lines and we`d all come together and do something at least to preserve American democracy, and then we can go back to fighting about immigration and abortion and taxes and things like that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also a role here, I think, that`s really important for courts, right, and for other institutions that preserve democracy other than which party wins the elections. And I think that`s something that we`ve seen in some ways, there`s big parts of the court system that really did hold in 2020 in a crucial, important way, right, when nonsense claims were brought before judges no matter what their, you know, the party that appointed them, they basically said these are nonsense.\par \par \uc2 But there`s real worry that is going to continue to hold and you`ve been focused and thinking about this. And I wonder where you`re thinking about this as we head into these midterms, which may very well see a change of power in one or both houses.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Yeah, so you know, it was not just the courts, but also principals, Republicans and Democrats who stood up to Trump. Those -- Secretary Raffensperger in Georgia didn`t find 11,780 votes that Trump wants. I mean, Trump had such a ludicrous set of theories. Remember, there were the Italian space lasers. Later we heard about the bamboo from China in the ballots.\par \par \uc2 A more sophisticated election denier could probably get away with a lot more and come up with legal-sounding theories like the independent state legislature theory that`s currently pending in a case before the Supreme Court that they`re going to hear later this year, where you can make an argument that could essentially try to subvert elections, but make it look a lot more respectable than what Trump tried to do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, it`s funny you say that because I remember very well. The speech \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  gave after January 6. And the thesis of it basically was, this isn`t the way you do it. The way you do it is you come up with arguments, the courts will -- that are colorable before courts. And that`s how you did it. Like, that`s how we did it, sort of did it in Bush v. Gore. And that really -- that stuck with me ever since, right?\par \par \uc2 The legal argumentation was so ludicrous that it got laughed out of court. But a better and more competent version of it is also something that to be on guard against.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:12]\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Oh, yes, absolutely. And there has been a change in the judiciary, you know, where we`re seeing some of these Trump appointed judges issuing opinions that I think we would have thought of in the past is off the wall. And we`re -- you know, we`re seeing more of that and we`re seeing fewer heroes who have been running elections continuing to be in those positions.\par \par \uc2 And of course, as you mentioned, there are a lot of these election deniers who are running for office and some are going to win. And they`re going to be running elections in 2024 as this big lie has metastasized. So, we`re really in a much worse position in 2024 than we were even in 2020.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And here, I will -- I will note and say as I often do, the future is unwritten, the outcome of this election completely undetermined. And in fact, there`s lots of reasons to think it`ll be more competitive, much more competitive than many of the sort of structural factors might have pictured, might have shown even, you know, eight months ago. So, if you care about this stuff, you should think about voting for sure.\par \par \uc2 Rick Hansen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HASEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, a federal judge says \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  signed a court document that knowingly included false voter fraud stats. What that means for the case against the disgraced ex-President next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, a federal district court judge ruled that ex-President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  signed legal documents describing evidence of voter fraud that he knew was false. The 18-page opinion is part of the case brought by Trump`s coup plotting lawyer John Eastman to keep his emails away from the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 Eastman has been citing attorney-client privilege, but Judge David Carter ruled that Eastman must turn over some of those emails that show that President Trump knew the specific numbers of voter fraud were wrong, but continue to tout those numbers both in court and to the public. That is because those emails are evidence of what may very well be a crime. "The court finds that these emails are sufficiently related to and in furtherance of a conspiracy to defraud the United States."\par \par \uc2 Nancy Gertner served as a U.S. District Court Judge in Massachusetts for nearly two decades, currently a senior lecturer at Harvard Law School, and she joins me now. It`s great to have you I -- the procedural mechanisms here are slightly confusing. So, I just want to walk through the context here and have you explain what the judge is saying.\par \par \uc2 Eastman is saying, I don`t want to turn over my emails to the January 6 Committee. He sued to block that. What is -- what does the judge have to find one way or the other to decide whether those emails go to the committee or not?\par \par \uc2 NANCY GERTNER, FORMER FEDERAL COURT JUDGE: Well, he turned over all the documents to the judge initially, I think it was in January. And then he said, as to some of these, Judge, you look at these documents and you say, tell me whether you agree that these are protected by the attorney-client privilege. And so, the judge read all the emails, they`re in the -- they`re described in this order and others, and he says, well, here`s a -- here`s a bunch of emails, for example, that are clearly related to pending litigation and legal strategy, and that`s protected.\par \par \uc2 But then he said, here are emails that are first of all, stating that I know that I have no case in Georgia or whatever the court was, but I`m doing this for the purposes of delaying certification that fit into the larger plot to obstruct the January 6 certification. And then I think the more serious one is, he says, he`s been -- writes to the President and says, you know, these statistics that we have been using in the court filings showing voter fraud are wrong, and essentially implies to that -- he`s giving advice to the President not to sign the certification, saying that these are true statistics and Trump signs it anyhow.\par \par \uc2 So, the judge found that this is -- this is called a crime-fraud exception not that -- not that the lawyer is himself involved in illegal activities, but the client is using the lawyer`s offices to effect a crime or fraud. And in this case, it`s obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s really -- it`s pretty astounding. Twice in a year, John Eastman has been before a court asserting attorney-client privilege over some set of emails and twice judges have found that the emails in question are subject to the crime-fraud exception, which is a fairly high bar, that like there`s a pretty good chance a crime was being committed here, dude, and you got to turn these overs. So, this is now the second time.\par \par \uc2 GERNER: Right but it -- but it`s also -- take the large picture. This judge looked at 600 emails. The numbers that he`s allowing to be turned over because the attorney-client privilege -- rather the crime-fraud exception were clear is maybe I believe 35 documents.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GERNER: So, I mean, he has parsed it. He`s given Eastman the benefit of the doubt. He`s gone through each one, and he`s evaluated each one. Yet we are all getting the highlights of this. But it`s actually a more complicated one -- complicated opinion given -- you know, in one sense, maybe one way to put it is he is doing what Judge Cannon should have done.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. That`s right. He`s -- yes.\par \par \uc2 GERTNER; That`s what she should have done.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He`s actually going -- yes, he`s going through it and saying --\par \par \uc2 GERTNER: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. So, he`s got -- so he says this -- in the order, he says four emails demonstrate an effort by President Trump and his attorneys to press false claims in federal court for the purpose of delaying the January 6 vote. The evidence confirms this effort was undertaken in at least one lawsuit filed in Georgia. This is again, the judge -- saying what the evidence is. Like, he has read this. This is what he says the evidence is.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:27]\par \par \uc2 GERTNER: Right. He`s read it And in that -- in that case, he says, you know, there`s like an email that says, hey, I don`t think we have a case to which Trump response I believe. Who cares? It`ll delay these proceedings.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And then the one that you said before, which has been the headline is, and this -- I just want to quote again from the order. President Trump signed a verification swearing under oath the incorporated inaccurate numbers are true and correct or believed to be true and correct to the best of his knowledge and belief, despite as we`ve learned from the judge in his ruling being warned they were not.\par \par \uc2 GERTNER: Right. Right. No, I think -- but that`s not -- I think you want to put it in context. This is not a document dump. This was a thoughtful decision, in fact, rejecting the January 6 Committee`s claims, most of these emails, but focusing on emails that were clearly not privileged, and that were fit within the crime-fraud exception which, as you said, is really a high bar.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The big thing that sort of really piqued my interest here is it now appears those -- I mean, this will be appealed, I suspect, but should this ruling stand, those documents would go over to the committee. And I have to say, for someone who I`ve -- you know, we`ve covered now for seven years in the program for my sins, that that Trump is never in emails as far as I can tell. I mean, very, very careful about being an email.\par \par \uc2 So, here he is, appears to be in emails, and emails that are going to go to the committee which itself I haven`t ever encountered before.\par \par \uc2 GERTNER: That`s actually a great point. I mean, we don`t actually have the emails in front of us, so we don`t know whether or not this was to X on Trump`s benefit.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GERTNER: Whether these were directly from the email account. We don`t -- we don`t really know that. All we know is that they opened up the door to this notion that even the litigation was engaged in bad faith knowing that they didn`t have a prayer, and then you add that, of course, to things like the testimony that \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and others have given that the President was told this over and over again that there was no basis to these lawsuits. And now you have an admission that there may be no basis to it, but we`re doing it anyhow because we wish to delay the gratification.\par \par \uc2 While you`re talking about litigation, this is litigation, not for the purposes of winning. This is litigation for the purpose of delay.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And it`s that admission to me that`s the biggest deal as someone who`s tracked everything in this story. Because the January 6 Committee, right, they have presented all this evidence the President was told. He was told by everyone. He was informed over and over again. This appears to be the first time I`ve seen some record of the president admitting himself that it`s a lie. And that seems to be a big deal going forward.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Gertner, always, always clarifying to talk to you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GERTNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, brand new reporting on the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  human trafficking scandals. A migrant who was tricked by the DeSantis team into rounding up other migrants tells all. One of the reporters who broke that really important story joins me ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The more we learn about Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` plan to drop asylum seekers on an island in the Atlantic Ocean, the more it looks sketchy, possibly criminal, like seems kind of off book Black Op. Now, thanks to incredible front-page reporting for the Miami Herald, we`re learning how DeSantis and his team of operatives were able to mislead vulnerable migrants into thinking they would receive housing and work.\par \par \uc2 We know thanks to previous reporting about a former \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  counterintelligence agent named Perla Huerta who was the point person in the DeSantis plot. This latest reporting shows how Perla paid and tricked a Venezuelan migrant into helping her execute her recruiting scheme. It also lays out how "DeSantis` San Antonio operation was far bigger and better organized than previously known with more than half a dozen recruiters, support staff from the ground, and some operational logistics handled from Florida.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the reporters who broke this story, Sarah Blaskey. She`s investigative reporter for the Miami Herald. Sarah, first of all, thank you and great reporting. I want to dive in here to the individual that you got to talk to who was recruited by Perla to be part of this scheme. Tell me about who this is and what you learn from him.\par \par \uc2 SARAH BLASKEY, INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER, MIAMI HERALD: So, Emmanuel is a 27- year-old Venezuelan migrant, much like the people that were sent to Martha`s Vineyard. He had recently crossed the Southern Border with Mexico near San Antonio and was living just outside of that migrant Resource Center, the shelter where everyone was living where Perla Huerta, as you mentioned, was recruiting for this program.\par \par \uc2 And she met him. And he thought, wow, this is an opportunity for me to change my life for the better. This woman is offering good things, I`ll be able to do something good, and maybe with this contact get ahead. He didn`t want to leave San Antonio. He didn`t need to. He had a court date there for his asylum hearings. But he thought maybe I`ll work with this woman who seems nice. She told him she was a military veteran. He trusted her.\par \par \uc2 And so, he started working. He started recruiting and vouching for this program. It was a friendly face. People believed him. And as you mentioned, Perla Huerta was then paying him in cash a couple of times to do this job for the program.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:34]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And he doesn`t -- just to be clear, so he himself in the -- in a position not dissimilar to the folks he`s recruiting. He meets up with Perla Huerta. She says I can pay you to do this work. And he`s not in on the stunt. I mean, he doesn`t know these people will be dropped off in Martha`s Vineyard, right? He thinks he`s getting paid. It`s like a win-win. He`s going to get paid and help these folks relocate to somewhere that will be helpful for them.\par \par \uc2 BLASKEY: That`s right. And he`s also meeting contacts that he thinks are going to be beneficial to him moving forward, right? This is a retired military veteran with a pension, she tells him. And so, he`s thinking, OK, this is probably good going forward. He does not realize he says until he starts getting messages from folks he sent to Martha`s Vineyard saying nobody knew we were coming. Nobody is here expecting us with jobs and housing like you had promised.\par \par \uc2 And Emmanuel started thinking, oh, my gosh, what have I done. And in WhatsApp messages reviewed by the Miami Herald, he starts messaging, Perla Huerta and says, hey, nobody knows that we were coming or that these people were coming. You know, there are families with children. They`re scared. And she ignores him for 33 minutes, and eventually responds and says, well, let me make a couple of phone calls. The state has to take care of them.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s the moment Emmanuel starts thinking something is odd here. But he actually kept working for the program. And I think that`s really interesting. She was able to convince him that this wasn`t political despite all of the news, and that they had actually done good. That despite the lies that he was uncomfortable with, that they had done good because people were better off in Martha`s Vineyard than they had been in this overcrowded center in San Antonio.\par \par \uc2 And Emmanuel thought that seemed true. You know, people were getting clothes. People were getting all of this stuff. So, for the next round, recruiting now for a Delaware flight that was eventually canceled, he said, he just started telling people the truth. You know, this is a ride out of town. We`re going to dump you somewhere that no one`s expecting you, but it`s a better -- it`s a better place than here. And that`s what he did up until the sheriff announced his investigation. And that`s when Emmanuel said he really realized something wasn`t right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The sheriff of Bexar County, of course, where all this took place who announced a criminal investigation. There`s also announcement that these folks would be -- could apply for new visas for people that are victims of crimes. That`s the people that were -- that were essentially trafficked to Massachusetts. You also uncovered finally here, just the scope of this, there`s like cash being stored behind the dumpster to pay people. Like, this entire thing is such a dodgy off-book operation it seems and also quite sprawling.\par \par \uc2 BLASKEY: That`s right. So, the final payment as all of this starts to unravel after the sheriff announces is his investigation, Perla Huerta leaves a cash payment for Emmanuel under a piece of concrete behind a dumpster at a local barbecue chain restaurant. She tells him basically that he -- you know, she and he can`t be seen together, that people are coming after her. He doesn`t quite understand what that means. But that`s when everything starts unraveling.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing that we uncovered during our investigation is that this operation really does stretch back to the governor`s office. I mean, there is an attorney who is now the Public Safety czar for DeSantis who was on the ground with this program, according to public records that were released last Friday by the governor`s office and also the \uc2 Florida Department of Transportation\uc2 . And then of course, he is text-messaging with the cheese Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, this is -- this comes right from the top, Sarah Blaskey who`s been doing fantastic reporting on this, thank you so much. And thank you for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  goes under oath for a defamation suit filed by a woman who has accused him of rape. Why Trump`s recent strategy to plead the Fifth will not help him here, ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The true Republican position on abortion is that it should be outlawed with no exceptions. It`s just the logical oral conclusion, of course, if you believe a fetus is a person, as the most important voices in the Republican Party and conservative movements say they do. Now, some Republicans have been open about that position. For instance, Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as recently as August.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: So, I said, I believe in life. I believe in life. And I said, you know, if anyone wants to have an exception, I said, not in my book. I said, I`m sorry. I feel bad for anyone that`s a victim of any kind of crime. I do. I feel like that. That`s terrible and that horrible, but we deal with that as it comes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I`m sorry, 10-year-old girl, that you`ve been raped. You got to just deal with it. Of course, Walker is alleged to have paid for an abortion personally and urged another. He denies that. Now, claims he didn`t do that and he never opposed exceptions. That has become though the Republican fallback, the exception, their get out of jail free card, you could say.\par \par \uc2 The way they are now trying to wriggle out of their highly unpopular position that they want to ban all abortions with no exception is by claiming, no, no, no, they favor exceptions like when the life of the mother is at stake. Let`s now Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina put it after he introduced a national federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:32]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Here`s what I would tell the Republican Party. Be not afraid. Stand up for the baby all over the country. I`m confident the American people would accept a national ban on abortion at 15 weeks with exceptions for the life of the mother, rape, and incest.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, post-Roe, we already live in America of exceptions for "life of the mother," and it`s horrifying. Missouri resident Mylissa Farmer was nearly 18 weeks pregnant, 18 weeks, when she was told her baby was not viable and her health was at risk, that there was nothing her doctors could do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MYLISSA FARMER, DENIED ABORTION: I thought I was going to die. I really thought I was going to die. They told me that my water had broke fully completely, that there was no amniotic fluid left, that she wasn`t going to survive, that there was no chance for her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But Missouri`s heartbeat law prohibits medical doctors from performing an abortion if the fetus has a heartbeat even if it`s life- threatening to the mother. Mylissa and her partner go to a clinic in Illinois that did the abortion.\par \par \uc2 FARMER: I said, well, it feels like the law is trying to kill me. It feels like the state of Missouri is trying to kill me with that options and the doctors want help and you`re not able to help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In Texas, 35-year-old Amanda Zebrowski faced a similar situation. Again, 18 weeks of pregnancy. She was pregnant. She was diagnosed with an incompetent cervix and told a miscarriage was inevitable but all she could do was wait.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Being told that you`re going to lose your baby and you just have to live with it for several days and there`s nothing we can do. I was left, you know, wanting either to get so sick that my life is at risk or that my baby`s heart stopped beating so that it can be over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Amanda Zebrowski did get very sick. Three years later, she had sepsis. And only then could doctors fight to save Amanda`s life. A high- risk obstetrician in Tennessee recently told The Wall Street Journal what a patient she had in her second trimester with rising blood pressure, who she believed could be facing a serious health emergency. Her unborn baby had been diagnosed with genetic abnormalities that meant the child wasn`t expected to survive.\par \par \uc2 The doctor said she thought the patient needed an abortion. She wasn`t confident that woman`s condition was dire enough to meet the legal standard imposed by the total abortion ban in Tennessee, and fear that being charged with a crime could upend her life for years. And so, she decided to send the woman on a roughly six-hour ambulance ride to end her pregnancy in North Carolina where she arrived with dangerously high blood pressure and signs of kidney failure.\par \par \uc2 So, these exceptions to abortion bans Republican politicians invoke to show how humane and compassionate they are, are just slogans trying to cover up this horrific reality which exists now. What an abortion ban with an exception for the life of the mother really means is that when you`re sitting in a hospital on the worst day of your life, being told by a doctor that the baby you`re planning to bring into this world will not make it and that your life is at risk, you and your doctors will have to sit around and wait with that. And there will be nothing they can do as you get sicker and sicker until you are on death`s actual door. And only then, with the laws of \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and Ron Johnson, and Greg Abbott, and the Republican Party allowed doctors to step in and provide the lifesaving care you need. And that is the world the reality they want to bring about for the entire country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, the ex-president was deposed under oath to answer questions in a defamation lawsuit by writer E. Jean Carroll. Three years ago, she -- you may remember, she accused \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of raping her in the mid-1990s in the dressing room of the \uc2 Bergdorf Goodman\uc2  department store which is right near Trump Tower. Trump has denied it at first telling NBC News "I`ve never met this person in my life." And then a few days later telling The Hill, "I say it with great respect. Number one, she`s not my type. Number two, it never happened. It never happened. OK."\par \par \uc2 In response to his statements, Carol sued, claiming that Trump`s denials damaged her reputation. In court, Trump`s lawyers argued he was acting in his capacity as President when he denied Carroll`s allegations a stretch. That`s a question now before the DC`s appeals court. Last week, a federal judge ruled it was time to move forward on the defamation case itself writing, "The defendant should not be permitted to run the clock out on the plaintiff`s attempt to gain a remedy for what allegedly was a serious wrong."\par \par \uc2 And so, this is why today, the ex-president of the United States was deposed under oath. So far, we don`t know much on how the actual deposition went. But Trump`s lawyer says he was "pleased to set the record straight today and call the whole thing a political ploy."\par \par \uc2 Harry Litman is a former U.S. Attorney, former Deputy Assistant Attorney General at the Department of Justice, and he joins me now. So, Harry just set the context here for a defamation suit. What the person suing has to show in order to be successful?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:13]\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Think of Alex Jones who lost to a billion-dollar verdict last week. She`s going to show that he said something false and knew it was false. That`s the basic core of it. And what`s the false thing they said that he never knew where it`s a hoax, all these things and she`s not my type. That`s the basic showing she needs to make.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, that would be -- I mean, we`re this to go to trial, let`s say, like the Alex Jones did, right, the truth or falsity of the actual underlying accusation which is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  raped me in a \uc2 Bergdorf Goodman\uc2  dressing room demonstrating that to a sufficient extent would be the question, right, the factual dispute before the court?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: That`s exactly right. At best, Chris, it`s going to be he said she said before a New York jury. It could be worse. He really has painted himself into a corner after repeating everything, like once again last week which by the way, probably moves out this whole sideshow about the Westfall act and can he get out of it. And that`s the best. She says she kept the dress and they`ve asked for his blood for a DNA sample, you know, recall what happened with Clinton and Paula Jones.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, it`s a -- it`s a salient lesson because Paula Jones was the lawsuit there and that`s when they just asked about \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2 . Robbie Kaplan, the lawyer here is sophisticated and aggressive. You have a lot of leeway in a deposition to ask about a lot of stuff. I`ll bet he faced the question, have you ever assaulted a woman and lied about it, Stormy Daniels, et cetera. I`ll bet there`s some very tough questions he had to face.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, what is the -- if you`re in this deposition, right, and you`re the -- and you`re the defense -- you`re the attorney for Trump, right. What`s the -- what`s the goal here? I mean, again, we should note that this accusation which he has denied, and in the grossest possible terms, she`s not my type, which he reiterated last week, which is obviously disgusting thing to say, that you know, there is evidence there`s corroboration of contemporaneous friends of her she informed about it. There -- she said she has the dress.\par \par \uc2 She -- you know, to the extent that there`s some sort of other ways of backing these up, there`s some evidence, what is the approach of someone in a deposition who`s being sued like Trump is in this case today?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Someone or Trump. The someone would be normally to maybe retreat to I don`t recall or try to settle. I think Trump, if the past is prologue, would hope for the best and then try to settle for whatever it takes, if it`s really horrific, because these do stay confidential unless it goes to trial. Jean Carroll says she will not do that.\par \par \uc2 And besides, there`s a whole another case coming about the assault itself which New York lies just past the ability for her to bring within the next year. So, what you try to do normally is not lie, be safe, etcetera. That is not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the client. If he had -- if he were even a modicum of being able to be -- to take direction from council, he would never, never have made those statements last week.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And E. Jean Carroll obviously settling, I mean, she wants to establish the facts of what happened I think is -- she has said is her motivation here. It`s not a motivation to settle or get payment. She says this happened and she would -- and she has been defamed, and she wants to establish for the record in a court of law that it did happen.\par \par \uc2 Now, he also can`t really -- I mean, you could always plead the Fifth in any situation. It`s constitutional right. But that really doesn`t help them in this context, right?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: It hurts him because you can use it as a civil case. And Robbie Kaplan, her lawyer can say, listen to what he said, don`t you -- because he took the fifth. Don`t you know that he really did it? And by the way, his denials are so absolute. Never was anywhere near \uc2 Bergdorf Goodman\uc2 , et cetera. They may be able to come up with things. And at its best for Trump, it`s a flat up credibility dispute that a New York jury doesn`t have to decide beyond a reasonable doubt, just more likely than not.\par \par \uc2 And if they find that she was defamed, damages could be substantial. And it`s against him personally. This is not like a political sideshow he can just, you know, push his way through. This is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the citizen, having to write a check.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And there`s the pacing of this too, which I mean, it`s just seems the judge to me is not going to let this be totally dilatory and at least has moved things forward to the -- to this point, which suggests that.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: He set trial for February. And you`re right, that`s why I said the deposition. They`re still deciding that other issue, but he said, hey, so what? You have to sit -- stand up and tell the truth, what`s the injury there. Of course, for Trump, it is, but you know.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: February is where the trial is set. Now, we`re going to continue to track this. Harry Litman, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is all in on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221020eiaj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 19, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Yasmin Vossoughian, Shannon Pettypiece, Pippa Stevens, Eugene  Daniels, Cal Perry, Gabe Gutierrez, Shaquille Brewster, Vaughn Hillyard,  Robert Gibbs \par \uc2 9,499 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC ANCHOR: Yasmin Vossoughian, in for Chris Jansing today.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we are waiting for remarks from the president. He`s expected to make a major announcement to lower gas prices amidst recent cuts by \uc2 OPEC\uc2 . We`re going to bring you that live.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in Russia, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  announcing that the country will impose martial law in the Ukrainian Regency illegally annexed last month, plus, the former president being deposed right now in the defamation case brought by writer E. Jean Carroll who alleges that the former president raped her back in the mid-90s.\par \par \uc2 And with just 20 days until the midterms and early voting already underway in dozens of states across the country. We are going to take a look at the key races in Arizona and Florida as well, including last night, a pretty heated debate between Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and his Democratic challenger Congresswoman Val Demings over everything from abortion to gun safety.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, AZ: The government is in that room because she brought them in there. She wants to taxpayer to pay for that abortion. That`s government involvement. Not just to pay for that abortion but to pay for them all over the world.\par \par \uc2 VAL DEMINGS, DEMOCRATIC SENATE CANDIDATE, ARIZONA: It means the senator should make a project --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I believe at this point --\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: How long will you watch people being gunned down in first grade, fourth grade, high school, college, church synagogue, a grocery store, a movie theater, a mall, and a nightclub, and do nothing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Congresswoman --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, up first, though, the president is set to give a really major speech any moment now, as soon as it comes up, we`re going to bring it to you, expected to announce the release of 15 million barrels of oil from the strategic reserves. This move of course is aimed at bringing those prices down -- those gas prices down right and coming exactly two weeks after \uc2 OPEC\uc2  announced cuts in oil production. We`re going to bring you the president`s remarks as soon as they get started.\par \par \uc2 To talk through all of this, I want to bring in senior White House reporter Shannon Pettypiece, CNBC markets reporter Pippa Stevens, and Politico White House correspondent Eugene Daniels, who`s also an MSNBC political analyst. Welcome to you all, guys, thanks for joining us on this.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get started for, Shannon, on kind of the way in which the White House is positioning this speech ahead of this speech to get started, I believe in about 14 minutes or so as of this moment, and what else we`re expected to hear from the president this afternoon?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON PETTYPIECE, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Well, just for some context, he has been -- nothing the president is going to announce or layout today is expected to have any sort of impact on oil prices. So essentially, these 15 million barrels are a drop in an oil barrel, or you know, they are just a fraction of what the United States uses in a week. And it`s part of a much larger release of these emergency oil reserves that the president announced months ago. So it`s just sort of the latest tranche of that.\par \par \uc2 Well, we also expect the president to sort of lay out plans for how the U.S. is going to replenish that emergency reserve in case there`s some sort of other urgent scenario that would come along where we need to tap into that. And then he`s also supposed to ratchet up the pressure on oil companies, again, accusing them of benefiting from this what he considers a crisis in the markets right now and taking advantage of consumers. We have heard that sort of rhetoric from the president before though, however, the timing of this is, of course, notable.\par \par \uc2 We are three weeks out from the election. Democratic strategists and pollsters are increasingly telling myself and my colleagues that they have a lot of concerns that Democrats are not doing enough to talk about inflation and the economy. And while the president does regularly bring up inflation in the economy, they are concerned that the message isn`t getting through to voters about what actions the president is taking now, specifically at the areas of their pocketbook hitting -- getting hit the most, and what more importantly, Democrats and the president will do going forward if they`re given another two years in office when it comes specifically to inflation and the economy. We hear a lot from Democrats about health care, abortion, crime, and safety.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: But they really struggled to land on a new message with the economy. And you can see the White House today starting to move in that direction and give an indication of what the closing arguments are going to look like here in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. And we`re going to be watching for those remarks once again, and bring it to you live as soon as they get started. Shannon, I know you got to go, so appreciate you sticking with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Pippa, let`s talk through some of this. And Shannon just put it out there right, this 15 million barrels of oil that could feasibly be released from the strategic oil reserves, not really going to do much right when it -- when it comes to oil prices. But nonetheless, it says, essentially, we`re doing something. The Biden administration putting the message out there we`re doing something. If you thought we weren`t doing anything, this is us doing something.\par \par \uc2 PIPPA STEVENS, MSNBC MARKET REPORTER: Yes, as Shannon said, this won`t really have that much of an impact because it is part of that larger 180- million-barrel release that the administration announced back in March. And to put this 15 million-barrel in context, we use about 20 million barrels per day every day here in the U.S. So, this is really not going to have that much of an impact. But gas prices, of course, are a key sticking point for voters ahead of the midterms, and so this is the administration, as you said, trying to signal they`re doing something when the reality is they don`t really have any control here, but neither do big oil companies.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: The White House has repeatedly pointed the finger at oil executives. But these are global markets, they do not set prices. And the latest move by the White House reportedly looking to limit businesses doing business with Saudi Arabia, as one source told me why would they seek a full-on confrontation with Saudi Arabia right before another round of sanctions against Russian energy. So, to put it -- to sum this all up, this is basically a bandaid for a much larger structural issue.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Well, Pippa, quickly, how are U.S. businesses -- how are they reacting to the possibility that the Biden administration is trying to limit them from doing business with Saudi? Is this something they would even be on board with? At the end of the day, they are a business and they`re concerned about their bottom line.\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Well, I think one of the issues here is that there are a lot of conflicting signals. We have reports that that could happen. There are also reports about limiting product exports. And so there`s a lot of people saying, well, we don`t really know what`s going on with these conflicting signals. At one point, the administration is saying please pump more.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: And then they`re also tapping the SPR. So why would that incentivize new production? And so I think within that environment, business leaders are very reluctant to make any big type of move or announcement when they just don`t know what the policy is going to look like going forward, particularly with the ongoing uncertainty around Russian energy and how those sanctions play out.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Eugene, let`s talk about kind of your thoughts on this closing argument, right? Yesterday, we heard about abortion rights. A lot of folks criticizing not saying you need to be talking about the economy. Look at the New York Times Siena College poll released just a couple of days ago, the number one issue for so many voters ahead of the midterms was the economy. Here is the president now today, right, about you talk about oil prices and subsequently, I am sure, the economy.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: No, absolutely. And everything that`s been said is exactly right. You have 80 percent of voters in a Politico/Morning Consult poll, saying that inflation will play a major role in their vote, 81 percent saying the economy, so that is both of them. And that is Democrats, Republicans and independents say that that is going to drive them or affect their vote in a few weeks. And I think the thing that this administration and Democrats writ large have kind of been hoping to do was make this not a referendum on President Biden and his economy, but about a choice between Republicans who they see as trying to take rights away, and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Some of those trends that we saw -- we saw a few weeks ago, where Democrats were getting a lot of that more excited about that. That has dissipated as more concerns about the economy, people keep hearing about the possibility of a recession.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: And so now the administration has to get people focused on the next kind I was saying what they`ve done and also what they plan to do moving forward. And I think that`s one of the things that Democrats have kind of struggled with. Because they`re also as they talk -- you know talk about abortion as the number one issue that the president is going to take on after the midterms if you send him enough senators to get a bill passed into his desk, people are saying, OK, but what about the economy?\par \par \uc2 And they`re -- the economy right now, it`s hard to sell people on it. It is not just inflation, there are good things, right? Unemployment is down. But when people look around, and they can`t afford gas so they can`t put all the food on the table they could last year, that is what they`re thinking about when they go voting.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. You`re talking about gas prices. I think it`s important to kind of put these numbers out there so we all really know what`s going on. So I think a lot of folks are confused as to where we are right now. Current average, $3.85, you see it there on your screen there. Last week $3.92, so we`re down this week, right? Last month, $3.68, last year $3.34, but the peak by the way over the summer, June 14, $5 a gallon back then, was the average.\par \par \uc2 Eugene, Pippa mentioned this. And I quickly just want you to see -- want to see if you have any reporting on this. I know Politico talked about this specifically in their reporting. They have aired complaints to executives or their ballooning profits and threatened drastic new restrictions, such as limits on companies` fuel exports, if the industry refuses to help ease the price of the pump -- at the pump. That`s according of course to some of your reporting at Politico. Wondering if the president is likely to address any concerns like this in a speech today?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yes. I think you know, it`s possible, we`re not really sure what the president`s going to say, this is something that is likely to come up. And a question about whether the president is going to tell people to not work in Saudi Arabia or whatever --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: And when -- in businesses, we talked about that and ask that question to \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , the Press Secretary yesterday, and she wouldn`t even touch it and said that you know, the White House feels like businesses should do their business. But still, there is the reporting, and we have been hearing that the president is thinking about doing this, that he wants to go after some of these businesses because of what Saudi Arabia did, and talk to them and say, hey, let`s not go over there.\par \par \uc2 But then where do they go? You have to give them a place to build it. So that`s another question for this administration as they try to convince folks they are doing all of the -- all they can.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: And in the last few weeks of this -- of this cycle as we -- as we get closer to voting, if you can`t convince voters that the issue that they care about are taken care of, that is what it comes down to, right? It`s turnout and persuasion. And so if (INAUDIBLE) your based out, and you can persuade people that you have done everything you possibly can, that is going to get them to vote for you. And that`s not really what Democrats are seeing and so they`re starting to get a little bit more nervous than they were a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes. I mean, in talking about doing business or not doing business with the Saudis, I mean, it`s an extremely sensitive geopolitical topic, especially when you see what`s going on in Iran as well. So it`s understanding why -- it`s understandable as to why they`re being cautious in the way they`re talking about this. Pippa Stevens and Eugene Daniels, thank you. And once again, thank you to Shannon Pettypiece who did have to go early from some more reporting there. We are still waiting, by the way, those remarks from the president on gas prices, the economy, again, we`re going to bring you those comments as soon as they get up and started.\par \par \uc2 Let`s head overseas now, everybody. You got explosions once again, rocking the Ukrainian capital of Kyiv today as the war reaches a critical point. And a short time ago, the Russian president declaring martial law in the four regions of Ukraine he illegally annexed last month. NBC`s Cal Perry is live for us in the capital city of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 So, two things here first I want to hit on with you, Cal. First is kind of just giving us the latest on the ground -- the feeling on the ground there in Kyiv specifically, and what this kind of declaration right of martial law means for these occupied regions.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, I think people here in the capitol are weary, they`re tired. This is nine days in a row that people who have either been woken up had Russia are interrupted, or had to flee to bomb shelters because of incoming drones and rockets. And the numbers from the government are stunning. More than 230 drones shot down in just a month, today alone, eight rockets and 10 drones, those drones coming this time from the north, from Belarus. So, this city is now in the crossfire in the same way that the East has been. Now, this city is taking incoming.\par \par \uc2 As for the martial law in Kherson -- specifically the city of Kherson, this is where Ukrainian troops are about to break through. They`re about to liberate this city. And Russia is now forcibly removing citizens from this city and moving them away from the city of Kherson towards Russia. They are depriving Ukrainian army of the ability to liberate the city with citizens in it. Now the Ukrainian army is going to have to try to decide whether or not they`re going to push those Russian troops back, which could take a lot of artillery, take a barrage, it could basically destroy the city, which is we think what the Russians want, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So, two places in which we`re able to see the content that you`re gathering there in Ukraine, it`s on television with us, of course, Cal, it`s oftentimes on your \uc2 Instagram\uc2  as well, which I follow pretty closely. And this morning, I saw something pretty disturbing. I know that you`ve been shooting inside schools, telling the story of kids getting educated on how to deal with nuclear war, the possibility of nuclear war. What did you see?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Yes. And I have to say, this is a particularly difficult shoot to go on, to film these kids. These are high school students who are drilling, as you said, for nuclear war. It`s not just the high school kids it`s some of the elementary school kids as well. This was our third day trying to get to this school. We had been stopped three days in a row because of incoming rockets and incoming drones.\par \par \uc2 I asked the children if they`re scared, they all said they`re not, that they`re not scared, that they`re tough, and that they don`t fear anything. And then you ask the teachers and you ask the parents, and they say, of course, they`re terrified. It`s incredibly frightening to put on these biohazard suits, these nuclear suits to drill to run down to the bomb shelter. The other thing that I found very difficult was a lot of the younger kids, the elementary school kids were given swim caps by their parents because they don`t have the real gear that the proper nuclear fallout gear. So, swim caps, swim goggles, the stuff that you and I dress our kids up to go swimming practice is what parents here are putting on their kids to go to bomb shelter.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: So, it was a very difficult scene to watch to be sure.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: The possible reality of that is just terrifying. All we want is for our children here and abroad to be safe. And this is the reality for so many Ukrainian children there.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Cal Perry, as always, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, everybody, at any time now, the former president facing a deposition in E. Jean Carroll`s rape defamation lawsuit. Joining me now is NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez and former U.S. Attorney Harry Litman. Gabe, give it - - give it to us what do we know so far, right? How long is this thing going to last?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Yasmin, good afternoon. Well, E, Jean Carroll tweeted a short time ago that this deposition was underway. But just a short time ago, she has apparently deleted those tweets. So right now, the timing of all this is still fluid. We do not know for sure whether this is underway, but I can tell you, as when the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  has reported that this was expected to take place at Mar-a-Lago. We`re here outside of the law offices for E. Jean Carroll`s attorneys.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 She has not commented on this, and Trump`s legal team has yet to comment on this today. But, Yasmin, as you know, this all dates back to a lawsuit filed in 2019. And this has been in legal limbo since then because the Trump legal team, a judge said last week was trying to delay this. Trump`s legal team have been trying to argue that Mr. Trump denied these allegations while he was president and so he was acting in his capacity as a federal government employee. E. Jean Carroll`s attorneys said no, the judge ruled that this should move forward.\par \par \uc2 And again, these allegations are that E. Jean Carroll alleges that back in the mid-90s, Mr. Trump raped her at a department store here in Manhattan. Trump for his part has said that this is "a con job" and is denied the allegations. But it is significant, Yasmin, that the judge ruled as last week that this deposition should move forward. And it could be significant no matter what happens with this specific defamation lawsuit, E. Jean Carroll has indicated that she does plan to sue the former president later this year or next month when a one-year window goes into effect.\par \par \uc2 You might recall, Yasmin, that earlier this year, New York passed a law that would allow victims -- adult victims of sexual assault to sue even if the statutes of limitations had gone away.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: So that lawsuit could come next month. And this deposition could also play a part in that, Yasmin.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Harry, before I go to you, I just want to remind folks what you`re seeing on your screen there as we`re having this conversation, of course, we`re keeping our eye on the White House expecting the president to speak up at any moment when it comes to some action that he`s going to be taken on oil prices.\par \par \uc2 Harry, two things for you, if you could respond first to kind of what we`re trying to figure out when it comes to these tweets that were put out there by E. Jean Carroll and then subsequently deleted, is that because we feel as if the deposition has not gotten up and going or is it more because she`s not supposed to be putting that information out there, one? And then to follow is, in this type of situation, right, this type of civil matter, can the former president essentially just plead the fifth, right, avoid giving any answers in this deposition that he has in the past?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: So, the first I`m going with choice two. She tweeted it out because it really did start you really shouldn`t. These are confidential proceedings. Her lawyer slapped her knuckles, she deleted it, but I think it`s pretty likely that it has in fact started. On the second, can he plead the fifth? Yes. He can plead the fifth. But this is a real case now. It`s a civil case. And that can be held against him.\par \par \uc2 And we`re talking now on like, sort of illegal skirmishes in the political realm where he got away with it. It`s a defamation case. And at the end of the day, a New York jury will decide how much he owes. Remember, Alex Jones`s billion-dollar verdict last week? That was a defamation case. I`m not saying he`ll be looking at that kind of price tag, but a pretty high one. He has just doubled down really imprudently on calling it a hoax and a lie. And that paints him into a corner for today. He can`t just say, oh, I don`t think I remember.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: oh, really, Mr. Trump? Well, why two -- why two weeks ago, then did you say this, etcetera? I think he`s fairly committed to a very strong categorical denial. And if she has any collateral proof, for example, she says she kept the dress now, almost 30 years that he thought that she was wearing when he assaulted her, it could blow any defense out of the water. And even if she doesn`t, it`s just going to be a credibility he said she said before a New York jury.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, Harry Litman, thank you. Gabe Gutierrez, thank you as well.\par \par \uc2 All right, still ahead this hour, everybody. Again, the president should be taken to the podium any moment now to announce more actions to address gas prices. We`re going to have it live for you. Also, with the midterms approaching, we are live on the campaign trail in Florida and Arizona. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right, welcome back, everybody. We are continuing to wait for the president, set to speak at any moment now on the administration`s efforts to ease gas prices. We`re going to have that live for you as soon as it gets up. We are just 20 days or so away from the midterms, right? Florida Congresswoman Val Demings went on the attack today after a fiery debate last night against incumbent Republican \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  for that state`s open Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Today, on "MORNING JOE," she said her opponent "has no clue about his primary responsibility to Floridians and to the American people." But recent polling is showing Rubio maintaining a small -- a small lead over Demings. I want to bring in now NBC`s Shaq Brewster from Palm Beach. We predicted it, Shaq, it came, it was fiery, and here we are this morning --\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: This afternoon, I should say, talking to through once again. Give us the key moments that really stood out to you.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Yes. And you know, Yasmin, one of the reasons why we knew it was going to be contentious, why we knew there would be attacks flying is because frankly, both campaigns telegraphed those attacks, both in comments and that they were making to reporters, but also when you watch the TV and you saw the campaign ads that were out there. And those ads and those message -- that line of messaging transferred and made its way directly onto the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  painted himself as a successful Senator, talking about his bipartisan accomplishments, saying that Val Demings hasn`t passed any legislation and wouldn`t be a party-line vote if she were elected to the Senate. Val Demings going after \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  for flipping positions after the Parkland shooting, saying that he made promises to the Parkland parents that he didn`t plan on keeping and talking about, in her words, how he played politics with pandemic funding and some economic issues. And then there was abortion. We knew this was going to come up. And I just want you to watch that exchange between the two candidates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: The extremist on abortion in this campaign is Congresswoman Demings. She supports no restrictions, no limitations of any kind. She voted against the former -- she`s against the four-month ban. She voted against the five- month ban. She supports taxpayer-funded abortion on demand for any reason at any time up until the moment of birth. That`s what she supports. That`s the extreme position here.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Senator, how gullible do you really think Florida voters are? Number one, you have been clear that you support no exceptions even including rape and incest. Now as a police detective who investigated cases of rape and incest, no, Senator, I don`t think it`s OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: That tone that you heard there --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BRESTER: That back and forth that you saw there.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: You saw that throughout the night during this hour-long debate. This is something that voters were watching. It aired in many of the major television markets across the state of Florida. But remember, there`s an uphill fight that Val Demings is facing here in this increasingly conservative state. The average polls or the average of polls shows \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  with a lead here. That`s tighter than the governor`s race, but still a lead nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 And you talk to officials statewide, and Republicans have an advantage in terms of the voter registration, so there`s an uphill battle for her. But you saw she was putting up a fight last night on the debate stage and \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  was fighting right back.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes, she certainly was. And we will be watching that race incredibly closely. Shaq Brewster for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 By the way, don`t forget to tune in to "MEET THE PRESS NOW" today live from Georgia. Chuck Todd will be joined by the State Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , Lieutenant Governor Geoff Duncan, and Atlanta Mayor \uc2 Andre Dickens\uc2 . That begins at 4:00 p.m. Eastern, streaming only on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s turn now to Arizona, everybody, where next hour, Kari Lake, the Republican nominee for governor is set to campaign with Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin. This has happened after last night appearing at an event with former representative \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2 , who ran for president in 2020 as a Democrat, but left the party last week and now supports Lake, the Trump- backed candidate.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is in Arizona. What a switch. Wow. Vaughn, take us through it, right? First and foremost, last night`s event and then the expectation from Governor Youngkin`s appearance today alongside Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right, Yasmin. There are several layers to this. Look, I mean, Kari Lake also voted for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  herself. She was a longtime registered Democrat until \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came around. And so really, these two women being up on stage together, you could say it was a perfect pairing. And I want to let you hear between the two of them last night, just a little bit of their exchange. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, REPUBLICAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, ARIZONA: I feel like a rock star up here, Tulsi.\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBART, FORMER U.S. REPRESENTATIVE: You are a rock star, Kari.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: If MLK, Reverend \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 ., were alive today, If JFK were alive today, if our founding fathers were alive today, they would be America First Republicans. I really believe that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: This is all a part of Kari Lake`s effort to appeal to a broader swath of the electorate. You could say there`s nobody that has been arguably more favorite candidate to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than Kari Lake over the last year, and they believe that they`re going to be able to have a great turnout, particularly in the rural parts of Arizona, those voters that came out in droves to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what was a narrow loss for him and 2020. But then you come in and turn your attention to what`s happening here today.\par \par \uc2 And Glenn Youngkin, just a half hour from now will be campaigning here in Tucson alongside Kari Lake. Now you`ll recall one year ago on the campaign trail. In Virginia, it was Glenn Youngkin that kept at arm`s distance from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He was laying claim to building a big tent Republican Party that would include Never Trumpers and forever Trumpers. And that is where you saw last night. I asked Kari Lake about her decision to come with Glenn Youngkin, she said look --\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Hey, Vaughn, the president is speaking now. Vaughn, let`s go to the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: 30 percent from the summer highs. Now, it`s down about $1.50 a gallon from their peak during the summer. And gas prices have fallen every day in the last week. Let me repeat, gas prices come down and continue to come down again. They`re now down more than 27 cents a gallon in Wisconsin this past week, 27 cents in Oregon, 16 cents in Ohio, 25 cents in Nevada, 17 cents in Indiana in just the last 10 days. That`s progress.\par \par \uc2 But they`re not falling fast enough. Families are hurting. You`ve heard me say before, but I get it. I come from a family if the price of gasoline went up at the gas station, we felt it. Gas prices hit almost every family in this country and they squeezed their family budgets. When the price of gas goes up, other expenses get cut. That`s why I have been doing everything in my power to reduce gas prices since Putin`s invasion of Ukraine caused these price hikes -- these prices to spike and rattle international oil markets. Excuse me.\par \par \uc2 I focused on how we can protect the American families from that spike and give folks just a little bit of breathing room as my dad would say. Today, I`m announcing three critical steps that my administration will take to reduce gas prices at the pump. First, the Department of Energy will release another 15 million barrels from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, extending our previously announced release through the month of December.\par \par \uc2 Independent analysis -- excuse me, independent analysts have confirmed that drawdowns from the reserves so far have played a big role in bringing down oil prices -- bringing them down. So, we`re going to continue to responsibly use that national asset. Right now, the Strategic Petroleum Reserve is more than half full, with about 400 million barrels of oil. That`s more than enough for any emergency drawdown.\par \par \uc2 With my announcement today, we`re going to continue to stabilize markets and decrease the prices at a time when the actions of other countries have caused such volatility. And I`ve told my team behind me here to be prepared to look further -- look for further releases in the months ahead if needed. We`re calling it a "Ready and Release" plan. This allows us to move quickly to prevent oil price spikes and respond to international events. Secondly, we need to responsibly increase American oil production without delaying or deferring our transition to clean energy.\par \par \uc2 Let me -- let`s debunk some myths here. My administration has not stopped or slowed U.S. oil production. Quite the opposite. We`re producing 12 million barrels of oil per day. And by the end of this year, we will be producing one million barrels a day, more than the day in which I took office. In fact, we`re on track for record oil production in 2023. And today, the United States is the largest producer of oil and petroleum products in the world.\par \par \uc2 We export more than we import. And I still heard from oil -- and I`ve heard from oil companies that they`re worried that investing in additional oil production today will -- in case demand goes down in the future, and they`re not going to be able to sell their oil products at a competitive price later. Well, we have a solution for that.\par \par \uc2 Today, I`m announcing a plan to refill the Strategic Petroleum Oil Reserve in the years ahead at a profit for taxpayers. The \uc2 United States government\uc2  is going to purchase oil to refill the Strategic Petroleum Reserve when prices fall to $70 a barrel. That means oil companies can invest to ramp up production now, with confidence that they`ll be able to sell their oil to us at that price in the future, $70.\par \par \uc2 Refining and refilling the reserve at $70 a barrel is a good price for companies and it`s a good price for the taxpayers, and it`s critical to our national security. To put it in context, since March, the average price of oil has been more than $90 a barrel, the highest since 2014. By selling from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve at the higher price of $90 earlier this year and then refilling it in the future at a lower price, around $70, it will actually make money for the taxpayers, lower the price of gas, and help bolster production.\par \par \uc2 All while totally consistent with my commitment to accelerate to transition to clean energy. So my message to oil companies is this. You`re sitting on record profits, and you`re -- and we`re giving you more certainty. So you can act now to increase oil production now.\par \par \uc2 The third thing I`m doing is I`m calling on oil companies to pass the savings on to consumers. Consider this. In the second quarter of this year, profits at six of the largest publicly traded oil companies were more than $70 billion. That`s $70 billion in just one quarter, 90 days. Seventy billion. So far, American oil companies are using that windfall of profits to buy back their own stock, passing that money on to their shareholders, not to consumers.\par \par \uc2 In fact, in the first half of the year, those same companies spent $20 billion buying back their own stock and, most importantly, buying back -- the most significant buyback in the last almost a decade. That`s great if you own a lot of stock in an oil company or if you`re an executive in an oil company. It puts a lot of money in your pocket. That is how you get paid. But it`s not the case for the vast majority of Americans paying at the pump.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 Here`s another thing. When the cost of oil comes down, we should see the price at the gas station at the pump come down as well. That`s how it`s supposed to work. But that`s not what`s happening. In the past two weeks, the price of oil has fallen $4 a barrel. And you think -- and thanks in large part to steps we`ve taken this year the price of oil has fallen nearly $40 a barrel since mid-June. That`s a 30 percent drop in the price of a barrel of oil.\par \par \uc2 But guess what? Gas prices haven`t fallen that much. And it`s not right. Gas prices at the pump should be lower. In fact, if retailers and refiners were earning the average profit they`ve made over the last 17 years, Americans would be paying at least 60 cents less per gallon for every gallon they buy. Say it again. 60 cents less for every gallon they buy. That makes a big difference in a family.\par \par \uc2 My message to the American energy companies is this. You should not be using your profits to buy back stock or for dividends. Not now. Not while a war is raging. You should be using these record-breaking profits to increase production and refining. Invest in America for the American people. Bring down the price you charge at the pump to reflect what you pay for the product.\par \par \uc2 You`d still make a significant profit. Your shareholders will still do very well. And the American people will catch the break they deserve and get a fair price at the pump as well.\par \par \uc2 One more thing I want to mention today. Our country needs to pass permitting reform to accelerate the development of clean energy. Right now, the process of getting clean energy projects approved is too cumbersome and too time-consuming. So, I`m asking the Congress, pass a permitting bill to speed up the approval of all kinds of energy production from wind, to solar, to clean hydrogen, because we need to get this moving now, quickly. Now.\par \par \uc2 It would take -- you know, this -- if we do this, it would take the historic clean energy investments that I signed into law and put them into action. In fact, one independent analyst has already estimated that the $369 billion we`re making in federal investments that will generate -- if we just that will generate $1.7 trillion in total public and private investments in the years ahead.\par \par \uc2 You can increase oil and gas production now while still moving full speed ahead to accelerate our transition to clean energy. That way, that way, we can lower energy costs for American families, enhance our national security at a very difficult moment. Let me close with this. I know it`s been a rough four or five years for the country. For a lot of families, things are still tough.\par \par \uc2 The choices made by other countries are affecting the price of gas here at home. That`s why I`ve been acting so aggressively. Without the steps we have taken over the past several months to ramp up production and lower prices and get relief to consumers, gas prices would be higher than they are today. And we`ll keep doing everything we can to keep it going to ensure that our energy independence and security is available and to lower gas prices here at home and to give folks a little bit of breathing room.\par \par \uc2 We just have to remember who we are. We`re the United States of America, for God`s sake. There`s not a single thing we can`t do when we put our minds to it. And we can strengthen our energy security now, and we can build a clean energy economy for the future at the same time. This is totally within our capacity. Totally within our capacity. Gas prices are coming down. We`re going to do everything we can to make sure they continue to come down and companies act responsibly so it`s reflected at the pump.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all. And may God bless you. And may God protect our troops. Thank you. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, Mr. President, what is your response --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don`t hear it. Can you speak louder?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: What is your response to Republicans who say you are only doing this SPR release -- to help Democrats in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Where have they been the last four months? That`s my response.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Is it politically motivated, sir, this move this close --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, it`s not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Three weeks before the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, it`s makes sense. I`ve been doing this for how long now? It`s not politically motivated at all. It`s motivated to make sure that I continue to push on what I`ve been pushing on. And that is making sure there`s enough oil that`s being pumped by the companies so that we have the ability to be able to produce enough gas that we need here at home, oil we need here at home, and at the same time, keep moving in the direction of providing for alternative energy.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:14]\par \par \uc2 That`s what I`ve been doing. Now, the problem is these guys were asleep. I don`t know where they`ve been. And they seem -- you know. The price at the pump should reflect what the price of a barrel of oil costs, and it`s not going down consistently.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Mr. President, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has declared martial law in parts of Ukraine. What does that say to you, sir, about where his thinking is on the war in Ukraine right now?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  finds himself in an incredibly difficult position. And what it reflects to me is it seems his only tool available to him is to brutalize individual citizens in Ukraine -- Ukrainian citizens -- to try to intimidate them into capitulating. They`re not going to do that. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Sir --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Sir, do you want to ban the export of U.S. petroleum products?\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. The president there at the White House making his pitch, right, three weeks out from the midterms. You heard it right there, economy, the number one issue for Americans. The president is telling you here is what we`re going to do about oil and gas prices in this country right about now. We know what`s happened with Saudi Arabia. We know what has happened with OPEC Plus, and here is the president`s response to that for the American people.\par \par \uc2 He has made a commitment to do everything in his power as he said to make sure gas prices remain low, and he mentioned, of course, some of the numbers that we`ve been seeing out there, gas prices down since last week as well.\par \par \uc2 Kind of going through a three-step process, take a look at my notes here to make sure I get it right. First and foremost is the release of these 15 million barrels of oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve from the Department of Energy. Number two is responsibly increasing oil production and providing incentive there to oil companies to get on board with this saying this, listen, you increase oil production domestically, we`ll buy that oil when we look 15, 20 years out, and there`s less of a need for that oil, especially as we`re moving more towards efforts to institute clean energy in our country.\par \par \uc2 And then number three, asking oil companies to pass off their profits onto the consumer, right? Or their savings as he put it. Talking about the profits that oil companies are making right now to pass those numbers off to the consumers, something that "Politico" reported that we talked about a little bit earlier.\par \par \uc2 Want to bring in now former press secretary under President Obama and now an MSNBC political analyst, Robert Gibbs.\par \par \uc2 Robert, great to talk to you. The president really making a major pitch there, right, three weeks out from the midterms as I mentioned. One of the top issues for Americans right now, the economy and specifically oil prices, the writing has been on the wall there, right? Essentially since this decision came out from Saudi Arabia along with OPEC P, and the president now saying, OK, here`s what we`re going to do about it.\par \par \uc2 Let me first get your reaction to what we just heard from the White House.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, obviously I think it`s very smart policy to increase the amount of oil that`s out there on the market. The increase in supply should, as the president talked about, drive down the cost of that oil and drive down the cost of refining it into gasoline.\par \par \uc2 We know that it takes gasoline to move products from and through the supply chain. So every part of transportation is really vital to our economy and driving down gas prices whether it`s for consumers or businesses as they move their products should help alleviate the cost, the high cost of inflation.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: What do you make of this incentive the president is essentially pitching to oil companies across this country, right. We see this country is moving towards clean energy, right, 15 to 20 years out. We`ve heard commitments, for instance, from the state of California, right. The writing is on the wall for these oil companies that the need is not necessarily going to be there as it is right now.\par \par \uc2 So you`ve got the president essentially saying, listen, let`s increase domestic oil production, get on board with this plan, and we will buy it back, if you`re selling it to us $70 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, I think it`s also a smart policy. I mean, look, we know what happens to the price of oil during recessions because the demand for it goes down, which means the price of a barrel of oil goes down.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: If you`re an oil company and you don`t want to produce or drill for or, you know, capture that oil at that moment because let`s say oil is $40 a barrel, then there`s a disincentive for you to begin to do that. If the president and the administration describe a price floor that companies can get and are guaranteed to get, that should increase their incentive to not worry about the market going up and down, particularly if we were to find ourselves in a recession.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:12]\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Tell me what`s going on behind the scenes here, if you will, right? Because you then have the president saying to oil companies, pass on your profits to the consumer. We have heard this across the board for the last couple of months. You got customers paying $5 at times at the peak, right, over the summer, a gallon of gas. But you have oil companies making record profits in lockstep with this, right?\par \par \uc2 So behind the scenes, is the president having conversations with these oil companies to say you got to do something? You cannot continue to profit this way while the American people continue to suffer? And what would incentivize them to get on board with this plan?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well, I`d imagine whether it`s the president, the vice president or members of the Cabinet that are having these discussions, I would absolutely believe that`s the case. I mean, they`ve probably been having these discussions for a while, and I`m sure ramped those discussions up at the announcement of \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and OPEC Plus changing their output numbers. And so I think it`s incredibly important that the president and his administration stay in touch with these business leaders. Look, everybody wins if the economy continues to grow, if inflation comes down.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: And I think that`s what the president and the administration are trying to guide these companies to be good actors in doing.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: You know what the president did today, even in this pretty short speech, he kind of talked the American people through what has been done, what is actually happening, right? He even talked through some of the things that he feels as if are misnomers that are out there about the process of gas prices and oil prices.\par \par \uc2 I`m wondering if you feel as if the administration is doing enough of that when it comes to inflation as well, right? We`re continuing to see inflation go up over and over again, along with you got Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  increasing interest rates over and over again as well, and the market`s just going crazy.\par \par \uc2 Is the administration doing enough to explain to the American people that you kind of have to be patient with a process like this? We`re not necessarily going to see results from essentially these rate hikes, right?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Right.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: And lowering inflation in the immediate future?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, I think the administration is doing a good job at this. Look, it is hard to have a message of economic patience at a time in which prices are high. I do think it was important you heard him say here that he understands what`s going on in the economy and he understands what the American people are going through.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I think it`s important that a leader that speaks about the economy speaks about it and lives in it in the world in which consumers are living in it. Look, he`d love to wave a magic wand and be rid of this. The challenge that we see is countries all over the world are dealing with this, right? We know what`s happening in Great Britain. We know what`s happening throughout Europe.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing even higher inflation costs in those places because of supply chain constraints coming off of COVID.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: And then what`s happened with Ukraine. I think it`s imperative that he keeps pushing this. He keeps having this discussion, and look, as politicians are on the ballot in three weeks, they`ve got to have this discussion with voters, too.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: So what else needs to be done, Robert, right? Because you`re absolutely right. You see what`s happening in the U.K. specifically. Their energy prices are insane right now, inflation is through the roof. They`re having major uphill battles, especially when it comes to their leadership as well and what`s happening there.\par \par \uc2 But when you are an American at home and you`re going to the grocery store and I say this every day and you`re paying $200 for a family of four, you know, you can`t afford that every week, right? So what are they not doing? What should they be doing that you feel as if they are not doing?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I don`t know that there is a list of things. I doubt there`s a list of things that they could be doing that they aren`t in reality.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I can`t imagine that this isn`t part of every day`s worth of meetings at the White House, figuring out whether it`s something that they can do through the Strategic Petroleum Reserve like they did today or other things that help alleviate the costs of everyday products for everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 Again, it is one of those things as I said, the economic message of patience is both a harder one to deliver and probably even a harder one to hear. But I think they`re actively involved in this. Look, it`s why, to your point, if you look at where we are versus Europe, we`re paying higher prices but less than they`re paying. So look, I think some of what we`re doing is working even through really the impacts of a pandemic and really through the impacts of, you know, somebody as crazy as Putin doing what he`s done in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:01]\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`ve talked about this for a while, right? We knew that he -- this incursion into -- invasion into Ukraine was going to cause wheat prices to spike. What was that going to go to global prices for food? We knew what was going to happen and we`ve talked about it for a while. This really is something that having to be managed globally and I think they`re doing the best job they can.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: Robert Gibbs, thanks for sticking with us through it all. Appreciate it. Great to talk to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back, everybody.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN: All right. At any moment, we could get more details on the former president`s deposition, the defamation suit filed by writer E. Jean Carroll. She is suing Trump for allegedly defaming her in 2019 after she accused him of raping her back in the mid-1990s. Carroll`s suit claims Trump ruined her reputation when he denied her allegations by saying she was, quote, "not my type" and only motivated by money. We`ll keep you posted when that happens.\par \par \uc2 And then new developments in the special masters review of documents seized from Mar-a-Lago. That special master asking former President Trump`s legal team to pony up the evidence as to exactly why certain documents should be shielded from the Justice Department`s review.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 In a phone call with Trump`s lawyers yesterday, Judge Raymond Dearie used a `80s pop culture reference, telling the lawyers this, quote, "What is the expression, where`s the beef? I need some beef."\par \par \uc2 And the family of George Floyd have announced they will file a $250 million lawsuit against Ye, the rapper formally known as Kanye West, for making false claims about Floyd`s death. West was on the hip-hop podcast "Drink Champs" where he questioned the cause of George Floyd`s death suggesting it was due to fentanyl use and not due to the actions of Derek Chauvin, the former police officer who knelt on Floyd`s neck for nine and a half minutes, and was convicted of murder back in April of 2021.\par \par \uc2 That wraps it up for me, everybody. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian. Thanks for watching. You can catch me again this Friday at 2:00 p.m. Eastern as well as every Saturday and Sunday from 2:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. Katy Tur takes it over next after a very quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 With their lead slipping away in the polls, the Democrats are trying to reframe the issue of the economy. With gas prices back on the rise, President Biden announced the release of 15 million barrels of petroleum from the Strategic Oil Reserve.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221020eiaj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2