ump can tell bold face laughable lies about the election, instigated deadly attack on the Capitol, stoke bigotry and hatred, and today, a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll shows that 71 percent of Republicans are comfortable voting for a candidate who thought the 2020 election was stolen. Sixty-five percent of Republicans say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 Who would think (ph) \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans who follow him are in fact a threat to democracy?\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports that to 291 Republican candidates, take a look at that, those are the ones in red and yellow, are -- have denied -- these of people running for office in this year`s midterm elections, they`ve denied the outcome of the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Republicans` refusal to acknowledge the legitimacy of the 2020 election two years later is a direct consequence of what we saw during the January 6th attack on the Capitol. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  instigated his mob of supporters to attack the Capitol to stop the certification of the election results. We all saw it. We all watched it.\par \par \uc2 And if you didn`t or you forgot that the January 6 committee is laid it out in painstaking detail with an undeniable conclusion. And as of tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has faced no penalty whatsoever for his role in it. He was acquitted you will recall in his impeachment trial by Republican senators. He`s not been indicted by Attorney General Merrick Garland`s Justice Department. Some of the foot soldiers have been, but none of the masterminds behind the January 6th insurrection have been charged. I`m using the term mastermind rather loosely this evening.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-one percent of Republicans are comfortable voting for a candidate who thought the election was stolen because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  instigated and attack on our democracy and he got away with it, but maybe not for long. NBC News reports that the January 6th committee is likely to issue a subpoena tomorrow to the twice impeached former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the vice chairman of the committee, said this about the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): We`ll be issuing a subpoena shortly both for his testimony under oath, as well as for documents and we`ll take whatever next steps we have to take, you know, assuming that he will fulfill his legal obligation and honor the subpoena. But if that doesn`t happen, and we will take the steps we need to take after that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. This subpoena, it could put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the sights of the Justice Department if he defies it like his buddy \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  who`s now possibly facing six months in prison for contempt for ignoring that subpoena. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is already under federal investigation for stealing classified documents, government records for January 6. He`s facing dire financial jeopardy from a civil fraud lawsuit in New York.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is set to be deposed in an entirely different thing, a defamation lawsuit brought by this woman, E. Jean Carroll, the columnist who alleges that he raped her in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, he is still free to attack democracy on his social media site and rallies for election deniers et cetera.\par \par \uc2 To protect democracy, you actually have to enforce the rules of a democracy, which means holding people who break those rules accountable even if one of those people is a former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TERRI MCCULLOUGH, CHIEF OF STAFF: They dissuaded him from going to the capital. They told him they don`t have the resources to protect in their, so at the moment is not coming.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): If he comes, I`m going to punch him out. I`ve been waiting for this, for trespassing on the Capitol grounds. I`m going to punch him out, I`m going to go to jail and I`m going to be happy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now for what it`s worth, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  acknowledged in that clip that she`d accept a jail sentence for breaking the law. She never said she`d lie. She never said she denied -- she said she wanted to punch \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out and she said she`d go to jail for it.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  spoke with my colleague Andrea Mitchell about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I would have punched him out, I would go to jail and I would`ve been happy to do so for our country.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC HOST: Would you have done it?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: He would`ve had the courage to come to the Hill. He`s all talk. But let me just say this. Our democracy is at stake when you define democracy as integrity of the vote. They want to suppress the vote. They`ve been doing that for a long time.\par \par \uc2 They want to nullify the results of an election. They are even proposing that after an election if they don`t like the results, they will change the rules that would`ve governed that election retroactively. So you have to recognize that they are undermining our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And if people think that they can be casual about the, they just don`t realize how serious the Republicans are about undermining it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, the question is. How serious are we about understanding that they are undermining democracy and stopping them?\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight, Joyce Vance, former United States attorney, professor at the University of Alabama School of Law, co-host of the very interesting podcast "Sisters in Law". Also, Paul Butler, professor of law at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , former federal prosecutor. They`re both MSNBC legal analysts.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, good evening -- good evening to both of you.\par \par \uc2 So let me ask you this, 71 percent of voters, according to "New York Times"/Siena poll say democracy is at risk, 7 percent identified it as the most important problem facing democracy.\par \par \uc2 I have issues with us. If you actually think democracy is at risk, how does this -- how does this fall to the bottom of the pile of things you think we need to solve right now?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know there are all sorts of memes out there, Ali, that say well, I was worried about the price of a tank full of gas so I didn`t worry as much about democracy when I voted. I think the problem here is the messaging that`s being done around this issue.\par \par \uc2 Look, people have their lives to be to live. If kids to get to school, elderly parents to take care of. They have a lot of pressing needs when it comes to kitchen table sorts of issues. So the responsibility that our politicians and our leaders have is to help people understand the concerns about our Democratic institutions. Without those institutions and the continued success of our form of government, then things will get much worse. And the cost of a tank of gas will be the least of our worries.\par \par \uc2 But it`s difficult to get people to stop what they are doing and focus on this, and those issues. That`s the challenge Democrats face heading into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I guess the issue Paul is that, look, as you know, I`m an economics reporter. I`m a business guy. I fully get why a tank of gas or a gallon of milk or the rent in your house, of the price of used car is really, really important.\par \par \uc2 It`s just not as important as losing democracy. We get to see this all over the world, right? In Ukraine, what they do to pay extra for attack of gas not have Russian invade them and lose their democracy.\par \par \uc2 Why are we so willing to be casual as \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said to Andrea Mitchell about this idea? We have acknowledged -- one part of our brain has acknowledged, wow, democracies actually risk but is not my main thing?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: We are not paying attention. Anybody who is not scared to death about the future of democracy in the United States, about the future of the rule of law is simply not paying attention, including not paying attention to the House panel that provided a roll roadmap for the DOJ to prosecute Trump presented overwhelming evidence that show the Trump knew he lost. He didn`t care. The fact that he lost the election didn`t mean he should stop being president.\par \par \uc2 He was fully aware that armed thugs were marching on the Capitol on January 6th and even after the mayhem and destruction have begun, Trump wanted to join the insurrectionists. He wanted to join him so badly, he literally fought the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this is tough evidence for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to ignore. The president of the United States who`s the leader of the executive branch of government was fighting to join a violent attack on the legislative branch to prevent the peaceful transfer of power. It wasn`t just a symbolic assault on democracy. It was a literal assault.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let`s talk about this a little Joyce. A bunch of legal things are happening to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the moment. He`s got to testify in the E. Jean Carroll hearing, although the last time he had to testify somewhere, he pled the Fifth 400 times I believe.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:04]\par \par \uc2 He may be subpoenaed tomorrow by the January 6th committee. He`s got the Mar-a-Lago stuff.\par \par \uc2 Where is the legal jeopardy? People ask me this. I`m sure they ask you this. They stop ministry and say is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ever going to face the consequences for anything he has ever done? Where in your opinion are we now in that process and is it likely to ever happen?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It`s everywhere at this point. I think people have a lot of difficulty believing that it will actually happen at some point, because Trump`s been so cagey about using delay to avoid any level of accountability for any type of misconduct that he`s engaged in over a large number of years. But you know in Monday on Manhattan, the criminal lawsuit against not him personally but the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , that will commence.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow in this deposition, Trump will face Robbie Kaplan who`s a streetwise lawyer who knows how to take a deposition. And it`s an open question frankly of whether he will be able to use the Fifth Amendment. These rape charges have long since passed, it`s possible he has no risk of prosecution and that he may not be able to assert that right. We`ll have to see how Kaplan handles that tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But I guess it`s good for the former president that he will have this experience with the deposition tomorrow because he will have the option I think of appearing before the January 6th committee. It`s fair to say on their timeline, it will be tough for them to enforce it. But the former president will have less choice in the matter in the very unlikely event DOJ summons him to their grand jury.\par \par \uc2 I say unlikely because DOJ doesn`t usually subpoena targets to the grand jury and it`s far more likely that it some point he`ll be using the experience against morrow to help when he testifies in his own criminal trial in regards to either the Mar-a-Lago documents or the January 6th in his role in the insurrection or both. That the say the walls are crumbling down on the former president, and although I appreciate how tough it is for people to have confidence that the legal system is up to Trump, one of these many cases will trip him up if not more than one of them.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What`s your sense of it Paul? In the case of the Mar-a-Lago situation, the handpicked literally the special master. That didn`t go the way they expected. In the case of January 6th, Trump has been blustery about it. Hey, I`m happy to talk to about, as long as it`s live, as long as I get the open mic, I`m good to go with that.\par \par \uc2 I think he still got some lawyers representing him in this matter. They can`t possibly be enamored of the idea that he`s going to go and say whatever he feels like to the January six committee. So in terms of these two things, Mar-a-Lago and January 6th, putting aside the E. Jean Carroll testimony tomorrow, give me your evaluation? To something get to Trump eventually or is this guy just Teflon?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: Yes and no. So, I think the Justice Department will make separate assessments regarding Trump`s exposure for January 6 and for Mar-a-Lago, and I think it will focus on his strongest case, but that`s a close call. Mar-a-Lago might be easiest to prove because now we have three kinds of evidence that Trump committed obstruction of justice, violated the Espionage Act. We have a least one eyewitness to will say that Trump directed him to move documents. We have the video surveillance footage of Mar-a-Lago of documents being moved. And we have the search warrant that found classified materials mixed with Trump`s own personal items.\par \par \uc2 But whether Trump is prosecuted for national security may depend on the questions we still don`t know the answers to, like what did Trump do with his top secret information. What was he planning to do with it? January 6, we don`t have to speculate there about the damage done. We know that at least five people died because of the insurrection, and many more people were injured.\par \par \uc2 As you pointed out, more than 800 people have been prosecuted but nobody at the top, Ali, which I think put some pressure on the Department of Justice to go after the big fish, including the former president.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I want to bring into this discussion, David Rohde, executive editor of news at NewYorker.com and MSNBC contributor. His latest for "The New Yorker" is about Donald Trump`s continued and relentless search for friendly judges nationwide between now and 2024.\par \par \uc2 I was intrigued by this article, David, because it seemed like the kind of article written about a person who`s actually still in office as president of the United States. But in fact it`s an ex-president who is searching for friendly judges. Tell me a little bit about this.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ROHDE, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, I think he`s lost to the Supreme Court. He`s just lost last week. He`s repeatedly lost at the Supreme Court. They turn him down a turn down is fake claims that the election was stolen. But he will just relentlessly try to find friendly judges as we said earlier.\par \par \uc2 It`s sort of a political win-win for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If he goes to court and somehow wins, he can claim that he`s being persecuted by the Justice Department as a victim and if he gets turned down by a judge, he would just then smear that judge and claim the judges are against him. The danger here in this gets into the polling you are seeing about democracy, Americans not trusting justice, not trusting courts.\par \par \uc2 Courts are supposed to be a fact finding exercise. Was the 2020 election stolen? Overwhelmingly, Republican and Democratic judges said no, but Trump is sort of chipping away at that constantly, calling into question basic fact and calling into question the fairness of our court system.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What do you make of this, David? Because, you know, Team Trump thought they got a big victory in getting Raymond Dearie as a special master. It has not worked out for them the same way.\par \par \uc2 Raymond Dearie has been incredibly critical of team Trump and in the few instances and opportunities he`s had to make decisions about things has not worked in their favor?\par \par \uc2 ROHDE: Yeah, just today, there was a conference call with judge theory in there was an argument that Trump`s lawyers and made any sort of question the basic premise. He said, where is the beef? Referencing a Wendy`s commercial.\par \par \uc2 Listen, I do think this hurts \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  politically. I am thinking a key thing in 2020 was that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the suburbs and that model Republicans are tired of all the noise and the craziness, so the more he flails around, the more he`s dragged into court, the more is accused, I think it damages him I hope and I assume in the eyes of independent voters and rebut moderate publicans.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joyce, you and Paul are both prosecutors. You understand the speed of which is just to cease to works, which, by the way, always frustrates people who wanted to move faster. And, generally, in your job, Joyce, you have not had to take into account the fact that election is coming up and people need to have this information in order to make good decisions. But that`s the reality where we are in America. Whether it`s January 6th whether it`s Mar-a-Lago, whatever the cases, the point is there`s an election coming up in three weeks from today.\par \par \uc2 And the information that can be gleaned from some of these legal inquisitions into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  might make it relevant to people who are casting a ballot, and might not. Maybe there`s nobody left who`s my needs to be changed at this point. Tell me how you square these two things. If you don`t want the justice system undermined by need for speed and politicization, on the other hand, it may be the only thing to commit some people that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is actually a dishonest person who maybe should not be the next president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So DOJ`s operating principle, really one of its prime directives is that it should do nothing to play a role in deciding who wins elections. That`s the reason for the policy we have talked to much about, where DOJ won`t indict or take major investigative steps against someone who is on the ballot within certain period of time before the election. There is been a lot of back and forth about whether that even applies to Trump. He`s not on the palette and my reading that policy quite frankly is that the prohibition is quite specific and it speaks in terms of actual candidates.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to Trump and I know, Ali, that because we have discussed so many times you object to the glacial pace at which DOJ moves, it is very frustrating when you are on the outside. But when you`re on the inside as a prosecutor, we really aren`t going at top speed, but there things it takes time. It takes time to have subpoenas return. It takes time to write and review briefs. So the fact that these involved corruption investigations will often take a matter of years isn`t very remarkable when you`re having to do the work with the resources that the DOJ can provide.\par \par \uc2 So, where does that leave us with voters? We are fortunate that we have Congress. We have the January 6th committee process. That is put an enormous amount of information out there for people who are willing to consume it. But I think that you are right. Something that may make a difference for people who have found it very difficult to move their views about the former president and his capacity to lead the country, and indictment might change that.\par \par \uc2 Criminal trials have a way of changing doubts. Nonetheless, that`s not an appropriate consideration for DOJ and we`ve heard \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  say over and over, he indicts his cases when they are ready. DOJ speaks in its pleadings and in the courtroom and not to the public and I think we will have to live with that, hopefully not with just this Justice Department but with others because we don`t want DOJ playing an outsized political role in our elections.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You are right and you are wise and to some Americans, that`s an unsatisfying answer, but I understand why you give it.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:03]\par \par \uc2 You`ve always been consistent in doing that.\par \par \uc2 I thank all three of you thank you for being with us and providing your analysis for us. Joyce Vance, Paul Butler and David Rohde, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, once again, debate night in America. In Florida, Democratic Congressman Val Demings just faced her opponent for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seat, the Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 . The congresswoman will join us next, to discuss what`s at stake in her race and beyond this November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): How long will you watch people being gunned down and first grade, fourth grade, high school, college, church, synagogue, grocery store, movie theater, a mall and a nightclub? And do nothing?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:25:19]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: In just six days, millions of voters in Florida are going to begin casting their ballots for the up term coming midterm elections. The first and only debate between Florida`s two candidates for Senate and it just a short time ago. Republican incumbent Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , faced off against Democratic Congresswoman Val Demings, the wide-ranging and at times contentious debate, especially on two top issues, abortion and one that is especially raw right now for Floridians after the sentencing of the mass shooter at the \uc2 Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School\uc2  last week, gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: You`ve done nothing, nothing to help address gun violence and get dangerous weapons out of the hands of dangerous people.\par \par \uc2 Florida, after Parkland, after you made promises that you had no intentions of keeping to the parents of Parkland, Florida passed legislation raising the age to have an assault weapon. Our primary responsibilities is the safety of Floridians, and, Senator, 24 years in election office and you have not yet risen to that occasion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): The truth of the matter is at the end of the day, Americans have a second amendment right to protect themselves. And these killers are there, if they are intent on killing as they are, they found multiple ways to get the hold of weapons and mass destruction. Every bill I`ve ever sponsored on abortion, every bill I voted for has exceptions. Every one of them does.\par \par \uc2 The extremists on abortion in this campaign is Congresswoman Demings. She supports no restrictions, no limitations of any kind.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: You have been clear that you support no exceptions, even including rape and incest. Now as a police detective who investigated cases of rape and incest, no, Senator, I don`t think it`s okay for a ten year old girl to be raped and have to carry the seed of her rapist.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, lots to unfold here. Joining us now is former Congresswoman Val Demings, a member of the House Intelligence and House Judiciary. She`s a candidate as you just are running against \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Demings, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 I -- you and I`ve talked a lot. I`ve never heard you called an extremist on abortion. Didn`t get to hear the end of that. We didn`t get to hear your response to Senator Rubio.\par \par \uc2 But what do you make of that? I mean, there are people who are -- and Florida is one of the few states in the neighborhood that is not as extreme as states around. It`s thought of a as a bit of a sanctuary for a moment. But there`s a lot of worry that abortion rights are going to go away in Florida, too. And yet, somehow, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  tried to paint you as the extremist on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Well, it`s great to be back with you. And, look, it was clear tonight certainly to me and certainly I believe to Florida voters and \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  has no clue about what his primary responsibilities are in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . It is to protect the health, safety and well-being of the people that he serves, especially during tough times. Natural disasters, mass shootings, even economic crisis, but certainly when constitutional rights are being threatened or like we have seen in the Dobbs case, a constitutional rights being taken away.\par \par \uc2 And to say I am extreme when he thinks that a ten year old girl should -- whose rape should carry the seed of her rapist? People who are desperate, that`s what they say. Desperate people do desperate things and I think the voters got a real good look tonight and who the real \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let`s talk a little bit about guns. That was an interesting comment where you set of those people are an intent on killing, they will find other ways. That`s empirically not true. In countries work count guns are not readily viable, including where I`m from in Canada, just doesn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 There are countries where there are mass shootings, and they`re -- they tend to be rare, the government tends to act and causes any loophole that caused it to happen. What \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  said tonight is untrue, but where do you go with that in a place like Florida in terms of gun restrictions?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: You know what, Ali? It`s untrue but it was also pretty ridiculous. Look, I spent 27 years as a law enforcement officer, served as a police chief and the reduction of gun violence was one of my primary concerns.\par \par \uc2 It`s about getting dangerous guns out of the hands of dangerous people. And imagine if we had a murder in Orlando as a result of gun violence and I said, you know what, y`all, let`s not investigate these cases realest take this law off the book is we can`t get them all.\par \par \uc2 That`s basically what the senator said and I`d like to know, how in the heck does he know whether if we have the proper laws in place, whether it wouldn`t result that shoe shooting would`ve happened or not? We have to do everything we can to protect Floridians and protect the American people.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:50]\par \par \uc2 So again, I hope that Florida voters got a good look at really who \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is, and what he said quite frankly with gun violence was ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 If you have listeners out there who are interested in supporting my campaign and working together to reduce gun violence, my Web site is ValDemings.com.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congresswoman, good to see you as always. Thank you for joining us on this busy evening after your debate. Congresswoman Val Demings of Florida. Always appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, today the president made it crystal clear that a fundamental freedom is on the line in this country in next month`s election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And together we`ll restore the right to choose for every woman in every state in America. Vote, you got to get out to vote. We can do this if we vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, former Texas state senator Wendy Davis. We spoke about her a little earlier in the show. She`s going to join us after the break.\par \par \uc2 [22:31:01]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER VILLAVICENCIO, OB-GYN: They want to go even further. They want a national ban. They want to put doctors -- doctors like me in jail for caring for patients with the very best fight. They want to force young victims of sexual assault, those with devastating field diagnoses and those who know they cannot be pregnant -- they want to force them to give birth.\par \par \uc2 All against a backdrop of a rising maternal mortality rate.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I want you to remember that the final say does not rest in the court now. It does not rest with extremist Republicans in Congress. And finally say about your right to choose rests with you.\par \par \uc2 So vote, vote, vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: President Biden today saying that if Democrats win in November, he`s going to sign a federal protection for abortion into law. The conservative Supreme Court overturning a constitutional right that`s existed for nearly 50 years was a shock to many, but perhaps not to people who live in red states.\par \par \uc2 They`ve endured Republican pushing for extreme agendas for years, and watch extremism seep into the Republican agenda nationwide.\par \par \uc2 For example, a decade ago, Texas Democrats led by state senator Wendy Davis in her famous filibuster were fighting an onslaught of anti-abortion bills from Texas Republicans. At the, time it was perhaps viewed in that national media as, well everything is crazier in Texas -especially conservative politics. Well, tonight we know that what was happening then in Texas was a canary in a coal mine.\par \par \uc2 The new NBC film, "SHOUTING DOWN MIDNIGHT" revisits the 2013 fight against abortion, culminating in Senator Wendy Davis`s filibuster from the vantage of a post-Roe world. Have a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WENDY DAVIS, FORMER TEXAS STATE SENATOR: When I was elected to the Senate, Rick Perry was the governor and David Dewhurst was lieutenant governor.\par \par \uc2 RICK PERRY (R), FORMER TEXAS GOVERNOR: And again my goal is to make abortion in any stage a thing of the past.\par \par \uc2 DAVID DEWHURST (R-TX), FORMER LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: In Texas, we are fighting tooth and nail to protect the unborn.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Republican politicians began to get more and more extreme right and extreme evangelical and carry those agendas as part of their elections.\par \par \uc2 DAN PATRICK\uc2  (R-TX), FORMER U.S. SENATOR: Matthew 18:6 says woe be to those who would harm the little children. Better it be have a millstone around their neck and drop in the depth of the sea.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Abortion became a way of coalescing support around the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREGG ABBOTT (R-TX): After Roe versus Wade, we kept fighting to restore the sanctity of lives we are fighting to restore the sanctity of life one life at a time and yes, one law at a time.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: And thus began a slow and steady march intruding upon that right in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, just try and remember back to this -- SB5. The bill that Wendy Davis was fighting in 2013 banned abortion after 20 weeks in addition to putting onerous requirements on abortion care facilities. SB8 -- the law that now Governor Abbott signed last year bans abortion after six weeks before many women know that they are pregnant and included an absolutely bonkers bounty law that would allow anyone to sue for up to $10,000 in court costs and any person that can sue any person involved in any way with an abortion.\par \par \uc2 However, the overturning of Roe has made abortion illegal in Texas and, performing an abortion in Texas could now get you thrown into prison for life.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Wendy Davis, former Texas state senator, the founder of Deeds not Words which is focused on access to reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 Her story and how it inspired girls and young women to get involved in politics is the focus of "SHOUTING DOWN MIDNIGHT", the first installment of a new MSNBC film documentary series called "THE TURNING POINT" which is executive produced by Trevor Noah. It debuts Sunday at 10:00 p.m. Eastern here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:58]\par \par \uc2 Wendy, good to see you. Thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thank you for having me Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It`s kind of remarkable because your fight against an abortion law which you thought at the time was draconian, probably one of the best known in American history. Everybody remembers you filibustering that. That law was far less bad than the one that passed in Texas and that has now been put into place as a result of the collapse over Roe.\par \par \uc2 I mean Texas has gone from bad to worse in terms of reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes. I mean who could`ve imagined Ali that at the time that would be the good old days right? 20-week abortion ban and shutting down almost every clinic in the state is what we were fighting back then.\par \par \uc2 And as terrible as it was, I think we understood deep in our gut that this day would likely come if we couldn`t bring enough attention to this issue and if we couldn`t bring a call to action forward for voters to understand exactly how very threatened that right was.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I remember earlier this year people younger than both of us saying this isn`t going to happen. Roe is established law. Every Supreme Court justice to answer that question. It`s not going to end. The right to an abortion is enshrines in law in America. Did you know ten years ago that that loss then could result in what we see today?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: I feared that it could rally but I always believed that people were going to finally pay attention to the fact that it was as threatened as it was. I felt like that moment in Texas would be a clarion call for people to understand that they needed to stop voting for Republican lawmakers who were telling them that they were going to take this right away.\par \par \uc2 And somehow, people continued to believe that it would never happen, and now here we are. And as President Biden said today, it`s in our hands now to make sure that we reclaim that right and the only way that we can do it is through voting.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: let me ask you about one thing that your filibuster did in 2013. It influenced a lot of young people who had grown up their entire life with abortion protections. Those young people are now in a position to vote, they are in a position to do things about it.\par \par \uc2 Do you hear from them? Do you have a sense of the influence that you had on young girls and women and men decided that your filibuster was their clarion call?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Well, that is the beautiful story in this film "SHOUTING DOWN MIDNIGHT", Ali, it`s the story of not of me, it`s a story of young women who got activated that day and have gone on to do incredible things.\par \par \uc2 And they are just a microcosm, the young women across our state and across this country who have seized their power, owned their voices and begun to move into the policy arena in a really profound way.\par \par \uc2 And the film at the end of the day is really a story of hope, a story of what can happen, the ripple effects that we can make when we stand up and we fight against an injustice and what those ripple effects can do moving forward.\par \par \uc2 And I`m certainly encouraged by what I see from young women across our state and across this country today, and I`m just so humbled to have been able to play such a small part hopefully in motivating what they are doing now.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Wendy Davis, good to see you. Thank you for being with us. We are looking forward to seeing the first installment of the documentary.\par \par \uc2 You can watch the premier of "SHOUTING DOWN MIDNIGHT" Sunday at 10:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC and streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, separating Republican talking points from reality on the state of the economy. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:43:57]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The fate of so many issues from democracy to abortion to supporting Ukraine could depend on what happens in the economy in the next three weeks. The economy and inflation are among the top issues for voters in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates are capitalizing on high price of inflation, not that they have said what they would do to lower them but that`s not the whole picture.\par \par \uc2 And could there be any surprises in the economy over the next 21 days to change voters` minds? The reality is the economy is actually quite strong, too strong by some measures, that`s why we have inflation.\par \par \uc2 The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports employers have added nearly 6 million jobs in the past year, and the unemployment rate recently matched a 50-year low. Employers would hire even more workers if they could find them. You know this anecdotally, you heard the story.\par \par \uc2 Nationally gas prices are way down from their record. Russian invasion induced highs in June, when that was about $5 a gallon. The stock market closed up after a solid report from some "Wall Street" banks, but it`s been down for a few months.\par \par \uc2 One business reporter summed it up. "Consumers are spending, they`ve got money, they`re employed, they`ve got good credit. Companies are in very good shape, but the U.S. economy remains relatively resilient. So what gives and how do you solve it?\par \par \uc2 [22:49:49]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. This is the person I talk to when I have these kinds of questions. \uc2 Diane Swonk\uc2  is the chief economist with \uc2 KPMG\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 My old friend, it feels like you and I have had a lot of important discussions over the years that sound the same but this isn`t one of them. We`ve not had this problem about pervasive inflation and how best to solve it?\par \par \uc2 We know that the Fed is supposed to raise interest rates and that`s like putting the brakes on the economy. It causes people not to borrow and not to carry debt on their credit card. It causes companies not to borrow money to expand and in theory that slows down the economy and everything should work out.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, that`s a mathematical theory. In real life, it doesn`t always work as smoothly as that?\par \par \uc2 DIANE SWONK, CHIEF ECONOMIST, \uc2 KPMG\uc2 : No, it doesn`t. In fact, my fear is that the brakes will be hit a little bit hard given how rate hikes by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as a de facto bank of the world that they ricochet around the world and we worry about shooting ourselves in the foot when it come back around.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: People have sounded in on this and you are one of them who says that these rapid rate hikes can be destabilizing. What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 SWONK: Well I think it`s really obvious when you look at what`s going on in the housing market. We`ve seen more than a doubling of mortgage rates over the last year, and the housing market is absolutely in a recession and crashing.\par \par \uc2 And what`s been so stunning about it is we are seeing markets where there are pockets were already prices are coming down even though the supply of homes is still very tight.\par \par \uc2 Many people did not expect that and that really gets to the sort of non- linear aspect of what is happening. The destabilizing aspects that rate hikes can amplify each other overtime. And they work with a lot. And so much of the slowdown in consumer spending related to the crash in the housing market is still ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 People have begun to slow down their purchases, the big household appliances and furniture, but not to the extent that we expect. It`s in the pipeline and I think that`s very important to take into account, because the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , they have made it their goal to get the economy back to price stability, even if that means an increase in the unemployment rate.\par \par \uc2 In fact they expect an increase in the unemployment rate. The question is, can they really contain that increase once it starts to rise and that`s what we`re worried about on the other side.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So what should happen? You`re expecting that the third quarter of this year is actually going to be pretty strong, 2.3 percent growth. There`s nothing remotely recessionary about that.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got higher wages, we`ve got people spending, we`ve got all sorts of interesting things happening. But you like others suspected by next year we could tip into a recession.\par \par \uc2 So given that that`s a widely held view, what should the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  do at this point?\par \par \uc2 SWONK: Well I think they are doing what they should do in a sense of they have moved into tight monetary policy from their perspective and they should raise rates. My concern is that they should slow the pace of rate hikes to assess what is going on before they wait for all the effects.\par \par \uc2 They sort of want to get to a higher rate than I think we need to get to but maybe we do, but it doesn`t need to be a (INAUDIBLE) now that we are finally into what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  believes is restrictive territory.\par \par \uc2 Now, they could be wrong but given the fact that we`ve already got the most interest rate sensitive sector in the economy in a recession, the housing market, it seems like a good time to slow down the pace of increases. Not completely stop them, but slow down the pace so it`s not so destabilizing.\par \par \uc2 And remember, like I said, this is not just the U.S. that is doing this. Central banks around the world are raising rates at the same time in response to the Fed. and that is amplifying the effects of rate hikes and increasing the risk of not only a recession here but recession abroad. Some economies are already tipping into recession and that`s going to have an impact on the U.S. economy as well.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Diane good to see you as always, my friend. Thanks you for being with us. \uc2 Diane Swonk\uc2  is the chief economist at \uc2 KPMG\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with an update from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [22:53:56]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Hundreds of thousands of Ukrainians are without power and water as Russia continues to bombard the country with airstrikes. According to Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , almost one third of the country`s power stations were damaged in the past week alone.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Cal Perry is in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A new chapter in what is an old strategy by Russian forces to try to hit the civilian population and make it difficult if not impossible to live in these cities.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we`re seeing playing out here in the capital of Kyiv. You can see, it is almost entirely dark behind me really for two reasons. The first people are in fear, and living in fear of these airstrikes that we`ve seen continue over the past eight days. The second reason is to conserve power.\par \par \uc2 Just a few minutes ago, the president of Ukraine coming out and telling people that there is indeed an energy crisis unfolding in the country, and he`s expecting people to conserve energy and that`s why the city is dark behind.\par \par \uc2 What we have seen play out is a wave of bombings and drones heading for Ukrainian cities. Today in the capital, at least three people were killed, four others wounded as again it was the energy infrastructure targeted by Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 Today it was rockets. Yesterday it was drones and the death toll has risen from yesterday. Five people were killed in the capital yesterday alone. And as I said it`s what we`re seeing play out across the country.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:49]\par \par \uc2 PERRY: In the southern city of Mykolaiv we saw overnight shelling there. An entire apartment building collapsing, and least one person found there in that rubble. And it is these ongoing strikes that have people here worried because this was a city in many ways here in Kyiv where life was returning to normal. People were coming back to the capital.\par \par \uc2 The front where the war was, was to the east and to the south. Now the front has returned to Kyiv and it is exactly what we expect \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants, to again to make it difficult for the people of the city especially as winter approaches.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. Cal Perry in Ukraine. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to see you on "VELSHI" this weekend.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" begins right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for October 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Steve Kornacki, Stephanie Gosk \par \uc2 8,080 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: People were coming back to the Capitol, the front where the war was to the east and to the south. Now, the front has returned to Kyiv. And it is exactly what we expect \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants to again, make it difficult for the people of the city, especially as winter approaches.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC ANCHOR: All right, Cal Perry in Ukraine. Thank you. And that is tonight`s "LAST WORD". I`m going to see you on Velshi this weekend. THE 11TH HOUR begins right now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:30]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Tonight, three weeks away from the midterms, President Biden makes a new push to get voters to the polls, while Republicans stoke fears but offer no solutions. Plus, the twice impeach former president likely hours away from getting a subpoena from the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 And we go inside the ultra-right movement and it`s pushed to win over young voters, while a young Democrat fights for his political life in the Midwest, as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. With only 21 days until the election and with his party`s congressional majority in jeopardy, President Biden is making a full court press to get out the vote.\par \par \uc2 Today, he raised the stakes for the midterms promising to legalize abortion, if Democrats can keep their majorities in Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: Come this November, we`re going to see what happens all over America. If you care about the right to choose then you got to vote. That`s why in these midterm elections are so critical like more Democratic senators, so the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and more Democrats to keep control the House of Representative. And if you do your part and vote, Democratic leaders of Congress, I promise you we`ll do our part. I`ll do my part. And with your support, I`ll sign a law codified Roe in January.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Meanwhile, according to recent polling, the top issues for voters are the economy and gas prices, which have been creeping back up in recent weeks. And tomorrow, the President is addressing it. He`s expected to announce an additional release of crude oil from the strategic petroleum reserves of as much as 15 million barrels.\par \par \uc2 But before we start our discussion, I want to get the latest on where things stand in the polls. And who is better to ask than our own Steve Kornacki. He of course is at the Big Board exactly where he will be three weeks from tonight.\par \par \uc2 Steve, give us an update because it was four months ago, it was all about the red wave. Roe happened and we saw Democrats make huge progress. The President got a lot done. And now here we are, things are looking tight.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it`s been an eventful year politically, and we`ve seen that expressed in the polling. So let`s take a look at where it is right now. What you`re seeing here is the generic ballot, the average in the generic ballot. This is the poll question we ask, Hey, who do you want to control of Congress? The Democrats or the Republicans?\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans right now have an average lead. You take all the different polls out there, 2.2 points for Republicans, three weeks out. How does that compare to this point in other recent wave -- in a recent wave midterm elections? Well, you can see it`s not, it`s lower than it was in `18 when Democrats had their way. They were up seven points at this point, lower than in 2010 when Republicans had that big wave in \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s first midterm, way lower than 2006 when Democrats a George W. Bush`s second midterm is such a big year.\par \par \uc2 But it is notably here now higher than it was at this point in 2014. This was Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s second midterm. And this was a wave that was kind of a late building wave. It caught a lot of folks by surprise, but Republicans in 2014 ended up flipping the Senate. They got to their highest level in the house since 1928. And that wave didn`t really materialize in the polls until late. So I think it may be significant or notable, at least, that this -- the number now is ahead of where it was in 2014. That hasn`t been the case the last couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned here`s a clue as to what might be happening. You mentioned that polling, this is from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , they`re asking yesterday, what`s the top issue on your mind? You add the economy and inflation together, that`s 44 percent saying the economy or inflation we`re getting to near half the electorate now citing economy or inflation as their top issue.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen Republicans have an advantage on those issues, notably abortion. Democrats obviously stressing heavily, it`s at 5 percent in terms of being a top issue in this poll.\par \par \uc2 What I think this has translated into is, this is the generic ballot. This is the trendline (ph). This is the story of the year you`re getting at. You go back to May, you go back to late spring, Republicans were up four points, the generic ballot and we were talking big red wave at that point. Republicans maintain that lead. It was the end of June when that Supreme Court ruling the Roe decision came down and look what happened over the summer months. The polling tightened.\par \par \uc2 Democrats you could see here in early August actually pulled ahead. They maintain the lead.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Look at that. Well into September, Democrats were leading on the generic ballot. We were talking about could they defy history. And you can see what`s happened since and it`s -- maybe it`s the news on inflation. Maybe it`s those red -- rising gas prices, but the economy and inflation really taking center stage again and with it, it looks like has come a gain for Republicans in the polls and the generic ballot question three weeks out, does that expand? Does that stay where it is, or the Democrats have one comeback left in them?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Steve Kornacki, thank you. And remember, you can catch the second installment of the Kornacki Countdown Friday at 10 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC and streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 The midterm is coming just as the January 6 investigation enters a critical phase. Sources telling NBC News the panel charged with looking into the insurrection will likely send a subpoena tomorrow to the one man believed to be at the center have the plan to overturn the 2020 election, the twice impeach ex-President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . Earlier tonight at the Harvard University Institute of Politics committee Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  talked about the committee`s decision.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): We`ll be issuing the subpoena shortly both for his testimony under oath as well as for documents. And we`ll take whatever next steps we have to take, you know, assuming that he will fulfill his legal obligation and honor the subpoena. But if that doesn`t happen, then we`ll take the steps we need to take after that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Cheney went on to call her work with the committee the most important thing she has done professionally. Sources telling NBC the committee plans to request a date after the elections for Trump to appear and to submit documents.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight, Shannon Pettypiece, veteran journalist and our senior White House reporter for NBC News, Carol Leonnig, Pulitzer Prize winning investigative reporter with \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, let`s start with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the subpoena. Why has the committee waited? And what are their options if and when Trump ignores this thing? Because let`s be honest, he`s going to.\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Steph, I`m not surprised that the committee waited until toward the end of its investigation, because first of all, what they`ve been doing looks a lot like a criminal investigation, even though we know it`s a legislative investigation. But, you know, there are some similarities. And we ordinarily will wait until the end of any investigation to invite or subpoena the target the main person that we`re looking at why? Because by that time, we`ve gathered all of the other relevant information. And we know how we want to go at the target when and if he testifies, which brings us to the next question, will he testify? I think the odds are slim to none and slim is fading fast.\par \par \uc2 I`d be very surprised if we ever see Donald Trump`s in a witness chair before the J6 committee. And if he does manage to sort of get pulled in, I can`t imagine he`ll do anything other than invoke his Fifth Amendment right against self-incrimination exactly as he did more than 400 times when he finally landed in the witness chair up in New York in Tish James civil investigation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Given how unlikely it is that Trump will, as Glenn put it, put his butt in that chair to answer questions. Why, Carol, was it important for the committee to make this move? \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  went on tonight to say there was no disagreement within the committee about getting Trump to testify.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, THE WASHINGTON POST INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: Well, if you`ve spent this many months investigating the actions of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and if you`ve turned your lens as the committee did over and over again to Trump`s actions, Trump`s role in inciting a group of people in lighting the mat that brought people to the Capitol, in a violent way with weapons.\par \par \uc2 If you are focusing so much on that person, it is not only fair, it`s appropriate to then let them have their say give them that opportunity. And also ask as many of the lawmakers have told our reporters here at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , ask \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to correct that which he believes is inaccurate about the presentation the committee has made. That`s just good old fashioned fairness and a thorough investigation.\par \par \uc2 I -- I`m with Glenn on this one. I think the chances of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually stepping forward for these questions is really small. It reminds me a little bit of when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sashayed down to his Chief of Staff`s office in 27, forgive me, in 2018, insisting to reporters that he was planning right then and excited to be interviewed by \uc2 Bob Mueller\uc2  in the Mueller investigation of potential collusion and obstruction of justice by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He gleefully told them on the record, this is what I`m going to do.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And what I learned later along with my colleague Phil Rucker is that very moment he had left a room of his lawyers where he had been persuaded that there was no way going to sit for an interview with \uc2 Bob Mueller\uc2 , and they canceled the tentative session that they had been set up for that interview.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Carol, I`m going to take your word for it, because I have never seen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sashay anywhere. But you have spent a lot more time in the White House than I have. Shannon, President Biden has said very little about the Jan. 6 investigation. But is there hope that these hearings are going to help Democrats in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON PETTYPIECE, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: : Well, I mean, certainly, and there`s a broader message, the White House has tried to have as an undercurrent here about the threats to democracy. And we have heard the president come out very strongly talking about that. So certainly not January 6, related but gets to the bigger issue under here, and the White House trying to elevate that issue of threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, as you noted a moment ago, you put up what are those top issues that voters are concerned about, and it has been dominated, overwhelmingly by inflation and the economy. And even abortion has sort of trickled down on that ranking order of priorities. And now the White House, we saw them today trying to elevate that message around abortion and remind voters of that.\par \par \uc2 So, it is certainly something that can motivate the base, it can provide a little juice to Democrats to turn them out. But I think in the closing days of this midterm campaign messaging, we`re going to overwhelmingly hear the White House talking about the economy and inflation and growing number of Democratic strategists and pollsters pushing candidates to talk more about the economy and the inflation and make that really their closing argument as they go into the final stretch here.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Democrats can they can talk about all the things President Biden has done to address the economy, they can talk about the Inflation Reduction Act, which we knew -- law, which we knew is going to take some time to implement, but that no Republicans voted for it.\par \par \uc2 Shannon, let`s stay on this abortion thing, though. The president saying keep us in power, you do your part. And we`re going to protect a woman`s right on the other side of the election. How can you make that claim when they haven`t done it a month ago? They didn`t do it today.\par \par \uc2 PETTYPIECE: Right. And, you know, anything can happen in the Senate, and no one really knows where people are going to fall on this issue. I mean, there is theory and thinking out there that if they could get a few more seats in the Senate, they could get past this threshold where they could get past the filibuster, get past that 60 vote threshold, no one thinks there`s going to be 60 votes to codify Roe v. Wade at this point, but the calculus among Democratic strategists is if we could get to, let`s say, 52 maybe we`d have enough to get over the filibuster. And if we could remove or alter or change the filibuster in some way, we could get legislation passed codifying Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 This is all theoretical at this point, though. And I think that`s a big caveat. But it at least is the White House trying to message if you give us two more years, this is what we`re going to do. Because again, talking to pollsters and strategists, they feel like one of the things missing from Democrats argument is not just what they`ve done, but what they`re going to do going forward and something voters want to hear. So here`s something tangible they can give to voters to say, Give us two more years and we`ll at least try to deliver this important issue for you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Here`s something tangible, Carol, a new audio book from Bob Woodward reporting that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew that the letters to Kim Jong-un that he took from the White House that he brought to Mar-a-Lago, he knew that they were classified when he had them. Given that, what do you do with Trump`s claims about not taking anything classified?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: I think this is as prosecutors like Glenn used to say to me in the hallways of the courthouse, this is a bad fact, is a real bad fact for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because he`s on a paying when Woodward my great colleague asked them hey, can I see the letters you wrote to Kim Jong-un? Trump`s response, and January of 2020, remember when those boxes are getting packed up is Oh, that`s so top secret. He literally uses the term top secret.\par \par \uc2 So it`s hard to claim ignorant of the classification of some of these records now based on this point. It doesn`t mean, you know, we`re any closer to the Department of Justice saying, Oh, great, now we can charge him, but it`s a bad fact that helps build the case about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew and when he knew it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We are closer to the special master in the Mar-a-Lago case getting set up with Trump`s reindeer games. Glenn, today, Ray Dearie did a throwback, a throwback to the ad saying to Trump`s lawyers, where`s the beef? He was not talking about 