blicans haven`t. Look at the Inflation Reduction Act, no republicans voted for it. It`s going to lower health care costs, it`s going to lower energy costs, it`s going to create jobs, and it`s going to create a more fair tax system. Now, that`s going to take time, but that`s actual progress. They don`t need to avoid talking about the economy. Can`t they lean into this?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Absolutely. And I think they should. And I think to some degree, they have to. I mean, you can`t simply leave the playing field on the most important issue. And to your point, if steps they`ve taken are now law that will help seniors afford prescription drugs, your patients better afford insulin. If things like that are helpful, they should be talking about and we can`t leave the playing field on the number one issue. That`s true regardless of the other issues that you want to talk about. You`ve got to have something to say in that town hall meeting out on the stump as you meet voters on the issue that`s most on their minds.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael, the majority of voters do believe that democracy is at risk. But as Robert just said, recent polling shows the economy is their number one issue. How do Democrats get the American people to understand if we do not have a functioning democracy? We cannot have a successful economy. If you`re wondering if that`s true, go look at China and Russia and ask those people how they`re feeling?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FROMER RNC CHAIRMAN: Well, you just did it, Stephanie. I don`t have to say you just did. It is the most common sense thing in the world. Let`s just go back to January, when the President gave the first State of the Union. He should have linked the economy and destiny democracy together. You`re talking about what`s going on overseas in Ukraine with Russia, tie that into the economy, help people understand what that economically means for us, as well as what it means to us to be in that fight to preserve democracy.\par \par 