ues.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t think they`re the only issues that people are talking about. I think you have Democrats out there framing the extremist beliefs that Republicans have, whether it`s about democracy, whether it`s about January 6, whether it`s about the future of our country, as well as things like abortion.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:03]\par \par \uc2 So, I do think you`re going to have a lot of people talking about a lot of these issues. It`s why quite frankly this is an issue that was probably at half where it is now, just a few months ago.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael, one of the things the majority of Americans do feel good about has been our response to Ukraine, working with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies. And today, Kevin McCarthy suggested that a Republican controlled House could oppose aid to Ukraine. Exactly the GOP position and people are down with that.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: It`s just so ridiculous. I mean, it`s just -- here we go again.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is it true?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: No, it is true. It is true. We`re not supporting Ukraine. Republicans don`t want to support democracy and freedom. They like the authoritarian bootstrapping, by Putin, because that`s what Trump wants, that`s what Trump wants. So if we`re all down. That`s what Orban wants. We`re down with that. The hell with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 All right, so you put us in power, we may not support them, because, you know, they`re just way too much in this democracy thing. Folks, they`re telling you, I mean, you open this segment about, you know, Republicans that telling you, you know, what they`re going to do? Yes, they are. They`ve been telling us for months what they`re going to do. They`re going to do exactly what Kevin just said, Oh, we`re likely to oppose more aid to Ukraine. Oh, guess what, we`re likely to, you know, have hearings on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , we`re going to impeach \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , we`re going to go through the list. They`ve been telling us. I don`t know what more you want. So y`all sit back and worry about the economy all day long.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the party you put in power is going to be putting its money, your money into investigating the son of the President, trying to impeach the President, trying to relitigate the past wrongs to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and going after the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 So just -- folks, this is not rocket science. I mean, I get -- I get the bottom line and how people are coming out of COVID and trying to recover. But look at what has happened to us that you have one party saying you know what? These freedom fighters, what are you going to do about the women and the young women in Iran? I guess we don`t care about what`s happening to them either. People who are standing up for democracy around the globe, why the United States recesses, retreats? I just, y`all need to get it together, because you`re about to mess this thing up three weeks from tonight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, then we`ll be watching. We`ll be voting. Robert Gibbs, Michael Steele, I`m sure we will see again, between now and election nights.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the making of the ultra-conservative celebrities. That`s right. I`m going to have a one on one with the author of raising them write a new book investigating the well-funded mission, targeting millions of young Americans when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:37:38]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Traditionally, young voters have skewed democratic, a conservative activist want to change that. Charlie Kirk, founder of the right wing group Turning Point is looking to influence elections with his social media presence and radio show. While other media figures on the right try to infiltrate pop culture, like \uc2 Candace Owens\uc2  posing with Kanye West and White Lives Matter shirts at Paris Fashion Week.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Kyle Spencer. She`s the author of the new book, "Raising Them Right: The Untold Story of America`s Ultra-Conservative Youth Movement, and its Plot for Power."\par \par \uc2 Kyle helped me here. I`ve got two teenagers at home. They don`t care about politics. They don`t care about civics, it -- they know exactly who \uc2 Ben Shapiro\uc2  is. You are embedded in this movement. How does this movement work that they are able to get young people into their communities?\par \par \uc2 KYLE SPENCER, AUTHOR, "RAISING THEM RIGHT": So it`s really interesting that you say this, because one of the things that they do is they traffic and culture war issues, and they avoid really very specific political points that kids who might know more about politics would actually be able to talk about or to think about, so they stay very tribal about them, us in them. They believe this, they want to do that and sort of stay away from issues.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And how are the school based culture war issues, such a win for the right, because it seems that what public schools are trying to do is be more inclusive, one would think that would be welcoming to young people.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: I think what they really do a lot of these movements and with these people is to really create fear and to really play on that fear. And one of the groups --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What fear that the trans (ph) community is taking over the role --\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: That they`re going to be represented.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: -- which is an outright lie?\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: That they`re going to be replaced that white boys are no longer going to be able to rule the world, that other people are coming in to take their place, that immigrants are coming in, that affirmative action is bringing people of color onto campuses.\par \par \uc2 And when they stoke this fear, the group that really responds are young boys. And so --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Young white boys.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: Young white boys, and they target that audience. And remember, you know, we think of these kids as being very lefty but foreign 10 kids voted for -- young people voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So help us understand because they`ve got these influencers in this right-wing movement and they seem to be influencers that I have not seen on the left.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: The right wing and the Republican Party has become like a celebrity making machine, which can be kind of counterintuitive because we think of Hollywood as being the ally with the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:09]\par \par \uc2 But people like \uc2 Candace Owens\uc2  and Charlie Kirk, and a lot of these other people that you and I have never heard of that our kids know about. These people are created. The Leadership Institute, which is a right-wing training academy teaches kids how to go out there work social media, teaches them how to do media, it`s been $25 million a year teaching young people how to get out there and be celebrities for the cause to co-brand themselves. This is very intentional. It`s not a mistake.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How much of what they`re pushing is outright misinformation.\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: Most of it is, a lot of it is, and that`s why they like to stay away from real issues and to stay on things like Hey, that guy on your campus who`s got a beard, he`s -- he wants to -- he wants you to work, he wants to take your money, he`s lazy, he doesn`t want to do anything. They`d like to stay in these kinds of areas that are not very fact based.\par \par \uc2 And they also like to do this thing where they kind of create the straw men. Charlie Kirk is very, very popular when he tells people this is what the left -- the leftist on your campus think they`re socialists. They want to take your money. You know, he -- he`s traffics in that because then people are arguing about things they don`t even believe in.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So where do we go from here? Is it being successful?\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: I think they`re doing an excellent job. And 