nction, weirdly enough they present as a much more functional family.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For whom?\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: They work together. They do not -- \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  does not come out and talk about the leader of his party. They keep it all behind closed doors. On some level that looks particularly for voters a lot more functional than the Democrats who are young people mistrust the older people, the older people mistrust the younger people. You know, you think about messaging --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What about all those young people who came out and voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who won the last election, that one couldn`t want argue that`s more functional?\par \par \uc2 SPENCER: That was functional. And you know what happened? We had very, very high turnout -- high voter turnout with young people in that election. What motivated them? I think it was fear. And fear is not really a great motivator for the Democrats. They have to have a better way to motivate people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, they won the last election. We`ll see what happens in three weeks. Kyle, thank you for joining us, Kyle Spencer. Again, her new book, "Raising Them Right."\par \par \uc2 Coming up, one person trying to be a functional Democrat in the Senate, Mandela Barnes. He`s making an attempt next when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:48:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Not being a buzzkill. Right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So listen for the Democratic.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: And sometimes Democrats are, right. It`s like, you know, sometimes people just want to not feel as if they are walking on eggshells. And they want some acknowledgement that life is messy, and that all of us at any given moment can, you know, say things the wrong way, you know, make mistakes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: President \uc2 Obama\uc2  with a pretty tough message to fellow Democrats a week from Saturday though. He is going to be at the all important state of Wisconsin stumping for Democrat Mandela Barnes, who is in a tight Senate race against incumbent Republican Ron Johnson. With us for more is Wisconsin`s Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes himself.\par \par \uc2 Mandela, let`s start with \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s description of the party. Is he right?\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. MANDELA BARNES (D) WISCONSIN: Well, I`d say from the very beginning, we started out this campaign focused on rebuilding the middle class. That`s what it`s all about. Wisconsin is a unique state. It`s a working class state. We`re just focusing on the issues that matter, the issues that impact people in my hometown of Milwaukee, and the issues that impact people all the way in Rhinelander, where I was just said earlier today, that`s been our focus is going to continue to be our focus throughout the entirety of the campaign because that`s exactly where Ron Johnson has left people behind. And we`re drawing the contrast as best as possible. People are responding to it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You are 35 years old. Democrats have scored a lot of points on student debt and marijuana. Is that enough to get young voters excited and out voting three weeks from now?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, those are two big issues that young people are getting fired up about, but also the other issues that Ron Johnson has continuously failed us when it comes to opportunity.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:08]\par \par \uc2 Ron Johnson says we have enough jobs in the state of Wisconsin. Now think about if you`re a person who`s getting ready to go to college or person is getting ready to graduate college, and one of your U.S. Senators says we have enough jobs in the state. What does that make you think? How does that make you feel? It shows exactly how out of touch he`s been. And other issues like climate change.\par \par \uc2 And the debate, Ron Johnson said this CO2 was not harmful. And he said there was nothing that could be done about it. We`re talking about a person who`s completely given up on our future. And that`s what people are frustrated. You know, people are always frustrated at politics and politicians, but to have someone who would disregard their lives and their livelihoods in such a way. It`s got people, young people especially fired up in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not even just about how bad Ron Johnson is. This is about our values and our vision, making sure that young people know that better is possible, that things don`t have to be this way, as a millennial. My generation is the first this probably going to be worse off than my parent`s generation. And that`s hard to think about. When my granddad moved to Milwaukee after World War II, he got a union job as a steel worker walked into the door one day, walked out 35 years later. And that reality is difficult when young people are just trying to get into the middle class.\par \par \uc2 But we`re taking this message all over the state if, you know, folks want to help us out, because we are just three weeks away from beating Ron Johnson, but it won`t happen without us. And I`m encouraging people to go to mandelabarnes.com to help us out.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The people across Wisconsin know what`s on his platform, know that he`s potentially looking to reform Social Security, which people are depending on.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: So I was going to say no at first when it comes to this platform, because if you look at the ads they`re running, none of them are talking about the wonderful things that Ron Johnson has done. Nobody`s talking about his plans, because he doesn`t have any. The reality is that he`s failed us for 12 long years.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll tell you, people are aware of his threats to Social Security. His plans to put Social Security and Medicare on the chopping block. You know, he`s voted to repeal the Affordable Care Act already at a time where it`s difficult for people to get health insurance in general as a lifeline for many folks. But people are aware of what he`s doing. And we`re holding him accountable every step of the way, not just for his position on Medicare calling upon, or excuse me, Social Security calling it a Ponzi scheme, even going so far as to call it candy in one instance. The fact that he tried to turn -- overturn an election by sending fake electors to the Vice President.\par \par \uc2 You know, if young people are losing faith and trust in the process, how can they be encouraged if again, one of their U.S. senators is just trying to make things more difficult for them, whether it`s casting a ballot or whether it`s making your own healthcare decisions. He celebrated the Dobbs decision. Young people are also especially angry and upset about losing fundamental rights and freedoms that Ron Johnson has no problem chipping away at.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, you got three weeks to keep chipping away. Lieutenant Governor, thank you for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Hey, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes from the state of Wisconsin. When we come back, the woman on a mission to tell the untold stories of female scientists when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:22]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m smiling for the last thing before we go tonight, because it is all about women on Wikipedia. It turns out only 19 percent of English Wikipedia biographies are about women. And that`s actually an improvement since 2014 when it was only 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 But one British physicist noticed this and has been doing her part to narrow the gap right and close to 2,000 separate entries about women and minority scientists over the last five years. NBC News correspondent Stephanie Gosk has more on this mission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): From the Boxer Rebellion to the history of the banana. Wikipedia has instant information on everything. At least that`s what British physicist Jess Wade believed until she noticed a gaping hole in the online encyclopedias content.\par \par \uc2 JESS WADE, BRITISH PHYSICIST: Women is so underrepresented and people of color are so underrepresented.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: And then a 33-year-old got to work.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (on camera): How many entries do you have in Wikipedia?\par \par \uc2 WADE: 1,757, 1,756, but who`s counting?\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): She`s dedicated on top of a day job spent in the lab.\par \par \uc2 WADE: I get home, I have my dinner and I have my designated Wikipedia time where I just kind of research and then write and then read and then edit and then write some more.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Writing bios on women like Gladys West.\par \par \uc2 WADE: She was born in Virginia in 1930. She went to a historically black college and university. She studied math. She became a math teacher at high school and then she worked for the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  on GPS.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Jess Wade plucked her story out of forgotten history, leading to West`s 2018 induction into the U,S, Air Force Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (on camera): Nice to think that you were able to give her just a little bit of nudge.\par \par \uc2 WADE: Like I can actually do anything, right. She`s the magic. I just -- I`m just that person who just kind of taps it closer to the goal line. And then she shoots and she scores.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): Wade says society is doing better getting young women interested in math and science. But it`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 WADE: We`re not doing enough to retain them in the system. And part of that is because we`re not celebrating them.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Now with her help, everyone can type in a search and give a round of applause. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And Dr. Wade told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  quote, I`m a tiny fish in a massive sea. But I`ll keep doing everything I can to make science a more accessible and inclusive place to be.\par \par \uc2 And on that very well informed note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Steve Kornacki \par \uc2 8,481 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Good luck with that, Donald. That`s tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN. 21 days to November 8, is anyone actually prepared for what a Republican Congress represents?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): You don`t want the old Republican Party of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 . We want the Trump Republican Party. We want the America first Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, Robert Draper on his new book and new reporting and why he says the Republican Party lost its mind in pursuit of power.\par \par \uc2 Then, another DeSantis political stunt caught on tape.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Listen, I know you`re caught off guard. I understand, right? So, you have a warrant. It`s for voter fraud, OK.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The dystopian footage of Florida`s election security force. Plus --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you care about the right to choose, then you got to vote.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The new White House promise to legalize abortion if Dems keep the control. And Steve Kornacki on exactly what the election landscape looks like three weeks out when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. We`re three weeks away from the Midterm Elections. Frankly, we have no idea which party will control the House or the Senate. But what we do know is that there will be enormous consequences for the country either way.\par \par \uc2 You know, elections are run on rhetoric and issues, personality, ads, vibes, like, what`s going on in the country, how people feel, their mood. But ultimately, the thing we are all deciding as a democratic society is who will wield power and what they will do with that power, on whose behalf they will wield it, in service of what goals they will wield it?\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve got a pretty good picture of what this version of the Democratic Party, the current Democratic Party will do with that power because we`ve been seeing it over the past two years. Just look at the past two days, for just a little example. One of them, Americans can now buy hearing aids over the counter -- this has been a long battle I`ve covered for years -- without having to get a prescription from a doctor first, which will also bring down the cost of these devices which are exorbitant.\par \par \uc2 This is thanks to pressure from the Democrats on the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . It`s been a priority of the White House. It says it will move -- it will save American families as much as $3,000 on these devices, OK. That`s one thing. Here`s another example in the last 48 hours. As of yesterday, the application for student debt forgiveness is now live. Most Americans with student loans are eligible for $10,000 in relief from the federal government. Others who were low income at the time they got their loans or -- can get $20,000.\par \par \uc2 Now, for those who are eligible and watching this and procrastinating, something I tend to do when I fill in a web form, just about my least favorite thing in the world to do, I have to say, having looked at the forum today, it is remarkably simple.\par \par \uc2 So, just two concrete, recent examples, tangible policy agenda items under Democratic administration under the Biden government. Now, when you`re running as the opposition parties, as Republicans currently are, you don`t have to be that concrete. You can just run against the status quo. You can complain about gas prices and the cost of living. Inflation is eight percent.\par \par \uc2 But with Republicans within striking distance retaking the House, probably favored to retake the House, you got to ask yourself, what would that actually mean, not just on the campaign trail? What would a Republican governance, a Republican majority in the House actually look like? What would they do? And the first thing you need to understand is that it will not look like the last Republican House.\par \par \uc2 The last time Republicans held the house 2017, 2019, remember, they had unified control of the government. They control the House, the Senate, the White House. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the newly inaugurated President, which means they had an incentive for the government to function, to not cause huge crisis and disaster. They understood or honestly, at least enough of them understood that intentionally wrecking the economy, for instance, would not be in their benefit. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really got that actually.\par \par \uc2 There was also the question of who is who using who last time around? I mean, was Trump using the Republican establishment, the Mitch McConnell`s of the world or was \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and the Republican establishment using Trump? Because remember, in that period of unified governance for the Republicans, the Trump administration, right, that Congress did not really accomplish any of Trump`s big priorities. He couldn`t even get Congress, his Congress, with Republicans to fund his border wall, his signature political issue. This is even as he threatened to shut down the rest of his party`s priorities over it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Build that wall. Now, the obstructionist Democrats would like us not to do it, but believe me, we have to close down our government, we`re building that wall.\par \par \uc2 It is desperately needed, we`re going to have our wall, we`re going to get our wall.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:05:12]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Part of the problem was he promised for two years that Mexico would pay for it, which it didn`t, which meant that we all had to. And he didn`t really get his wall. Instead, in the big major legislative pushes, Republicans did what \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Washington lobbyists, and rich GOP donors wanted. They do what they always do. What is it? They cut taxes for the rich. They cut taxes for corporations. And they spent months bogged down trying to repeal ObamaCare which they ultimately failed to do.\par \par \uc2 So, that was the last time but different this time. Because if Republicans take back the House, first of all, the establishment will not be nearly as powerful as it was back then. The new House will be a party of Republicans who want to wreak havoc, who want to destroy American institutions, who operate on trolling and low trust just for the sake of it. This is the party of \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , Jim Jordan, and of course, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But here`s the other thing you got to keep in mind. With a presidential election coming up and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House, it will be in the short-term interests of the Republican House to make conditions in the country as bad as possible. It`s just simple. The worst things are for the country, while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is president, the better Republicans have a shot at retaking the White House in 2024.\par \par \uc2 So, a Republican House, yes, they will pass massive tax cuts for the rich. They will pass I think a national abortion ban for the first time. They will cut or eliminate entitlement programs. They`re already talking about doing it. But with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  holding veto power in the White House, Republicans will not be able to enact their entire dream agenda. So, what does the House of Representatives actually look like with Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy in charge and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House?\par \par \uc2 Well, first, there will be a dangerous showdown over the debt ceiling, with Republicans once again, essentially strapping a suicide vest on to hold the entire economy hostage in exchange for massive cuts to social spending programs. I`m not making this up. Listen to Kevin McCarthy. He all but said so today in the interview with Punchbowl News.\par \par \uc2 I quote him here. "If people want to make a debt ceiling for a longer period of time, just like anything else, there comes a point in time where OK, we`ll provide you more money but you got to change your current behavior. We`re not just going to keep lifting your credit card limit, right? And we should seriously sit together and figure out where we can eliminate some waste."\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s the gentle version to be clear. By eliminate some waste, I don`t know, you can mean cutting Social Security and Medicare, possibly even setting them on a track to be sunsetted entirely as some of the pros are voted on every year. Now, the last time Republicans tried to stunt with the debt ceiling back in 2011, and Lord knows I remember this well, it was mostly about sending a message to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now, they threatened to reject a debt ceiling deal, they made a biggest thing publicly, and then they extracted a bunch of excruciating, painful cuts that genuinely hurt the recovery and hurt America and Americans. But ultimately, they did shake on a deal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to announce that the leaders of both parties in both chambers have reached an agreement that will reduce the deficit and avoid default, the default that would have had a devastating effect on our economy. This compromise does make a serious downpayment on the deficit reduction we need and gives each party a strong incentive to get a balanced plan done before the end of the year. Most importantly, it will allow us to avoid default and end the crisis that Washington imposed on the rest of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It wasn`t Washington and impose that crisis, it was the Republicans, though \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  didn`t like to say that. That was 11 years ago. So, think of this. Is there any reason to believe this Republican Party in a post Trump world with Trump watching, with Trump basically acting as a kind of Speaker of the House in absentia, right, is there any reason to think they would reach an ultimate compromise with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to not default?\par \par \uc2 As we said, these Republicans have every incentive to intentionally wreck the economy if it means kneecapping Democrats in 2024. So, that`s first priority. That`s perhaps the most immediate concern with the Republican House, by no means the only one. Number two, they will also cut off military aid to Ukraine. Again, McCarthy openly admitted as much today. "I think people are going to be sitting in a recession. They`re not going to want to write a blank check to Ukraine. They just won`t do it."\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of the blank check, as a percentage of the U.S. defense budget, it`s very small. And also, keep in mind, the unified bipartisan position on Ukraine is walking in very fine line here. Since the invasion back in February, we provide enough weapons to Ukraine to fight off an unprompted, unjustified invasion in which Russians have been engaged in war crimes, but not so much as to facilitate a conflict that spills over into a war with \uc2 NATO\uc2 . It`s not easy to do.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:09]\par \par \uc2 So far, I have to say, the plan seems to be working pretty well. There`s peril on either side, believe me. Ukraine successfully and remarkably, not just keeping Russia at bay eight months in the invasion, they`ve even reseized captured land. They`ve taken out hundreds of Russian tanks. It has been one of the most startling and remarkable military performances in recent memory.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy says he`s willing to jeopardize all that. Without \uc2 USAID\uc2 , who knows we may very well see the nightmare scenario Putin`s forces marching into the Ukrainian capital of Kyiv and taking the country by force. By the way, after they did -- what they did in a town like Bucha, what do you think would happen to the Ukrainians there after that?\par \par \uc2 Having said all that, the ultimate priority of a Republican House will be the third thing that we will see. Ukraine, debt ceiling, and that will be public spectacle, painful public spectacle by any means of their disposal. Republicans will almost certainly impeach \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . You can`t predict the future. I can`t tell you what the odds are. It seems very likely to me. And we know that will because they just keep talking about it.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  of New York, the third-ranking Republican House told the New York Post, she is mulling impeaching Biden. "There`s an egregious abuse of power and high crimes and misdemeanors, that means anything is on the table." If Republicans take the House, Stefanik will be one of the most powerful people in the government. And even Fox News is warning she needs to pump the brakes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  says Republicans could move to impeach Biden next year. I think that`s a rotten idea, Brian. I don`t think people want another impeachment fiasco. I`m opposed to it. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: I am too. What happens is the vote on partisan lines, the whole country stops for two and a half weeks. And I don`t know what any -- maybe somebody writes a book that a couple of people want to buy, but for the most part, it`s a waste of time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The astounding amount of sense there. Now, it would be a waste of time, but what Brian Kilmeade seems missing there is that wasting time is exactly what they`re going to want to do. And not just that. Think about this, OK. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be the effective House Speaker. They will answer to him. Do you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  impeached? Of course, he does. Are they going to tell \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  no?\par \par \uc2 And here`s the thing, it won`t end with Biden either. So, imagine the following scenario, which again, I can`t give you odds, I`m not Vegas, I just follow the news closely and this is what I predict. Imagine a situation with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gets indicted by the Department of Justice. I think most people would agree it`s at least plausible that could happen. I don`t know if likely, but it`s a definite possibility.\par \par \uc2 If that were to happen, and Republicans control the House, what do you think is going to happen? It seems to me overwhelmingly likely they would move, Republicans in the House, to immediately impeach Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  in response. In fact, even if Garland doesn`t indict Trump, even at the Department of Justice doesn`t indict Trump, there will be called for Garland`s impeachment in any way simply because he investigated Trump at all. Just ask -- again, I`m not making this up. Just ask \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , the de facto head of the MAGA caucus.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREENE: I will not stand by and watch \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , turn the Department of Justice into a political weapon and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  sending it after parents that are addressing their school boards, people that walked in the Capitol and have been held in jail for nearly two years while Antifa and BLM riots -- rioters go free are never held accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The people she`s talking about in jail, those are the insurrections, the ones that violently bashed in the brains of cops while we all watched on video, the ones that concussed them, the ones that took their gun, those ones, that`s what she`s talking about. Greene is ready to make good on her threat and she told me -- as she told my next guest, "There`s going to be a lot of investigations. I`ve talked with a lot of members about this."\par \par \uc2 And it would be foolish to assume Greene would be a simple bit player in a Republican-controlled House. In that same interview, she also said Kevin McCarthy would have no choice but to pledge fealty to her, pursue her aggressive governing strategy. "I think that to be the best Speaker of the House, and to please the base, he`s going to give me a lot of power and a lot of leeway. And if he doesn`t, they`re going to be very unhappy about it. I think that`s the best way to read it. And that`s not in any way a threat at all. I just think that`s reality."\par \par \uc2 That is what the country, that is what all of us as citizens are facing down this November. That is what you`re voting for. If you vote Republican. It just is. That`s what you`re going to get. And again, whatever you think about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democratic Party, they do have a policy agenda. And this is the other thing to keep in mind. They genuinely do have a basic political incentive, the most basic political incentive to try to improve conditions for broad swaths of the country.\par \par \uc2 And even when they don`t have the incentive, Democrats still tend to be oriented in that direction, I got to say. Do you remember when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president and COVID hit and Democrats were willing to pass the trillions of dollars of spending in the Cares Act, and send out stimulus checks which Trump would then put his name on ahead of an election in which Trump was standing for reelection? Does anyone really believe if the tables are turned Republicans would do the same?\par \par \uc2 Robert Draper is a contributing writer for the \uc2 New York Times Magazine\uc2 . He wrote that profile of \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  that I quote it from a moment ago. He`s also the author of a new book, which is out today, Weapons of Mass Delusion: When the Republican Party Lost Its Mind.\par \par \uc2 Robert, I`ve long admired your reporting on the Republican Party, and this is no exception. First, let`s start with this question of the agenda because it`s part of the subject of your \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  profile and part of the subject you`re reporting. What do you think of what it would really mean to have a Republican House come January?\par \par \uc2 [20:15:50]\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DRAPER, CONTRIBUTING WRITER, NEW YORK TIMES MAGAZINE: Sure. Chris, a couple of things on that. The first is that, without question, they will become the party of retribution. And as \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , I suppose to her credit, said out loud to me, there`s going to be a lot of investigations. There will also be a punitive approach towards the Democratic Party that stripped her of her committee seats and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  as well. You can expect the same kind of treatment to be given to certain people like Adam Schiff, and \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , and others who are offending objects to Greene and others.\par \par \uc2 There will be a host of investigations that ultimately will result in impeachment, as you were alluding to. It`s hard to say which Article of Impeachment they`ll come up with, because \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  has filed articles of impeachment against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  literally since his first full day in office. So, that`s the first thing.\par \par \uc2 But it`s -- I don`t think that it`s just going to be a time-wasting exercise, Chris, I mean, it remains to be seen whether they can wield their power to get things done if they want to be done. But what \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  was telling me was that she believes the Republican Party -- you described the 2017 to 2019 period, when they ran the table with power and did very little with it, she said that was a disappointment to her.\par \par \uc2 She wanted it to change. And so, you could expect to see a rolling back of all climate change regulations, a nationwide ban on abortion, or rolling back on gun safety laws, the completion of the border wall, a moratorium on immigration of any kinds. These are all part of Marjorie Taylor Greene`s agenda, which she insists is essentially the MAGA agenda that basically Kevin McCarthy will have to kowtow to.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s the key thing, the dynamics. I mean, people forget that before \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  ever entered Congress, the House Republican caucus was essentially ungovernable. I mean, that`s why -- that`s why Boehner retired. That`s why \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  didn`t want to do the job and got dragged into doing it and hated it so much that he retired because just couldn`t take it anymore.\par \par \uc2 So, Kevin McCarthy is going -- and now, this is going to happen with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just off stage being like, do this do that. Like, none of those -- Kevin McCarthy doesn`t have the power to tell him -- if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says impeach \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , what`s McCarthy going to do?\par \par \uc2 DRAPER: Yes. I mean, this is not a case, Chris, where either McCarthy can attempt to herd the cats as it were, to which Boehner and Ryan and others before him tried to do, or a case where he will just simply humor people like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and others of the MAGA caucus by giving them one plum committee assignment or giving them one opportunity to offer a piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 No, her point is that the MAGA base is the center of gravity and the Republican Party. I think she happens to be right. And that if McCarthy knows what`s good for him, if he wants to not only become speaker, but to stay speaker, then he will essentially have to acquiesce to this agenda.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You write this from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . You say, in victory, Greene`s voice might well become drowned out amid the growing chorus of MAGA supplicants. Impeach Biden when she first proposed it in January 2021, eyes rolled. That was all but given House majority would set up -- seize upon some rationale to swiftly begin impeachment proceedings. Democrats were not just radical socialists but communists. Greene would even begun making this assertion back in June 2021.\par \par \uc2 Now, even the NRCC -- that`s the Republican Campaign Committee -- has solicited donations warming -- warning of creeping communism under Pelosi as Democrats. What is your understanding of how it got this bad?\par \par \uc2 DRAPER: Yes. Well, I guess there are two parts to it, Chris. One is that there is an incentive structure for getting this bad in the form of online donations in the form of social media. The more outrageous you are, the more are hyperbolic you are, the more attention that you get. The attention economy manifestly rewards this kind of behavior.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing that`s happened is that it`s not just a matter of the person who screams at the top of their lungs who gets the most money. It`s the MAGA base has been convinced over time that the Democratic Party is not just wrong about issues, it`s not just immoral, but in fact is incorrigibly evil. And this has been the belief system, of course, that underpins some of the wildest conspiracy theories. But it really has persisted, even as QAnon, for example, has been on the wane that this is a kind of existential call to arms.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the concern that some people have about \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , for example, calling herself a Christian nationalist. To her, it`s just a literal term, meaning a Christian who loves her nation. But to other people, it means that you are essentially an army chosen by God. And that`s basically what we saw at the Capitol on January 6.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:28]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I think, well, one thing I would add to that as well, I think in this -- I think it gets to this sort of agenda question which is the collapse of Reaganism basically of some sort of coherent vision of what conservatism is an ideology says about relationship between personal liberty and government and markets and it`s just there`s nothing there. There`s rage, and there`s like this hodgepodge, but there`s nothing left. And into that vacuum box, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the rest.\par \par \uc2 