hat?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 , PRESIDENT, PLANNED PARENTHOOD ACTION FUND: Well, Chris, it`s great to be here. And it`s great to be here on a day when President Biden showed up really strongly and made clear that if we get to 52 in the Senate, we actually have the opportunity to codify Roe in federal legislation, and that would be a huge win for this moment.\par \par \uc2 And I think what you`re seeing in the polls, is actually not reflecting how people think about access to abortion, right? It is for many people an economic issue, right, forcing people in this moment to carry -- force them into a pregnancy that, you know, when their families already completed or at a time when they`re not ready to yet start a family is not actually the right move when people are facing an economy that the administration is working hard to address.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that some sometimes we parse these out as single issues, but they`re really much more intersectional and that`s how voters think about it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`ve also just continued to get -- and again, one of the things that makes this challenging, right, is that the stories -- most of the stories will never know about what Dobbs has done to women, the vast majority, it`s an intimate decision. People don`t want to tell it in public. But every once in a while, every week, every other day, we get a story like this one.\par \par \uc2 This one comes out of Missouri. I want to get your reaction to it. This is a woman named Melissa Farmer. She was pregnant. She had a fetus of 17 weeks and that -- it had a preterm premature rupture of membranes at 17 weeks pregnant. There`s no chance for a baby -- she`s not going to make it. It`s putting my life in danger, Melissa farmer said. She remembers telling an aide to her state Senator Bill White. She called him up because she couldn`t get an abortion. The aide told her he would reach out to Attorney General Eric Schmidt, connected her with Choices Medical Services, which is basically an anti-abortion clinic in Joplin. She never heard back of what - - about what Schmitt said.\par \par \uc2 This woman was forced to carry a fetus with a rupture of membrane. She had to ultimately go to Illinois, because it wasn`t life-threatening enough. And there are hundreds if not thousands of stories around like this around the country. Do people know this is happening?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Chris, I mean, it`s a devastating story. And yes, we have been seeing this all along. We started seeing this in real time over the last year in Texas during SB-8. And now it`s just become more mainstream now because of the dogs decision. There are people who have unintended pregnancies that are no longer viable, and the providers are afraid to offer the best medical care to them which would be to terminate the pregnancy. The insurers, the hospitals are afraid to allow the patients to have those procedures, and so they are literally sentencing these patients to wait until they become septic in order to get care.\par \par \uc2 There`s no world where, you know, you are disrupting the kind of trust that needs to happen between a patient and provider. You`re asking providers literally to become lawyers to figure out whether or not they will become compliant in order to give them the best care. And you know, patients will be harmed by this. You know, sending somebody who could potentially be septic at any time on a plane, in a car, to drive upwards of 400 miles just to get access to abortion care when they need it is really unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 And for those people who say, this is what it`s about that you know, they are saving -- they really care about the health of the life of the mother, like this is what they are sentencing people to.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And to your point, The Wall Street Journal had a great article about navigating this, doctors navigating this terrain. This is in Tennessee. The doctor said she thought the patient needed an abortion. Tennessee has a total ban on the procedure. She decided to send the woman on a roughly six-hour ambulance ride to end her pregnancy in North Carolina where she arrived with dangerously high blood pressure and signs of kidney failure. The doctor said she kept asking if she was going to die. I kept saying I`m trying. I`m trying. We`re going to make it happen.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, exactly. So, we have been seeing this absolutely all along. We have been warning folks that this could be a consequence. And you know, and again, like what are we asking people to take on in the middle of this, you know, these medical burdens? And then we`re also asking all of the people who would have to enforce these bans. Are they going to investigate every miscarriage? Are they going to investigate every sepsis case? Are they going to investigate, you know, the real good judgment of so many providers who are really just trying to provide the best care for their patients?\par \par \uc2 This is what Dobbs has wrought. And I really do believe that, you know, come election day, people need to take these stories into consideration.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, you know, we can imagine a \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Policing Task Force knocking on the door with their bulletproof vests to ask the woman who`s had a miscarriage some questions about what happened. That is not an inconceivable universe that awaits us. \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 18, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Blayne Alexander, Garrett Haake, David Fahrenthold,  Wilfred Frost, Cal Perry, Steph Twitty, Ryan Reilly, Charles Coleman, Ali  Vitali \par \uc2 8,319 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: "ANDREA MITCHELL REPORTS". CHRIS JANSING REPORTS starts right now.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 With just a few weeks to go in the midterm still too close to call, today, President Biden is putting his time and his energy behind an issue that frankly, most Americans say is not their top priority. You heard it just moments ago he said he would introduce a bill to protect abortion rights if Democrats can hold on to Congress. It`s clearly an effort to motivate voters. His last words were vote, vote, vote. But with economic worries, not abortion, top of mind for most Americans, the question is, will it work?\par \par \uc2 Plus, would you pay more than $1,000 for a hotel room? A new House investigation finds that the Secret Service had to do just that repeatedly because that`s what the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  charged them to stay in their hotels. We`ve got details on this alleged scam, breaking it down with a reporter who knows more about Trump`s finances than almost anyone, The New York Times`s David Farenthold.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of money, nobody likes to pay baggage fees at the airport. It`s even worse if you`re being unfairly charged. But that`s what happened to customers of one major airline, so they fought back and they won. We`ll dig into the details of the $7.5 million settlement by \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , and how you might get a piece of that money.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the midterms. And as Election Day creeps closer, it seems like we`re getting less clarity and more question marks. Candidates who are supposed to be competitive are struggling. Races that weren`t supposed to be close are neck and neck. And the issue that Democrats thought would propel them to victory just three months ago, abortion, is no longer a top priority for most voters, overwhelmed in the closing weeks of the campaign by economic worries. But today, President Biden is trying to bring abortion back into the national spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you care about the right to choose, then you got to vote. That`s in why these midterm elections are so critical, elect more Democratic senators to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and more Democrats to keep control of the House of Representatives. And folks, if we do that, here`s the promise I make to you and the American people. The first bill that I will send to the Congress will be to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, the question now is whether this changes how abortion resonates in the Senate races that will decide control of Congress. In Georgia where abortion allegations have dogged Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , polls from September showed just 5 percent of voters even list it as their top issue. And in fact, last night, Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and his opponent \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  spent far more time debating the economy than abortion, but they flipped the script we`ve seen another race with the Democrat complaining about the economy and the Republican defending it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2 , (R-GA): Our recovery has been as good as any state in the country. We have had two record years of economic development.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, DEMOCRATIC GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: This is a governor who`s for the last four years has beaten his chest but delivered very little for most Georgians.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Blayne Alexander is live in Atlanta, \uc2 Andrew Tobias\uc2  is the politics and Statehouse reporter for The Cleveland Plain Dealer and Alaina Beverly is a former White House aide and served as national deputy director of African American outreach in the 2008 \uc2 Obama\uc2 -Biden campaign. It`s good to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 Blayne, we talked about these unexpected outcomes. The Senate race was expected to be pretty close, but Abrams has underperformed pretty dramatically coming out of last night`s debate. What`s the candidates` strategy for the last three weeks of these campaigns?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, you know, certainly I think it`s good news for both campaigns that voters actually heard from both candidates on the very first day, the very same day that they went to the polls for early voting. You know, these are kind of the things that they want to stay with voters going into this final stretch before Election Day. I think a couple of notable things that we can expect in the last couple of weeks, one, early voting is certainly going to be a key for both of them. It`s something that we know is something that`s strong for Democrats. We know that if the early voters, people come out and vote early, typically, that`s good news for Democrats. And a couple of democratic sources underscored that point for me today, looking at these record-breaking numbers out of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But one thing that`s interesting is that we`re also seeing that same message from Republicans. Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has several times said to his supporters, go out, vote early, we want to show early momentum. It`s something that he said on the campaign trail at events that I`ve been at, and it`s something that he repeated earlier today, even on Fox News. So, when you look at that, and also you look at the fact that Georgia now has about 1.6 million more registered voters than we did back in 2018, it certainly adds a couple of variables in there.\par \par \uc2 Now when it comes to strategy as to where those votes are going to come from. Yes, of course, it matters to Atlanta but both campaigns are really looking to other areas where typically they may not see traditional votes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 For Democrats, for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , it`s the rural areas around Georgia. She`s told me in interviews, and she`s made it clear that she knows she`s not going to take the four counties, but if she can pick up individual votes, that may be enough to make up the difference. For \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , however, he`s going to be looking to the suburbs hoping to flip people over on his side that didn`t vote for him 20 -- in 2018, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And a good reminder that even though we say three weeks away from Election Day for folks there, some of them are already voting. Elena, I want to talk about the president and his strategy. As the leader of his party, obviously, he`s trying to shift the conversation back to abortion. And I get it because when he says, look, remember how you felt that day when Dobbs came down. Because the polls were very different then, but the polls now show people are more worried about the economy. So my question is, is that the speech you should be giving right now?\par \par \uc2 ALAINA BEVERLY, FORMER NATIONAL DEPUTY DIRECTOR OF AFRICAN AMERICAN OUTREACH FOR OBAMA `08: I think that it actually is still motivating. I think that certainly as abortion rights or as the overturn of Roe v. Wade goes farther back in the rearview mirror, people need to be reminded of how important this right is, about how important it is to ensure that we have control of the House and the Senate. Because if the Republicans are to get a majority, that they would have a nationwide ban on the right to abortion and the right tool, a woman`s right to choose. And so, he`s trying to motivate our voters to get to remember how critical these midterm races are going to be.\par \par \uc2 Let`s remember that the Republicans only need one net gain in the Senate in order to have a majority. And people need to remember -- the voters need to be reminded of the fact that their vote matters not just for their local race, and not just for what is happening in their individual communities, but what it means for governance across the country on critical issues like a woman`s right to choose. Certainly, the economy is important and pocketbook issues tend to motivate voters. Just yesterday, my former boss, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was talking about how the Democrats need to be better on messaging on issues of the economy and make sure that we understand what voters are feeling. But voters are also invested in a woman`s right to choose and a woman`s right to bodily integrity. And that`s of course --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I don`t think there`s any doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY: Yes,\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The question is, do they vote on it? And I wonder if there`s a parallel, Alain, to 2010 when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  blamed the economy, for the Democrats quoting him shall lacking in the midterms. He had spent a ton of time focused on health care instead of the economy. He admitted later, it was a mistake. And so the question is. Understanding the upset, understanding how people feel strongly, especially in the Democratic Party about abortion, is Biden making the same mistake?\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY: I know -- I think it`s both end. I think that we need to be talking about the economy and economic harm and economic -- and economic hurt, as well as these critical issues that motivate the base. That will motivate women that will motivate turnout in for suburban women and for individuals who are seeing Republican leadership as being a return, a removal of rights, the stripping of rights as opposed to access to rights. I think it is both end in this instance.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, we are seeing a rebound in Republic -- in the polling for Republicans in the same way that we saw that wave in 2014. But I think that the way that you stymie that wave is by talking -- speaking about what is happening in the pocketbook, as well as what it means for larger issues of governance and the rollback of our rights.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Andrew, let me talk to you about the debate last night in Ohio, Ryan versus Vance, a race too close to call right now. It was kind of fascinating to watch them trying to keep their distance from their party leaders. And I want to show just a little part of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, OHIO: This guy goes on national TV and says I love \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  that has the audacity of accusing me of kissing anyone`s rear end. It`s pretty rich.\par \par \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, OHIO: I ran against \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , J.D., for leadership. And you have to have the courage to take on your own leaders. These leaders in DC will --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, you surely have some.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: They will -- they will eat you up like a chew toy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Andrew, you wrote about the debate, you said it didn`t break a lot of new ground, but the candidate should have more personal contempt for each other this time around. Do you think this moves the bar at all? What do you make of the overall debate last night, Andrew?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TOBIAS\uc2 , POLITICS & STATEHOUSE REPORTER, CLEVELAND.COM & THE PLAIN DEALER: Yes. So, one of the things I was looking for going into it was that it was being conducted in Youngstown and that`s basically where Tim Ryan is from, he`s representative in Congress for almost 20 years. And so, I was wondering if you might be focusing his message more on a kind of, you know, what he`s done for Youngstown, tried to speak to more like a regional message because it`s a really important political region for the state. But instead, you saw him talking about these national issues. You taught these -- I`m talking about abortion and stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And so I think it really depends on you know -- that our brother -- I think that reveals that Ryan was more speaking to people who might catch a clip on social media as opposed to kind of making a presentation to the viewers of the debate itself. And so you saw him go after Vance, saying he`s made a lot out of that comment that former President Trump made about him kissing his rear end, and as J.D. Vance said so politely, and stuff like that. And so, I really think that like I said, it`s -- it speaks to Tim Ryan, focusing on voters who might be motivated by things like abortion, by things like Jan -- the January 6 riots, and things along those lines, as opposed to maybe a broader economic message. And I think that speaks to maybe how this kind of stuff gets consumed through social media and the like.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Few months ago, Ryan was far ahead. Now, as I said, it`s too close to call. What`s your observation on the ground as you talk to voters? What`s going to make the difference in the end?\par \par \uc2 TOBIAS: I mean, this is a really generic thing to say, but I think it all comes down to turnout.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Turnout, turnout, turnout, yes.\par \par \uc2 TOBIAS: I mean, though, the polls here have shifted slightly in J.D. Vance`s favor, he`s led in the last two or so that have come out you know, it makes sense to me that President Biden be talking about abortion, trying to mobilize Democratic voters, trying to remind them of that issue, trying to meet the sort of built-in Republican enthusiasm that`s going to be there just because of backlash to you know whether it`s inflation or just generally Democrats controlling the White House. And so -- I mean, it just really depends on who shows up to vote.\par \par \uc2 You know, there`s a lot of kind of, like, chaos in this in terms of how the abortion issue might kind of scramble. People around that might generally vote, you know, maybe they`re -- you know, the so-called, you know, Republican-leaning woman who might, you know, cross over and vote for Democrats definitely who Tim Ryan`s been focusing on heavily in his race. But then, you know, we`ve seen signs of Republican voters. There`s a 25 percent increase in early vote requests in Trumbull County, which President Trump won by 10 points. That`s where Tim Ryan lives. It`s the Youngstown area. And so it`s just really going to depend on who shows up.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So you won`t be so foolish as to predict the outcome of the Senate race. How about a little baseball game between the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  and the Guardians at 4:07 p.m.?\par \par \uc2 TOBIAS: I`m hopeful that we`ll have one today. That don`t -- that didn`t happen last night, so it`s not a given but I`m hopeful.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Andrew Tobias\uc2 , Alaina Beverly, I want to ask you for your predictions either. Thank you both so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And now we`ve got another big story that we`ve been following. It`s about threats, finger-pointing, intense pressure, a combination of potentially deadly factors described in detail in the new House investigation, finding that the Trump administration cared more about optics and politics than public health during the darkest days of the pandemic. The report concludes there was a concerted effort to suppress scientific reports and bully top officials at the CDC, all in an attempt to keep the pandemic and thousands of deaths connected to it from reflecting poorly on then-President Trump.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC senior Capitol Hill correspondent Garrett Haake. First of all, Garrett, it`s great to have you back. Welcome. We knew Trump didn`t see eye to eye with a lot of his officials, but this report really digs deep into what was going on behind the scenes. What are your headlines out of this report?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris, it`s good to be back. And this report comes with receipts in the forms of transcripts with so many officials up and down \uc2 HHS\uc2 , and CDC, other agencies tasked with not just combating the coronavirus but shaping the public health policy and communications around it.\par \par \uc2 In this report, we hear from officials like Dr. \uc2 Robert Redfield\uc2 , then the CDC director who said that putting together reports on the Coronavirus with the kind of oversight they were getting from the Trump White House gave him PTSD. That he says that the agencies like the CDC were effectively muzzled, prevented from giving briefings, forced to go through the White House or the vice president`s office if they were going to give interviews.\par \par \uc2 And then even the integrity of some of the basic scientific releases. These mortality reports that were sent out on a regular basis were compromised by political officials, first, students of the Trump campaigns or the Trump White House. One individual is named here, \uc2 Michael Caputo\uc2 , who was a former longtime Trump political associate who was put in charge of comms at \uc2 HHS\uc2  early on in the pandemic. He`s described as having bully-ish behavior trying to shape all of the communications from the federal government not to be the most scientifically accurate or the most accurate for public health. But this report alleges, meant to be as maximally beneficial to the Trump White House as the pandemic transition into the summer and into the political season in the fall of 2020, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Garrett Haake, back on Capitol Hill for us, good to see you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Millions of your taxpayer dollars spent for hotel rooms, the shocking new findings from a congressional investigation into the profits for former President Trump`s travel. Plus, British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  fighting for her job, we`ll go live to London amid new calls for the new prime minister to take a hike. And the baggage fees you pay at the airport may not have been legit, why one major airline is now paying up big time.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A shocking new investigation shows that Donald Trump`s company made a fortune while housing the Secret Service agents protecting him and his family. According to the report released by the White House -- by the House Oversight Committee, \uc2 Trump Hotels\uc2  charge his protective detail more than $1.4 million during his four years in office. \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2 , who ran the company at the time, responded by saying in part "the Trump family is likely the first family in American history to have not profited off the \uc2 United States government\uc2 ." And back in 2090, he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP, SON OF FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: So, if my father travels, they stay at our properties for free, meaning, it was like you know cost for housekeeping effectively means you actually have to legally charge government something. So everywhere that he goes, if he stays at one of these places, the government actually spends a -- meaningless it saves a fortune because if they were to go to a hotel across the street, they`d be charging $500 a night, whereas, you know, we charge them like, you know, $50.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But it wasn`t $50, in fact, taxpayers were charged as much as $1,185 a night for those rooms, more than five times the recommended government rate.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in a man who won the Pulitzer Prize for his reporting on Donald Trump`s financial scandals. David Fahrenthold, an investigative reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , and an NBC News contributor. Always good to see you, David.\par \par \uc2 The House investigation found that Trump visited his own properties well over 500 times during his presidency, and Secret Service agents, of course, are required to stay at those properties because they have to provide protection. You describe it as "an arrangement in which Mr. Trump and his family effectively turn the Secret Service into a captive customer of their business." You`ve been looking into these financial dealings for years, what stands out to you from this House investigation report?\par \par \uc2 DAVID FAHRENTHOLD, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, there -- thing that we didn`t know until yesterday was the highest end that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was charging these secret service agents. We had seen that there were charges for that $1,185 or $1,160, but we didn`t know if was that one room, was that five rooms. We know now, conclusively that was just one room.\par \par \uc2 And so that -- I think the clip you played from \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  is really instructive. That`s what the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was saying throughout Trump`s presidency but that they weren`t charging it. It`s free. It`s only $50. It saves the government all this money, but couldn`t be further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 Now the records come out, we see they were charging routinely over $500 -- $600 a night for hotel rooms, just because they could and the Secret Service had no other choice. They had to be there. They were a captive customers.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If as the report suggests Trump`s family business leverage the presidency to his financial advantage, is that against the law?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: No. The president is exempt from the conflict-of-interest rules that would apply to almost any other federal government. So if you were a middle manager in the Department of Energy, you certainly couldn`t direct government business to your family business but if you`re the president, you can and so that -- there`s nothing illegal about that. In this case, that what strikes me though, is the real contrast between what they told the public, what they said they were doing, and what they were really doing behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which is something you`ve written about in other instances, as well. You also write this. The records also made clear that \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  provided a misleading account of what his company was charging, to your point. So, could there be any legal implications for family members?\par \par \uc2 FAHRENTHOLD: Out of this, I don`t -- I haven`t heard anybody say you could make a legal case against them for misrepresenting what they were saying to the public. Certainly, in their official dealings with the Secret Service, which were all hidden until these records came out, starting in 2020, they were honest about what they were charging. You know, like, we don`t have very many rooms, there`s a lot of demand, you`re going to -- you`re going to have to pay this rate of more than $1,000 a night. So, you know, person to person in those individual transactions, they weren`t duping the Secret Service, they were just misleading the public and, sadly, I think that`s not going to be a crime. They`re going not to be prosecuted for it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: David Fahrenthold, always good to have you on the program. Thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 Her agenda gutted, her popularity plummeting. Today, British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is in serious trouble a little more than a month into her first term. Two new polls show a majority of the British public and her own party wants her out, literally turning her into a joke after the economist compared her unfavorably to a head of lettuce. The Daily Star picked that up, posting a live link to a lettuce cam to see which has the longer shelf life, Truss or the lettuce. There are even betting odds over which will last longer. In an attempt to stay for job, Truss apologized in a BBC interview for her heavily criticized economic policy that sent the markets tumbling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ TRUSS, PRIME MINISTER OF THE UNITED KINGDOM: I recognize we have made mistakes, I`m sorry for those mistakes. But I fix the mistakes. I`ve appointed a new chancellor. We have restored economic stability and fiscal discipline. And what I now want to do is go on and deliver for the public.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It was Truss`s proposed tax cuts combined with a steep drop in the pound and the unsteady market that upset many in Parliament. For more, I want to bring in Sky News Wilfred Frost. Hey, Wilfred, so in spite of this lettuce joke, this is serious stuff, several members of parliament calling for Truss to be gone, including opposition parties who want a general election, and the question, not tonight, I guess is can she survive?\par \par \uc2 WILFRED FROST, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: I think, Chris, now is a question of when she goes and not if. And of course, in the meantime, we just have an unbelievably weak prime minister, particularly when it comes to economic issues highlighted by all of those U-turns you just mentioned. I think it`s unlikely.\par \par \uc2 However, there`ll be a full general election. The next one`s scheduled for December 2024. To bring that forward would require Conservative MPs to vote for it. And they`re not going to do that when they`re quite so far behind in the polls, as they are right now. But she is very much at the mercy of her own MPs. And for them, the timing of all this is really a question of when would be the least humiliating for them as a party having of course just changed their previous prime minister through the same process by getting rid of \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  earlier in the summer.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Just on the very short term, Chris, all eyes tomorrow on the prime minister`s questions in parliament where \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  will have to face the Labour leader and he`s expected to really highlight how weak she is, how many U-turns she`s done, and frankly, the fact that she`s lost her mandate that might crystallize her own MPs to move faster or small chance, it might unify them a little bit. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Small chance, you talk about the fact that they`re going to highlight how weak she is but we`ve all watched from here on the other side of the pond, just how those parliamentary gatherings can play out. What are you expecting to see tomorrow really?\par \par \uc2 FROST: Well, listen, and just yesterday, we did see \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  in Parliament briefly, but she was sitting silently, somewhat somber, somewhat humiliated behind her new finance minister who clearly stole the show and tore up her economic policies. Tomorrow, she`s the one speaking. She`s the one answering the questions.\par \par \uc2 It`s never been seen as one of her strengths, public speaking, throughout all of the hustings over the summer. She has to step up in just an unfathomably strong way to give herself any chance of re-gaining some kind of footing, some kind of respect, not just from her own party, from the opposition, and from the country. And as I mentioned, expectations aren`t particularly high.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Fred Frost, always good to see you, my friend. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Russia`s brutal attacks on civilians and critical infrastructure, what it means for Ukraine`s fight heading into the winter`s freezing months. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:44]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Russian attacks on critical Ukrainian infrastructure have left the entire country bracing for blackouts. The president, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , announced hours ago that recent attacks have already destroyed a third of the country`s power grid leading parts of Kyiv and other cities with no power or water. Just last night another brutal attack in Eastern Kyiv damaged power supplies and killed two people including a pregnant woman.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Cal Perry in Kyiv, and MSNBC military analyst Lieutenant General Steph Twitty.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Cal, this latest round of attacks looked devastating. What are you seeing on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, deadly as well. At least three people killed in the capital city today alone. Five yesterday. That brings the death toll to at least eight people dead in 24 hours, dozens others wounded. As you said the targets are these energy infrastructure specifically the power generators. You can see the city behind me tonight almost entirely blacked out for two reasons.\par \par \uc2 One, the fear of future airstrikes, two trying to conserve power. Energy officials here saying turn off your lights or start breaking out the candles. They are worried, and as you have alluded to, the winter months are just around the corner.\par \par \uc2 Now as for these drones and these rockets, we are seeing them continued to be fired in waves, that is waves of dozens of rockets and drones at once. It seems clear the intention is to overwhelm the air defense systems here in and around the capital. We can see them as they go off the air defense systems. It`s clear and you can hear the drones, and, again, it is sending them in batches as an attempt to hit these targets.\par \par \uc2 The other thing of note here, and we have continued to see this in what really is an eight-day campaign by Russia to drop as many ordnance as they can on these cities, we`re seeing them miss targets. We know what the energy infrastructure targets they`re trying to hit. They continuously miss these targets and hit civilian areas instead.\par \par \uc2 It`s one more indication of what you hear from officials here which is even if the Russian is in many ways inept or incompetent it doesn`t make them any less deadly, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cal, thank you as always for your reporting there.\par \par \uc2 General Twitty, this lack of power, water, obviously bringing misery, but also danger. And as Cal mentioned the winter months are approaching which could escalate in already desperate situation. So in your estimation how does Ukraine fight back against these relentless attacks and is the West doing enough to help?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. STEPH TWITTY (RET), NBC NEWS MILITARY ANALYST: Yes, so, obviously this is a strange in strategy. The Russians have been unable to get it done on the ground. And so this change in strategy now is to terrorize and break the will of the Ukrainian people. That`s why he`s doing this. To break the will of the Ukrainian people. And so what we have to do now, first of all, the Ukrainians have stepped up to the plate with some pretty good air defense systems. They got the S-300, they got the RST system that came out of Germany.\par \par \uc2 And what we have to do from the West is provide them more systems and the Pentagon is working towards that. \uc2 NATO\uc2  is working towards that. It`s going to take time first to train the Ukrainians and then take time to get these systems in. And so that`s what the Ukrainians will need more advanced air defense systems and radars to be able to counter this significant threat.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: General, the Russian draft, meantime, has been chaotic at best. Reports of little or even no training, lack of food, any semblance of organization, and now Moscow, the city, has become the first place to end the unpopular mobilization of troops. They say they have enough people already, but could it be a sign Putin is worried about the growing unrest among his people not just from the draft but, frankly, the number of Russian soldiers coming home in body bags?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Yes, I would agree with you. But it`s also a sign of desperation. And so it is estimated that the Russians have lost somewhere around 80,000 soldiers. That`s either killed or wounded in action. And they`re having a hard time making their numbers, that 300,000 that you know that he mentioned. He`s having a hard time doing it. A mass exodus of males left the country, plus you have moms and dads, they are unwilling to sign their kids up, and so he`s going to have a hard time meeting these numbers.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 And that`s why he`s also upping his ante with all these attacks here because again he can`t get it done on the ground. He can`t get this recruitment (INAUDIBLE), and so he`s still in a bad place now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let me ask you about is essentially the reverse of that with Ukraine, and the importance of what`s happening outside of what is clearly a determined Ukrainian army. The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports that when the air raid sirens finally stopped on Monday morning many people came out of their underground shelters and simply went to work, and Zelenskyy said the enemy can attack our cities but they won`t be able to break us.\par \par \uc2 How much does the resolve of the Ukrainian people play into the success of the Ukrainian military?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: I got to tell you this is the most fascinating thing that I`ve seen. The will not only of the Ukrainian military but the will of the people. And that provides motivation for the soldiers out there. As you know the Ukrainians have a lot to fight for. First of all, they`re fighting for their lives but they`re also fighting for the Ukrainian way of life and their democracy. And so by having the civilian population come out and try to get to some type of daily normalcy is a good thing not only for the country but also for the soldiers to see because it provides them that continued will to fight.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lieutenant General Steph Twitty, so good to have you on the program. Thank you, sir. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Shocking revelations in the Oath Keepers trial. What the group`s leader was doing days before January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And Kevin Spacey takes the stand. What the actor had to say in his own defense is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:56]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today jurors heard new details from an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent who has been investigating the right-wing Oath Keepers at the seditious conspiracy trial for five members of the militia group. The agent finished her third day on the stand by revealing more about the group`s alleged plans to stage an armed rebellion on Washington.\par \par \uc2 The January 6th plot was foiled before it could take place. When asked by a prosecutor whether the \uc2 FBI\uc2  must let a conspiracy play out before it disrupts it, the agent responded, I hope not.\par \par \uc2 For more let`s bring in NBC`s Ryan Reilly who is at the courthouse, Charles Coleman is a former prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 So, Ryan, a juror this morning was removed after testing positive for COVID but testimony continued. What more did jurors hear about today?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Reporter: So we`re hearing a lot more about what Stewart Rhodes was up to in the days up to the January 6th attack, in addition to sending texts to the legal counsel for the Oath Keepers, who he`s apparently in some sort of sexual relationship with. He also was buying a lot of weapons on his trip and prosecutors actually showed a map of all the stops that he made along the way to load up on all of these weapons as he was making his way to D.C. ahead of January 6th.\par \par \uc2 I think what`s really interesting that we`ve heard from this \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent recently is that there was sort of a dual purpose to a lot of what the Oath Keepers were up to. They were in fact providing security for a lot of these high-ranking Trump affiliated officials or people in sort of the conservative world ahead of January 6th. Of course we know well about the relationship with \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , and we know that they were providing some security for, for example, Latinos for Trump, one of the organizations that is closely affiliated with the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 So there also might be some connections that have come under more scrutiny going forward here. But essentially the \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent was saying that you don`t have to exclusively -- this does not have to exclusively be a plot to attack the Capitol. They can have this dual purpose. They can also have been working security for various officials in addition to coming around this plot to oppose the peaceful transfer of power through force.\par \par \uc2 So I think what we`re looking for coming up this week is testimony from some of the cooperators in this case because at the moment the government is about 60 percent of the way through its presentation here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Charles, central to the prosecution`s case is this allegation that the Oath Keepers were assembling a quick reaction force, essentially an armed mob, located at a hotel across the river from D.C. Prosecutors alleged that this QRF was meant to occupy Washington, D.C. and prevent the transfer of power. The DOJ has been successful getting convictions for individuals of course who breached the Capitol, but how much more difficult is it to prove a conspiracy case like this?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Well, Chris, the wider that you go, the harder that it gets. And I think that that`s the whole point of why prosecutors in this case are approaching it the way that they are in terms of laying out the overall plan, number one, so that they can then help people understand on the jury how everything that they are then presenting in terms of evidence and testimony fit into that actual plan.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of those things where you really have to sort of start at the broadest 30,000-foot view and then narrow in and zero in one by one, so as you can see where each and every one of the defendants actually played a role and played a part if you`re trying to convict all of them at the same trial. And so it is much more difficult but I think that the strategy that we`re seeing from the prosecution in this case is exactly on point. It`s what you`re supposed to do. It`s how you actually tie everyone into the central plot by explaining what the plan is and then explaining how each of them fit and played a role into the larger picture.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. So let me ask you about one of the specifics. Under cross- examination by the defense and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent acknowledged that the group`s stash of weapons was stored in Virginia, that it was not brought to D.C. on January 6th, and so the defense lawyer asked. So the armed rebellion was unarmed and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent responded the armed rebellion wasn`t over.\par \par \uc2 In a conspiracy case, does the government need to prove that an illegal act was taken or only that it was planned?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, there are different standards and in a case like this given the charges they have to go beyond mere preparation. And so mere preparations are legal standards which basically said, oh, we had an idea of what it was but when you go beyond that and you show the actual intent of what it was to take more stops -- steps, rather, into creating something that fit the plan that you`re alleging was part of the conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 Now you are getting to a big part of the actual act which is necessary in order to secure a conviction. The second piece to that of course is going to be intent. You establish the intent in a case like this by laying out the plan so that you can see exactly where they were going, exactly what they were trying to do and how they came together. And so with those two things, that`s how in a case like this, you know, the government will be able to establish a notion of conspiracy without necessarily --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So we obviously have lost Charles. I`m assuming that we still have Ryan.\par \par \uc2 And so, Ryan, I want to ask you about -- no, we don`t have Ryan either? Oh, we have Charles back. Go ahead, Charles. Finish what you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I`m here. I was saying that essentially you need both of those pieces. You need to be able to explain the plan so that you can see clearly. And then you have to be able to identify what steps beyond mere preparation the defendants took that fit in accordance with that plan so you can ultimately look for that convictional conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And Ryan, let me go back to you for a second. What`s the betting money on? Will Oath Keepers leader Stewart Rhodes take the stand?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: I think he will. That`s been his intent, and the fact he`s done a media interview which his lawyers, I don`t think, were very happy with during the course of the trial, so I do think that we`ll hear from him and will have an interesting defense essentially that he`ll be trying to make which is that -- essentially that he thought that he was -- like he was waiting on Trump to invoke the Insurrection Act and thought he was doing, everything that he was doing would be legal under his interpretation of the Insurrection Act.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Isn`t it generally believed, Charles, that the only way a defendant in a serious prosecution takes the stand is if you`re really desperate, you think you`re losing? But, ultimately, right, it`s their decision. They`re the ones whose future is on the line.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes, I think your point is a very well taken one, Chris. Most of the time you take the stand as a defendant when you feel like I`ve got nothing left to lose. So you feel like you`re behind in the score card and you want to try to give yourself a puncher`s chance to try and turn things around by taking the stand and somehow connecting with the jury. But generally speaking, it`s going to be ill-advised.\par \par \uc2 You`re stepping into an arena that you`re not familiar with against trained professionals. These attorneys are really, really willing and ready to actually pick you apart. And so it`s usually not something that attorneys recommend doing. But in most cases, if you see it occur it`s because you have an overconfident defendant or someone who just know that this is their last ditch effort.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We will continue to watch it. Charles Coleman, Ryan Reilly, thank you both, gentlemen. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Sobbing on the witness stand, disgraced actor Kevin Spacey, denied sexual misconduct allegations against him and recounted what he called his terrifying upbringing as the son of a Neo-Nazi. Testifying in his own defense in a New York federal courtroom, Spacey denied actor Anthony Rapp`s allegations that he made a sexual pass at him when Rapp was as minor.\par \par \uc2 Breaking down several times, he told jurors about his hatred of bigotry and intolerance and it comes from complicated family dynamics. (INAUDIBLE) filed a lawsuit against Spacey in 2020. His lawyers are seeking $40 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, how third party candidates in crucial races could potentially shake things up in the midterms. And baggage fees. That infuriating and pricey add-on at the airport, now a major airline is paying the price. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:50]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now polls are showing that critical \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seats and control of governor`s mansions could come down to just votes, a few votes, and making things even more complicated third-party candidates.\par \par \uc2 Here is NBC News congressional correspondent Ali Vitali.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN (I), UTAH SENATE CANDIDATE: I`m not going to Washington if we prevail to be a boot licker for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Evan McMullin isn`t kidding himself.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I think party does still matter.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: He`s just hoping Utah voters can put it aside.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I`m a registered independent.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: At least for now.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I don`t care what your party affiliation is, I don`t care who you voted for in the last election. You know, if you are committed to our core ideals as a country.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: His long-shot bid against incumbent Trump-backed Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  is as much about Utah as it is about the future of the Republican Party. One McMullin used to call himself a part of.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: There`s no going home to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for true conservatives.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: In 2016 McMullin ran as an independent for that reason, and Lee, his now opponent, actually voted for him then. But times have changed.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): Are you ready to stand with me and millions and millions of others who want four more years?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Unlike most third-party candidates, McMullin is on a one-on-one race. Democrats staying out of it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it`s important to look at the reality of the races that we`re running. A Democrat was not going to win the Utah Senate race.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: It comes as 39 percent of Americans say they`re disillusioned with the two-party system that nevertheless still reigns supreme.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): In ruby red Utah, McMullin is in uphill climb. That`s not unusual for independent candidates running in a political landscape so often cast in red versus blue. And McMullin is not the only one trying to upend the political status quo in 2022.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): Betsy Johnson is running as an independent for Oregon governor, a state that selected Democrats to the job for the last 32 years but is now rated a tossup in large part because of Johnson.\par \par \uc2 BETSY JOHNSON (I), OREGON GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I`m not a D, I`m not an R, I`m an O. I am responsible to and responsive to Oregonians.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: With a message that doesn`t stick to the party line.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:00]\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I believe in a woman`s right to bear arms and I believe in a woman`s right to bear children when she chooses.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Which is, of course, the point.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Independents choose to do it the hard way. The two entrenched parties come with their own base of support, their own base of money.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But that`s also been enough to turn some people away. In Missouri conservative John Wood briefly entertained a bid for Senate to ensure controversial \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  didn`t win the seat. When Greitens lost the primary, Wood dropped out. And ousted GOP Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  also toying with an independent bid. This one for president.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I certainly will do whatever it takes to make sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn`t anywhere close to the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But in 2022 independents could cause big election surprises.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: This is a political realignment what we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Even if they don`t stay outside the parties forever.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Is it a temporary realignment for you?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: It`s hard for me to look beyond November, to be honest. I don`t know sort of where I`ll be from a party affiliation perspective, you know, 10 years from now. I just have no idea.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Our thanks to Ali Vitali for that report.\par \par \uc2 If you`ve ever felt frustrated over those baggage fees at the airport this next story just might bring a smile to your face and potentially add a little cash to your wallet. \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  has now reached a settlement in a class action lawsuit over bogus baggage fees, agreeing to pay at least $7.5 million to customers. The settlement applies to passengers who were told their checked bags would be free or who had credit cards entitling them to free bag checks but were charged anyway.\par \par \uc2 So the settlement applies to those two sets of passengers who were incorrectly charged. They have to file claims to get a full refund. But American will have to pay up even if total claims top that $7.5 million.\par \par \uc2 And that does it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. And I`m sticking around for "KATY TUR REPORTS" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for October 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Cal Perry \par \uc2 7,834 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: About 25 years before Bill Russell got to brag repeatedly for the rest of his life, about his daughter being a graduate of Harvard Law School. Bill Russell gets tonight`s "LAST WORD" and he will get tomorrow night`s "LAST WORD" in Boston. THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:30]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE: Tonight, more evidence of the big grift. The Secret Service charge exorbitant rates at Trump properties even after he lost. Republicans are quick to criticize spending. So where`s the outrage over this taxpayer fleecing.\par \par \uc2 Plus, with just 22 days to go, which issues are driving voters to the polls and what election denying candidates are saying now about accepting new results?\par \par \uc2 Then, the new tool to help Texas parents during emergencies like school shootings, but critics say it does nothing to solve the problem. We`ll get reaction from Texas as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. Election day is now just three weeks away with control of Congress and a host of other key state and local offices all on the line. Also on the ballot, the future of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Today, early in-person voting began in Georgia, where the races include a pivotal Senate seat and a rematch for governor. One of those early voters was Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  himself, who`s battling Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and we will hear some of what Walker is telling NBC News just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the governor of Georgia Republican \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  debated Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  for the first time since their matchup in 2018.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D) GEORGIA GOVERNOR CANDIDATE: This is a governor who for the last four years has beat his chest but delivered very little for most Georgians. He has weakened gun laws and flooded our streets. He has weakened our privacy rights and our -- and women`s rights. He`s denied women`s access to reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2  (R) GEORGIA: I would remind you that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  campaign to be Joe Biden`s running mate. She supports these policies that have raised taxes on hardworking Americans in Georgians when they promised they would not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And in Ohio tonight, Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican JD Vance held their second and final debate. We`ll have more on that ahead as well.\par \par \uc2 This midterm campaign season we know this has included some disturbing and open appeals to racism. And it hasn`t ended. Just this past weekend, the former president himself added to all of it, with a social media posts out of nowhere, attacking American Jewish people questioning their loyalty to the United States. Today, the White House had this reply.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARTY: Donald Trump`s comments were antisemitic, as you all know, and insulting, both to Jews and to our Israeli allies. But let`s be clear for years, for years now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has aligned with extremist and antisemitic figures. And it should be, it should be called out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That was the White House`s statement. Republicans in office said nothing. Now of course, the former president has been out of office for nearly two years, and only now we`re learning just how much money his business was making off of us, U.S. taxpayers in a real life fleecing of America.\par \par \uc2 According to a House Oversight and Reform Committee investigation, Trump hotels charge the Secret Service agents who are there to protect Trump and his family more than five times the recommended government rate. Trump`s son Eric ran the family business while his dad was an office. And back then he denied reports that the Trump organization might be overcharging the government. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC TRUMP, \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 ` SON: You know, this is one other thing that people don`t ever kind of give us credit for anytime the government comes in, you know, if my father travels, they stay at our properties for free meaning, like, you know, cost for housekeeping effectively means you actually have to legally charge government something.\par \par \uc2 So, everywhere that he goes, if he stays at one of these places, the government actually spends a meaningless it saves a fortune. Because if they were to go to hotel across the street, they`d be charging 500 bucks a night whereas, you know, we charge them like, you know, 50 bucks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you know why no one gives them credit for that, because it`s a big fat lie. For facts sake. Let`s look at the reporting. According to NBC News, quote, in one ledger obtained by the Oversight Committee and published in the report, the Secret Service was charged $1,160 for a room at the Trump International Hotel in Washington DC for agents who are protecting \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  on March 8, 2017. The government rate for DC on that night was $242.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And according to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  quote, the committee released secret service records showing more than $1.4 million in payments by the department to Trump properties since Mr. Trump took office in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Late today, the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  issued a statement that read this in part, quote, any services rendered to the \uc2 United States Secret Service\uc2  or other government agencies at Trump owned properties were at their requests and were either provided at cost heavily discounted or free. Well, that is not true.\par \par \uc2 The numbers don`t lie, and it is not smart business practices when taxpayers are footing the bill. That is your money.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still waiting for that subpoena from the January 6 committee for testimony and documents. The official request is expected to be filed suit.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight, Luke Broadwater, Pulitzer Prize winning congressional reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , former Missouri senator and MSNBC political analyst Claire McCaskill, and civil rights attorney Charles Coleman. He`s also a former New York prosecutor and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Claire McCaskill, I see that you`ve retired to the library earlier today, I saw you in the kitchen with a Bundt cake. I`m glad you moved, it would be the only thing I would want to talk about. But we need to talk about politics. That was a Bundt cake, wasn`t it?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: It was a Bundt cake, a lemon Bundt cake.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It looked amazing. All right, let`s talk about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . I want to start by sharing a bit of his conversation with our colleague, Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Let`s talk about that moment at the debate where you flashed the badge. I don`t know if you have the badge with you. But a lot of people are wondering what was that badge? Do you have --\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: That`s a legit badge.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: What is it?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: That`s a badge that I was given by a police officer and I do have the badge.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You have it?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I carry with me all the time. It`s not -- it`s a real badge. It`s not a fake badge. It is a real badge.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Who gave you the badge?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: This badge is from -- this badge -- I have badges all over the -- all over Georgia, even from Chatham County.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Claire, at best, this is pure nonsense. At worst, it`s pathological lying. And this race is close. What is going on in the state of Georgia?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, first of all, it`s hard to every one of these races, Stephanie. I mean, everyone needs to understand that there are six Senate races that are within the margin of error, six. So this could go either way. And anybody who thinks any of these states are wrapped up, including with the guy who wants to be like Elvis, and have a lot of fake badges that he can brag about. He has an honorary badge. It`s a little bit like getting the key to the city. It means nothing other than that Sheriff kind of wanted to rub up against a great football player, just the way Nixon wanted to rub up against Elvis when he gave him his fake badge.\par \par \uc2 So this is silly. And we`re spending a lot of time on it. But early voting started in Georgia today. And if the people of Georgia do not take this seriously, they are going to get that guy with the fake badge on the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And it will not be pretty because he will be voting exactly the way the far right tells him to vote, because I have a feeling most of these positions he holds, he`s been told to take those positions by his handlers.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Luke, I remember early in the cycle, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  voice and he was worried about candidate quality. This has to be what he was talking about. But doesn`t \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  have a hand in who they chose?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Yes, well, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  early on was in fact, specifically worried about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . But he quickly came around to the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  train and is now all on board and defends \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , left and right against every one of his missteps and, you know, flaws.\par \par \uc2 You know, in the primary, it was very -- Herschel Walker`s primary opponents said, Look, if you nominate this guy, the entire campaign will be about all of his failings. He`s got a huge closet full of skeletons and it will consume the entire campaign. And that primary opponent was exactly right. That`s -- that is exactly what has happened during this race.\par \par \uc2 Now, the issue is, of course, that Georgia is about as purple a state as you could possibly imagine. The last election was so close. It`s really a 50-50 state. And so both sides are very locked in and the race really could go either way. But I do think the candidate quality has a Warnock with the edge right now, and I think he`s up by about three or four points depending on the poll.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Charles, not one Republican in office has said anything about Donald Trump`s disparaging comments about American Jews, not one.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 [23:10:07]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, Stephanie, we`ve reached a place in American politics where we are so polarized that it is not only advantageous but downright profitable to ignore consistency, and to have no regard for hypocrisy. And the reason I say that is because the GOP has, for a very long time, made it a point to talk about its notion of inclusion. And what we`re seeing right now is the complete opposite of that, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has not shown himself already to be toxic enough to candidates in their party where they can afford to go against him.\par \par \uc2 And so even though they may not personally agree with that ideology, they don`t care about consistency. They don`t care about inclusion. They don`t care about being called -- being called on things that they may have said earlier, because in this space, it may be harmful to go against something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said.\par \par \uc2 And so we`ve reached that place in terms of this dialogue, where even as this may contradict something that has been put on the table before, even as it speaks directly against the notion of inclusion, it`s something that they are not willing to speak out against, because it`s too profitable to stand in line with.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Claire, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, Trump`s in charge and Trump stands for a couple of important things. Lying is OK. Grifting is OK. I mentioned lying, lying is very important. You lie and then you lie some more and the consequences are minimal, because most of the Republicans are cowards in terms of standing up to him.\par \par \uc2 If you look at history, Stephanie, at times when Republicans did and said things, not nearly as egregious as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Republicans stood up. They stood up for democracy. They stood up for their country. They stood up in the face of bad actors in their own party, not this time. They all are hiding under their desks, they hope you`ll go away. Most of them that are in office can`t stand him. But he is in charge.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: From his private home in New Jersey or Florida, because we should remind our audience, he`s a liar and a loser.\par \par \uc2 Luke, let`s get into this example. You have a lot of reporting on it a real life example of the fleecing of America. The Trump family overcharging the Secret Service. Give it to -- give it to a straight what exactly happened because we just watched that video of Eric going, we don`t even charge anyone. I mean, we deserve more credit for it. Lie.\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yes, the House Oversight Committee released a trove of documents today which were all the bills, not all the bills, but a good amount of the bills from the Secret Service to the Trump, I`m sorry, from the Trump Hotel for the Secret Service, all the payments that were made. And it`s about $1.4 million that they could detect that were -- that was charged to the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 And what made this so galling in the view of the committee and the view of many taxpayers is that some of the rooms were coming for up to $1,200 a night. And previously the most we knew in the media that Trump had ever charged the Secret Service was about $650 at Mar-a-Lago, which people thought was a rip off at the time. This is essentially double that at the Trump International Hotel.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  put out a statement afterwards that said that, you know, they actually save taxpayers money doing this, but he did not address the specifics in the report and the documentation that the committee put out today.\par \par \uc2 And I talked with Chairwoman Maloney about this, and she said what galled her the most was over and over again, all the lies that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  told about what they were actually charging, you know, to say was $50, when it was really $1,185 is a --is a pretty big difference. So it`s not just the exorbitant it`s also the deceit in her mind.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, galling, grotesque, offensive, but here`s the thing, Charles, if it`s not illegal --\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: -- Trump`s going to keep on doing it.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: You`re absolutely right. I think there`s an important distinction between unethical and illegal. And given what we know and what we don`t know. So far, it`s very clear that this is unethical. But whether it`s illegal remains yet to be seen.\par \par \uc2 And I think that this is just the latest example of how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has on many different occasions in many different ways expose loopholes in our system. These loopholes need to be closed. They need to be closed by policy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But guess what, Charles, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been out of office for two years.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Right? Democrats said vote for us. We`re going to close these loopholes. So this can never happen again. Do you know how many loopholes have been closed?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Zero. And the thing about it, Stephanie, when you get to that point, it only makes a space for another \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to emerge to abuse this sort of thing. Look, we`ve got to be honest about how we got it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He`s not the president. Lie, cheat, steal, it`s OK.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:00]\par \par \uc2 See, when you -- when you talk about what it is to be privileged in America, these sorts of loopholes constitute hustling and this was a grand hustle executed by the Executive Office of the United States of America. And when you talk about that, it`s important to understand that many of us have almost been conditioned to expect or accept this.\par \par \uc2 So it is incumbent upon Democrats, if anyone is to take them seriously, that they have to address this with policy that addresses things like this, to close -- to close these loopholes and stop these privileged people from getting away with this type of grifting at the expense of taxpayers like me and you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But here`s the thing, Claire, everything that Charles was saying makes sense. But when you talk to the American people, they`ve had -- they`ve been conditioned to believe its Democrats. They`re the ones who overspend. They`re the ones who don`t know how to handle money. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Biden and House Democrats reckless spending and failed far left policies have plunged our economy into a recession.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Raising spending to the tune of hundreds of billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: More tax and spend will make this problem worse, not better.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY) MINORITY LEADER: Democrats chose to spend so recklessly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Democrats want to tax more, want to spend more.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They`re pushing this reckless tax and spend bill stop spending money.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So let`s get this straight, Claire. Republicans have a huge problem with what they say are Democrats overspending, but they are A, OK with a president ripping us off.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, worse than that Stephanie under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We had record debt and deficits. I sat on the floor of the Senate and watched Republicans vote, big spending bill after big spending bill.\par \par \uc2 You know, I remember \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , who`s in a race for his life out in Utah. I remember in 2010, the Tea Party, remember that? Remember that it was all about government spending. Well, \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , tomorrow would be a good day for you to stand up to government spending. Tomorrow would be a great day for you to say I`m not going to put up with the government being overcharged by anyone. And I will wait to see if he does that. I`m betting he won`t because they only care about spending when they`re the ones not doing it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Please don`t hold your breath, Claire. We care too much about you. Luke, let`s talk about this Trump subpoena. How soon could we see it happen? I mean, we know he`s going to resist.\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Right. So they`ve been working on it all weekend. They want to make sure it is airtight. I would guess that we see it tomorrow, actually, so that, you know, the Committee voted Thursday to start this process where the staff would draft the subpoena. They`ve got to go through now and determine exactly which documents they want to demand from the former president and create a pretty lengthy list of different lines of inquiry, what kinds of communications, what kinds of various documents they want to -- they want to get from the former president.\par \par \uc2 And then also to set a time and a place that to do the interview and to propose it to the former president. So that -- that`s a bit of negotiation a bit they`re working for with the committee right now. But I wouldn`t be surprised if we see it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has put out there with some conversations through his aides that he would consider doing it live. And, you know, from the discussions that committee is having that I`m aware of, they seem that they would be open to that if he were willing to do it.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don`t believe he would ever actually go through with that. No lawyer would let him do that. It would -- he be -- I mean it would be a total gift to the committee for him to do that. But, you know, that is what we`re hearing from the Trump quarters.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Doing it live baloney. Charles, new topic and a little legal clarification. \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , longtime ally of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The Department of Justice wants him sentenced to six months in prison. He`s asking for probation. And he wants it delayed pending his appeal. Does it even work that way?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: It might. This is the name of the game. And ultimately, Stephanie, what we`re headed for as a conclusion, one way or the other this -- as anticlimactic as the end of The Sopranos. And I mean that very seriously, in as much as \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  relishes being the martyr. So if he ends up in jail for six months, he comes out he`s raising money, he`s back with his platform that`s grown even larger because of the fact that he spent this time period in jail.\par \par \uc2 And if he doesn`t, then of course, the American people feel like no one was held accountable. But this is the name of the game you try to appeal the conviction. And then after that, you ask that it be stayed until the appeal is actually heard.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether the judge is actually going to be sympathetic to that is an entirely different conversation. But this is fairly routine for him to fight for something low and then to fight for a delay because it all plays to his advantage.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Claire, realistically, what do you want to see happen here?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, this -- I said this earlier today and I really mean it. This may be the most important six months sentence ever imposed. If the judge follows DOJ`s recommendation, and six months is basically within the guidelines for this blatant (ph) offense that he did. It`s -- it could be more, but they`re really aren`t factors go above the guidelines. So, six months, usually six months are for petty thefts or trespassing. Not for felonies. This is a very important crime. And I sure hope he does every day of six months.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, we`ll be watching. Luke Broadwater, Claire McCaskill, Charles Coleman, thank you all so, so much. When we come back, as early voters are heading to the polls, why how they feel doesn`t necessarily align with reality. Three weeks from the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And later, it is sadly inevitable. So when the next school shooting happens in Texas, wait until you hear the Republican plan to help children. We`ll ask a Texas lawmaker about it. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a very important Monday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:25:44]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Which was 22 days until Election Day, democracy is very much on the ballot. Yet it is still not the number one issue for voters. \uc2 New York Times\uc2  polling has the economy and inflation as the top concerns. But as we have said before, this economy is neither good nor bad, it is complicated. And how people feel about the economy does not always tell the whole story. Do not forget, we are coming out of a pandemic. And there are some very good things. Unemployment is it three and a half percent. Right now there are 10 million open jobs in this country.\par \par \uc2 And while inflation is a problem, and it remains high at 8.2, this is not a uniquely American issue. It is happening all around the world. And the inflation rate here falls right in the middle when you compare it to other countries. We are still lower than Mexico, the UK and the Netherlands.\par \par \uc2 So let`s go deeper on politics and into voter thinking with Juanita Tolliver, a veteran political strategies to progressive candidates and causes, and Tim Miller, contributor to The Bulwark and former communications director for Jeb Bush. He wrote a new book "Why We Did It."\par \par \uc2 Tim, a while back, you call this economy annoying. People don`t like the high prices, they`re struggling with them, but they were still willing to pay them. Do you think high prices are going to drive people to the polls?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: I think it`s getting annoying again, Stephanie. And there`s a period of time in the summer where gas prices were coming down, it wasn`t quite as in your face. Some of the disruption to the workplace was getting better, you know, right. If you go to your local coffee shop, there`s that period of time where there only be one barista working like why can`t, you know, what -- why am I waiting these long lines? Why does everything cost more? And these are a little bit of first world problems, of course, but they`re the types of things that people deal with day in day out. And they want to punish the party that is in charge if they`re -- if their paycheck isn`t going as far as it used to, you know, everything they have to do is kind of a pain in the butt.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that now we`re seeing that again, as gas prices begin to come back on the rise. And we have issues in the fall. And I think the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  poll today showed that there are people that don`t like the state of the economy right now. And if that is their number one priority, you know, they might take it out on the party in power.\par \par \uc2 This is something that`s happened historically. This isn`t new right now. I totally agree with everything you said the intro, but I was a global problem. And Biden doesn`t have a magic fix inflation wand, but that doesn`t change the political reality.\par \par \uc2 I think there`s some other things happening like what you`ve been talking about the show the crazy Republican candidates, the threats to democracy, Roe v. Wade, there are other things that are offsetting that. But I think that the economic issue is not something that is just, you know, we can just brush it aside. Obviously, it`s going to be central to the midterm.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, I`m not brushing it aside. But here`s what I don`t get, Juanita. If the economy is supposedly this huge thing that`s going to hurt Democrats, what policy proposals have any Republicans put forward? Right? If I want you to break up with your girlfriend for me, I`m going to have to offer you something. I`ve heard zero from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: That part Stephanie and that`s exactly what Democrats need to be calling out as they continue to paint the contrast between what they`ve done and the absolute obstruction and rejection that`s come from Republicans. Democrats need to be out there talking about we`re lowering prescription drug prices. We`re lowering and capping insulin costs. We are making hearing aids available over the counter. We`re canceling up to $20,000 of student debt and making corporations pay their fair share. That is what voters need to hear on repeat.\par \par \uc2 So not only know that help is on the way, but that Republicans won`t do squat for them. They`ve demonstrated that over the past two years, and will continue to be that way. I think if Democrats hit that messaging, right, then that will also follow voters into the polls as well as the feelings that they have about their personal finances.\par \par \uc2 And while I don`t think that`ll fully counteract that, I do think that showing that contrast will pin an explicitly clear picture about who`s actually fighting to lower costs for people and their families.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, let`s talk abortion because tonight \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  took on JD Vance`s stance on abortion during the Ohio Senate debate. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH) OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: JD and his extreme crew they want to have a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:01]\par \par \uc2 They`re not happy with people having to go to Illinois. They want people to get a passport and have to go to Canada, largest governmental overreach in the history of our lifetime. He called rape inconvenient, like this is not a guy who`s ready to protect the rights of women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ryan then pressed Vance to outline the exceptions he would accept in an abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R) OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: An exception in the case of incest for example, I`ve heard a number of pro-life people say that, but an incest exception looks different at three weeks of pregnancy versus 39 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Juanita, I can`t even hear this one more time, OK. Please clear this up. Because there -- people are not walking into abortion clinics at 39 weeks pregnant and saying I`ve changed my mind. Yet we hear this over and over from Republicans. It`s disgusting, and it`s not true. Should Democrats be doing more to clear this up?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I think Democrats are doing a lot to clear it up. And I did appreciate your tweet earlier, Stephanie, where you`re like no pregnant person is trying to get an abortion at 39 weeks. Often in that scenario it`s a tragic situation and a loss that they`re experiencing. And so that frame needs to be on repeat.\par \par \uc2 And I also appreciate \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  calling out this extremist agenda that JD Vance supports. And that paired with the ad from \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  that premiere today called Lock Her Up where they`re arresting women who are seeking abortion care, needs to be the drumbeat because this extreme ism isn`t something that these candidates are exaggerating.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are right to call it out. Because what this is, is a national ban and if JD Vance says that`s totally reasonable, voters should be scared, that should scare the crap out of voters in Ohio, that should scare the crap out of voters across the country because this is explicitly what Republicans are committed to moving forward if they take the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tim, Kristen Welker asked \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  about abortion tonight he fumbled. He bumbled. He did not tell the truth. Yet somehow his race against \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is within the margin of error. Are there people in Georgia that are truly behind \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? Or are they just voting against Democrats?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Look, a little bit of both. I think Herschel obviously has a fan base from his time as a football player and there`s some Republicans who will get on board with a lot of insane stuff. I don`t think that should be a surprise to anybody given who the last president was.\par \par \uc2 I do think though Herschel is being punished for, you know, by the voters for his obvious incompetence, obvious lack of preparedness, his compulsive lying, his hypocrisy about this -- about the abortion issue while he`s pushing one of the most extreme abortion positions in the country.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why he`s losing \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in the polls in a close race while \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  the other Republican on the ballot is beating \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  right now in the same polls. Crowd -- there are a significant number of crossover voters, the same voters they`re the reason why Georgia went for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and 2020.\par \par \uc2 Those same voters right now are voting for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and those are the key important voters in this election and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not doing himself any good waving around that phony police badge, you know, trying to appeal to that swing that -- to that swing part of the electorate. So, you know, while -- I certainly wish \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  had 8 percent instead of 46 percent, it`s true that he`s being punished by the key swing segment of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Something that should matter to all Americans if relevant of your political views is election denying. It is a huge issue in this midterms. Evan McMullin brought it up in tonight`s Utah Senate debate with \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN (I) UTAH SENATE CANDIDATE: But for you to talk about the importance of the left -- the Electoral College, I think is rich. I think you -- you know exactly how important it is. And I think you knew how important it was when you sought to urge the White House that had lost an election to find fake electors to overturn the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 Senator Lee, that was the most egregious betrayal of our nation`s constitution in its history by a U.S. senator, I believe, and it will be your legacy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And we just heard Arizona governor candidate Kari Lake refused to say whether she would honor the election results. Juanita, do people realize that if one of the two major political parties in the United States simply doesn`t accept how elections work, then democracy will fail?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: That`s absolutely right. And I think that`s a message that we`ve been hearing from Democratic candidates. And it started with President Biden calling out the threat that Republicans face if or the threat that our democracy faces if Republicans like that are elected.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake talking about you won`t accept the election results and dodging that question, even this call out of \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , that needs to happen every single day so that voters can easily identify these election deniers, because we all know about that FiveThirtyEight analysis that showed that 60 percent of voters will have at least one election denier on their ballot.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:00]\par \par \uc2 So now it`s about spot the liar, spot the election denier. And that`s something that Democrats need to call out every single day as we get closer to the midterm.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Juanita Tolliver, Tim Miller, thank you both. Before we go to break, I want to share some good news for people across this country, millions of Americans now have access to less expensive over the counter hearing aids as of today.\par \par \uc2 Under a new \uc2 FDA\uc2  rule beginning today, stores and pharmacies are now able to sell the devices without a prescription or an exam from a doctor. For those with mild to moderate hearing loss, that could mean a savings of thousands and thousands of dollars. That right there see good news.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Russian drones raining down in Kyiv, where inside Ukraine as a deadly wave of kamikaze attacks are striking the capitol when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:40:30]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR VITALI KLITSCHKO, KYIV, UKRAINE: The Russians tried to destroy critical infrastructure of our hometown. Before the winter, they want to switch off the electricity and the mega attack to our power plants stations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That is just some of what we heard from the mayor of Kyiv earlier today. Russia has attacked Ukraine`s capital city with a wave of kamikaze drones. President Zelenskyy responded by saying terror must lose and will lose. Our friend Igor Novikov, who was former adviser to Zelenskyy was not far from the strikes and described what he experienced earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IGOR NOVIKOV, FORMER ADVISER TO ZELENSKYY: This morning, I woke up to chaos. So there were air defense missile explosions, there were drone explosions, there was gunfire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Our own Cal Perry is in Kyiv as the Russian step up their attacks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Stephanie, it was the second Monday rush hour interrupted by the sounds of explosions. But these were not like the sounds we heard last Monday. These drones are unlike anything any of us have ever heard. They fly low. They`re loud. They sound like mopeds. And again, they fly very slowly through the sky so you can see them.\par \par \uc2 In fact, some of the residents here in Kyiv fired their weapons, fired their guns into the air trying to shoot them -- these drones out of the sky. Originally, officials had said that was a good idea. By the end of today, they`re saying that`s a bad idea. And people should leave it to the army and to the police. It`s one more dynamic here that`s changing on the ground. 28 of these drones fired at once. This morning, five of them making it through air defenses, and hitting that residential building, at least four people dead, another four people wounded, dozens of people rushed to hospital, it was a very difficult scene to witness as rescuers were trying to find anyone alive trapped in that rubble.\par \par \uc2 And the government has long told us to expect this. They say that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  would stop using the more expensive cruise missiles and change to these crude, cheap drones. But the damage they do is extensive. And they`re not very good at targeting individual things. Those cruise missiles are better. These drones seem to be missing the target, I can tell you. The target that the Russians were looking for an energy infrastructure target in that neighborhood. They`ve been trying to hit for more than eight days now. And they continue to miss. And they`re hitting these residential areas that are behind them.\par \par \uc2 So whether or not you`re talking about this military being incompetent, not being able to hit the target or targeting civilians directly. It really doesn`t matter because it`s deadly nonetheless. And people here continue to die in large numbers. And what the government here says is that while these battlefield gains are being made by Ukrainian troops in the East and in the south that this is one more indication, they say that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is getting more and more desperate using cheaper and cheaper weapons, trying to bring terror here to the civilian population. Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Our thanks to Cal Perry for that important report. Coming up, it is the state with the most school shootings in America. But instead of a gun safety plan, Texas schools are given out DNA tests. We`ll talk to state senator Roland Gutierrez about the plan parents are calling chilling when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:48:17]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is something no parent wants to think about, having to identify your child by DNA after an emergency. The Houston Chronicle reports this week, Texas is now distributing DNA and fingerprint kits for parents who want to participate. It is part of a law passed by the state legislature in spring of 2021 meant to help find missing or trafficked children.\par \par \uc2 But the Chronicle reports quote, some families have found the program chilling, considering the police as parents waiting to find out if their children were slaughtered at Robb Elementary to provide DNA samples to help identify the dead.\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight to discuss Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez, whose district includes Uvalde. Roland, does this underscore how little has been done in Texas when it comes to gun safety? I mean, what is the goal of this program?\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. ROLAND GUTIERREZ (D) TEXAS 19TH DISTRICT: Oh, absolutely, Stephanie. We haven`t done anything on gun safety. This governor it took -- the Florida governor 23 days when they had after Parkland to be able to enact meaningful gun common guns and solutions. Greg Abbott has taken over five months and still done nothing and has told us that he`s going to do nothing. We have a speaker of the House who says that we can`t -- we don`t have the votes to do this. The fact is they don`t have the political will.\par \par \uc2 74 percent of Republican women want to see common sense gun solution. 64 percent of all Republicans want to see common sense gun solutions like raising an age in the state of Texas to 21. Unfortunately, this law that you`re talking about it was something that probably made some sense it was about kidnapping and whatnot.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:05]\par \par \uc2 But right now, it`s probably not the best time to have school districts talk about it when people are so, so fresh in their emotions.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When I hear you say, we don`t have the votes, they`ve been given all of this. How does Greg Abbott and other Republicans in your state feel so confident that they have the votes to win in their upcoming elections, especially given what just happened?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Right. Stephanie, I think that there`s a sea change in Texas. I think that women are upset, not just because of the dog`s decision. But, you know, this situation here, you have moms and dads that bury their children that needed DNA to identify their kids. And you have Republican leaders that have chosen to do absolutely nothing to double down in the insane and say, hey, it`s unconstitutional to have an age limit when we already have one for handguns when 18 states have one, using case law that is factually distinguishable.\par \par \uc2 Greg Abbott needs to come to terms with the fact that we have to do something. We`ve done zero on accountability. We`ve done zero on common gun sense solutions. It is my hope that in the next three weeks, people certainly let them know what they think about him doing absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, the entire Uvalde School District Police Department was just suspended. What does that do?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: I think it was a step in the right direction as far as accountability, Stephanie. But we saw last week, there was a story about the Department of Public Safety, which is our state highway patrol or state troopers, and their involvement and lack of involvement, their failure to act. I feel like that story and others that have come out are showing us exactly what I`ve been talking about. The DPS certainly was in charge that they failed to act and yet, Greg Abbott doesn`t even call for any accountability.\par \par \uc2 Remember \uc2 Steve McCraw\uc2 , the director of DPS direct reports to Greg Abbott. And Greg Abbott has done nothing in that space that to ask for accountability. I hope that firing of those or suspending of those police was just a first step. And hopefully our governor or our new governor will take the right actions after November 8 to make sure that we can get rid of these people that were responsible for their inaction.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Accountability can happen in this country. It`s called Election Day. State senator Roland Gutierrez, thank you for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, I have got some very, very good news about a possible cancer vaccine on the horizon when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:41]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: We lost our son to cancer. And I think through finding purpose, which was our Cancer Moonshot that it helped me and it helped Joe sort of go on.\par \par \uc2 Cancer is one of these issues. You know, it`s not, you know, a red issue or blue shoot. Cancer affects every American family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, let`s shoot for a sweet dream. The Cancer Moonshot. Tonight, the first lady sat down with conservative media outlet \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  to discuss the administration`s efforts to end cancer. This, of course, has been a very personal issue for the Biden family, losing their son Beau to brain cancer in 2015.\par \par \uc2 In a tweet yesterday, Dr. Biden showcase the White House lit up in pink for breast cancer awareness month, saying this quote, in honor of the loved ones we`ve lost, those who are still fighting those who have survived breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 There is also a major development overseas on the possibility of a cancer vaccine. The married founders of \uc2 BioNTech\uc2 , which partnered with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  on the COVID vaccine, saying they`re using what they learned from making that vaccine to potentially create personalized cancer vaccines and get this, they could possibly be available sometime before 2030.\par \par \uc2 The way it works, mRNA teaches the immune system to recognize and target cancer cells for destruction. So they do really feel a cure for cancer is in sight. Here`s what they told the BBC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OZLEM TURECI, CO-FOUNDER OF BIONTECH: Yes, we feel that a cure of -- for cancer or to change in cancer patient`s life is in our grass (ph). Every step, every patient we treat in our cancer trials helps us to find out more about what we are against and how to address that. Yes. There was a scientist we are always hesitant to say we will have a cure for cancer. We have number of breakthroughs and we will continue to work on them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [00:00:04]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, here`s hoping for continued breakthroughs in curing this devastating disease. And on that very hopeful and optimistic note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I will see you at the end of today.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221018eiah00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Glenn Kirschner \par \uc2 8,059 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS GLOBAL NEWS EDITOR: It`s again, to try to force the city behind me into blackness. But so often, they`re missing and they`re hitting these residential areas. It`s one more indication that while the Russian army may be inept, while they may be incapable of striking targets directly, they are still capable of being very deadly and lashing out at these residential targets as they continue to lose ground on the battlefield both in the eastern part of the country, Joy, and in the southern part.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Cal Perry, thank you very much. And that is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN. An ex-president`s wild attack on American Jews condemned by the White House.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Donald Trump`s comments were antisemitic, as you all know, and insulting both to Jews and to our Israeli allies.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to Kanye West. The undeniable antisemitism of MAGA Republicans. Then --\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: This is from my hometown.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Does that have arresting authority or it`s an honorary badge.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Herschel Walker`s new explanation for his badge stunt. And a new logic-defying denial in his abortion scandal.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: She`s the mother of my child.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Plus, the fleecing of America. New details on the exorbitant prices \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  charged to have Secret Service stay at his hotels. And we have reached the sexting evidence phase of the Oath Keepers seditious conspiracy trial when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Well, this weekend, the ex-President of the United States threatened America`s Jews. On Sunday morning, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took to his fake \uc2 Twitter\uc2  website to brag about how no president has done more for Israel than him, but American Jews he said are not grateful. "U.S. Jews have got to get their act together and appreciate what they have in Israel before it is too late."\par \par \uc2 Now, to some degree, we`ve all become inured to this kind of disgusting rhetoric. It`s certainly not a new take from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who has always, always been clear that he sees Jews, Jewish Americans, first and foremost as loyal subjects of the State of Israel, not fundamentally American, fundamentally foreign. He has repeatedly referred to Israel as American Jews country, to its leaders as their leaders.\par \par \uc2 Just listen to how he spoke to the attendees at a White House Hannukah celebration in 2018.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to thank Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , a tremendous supporter, a tremendous supporter of yours. And Karen, and they go there and they love your country. They love your country. And they love this country. That`s a good combination, right? And \uc2 David Friedman\uc2  who -- I don`t know if David`s here. Your ambassador is great.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, just to be clear, he says to American Jews, your country. They love your country and this country. And I should note that \uc2 David Friedman\uc2  he`s talking about. David Freeman, he was the U.S. ambassador to Israel, not the Israeli Ambassador, which is to say, at that point, \uc2 David Friedman\uc2  was all of our ambassador as Americans. He represented our American government. He wasn`t some special foreign Jewish dignitary.\par \par \uc2 And of course, according to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , American Jews who don`t support him, are not sufficiently loyal to Israel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: In my opinion, you vote for a Democrat, you`re being very disloyal to Jewish people, and you`re being very disloyal to Israel.\par \par \uc2 We have to get the people of our country of this country to love Israel more, I have to tell you that. We have to do it. We have to get them to love Israel more because you have people that are Jewish people that are great people. They don`t love Israel enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, this has all been here in front of all of us from the beginning. It`s all a natural combination of all that, that antisemitism that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lashed out like this weekend, saying Jews better appreciate what they have before it`s too late. And again, you can interpret those exact words however you want. You can come up with defenses for why he was talking about this or that. The message and the threat are very clear. Get your act together American Jews.\par \par \uc2 And again, you can see this and you can say, well, look, it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He tried to ban a billion people across the world who were Muslim. He told the American Congresswoman to go back to their country. We all know what he`s like. And we know the Republican Party will remain utterly devoted to him no matter what awful, awful, outrageous thing he says.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. This is bigger than that. And I think it`s genuinely much more disturbing. So, take this tweet from the staff of the Republicans on the House Judiciary Committee. They`re the minority committee, although they have a good shot of being the majority. And they`ve carved out a niche, this particular \uc2 Twitter\uc2  handle -- I don`t know who runs it -- of regularly tweeting like intentionally outrageous inflammatory things.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:00]\par \par \uc2 In October 6, they wrote simply "Kanye, Elon, Trump" which is I got to say, actually a pretty effective, succinct, honest expression of their worldview. Like that`s what they like. That`s their Mount Rushmore, antisocial people who act like enormous jackasses and bullies. That is actually the simplest version of the Republican MAGA worldview. Of course, that tweet came in a moment of peak right-wing celebration of one Kanye West after he wore a T-shirt declaring white lives matter. And Fox News host \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  flew across the country to go kiss his ring and was just delighted. He invited Connie in his show-free fawning interview, introducing him as some sort of misunderstood creative genius.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , HOST, FOX NEWS: The enemies have his ideas dismissed West as they have for years as mentally ill, too crazy to take seriously. Look away, ignore him. He`s a mental patient. There`s nothing to see here. But is West crazy? You can judge for yourself as you watch what we`re about to show you. He has his own ideas. We can say that. Creative people tend to. That`s why they`re artists not actuary. But crazy? That was not our conclusion. In fact, we`ve rarely heard a man speak so honestly and so movingly about what he believes. But again, you can judge for yourself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Tucker had to sit there the whole time being like yes, yes, Kanye, more, more. By the way, Kanye West is a musical genius, absolutely. You know, Bobby Fischer was a genius at chess. You know, genius is a complicated thing. But as it turns out, the viewers of that show actually couldn`t even do the thing they were set up to do which was judged for themselves. That`s -- remember the whole setup for this as they say he`s nutts, take a look for yourself, what do you think. Because Tucker edited out several of West`s most disturbing statements, including this antisemitic comment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KANYE WEST, RAPPER: My kids are going to a school that teaches Black kids a complicated Kwanzaa. I prefer my kids knew Hanukkah than Kwanzaa. At least will come with some financial engineering.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, because the Jews, finance, they`re good with money. But that wasn`t in the interview so you couldn`t judge that based on what he said. But again, that was just the tip of the iceberg, OK, because the very next day, Kanye tweeted that he was going to go to "death con 3 on Jewish people." And then a well, a fairly deranged thing about how you -- Black people are the actual Jews which comes from a whole complicated corner of the internet I won`t get into.\par \par \uc2 But those same conservatives who were praising Kanye just days before who just decry they love nothing more than to go crazy about any perceived antisemitism for their political enemies, particularly Muslim women who are Democrats in Congress, the way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does, they were all silent, remain silent, as Kanye has said he`s going to go "death con 3 on the Jews." Not only that he hasn`t stopped.\par \par \uc2 Again, this is one day after the interview, he`s ramped up his rhetoric. Yesterday he pops out on a podcast where he rants about Jewish control the media, arguing that Jews control every influential industry and use it to exploit others while calling \uc2 Disney\uc2  a "Jewish platform" and blaming Jewish Zionist for spreading stories about his ex-wife, Kim Kardashian and her ex- boyfriend Pete Davidson. Textbook stuff, folks. Read the book.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason why the people marching with the tiki torches in Charlottesville, the very fine people, as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once described them, claimed were defending their Confederate heritage. There is a reason those men you see there were chanting Jews will not replace us and blood and soil. This is not a fluke. It`s not like oh, wow, weird. Antisemitism over there and antisemitism over there is popping up a lot.\par \par \uc2 The kind of right-wing spaces discourses that Kanye West and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are occupying, I suspect the kinds of stuff they`re getting fed into their brains by the 