ge, they didn`t even make it to the jury. So, I think the prosecutors are well advised to present all of this evidence, but it almost feels like the seditious conspiracy count itself has been proved. And now we`re all sort of waiting for the case to transition to the cooperating witnesses, the insiders who pleaded guilty, flipped, and are prepared to testify.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, just one more follow-up about Rhodes and Kellye Sorelle. I mean, that she is the lawyer putatively, but the texts between them suggesting they had some kind of other relationship were used to sort of say that you can`t protect their communications for attorney-client privilege if they had this other thing going.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, I think there was a collective wince in the courtroom when the FBI Special Agent began reading these really graphic texts. This was not the language of subtle romance, let`s just say. And it really did put the lie to this claim that it was strictly an attorney-client relationship between Rhodes and Sorrelle unless it was an attorney-client relationship with benefits.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:01]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, very well done, Glenn. Let me -- let me come to you, Danya on this -- oh, wait, we`re now showing the text. Oh, there we go. Speaking of -- you need some come over. I got my girlies -- I got my girlies for the night. I don`t need to read this on live. You get the idea.\par \par \uc2 Danya, we have -- we have a motion today from \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . The DOJ in a court filing -- so, Bannon has been convicted by a jury of his peers in Washington DC of contempt of Congress. He`s awaiting sentencing. Today, the DOJ filed -- asking for the -- six months. The defendant`s contempt of Congress is absolute undertaken in bad faith. Mandatory minimum sentence of one month in prison is insufficient to account for, punish, and deter criminal offences. Court should instead impose a sentence of six months and the top end of the Guidelines` advisory sentencing ranging, fine the defendant $200,000. What happens next in that case?\par \par \uc2 DANYA PERRY, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: So, Bannon filed his responsive pleading saying that the judge should send him to no incarceratory sentence. And moreover, the judge should stay the imposition of any sentence until they can appeal the judge`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, he wants to stay out of prison until -- for as long as he canobviously.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: He want to stay out of prison, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And that pending an appeal.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Correct. And I think he believes that I think he`s probably right, that delay is his friend here. So, we`re not talking about a big, you know, window of -- there`s only one to six months really that the judge could possibly impose. He could stay out, of course. He did make some comments in his ruling that indicated that he thought that there was something to the defense arguments that --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The judge you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Correct. Yes. So, he might do that. But I think if he does not -- I think he`s likely to hit Bannon pretty hard. The government made a pretty strong case that this is as contemptuous as contempt gets.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I mean, it`s funny because like, contempt has a legal meeting, and then it has like a, you know --\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Every day.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- every day meaning. And like he`s clearly in the everyday meaning, like completely contemptuous of Congress. There`s also the sort of third legal story that we`re like looking at right now is we know that the committee voted to subpoena \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on Thursday. Jamie Raskin came in the program and explain why and why it was important and not just symbolic. We`re expecting to get the actual subpoena.\par \par \uc2 And those in the past have been fairly informative about what the committee knows and what it`s seeking. So, what do you -- what do you anticipate we might learn from the subpoena?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: It could be volumes. As you point out, the subpoena as we`ve seen -- there`s quite a few of them. We`ve seen \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , we`ve seen Alex Jones, we`ve seen -- we`ve seen many of those who have chosen to come forward, others who`ve been in court proceedings. And yes, they`re pretty detailed. They list out an itemized detailed list of what documents the committee is looking for, and also general topics with subheadings essentially. Here are areas that we`re pretty sure that you`re going to have information about.\par \par \uc2 So, the whole set of hearings essentially have revolved around former President Trump`s actions and inaction on the day in question and before and after January 6, so it could go on for volumes, but probably they`ll keep it succinct and to the point. And, you know, then we`ll see what he does. I think it`s an interesting gambit.\par \par \uc2 And, again, as with Bannon, I think delay has been former President Trump`s friend, and in this case, probably his best friend.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. There`s also some -- it`s an interesting precedent. I think Truman was actually subpoenaed before congressional committee after he left and he didn`t go and he wrote why. So, he has actually a little bit of precedent to stand on. But Glenn, Jamie Raskin made an interesting point to me where he said, look, the reason we didn`t do this at the beginning as people criticize was, we were not going to get drawn into some long drawn out legal battle about whether he would or wouldn`t that would essentially sort of suck the oxygen out. We did all our due diligence, we talked to all these people who cooperated. There are still some things we`d like to know from him. And that`s why we subpoenaed him now. What do you think of that logic?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: I think it makes sense. And as Danya knows as well, we usually wait to subpoena the target of the investigation, if at all, at the very end after we`ve collected all of the other information that will inform how we want to go at that person once we subpoena them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And in this case, I mean, we`re not we`re not dealing obviously with a criminal investigation. It`s, it`s Congress. But as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  pointed out today, and a lot of people have, there is a kind of quasi-criminal prosecution happening here, at least in the sort of public imagination in terms of what we said.\par \par \uc2 Maggie Haberman noted that Trump is bragging about wanting to use -- do that -- do it live if he actually responds to subpoena but just color me a little skeptical about we will actually see that. I`ve heard that before. Glenn Kirschner and Danya Perry, thank you both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Thank you, my friend. Much appreciated. And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour. I`m really happy to have you here.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221018eiah00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 17, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Kristen Welker, Blayne, Jesse Kirsch, Alexander,  Ryan Reilly, Ali Vitali, Barbara McQuade, Peter Baker, Cal Perry, Peter  Zwack, Jonathan Lemire, Maura Barrett \par \uc2 7,927 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 With economic fears running high, a new poll shows Republican support surging. Independent women are abandoning the Democrats in droves turning a 14-point advantage for the party into an 18-point deficit in just four weeks. Does it show that the Democrats` multimillion-dollar decision to focus on abortion instead of the economy might have been a mistake?\par \par \uc2 Plus, DOJ calls Steve Bannon`s bluff, the former Trump aide who said he was willing to go to jail to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They actually get his wish, the Justice Department now recommending the maximum sentence for defying the January 6 committee`s subpoena. Is that a warning to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who`s about to get a subpoena delivered from the committee anytime now?\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this video out of Ukraine. Police officers firing into the air as the Capitol comes under attack. They`re not firing at soldiers. They`re firing at kamikaze drones trying to pick them off before they slam into their targets and explode. We`ll have a live report from inside Kyiv in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the midterms and some poll numbers that almost have to be seen to be believed because they show Democrats losing huge chunks of voters to Republicans right now at the same time that the economy is peaking as the critical issue for voters. Take a look at the latest New York Times Siena poll, Republicans opening up a 10-point lead among independents after being essentially tied just a month ago. Democrats` 13- point advantage among women in September, that`s gone, and among female independents, the biggest swing of all, Democrats leading among that group by 14 points in September now down by 18. As we said, a 32-point swing and just for weeks.\par \par \uc2 While we can`t attribute all of that to the issues, it does come as Republicans have spent the bulk of their ad dollars, $150 million on economic themes. Democrats have largely avoided that, something Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  recently criticized as political malpractice. Instead, Democrats have put almost $100 million into ads focused on abortion rights. In states like Georgia and Ohio, the economy is of critical importance. And candidates in both of those Senate seats are essentially in a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 We want to bring in NBC chief White House correspondent and co-anchor of the weekend "TODAY," Kristen Welker, NBC`s Jesse Kirsch is following the Ohio Senate race from Youngstown, and Basil Smikle is a Democratic strategist and former executive director of the New York state Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, I want to start in Georgia because you just sat down with a Republican candidate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , trying to clear up some of the questions surrounding his candidacy. What was he forthcoming with you and what was your sense of how he feels he`s doing in this race?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC ANCHOR: Well, Chris, I think he feels emboldened in this homestretch of the race, in part because he had his campaign feel as though he did have a stronger than-expected showing in that debate with Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  on Friday. They lowered expectations and from their perspective, they feel as though he surpassed those expectations.\par \par \uc2 Look, this was a wide-ranging hour-long interview. I talk to him about everything from the economy, his plan, his focus, he says is to tout energy, independence. I also spoke to him about former presidents Trump and former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . And of course, I did press him on those allegations that an ex-girlfriend says that he urged and then paid for her to have an abortion back in 2009.\par \par \uc2 We were able to speak with that ex-girlfriend. We obtained what she says is the paperwork, copies of a $700 check, copies of a receipt from the women`s clinic where she alleges that she got this procedure. And I did confront him with the paperwork. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: This is still alive because she`s the mother of my child. So, you`re going to see my check -- somebody`s given her a check so that`s why I`m saying this a lot.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Do you know what the $700 check this for?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I have no idea what that can be for.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Is that your signature on the check though?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No -- I mean it`s -- it could be it. I mean it doesn`t matter whether it`s my signature or not.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voiceover): When pressed, Walker said definitively for the first time that the check is his.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 WALKER: This is my check.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Why should voters believe you in this moment in your work?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Voters should believe me because I`ve been very transparent about everything I`ve ever done.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: This is obviously an issue that has loomed over this race. But again, Chris, I go back to where you started the conversation, the economy, and Walker is really trying to keep the focus on that very issue, as well as the issue of crime. That`s another big one for him.\par \par \uc2 So we pressed him on all of this. I asked him if there was any common ground to be found with Democrats and with President Biden in particular, can he name one thing that he supports that President Biden has done, and he said, initially, jokingly, he likes the fact that he eats ice cream. When I continue to press him, he said, look, he supports the fact that he ran on being a unity candidate, but he criticized him for not being able to achieve that. But taking a step back and underscores just how divisive this political climate is right now, Chris. And again, this is a very tight race and even small things around the edges could impact voters in these final days.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Basil, when you look at the poll, then that shows independent women decisively moving away from Democrats in the past month, would you say it`s a mistake for Democrats who have focused so much on abortion, not the economy? What do you think is going on here?\par \par \uc2 BASIL SMIKLE, FORMER EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, NEW YORK STATE DEMOCRATIC PARTY: Well, I don`t think it was a mistake for Democrats to focus on reproductive rights. That in and of itself was a largely mobilizing motivating issue. But Democrats are smart and can pivot and converse on a thousand other issues that I think \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  was right. They did not talk about enough.\par \par \uc2 When you started to see candidates like \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  in New York closing the gap with at the local who is the sitting governor, in his race for that seat, he took abortion rights off of his website and started to focus on crime and there was a shooting in front of his house. So, you know, he`s leaning into this issue that is, you know, resonating with a lot of -- a lot of voters.\par \par \uc2 Democrats haven`t been able to talk about the reduction in prescription drug costs. They haven`t been able to talk about a significant cost of living, increase in Social Security, all those things that put money back into people`s pockets, which would have been able to challenge Republicans on these areas that they`re -- that they`re -- that they`re building.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But does it really, Basil, I guess I`m looking at statistics that say for example, when it comes to the grocery store, 70 percent of the purchases in multiperson households are made by women. Women are more likely to hold jobs that pay less. What -- I`m not in any way, disregarding that what the Democrats say, what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has gone out and sad about the accomplishments they have made.\par \par \uc2 But when you go to the grocery store, and you wonder whether or not you can afford a gallon of milk, it`s a different equation. And I`m wondering how quickly -- if they need to turn, how quickly they can turn? We`re in a different world than we were when you and I started out, and you needed like a four-month lead time to run a -- to run a commercial.\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: No, it`s -- no, it`s an important point. I mean, this is -- this is where the Democrats need to pivot and pivot hard. I do think there is time to do that. But the -- but there is going to be a problem for us because we haven`t been talking about it all along. I don`t think it`s malpractice that we didn`t start talking about that in the spring and summer because Reproductive Rights was the conversation of that day. But we needed to be talking about other things.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, let me go back to Georgia because NBC`s Blayne Alexander is there in Marietta, and people may not have time to pivot, right because people are already voting. They`re starting to vote today, early voting first day in Georgia. We know from past elections, the bulk of Georgians cast votes ahead of time as opposed to on Election Day. So, what are you seeing on the ground in terms of strategy and kind of the pressure that it all really does start today?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, you`re absolutely right. Ballots are already being cast and people are taking full advantage of early voting. In fact, there was a line here before the sun came up before the doors opened several dozen people waiting to get inside and cast their ballots. So it`s certainly very clear that we`re already seeing people go to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Now what that means in terms of strategy is that essentially, we`re looking at election season, not just Election Day when it comes to candidates rallying people to get out to the polls. Now with Democrats, you know when I`ve gone to \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  events and kind of talk with her people, essentially you`re hearing this get-out-the-vote effort that has been a continuous throughout her campaign, urging people to go to the polls to cast their absentee ballot, something they`ve been able to do for several days now. But what`s interesting is that you`re hearing almost the same thing from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Typically, that`s a very democratic message. But when I went to a governor Kemp campaign rally several weeks ago, he admitted Democrats have outworked Republicans on their ground game for the past few cycles. And he said quite simply, we can`t let that happen again. So, he`s also urging people to go out to vote early. I do think it`s notable to point out though, that on this first day of early voting, we`re also seeing the first head-to-head debate between the two gubernatorial candidates between Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So it is certainly not noticeable that on the day that voters are going to head to the polls, they`re also going to be hearing from these candidates. They`re going to be reminding them of the issues from Democrats from \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , when I`ve spoken to her, she`s made it clear that abortion is going to be a central focus of her platform, that`s something that she`s going to be bringing forth, Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , of course, going to be reminding people of his record, each of them trying to pick up voters that they didn`t get the last time around and 2018. And so the debate tonight at the beginning of early voting is going to be a good chance for them to really try and do that, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Blayne, thank you for that. I want to go over to Jesse in Ohio where race is super tight. This is a state where unemployment -- the unemployment rate is higher than the national average. The median income is below the national average, Jesse, and they`ve got another big debate tonight. What are we expecting?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, more of what we`ve just been hearing about just now, Chris, the economy. J.D. Vance, specifically, was talking about groceries at the debate last week. He was speaking about it on cable news earlier people being able to afford food at the grocery store. And I think that is what Republicans are hoping in this race, as in others will resonate with voters.\par \par \uc2 And on the debate stage last week, we specifically saw a moment where \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the Democratic nominee, a congressman tried to tie J.D. Vance to right-wing extremism, and he listed off some candidates and members of Congress who are seen as far-right. And J.D. Vance`s response was let`s talk about something people care about, like what they can afford at the grocery store. And so, I think we will continue to see that kind of pivot from the Republican nominee. Ryan, for his, for what it`s worth, though, has defended some Democratic positions on dealing with the economy.\par \par \uc2 For example, Vance took swipes at Ryan over the Inflation Reduction Act, and Ryan defended his vote on that legislation. So, he`s not backing away from what Democrats are doing on legislation, but he`s also trying to peel away Republican voters, Chris. And that is really what Ryan is banking on is that there`s going to be some far-right fatigue from Trumpism fatigue, from Republican and moderate voters that he can peel away. Vance, though, will tell you that`s all a facade and that Ryan is just a hard-left Democrat, someone who is always voting with Democratic leadership in this state and across the country.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What time does the debate start?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: 7:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, so they`re up against --\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: I know that`s going to coincide interestingly, Chris, with something else you`re going to be (INAUDIBLE) there in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, they`re going to be up against the Guardians.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: (INAUDIBLE) go Ohio.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Good luck with getting Clevelanders to watch that debate. OK, Jesse, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 So let me just wrap this up, Kristen, by asking you about president -- former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . I mean, Biden has been out there talking about the economy, what he believes that his administration Democrats have accomplished. \uc2 Obama\uc2  remains the most popular Democrat. He`s going to Georgia, Michigan, Wisconsin. But when I look at releases announcing them, they often focus on abortion, voting rights, defending democracy. Have Democrats settled on a closing message? Do we know what we`re going to hear from \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  on the trail?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Chris, I would not be surprised if we heard the former president who as you rightfully note is very popular among Democrats. Yes, he talked about the issue of abortion, but I also think he is going to be out there defending the Biden economy. You will hear him make the case for why he will argue things are moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 Remember when former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was running for re-election and former President Clinton was dubbed at the time the explainer in chief to talk about Obamacare? I think that you can expect from President \uc2 Obama\uc2  to take on a very similar role to try to make the case in a detailed way to voters of why he -- why President Biden, why Democrats believe things are moving in the right direction. And think about the \uc2 Obama\uc2  coalition, Chris. Really targets those key groups that you were talking about, women, independents, voters of color.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Absolutely. Kristen, thank you. Blayne, Jesse, and Basil appreciate you guys.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, in just the last hour, jurors at the seditious conspiracy trial of the right-wing Oath Keepers militia have heard disturbing new evidence about the group`s plots to stash guns in a hotel just across the Potomac River from DC on January 6, also threats to declare guerrilla war. The trial`s third week began this morning with an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent returning to the stand. She has been walking jurors through the violent rhetoric shared by the defendants in the lead-up to the Capitol attack, including a message that suggested "mowing down masses."\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC`s Ryan Reilly. He`s been covering the trial from the start. He joins us from outside the courthouse. Ryan, what`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, MSNBC JUSTICE REPORTER: Sure, so we`ve learned a lot about Stewart Rhodes`s personal life in this in -- this latest day. We heard a lot about the expenses that he was making for the Oath Keepers. He was buying guns in the lead-up to January 6 and buying guns afterwards. There had been a lot of reporting and a lot of accusations from members of the Oath Keepers organizations about how exactly Stewart Rhodes was spending that money.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 We also heard some new information about his personal life. It turns out that Kellye SoRelle, who was the general counsel for the Oath Keepers, or at least was purportedly the general counsel for the Oath Keepers after the January 6 attack, was in a sexual relationship with Stewart Rhodes and they revealed some of those text messages during this trial. That had been an issue because they`re -- the defense attorneys at least had said that there these were protected by -- protected by attorney-client privilege, but there was nothing related to legal exchanges in some of those messages that jurors were just seeing in court just in -- within this morning.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan Reilly, always good to see you, my friend. Thank you so much. I know you`ll update us if we get other big breaking news out of that trial.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, prosecutors sending a big message to big names to find congressional subpoenas from the January 6 committee, with jail time on the table is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  getting the message, plus deadly kamikaze drones raining down from the sky in Kyiv, the race to save survivors in Ukraine`s capital as officials scramble to defend against those attacks and the only known survivor of a suspected serial killer shares her terrifying encounter ahead. You`re watching "CHRIS JANSING" reports only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New today, prosecutors are sending a clear message to anyone who wants to defy a congressional subpoena, do so at your own peril. DOJ is recommending that Trump adviser, \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  get six months in jail following his conviction for refusing to testify before the January 6 committee. This is from the sentencing memo. "To this day, Bannon continues to unlawfully withhold documents and testimony that stand to help the committee`s authorized investigation to get to the bottom of what led to the January 6 and ascertain what steps must be taken to ensure that it never happens again. That cannot be tolerated."\par \par \uc2 There may be a message in that for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as well, who was about to get a subpoena from the committee but who has yet to commit to testifying. And if he defies that subpoena, will the House demand prosecution as they did with Bannon?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STENY HOYER, (D-MD): We`re going to treat him just as we would any other citizen that refuses to testify, go to court, and ask that that individual be held in contempt in order to testify.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2 , (D-FL): With previous subpoenas, what you`ve seen the committee do is be very deliberate and take the response to our subpoenas on a case-by-case basis.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2 , (R-IL): That`s a bridge we cross if we have to get there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For more, let`s bring in NBC`s Ali Vitali, Barbara McQuade, a former U.S. attorney and an MSNBC legal analyst, and Peter Baker, Chief White House correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  and an MSNBC political analyst. A lot to talk about, but Ali, I want to get to this new news that you just brought to us out of the January 6 committee. Look, there`d been all this talk about the possibility of an interim report that could come before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That would require a vote of the committee, so it could be complicated, but and this is what caught my eye from your new reporting. There is a real possibility we could see some new information. What might that look like?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, that`s exactly right, Chris. Because this is a committee that has shown so much publicly in its hearings, but still has things on the cutting room floor that can help Americans understand what happened on January 6, and the roles that were played by the former president as well as other people in his orbit. And so, it`s entirely possible that while we won`t see an official interim report for the reasons that you laid out, and, frankly, just where we are on the calendar before Election Day, it is entirely possible that the committee could release an unofficial interim report that looks like a maybe new video that they could tweet out. We`ve seen them do that before. Or maybe it looks more like a staff report.\par \par \uc2 That`s something that our sources have told us is a real possibility in these coming weeks that while we won`t necessarily see the committee in formal fashion, we could hear from them again with new information. And this is something frankly, that Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  said to us on Friday. He said that there is the possibility that they release something before Election Day, whether you call it an interim report or something else. But this is one of those situations where what`s in a name actually really matters because one is formal and requires a lot of procedural things. And the other is more unofficial, but still gives the public new information.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, without a doubt, their tweets have been very powerful.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let`s talk, Barbara McQuade, about the things that the committee didn`t get. And that includes testimony from \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . His sentencing is scheduled for Friday. How strong is the argument DOJ is making that he should get six months? Is that likely, or are they aiming high?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: I think if anything, Chris, they`re aiming a little low here. They are showing you a very measured response. If you read the sentencing memo, what it says is that the sentencing guidelines is a range of zero to six months, and so they`re asking for the top of that range of six months. But under the statute, they could actually seek as much as two years. That`s because you can double-stack, the two counts for which he was convicted, each with a statutory maximum of one year. They also cite some comparable cases where they talk about the need to avoid unwarranted disparities between this case and other cases. And they even cite some cases where defendants were sentenced to a year in prison.\par \par \uc2 They note some of the really egregious factors here that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  had bad faith in refusing to comply with his subpoena and even boasted about his refusal to do so. So, I think six months seems likely and if anything, maybe a little modest in terms of the request by DOJ.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Interesting. Well, Peter, it`s worth reminding people of just how rare this is. Bannon is the first person to be convicted of contempt of Congress at trial since 1974. And we`re waiting for his lawyers to submit their sentencing recommendation. What has been his argument for ignoring Congress besides, he has no respect for anybody on the committee, frankly.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think that`s basically -- yes, look, this is a political thing for him. He`s happy to be a martyr. He`s happy to be, you know, a hero in effect for the MAGA crowd, somebody who`s taking it for the former president. Remember, he, of course, had been at one point excommunicated from the former president`s circle, and he worked his way back in by, you know, showing loyalty in this sense. And I think this is -- this is what he`s doing at this point.\par \par \uc2 If you listen to what he has said publicly, since the, you know, the subpoena was first issued, he has made this a political, you know, selling point for himself and for former President Trump. There is no legal argument here of any consequence, the judge made that clear even as even they got to trial. Barb certainly knows that better than I do. But you know, he`s going to -- he`s going to milk this for what it`s worth, and six months in prison may be worth it from his point of view to keep his brand as the -- as a renegade in favor of the Trump movement, you know, very prominent.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And it brings us to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Peter. Do your sources tell you he wants to appear or just that he wants to appear to want to appear before finding a reason not to?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Look, if history is a guide, he`ll talk a big game about how he`d love to talk and he`d love to give his side of the story, he`d love to appear and give, you know, and engage with the committee, and he`d never actually do it. That he did that with the Mueller situation. He did that in the first impeachment. He did that in the second impeachment. Each and every time he`s talked about how much he wanted to get out there and do it. And each and every time he either was talked out of it by his lawyers, if you want to believe that version, or he himself realized that it was a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 As any lawyer, you know, with any professional experience would not want \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be taking an oath, to tell the truth, where you might, in fact, find him contradicting himself in the space of his own testimony. We interviewed him twice for our book that just came out, The Divider. And in the period of those three and a half hours of interviews, he contradicts himself repeatedly. And he basically told us all kinds of things that were not true. So, if you`re a lawyer, you`re not going to want him to go up there take the oath, to tell the truth, the whole truth, nothing but the truth and expose himself to a possible charge of perjury.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Understanding that the possible charge of perjury, Barbara, how do you weigh that if you`re his defense team, against you get a legitimate subpoena from a sitting committee of the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, he always has the ability to invoke his Fifth Amendment rights against self-incrimination. And lawyers may advise him to do that. The downside, of course, is Trump himself has repeatedly disparaged people who invoke the Fifth and says only criminals take the Fifth, etcetera. He did invoke the Fifth Amendment many, many times when he appeared for deposition before New York Attorney General Letitia James. And so perhaps now having gone down that road, he is willing to continue.\par \par \uc2 But it seems more likely to me that he will use whatever tactics he can to stall, at least run out the clock until after the midterm elections, and maybe after the new Congress is seated in January, in hopes that they will disband. Because I think he doesn`t want to face that moment where he has to be there under the glare of the lights invoking the Fifth Amendment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Peter, Trump was not without controversy just this weekend, facing backlash after he posted the attack on American Jews on his social media site yesterday, writing in part, no president has done more for Israel than I have. Our wonderful evangelicals are far more appreciative of this than the people of the Jewish faith. U.S. Jews have to get their act together and appreciate what they have in Israel before it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 I mean, his argument in the past has been that his son-in-law and that now his daughter and his grandchildren are Jewish. But what are you hearing about this particularly? I mean, you can also put it in a -- in a context of everything he says and does could potentially have an impact on the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, look, it speaks for itself. I mean, I think it tells you how he views you know, America`s ethnic and racial, you know, diversity. People need to be grateful to him in large categories and hard categories defined by religion or ethnicity or race. That`s how he has always approached it.\par \par \uc2 He was mad that the criminal justice reform bill that \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , talked him into supporting during his presidency, didn`t get him more appreciation from African Americans. He said it very bluntly, repeatedly. He has time and time again, said to various groups in America that they needed to view him and give him an appreciation. And that`s -- and that`s how we`ve used all of these things.\par \par \uc2 Our policy issues aren`t about policy. They`re about, you know, what it means for him and whether people are loyal to him either individually or as a group. Now, does that affect the midterms? I don`t know. I mean, I think the more he is out there, the more the Democrats are happy because they`d rather have be a reference -- a choice rather than a referendum.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:03]\par \par \uc2 A choice between Biden Democrats and Trump Republicans is the sale that they think they can make. At least in many constituents. But the more it`s about Biden, about the economy, you know, the more Trump were to, you know, pull back on the public stage, that would be better seen from Republicans. And former President Trump doesn`t seem to see it that way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali, Barbara, Peter Baker whose new book is "The Divider" along with Susan Glasser, thank you all so much. Appreciate you being with us today.\par \par \uc2 Deadly kamikaze drones exploding a building in Ukraine`s capital. Can these attacks be stopped? And what it means for Ukraine`s hope of continuing to push back Russian forces?\par \par \uc2 We are live in Kyiv next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The latest strike by Russian forces in Ukraine hit Kyiv just hours ago, leaving a trail of blood and destruction as a kamikaze drone slammed into an apartment building. The Russians are using Iranian-made drones strapped with explosives. In this attack, 28 of them were launched, 23 shot down, but five hit their mark, exploding in and around that building. Police officers forced to try to shoot down the drones by firing into the sky.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian officials said the attack left three dead, four hurt, 14 more had to be rescued from the rubble.\par \par \uc2 For more I want to bring in NBC`s Cal Perry who is in Kyiv and Brigadier General Peter Zwack.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Cal, what has it been like on the streets of Kyiv?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It`s a frightening night here in the capital. Just in the last five minutes, we have witnessed anti-aircraft fire striking a drone out of the sky. Maybe two kilometers from where I am. So there is now a fresh wave under way. This is the third wave, third wave of drones that have been fired at Kyiv so far today. As you said, 28 in total in the morning. 23 of them shot down. Five hitting their target.\par \par \uc2 It is important to know that these are crude weapons. These are basically rockets that have a crude engine on the back of them. You can hear them coming into the city. It`s a very distinct sound. They are very difficult to stop with the air defenses because of course they have some civilian components on them.\par \par \uc2 What the Russians are aiming for are critical electricity infrastructure. At least that`s what they claim that they`re aiming for. What they`re hitting are civilian areas. And that is what collapsed that apartment building. That is what killed four people today, wounding another four people. At least 19 people were rescued from the rubble.\par \par \uc2 But again, Chris, in just sort of the past five or 10 minutes, we witnessed another drone shot out of the sky and the sound of some more explosions here on the Capital. So we`ll wait and find out what rescue officials have to say about the most recent wave of attacks.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What do you make, General, of this wave of attacks and the strategy that the Russians have decided to use here?\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PETER ZWACK (RET.), GLOBAL FELLOW, WILSON CENTER: Chris, first of all, it shows I believe the bankruptcy of their overall campaign to dominate to take Ukraine. Their forces have been repulsed and defeated and embarrassed in the field. And while the fighting is tightening up again, they can`t beat Ukrainian military in the field. They use the -- I think the fact of the Kerch Bridge being blown up over a week ago, there`s a lot of flurry going on. Yes, they want to get the infrastructure and water and all of that, and get at the grids but this is also an intimidation campaign.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think it`s playing out. It will tough them like the Nazi blitz -- the London blitz in 1940. This is just going to toughen Ukrainian resolve. And most of all the world more and more sees just how vicious and mean spirited this invasion is. But it shows the bankruptcy of the overall Russian strategy. Whatever the reason, with foreign rockets and missiles almost indiscriminately and killing a lot of civilians. No, it`s failing for the Russians.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It is killing people, however. So what they do to protect against it is firing into the sky trying to hit these drones before they reach their target. An effective way to deal with it. What does Ukraine do?\par \par \uc2 ZWACK: No, it`s not enough. And a lot of nations, including our own, are doing all we can to get more and upgraded air defense systems. And what is notable, your reporter did bring out and we`ve been reading is a lot of these Iranian and other provided drones have been shot out of the sky. This is the grisly, the nasty part of this mano-o-mano without quarter war that`s going on in there.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got to continue to amp up the support and the systems and the international condemnation so the pressure hits the Russians domestically that these are war crimes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Meantime, China`s leader \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is on the cusp of locking down another five-year term. So I want to ask you about that as well because he warned in a speech against foreign interference in Taiwan, saying that China reserves the option of taking all measures necessary. What does that kind of talk mean for strategic military planning in the region by both the U.S. and its allies?\par \par \uc2 ZWACK: It is definitely a strategic messaging. It is also something, if we follow his reading, something that he believes in. I think his legacy is in part the reunification. You know, what we call self-governing Taiwan. Bottom line is that Taiwan is a vibrant, young democracy that wants no part of that. And if there is anything that \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  should see is what Ukraine has done, has pulled together a coalesced, a lot of the free-minded nations in the world including in the Pacific, that no, an invasion of Taiwan, independent, free-minded Taiwan, is not going to be tolerated.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 Now, whatever the response would be is the key question. Bottom line, China has been growing its military, it`s been growing its nuclear arsenal, and its rhetoric continues to grow.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Brigadier General Peter Zwack, thank you so much as always. And our thanks to Cal Perry in country as well.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, just today, several \uc2 European Union\uc2  foreign ministers called for sanctions against Iran for the transfer of those drones to Russia. The news comes amid ongoing anti-government protests in Iran and after its most notorious political prison exploded in flames this weekend with the death toll rising. Officials say eight inmates were killed. That`s a new number today. Hundreds of prisoners are held there, including two American citizens. A state news agency says the fire was started Saturday by inmates burning their uniforms at a warehouse and that there were fights between prisoners and staff. NBC has not been able to confirm that report.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just Kari Lake. Hundreds of Republican candidates are already refusing to say they`d accept next month`s election results. The potential impact on key races before and after election day. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:22]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: With voting under way in many states today, 60 percent of Americans will have an election denier on the ballot. In fact, one new analysis shows that nearly 300 of the top Republican candidates in the country have said they think the 2020 election was rigged. That`s more than half nationwide. And now many of them are saying next month`s midterms might be rigged, too. But only if they lose.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Arizona Republican nominee Kari Lake yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN ANCHOR: Will you accept the results of your election in November?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: I`m going to win that election and I will accept that result.\par \par \uc2 BASH: If you lose, will you accept that?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I`m going to win the election, and I will accept that result.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in Jonathan Lemire, White House bureau chief for "Politico" and host of MSNBC`s "WAY TOO EARLY."\par \par \uc2 So, you know this, Jonathan, she`s just one of the most high profile candidates but far from the only one, hundreds of them, including many running for secretary of state, have said 2020 was illegitimate. Many won`t of them say that they will accept the results of this election. What are you hearing from Democrats now about what election night could be like in November, not to even mention 2024?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: Yes. The big lie is on the ballot, Chris. This is -- I wrote a book of that same name as its title and it details how Trump`s false claims of election fraud didn`t just end with the violence on January 6th but it has shaped the Republican Party today. And that`s what we`re seeing right now with all these candidates. And Kari Lake, perhaps the most visible but far from the only one.\par \par \uc2 You`re right, just a few others. \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , the Republican gubernatorial candidate in Pennsylvania. A number of state secretaries of state candidates, including Mark Finchem in Arizona have said that not only would they, if elected, do what they can to undo the 2020 election. Now of course let`s be clear that can`t happen.\par \par \uc2 But have made no guarantees that they will either, A, concede this fall if they were to lose or, B, if elected, would do the same in 2024. They would also not commit to certifying the appropriate winner there if their candidate did not emerge victorious.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are deeply worried about this. This is such a test of our democracy. And they`re using this as one of their many messages to try to get voters to the polls this November. And polls suggest that, yes, threats to democracy, defending democracy ranks high but it ranks well behind things likes abortion rights and certainly most of all economic issues.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So on election night, I mean, it`s not unusual for there to be a recount or not unusual for there to be people who contest, right, something that happened at a particular voting precinct. They`ve had problems with machines or whatever. But are we potentially looking at widespread questions being raised about maybe dozens, even more races?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Yes. Strategists in both parties have suggested to me that what we saw in 2020 when the results have actually couldn`t be called for several days, that might be the new normal. It`s in part because more states are using mail-in balloting now, although less we should say than the height of the pandemic in 2020.\par \par \uc2 But also we have seen now what Trump did and what his lie has given birth to is this belief among some, not all, but many Republicans that they feel like if they were to lose, that something must have been off with the election. It must be rigged, it must be fraudulent. Of course there`s no evidence of that. And let`s be clear, candidates have every right or contest the results.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes there are appropriate legal challenges to bring. Even in 2020. No one said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t have reason to file a lawsuit. He`s well -- in a number of states in the days after the election. The issue is once those legal challenges are heard, you`ve got to accept the result. That`s what our democracy is based upon, is the peaceful transfer of power. The loser ceding to the winner.\par \par \uc2 And this is in doubt now for the first time in our history. Trump of course didn`t do it. And now a lot his acolytes this fall are pledging that they may do the same.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan Lemire, always good to see you, my friend. Thank you for coming on. Again, just to remind folks, the name of the book is "The Big Lie." Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 A suspected serial killer arrested in Stockton, California. A victim describes her chilling story of survival as police reveal how they tracked down the killer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:35]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He was out hunting. That`s how Stockton Police Chief Stanley McFadden described the man believed to be behind a string of six homicides. Officials arrested 43-year-old Wesley Brownlee Saturday after a series of tips helped them to track him down. One of his victims Natasha LaTour was shot at least nine times but survived.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NATASHA LATOUR, SURVIVOR: There was no words exchanged. Didn`t say anything. Didn`t come any closer. Just started shooting, I just saw flashes. I mean, and then knowing that there`s the moving train, or like some ridiculous high ass fence that I couldn`t even attempt to climb with no slats and, you know what I mean? I mean, and behind me an open field so I ran towards him knowing that I`d have to get closer to the street if I wanted to survive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Amazing story. I want to bring in NBC`s Maura Barrett in Stockton. There have been so much concerns. Serial killer on the loose. The headlines were blaring in the papers for days. Now this ends more than a year-long search.\par \par \uc2 First of all, when will he be back in court and how exactly were police able to finally get him?\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, police are thanking the community, Chris. They received hundreds of tips, which allowed them to locate the suspect early Saturday morning, and the language they used describing the arrest was just so chilling and reflects what neighbors were feeling here throughout the last year. They said that he was on a mission to kill just moments before they caught him.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 They said he had a pattern of preying, starting out in his car, and then stalking his victims on foot. And so understandably, neighbors are feeling this collective sigh of relief, a weight off their shoulders. People that I spoke to yesterday saying that they feel like they can go back to normal. Some people telling me that they had stopped going out after dark. They had canceled plans. They wouldn`t go out alone. And this is something that has been plaguing this community, as you said, for well over a year.\par \par \uc2 Now we do know the suspect has a criminal background, a criminal history, some drug-related charges and a DUI. He`s being held without bail here in the county jail on suspicion of at least one murder offense and assorted weapons offenses. The D.A. said that there is no official determination on charges yet because they`re still waiting for ballistic evidence to come back to determine whether or not the gun that they found on the suspect was connected to all of the attacks.\par \par \uc2 Again, these are attacks that happened just blocks apart in some cases, so really closely together, though officials say there`s no motive to be determined yet either or any indication that these were race-based attacks or targeted, as six of the victims that passed away were Latino men, some of them were homeless. And so this is something where there`s a lot of questions left to be answered and so we`re hoping to see more as the D.A. brings official charges at the suspect`s arraignment tomorrow afternoon -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Maura Barrett, thank you so much for that update. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to tune in to CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par 