 dash any hopes of holding or even expanding their majority in that chamber, leaving them with another contentious, evenly split Senate, like the one that they`ve been doing these past two years.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t help that key Senate races in Georgia, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania and in Nevada have tightened up, leaving Democrats to question how the contest got and remains so close. It`s looking like a complicated picture for the party no matter what happens next month. And we are giving you a clear view of it tonight on POLITICSNATION starting, as always, with the perfect guests.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2 , Democrat of Minnesota. Senator, I appreciate -- first of all, I appreciate you joining us tonight. I want to get to some of the deeper implications of this upcoming election for the Senate by starting out broadly. What must Senate Democrats prioritize should they grow or at least hold on to their majority after next month?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TINA SMITH (D-MN): Well, thank you, Reverend Sharpton, I`m so glad to be with you again. And you are exactly right. I mean, these races, I`m optimistic. But these races are highly competitive. And Democrats across the country are facing an onslaught of dark money, which means that though we have excellent candidates and excellent campaigns, the need to keep on fighting for these last three weeks just could not be more intense. So, we need everybody to get involved.\par \par \uc2 We have been incredibly productive with just a 50/50 Senate. I think it`s been the longest 50/50 Senate that we have ever had in our history. But what we could accomplish if we were able to have 52 senators is really what keeps me campaigning every minute that I can. Let`s pass voting rights, let`s put the protections of Roe versus Wade into law. Let`s finish the job on childcare and public safety and gun safety. There`s so much work that we still have to do despite how productive we have been this last year or so.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Will the fight over abortion rights be the issue that keeps Senate Democrats in the majority next month, in your view?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: In my view, the abortion rights issue is a pivotal issue, and here`s why. For women, abortion rights is not a political issue. It is a personal issue. It is an issue of personal freedom and it`s a question of whether or not government is interfering in our own health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why I see here in Minnesota when I`m out campaigning, people are mad about this. They are galvanized to make sure that their voices are heard and that is exactly what I understand is happening all over the country as people consider the fact that an extreme part of the Republican Party is trying to take away their freedom to decide for themselves what kind of health care they want.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as I mentioned at the top, Politico reporting this weekend that the Senate may be facing down another evenly split Senate if Democrats fail pick up additional seats in states like Wisconsin, in addition to potential losses in states like Nevada. Senate Democrats racked up some substantial wins over the summer but those wins were hard fought and hard won. Is the caucus ready for more of that if the chamber ends up evenly split again?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, I first have not given up nor do I think we should give up on adding Senate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:06]\par \par \uc2 And we have to defend our four most vulnerable senators in New Hampshire, in Georgia and Arizona and, of course, Nevada, where my good friend, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , has a tight race.\par \par \uc2 I just want everyone who is thinking about how they want to help to go to defendthesenate.org and get involved in these campaigns. But the opportunities, Reverend Sharpton, to add senators, I think, we`re very focused. And we want to do that because there is so much work that the American people need us to do. And we cannot give up on that. We won`t.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, While we have time, we know this weekend that former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  will be on the campaign trail for Democrats in Michigan, in Wisconsin, in Georgia at the end of the month. What does he bring to the trail in those particular races, in your view?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, of course, it is great to have President \uc2 Obama\uc2  out campaigning. He is such a powerful messenger. He is incredibly popular. And I think that he could really help all of us to bring home the distinct difference between Democrats and Republicans in these pivotal races, the extremeness of the Republican Party candidates and the strength of the Democratic Party candidates.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m excited to know that he`s going to be out there campaigning in our neighboring state of Wisconsin and, of course, Georgia to help Senator Warnock, Reverend Warnock, who is such an amazing senator.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the congressman, \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , Democrat of Maryland and majority leader.\par \par \uc2 Well, let us take a break and we will be right back after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:10:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joining me now, Congressman \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , Democrat of Maryland and House Majority Leader. Congressman Hoyer, great to have you with us again.\par \par \uc2 Let me go right to it. Barely three weeks before America votes, and, broadly speaking, as House majority leader facing down an election in which Democrats have long been expected to take a hit in the House, likely losing the majority. What is the consequence for America, if that does happen? And what are the party`s priorities if it doesn`t happen?\par \par \uc2 REP. STENY HOYER (D-MD): Well, first of all, I don`t accept premise. I think that we are going to win. They said we`re going to lose in New York, Congressman Ryan won that election. They said we were going to lose Alaska, Mary Peltola won that election, the first Democrat 50 years. Now, that`s just days ago, about a month-and-a-half ago. And in Kansas, they were sure that Kansas voter, a red state in the middle of America, was going to vote to not allow women to make their choice on their reproductive rights, they almost, 60-40, did the opposite.\par \par \uc2 So, I really believe that we`re going to win the majority, we`re going to keep the House and we`re going to expand in the Senate. And I think we can expand some in the House as well. So, I don`t accept that. But if in fact it doesn`t happen, in my view, America is going to be locked up, the president will not be able to get support, either internationally or domestically, for his programs, and that will not be good for America, particularly at a time of real challenge and a time when we`re trying to make sure that Americans can meet inflation, we bring inflation down, that`s really hurting our people. We know it. And we`ve taken action to address it.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, our Republican friends have not supported that legislation that we passed. But I think the American public are going to think that they want some stability, as they voted in 2020, to have some rationality and some positive policies that help them make it in America. We want to have people over politics, which is, I think, what we have pursued, we want to lower their costs, get at inflation. The president talks about that. We`re working at that. It`s tough. It is a worldwide phenomenon. We want to see better paying jobs and we want to see safe communities. We passed legislation on both of those subjects. Most Republicans have not supported that. That it`s a shame. But if they took over, I think, we are going to see a very negative time in America, great instability in America, and instability in our economy, which will not help us bring prices down.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congressman, as we look at this midterm election, we always hear these are the most important elections of your life. But this is really important. I mean, we are dealing with gerrymandering, of voting, we`re dealing with democracy itself, in my opinion, on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Last week, many of us were captivated by the footage of Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  on January 6, coordinating the leadership response to the attack on the Capitol. I read in The Atlantic this weekend an account of how Republican leaders \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , responded to the news that the Capitol was under siege, the danger they themselves were in, in no small part because of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I was struck to read in Politico that after it became clear to McConnell that immediate help from the Pentagon would not be forthcoming, he then went and search of Speaker Pelosi and Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  for help.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:02]\par \par \uc2 Politico writing, quote, the scene underscores that Republicans on that day knew and agreed that the man they defended for four years would not lift a finger to help them or his allies in Congress. Instead, they turned to Democratic leaders and saved themselves, and you are one of those leaders, Congressman Hoyer. What is your response to that report?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Well, I think we were united. I think that`s correct, in the anger, the shock, the disappointment. As we were all communicating, I was communicating with Governor Hogan and asking them get the National Guard from Maryland into the D.C. area, to the Capitol, to help with enforcing the law and saving people`s lives and getting us back into the Capitol so we could do our constitutional duty. So, we were together on that. We were all together in the conviction that we were going to go back that day, that night to make sure that we did in fact finish the job that we are supposed to do constitutionally.\par \par \uc2 Sadly, though, Rev, what has happened is we have gone from that night we were unified in opposition to what can only be called an insurrection, as a coup-like action by the president of the United States and his followers to supplant their views for the voters` views. Unfortunately, that has now turned to rationalization of that day.\par \par \uc2 And I think what the committee has done so well is to show day-by-day, hour-by-hour, video-by-video Republicans working for Trump and Republicans generally who testified to the committee, it was almost all Republicans testify, that the president, President Trump, did not care and in fact intimately involved in the preparation of, the planning of, and the execution of. And he invited everybody to his speech, he incited them in that speech and he deployed them to fight like hell and Stop the Steal.\par \par \uc2 There was no steal. He perpetuates this lie and, unfortunately, too many folks running for office right now, who may be in charge of running our elections, notwithstanding 60-plus courts deciding upon the evidence there was no evidence that anything was stolen or that fraud had any effect on the election, the overwhelming victory of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, this rationalization has led itself, Rev, to the fact where the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , shockingly, in my opinion, sadly, in my opinion, referred to the activities of January 6, which the American people have seen over and over and over again, which was a violent insurrection here that resulted in the deaths of a number of people. They described that as legitimate, political discourse. That was the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  overwhelmingly adopted the resolution, calling it legitimate political discourse.\par \par \uc2 If we are telling the American people one of the major parties, supposedly responsible parties, telling the American people that what they saw on January 6th was a legitimate political discourse, then we are headed for anarchy and chaos. And I believe the American public responded positively in the 2020 election. And I think this election, by the way, is the second most important. I think 2020 was the most important in that Trump was rejected for a second term. We couldn`t impeach him because we didn`t get any Republican or not enough Republican help to do so. We had some very brave, honest Republicans who were prepared to stand up and say the emperors had no clothes but not enough.\par \par \uc2 But in the election of November of 2020, the American people said, we do not want this to continue. This is not America, this is not a democracy. And I think now, this year, two years later, our democracy really is at top of the ballot.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: They do want to have to seal the deal with these midterms, as you say.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring this to you, Congressman, two of your colleagues in the House, \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , both of whom sit on the January 6th committee, told Sunday morning shows today that the panel has yet to determine its next steps if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not comply with its subpoena. What can congressional Democrats if the former president tries to simply run out the clock of this subpoena hoping Republicans take Congress in November?\par \par \uc2 [17:20:01]\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Well, it`s very difficult because time is short. But we`re going to treat him just as we would any other citizen that refuses to testify, go to court and ask that that individual be held in contempt and ordered to testify. A former president is not above the law anymore than the president is above the law.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So, Democrats are prepared to go to court and ask for the subpoena to be enforced, if that becomes necessary?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Rev, I think we -- from my standpoint, I haven`t talked to the committee about it. I don`t want to speak for the committee. They obviously don`t want to make a judgment until they know for sure what actions former President Trump is going to take. But, yes, I think that is an action, which, from my standpoint, ought to be taken.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Let me ask you this. You and I have always talked and worked around issues of racial inequality. I remember marching many years with you, as you supported \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and the commemoration of the march of voting rights across the Edmund Petus Bridge and you being right there with -- in Selma, Alabama, with \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I want to raise this to you. A new poll number -- new poll numbers on a key voting bloc for Democrats say 67 percent of black women are more motivated now than ever to vote. That`s according to a new national poll commission by the nonprofit arm of Higher Heights for America PAC, a political action committee that works to elect black progressive women nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The overwhelming majority of black women surveyed said the cost of living, inflation, are the issues they are most concerned with, the majority of respondents adding that they felt underrepresented going into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Given how pivotal black women voters are for Democrats, do you think the party has done a good enough job addressing the specific concerns of that inequality going into November?\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Well, we passed a lot of pieces of legislation in equality for women and pay, as you know. Unfortunately, that hasn`t passed the Senate. Why? Because of the filibuster. We don`t have the 60 votes to do that. We passed the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act, which every Democrat supported, every Democrat in the House supported, every Democrat in the Senate supported that, but we didn`t have the 60 votes that we needed to get that passed.\par \par \uc2 We passed a bill on Inflation Reduction Act, which dealt with costs, health care costs, other costs, with climate sots, energy costs. It is obvious why we`re all concerned about costs because we go to the grocery store and we see prices for bread, milk and baking and other necessities going up very substantially, and people are worried about that. And we need to bring those down. We passed a food and fuel act to try to help bring down those prices and to help fix supply chains. Unfortunately, again, almost all Republicans voted against it, it hasn`t moved in the Senate at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 But in the Inflation Reduction Act and in the plan that we passed early in the Biden administration, the rescue plan, we did a lot to make sure that people could meet their costs, their mortgage payments, their car payments, their food costs, and so they didn`t fall through the floorboards. Then that made a huge difference for people who had been exalted by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And on inflation, we are in the middle of a pack of economically-advantaged countries around the world in terms of inflation. We are not the lowest but certainly not the highest by far. As a matter of fact, as I said, we are just about in the middle. But we need to continue to act. And we`re going to make sure that we can bring prices down and keep supply chains open and make sure that we are making things in America, creating good jobs for our people and making sure they feel secure in their retirements.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. House Majority Leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , thank you very much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, young people took to the streets for justice during the civil rights and \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  demonstrations of 2020. Now, it is time to rise up again and vote to talk about it, ahead.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a good Sunday to you.\par \par \uc2 Iran state-run news agency reporting at least four people were killed and 61 hurt following a fire at a prison that houses political prisoners and also said some inmates set fire prison uniforms at a warehouse and said there were skirmishes about prisoners and staff.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:11]\par \par \uc2 NBC News has not been able to verify those reports.\par \par \uc2 The world`s second largest economy is about to choose President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  for a third five-year term. The process starts with China`s 20th party congress kicking off today. This would make Xi China`s longest tenured communist ruler since Mao Zedong.\par \par \uc2 And an arrest was made in a year-and-a-half-long case of a series of killings in Stockton, California. Wesley Brownlee was taken into custody Saturday related to six homicides. Police traced him down following a string of tips saying, Brownlee was armed with a handgun and possibly searching for another victim at the time of the arrest.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As I I traveled across the country in my capacity as head of National Action Network, I run into many inspiring young people who are excited to vote in the midterm elections. That`s why this week I was surprised and disappointed to see new polling that suggests many Gen Z voters are wavering in their enthusiasm for politics.\par \par \uc2 And NPR Marist poll found young people are the least likely group to go to the polls this November. And a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , ABC News survey found less than half of respondents between 19 and 29 years of age say they are absolutely certain to vote.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been at this a long time and I can understand the cynicism. American politics are messy, and can be ugly at times. It`s easy to feel like your vote does not matter. But it does. Look at what President Biden and the Democrats have been able to do with only the most narrow of margins in Congress. They passed the first major gun safety legislation in nearly three decades, paving the way for red flag laws and expanded background checks. They enacted the largest climate and clean energy investment in U.S. history as part of the Inflation Reduction Act. And President Biden has used his executive powers to cancel tens of thousands of dollars in student debt for millions of borrowers. He also pardoned thousands of Americans with federal marijuana offenses, many of them young black men.\par \par \uc2 These are all issues young people care about. And Democrats have delivered on them. Of course, there is still much, much more progress to be made. We need the police reform that young people demanded in the civil rights and \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  demonstrations of 2020. We need election laws to preserve our democracy for future generations. We need federal protection to ensure access to safe legal abortions. And we need to rollback institutionalized discrimination against blacks and other people of color and immigrants and LGBTQ communities. But the only way any of this is possible, is if we all vote. Young people have just as much to say in our country`s direction as any on the group but only if they rise up and exercise their power, will they be heard. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:36:30]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. Let`s turn to my political panel for their insights on today`s top stories. Joining me now is former Republican Representative \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  of Florida. And Atty. Jennifer Jones Austin, CEO of Federation of Protestant Welfare Agencies and Share of the New York City Racial Justice Commission.\par \par \uc2 Carlos, let`s talk about an important voting bloc, who will help to decide many close races, many close elections in these midterm elections? That`s the Latino vote. New polling released, Friday, found Hispanic voters still favored Democrats overall. But the gap between the two parties has narrowed since 2018. Now, this comes as political reports, Republican candidates and outside groups are still playing catch up in terms of total spending for Spanish campaign ads, as Democrats outpaced them three to one on Spanish language TV and radio ads this election cycle. But what`s behind the narrowing between the two parties? What attracts Hispanics, to Republicans, when they aren`t making the same investment in their communities as Democrats are?\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. CARLOS CURBELO, (R) FLORIDA: Well, for one Reverend for a long time, Democrats have taken Hispanic voters for granted. They just assume that old line, I think you remember, demographics is destiny. They assume that as the Latino community grew in the United States, that they would automatically support the Democratic Party and keep joining the Democratic coalition. Well, it turns out, you can`t take any voter for granted, you actually have to show up and ask for the vote. And Democrats now understand that they can`t take that community for granted. And this cycle, they are making big investments in some of these districts to try to win back the seats here.\par \par \uc2 In South Florida, there`s a big race, where that -- those efforts by Democrats will be put to the test. But for a long time, Democrats took these voters for granted. Republicans started showing up and also Republicans are talking about the United States in a way that the Latino can commute it -- connect more with as opposed to Democrats who tend to focus on immigration, when they talk to Latino communities. Again, that`s changing, but that`s been one of the mistakes that led to the erosion in support among the Latino community for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I suddenly identify with the black community having been taken for granted. So, I absolutely understand that feeling. You know, Carlos, sticking with you a minute. A state with a number of -- where the numbers are large say for Latino voters, Arizona, Democrats worried that \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , the Party`s nominee for governor is fumbling a chance to defeat Kari Lake in one of the most closely watched races of the 2022 campaign. Republican Kari Lake is gaining momentum and national intention, even as she amplifies Donald Trump`s lie that the 2020 election was stolen and in races hard right stances on abortion and immigration. Arizona was a key state Biden`s 2020 presidential win. How worried are you about what`s going on there now?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, I do think that the Democratic nominee there has to do a better job of showing up and going on often. You know, the Latino community, sure they have been gravitating towards Republicans in recent years but a lot of Latinos don`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , a lot of Hispanics came from countries where democracy broke down, institutions broke down.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:17]\par \par \uc2 So, they can really relate to that democratic messaging about the 2020 Election, Donald Trump`s lies. But it seems like in Arizona, Democrats are playing a prevent defense there. Being too careful and that could really end up hurting them, especially if someone like Ms. Lake who is a dangerous election denier does make it to the governor`s mansion there.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Jennifer, in the coming weeks, New York City residents will be voting on a set of racial equity ballot proposals. And I know you`re deeply involved in and among your other titles I gave you, National Vice Chair of the National Board of National Action Networks, I know of your involvement here. And these proposals, these equity proposals include an initiative to add a preamble to the city charter that emphasizes the city`s racial diversity, also establish a racial equity office, plan and commission and measure the true cost of living that will go beyond simply measuring poverty to gauge the actual cost of living in the city. Now, what makes these three components so important? Would you like to see other cities also adopt initiatives like this?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER JONES AUSTIN, NYC RACIAL JUSTICE COMMISSION CHAIR: Indeed, indeed, Reverend Sharpton. I think one of the things that we need to appreciate, especially as we`re looking towards the midterms, and as we`re centering on what is happening with the Latino vote concerning Republican versus Democrat, is that the issues that we`re grappling with in this society in this nation, when it comes to an equity, concern African Americans and Latinos.\par \par \uc2 When we look at wage deprivation, it is Latinas who have the lowest wages. I think it`s right now it`s maybe between 55 and 56 cents on the dollar for every dollar earned by white men. When we look at climate issues, there`s a big concern about what`s happening in communities that are largely populated with persons of Hispanic origin. When we look at policing, the issues attend the same there, as they do for black and black communities.\par \par \uc2 And so, what we`re seeking to do here in New York City, and we want others to take a look at is making racial equity and justice, a central government function. And so, what these proposals are aimed at doing is putting racial equity and justice at the core, at the heart of governing functioning - functions, excuse me.\par \par \uc2 Looking at a preamble that casts a vision for what kind of city we want to live in, that essentially looks at quality health care and education, as being available but to everybody, regardless of the zip code that you live in, regardless of your ethnicity, your race.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And it will be on the ballot when New Yorkers can vote for this commission. And let me ask you, Jennifer, from your perspective on trying to improve racial equity in New York, I want to get your take on the Los Angeles new political maps, how people have heard about the audiotape in which the former L.A. City Council President was caught saying just some base racist things. But they are made the aren`t familiar with what the topic of conversation was about, which was redistricting and different groups fighting for political power in the city. It`s a hard situation to deal with, because a lot of these groups are people of color, who are facing discrimination in other ways. What`s your take on this issue? Because we keep hearing about the racist things that were said. But they were talking about how they cut up district lines, that would be one group against another. And people are not talking about that. What`s your take on that?\par \par \uc2 JONES AUSTIN: The challenge there in Los Angeles, and it`s playing out everywhere, is that when you do redistricting, you`re actually trying to move people around so that you can get greater voting blocks and certain people. And what they try to do is then less than the voting blocks, like so for African Americans, right now, Latinos represent nearly half of the Los Angeles, but they do not have a significant majority. They don`t have a majority of the Latino city council seats. And so, they`re looking at this trying to figure out how do they get more seats so that they have greater control?\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest issue here is this cancer -- it`s like a cancer to divisiveness. And really, the issues of Latinos are the very same issues for persons of African descent. As I just talked about, whether it`s healthcare economics, where we`re talking about climate control, these are the very same issues. And so, what we need to be centering on is not divisiveness, but unity. And essentially writing for one another. The biggest issue playing out here is that we know that in the next several years, the minority, if you will, become the majority, if people of color, actually act in aggregate act, as a coalition --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: If they`re together then a majority, if they don`t divide themselves, especially since we place --\par \par \uc2 JONES AUSTIN: So, we got this divisiveness going on. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Especially since we faced some of the same issues. \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  and Atty. Jennifer Jones Austin, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Going back to midterms now, the White House has announced today President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is headed to Fort Lauderdale, Florida on November 1 to campaign for gubernatorial candidate Charlie Chris, who`s running to unseat rising GOP star incumbent Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . It is just one of several hotly contested races in the Sunshine State.\par \par \uc2 Another one is Florida`s 27th district in Miami Dade County, where Democratic State Senator, Annette Taddeo, is challenging Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Maria Elvira Salazar\uc2 . State Senator Taddeo is joining us now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, to start -- thank you for being here. To start, you`ve made abortion rights, one of the key issues in your campaign, following the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe vs. Wade this past summer. Meanwhile, in contrast, your opponent, Congresswoman Salazar praised this decision and has voted twice against Women`s Health Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 Also on her record, Congresswoman Salazar voted against impeaching president -- former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  following the January 6 riot at the U.S. Capitol. And has proudly perpetuated Trump`s big lie, among other conspiracy theories. The Republican Party recently spent nearly $2 million on TV ads for Salazar, do you think this scared your opponent, is out of step with the voters of South Florida, and that you may be able to use that in a way toward victory?\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. ANNETTE TADDEO, (D) FLORIDA CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Well, my MAGA Republican opponent is definitely out of step with our district. And, you know, in our district, a lot of people come from other countries. We come here for freedom. And freedom means freedom to make your own decisions about your own body. And she keeps voting against that. But it`s also about democracy. And to have a representative of Miami, who is an election denier is completely unacceptable. And let me tell you, in Miami politicians love to talk about freedom in Cuba, Nicaragua, Venezuela. I came here from South America, from Colombia after my father was kidnapped by \uc2 the FARC\uc2 , a terrorist group. So, I know what it`s like to have to flee. But we understand that freedom in those countries means that we have to defend democracy right here in America. And that`s not what my opponent has done.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, you are running in this race as a Latina of Jewish faith. And you are poised to become the first Hispanic Jewish woman elected to Congress. How have these identities shaped your politics?\par \par \uc2 TADDEO: Look, obviously our religion is part of what makes us who we are. And for me, to come along means that leave the world a better place than the way you found it is something really, really important in my faith. And I know that this community has a lot of people that care about making sure that people have freedoms, and that we accept refugees that come here seeking freedom and not just use them as political pawns. And then stay quiet when that happens. And these are the contrasts. This is the reason why I am ahead, just pulled ahead only by one point but pulled ahead of my opponent. And that`s why $2 million by Kevin McCarthy because they know that we are in a position to win this race, and that it`s a race that they have to win to keep control for them, for him to get the gavel and we can have him get the gavel. We know that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And now before I let you go, Republicans have been on a winning streak in Florida over the last several years ever since former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and then Senator \uc2 Bill Nelson\uc2  won the elections in 2012. Republicans have swept all races for President, Senate and governor in Florida, causing America`s largest swing state to become increasingly red, rather than purple. How do you think Democrats can make Florida a swing state again?\par \par \uc2 TADDEO: We actually have to create the coalition of voters to win which is what I have done. I represent in the State Senate, I actually flipped a seat in a special election, the first time in Florida history, a seat from red to blue to become the first Latina Democrats in Florida history to serve in the Senate. And it is a seat that voted for Trump by six points.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:07]\par \par \uc2 So, we can do it if we create that coalition of voters. And we bring people together. And this community in particular likes people that puts the country first. And that`s the reason why something shocking. The former Republican of Florida`s Republican Party has endorsed me, a Cuban American against me, his name is Al Cardenas, and these are the kinds of coalition`s that we`re building and the momentum that we have and why I feel that if we keep doing it, we`re going to win in November. And eventually turn Florida back blue like we should because it goes through the Latino vote and through Miami-Dade County.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Florida State Senator Annette Taddeo, thank you for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This Tuesday will be the birthday of \uc2 NBA\uc2  superstar Brittney Griner, who remains in a prison in Moscow, being used in my opinion by the Russian government as a political pawn. She was arrested there February 17. And has remained that ever since has been tried. We cannot forget her as early in her incarceration on this show. And outside as my capacity as head of national action we`ve talked about free Britney. Remember her on Tuesday on her birthday. I remember even in July. I went to Chicago on the eve of the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  All Star game and joined Brittney`s wife Cherelle and had a press conference saying we must get BG free. It`s BG Day this Tuesday. Unfortunately, she will not be able to join us, but she should know that people have not forgotten her and not forgot that they will use somewhat of a statute to make a political statement.\par \par \uc2 I`m happy that President Biden in the State Department has spoken out about her and other hostages. But we cannot let it be silent for one moment. Tuesday`s BG Day. Free BG. We`re going to stay on this issue until BG comes home. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:14]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez, my colleague, starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221017eiag00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for October 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,995 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:07]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Race to the finish.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the nation is counting down the three weeks left until midterm elections. Early voting is currently underway in several states. Candidates are squaring off in debates going this weekend, and as the clock runs out, Democrats are calling on their biggest guns.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is in Oregon today stumping for the Democrat in that State`s gubernatorial race, which has become surprisingly close in a reliably blue state.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is in Michigan, pushing early voting and raising money for Democratic governor Gretchen Whitmer`s reelection campaign, and then there`s the news this morning that former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  will kick off his own midterm campaign travel with stops in at least three swing States later this month -- Michigan, Wisconsin, and of course, Georgia, where Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  faced off in their sole debate last night.\par \par \uc2 Walker, if nothing else, seemed to be working from the playbook popularized by his patron \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , constantly interrupting Warnock, flouting debate rules, and mechanically denying the personal allegations that have dogged his campaign for the last month. The knives are out with just 23 days left to go, and we dig into all the major campaign headlines tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 But as the campaign season enters its final stretch another campaign, the Congressional probe investigating the January 6th attack appears to have drawn to a dramatic close after its ninth and reportedly final hearing.\par \par \uc2 The House Select Committee wrapping its case with a dramatic ending this week. Its unanimous vote to subpoena \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his role in the events of the insurrection, Trump responding yesterday calling it of course, a "witch hunt," but not saying whether or not he will ultimately comply with the subpoena. Probably because, like so many things, the future of the probe will be decided by this upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 Deep show tonight with three weeks left. Let`s get started.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2 , Democrat of New York and Chair of the House Foreign Affairs Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, first of all, thanks for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Before I get to probably the biggest political news of the week, Election Day 2022 is just over three weeks away. Early voting underway in several States, opens up here in New York State in two weeks. Are you confident that Democrats will hold on to the House? And based on what you`ve seen, why?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 GREGORY MEEKS\uc2  (D-NY): Yes, Rev. I`ve been going around the country, I`ve stopped in many of those states that are up for grabs and I see the motivation of individuals, of Democrats coming together because they realize what is on the line here.\par \par \uc2 You know, generally what you would have is a downward trend as far as turnout is concerned, but people now understand by what you see through the January 6th hearings, that democracy is at the line -- is on the line, that you see the Supreme Court taking away rights as opposed to given rights and they want to know what`s next. And they also see a President that has kept his promises -- the first Black woman to be on the Supreme Court, to make sure that we reduce -- you know, the Inflation Reduction Act, to reduce it make sure that the price of drugs will be capped for seniors and that they can`t -- and that Medicare and Medicaid can negotiate those prices, the Transportation Infrastructure Bill.\par \par \uc2 So the promises that was made coming out of the greatest pandemic that I think we`ve seen in over a hundred years and what the President has done and accomplished, the war in Ukraine with the aggression of Russia, and making sure we are keeping our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies together.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the people are coming to the realization of what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats have done, and that is why you see Biden`s numbers going up and you see that people are not predicting any longer the tsunami that they thought was going to take place with Republicans over Democrats in every news headlines over headlines, they`re starting to say it`s a 50/50 shot and when you see that, you`re going to see the turnout increase.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:14]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let`s go to the January 6 investigation. House Select Committee investigating the January 6th attack on the US Capitol, ending its ninth and purportedly final hearing with a unanimous vote to subpoena \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 With barely three weeks to go before the midterm election that will determine the future of the probe, of course, a GOP takeover of the House next month is likely to mean the end of the congressional investigations into Trump come January. What can congressional Democrats do to continue this probe or some form of it should Republicans ended up taking the House next month?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: The first thing is, we are not going to let the Republicans take the House, I don`t think that the American people are going to let the Republicans take the House, because they know they want to cover up and they remember what took place when they were there.\par \par \uc2 You know, they brought in, you know, after subpoenas all came and \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and others, and they came in and testified. You see in the visuals here, particularly, a number of them trying to avoid the kind of subpoenas that were taking place to come in and to testify before the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 So, Democrats are going to maintain because what we`re trying to do is to show, you know, Rev, I`m the Chair of the Foreign Affairs Committee, we try to lead the world and talk about democracy and talk about how in fact, we have accountability and that is exactly what the January 6 Committee is doing. They are holding the rules and regulations and strengthening our institutions so that democracy continues to grow and thrive and individuals -- and show that no individual is above the law.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that you will see that same drumbeat until such time that the Justice Department ultimately takes over and does its homework or does its work and we are going to see whether or not indictments come down as a result -- a long awaited result of what took place within the Committee.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s stay right there. Let`s stay right there in your capacity as Chair of the House Foreign Affairs Committee.\par \par \uc2 As Russia`s war in Ukraine continues to drag on particularly in the country`s south and tensions appear to be escalating between North and South Korea over the former`s military demonstrations this weekend, are you concerned about either conflict creating an October Surprise that could spell trouble for Democrats and the President ahead of next month?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: Well, you know, you never say never, but I know that I have been to that region. You know, Rev, I was in Taiwan and South Korea, Japan, and Singapore. I`m going back to that region in the next week or so, going to India and Australia and I`ve been to various other European allies.\par \par \uc2 So one of the things that I think that is clear to the Russians and to others, that the democratic countries of the world are locked in and they are working together. That is one of the things that President Biden has made sure that we are working collectively.\par \par \uc2 And so I doubt if Russia or China or North Korea will do anything other than trying to, you know, display power like what took place when we left Taiwan. The North Koreans had missiles that went over Taiwan and went into the waters not far from Japan.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s a display, I think that they`re not -- you know, will not continue to make that mistake of actually attacking someone of our allies, because they know the ramifications of that and would be tremendous.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m pretty sure from all signals that I`ve gotten that I don`t anticipate that type of an offensive, but the United States and the President, I can tell you, if an accident took place in that regard or if there was some kind of aggression of that nature, there would be reprisals and the United States is in fact ready to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you this, Congressman, quickly because I`m out of time, I want to get your reaction to a pair of National Security concerns that emerged this week out of the January 6th investigation.\par \par \uc2 First is the late breaking news last night that the Select Committee has requested all communications between the Secret Service and the far-right Oath Keepers leading up to January 6th, ad second, there is this redacted e-mail publicly revealed this week, addressed to a top \uc2 FBI\uc2  official by an unnamed external source suggesting a large percentage of \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents was sympathetic to Trump and the insurrectionists at the time of attack, what`s your take on these developments?\par \par \uc2 [17:10:13]\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: I think that has to be thoroughly investigated clearly. You know, that`s one of the reasons why, you know, individuals say you`ve got to hold law enforcement accountable, because you have some rogue people in any profession, but in law enforcement, particularly.\par \par \uc2 You know, Rev, I`m a former prosecutor, I am a former District Attorney -- Assistant District Attorney in Queens County, and a former Special Narcotics Prosecutor. I always had to vet my police officers and I knew I had some that did the job overwhelmingly, those did the job right. But you had some bad apples, and the only way you could get the bad apples out is by investigating and looking at all of the evidence and all of the facts.\par \par \uc2 So, it helps -- the law at a point will help the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , it would help the Secret Service if in fact, there was an investigation, and you found that there was some that were sympathizers for the insurrectionists that was on January 6th, and get them out, that helps the overwhelming good.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that there needs to be a complete and full investigation so that we can get out those that were the sympathizers and did not do what they were supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, Congressman, Chairman, \uc2 Greg Meeks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Nan Whaley\uc2  candidate for governor of Ohio, and also former mayor of Dayton, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 NAN WHALEY (D) OHIO GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Reverend Sharpton, great to be on today.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Miss Whaley, you`re running for the gubernatorial seat in Ohio as a Democrat against current Republican Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 . There have been only two Democratic Governors in Ohio in the last 40 years.\par \par \uc2 To start, what made you want to take on the incumbent Governor?\par \par \uc2 WHALEY: Well look as Mayor of Dayton, a job which I love, mayor is a job where you have to be visionary about where you want your community to go. But at the same time, you have to fill the potholes and plow the snow or they will kick your tail out of office, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 So we noticed that communities across the State had local leaders doing their level best to move their communities forward, but they didn`t have a partner in the Governor`s seat, and really, as we`ve seen the decline of Ohio because \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  has been in office since I was 10 months old for the past 46 years, Ohio has gotten further and further behind in his leadership and we deserve better than what we`re getting at the Statehouse.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, if elected, you`d be just the second female Governor of Ohio in its history, the first only served briefly on an interim basis.\par \par \uc2 You`ve made abortion rights a centerpiece to your campaign vowing to amend the State Constitution, protecting abortion access in Ohio after the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe v. Wade. Why is this a top priority for you?\par \par \uc2 WHALEY: Well, look, Reverend the minute June 24th happened, all women across the country lost a right and it was devastating for us. It has never happened in my lifetime, a group of people to lose a freedom.\par \par \uc2 But if you were in Ohio, it was terrifying. Seven hours after the abortion -- Roe fell, we witnessed the six-week abortion ban go in place in our State and we have seen the devastating ramifications from that.\par \par \uc2 A 10-year-old in Columbus who was raped had to go across State lines to terminate her pregnancy. When \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  was asked about it, he said he doubted she existed and if she existed, she was lying.\par \par \uc2 And when Columbus police arrested her rapist and he confessed, \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  only had two words which were, "No comment."\par \par \uc2 This is a basic freedom. It`s an economic issue, and it is so out of step. Mike DeWine`s position is so out of step as he plans to go as far as possible on the issue after Roe fell, his words again, not mine, and we don`t even have to imagine what that is in Ohio because there is a bill in the Statehouse that bans in vitro fertilization, bans birth control, bans abortion at conception.\par \par \uc2 And you know, Reverend Sharpton, you`ve been to Ohio, Ohio is a commonsense state. It is a pro-choice state. Eighty-two percent of Ohioans believe we should have some sort of access to abortion. This is an economic issue for our State, it is a freedom issue for women and it must stop if we want Ohio to be a place that people want to come and grow and be a part of.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s turn to another issue, the issue of guns.\par \par \uc2 You were the mayor of Dayton when a gunman killed nine people within seconds at a bar in a popular entertainment district there. And earlier this year, Governor DeWine signed a bill allowing Ohioans to carry a concealed handgun without a permit or training.\par \par \uc2 This is a personal issue for you. How would you address gun violence in Ohio if elected in contrast to your opponent?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:04]\par \par \uc2 WHALEY: You are right, Reverend Sharpton, this is a personal issue for me. I stood with the people of Dayton the day after that shooting where nine people were killed and 27 more injured in 32 seconds, and watched thousands of Daytonians come together and \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , and when the \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  got up to speak, the people of Dayton shouted, "Do something," so loudly, the Governor had to sit down and couldn`t finish his comments.\par \par \uc2 The next day, he told me he intended to do something around gun safety, and I took him at his word. I stood next to him when he unveiled his bill and then I watched him do absolutely nothing to move it forward.\par \par \uc2 You know, the radicals and extremists got a hold of him and keeping our community safe is just something he talks about, but no action happens. And you`re right, permitless concealed carry is something he signed, that makes an officer-involved shootings go up by 14 percent in our communities.\par \par \uc2 And then in the wake of Uvalde, his answer was to arm teachers, bus drivers, and cafeteria workers with very little training.\par \par \uc2 Again, law enforcement was against this and so were teachers. This is what we are getting with a Governor who is so out of step, and let me be clear, again, Ohio is a commonsense state. Nine out of 10 Ohioans believe we should have universal background checks. This is what we need to do to move forward, it is to roll back these backwards bills that put our communities and make our communities less safe, pass universal background checks in the State and then pass red flag laws, so we can make sure that our places of worship, our schools, our football fields -- on Friday nights now, we`ve had three shootings in Ohio during Friday Night Lights across the State.\par \par \uc2 Enough is enough, and we deserve better in Ohio, and we can`t get it from \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  because he says what is politically convenient at the time, but when the rubber meets the road, he is right with the extremists and radicals.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Ohio has been the subject of a few high profile police killings. This year, 25-year-old, Jayland Walker and 20-year-old Donovan Lewis was shot and killed by police, both victims were Black. As you know, I`ve been to Ohio with several of those cases.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the murder of George Floyd in 2020, you introduced five steps of change for the City of Dayton as Mayor. If elected as Governor, what is your plan to hold Ohio Police Departments accountable for police brutality, especially against the Black community?\par \par \uc2 WHALEY: Yes, as the former Mayor of Dayton, I saw this firsthand. I led the police reform process that brought together the community and police to rewrite our policies and make everyone safer, and we found common sense policies that everyone could agree on.\par \par \uc2 We actually, Reverend, actually just won an All-American city award because the process in Dayton was a bottom up approach, not a top-down approach, and that`s what I think is key whenever we`re doing this work, that we talk to folks and engage people that are most impacted in the problem. And in the case of police reform, it is the African-American community and the police coming together to find solutions.\par \par \uc2 Now the State of Ohio can do something that I think would be very helpful to local communities, is immediately when there is an officer involved shooting or interaction, the State immediately investigates so that way, communities don`t have to investigate themselves. There can be transparency and an outside process that is very, very important for local communities to continue to build trust with their communities, and make sure that the investigations are fully transparent, and that they actually happen.\par \par \uc2 Right now, that is not happening. The State can actually turn down local communities if they don`t want to investigate and that puts a big burden on the communities that want transparency and an outside investigator to look into these issues.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Nan Whaley\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We should mention, the POLITICSNATION team has also reached out to the DeWine campaign and extended invitation for him to join us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: Two scandals, two parties, and two different outcomes.\par \par \uc2 We`ll break it down in this week`s "Gotcha."\par \par \uc2 And later, Herschel Walker`s debate performance looked very familiar. But will it work with voters? My political panel weighs in.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui, with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, good Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Starting with breaking news for you. In Iran, a fire has broken out at the Evin Prison in Tehran. Footage posted online shows flames and smoke blazing. Gunshots and alarms were reported says Iran state media. The fire has reportedly been contained.\par \par \uc2 Student loan forgiveness applications, they are starting. The Department of Education launched a beta test of its Forgiveness Website, Friday. Applications will be processed when the site officially launches later this month.\par \par \uc2 And holiday travel for this upcoming season is set to be one of the most expensive on record says travel booking group, Hopper. Thanksgiving airfare prices are currently up 25 percent from last year and up by 55 percent for Christmas.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The Republican Party loves to talk about itself as the party of personal responsibility. The concept is at the core of its views on the economy, law and order and many other topics.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes time to stop talking about personal responsibility and actually hold colleagues accountable, a bad different picture emerges.\par \par \uc2 In the past week, two elected officials, one Democrat, one Republican were both caught in a scandal for saying things that were very racist. The Republican, US Senator Tommy Tuberville of Alabama stood in front of a crowd at a Trump rally in Nevada, implying Black people are criminals and Democrats want to give said criminals reparations.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Not only was Tuberville not punished for his racist rhetoric, other Republicans actually tried to defend or downplay his remarks. Congressman \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  of Nebraska told "Meet the Press" that Senator Tuberville should have been more polite.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s compare that to the case of Los Angeles City Council President Nury Martinez when audio emerged of the California Democrat, making racist comments about Black people and indigenous Mexicans behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 Her fellow colleagues in the party were rightly shocked and offended. She chose to apologize and eventually resigned.\par \par \uc2 The contrast between the two stories could not be more clear. When a Democrat was caught behaving badly, she was held accountable. When a Republican did the same, colleagues rallied around him. And this isn`t the first time. In 2017, former US Senator Al Franken was asked to step down following the wave of accusations of sexual misconduct. Democrats were unwilling to tolerate allegedly inappropriate behavior, even from one of the party`s most prominent politicians.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, this was happening, Republicans were falling in line behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his presidential term got underway, choosing to look the other way as he embroiled himself in a series of ever escalating scandals, such as appearing to sympathize with White supremacists after Charlottesville and taking on his own Intelligence Agencies over Russian meddling in the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 At the time, it seemed to some that Republicans were compromising their values for Trump. But half a decade later, it appears that Trumpism defines Republican values.\par \par \uc2 It is almost unimaginable now to think that high ranking Republican politicians would apologize or resign over any scandal no matter how serious. So, the next time you hear Republicans pontificating about personal responsibility, tell them to follow Democrats` example and get their own house in order first.\par \par \uc2 I Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:37]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 There are several topics to discuss, so let`s turn to my political panel for the analysis.\par \par \uc2 Joining me is Reed Galen, cofounder of "The Lincoln Project," and Atima Omara, Democratic strategist and president of Omara Strategy Group.\par \par \uc2 Reed, let`s start with the Georgia Senate debate last night between Senator Raphael, Warnock and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . The two candidates addressed topics such as abortion, crime, and Medicaid expansion. But the most unusual moment came from Walker during the question about crime. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I am with many police officers, and at the same time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Walker, I need to let you know, Mr. Walker, you are very well aware of the rules tonight.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you have a prop. That is not allowed, sir. I ask you to put that prop away.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, it is not a prop. This is real.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now going into the debate, Walker was down in the polls after recent allegations against the Republican Senate nominee. It seems like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has taken debate cues from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with his constant interrupting and rule breaking. Is it going to hurt him with voters?\par \par \uc2 REED GALEN, COFOUNDER, THE LINCOLN PROJECT: You know, it might, Reverend, but I`ll tell you this, I mean, his campaign has been so insane to begin with that I`m not sure if he is not down 20 already, that he won`t make this a close race just on the natural already.\par \par \uc2 I don`t understand. I`ve got to be honest with you. I`ve seen a lot of things.\par \par \uc2 Just 12 years ago, there were not one, but two Republican US Senate candidates who even approached language like this, behavior like this, and they were left dead in the water. But here he is.\par \par \uc2 We shouldn`t take anything for granted. I would say that you`re absolutely right. He acted just like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He was going to interrupt. I thought, the Reverend Warnock did exactly what he needed to do, talked about his record, talked about what he wants to do for the people of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that this is one where just like it is an unusual year everywhere, this Senate race is going to come right down to the end. I do think that Reverend Warnock will ultimately win, but we should not take anything, again, anything for granted, as we were these last 24 days before the election.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Atima, also on the midterms` front, former President Obama is traveling to various cities pushing midterm early voter turnout in support for Democrats in key races in Georgia, and Michigan, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the final stretch, how much of a difference can the former President make in swing voters?\par \par \uc2 ATIMA OMARA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: I think a decent amount. There are some who voted for him in 2008, and again in 2012, and still think fondly of him. I know, there are several folks, even some Republicans who are like, I would have been happy with a third term of Obama.\par \par \uc2 So you know, he is going to Wisconsin, where he won both times and so, you know, I think, you know, getting sort of that old magic going on the road for those who remember his time as President rather fondly I think will be helpful in some of the pivotal States.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Reed, switching gears now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is responding to his soon-to-be served subpoena with a lengthy letter to the January 6 Committee Chairman Congressman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , after it was announced at this week`s hearing.\par \par \uc2 He is not yet saying if he will comply with the demand for documents and testimony, but he used the letter to double down on his false claims about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This might have been the last hearing for the Committee. How does the panel`s investigative work compare to hearings in the past, such as the 9/11 hearings, which led to the creation of Homeland Security and the Watergate hearings, which led to Nixon`s resignation and major government reforms?\par \par \uc2 Will the January 6th Committee lead anything major? And if not, can we say it was successful?\par \par \uc2 GALEN: Well, I think we can say it was successful, Reverend, because the Committee, a bipartisan Committee, we should note did an excellent job of laying out the case that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew all along what he was doing. We even have testimony from one of his aides going back as early as July of 2020 that he was never going to go quietly, that it was a conspiracy between him, some donors, some activists, some militia-type groups.\par \par \uc2 They knew they were going to commit violence on that day. They wanted to stop the certification process, and they almost succeeded.\par \par \uc2 Will it have an effect? I think that it very well could lead to not only the indictment of some of Donald Trump`s closest advisers, but also the former President himself, which I think he certainly rightly deserves, not only for this, but certainly many other things.\par \par \uc2 Do I think it will have long-term impact? Not structurally, like you described after Watergate or 9/11 simply because I don`t think that the time or the place or the government we have now or the politics we have now will allow that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Atima, according to a new exclusive report in "The Wall Street Journal," two people in Donald Trump`s inner circle are talking to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about moving government documents in and around Mar-a-Lago. NBC has reached out to all parties for comment, we have not yet heard back from Trump.\par \par \uc2 How damaging is this to Trump`s team`s claims he complied with the DOJ demands?\par \par \uc2 OMARA: I think, this is pretty damaging, because these are people who are in his inner circle and obviously, they are doing the math and they`re like, well, you know, if they`re not going to put it on him, I`m the one who is facing jail time and I don`t have, you know, money to spend on lawyers, or any other type of defense. So, I`m going to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 So I think, you know, as it was very damaging to see some of his other aides come forward, like Cassie earlier this summer, in the summer with the 1/6 testimony, I think this could be depending on who it is, but definitely, if they have evidence of having moved on his behalf, and told directly, could lead to some additional damage for him, sure.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Atima, before we go, you`ve previously served as Board Chair and Vice Chair of Planned Parenthood Metro Washington Action Forum.\par \par \uc2 OMARA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator Bernie Sanders wrote an op-ed this week, entitled "Democrats Shouldn`t Focus Only On Abortions In The Midterm. That`s A Mistake." is the issue of abortion, still giving Democrats momentum going into the midterms? How can they keep it top of mind for voters?\par \par \uc2 OMARA: It`s definitely top of mind for voters. You know, I`m always fighting when I hear Senator Sanders talk about abortion as if it is not tied to economic concerns, it is definitely one of the leading indicators for folks who decide when they want to have families when you look at polling, it is like can I afford to raise a child in this country with child care and other things?\par \par \uc2 But when you look at polling for what is motivating voters, \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  put out a poll just earlier this week that showed 76 percent of voters across a political spectrum are motivated by what happened with Roe to actually support candidates who will protect abortion access this fall.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s still top of mind in most of the States that are going to be competitive for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  and in Congress. Abortion and economy are very much in step.\par \par \uc2 So you know, as long as Republicans who are trying to pass bans, restrictions, you`re hearing some of these horror stories coming out of States, it is just going to stay top of mind for individuals in this country if they go to the polls this fall.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Reed Galen and Atima Omara, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Comedians, Eric Andre and Clayton English join us to talk about a lawsuit that is no laughing matter. They`ll explain why they say they were targeted at one of the nation`s busiest airports. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: An \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  Program aimed at curbing drugs in the city`s airport is under fire after two Black performers, comedians Eric Andre and Clayton English filed a Federal lawsuit this week, alleging they were racially profiled and questioned about drug possession on separate occasions by police officers as they attempted to board aircrafts at \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Their suit against the Clayton County Police Department and the District Attorney`s Office contending that with this program, in addition to being unconstitutional, It "targets are selected disproportionately based on their race."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, comedian Eric Andre, host of "The Eric Andre Show," and comedian Clayton English, season nine winner of NBC`s "Last Comic Standing."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Andre and Mr. English, thank you both for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ANDRE, HOST, "THE ERIC ANDRE SHOW": Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 CLAYTON ENGLISH, COMEDIAN: It is a pleasure. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, to recap a bit more for our audience. Your lawsuit contends that you were both stopped and searched in separate instances about six months apart for trying to board an aircraft at \uc2 Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International Airport\uc2 , the result of a program designed to curb drug trafficking through random "consensual encounters" according to Clayton County Police. But your lawsuit contends that you were singled out, racially profiled because you were both Black in both instances.\par \par \uc2 You were asked to hand over boarding passes and identification. Now, in your case Mr. English, the lawsuit claims that you submitted your bag for officers` search not believing you had a choice in the matter. In your case, Mr. Andre, A Clayton County Police statement says that you agreed just Officers search, not believing you had a choice in the matter. In your case Mr. Andre Clayton County police statement says that you agreed to such a search willingly.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We reached out to Clayton County Police Department, a spokesperson reiterating that the department does not have a comment at this time as litigation is pending.\par \par \uc2 Apart from that, gentlemen, is there anything I`m missing here? Mr. Andre, first and then you?\par \par \uc2 ANDRE: Yes, it wasn`t really consensual.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I got on the -- I was coming home from a work trip. I was filming an \uc2 HBO\uc2  show in Charleston, South Carolina, got to the Atlanta Airport to connect to go to Los Angeles back home. You know, I went to the gate, they look at my ticket. I got that awkward, claustrophobic jet bridge and two White Clayton County police officers, out of nowhere, kind of almost like ambushed me and started asking me if I was carrying drugs on me, if I was transporting drugs, if I was using drugs, buying drugs, they went through all these specific drugs.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I was the only person of color in front of me or behind me, and clearly they were racially profiling me. The whole experience was humiliating and dehumanizing. And if it`s not really consensual, when two cops pop out and start berating you and barraging you with all of these questions about drugs, you don`t feel like that you have the right to leave.\par \par \uc2 And then, they ask if they could search my bag, finally, after all this questioning and I went, "Do I have to say yes?" And the older cop was like, "No." And then I was like, "Well, then no."\par \par \uc2 So it was traumatizing, and it is not the only time I`ve been racially profiled at the airport. I have been racially profiled in like a lot of airports all over the country. So it is a --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What about you, Mr. English?\par \par \uc2 ENGLISH: Yes, my -- part of the reason I reached out to Eric Andre was because our stories were so similar. When I was going to the airport, you know, this is past security. This is past everything. I give him my ticket to get on the plane, on the jet bridge, two officers confront me basically cornering me. They were on both sides of me. So, there was really nowhere for me to go.\par \par \uc2 People are squeezing around me. It is humiliating because people are looking at you like, "Oh, what is he doing? What`s going on here?" And I`m the only one being stopped. They tell me that they`re looking for cocaine, methamphetamines, do I have any of these things? And at this point, my biggest concern is making this flight because I have a writing job that I`m trying to get back to in LA. That`s, you know, things like this are time sensitive. So, I have to be there tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 So in my mind, and this may be partly for being from the south, a lot of times, we`re just taught whatever police do, you just need to comply to get yourself through the situation. And this was one of those times.\par \par \uc2 And just --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now --\par \par \uc2 ENGLISH: What was that?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 ENGLISH: They say, "Can we search the bag?" I knew I didn`t have any of those things, but the way they were asking me the questions, I started doubting whether I had done anything. So they searched through everything. They handed me my ID, they handed me my boarding pass, I was able to get on the plane.\par \par \uc2 But the rest of the time, I`m just thinking, "Man, they`re going to come arrest me once everybody gets on the plane." I`m not sure what I did. I know I didn`t do anything. But I`m feeling like, "Man, did somebody say I did something? What did they think? What are they thinking?" And that`s what I was stuck with for the remainder of the flight.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t say anything to any of the people that worked at Delta. I wanted to ask, "Is this something that normally happens?` Because in my experience, I`ve been profiled before, but never on the jet bridge in this manner, and where it just seemed to be solely me getting harassed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, on that point, according to an Associated Press assessment of Clayton County Police records, there were 402 jet bridge stops between August 30th, 2020 and April 30, 2021 around the time of your respective encounters. Out of that 402, the passenger`s race was listed, in 378 of them, 56 percent of those stops were of Black people, people of color accounting for 68 percent of the stops.\par \par \uc2 According to your lawsuits, which adds that out of those 402 stops, only three resulted in reported drug seizures. From that only two people were ultimately charged.\par \par \uc2 Mr. English, what do you make of those numbers?\par \par \uc2 ENGLISH: I mean, I think the numbers speak for itself. I think there was an interesting stat that said the odds of them randomly selecting that amount of Black people or people of color was like one in 100 trillion. So, I don`t think it was a drug interdiction program, but because just the logic center in my brain says, who tries to get drugs when they are leaving your jurisdiction?\par \par \uc2 So I think what the other findings show is that they were confiscating money from people and large sums and if the stats are what they are, then those people are probably Black people.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You, Eric Andre, how do you look at those stats? And do you have any final thoughts quickly, we are running out of time.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE: Yes, it is the same thing. I think African-American airline passengers only make up about eight percent of the population. So, how could we be stopped 54 percent of the time or higher?\par \par \uc2 And what Mr. English and I are trying to do is not just represent our case, but we are using our platform and our resources to stand up for the community we identify with for the people that don`t have our platform, who don`t have our resources. So it`s not just about the two of us. It`s about the community and us standing up for our own community because this is clearly unethical.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right --\par \par \uc2 ENGLISH: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Eric Andre and Clayton English, thank you both for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE: Thank you, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: My Final Thoughts after the break. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:50:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In this past week, I spent much of it on the road in my capacity as President of National Action Network. Last Sunday, I preached the morning service at Howard University`s Rankin Chapel and then Tuesday, joined Black contractors in Cleveland, Ohio, fighting for contracts and economic justice. And Thursday evening was in Atlanta, I gave the addressed after two days of rededication of the Martin Luther King Chapel in Morehouse College.\par \par \uc2 But the trend I picked up between these three and other communities that I talk to by radio, is that people are picking how they`re going to vote based on personality who they like, who they think has the best optics, who speaks well.\par \par \uc2 This election is more serious than personality. It should be taken more seriously than who is eloquent and who is not. The very nature of democracy, our rights to vote, our rights for women to choose, our right in terms of dealing with climate. That`s what you need to vote about and we need to vote like our lives depends on it, because it does.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with my colleague, Alicia Menendez will return tomorrow. Tonight, we bring you this week`s January 6th Committee hearing in its entirety.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:29]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221016eiaf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE KORNACKI COUNTDOWN for October 14, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alex Wagner, Steve Kornacki, Ellison Barber, David Plouffe, Rich  Lowry, Shaquille Brewster \par \uc2 8,755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: In addition to DeSantis` stunt potentially helping the migrants, it is now also the subject of a Treasury Department probe looking into whether DeSantis may have misused COVID-19 relied aid to fund the flights. You know what they say about karma.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us tonight, Rachel will be back on Monday, and I will see you on Tuesday. And now, with just 25 days until the election, it is time for something special, the Kornacki Countdown, with Steve Kornacki.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC HOST: And we are 24 days, 19 hours, 59 minutes, 38 seconds and counting until the first polls close on election night. Thank you so much for joining us for this election countdown.\par \par \uc2 And here is what`s going to be happening every Friday night from now until election day. We are going to be right here, at this hour, at this board, bringing you the latest in polling, the latest data, the latest on the ground reporting and expert analysis as we draw near one of the most anticipated midterm elections in modern memory. That`s going to be November the 8th.\par \par \uc2 Already, voting started in more than a dozen states. That includes some of the most critical battlegrounds on the board, Pennsylvania, Florida, Wisconsin, just within the last couple of days, Ohio and Arizona, too. And in Georgia early voting set to begin this coming Monday. And it is the peach state where we start tonight with our breaking news.\par \par \uc2 A head-to-head televised showdown this evening between Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , and his Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now all year, the Georgia Senate race has been one of the most closely watched in the country, but that has been even more true than ever over the last 10 days. It began with that reporting first from The Daily Beast, then by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , that Walker had pushed for and paid for his then girlfriend to have an abortion in 2009, a charge that Walker has denied.\par \par \uc2 Now since that reporting, this has become the question when it comes to the battle for control of the Senate. Will it cost Walker the voters? And if it does, will it cost Republicans a seat they may need to win if they`re going to gain control of the Senate? We knew that that topic was going to come up tonight, it did, here`s how it played out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUCK LANFORD, ACHOR, WAGA-TV: Your former girlfriend made public accusations, saying you paid for an abortion, and that even courage her to have another. In and ABC News interview this week, you said that the accusations are, quote, "all lies." For the voters watching tonight, can you explain the circumstances surrounding these claims? You have 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: As I said, that`s a lie. You know, most thing, I put it in the book, one thing about my life, is I`ve been very transparent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: That was only a small part of what proved to be a contentious hour-long debate. Abortion, inflation, health care, Donald Trump`s claims about the 2020 election, all topics producing potentially significant moments. Here is a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TINA TYUS-SHAW, ANCHOR, WSAV-TV: Did President Biden defeat former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: President Biden won and Senator Warnock won, that`s the reason I decided to run.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): The patient`s room is too narrow, and small, and cramped a space for a woman, her doctor, and the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . We are witnessing, right now, what happens when politicians, most of - -most of them men, pile into patient`s rooms. You get what you are seeing right now.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: And the women of Georgia -- the women of Georgia deserve a senator who will stand with them. I trust women more than I trust politicians.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Ma`am, may I respond?\par \par \uc2 LANFORD: Very quickly, Mr. Walker.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: You know, it is, and I heard about, it and I heard he was a talker but did he not mention that there was a baby in that room as well? And also, did he not mention --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 LANFORD: Raise your hand if you support a federal minimum wage. Mr. Walker, you did not raise your hand, so I`d like to ask you, first of all, why not, and what would you do to help struggling Georgians?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, you said a federal -- I think, right now, and he said it, I want a federal government, no, he wants a federal government to run your life. Right now, you have the world with different corporations, to see just where they can pay.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: I think people need a livable wage. And some of that is being addressed right now in the strong labor market. You can support police officers as I have done through the COPS program, through the Invest to Protect program, while, at the same time holding police officers like all professions accountable.\par \par \uc2 [22:04:52]\par \par \uc2 One thing I have not done, I`ve never pretended to be a police officer.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: And -- and I have never threatened a shootout with the police.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: And you know what`s so funny? I am one with many police officers. At the same time --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: Mr. Walker, Mr. Walker, Mr. Walker --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No, no, no. When he said the problem with the truth --\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: Mr. Walker, excuse me, Mr. Walker, please, out of respect --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: The truth is in here.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: I need to let you know, Mr. Walker.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: You are very well --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: -- aware of the rules tonight.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: And you have a prop.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: That is not allowed, sir.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: I ask you to put that prop away.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, it`s not a prop, this is real. And he said I have a problem - - I`ve never worked with law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: But it is considered a prop, Mr. Walker, excuse me, sir.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: You`re very well aware of the rules, aren`t you?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, he --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: Aren`t you aware of the rules?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: He brought the truth, so let`s talk about the truth.\par \par \uc2 TYUS-SHAW: Thank you for putting that prop away.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes. What I want you to do is get off the government health care, and get on the health care he`s got.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: Georgia needs to expand Medicare. It costs us more not to expand. What we`re doing right now is we`re subsidizing health care in other states.\par \par \uc2 LANFORD: Would you support President Biden running for a second term in 2024?\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: I have not spent a minute thinking about what politicians should run for what in 2024.\par \par \uc2 LANFORD: Would you support a Trump 2024 run?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, I would. And let me tell you. President Trump is my friend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, let`s go now to two reporters who are in Georgia for tonight`s debate, NBC News correspondent, Ellison Barber, and \uc2 New York Times\uc2  political reporter, Maya King.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us. Ellison, let me start with you. Basically, what was each campaign hoping to achieve through tonight`s debate, and what are they saying afterwards about whether they achieved it?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So, going into the debate night, we were expecting to hear \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  really make an aggressive push to try and tie Senator Warnock to President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does not have a very high approval rating in the state.\par \par \uc2 In a poll by the AJC back in September, among likely Georgia voters, the president`s approval rating overall was around 37 percent. We`ve seen Walker do that throughout the campaign, when I was speaking to sources close to the Walker campaign. They were adamant that he was going to aggressively make that point tonight. We saw him try and bring that up a number of times.\par \par \uc2 Warnock`s rebuttal to that was he said something along the lines of, it is clear to me that my candidate would like to be running against anyone, except for me. On Warnock`s side, we expected to see him come in and highlight a lot of the work he has already done in Washington, D.C., work he says is directly helping voters here in Georgia. And also highlight some of the bipartisan legislation that he has worked on. We saw him do that tonight.\par \par \uc2 It was a very interesting, quick back and forth debate, both sides are already declaring victory. I have my phone out because I`ve been texting the sources from both campaigns. Warnock`s side, they feel like he did a very good job tonight.\par \par \uc2 His campaign manager, already released a statement saying, in part, there is no question that Reverend Warnock won tonight`s debate, and showed Georgians all the reasons they elected him in the first place, while \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  offered no solutions, lied, and proved once again that he isn`t ready to represent Georgians.\par \par \uc2 Walker`s campaign manager tweeted that he dominated the debate tonight, he had campaign surrogates in the spin room after singing his praises. I just text from another Walker campaign source, that`s the word they used again, dominated on Warnock side.\par \par \uc2 A source close to his campaign, when I asked how he thought Warnock did tonight, the first text he sent was, well, for starters, the reverend did not flush a fake police badge. That was a really interesting odd moment where you have the moderator telling Walker, you know the rules, you can`t have any sort of prob. And he held up what appeared to be some sort of police like badge, even though he, himself is not a police officer. Steve?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, interesting moment there, indeed, Ellison. Maya, you`ve done some reporting, and I know you`ve written about Republicans rallying around \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in the wake of these allegations about paying for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 This is the kind of race, though, obviously when you look at the polling in it, where even if most Republicans stick with Walker, if there is just a small amount of slippage there for him with that Republican base, that could make the difference between winning and losing this case.\par \par \uc2 I`m curious, in your reporting, have you detected, have you picked up on any cracks in that Republican support for Walker, even if it is small?\par \par \uc2 MAYA KING, POLITICAL REPORTER, NEW YORK TIMES: Well, I`ve certainly talk to a number of conservative voters who have expressed some concerns. Yes, who have said that they are all in behind Governor Kemp, but have not yet made up their mind about whether or not they wanted to support \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think tonight was the chance -- a chance for Walker to make an appeal to those voters. You hear, at the end in his closing statements, he said, for those voters who are nervous about voting for me, calling himself a non-politician, he essentially made a pitch to them, saying I will be ready on day one to represent you in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:04]\par \par \uc2 The difference, though, is that those are also the voters that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  has spoken to repeatedly, not only on the debate stage talking extensively about his work with Republican senators, but also on the airwaves. He`s cut advertisements with a similar message. So, it`s clear that there`s a slice of the electorate in Georgia that both candidates are absolutely angling for in these next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, yes, there definitely are some swing voters here, the entire balance of the Senate, by the way, could come down to how those swing voters split here.\par \par \uc2 Maya King, Ellison Barber, thank you very much for joining us after a lively debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 And let`s take a look here, I head back over to the board. Speaking of where this race stands, some of the numbers let`s take a look here. A closer look at Georgia in the polling, how this fits into that question of Senate control.\par \par \uc2 So, what you are seeing here, this is the current polling average in those Georgia race. And Warnock is leading Walker here. We have had a couple of polls that came out since those allegations about 10 days ago were lodged against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And you could see here it`s a little bit more than three points the lead that Warnock enjoys over Walker. The x-factor to remember when you think about Georgia, and I probably don`t need to remind you of this if you remember the 2020 election, Georgia is a runoff state.\par \par \uc2 And there is actually a third candidate in this race, a libertarian. Typically, the libertarian is going to get 2 to 3 percent of the vote in Georgia. Well, remember what happened in 2020, both of those Senate races went to runoffs.\par \par \uc2 If neither one of these candidates can crack 50 percent on election night, then guess what, Georgia goes to a runoff. The runoff would be held on December 6. When you look at the broader Senate map, it is not impossible at all that this control of the Senate could, again, come down to a runoff in Georgia. We would then have a month after the election of essentially overtime with these two candidates having the runoff to determine control. That is not impossible.\par \par \uc2 Two other things to note here, when you look inside the numbers, one of the polls that came out this week, and Emerson poll, there is a bit of a difference there, maybe it speaks to some of those allegations against Walker, the favorable, unfavorable score for Warnock, his favorable is just a tick over 50, for Walker, he is a tick upside down there.\par \par \uc2 There is a slight potentially significant difference there just in terms of how these candidates are viewed personally. And then, you heard Maya talking about this, there is also a gap here between the Senate race, and the governor`s race.\par \par \uc2 And the governor`s race in Georgia, the Republican \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in the poll average, a comfortable lead form him over \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , at least right now. There appears to be something we thought might be extinct in politics in Georgia, split ticket voters. Folks voting for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , but they`re not voting for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , at least not at this moment in the Senate race, but instead going for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is that a dynamic that is going to persist until election day? So, let`s talk more about those dynamics in Georgia. Now joining us is Charles Bullock, he`s a political science professor at the University of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Professor Bullock, thank you for joining us. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Look, you know the lay of the land in Georgia, you saw that debate tonight, you have a sense of the electorate. I`m curious, do you think that anything that happened in that debate tonight will affect this election, one way or another?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BULLOCK, POLITICAL SCIENCE PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF GEORGIA: I don`t think anything that happened tonight is going to make a dramatic change. No one, apparently, made a statement which a misstatement kind of thing which then becomes the news, the kind of thing which may even work its way into a political ad if someone says something completely out of left field.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the candidates essentially held their own tonight.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: The -- we played two clips when it came to the subject of former President Trump. At the end of the debate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was asked, would you support Trump or 2024? And he immediately said that he would.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the night, though, when he was asked if Biden had legitimately won in the election in 2020, he said flatly that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had won the election in 2020. That puts him at odds, obviously with everything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 