? Or are they poised to pull off something practically unheard of for a White House party in a midterm election?\par \par \uc2 David Plouffe and Rich Lowry will be here to tackle that, next.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, our countdown continues to the first poll closing inside now of 24 hours -- 24 days and 20 hours until election day. Let`s take a look at the big picture view right here of the midterm climate here.\par \par \uc2 Again, 24 plus days out from the election. So, one thing we always look at in the midterm is the president`s job approval rating, typically the lower it is, the worse the president`s party does.\par \par \uc2 Here is Joe Biden`s approval rating, the average, it`s 43.3 percent. Put this in some perspective. Here is his recent predecessors. This is where they were this exact name pointed in their first midterm. And you see Biden`s numbers right there, where Trump`s where four years ago, Bush, excuse me, Obama, in `10, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  back in `94, the one exception, Bush in 2002.\par \par \uc2 And what this translated into, the numbers, like Bidens, in Trump`s case, in Obama`s case, in Clinton`s case, those were dozens of lost House seats, and the president`s party lost control of the House in each one of those elections.\par \par \uc2 Biden`s approval rating puts them in that category. Simple story, right? No. Here`s where it gets complicated. Another thing we look at that, the generic ballot. The poll question that asks, which party would you like to vote for, Democrats or Republicans to control Congress.\par \par \uc2 And you could see right now Republicans lead on that question, it`s by nine-tenths of one point. And what you are looking at on the screen are the recent wave of elections, 2018, 2014 was a wave for Republicans, 2010, 2006. This is where the generic ballot was in all of those years. It`s lower this year, it`s closer, tighter than it was and all of these wave