ably going to get anyway. It`s coming to his column. But sure, that sounds like a race that`s going to come down to at most, four points, could be two points, could be a point.\par \par \uc2 And so, things like turnout in Philadelphia, and in Pittsburgh, you get the suburban margin that Biden got, that \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  is going to get, super important. But Pennsylvania is key just because that is the Democrats best opportunity. I still think Wisconsin is going to be close, but Pennsylvania is the best opportunity for the Democrats to steal a seat. And when things are this close, just again, in terms of that chess match, it`s incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Rich, our last statement we were talking about, that Georgia race. And Charles Bullock from University of Georgia was talking about that Kemp, Warnock voter in Georgia who basically is saying, you know, hey, look, Trump is playing a role in that.\par \par \uc2 That, you know, Kemp was that political war with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that maybe appeal to a voter in Georgia, a suburban voter who doesn`t really like Biden, isn`t not about the Democrats, but also really doesn`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And he`s comparable with Kemp, but not sold on Walker.\par \par \uc2 And I think that dynamic could present itself in a number of suburban areas around the country, potentially, that Republicans are counting on. Is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a hindrance right now, his prominence? Unusual for a former president. Is he a hindrance to Republican efforts?\par \par \uc2 LOWRY: Yes. No, no, absolutely, he is. But you know, he`s not the most important issue in the election. And Georgia is going to be very interesting, because that Kemp margin is really going to matter. It seems like he`s likely to beat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , 52, you know, with the big victory. What if it gets up 53, 54, then you can see him, potentially pulling \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  over the top, and over 50 percent avoiding a runoff.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:59]\par \par \uc2 But I don`t think they`re going to be Kemp or Warnock voters. I think there`s going to be a fallout on the Kemp vote, obviously so that if he`d come under oath maybe Kemp, a libertarian candidate. But I don`t think many people are going to be pulling the lever both for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By the way, Governor Kemp has proven himself one of the most impressive Republican politicians in the country.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Rich Lowry, David Plouffe -- thank you both for joining us. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And up next, well, we`re just talking about it. That 50/50 Senate. We`re going to go deep on all the battleground states and we`re going to take a look at each party`s target list. Republicans have a set of seats they`re trying to hard to flip. What do the numbers showing in those states?\par \par \uc2 And Democrats have their own list too. How are they doing in those states? Which party has the clearest path to control of the senate? That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:44]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: We started in Georgia tonight. We talked about Pennsylvania, a few of those other senate battlegrounds in the last five.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a deeper look here at the battle for control of the Senate. Remember it`s 50/50 right now, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  breaks the tie. That`s why Democrats have control. Republicans, their magic number is they need a net gain of one seat. That would deliver Senate control to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So let`s break this into two categories. What you see here, these are the five, probably most vulnerable Democratic seats. Any of these that Republicans win are pick ups for Republicans. And what do you are seeing is the current polling average in each one of those states.\par \par \uc2 You see that there is one, currently one, Democratic held seat in Nevada where Republicans lead the poll average. This is \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , the Republican challenger leading \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , the Democratic senator by an average of 1.7 in the polls. So that, right now on paper, is the most ripe pick up opportunity for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 If they could get that, and then hold all of their seats that they currently have, that would give them control of the Senate. Right now, the biggest problem that Republicans have, we mentioned this last block is Pennsylvania. This is \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , Republican, he`s retiring, but it`s a Republican seat, this is the only Republican seat where Democrats, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , currently lead in the polling average.\par \par \uc2 So right now, Republicans on the polling average, best position to get the gain they`re looking for in Nevada, Democrats best position to offset it in Pennsylvania. That`s what raises the stakes, potentially, in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  were ever able to overcome Warnock there, if it ever came down to a runoff, and potentially that could be decisive. That`s what makes a state like Ohio or North Carolina such a potential wildcard. Folks have been looking at Ohio the entire campaign, wondering when will the polls break in Vance`s direction. This is a state that went comfortably for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  twice. And yet, Vance leads on average by less than one point right now.\par \par \uc2 We will talk about this a little bit later in the show. The polls have been off in states like Ohio, in most recent elections. So is that a factor here as well?\par \par \uc2 But again, the name of the game here for Republicans is to get a net gain of a seat. But for Democrats, every win they could get in this column, if they could get a couple of wins from the Republicans column, almost certainly, they would then continue to control the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And speaking of that one of the most hotly contested races, it is in Wisconsin, Ron Johnson the Republican, you see, this is an unusual position for him to be leading in the polls. He`s come from behind and won twice, he is running for a third charm. His opponent, Democratic lieutenant governor, Mandela Barnes, they had their own debate just last night.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a look at some of the highlights from that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON JOHNSON (R-WI): My opponent has little to no work experience in the private sector, and other than hollow left-wing rhetoric, I`m not sure what he has ever accomplished, or really what he has to offer.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. MANDEL BARNES (D-WI): There`s no commercials talking about Senator Johnson`s records, he has nothing to run on. He hasn`t done a single thing for us.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: The dollar you held to start the Biden administration is now worth only 88.3 cents, it`s crushing everyone.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: We need to give working class families some relief, the middle class tax cut. We need to make the child tax credit permanent. We have to stop giving tax deductions and tax breaks for the wealthiest people.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: -- I understand how high the cost of gas is because I fill up my own gas tank. I drive myself around the city. Unlike Lieutenant Governor Barnes who is chauffeured around by state patrol, costing taxpayers over $600,000 through last November.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: The senator should be so audacious, if he fails to mention that taxpayers had to foot the bill for his private plane trips between D.C. and his $3 million mansion in Florida -- the vacation home.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Let me be very clear, I want to save Social Security. I want to save Medicare. The greatest threat to any government program is the massive, out of control deficit spending, and our growing debt.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: When Senator Johnson talks about making Social Security discretionary spending, that means he is coming for your retirement.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We need lots enforcement, and the problem with the whole defund movement, which he has been a supporter of, it dispirits law enforcement, they`re having a hard time recruiting members, and we aren`t going to have the law enforcement officers we`re going to need to reduce crime.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: No police officers in this country are more dispirited than the ones who were present at the United States Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Please hold off. Please hold off on that.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Senator Johnson has sponsored eight different national abortion bans. He sponsored abortion bans that had no exceptions for rape, incest, or the life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I proposed a one time, single issue referendum to decide at what point does society have the responsibility to protect life.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:53]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Barnes, you go first. What do you find admirable about your opponent?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, not necessarily. I do think, you know, the senator has proven to be a family man, and I think that`s admirable.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I appreciate the fact that Lieutenant Governor Barnes had loving parents -- school teacher, father worked through his shift. He had a good upbringing.\par \par \uc2 I guess what puzzles me about that is with that upbringing, why has he turned against America. I mean why is he finding America awful?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, that was that Wisconsin Senate debate last night. Again, in the polling average Ron Johnson is ahead by a little bit more than two points right now.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of Wisconsin, it`s also the site, every four years, it`s the site of a closely watched governor`s race here. It couldn`t get much closer than this in terms of the polling.\par \par \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent, tied in the polling average with \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , his Republican challenger -- 47.8, 47.8. It seems fitting for Wisconsin, you have a perfect tie, in the polling average. One of several Midwest states here with governors races we are keeping a close eye on nationally.\par \par \uc2 We are also watching Michigan \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent there. She does have a lead over her Republican challenger, Tudor Dixon. That is within ten points at this point. We are just a tick shy of 50 points.\par \par \uc2 And in Michigan last night, Whitmer and Dixon met in a debate. That debate was focused on abortion, gun violence, the state`s handling of COVID-19. Take a look at that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): When Roe fell, Mrs. Dixon celebrated that. She said it didn`t even go far enough. She said she wanted to make abortion a felony, no exceptions for rape, incest, or health of the woman.\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R), MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: My position on abortion is clear. I am pro life, with exceptions for life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 You should understand her position. It`s extremely radical. It`s abortion up to the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Tudor Dixon posted on her social media a picture of her shooting a gun. With the caption that said, "gun control means using both hands".\par \par \uc2 DIXON: \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  has made it pretty clear she wants to make sure she takes away any protection you can have. She wouldn`t allow protection, even inside of the school where we know that is the best-case scenario, if we have someone that can shoot down a shooter.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: While my life was being threatened for making her decisions to save lives across our state, Mrs. Dixon was spreading conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Dixon was downplaying masks, saying that this would go away without getting vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: She knows that her response was so completely horrendous, in fact she closed restaurants down longer than any other state and we lost 3,000 restaurants.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: My opponent is proposing policies that increase guns, decrease oversight, and eliminate gun free zones. That`s not going to make us any safer.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: This is a woman who shut down the state and had to have her powers, her powers, removed by the Supreme Court. Do not trust her with another four years to terrorize you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. That was Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight in Wisconsin, we just talked about it that close race there, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 . They squared off in their first -- and this may be their only debate. Here`s what happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. TONY EVERS (D-WI): To mitigate against inflation, we are going to lower our taxes on the middle class folks in the state of Wisconsin by 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R), WISCONSIN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , weak on inflation, canceling the Keystone Pipeline. What can we do? We can put more money in people`s pockets. I`m a businessman. I understand macroeconomics.\par \par \uc2 EVERS: I am somebody that actually believes that we have some really thoughtful things that we can do around guns. Universal backgrounds checks are one. Red flag laws are another.\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: The left always just wants to take away guns and think that is the problem. I`m responsible gun owner, I will protect your Second Amendment rights.\par \par \uc2 EVERS: The last election was safe, secure, and there was not fraud.\par \par \uc2 MICHELS\uc2 : There is a non partisan legislative commission that did find a illegal voting did happen in the last election.\par \par \uc2 EVERS: Women should have the ability and the right to make decisions about their health care, including reproductive health care, and that includes abortion.\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: I am pro-life, and I make no apologies for that. But I will tell you who the real radical is. The real radical is Governor Evers, where he is for allowing abortion as late as at the time of birth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: And joining us now from Madison, Wisconsin, MSNBC correspondent Shaquille Brewster. He was in the room for that governor`s debate just a few hours ago. And he joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Shaq, thank you for taking time to talk to us. So Wisconsin, it is one of the preeminent battlegrounds every election year, midterm, presidential.\par \par \uc2 Talk if you would about the dynamics that are defining this governor`s race. And I`m curious, just being on the ground out there, are they different at all than the dynamics in the Senate race, where we saw some of the clips from that heated debate last night?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, if you want to compare tonight`s debate to what we saw last night, or what we saw tonight, two dramatically different themes.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:57]\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: It was a much more contentious debate last night. This tonight, the debate tonight, you saw much more polite contrast drawn between the two candidates.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s not to say that there weren`t differences in this debate. We saw \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  come out pretty sharp. He was on message. We saw him attempt to moderate some of those positions that he has been getting attacked for on the airwaves -- on abortion, for example.\par \par \uc2 We know that previously. And he has said, and he stands by, his support for the current ban on abortion in the state of Wisconsin that was written in 1849.\par \par \uc2 But he emphasized tonight that he would support exceptions if the legislature sent him that legislation for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 On the issue of election integrity, while he has been ambiguous before on how he would certify the election or how he would treat the election certification in future elections, he said, unequivocally, tonight, that he would certify the election, but said he would do that because he would sign some of the Republican bills that were passed or that would be passed if he were governor in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 On the other side, you saw Governor \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  trying to stay aggressive, trying to stay on the offense. And painting himself as the guard rail, the protector from some of what he calls the extreme policies of the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 So one thing that you notice is that you still have those abortion and economic issues top of mind. But they play a little bit differently here in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right, Shaq Brewster in Madison. Thank you so much for joining us tonight. Appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 And coming up we are going to take a deep dive into the polling. We are showing you the numbers -- there`ve been some big misses. We`re going to look at what the biggest miss was in the 2020 election and what it could mean for 2022. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:46:38]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: As our countdown continues, that first poll closing on election night is starting to feel close. We are going to take a look now. We`ve been showing you during the show some of the polling from some of the key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 And of course, anybody who lived through the 2020, the 2016 elections can tell you the polls have not always been getting it right recently.\par \par \uc2 One thing to keep in mind here I think that`s important is the polling misses that we saw in 2016, that we saw in 2020. For that matter we saw some in the 2018 midterms as well.\par \par \uc2 They haven`t really been evenly distributed. They haven`t been -- every state missing the same way. There`s been one particular region in the country where the misses, the gap between where the polling is and where the election results end up as the most pronounced -- that`s the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 And so with that in mind, there are two states in particular that are crucial to that battle for Senate control this year where there were some significant polling. This was both in 2016, and in 2020. They are Ohio and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you this. This is in 2020. This is what the final poll average looked like in each one of those states. Remember Ohio has got that big senate race this year. J.D. Vance, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . Wisconsin has got the senate race, we were just talking about Mandela Barnes, Ron Johnson.\par \par \uc2 So the final numbers in 2020, the average, Trump was leading Ohio by one point. We were talking about could Biden win Ohio. And Biden seemed to be in control in Wisconsin, 6.7 was his average lead. There was a poll that had him up 17 points, close to the election, one poll was out there.\par \par \uc2 Here is what actually happened in those states. Biden did win Wisconsin but by a whisker. There was a polling miss there six points on average. And Trump did win Ohio, but he won it easily, over eight points, a polling miss there of more than seven points.\par \par \uc2 So that is the question a lot of people have been asking here. Especially looking at these Senate races in Ohio and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the polling, has the polling been missing something in these Midwest states. Have the pollsters figure out what it is? What is going on in those Midwestern states in particular?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the perfect person to ask. He is Charles Franklin. He`s a professor of law and public policy at \uc2 Marquette University\uc2 . He is director of the best poll in Wisconsin. Marquette Law School poll they were just out with a new poll this week.\par \par \uc2 And Charles, thank you for joining us. And I shouldn`t say, as we talk about the misses in 2020, you actually had I think one of the better polls there in 2020, in terms of coming close to the result here.\par \par \uc2 But I think you know what I`m talking about when some of the other polls that were taken, there was a real dramatic example. But really missed Wisconsin badly. And in states like in that area -- Iowa, Ohio, Wisconsin. Do we know why the misses have been so pronounced in that region?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES FRANKLYN, MARQUETTE UNIVERSITY: Well, everybody in polling has been working hard to address that. And we`ve done what we identified. But the fear is that Trump supporters have been less willing to respond to polls. And so we`ve adjusted things to try to capture those Trump voters.\par \par \uc2 But even so, we`ve underestimated the Trump vote. Even as we`ve gotten the Democratic vote pretty well. Now, we were badly wrong. Everybody was badly wrong in 2016. We improved a bit in 2020. And there was, as you said, a lot of variation in the errors.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things is, blue-collar workers. Folks without a college degree in the Midwest make up a particularly large share. Well over 60 percent of registered voters in Wisconsin don`t have a college degree and when we look at the changes that have taken place in the last few years, white men without a college degree here in Wisconsin have gone from being four points more Republican than Democrat to 25 points more Republican than Democrat.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:00]\par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN: So there`s been a big movement in the partisanship of the exact segment of the electorate that is fairly -- that`s harder than average to reach.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes. So I`m curious as a pollster, how do you look at -- you came out -- as I said, this week with a poll in the Senate race with Ron Johnson ahead. And I was looking back, it`s one of the only polls I`ve seen in a couple of cycles in these three campaigns where Johnson has actually been ahead.\par \par \uc2 You know, I talk to people in Ohio who say, look, the polling is real close there but you know how Ohio goes. Vance will end up winning by six or seven. When you get a poll results that shows you Johnson already ahead like that, do you think maybe you`re missing some voters when he`s ahead by more than you think you`ve done a better job of getting those Trump voters? How do you read that?\par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN: Yes. Well, you worry about that, mostly. But the real thing is, with Johnson, in 2016, we showed him behind early in the year. But we showed him steadily closing the gap until it was just a pure toss-up in the last poll before the election.\par \par \uc2 So there are campaign affects. This year we saw Johnson behind in August. But then tied in September and now ahead. There have been other polls at four points and five points Johnson leads. But there`ve also been a couple of ties right now. People should look at the range of results.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right. Yes, that movement does sound like it`s important.\par \par \uc2 Charles Franklin, Marquette Law poll, thanks. Really appreciate you taking a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 All right. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:28]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: We`ll pick our countdown up next Friday right here at 10:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And right now, "THE 11TH HOUR" begins.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221015eiae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for October 14, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez \par \uc2 7,436 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:40]\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: We`ll take our countdown up next Friday right here at 10 p.m. Eastern and right now, THE 11TH HOUR begins.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, more incredible and never before seen video from January 6, as the committee hints at a cover up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the dramatic debate tonight in Georgia, Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  go head-to-head just days before Georgia and start to cast their votes.\par \par \uc2 Then, a critical week for our democracy and what leadership looks like in the face of crisis. Jon Meacham is here as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, I`m Alicia Menendez in for Stephanie Ruhle. We are still waiting for a definitive answer on what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  intends to do about that subpoena he`s getting from the January 6 Committee. He did release a rambling 14-page letter today, where he repeated his election lies. But gave no indication of whether he`ll actually testify under oath before the Committee.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, NBC News has obtained more never before seen video of congressional leaders on January 6. The video shot by House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s daughter Alexandra, an accomplished filmmaker. One part shows Pelosi on the phone with then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D) HOUSE SPEAKER: (Inaudible) was saying we`ve got to make plans and this takes organization, why don`t you just put people in vehicles logistically to get there. There is leadership of the National Guard at the Capitol with the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  leadership, but they have not been given authority to be activated. So that, you know, in other words that leadership can be taking place simultaneous to people coming in trucks, to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, I just spoke with Vice President Pence and he was going to see, because Mitch wants to do it in the Capitol. But we`re being told it could take days to clean up and make sure there are no hidden, you know, bomb.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The riveting new video also shows Pelosi discussing the logistics of returning to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I`m very concerned about that giving any long-term notice to anybody that we`re going back. They could engage in their First Amendment rights for being really disruptive, even outside the Capitol. And they can still look like we`re not really in a good place.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Is can be over fast, or this can go well into the night. And I`m sure, the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  don`t do that on top of all the trauma today, but we may have to have some ancillary, other security there to get us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Now that the committee has revealed so much what went into January 6, a big continuing unanswered question, still whether there will be any accountability for those behind the attack. Earlier today, Committee Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  was asked whether the former president broke the law, and if he should be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING JAN. 6TH COMMITTEE VICE CHAIR: The Committee has the responsibility to make decisions about criminal referrals. And I, of course, have my own views about that. I don`t want to get ahead of the committee`s discussions on it. I would point people to Judge Carter`s opinion in which he said that it`s more likely than not that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and John Eastman violated at least two federal criminal statutes. And so, I again, I want to -- I don`t want to get ahead of the committee, but I think I think you will see the committee`s work in this regard done in a unanimous way. And I think it`s -- I think there`s no question about the answer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: As you well know, the 1/6 committee`s work is one of many investigations into the former president.\par \par \uc2 We are also following the ongoing investigation into those classified documents recovered at Mar-a-Lago. According to The Wall Street Journal, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is seeking additional information from two Trump aides about those documents. Investigators reportedly spoke to the aides, months before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched Mar-a-Lago. And sources tell the paper they have been trying to talk to them again.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:10]\par \par \uc2 And today, there was yet another development and a legal battle over that special master`s review of the documents. The Justice Department has now filed an appeal with the 11th Circuit court, arguing the review should end as Judge Cannon was wrong to order it in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And with that, let`s bring in our leadoff panel tonight, Philip Rucker, Pulitzer Prize Winning Deputy National Editor at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , David Jolly, a former Republican and a former member of the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 . He`s now Chairman of the Serve America Movement, and Tali Farhadian Weinstein, a former federal and state prosecutor in New York, she clerked for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Sandra Day O`Connor\uc2 , all three are MSNBC contributors.\par \par \uc2 Philip, this afternoon, our colleague Nicolle Wallace asked \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  about the potential for a cover up from the Secret Service. I want you to take a listen to what the Congressman said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D) CALIFORNIA JAN. 6th COMMITTEE MEMBER: Let me just say I have a lot of questions about all of it, about coordination among witnesses, about the advice that they may have been receiving from some attorneys, about their veracity. You know, I just think we -- there`s more questions than answers at this point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Phil, always more questions than answers. What are you hearing tonight?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, THE WASHINGTON POST DEPUTY NATIONAL EDITOR: Well, I think she`s right, that there are more questions than answers. And as you say, that always has been true. But especially in this case, remember, the committee was seeking those text messages from January 6 among Secret Service agents. And those were deleted, because of a sort of technical bureaucratic step according to the Secret Service that was going on inside the government. But it means that a lot of the communications the record of communications that day, doesn`t exist exists.\par \par \uc2 The committee had new evidence, including some emails documenting concerns in the Secret Service, warnings by the Secret Service to the White House about dangers that could occur on January 6, but that is not the full documentation. And we`ve yet to hear in any sort of public testimony, let alone a legal proceeding. Officials in the Secret Service account for what was happening that day. And I think importantly, account for any sort of political motivations or sympathies, that there might have been among the former president`s detail for what might have been happening in and around the White House on January 6.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tali, there are political implications, there are safety implications, talk me through the legal implications of a potential cover up?\par \par \uc2 TALI FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN, FORMER NY FEDERAL AND STATE PROSECUTOR: Oh, well, you know, there are substantive crimes, Alicia. And then there are cover ups or what we`ve called obstruction. And that`s really running through so many of the potential criminal charges that we`ve been talking about whether in the context of January 6, all the way over to the Mar-a-Lago investigation. Both the original crimes and then the people who enable them or tried to thwart law enforcement, from figuring out what was going on. I think the implications are huge.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: David, you read, I`m sure, the former president`s rambling 14- page letter, I am sure you have heard his statements where he says that he would, you know, love to have a chance to say everything so long as it was live and in public. Do you think though there is actually any chance Trump complies with the subpoena? And what happens, David, if he doesn`t comply?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, SERVE AMERICA MOVEMENT CHAIRMAN: So, look at every lawyer in the world would say there`s no way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should do this. And I don`t think he responds to the subpoena at all. But we also know he has a pension for the stage. And so perhaps, Alicia, he tries to make a moment, I really doubt that`s the case.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I think ultimately happens, though, (technical difficulty) and the interesting thing is, the subpoena will have to go from the committee to the full House, and ultimately, does it get referred over to DOJ if he doesn`t comply. But what happens come January 6, maybe when the new Congress is sworn in, is depending on who controls that Congress, the subpoena could be withdrawn, and the enforcement action could be withdrawn. And ultimately, if I were -- if I was playing Vegas here, I would suggest that if Republicans win the House on November 8, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs out the clock, and the Republican House withdraws the subpoena. But that is also an indictment then of the incoming leadership of Kevin McCarthy, Steve Scalise and others who say, even though the former president has something to say, we don`t want to hear it.\par \par \uc2 It isn`t it -- look, the indictment of the entire January 6 committee is laid at the feet of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This subpoena whether it`s withdrawn or not, will remain at the feet of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Phil, your sense of how likely a criminal referral is at this point?\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: Well, I think you just have to take Congresswoman Cheney at her word, the Vice Chair of that committee. She has indicated very strongly that there -- that that`s the likely sort of end step here by this committee. She hasn`t, of course outright said so. But if you read the tea leaves, read between the lines of her public statements over the last several weeks, it certainly seems like momentum is building in that direction on the committee for a criminal referral. And remember that the committee has laid out what some committee members have argued to have been violations of the criminal code violations of statutes rather, by the former president and by others. And so, I do expect that there could be a referral. I just don`t know exactly what form that takes or when or what the sort of vote on this committee could look like.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So, Tali, that is Phil`s take, as a journalist. I want to hear your take as a prosecutor, is a criminal referral, likely? And can you give us a sense of what a prosecution would look like in a case like this?\par \par \uc2 FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN: Well, my take as a prosecutor, Alicia, is that it doesn`t really matter whether or not the committee makes a criminal referral, as a -- just a realistic matter, this entire proceeding. All of these hearings have been a criminal referral, or they have collected lots of information that points toward criminal prosecution, and I think has made a lot of progress on some of the harder aspects of prosecution. We were so many miles away from where we were around January 6, and when this committee started to get going, when we used to hear people say, well, maybe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually thought that he won that election. And he`s so disconnected from reality, that maybe this was not a fraud, because he believed what he was saying. And I think it`s really impossible to hold on to that idea to believe that after yesterday.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, whether or not the committee makes a referral, it just doesn`t matter in terms of prosecutors picking up this evidence, taking the ball from here and then starting to think about how they might put together a series of cases.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Philip, the committee played testimony from several high-ranking officials on Trump`s apparent order to withdraw from Afghanistan following his loss. Here`s what General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  said. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , U.S. JOINIT CHIEF CHAIRMAN: It is odd, it is not standard. It is potentially dangerous. I personally thought it was momentarily not feasible, nor was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And Philip, you covered Trump for years, did what you heard from the committee yesterday, surprise you?\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: You know, it didn`t surprise me. But it was interesting and revelatory, because it added more texture and nuance to something that we knew was going on based on reporting at the time. We knew Trump wanted to get troops out of Afghanistan. It`s something he had been pushing within his cabinet and national security team for some time. And we knew in those final months of the presidency, that he was acting erratically that he was proposing what military leaders viewed to be erratic proposals, including in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 But to hear Milley, you know, on the record, effectively with the committee, spelling all of this out in plain English, and explaining the dangers, what he viewed as the leader of the military, what he viewed to be the dangers of that withdrawal, you know, done on such short notice by the outgoing president. That was, you know, revelatory, I think for the American people.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So, Tali, these are just some of the developments from the 1/6 committee. I also want to ask you about the Mar-a-Lago investigation, the Justice Department is trying to stop the special master review. Can you talk us through their argument?\par \par \uc2 FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN: Yes. So, they really sharpened and they`re filing today, the argument that they`ve been making all along, which is, this is completely out of order. It just made no sense to them. And I think that they`re right about this, that Judge Cannon took jurisdiction over this criminal case, rather than having \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  make the arguments he wanted to make in front of the magistrate who was supervising that search warrant, and then continuing up that chain.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think the upshot of all this is that Trump probably delayed matters here by about three months. I mean, this entire thing is a distraction. It looks like it`s going to be resolved by maybe mid-November. Those are three really important months for him. But I think it`s hard to believe that he is going to prevail in the 11th Circuit. And I think that order will eventually be restored all of this will go back to the original magistrate. And the government will continue to put together its case as it has been.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:11]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: David, all of this, the work of the 1/6 committee, the Mar-a-Lago investigation, it is all in the ether as we are 25 days out for midterms. You have written a column saying a Democratic victory would be a sea change in politics.\par \par \uc2 I think we have heard a lot about the stakes as it relates to how fragile things feel in this moment. But when you say, a sea change, what do you mean?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yeah. What I mean is this, history suggests that this should be a layup for Republicans. The global economy has wrecked inflation on the domestic economy. Russia is flirting with nuclear war. We have a president`s numbers who are underwater. And history suggests that his first midterm would be a boom for Republicans. But we`re not seeing that. And the question is this, in a post Dobbs world in which the Republican Party itself has followed Trumpism now for six years to see Democrats have a sweeping, enormous election in 2018, to win the presidency and reject Trumpism in 2020, it would be routine for Republicans to win the House and maybe the Senate and 2022. But for Democrats to hold the house in the Senate might be the early indication that the entire American landscape has changed politically. And is beginning to say to the current trajectory of today`s Republican Party, you are not a majority party in the United States any longer. Look that the Democrats have to thread together a number of wins to get to that moment, but Alicia, Democrats win on November 8. I think it`s more than just playing the horse race game. This would suggest that the American people are moving past today`s Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I mean, Tali, part of what`s so interesting there is that we have this conversation, we come back to over and over again, about accountability. I think there are those who argue that that accountability will ultimately be electoral. I wonder from your vantage point, what is it going to take to see some accountability for the planners of January 6, are there going to be repercussions?\par \par \uc2 FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN: Well, I hope that there will be accountability in various processes. So that includes in the political process, it includes in federal courts, and it includes in state courts. Remember that of all of the various investigations that we`ve talked about over the last six months, the case in Georgia for interference with the election, I think, to me appears to be the strongest in the DA there\par \par \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has been open that she is hoping to bring charges by a self- imposed deadline of the end of this year. So that`s one. The Mar-a-Lago investigation, as we`ve talked about is proceeding and there was some reporting today that the government may be well on its way to developing search warrants probable cause to look at other premises where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have stored other documents that he was not supposed to have. And so, there is movement on various fronts. And all of them are happening in parallel. I don`t think we`re going to have to choose among them.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Multiple paths to accountability, time though, is of the essence. Philip Rucker, David Jolly, Tali Farhadian Weinstein, thank you all so much for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the key moments from tonight`s Georgia Senate debate between \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  with just 25 days until the midterms, did it move the needle?\par \par \uc2 And later Jon Meacham is here on how history will view this week`s January 6 hearing, the clear choice he says Republicans have to make about the future of the country. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:36]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Just three days before early voting begins in Georgia, Senate candidates \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  faced off tonight in a highly anticipated debate. Here is just some of what happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Did President Biden defeat former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , (R) GEORGIA U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: President Biden won and Senator Warnock won, that`s the reason I decided to run.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The former girlfriend made public accusation saying you paid for an abortion and that you encouraged her to have another?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I say that`s a lie. And on abortion, you know, I`m a Christian. I believe in life. And I tell people this, Georgia is a state that respects life. And I`ll be a senator that protects life. And I say that was a lie and I`m not biting down.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 , (D) GEORGIA: The women of Georgia deserves a Senator who will stand with them. I trust women more than I trust politician. One thing I have not done, I`ve never pretended to be a police officer. And I`ve never -- I`ve never threatened a shootout with the police.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I am with many police officers and at the same time --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Walker -- Mr. Walker.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Walker, you are very well aware of the rules tonight.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you have a prop. That is not allowed, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Recent polling shows the incumbent Democratic senator holds a seven-point lead over his Republican challenger.\par \par \uc2 With us for more, Greg Bluestein, a Political Reporter for the Atlanta Journal Constitution and an MSNBC Political Contributor. He`s also the author of, "Flipped: How Georgia Turned Purple and Broke the Monopoly on Republican Power." And Julian Castro, former HUD Secretary and 2020 Democratic Candidate for president.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:14]\par \par \uc2 It`s good to see you both. All right, Greg, your big takeaways from this debate?\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, POLITICAL REPORTER, ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION: Well, this was a make-or-break moment for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . He didn`t break. He had some rough moments. He didn`t break. And to some supporters, he emerged even stronger. But at the same time, Senator Warnock`s campaign scored some key points as you just saw some zingers that he can highlight to his supporters to raise some money and to energize his backers.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tell me what you think those zingers were that landed?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: While the one that we just saw where he highlighted two of Herschel Walker`s biggest vulnerabilities in one fell swoop. One, Herschel Walker`s lie that he`s served in law enforcement. And secondly, his history of violence against women, including threatening a shootout with police, which undercuts Herschel Walker`s support for law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yeah, Julian, I am curious, watching this race generally, watching this debate tonight, how much of it is going to come down to national politics to the way that voters are feeling about the economy, to the way that they are feeling as a response to the Dobbs decision? How much of it is going to come down to these candidates themselves? The things we have learned about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in the last few weeks, and how much of it is going to come down to dynamics in the state of Georgia? Your sense, as you watch this unfold about what`s really going to be on voters top of mind as they begin to vote this Monday?\par \par \uc2 JULIAN CASTRO, FORMER HUD SECRETARY: I mean, whether we`re talking about Georgia or Arizona and Nevada, a number of other states, a lot of it has to do with national politics, how well President Biden is doing, how well people feel the economy is doing in a number of other issues. So yes, what we`ve seen over the last few years is that these elections are more and more nationalized.\par \par \uc2 It`s also true that especially in a state like Georgia, it`s going to be a very close race. And when you have a very close race, that means that all of the other things that you mentioned, Alicia, come into play, including, of course, candidate quality. And unfortunately for the GOP, they just have a terrible candidate here, going into this debate, you know, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  took the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  playbook of trying to set the expectations so low, I mean, he said, I`m like, you know, underneath Dante`s Ninth Circle of Hell or something. And he barely, I think, came out of their meeting that expectation. He came close to breaking to use Greg`s terminology when he took a fake badge out to try and, you know, I guess reinforce the point that he had worked with police.\par \par \uc2 He really, you know, he didn`t have a huge mistake, but I don`t think he did himself any favor. And the polls have consistently shown over the last few weeks that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  has built a small but steady lead. I think that nothing that happened tonight is going to change that.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: You know, Greg, something I found interesting, Yamiche Alcindor, our colleague was in Georgia talking to voters a few days after some of these stories had begun to emerge about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and people who were Walker supporters, they really seem to be pretty unmoved by these revelations. And I thought it was interesting tonight that Warnock really didn`t touch the personal accusation, stayed pretty far from them. And I wonder what you made of that dynamic?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yeah. And that was no surprise, because on the campaign trail, Senator Warnock is hardly talked about it. He`s only really talked about those abortion allegations when pressed by reporters. And when he is, he says that they`re disturbing. They`re part of a disturbing pattern, but he -- and he highlights his stance supporting abortion rights. And then he kind of shifts the subject. And that`s part of his strategy. He`s worried about energizing Republicans by intervening in this scandal, and giving Republicans a way to unify around a shared enemy in him. And so, he`s been steering clear, but what he hasn`t steered clear of at all, is those allegations of violence, the threats that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has leveled against his ex-wife, Cindy Grossman and other women in his past that came up prominently in tonight`s debate.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Julian, you know, it`s funny when we talk about states that have big Latino populations. We often talk about Florida, we talked about Texas, Arizona and Nevada. Georgia is a state that has an emerging Latino electorate. I want to look at some new polling. This is national polling on Hispanic voters, support for Democrats in Congress at 63%. That`s a big number. But it is less than in previous years, kind of mirrors what we`ve been seeing from polling, which is that democratic numbers are softer than Dems would want them to be but Republicans have not been able, Julian, to seal the deal.\par \par \uc2 CASTRO: Well, they haven`t been. And as you mentioned, you know, a great poll, but the caveat here that it did not delve into individual states, right? So, there is a growing sizable Latino population in Georgia. He`s also been organized at least on the Democratic side and it was key to helping to put Ossoff and Warnock over the top and those run offs.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:10]\par \par \uc2 And so, the poll is instructive. I think, though that Latinos are thinking about the economy. They`re thinking about the issue of abortion. One of the more surprising findings in the poll was it, Latinos had, by 28 points more confidence in Democrats on the issue of abortion that flies in the face of the conventional wisdom of what most people think about the Latino community. All of those issues are going to be relevant hear that in the debate tonight, for instance, I mean, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  made the contrast, very clear. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  doesn`t support any exceptions to a total ban on abortion. And he`s going to protect the right of women to choose.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And this contrasts, do you trust politicians, or do you trust women to make these decisions?\par \par \uc2 Greg, before we go, I got about a minute left, give us an update on the Fulton County investigation. Where does it stand?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Well, it is sort of not really on a pause, but you`re not going to see too much news until after the election. The subpoenas are still being filed. There`s still activity behind closed doors, but there`s not going to be a huge major development. Part of a pre-existing agreement from Fulton County prosecutors not to make big news before the midterm.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Greg Bluestein, Julian Castro, as always, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Jon Meacham is here to discuss this week`s January 6 Hearing and how future generations will remember leadership under pressure. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:36:14]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2 , (D) MISSISSIPPI JAN.6 SELECT COMMITTEE CHAIR: This is a question about accountability to the American people. He must be accountable.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: The Committee directs the Chairman to issue a subpoena for relevant documents and testimony under oath from \uc2 Donald John Trump\uc2 . Aye.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D) CALIFORNIA JAN. 6 SELECT COMMITTEE: Aye.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D) CALIFORNIA JAN. 6 SELECT COMMITTEE: Aye.\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR, (D) CALIFORNIA JAN. 6 SELECT COMMITTEE: Aye.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2 , (D) JANUARY 6 SELECT COMMITTEE: Aye.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Aye.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Aye.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: After an eventful week like this one that ended with a subpoena for a former president, we need to hear from someone like presidential historian Jon Meacham. He sometimes advises President Biden and is the author of, "And There Was Light: \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  and The American Struggle." It is out Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Jon Meacham, thanks for staying up late to be with us. You know, it strikes me that so much of your work deals with presidential leadership. But we`ve seen a lot of examples this week of another type of leadership. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: There has to be some way we can maintain the sense that people have, that there`s some security or some confidence that government can function and that we can elect the president of the United States. Do we go back into session?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We can go back into session. But now apparently everybody on the floor is putting on their desk masks to prepare for breach. Well, I`m trying to get more information.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: They`re putting on their?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Tear gas masks?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ:\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jon, her big question in that moment of crisis, how do we signal to the American people that they can have faith that our government will function? How will history remember that moment?\par \par \uc2 JON MEACHAM, PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: Well, I`m a great admirer of Speaker Pelosi`s and so you should hear this with this context. She`s a friend. And I can tell you that she speaks in private when there are no cameras in exactly the same way. There`s a patriotism. There`s a persistent sense of civic duty there. I think she`ll go down is arguably the greatest Speaker of the House in American history, or certainly in the top one or two.\par \par \uc2 What you see there is what scholars sometimes call moral leadership or spiritual leadership. It`s not unlike what Winston Churchill did in wartime Britain. It`s a remarkable kind of steadiness. I`m reminded of when President Reagan was shot in March of 1981, President -- Vice President Bush came back and brought a kind of order to the chaos that was unfolding in that very young White House. And I think you saw that with Speaker Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 You also, we heard it, though we didn`t see it. We saw it with Vice President Pence. So, this is not a partisan point. Pence seemed strong. He was facing enormous, indeed, existential threat in the literal sense of existential. And I think that`s what we want in our leaders is we want people who believe in the Constitution. They believe that in a document that may not be perfect, but it is in fact about a journey toward a more perfect union.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: You know, also just remarkable that Speaker Pelosi`s daughter, Alexandra, who is an accomplished filmmaker was with her that day, was taking that footage, the fact that we were able to see it. You know, I have to ask you subpoenas have happened to other presidents. But can you just help us put this request into context, John?\par \par \uc2 MEACHAM: Well, presidents are not above the law. And let`s also be clear here, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not President. So, he is a former president. So, let`s sort of step back and remember that.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:07]\par \par \uc2 We`re approaching actually the anniversary of when maybe it`s already happened this week, when Gerald Ford voluntarily went to the House subcommittee of the House Judiciary Committee to testify about the Nixon pardon. George Washington went voluntarily. Again, very early on, obviously.\par \par \uc2 And it`s very clear, I think, both common sense and constitutionally, that a president is not above being held accountable for his or her actions. And if we saw anything, over the last five years, we have seen a new that the law has to be more important than any single person or party. You can`t be for the law when it hurts the other guy, but be against it, when it might be deleterious to your interest. That`s not the way the rule of law works.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: In a piece for a Time Magazine, you wrote, an American president must be committed to something larger than his own hold on power. And the American people must be willing to accept the give and take of the constitutional order, even when, especially when events and moral claims, calling us to give, rather than to take, tell me more?\par \par \uc2 MEACHAM: It`s the fundamental question of, this is going to sound pretty grand for a Friday night. It`s a fundamental question of human affairs, and certainly of representative democracy. If I don`t see you, as someone of equal dignity, and right, then I can take something from you and not feel guilty about it and feel as though I might do so with impunity. And we have to see each other as neighbors, not just because it`s the right thing to do, though, that has the virtue of -- it has the virtue of being the right thing to do. But it`s the intelligent and reasonable and self-interested thing to do. Because if I defend your rights, you`re a hell of a lot more likely to defend mine. If I lend you a hand in the morning, you`re more likely to lend me a hand when I won in the afternoon. That`s the way democracies work. And if you believe that every political question, that every cultural conflict, that every difference of opinion, is a cause for total war, then democracy can`t, as Lincoln would say, will not long endure. Because it means we`ll be at each other`s throats every minute of every day.\par \par \uc2 And sometimes, democracy requires us to cast a vote, or offer a kind of forbearance to people with whom we fundamentally disagree about specifics, but with whom we agree on the larger protection of the project. And to pull this back to Speaker Pelosi, you don`t have to agree with everything that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  believes politically and philosophically, but I defy anyone to watch that footage and not say that is someone who -- without whom we were very fortunate that she was -- she was where she was when that crisis came.\par \par \uc2 Same with Vice President Pence. And there are people on in American politics and American life, who would want to be for Pelosi and not be for Pence. But in this case, they were doing the right thing. And therefore, I think we salute them. And there may be some liberals who whose heads exploded at that thought about Vice President Pence. But he adhered to the rule of law. He adhered to constitutional custom. And God help us if he hadn`t.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jon Meacham, I look forward to the week where nothing of consequence happens that you and I can sit here and talk about whatever you like Jon Meacham, as always, thank you. His new book, "And There Was Light: \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  and the American Struggle." It is out Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, when you look at the uncertainty tens of thousands of foster care children face every year, when they age out of the system. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:49:13]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: For many young adults turning 21 is a celebration. But for those emerging from the foster care system, it can be yet another hurdle to overcome. NBC`s Antonia Hylton has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA ABRAHAM (ph): Everybody looks forward to the 21st birthday. But in my case, I`m old enough to not have anywhere to live.\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC CORRESPONDENT: Christina Abraham (ph) is a child of the foster care system. Born in Harlem, she`s lived in New York City her entire life. Her 21st Birthday lose like a day of reckoning.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): To feel like, you know, I could die tomorrow and nobody will know. Nobody cares about me. And that is a terrible feeling. I feel like you`re in a world full of billions of people and your life does not matter to anybody.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Christina doesn`t know if a group home will let her stay past her 21st birthday. When in New York like in most states foster youth are supposed to leave the system. She hasn`t found an apartment, she can afford.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:04]\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): I`m looking at nothing really even though I want everything I`m looking at nothing.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Christina wants to return to Harlem, steeped in rich black history, despite the pain she endured here.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): This is where it all started.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: It`s where she entered foster care after an abusive childhood. Christina became a permanent ward of the state at eight. She remembers her mom leaving her alone here. Her mother says official separated her from Christina and she was not allowed to say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): You`re just a little person, did you understand what was happening?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): Unfortunate unfortunately, I did because this had been the second time now.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Christina spends almost all her free time looking for a landlord who will accept a city rental voucher, 21 comes and goes without a party. Nearly two months later, good news.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: She might be able to move into a studio in Harlem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And this is the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Then Christina says, the city foster agency failed to submit her housing paperwork in time. She`s still living in the group home.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: What`s it like to still be there?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): Disappointing.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Public areas in the home are under constant surveillance, she hangs a curtain in front of her bedroom door.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): If you lift it up, there`s a window. And so, anybody who walks by can look inside of your room at any time.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Overseeing the city`s foster care youth is Jess Dannhauser, Commissioner of the Administration for Children`s Services. He can`t comment on specific cases, but did address broader criticisms.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): We`ve seen some of the conditions of the group homes and tad descriptions of violence of a lack of privacy, is that safe and secure?\par \par \uc2 JESS DANNHAUSER, COMMISSIONER OF THE ADMINISTRATION FOR CHILDREN`S SERVICES: We`re absolutely would not accept that being a place where we want young people to be in a place that`s not safe or secure. My whole goal is commissioner is to make this system more humane. So, we`re building out this extra support through coaches all the way into 26 years old.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Two and a half months after turning 21, Christina finally leaves the group home for good. She now has the keys to the Harlem apartment, but only $150 to your name.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Are you missing meals?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): Yes. Yes, I am. I`m am -- most time, not having breakfast or lunch. But I will have dinner.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: To help get her on her feet, a charity called hearts to homes gives Christina supplies. Linens, dish soap, chair. But she has no credit card, no credit score, she has no bed, and her small savings needs to last for almost three weeks until she gets a check from her new job.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): Sometimes I`m excited to start my job so I wake up with a smile on my face. I`ll be it off the floor. But still, other days I`m tired.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: She`s also a survivor and determined, 99 days after she turned 21, Christina invites us to see the very first home, she could call her own.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Hi?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): Hey.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: A new bed and food in the fridge feel like a triumph.\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): And we have my favorite.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: When you look out at this version of New York, in your personal space, what`s the message you receive?\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM (ph): Harlem.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Born again in the same neighborhood, this time with the chance to chart her own path. Antonia Hylton, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Coming up, there is a brand-new bilingual children`s book hitting bookshelves right now. And you might be surprised who wrote it. We`re going to talk to one of the celebrity co-authors, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY FALLON, "THE TONIGHT SHOW STARRING JIMMY FALLON" HOST: When you have a book, I mean, for kids you do it, I did, starting as a joke when I was just, you know, I did a book called, "Your Baby`s First Word Will Be DADA" and it was funny and, you know, but then when you see a kid reading your book back to you, get ready. I mean it is an awesome feeling. It is an emotional, it is so cool.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The last thing before we go tonight, "Con Pollo," in addition to his hosting gig on The Tonight Show Jimmy Fallon has written six children`s books. His latest is a bilingual book called, "Con Pollo," and it is co- written by the multi-talented Jennifer Lopez. I sat down with Jimmy and the book`s Illustrator, Andrea Campos, to talk about the magic of writing for children. Here`s a part of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: This is your sixth children`s book?\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Yes, it`s that while.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yeah. But the first that is bilingual, why was that important to you?\par \par \uc2 FALLON: I wish I knew language earlier in my life or, you know, had the opportunity to learn another language because I did dedicate this book to my Spanish teachers, which is Ms. Shermer (ph), Ms. Brzezinski (ph) and Mrs. Hoolahan (ph).\par \par \uc2 ANDREA CAMPOS, ILLUSTRATOR: Hoolahan (ph)?\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Mrs. Hoolahan (ph) taught me Spanish, the greatest Spanish teacher in upstate New York. I just wish I started earlier just so kids can see like, oh, you can and it`s cool, and it`s fun. And, you know, and just grow that. And you`re like, oh, I can do that. And then I`ll learn a bunch of languages.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS: When I was a kid I kind of grew up at a time where like speaking Spanish wasn`t cool. I just remember like at school I was explicitly told like don`t, you leave that at home. You know, you show up as a different version of yourself here. So now knowing that I`ve gotten to work on a project that is, you know, promoting bilingualism is teaching kids Spanish it`s really special.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It is really special. You can catch the rest of my discussion with Jimmy and Andrea on my show, American Voices this Sunday 6 p.m. Eastern right here. are on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:01]\par \par \uc2 On that note, I wish you a good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thank you for staying up late and we will see you on Monday.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221015eiae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 14, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,250 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: She`s president and grandma all in one. Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  won the week, baby. Susan Del Percio, Dean Obeidallah, thank you. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Will you ask the president to make a statement? Answer my question before someone gets hurt.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The scramble to save democracy on January 6.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: We`re being told it would take two to three days to clear the Capitol. Yes, Mitch is here.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: We will not get the Capitol cleared out for two-three days. We should do it here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , one of the leaders in that room, joins me live.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I want him to come. I want to punch him now. It`s all I have -- I`ve waiting for this. They`re trespassing on the Capitol grounds. I want to punch him now and I`m going to go to jail and I`m going to be happy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  on the Trump response to his committee`s subpoena. Plus --\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Response to the wild charges of Lieutenant Governor Barnes. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  set me up.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes on what happened in Wisconsin last night. And what we learned from the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  debate with Senator Raphael Warnock in Georgia tonight.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): We will see time and time again tonight, as we`ve already seen, that my opponent has a problem with the truth.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. I got to say, it is amazing that in the year and a half since January 6, something that we`ve covered almost every night in the show, we have continued to learn more and more about the coup almost every week. I mean, honestly, almost every week, we`ve gotten some new piece of information, sometimes a lot of new information about what happened in the lead-up to and on that day.\par \par \uc2 And almost without exception, every single new fact, every single new revelation has made it look worse, more sinister, more dangers, closer to the end of (AUDIO GAP) as all the information we have now about January 6 also confirms the basic dividing lines of that day. Every political leader from both sides of the aisle stood together against the violent coup and on the side of the constitutional order. Even those members of Congress who vote for the -- voted for the coup just few hours later. Everyone in the White House was opposed to the violent coup as well. The only exceptions were \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the mob, and the organizers of the mob. And now we have even more new evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the committee played some of this incredible, gripping, never- before-seen footage showing congressional leaders coordinating the response to the attack on the Capitol. The video was shot by Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s daughter Alexandra, an accomplished documentary filmmaker working with \uc2 HBO\uc2 . And as NBC has obtained much more of that video from the afternoon the sixth, after the speaker, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and the rest of leadership were evacuated to a nearby Pentagon installation Fort McNair.\par \par \uc2 We now have a very clear and harrowing record of how Democratic and Republican congressional leaders fought to protect the Capitol and do their constitutional duty despite the best efforts of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies. One of the members of congressional leadership that was in that behind-the-scenes footage during the siege on the Capitol was Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  of South Carolina. He`s the House Majority Whip, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, we`ve seen the images of you in the room with the speaker, with Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . I just wanted to get your sense of what it was like in that room. It`s such -- it`s such a strange and surreal setting. There`s such high degrees of adrenaline. You`re watching people think out loud and process in real-time. What was it like to be there?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM CLYBURN (R-SC): Well, thank you very much for having me, Chris. You`re right. You used the right words surreal. You know, I grew up here in South Carolina. I graduated college. I started my professional career as a public school teacher teaching history in the Charleston County Public School. I taught my students the fundamentals of what this country is all about. And I usually contrast what was going on in this country and what was going on the third-world countries to get my students to understand that this country is far and above all of that. Yes, all of that, into question with me.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:06]\par \par \uc2 I could not believe what I was seeing. Even when I left the floor, I heard noise outside. And we went off into this undisclosed location. But until I watched the television monitors, I just could not ever believe that I would be seeing that. And I watched \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  interact with the Vice President, interact with the other Republican leaders of the Senate, McConnell, Scalise, Hoyer. Were all in that room working on one accord. Only when we came out of the room when I started hearing all of this foolishness that nobody in that room, or at least the Democrats were not doing, what needed to be done to preserve the integrity of the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 I`m so glad that people are now getting to see all this video, because they now see that we were doing what was necessary, did not have any support from the White House to get it done.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right now, the image that we`re showing, I don`t know if you can see it, is a leadership huddle, a bipartisan leadership huddle and bicameral. So, you`ve got Senate, House, Republican, Democrats all around. And in the image that`s sort of slightly more zoomed out, you can see Steve Scalise. He`s wearing, I think, an American flag mask. You know, he would later go on to basically say -- and this became a Republican talking point that has been off repeated -- you see him right there -- over and over again, that somehow \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  was responsible, that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  didn`t -- she declined the National Guard, that she didn`t do anything.\par \par \uc2 And I mean, I just got to ask you, how it felt to hear that over the last 18 months when you know they all stood in the room and watched her caught - - literally call for the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Yes. Nancy was calling the mayor of Washington. And I remember very clearly saying to her. I said, Madam Speaker, all the information I get is that the authority that the mayor once had with the National Guard here in DC is not there anymore. This administration has taken that away. And \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  got on the phone and call the Governor of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 And I said to Nancy, look, give Northrup a call. Virginia is just as close to us, if not closer, than Maryland. And she did get on the phone. She did talk to him. And they were doing everything they could. They just did not get the kind of support that we should have gotten from the White House. And we all now know why.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also some footage in there that suggests that the speaker, particularly, who you know, has along with Senator McConnell at this point, sort of custody of the Capitol, right, as an institution and also physically is a building, is getting briefed that the Secret Service is aware that there is a plan for the President to come to the Capitol, that this may or may not happen, that the Secret Service is trying to stop him from. Was that -- was that known in that room that it was a possibility that the President himself might actually descend on the Capitol, essentially, to lead the mob?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, we didn`t know about the president and what may have taken place in the automobile with him wanting to go to the Capitol. But what we didn`t know, though, was that something untoward was taking place. Because I remember very clearly, someone came from the Capitol to give us information, and she kept looking at me rather strangely.\par \par \uc2 And finally, I walked off and talked with her. And I was able to go back and said to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , we are getting conflicting information. This young lady is telling us what she`s been told to tell us, but she doesn`t feel or believe it herself because he confided in me that she didn`t. And so, we were able then to take further steps based upon the information that we were getting, as opposed to the information that they wanted us to get.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me just follow up on that. And I don`t want to, you know, betray confidence. But when you say today or who`s -- what was the channel for this information that was not -- that was some kind of spin or obfuscation in your -- in your estimation? Who was it? What institution was telling you wrong information?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:02]\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, the Capitol -- the head of all -- the Sergeant at Arms left within days, the head, or the chief of the, what we call it, the \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  left within days. So, something untoward was going on. And we saw it when there were members in the mobs saying they are on outside. What did they mean by that? Why is it that they could come and not go to the door with my name on the door but ended up in my office in my so-called hideaway? How did they know where that office was?\par \par \uc2 So, there was too much going on there. And I think things are coming out now, that people know that there were some people wearing the blue uniform that was at least sympathetic to what was going on with that mob rather than doing the job that was needed to be done in order to protect the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s some reporting on that as well at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . This is adjacent obviously -- I want to be clear with people that are watching, there`s a bunch of different law enforcement and security apparatuses here. There`s the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 , which is under the control of the U.S. Capitol, which is the Congress. There`s, of course, Secret Service, which is part of the federal government under the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . There`s the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , which is under -- part of the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to read to you from this memo that was sent, that we`ve recently unearthed by NBC News through a FOIA. This is a letter from an unknown \uc2 FBI\uc2  insider to a top \uc2 FBI\uc2  official. This is a week after January 6, that there is at best -- a sizable percentage of the employee population that felt sympathetic to the group that stormed the Capitol and said it was no different than the BLM protests of last summer. Several officer lamented the only reason this violent activity is getting more attention is because of political correctness. I wonder what you make of that.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: That is what this country has come into. And our hope that the people of goodwill in this country will step up. You know, \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 ., his letter from the Birmingham City Jail, talked about the fact that the people of ill will in our society was making a much better use of time than the people of goodwill. And he wanted at the time that we are going to be meant to repent not just for the vitriolic words and deeds of bad people, but for the appalling silence of good people.\par \par \uc2 The good people of this country have got the break their silence. They`re being silent. And we know full well that something untoward is taking place. In many instances. I`ve seen it. I live near Fort Jackson. I visit Camp (INAUDIBLE), Fort Bragg. We know that some bad people have infiltrated the military, some bad people have infiltrated law enforcement officers. And the people of goodwill have got to speak up. You got to stop this. Because if you don`t, we`re going to lose this country. And that`s as simple as I can put it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , very powerful words. I feel like I learned a lot in that interview. And I really thank you for making time for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, we have an extended version of that behind-the-scenes footage in a moment. Also, the disgraced ex-president sends a 14-page response the January 6 Committee just hours after they unanimously voted to subpoena his testimony. We`ve got committee members \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  who will join me on that next. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As we mentioned on the day of January 6, congressional leaders were evacuated to a secure location close to the Capitol. And with them was the daughter of the Speaker of the House Alexandra Pelosi, who is an accomplished documentary filmmaker working with \uc2 HBO\uc2 . She took a whole bunch of footage. She was there as this incredibly historic moment unfolded. Some of it was played yesterday in the hearing, you might have seen.\par \par \uc2 Now, MSNBC has obtained much more of that video. Here`s an extended version of what happened that afternoon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TERRI MCCULLOUGH, CHIEF OF STAFF TO \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : The Secret Service said they`d have dissuaded him for coming to Capitol Hill. They told him they don`t have the resources to protect him here. So, at the moment, he is not coming but that could change.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I want him to come. I want to punch him now. It`s all I have -- I`ve waiting for this. They`re trespassing on the Capitol grounds. I want to punch him now and I`m going to go to jail and I`m going to be happy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Bring her now. Bring her out here. We`re coming in if you don`t bring her out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If Pence cave, we`re going to drag him through the streets. You (BLEEP) politicians are going to get (dragged) to the streets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Senator Schumer is at a secure location and they`re locked down in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: You have to send the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: And the National Guard and DOD denied it? I want to call someone right now. I want to speak to the Secretary of Defense.\par \par \uc2 OK, well, DC has requested the National Guard and it`s been denied by DOD. I`d like to know a good (BLEEP) reason why it`s been denied. I apologize for being so (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Don`t apologize.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:02]\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Please move in. The whole Capitol is rampaged. There`s a picture of someone sitting this chair in the Senate. We`ve been evacuated. There`ve been shots fired. We need a full National Guard component now. We need them fast. We`ve all had -- I`ve never seen anything like this. We`re like a third-world country here. We have to run and evacuate the Capitol, 400 congressmen, 200 senators and all the staff, OK. We need help right away. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Rep. \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  (D-MD): Our staff have been locked up and barricaded in their offices. So, there`s a critical situation and risk of loss of life.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: We have some senators who are still in their hideaways. They need massive personnel now. Can you get the Maryland National Guard to come to?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: They`re just breaking windows. They`re doing all kinds of -- it`s reall