ises.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We turn now to a member of the January 6 Committee, Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  who`s a Democrat from Maryland. He was of course, at the hearing yesterday where some of that footage was played. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, I guess I just first want to get your impression of this footage. I mean, what it says about what was happening that day, and to me the awareness amongst all parties that what was happening was a violent attempt to stop the peaceful transfer of power and that the thing that had to happen was the transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Yes, there was no doubt about that. I think why - - the reason why yesterday`s hearing was such a breakthrough was because before, people had seen the rioting take place and they were fully aware that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was sitting around in his White House dining room doing nothing.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:11]\par \par \uc2 But what they didn`t see was the spectacle of Democratic and Republican leaders scrambling to try to get military and police reinforcements in order to put down the insurrection and try to keep the peaceful transfer of power going.\par \par \uc2 But yesterday, I think the American people saw all three of these things happening simultaneously. And I think it had an electrifying effect on people`s understanding of how serious and grave the situation this was.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, NBC NEWS HOST: You know, here`s the thing I keep coming back to you. There`s this unity of purpose, you see, and in some ways, you know, you`ve used this term, which I`ve -- I think appropriate from you about the constitutional order.\par \par \uc2 And you see, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and Speaker Pelosi and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , all these people working essentially on the side of the constitutional order against its enemies, those enemies being the mob and the president.\par \par \uc2 What I still can`t -- and that`s uplifting in its way. What I still can`t quite square is A, that they went back in that room and voted against certification, majority of Republicans and B, that 18 months later, they`ve basically capitulated to the enemies of the constitutional order, both in word and deed.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, you know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has transformed people`s understanding of what normal politics is. And normal politics now in the Republican Party is to bow down to him like a cult leader, and basically to do whatever he says.\par \par \uc2 Now, when we were forced out of normal politics into abnormal, extraordinary politics, a number of these Republican officials understood how serious an emergency we were in and they acted, you know, with different degrees of conviction to try to help Pelosi and Schumer and the rest of us get through the crisis.\par \par \uc2 But the moment it was over, they lapsed right back into the general power dynamic within the GOP today, it is definitely a scary thing to see happen. But it just underscores the importance of the rest of us standing up for the constitutional order, and demanding that the right to vote and democratic sovereignty be respected.\par \par \uc2 Because as I tried to say, you know, at the end of my little section yesterday, Lincoln put it best, he said, an insurrection is an attempt to usurp the will of all the people working through the government and put it in the hands of the people who pick up some guns or use violence to have their way against everybody else.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: At the end of yesterday`s hearing, your committee voted to subpoena the president. And it was interesting to see people`s reactions, some were glad to see that, some felt that it was essentially a stunt or that it didn`t have real bite that it was happening at the end is a kind of final, almost symbolic, expressive action.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the subject that subpoena wrote this completely unhinged and deranged, 14-page letter. What do you say to those who say that this is symbolic or expressive?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: You know, the big question I got today from, you know, people in the right-wing and some reporters was, well, why didn`t you guys do this six or eight months ago?\par \par \uc2 In other words, so the entire project of trying to get to the facts of what happened would be consumed in a fight with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which he would love. Turning it into a circus about, you know, his absurd claims of executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 No, what we did was we went out and we conducted a real investigation, we showed America how the vast majority of people respond when called upon to do their legal and patriotic duty of telling Congress what they know about this violent attack on America.\par \par \uc2 And then, we authorized and directed the chair of the committee to go and subpoena Trump. And already he`s playing his silly games.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that letter, including the use of the Royal we, which was pretty jarring is just an outrageous distraction from the reality to the extent there`s anything of substance in there. He`s saying, why didn`t you go and investigate the election fraud? You didn`t ask any questions about the election fraud.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the election fraud is nonsense. And all he has to do is listen to his own attorney general or his own White House Counsel, or more than 60 federal and state court judges who ruled on it.\par \par \uc2 But in any event, we`ve asked a number of people who are still under the spell of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Well, what exactly is the voter fraud that wasn`t detected by any of these investigations or court rulings or members of the president`s cabinet and they can`t say, they just have a feeling.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, Trump uses that too in this enormously revealing letter where he says lots of people feel that there was fraud. I`m sorry, your feelings Mr. Snowflake cannot dictate 