e in Congress, he doesn`t see a need for us to raise the minimum wage. He sees it as his personal responsibility to push eight different national abortion bans. He supported abortion bans with no exception for rape, incest or life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 He`s coming after folk`s retirement security. He is ready to put Social Security and Medicare on the chopping block just to provide more tax deductions, more tax benefits for people in his tax bracket for himself, for his own companies and his own investments. And that`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator Johnson has talked about putting Social Security, Medicare and other Medicare, we call them entitlements or social insurance programs on an annual budget, so they would have to be voted for once a year.\par \par \uc2 How serious do you think he is? And Republican leadership is about a plan of going after that spending should they win control in the next election?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, I think he`s very serious about it. Well, one of the first plans that came out from the reauthorization was five years. Ron Johnson said that wasn`t extreme enough. Instead, it should be every single year.\par \par \uc2 And look, it should be no surprise what he`s willing to do. He voted to repeal the Affordable Care Act, he led the charge and you can rest assured that he would try to do that, again, taking a lifeline for so many people in this (AUDIO GAP) so many people across this country who would not otherwise have health insurance.\par \par \uc2 That is exactly who he is that`s what -- that was he has been. That will continue to be if he`s reelected, he`ll stop at nothing to make life harder for working people and that`s what frightens me.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:02]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Wisconsin, Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes facing off Ron Johnson in the seat race for Senate in the state of Wisconsin. Thanks so much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Thank you for having me.\par \par \u