e Republican candidate for governor, for secretary of state, for attorney general, all say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": I think it`s important to underscore that these in many cases women are the last frontier between the country and a precipice of election denialism. These are not just Republican candidates. Don bold, I carry lake, these people are a new strain of conservative, and it gives conservativism a bad name, but this is really, they are the gatekeepers between democracy and chaos in a lot of respects, so paying attention to these candidates and listening to what the Democrats have to say about the races is critical, with just 27 days to go.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We really have to have as many candidates on as we possibly can and that`s what we`re doing tonight.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Can`t wait to see it. Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 I want to begin tonight with a pledge to you, the audience of this program, this is a serious pledge, that I will fully present and analyze any evidence that emerges to indicate that Donald J. Trump might not be guilty of obstruction of justice and violating the espionage act. But so far, no such evidence has emerged in any way.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s lawyers have offered no hint of a defense to the potential criminal charges of violating the Espionage Act. And tonight, in the breaking news of the night, the evidence has taken a turn that could be what a criminal jury finds to be proof, beyond a reasonable doubt, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed those crimes. There is an informer inside Donald Trump`s home, in Florida. That is the breaking news. Reported tonight in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ` under the headline, Trump worker told \uc2 FBI\uc2  about moving Mar-a-Lago boxes on ex-presidents orders.\par \par \uc2 In each one of the Justice Department legally legal filings in the appropriate titled case of Donald J. Trump versus the United States of America, the Justice Department has repeatedly used a phrase that is repeated that appeared in last night`s filing. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  uncovered evidence. There are two key points in the story, where the Justice Department leans very heavily on some variation of that phrase, every single time. The Justice Department lays out the chronology of their investigation in a legal filing in court. They always say that after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  returned 15 boxes of documents to the government, one year after he left the presidency, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  somehow knew that there were still more government documents in Donald Trump`s possession.\par \par \uc2 And last night`s filing, the solicitor general put it this way: the \uc2 FBI\uc2  developed evidence that additional boxes remaining at Trump`s residence at the Mar-a-Lago club in Palm Beach Florida were also likely to contain classified information. That is such a loaded sentence. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  developed evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had more boxes of classified documents. How do they do that? How did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  find that evidence? How did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  find that out?\par \par \uc2 And then, in every chronology prosecutors have presented, they always describe, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents and a prosecutor going to Florida on June 3rd to personally take custody of the remaining documents in don Trump`s possession. And when they take those documents, that`s the day when Trump lawyer Christina Bobb was reportedly talked into signing a certification that it was completely false, a certification saying the documents being handed over on June 3rd were the very last government documents in Donald Trump`s possession.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:18]\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had been hit with a subpoena for those documents by the government. So he had to turn over all of the remaining documents on that day because defying the subpoena would