people familiar with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 It gets worse. When did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tell the Trump employee to move the boxes? "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is reporting that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told the Trump employee to move the boxes after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  received a subpoena from the federal government for those boxes. The fact the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ordered the movement of those boxes after he received a subpoena could be proof beyond a reasonable doubt of Donald Trump`s intention to commit obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 It gets worse. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is reporting that there are multiple witnesses, multiple witness accounts, about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ordering the boxes moved after receiving the subpoena in May. The people familiar with the investigations that agents have gathered witness accounts indicating that, after Trump advisers received a subpoena in may for any classified documents remained at Mar-a-Lago, Trump told people to move is boxes to his residents at the property.\par \par \uc2 And it gets worse. It`s all on video. That description of events was corroborated by the security camera footage, which showed people moving the boxes.\par \par \uc2 After the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and federal prosecutor visited Trump residents on June 3rd, armed with a subpoena and, and obtain those documents, they went back to Washington and sent another subpoena to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  demanding the security camera video recordings from his residence. Those video recordings are now clearly the evidentiary equivalent of the audio recordings made in the Oval Office by President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  while President Nixon was ordering crimes to be committed by his staff. Those audio recordings of Nixon, when obtained, under subpoena, forced the Republican president to resign the presidency before he would have been impeached in the House of Representatives and removed from office by the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:10:14]\par \par \uc2 They were the proverbial smoking gun and the evidence against Republican President \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 . At the time, President Nixon could have legally destroyed those audiotapes before they were subject to a subpoena. And probably then survived that investigation.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could have destroyed the security video recordings at his Florida residence before they were subject to the subpoena. Everything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did with those government documents, fully classified documents, that he brought to Florida, was profoundly, deeply, and relentlessly stupid. And the evidence increasingly indicates that it was probably criminal.\par \par \uc2 Everything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said publicly about the case has been deeply stupid and some of it has been self incriminating, including it around the last week when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  admitted that he was knowingly in possession of the documents and then said that he kept the material, quote, because it`s mine. That, of course, is evidence of the criminal intent to keep those documents for the rest of his life.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s reporting in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", an unnamed person who worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  quotes what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said while the documents were under subpoena. They are my documents, Trump said, according to an aide who spoke to him. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , no doubt knows who that aid is and we can expect that aid to some be an under oath witness adding to what could be proof beyond a reasonable doubt the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is guilty of obstruction of justice and violating the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Daniel Goldman, former House majority counsel, and first impeachment trial \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He`s also a former assistant U.S. attorney for the Southern District of New York and is now a candidate for Congress in New York`s tenth congressional district.\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Paul Butler, a professor of law at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  and a former federal prosecutor. And Glenn Kirschner, former federal prosecutor. They`re both MSNBC legal analysts.\par \par \uc2 And so, it is to this team of former federal prosecutors that we put the evidence tonight.\par \par \uc2 And, Daniel Goldman, as I have said, I will rush to the microphone here on this program and deliver and analyze at length any evidence that emerges, any evidence that emerges that is helpful in some way to Donald Trump`s criminal defense. So far none of it has emerged, that I`m aware of.\par \par \uc2 What do you see in tonight`s reporting by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 "?\par \par \uc2 DANIEL GOLDMAN (D), NEW YORK CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Well, it`s confirmation of what we suspected about the basis for the search warrant, the Department of Justice got. Clearly, they received a certification that they had obtained all the documents pursuant to a subpoena from Mar-a-Lago. But they subsequently had a witness come and testify. Interestingly, that witness initially lied and then after being re-approached by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , came clean to indicate that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  intentionally intended to move documents in order to conceal them from the Department of Justice and failed to comply with the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 That is why we have the bases not only of being in possession of information relevant to the national defense, but also the potential crime of obstruction of justice because he is knowingly and intentionally trying to subvert a grand jury subpoena. So we have a little bit more color now on what led the department of justice to get that search warrant, that ultimately yielded what "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports to be 103 classified documents. This is getting worse and worse for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , as you pointed out, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 All of the information coming out is harmful to him and is helpful to the department of justice`s case. As you also pointed, out the more he talks, the worse he makes the situation for himself.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to go over that part of the report tonight. You just mentioned that this witness changed the story after the first telling of the story.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:03]\par \par \uc2 Here is the way "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports that. I know in all of your experience as federal prosecutors, I know this is something you have seen before. So I just want to deliver this now.\par \par \uc2 The employee, who is working at Mar-a-Lago, is cooperating with Justice Department and has been interviewed multiple times by federal agents according to the people familiar with the situation who declined to identify the worker. In the first interview, these people said, the witness denied handling sensitive documents or the boxes that might contain such documents.\par \par \uc2 As they gathered evidence, agents decided to re-interview the witness and the witnesses story changed dramatically. These people said. In the second interview, the witness described moving boxes at Trump`s request.\par \par \uc2 Paul Butler, what do you make of that sequence in the testimony?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: There is likely not just one witness. It`s probably people who moved these hundreds of thousands of documents around. So this one witness would have some credibility issues, so here she could be corroborated by other employees and importantly forensic evidence like the security camera video. The questions that the Justice Department has for this one witness now, Lawrence, is why did you change your story? I don`t think you have to be a rocket scientist to think that that evidence might also point to Donald Trump`s.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn Kirschner, in your experience as a federal prosecutor, when you`re dealing with a witness who`s on the criminal side of the crime, how uncommon is it for them to tell a story that then changes as they continue their cooperation?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Lawrence, I can tell you how many times I would receive a new assignment, pick up a new case, and I would start meeting with the witnesses. You know what they would say right out of the box? I didn`t see it, I don`t know anything about it, can I go home now?\par \par \uc2 And then we would have to work through the evidence, the information, get to the bottom of why they were denying seeing something that, frankly, we had other evidence that they had seen, maybe even participated in. It was entirely usual for witnesses to first tell prosecutors or \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, I don`t know anything about this crime. That could be because they are complicit and they are worried about getting themselves in trouble.\par \par \uc2 It could certainly, as my friend Paul says, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was putting pressure on them. Or, look, they have seen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  turn on other witnesses who crossed him in a very public and even dangerous way. Why would anybody enthusiastically say, I have information about the crimes of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to go to another point in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  tonight, inside Trump world and for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It is within Trump`s orbit there have been months of dueling accusations and theories about who may be cooperating with the federal government. Some of the former presidents closest aides have continued to work with Trump even as they have seen \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents show up at their houses to question them and serve subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Goldman, in your experience, that kind of atmosphere happens when an investigation is centered on a particular workplace or group of people. What are the kind of dynamic things that develop when everyone in the Trump world is suspecting someone else?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yeah, it`s the prisoners dilemma, right? It`s which one is going to be the first one in to cooperate, and which one is going to resist cooperation because they are afraid of retaliation by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? I will tell you, Lawrence, it is no laughing matter here the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the target of this investigation. When I lead the impeachment investigation, I received threats because, from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters.\par \par \uc2 I prosecuted mob offices, violent Russian organized criminals, and the most threats, the most fear I ever had was doing the impeachment investigation. So it is real, the threats. I mean, you saw what happened with the whistleblower. This is no joke. What probably happened in this case is that that witness was questioned, lied, because he was afraid of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , then the \uc2 FBI\uc2  gets the surveillance tapes and they see this individual moving boxes after the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:05]\par \par \uc2 They then go back to the guy, or woman, and they say, here`s a video of you moving the boxes when you said you didn`t. So it`s time for you to come clean. That is often how this sort of thing happens, like Glenn said. When you have other evidence, you presented and there is no choice for that person`s but to cooperate. My suspicion is that`s what happened here.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Paul Butler, talk about the significance of this movement of the boxes ordered by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , taking place after the subpoena arrives and he is saying to them take those boxes out of the storage place where the \uc2 FBI\uc2  knows they are and move them to my residents, which, I think, explains why, as part of that search took place in the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  residents section where they went into his office room and found classified documents in his desk.\par \par \uc2 But the legal significance of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying, all right, let`s start moving those boxes after the subpoena arrives.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: The former president knew that there was a grand jury subpoena for these documents. If Trump, as "The Post" report suggests, directed his employees to move these documents to his private quarters is not only obstruction, it`s a potential violation of the Espionage Act, which criminalizes high highly sensitive rip evidence pertaining to national security.\par \par \uc2 And, Lawrence, the Justice Department`s policies not to prosecute of the evidence is just that the documents were not handled properly. There has to be some plus actor like obstruction or willful concealment, which is exactly what the evidence in the post report suggests.\par \par \uc2 Both Lynn and Daniel know that as a prosecutor, sometimes you`re an investigative, you get lots of questions, but at a point the evidence forces your hand. \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is getting awfully close to that point with this extremely incriminating new evidence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Glenn, quickly, before we go, you`ve got \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ordering the boxes moved after the subpoena arrives, apparently to more than one person. It`s not just going to be one witness who says he told me to do that. We`re not going to just see one person on video moving the boxes based on what we are hearing tonight.\par \par \uc2 And you also have, in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " report, a new line, a new quoted line from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , directly to a person quoted by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " word on Trump says that material belongs to me.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Lawrence, what I see in that "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting taking an aggregate is so far beyond evidence of probable cause, which is the evidence we need to arrest and indict. It is beyond the preponderance of the evidence. It is beyond clear and convincing evidence.\par \par \uc2 I see proof beyond a reasonable doubt. Really, the only thing left to do when \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  believes the time is right, is to indict this case and presented to 12 jurors, because those are the only people who can decide whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  `s guilty or not guilty. And absent presenting this evidence to 12 jurors and letting them is the conscience of the community make the decision, I think we lose trust and we lose faith and we lose confidence in the institutions of government.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn Kirschner, Paul Butler, Daniel Goldman, thank you all very much for starting off our discussion tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the billion dollar verdict against Donald Trump`s friend and supporter, Alex Jones, for his poisonous lies about murdered children, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, a jury in Connecticut ordered Donald Trump`s friend and supporter Alex Jones to pay $965 million to the families of some of the victims of the mass murder at Sandy Hook elementary school because Alex Jones spent years poisonously lying about those murdered children and their parents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANCINE WHEELER, MOTHER OF SANDY HOOK VICTIM BEN WHEELER: I watch them all. I watch them all. I have seen all the videos. It is one thing to lose a child. It`s quite another thing when people take everything about your boy who is gone, and your surviving child and your husband in everything you ever did in your life is on the Internet and harass you.\par \par \uc2 MARK BARDEN, FATHER OF SANDY HOOK VICTIM DANIEL BARDEN: It`s so hard to go see your seven-year-old child headstone into here that people were desecrating it and urinating on it and threatening to dig it up. I don`t know how to articulate what it feels like. But that`s where we are. All our abilities to hold on to Daniel`s memory, to cherish and honor him, has been tarnished with toxic hate and lies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Jim Bergenn, Connecticut attorney with extensive experience in criminal and civil litigation. He`s an adjunct professor at the University of Connecticut School of Law.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 This case is, of course, governed by Connecticut law. Given the guidance that the jury had through Connecticut law, were you surprised at this verdict?\par \par \uc2 JIM BERGENN, \uc2 UCONN\uc2  SCHOOL OF LAW ADJUNCT PROFESSOR: Actually not. The volume of money allocated very specifically, Lawrence, to each of those individual complainants, plaintiffs, is following the law precisely.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:42]\par \par \uc2 So it is a testament to the judges discipline and the quality of the lawyering because notwithstanding Norm Patterson`s extraordinary efforts to retain the confidence of his client, he had to abide by the rules. And so I believe that it is going to be upheld. I don`t think it`s even a close question.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So you expected to be upheld on appeal and then that would bring us to the collection stage, which is always a difficult stage in civil litigation, when you win these judgments, collecting the money is another matter. What is the future in this litigation on collecting this judgment from Alex Jones?\par \par \uc2 BERGENN: Any bankruptcy judge is licking her or his chops because the powers, especially when you see today, he still tried to pump money out of his people who believe his crap.\par \par \uc2 So as a consequence, the money he made today is going to be available. Any money that came in from the time he knew that he was at risk for losing the substantial amount of money that he doesn`t have any more is potentially a fraudulent transfer and the bankruptcy court is going to be very aggressive. And he can`t do what he did with this judge where he just defies the judge, doesn`t follow the law.\par \par \uc2 In the bankruptcy court it wouldn`t surprise me if we`re going to see some contempts, because he even taunted the bankruptcy court. We can do this for years and tie him up.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that Chris Mattei just mentioned in your last hour, that this may be non dischargeable in bankruptcy so that is essentially financial suicide. He is going to have this -- if this is bound to be non dischargeable which I think it qualifies under the law of culpa (ph), then personally he can never attain and acquire assets because they can be attached because they are non dischargeable in bankruptcy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You mentioned Chris Mattei, he`s the lawyer for some of the families in this case. And he was telling Alex Wagner, this point you are making, which is bankruptcy proceedings, the point is essentially, it will not protect Alex Jones from this judgment that can be pursued for the rest of his life.\par \par \uc2 BERGENN: In fact, you go back to the time when it was filed. The case was filed and he`s still making money then. All that money that he made from that day forward -- whatever he did with that money is potentially something that has to come back. A constructive trust can be imposed on that and the judge, seeing especially the judge is being taunted by him, even on the day of the verdict -- "no, we`ll just tie it up, spend a lot of money, I don`t have any money".\par \par \uc2 Well, evidence is plain. He controls everything. He alone has all the money, all the power. That`s not even in dispute. So any place that money went, he is liable.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Attorney James Bergenn, thank you very much for your expert guidance on this tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BERGENN: Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, what do you do when your Senate campaign opponent says \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the real winner of the 2020 presidential election and then a month later says oh no, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the election?\par \par \uc2 And what do you do when your Senate campaign opponent favors a national ban on abortion in all 50 states and then changes his mind and says the question of abortion should, of course, be left up to the gentleman? That`s the term he used. The gentleman of the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 That`s who New Hampshire`s Democratic Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  is running against. Senator Hassan joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:48]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We are 27 days away from the election that will determine, among other things, whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ever appoints another federal judge. If Republicans control the Senate they will completely stop the Senate confirmation process for federal judges for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 If a Supreme Court vacancy opens up Republicans will keep it vacant until there is a Republican president, no matter how many years that takes.\par \par \uc2 Here is Don Bolduc, the Republican candidate for Senate in New Hampshire, who wants to help Republicans take control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC, NEW HAMPSHIRE REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: Trump won the election, and damn it, I stand by my word (ph).\par \par \uc2 I`ve come to the conclusion and I want to be definitive on this. The election was not stolen. Was there fraud? Yes. But elections have consequences and unfortunately President Biden is legitimate president of this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The Republican candidate for Senate in New Hampshire is running scared from his own words, as are some other Republican Senate candidates around the country.\par \par \uc2 Don Bolduc has flip-flopped on the issue of abortion also, first saying that he would vote for legislation like Senator Lindsey Graham`s proposal on a national abortion ban for all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 And now saying he actually believes that the final word on abortion belongs to the gentleman of New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:52]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: It belongs to the gentlemen right here, who are state legislators representing you as the best way, I think, as a man, that women get the best voice, at the state level, not at the federal level. And it`s really Senator Hassan that doesn`t understand this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is New Hampshire`s Democratic Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , member of the Senate Finance Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, did you convince him that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the presidential election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): Well, thanks for having me on, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Look, Don Bolduc is an election denier. Not only did he spend over a year stoking the big lie at stop after stop around our state, he also says that if 2024 doesn`t go for his preferred candidate, he will work to overturn that election. And just this week cast doubt on the 2022 election, suggesting if he loses he may not accept the results.\par \par \uc2 So he is trying to mislead Granite Staters about his election denial, but he keeps reinforcing it even as \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and his super PACs are coming into the state with just tens of millions of dollars -- $23 million in spending in the last eight weeks in negative ads against me to try to conceal his record from the voters of New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Similarly he is somebody who would vote for a national abortion ban. That`s way out of step with where Granite Staters and he`s trying to conceal that too. This race is close and we`re going to have to push really hard and need the resources to hold Don Bolduc accountable, so people know how extreme he is.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, as you go forward, how do you know -- I mean this, how do you know what he`s going to say tomorrow about abortion, as one example, since it changes from day to day? Which position are you running against with him?\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Don Bolduc does not believe that women should have the fundamental right to make their own health care decisions. His record here is really long.\par \par \uc2 When the Dobbs decision was decided he said women should rejoice at that. Then as he tried to hide that position, along with his position, he said he would never vote against any anti choice legislation at the national level. Now he`s trying to hide that position with comments like the ones you just talked about.\par \par \uc2 And of course he`s got the whole Republican national establishment behind him. By the way, he also wants to end Social Security, having said recently that since Social Security has been around since 1935, it`s time to change it.\par \par \uc2 So what we need to do is hold him accountable. His record is really, really clear. The concern I have in this race, polls are tightening. We`re within in margin error territory now here.\par \par \uc2 I won my last race in 2016 by 1,017 votes. New Hampshire is a very purple state. McConnell and the national Republicans are trying to buy this seat and put an election denier into the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We can`t let that happen. But we do need people to stay engaged with this campaign to get it over the finish line. So I`m hoping folks will find out how they can do that by going to MaggieHassan.com.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When you speak to New Hampshire voters and they are asking you, what did I get -- what did I get from Senator Hassan from my last vote six years ago? What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: I tell them that I have tried to follow the example of the people of New Hampshire who put aside differences and get things done every day. I have a bipartisan record of working on things to lower costs such as banning surprise medical bills, bipartisan. I was one of the lead negotiators on the bipartisan infrastructure bill that will finally get high-speed Internet to every corner of our state. I was one of the authors of the Chips and Science Act, lowering costs, bringing manufacturing back to the United States, making sure we have a vibrant economy that can outcompete China and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 And I have been recognized for my bipartisan work, which is just so consistent with the whole kind of way Granite Staters do things, ranked by the Looker`s Center (ph) is the most bipartisan senator in the country.\par \par \uc2 So I talked too about the things we still need to work on. Lowering energy costs in particular, making sure housing costs and childcare are more affordable.\par \par \uc2 And then I also talk about the things that Democrats have done together, allowing Medicare to negotiate for prescription drug prices, standing up to big pharma in the process. Standing up to big oil to help give people tax cuts to invest in energy efficiency and help with the whole economy pivot toward a clean energy future.\par \par \uc2 Those are the things that we have achieved but we obviously need to do more.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:52]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: New Hampshire Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Thanks so much for having me on. Take care, and be safe.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Republicans have, in effect, pledged to never again run a fair election in Arizona if they win their campaigns for governor, secretary of state, and attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Arizona`s Democratic candidate for governor, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:49:53]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Early voting began today in Arizona, a state where democracy itself is on the ballot. The Republican candidates for governor, Senate, secretary of state, and attorney general in Arizona have all denied the results of the 2020 election. That includes Donald Trump`s candidate for governor, former local Fox Host Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake has said that she would not have certified Joe Biden`s victory in 2020 if she had been in charge. She field a brief in support of a lawsuit to abolish mail-in voting. She supports Arizona`s abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Arizona secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  who is Democratic candidate for governor has refused to debate Kari Lake saying that it would create a circus atmosphere because of Kari Lake`s tendency to cause chaos and Kari Lake`s embrace of conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Kari Lake disrupted the start of a \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  Town hall by sitting in the front row demanding that \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  come out to debate her. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  did not do that. And eventually Kari Lake left.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Arizona secretary of state \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . She is the Democratic candidate for governor in Arizona. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 What will happen to democracy and fair elections in Arizona if Republicans win the governorship?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D-AZ), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Well, hi Lawrence. it`s great to be back, thank you so much. You know, you said it yourself. Democracy is on the ballot in 2022. And that is certainly the case in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 We have my opponent is an election denier. She has centered her (INAUDIBLE) campaign around Trump`s big lie and is running for office so that she can change the rules and overturn the will of the voters in future elections.\par \par \uc2 She is dangerous and too extreme. She has no ability to govern. She doesn`t understand policy or legislation. And she would create untold levels of chaos in the state of Arizona if she were elected governor.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: As secretary of state, you made sure in every way that you could, that the 2020 election was fair, that the votes were counted accurately. Everything that you did in office as secretary of state in that election was then in effect certified again and again and again by others who looked at the results in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 What will it mean if the next time there is a Republican secretary of state in your position?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, a Republican secretary of state in and of itself will not be bad but it`s the Republican nominee that we have in Arizona who is not only an election denier, an Oath Keepers, he`s an insurrectionist, who was at the Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 He`s defending the actions of the people who stormed the Capitol, and has said if he were secretary of state in 2020 that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would still be president and certainly has continued to fuel these election conspiracy theories, fuel threats against election workers in the state of Arizona, and certainly has no understanding about how to even run elections in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Is there a potential for some kind of clash like we`ve never seen before if you are the Democratic governor of Arizona and you have a Republican secretary of state who simply lies about what the next election results are? What can you in the governorship do about that?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Well, look I`ve said all along that we have checks and balances in our systems, and that is what has kept us safe so far.\par \par \uc2 So one person acting alone isn`t going to be able to overturn the will of the voters. We have a check from the governor`s office who also has to sign off on certifying the election. The governor has to approve the election procedures manual. And the courts can certainly take action to stop someone who is not following the law, which would be the case if a secretary Mark Finchem decided not to certify the elections because he didn`t like the results.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Arizona candidate for governor, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us once again tonight.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:30]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The House Select Committee investigating the January 6th attack on the Capitol will hold its next hearing tomorrow at 1:00 p.m. Washington time. MSNBC will begin special coverage at noon ahead of the hearing, one hour before the hearing.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:42]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And tomorrow night I will join Rachel Maddow, Nicolle Wallace, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , Alex Wagner, Chris Hayes and others for special prime time coverage of that hearing, a recap of the high points of the hearing. That will begin at 8:00 p.m. Eastern, followed by a special edition of the LAST WORD at our regular time of 10:00 p.m. tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.LAST WORD\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221013eiac00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 12, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ellison Barber, Dasha Burns, Cornell Belcher, Ali  Vitali, Kelly O`Donnell, Cal Perry, Ken Dilanian, Tom Costello \par \uc2 8,318 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 High-stakes political drama unfolding today in two of the closest Senate races in the country, with two embattled candidates trying to keep their shot at victory from slipping away. In Georgia, Republican nominee \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is tripling down maybe quadrupling down on his denials about an ex- girlfriend`s abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: Flat-out denial --\par \par \uc2 LINSEY DAVIS, ABC NEWS ANCHOR: Possible it happens and you don`t --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No. Flat out they`re all lie, a lie, lie, lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And in Pennsylvania, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  giving his first in- person interviews since suffering a stroke to NBC. He claims he`s good to go, but he won`t release his medical records to prove it. We`ll have live reports from both states to show you that interview and ask Pennsylvania Democrat Madeleine Dean whether she thinks voters deserve more. Plus, will there be a smoking gun waiting at the end of the January 6 investigation on Capitol Hill? The answer, just 24 hours away. And finally, it turns out intentionally slamming a $330 million spacecraft into the side of an asteroid is a good thing. How NASA`s grand experiment that could one day save the planet, one from science fiction to science fact.\par \par \uc2 But we start with politics, a new dramatic twist in two critical races, Georgia and Pennsylvania. For Fetterman, national Democrats assumed he benefits from a weaker candidate in Mehmet Oz. But the last two months have seen Oz cut Fetterman`s polling lead in half. And new questions about Fetterman`s health have put the candidate squarely on defense.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was expected to embody the conservative values of the Republican base and, of course, he has Donald Trump`s endorsement but polls show \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  leading, the repeated allegations against Walker undermining his support among women. And now the race is so close. It is very possible neither candidate gets 50 percent in November, and that would force a runoff.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ellison Barber is in Atlanta, Dasha Burns in Pittsburgh, and Cornell Belcher is the Democratic strategist, a pollster, an MSNBC political analyst, and the founder of Brilliant Corners Research. So, Ellison, despite all the allegations, the race in Georgia is still essentially a dead heat. Have you been able to assess on the ground what kind of impact the allegations have had on Walker`s candidacy?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean look, this is dominating headlines here. People are paying attention to it. We`re starting to see all those slight some movement in the polls. You have two new polls out today and in both of them, Warnock is slightly ahead of Walker, and one of them, a Quinnipiac poll, Warnock is actually above that 50 percent threshold.\par \par \uc2 That being said, this is still a really tight race. And while you`ve seen some -- very few, but some Republicans at the state level talk about concerns they have in regards to Walker`s candidacy, we`re seeing national Republicans really rally behind Walker. And in terms of the Republican base here, most seem to still be in Herschel Walker`s corner. He did give his most extensive denial of those allegations that he previously paid for an abortion for the mother of one of his children in an ABC interview that aired last night. Listen to some of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: But at this point, you now know who she is.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Did you ever have a conversation with this woman at any time about an abortion?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Did you ever, to your knowledge give money to pay for the cost of an abortion?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Is she lying?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, she`s lying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: People are going to be paying very close attention to the debate here on Friday. One other thing worth noting today, \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is the third outlet to now report that they have seen a copy of the $700 check Walker sent this woman in the days after she reportedly had an abortion procedure. NBC News has not independently verified that check, but the details in the new report from \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , Chris, they match details in reports we`ve seen from The Daily Beast and the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And she has -- she also has that receipt from the clinic according to those other sources which we have not confirmed.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you, Ellison. We appreciate that. So, Cornell, we don`t know why because they didn`t ask the question in the poll about the abortion controversy. But Walker is losing ground with women. There is no doubt about that. But the two candidates are still basically tied. So, what do you take away from those two facts?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: What I take away from the -- take away from it that, look, it is a Georgia is a toss-up state. And we -- and by the way, thank you for pointing out in that poll that a 48, 46.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s not really a lead. And sometimes we, in the -- in media, we say well, he leads 48, 46 and for average voters, that means that Warnock is winning, and he`s going to win the race. And that`s true -- that`s not true. It`s a toss-up. That race can go in any direction.\par \par \uc2 Look, you know, this was a state that what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got 49.2 percent of the vote in. I suspect that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to get the -- he`s going to get darn near every one of those Trump voters because if it didn`t disqualify \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , why should disqualify \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? So, in the end, this is going to be a tight race. What is interesting about Georgia is if you look at Warnock`s performance the last time around, you know, Warnock won women by roughly eight points over Loeffler in the -- in the last election.\par \par \uc2 The question for Republicans in that state is, is Warnock doesn`t have a chance to get ahead of -- I mean Herschel doesn`t have a chance of getting ahead of Warnock if, in fact, Warnock holds on to that gender gap, if in fact, women voters -- and it looks like in this poll that women voters are still plus eight points for Warnock. So, the question becomes with these -- are these allegations and these -- and by the way, but even before the abortion stuff, allegations about abuse. With all these allegations, how do Republicans in Georgia shrink that gender gap? How do they shrink Warnock`s advantage among women voters, as opposed to -- opposed to a growing? I got a feeling that on the Election Day --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, what about the reverse of that question, Cornell, which is how to -- how do Democrats take advantage of it? Because we know the Republicans are pretty much all in. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  just said I think we`re going to stick with Walker, I think they`re going to hang in there and scrap to the finish. So what do the Democrats need to do?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: I think that -- I think the Democrats are doing what they need to do and that is actually put his record out there, right? He has a horrible -- horrendous record on women`s issues and all of this are proof points to that so it`s disqualifying to the majority of women voters. And again, I think if Warnock pulls this out, which I think he`s going to pull it out, it`s going to be because quite frankly, that gender gap that we saw developing in 2018, and 2020 is going to continue to grow, not shrink in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I look at -- I look at Warnock and I -- and I -- and I see no way that they`re going to shrink that gender -- that eight-point gap down to three or four points which they needed in order for them to win that state. Democrats as they`re doing in Georgia, they`re doing all across the country, continue to drive these women`s issues, continue to drive the gender gap, and then make the electorate look more like 2018 than it did in 2010.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. Dasha, you got the first in-person interview with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  since his stroke, what were your impressions, and how do you think that all is going to affect his candidacy?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, Chris, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has said time and again, I`m not your typical politician and this was not your typical political interview. I want to set up with what you`re about to see, as I played this portion of the interview for you here, we agreed to use closed captioning during the interview. This is an accommodation that the Fetterman campaign has been open about. He has lingering side effects from the stroke, including auditory processing issues, which essentially means that he has a hard time understanding what he`s hearing sometimes. And so, he uses closed captioning to address that issue. Meaning he`s sort of reading our questions, as we`re asking them. It`s a live transcription there.\par \par \uc2 Now, experts say that this is something that is a common symptom of strokes, it`s -- doesn`t mean that he`s cognitively impaired, meaning his decision-making abilities, his problem-solving abilities, his memory that`s not necessarily affected, and he can fully recover from this. But what every medical expert we`ve talked to has said the caveat they`ve given is that they cannot fully assess a patient without all of the information. And we`ve asked time and again for medical records, we`ve asked to have a conversation with his doctors and I asked in this interview, why he has not revealed that information to reporters. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Don`t voters deserve to know your status now?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATE NOMINEE, PENNSYLVANIA: Being on in front of thousands and thousands of people and having interviews and getting around all across Pennsylvania, that gives everybody and the voters decide you know if they think that it`s really the issue.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But the voters aren`t doctors, right? I mean, look, you have styled yourself as the noble candidate, right? It`s why a lot of people are drawn to you. Sometimes that`s easy and sometimes like in moments like this that gets tested, so why not follow through on that and be fully transparent and release all of that information?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Oh, and I said I am putting everything tested. I am being out and being tested day in and day out. And you know if my doctor teams already said that I`m fine and I`m ready to go on that, then I`m not really sure that`s much more beyond transparent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And, Chris, Fetterman is set to debate his Republican rival, Dr. Mehmet Oz on October 25. The voters that I`ve been talking to, I`ve met some folks who are still undecided, they say they are very much waiting to watch that before they make their decision on who they`re going to cast the ballot for, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dasha Burns, great interview. Thank you for that. Cornell Belcher, always good to see you. I want to bring in Pennsylvania Democratic Congresswoman Madeleine Dean. It`s good to have you here. Is it fair for a voter to say yes, maybe I have -- maybe I have a chance to see him on the campaign trail, or I saw the interview that Dasha Burns did, but I want to know what his doctors are saying now before I go to the ballot. Should he just put everything out there right now?\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN, (D-PA): I don`t have an answer for that. But what I will say is I`ve spent time with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and his wife over the course of this campaign and previously, I also was in touch with him after the stroke. He was recently in Montgomery County at our community college in front of nearly 3000 beaming, cheering crowds, a lot of Fetterwomen T- shirts in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 He did phenomenally well. He was funny. He was honest, as he was in that interview, about sometimes smooshing his words together. I have to admit I do that as well. So I have to tell you, just from my personal conversations with him from his work with that entire crowd, and then with a smaller crowd, he was funny, he was capable, and I don`t know what doctor`s reports would reveal today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think -- you know what it feels like to be in the halls of Capitol Hill. Do you believe that currently he can function in that capacity?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Oh, I have every confidence. The other thing that has to come --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think it will hurt him though with some voters?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: It may, but I think what actually helps him is values. What does he stands for and what does the snake oil salesman, Mehmet Oz stand for? He stands for selling you supplements and lying to you. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  believes in the values of, for example, protecting a woman`s right to choose and privacy. Mehmet Oz thinks abortion should be illegal, would criminalize it. Just take a look at their values. Mehmet Oz, election denier, hugs up to Trump. What does \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  care about? Protecting our democracy.\par \par \uc2 Those two are probably the most important issues that really face voters this election cycle. So would you actually let the snake oil salesman who`s mocking \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  for having had a stroke, a medical doctor allegedly mocking the man for having had a stroke, be the person to sit? Do you think that`s somebody capable of leading us in the Senate? I certainly know that my suburban Philadelphia voters say no way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Big picture. You`ve talked about the fact that you`re excited about the political talent that is in your party, Fetterman, and others. And you could be somebody who could go into a higher profile role. There are a lot of people who are talking about the fact. They`re concerned about Joe Biden`s age. They`re concerned about \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , people who have been serving for decades and decades and decades. Is it time for fresh blood?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Fresh blood is already coming in. You know that I can --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In leadership.\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Yes. And it`s already coming in, in leadership. You know, I came in in `18, sworn in `19, and have had the chance to work with our talented top three, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , and whip Clyburn. But then you know that we also have Katherine Clark. We -- this is generationally different. Katherine Clark, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Pete Aguilar.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is it time for them to replace the Steny Hoyer`s, the Nancy Pelosi`s?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: I actually look forward to number one getting through this election and making sure we hold the House. And to your original point on this, bring in these talented people. It excites me like 2018 did. We brought in such diverse folks who said I want to step up. I wasn`t thinking about politics at all. I want to step up. Women, diversity of all types with great careers in their past, and they are a part of our caucus. This election cycle gives us a second chance at that same kind of wave of talent that will begin to fill in and move up in our leadership.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you quickly about something we know according to the polls. Democrats are very interested in the issues of democracy and their rights being taken away and presumably quite a few independents and rural swing voters as well. Big day tomorrow, you know, all the folks, you`ve been an impeachment manager, you know, the pressure. People are looking for a blockbuster final hearing. Are they going to deliver it?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Well, I would not be sort of a movie critic or a blockbuster predictor. But I can say that our history tells us they`ve done an extraordinary job. I could not be prouder of this bipartisan 1/6 committee, headed by \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and Benny Thompson. Of course, my good friend, all of the folks on there are my friends.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But should we expect something that people would say, OK, that was worthy of wrapping up this whole thing?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 DEAN: I`m confident of it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Really?\par \par \uc2 DEAN: And here`s what will be revealed at a minimum that I know of. The culpability of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the January 6 insurrection will be cemented in our history books forever. If he had only accepted electoral defeat, none of this would have happened. Think about that. But the other thing I know that they will emphasize is that 1/6 was not a single day in history. The precious nature and the precarious nature of our democracy is still in the pangs, in the balance with all of these election deniers on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Madeleine Dean, thank you so much. We appreciate you coming in today.\par \par \uc2 DEAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Appreciate it. This just in, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been ordered to sit for a deposition next week in a high-profile defamation lawsuit, according to the Associated Press. Writer E. Jean Carroll says Trump raped her in the dressing room of an upscale Manhattan department store in the mid-1990s. Trump has repeatedly denied that claim, and Carroll claims his denial of the allegation damaged her reputation. Trump`s legal team has tried to delay the lawsuit and prevent him from being questioned, but U.S. District Judge Lewis Kaplan decided it`s time to move forward writing "the defendant should not be permitted to run the clock out on plaintiffs attempt to gain a remedy for what allegedly was a serious wrong."\par \par \uc2 And President Biden will soon arrive in Colorado, starting a west coast swing that will take him to LA later today, where he has now weighed in on a controversy over city hall mired in scandal. We`ve got more on that next. Plus a new hearing with new evidence, what we can expect to hear from the January 6 committee on Capitol Hill just 24 hours from now. And in historic first from \uc2 NASA\uc2  that could one day, well, save the world. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re getting new details on what we could hear from the January 6 committee tomorrow, and it can be summarized in a single name, Trump. Axios reports today that the committee wants to "minimize ancillary players becoming a sideshow and keep the focus on evidence tying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the attack." Earlier today on this network, a former police officer who was injured at the Capitol reminded us what Trump did or rather did not do that day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FANONE\uc2 , FORMER DC METROPOLITAN DC OFFICER: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sat for 187 minutes and didn`t do (INAUDIBLE) while hundreds of police officers were fighting for their lives on the Capitol steps. And we now know that he was watching intently throughout that entire period.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Ali Vitali is with us on Capitol Hill. Nicholas Wu, congressional reporter for Politico joins us as well. Ali, I don`t know if you had a chance to hear Madeleine Dean, but she just indicated and I know she has been talking to committee members, she feels awfully confident about what they`re going to be able to deliver tomorrow. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: And look, Congresswoman Dean is someone who`s been at the back of most of these hearings because she is someone who was in the house gallery on January 6. She and I have talked extensively about her experience. And frankly, for Dean and other members of people in this capital community, the accountability factor that having this aired in the public eye and presenting the public narrative, that`s the key point of this committee.\par \par \uc2 Now the committee members would say that their responsibility here goes a step further and ultimately making a final report that will come at some point before the end of the year. But here we do expect them to tease out new details, new video, things like documentary footage of \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  talking about how even before we were in the month of November of 2020, how he thought that the former President Trump should no matter what say that he won regardless of what the votes actually looked like. And we know that that`s what Trump ultimately endeavored to do.\par \par \uc2 So the focus will inevitably be on Trump, Chris, you`re right. But there were key players in his orbit who can speak to what the committee has tried to do all along, which is establishing his mindset trying to show what the former president knew and what he didn`t do on January 6. There`s officer Fanone points out there too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Nicholas, I think one of the most interesting interviews people are waiting to hear about is Ginni Thomas, right, the conservative activist, and wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . That interview was not videotaped, but how likely are we to get some insights into what she had to say to the committee tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS WU, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, POLITICO: Oh, kind of remains to be seen exactly how they`ll deploy Ginni Thomas`s testimony. We`ve seen with past interviews the committee had, where they weren`t necessarily recorded, you`ll have a staff member or a committee attorney reading up part of the interview. But at the same time, committee members that I`ve talked to didn`t really seem to think that they actually gathered that many new leads or new information from Ginni Thomas.\par \par \uc2 The chairman, \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , told us recently that -- you know that what she told them was fairly typical, actually, of what a lot of people who came to the Capitol on January 6 or were involved in election denialism on January 6 thought, right, that being that they thought that there were issues with the election, and that motivated them in some way to you know, join in something else as we saw that she did, and trying to message officials to try to contest the election results.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, all of this, obviously, Nicholas, could be an important motivator in getting Democrats to the polls because we`ve seen polls that show that there is a lot of concern that they see Trump Republicans as a threat to a whole range of their rights. But you wrote earlier this week about showing up in ads and that it`s really not. Is that -- is that because candidates don`t think it`s going to move the needle with undecided, what`s happening in terms of Democrats and them actually putting money into this message?\par \par \uc2 WU: Democrats I talked to saw a lot of the former president`s legal concerns that being things related to Mar-a-Lago, the New York Attorney General investigation, Fulton County in that kind of issue, as really being less of something that played well in swing districts and more of something that was an appeal for the base. It was something that would help them raise money. The difference, though, is that January 6, actually is something that we see show up on the trail in very candidate-specific cases.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen Democrats take out ads, for example, against Republicans that were at the Capitol on January 6. And just now I was watching a debate in Virginia`s second congressional district. They are not too far from Washington DC, where Congresswoman \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  is gotten a pretty tight reelection match against Jen Kiggans, a Republican.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Luria, is of course, on the January 6 committee and we saw in her closing argument, she actually tried to make her work part of her message to the constituent. She said that it would be one of the most important things that she`s done in her career as she was trying to prevent a clear and present danger to the country. And so this is where we`re more likely to see January 6 show up on the campaign trail in these individual races rather than in broader messaging.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, Nicholas Wu, great to have both of you on the program. Thank you so much. And don`t forget to join MSNBC for special coverage of the next January 6 hearing. That`s tomorrow starting at noon, Eastern on MSNBC, also available on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 And now to the escalating political controversy taking over Los Angeles right now, President Biden will be there later today after joining in with calls for the resignations of three city council members. Audio was released of them making racist and offensive remarks about their colleagues, constituents, and different LA communities. Councilman Mike Bonin, whose adopted black son was a target of hateful remarks, did not mince words about how painful this moment is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BONIN, LOS ANGELES CITY COUNCIL MEMBER: I take a lot of hits, and how I know I practically invite a bunch of them. But my son, man that makes my soul bleed, and it makes my temper burn. And I know I`m not alone because Los Angeles has spoken and feels the same way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC News Senior White House Correspondent Kelly O`Donnell. You saw the emotion there, that president decided he was going to weigh into this full throttle. What do we expect to hear from him today?\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I have to kind of characterize this. The president has not spoken about this himself directly. It has been through his press secretary. And she has said that the president`s view is that these members of the council should resign, so we will be watching to see how -- when he is in California, how much he wants to step into this directly. Of course, this is very much in line with the president`s views on matters like this.\par \par \uc2 And often, as you know, so well, Chris, when it comes to subjects that would be at a local city council level, very often that would not rise to the president`s involvement but this is the kind of issue that transcends a lot of American life. And it is the sort of corrosive kind of issue that is part of what brought \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  into the run for the White House that has him in office today because it`s not that far off from the soul of America point of view that has animated a lot of his reason for wanting to be president at the time that he decided to enter the race back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 So, the president will be in California. We have not been given a lot of details about what his West Coast swing will include in terms of how he will be engaged with some of the Democratic candidates who were on the ballot out there. But Los Angeles has a mayoral race, so he`ll be a part of that. He`ll be doing fundraising and he`ll be doing some official events on the way as well. He`s currently flying with Air Force One, traveling west as we speak, but his spokesperson, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  has made it clear that the president does have an opinion on this subject and feels very strongly about it, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kelly O`Donnell, always good to see you from the White House. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Right now, all around the Western world, Ukraine, giving us an inside view into harrowing rescues, like this one in the wake of Russian airstrikes. What President Zelenskyy says he now needs to stop it from happening again and again and again? We`re live in war-torn Kyiv next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:31]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A harrowing scene out of Ukraine today. This video released by Ukraine State Emergency Service that shows a family literally being pulled out of the rubble after being trapped in a cellar of their own home following a Russian missile attack.\par \par \uc2 NATO`s top leader is now calling for a scaled-up air defense to protect the skies of Ukraine after a wave of deadly strikes from Moscow devastated towns and homes like the one there in Zaporizhzhia. Right now, President Zelenskyy is meeting with global financial leaders about funding for Ukraine, even as more help is arriving from Western allies including a new air defense system from Germany, amid range air defense system on the way from the United States, President Biden saying the Russian leader "totally miscalculated this invasion and contingencies are ready should Moscow use tactical nuclear weapons."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: I don`t think he will. But I think it`s irresponsible of him to talk about it. Once you use a nuclear weapon, the mistakes that can be made, the miscalculations who knows what would happen.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN HOST: Have you asked the Pentagon to game it out, though? I mean, just in case?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The Pentagon didn`t have to be asked.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Cal Perry from Kyiv and Evelyn Farkas, Executive Director of the McCain Institute and the former Deputy Assistant Secretary of Defense for Russia, Ukraine and Eurasia.\par \par \uc2 Cal, I`ve seen you walking around Kyiv, you have seen firsthand what these missiles have done. How much of the people you`re talking to depending on the decisions that are being made right now with world financial leaders with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and other Western allies?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, the people of Ukraine right are relying on this. The people of Kyiv are relying on the air defense systems, which is what they`re talking about. You look at the church behind me, right? The church behind me is usually brightly lit. It is stunningly beautiful. This is a city that is intentionally dark tonight because this is a city that is right now living under air raid sirens. We are under an air raid alert. We`re hearing the distant thud of at least air defense systems going to work. So, it is something that people live with here. Every day it is all the more real because of what happened on Monday. And as you can see this damage on the left side of your screen, we`ve been watching people not just sort of try to repair and get life back to normal but to survey the damage, because this city has not felt this way really since April, since the Russians withdrew from the suburbs of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 And even then, we didn`t have air strikes like we do now. We spent the day watching children survey a playground that they played out up, their entire lives that was destroyed by these rockets. So, it has changed lives here. It is changed the way that people are going about their lives and of course the cost in blood has been very real. At least two dozen people were killed. More than 120 were wounded in just 48 hours across the country.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:17]\par \par \uc2 When you look at Zaporizhzhia, that gripping video that you played of that family coming out of that rubble, 70 people have died in just two weeks. So, these airstrikes are effective on infrastructure, and they are killing a great many people. Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cal, thank you so much for your ongoing reporting. We appreciate it. So, Evelyn, it`s not just \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who`s warning about Putin and warning Putin, frankly, the head of British intelligence, its spy chief made a rare speech yesterday calling Putin`s judgment flawed and I want to play another key section of that speech.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEREMY FLEMING, U.K. SPY CHIEF: Russia`s forces exhausted, the use of prisoners as reinforcements and now the mobilization of 10s of 1000s of inexperienced conscripts speaks of a really desperate situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s what we are increasingly hearing that it`s increasingly desperate on the part of the Russians. Was he speaking as much to Putin and the Russians as to the west? And is the real message here, though, maybe we have an opening to shut this down. Let`s be united and do it. What did you hear in that?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN FARKAS, THE MCCAIN INSTITUTE EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: I think that`s what I heard, Chris, that the Russians are weak, you know, they suffer from low morale, poor equipment, that`s insufficient and in need of repair. They can`t get some of those components for the more advanced weaponry from overseas because of the sanctions. They suffer from poor logistics, poor communications, they don`t have enough trained personnel, the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 So yes, the moment right now is to help the Ukrainians actually push the Russians out of their territory. And frankly, it`s long overdue for protecting civilians. As you and Cal pointed out, there have been 1000s of Ukrainians who have been killed and injured, innocent civilians, the Russians are not playing a clean war, if there`s ever a war that`s clean, which there isn`t. But they`re playing dirty, they`re breaking international law. And so, we need to really double down to force the military out and to protect the civilians.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So obviously, those are the goals, but you`re somebody who worked at defense, you know, this stuff, the asks from the Zelenskyy, can they help shorten the war, or end the war?\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: Well, they certainly can do all both things, Chris. Because the asks, you know, providing the -- he wants tanks, he wants artillery to take out Russian positions, all of those things will help push the Russians back and deaden the impact of their artillery, they will make the artillery neutral. So, the Russian -- so the Ukrainian forces can go forward. Everything he`s asking for will decrease the amount of time the Ukrainians have to fight in theory, if they can get it fast enough.\par \par \uc2 Putin, on the other hand, is counting on us being too slow and decisive not giving enough equipment, and he`s hoping to drag this out. So, it`s really in our interest to back Ukraine up and get as much as we can to them. The four air defense systems the Germans are providing that`s great. It`s not enough. They`re clearly more than four cities in Ukraine that need defending.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yeah, so Evelyn, the President also called \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , a rational actor who miscalculated this war. Where do you think nuclear weapons factor into the equation? Because there`s a lot of folks who think the more he`s backed into a corner, the more likely he is to do something irrational, is Biden right to think he won`t go there?\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: I think, Chris, that President Biden`s correct that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is a rational actor. Yes, he`s -- you know, he`s motivated by this Neo Imperial vision and to try to cement his place in history. But he`s rational. He`s not a crazy man. He is threatening the use of tactical nuclear weapons to try to intimidate, not so much the Ukrainians because I think he realizes he`s not going to intimidate them. But he`s trying to intimidate the west into halting or slowing our support to the Ukrainian government. It`s in the first instance about that, of course, you can`t rule out the possibility that he might use them because his doctrine does say that if the existence of the state is at stake, or if the military wants to try to defeat an adversary on the battlefield, they can use tactical nuclear weapons in the latter case, but he knows the next day as President Biden put it, the world will be very different. The international community will be up in arms and he likely won`t be able to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Evelyn Farkas, it`s always good to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The DOJ wants the Supreme Court to stay out of the Mar-a-Lago documents fight, the latest on that, and I`ll be talking to the author who argues it`s inevitable the Attorney General will indict former President Trump, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:44:05]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have breaking news, literally just in on the accused New York City subway shooter you`ll remember that 10 people were shot on the city subway, 13 others injured, while he was supposed to be in court today, Frank James. And he just didn`t want to go. So, when they told the judge that he was having none of it and said among other things in the courtroom. I am not tolerating that. I will tell him this is not a dress rehearsal. It`s real life. And I ordered that he be brought to the courtroom. So, the court is adjourned. They`re waiting for officials to go get him and make him leave where he is in lockup and show up in the courtroom. That could take a couple of hours. We`ll keep you posted on that.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, DOJ has made its case to the Supreme Court to stay out of the fight over documents that were seized at Mar-a-Lago arguing, among other things, that Trump has no plausible claim of privilege or for return to the documents. And while that fight escalates, the central question remains will Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  take the unprecedented step of seeking an indictment against the former president?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 A provocative new piece in The Atlantic suggests it`s inevitable and could come sooner than you might think. For more I want to bring in NBC`s Ken Dilanian and the author of that article Franklin Foer, National Correspondent for The Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Ken, 