 NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, two things have happened. I think, one is that, the Attorney General of the United States, you sit in this position where you can see everything that`s happened to American democracy and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , who is somebody who came into office as a very cautious consensus minded Institutionalists. I think that a lot of his faith in American institutions has begun to wobble as he`s seen this kind of grant assault on democracy. And I think that`s led to a bit of a shift within his own thinking where steps that he wouldn`t have wanted to take at the beginning of 2021 are steps that he`s now more willing to entertain.\par \par \uc2 But I think the second thing is, is that as the Mar-a-Lago case has filtered forward, you have Trump assaulting the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , accusing them of planting evidence, you have a case that unlike the January 6 investigation, which is a complicated one that`s much more black and white is very much about the rule of law, which is an animating principle for America. It`s the thing that he`s talked about, for all of his life. And so, the question of whether indict Trump over the Mar-a-Lago document of obstruction of justice, or whatever the charge is, then becomes a question about, does the rule of law apply to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? And I think in Merrick Garland`s mind, that won`t be a very, you know, it`d be hard to make that decision. But I think, as a matter of principle, as a matter of morality, it becomes much clearer.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And as a reader, I think you made a strong case that given his personality, and given the person he`s been throughout his career, it may be the only choice he can really make. I guess my question is, is there such a person in this day and age who can ignore the political pressures, stay true to his job and stay true to who he is?\par \par \uc2 FOER: Well, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has taken so much heat, we`ve had this split screen where you have the January 6 investigation on Capitol Hill, which has presented a very clear narrative of what happened and Garland has taken so much heat because he hasn`t told the same vivid story you have. The January 6 Commission makes it sound so simple, and Garland who is somebody who is meticulous who is all about procedure, who`s kind of considers himself immune to political rage, has moved in this very slow, step by step meticulous process of investigating the events of January 6, where he started with the people who stepped foot in the Capitol, and then kind of worked his way up the chain.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, according to reports from the Grand Jury is getting closer and closer to Trump`s inner circle, therefore, closer to Trump himself. I think that it`s really important to manage this case in a way that is above politics. That`s not -- that doesn`t reckon any sort of way of political revenge. And even if it`s excruciating for some people, you, know the case if it`s brought needs to be brought in a way that sticks, that gets the desired result and that isn`t half baked, that doesn`t set aside scope of getting evidence. And so, I think that`s the virtue of the way that Garland approached these issues.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:21]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I can recommend this article enough whether or not people agree with your conclusion the way you think about this and report this is so fascinating. Franklin Foer, thank you. Ken Dilanian, always good to have you on the program as well.\par \par \uc2 FOER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It may sound like a Hollywood sci-fi movie, but it is Israel as it gets, \uc2 NASA\uc2  now calling its asteroid strike test, a major success. Why that could one day save all of us here on Earth, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If a life-threatening asteroid is ever hurtling toward Earth, it looks like \uc2 NASA\uc2  has this covered. The agency says it`s saved the world test was a massive 