uments case. Late today, the Justice Department responded to Trump`s bid to get the High Court involved in the fight over classified documents recovered from his Florida club. One former prosecutor explained exactly what Trump`s legal advisors are trying to get done here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: It looks like the Trump folks want to get the classified documents included back within the group of documents. that magistrate Judge Raymond Dearie acting as a special master will be able to review.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re also learning more about Trump`s move from the White House to his club in Florida. NBC News has obtained some 165 pages of emails between the Trump White House staff and government officials about shipments specifically to Mar-a-Lago. Those emails also included images of these boxes on your screen, which the government says were packed and even repacked by Trump White House officials. Trump has falsely claimed government workers pack those boxes, implying they were responsible for the White House records ending up in Florida. Again, that claim is not true.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the January 6 committee is preparing for what may be its last hearing on Thursday. NBC News reports the Secret Service has given the committee more than 1 million electronic communications sent by agents before and on the day of the riot. The Secret Service spokesperson tells NBC News this, "While no additional text messages were recovered, we have provided a significant level of details from emails radio transmissions, Microsoft Teams chats and exhibits that address aspects of planning operations and communications."\par \par \uc2 We`re also following the January 6 Oath Keepers trial. Today was the start of week two of testimony. The group`s Leader Stewart Rhodes, and four other senior members are charged with seditious conspiracy. They`ve all pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 We get a lot to get to. So, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight. Jonathan Allen, Senior National Politics Reporter for NBC News, Luke Broadwater, Pulitzer Prize Winning Congressional Reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , and former Federal Prosecutor Glenn Kirschner.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, let`s start with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . What is he trying to get out of the Supreme Court and then explain the DOJ`s response?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: You know, he`s trying to get an assist. I think at the at the bottom of it all, Steph, he`s looking for more delay. Because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , when he goes to court, he`s always looking to run out the clock and push off his ultimate day of reckoning. So, what he`s trying to do is get the classified documents that, let`s call it what it is, he stole from the government, he had no right to take from the government. And you know, the DOJ brief sets out pretty compellingly that he has no ownership interest in the documents. He has no possessory interest in the documents. And they make clear the Judge Cannon`s order is their word, unprecedented, because she is trying to direct the executive branch, what they can and can`t do with their own classified materials, which is pretty extraordinary. So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, I think, at the bottom of it all, looking for more delay to push his ultimate day of reckoning down the road.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, here`s the thing, Luke, he usually gets it. We can make fun of it all night long. He`s running the clock. This is how he rolls. But for the most part, we`ve seen him get his way over and over. So, when are we going to actually hear from the Supreme Court on this?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Yeah, well, that`s a good point. I mean, now I will say the Supreme Court in a previous case regarding documents from the January 6 committee wanted from the archives did side with the January 6 committee all of the justices except for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . And so, if that has any insight into how they view to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his treatment of documents the types of things he wants to control and keep in his control and out of the government`s control. You know, maybe that is a good sign for the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:15]\par \par \uc2 But I agree with Glenn, this is about delaying and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has successfully delayed investigation after investigation. I mean, we just got Congress just got documents related to his taxes after like four or five years, I mean, it was, you know, these legal battles can go on forever. And so -- and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that if he can retake the White House, that he can make some of this, or he can try to make some of this stuff go away. So, he is definitely trying to delay this as long as possible.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, so that`s the thing, Jon, it may be reprehensible. But it works for Trump. So, we shouldn`t be surprised that he`s going right back to his move from his playbook. Does this seem like to you, this is just a Hail Mary play. He`s trying anything he possibly can?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER: Well, I think Luke brings up a great point that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is president, and again, all of these things go away. And if he can delay the adjudication of all of these things, for long enough to get elected president, that that means he can wipe the slate clean, whether that`s because he appoints new people at the Justice Department through appointing new people, the judiciary, or of course, the ultimate act of pardoning himself.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but hold on, Jon. That`s his only defense. We have not heard, it has been weeks and weeks and weeks now, since the search of Mar-a-Lago and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyers have yet to say why he took the documents, why he lied about them, why he kept them. And his only defense is going to be, I`m going to run the clock, because maybe I`m going to be president, what does that tell you about what he may have really done here, like he`s gotten nothing else?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Well, let`s look at this playground fight that`s going on. Here`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who takes the government documents and says, well, they`re mine. He seems to believe that possession is actually 10 tenths of the law. He takes the things from the White House to belong to the American public and just screams they`re mine, they`re mine, they`re mine. And it turns out that there is, in fact, a justice system. And there are judges and we`ll see what the Supreme Court has to say on this very narrow issue. But in order for him to prevail on this question, it seems pretty obvious that he`s going to have to be in a place to control those levers of power rather than relying on other people who can actually read the law.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s be clear, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says they`re mine, they`re mine, they`re mine on television, and on radio shows. He never says it under oath in a court of law, because he knows they`re not his.\par \par \uc2 The Atlantic has a piece out called the inevitable indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And I want to share with the author said earlier tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN FOER, THE ATLANTIC STAFF WRITER: In contrast to January 6, which is a very complicated case, the Congressional Commission has portrayed things in a very clear sort of way, but they`re not making a case that can stand up and a court of law, but the Mar-a-Lago case, I think is much more black and white. It relates to a very core principle that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , it`s just central to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , which is the rule of law that there`s nobody in this country who stands above the law. I think it is inevitable that he brings an indictment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 