e able to plausibly say, well, if Trump had committed crimes, you know, the Department of Justice would have indicted him. So, I guess this is a playbook that we can replicate. And I don`t believe America can withstand another four years, like the four years we just suffered.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Luke, we are less than 48 hours from what is likely going to be the final January 6 hearing. What kind of evidence can we see on Thursday?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Well, the Committee is keeping this pretty close to the vest so far. We are picking up a little bit of information. We know that there will be some, at least one or two surprises, and we`ve been promised some significant new information. We know we`re going to hear more about \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 . We know that they`ve obtained this evidence from these dangerous filmmakers that have trailed him for weeks, including on January 6. And we know that Stone was in close contact with several Oath Keepers and Proud Boys who are charged now with felonies for conspiracy to commit sedition against the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re definitely going to hear more about that. We probably will hear more about the Secret Service text messages you mentioned earlier. We might hear more about discussions about the 25th amendment. We know some cabinet members are having those. And I do think we`re going to hear more about continuing threats to democracy. But there is one overriding message from the January 6 committee. And that is there`s no one else to blame for January 6, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I do think they`re going to try to recenter the country`s focus back on what he did on January 6, and in the build up to January 6.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but let`s talk about those Secret Service messages. Because at first when I first read, we`re going -- they retrieved a million electronic messages that seemed like a wow, like, oh, my gosh, all those deleted messages. Now we`re getting them. We`re not getting any of the deleted messages. We`re getting the information that was already out there put together. So, doesn`t that leave a big giant hole? Isn`t the money in the deleted stuff, John?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Yeah, I mean, obviously, you`d like to have access to the deleted stuff if it was available if you`re an investigator, and certainly the public would like to have access to that, at the same time, the connection of dots is always very important to telling the story of what happened. And I think that that`s something that not only is the January 6 committee interested in, but if the Justice Department does eventually move forward with charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they will be very interested in pulling out the narrative and in crafting the narrative and putting those dots together and making it easier for the public to understand.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are we just going to roll over on this, Luke, that like, well, the messages were deleted, they`re never coming back. We`re talking about the Secret Service, they don`t have a backup system, something more foolproof, right? When I worked in investment banking, they kept every single phone record for seven years, all of our notebooks for seven years, I can delete all my emails here at NBC. And I assure you, when and if I get in trouble, this company will retrieve them. So, is the American people really -- are we really supposed to believe? Well, gosh, darn it Secret Service deleted and just can`t get them back?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: You know, it is shocking to me, I`m not a tech guy. Early on in this investigation, there was some belief that some sort of, you know, tech wizards could recover these things that were deleted. But so far, they haven`t been able to. And I`m not exactly sure how this all this stuff works. But in the changing over of the phones, they have not been able to recover any of these text messages from January 5, and January 6 that the Committee really wants.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, was