\par \uc2 SWISHER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, she is a mid-term influencer, have you heard that before? Dosing out partisan takes in small digestible bites for people who don`t actually pay attention to politics or do something more shocking? Watch the news, the one-time congressional insider behind, Emily in Your Phone, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:41]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMILY AMICK, ATTORNEY: I don`t need to tell you what`s on the line this midterm election. It`s abortion. It`s democracy. But what you might not know is that Republicans are expected to win. It`s gotten closer in recent weeks. But statistically, statistically, Republicans should gain 27 seats. That`s the average the non-presidential party gains in a midterm election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Our next guest is drawing back the curtain on politics one \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post at a time. And as a former counsel to Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , she knows what she is posting about. You might not know her name, but you should, because what she`s doing is important. And that`s why we wanted you to meet her.\par \par \uc2 With me now, Emily Amick of the \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account, Emily in Your Phone. Emily, I want to start with how you ended up doing this, right? You weren`t like a frustrated person at home with like all the misinformation out there and said, I`m going to start making videos. You want a big job on the Hill working for \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , and you walked away from it to make 62nd tutorials on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . Why did you do this?\par \par \uc2 AMICK: Yeah, you know, during the 2016 election cycle, like many people, I spent a lot of time on social media. And more than just looking at pictures of people`s cute pets, which I did a lot of. I saw that conservative influencers were growing these huge followings. And they were developing these communities with tremendous trust for everything they had to say about politics. And it was targeted women. And it was using the vernacular of the platform. And it was connecting with these women. And I didn`t see Democrats doing it. And I said, you know, rather than just complain about what I think people aren`t doing, I`ll do it myself.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, what these are, you are doing this sort of school of rock style, you know, without the rhyming or songs, tutorials for us. What is the impact been? Who`s the audience? How important is this? Sad to me that some people don`t watch the news. But shockingly, they don`t.\par \par \uc2 AMICK: Shockingly, they don`t all watch the news. And people are looking for a way to bring the news into their home. You know, when they have a second while the baby is moving between the bath and the bed, things like that. They just have short moments to consume news. So, it`s a lot of moms, most of my followers are women in their 30s but not all. And, you know, they are folks who are looking also for ways to take action. They want to take a little bit of their time and they want to contribute to big political change and that`s another thing that I try to let them know, give them some ideas how they can do that.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:03]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are you talking to the Democratic Party about them taking a bigger role in doing things like this? You`re doing this independently, right? In the last month, we saw the White House, hire a new social media leader from the state of New Jersey, and we`ve seen that account go full savage, does the whole party need to do more of this, especially around their messaging?\par \par \uc2 AMICK: Yeah, you know, I think that a thing that Democratic Party really needs to invest more into is messengers, people who have relationships of trust with different niche communities, and can speak to the interests, the wants, the needs of those specific communities. And there`s definitely people who want full savage White House and like I LOL, I thought it was hilarious. But there`s room for people like me who are doing something different, who are giving facts who are explaining policy issues, and who are giving folks targeted actions they can take on the hill.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you think Democrats have an effective messaging strategy right now, because Republicans, whether or not what they`re saying is true, they pick a message and they go over and over, right? You could turn on Fox News anytime of the day, any day of the week, and they`re just going to yell, immigration, inflation crime over and over and over. It`s like, there`s no other stories out there. But that`s their story. And they`re sticking to it. How do you think Democrats are doing on messaging?\par \par \uc2 AMICK: Look, the Democratic Party is a big tent party, right? And it is what it is, and we can`t fight it. We can`t be a homogenous Republican Party. So, we have to work with what we have. And I think there`s a lot of beauty in the diversity. And I think, you know, we have two really big challenges this midterm cycle. We have to, one, explain to people what we - - who we aren`t, right? Like, we aren`t going to end democracy. We aren`t going to enact a national abortion ban. And then we`ve explained who we are. And that`s the hard part that sometimes we don`t always do where, you know, going to choose people over corporations, we`re going to fight climate change, because we think it`s real. We are going to focus on helping parents by supporting paid leave, by supporting public schools, all these different things that we are offering, folks. You know, John Fetterman, just put out a really great ad today, talking about how he`s going to go after massive profits that corporations are earning on our backs. And I think that`s the type of values forward messaging that we really need to lead into this cycle.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Messaging Emily, that is your jam, which is getting it done. Thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 AMICK: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I really appreciate it. Our special thanks to Emily in The Phone, I can tell you, she`s made me a lot smarter with a lot of her posts.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, from her performances as a conniving, controlling KGB mom, to an absolutely delightful singing, dancing teapot. We remember the great Angela Lansbury, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:25]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you hear that music? If you are my age or older, you certainly know it. The last thing before we go tonight, remembering Angela Lansbury. The legendary "Murder, She Wrote" star died in her home in Los Angeles today at the extraordinary age of 96. She was just five days shy of her 97th birthday. Her acting career spanned almost eight decades. That`s 80 years. Captivating audiences in so many memorable roles on stage and screen, our won Anne Thompson looks back at her amazing life and her storied career.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Angela Lansbury defied typecasting.\par \par \uc2 ANGELA LANSBURY, TELEVISION LEGENDARY STAR: Life is war, don`t count the casualties.\par \par \uc2 I was a young character actress, and these days that word is a dirty word and our business character actress.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Her first two movie roles garnered Oscar nominations, but in 1962 is the mentoring candidate, Lansbury was unforgettable as Lauren`s Harvey`s manipulative mother.\par \par \uc2 LANSBURY: Why don`t you pass the time by playing little Solitaire?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: In real life she was just three years older than Harvey.\par \par \uc2 LANSBURY: I think Manchurian Candidate was probably the best thing that I ever did on film.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Frustrated that Hollywood was making her old before her time, Lansbury went to Broadway and to career making roll Mame.\par \par \uc2 LANSBURY: I`m not like Mame at all, but I love playing her. Because for the first time in my life, I was admired as a woman.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: She won five Tony Awards, and this year, a Lifetime Achievement Award. Born in England, Lansbury married twice and had three children. Her remarkable range kept her career strong as she aged.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: A dozen seasons is Jessica Fletcher on TV`s Murder, She Wrote.\par \par \uc2 LANSBURY: One of you adolescent, please, tell me what`s happening. And Mrs. Potts in Beauty and the Beasts.\par \par \uc2 LANSBURY: True as it can be.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Leaving a legacy of characters that were just sensational. Anne Thompson NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How lucky are we to have been alive at the same time as the great Angela Lansbury. She will not be forgotten. And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. I am so grateful to be back here in this chair with you.\par \par \uc2 And from all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, I want to thank you for staying up late with us, and I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221012eiab00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Mehdi Hasan, Alex Wagner \par \uc2 7,969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". And don`t miss "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" at 9:00 p.m. She`s going to be joined by former DC Police officer \uc2 Michael Fanone\uc2  who was injured in a January 6 attack on the Capitol. Plus, Rachel Maddow joins Alex on her new podcast Ultra. That`s awesome. But first, you get Chris Hayes, and he starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHDI HASAN, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN. Mar-a-Lago meets the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 JOY VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: This amounts to an absolutely unprecedented effort to interfere with a criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Tonight, what we`re learning about today`s important new DOJ filing and will trump Supreme Court cronies help him obstruct a criminal investigation?\par \par \uc2 Then, a surprise Republican Senate seat suddenly in trouble. Mike Lee`s independent challenger joins me live.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Senator Bernie Sanders on why he says control of Congress will come down to an economic message.\par \par \uc2 And Rebecca Traister on the campaign trail recovery of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and the TV doctor attacking him for his health.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Now, after having that stroke, I really understand, you know, much more kind of the challenges that Americans have day in and day out.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: ALL IN starts now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN (on camera): Good evening from Washington, DC. I`m Mehdi Hasan in for Chris Hayes. Whatever you think of the ex-president and his brazen lies and his ludicrous lawyers and his alleged crimes, Donald Trump`s strategy is kind of working. Look, it is undeniably the case that he improperly took extremely classified documents and stashed them at his Florida home slash resort. Those documents reportedly even included nuclear secrets, some of the most closely guarded information the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  holds. And then, Trump`s lawyers apparently misled the government about the nature and number of those documents.\par \par \uc2 None of that is really in question. But the thing is, this isn`t August, and we are not talking anymore about the classified documents scandal itself. Instead, we are talking about the legal process by which the documents will be reviewed, which is what Trump wants. Today, the Department of Justice had to release a formal response to Trump`s own desperate appeal to the Supreme Court over those pilfered documents.\par \par \uc2 We will get to that response in a moment, but first, I think it is worth taking a look at all the steps Trump has taken to distract the public and the media and the Democrats leading up to this latest development. After Trump workshopped a number of outlandish lies including the claim that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted the top secret documents at Mar-a-Lago, he decided to do what he always does, sue. So, he went judge shopping to see if he could find a sycophant within our judicial system who would tie themselves into legal knots to give him the outcome, the cover he wanted, and they paid off.\par \par \uc2 Trump was assigned right-wing Judge Aileen Cannon whom he himself appointed and who was confirmed by the Senate after he lost the last election. Cannon, a \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  member, you`ll be shocked to hear, quickly went to work dismantling basic legal logic on behalf of the guy who put her on the bench. Democrats take note, this is why the federal judiciary is so important. And it is why Republicans like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  have dedicated their political careers to reshaping it in their image for at least the next generation. Because the law sadly tends to be whatever an unelected judge decides it is, which is how you end up with a judge like Aileen Cannon issuing a legally dubious ruling appointing a special master to review the documents at Trump`s request, essentially blocking the executive branch via the Department of Justice from reviewing its own documents.\par \par \uc2 Now, the government appealed that ruling which legal experts called laughably bad and untethered from the law, and a Federal Court of Appeals seemed to agree essentially blocking part of it from taking effect. But Trump was not satisfied getting most of what he wanted, so he put his lawyers to work again, this time appealing all the way to the Supreme Court. In fact, the appeal was written in such a narrow and specific way, Constitutional Law Professor Steve Vladeck posited, "Trump demanded that his lawyers take this case to the Supreme Court, and they found the one argument they could make that was not factually or legally frivolous."\par \par \uc2 Now, you might be asking why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted to take this case all the way to the Supreme Court. Well, there is this one key rule on the Supreme Court where individual justices handle emergency appeals from specific district courts. And the Justice overseeing the 11th circuit whose decision Trump is appealing is none other than \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, in addition to being arguably the most conservative justice on the court, Thomas is also the husband of far-right activist Ginni Thomas who in addition to pushing bonkers QAnon conspiracy theories to Trump White House officials, also personally work to try and help facilitate Trump`s attempted coup, including participating directly in that possibly criminal fake elective scheme. Ginni Thomas also says things like this witch make it clear she and her husband are in the tank for Trump.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:23]\par \par \uc2 GINNI THOMAS, WIFE OF JUSTICE \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 : I am happy to be here. But my husband told me that the President asked about me twice, so I`m wearing my Trump button, we need -- hey, it is the time to be behind whoever is our president because there`s a war for our country and the things that we believe in going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Now, despite the obvious conflict of interest, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has so far refused to recuse himself from cases involving the ex-president in January 6. And this latest appeal is no exception. Earlier this month, Thomas told the Department of Justice, it had to file a formal response to Trump`s appeal, and the DOJ did so just a few hours ago. In that response, the Department of Justice make clear just how ridiculous it finds this entire process.\par \par \uc2 "Because Trump has no plausible claims of ownership, or privilege in the documents bearing classification markings, he will suffer no harm at all from a temporary stay of the special masters review of those materials while the government appeal -- while the government`s appeal proceeds." But as we have seen before the merits of the DOJ`s argument may end up having very little impact on Justice Thomas` ruling, because in this case, the law means whatever he says it means.\par \par \uc2 Let`s be clear. The only reasons Trump hasn`t been indicted by now is because he`s a former president and because he has handpicked judges on his side, which is a point that former prosecutor Joyce Vance echoed on this show last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: You`re a former prosecutor. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he was just an ordinary person, not a former president, not Trump, there would be no special master, there would be no supreme court involvement, he would already be indicted by now, wouldn`t he?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think that`s correct.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: In a new article for The Atlantic, journalist Franklin Foer who spent months studying and interviewing Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , comes to the conclusion that Garland ultimately will indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . "That`s what Garland tried to emphatically explain over the past months. Every time he`s asked about the former president, he responds, no one is above the law. He clearly gets frustrated that his answer fails to satisfy his doubters. I believe that his indictment of Trump will prove that he means it."\par \par \uc2 But if he doesn`t indict Trump, a lot of Americans will be asking whether the rule of law still exist in this country, whether everyone is equal before the law, or whether some people living in resort homes in Florida are just above the law. Asha Rangappa is a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent and is now an attorney and senior lecturer at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 . Harry Litman is a former U.S. attorney and former Deputy Assistant Attorney General at the DOJ. They join me live.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for coming on the show. Harry, let me start with you. Franklin Foer says he thinks \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is going to indict Trump. Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, that is a $64,000 question. I do agree. And he`s right is fair to say -- to really distinguish between Garland`s view of himself as a judge and as a prosecutor. What he says though, is at the end of the day, the politics will be so roiled on either side, he will just consider the evidence only.\par \par \uc2 I actually think he won`t let himself off so easy. He`ll consider everything, including the politics, but I think that factor has now begun to weigh strongly in favor of indictment because Trump has been so brazen and the overall effect of not indicting would be such disrespect for the rule of law. That would be my best guest.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And Asha, a lot of liberals are sharing Franklin Foer`s Atlantic piece today. But Foer was also rather bullish, we should remember, on the Russia probe into Trump which didn`t go anywhere legally. I`m reminded of that since deleted tweet that goes well, I`d like to see old \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wriggle his way out of this jam. Trump wriggles his way out of the jam easily. Oh, well, nevertheless. What do you make of the kind of optimism of some that Trump is going to get indicted, that he is going to be held to account?\par \par \uc2 ASHA RANGAPPA, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Well, Mehdi, I think that the Mar- a-Lago case is very unique and different than the kinds of shenanigans that Trump has engaged in before because it`s quite straightforward and it`s fairly simple. This is a stolen property case. He took records, classified records that were not his, he kept them in a place where he was not authorized to have them, and he lied about whether he had given it all back.\par \par \uc2 This is something that people understand. People can understand the national security implications of it. And it`s something that other people have been pressing accounted for far less, quite frankly. And so, I think that it`s a case that Garland can sell to the public they can get their minds around it. They understand why someone would have to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 And importantly, I think from an evidentiary perspective, this is very straightforward in the sense that these rolls Trump`s actions. He actually pretty much confesses to it on his social media platform every day. And we don`t need to get into conversations he had with all these other people to get to his state of mind the way that we do for conspiracy charges, for example. So, I do think that is a very strong case to make.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:38]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I`m glad -- I`m glad you`ve laid it out so straightforwardly, because as I was pointing out in the introduction, it is straightforward but he`s managed to muddy the legal waters. And Harry, what do you make of this latest DOJ response today? They make it pretty clear they think this latest Trump appeal to the Supreme Court is totally unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Look, the whole satellite litigation with Cannon is unnecessary. And as you said, at the top -- and by the way, in their filing, they trash her. It`s real indecorous SG language, but they really assail the ruling of Cannon.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I think. You know, I clerked on the court. And one of the things clerks do is actually write memos and say, should you take a case? I am very confident that 10 out of 10, 100 of 100 clerks would say the following if they got this stay application. There`s a teeny little jurisdictional issue in there. But as the SG says, Trump hasn`t begun to show that he he`s irreparably harmed which you need to grant a stay.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, this is a bad case to bring up. This teeny Picayune issue therefore deny. That`s -- without a doubt what would happen in a routine case, and here I think as well. It would be interesting -- it will be interesting to see if one or two, maybe three justices say something in his favor, but it is real -- it would be such a departure from how they normally handle cases for them to accept it. But I think even for this Supreme Court, it is a bridge too far. I think in fairly short order, they will deny the motion -- his motion for a stay.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Key keywords there, Harry, "even for this Supreme Court." We know that the Supreme Court is capable of literally ending.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: You got to get to five, five for a stay.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Asha, Trump has -- Asha, Trump has -- fair enough. Asha, Trump has time and time again, admitted he took the documents. You mentioned earlier, he confesses on his social media platform. He confessed at a rally over the weekend. Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I had a small number of boxes in storage at Mar-a-Lago guarded by Secret Service and my people and everybody. I mean, it`s safe. There is no crime. It`s not a crime. And they should give me immediately back everything that they`ve taken from because it`s mine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Asha, do you think any of his multiple lawyers ever tell him to stop confessing to crimes in public?\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: I`m sure they do. And we`ve seen lawyers leave because he`s a pretty bad client. But you know, he`s making these claims in the court of public opinion and they don`t fly in a court of law. And I think with regard to his appeal to the Supreme Court, it is quite technical, and it is very narrow. And in fact, the relief that he`s seeking, it`s not really clear how it`s going to move the ball for him except on this one point that if the Supreme Court does bite, what it would result in is that the government would have to turn over classified documents back to the special master four review, which would mean that not only the special master, but Trump`s lawyers would be able to access them.\par \par \uc2 So, I think number one, we have to ask, why does he want that so badly? And, you know, I think that we need to consider whether the gameplay here is to force the government`s hand to make a choice to say, well, these are so sensitive, we don`t want to turn them over, maybe we just need to drop the case. This is -- this is called grey mail. I don`t think that will happen because Garland has gone too far down the road. But I can`t see why else he would be pursuing, as Harry said, this really on winning argument on this very narrow technical jurisdictional point.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Very good points. I wish we had more time to discuss this. We`ll have to leave it there. Asha Rangappa, Harry Litman, thank you both for your analysis. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Coming up, after the conservative Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, pro-abortion protests spread across the country. But with exactly four weeks until Election Day, Senator Bernie Sanders is now warning Democrats not to only run on abortion rights. And Senator Sanders joins me next to explain why.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: There are 28 days left until the Midterms and it is clear from the polling that abortion rights and the economy are two of the major issues dominating the race. In fact, until the right-wing Supreme Court took away national abortion rights with their decision and Dobbs, inflation had pretty much been driving all of the political conversation.\par \par \uc2 But now, there is a divide between those two issues and how each party is performing on them. If you care most about the economy, you are more likely to vote Republican. And if you care more about most about abortion, you are more likely to vote Democrat. But one senator is calling out this disconnect and arguing that Democrats will not just win on abortion or just lose on the economy. Senator Bernie Sanders, Independent of Vermont, writes in The Guardian that Democrats need to focus more on a pro-worker agenda, saying, "while the abortion issue must remain on the front burner, it would be political malpractice for Democrats to ignore the state of the economy and allow Republican lies and distortions to go unanswered.\par \par \uc2 Senator Sanders is a former Democratic presidential candidate, and he joins me now. Thank you so much for coming on the show. Senator, President Biden has a pretty amazing, unprecedented record on job creation. Gas prices are ticking up after a 99 day fall because of \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , Saudi Arabia, Ukraine, not because of him. So, what does he -- what are Democrats supposed to do to win people over on the economy? Republicans have the lead on this issue. It might be unfair --\par \par \uc2 [20:20:40]\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): That`s right. Mehdi --\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Is there anything Democrats can do to change that?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, it`s not a question of being unfair, it`s totally absurd. You have a Republican Party who on every single issue impacting working families is on the wrong side. And yet what polls show, it says that people think that Republicans are going to do a better job for them than Democrats. So, what we have got to do is A, of course, deal with the abortion issue. What the Supreme Court date is totally outrageous. Of course, we have to go after Republicans who are trying to undermine our democracy that people fought and died to preserve.\par \par \uc2 But what we have got to do is take the attack Republicans on the economy. Don`t be on the defensive. You got 60% of the people in this country who are living paycheck to paycheck. Millions are working for starvation wages. I introduced legislation for a $15 an hour minimum wage. I got zero Republicans. We`re the only major country on Earth not to have paid family and medical leave. We need at least 12 weeks. Zero Republicans support that. Only major country not that universal health care. No Republicans support that.\par \par \uc2 At a time of massive income and wealth inequality, the American people want billionaires to stop paying their fair share of taxes. No Republicans support that. So, what I am saying is, you got to go on the offensive. These people have phonies. They are corporate -- you know, w1orking for corporations. We have got to take the fight to them.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And Senator, I think a lot of people watching at home will nod their heads with you and say go on the offensive against the Republicans who are the party of the tax dodgers and the billionaires. But you also write in your guardian piece, and I quote, I am alarmed to hear the advice that many Democratic candidates are getting from establishment consultants, that the closing argument of Democrats should focus only on abortion. Your critics Senator would say that your argument is a straw man that no Democrat is actually running only on abortion or assuming that they can win, only focusing on abortion. What is your response to them?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, of course, you know, and if you read the article, it make that clear. All that I`m saying is when the American people in poll after poll think that the economy, thinks that inflation are the two major issues, you cannot ignore those issues. And in fact, not only should we not ignore those issues, we should make it clear to working families throughout this country, many of whom were prepared to vote Republican, that if they vote Republican, they`re voting for a party, which is on every single major issue economically is against their interests. That`s the fight that we have to make.\par \par \uc2 And let me repeat again. I think that Supreme Court decision on abortion is a total outrage. This is an issue, we must continue to stay focused on. The overwhelming majority of the American people understand that it is a woman who has the right to control her body, not state government. So, I`m not suggesting don`t focus on that issue, but I`m suggesting get on the offensive on economics as well.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, as you say, abortion, big issue, economy, big issue. Let me ask you this. What about preserving democracy? Shouldn`t that be the biggest issue that all of you run on against a fascistic anti-Democratic Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, it is obviously. I mean, you have a right-wing party now, tragically. We`re not debating health care. We`re not debating climate change. I mean, the major issue in many ways is whether or not we preserve American democracy. Unbelievable, but that is where we are right now. So, of course, that has got to be an issue.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re talking about what you do in the last month, you`re asking yourself, How do I win the damn election? What are the issues that are getting me votes? How do I get votes away from Republicans into progressive Democrats, into Democrats? And you know, that`s kind of what I`m focusing on right now.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Senator, last night was the Ohio Senate debate where Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  had this to say about President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you want President Biden to run again in 2024?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH). No, I`ve been very clear. I`d like to see a generational change. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the president, everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , very clear there in his view. Are you going to run again in 2024 considering you`re a year older than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? You recently said you haven`t ruled it out.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: You know, I don`t think the American people stay -- I don`t think the American people are staying up nights wondering whether I`m going to run or not. What they all worried about is whether they`re going to have health care, whether they`re going to be able to send their kids to college, whether we`re going to deal with climate change.\par \par \uc2 Let`s focus on the issues of importance to the American people. Let`s get out there. Let`s knock on doors. Let`s make the phone calls. Let`s raise the money. Let us make sure that Democrats continue to control the House in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:24]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: But you`re not ruling it out.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Right now, my focus is on doing everything that I can, and I`ll be going around the country to make sure that Democratic House members win and Democratic senators win. That should be the focus of everybody right now.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: OK, so one of the big issues is gas prices. Saudi Arabia has been blamed for the rise in prices at the pump. White House official said today that the President is reevaluating the U.S. relationship with Saudi Arabia, but you`ve been very critical of that relationship. Would you support tougher action against the Saudis, pulling American troops out of the country, ending arms deals?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: How far would you go?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: You got it. Keep going. Look, you have a monarchy who treats women as third-class citizens, torture people, they killed Khashoggi, dismembered his body. And now they are siding with Putin in the war in Ukraine and are forcing Americans and people all over the world to buy higher gas prices. So, you know, this is a regime, to tell you the truth, I have never been fond of. And I think the President is moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Senator Bernie Sanders, we will have to leave it there. Thank you so much for your time. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Still ahead. He`s a former Republican challenging a sitting Republican senator and the race is much closer than you might think. Utah Independent Senate candidate Evan McMullin joins me live. That`s next. Don`t go away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Imagine you are a Republican senator running in a tight race for reelection, and your opponent who is closing on closing in on you in the polls is not a Democrat, he`s an independent. And to cap it all off, six years ago, your opponent ran for president and you voted for him.\par \par \uc2 I`m talking about the great State of Utah where incumbent Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  is being challenged by Independent and ex-Republican Evan McMullin. You will remember McMullin, a former undercover CIA officer, ran for president as a third-party never-Trump candidate in 2016. But Lee has gone from opposing Trump and backing McMullen six years ago to becoming a hardcore Trump loyalist, going as far as trying to help the ex-President pull off his attempted coup.\par \par \uc2 Lee texted Trump Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to help get crazy conspiracy lawyer \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  in to see Trump and asked Meadows in late November 2020 "Please tell me what I shouldn`t be saying as Team Trump worked to overturn the will of the people. With less than a month to the midterm elections, McMullin is convinced Democrats not to run a candidate in this deep red state, essentially ensuring a one on one matchup with Senator Lee, and that may be paying off.\par \par \uc2 A new poll released yesterday shows well and trailing Lee by just four points 37 to 41 in a state Lee won six years ago by more than 40 points. And right now, 12 percent of Utah`s voters are still undecided. Joining me now, Evan McMullin, the Independent Senate candidate running against Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  in Utah. Thanks so much for coming on the show. Let me start by asking you this. Do you think Senator Lee will be voting for you again next month?\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN (I-UT), SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, great to be with you, Mehdi. I don`t think so. I think this time, he may -- he may -- might -- he might make another decision. But I`ll tell you this, he should vote for me because in 2016, he voted for his principles. He stood by those principles in order to warn the country about an authoritarian threat from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He called on Trump to leave the race after the Access Hollywood tape, he encouraged me in private discussion to run against President Trump. And then of course, as you mentioned, on election day, he voted for me.\par \par \uc2 But since then, of course, he decided that it was more advantageous to cater to the extremes and to get on board with the rise of extremism that I believe threatened and still believe threatens the country. And then, by the time 2020 rolled around, Senator Lee was willing to even sacrifice our Constitution and his oath to it in order for him and his allies, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and his other political allies to hold on to power despite the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 And he was trying to recruit people to overturn our democracy. And that`s one of the many reasons he needs to be replaced.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: In doing so, would you go as far as saying he violated his oath of office, he violated the Constitution, he violated the law?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I think he violated his oath, certainly. And I think he violated the Constitution. I think he violated and abandoned the sacrifices of 14 generations of Americans who have worked so hard to create what we have in this country. And I know it`s not perfect. We`ve got a long ways to go to more perfectly realize our values of liberty and equality.\par \par \uc2 But my ancestors fought in the Revolutionary War. They trekked across the plains in the Rockies to flee religious persecution in order to arrive in the state we now call Utah almost 200 years ago. And Senator Lee representing this state now was willing to sacrifice all of that for his own power and for the power of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , someone who had lost an election, someone who had been removed by the will of the people but who wanted to stay in power regardless. Senator Lee tried to assist that effort. And it is an egregious betrayal of even the American idea.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:20]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, 12 percent of voters in Utah say they are undecided still. There`s 28 days to go until Election Day. How do you convince those undecided Utah voters to ditch \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  and go with you, particularly Democrats in that state who don`t have a candidate running, maybe nervous about your formally right-wing politics on issues like the economy and abortion?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Well, what unites us all is, is our commitment to our core ideals that we`re all created free and equal, and therefore, we have a system of self-government, a democratic republic in which we select our own leaders, and they remain accountable to us. And when we vote them out, they go. You know, that`s what we`re all committed to. We`re committed to truth and to reason.\par \par \uc2 And we share a lot of common ground and overcoming major challenges of the day, whether it`s inflation or the high cost of health care or protecting the environment. I could go on and on. But this coalition is united around our core ideals and a lot of common ground on those serious issues. As far as the undecideds are concerned, thankfully, they lean against Lee. Most of them, supposed Lee. They`re looking for an alternative.\par \par \uc2 And so, the name of the game in this race or for the next four weeks for us is reaching those independents, because we know we have a message that resonates with them. It`s all about reaching them, which is why we invite everyone to come in and support the effort at evanmcmullin.com across Utah and across the country and we need -- we need everyone`s help in the last four weeks.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I know you`ve said in the past, you`re running as an independent, and you`ve said in the past that you don`t plan to caucus with either party in the Senate if you get elected next month. But I have to ask, either the Republicans or the Democrats will control the Senate come January, which party do you want that to be? Who will a Senator McMillan be supporting to be Senate Majority Leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  or \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Well, I`m going to be an Independent and I`m not going to caucus with either side. So, both sides are going to choose their leaders, I won`t have a vote in either party`s decision-making process on who leads them. I will say beyond that, that my priorities are defending American democracy, and standing up to the extremes and threaten it, lowering health care costs, lowering inflation, and protecting our environment.\par \par \uc2 In Utah, we face many environmentally related challenges, a lack of water and air quality. Those are my priorities. So, I`m going to work with senators who are going to allow me to make progress --\par \par \uc2 HASAN: But just -- sorry, sorry. Just to jump in and be specific before we run out of time. If it`s 50, Republican senators, 49, Democrats and you, control of the Senate will depend on you. What will you do in that situation?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Well, we`ll see if it comes down to that. But I`m going to put Utah`s interests first. I`m going to work with members of both parties to get things done on those priorities. My number one priority is defending American democracy, and lowering health care costs, lowering inflation, and protecting our environment. And I`m going to be in coalition with other senators who are going to allow me to get that done.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll have conversations when I get there. If it comes down to me, I`ll have conversations and we`ll see which will allow me --\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I`m pretty sure --\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: -- which will allow me --\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I`m pretty sure if you`re number one issue -- Evan McMullin, I`m pretty sure if your number one issue is defending democracy, you won`t be able to build a coalition with those Republican senators, but we`ll have to leave it there. Thank you so much for your time. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Still to come, we knew House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy was a craven hack, but now we`re learning the extent to which McCarthy is willing to compromise any morals he might have. That`s next. You don`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: On January 6, 2021, as the armed mob descended on the Capitol threatening the lives of the members inside chanting hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  while gallows stood on the grounds, House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy finally got through on the phone to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He told the then-president to call off the riot. And Trump reportedly responded, "Well, Kevin, I guess these people are more upset about the election than you are."\par \par \uc2 Today, we learned what McCarthy reportedly said back to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . According to a new book by journalist Robert Draper, Leader McCarthy yelled, "More upset? They`re trying to effing kill me." So, that is how Kevin McCarthy actually felt on January 6 when he believed his life was in danger. But weirdly, that is not the tone we have heard from Kevin McCarthy since over the past 18 months.\par \par \uc2 Leader McCarthy has downplayed that armed insurrection and attacked Democrats and the committee investigating the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Democrats are using January 6 to avoid accountability. Speakers Pelosi Select Committee on January 6 is unlike any other committee in American history. In fact, it is the most political and least legitimate committee in American history.\par \par \uc2 There is nothing that I can provide the January 6 Committee for legislation of they`re moving forward. There is nothing in that realm. It is pure politics.\par \par \uc2 One thing I know is that since Nancy has appointed this political committee, gas has gone up $1.86.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:03]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And that is just what he has said in public. A few weeks after January 6, Kevin McCarthy went crawling down to Mar-a-Lago to suck up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and pose for this smiling photo. We now know thanks to Robert Draper`s reporting that behind closed doors, he also berated the GOP Congresswoman \uc2 Jaime Herrera Beutler\uc2  for revealing some details of that conversation he had with Trump on 1/6.\par \par \uc2 According to Draper, McCarthy yelled, "After all the work I have done for you, you should have come to me. Why did you go to the press? This is no way to thank me. And in a private meeting last summer with two police officers who defended the Capitol and the mother of Officer Brian Sicknick who died after 1/6, McCarthy claimed that Trump had no idea his supporters were carrying out the attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: He wasn`t watching TV.\par \par \uc2 GLADYS SICKNICK, MOTHER OF OFFICER BRIAN SICKNICK: He knew what was going on. He knew what was going on. People were fighting for hours and hours and hours. This doesn`t make any sense to me.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I`m just telling you from my phone call, I don`t know that he did know that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Former Officer \uc2 Michael Fanone\uc2  secretly recorded that audio and he will join my colleague Alex Wagner in the next hour, so be sure to stick around for that. Now, what all this makes clear is that Kevin McCarthy was well aware that the January 6 mob was not a group of tourists or patriots or just people in the wrong place at the wrong time. And it is also a reminder that he may be among the most cowardly, duplicitous and hypocritical politicians in Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So much is riding on these upcoming Midterms and on which party ends up controlling both chambers of Congress. And therefore, so much is riding on one particularly tight Senate race in the crucial swing state of Pennsylvania. Between the state`s Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican TV Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 Back in May Fetterman had a stroke which Oz, the doctor, is trying to use against him. Tonight, Fetterman spoke to NBC "NIGHTLY NEWS" about the race and the challenges with language that he faces as he`s recovering.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: To be precise, I use captioning. So, that`s really the -- that`s the major challenge. And every now and then I`ll miss a word, every now and then. Sometimes I`ll maybe mush two words together. But as soon as I have captioning, I`m able to understand exactly what`s being asked. But even after the stroke, immediately after that, I was able to read everything, and I haven`t lost any memories or anything like that. It`s just really the lingering issue that I have.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean, I know before this you`ve admitted you hadn`t really thought about your health very much. You hadn`t taken great care of yourself. What`s it like now for you -- is there some frustration and having to face this, you know, every day?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It`s not frustration, it`s just a challenge, actually. And I always thought I was pretty empathetic -- empathetic -- I think I was very, excuse me, empathetic. You know, that`s an example of the stroke, empathetic. I always thought I was very empathetic before having a stroke. But now, after having that stroke, I really understand, you know, much more kind of the challenges that Americans have day in and day out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And that paradox of how a stroke that physically weakened Fetterman may have strengthened his connection to ordinary voters is part of what journalist Rebecca Traister writes about in her cover story for New York Magazine titled The Vulnerability of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Rebecca Traister, author of that profile and a writer at large for New York Magazine. Rebecca, thanks so much for coming on the show. What was your biggest takeaway from the time you spent with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and his campaign? Was there anything that really surprised you?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA TRAISTER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, NEW YORK MAGAZINE: Well, it was very striking following his campaign so closely over the past month, to see how swiftly his health was improving, and how that improvement was on public display. I was really struck. I started reporting on him very soon after Labor Day, which was just a couple of weeks after he`d started appearing at rallies and in public again.\par \par \uc2 And you could see almost a day-by-day and certainly a week-over-week improvement in his ability to address crowds, his ability to be loose and his confidence in front of crowds. And that was really striking. And the thing that was striking alongside it was that that visible improvement was happening alongside this building press narrative, certainly on the right- wing and also in in some major newspapers, about how he was hiding something about his health.\par \par \uc2 And that struck me as very strange because so much about his condition he was putting right out there. And again, I think finding a connection with voters and people who were seeing in him something that they recognized and watching him be open about -- now I`m just -- he was facing, be open, be willing to make those stumbles to acknowledge that he was going to make verbal stumbles. It was just such -- there was such discordance between what I was reading in the papers and what I was seeing at rallies and in interviews, including on television.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And of course, Mehmet Oz, his Republican opponent, has attacked Fetterman over his health. He said this last month before the candidates agreed to a date for a debate. Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:10]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is either healthy and he`s dodging the debates because he does not want to answer for his radical left positions, or he`s too sick to participate in the debate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Rebecca, I know that in the modern GOP, the cruelty is the point. But for a doctor to be mocking or weaponizing an opponent`s health is pretty damn cruel and cynical, is it not?\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: Yes, it`s horrifying. It`s incredibly Trumpian. He is a doctor. Now, he`s also a doctor who then built a lot of his career and his power and his name which he`s now leveraging for this campaign on being a television doctor who promoted medically questionable supplements sort of aimed at women, many of them weight loss drugs. You know, his actual record around medical transparency is pretty grim at this point.\par \par \uc2 But no, the cruelty is incredible. And his spokesman, you know, when I called Dr. Oz`s spokesman for response to my Fetterman story, and I brought up the mocking tone, you know, the Fetterman spokesperson didn`t back away from it. He said, we told him to eat his vegetables. I mean, it was this incredibly anti-science, anti-medical cruelty, you know, aim to make an opponent who is clearly again, getting better and better week by week in front of millions of people, you know, to make him seem weak. And it was -- it was incredibly cruel and having nothing to do with the doctor`s responsibility.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: We should also shouldn`t forget that Dr. Oz is the TV doctor who helped massage Trump`s own medical records or medical information back in 2016. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  still leads Oz in the polls narrowly. The race is tightening. Why do you think that is? Do you think as Politico reported yesterday that the Dobbs decision is fading in terms of the impact on the Democratic grassroots side?\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: Well, I think that there`s -- there is always going to be a narrowing in polls, right? This always happens in the weeks before an election, right? Partisan loyalties kick in. Republicans who hadn`t necessarily been warm about Oz, through the summer, a lot of them were going to come home as they say, right? And this was going to get to be a tighter race.\par \par \uc2 One of the things I`ve noticed, I was reporting this story as those polls were really tightening. As of now, they seem to have stabilized a little bit. And it`s always tough -- you know, I`m always dubious about polling at this juncture, so I think it`s tough to say. But I think that the tightening was natural. And I also think -- I mean, I was watching just zillions of right-wing dollars stream into Pennsylvania airways, and those things have an impact. You know, that`s something that was very clear that the right-wing Super PACs, including some aligned with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , were just flooding Pennsylvania`s airwaves with attack ads on Fetterman throughout the month of September. And I think that probably had an impact too.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, Bernie Sanders was on our show earlier tonight. He is arguing that it is a mistake for Democrats running for office to focus only on abortion in these upcoming Midterms. Does he have a point that the economy is still a huge very daily issue for millions of Americans, and Democrats can`t afford to ignore it?\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: I think that there -- I agree with Bernie a little bit in that I -- what he wrote in his Guardian article was that there were two -- there was an attitude that Democrats could just coast on abortion into the elections. And as somebody who has been very critical of how Democrats have talked about abortion over decades, I have some sympathy for that because I have seen this summer, post-Dobbs and more importantly, post-Kansas, a little bit of this attitude that if we just say the word abortion, abortion, abortion over and over again, that`ll be it.\par \par \uc2 But where I differ with Bernie is I think it`s very -- I don`t think you can silo abortion off from economic issues. One of the -- my frustrations with how Democrats have talked about abortion for years is that there hasn`t been acknowledgment that abortion is absolutely an economic issue tied into paid leave, higher wages, childcare, college costs, affordable housing, health care. All those economic issues are one and the same with reproductive health care and access.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Yes. It`s a very good point there. There`s a false distinction between abortion rights, economic rights, and the false distinction between, as we`ve discussed before on this show and other shows, between kitchen table issues and democracy which is also a kitchen table issue. Everything is a kitchen table issue.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Traister, thank you so much for your time and for your reporting. Much appreciated.\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And this quick programming note. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is going to be interviewed by Lawrence O`Donnell on the "LAST WORD" in just over an hour. Don`t miss that.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Mehdi. Thank you for another great show.\par \par \uc2 And thank you at home for joining us this hour. In just a moment, I`m going to be joined by Rachel Maddow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221012eiab00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Jesse Kirsch, Jonathan Lemire, Ryan Reilly, Cal  Perry, Barry McCaffrey, Allie Raffa, Julie Tsirkin, Kerry Sanders, Jacob  Ward \par \uc2 7,904 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Talk about a political pressure cooker. Democratic and Republican campaign officials are juggling as many as 10 competitive Senate races right now with controversy over how national Democrats are spending critical campaign cash. We`ll dive into that dollar`s dilemma with early voting already underway in many states. One state on the bubble, Ohio, and last night`s huge debate between Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican J.D. Vance did not disappoint as combative as you`d expect from a toss-up race. But this morning, Ryan said Vance is getting more help from his party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, OHIO: \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  gave him $40 million, Peter Thiel gave him 15 you know, we`re out here slog and it`s David versus Goliath but I think we got a really, really good shot to win this thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In Ukraine, yet another new round of missile strikes, as Russian officials celebrate what they say is a new phase of the war, one that apparently focuses on killing civilians instead of soldiers. So, what is this new phase and what does it mean for what`s ahead? I`ll ask four-star general Barry McCaffrey in just a minute. And we`ve now learned that among the women accusing disgraced movie producer \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2  that his sex crimes trial in California is the governor`s wife. We`ll have the latest from the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the midterm races and the growing alarm that Democratic decision-makers may be letting winnable races slip away. That includes races in Wisconsin, North Carolina, Pennsylvania. Across the board, Democratic candidates have spent way more on their own ads than the party has. While on the Republican side, the bulk of the spending has come from the National Party and outside groups. Case in point, Ohio, where Republican J.D. Vance has come up with just 10 percent of the cost of his ads, compared to more than 80 percent for Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, MSNBC HOST: Why does \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wants to win this seat more than national Democrats?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Yes, I don`t know. I mean, that`s the question I`ve -- as you know, and I`ve been on your show for years now, I`ve always expressed the level of frustration with the National Democratic Party, the disconnect, the working class people, and here you have a blue-collar candidate who`s, you know, in the last two public polls, we`ve been up three points.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As to how exactly Democrats are spending money according to AdImpact, a third of all Democrats` spending on TV ads last month focused on abortion. But not everyone thinks that`s the smartest way to spend. In a new op-ed, Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  says "while the abortion issue must remain on the front burner, it would be political malpractice for Democrats to ignore the state of the economy and allow Republican lies and distortions to go unanswered."\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC Jesse Kirsch. He`s following the Ohio Senate race from Cleveland. Jonathan Lemire is White House bureau chief for Politico, host of MSNBC`s "WAY TOO EARLY," and an MSNBC political analyst. And Basil Smikle, Democratic strategist and former executive director of the New York state Democratic Party. Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 So, Basil, let`s start with where Democrats are spending. Are they leaving winnable races on the table, or is it simply there isn`t enough money to go around and they`ve always got to make these tough decisions?\par \par \uc2 BASIL SMIKLE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, I think the latter point particularly having to run the state party. You know, you`re dealing with donors all the time, and you have to make really tough choices about what`s reasonable, what`s not, and where to put those resources, where to send other donors. The reality --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So is the message that \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  can`t win in Ohio, though?\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: No. I actually don`t think that should be the message. Quite frankly, I think he can win in Ohio. And what I was saying is that you -- we have to take a page from Georgia in 2020 for all the lessons that we learned from the Georgia races. The -- one of the important takeaways is that the Democrats, they`re contested in every race, at every level, wherever they could. And that I think, is the -- is the lesson going forward.\par \par \uc2 It has to be, you know, going back to Howard Dean, the 50-state strategy and not the battleground state strategy. And I think what`s happened here is that there are some in the party that believes that Ohio is no longer the battleground that it was, it`s more red state than a purple state, and that perhaps they should spend their money elsewhere. I don`t agree with that. This is -- this to be -- to me is a winnable seat and we should not among others be giving up before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Jonathan, Ryan isn`t alone in his frustration with national Democrats. Just one other example is Cheri Beasley in North Carolina. She said she needs help she`s not getting. But Democrats have been burned in the past by spending money on races they couldn`t win, right? So is maybe the net effect they`re being too cautious this time? What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, let`s start with Ohio. First of all, there`s been a large distrust of the polling in Ohio over the last few cycles. Let`s recall in just two years ago, in 2020, polls showed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in a more or less dead heat with then-President Trump. In fact, the Biden campaign was so optimistic they dispatched Biden to Ohio just about two days before Election Day and he lost that state by about eight points. It wasn`t even close.\par \par \uc2 Though, those polls in Ohio tend to overestimate Democratic turnout. That`s the fear at the party, and they didn`t want to get burned there again. But they should be careful. And North Carolina is a good one to bring up here because so Democratic strategists looking at the map and growing increasingly nervous. They feel like Republicans have an advantage in Nevada, where Democrats may have a hard time hanging on to that seat. So in order to keep 50-50 and therefore the majority, they got to pick up one somewhere.\par \par \uc2 All along, the thought was Pennsylvania. And they`re still optimistic about Pennsylvania but they can`t help but notice the polls have tightened there. And there`s also a close in Georgia despite the Staley scandals engulfing \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , those polls seem tight as well. So, therefore, they might need to expand the map somewhat. Ohio`s a possibility, North Carolina is another Democrats say look at that one, the race is really tight for the National Party, they say is going to have to make some investments here in this last month to make it possible.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Jesse, let`s talk about Ohio. Where do things stand after last night`s debate? And is there any indication you`re hearing that \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , does he think he might get a little more money in this final stretch?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: You know, Chris, if you ask both candidates, they`ll tell you they won the debate last night. J.D. Vance, so much so that in the aftermath, when \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  came and spoke to us in the spin room, instead of coming himself, Vance sent a surrogate who told us that the winner doesn`t really need to spin. So, both candidates will tell you they won last night.\par \par \uc2 As for democratic help, I think \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  likes this personification of David versus Goliath, as he put it. You played that clip a short time ago. He wants to keep the party at arm`s length. So he may want the money, but he at the same time wouldn`t want people to think he is courting that money. So much to the point that last night, he said that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  should not run for re-election. He criticized Vice President Kamala Harris`s border comments and saying that it is secure. So he is trying to keep himself separate from party leadership, trying to court people on the right, but J.D. Vance will argue that is farcical. And so that is where we continue to see playing out here.\par \par \uc2 And again, he has been spending money so as J.D. Vance. We`re starting to see more momentum on the Republican side of things. But all that Republican money coming here means that it`s not being spent elsewhere. So it is certainly closer than Republicans would have expected months back you might think, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Basil, there`s money and then there`s messaging. Is \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  right about Democrats may be depending too much on the abortion message? We saw it last night. I watched the debate. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  talking about the Bipartisan Infrastructure Bill, the CHIPS Act, big economic wins for the Democrats, does Sanders have a point?\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: Yes. I actually agree with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  here because I do believe that abortion and reproductive rights should be on the front burner. It`s obviously very important. But you know, for a lot of other voters across the country, in suburban and rural areas, there may be other issues that we should be able to talk about, and Democrats are conversant in all of those issues. We just need to be able to talk about them and not shy away from them. We have to build momentum and mobilize voters wherever we can in any way that we can. And I think that`s the message that \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  has.\par \par \uc2 And just a quick point to tack on to our earlier discussion, the concern that -- one of the concerns that I have, particularly with not being able to talk about a variety of issues, and then not investing in a lot of these candidates, is that it may have a chilling effect. You look at -- you look at Mandela Barnes and other candidates that are running statewide offices, these are young candidates. This is the future of our party. And if we`re sending the message that we don`t want to invest in you and we don`t believe that you can actually talk on about all of the issues that really do affect so many of the voters in the state, then that sends a message that perhaps the Democratic Party is not for them. And that`s not where we should -- we should be.\par \par \uc2 Wes Moore is doing a great example of talking to voters across the state in many different ways across many subject areas in Maryland. That is a -- that is a paradigm for the future. And I think the party should be investing in that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Jonathan, in another major issue we know is important from polls is the state of our democracy, and it`s going to continue to be in the headlines because of the January 6 committee this week, the Oath Keepers trial as well and of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . J.D. Vance leaned into his defense of the former president in the debate last night. Let`s watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE, REPUBLICAN SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, OHIO: I was alive during 2016 to 2020. And what I saw was consistently rumors that finally, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was going to be indicted, that he was going to be accused of something legitimately criminal, of course, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  despite his commercials voted to impeach him twice. I have seen nothing that would suggest that the president United States should be thrown in prison.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So again, you know, Jonathan, we`ve got to speak here in coming up Thursday. We don`t know if there`s something big coming out of it. But do you think the battle over democracy even now could be a wild card in the midterms? And we`ll see what happens on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Yes, we will. We`ll have to see the impact of that, that January six hearing coming less than a month before Election Day, and the party -- the committee members have made no secret off camera that they hope that it will impact things on voters` minds when they go to the ballot boxes. Impulse suggested it is one of the top issues. And then itself, the fact that -- think back four, 8, 12, 16 years ago, the fact that defending the nation`s democracy will be a top issue shows you just how things have changed in the era of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It does lag behind abortion rights and it certainly lags behind the economy, which by all polling, including in Ohio, is still the top issue.\par \par \uc2 But it is something and they`re going to be some voters. And so Democrats hope that independents or Republicans who are just tired of Trump and ever the chaos he brings, will look at that and say ourselves, we can`t go down this road again. And even if I don`t necessarily love \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , even if I`m not a Democrat, even if I don`t necessarily love \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , I don`t want to empower someone like J.D. Vance, who is an apologist for Trump and the insurrection. So, they are hopeful there. And, Chris, lastly, since we`re on the subject of Ohio, I know you`re a Clevelander so let me please say go Guardians beat the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: Go, go, go.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s all?\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: That`s not for this prosecute, I can`t cosign that. That`s a prosecutor`s --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I know you`ll be supposed to come back later in the show. You`re pressing your luck there.\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: Jesse Kirsch, who I know is a new Guardians fan because he`s moved to Cleveland. Jonathan, thank you so much, even though you hate the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  more than you love the Guardians. And, Basil, you are sticking around.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: You`re putting the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  fan in a tough spot, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK, all right. Fair enough. What were we talking about? Oh, yes. I`m so excited about the game tonight. It`s not just the committee that`s keeping the insurrection in the news. Let me bring in NBC`s Ryan Reilly, who was following the Oath Keepers trial at U.S. District Court in DC. I will not ask you who you`re rooting for tonight, get us up to speed on where the trial stands right now.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, MSNBC JUSTICE REPORTER: Well, \uc2 Phillies\uc2  fan over here, so a whole different story. But I would say, you know, essentially what we`ve gone through in the -- in this trial so far is a lot of testimony from \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents sort of laying down the basics of the case and sort of laying out how over the months after the January rather after the 2020 election, the rhetoric began to heat up within the Oath Keepers group. And in fact, there`s -- as Oath Keepers in North Carolina who broke away from the larger organization because of concerns with which Stewie as he`s referred to Elmer Stewart Rhodes III had been taking the group and some of the extreme rhetoric that we use.\par \par \uc2 In fact, during today, they showed an interview that he had done with a journalist in November on the street talking about how the Oath Keepers would resist any law that was passed by President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And you know - - so it was sort of building this case about how the Oath Keepers were preparing to interrupt the peaceful transfer of power. What we`re expecting, in the near future, should be testimony from some of the cooperating witnesses, some of the Oath Keepers who are -- made a deal with the government, and are testifying about this. And three of them, in fact, have said that they were, in fact, part of this seditious conspiracy and that there was, in fact, this agreement to oppose the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of this does get a little bit next because there are other reasons why the Oath Keepers were there at some of these events, in November and December, and in fact, in January as well. They held forth that they were providing security services for a lot of people in Trump`s orbit. And in fact, they had, in fact, been working with \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , there were communications that were revealed today with a Stop the Steal organizer, and there were events that were supposed to take place at the Capitol. So sort of separating that and making sure that it`s clear that everyone who has been charged was a part of this larger push to interrupt the peaceful transfer of power, I think is what prosecutors have to bring for.\par \par \uc2 You know, for Rhodes, I think it`s kind of an -- it`s easier for them to make that case because there`s so much direct rhetoric coming from Rhodes about what this plan was all about. But what some of the other defendants are going to try to say is that they were just part of this security effort. And that, you know they had no plans actually enter the Capitol of that building that day. And are going to come up with reasons why they entered the Capitol building that day and for claim that they were trying to provide security for the officers who were attacked that day and suggest that they were helping law enforcement in some capacity. And prosecutors would just dismiss that out of hand and say that that wasn`t the case. And that`s what they, in fact, were planning to oppose the peaceful transfer of power and had no right to go in the Capitol that day, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So much more to come. Ryan Reilly, thank you for following it for us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Closing arguments now underway in another trial we`re watching. Will the Parkland High School shooter get life in prison or the death penalty? But first, \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders hold an emergency meeting as Russian airstrikes rocked Ukraine for another day, or we had a major turning point in the war? And Russian hackers claimed responsibility for cyberattacks targeting several U.S. airports. Why that could matter to you? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, we`re watching rapid escalations of the conflict in Ukraine, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  calling it a turning point. And at this critical juncture just this morning, President Zelenskyy asks the West for more sophisticated air defense systems during an emergency meeting with \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders. The situation is getting worse with the familiar sounds of missile warning sirens ringing all across Ukraine for a second day, those missiles hitting critical infrastructure in the city of Lviv, leaving 30 percent of people there without power, another strike killing someone in Zaporizhzhia. Ukraine`s ambassador said this at the United Nations Special Session.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERGIY KYSLYTYSYA, UKRAINE AMBASSADOR: There was family. It was in a residential building under attack, unable to go to a bomb shelter because there was no electricity because Russia has already killed some of my family members. And we see no end to that cruelty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Cal Perry, who was on the ground there in Kyiv. Four-star retired \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  General Barry McCaffrey joins us as well. Cal, two missiles as I understand it was shut down by air defenses this morning in Kyiv. I wonder what you`re seeing in terms of damage and how the people you`re speaking to are holding up amid this onslaught.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Look, I think people are weary and they`re exhausted. This is a city that didn`t get any sleep last night, the air raid sirens went almost through the night into the morning. And as you said, at least two missiles intercepted over the Kyiv area. And when that happens, it`s loud, you can hear it, and people are scared of further rocket attacks. There were 16 rockets that were intercepted across the country today, as you said, though, some of them were not and some of them hit their targets and killed many people.\par \par \uc2 The total number of dead right now stands at 19 from two days of shelling from Russia with more than 105 wounded. We have seen more than a hundred rockets fired in just the last 24 hours. And as you said, they`re starting to target infrastructure targets. That is power targets. More than a third of people in Lviv are without power. There was fear in the capital city where I am, that we could see rolling blackouts. They`re putting that off, they say for at least 24 hours but it gives you an idea of how the infrastructure grid has been badly damaged here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 And as we turn into the winter months, it`s of great concern here because, of course, people are going to get cold. So the concern is that this is what we`re going to see more and more from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Now, as he either misses those targets or intentionally targets civilians, that too is having a terrible effect. This is a city where many people came back from overseas. They were hoping to have settled back at home, get to school, the start of school was supposed to be in this week. School has been canceled this week. Life for right now is on hold, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you so much, Cal Perry. We appreciate your reporting from there. General, is there any question about whether or not there are definite targets that are civilians?\par \par \uc2 BARRY MCCAFFREY, MSNBC MILITARY ANALYST: Look, Chris, it seems clear to me that Putin has run out of ideas. His military operation has failed. It`s very unlikely to garner any more strength from the badly conducted conscription effort to bring in 300,000 more troops. It is clear to me that this campaign of terror against Ukrainian civilian targets is impossible to imagine that it will turn around the military situation on the ground. I mean, the Brits absorb 30,000 some odd killed from the Germans and never flagged in their commitment to defending the island. So, the Ukrainians are clearly being tormented by Russian terrorist attacks, which are unlikely to have any positive influence.\par \par \uc2 I think the other thing we`re starting to see now is growing internal dissent in Russia. Look, Putin is sacked eight of his generals, and I might add another 10 have been killed. He personally taken command of the operation. He doesn`t know what he`s doing. Tactically, the Ukrainians are seeing considerable success on the battlefield. So the bottom line, all this to me is we need to up the commitment to Ukraine and get them not just air defense systems, but ATACMS missiles and M1 modern tanks. That`s where we need to go.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So for those of us who are not military experts, why would that make a difference at this point? And is this point, as some people have suggested a turning point, General?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, I think from the Russians, it isn`t a turning point. But what they`re doing is not going to influence the ground campaign in any significant way. This guy has run out of options. The wheels are coming off, the Russian conventional military forces. Enlisting Belarus as an ally is unlikely to make any dramatic change. Belarus is a welfare state with a fairly small 60,000-soldier military. So I think what we got to assume is that the Ukrainians have the courage and the political and military leadership to defeat the Russians.\par \par \uc2 If we give them the right tools, ATACMS missiles, we need to tell them don`t strike Russian targets inside territorial Russia, but we need that let them go after command and control and logistics hubs at a greater range of 300 miles. They need M1 tanks which will overmatch the Russian T-80 tanks. And by the way, they`re running out of t-80 tanks too. The Russians have lost a division`s worth of armor captured by the Ukrainians, simply unbelievable. The biggest supplier of armor to the Ukrainian military has been the Russian army.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And then finally I think it`s clear they need better air defense. These Iranian drones are causing enormous damage. They`re low-flying. They`re fairly slow. They`re hard to pick up. So, we need to sort out the technical challenges of assisting them on air defense.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: General Barry McCaffrey, always great to have your expertise. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senate hopeful \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  campaigning in Georgia today as the scandal plague candidate gains news support on the ground from two Republican Senators.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, Herschel Walker`s troubled Senate campaign is getting some Republican reinforcements as he continues to be battered by allegations he paid for an abortion. Senators \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and Tom Cotton are in Georgia hoping to firm up the GOP base in a race against incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  that`s too close to call. In fact, a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " analysis suggest that the race is so tight even the loss of 1 in 50 potential voters could swing it in favor of Mr. Warnock.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC`s Allie Raffa and Basil Smikle is back with us.\par \par \uc2 Ali, you were at the Walker event. Tell us more about what we`re expecting there.\par \par \uc2 ALLIE RAFFA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris. We`re expecting Senators \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and Tom Cotton to arrive before this 2:00 p.m. event to stump for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . And this Georgia Senate race has really become the biggest test of whether scandals matter in this era of hyper partisanship, whether they matters to voters. And now you have these two senators coming forward today to present this message to other Republicans that it`s OK to back \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , that these scandals at the end of the day shouldn`t matter at least publicly in this Georgia Senate race that he is the best and quite frankly right now the only option they have.\par \par \uc2 So you`re seeing these two senators come here today. You`re seeing the NRSC staff up here in Georgia, redirect ads, spending money from a New Hampshire Senate race, bringing it back down here. Because Republicans know how critical this Senate race is if they want to win back control of this current 50-50 Senate. This is one of five key critical Senate races that they are paying very close attention to and really are hinging this fight for control back of the Senate on.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re expecting both of them to arrive here today and again shore up support with Republican voters here, showing that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is their candidate. That there`s no question he has their support. And I spoke with a couple of Republicans just a few minutes ago who told me they don`t even think that this appearance today, this visit by these two senators is necessary. That they`ve had their mind made up for months now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s so interesting, Basil, she makes a really good point, doesn`t she, about do any kind of controversies matter anymore? Do scandals matter? There`s an interesting subtext I think to this race because the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reported about a private event for Walker just after this whole controversy started, and an evangelical pastor made comparisons apparently to God`s protection of King David in the bible saying Walker would be protected in a similar way.\par \par \uc2 And the "Times" called the Georgia Senate race an explicit matchup of two increasingly divergent versions of American Christianity. Walker, conservative Christianity and right-wing politics, while Ralph Warnock who of course is a pastor represents the southern tradition of faith-based civil rights.\par \par \uc2 I guess my question is in a race as tight as this one where every single vote matters, can faith be an important component to who wins or who loses?\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: Well, faith is always an important component because there`s many theologians and especially black theologians will tell you that the Old Testament is replete with social justice and the prophets are social justice advocates. And in that tradition I think is Reverend Warnock, who is arguing in support of all Georgians and for Americans, you know, who are at every different sort of ethnic of life whether rural, suburban, wealthy or poor.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is sort of embodying a different kind of faith, which is a big departure from George W. Bush`s evangelical Christians that sort of brought him into office. This is a very community, a community that`s willing to elect a celebrity who is less ideological and more of a vehicle for Republican ideology. And that`s what I think is really scary here. This is not about social justice. This isn`t even about faith.\par \par \uc2 This is about electing people on the right who are just going to do the bidding of the party, whatever that is. It`s not even -- it`s not about Christianity at all. And I think that`s the important lesson that whether I can talk about it or others can talk about it, it`s an important point to note, that this isn`t about religion. This is about passing policy that is actually antithetical to the Christianity that Walker says that he supports and raises.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I`m guessing that`s part of the message of this new TV ad that was released by a group called Georgia Honor. They`re tied to a Democratic super PAC, the Senate majority PAC. And it highlights that tweet by Herschel Walker`s son Christian. Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: "He threatened to kill us and had us move six times in six months running from his violence. Showing up at his ex-wife`s house armed with a gun and refusing to leave, even making violent threats outside his son`s children`s party. Six moves in six months, running from Herschel Walker`s violence."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So my question, how many undecided do you think there are? Because as Allie just said she`s talking to people who, you know, they`re there but for no reason, they`re not looking to be, you know, pushed one way or another. How effective could an ad like that be if there are a lot of voters on the fence or even a few?\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: Yes, I don`t know that there are going to be a lot of voters on the fence. My sense is that so many of them have made up their mind and even independents who are, you know, tend to trend one, whether Democrat or Republican from the minute we start talking about them they`ve already decided. So I think it matters only in the sense that it`s just more opportunity for voters to sort of the message sink in a little bit so if you are intending to vote for Warnock this might push you over the edge to actually get out the door and do it.\par \par \uc2 You know, and that`s really the rub here. It`s about turning intent to behavior. Yes, I want to support Warnock so how do we actually make sure to pull you out and do so? So a couple of points here and there but I think voters have made up their minds.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Basil Smikle, who I always appreciate except on game day, and Allie Raffa, thanks to you as well.\par \par \uc2 We have some breaking news just coming into us from Capitol Hill, where police are investigating a suspicious substance. I want to bring in NBC`s Julie Tsirkin.\par \par \uc2 What`s going on there?\par \par \uc2 JULIE TSIRKIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I just left the hallway outside of Congressman Bennie Thompson`s office. That`s actually where this substance was sent to. U.S. Capitol police are not responding to our official inquiries, but we do have from two law enforcement officials, who say that this was a suspicious substance or powder, they say and that it may have been sent in or on a letter to the January 6th chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now we don`t know that 100 percent certainly from \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2 , but officials and sources I spoke to are telling me that is the likely case. It`s also being kept off police radio. They`re trying to keep this a little bit under wraps. As you can see it`s obviously a sensitive matter, especially with the 10th and potentially final January 6th Committee hearing happening this week on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Of course I should note the House and the Senate are out right now, so there are no lawmakers in the building. There`s no lawmakers in the House either, especially concerning \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  here. So we`ll just have to see what happens in the next couple of hours as law enforcement officials are continuing to investigate what this could potentially be.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Julie Tsirkin, thank you for that update. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 The sentencing trial for the Parkland high school shooter is now underway. The killer`s striking comments from \uc2 YouTube\uc2  shared by the prosecution ahead of that big decision by the jury.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Life in prison or the death penalty, that`s what the jury will soon be left to decide in the sentencing trial of the Parkland school shooter. Closing arguments are happening right now. It`s been almost a year since he pleaded guilty to 17 counts of murder and 17 counts of attempted murder for the 2018 massacre at \uc2 Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School\uc2  where 14 students and three staff members were killed.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Kerry Sanders is outside the courthouse in Fort Lauderdale, Florida. And former prosecutor David Henderson is also with us.\par \par \uc2 Kerry, I don`t know if you`ve had an opportunity to talk to any of the families. What are you hearing on the ground?\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It is an extremely difficult day, but when you consider that they have been waiting since 2018 to reach this point whether a 12-member jury will determine whether it`s going to be a life sentence or a death sentence, it is, as they would say, about time. Some have told me they wish that there had been another shot fired that day and that Nicholas Cruz would have been killed on the campus after he went on that rampage and left so much carnage in the wake of so many lives, broken lives, family members who wonder if ever there is going to be a sense of what they might call true justice.\par \par \uc2 Many say that they are just going to wait to see what the jury has to say, but clearly, some believe that the only appropriate sentence here is a death sentence. Debbi Hixon lost her husband Chris that day. This is what she had to tell me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: What is the appropriate punishment here?\par \par \uc2 DEBBI HIXON, HUSBAND KILLED IN PARKLAND MASSACRE: At the end of the day, nothing is going to change what`s here for us. But I just personally don`t feel that this individual should be sucking anymore energy out of our community, any of our resources, so for me, I think that the best justice that we`re going to get is the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: But is that ever going to be justice for you?\par \par \uc2 HIXON: Chris will never come back, so, you know, what kind of justice is there really going to be? Our life is changed forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: If there is a unanimous decision for the death penalty, then the judge, in all likelihood, would follow that. And then there would be a death sentence. In Florida, unlike other states, there are two methods of execution in the state. Lethal injection, or if the inmate chooses the electric chair and at least one inmate, since the electric chair has been brought back into the picture, has requested to be electrocuted, but then that execution was stayed.\par \par \uc2 There is just so much emotion tied up in all of this, and you can imagine for the jury, who has been listening to this since July, every painstaking detail, looking at the videos that were recorded by the security cameras, hearing from the survivors who were shot, going through all of that, for them it`s likely that they will need some sort of psychological counselling themselves after what they`ve heard. Chris?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:14]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kerry, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And David, I can`t even begin to imagine sitting through that trial, the emotions running so understandably high, when you listen to Debbi Hixon having lost the most important person arguably in her family`s life. So from a legal standpoint, though, what does the jury have to weigh when they decide between life and death?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HENDERSON, ELLWANGER LAW AND FORMER PROSECUTOR: Well, Chris, no case is like this one, though I have tried cases involving multiple deaths where the state was seeking the death penalty. What the jury has to weigh is there are mitigating factors. Is there a reason to give him life instead of death? And they also have to consider whether or not there are aggregating factors, which are so overwhelming obvious in this case, it`s hard to articulate them objectively, that is, were other people put at risk, the severity of the circumstances under which the crimes were committed.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you, even though that`s a legal analysis, that`s not how juries look at it. Juries are going to go back, they`re going to ask themselves the question, should he be put to death for what he did, yes or no. I would say they were moving away from the death penalty as a society, but you have to remember that people on this jury had to be able to say they can follow the law. There are circumstances where they can give the death penalty. And so it`s very likely that that will be the outcome of this trial.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: David, meantime, in Los Angeles, \uc2 Harvey Weinstein\uc2  is facing five accusers. And it was just revealed that Governor Gavin Newsom`s wife is one of those accusers. He of course has pled not guilty but a common defense tactic is trying to paint the alleged victims as liars, right? Do they misunderstood? Hear you have a woman with an \uc2 NBA\uc2  from Stanford, someone who is speaking in public, less likely arguably then to be intimidated by cross-examination. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: That is, and we`re in this position because the defense lawyers have been following that adage of trying to paint their accusers as liars. That`s the whole reason she can testify in the first place, which is a really important component of her testimony. Getting to your point, when I first started dating my wife, she liked these "Law and Order" shows, and I used to hate them. She`d ask me why, and I`d say because everybody is picture perfect when they testify on "Law and Order." That doesn`t happen in real life.\par \par \uc2 But here`s someone who will be a picture-perfect witness. But she represents something more important and that is the way the rules have changed for prosecuting people who sexually abuse other people. Now we can get into their past histories because they have tried to portray people as liars in court. And that`s why the defendants can no longer isolate people when they actually go to trial.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: David Henderson, thank you so much. Good to talk to you today. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some new information on that breaking news from Capitol Hill. Just moments ago, \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  tweeted that they investigated that letter with concerning language from the Rayburn House Office Building. It was determined not to have anything dangerous inside. \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  adding they are working to determine who sent that letter and why.\par \par \uc2 Our Julie Tsirkin reported moments ago this was found outside January 6th Committee chairman Bennie Thompson`s office.\par \par \uc2 And right now we`re also following breaking news out of Pennsylvania. At least 27 children and daycare employees were rushed to the hospital following an apparent carbon monoxide leak this morning. Firefighters raced to the scene after calls of an unconscious child at Happy Smiles Learning Center in Allentown. Carbon monoxide monitors revealed dangerously high levels of that odorless, colorless gas prompting a full evacuation of the building. The good news is all those taken to the hospital via ambulance are in stable condition.\par \par \uc2 New today charges have been formally dropped against Adnan Syed for the 19999 murder of his former girlfriend Hae Min Lee. You`ll likely remember the case from when it was investigated in the first season of the wildly popular serial podcast in 2014, a season that got about 300 million downloads, according to NPR. Syed`s conviction and life sentence was overturned last month after a court found that exculpatory evidence was not shared with his defense team.\par \par \uc2 Russian hackers targeting a new -- taking on a new target, knocking down more than a dozen U.S. airport Web sites. Just how dangerous are these attacks and can we stop them? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:19]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What happens when your town runs out of water completely? A small town in central California is about to find out. The city of Coalinga has only one source of water. It`s an aqueduct managed by the federal government and officials say it could be empty before the end of the year. Many residents are stockpiling water. If a solution is not found, the city may be forced to buy its water at a much higher rate on the open market.\par \par \uc2 And Coalinga is not alone. Cities across the West Coast are struggling with rising temperatures, droughts and shrinking water supplies. We will continue to follow this story for you.\par \par \uc2 A group of pro-Russian hackers took down more than a dozen Web sites for major U.S. airports including Hartsfield Jackson in Atlanta, L.A. International and more. The group behind the hack is known as Kill Net and they`re targeting countries supporting Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s technology correspondent Jake Ward is following this story for us.\par \par \uc2 Hey there, Jake, I mean, there`s no indication I think any flights were actually affected by this hack but how much access did this group get and what`s the concern here?\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD, NBC NEWS TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, the concern certainly is that there has been a stepping up of hostilities when it comes to cyberattacks. We have seen attacks on regional hospitals, on school districts, and last month, on a pair of states, Kentucky and Colorado. Both of whom lost access to their sites where election results might be posted for instance about a month from now.\par \par \uc2 All of that tremendously concerning. Yesterday`s attack is of note because of course it is airports, right. The lifeblood of transportation across state lines here in the United States. The good news here is that in spite of the scary sounding name, Kill Net, that particular group is really only a specialist in a particular kind of attack.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 And that`s called denial of service attacks. These are the sorts of attacks in which too much traffic comes out a Web site and shuts it down in that way. These hackers did not get access inside any essential infrastructure of the air space. They didn`t get into the air traffic control system. They did not get into reservation systems. The kinds of things that would truly bring our air infrastructure to its knees.\par \par \uc2 What they did instead was shut down the public facing Web sites of these major airports that you mentioned. I was standing here in Oakland, California. That is not one of the ones that was affected. Really only a handful, about a dozen of them were touched. But, you know, that in the end is really again just the public facing part of the airport. The part where you would look up, you know, how long the security line might be.\par \par \uc2 So we can take some comfort from that, but what is alarming of course is that as Russia-Ukraine heats up and as these pro-Kremlin hackers reach out, more and more they seem to be looking to inflict pain or at least in this case annoyance on companies that are aligned with Ukraine -- Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jake Ward, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 And we now know when an event that is likely to be watched by millions around the world will be. Buckingham Palace says King Charles` coronation, the first coronation of course in 70 years, dubbed Operation Golden Orb, will happen May 6th. The Queen Consort Camilla Parker Bowles will also be crowned. King Charles is expected to sign a proclamation later this year formally declaring the date.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 Eastern Time right there on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221012eiab00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Joyce Vance, Jason Johnson \par \uc2 7,916 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JASON JOHNSON, MSNBC HOST: The first two episodes of the MSNBC original podcast series Rachel Maddow Presents Ultra are now available. Scan the QR code on your screen or check it out wherever you get your podcast. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEHDI HASAN, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (R-AL): They want to control what you have. They will reparation because I think the people that do the crime are owed that. (BLEEP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: The dog whistle becomes a bullhorn. The very open and very dangerous racism and antisemitism on full display in the GOP. Plus --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They should give me immediately back everything that they`ve taken for them because it`s mine. It`s mine.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Did the disgraced ex-president just incriminate himself. Then, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  on Trump`s pick to represent Georgia in the Senate. Plus --\par \par \uc2 MARK HAMILL, ACTOR: I`m not used to be being contacted by world leaders.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: My conversation with Star Wars star Mark Hamill on his new role helping Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And Rachel Maddow on a forgotten but extremely relevant part of American history.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Where we get to from that is a Nazi plot to distribute \uc2 German government\uc2  propaganda in the United States through the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 . That happened.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Good evening from Washington, DC. I`m Mehdi Hasan in for Chris Hayes. There has always been a racist, white supremacist fringe to the modern Republican Party. It hasn`t always been overt, but it has reared its head from time to time going back decades. Republican leaders, even presidents have indulged it. For example, tapes from the Nixon era show the president engaging in blatant anti-Semitism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD NIXON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: All of the Jewish families are close, but there`s this strange malignancy now that seems to creep among them. I don`t know, the radicalism.\par \par \uc2 The Jews are born spies. You notice how many of them are? They`re just in it up to their necks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  infamously popularized the racist trope of the welfare queen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In Chicago, they found a woman who holds the record. She used 80 names, 30 addresses, 15 telephone numbers to collect food stamps, Social Security, veterans benefits for four non-existent deceased veterans husbands, as well as welfare. Her tax-free cash income alone has been running $150,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: George H. W. Bush campaigned on racist stereotypes about black people in crime, most famously the Willie Horton ad in 1988.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE H. W. BUSH, FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Dukakis not only opposes the death penalty, he allowed first-degree murderers to have weekend passes from prison. One was Willie Horton who murdered a boy in a robbery stabbing him 19 times. Despite a life sentence, Horton received 10 weekend passes from prison. Horton fled, kidnapped a young couple, stabbing the man and repeatedly raping his girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, they have been doing this racist dog whistling forever. Every Republican president going back 50 years has known that their party includes racists, anti-Semites, and white nationalists, and they have all catered to those people in some shape or form. But what makes our current political moment so scary, is that that fringe element has now become mainstream. And if you thought it was bad during the Trump presidency, well, it`s gotten even worse since he left office. The past 18 months have been like racist Trumpism on steroids.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend at a Trump rally in Nevada, Alabama Senator Tommy Tuberville, spouted the same racist tropes about black people and crime that we heard 30 years ago. And just like in the Willie Horton ad, he began by blaming Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUBERVILLE: Some people say well, they`re soft on crime. No, they`re not soft on crime, their pro-crime. They want crime. They want crime because they want to take over what you got. They want to control what you have. They want reparation because I think the people that do the crime are owed that. (BLEEP). They`re not owed that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: The people that do the crime are owed reparations. Note the cheers from the crowd there. And that was especially disgusting coming from Tuberville, a guy who spent decades working with Black athletes as a head football coach at several universities. Just yesterday at yet another Trump rally, it was Georgia Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , oh yes, spearing vile anti-immigrant rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): Joe Biden`s five million illegal aliens are on the verge of replacing you replacing your jobs and replacing your kids in school and coming from all over the world. They`re also replacing your culture and that`s not great for America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:05:02]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Replacing you, replacing your culture. To be clear, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  there is espousing a neo-Nazi conspiracy theory known as the Great Replacement. It`s the same vile ideology that the White Supremacist mass shooter at the Pittsburgh Tree of Life Synagogue ascribed to. What used to be disguised with dog whistle language and innuendo is now right there at the surface. It is so normalized on the right.\par \par \uc2 Watch how late last month at a North Carolina rally the crowd just assumes that the ex-President is talking about the N word even if he says he wasn`t, and they shouted back at him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, put mentioned the N-word. You know what the N-word is? It`s no, no, no, it`s the nuclear word. He mentioned the N-word yesterday, the nuclear word. Not supposed to be mentioned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Now, the icing on the racist cake came in the wake of Kanye West`s promotional tour on Fox with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  on Thursday and Friday night and the tweet that followed in which he threatened to go "Death con three on Jewish people." \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has since shut down Kanye`s account but this tweet remains up from Republican attorney general \uc2 Todd Rokita\uc2  of Indiana defending Kanye West`s "independent thinking."\par \par \uc2 Now, Rokita since claimed that he was referring to the hypocrisy of the media and Hollywood elites. But it is difficult to imagine that he had not seen Kanye West`s anti-semitic comments that the whole internet had been talking about all day long. And notably, not a single elected Republican has stood up to condemn Attorney General Rokita for those comments, or Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  for espousing Great Replacement Theory or Senator Tommy Tuberville for his claims that people who want reparations are criminals.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`m Muslim. I`m used to Islamophobia being mainstream as it has been for a while now, sadly. But I never thought anti-Black racism and anti- Semitism would become mainstream again in America and so quickly. And it`s only going to get worse as we approach the Midterm elections and the next presidential campaign. Because there are no consequences, no penalties for this openly racist rhetoric on the right. In fact, there are rewards. So, let`s be clear. This is not racially tinged or allegedly racist stuff. This is brazen racism, plain and simple. And we all need to call it out.\par \par \uc2 Brittany Packnett Cunningham served on Obama`s Task Force for 21st-century policing. She is now the host of the Undistracted Podcast. Stuart Stevens worked on five Republican presidential campaigns and is now a senior adviser to the anti-Trump Lincoln Project. He`s also the author of It Was All A Lie: How the Republican Party Became \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They join me now.\par \par \uc2 Brittany, let me start with you. We know all the euphemisms that are used to push white supremacist messaging. Oh, we care about history and Confederate flags. We just want to build a wall. All the ways to express racism in a kind of politically acceptable way. But Tommy Tuberville just - - he goes straight back to Black people are criminals. What empowers him to do that?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY PACKNETT CUNNINGHAM, HOST, UNDISTRACTED PODCAST: I just want to be clear, it is Indigenous People day and we are talking about a racist white man fear-mongering to suggest that some of us want to take his stuff. And if it weren`t so evilly ironic, I`d be laughing because the entire idea is preposterous.\par \par \uc2 To your point, Mehdi, a lot of us are much more used to a polite racism, one where they bake the racism into the policy, but they don`t yell about it, right? Like that`s uncouth. But here`s the good news. Nobody can now say they didn`t know. So, whatever is empowering him, whether it is the kind of reward that we saw to take \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all the way to the White House, the fact that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is being backed up by the Republican Party, there have been lots of rewards for being exactly this racist and none of us, none of us can say we didn`t know. None of us can say we can`t connect the dots on this thing, because the dots are this. Every policy from refusing immigrants to Islamophobia, to wanting to push back against the cancellation of student debt. All of that is connected to the idea that they do not want to be replaced or lose the power that they`re hoarding.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, if \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  has like all of the things that 3000 people need, that`s fine because that white man earned it. But for the rest of us, there`s not enough clean water or clean air or food or money to go around, right? The rest of us are not allowed to share. This is what is being pushed toward us and we need to be very clear about what`s happening, about how we connect the dots. And we need to not just make it unpopular to say it, we need to make it unpopular to legislate that way.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: It is it is evilly ironic to use your phrase. Stuart, Britney is talking about, you know, people can`t say they didn`t know. Just to be clear, the thesis of your book is that all this was always there and some Republicans just close their eyes to it, right?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:15]\par \par \uc2 STUART STEVENS, SENIOR ADVISOR, THE LINCOLN PROJECT: Yes, I mean, I think that we were guilty of denial. I think that there were those of us who believed that the party would expand, that it had to expand if only because America was changing. But we were wrong. 85 percent of Trumps coalition was white. The country is somewhere between 57 and 60 percent white. And the great failure of the Republican Party is to come up with the policies that would appeal to these people and get them wanting to vote for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, instead, all of this is about race, everything that happened about not certifying the election was about race. All of these illegal votes from places like Philadelphia and Atlanta and Detroit where there were these predominantly African Americans. And it is only going to get worse because the party has accepted this and legitimized it. And it`s -- the only way to do it is to defeat these people at the polls.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: They`ve accepted, they`ve legitimized, they`ve encouraged it. Brittany, Kanye West`s comments both suggesting P Diddy is controlled by Jews and threatening death against Jewish people. Horrifically anti- Semitic, very dangerous given his platform. Where does that kind of hate speech come from? And will there be any consequences for him, especially with his new friends on the right?\par \par \uc2 CUNNINGHAM: I`m absolutely terrified that there won`t be enough consequences. I mean, look, I used to be a Kanye fan. I think I played College Dropout when I was in college, as much as I`m playing Beyonce`s Renaissance Now, and yet I`m very clear on who Kanye is and who he has been for a long time. He`s been deeply misogynistic, he has been anti-Black. And it`s just a hop, skip and a jump from there to become clearly and virulently violently anti-Semitic.\par \par \uc2 So, these things, sadly, are not surprising if we look at the trend of his behavior for a long time. And sadly, there is a marketplace for that. He`s not the first person nor will he be the last person to use "controversy" but what is really hate, evil, and discrimination to sell T-shirts, to sell shoes, to sell a lifestyle, to sell an ideology. So, my hope is that people will withdraw their money, their funds, and their support from something like that. But far too often, we see the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And Stuart, several Republican Senate candidates, Republican members of the House have espoused Replacement Theory, this neo-Nazi conspiracy that we saw \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  doing. And Stuart, an AP poll from May found that 36 percent of Republicans believe they will lose political influence because of immigration, because of being replaced in their dark fantasies, or dark nightmares.\par \par \uc2 What could be done about this? I mean, you`ve advised Republicans. If the Democratic -- the \uc2 DCC\uc2 , the White House operatives. Stuart Stevens, how do we fight back against this stuff on the right? What would you say?\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Well, I think you have to call it out for what it is. This is a deeply anti-American belief. And everything that is happening now is very anti-American on the right. It`s an authoritarian movement, not a normal political party. And look, this is all tied to with the election denial. When you don`t believe that the President United States is a legally elected president, you don`t believe you live in a democracy, and you then move straight to this autocratic system. And that`s what they want. That`s why they like Putin.\par \par \uc2 And you have to call this out. You know, I`m a big believer. If I was running the Democratic Party, I would put democracy on the ballot. And I think it`s so great that the President speaking out about this, because this is one of -- maybe the two last chances we have to defeat this.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Well said. And Brittany, last word to you. 30 seconds left. Do you believe the Democrats are doing enough to call out what Stuart is calling an authoritarian racist cult basically?\par \par \uc2 CUNNINGHAM: Well, I certainly believe that there`s much more to be done. Like I said, at the beginning, we have to make it unpopular to say these things. We have to make it unpopular to legislate in that way, which means that Democrats have to widen the chasm between the kinds of policies that Republicans are putting forward and the kinds of policies that they are putting forward.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that there are necessary bipartisan things, perhaps in the future, but right now, there needs to be a clear right white line between one party and another, not just in the rhetoric, but in the policies that are put forth and passed with a rubber stamp.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Well said. We will have to leave it there. Brittany Packnett Cunningham, Stuart Stevens, I always appreciate you both. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Coming up, another one of Donald Trump`s lawyers is cooperating with federal authorities. What she may have told them and what it might mean for the disgraced ex-president? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: In the pantheon of illustrious lawyers hired by \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , there are the names of true luminaries like Roy Cohn who in the 1950s helped Senator Joe McCarthy in his notorious hunt for communists in the U.S. And there`s \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , the disgraced former mayor of New York City. And \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , whose reward for over a decade of work in the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  was disbarment and a three-year prison sentence.\par \par \uc2 And then there is this lawyer Christina Bobb, a former host on the far- right One American News Network who Trump reportedly hired after watching her on TV defend his stolen election line. Yes, that`s how we find people to work for him. Bobb was at Mar-a-Lago on the day of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search back in August. She went on TV afterwards, refusing to shoot down the ridiculous conspiracy theory that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had planted evidence.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:25]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA BOBB, TRUMP LAWYER: I don`t think that there was actually anything there that`s worthwhile. I will see what they come up with. You know, if they did, it will be interesting, especially since they precluded me from actually watching what they did. But at this point, I don`t necessarily think that they would even go to the extent of trying to plant information. I think they just make stuff up and you know, come up with whatever they want.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the way that they will have to proceed in order to actually try to indict the president because they don`t have anything. There`s just nothing there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: There is just -- there is just nothing there, she said. There is just nothing there. Bobb is also the lawyer who signed a statement certifying to the DOJ that all sensitive documents stored at Mar-a-Lago had been returned before that August search when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  found over 100 additional classified record. And that is what may have put Bob herself in legal jeopardy. NBC News is reporting that on Friday, Christina Bobb spoke with federal investigators. Remember, to be a Trump lawyer, you often need a lawyer of your own.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Joyce Vance, who has never been a Trump lawyer, but she is a former U.S. Attorney for the Northern District of Alabama and an MSNBC Contributor. Joyce, thanks so much for coming on the show tonight. Christina Bobb told investigators she signed the statement certifying there were no more sensitive documents after another lawyer, Evan Corcoran, told her to sign it. And she insisted on a disclaimer that she hadn`t searched for any more documents herself.\par \par \uc2 Do you think Bobb is a target of the investigation or she serving as a witness of what?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Federal prosecutors don`t usually sit down and talk with a target unless there`s some sort of a cooperation deal in the works. That`s not always true. There are occasions where you might - - perhaps they could come in with their lawyer to try to convince you that they shouldn`t be indicted at all. But one prospect that certainly is on the table here is that she could be cooperating.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the reality is here, Mehdi, it`s in some ways painful to listen to her. She was clearly in over her head. But any lawyer, anyone who`s been to law school passed the bar practices should understand that when a lawyer asks you to do something that they are unwilling to do themselves, that you should tread very carefully. And she didn`t and she may well have exposed yourself to criminal liability as a result.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: I mean, the people in Trump`s orbit aren`t known for treading carefully. Let`s be honest about that. Joyce, I want to play you some contradictory things that Trump said at his rally Sunday about the documents found at Mar-a-Lago. Have a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They lose documents, they plant documents. Let`s see, is there a book on nuclear destruction or the building of a nuclear weapon cheaply? Let`s put that box -- let`s put that book in with Trump. I had a small number of boxes in storage at Mar-a- Lago guarded by Secret Service and my people and everybody. I mean, it`s safe. There is no crime. It`s not a crime. And they should give me immediately back everything that they`ve taken from because it`s mine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: First, he says the documents may have been planted, then he admits he had them and he wants them back, including the planted ones. Put aside the ridiculousness of his line of argument, did he maybe just incriminate himself again?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It sounds like a confession to me. And the worst part of it is where he tries to explain away the fact that he has documents there. He doesn`t say classified, but it`s certainly implicit. And he says, they`re under guard from the Secret Service and my people. Well, that doesn`t matter. The law doesn`t care who you have overseeing the safety of classified documents that you have illegally taken from the government`s possession.\par \par \uc2 And this really is quite a spectacular piece of evidence that prosecutors could use if Trump was indicted. It`s something that I would play for the jury as frequently as possible throughout the trial.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, just switching gears a bit. The DOJ has until tomorrow to respond to Trump`s request for the Supreme Court to intervene in the documents case. What will you be looking for joy in the DOJ response? And also, should Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  be allowed to have any involvement in anything Trump-related?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, unfortunately, that`s not up to anyone external. That`s up to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , and he`ll have to make that decision about whether or not he should participate.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Sadly.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: But look, he`s the circuit justice. He`s the Justice who`s allotted to the 11th circuit. These sorts of matters would come automatically to him. So, I don`t see anything nefarious in that. And the briefing schedule that he has said is entirely appropriate. The issue itself here is a technical legal one involving jurisdiction. And it`s rather an interesting one involving what the right time to take an appeal is. Trump`s lawyers say that DOJ was premature and that the 11th Circuit was wrong to act.\par \par \uc2 So, I think for appellate nerds like me, this will be a fascinating discussion. Most of the country will simply be looking for the outcome here and seeing whether the Supreme Court will preempt the 11th circuit or let them continue to hear issues that evolve on this matter.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Last quick question, Joyce. You`re a former prosecutor. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if he was just an ordinary person, not a former president, not Trump, there would be no special master, there would be no supreme court involvement. He would already be indicted by now, wouldn`t he?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think that`s correct.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Let`s see if we can actually hold him to the standards of normal people. Joyce Vance, we will have to leave it there. Thank you so much for your analysis as ever.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Still to come, the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  abortion scandal causes a rift in the Georgia Republican Party. Will Democratic Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  be able to capitalize on that in her race for the governorship against \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 ? She joins me live after a very short break. Don`t go away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: 642 days after Donald Trump`s attempted coup, there are five members of the far-right group the Oath Keepers on trial for seditious conspiracy. Last week, a member of another far-right group the Proud Boys pleaded guilty to that same charge, and there are more trials set to begin later this year. And it may seem like these trials are unprecedented, but they`re not.\par \par \uc2 My colleague, Rachel Maddow has a new podcast which is currently number one on the \uc2 Apple\uc2  podcast chart, all about the time that Nazi conspirators in America faced a seditious conspiracy investigation in the wake of World War II. And another colleague whose show of course I`m hosting tonight, Chris Hayes, interviewed Rachel about this episode of our history that holds new relevance given our current circumstances.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We start off with the story of the senator who you`ve never heard of.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And where we get to from that is a Nazi plot. You distribute \uc2 German government\uc2  propaganda in the United States through the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  and through the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: With an actual like handler, like a Nazi handler.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: A paid agent of Hitler`s government working with members of Congress and members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  to distribute literally propaganda written by the \uc2 German government\uc2  targeting the American people in quantity. That happened. The Justice Department was like, we should do something about this. This is -- this isn`t good. And the marrying of those two things a violent threat to overthrow the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , multiple plots along those lines.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Hoarding guns, training with those guns and weapons, ordinance, I mean --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes, oh, yes. Stockpiling bombs and detailed plans. That happening at the same time that members of Congress and members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  were being roped into an a plan involving a Nazi agent to not just not just sort of leverage their pro-German or anti-war sympathies, but actually do Hitler`s work in this country.\par \par \uc2 The combination of those things is something that the Justice Department tried to tackle through a mass sedition trial in 1944. And the Justice Department, I think, was on to something in terms of the way those things were connected. But the effort to force a resolution to it through the criminal law and through the courts didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 All sorts of other efforts against those forces did work. And those are some of the really interesting stories that I think are very much lost to history. But it`s this great -- I think, very resonant tension between something happening that does seem to require a criminal law response that is not well managed by a criminal law response. And you need the prosecution, you need the investigation, you need the Justice Department to be engaged in things like this, but it`s actually not the solution.\par \par \uc2 And that just -- to me, that`s -- when I started to understand the story, that`s when I felt like oh, I need to make it right away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Chris` full interview with Rachel comes out in just a few hours, tomorrow morning. So, go right now and subscribe to Why Is This Happening? if you haven`t already. And the first two episodes of Rachel`s new podcast, Ultra, are out now. Rachel will have much more on it tonight at 9:00 p.m. so do stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 But coming up next, in the battle with what he calls the evil empire, President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  can now call on none other than Luke Skywalker. I`ll speak to actor Mark Hamill. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:35:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. GEOFF DUNCAN (R-GA): -- about the process that we went through to pick \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , right? We let \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  dominate the scene and handpick somebody that he wanted to win and not necessarily who is best to represent Georgia in the Senate. And we`ve been here before, right? We`ve watched him trainwreck our election process. We watched him run roughshod across to \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seats who are trying to stay in office, and then we watched him run \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  against Kevin -- \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: That was the Republican Lieutenant Governor of Georgia who you will be shocked to hear is not on the ballot for re-election this November, talking freely there about how his party is stuck with the Trump-endorsed Georgia Senate candidate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . This all follows the enormous scandal surrounding Walker stemming from a report by The Daily Beast saying that Walker, a guy running on a platform to ban all abortions reportedly paid for an abortion for an ex-girlfriend in 2009.\par \par \uc2 Walker denies this and NBC News has not yet independently verified these allegations. But even before all of this, it was completely clear that Walker was manifestly unqualified to serve in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And the latest Walker scandal is helping Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  who has opened up a lead in the polls.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:17]\par \par \uc2 An aggregate from FiveThirtyEight shows Warnock leaving Walker about 48 to 44. But this boost does not appear to be helping the other Democrat running statewide next month in Georgia, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who is currently trailing incumbent Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  by over five percentage points according to FiveThirtyEight. So, it`s looking like a fair few Georgians are planning on splitting their votes next month.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to discuss all of this and more is \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the Democratic candidate for governor in Georgia. Thanks so much for coming back on the show. According to the polls, there do seem to be plenty of voters in Georgia, Republican voters, Independent, swing voters, who are willing to vote for Raphael, Warnock, but not for you. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-GA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I think that polls are a snapshot and it`s a question of who turns out to vote on Election Day. I think that there are those who are watching this race on the Republican side. And what they are seeing very clearly is the hypocrisy of Republicans. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  said in response to the allegations against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  that it was a personal choice, and he doesn`t get involved in the personal choices of others. And yet, this is the same governor who has imposed a six-week abortion ban on women interfering with their personal choices. He is someone who does not believe in marriage equality, interfering with the personal choices of who people love.\par \par \uc2 He has made it his business to interfere in the personal choices of Georgians, except for the Georgians that are on the ticket with him. And the reality is that the hypocrisy that we are seeing is going to continue to move voters into our column, and on election day, will put Democrats in office.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And you have Republican voters in Cobb County who were trying to challenge the eligibility of more than 1000 voters, most of whom are people of color. You have the Kemp campaign running ads against you in which your skin color has been darkened. How much is racism driving the GOP effort to save the governorship in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I think unfortunately, what we`ve seen from \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is a continuation of 12 years of racist behavior when it comes to voting rights. Let`s be clear, this is the governor who purged 1.4 million voters, who disenfranchised and held hostage 50,000 voter registrations in 2018, who unfortunately this year -- or sorry, in 2021 passed a leading law that has become the pattern across the country for voter suppression SB 202. A law that makes it more difficult to apply for an absentee ballot, especially if you`re a senior citizen or if you are a person of color who speaks English as a second language.\par \par \uc2 He also authorized White Supremacist groups like those that are challenging thousands of voters across this counties in the state of Georgia. And they can continue to do this up to election day. What happened in Cobb County happened in Gwinnett. County, which is the most diverse county in the state. And what we have seen is that SB 202, under \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is a continuation of a racially charged approach to vote -- achieve voter suppression, because the electorate in Georgia is quickly changing and he does not like the change. And what happened in Cobb County, luckily, those challenges were dismissed. But the reality is, those challenges can continue because he`s outsourced voter purging.\par \par \uc2 And what is more concerning to me is that they are disproportionately people of color, disproportionately students who may receive this ominous letter in the mail within days of election -- of the election saying your right to vote has been challenged. And for many of these voters, they will see this as a reason not to vote. That is how it becomes voter suppression. So, whether they are successful or not in the challenges, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  authorized unlimited challenges to voting rights in the state of Georgia, and that is unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: According to Politico today, referring to the midterms and midterm polling, "what clouds the picture is that the much-discussed improvement for Democrats since the Dobb decision -- the Dobbs decision -- excuse me -- on abortion also coincided with a 99 day drop in gas prices. Now, gas prices are rising, and despite a good jobs report on Friday, the news is filled with gloomy headlines about the economy.\par \par \uc2 So, is the dubs effect fading as the economic storm clouds gather? That`s what Politico asks. I`m wondering do you think abortion, what are you seeing on the doorstep, is it falling out of focus as a major issue this fall?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Not at all. It is such a clear issue for women in the state of Georgia. You have to remember, we have 82 counties without an OBGYN, 64 counties that do not have a general surgeon, 18 counties that do not have a family doctor, nine counties without a doctor at all. We`re about to lose a level one trauma center in mid --in the center of Atlanta, one of only five in the state. The sixth hospital to shut down under this governor.\par \par \uc2 Under this governor, one in five have women does not have access to health care. And now with the specter of forced pregnancy, because \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is the governor of Georgia, this is a daily conversation because it is as much an economic issue as it is a health care issue. And \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has proven time and again he does not care about the women of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:19]\par \par \uc2 He does not care about their health care, he does not care about their economic situation. And under his extreme and dangerous policies, we`ve lost the all-star game, we`ve lost access to music Midtown, and we are going to lose the entertainment industry, because he continues to put his own personal choices about our freedoms ahead of the needs of Georgians.\par \par \uc2 And what we know is what we saw in -- what we saw in Kansas, what we saw in New York, what we saw in Alaska, and what we`re seeing right now in voter application, submissions. 60 percent of which are coming from women. That is a high watermark we have never seen in Georgia. And I think it shows that women are very much concerned about the future of their lives, the concerns about their bodies, and their right to health care and the right to make their own choices. And as the next Governor of Georgia, I can repeal that law and protect the women of Georgia and the women of the South.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, sticking with this issue of abortion and Republican extremism and hypocrisy, there`s this controversy surrounding \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . It`s caused Geoff Duncan, the lieutenant governor to publicly disowned Walker. But most Republicans in Georgia and nationwide have not done that. Top. Republican senators are going to Georgia to campaign with him. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , as you mentioned earlier, the governor who you`re running against, has not disowned Walker or unendorsed him.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of the allegations against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? Is he a hypocrite if they`re true? And should \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  be disowning \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  at this point?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Hypocrisy is a contagion in the Republican Party. And it seems to have zero -- patient zero here in Georgia and \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . If he believes as he states that there should not be a right to an abortion, then he should absolutely stand in that position. But that is not the issue here. He does not believe in a woman`s right to choose. He does not believe in the freedom that we should enjoy the freedom to vote, the freedom to control our bodies. He also opposes marriage equality.\par \par \uc2 And we know that right now, what we`re facing in Georgia is the possibility of essentially a governor who`s either in line and in lockstep with \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , as he dismantles our freedom, or a governor who stands up to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , and stands up for Georgians. That`s my intent. And I believe that in 29 days, that will be the reality.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And last question for you. We had Stuart Stevens, the former Republican strategist on the show earlier. He said Democrats should be running on democracy. That`s what they should be putting on the ballot. Is that the top issue for you in your run or is it abortion rights? Is it the economy? What is it?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Democracy undergirds every issue because the only way we get the freedom to control our bodies, the only way we get education, health care, housing, the only way we can force leaders to listen to us is through democracy. And that is why I will always stand up for voters and demand access. That is why I`ve pushed back every time I hear people lauding \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Refusing to deny the outcome of election does not absolve you of your participation in denying access to elections. And that has been the raison d`etre for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  for more than 12 years. And this year, it has to end. And that is why I`m encouraging every Georgian to understand that democracy has to be defended. And we can do that on November 8.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , we will have to leave it there. Thank you so much for your time. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: It`s going to be a fascinating race in Georgia both in the Senate and for the governorship. But still ahead, tonight, after another embarrassing setback, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  lashes out with airstrikes against Ukrainian civilians. The man you probably know as Luke Skywalker, Mark Hamill, also serves now as an ambassador for Ukraine`s drone program. You can watch my interview with him on that issue next. Don`t go away.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Today, Russia launched the most extensive and intense bombing of Ukraine since the invasion began targeting infrastructure and civilians. Roads, power plants, and even a playground with damaged or destroyed. The Ukrainian government says at least 14 people are dead and dozens more are injured. The Russian missiles strike across the country including the capital city of Kyiv, and the western city of Lviv.\par \par \uc2 The Kremlin says the strikes are retaliation for an explosion that destroyed a key bridge connecting Russia and annexed Crimea on Saturday, Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  said he spoke with President Biden after the attacks today and that air defense "is currently the number one priority."\par \par \uc2 That has been a primary focus of Ukraine`s defense strategy for months in fact, and Zelenskyy has been asking for help on that front from everyone, even from celebrities like Luke Skywalker himself, actor Mark Hamill, who just became an ambassador for Ukraine drone program. And in his first American TV interview on my show on Sunday night, he told me what it was like getting that call from Zelenskyy and what he`s being asked to do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: I was really surprised to be contacted by President Zelenskyy. I assume -- I didn`t ask why. I mean, I think they saw my support for Ukraine on my \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed. But, you know, it`s -- I`m not used to be being contacted by world leaders. You know, I`m a court jester. I am -- I`m a non-essential worker. I do cartoon voices and TV and movies. So, I was honored to be contacted by him.\par \par \uc2 And basically, what they were wanting me to do was become the so-called ambassador, which is a glorified word for representative of their army of drones, because drones define war outcomes. There -- it`s essential for Ukraine to protect their border, to protect their citizens. And, that`s what I -- that`s what I hope people will do. Over 115 countries have donated to various causes, not just -- yes.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:36]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: We know that the President wants these drones to kind of maintain air surveillance. We know that Russian air dominance was his problem at the start of the war. We know that there`s a very long border between Ukraine and Russia that needs monitoring. But are all these drones, non-lethal drones, lethal drones, a mix of drones?\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: I think it`s a mix. Obviously, the Russians have many, many more drones than Ukraine does. And they are, you know, used to bomb civilians and so forth. But you know, the -- one thing I wanted to mention, too, if you have -- you know, you can donate money. But if you have an actual drone, you could donate that, or dronate that, as they say.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: And -- well, what attracted you to this role? Why did you want to do -- and why do you think Ukraine has captured the imagination and support of the American people like no other foreign conflict or country that I can think of in living memory?\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: Well, we`ve never seen a country unilaterally invaded, I mean, in my lifetime. I guess it goes back to World War II. But it`s the classic, you know, David versus Goliath. You know, everyone expected Ukraine to just immediately surrender to this invading force. And I think, to see them fight back with such courage. It`s been an inspiration to see.\par \par \uc2 Today`s Republicans though, the Marjorie Taylor Greenes, the \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , or Donald Trumps, they don`t really share that view. They don`t really want us to be funding or supporting Ukraine. They don`t think Russia is that bad. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  marked -- just said yesterday, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  almost forced Putin to invade Ukraine by taunting him with dumb rhetoric. That`s Trump`s words. What do you make of the -- what is your view of Russia and what do you make of the GOP view of Russia?\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: Well, you know, someone asked me in an interview with if my -- the actors in Star Wars supported Ukraine, and I said, well, I haven`t spoken to them personally, but who would support Russia? And then I thought, oh, yes, Fox News, OAN Newsmax. It`s really, really strange. I don`t get it. But we live in the fact-based community. Unfortunately, when you have people that believe the election was stolen based on no evidence, you know, you`re entitled to your own facts -- you`re entitled to your own opinion, you`re not entitled to your own facts.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the thing that I think is -- that the former president is -- the damage will last for years and years because he`s convinced his supporters to believe that we don`t have free and fair election, despite no evidence whatsoever. And that`s going to last for a long time to come.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: So, you`re clearly very political. That`s how you and I found each other on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with our political tweet.\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: Right.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Just yesterday, you mocked \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  saying, even if he turns up to a debate with \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , Warnock will still be addressing an empty podium. Have you always been politically engaged, politically passionate?\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: Well, I have been but I`m not publicly. In other words, I`ve always voted as I said, No one`s ever asked for my help until Michael Dukakis asked me to go out on the campaign trail. It was in the last month or so. So, he was in a downward spiral for sure. But what happened was that when I got on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , I think your personality comes out and your likes and dislikes, because I`m not an activist. You know, I`m not a pundit. But you know, I`m just an American citizen who has an opinion.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: One last question I have to ask, Mark, before I let you go. It`s a Star Wars question from a fanboy.\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: What was it like to be back on the screen as Luke Skywalker? Well, kind of back on screen, aided by CGI in the TV series the Mandalorian on \uc2 Disney\uc2  plus after all those years? I mean, I literally like many whooped out loud when you arrived in that fight scene in the season two finale.\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: Well, John Favreau and Dave Filoni invited me over to watch some footage. They said they wanted my opinion. I thought well, that`s unusual. No one has done that before when it came to Star Wars. Certainly, not in the sequel. So, I went over. They showed me the footage. And then they proposed was this idea which I was gobsmacked, as you British are fond of saying, because you know the de-aging process and the whole thing. I mean, I never thought I go back and play that part again.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: We`re sadly out of time but I appreciate you joining me. Mark Hamill, may the force be with you.\par \par \uc2 HAMILL: Thanks so much for having me, Mehdi.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASAN: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. May the force be with you too. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel. Congratulations on your podcast.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221011eiaa00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for October 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 7,316 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, the quiet part out loud.\par \par \uc2 Right now with barely four weeks before midterm elections, Republican candidates have reverted to what has always worked for them, racism and moral hypocrisy on cue, as expected. Last night, Alabama Republican now Tommy Tuberville joined \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at a rally for MAGA candidates in Nevada, the sitting senator from one of the nation`s blackest states said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (R-AL): The Democratic Party, they have a majority. They can stop this crime today. Some people say, well, they are soft on crime. No, they are not soft on crime. They are pro-crime. They want crime. And they want crime because they want to take over what you got. They want to control what you have. They want reparations because they think that people that do the crimes are owed that (BLEEP). They are not owed that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`ll note that Tuberville stopped short of identifying just who these criminals are, just who it is the Democrats want reparative justice for. But then for his audience, identification is unnecessary. Because whether it`s a campaign ad or a Trump rally, GOP candidates in swing states have weaponized crime as the cudgel against Democrats. And with that, they`ve embraced a time honored tradition on the right, when all else fails, blame black people, even in states that they barely live in.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the irony of insurrection apologies, making neck and knee racist statements while criticizing Democrats for being soft on crime is not lost on me. But what gives me just as much pause as the racism is that more than half of the Republican general election candidates seeking statewide office or federal office are in denial that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the presidency two years ago. Some of them have made this theme central to their campaigns. And they are on the cusp of potentially remaking Congress.\par \par \uc2 As you can tell, we are starting off strong tonight on POLITICSNATION, and that is noble. Here we go.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congresswoman Kweise Mfume, Democrat of Maryland. Congressman, I appreciate you being here, as always. I have to start off with your reaction to that sound we played at the top, sitting Republican Alabama Senator Tommy Tuberville saying Democrats want crime, that they want to take what Republican MAGA voters have presumably so they say can give reparations to those responsible for crime.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Tuberville is not the first or only GOP politician that employed dog whistles connecting Democrats and black people to crime ahead of next month`s elections, but you also have Senate races in Wisconsin and Pennsylvania, Republican candidates in both those races attacking Democrats over the issue. Some Democratic critics calling the attacks racist dog whistles. Though, in Tuberville`s case, it`s more like a scream. What should Democrats do in response, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 REP. KWEISE MFUME (D-MD): Democrats should take note, first of all, that these are not things that periodically happened. It always happens right before a major national election. And his comments are about the most vicious, vile, repugnant, parochial, racist things that I`ve heard in a long, long time. This is how violence gets started against black people, as in the case in Buffalo, against Latinos, as in the case of Uvalde, and people who simply worship it, as in the Charleston Church, because people take that, the sick once, and they`ve figured that they have got to do something to extend the senator`s philosophy.\par \par \uc2 I would hope that every elected official on both sides of the aisle condemn that. He is a bigot. And until he says something different, he will always be seen as a bigot. But more importantly, there are a lot of black people and that`s state. In fact, you know, a large number of black voters who are Democrats and Republicans who are outraged, I`m sure, at this as well.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:03]\par \par \uc2 So, we see this, it`s a playbook. It happens every time we get close to a national election. And in this instance, it has just simply gone too far. Black people have had it being the targets of crime and the targets of this and the targets that. Every time there`s a reason something is wrong, we are the ones that are responsible.\par \par \uc2 And nobody is asking him to give a comment on reparations. Reparations were already voted on in the Congress in the 1980s for Japanese-Americans. It`s not something new. Even the bill that`s there just creates conditions to study and report back. So, he`s couched that in the framework of they won`t what you have, just what he said. They want to take what you have, which is what he implied, and they will take it and turn you around, which is the summation. It`s a bad, bad race, a bad track to get on. I would hope people speak up. And I doubt very seriously if Tuberville will ever apologize.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And let`s not be confused with the reparations that is now being moved into Congress by your fellow -- your colleague, Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2 , talking about reparations for African-Americans. He said that we were getting crime as reparations. So, clearly, this is more than one can bear.\par \par \uc2 But, Congressman, going back to your former life as head of the \uc2 NAACP\uc2 , and I worked with you as the head of the National Action Network when you were there, with you championed that reform of marijuana laws together with those of us in the civil rights community at the time, including reparative justice for communities harmed by the war on drugs.\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to get your take on President Biden federally pardoning thousands of Americans incarcerated for simple marijuana possession and calling on the Department of Justice and Health and Human Services, respectively, to review marijuana federal drug classification. What do you want to see happen next?\par \par \uc2 MFUME: Well, I think people have to couch it in the right terms and to know that marijuana usage is a similar, whether you are an African- American, Latino, white American, or anything else, there is no disparate use because of certain group that you are in that`s racial.\par \par \uc2 This situation has gone on for a long time. As you know, most of the states in the nation are moving toward decriminalizing marijuana. I think the president is doing what where we are today. He`s doing where states are in terms of taking a look at this and realizing that it`s a health issue more than anything else. Decriminalizing it to get rid of the crime and making sure that people have a chance to be able to apply for a job, be able to get employment, get an education, find themselves as a productive member of society, again, without being tainted by this curse of a sentence as a result of marijuana possession or marijuana usage.\par \par \uc2 So, he, in my opinion, is doing the right thing at the right time. He`s following the states in this regard. And the whole issue of marijuana is something that the country is going to deal with, whether he deals with it or not. So, I applaud his efforts in that regard.\par \par \uc2 Quickly, we are out of time, Congressman, but I want to make sure we have time to hear about this bill you and Maryland Democratic Senator Ben Cardin introduced last month to create a national council of African-American history and culture. What does it entail and what are you trying to push back against that`s happening in red state classrooms right now?\par \par \uc2 MFUME: Well, what we are trying to push back against is this notion that critical race theory is taking over our schools and destroying our kids and making some of them feel guilty. The fact that folks are being removed from libraries every day and the fact that a lot of teaching now has been performed so that the teaching of American history does not include the contributions or the work of particularly African-Americans, is moving at a rapid rate.\par \par \uc2 So, this bill creates, within the national endowment of the humanities and arts, an opportunity for us to create a 12-member commission designed to study and to report back on African-American history and culture since the beginning of the nation. We think it`s the right thing to do. Many teachers have applauded us on this. And Senator Ben Cardin of Maryland and myself have put forth that legislation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Congressman Kweisi Mfume, thank you for joining us again.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is former Attorney General under President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , the first African-American attorney general in the history of this country, \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , chair of the National Democratic Redistricting Committee.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Holder, first of all, we appreciate you being with us tonight, and let me get to why you are here first and foremost. The Supreme Court reconvened last week, hearing oral arguments Monday over whether to uphold Alabama`s voting map, in which six of seven congressional districts are majority white and Republican, only one is majority black.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:16]\par \par \uc2 The imbalance was noted by a federal court panel earlier this year, which joint plaintiffs in ruling that the map diminished black voters` influence by confining it to what, again, is one district in the state that is nearly 30 percent black, in violation of the Voting Rights Act. Alabama pled to the Supreme Court to block that ruling. In February, five of the court`s six conservative justices agreed that it be revisited, allowing the map to stand during next month`s election.\par \par \uc2 The kicker, for me, is that Alabama has argued that drawing a second district for black voters would itself be racially discriminatory because it would favor black voters at the expense of other voters. Yet, with all that, it appears after this past week, the conservative majority on the Supreme Court is poised to uphold the map. Do you have -- first of all, do I have all that right, Mr. Attorney General?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: Yes, you do have it right. I mean, African-Americans comprise about 27 percent of the population in Alabama. The black population in Alabama is sufficiently numerous and geographically compact so that you could draw a second black opportunity district, which is consistent with the Voting Rights Act passed in 1965, interpreted by the Supreme Court previously, reauthorized overwhelmingly by Congress. And every time it was reauthorized, it was signed by a Republican president.\par \par \uc2 For them to rule against the plaintiffs, and these are plaintiffs who we have supported, to rule against the plaintiffs and to rule for the state, this Supreme Court would have to reinterpret the Voting Rights Act of 1965, which, as I said, has historically had overwhelming support.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Mr. Holder, according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , 299 of the Republican Party`s candidates for Congress and top state executive positions are election deniers. That`s more than half of all GOP candidates. More than 170 of them favored to win their race. In addition to holding power over state elections, election deniers, in what may become a Republican controlled House, could end up choosing that next speaker, who could potentially preside over another contested presidential election in 2024. How do you fight this kind of existential threat to our democracy, Mr. Holder?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, one of the things we have to do is focus on making sure that we elect people at the state legislative level who are going to stand for our democracy. And here is the deal. I think that inflation is important, crime is obviously important, we need to deal with those issues, the price of gas, the ability to get baby formula. But the most important thing that`s on the ballot this November is our democracy itself. What kind of nation are we going to have?\par \par \uc2 Half of the people, as you say, half of the people running for Congress in these important state races are election deniers. If they are elected, they have already indicated they don`t believe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the election back in 2020 and they have pledged to use their power to try to make sure -- and one of them talked about having a coalition of secretaries of state to make sure that things are done, in his words, the right way the next time. And I`m sure that will probably mean something that` inconsistent with the way people want to have the votes counted but consistent with their ideological desires.\par \par \uc2 So, our democracy is on the ballot this November, and I hope that will drive people to the polls.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I want to leave some time get your take on the current climate ahead of the midterm elections, because I respect your judgment, as much as any five people in the country, and one of the other five is Mrs. Holden, but we will talk about that off air. But Republican Senator Tommy Tuberville`s comments last night at a Nevada Trump rally, suggesting that Democrats want crime so they can take from MAGA voters and give whatever it is they are taking to criminals in the form of reparations. As a veteran of the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration, what`s your reaction to that kind of rhetoric?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, you know, people, they will show you who they are by what it is they say, by what it is that they do. I`d like to hear this senator tell us exactly who they are, who you`re referring to here.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:00]\par \par \uc2 He demonstrated who is. He`s a person who made a reputation as, I guess, a pretty good football coach. He`s a terrible senator but a pretty good football coach on the backs of young black men, you know, who sacrificed, risked concussions, and all the other things in order that he might be successful. And now, as a representative of the state of Alabama, as, again, as I said, with a 27 percent population that African-American, he is saying things that are, at best, at best, race-tinged, and at worst, racist.\par \par \uc2 And I think that people in the Republican Party need to take him to task. I need to hear from other Republicans` criticism of what it is he said. And I want to hear the Republicans say that they have got to distance themselves from those remarks. What he said is extremely dangerous. It`s the kind of thing that we have seen too often in this country, the kind of racial divisiveness, racial polarization that leads to frequently to violence. And so I would hope that we will see him taken to task for what are some really awful remarks.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the logic of what he is saying, he sounds like a damn fool, in addition to everything else.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And the fact of the matter is adding insult to injury, but many of the crime -- much of the crime that is happening is certainly not all done by blacks and much of the crime that is done by blacks are against other blacks. So, what preparations are we talking about?\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this. You were assistant attorney general in the 90s with many of us debated, and you were part of those discussions about how we deal with drugs, how we make sure that we don`t overincarcerate. How do you deal with -- respond or react to President Biden`s pardon of marijuana possession on a federal level this weekend, calling for governors to do the same on a state level?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: I think that`s the exact right approach. I mean, to make sure that people who have been charged with or convicted of federal possession of marijuana get pardoned, to talk about the rescheduling of marijuana, it is now schedule 1 drug, which is just absurd, and then call in the states to make sure that they do similar kinds of things. That`s consistent with what we try to do during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  years when I was attorney general, as I tried to unwind this so-called war on drugs, which has had an impact, a disproportionate negative impact on communities of color, it was unfair, when you look at the crack powder disparity, and a whole range of other things.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that what the president did in announcing this new policy is exactly right and it`s consistent with where the nation is. The nation views marijuana use now in 2022 in a fundamentally different way than it did back in the 1990s. And our laws need to reflect this societal change in the youth in the way in which we view the use of marijuana.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I know. And, again, I note, you took that position when you were assistant attorney general in the 90s, and when you became attorney general, you tried to unwind it, and I can be a witness to that.\par \par \uc2 Former U.S. Attorney General Eric Holden, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, we are checking it on a historic and hotly contested Senate race in North Carolina. Democratic Candidate Cheri Beasley, Judge Cheri Beasley, joins us fresh off of Friday`s fiery debate.\par \par \uc2 And later, President Biden pardons thousands of nonviolent marijuana offenders, and it was high time. Now, the push has begun to spark a similar reform movement in the states.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a good Sunday to you. A Russian missile attack Sunday struck an apartment block in residential buildings in the southeastern Ukrainian city of Zaporizhzhia. It killed at least 13 people and injured 87 others. This includes ten children, according to Ukrainian officials. The city is home to Europe`s largest power plant.\par \par \uc2 South Korean officials warn North Korea`s recent missile launches could signal it soon resuming nuclear testing. North Korea fired two short-range missiles into its eastern waters this weekend. This is the seventh launch in recent days.\par \par \uc2 And Hurricane Julia made landfall this morning in Nicaragua. Life- threatening flash floods and mudslides are expected. That storm has since weakened to a tropical storm but is still producing winds up to 70 miles an hour.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. There`s 30 days left until the midterm elections and early voting kicks off in North Carolina in just over a week. The race for Senate in the Tar Heel State is going down to the wire. According to the RealClearPolitics average, Republican Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  leads in the polling by just a percentage point-and-a-half over his Democratic opponent, Cheri Beasley.\par \par \uc2 Former North Carolina Supreme Court Justice Cheri Beasley joins me now. Justice Beasley, let`s start with the core issue in your campaign, reproductive rights. Your opponent, Congressman Budd, is one of the co- sponsors of the House version of a national abortion ban that would ban abortions after 15 weeks. How has the overturning of Roe redefined your campaign and what are your plans to address this issue if you are successfully elected to the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SENATE CANDIDATE CHERI BEASLEY (D-NC): You know, reverend al, this is a really critical issue for folks in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 People are angry and they are afraid. They are scared. I heard from the grandfather just a few nights ago who was very fearful for his granddaughters.\par \par \uc2 And while my opponent, Congressman Budd, is a cosponsor for this legislation, he has been very clear that he has aligned himself with the extremist faction of his party and has promoted an absolute ban on abortion without exceptions for rape, incest or risk to a mother`s health. And so we know that that means that women who are sexually assaulted will be forced to carry the pregnancy to term, women with ectopic pregnancies miscarriages their bodies won`t release or septic uteruses. I mean, the lifesaving treatment for those conditions is an abortion. And if they can`t get it, it means when women will die, and that`s unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 We know the real fundamental question here is that this is the right of a woman and her position to make this decision without government interference. And I will fight in the Senate to make sure Roe v. Wade is the law of the land and it has been a constitutional right for almost 50 years.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, a new \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  report reveals nearly 300 Republican nominees on the ballot this November across the country are election deniers. This figure includes eight for North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Now, during your Senate debate on Friday, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  acknowledged that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the president of the United States, although he was one of many members of Congress who voted against certifying the 2020 election. Now, we reached out to your Republican opponent, invited him on the program to explain this. But what do you make of his past and now his present stances on this very fundamental issue to our democracy?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: Well, you know, Reverend Al, he`s been dancing around this issue. And the reality is, at January 6th, he called those folks just patriots standing up. And even after all of that violence, he voted against certifying the election. And even today, when he was asked during that forum whether he would accept the election results in this election, he was dancing around the issue and what`s not clear about whether or not he would.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the reality is we have to believe that elections are administered in a fair way and I will support and accept the results of the 2022 election. But what we also know is that Republicans have spent millions and millions against me in my race. I`ve served as a judge and chief justice of the Supreme Court for over two decades. And they are working really, really hard and they don`t spend that kind of money unless they know that they could absolutely lose this race.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And it`s very difficult to paint you as soft on crime when you`ve been the one sitting on the bench for the last couple decades.\par \par \uc2 But you`ve highlighted also your opponent`s ties to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The former president has endorsed Congressman Budd and campaigned with him. During Friday`s debate, Representative Budd, tried to turn the tables, calling you a rubberstamp for President Biden. What is your response to this? Do you feel this race will be defined by national politics or issues closer to home in North Carolina?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY\uc2 : I`m talking with folks in North Carolina about what they care about, which is the fact that the next senator needs to fight hard to lower costs. The bottom line is Congressman Budd has to defend his rhetoric. He has done not a thing for North Carolina. He`s been in Congress for six years and he`s voted in the interest of corporate interests, special interests, and his own, and not the interests of the people here in the state.\par \par \uc2 He voted against lowering the cost of gas and took thousands of dollars from big oil companies. He voted against lowering the cost of medications and took thousands of dollars from big pharma.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, Congressman Budd has shown who he is, he does not speak for North Carolina. And for all the ways in which people need the next senator to be responsive, he has not done so. And when people show you who they are, you better believe them.\par \par \uc2 I would ask your audience to please, for more information about my campaign, to please go to cheribeasley.com. Democracy is on the line. And we`ve got to fight back and make sure that not only we are successful for today, but this election is so important for our future.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We are out of time, but let me ask you this briefly. Today, there have been only 11 black U.S. senators elected in the nation`s history and two were black women. What would it mean to you not only to represent the Tar Heel State you love but also become one of the few black women to ever hold this position?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY\uc2 : I`m not at all -- it doesn`t fall short on me that representation really does matters in all our government (INAUDIBLE), whether it`s the Supreme Court, or the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . And I certainly know there are a whole host of issues that I`m prepared to fight for, for North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:02]\par \par \uc2 It`s important that we have diversity and the -- our talents represented at the table. And while it`s important that how the Senate looks, it is also important how the Senate works. And what I know is that it is absolutely not working for North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Cheri Beasley, the Democratic candidate for Senate in North Carolina, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told his supporters to fight like hell and some of them did. The January 6 Committee is gearing up to make its strongest case yet against the former president. Our political panel will have a preview, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Let`s turn to my political panel for their insights on today`s top stories. Kimberly Atkins, a \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2  Columnist and Political Analyst, and Rick Tyler, Republican Strategist and Co-Founder of foundry strategies.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:06]\par \par \uc2 Kimberly, in four days the January 6 Committee will reconvene for its final public hearing. Let`s take a listen to former senior technical advisor to the committee on the Sunday show about how you expect investigators to make critical connections?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORMER REP. DENVER RIGGLEMAN, (R) VIRGINIA: They`re marching to the tune of one drummer. And that drummer was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who really was the person who said this election was overthrown. And by December 19, after his tweet, we see sort of a full court press into January 6.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What do you expect? Well, will Thursday culminate to a grand finale for the Committee?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, BOSTON GLOBE COLUMNIST: I think that`s what I`m expecting. I think that`s what it has to do. A crucial part of the committee`s work is gathering facts and explaining in the way the American people can understand what happened and understand the importance of what happens. And so, I think this final hearing, will both be revealing whatever is left for this committee in their fact finding, and they`ve said that there is more in terms of footage, in terms of evidence that they`re going to put forward. But they need to bring it all together to paint a picture of exactly what this means. I think that that is right. That it will, in fact, point to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a very meaningful way, but also involve other people.\par \par \uc2 One thing I`m looking for is what if anything, the Committee says about the recent testimony of Ginni Thomas, the wife of Supreme Court Justice, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , she`s one of the folks who have been interviewed since the last hearing. And, of course, she was at January 6. And she -- we know from reporting that she urged people in various states, lawmakers in various states not to certify the results of those elections. So that`s one piece that I`m looking for. But I think that this will really tie together the whole story, the Committee has been trying to tell all these months.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, what will you be looking for, as from the January 6 Committee, and also political reports, Republicans are winning on pocketbook issues, but GOP governor`s attempts to make immigration more of a top concern for voters fell flat. Now, right now, the top three issues nationally, are still the economy, threats to democracy and abortion. According to the polling, Republican governors, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Greg Abbott said Venezuelan migrants to northern states to make a political point. It didn`t fare well across the country. But what does this move say to their base ahead of the reelection bids?\par \par \uc2 RICK TYLER, MSNBC REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, first, let me say about the January 6 Committee, I too, am looking for them to essentially make an indictment, not a legal indictment, but an indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s where this is all leading to because many people have been convicted and including someone who got a nine-year sentence who`d never entered the Capitol building because he was seen as coordinating events that led to the breach of the Capitol building, and the fighting against the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in that category. And so, I think they`re going to lay out Donald Trump`s culpability in all this in a very powerful way. In terms of the midterm elections, I think anybody who says they know exactly what`s going to happen, doesn`t know what they`re talking about. But there are two major issues and one is an economic issue. The other is the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 A month ago, the Dobbs decision was quite strong, and Democrats certainly polled that they were going to vote based on the Dobbs decision. Since then, the intensity of that issue has diminished. It`s still strong, but it`s not as strong as it was. In any end, most Republicans and most independents still look at pocketbook issues. So, to the Democrats, of course, and they`re hit by them every day. So, gas prices have come down a little bit. But overall food prices, gas prices, inflation is quite high that impacts people`s ability to spend money in which they want to or make ends meet, if you will. So, I think that`s probably strong for the Republicans in the closing of weeks of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me stay with you a minute, Rick. This Tuesday, Senators \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  and Tom Cotton are going to rally for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Senate candidate in Georgia. Now, this comes as Donald Trump`s Super PAC is swooping in at the last minute to buy ads for the Georgia Republican Senate candidate following a bruising week of headlines. What do you make of Republicans unwavering support of Walker in the wake of the recent allegations against him?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Well, it`s interesting because the Republicans abandoned Todd Akin for a mere utterance and not an act. But here they are backing up \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and it all has to do with power. But \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  will be on the ballot. There`s no possibility of removing him from the ballot. So, they`re going to try to get him across the finish line any way they can. But they are looking rather hypocritical doing so.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Kimberly, Alabama`s Senator Tommy Tuberville delivered an overtly racist statement during his speech at a Trump rally last night in Havana where he mentioned Democrats awful reparations, owed to people who do crimes. What does statements like that, what does it say about the politics of today? Are some Republicans willing to lean more into racist, rhetoric to appeal to their base?\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: In a word, yes. I mean, I think they believe that what has worked in the past, is fear and demonization. We saw that with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , most clearly talking about building the wall and how people who crossed the border from southern countries are rapists and murderers. It`s easy to use fear, to get the idea that people of color who are just entitled to equal rights as everyone else, and lawmakers who are trying to ensure that are trying to give something to scary people and black people have been demonized as scary for the entire history of our nation. And that`s a playbook that Republicans have repeatedly gone to, the same way that with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , they`re hoping that the same thing that happened after Donald Trump`s Access Hollywood tape was released what happened again, which is voters wouldn`t care.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure that they don`t -- I`m not sure that the Republican establishment may be behind these kinds of tactics. I`m not sure that all Republican voters are. And one thing that I`m taking some hope in and that`s all that can have is that when we talk about concern for democracy being a big issue on the minds of voters, it`s not just on the minds of Democratic voters, although the percentage is higher on that side. Republicans are concerned about that, too, yes. So, what that means is in the behind -- in the eye of the beholder, maybe they think it has to do with election fraud or something. That talked to Republicans were genuinely concerned with democracy, gas prices go up and down. But democracy is fragile, and if that`s broken, that`s difficult to repair. So, I`m hoping that this time around that the regular playbook doesn`t work the exact same way but I think Rick is right, we won`t know until the election results are counted.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Kimberly Atkins and Rick Tyler, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, legalize weed has big business, and it has been big binges in many states for several years. But it took until this week for an American president to start repairing the damage unjust marijuana laws have done to African Americans across this country. We`re going to be talking about much needed next steps when we come right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:46:42]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  announced on Thursday that he will grant pardons to 1000s of Americans charged with simple marijuana possession. He also ordered a review of federal marijuana regulations, and urged state governors to take similar actions.\par \par \uc2 My next guest has been at the forefront of the fight for reform of the country`s marijuana laws, we`re join by Congressman Troy Carter, who represents Louisiana`s Second District.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, you are the lead sponsor of the bipartisan Marijuana Misdemeanor and Expungement Act, which seeks to clear the records of people of the people who have been pardoned by President Joe Biden`s executive order. Why do you feel it`s crucial to take this one step further, to clear the records of the 1000s of people who have been charged for simple marijuana offenses?\par \par \uc2 REP. TROY CARTER, (D) LOUISIANA: Thank you very much, Reverend Al for having us here to talk about this very important issue. The expungement is the natural next step. Having a pardon, obviously is huge. And I applaud the President for this action. We were in the White House in April to talk about this very thing. Very happy and very proud and honored that the President is moving forward with the pardons. But the natural next step is the expungement. Because we know that people that have these cases on their record, can`t access federal loans, in some cases, can`t access jobs, and some cases, can access housing in many cases.\par \par \uc2 So, this gives us an opportunity to have a clean start particularly given that in some 38 states, marijuana in some shape, form or fashion has been legalized. And so, to have people that are still saddled with this mark, when we know that the prosecutions have been disproportionately heavier in communities of black and brown, this is an opportunity to set the record straight and to balance the scales, if you will, in the way of true justice.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, not a decades long war on drugs has led to an explosion of the prison population, mostly targeting black and Latino communities. Following the announcement on Thursday, activists and lawmakers said that these marijuana pardons represent a step forward racial equity with this executive action. President Biden said he was trying to address decades of discriminatory drug policies that disproportionately hurt or harmed people of color.\par \par \uc2 Now, studies have found that despite similar consumption patterns, black Americans are nearly four times as likely as whites to be arrested for marijuana related violations, though they have the same pattern of using and possessing marijuana. Take -- what is your take on this, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: We see that every day, we have seen it for years. And unfortunately, we still see that level of disproportionate prosecution, disproportionate arrest, disproportionate challenges or arresting individuals or probable cause.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:02]\par \par \uc2 And oftentimes that is a use that turns into something that`s escalated, that ends up being dangerous and deadly. So, we know that taking away these tools, if you will, for -- let`s say rogue cops, who are looking for reasons to pick a certain segment out to challenge them or to abuse their rights. We`re balanced in that field to make it level. This is all a part of the criminal justice reform. This is all about making sure that people are not being held in prisons for misdemeanors or being labeled for the rest of their lives for something again, that is legal. And I underscore that is legal, and people are making millions of dollars in our country illegally selling cannabis. Yet, people and many communities are still treated as if it were a drug that were being prosecuted at the fullest extent.\par \par \uc2 We also know that the President is asking that there`s a revisitation on the schedule. We know that marijuana, believe it or not, is actually scheduled in the same class with heroin higher than fentanyl. And fentanyl is about as deadlier drug --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Higher than fentanyl?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: Yeah. Yes, sir, higher --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me say one point you just touched on, Texas Governor Greg Abbott said on Thursday that he won`t consider President Biden`s call for governors to pardon simple state possession offenses. His opponent \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  said he would.\par \par \uc2 Now meanwhile, research shows that support for legal marijuana is currently at a record high of 68%. Recreational marijuana use is currently legal in 19 states, and is decriminalizing in 27. As you better proponent of marijuana legalization in your state of Louisiana, what do you think is stopping more state from legalizing the substance?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I`d be honest with you and telling you that I think part of what has been slowing the process down is some are waiting on figuring out how they can monetize it. This is we`ve seen this happen with casino gambling. We`ve seen this happen in many cases where the engine to slow down an enterprise until some have an opportunity to figure out how to capture or monetize it. Instead of allowing the marketplace to determine it, many states have allowed the marketplace and I think that`s what we should do. And I think what ends up happening, why you find this slow roll into these things. And not necessarily because of a disbelief and the efficacy of the legislation, many times it`s because in an effort to control the economic engine of it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, and while I have you, Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 , who represents your state of Louisiana, recently released a new ad campaign, a campaign that I should say, as he seeks reelection this midterm season. And then he uses language that underscores rhetoric on the war on drugs saying, "If you hate cops, just because they`re cops, the next time you get in trouble, Call a Crackhead." What`s your reaction to an ad like that?\par \par \uc2 CARTER: I think it`s unfortunate that Senator Kennedy chooses to blow the dog whistle of a racism. Listen, we all recognize that there are good cops, and there are bad cops. But it`s somehow suggested to use this dog whistle of an attack to use hurtful language about an issue that we know is in fact as much an issue as the opioid crises. When people were addicted to crack in the mid-80s, it was oh lock them up. But then when it became an issue of opioids, then we recognize, wait a minute, there`s an illness associated with this. While the same thing is applicable for those people that are addicted to crack cocaine, we now recognize that we should do something about it by providing treatment.\par \par \uc2 Arrest people who break laws and commit crimes and people who are addicted to drugs should get treatment. And for Senator Kennedy to try to use this as a political ploy, it`s unfortunate, unnecessary, and it doesn`t do any good unifying our country, but moving forward with the resources we have to protect and to serve.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman Troy Carter, you succeeded a very effective Congressman \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2  and I was told him that it`s going to be hard once he left and went to the White House and he said you ought to see the guy coming behind me, in the early forecast, it`s been very positive. I heard a lot about you last week. We both read the Congressional Black Caucus. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:58:56]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I preached this morning at \uc2 Howard University\uc2  here in Washington, and I`m doing other colleges, I speak at Morehouse College later this week, I`m saying to young people, you have four weeks to the midterm elections. And you and your parents and everybody you know needs to vote.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because when you have people like governors standing up, using straight out racial references, calling reparations something that is aligned with crime, when you have senators saying crackheads is who you should call next time, there`s a crime. They are not even trying to be subtle. And they`re guessing and hoping that you won`t vote in numbers that will stop them.\par \par \uc2 When they`ve taken women`s right to choose, you have got to vote in big numbers. You want to know who`s on the ballot. You are. I`m not telling you who to vote for. I`m telling you come and vote for yourself and protect your interests. Anything else is not acceptable.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me, thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez, my friend, starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221010eia900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for October 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Susan del Percio, Juanita Tolliver \par \uc2 6,530 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:38]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Crunch time.\par \par \uc2 The midterms are just 30 days away and candidates are scrambling to make their final pitches to voters. As a country, we are confronted with serious issues, including a volatile economy, war in the Ukraine, weakening democratic institutions and the spiraling effects of climate change, and yet, many Republicans have narrowed their focus to a few familiar and divisive topics.\par \par \uc2 From sea to shining sea, GOP hopefuls are digging into a very old bag of racially-tinged tricks to paint a picture of a country ravaged by out-of- control criminality. There is little evidence to support these claims and even less to connect the supposed crime wave to any democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 This fall of discontent follows a summer of overreach. Many of the same Republicans applauded a Supreme Court ruling, allowing States to outlaw abortion and then double down on it by calling for a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans are understandably outraged to see a right they cherished for half a century vanish in the blink of an eye, and a true political cost to the GOP for their arrogance and hypocrisy on the topic is just beginning to materialize.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot to cover the perfect guest to talk about it. So let`s get started.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is New York Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Chair of the Democratic Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you, first of all, for joining us, and I want to go right to it. I`d like to hear about the Democratic message to voters in a moment. But first, I want your thoughts on how Republicans are framing this race. Take a look at this ad put out earlier this week by Louisiana Senator \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): Look, if you hate cops just because they are cops, the next time you get in trouble call a crackhead.\par \par \uc2 I`m \uc2 John Kennedy\uc2 , and I approve this message.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, if Senator Kennedy cared about drugs in his state, he would know the real problem isn`t crack cocaine, a drug the right has associated with Black people since the 1980s, it is opioids. Nearly half of all drug deaths in the State are caused by opioids, according to the latest data from the \uc2 Louisiana Department of Health\uc2  and these cases are being driven solely by street crime.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-four opioid prescriptions are being written for every hundred people in the State, but none of those facts mattered to Senator Kennedy, do they Congressman?\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): They don`t, and good evening, Rev. It is great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic vision for America will be to continue to defend family`s freedom and the flag and it is quite unfortunate that the Republican vision for America right now is anchored in fear, fake news, and fraudulent ideas. And the Kennedy ad is just an example of trying to distract the voters because Republicans over the last two years really have not articulated a single idea to address the challenges facing the American people.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, we`ve been getting big things done from the American Rescue Plan to the infrastructure investment and Jobs Act to the Inflation Reduction Act and other pieces of legislation that are going to lower costs, create better paying jobs, and allow for safer communities. More needs to be done and we are prepared to do that work.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let`s stay with the topic of drugs for a moment.\par \par \uc2 This week President Biden announced he would pardon thousands of Americans convicted of marijuana possession at the Federal level.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As head of the National Action Network, I applaud the move, but remain deeply concerned about the lack of justice on the State level even in States that have legalized marijuana, there are thousands of people, many of them Black, disproportionately Black, who have had their lives ruined for nonviolent offenses related to the drug, which is why what the President is doing his important -- very important.\par \par \uc2 Is there a role Congress can play in addressing this? And how do Democrats continue to make progress on the issue in an environment when anything they do will be used by Republicans to accuse them of being soft on crime.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Or we can be smart on crime and that involves fixing our broken criminal justice system and ensuring that individuals can successfully reenter society, use their natural God-given skills and ability to become productive citizens. That`s why I applaud the action that has been taken by President Biden, and certainly there are steps that we can take in the House of Representatives in the House, the effort to try to make sure that we are reforming our marijuana laws across the country, and enabling people to benefit from the legalization that has occurred in State after State after State about America.\par \par \uc2 That effort is being led by Chairman \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  of the Judiciary Committee, along with other leaders like Barbara Lee, and \uc2 Earl Blumenauer\uc2 ; In the Senate, Leader Schumer has legislation partnering with \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  and others that we hope might be taken up in the aftermath of the midterm elections, building upon the work that President Biden has done.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s turn to efforts to hold the House. There is a feeling this summer that Democrats had gained some positive momentum after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade, but as the election draws closer, races are tightening. In Illinois, Democrats are now worried they could end up losing a seat as the party continues to struggle in districts outside of major urban areas.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing as you talk to your fellow Democrats across the country right now?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, we have to make sure that the electorate understands this is not simply a referendum on the moment that we may find ourselves in. So of course, as a whole host of challenges coming out of the COVID-19 pandemic, but it is choice between Democrats who are going to continue to get big things done for everyday Americans and put people over politics, and extreme MAGA Republicans. They are extreme on reproductive freedom. We support it, they want to criminalize abortion care.\par \par \uc2 They are extreme on Social Security and Medicare. This is an important part of the fabric of our society in our view; Republicans actually want to end Social Security and Medicare in five years, and they of course are extreme on our democracy. We believe in it. Apparently, many Republicans do not.\par \par \uc2 And so when the electorate locks in on the stakes of this midterm election, we believe we have a strong chance to hold the House of Representatives based on the power of our accomplishments and our ideas.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s finish on the big picture. A month before the Election Day, while I`m often appalled by the Republican message to voters, there is no doubt they have one. We mentioned the imaginary crime wave GOP candidates are talking about all over the country. A "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " analysis this week also found that over half of Republican candidates are 2020 election deniers, which we have to conclude is a feature, not a bug in Republican politics, something meant to fire up their political base.\par \par \uc2 What is the message meant to fire up the Democratic base at a time in this cycle, when enthusiasm and turnout is critical?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Everything that we care about is on the ballot. Democracy itself is on the ballot. \uc2 Liberty\uc2  and justice for all is on the ballot. Reproductive freedom is certainly on the ballot leaning in to better paying jobs and economic opportunity in every single zip code is on the ballot and we cannot afford to turn the keys of the House of Representatives or the Senate over to these extreme MAGA Republicans who are going to take our country backward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you as always, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  from Brooklyn, New York I might add.\par \par \uc2 Now to a local Senate race with national implications. Joining me now Democratic candidate for Senate in Wisconsin, Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Lieutenant Governor Barnes, thank you for joining us tonight. And four weeks out from midterms right now, you`re in a very close race to unseat Wisconsin incumbent Republican Senator Ron Johnson, who has targeted you with a barrage of negative campaign ads, many of which fixate on crime in your State.\par \par \uc2 Of course, when it comes to certain types of crimes, like forcibly breaking into the US Capitol to overturn election results, the Senator has been far less punitive when describing the insurrectionists. Then there was this exchange in last night`s debate between you and the senator. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Whenever I see a police officer, I go up to him, I say thank you for your service. And if I have time, I say, please, don`t be dispirited by the loud few who are trying to defund you.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. MANDELA BARNES (D-WI): I`m sure he didn`t have the same interaction with 140 officers that were injured during the January 6 insurrection. One officer was stabbed with a metal stake, another crushed between a revolving door, another hit in the head with a fire extinguisher.\par \par \uc2 So, when we talk about respect for law enforcement, let`s talk about the 140 officers that he left behind because of an insurrection that he supported.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Anything you`d like to add a day later, Lieutenant Governor?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, absolutely. Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 And like you mentioned, there are certain things that just don`t count as prime to him. He called those folks patriots. He said they were tourists. He made up every single excuse in the books.\par \par \uc2 And then he talks starts to talk about cities like Milwaukee, and the only time he brings up Milwaukee is talk about it in some pejorative sense. And the reality is, he has pointed all his fingers, but has not lifted a single one that tried to help us.\par \par \uc2 Now, I`ve experienced that violence personally; I`ve lost too many friends that I care to count. I mentioned that last night in the debate stage when the topic of crime came up because this is deeply personal to me. It`s why I got involved in public service in the first place because people like Ron Johnson refuse to do anything to deal with the carnage that has taken place on city streets all across his country.\par \par \uc2 And with that being said, he had an opportunity to support public safety, law enforcement, and crime prevention initiatives with the American Rescue Plan Act that allowed us to invest $100 million in the public safety, he voted against it because he wanted to play politics.\par \par \uc2 Now, what we are up against is something that I have never seen before. The senator is lying. He is trying to distract. He is blaming me for stuff I had nothing to do with. And they are pouring millions -- tens of millions of dollars into this race in false, misleading, and negative advertising and that`s why I need help from folks to get over this hump.\par \par \uc2 We have to raise enough money to keep our message and hold Ron Johnson accountable on air. We have to talk about our plan to rebuild the middle class, talk about the folks Ron Johnson continues to leave behind.\par \par \uc2 For those viewers who are interested go to mandelabarnes.com. Learn more about what we`re doing to help support the efforts so we can get Ron Johnson out of office, someone who tried to send fake electors to the Vice President because he wanted to overturn an election because he didn`t like the results, a person who is so extreme and out of touch with the majority of people in Wisconsin when it comes to abortion access.\par \par \uc2 He supported eight different national abortion bans. He supported abortion bans that had no exception for rape or incest. He is a person who`s going to send our jobs out of state and even out of the country, because that is what he says to make sense for his wealthy donors.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He supported eight different abortion bans and then now this week, Republican legislators in your State blocked a voter referendum from getting on the ballot next month, whether to allow for abortion in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Abortion having been immediately banned in your State, after an 1849 law went into effect following the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe in June. A recent poll shows that nearly two-thirds of Wisconsin voters are against the reversal of Roe that includes nearly a third of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been hitting Senator Johnson hard on this issue of abortion rights. Today, you kicked off your Ron Against Roe Tour and you attended a rally hosted by Planned Parenthood in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 With this issue -- will this issue, I should ask, will it energize Democratic voters in your State enough that Democratic candidates, yourself included to succeed in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: And this is beyond Democratic votes. This is an issue about freedom. It is about personal rights that Ron Johnson could not be more -- cannot be less concerned about.\par \par \uc2 The reality is he told people, he told women if you don`t like the laws of your State, you can just move. You know, have a 1849 criminal abortion ban on the books. This is a person who is not concerned about quality of life, he is certainly not concerned about children when they get here his quote was that it is not his responsibility to take care of people`s children in this country.\par \par \uc2 I can`t think of any other responsibility we have as policymakers than to nurture and invest in the future. Ron Johnson doesn`t seem to get that. He is against making the child tax credit permanent, something that would help out struggling families deal with rising costs, but that is of no concern to him.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:31]\par \par \uc2 BARNES: So, not only are you talking about the issue of choice and the right to make your own healthcare decisions, we`re talking about everything else that comes along with it. The economic opportunity that Ron Johnson is consistently denying folks, we had a chance to create thousand good paying union jobs, family supporting jobs in \uc2 Oshkosh\uc2 , where Ron Johnson is actually from, they decided they are going to move the jobs to South Carolina, Ron Johnson`s response was, we have enough jobs.\par \par \uc2 Now, if that is your thinking, how do you expect people to be able to take care of children? How do you think people are supposed to be able to support a family if you are denying them that opportunity? He is, like I said, he is offshoring. But he said it makes sense, like this is an out of touch person, and we deserve so much more in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, while we are talking about the polls in your State. I want to play another clip from last night`s debate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I voted to confirm, you know, not to disallow any of President Biden`s electors. So from my standpoint, this is a non-issue, but I know it`s being blown out because everyone wants to focus on January 6.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So first, to put this in context, Senator Johnson did eventually vote to certify the election, but this was only after he tried to hand Vice President Pence a set of alternate or fake electors, a fact that was uncovered by the January 6 Committee that the senator later admitted to a local reporter.\par \par \uc2 Now the Senate is saying this was an insignificant detail. What`s your opinion?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, the significant detail here is that his plan to overturn an election didn`t work. So of course, he backed off from it when he didn`t get the result that he thought he is going to get.\par \par \uc2 The Vice President`s office rejected their attempt to overturn the election. That`s what that comes down to. It wasn`t him having a change of heart, it was him being denied. It was him being denied his attempted denial of our vote, of our voice.\par \par \uc2 He looked every single voter in the eye in this State, he says your vote doesn`t count. And that`s because the person he didn`t want to win won an election. So, instead of accepting the results, he decided that he will try to change the results and it is a person who cannot be trusted with our democracy as he is a person that can`t be trusted with our health or our safety.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Finally, I`m out of time, but I have to actually just quickly, I`m on radio in your State. How are you and your team working to get Black voters concentrated largely in Milwaukee to turn out next month?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Hey, Milwaukee is my home. We`re talking about the district I represented as a legislator, we`re talking about the community I spent my entire life in, and it is always a good time going back home. We were just in my high school at that rally that you showed the footage from.\par \par \uc2 We`re showing up everywhere. We`re showing up often and we`re giving people a reason to vote. Ron Johnson being bad isn`t enough, we are -- we actually just kicked off a huge canvas today. I went to knock on several doors myself and we are going to maintain our presence as we have from the very start. We kicked off the campaign right in the heart of the City of Milwaukee at the Sherman Phoenix, which has a lot of significance when it comes to addressing some of the challenges we face.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Oh, yes. All right, Mandela Barnes, the Lieutenant Governor running for Senator in the State of Wisconsin, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  spent the week in political hot water. I`ve got a whole lot to say about the allegations against him and the Republican Party standing by their man.\par \par \uc2 You don`t want to miss this week`s "Gotcha."\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, good afternoon to you. A new setback for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 : A massive explosion hit a strategic bridge linking Russia and Crimea, which was formerly controlled by Ukraine. It knocked out a key military supply route for Russia`s military.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine officials called the attack a "sick burn." Russian officials say there is no official timeline for repairing the 12-mile bridge.\par \par \uc2 North Korea launched another suspected ballistic missile today aimed east says South Korea and Japan officials. This launch marks the sixth round of weapons tests in just two weeks. Today`s launch led Japan officials to urge citizens to seek shelter.\par \par \uc2 And thousands of women took to the streets in Washington, DC and cities across the country in support of reproductive rights. The organizers say the Women`s Wave Day of Action is to activate voters ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The midterm elections are less than a month away. Early voting in Georgia starts in just nine days. It is the most critical point in the campaign, and yet, in the Peach State, this week, the headlines were dominated by a story published by "The Daily Beast" about an abortion allegedly paid for by a Republican Senate candidate, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Walker denies the allegation and NBC has not been able to verify the report. I do not agree with Mr. Walker`s politics, but it gives me no special pleasure to see an accomplished fellow Black man have his personal life become a matter of public debate. However, we must make no mistake about why this story is relevant.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The problem isn`t that Mr. Walker and his ex-girlfriend allegedly made a difficult private decision, it is that he and other Republicans are running on a platform that would deny everyone else the same choice and just listen to how some of his fellow Republicans have chosen to defend him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So does this change anything? I mean, do you want my opinion? Are you listening? Not a damn thing.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t care if \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid to abort endangered baby eagles. I want control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It`s hard to see how talking plot points like these will help \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  repairs reputation in the eyes of Georgia voters. It is even more difficult to understand how they can appeal to anyone with sincere moral objections to abortion.\par \par \uc2 What these types of comments do successfully highlight in my view is that Republican values are often no more than political props.\par \par \uc2 Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  votes to deny other States` disaster assistance while he was in Congress and rails against Federal government all the time, then he eagerly demands his share of the cache when his own state is struck by a storm.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ran an entire campaign blasting \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  for her handling of a few classified e-mails, and then according to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , he stashed boxes of highly confidential materials at his Mar-a- Lago resort. The list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 In less than 30 days, the hotly contested Georgia Senate race will be decided. If \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  loses, some will say it was his personal life that sunk his chances, but that is simply not true.\par \par \uc2 Republicans will have defeated themselves with what appears to be their shameless hypocrisy in pursuit of power.\par \par \uc2 If a loss comes about, it is a loss they deeply deserve.\par \par \uc2 I gotcha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:28]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s dive deeper into our discussion of today`s big stories with my political panel.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Susan del Percio, Republican strategist and Juanita Tolliver, host of Crooked Media`s "What A Day" podcast and as well a Democratic strategist, both are MSNBC contributors.\par \par \uc2 Juanita, first to you. In five days, the January 6 Committee will hold its ninth public hearing. The panel plans to refocus on Donald Trump`s culpability in the Capitol riot and lay out how his efforts contributed to clear and present danger to the democratic institutions and the voting system.\par \par \uc2 As the committee holds, what might be its last public hearing? How can this investigation help shape future elections?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Look, the first thing they need to do is what the original mandate was, deliver the truth to the American public and they have not reneged on that mission. They have delivered every single hearing they`ve had, and they`ve shown that they`re worth the wait, and so even though this was delayed by a week or so, I`m looking forward to hearing additional testimony they`ve received since the last hearing, whether that be from Ginni Thomas or others from the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 I`m also looking for the footage from \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , and his documentary, as well as the documents that they received from Secret Service that were not deleted.\par \par \uc2 We know this is a prime issue for voters, Rev, when majority of voters say that this will be a guiding principle when they are heading into the ballot box. So, the Select Committee should be explicitly clear and concise and not only recapping all the evidence they have, but the accountability they want to see because of that threat, that clear and present threat that is presented from Trump`s action and other Republicans and other officials who aided him.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So Susan, right now, a majority of GOP candidates across the country are 2020 election deniers, which poses a serious threat for elections going forward. Now this week, Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  spoke out against election deniers in her state.\par \par \uc2 She sent a strong warning to Arizona voters, but the Republicans running for Governor Kari Lake and Secretary of State nominee Mark Finchem calling them both threats of democracy.\par \par \uc2 What can we done to curb these election conspiracies? Or is it already too late?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it`s too late as far as picking the candidates go, but I think someone like \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  can frankly go further, put her money where her mouth is. She has got $7 million dollars in her PAC so she can go use it.\par \par \uc2 We know from 2020 that there is a sliver of Republicans, the five to ten percent that are willing to say no to Trump and his craziness, and that we will be able to -- they would be able to swing an election.\par \par \uc2 The other part of that though, is pay attention to the Governors races, because the State say you`re talking about, including Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Michigan, they have majorities in their Statehouses of Republicans, those Democratic Governors need to be there to veto that legislation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, sticking with you. A new reporting in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," an article out today is about how former President Trump did collected demands for documents wanted by the National Archives. The article says, sources say: "Trump floated the idea of offering the deal to return the boxes in exchange for documents he believed would expose the Russia investigation as a `hoax` cooked up by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 "The Times" reports aides never acted on that idea. NBC News has not yet independently confirmed this new report and is reaching out to the Trump legal team for comment.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this report, though, Susan?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Well, it seems like he`s guilty on both counts. I mean, it`s easy to see he thought that we could be that transactional, Rev. I mean, it comes as no surprise, but he basically said, I`ll give in on one crime if you give me the records that will help me cover up the other crime or the other wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 So only a person like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thinks he could trade in classified information, and let us be clear, that would also be illegal.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, hundreds of thousands of undocumented migrants who were brought to this country has children now face more uncertainty. After a Federal Appeals Court ruled that the Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals or DACA is unlawful. The Court is letting current DACA recipients stay in the US while the \uc2 Obama\uc2 -era program goes back to a Lower Court in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Now President Biden has called on Congress to pass permanent protections for DREAMers including a pathway to citizenship. How much will this ruling heighten the stakes for this year`s midterms?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I think it`s huge, especially when Latino voters are going to the polls to make their decisions, because let`s be real, if Republicans take back control of Congress, no less one chamber or the other alone, that pathway to creating any type of legislation that will help ease the anxiety they have been experiencing for years related to their future in this country will be closed. That door will be closed, because you better believe no Republican in the House, no Republican in the Senate is going to be on board with creating any type of legislation that will positively help people who have immigrated to this country.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think this is something that Democrats need to emphasize, especially in House and Senate races to make it clear where they stand on trying to deliver a pathway, a future for DACA recipients and other undocumented individuals in this country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you both very quickly. Give me your predictions. Juanita, give me your prediction on what will happen in the midterms in the Senate and the House? Will the Democrats maintain and grow? Will the Republicans overtake?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: look in the Senate I feel like Democrats have a narrow chance of keeping it, but they have to play their cards straight especially in these races that we are seeing in Georgia, Pennsylvania, and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 But in the House sadly, thanks to redistricting and what Republicans did there in picking up five seats, I feel like that`s a called game.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Yes, I agree with Juanita. Five seats for the Republicans to pick up in the House is guaranteed as anything is in politics. And I think we`re looking at probably a 52/48 Senate. I can`t tell you which way it`s going to go.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Susan del Percio and Juanita Tolliver, thank you both for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 After the break, hurricane survivors in desperate need of Federal help say they are having trouble figuring out how to get it.\par \par \uc2 My next guest will try to clear up the confusion.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Recovery efforts continue after Hurricane Ian, one of the most powerful storms that ever hit the United States. It led a path of destruction last week. Officials in Florida have been able to confirm at least 130 deaths attributed to the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  toured damaged areas of Florida`s coasts with the State`s Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , as the two leaders vowed to put their political differences aside to help Floridians recover from massive damage.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, over 200,000 individuals had applied for assistance from the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2  or FEMA, and even though President Biden issued an expedited major disaster declaration for the state, many have reported delays in receiving aid, supplies and shelter that is desperately needed.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Marcus Coleman, the Director for the Department of Homeland Security Center for faith based and Neighborhood Partnerships, which is part of the \uc2 Federal Emergency Management Agency\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining me today, Mr. Coleman.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you, while FEMA has been helping bring relief to thousands of people, some Floridians have reportedly expressed frustration over the lack of assistance.\par \par \uc2 Residents, particularly in Southwest Florida, told "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " that they were having trouble navigating the process of applying for Federal disaster relief, and have not seen much evidence of Federal boots on the ground. What`s your response?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS COLEMAN, DIRECTOR, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY CENTER FOR FAITH BASED AND NEIGHBORHOOD PARTNERSHIPS: Reverend Sharpton, thank you for having me this afternoon. And again, my prayers and continued thoughts continue to deal with those impacted by Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 My response is simply that it is going to continue to take a whole community effort and a national coalition effort to make sure that we are getting better every day, and that we are meeting the needs of disaster survivors.\par \par \uc2 We still want people to dial 1-800-621-3362. For those that are able, they may find it more effective and efficient to go to disasterassistance.gov. For those that are able to register online, you can skip the time in terms of waiting in line and register there. But we recognize that that`s just part of the government response.\par \par \uc2 I was honored to be on the call yesterday with the President of the National Panhellenic Council and some of her leadership, as many of those members of the Black fraternities and sororities you are going neighborhood to neighborhood today and we equip them with information that they are sharing with disaster survivors, but we all recognize with all the humility that this is going to be a long recovery, a hard recovery, and the only way we`ll get through this is through working with the coalition of partners.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, last week, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was discussing disparities people of color, generally face in extreme weather conditions. And she said that, "We have to address this in a way that is about giving resources based on equity." Several studies have found that FEMA has a history of providing less aid to communities of color facing disaster relief, compared with their White counterparts.\par \par \uc2 What is FEMA going to do to ensure that communities of color affected by Hurricane Ian are receiving adequate and equitable relief?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, prior to landfall of Hurricane Ian, in fact, since I`ve been in this position, we`ve made a concerted effort to engage a broad coalition of partners that represent the rural community and different communities. I know, I mentioned some of the organizations in the last segment that we spoke, but it is a concerted focus of us to make sure that those national partners and those local partners that are in communities that we are equipping them proactively with preparedness and recovery information.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we are going to continue to get ground truth from our local government counterparts and our State government counterparts, but we also need that ground truth from leaders in our community.\par \par \uc2 And so I think about some of the survivors like someone I spoke to with ties to the River Park neighborhood on Thursday, but we were able to connect with her and her sister to ensure that our Disaster Survivor Assistance Team members were able to get to those neighborhoods in those places and spaces where people haven`t felt like they`ve seen the presence of boots on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Another example, we are working with a pastor in the Dunbar community or in the Fort Myers neighborhood, and we actually have Disaster Survivor Assistance Team members are providing information as well.\par \par \uc2 But we know that trying to change this system is a long term effort, and again, it`s going to take that coalition of partners that are focused on children on rural communities, and on those historically underserved populations, including the Black and African-American community, and the Hispanic and Latino community to get through this process.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, starting today, the \uc2 US Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  tasked by FEMA will provide temporary repairs for roofs damaged by Hurricane Ian in five counties of Florida at zero cost, calling it Operation Blue Roof. This program seeks to temporarily help protect homes from further damage before more permanent repairs are made.\par \par \uc2 Will you ask other organizations to step in to help out and hasten the recovery?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes, and thankfully, they already have. There is also a non- government program called Crisis Cleanup that is helping with debris removal on private properties and we have a whole coalition of partners through the National Voluntary Organizations Active in Disasters. That group is like Team Rubicon Operation Hope, led by \uc2 John Hope Bryant\uc2 . His organization provides free disaster financial counseling to survivors, but then we also have, of course, \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2  and other organizations.\par \par \uc2 And so, it is going to be that same coalition last week mentioned with the \uc2 NAACP\uc2 , Urban League, and some of these other disaster relief partners to make sure that we`re doing everything we can to meet the needs of survivors today and in the weeks and months to come.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Marcus Coleman of FEMA, again, thank you for being with us another week.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, countless Americans will be getting their lives back. But is it enough? I`ll explain in my Final Thoughts after a short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:33]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Earlier this week, President Biden issued a decision to pardon prior Federal simple weed convictions and set the stage for decriminalizing the drug at a Federal level. Here is my take.\par \par \uc2 The United States will never just legalize marijuana until it reckons with the outdated policies that equated thousands of young Black men with hardened drug pushers. President Biden`s righteous action will give countless Americans their lives back.\par \par \uc2 They were thrown behind bars for years on end for simple possession. A nonviolent offense for a substance that red states and blue states are now legalizing at a furious pace. This held back those individuals from jobs, from homes, and the general dignity they now get back from this full pardon.\par \par \uc2 My organization, the National Action Network began pushing for these reforms nearly over a decade ago when it became clear that conversation around legalization began to change. We will continue to monitor the legalization and hold the Federal government to its word and looking forward, I echo the President`s call on Governors to follow suit and deliver this same justice at the State level. They cannot legalize marijuana at the Statehouse until they rectify what went on at the jailhouse.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to wish a Happy Birthday to a special person.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  turned 81 years old.\par \par \uc2 For more than 55 years, he has been a dominant force for change worldwide, and has made concrete change. He has been in my life, a real mentor and role model, and many of us would not be where we are had it not been for Jesse Louis Jackson.\par \par \uc2 And as he now deals with Parkinson`s disease, he is still on the case, still fighting. I called him early this morning. We had prayer on the phone. He was already up at six o`clock, working to get water down to Mississippi where people are still trying to deal with contaminated water.\par \par \uc2 Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  has made a difference in his life and continues to do so. I join millions and sang Happy Birthday to Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  will join me to discuss the fight for voting rights in the United States.\par \par \uc2 "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:05]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221009eia800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for October 07, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jonathan Capehart, Stephanie Ruhle, Gabe Gutierrez \par \uc2 7,819 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JULIA LOUIS DREYFUS: What? Gary Cole was on The West Wing?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY WHITFORD: Yeah. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 JULIA LOUIS DREYFUS: Jesus. If I knew that we never would have (bleep) hired a month Veep.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Bradley Whitford and Julia Louis Dreyfus get tonight`s Last Word.\par \par \uc2 And a programming note Rachel Maddow will join us on Monday at 10 p.m. right here on the Last Word to discuss her new podcast Ultra, about American Extremism. The first two episodes will be available Monday morning wherever you get your podcasts. THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Tonight, in another week of legal setbacks for the former president, there are still more twists in the case of the missing documents, while the current president ends the week with a troubling take on the risk of nuclear Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 Then, countdown to the midterms with control of the House and Senate on the line, there`s even more abortion controversy in the crucial Senate race in Georgia. Plus, the United States of America at a crossroads, Scott Galloway offers solutions with his unique perspective on a nation he says is adrift. Stephanie goes one on one with the author as THE 11TH HOUR it gets underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, I`m \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , in for Stephanie Ruhle. The Breaking News this hour, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is now reporting that Trump endorsed Georgia Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  urged his ex-girlfriend to have a second abortion after he allegedly paid for that first abortion, which was reported by The Daily Beast. Walker has yet to respond to this latest report.\par \par \uc2 And just a short time ago, The Daily Beast added to the story. It`s been breaking all week, reporting that Walker`s wife reached out to his accuser earlier this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And NBC News tonight is reporting, "Walker is now confirming for the first time Friday, the identity of the woman." In a brief interview, Walker said this was also the first time the woman who is the mother of one of his four children mentioned to him or his wife that she had had an abortion.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have more on this still developing controversy a bit later in the hour. But tonight, we`re also tracking the very latest on the former president standoff with the Justice Department over those White House Records seized from his Mar-a-Lago club.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has now confirmed the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  story that the Justice Department told Trump`s legal team that it suspects he`s still holding on to some documents.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, \uc2 Rolling Stone\uc2  is reporting that investigators have spoken to several witnesses asking if Trump is holding sensitive documents at his other properties, like Trump Tower and his Bedminster, New Jersey club. NBC News has not independently confirmed that reporting.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the first full week of testimony has wrapped up in the federal trial of five Oath keepers accused of seditious conspiracy on January 6. Today, prosecutors showed the jury texts sent by Founder Stewart Rhodes in which he seems to promote the use of force and begs Trump to invoke the insurrection act after the 2020 election. Jurors were also shown this speech from a pro-Trump rally just a few weeks before the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEWART RHODES, OATH KEEPERS FOUNDER: Show the world who the traders are and then use the Insurrection Act to drop the hammer on them. And all of those veterans who swore that oath until you`re age 65, you can be called up as the militia to support and defend the Constitution. He needs to know from you that you are with him that he does not do it now while he is Commander in Chief. We`re going to have to do it ourselves later in a much more desperate, much more bloody war. Let`s get it on now while he is still the commander in June.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: There`s also the ongoing investigation into possible interference in Georgia`s 2020 election. Atlanta area District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  reportedly wants former House Speaker \uc2 Newt Gingrich\uc2  and ex-Trump National Security Adviser \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  to testify before a grand jury. You may recall that before Trump left office, he pardoned Flynn, who had twice pleaded guilty to lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . The guy currently in the White House was facing a lot of questions today about his warning last night that the world is at risk of a nuclear Armageddon if Russia deploys tactical nuclear weapons against Ukraine. Today, the White House said those comments were not based on any new intelligence.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s bring in our leadoff panel, \uc2 Jon Allen\uc2 , Senior National Politics Reporter for NBC, former Federal Prosecutor Glenn Kirschner, he tried hundreds of cases in his 30-year career, including murder trials, organized crimes and precedent setting cases, and Tracy Walder, former CIA Officer and FBI Agent. She`s the author of the book, "The Unexpected Spy." Thank you all for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, we have this new reporting about the possibility of documents stashed in Trump Tower and Bedminster. Justice is also reportedly telling Trump lawyers they suspect he still has missing documents. Could this all be part of a last-ditch attempt to offer Trump a chance to turn everything over before an indictment?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: You know, Jonathan, how many times will the government ask and demand and cajole and subpoena and get search warrants for documents that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stole, let`s call it what it is, from the government and unlawfully concealed at Mar-a-Lago and perhaps elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 You know, I almost got a chuckle when I saw the counter-intelligence prosecutor from the Department of Justice Jay Brad, former colleague of mine from the D.C. U.S. Attorney`s Office, again, demanded that Trump give over the documents. But if he didn`t give them to the National Archives, or to the grand jury, and if the \uc2 FBI\uc2  didn`t get them all, why in the world do we think he would, you know, divest himself of whatever documents he still has.\par \par \uc2 I think there is but one, you know, labels for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at this point. He`s a classified documents terrorist. And it really feels like it is time, past time, for the Department of Justice to do what it would do to any of us if we continue to unlawfully conceal, you know, classified information, National Defense Information, they ought to lock them up, they ought to indict Him, they ought to hold him accountable and, you know, put a stop to our long national nightmare that we`ve been suffering through.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Tracy, given all that`s been reported about just how Trump treated the intel community, are you convinced he still has more classified records?\par \par \uc2 TRACY WALDER, FORMER CIA OFFICER: I am absolutely convinced that he has more classified records, I really have just assumed it all along. I mean, I agree with Glenn, I think, you know, they begged and pleaded for months, even almost years, for him to hand over these documents. And he hasn`t done it. And, you know, he squires them away at Mar-a-Lago, he has all of these other properties.\par \par \uc2 I think we have to assume, quite frankly, that they`re there. And I`m wondering if kind of putting this out there, sort of a tactic and that they are probably going to serve a search warrant at some point in the near future on these properties. But what`s more concerning to me is, you know, a former CIA officer, again, erode the confidence that our human assets overseas have in us and have in our intelligence collection capabilities. You know, and I think that`s a really important thing to think about.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Tracy, I`m just wondering, given these reports, does it seem as if the intel community or DOJ has a pretty good idea of what`s missing?\par \par \uc2 WALDER: Given these reports, and I feel like they probably do. They are conducting that damage assessment right now. And my best guess, is that they are starting to see sort of where the holes were. I mean, again, we did have reporting that came out about a year ago, I believe, talking about securing our assets better overseas, because a lot of them had been compromised. And so, I`m guessing that they have a pretty good inventory in terms of what hasn`t been handed over and sort of where the holes are.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And so, Jon, the Republican Party has been the party of law and order, the party of national security, is there any sense of how party insiders are reacting to this continued drama over documents? I mean, Trump has yet to offer any real defense for what was found in Florida? Are these folks saying, just get the stuff back already?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER: It`s a great question, Jonathan. Certainly the Republican Party is the one that has branded itself, the National Security party in the law and order party, but those are obviously not the rules by which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has played and certainly there are Republicans who say, who have said publicly and certainly those who have said privately, that the President -- former president shouldn`t have taken these documents with them, that there are concerns about national security and that even if there weren`t the records belong to the United States, not the president. Of course, many of those people are looking at presidencies in the future what how wins at the end of the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  presidency or the next presidency.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:05]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Glenn, I want to talk about -- switch gears here, and talk about the Oath Keepers trial, because you were in court this week for that trial. Here`s what Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  said tonight about the founder`s defense and his call for Trump to invoke the Insurrection Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 , (D) MARYLAND JAN. 6 COMMITTEE MEMBER: The whole thing is absurd to begin with, because he`s saying, it`s like someone being charged with murder, saying, well, I thought I was going to be deputized by the sheriff, but I wasn`t. So, in other words, I had no legal authority, but I thought I was going to have legal authority. That doesn`t help you.\par \par \uc2 In any event, the President doesn`t have the authority to just turn the Proud Boys or the Oath Keepers or any other private militia into an official state militia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And so, Glenn, I would love your response to that. And what did this week reveal about the Justice Department`s prosecution strategy?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yeah, so Jonathan, first of all, my response to that, as Representative Raskin is exactly right. It is no defense to say I was following orders. I hoped would be given, but never were. Oh, and by the way, if they had been given, I would not have been subject to those orders anyway, because I`m not in the military. So, it`s a defense that fails for multiple reasons. And there`s been evidence introduced over the course of the last week that Stewart Rhodes authored an open letter that said, you know what, Trump, if he fails to invoke the Insurrection Act, we the Oath Keepers, will take up arms and take matters into our own hands.\par \par \uc2 My observations about the trial overall, are that it`s surprising how much direct evidence of guilt, we have prosecutors ordinarily build cases, on circumstantial evidence, we`re generally not so fortunate to have. For example, a videotape of the defendants breaching the Capitol, inside the Capitol and then bragging about taking the capital, the evidence is quite strong. Now, of course, there`s a long way to go, the defendants haven`t put on their defenses yet. And no case is a sure winner. But it sure looks like this case is sort of on the road to convictions.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Jon, I`m going to come to you here in another switching of the gears and talk about the President`s comment last night about, you know, the possibility of us being at nuclear Armageddon, you spend time with President Biden, what does his warning reveal about how he sees Putin, and what he`s capable of doing when cornered?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Well, number one, I think he takes \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  as a serious threat. He has been dealing with Putin for decades now, first, as a senator, then as vice president, and now as President of the United States. But I thought -- I think what`s also important here, Jonathan, is a little bit of the political context, it is important for the President to basically explain to the American public what the stakes are, that are involved in this fight in Ukraine, because what`s happening in Congress is you`re seeing conservatives who have supported the Ukraine effort in the United States starting to peel away. And I think there`s some concern on the President`s part that they may even happen in his own party if people lose interest.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that the, you know, look, Putin has suggested that he might use nuclear weapons. And while the White House is saying that there`s no new intelligence about that, it is important for the President if he wants to be able to continue the support for Ukraine that he`s been getting, particularly in light of some of the domestic challenges, to make sure that the American public believes that the stakes are as high as they are, which is, you know, he does not trust \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to have restraint.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And to that point, Tracy, tonight, Right Wing media is arguing that President Biden`s warning about Putin and nukes is endangering the United States. Are they right?\par \par \uc2 WALDER: I think they`re dead wrong. And the reality is, is look, I`m not saying that Putin is going to use nuclear weapons tomorrow. But the reality is, is I agree with Jon, this is a very serious issue. And, you know, I don`t know that I see an off ramp for Putin in this situation, you know, how does this end for him? This doesn`t really end unless he feels that he has a fully decisive victory in Ukraine. And right now, he doesn`t have that. And quite frankly, his own people are turning against him because he`s drafting them and they don`t want to go.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think I am concerned because I don`t see a clear off ramp for him. How does this end and the only way that it`s going to end if it doesn`t go Putin`s way is with something very, very drastic.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And Jon, let me squeeze one more question into you this time about Georgia before we go, the Fulton County D.A. appears to be heading towards subpoenas for Gingrich and Flynn in the 2020 election interference investigation, what could those two offer to the inquiry?\par \par \uc2 [23:15:11]\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: I mean, obviously be great to hear what their testimony is. \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  is somebody has been very close to former President Trump, as was Newt Gingrich and Gingrich obviously has a lot of relationships within Georgia. I think these are two key witnesses, if they in fact, end up giving testimony. And you know, that obviously, it`s moving along a pace, this effort by President Trump to literally overturn the will of the Georgia voters.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And one more quick question, the slide in question here, Jon, the likelihood, I know that D.A. Willis said that they`re not going to do anything until after the election. Any clues or hints about how quickly after the midterm elections in 31 days \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  will move?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: You know, I don`t know exactly what day but I would imagine that it will be very quickly after the midterm elections. Obviously, she`s trying to give some window here for the politics to play out without the investigation being at the top of the news, without it being either one affecting the other and at the same time. Most of the evidence here has existed for quite a long time. I`m not sure there`s a lot of obstacle to her moving forward once these elections are done.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: \uc2 Jon Allen\uc2 , Glenn Kirschner and Tracy Walder, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, with just 31 days until the election that may determine the future of America, and with October surprise after surprise, we look at what has voters worked up right now.\par \par \uc2 And later, he`s making the case, the nation is now adrift but can still be fixed. Stephanie goes one on one with author, professor and podcast host Scott Galloway. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:21:36]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: One month until the midterms and Democratic candidates are now hammering the Republican opponents on abortion. Here are some moments you might have missed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FRANKEN, (D) IOWA SENATE CANDIDATE: It`s known that he`s got some anti-woman activity in his career.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK GRASSLEY\uc2 , (R) IOWA: My colleague, you`re in no position to lecture me about women.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. \uc2 DARREN BAILEY\uc2 , (R) ILLINOIS CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR: \uc2 J. B. Pritzker\uc2  stays up at night trying to dream up new abortion laws. Those issues are dividing us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY, (D) ARIZONA: He`s called the abortion demonic, a religious sacrifice. I don`t even know what that means, folks.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN CURTIS, (R) UTAH: Most of these decisions are made by men. I wish it were other than that. I wish as a man I didn`t have to make this decision. I wish women could make this decision.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , (R) GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: This here, the abortion thing is false. It`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That Georgia Senate race is among the most closely watched right now. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has denied the Daily Beast reporting that he paid for an ex-girlfriend`s abortion, and had a child with that same woman. Now, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports Walker urged the woman to have another abortion two years after the first ending their relationship after she refused and have his son.\par \par \uc2 Walker told NBC News in a brief interview that this was the first time the woman told him she had had an abortion. The Walker campaign shared text messages between the woman and Walker`s wife.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in democratic pollster and MSNBC Political Analyst Fernand Amandi. And Stuart Stevens, a Veteran of the \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  Presidential Campaigns. He`s now with the Lincoln Project and he is the author of, "It Was All a Lie: How the Republican Party Became \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stuart, despite the scandal surrounding Walker, this race is close. Why?\par \par \uc2 STUART STEVENS, THE LINCOLN PROJECT SENIOR ADVISER: Well, it`s close structurally, because Georgia really is a blue state now. You know, this is extraordinary what`s happened here, in the Republican Party. There was an old saying in the Soviet Union that was attributed to Solzhenitsyn, that it goes, they know that -- we know that they`re lying, but they keep lying to us. And we keep pretending to believe it. That pretty much sums up the state here with the Republican Party, and particularly with Walker, everybody knows he`s lying. They`re pretending that they know that he`s not lying. While they know that he`s lying. It`s just, you know, this guy`s going to lose this race. And the question is, what else is the Republican Party and Georgia going to lose with him? And why is it that any other statewide elected officials in Georgia or any other Republican officials or prominent Republicans have called them out for this? At the end of the day, if you have to stand for something besides election if you`re going to be a viable political party.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Amen to that. So, Fernand, Reverend Senator Raphael Warnock is out fundraising \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , but he avoids talking about Walker`s family. Is there anything to gain by going on the attack against Walker?\par \par \uc2 FERNAND AMANDI, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Jonathan not in this case. I mean, look, I think Stuart said it best. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to lose this race. And I think Raphael Warnock knows that better than anyone.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:00]\par \par \uc2 But part of the reason why I think you see this Republican effort to try and you know raise up this dying dead candidacy of Walker is because to admit that he has lost this race when you then factor in what`s going on in Pennsylvania where \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  looks like he`s got to absolutely flip that seat defeat Dr. Oz. You look at what`s happening in Arizona where Mark Kelly also is going to hold that seat. Even states like Ohio, where J.D. Vance is two points behind.\par \par \uc2 You admit that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has lost this race with the Republicans are then doing is that the Senate is lost, the fundraising stops, all of them went and comes to a crashing halt. So, they`re going to do whatever they can in their power to sell this lemon on the Republican voters and Republican donors and say that this race is competitive when the polls I think make it clear, an average four-point lead in a state like Georgia means Warnock is in prime position to get elected to another six years and keep the Democratic majority with that.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Fernand, since you mentioned, Ohio, the Ohio race. Ohio Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is running for Senate, as you mentioned, but he`s got a new campaign ad and he posed this question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 , (R) OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: Some people think that they have to agree with their politicians 100% of the time, and I asked these people, are any of you married?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: He goes on to suggest the focus should be on stopping the stupid fights and finding common ground. This is to both of you, can he convince voters in both parties with that approach? Stuart, you go first.\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Sure, look, I think this guy can win there. I work for the last Republican Senator Rob Portman. The state is a state which Scott Sherwood Brown, it`s wonderful for a Democrat with the right kind of message. What you really need to be looking at here is what are independents doing in this race. And what that ad is a direct appeal to independents who feel alienated from both parties, and just want to let problem solvers and that`s a sort of the antithesis of what J.D. Vance is. J.D. Vance is another fraud. He`s sort of a better educated version of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . And people I think, feel uncomfortable with the guy. And I think if Ryan can close out strong here, he can win this race.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Fernand, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 AMANDI: So, Jonathan, that is an ad of a confident campaign that is going on offense, and that they think they have the wind at their back. That type of ad suggests to me like Stuart alluded to, they`re looking at the polling, they`re targeting in on what is that segment of the Ohio electorate that they need to get them fully over the top. And I think its independence. And look, it`s been part and parcel of what Tim Ryan`s approach has been. He has been very muscular in his message. He`s not afraid to talk about issues that, you know, might even read a little bit of the Democratic perspective and other states, but in Ohio, it`s working, it`s playing out, and we know it because the polls say so.\par \par \uc2 Again, I think if that`s another race that the Republicans lose, I mean, you could be talking now about a two, three seat advantage in the Senate for the Democrats if they hold, and then the Biden agenda is back on in a big way, because that changes the game considerably.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Fernand Amandi, Stuart Stevens, thank you both for coming to THE 11TH HOUR.\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, he`s sounding the alarms about a nation adrift. But Scott Galloway is also offering up some solutions. He`s one on one with Stephanie when THE 11TH HOUR continues. And wait until you hear what he says is the toughest reality for young Americans. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:33:23]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: COVID, political extremism, inflation, the future of our democracy with so much coming at us, it`s hard to take it all in. Well, my dear friend Scott Galloway always has a unique way to explain what`s going on. And in his new book, "Adrift: America in 100 Charts." He looks at everything from the size of container ships to online dating, to life expectancy, all to explain America today, and more importantly, where he thinks we`re headed.\par \par \uc2 Scott is a marketing professor at \uc2 NYU\uc2  and a co-host of the very successful podcast, Pivot and I`m thrilled to have him here with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Scott, let`s talk about this book. You give us a history lesson, right? 1945 until today. But not through traditional storytelling. It`s all through charts, why? To combat misinformation?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY, "ADRIFT: AMERICA IN 100 CHARTS" AUTHOR: Well, it says we`ve had the alphabet for about 1500 years, but we`ve been interpreting images on cave walls or meeting people based on where the sun was in the sky. So, we just absorb pictures six to 60 times faster than words. So, people just get a problem when they see a chart much faster than when they see words.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, you write in the book that nations prosper or perish based on how they respond to crisis.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: COVID, how do we respond?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: We`re going to need a bigger boat. I think it`s a mixed -- it`s a mixed bag. First off, it demonstrates what`s best and what`s I think worst about America right now. The first is no one`s lining out to get a Chinese or a Russian vaccine. The most important product of the last 50 years isn`t the iPhone. It isn`t the microprocessor. It`s the vaccine. We would have lost another one to 2 million people without these vaccines. We have the best scientists. We have the best distribution.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:06]\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, despite the fact that we have these vaccines, the polarization our society, meant a lot of people unnecessarily perished. And we also have, we politicize them, we`re angry about them. And we don`t celebrate the fact that the brightest people in the world all want to come here, we don`t celebrate the fact that these vaccines were essentially government funded. And whether it`s \uc2 Moderna\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2  or \uc2 Apple\uc2 , we have a small group of people who are the most fortunate people in our nation tech billionaires, who tend to be the first ones to sort of criticize the government not acknowledging that every one of these big companies has built a layer of innovation on top of middle-class taxpayer investments that were very forward looking.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s where I want you to go, because when you talk about the greatest innovation, you don`t actually say the vaccine, you don`t say the smartphone or refrigeration. You say our greatest innovation is the middle class. Explain this to us?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Well, I agree with \uc2 Peter Drucker\uc2 , The Economist in the 60s, the whole point of an economy is to create a middle class. They fight our wars, they pay our taxes, they`re responsible for the majority of society`s gains. And to a certain extent, the greatest you could argue one of the biggest accomplishments of the last 50 years, is when he went early want to explain China`s relative ascendancy, it`s that they have brought a half a billion people into the middle class, whereas our middle class has declined. And that`s the bad news. The good news is just as we`ve attacked the middle class, we can rejuvenate it. There`s this illusion of complexity, that people who want to continue to cram more wealth in the top 1% or tech, who claim they can solve these complicated problems around polarization or election misinformation, we can absolutely undo these problems with one small child tax credit, we reduced childhood poverty by 50%.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And then we got rid of that child tax credit, let`s remind our audience.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: And then we struck it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But are you being sort of overly romantic when you think about that, that American middle class of the 1950s and 1960s, because it was all well, and good for June and Ward Cleaver, but it left out all sorts of Americans?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Yeah, the heteronormative view that we associate with the middle class no longer makes sense, right? Only a quarter of America, the traditional heteronormative household. But at the same time, that middle class household that has a compact with America, that their kids will do better than them, always happened until the last five years, for the first time in our history, a 30-year-old man or woman isn`t doing as well as his or her parents were at the age of 30. And at the end of the day, that`s the whole shooting match. That`s all we want, right? Is we want to be part of a society where we pay taxes, we work hard, we play by the rules, such that we can be successful. And more importantly, our kids can be more successful than us.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Was that the most distressing chart for you? Because you -- these charts are like a tour of doom, right? And we like to say, I just want to live in a country, I want to create a world where my kids get to live in a better, smarter, stronger place. That chart, was that the toughest reality for you to look at?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: No, I think the most upsetting chart is one that gets a lot of attention for the wrong reasons. And that is the Pew. Pew did a study on young adults. And when you`re walking down the avenue, that is America, one in three men under the age of 30, haven`t had sex in the last year. And you hear the term sex in your brain or new .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With another person, because porn has never been bigger?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Well, that`s an entirely different talk show. And I would acknowledge we`re in the midst of the greatest unsupervised experiment on young men in history, and that is porn. And I would argue --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Because they`re have sex, it`s just not with anyone else?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: And I would argue that it not only creates unreasonable expectations around relationship with a woman, but it decreases their mojo to go out and develop the skills to actually have their own physical intimate encounters. But going back to the fact that so many, few men are establishing relationships, sex is a key component of establishing what is the elemental foundation of any society. And that is a long-term loving relationship.\par \par \uc2 And we are producing too many young men who aren`t attaching to school. They`re not attaching to work, and they`re not attaching to romantic partners. The most dangerous person in the world, Stephanie, is a young, broke and alone male, and we are producing way too many of them. And the fact that half of kids under the age of 18 are only 50% of kids who are not seeing their friends every day or it`s been cut in half. The fact that in the next five years, there`s going to be two female graduates of college every one male graduate. And something we don`t acknowledge on the left is that women aren`t interested in mating with men who aren`t economically viable. And --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why do we hate those women? Because when it was reversed, when it was more men graduating from college, you didn`t see women lining up to hate them. And now those young men look at those women, those successful women, and they look at them with anger with fury, we`re -- explain this one to me.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:01]\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: So, you`re absolutely right and when these men get isolated, they are more prone to misogynistic content they`re less likely to believe in climate change. They`re more prone to the conspiracy theory. The guy who attacked Salman Rushdie that wasn`t about a fatwa, that was about a young man living in his mother`s basement. So, what I would argue, though, and I get pushback from feminists on this, that I want to be clear, no one has an obligation to service men who weren`t having sex.\par \par \uc2 When I was in college, the majority of my friends were having sex with other men. What I would say, though, is unless we make an investment in young people such that they`re economically viable, and they can establish relationships. And part of that is discipline around, not watching a lot of porn, getting a job, going to school, taking responsibility for your own life. But without a viable class of young men who are doing well, no group has fallen further faster in our nation than young men.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you think this divisiveness, this isolation is worse than it`s ever been before? Or do you think it`s just on display because social media magnifies it?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Well, it`s not just social media, aid is worse. I mean, another disturbing chart. 54% of Democrats are worried that their child is going to marry a Republican, that`s third of each party --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s absurd.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: A third of each party sees the other party as a mortal enemy. There`s a great photographer colorized and World War II photos and this is wonderful photo of these young men with -- getting off a landing aircraft - - landing craft, going on to Omaha Beach and the invasion of Normandy, two out of three would make it off that beach, average age 26, average salary inflation adjusted basis 800 bucks. I can`t imagine any of them look left or right and knew that he was a Republican or a Democrat. We are geopolitically probably stronger than we`ve ever been. We`re food independent. We`re energy independent. The brightest people in the world want to come here. There`s more people becoming billionaires, and then they become billionaires, they decided to turn their company into a nonprofit, focused on climate change. The CEO of Patagonia just decided. We have never been stronger relative to other nations.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, are we just capitalizing on and monetizing the divisions, because you just listed a whole bunch of extraordinary things, and left out just common decency and neighborly behavior, which we`re also exhibiting every day?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: For where I was headed was the problem is the call is coming from inside of the house, we`re eating each other internally, we`ve decided that we are each other`s enemies. And I say to young man, you`ve got to separate the person from the politics and something we forget. And then I think is a truth is that Americans greatest allies will always be other Americans. We need some sort of social service that that puts people in the company of other Americans, not against people in other political parties.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, well, then that takes me to the chart that stood out to me, the one on immigrants. OK, in 2018, more than half of our unicorn companies here, right billion dollar plus private companies were founded or co- founded by immigrants. Yet, we don`t even have immigration policy in this country. How do you square that?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Oh, we shouldn`t.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We hate our newest, greatest asset?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: If the \uc2 Tampa Bay Buccaneers\uc2  every year had the top draft choices across the entire college environment, and they decided no, we don`t want the top two, we get the number one draft choice from every nation in the world every year. And we`ve decided that we don`t want that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: I don`t entirely understand, I think we`ve politicized and demonized immigrants. And if you look at immigrants in our society, they`re much more likely to move up the income ladder. It`s very difficult, whether it`s \uc2 MasterCard\uc2  or \uc2 Google\uc2 , or \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , you`re talking about immigrants, a third of the NASDAQ is run by first generation immigrants. So that`s the secret sauce and to turn away number one draft choices every year just seems insane to me, it makes absolutely no sense.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So why did you write this book? Because you write about American exceptionalism which you celebrate, which you believe in, but you`re down on the American dream? So, what are you trying to tell us?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Well, it`s he used an old \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  adage, I don`t think there`s anything wrong with America that can`t be fixed with what`s right with it. We have huge problems. But you have to diagnose the disease and not go after the tumor, go after the disease. We need to identify the young men are failing. The polarization is tearing us apart, that this massive income inequality is not only unjustified, but all of these things can be fixed.\par \par \uc2 Big tech likes to pretend that oh, the internet is just a mirror of the world that these problems are complex. And yet we take one account down from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and 30% to 60% of election information-- this misinformation goes away. We can absolutely solve these problems. This is the middle class is an accident of World War II, and it requires investment. But when we make these forward leaning investments, when we have R&D as a function of infrastructure investment, we`re spending about a 10th of what China is, when we put men and women into space, when we decide to research vaccines, when we put GPS satellites into the air, we have amazing companies that develop. But we can`t have the CEOs of EV companies constantly criticizing governments and saying government just needs to get out of the way. Did he want us to get out of the way when we gave him a $450 million loan?\par \par \uc2 [23:45:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ah-ah.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Is he against all the charging stations that middle class taxpayers are going to pay for?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: No, sir.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: So --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re talking about you, Elon Musk.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Who is -- who are the most loyal Americans, who are the most patriotic? The one who have invested the most. Veterans? Who are the ones most apt to criticize the greatest organization, the most noble organization in history, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , our most fortunate and that is young tech billionaires. It`s time we stopped, quite frankly, as posting government in realize that we are here, the prosperity, we are here, the connective tissue, that thing that connects you and me, is this wonderful thing called \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . It`s us, and it`s been the most successful investor. It`s turned back fascism. It`s fed the poor 50% of donations globally come from us philanthropy. And it`s time that we said, OK, we`re Americans. And let`s show -- let`s show it some affinity and some goodwill for our connective tissue in our institutions. And as the good people in the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The thing that connects you and me is friendship, we are real.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Thank you for saying that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You devoted this book, you dedicated this book to your cousin Andrew, who died of COVID complication?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Yep.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: You know, that`s hard. It`s -- Andrew, thank you for bringing that up. I wasn`t expecting that. My cousin was this tall, strapping, handsome kid. And --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You put strapping and handsome?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: All that, yeah, me but much more charming and good looking. And he made some terrible decisions. He decided not to get vaccinated. And through a series of unfortunate accidents, I could have created a list of all of my family members who were most susceptible to COVID. And he would be at the bottom of the list, but a series of bad decisions. And he ended up on a ventilator and crashed and died at the age of 52 and left a nine- year-old boy an orphan.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why didn`t we get vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: A series of bad decisions, but also, I think the polarization and politicization of our media that gives young people and a lot of us the excuse to think there`s both side ism around science. I think a lot of Americans made terrible decisions. And a lot of people unnecessarily died because of the politicization of everything, and the bad feelings we have towards each other and politicizing science and the truth and until we have a truth again, it used to be the Republicans and Democrats came together around this thing called science. We said, OK -- we said, OK, this is what the data says. This is what the science says. And now we`ve politicized science around our political leanings. And I think it`s cost us dearly, including the lives of a bunch of people. So yeah, and it was devoted to Andrew Levine (ph).\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, tomorrow is a new day, and Democrats and Republicans can get together.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: There you go. I hope so. Thank you, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Scott, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GALLOWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I appreciate it. Scott Galloway, again, his new book, "Adrift: America in 100 Charts," take it from me, you need to read it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And just ahead, two devastating hurricanes within just five years has some in Puerto Rico starting from scratch. Again, we`ll hear from a prominent and creative person determined to help the island he loves yet again, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:53:06]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: It`s been almost three full weeks since Hurricane Fiona slammed into Puerto Rico, and tonight there are still 10s of 1000s of people there left in the dark. The island is struggling to recover just like it did five years ago when Hurricane Maria cause catastrophic damage there. And just like he did then, singer and playwright Lin-Manuel Miranda has traveled to Puerto Rico to try to help. He spoke with NBC News Correspondent Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: In Puerto Rico, resiliency has almost become cliche.\par \par \uc2 LIN-MANUEL MIRANDA, ACTOR & SINGER: So much of Puerto Rico has been lifting itself up and our job is to have their backs.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Actor and playwright Lin-Manuel Miranda is once again advocating for the island, much like he did following Hurricane Maria. When he brought his iconic Hamilton here. Now, he`s part of a coalition trying to revive the coffee industry by distributing millions of climate resistant seedlings to farmers.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA: I want to give back to the island that has given me so much.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: While Category I Fiona did not ravaged Puerto Rico quite like Category 4, Maria, the U.S. territory with three and a half million American citizens still finds itself fighting for attention on many fronts.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA: There have been earthquakes, there have been failures, manmade and natural and the best way I can help is with artists and arts organizations on the island.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Miranda`s Flamboyant Arts Fund has also partnered with Google Arts and Culture to digitize hundreds of artworks, among those artists, Maria de Mater O`Neill.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What does resiliency mean to you?\par \par \uc2 MARAA DE MATER O`NEILL, PUERTO RICAN ARTIST: It`s an insult.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Why?\par \par \uc2 O`NEILL: Because, what am I supposed to do? I`ve been fighting all my life.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: For her recovery is not about resiliency, but empowerment.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Do you see yourself in this reflection?\par \par \uc2 [23:55:00]\par \par \uc2 O`NEILL: Claro, agui, that`s me. I have changed. The ever-changing future.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: On an ever-changing island, power can come in many forms, from electricity to art, to agriculture.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA: I just feel really proud to be a part of this recovery.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: And with each passing storm, resiliency takes on new meaning. Gabe Gutierrez, NBC News, San Juan, Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And on that note, I wish you a good night. Be sure to tune in this weekend for the Sunday show when I`ll talk with a former advisor for the House Congressional Committee investigating the January 6 attack on the Capitol Denver Riggleman.\par \par \uc2 From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late and see you this weekend.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United State