 \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thanks, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a great show.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Well, we have breaking news tonight on top of breaking news about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "" revealed tonight that the woman who said \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  made her pregnant and paid for her abortion has now also documented for "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "" a second pregnancy that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  asked her to abort. She refused and that child is now 10 years old.\par \par \uc2 After that story broke in "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," the "Daily Beast," which did their first reporting on \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paying for an abortion, released this text between the woman who first had an abortion for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and then had a baby the next time \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  got her pregnant, and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's current wife. Here are those texts.\par \par \uc2 "Did you know Herschel paid for my abortion the first time? Or that he told me it wasn't the right time to have the baby that she did have."\par \par \uc2 "This makes me incredibly sad," is the reply from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's wife. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's current wife does not say anything about the abortion. She says, "This makes me incredibly sad. You know I have continually tried to bridge a better relationship between you and Herschel."\par \par \uc2 Now, we are going to have more on this later in this hour. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's current wife never, ever responded to the question about what she knew about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paying for the abortion. We are going to cover more of this later in the hour when we will be joined by Georgia residents and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " columnist Charles Blow.\par \par \uc2 But first, today, the special master, Judge Raymond Dearie, and the appropriately titled case of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  versus United States of America issued a two-and-a-half-page order that indicates the processing of the non-classified documents in the case appears to be moving smoothly with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's lawyers agreeing with the government lawyers that several documents they have already analyzed are not protected by attorney client privileges, or any other privilege.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, according to reporting in "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "", the Justice Department faces the possibility of having to obtain more search warrants to search more Trump properties for more stolen government records and classified records. "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "" is reporting that the Justice Department suspects what everyone else in the world suspects, which is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still has more documents and that he has not turned those documents over to the government.\par \par \uc2 The "Times" reports, "A top Justice Department official told former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's lawyers in recent weeks that the department believed he had not returned all the documents he took when he left the White House, according to two people briefed on the matter. The outreach from the official, Jay I. Bratt, who leads the department's counter intelligence operations, is the most concrete indication yet that investigators remain skeptical that Mr. Trump has been fully cooperative in their efforts to recover documents. It is not clear what steps the Justice Department might take to retrieve any material it thinks Mr. Trump still holds."\par \par \uc2 Well, let's see. They tried asking politely for over a year and that didn't work. Then they tried to subpoena to get all the remaining documents from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Florida residence, and that didn't work. And then they got a search warrant and they only searched two rooms in a very large resident, a basement storeroom and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's office, where they found classified documents in his desk.\par \par \uc2 And that is not his only desk. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a desk in his Manhattan home at 725 Fifth Avenue. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a desk at the golf club in New Jersey where he spends most of his time in the summer. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  obviously likes having classified documents close to him so that he can wave them around and show them to people as you have reportedly done.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 Would \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leave all -- all of his beloved classified documents in Florida when he goes north for the whole summer? If a heroin dealer had three residences with three desks, would the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just search the Florida one and just assume the heroin dealer would never take heroin with him for the summer and keep it in his New Jersey golf club and he would never take heroin with him to what used to be the home he lived in most of the time in midtown Manhattan, the place where he has lived the longest of anywhere, he's lived in his whole life.\par \par \uc2 "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "" reports that the Trump team of lawyers disagreed on how to respond to Jay Bratt's communication to them, and that the Justice Department does not believe that they have retrieved all of the documents.\par \par \uc2 "The outreach from the department prompted a rift among Mr. Trump's lawyers about how to respond with one camp counseling a cooperative approach that would include bringing in an outside firm to conduct a further search for documents and another advising Mr. Trump to maintain a more combative posture. The more combative camp, the people briefed on the matter said, won out."\par \par \uc2 The question on this Friday night is, how combative is the Justice Department going to be? With the answers to that, we are joined by Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney and former deputy assistant attorney general. He is now a legal affairs columnist for the "\uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Also joining us, Bradley Moss, a national security attorney. And Ben Rhodes, former deputy national security adviser to President Obama. He is an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 And Harry, I just want to begin with you on this order by the special master tonight. It's a very -- it's not an easy document to read, two and a half pages. I did my best with it. I tried to decipher it. Have a feeling about what is going on there, but what do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: So, here is the deal, Lawrence. There are these documents, not the classified ones, remember, that he is reviewing, 90 or so of them Trump is making a claim of attorney client privilege. Dearie is just going to call balls and strikes on that, which is what special masters do. You wouldn't have needed him here, but that's straightforward.\par \par \uc2 Then there are another 28 documents where he is not claiming attorney client privilege. And these are the most interesting to me because what Dearie is saying is you give these now to the case team, privilege review team, and case team you confer with Trump about whether there is any executive privilege claim to be made, and get back to me by October 20th.\par \par \uc2 That means by October 20th, Trump is going to have to come forward with some theory or else shut up about why there are any kinds of executive privileges there that give him some right to the documents. And I think the DOJ is confident enough and if they are letting the case team look at it.\par \par \uc2 They would never let the case team look at attorney-client privileges, that could be a death ray for them, but they are letting them look and confer with Trump's team, and where Trump's team is going to have to come forward in two weeks and say here is why we think these executive privileges claim -- documents should be -- come back to the president, and I think there is no legal argument they can make and that will flush them out.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Okay. Harry, as our former prosecutor among us tonight, I have to begin with you also on where the Justice Department stands tonight. Here is a guy with three residences that he uses. He's got classified documents in the desk of one of them. He's got a store room full of classified documents, and he's got two other residences.\par \par \uc2 J. Bratt, Justice Department, communicates with the Trump lawyers, we think he still has some more stuff. And the Trump lawyers decide, after a little fight amongst themselves, to go with the combative response to that. So, what is the Justice Department, what should be the Justice Department's response to the combative response?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: They're going to be as combative as they can to be under the law. I just want to be clear about that. First, point one, when Jay Bratt says that, that's not casual. They have good reason to think it and it's almost like a soft target letter to Trump saying you are really flirting with further exposure for obstruction.\par \par \uc2 But, remember, probable cause -- you need to get a warrant, probable cause that evidence of a crime is at a particular place and it's fresh. And I think they just don't have it yet. They had it before on August 8th because they had someone who was saying, you know, the day before, this is there.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 I think they don't have it yet, it's not that they are being at all skittish or squeamish, but they don't have the legal tools at their disposal to force him to cough it up. But they are giving him stern warnings that this is compounding the case of obstruction against them.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Okay, Harry, quick follow-up here because I know everyone in the audience out there now, millions of people are out there saying, now, wait a minute. You found a bunch of classified documents at one of his homes, why isn't that enough probable cause to search the rest of his home?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Probable cause means more likely than not for specific reasons. You're going to find it there and it's a pretty good surmise, and Brad has it, but you know, he's got four different homes. You've got to specify which one and give real reasons to the magistrate. I think they just don't have it now otherwise they'd have stormed in and served another warrant.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Bradley Moss, say you've got a client who they've already found hundreds of classified documents in his possession in three different loads of stuff that you've obtained, including one load from a search warrant. And the government says to you as a lawyer representing that person, we believe he has more. What are the burdens that are on the lawyers to respond in a situation like that and where do they run into possible risks to themselves in that situation?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY MOSS, NATIONAL SECURITY ATTORNEY: Sure. So, the lawyer is still permitted to, you know, zealously defend the client there, but not necessarily required, under any circumstance, to put the client in greater jeopardy or to commit or to admit or to confess to anything.\par \par \uc2 But you do have an obligation to make sure that whatever you say to the government as the lawyer for the clients, you have sufficient reason to believe is going to be truthful that you are not committing perjury, and that you're not assisting the client in committing some other kind of crime trying to conceal things.\par \par \uc2 So, there is very much a fine line, you know, a needle you have to thread here in terms of how much do you push back on the client? How much do you try to ensure the client has actually complied with their obligations while still remembering your obligation there is to the client. You have to protect their rights, they still have rights, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still has rights here, as he has been vetted for even yet while maintaining your ethical obligations as well and not committing a crime in your own stead, by your own actions.\par \par \uc2 It's a very fine line they're going to have to walk. I certainly think they should had gone with the less combative approach, you know, that's what the lawyer was brought in to do to fix. It would have been a lot better for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But of course, his worst angels always been out.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Ben Rhodes, I have vividly in my head your description of the way you had to handle these kinds of classified documents when you were working in the White House. And even that description of having the burn bag where they would, at certain point, end up because after you've read them, the next thing for them -- to happen to them is that they must be burned for some of these documents.\par \par \uc2 When you hear about the way those documents were held at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's Florida residence, and he continued, apparently, well founded suspicion by the Justice Department that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still had some. He's got them somewhere. He's got them in New Jersey. He's got them in Manhattan. He's got more of them in other rooms in Florida that they didn't search. What are the dangers of him continuing to hold these documents?\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, the dangers are that there'll be exposure of U.S. intelligence, sources and methods, human beings around the world who help us gather information, sensitive intelligence collection program using technologies around the world, or even just assessments the United States has about a variety of other governments, right. Who wouldn't want that information out there?\par \par \uc2 We know that there is (inaudible) regarding the French president there was at Mar-a-Lago, that's already probably created a strain in a really important alliance that the United States has with France because the French president is probably wondering why our assessments or information relevant to me down in Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 And to build on what I said, Lawrence, you know, to transport classified documents, as I sometimes had to do from a secure facility in the White House, let's say just an abandoned Air Force One. I used to carry it in what's called a lock bag. Literally a bag that has a heavy zipper, a heavy, difficult to destroy exterior and a lock and key, right?\par \par \uc2 These are not documents that are meant to be hanging around Trump Tower or Mar-a-Lago or Bedminster or any of these places that are totally not secure that are filled with people walking through there that have been intelligence targets for the Chinese intelligence service, the Russian intelligence service, the Iranian intelligence service.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:05]\par \par \uc2 Do you think that those foreign adversaries have not been trying to get people in the door at one of these parties at Mar-a-Lago or one of these golf tournaments at Bedminster? Of course, they have. And so, the danger is it could expose really sensitive secrets of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  and I think that the part of what the Justice Department or the U.S. intelligence community has probably done is kind of reverse engineer, like what kind of information wasn't returned to us by President Trump or what kind of information have we heard from people that worked for him, was he kind of taking with him into the residence or the White House that might have disappeared in one of these boxes?\par \par \uc2 They probably have some pretty good idea of what they're concerned about being out of the custody of the \uc2 United States government\uc2  that is informing all of this, right, all of these interest in recovering what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has taken. And again, the long pole in the tent here, the thing we don't know is why did he take these documents to what purpose? And that's I think information that ultimately will come out.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Yeah. That point about purpose has never been responded to by Trump in any way. Bradley Moss, one reason we know that there are more is that the archives has said that there are more. The archives in communication with the January 6th committee has said we do know that we do not have custody of everything we should.\par \par \uc2 And so that's kind of where this began for the investigation before the Justice Department got involved whereas the National Archives saying we do not have everything, and that eventually brought in the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Yeah. This is the national archives doing exactly what they are supposed to do. This is their job in this situation. They are the repository for these records, especially for presidential records. This is their job. You know, it's laughable when you see all these commentary from former President Trump, you know, calling them this woke leftist organization.\par \par \uc2 National Archives are basically the supreme nerds of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . They are the (inaudible) people. They are that librarian who oversees everything. That's all they are doing. They got a laundry list or grocery store list, whatever you want to call it, of things they know are supposed to be there that was supposed to be turned over. Here is from this file that we know is missing. Here are these records on these letters with Kim Jong-un, things like that.\par \par \uc2 That's all they're looking. Just the document and archive it. And the fact that they still can't get all of these, the fact that they still can't identify where the remaining pieces are is why this remains such a serious concern and why the DOJ is pushing as hard as they are.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: And from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  inventory of what they found, 48 empty folders previously containing classified records, 40 empty folders designated for sensitive documents and marked return to staff secretary and military aide. What used to be in those folders? That's one of the ongoing questions. Bradley Moss, Harry Litman, Ben Rhodes, thank you all very much for starting us off on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Have a good evening.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thank you. And coming with breaking news in the Georgia Senate race, "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "" is reporting tonight that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  urged his former girlfriend to have a second abortion after he paid for her first abortion. And the "Daily Beast" has now published new text messages in that case. Georgia resident and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " columnist Charles Blow will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Senator, most candidates in your position would use these latest allegations against your opponent, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , to their political advantage, as political ammunition. Why haven't you done that?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): Well, I think that the people of Georgia have an opportunity to look at his record and to look at mine, and be very clear, there is a stark difference between the two of us. The differences between me and my opponent couldn't be more obvious. I think that we have seen some disturbing things, we've seen a disturbing pattern, and it raises real questions about who is actually ready to represent the people of Georgia in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: That was Senator Raphael Warnock campaign for reelection in Georgia today on a day when his opponent, former football player \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who was chosen to run for Senate by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , faced more revelations from a former girlfriend who says that he urged her to get an abortion in 2009 and paid for her abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "" is reporting tonight that the still unnamed woman is the mother of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's 10-year-old son who was born after a pregnancy that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  also wanted to abort. "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 "" is reporting that the woman says \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , quote, "paid for her abortion in 2009 and that he urged her to terminate a second pregnancy two years later. They ended their relationship after she refused.\par \par \uc2 In a series of interviews, the woman said, Mr. Walker had barely been involved in their now 10-year-old son's life, offering little more than a court ordered child support and occasional gifts. As a father, he's done nothing. He does exactly what the courts say, and that is it, she said. He has to be held responsible just like the rest of us. And if you're going to run for office, you need to own your life."\par \par \uc2 The woman showed "The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " the $575 receipt for her abortion procedure, and a copy of a check from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  reimbursing her, as well as a get-well card signed by \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . All of that documentation has already been cited in the "Daily Beast's" original reporting of the story earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 But what "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is adding tonight is that there was second pregnancy in the relationship and that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wanted to end that second pregnancy also.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:04]\par \par \uc2 And the woman's refusal to end that pregnancy ended their relationship. The "Times" reports that a year after the child was born, quote, "the woman filed for child support in Manhattan where she was a graduate student at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  and struggling to make ends meet, according to a statement from her lawyer at the time. Mr. Walker was initially ordered to make payments first of $2,500 a month, and later $3,500 a month."\par \par \uc2 "The Times" reports that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's current wife asked the woman in June, quote, "To publicly vouch for Mr. Walker's on time child support payment in an effort to make the case that Mr. Walker was a responsible father. The woman said she declined."\par \par \uc2 And just a few hours ago, the "Daily Beast" followed up the "Times" reporting with even more messages between the still anonymous woman and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's current wife. They published this text exchange in which the anonymous woman asks Walker's wife, "Did you know Herschel paid for my abortion the first time? Or that he told me it wasn't the right time to have their child."\par \par \uc2 Walker's wife responds saying, "This makes me incredibly sad. You know I have continually tried to bridge a better relationship between you and Herschel, putting the child first. I've witnessed every day Herschel pray for you, the child, and everyone in our family. I have seen him call and text regularly to have a relationship with the child and he gets no response and the sadness when he doesn't hear back. He has always strived to be more than financial support. God is a good God. I love you both." The woman and then responds, "Are you kidding me? Stop it. Stop lying."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " opinion columnist Charles Blow. He is an MSNBC political analyst and a resident of Atlanta, Georgia. You are in the middle of this cross fire in Georgia tonight, Charles. What do you make of this latest round of revelation upon revelation. Now, we're getting text messages between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's current wife and the woman involved in the abortion story.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Listen, abortions are not the problem. Every woman should have the option to get a safe abortion. That is not the problem for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . The problem with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is that he appears to have lied about it. He appears to have a campaign where he was so antiabortion and he wanted to have no exception of any sort in cases of women's pregnancies for abortion.\par \par \uc2 And this is the same man who appears to have not only paid for an abortion, but tried to pay for another one. It is the hypocrisy of it all that kind of chase people. And that is a thing here like, I think, you know, what we are seeing is that nothing really pierces the bubble. Maybe this does, but you have a Republican electorate that wants to hear lies that soothe more than they want to hear truths that hurt.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has a lot of truths that hurt. And he is taking the path that Trump did, which is just deny it. Just tell them a lie, they want to hear a lie, they want to believe something other than truth because the truth is not in these guy's favor.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: And the most recent why he is told about this, which is now a provable lie, is that he has been saying all week I have no idea who this could be. He said that on Fox. He said that to \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . He said it to all the right-wing radio and T.V. he could do. He lied all week saying I don't know who this could possibly be.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Right. And you can say if you or somebody who just wants to flip the Senate, you just want the Georgia seat, you can say, okay, you know, he was trying to get out of this embarrassing (inaudible). You can't say though that this person wanted to abort this child and abort this pregnancy, and this is a person who believes that, you know, this is a child of moral (ph) conception.\par \par \uc2 This is a person who believes that they have no exceptions, right? You can't have both of those things. And you also cannot have a person who has nothing to do with the child's life. That's what the mother basically said that, you know, there has been three meetings in 10 years? How do you wrap that up? How do you, as Christian conservatives, make that make sense?\par \par \uc2 [22:30:05]\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: And he started the campaign around I think one acknowledged child. Another four, let's listen to what his son, Christian Walker has said about this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER, SON OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Family values, people.\par \par \uc2 He has four kids, four different women, was not in the house raising one of them. He was out having sex with other women.\par \par \uc2 Do you care about family values? We were told at the beginning of this he was going to get ahead of his past. Hold himself accountable, all of these different things and that would have been fine, go ahead. He didn't do any of that.\par \par \uc2 Everything has been a lie, everything has been downplayed, everything has been cutting corners. The whole thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: He started off as a supporter of his father's candidacy. What was your reaction to that, Charles?\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Listen, Christian is problematic unto himself. So I understand that. I understand also, however, he is probably hurt by all of this. You can be a really toxic person and also have feelings that get hurt. Both of those things can be true of Christian Walker.\par \par \uc2 But it is true that Christian walker is a really toxic person. But the message there is still the message. Which is, he is not, in that segment, saying things that are not true.\par \par \uc2 These are the facts of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's life like. You know, by the time we get to election day, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  could have 12 kids running around.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Charles Blow in the thick of it, in Atlanta through this campaign. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thank you. And coming up, Wisconsin's Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes just wrapped up his senate debate, senate campaign debate with Republican Senator Ron Johnson. He will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MANDELA BARNES (D-WI), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Under my plan, dangerous people don't get to buy the way out of prison. Now, Senator Johnson might not have encountered a problem he couldn't buy his way out of, but that is not reality for a majority of people in the state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:32:03]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have proposed reviewing Social Security rather than having it be what's called an entitlement, which means it goes on.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RON JOHNSON (R-WI): You are putting words in my mouth.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I was talking about --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your words.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: That was right wing Trump worshipping Republican Senator Ron Johnson in his debate tonight in Wisconsin against our next guest Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden said this about Ron Johnson's plan to cut Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The Senator in charge of electing Republicans in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  this year has proposed a plan to put Social Security and Medicare on the chopping block every five years.\par \par \uc2 Enter the Senator from Wisconsin, Ron Johnson. He thinks waiting five years is too long. And he says Social Security and Medicare should be on the chopping block every single year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Ron Johnson is echoing Republican senators who have been saying things like that for decades about Social Security. Ron Johnson, who has supported banning all abortions, is now walking back from that position and proposing a statewide referendum for Wisconsin voters to decide the future of abortion in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: The question that has to be answered is at what point does society have the responsibility to protect life? It's just that simple.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: When you take a position like that, there is so much that you don't always see. Let's talk about Texas as an example. There's a woman who had to be hooked up to a breathing machine because she had complications with her pregnancy and cannot get an abortion. A ten year old girl in Ohio was raped and had to cross state lines to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 That is Ron Johnson's America. The senator would rather make a political point than do the right thing to protect families and to protect the health of women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: When Ron Johnson tried to portray himself as a supporter of law enforcement, this happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Whenever I see a police officer, I go up to him and say thank you for your service. And if I have time, I say please don't be dispirited by the loud few who are trying to defund you.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: I'm sure you did not have the same interaction with the 140 officers that were injured during the January 6th insurrection. One officer was stabbed with middle stake. Another crushed between a revolving door. Another hit in the head with a fire extinguisher.\par \par \uc2 So when we talk about respect for law enforcement, let's talk about the 140 officers that he left behind because of an insurrection that he supported.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Joining us now is the Lieutenant Governor of Wisconsin, Mandela Barnes. He's Wisconsin's Democratic candidate for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight after this debate. What do you - - for you, what is the most important point that Ron Johnson made in the debate that you need to answer?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well, the thing is, Ron Johnson can't be trusted. Ron Johnson can't defend his dangerous position on abortion. He supported eight different national abortion bans. Some with no exceptions for rape, incest, or life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 You can't defend his position on January 6th. He can't defend his position when it comes to shipping good paying jobs out of state and overseas. Ron Johnson continues to leave working people behind. He definitely cannot be trusted with our health or to keep us safe.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:55]\par \par \uc2 BARNES: But I will tell you, there is big money interests that are supporting him. Ron Johnson has had an unprecedented sum of money that has been dumped into this race to try to discredit me.\par \par \uc2 And we need help. If folks can go to MandelaBarnes.com, it will make a huge difference to get rid of this person who does not care about your rights, who would absolutely vote to support another national or -- to support a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Someone who will go to the same length that he you went to last time to overturn a free and fair election if he does not like the results.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: The latest poll shows you in a straight tie, 47 to 47 with Ron Johnson. But a poll of the people of Wisconsin on issues of abortion shows only 5 percent support for a total ban on abortion, which has historically been the Ron Johnson position on this, even though he seems to be trying to get away from that position that is supported only by 5 percent of the people in Wisconsin. Where is abortion in the priority list for voters in Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: It's in an issue of freedom. This is about personal choice, peoples rights. And politicians should not be able to get in the way of women making this decision. And doctors should not be criminalized for guiding women through the most difficult decision of their lives.\par \par \uc2 I'm an only child, but I'm not my mother's only pregnancy. My mother had a pregnancy before I was born that was complicated. She had to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Under Ron Johnson's plan, my mother would have been criminalized for making the best decision for her health. It's too out of touch and too extreme, it's personal for me and it is personal for so many others across the state, and so many others across the country.\par \par \uc2 People are way closer to abortion stories than they may even realize. And that's why Ron Johnson is trying to walk back. But he cannot, because the evidence is clear. And he talks thoughts about this referendum, he knows that is not reality, it will never happen.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: The Social Security issue is something that Republicans have been -- they have been saying like what Ron Johnson has been saying for decades, literally for decades. But usually when they say it, they do get in trouble politically, and they tend to run away from it as we have seen - - as it seems to be that's what Ron Johnson is doing in the debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 He has said that Social Security should come up for regular funding reviews. There should be nothing automatic about it. You should absolutely have no right to get a cost of living increase in Social Security, automatically, because the cost of living has gone up. And in this year of inflation, the cost of living increase for Social Security is the largest it has been in many years.\par \par \uc2 Ron Johnson, and many Republicans, have always opposed that. What was he saying tonight about his new position on Social Security?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Well again, another issue he tried to walk away from. The reality is, people have worked their entire lives for the Social Security benefits that they're receiving. Ron Johnson simply doesn't think you should have it. Ron Johnson has only been in it for his wealthy donors.\par \par \uc2 That's what I said when I closed out the debate, if you are a multi millionaire, or a donor to his campaign, sure, he might look out for you. But if you're like the rest of us, if you're a working class person in the state, times are going to be tough.\par \par \uc2 He sells out working people every chance he gets, that's what's at snake with this election. People's retirement security, people's right to make their own health care decisions. It is all on the line right now. Ron Johnson has no problem taking that away from you.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Has Ron Johnson pledged to abide by and accept the results of this election?\par \par \uc2 BARNES: I don't know if that is clear for sure, I know I certainly have said that I do plan (ph) to accept the results of the election. You have to ask him directly about that one.\par \par \uc2 You saw what happened when he did not get the election result he liked in 2020. He tried to send fake electors to the vice president then tried to say his involvement only lasted a matter of seconds.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Democratic candidate for senate in Wisconsin Mandela Barnes, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Coming up, we have breaking news from Uvalde tonight where the entire school district police force has been suspended -- the entire police force has been suspended. State senator Roland Gutierrez will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:15]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Here's a sentence I've never said before. The entire police department has been suspended. The entire police department for the school district in Uvalde, Texas was suspended today. A school district put out a statement today which reads in part, "Recent developments have uncovered additional concerns with department operations. As a result, the district has made the decision to suspend all activities of the Uvalde consolidated independent school district police department for a period of time. Officers currently employed will fill other roles in the district."\par \par \uc2 Officials have not said what the recent developments are that provoked this. The district placed Lieutenant Miguel Mike Hernandez on administrative leave. Lt. Hernandez had taken over for the fired police chief, \uc2 Pete Arredondo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:49:47]\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Yesterday, the school police fired Officer Crimson Elizondo (ph) who joined the department after the mass murder at Robb Elementary School. She was fired after a CNN report on Wednesday revealed she was not only on campus during the May attack as a Texas state trooper, but under investigation over her actions while a gunman killed 19 students and two teachers in which she was recorded on body camera video saying, "If my son had been in there, I would not have been outside, I promise you that."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez. He represents Texas' 19th district which includes Uvalde. Senator, thank you very much for joining us once again. You have been an invaluable guide for months now through this tragedy.\par \par \uc2 It is a small police department, six or seven members -- what brought this about? The basically temporary disbanding of it, at least?\par \par \uc2 ROLAND GUTIERREZ (D-TX), STATE SENATOR: Well, thank you Lawrence for having me. I mean I think that we have to put all of this in perspective. It's been so hard on so many people. 19 children died and two teachers -- God knows how many children would have been saved if the police had actually gone in on time.\par \par \uc2 The average mass shooting, it's said that we have statistic, but it's 10 minutes. This one lasted 77 minutes where 390 plus police officers stood around, including officers like Ms. Elizondo who was within with the Department of Public Safety.\par \par \uc2 It has taken parents and some investigative reporting to finally get something done. And yet, this governor has failed to do anything for these people that is of merit and of value. We absolutely need to go to the next step and start looking at the Department of Public Safety and that still has not happened today yet, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: The Department of Public Safety which is a state police force under control of the governor, was the largest police force that was present that day. And all of the heat, all of the pressure, has been on this tiny six -- seven person police force that was assigned to the schools.\par \par \uc2 Why has there been no momentum toward discovering what the state police were doing?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: You've had one district attorney, Republican district attorney, who has said that there's a criminal investigation, and therefore she cannot release the data, Lawrence. As of ten days ago, she met with the parents and said, I can't indict anybody, I'm not going to indict anybody. But I'm not going to release anything until after November. Ironically, we have an election on November 8th, as you know.\par \par \uc2 It's astounding to me that we can't get to the real information so that we can give these families some sense of closure, so that we as policy makers can get something done. We are here today because people have chosen, and made choices, and those choices have been to not tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Public Safety, as you know and you have reported, has time and time again doled out misinformation to the people of Uvalde and the people of Texas, in a wholly, grossly, negligent manner. We need to do better. This governor needs to ask for this accountability.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: And what does it say to the people outside of Uvalde, and their public school systems all over the state of Texas? That there hasn't been a state level accountability here.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Well, you're right, Lawrence, there hasn't been a state level of accountability, the so-called leaders of the state, the Republicans that are in charge, they said well, we have done something, we doled out $100 million -- $50 million of which were for ballistic shields.\par \par \uc2 We had plenty of ballistic shields and Kevlar and long guns, and everything else. We needed $100 million worth of (INAUDIBLE). The fact is, we had everything that was needed to go into the room and yet the leaders haven't fixed the basic problem in Texas, Lawrence, which is access to guns.\par \par \uc2 The common denominator here is young men having access to militarized weaponry. And this governor, because he is so lost in his own ambition, refuses to attack the basic thing.\par \par \uc2 So it says to the public, I guess that Abbott just doesn't care about us.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez, thank you very much for joining us again tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Tonight's LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:33]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Time for tonight's LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 The casts of "The West Wing" and "Veep" are teaming up this weekend to help Democratic candidates in a livestream event.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIA LOUIS DREYFUS, ACTOR: "President Bartlett and President Meyer.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY WHITFORD, ACTOR: Josh, Toby, and C.J. together with Amy, Dan, and Jonah.\par \par \uc2 DREYFUS: Just imagine, Kent Davidson facing off with Vice President Bingo Bob Russell.\par \par \uc2 WHITFORD: They can't meet.\par \par \uc2 DREYFUS: What?\par \par \uc2 [22:59:54]\par \par \uc2 WHITFORD: They can't meet because they were both actually played by Gary Cole.\par \par \uc2 DREYFUS: What? Gary Cole is on "The West Wing"?\par \par \uc2 WHITFORD: Yes. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 DREYFUS: Jesus. If I'd known that, we never would have (INAUDIBLE) hired him on "Veep".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Bradley Whitford and Julia Louis Dreyfus get tonight's LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 And a programming note, Rachel Maddow will join us on Monday at 10:00 p.m. right here on THE LAST WORD to discuss her new podcast "ULTRA" about American extremism. The first two episodes will be available Monday morning, wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221008eia700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 TOURE, CREATOR, BLACK MINDS MATTER NEWSLETTER: It is a deep look at what happened with Jeffrey Dahmer. And I want to point out. A lot of people are like, you know, man, that`s hard to watch because a lot of Black people get killed in that. Yes, totally true. However, there are a lot of Black heroes in this who are standing up to Dahmer, standing up to the system that is ignoring them. Niecy Nash`s character, there`s Jesse Jackson character, there`s also --\par \par \uc2 JASON JOHNSON, MSNBC HOST: We got go Toure. We got to go. I just got to say this quick. If you don`t want to be depressed, there`s other things you could also watch. I think who won the week is Uvalde School District that got rid of all their police officers. Toure and Atima Omara, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 ELMER STEWART RHODES, FOUNDER OATH KEEPERS: Trump actually failed. We were calling for him to use the Insurrection Act all through the summer. He did not do it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Day four of the seditious conspiracy trial.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Trump should have acted and should have just been just manned up and done it, but he didn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, new evidence of just how hard the Oath Keepers press for a coup and Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  and what it all means for the Committee`s final hearing. Then, even more new reporting suggesting the Justice Department thinks \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still hiding documents.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new reporting that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  urged his ex-girlfriend to have a second abortion. And Joe Cirincione on the President`s candid remarks about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and nuclear Armageddon when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Let me begin tonight by setting the scene for you at the E. Barrett Prettyman Federal Courthouse in Washington DC. That is where five members of the right-wing gang the Oath Keepers are currently on trial for seditious conspiracy in connection with their plot to keep \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in power after he lost the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been bringing you updates from this courthouse as the trial unfolded this week. And today, we saw by far the most jaw-dropping evidence yet as the Department of Justice revealed exactly with the leaders of the Oath Keeper Elmer Stewart Rhodes III was saying and planning in the months leading up to January 6.\par \par \uc2 Now we do already know quite a bit about Rhodes and what he got up to in those months after the election. He was gathering a group of Oath Keepers in Washington DC. He wanted then-President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to invoke the insurrection act and deputize his group under that old law as a sort of state-sanctioned militia. He was preparing to fight a bloody civil war to stop the peaceful transfer of power. And we know all of that because Elmer Stewart Rhodes III, a Yale Law grad, said all that himself in public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RHODES: We have men already stationed outside DC as a nuclear option in case the attempt to remove the president illegally, we will step in and stop it. So, I`ve got good men on the ground already. We`ve been doing recon there last week. And we`re sorting out where we`re going to be staging, and we`ll be there. We`ll be inside DC, we`ll also be on the outside of DC armed and prepared to go in if the President calls us out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, the defense attorneys for Stewart Rhodes are arguing that he believed everything he did in the lead up to January 6 was legal, because he thought \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would invoke the Insurrection Act. They claim that Rhodes and the Oath Keepers were just preparing themselves, waiting for Trump before they acted. But the Department of Justice has already poked a big hole in that argument. They played this clip of Rhodes from November of 2020 explaining that the Insurrection Act was actually their legal cover.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RHODES: We also want to have some our best men posted up outside. We`ll have to make a determination of who goes where and who`s going to be on the outside and who`s going to be inside. So, we`re going to have a QRF, armed QRF. But that QRF will be awaiting the President`s orders, OK? That`s our official position. And the reason why we have to do it that way is because that gives you legal cover.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s our official position. You know, someone is speaking in good faith when they say that. Now, the QRF there that he references is short for quick reaction force, which is what the Oath Keepers called their teams that are armed heavily and ready to deploy. And that brings us to today`s testimony when we got to hear how Elmer Stewart Rhodes was directing the Oath Keepers in private.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ called the \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agents of the stand who investigated the case. They asked him to read aloud for the jury some of the messages they obtained during the investigation that Rhodes sent to Oath Keeper group chats. They paint a picture of a man who was dead set on marshalling violence, to wage war against the rightfully elected government and stop peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 On November 25, 202o, three weeks after Trump lost the election, Rhodes wrote to a group called Old Leadership Chat, "Time to get serious about training and force on force is the way to go." Two days later he elaborated, "It will be 1776 all over again or more like 1775 all over again." On December 14, the day when the electors cast their votes formalizing Joe Biden`s win, Rhodes told the group, "Trump has one last chance to act. He must use the Insurrection Act unless we fight a bloody civil war revolution."\par \par \uc2 The following day, Rhodes wrote to another group. That one called themselves friends of \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  or friends of Stone. "I feel like I`m taking crazy pills. LOL nonsense. He must do it now before January 6, referring to Trump, again, invoking the Insurrection Act. He brought it up again to keep the oath -- to the Oath Keepers leadership group at the end of December, saying he thought that Trump "now understands that the Insurrection Act and warfare with him as commander in chief is the only way that he can save our republic. But he needs to know that if he doesn`t do it, we will. And if we have to do it ourselves without him as Commander in Chief, it will be exponentially harder and many more of us will die."\par \par \uc2 On New Year`s Eve, Rhodes was clear about what he believed would happen in the following week. On the sixth, they are going to put the final nail in the coffin of this republic unless we fight our way out. With Trump preferably or without him, we have no choice.\par \par \uc2 Those messages show that what Elmer Stewart Rhodes was planning for and anticipating was a bloody, a violent insurrection. And ultimately, it also revealed a fascinating irony that I can`t quite get past. Like everyone else, every single other person who has ever placed their trust in one \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Rhodes, a man apparently willing to die on Trump`s behalf, ended up disappointed because the ex-President never did pull the trigger on the Insurrection Act. In fact, on Inauguration Day 2021, Rhodes whined about being let down by Trump and alarmingly issued a call to action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RHODES: It`s just amazing that Trump will let the election be stolen out from under him and then let our country be stolen like this, our government. So, we have to now prepare to walk the path of the Founding Fathers and declare your independence from that illegitimate regime. We still defend the Constitution, but they`re the ones that are suppers and the violators and declare that and then get strong in your communities. You need to be raising local militias in your towns and counties like the Founders did. You need to then nullify, refused to comply. And when they come for you, you defend yourselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, Stewart Rhodes is at the center of one of two seditious conspiracy cases the DOJ is currently pursuing. The second involves another right-wing gang, The Proud Boys, who are also connected to longtime Trump adviser \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 . Yesterday, one of their high-level members pleaded guilty to the first -- the first of the Proud Boys, in this case, to do so and agree to fully cooperate with prosecutors. There are Oath Keepers who have done that in the other case as well.\par \par \uc2 And as the DOJ continues its cases against these two right-wing gangs both for seditious conspiracy, the January 6 Committee has also been hard at work connecting the dots between their efforts in the ex-president`s administration. And that sets the stage for the Committee`s next hearing scheduled for Thursday at 1:00 p.m. We know they`re planning to show more video from a documentary team that followed \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  after releasing this clip last month.\par \par \uc2 And as we`ve seen from the trial, there`s some interesting new evidence, the connections between \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  and the far-right group the Oath Keepers. Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland is member of the January 6 Committee, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, it`s good to have you. First of all, are you following this trial at all? Is the committee following?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Very closely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I`m wondering if anything, there is a surprise to you. Obviously, the January 6 Committee and federal law enforcement have different tools at their disposal, different investigative methods. They can, for instance, in the case the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Department of Justice, have cooperators who will then give them things. Are you learning from the trial?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, we`re learning that Stewart Rhodes` only defense which is that he anticipated the Insurrection Act being invoked by the former president has already collapsed. They`ve already put on evidence demonstrating that he saw that as legal cover for what he was going to do regardless. He anticipated it being a far bloodier exercise without the thin veneer of this presidential order. But the whole thing is absurd to begin with because he`s saying, it`s like someone being charged with murder or saying well, I thought I was going to be deputized by the sheriff, but I wasn`t. So, in other words, I had no legal authority, but I thought I was going to have legal authority