eing the transmission belt between the highest precincts of power in the Republican Party, and then street fascists and hooligans. I mean, that`s how he`s defined his role. We saw that in 2000 during the Gucci riot as well.\par \par \uc2 But -- and \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2  also has been doing his outreach. So, I think -- I called these guys, the Flintstones. They are really the critical point of contact between the Trump campaign to overthrow the 2020 election and the domestic violent extremist groups. I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was pretty careful not to get his hands dirty with that, except in terms of what he would say in public, such as Proud Boys stand back and standby.\par \par \uc2 So, he made his intent clear at that broad level. It`s kind of like when he said to Putin to Russia, go out and find the emails. He would do it to that extent broadcasting his general intent. But in terms of working with them specifically, that was left to subordinates.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s interesting, this sort of -- this kind of off-book outsourcing to Stone who clearly is sort of in the middle of a lot of this. I mean, that`s just a documented fact we saw who was providing him security and the like. Is there anything you can tell us to look for or any broad contours of what to expect for on the hearing on Thursday?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, you know, the central culpability of done Trump is how we began the story and in some sense it has to end there. I do hope that we will be able to underscore the continuing clear and present danger that democratic institutions and the right to vote face in America because of this onslaught unleashed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We`ve got a majority of GOP candidates across the country who are now election deniers, and that obviously poses a serious threat of electoral destabilization.\par \par \uc2 Going forward, there are these heightened threats around galvanized domestic violent extremist groups, and then the continuing