ing right?\par \par \uc2 And the problem here is that I focus in on the line because one of the -- you know, Republicans are making a raft of bad hypocritical and frankly, pathetic arguments in his defense, but one of the consistent ones that you keep hearing is this idea that he is born again, this idea that people make mistakes in the past and then they can be forgiven.\par \par \uc2 Well, one of the first keys to forgiveness is admitting that you have done something wrong. And as of right now, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  takes no responsibility -- another word Republicans like to hypocritically throw around -- he takes no responsibility and he takes no honesty in his own actions. I mean, Chris, look, I am Catholic. We`ve got rules on this, all right. I know how many Hail Marys I have to say if I get a C in French, all right.\par \par \uc2 And the entry point to forgiveness is telling the truth, something that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  seems constitutionally unable to do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s a really good point. I was just saying this on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I mean, I too, I was raised Catholic. And, you know, you got to -- you got to come clean before you -- before you get to be forgiven. You don`t just to say, I`m saved by the grace of Jesus Christ, ergo, I got to get out of jail free hall pass the rest of my life. But crucially, also, in the non- theological context, just political context, when this story broke, you`ll remember the Georgia political operative Erick Erikson said, oh, I thought we all knew this. People change.\par \par \uc2 And he got pilloried for that understandably because it showed a certain hypocrisy about how seriously he takes abortion that is it indeed the murder of a child -- you don`t seem to think so -- but also had to delete it because it looked bad. But that was the correct political instinct. The correct political instinct was to do that and not lie and say this is defamatory. I`m suing because the woman in question is out t