ssion. So, I think that`s probably the big tip-off.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:11]\par \par \uc2 And from our reporting, we know that Trump`s own legal team is divided over whether or not to cooperate with the government in its request for the remaining documents.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s say more about that, because obviously he hired this very well-regarded lawyer down in Florida, who was a former solicitor general of the state, paid him a large retainer, if I`m not mistaken upfront, there`s been some reporting that he has been kind of sidelined from these discussions. But there`s a little bit of internal dissension among Trump`s legal counsel about which way to go, what is the debate?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: Sure. Yes, so you`re right, Christopher Kise, he`s the rare Trump lawyer to be paid. So, as part of the debate is that Kise himself would like for Trump to come clean if he has more documents, he would like to have a third party come in and search whatever of Trump`s properties needs to be searched, so that the third party can give the government a clean bill of health and a full assessment of what that party has found.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are other people in Trump`s legal orbit who have tried to convince the former president that he should actually fight this. And then if he does have anything, that it`s his, and that he shouldn`t try -- and that he should not give it back.\par \par \uc2 Now, it seems right now that it`s the latter camp that`s winning out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Do we know the empty documents? I mean, again, like this is not my area of expertise. But it certainly seems like if you see the empty documents, one of which, by the way, is on display at Trump`s bar in New York City. That`s it right there.\par \par \uc2 They come -- they`re not like -- my understanding of this is they don`t come just floating around as a standard product. Like they`re always married to the document as a matter of procedure, right? Is that right?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: 