y of the story, what you see is, you know, a letter from the National Archives and then a, you know, another letter and then the subpoena, and then the visit from the head of counterintelligence.\par \par \uc2 And then, the search does seem like they really are trying, and it does also seem that Trump truly believes there`s a debate here but whose they are. And he seems to think they`re his, which is what the kind of rock bottom of the dispute is.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: I mean, that`s one way to look at it. But the end of the day, they aren`t his and we`ve been told this, and he`s been told this, including by his own lawyers, and he still refuses to give them back.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that this opens up to really important questions for the Justice Department. One, how are they going to get them back? From our reporting, no one has made any decisions about whether or not this is something to negotiate, whether or not they should just get another search warrant, etcetera. So, that`s one, that`s sort of the near-term question.\par \par \uc2 The far-term question is, what does the department ultimately do about its handling of the situation --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: Any sort of criminal charges, because it might be hard to make a strong case in court about the actual national security assessment and some of the other charges on the Espionage Act, partly because if the information -- this is the bitter irony of it, if the information is extremely sensitive, it`s unlikely that it will be allowed to be used in a court of law. It`s hard to make these arguments.\par \par \uc2 But the -- but what seems like obstruction is how I`ll put it seems to be playing out in public back and forth in real-time. And how can the department address that? And will they ignore it?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it seems like they`re being backed into indicting the guy which is bizarre, we`ll say independent of the actual what happens, which I can`t predict.\par \par \uc2 If I were writing a screenplay, a comedy. By far the funniest thing that could happen would be a search of Bedminster where they find a bunch of classified documents.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:08]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, exactly. Like that`s definitely the funniest thing to happen. And so, who knows?\par \par \uc2 Katie Benner, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the massive disparity in COVID deaths between Republicans and Democrats and what it means for this winter surge, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All week, I`ve been thinking about this new study that just published about the danger of not taking COVID seriously, and it`s confirmed what I suspected from the beginning when many of us did, we have seen Republican politicians and Fox News anchors, specifically undermining and attacking COVID vaccinations specifically for cheap political gain or ratings.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:18]\par \par \uc2 This is what they are still saying on Fox News, 2-1/2 years into the pandemic. And remember, that not a single thing here is true.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, FOUNDER, OUTKICK: Effectively, in my opinion, these COVID shots are like used cars that didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KIRSCH\uc2 , ENTREPRENEUR: Clearly, you are more likely to be injured or dead from the vaccine than if you were unvaccinated.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Now the CDC is actually saying that people that are boostered have a greater likelihood of getting the current variant of COVID.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The new variant booster, they want to sell that after telling us the pandemic is done? Forget it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, none of that is true. Now, there is good evidence that hundreds of thousands of people are dead, and shouldn`t be because of disinformation like that.\par \par \uc2 New study by the \uc2 National Bureau of Economic Research\uc2  found "Political party affiliation was associated with excess death rates at the individual level during the initial years of the COVID-19 pandemic. Registered Republicans in Florida and Ohio had higher excess death rates than registered Democrats, driven by a large mortality gap, get this, in the period after all adults were eligible for vaccines."\par \par \uc2 OK, that`s the key part, that last part.\par \par \uc2 Republicans had higher excess deaths that were made worse after vaccines were available. So, look at this chart from the study. At the beginning of the pandemic, you see the excess death rates go up and down, but they`re pretty much the same between the red and the blue lines, they`re basically touching.\par \par \uc2 The line start to diverge after May of 2021 when vaccines become widely available, the red line, the one on the higher up representing the excess death rates of registered Republicans start separating and rising higher than the blue line, which represents the excess death rate of registered Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And you can see, as of the most current data, the red line is just way higher.\par \par \uc2 After April 5th, 2021, when all adults were eligible for COVID-19 vaccines in Florida and Ohio, the gap in death rates between Republicans and Democrats increased to a 153 percent difference.\par \par \uc2 In plain terms, there are so many people who died of COVID because they listened to people on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing that is really maddening to me is that in some ways the most cynical voices around vaccination ended up winning a weird kind of victory because they`ve sort of politicize the straightforward and easy COVID prevention measures, mitigation measures, the vaccine. And that combined with an understandable COVID exhaustion, which we all feel has made the U.S. wildly under-vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 I know it sounds crazy to say that but that`s the case. There is an enormous amount of easy COVID prevention being done that is abandoned. And it`s leading to thousands of people dying that don`t have to.\par \par \uc2 There has been a noticeable drop off in vaccination rates too. Nearly 93 percent of people 65 and older, the most vulnerable, got two shots, the original vaccine or one shot of JnJ. OK, that`s good, although still can be higher. Only 45 percent of seniors have gotten or intend to get the two boosters.\par \par \uc2 And again, that matters because seniors are the ones who are dying at wildly disproportionate rates. People 65 and older account for 16 percent of the total U.S. population, but 75 percent of all COVID deaths to date.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s a new booster, it`s called the bivalent booster. It`s built off of the latest variant Omicron, which other variants are sort of developing out of. It has very promising data around it, and it`s designed to handle Omicron and things that come out of it.\par \par \uc2 Every adult is eligible to get it. Most people don`t know about it, or don`t plan on getting it. I`m telling you, OK, listen to me. It`s our third time around this merry-go-round. Another COVID winter, there`s going to be a lot more COVID this fall and winter, it`s going to happen. It`s like flu in cold season.\par \par \uc2 If for no other reason than just give yourself and your loved ones particularly seniors in your lives. Just a slightly better chance of not having to be knocked out for a week or two by COVID or giving it to someone you love. Please, I am urging you, go get this new bivalent booster.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:53:58]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Got to say that lately the threat of nuclear war has felt closer than at any point in my lifetime. There`s a reason for that.\par \par \uc2 On early Tuesday morning, for example, North Korea fired a long-range missile directly over Japan. It was the first missile Kim Jong-un launched toward his neighbor in five years, and the longest-range missile North Korea ever fired. Across northern Japan, sirens went off warning for people to take shelter.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  who has been openly threatening to use nuclear weapons since the day he invaded Ukraine. But it`s gotten worse recently as Russia faces more battlefield losses and a chaotic mobilization of new military forces at home. Putin`s recent latest threat was ominous.\par \par \uc2 Not long before he illegally annexed more Ukrainian territory, he said he would defend his country`s "territorial integrity" with "all weapons systems available".\par \par \uc2 All this leading to President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  reportedly issued quite a startling warning last night at a private fundraiser, telling people that "we have a direct threat of the use of nuclear weapons if in fact, things continue down the path we are going and that we have not faced the prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban missile crisis, which was in 1962."\par \par \uc2 [20:55:19]\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in one of the foremost experts on the subject, Joe Cirincione, who spent decades studying the threat of nuclear weapons, who wrote recently an op-ed in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  titled: Putin says nuclear threat is no bluff. We should take him at his word.\par \par \uc2 First of all, Joe, what do you think of the president saying this is the most imminent threat of nuclear weapon deployment since 1962 on the Cuban missile crisis?\par \par \uc2 JOE CIRINCIONE, NATIONAL SECURITY EXPERT: Thank you for having me on, Chris.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is correct. We`ve had lots of near misses. But we are closer to the intentional use of nuclear weapons now than we`ve been in this 60 years since those terrifying 13 days in October 1962. The president is absolutely correct in his assessment.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, one thing I`ve heard is this, Putin`s military has performed very poorly, they`ve been forced to mobilize the country and institute essentially a draft even though they call it that.\par \par \uc2 And as his forces lose ground, Putin is reaching for a nuclear threat as a means of getting people to come to the negotiating table to arrest those losses, and basically get a negotiated settlement that keeps some of the territory they`ve won before it gets lost, and that if you take his threat seriously, you`re sort of playing into his hands of using this as a tool of coercion. What do you think about that argument?\par \par \uc2 CIRINCIONE: We do have to take his threat seriously, this is not a bluff. He has the means to do this. He has over 6,000 nuclear weapons. We failed to eliminate nuclear weapons after the Cold War, so we left him with his arsenal, he has the method to use them.\par \par \uc2 Russian doctrine explicitly calls for using nuclear weapons first in a conventional battle in order to turn the tide of war, and he`s got the motive.\par \par \uc2 He`s not only losing the war in Russia terribly, the Russian army looks very fragile at this point. But now he`s opened up a second front in Russia, and he`s losing that, in part being attacked by his right-wing base, urging him to use nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 So, means, method, motive. This is quite serious. That doesn`t mean we give into it. That means we have to do what I think President Biden is doing, building up a global response to try to deter Putin from going down this nuclear road.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, what does deterrence look like? What does deterrence look like right now?\par \par \uc2 CIRINCIONE: It`s a combination of things, most importantly, demonstrating to Putin that this is not a winning move, this will not end the war, this will lose it for him.\par \par \uc2 So, he will be diplomatically isolated. He thinks the economic sanctions are tough now, wait until he is cut off from the international banking system. And all energy supplies are banned from purchase from Russia.\par \par \uc2 There are military options, including conventional military strikes on the army in Europe, if you could bring in an extreme case, bring \uc2 NATO\uc2  directly into conventional warfare here, this cyber options.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I don`t want to get too over the top here, but we could turn Moscow dark with cyber warfare, we have a lot of things that we can do that we`ve held back.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, a psychological warfare, you could be reaching out directly to military leaders in Russia, and reminding them that we know where they live, we know how to reach out and touch them, and that there is life after losing a war if they break with Putin and not -- and encourage them not to take this step towards Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, so you have just listed a bunch of deterrence that one could articulate, that are plausible that are non-nuclear deterrence, right?\par \par \uc2 So, just let`s do a quick mutually assured destruction recap here, right, the sort of doctrine that comes out of the work on mutually assured destruction during the pike peak of the Cold World War, following Thomas Schelling`s work on game theory is like, you fire nukes, we fire them all. The world gets obliterated, nuclear winter, no more humans bye-bye. That logic means no one ever tries to use one.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me like we`re in a different world now, we`re talking about tactical nuclear weapon and other means of deterrent but like, is that powerful enough to turn?\par \par \uc2 CIRINCIONE: That`s exactly right. So, the world you described was 60 years ago, October 1962. This is a different world where you`re tempted to use a nuclear weapon in a limited way, one, two, a half dozen to try to stop the war in its tracks, to try to break the European support for Ukraine, to play into so-called peace sentiments in the U.S. and split American support for Ukraine. That`s what you`re trying to do if you`re Putin.\par \par \uc2 So, you`ve got to try and counter that in a way that doesn`t bring you, the U.S. to the brink of using a nuclear weapon. Here`s the other thing President Biden was about -- was correct about, there is no safe way to use a tactical nuclear weapon that doesn`t bring us down this world.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:12]\par \par \uc2 War Games repeatedly show that when you start down this road, there are major escalatory risks. That`s what Biden is trying to do, deter him from doing it and prepare responses that are non-nuclear should he do it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. Joe Cirincione, that was very illuminating, if not super encouraging. I appreciate it very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s the week, it`s Friday night. That`s ALL IN for this week. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221008eia700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL; October 06, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Peter Beinart \par \uc2 7,737 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:35]\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex. Thank you and good to see you. You have a great evening. We will see you tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": You as well.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, can an October surprise change the outcome of the midterms this year? We are 33 days away from finding an answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 The first major October surprise of the election season arrived on Monday, with "The Daily Beast" reporting the Georgia Republican senator and anti- choice candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for his then girlfriend`s abortion in 2009. "The Daily Beast" is now reported that that woman is also the mother of one of her four children. We`re going to have more reporting on that later in the show.\par \par \uc2 Republicans however continue to supporter Herschel Walker`s candidacy and recent polling shows that he is in a tight race with the Democratic incumbent, Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is one October surprise that could change the course of the midterm elections, and it`s coming from over 70,000 miles away, in Saudi Arabia. OPEC Plus, the alliance of oil producing nations led by Saudi Arabia and Russia, announced a 2 million barrels per day cut in oil production yesterday. OPEC Plus decision could lead to a potential spike in gas prices, just 33 days before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Today, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had a one word reaction to \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus`s decision, disappointment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What is your reaction to the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus decision?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Disappointment. We are looking at alternatives that we may have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus`s decision comes after the Biden administration spent more than a week urging with producing countries to increase their oil output.\par \par \uc2 Ali Shihabi, a Saudi analyst, told "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", quote, it is certainly not a hostile, anti-Biden act. It has nothing to do with Biden. It is to keep the price in unacceptable band, end quote. Okay.\par \par \uc2 So how should we analyze this set of facts? This summer, Saudi Arabia paid former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to host a tournament at his golf courses, which is a move much criticized as so called sports washing, attempting to launder its reputation on human rights abuses with people who don`t care about that sort of thing. People like the former president, who bragged to reporter Bob Woodward that he, quote, saved \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , after a Saudi death squad had "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporter Jamal Khashoggi killed and dismembered with a bone saw.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s words, as quoted in Woodward`s book called "Rage", quote: I saved his ass. I was able to get Congress to leave him alone. I was able to get them to stop, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Those were Trump`s actual words about helping a dictator avoid strict routinely for dismemberment of a journalist for an American newspaper who was mildly critical of his regime. I saved his ass.\par \par \uc2 It`s a far cry from real politic acceptance that sometimes a president has to act in an unsavory way to serve a superseding interest for the United States. Donald Trump`s top advisor, his son in law, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , it was famously text buddies with MBS. He openly admitted to advising him on how to weather the death and dismemberment matter.\par \par \uc2 Six months after leaving the White House, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  received a two billion dollar investment from a fund that was led by \uc2 Mohammad bin Salman\uc2 . The reason for that confidence in his business eye cumin remains woefully unclear.\par \par \uc2 There were lesser moments as well, and we will remind you that Donald Trump`s first official foreign visit in 2017 after he became president was to Saudi Arabia, which is a remarkable break for presidential tradition. During the first two years of Donald Trump`s presidency, the Saudi government spent $270,000 by booking 500 rooms for their lobbyists in Donald Trump`s D.C. hotel. Those payments came just before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  finalized a $110 billion weapons deal with Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 Born and bred New Yorker \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who told many telltale about 9/11 over the years, called into a television station on September 11th, 2001, bragging that the destruction of the Twin Towers might have had the upside of making a building that he owned the tallest in New York.\par \par \uc2 This summer, when asked about the families of 9/11 victims who were protesting the Saudi backed golf tournament at his club in New Jersey, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, quote, nobody`s got into the bottom of 9/11, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 Nobody`s got into the bottom of 9/11?\par \par \uc2 [22:05:02]\par \par \uc2 Really? Fifteen of the 19 hijackers were Saudi citizens. \uc2 Osama bin Laden\uc2  was born in Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia denies official involvement in the attack that killed nearly 3,000 people. Denial.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , by the way, also denies that he started the war in Ukraine, and that Ukrainians want to live in Ukraine, and not in Russia. A rising oil prices could translate into more funding for Vladimir putin`s war on Ukraine. It is a war which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  fairly unspoken they once the other side, Ukraine, to win.\par \par \uc2 OPEC Plus, and Saudi Arabia in particular, has not condemned putin`s war. Russia has never condemned Saudi Arabia`s invasion of Yemen. These are just facts.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to close with one more fact. Gas prices move the needle in American elections, and democracy is on the ballot in 33 days in America. Thirty-three days, if democracy loses in America, what chance does it have in Ukraine or the Middle East? Who wants democracy to win? Who wants it to lose?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Peter Beinart, professor of journalism and political science at the \uc2 City University of New York\uc2 . He is an MSNBC political analyst. And Nicholas Kristof, a Pulitzer Prize-winning journalist, and author, soon to resume his column at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you so much for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Peter, I will start with you. We don`t know why \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , or \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , or any of these guys got involved. I will say as an economic journalist, the price of oil was not in a place that demanded a 2 million barrels a day cut. It was in the 80s. And so it can only take the facts as we get, them how do you look at these?\par \par \uc2 PETER BEINART, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: It might have been more than one motivation. It might have been that they were genuinely afraid that a recession would push the price of oil down. But as you laid out, it certainly is the case that the Saudis would rather have the Trump era Republican Party, and indeed the Trump family in power then the Biden administration and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Why is that? Because the Trump administration is easier to buy. The Trump administration -- and the Saudis have already started to do that. They have made huge investments in Trump`s own wallet, in Jared Kushner`s wallet. They have also invested big money in Steve Mnuchin`s investment fund, the former treasury secretary.\par \par \uc2 And they have clear evidence of Trump`s first term that virtually nothing that they could do would lead him to decide that the United States should put pressure on Saudi Arabia. So, why would they not essentially want Republicans to do better in these midterm elections?\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Nicholas, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  is a cartel, it has been around since 1960. It`s got 13 members. OPEC Plus is another ten members. The biggest, most influential country in OPEC`s Saudi Arabia, the most influential country in Saudi -- these are two groups who don`t like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  very much. And know that while inflation is happening all over the world, people associated with gas prices.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS KRISTOF, PULITZER PRIZE-WINING COLUMNIST: And frankly, I wish that we had learned lessons from dealing with Putin in understanding the danger of bolstering a leader and thinking that somehow we can manage him. We applied that lesson to \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , the head crampons of Saudi Arabia. Biden expresses disappointment, but when somebody stabbed here in the back, your country in the back, has embraced Russia, and made it more difficult to sanction Russia, and produce its oil revenues. That is not just a reason for disappointment. It`s a reason for a really tough response.\par \par \uc2 Of, course this is just one action on one day, this is a pattern for MBS. For years now, you mentioned the invasion of Yemen, the world`s worst humanitarian crisis. He also kidnapped Lebanon`s prime minister. He created a rift with Qatar, which is also badly damaging to America`s interests. It has savaged dissidents at a home, including women`s rights activists.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Peter, there`s been a new development in this, and that is for two nations that are not big fans of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  right now for different reasons. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  made comments tonight at a fund-raiser, talking about the threat of nuclear response from Russia. I just want to read you a little bit of what he says.\par \par \uc2 He says: This is the first time since the Cuban missile crisis that we have a direct threat of the use of a nuclear weapon, if in fact things continued on the path they`re going. I`m trying to figure out, what is his off ramp? Where does he find a way out? Where does he find himself in a position that he does not only lose -- not lose faith, but lose significant power within Russia?\par \par \uc2 We have not faced the prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban missile crisis. He`s not jokingly talked about the potential use of tactical nuclear weapons or biological or chemical weapons because his military is significantly underperforming.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 I do not think there is any such thing as the ability to use a tactical nuclear weapon and not end up in Armageddon. I did not realize how much -- this is the interesting part. I did not interest -- how much serious damage the previous administration did to our foreign policy. That is the clearest response so far that we have had from President Biden on the saber rattling from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What do you make of Putin talking about nuclear weapons, and the use of them?\par \par \uc2 BEINART: I think that we are the closest we have come to the prospect of nuclear war since the early 1980s. This particular moment during the Reagan administration where there is a nuclear exercise called evil archer that the Russians that was actually the real thing, and essentially went to DefCon 1. It has been a long time.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that we really don`t know what Vladimir putin`s thinking patterns are. He has shown a willingness to do really irrational, almost catastrophically and rational things. It`s not clear how good our sources are to talk to him are.\par \par \uc2 That is what is so frightening. It`s also not easy to see, as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said, what the off ramp is. I mean, assuming that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is not opposed, there is not going to be a peace agreement anytime soon, nor frankly should there be.\par \par \uc2 And the more success that yet the Ukrainians have on the battlefield, the more desperate and dangerous Putin becomes. It`s a very frightening situation.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Nick, I want to get your take on this. There are a few options, I hate to say this but there are small nuclear weapons that, in the arsenal of 1,200 weapons that Russia has, they could target a sparsely populated part of Ukraine, or one of these places where there is a concentration of Ukrainian soldiers. They could target other place in Ukraine that is not in the Russian controlled territories.\par \par \uc2 What do you think could happen here? Because the one danger of having a nuclear weapon as a deterrent is that once you use it, the deterrent effect is gone.\par \par \uc2 KRISTOF: That is true. I think that the general view among Russian experts and nuclear experts is that it is unlikely that Russia will use a tactical nuke, or any other weapon. But it`s possible. Odds are who knows. If it`s at 20 percent, 10 percent, nobody knows?\par \par \uc2 And, you know, that might be dropping it offshore, it might be dropping it in a remote area, hitting a Ukrainian facility. But in some ways, I think the off-ramp metaphor is the wrong one. There is not a good off-ramp.\par \par \uc2 Putin is going to be making his calculations, you know, is he better off using a tactical nuke, or better off not using it? And we can make sure that he understands that if he uses it, he gains little military advantage, and every one of his ships in the Black Sea is gone, every bit of Russian presence, everything in Ukraine is gone. And he will be ostracized all around the world, the economy will be devastated. We can we can affect his calculations, and I hope we will.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Peter, Senators Durbin and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  have called for something called NOPEC, which is an act that would treat \uc2 OPEC\uc2  like the cartel it is, and allow lawsuits against it.\par \par \uc2 Durbin says this: From unanswered questions about 9/11, the brutal murder of journalist Jamal Khashoggi, and the exporting of extremism, to dubious jailing of peaceful dissidents, and conspiring with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to punish the U.S. with higher oil prices, the Saudi royal family has never been a trustworthy all of our nation, it`s time for our foreign policy to imagine a world without this alliance with these royal backstabbers.\par \par \uc2 Given \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus`s decision to dramatically reduce oil production, it is time for the Senate to pass this important legislation in the lame duck session.\par \par \uc2 What do you -- it`s interesting, it`s strong language. Would it matter? Nobody has been able to dismantle the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cartel since 1960. What with this, do and why would it be interesting?\par \par \uc2 BEINART: Well, I think that it is very important for the U.S. to try to come up with the fundamentally different foreign policy towards Saudi Arabia. One of the worst humans rights abusers in the world, according to \uc2 Freedom House\uc2 , the human rights are even worse than Iran and Russia`s, if you can believe that. They`re clearly under \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , they don`t essentially see the United States as an ally.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, and we need to be honest about this. If that the Saudis, and also the Emiratis, the UAE, have weaponized the corruption of our own political system. They have dumped both huge amounts of money into lobbying firms, into think tanks, into former politicians. It`s not just the Trump family.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons that America has not been able to think clearly about a different kind of policy towards both of these brutal, autocratic regimes, is because they have spent so much inside -- so much money inside of Washington. Both parties have been tainted by it.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Nick, I want to just read you something Eugene Robertson, our good friend, wrote in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ". He said that Biden tried to decide to get the U.S.-Saudi relationship back on track consistent with American values interests -- hence the visit and fist bump in July.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:09]\par \par \uc2 This oil production cut is the thanks he gets. Biden needs to realize that Saudi Arabia under MBS is more part of the problem that part of the solution, and to adjust U.S. policy accordingly, end quote, which is similar to what Peter was just saying.\par \par \uc2 Biden did try, he got a lot of criticism, including from me to making that trip to Saudi Arabia, which I didn`t think it was a good idea. But he thought so, and he`s got good advisers. This is what happens, what is your take on it?\par \par \uc2 KRISTOF: I think there was a misperception, and it is true in one candidate, and it`s true in Riyadh. Somehow, if Saudi Arabia that has the leverage in our bilateral relationship, because of their oil. And, you know, obviously, they do have some leverage, they can cause some damage. But at the end of the day, Saudi Arabia depends for its existence on our security support. Saudi Arabia`s military forces cannot beat a ragtag militia in Yemen, and add to be bailed out.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, people can, say they can buy arms from France or Russia, yes, they can. But they can`t get the implicit security guarantee that comes with us. And I think that Gene is calling for a reassertion of that sense of the levers that we have, and I think that President Biden would be well placed to try to reestablish that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yeah, we`ve talked about the economics and politics, but you`ve brought up an important point. The security guarantees, implicit and explicit the Saudi Arabia has. That`s not a two-way street. Saudi Arabia has got that from the United States.\par \par \uc2 Guys, what an interesting conversation. I love having you both here. Thank you so much. Peter Beinart, and Nicholas Kristof, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 All right. Up next, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  says he wants to ban all abortions, says he doesn`t know the woman who claimed he paid for her abortion in 2009, which has made all of the more interesting now that new reporting indicates that she is allegedly also the mother of one of his children. This is just one of the many alleged hypocrisies in Herschel Walker`s past. So why is the Senate race between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  statistically tied?\par \par \uc2 We`ll ask Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , he`s standing by. He`s the chair of the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:22:01]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It has been what you would call it a weird week in Georgia`s Senate race. On Monday, "The Daily Beast" reported that in 2009, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , currently running on a platform of opposing all abortion with no exceptions, paid for a woman that he was involved with to have an abortion. The woman provided "The Daily Beast" with a receipt from the abortion clinic, and pictures of a get well card that Walker sent to her, as well as a check for $700. The card reads: rest, relax, recover. It also included a personal note from Walker that it says, pray you are feeling better.\par \par \uc2 I should note that NBC News has not been able to verify "The Daily Beast" reporting, which was followed by swift denials from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I never asked anybody to get an abortion, I never paid for an abortion, and it`s a lie. I`m going to continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, on Wednesday, Walker claimed that he has no idea who this woman could possibly be.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Everyone is anonymous, everybody is leaking, and they want you to confess to something you have no clue about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, it is that comment right there, that appears to be a bridge too far for the woman in question. According to a follow-up story, "The Daily Beast" posted last night, the woman is also the mother of one of Walker`s children, and was in a relationship with him for years, even after the abortion. She reportedly called Walker`s defense, ridiculous. She said, quote, I was stunned, but I guess it also doesn`t shock me that there are just so many of us that he truly doesn`t remember, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s at this point that Walkers denials get harder and harder to believe. Here`s what he told conservative radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  when asked about this latest report this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I know this is untrue, and I know it`s untrue. They keep telling me things that are totally untrue. I`m not sure why they`re being told, I know nothing about any woman having an abortion. So they can keep coming at me like that.\par \par \uc2 They`re doing it because they want to distract people. I know that. I have already been forgiven, and if I`ve been forgiven, why would I not be forgiven of something like that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: If you didn`t follow that, that`s okay.\par \par \uc2 At a campaign event in Georgia today, Walker was given another chance to explain the situation. I`m going to give you a wild guess as to how that went.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: You said that if this did happen, there is nothing to be ashamed of. How do you square that? You said that this morning on Hugh Hewitt`s show --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I never said that. I was talking about something totally different. I said, with my ex-wife, it didn`t have anything to do with what this woman said. This right here, this abortion thing is false. It`s a lie. That`s what I said.\par \par \uc2 As to anything happened with my ex-wife, or what Christian was talking about, I don`t know. But as I said, if anything happened, there`s nothing to be ashamed of, my ex-wife and I have been the best of friends, her husband and my wife. That`s what I have said. I`ve said nothing about anything that happened, because I said that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:25:07]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, to clarify. Walker says he did not pay for a woman if he was in a relationship with to have an abortion, but if he did, it would be nothing to be ashamed of, or something like that.\par \par \uc2 That doesn`t sit right with Walker`s ex. She told "The Daily Beast", quote, he seems pretty pro-choice to me. He was pro-choice, obviously. He picks and chooses where it is convenient for him to use that religious crutch, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s take a step back for a second. Midterms are 33 days away, and somehow, this is the story that might sink Walker`s campaign? Keep in mind, we`re talking about the guy that said this back in July, when talking about the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Since we don`t control the air, I was deciding to go over to China, bad air. When China gets the good air, their bad air has to move. And so it moves over to our good airspace. And now we have to clean that backup.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: This is what he said when he was asked two days after the murders of school kids in Uvalde, Texas, about how to stop mass shootings in this country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: What we need to do is look into how we can stop those things. You know, you talk about doing a disinformation -- what about getting a department that can look at young men that are -- or women that are looking at the social media. What about doing that? Looking into the things like that, if we can stop it that way. But they want to just continue to talk about taking away your constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 (EDN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, for some reason, these coherent responses to questions about serious issues that we face in America are not disqualifying, according to many Republican voters. Neither is the hypocrisy of Walker`s public stance on abortion versus what he allegedly does in private, or lying about his family, his education, his post-football employment, which he has done.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, none of it matters. This is the sad reality where we are right now, in Georgia, and in many parts of the country. With all of Walkers baggage, polling shows that he and Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  are in a statistical tie. That`s because of the margin of error. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the Democratic Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  of Michigan. He`s the chair of the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator Peters, good to see you again, it`s been a while. I appreciate you joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is one example of -- I think I can make up four or six Republican senatorial candidates across the country who say some wacky stuff. What is going on, and why are these candidates still competitive? You got one in Arizona, you have one in Pennsylvania, you have one in Wisconsin, you have one in Georgia, there are a few other examples.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 GARY PETERS\uc2  (D-MI): Well, you are right about that, Ali. There is no question if you look at candidate quality between Democratic incumbents and our candidates in states, versus the Republicans. There is a clear contrast, it is a very clear. You played some of the comments of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  made. It`s very important for the voters in Georgia to take a look at those comments, to compare and contrast with \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . I think the difference will be very clear, as voters look at that.\par \par \uc2 We also knew that these are tough states. We are competing in battleground states, that we knew would tighten as the election got closer to fruition. We also know that Republicans right now are dumping in unprecedented amounts of money, outspending our candidates in these races. They don`t let truth get in the way of their ads, as they attack our candidates with negative, nasty ads.\par \par \uc2 But they are pouring money into these races, and they`re tightening, that is why is this incumbent for us to make sure the Democrats across the country to understand what is at stake in this election, understanding what is happening in each of these states, and support our Democratic candidates, and support the DSCC.\par \par \uc2 We have to defendthesenate.org, which is our website that is critical for us to have the resources to fight back against the desperation tactics that we are seeing from Republicans in these states. We have better candidates. We have better incumbents, it is clear. But we have to have the resources to make sure that we can run strong right across the finish line in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I just want -- I want to go back to this fund-raiser that President Biden was at this evening. I believe you are there as well. He made some very, very strong comments about Russia, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , and the threat of nuclear weaponry. In, fact he said that we have not faced the prospect of nuclear Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban missile crisis. I did not realize how much serious damage the previous administration did to our foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 You happened to be the chair of the homeland security committee. Control of the Senate in this particular instance is directly tied to our national security, given the saber-rattling that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is up to right now.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:41]\par \par \uc2 PETERS: There`s no question about that. It is a very serious situation. We have to continue to stay strong, supporting the Ukrainian people and the Ukrainian military against Mr. Putin.\par \par \uc2 It is clear that he feels that he has lost momentum, that he is being pushed into a corner, and is making some very dangerous comments. He needs to be called out.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, President Biden understands the gravity of the situation and is working very hard to keep our allies all engaged in this as well. He`s certainly the right person to be the president at this very pivotal time in our history.\par \par \uc2 And there is no question, the previous administration did an incredible amount of damage in terms of foreign relations with our allies. Thankfully, President Biden has been able to rebuild that, bring a coalition together, to stand against a thug and a dictator who is in the process of murdering people in Ukraine. And we need to stand with the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I want to ask you about candidate quality. You just talked about that. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has used those words, right. He has expressed concern about candidate quality. Your counterpart in the Republican side who runs the Republican Senatorial Committee is not concerned about the Herschel Walkers of the world.\par \par \uc2 I want to just put on the screen a few states -- Pennsylvania, Ohio and Wisconsin. Tighter races than you might predict in some of those places, your point about you have to get this message out. If candidate quality is a real problem, how do you convey that to voters? How do you -- what do you do about that because you are probably not going -- I don`t know if you`re going to get Republican voters to vote for Democratic candidates, but you might activate Democratic voters to come out. What is the methodology?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, certainly, there is no question that we need to have turnout. Midterm elections already have lower turnout than you would have in a presidential election. And so turnout makes a huge difference in the midterms. Always important, certainly in presidential elections as well.\par \par \uc2 But it makes a difference in the midterm. We need to have Democrats engaged, to understand that our democracy is under assault by Republicans, and particularly these very extreme candidates that are running in these battleground states. These are candidates that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  basically handpicked and put in each of these races.\par \par \uc2 We have to make sure we are making a very emphatic statement that this is not acceptable. We have to win these races. We need to have the resources to mobilize folks.\par \par \uc2 That`s why DefendtheSenate.org, our Web site at the DSCC is just important to raise resources to help these candidates turn out folks on election day. And that is going to make the difference. It is going to be about voter motivation at this point, and getting Democrats out in force.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  of Michigan, thanks for joining us tonight. We always appreciate your time, sir.\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Coming up today, President Biden celebrated a new $20 billion investment by \uc2 IBM\uc2  in New York`s Hudson Valley that he says was sparked by a bill that he signed into law.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was joined by New York Congressman Pat Ryan, who this summer did what Democrats across the country are hoping to do next month -- win in a competitive seat at a time of high inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PAT RYAN (D-NY): It is huge. I mean I`m trying to hold back the expletives in my -- that I`m thinking. It`s a BFD. It`s a BFD.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We`re going to discuss the BFD with the new congressman, Pat Ryan, up next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:23]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Folks, we need to make these chips here in America to bring down everyday costs and create good paying American jobs.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Between the Chips Act, and the bipartisan infrastructure law, we have made it clear that the United States will be the leader in global manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. that was President Biden and New York`s newly elected Congressman Pat Ryan.\par \par \uc2 Pat Ryan won the seat in a special election in August, in a part of the state where Democrats are hoping to turn economic gains into votes.\par \par \uc2 The two men appeared alongside New York`s Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and Congressman \uc2 Sean Maloney\uc2  at an \uc2 IBM\uc2  facility in Poughkeepsie, New York today where the company announced a $20 billion dollar investment in New York`s Hudson Valley Region, working on semiconductor chips, quantum computers, and artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 Just a few days ago, semiconductor manufacturer Micron pledged up to $100 billion over two decades to build a new computer chip factory in upstate New York.\par \par \uc2 Both companies credited Biden`s Chips and Science Act. The bipartisan law offers more than $52 billion in subsidies and tax credits to companies making semiconductors, microchips in the United States.\par \par \uc2 With the economy on top of mind for many voters ahead of next month`s election, the president also said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You`ve created 678,000 new manufacturing jobs, where -- and we`re just getting started. Where is it written that we can`t lead manufacturing in the world? I don`t know where that is written.\par \par \uc2 That`s one of the things that the Chips Act is going to change. The law that is going to build the future in a proud, proud legacy, not only for \uc2 IBM\uc2 , but for the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining me now is New York Congressman Pat Ryan. His district includes the Hudson Valley, a bellwether that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won in 2016. President Biden won in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Ryan, good to see you. Thanks for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:56]\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Just tell our viewers a little bit about you. You won this race by two points in what we call a bellwether district. You won after the Dobbs ruling, some people thought that your victory may have been indicative of the sentiment regarding that.\par \par \uc2 Our own polling here at NBC indicates that democracy, threats to democracy are the biggest concern out there.\par \par \uc2 But that`s closely followed by the economy and inflation, and things like that. How do you manage that? How do you -- what do you lean into when talking to your constituents?\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the opening of this plant or the change to this plant is remarkable. But at the same time, you`ve got two very distinct issues that you need to talk to your constituents about.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, we centered our special election in standing up and fighting for freedom. We talked about the despicable attack on reproductive rights and abortion rights as a threat to freedom of all Americans. And we saw this overwhelming response from not just Democrats, but Independents and Republicans that said that is not who we are as a country when we`re are taking away fundamental rights.\par \par \uc2 And so the campaign then, and going forward now into November is about both fighting for rights, and fighting for relief, fighting for economic health and support, as people are feeling tremendous pressure.\par \par \uc2 So today`s announcement, $20 billion in the Hudson Valley, in partnership with \uc2 IBM\uc2 , with the president to deliver his leadership, this is huge. As I said, it is a BFD.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It`s a BFD.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Not just for our community but this shows -- this shows America leading again in manufacturing here at home. And I am so proud of that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I want to -- I want to put on the screen, our polling that indicates the threats to democracy amongst Democrats is the biggest issue. Cost of living is next. Jobs and the economy are third.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people are worried about inflation, and they often associate it with gas prices. But in fact, much of the inflation we see in this country, particularly in the price of cars, is about chips. Do people register that? Is that clear to your constituents that that is the kind of thing that`s going to fix supply chain issues and inflation issues even more than the price of gas?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, the president talked about that today and he is spot on. We all learned a lot about this term of supply chains in the last few years here, and one piece where we have outsourced away our own destiny and our own control and chips are being made outside of the U.S. We were held hostage by other countries, and weren`t able to assert our own economic independence.\par \par \uc2 So bringing that back, going from a situation where when I was a kid, 40 percent of semiconductors were made here in the U.S. Now it is 12 percent.\par \par \uc2 We have to change that. That`s what the Chips Act does. That is what this announcement today does. And the linkage between these economic issues and pressures and threats to our democracy -- these aren`t separate issues. They are all intertwined because we know, right out of the authoritarian playbook, when people are feeling that economic pain and pressure, it is very easy for authoritarians to come in and make these crowds for rights and threats. We have to really fight on both fronts. Again, for rights and for relief.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That line between people`s insecurities, and their economic state is direct. Thank, you sir, good to see you this evening. Thanks for being on our show. Congressman Pat Ryan of New York.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, it is amazing just how quickly politicians change their positions when disasters hit their state. Republicans in Florida voted against major disaster funding. And now they are depending on it after Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic Congresswoman Val Demings joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:43:46]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We were very fortunate to have good coordination with the White House and with FEMA from the very beginning of this. And that basically set off the massive mobilization that we had ready to be able to respond to the storm.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That was Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  yesterday, praising the Biden administration and FEMA for its urgent response in helping the victims of Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 Let`s not forget, that is the same \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who voted against federal disaster aid to help the victims of Hurricane Sandy when he was a member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 How quickly politicians changed their tune when disaster hits their state. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is not the only Florida Republican playing politics with disaster relief. Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  missed a vote last week that included $18.8 billion in funding for FEMA to help states recovering from natural disasters including Florida.\par \par \uc2 Florida`s other Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , along with every Florida house Republican, including those representing districts that were devastated by the storm, voted against the bill.\par \par \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  then asked the Senate Appropriations Committee for emergency funding for Florida`s recovery. It`s getting hard to keep track of.\par \par \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  said he would vote against any federal aid package for Florida, like he did with the $50 billion aid package for Hurricane Sandy, if it contained anything other than disaster relief for Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:55]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN HOST: Are you telling me that if Hurricane Ian relief contains anything that smells like pork, you will vote no?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Sure. I will fight against it having pork in it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining us now is the Democratic Congresswoman Val Demings of Florida. She`s a member of the House Judiciary Committee. She is running for \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in Florida against \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Representative Demings, good to see you again. Thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): Always good to be with you as well.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Our thoughts to the people of Florida, I spent most of last week there covering this disaster, which is very, very serious which sort of begs the question, what is \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  up to?\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Well, that`s a really good question, Ali. You know, I chair the subcommittee through Homeland Security on Emergency Preparedness Response and Recovery. And we have been on the ground here. We`ve been -- we had a meeting with the FEMA administration, assessing the damage.\par \par \uc2 I had just gotten back a week or so ago from Puerto Rico, looking at the damage there from Hurricane Fiona and came back to Ian, and you know, I saw you covering this tragedy in southwest Florida that was hit hard. We had flooding here.\par \par \uc2 I`m not sure what \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is doing other than playing politics. You know, to not show up to vote when you already have a reputation of being the absentee senator, to not show up to vote is almost worst than showing up and voting wrong.\par \par \uc2 But then you ask for additional hurricane relief, and then you praise the Biden administration for their response. I`m not really sure what is going on, but I know politics is at the core of it.\par \par \uc2 And Florida, those people are struggling as you all know, need more right now than a senator who simply knows how to play politics.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I also want to talk about where Florida`s Republican leaders have been on climate issues. It hits up against a wall WHEN during a hurricane you start talking about climate stuff.\par \par \uc2 But Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  voted against last year`s bipartisan infrastructure law which devotes some $50 billion to help states better prepare for events like Ian because they said it was wasteful.\par \par \uc2 It provided billions of dollars for seawalls, storm pumps, elevating homes, flood control and other projects. Many of those measures were co-written by another coastal Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  of Louisiana, who called that bill a major victory for Louisiana and our nation.\par \par \uc2 What is up with Florida on this one? This is important. Everything I saw last week indicated to me that storm walls, and raising houses, and storm pumps, and flood control, might have saved lives.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Ali, you`re absolutely correct. I mean the bipartisan infrastructure deal as you`ve indicated had billions of dollars that could go to really helping to improve our resiliency, but ultimately dealing with climate change.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the experts say no, climate change does not create a hurricane, but because of the ocean waters, when the ocean waters become warmer as a result of global warming, then the storms become more intense, bigger, more devastating.\par \par \uc2 And Lord knows we have certainly seen that right here in Florida. Unbelievable flooding in Miami where \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  lives. But \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  has said the climate is always changing, that he does not have a plan to deal with the weather. That is just really shameful.\par \par \uc2 So the only thing I can think of again, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is in denial. Florida certainly deserves better, especially when Floridians are hurting.\par \par \uc2 Look, I am running for the United States Senate of Florida. Floridians are interested in adding somebody who`s going to address their issues, especially when they are in trouble.\par \par \uc2 I`m asking them to vote for me, my Website is ValDemings.com.\par \par \uc2 V5r: Let me ask you about the status of the situation in Florida. We`ve had more than 100 confirmed deaths now, there may be more to come. You have seen it. I mean there are people who are -- they get passed by, they get forgotten. These storms do not treat everybody the same. There are people who don`t have insurance, who have lost their cars, which is an impossibility to get around southwest Florida.\par \par \uc2 What do we do? What do we do because we move away, right? We go away and everybody sort of forgets, and it was last week`s news. But it`s not last week`s news, and it won`t be for many months for a lot of Floridians.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Ali, as you all know, I spent 27 years as a law enforcement officer. I was on duty in 2004, during Hurricanes Charlie, Francis and Jean. I`m more than familiar with hurricanes and the unbelievable devastation that they leave behind.\par \par \uc2 But what we have seen with Ian has just been hard to deal with. You have seen the devastation in southwest Florida. There are people who have lost everything.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:50]\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: And you`re so -- you`re absolutely correct. I mean we have toured some of the underserved areas right here in central Florida. And you know, people regardless of who they are, how much money they have, what their zip code is, the color of their skin, when they are in trouble, our job as government officials is to help them recover.\par \par \uc2 And what we have seen is that in underserved areas, including rural areas in Florida, many times the infrastructure is not there to begin with.\par \par \uc2 Or if it is, it is shoddy construction. And so we have got to do better, we have certainly seen those issues there. We`ve got to help people rebuild, become more resilient, regardless of who they are, or where they live.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congresswoman, good to see you. Thank you for joining us tonight. We will continue to keep Florida in our thoughts.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Val Demings of Florida.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:49]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As I said when I react as president, no one should be in jail just for using or possessing marijuana. It`s already legal in many states. And criminal records for marijuana possession have led to needless (INAUDIBLE) to appointment, to housing, educational opportunities.\par \par \uc2 And that`s before you address the racial disparities around who suffers the consequences. Too many lives been upended because of our failed approach to marijuana. It`s time that we right these wrongs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: President Biden`s big announcement is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 I will see you this weekend on "VELSHI" Saturday and Sunday starting at 8:00 a.m. only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" begins right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221007eia600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for October 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayman Mohyeldin \par \uc2 7,914 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: -- led to needless barriers to employment, to housing, educational opportunities.\par \par \uc2 And that`s before you address the racial disparities around who suffers the consequences. Too many lives have been upended because of our failed approach to marijuana. It`s time that we write these wrongs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: President Biden`s big announcement is tonight`s Last Word. I will see you this weekend on Velshi Saturday and Sunday starting at 8:00 a.m. only on MSNBC. The 11th Hour begins right now.\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST: Another night, a new report about documents at the former president`s beach club the government thinks there may be more documents they have not found. And today startling testimony from a so called Oath Keeper about the insurrection, a phone call he overheard more trouble for the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is struggling to explain away reports he paid for an abortion. Everything he says just leaves more confusion.\par \par \uc2 Then, President Biden answers the Republican attacks on law and order by keeping campaign promises all about justice as the 11th Hour gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, everyone, I`m Ayman Mohyeldin in for Stephanie Ruhle. Right now breaking news on the Mar-a-Lago documents investigation questioning whether the former president is fully cooperating with the feds.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting the Justice Department has indicated to Trump`s lawyers that it suspects he still has not yet returned all the documents taken from the White House and the paper says that skepticism has led to a split in Trump`s legal team.\par \par \uc2 The Time says DOJ has declined to comment on this report. But the paper notes one of the potential crimes cited in the request for the Mar-a-Lago search warrant was obstruction and the Justice Department`s criminal prosecution of the January 6 insurrection is moving full steam ahead just as House investigators prepare for what could be their final public hearing.\par \par \uc2 Now, the House Select Committee says they will take place one week from today at 1:00 Eastern and this will be the ninth session in a series of hearings featuring stunning testimony from top aides, advisors and allies to the former president.\par \par \uc2 We also heard about militia groups activities before and during the capital riot.\par \par \uc2 Today, a leading member of one of those groups pleaded guilty to seditious conspiracy charges related to the riot. Now the Justice Department`s has Proud Boys member Jeremy Bertino is the first from the group to plead guilty. He`s also the first 1/6 defendant to be convicted of planning to oppose the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now prosecutors say he will cooperate in upcoming conspiracy trials against the Proud Boys, their leaders and other members.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is in the first week of another January 6 edition trial, this one against the Oath Keepers militia group. And today one former member told the jury that the founder may have been in contact with the Secret Service in September of 2020. Former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner was in the courtroom for that testimony.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, FMR. FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: The first witness was a gentleman, as you said named John Zimmerman and there was an eyebrow raising moment when we heard him tell the jury about a Trump rally in Fayetteville, North Carolina in September and some of the Oath Keepers were there. And Zimmerman said I was listening to a phone call. I couldn`t hear who was on the other end of the call. But Stewart Rhodes was talking to somebody and Stewart Rhode said it was a Secret Service agent and Zimmerman who is retired military himself said based on the context of what I was hearing, it sounded like he was talking to a Secret Service agent.\par \par \uc2 The other main takeaway from Zimmerman`s testimony, which I think was atmospherically really bad for the Oath Keepers was he said that they attended the Million MAGA March, the so called Million MAGA marched in mid- November of 2020.\par \par \uc2 And Stewart Rhodes said, listen, there are probably going to be Antifa members and BLM members there. And we know his words they like to prey upon the weak so what I think we should do is dress up like we`re vulnerable. We`ll make ourselves look old will carry canes, and if they try to attack us, we will quote give them a good beat down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And late tonight the Secret Service issued a statement saying in part, we are aware that individuals from the Oath Keepers have contacted us in the past to make inquiries. The statement goes on to say it is not uncommon for various organizations to contact us concerning security restrictions and activities that are permissible in proximity to our protected sites.\par \par \uc2 Now as today`s proceedings got underway, the judge ruled the jury could not see a so called Death Wish List that was found in the home of another Oath Keeper defendant. On that list were the names of Georgia election workers Ruby Freeman and her daughter Shaye Moss, both testified before the January 6 committee about being targets to vote voter fraud conspiracies that were spread by Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:05]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUBY FREEMAN, JANUARY 6TH HEARING WITNESS: I won`t even introduce myself by my name anymore. I get nervous when I bump into someone I know in the grocery store who says my name. I`m worried about who`s listening.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I understand that people once showed up at your grandmother`s house?\par \par \uc2 SHAYE MOSS, JANUARY 6TH HEARING WITNESS: They just started pushing their way through claiming that they were coming in to make a citizen`s arrest. They needed to find me and my mom they knew there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And on top of all that law enforcement officials in Georgia are currently investigating possible interference in the 2020 election by Trump and his allies.\par \par \uc2 Just this week, the Fulton County District Attorney there \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  asked the judge to sign off on search warrants in that case a possible sign that the investigation may have moved into a new phase.\par \par \uc2 We are now about a month away from the midterm elections and a new report in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  points out and alarming reality. According to the paper, a majority of Republicans, yes, a majority of them certainly the nominees on the ballot for House, Senate and key statewide races about 299 and all have denied or question the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Let that sink in for a minute. Tonight the political focus is on the state of Arizona where one of those election deniers Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  is debating Democratic incumbent Mark Kelly. Kari Lake, the state`s Republican nominee for governor also a vocal denier of Joe Biden`s 2020 win is on the ballot as well.\par \par \uc2 And last night January 6 committee Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  warned that candidates like Kari Lake are simply putting American democracy at risk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): If you care about democracy, and you care about the survival of our republic, then you need to understand, we all have to understand that we cannot give people power who have told us that they will not honor elections. What happens here in Arizona is not just important for Arizona, but it`s important for the nation and for the future functioning of our constitutional republic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Lots of stories to break down and with that, let`s bring in our leadoff panel for the evening, Charlie Savage, Washington correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  He`s also the author of Power Wars, the relentless rise of presidential authority and secrecy. Frank Figliuzzi, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Assistant Director for counterintelligence and Chuck Rosenberg, a former U.S. attorney and a former senior \uc2 FBI\uc2  official. Gentlemen, it`s great to have all three of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, I`ll start with you, your colleagues latest reporting on the skepticism about Trump returning documents, what could this signal about how the Feds view this case right now?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SAVAGE, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, "THE NEW YORK TIMES": Well, there have been some hints and insinuations in some of the Justice Department`s filings in the Mar-a-Lago documents case, which is obviously getting very complicated with appeals and all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s been hints in there that they don`t think they are not sure they have everything. And one of the arguments they`ve made for why they need to have access to the documents now. And the special master process interfering is that they think that the documents that they do have in hand could offer clues to whether there`s anything else still out there. And so we`ve seen these insinuations of these hence, already in various documents that have come out.\par \par \uc2 And so the reporting by my colleagues today shows that there`s something behind that it`s not just strategic to try to increase the chances of there, you know, even looked at favorably by these appeals courts. They really do appear to think there`s something more missing, and we know that there were the almost four dozen folders that were empty that they seized that had a classified markings on them.\par \par \uc2 And this seems to indicate that they are still looking and one of the questions of course, is if they do think there`s more do they think it`s at Mar-a-Lago? Or do they want to know what`s in the drawers and the storage cabinets at places like Trump`s compounded Bedminster Golf Resort? So that`s, how I read that.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. And we`re going to dig into that in just a second. But I do want to ask you about this other part of your colleagues reporting, this new report suggests that the document request has divided Trump`s lawyers, do you have any more insight or what do you -- what more do you know about that part of the story?\par \par \uc2 SAVAGE: Well, there`s been chatter that there is disagreement within their legal team. Different news outlets have reported that in different ways. The suggestion has been that Chris Kise is an appellate lawyer, sort of a serious guy from Florida, who was hired on more recently was suddenly being sidelined. But then that doesn`t seem to be quite right because he was the lead signatory on their appeals court and Supreme Court briefs.\par \par \uc2 But he seems to be no longer involved in the night fighting down at the special master level. Something seems to be going on, but we`ve been having trouble quite crystallizing what it is.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And so what my colleagues were told was, this is what it was really about that he wanted to cooperate with the government when the Justice Department said, Hey, we think there still may be more, he wanted to hire a firm to come in and do a forensic audit that the government would trust as opposed to maybe the \uc2 FBI\uc2  going through the stuff or having the government to have to trust from people after they`ve repeatedly apparently deceived them about whether they everything had been turned over.\par \par \uc2 And then Kise was pushed aside for that. His suggestion was rejected by others who wanted to take a more militant or resistant stance and apparently prevailed, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Frank, as Charlie mentioned, there, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  found, you know, dozens of folders, if you will, with classified or sensitive document markings to parts for you, you`re a former counter intelligence official, what in the world would go through your mind if you believe these documents are still missing? And do you believe that that is what the DOJ is referring to when they say there are still documents that we do not have in our possession.\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, FORMER \uc2 FBI\uc2  ASSISTANT DIRECTOR FOR COUNTERINTELLIGENCE: So when you conduct a risk or damage assessment, part of this really is like putting together a puzzle if you have a trove of documents in front of you. And there are pieces and parts missing pages missing, remember, at the most sensitive level, and some of these are not only top secret, but also compartmented. These may be numbered. There are limited numbers of copies out there, and then pagination and initials of who`ve seen them.\par \par \uc2 When you`re missing things, particularly entire folders that are empty, you lead to the conclusion that, you know, we haven`t gotten everything back. And as Charlie said, where is it? It`s not necessarily at Mar-a-Lago. Remember, a search warrant was executed because probable cause was developed, likely through sources of information, that there was stuff at Mar-a-Lago. Now the question is, where else could it be, and that`s going to require sourcing and vetting for a source search warrant.\par \par \uc2 So there`s going to be other legal options that DOJ might try first. Chuck will be an expert on that, but they could actually tell the court to intervene, please ask Trump to give this back. Secondly, they could go to Trump and say make a statement that says you`ve returned everything. Now they`ve got him in a legal document. And then lastly, if they ever develop probable cause they can start searching other locations.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Interesting. Chuck, I`ll give you a chance to respond to that. Because I mean, tonight, some legal experts are speculating that report may indicate that the Justice Department is close to an indictment. Are you ready to go there based on what you just heard as well, from Frank with the questions that may be swirling around among DOJ officials this evening?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK ROSENBERG, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Well, following Frank is always easy, because all I have to do is say that he`s right, Frank`s right. There`s a bunch of ways that the government can try and get this stuff back. They might develop probable cause that a crime has been committed and that you would find this stuff in Bedminster or at Trump Tower in Manhattan. It`s possible it`s already happened Mar-a-Lago, we know that. They could ask for it orally or by letter. They can even issue a grand jury subpoena, as Frank suggested, they might try to use the court in an effort to pin Trump down on the question. Do you have any more stuff? And if so where is it?\par \par \uc2 That said, there`s also the possibility that they don`t get the stuff back. Trump either doesn`t have it or says he doesn`t have it, and they can`t prove otherwise. Or they can`t develop probable cause for another search. And that`s going to be hard for the reasons Frank just explained. And that Charlie knows well, in order to do that damage or risk assessment, you need to know what`s missing.\par \par \uc2 And if you it`s only conjecture, you don`t know what documents are missing. Or you know, what documents you recovered, but you don`t know who else has seen them or how they`ve been handled? It makes for very difficult calculations by the intelligence community, trying to protect their sources and methods, Ayman.\par \par \uc2 So, lots of unanswered questions. I don`t know that we`re any closer to an indictment to answer your more precise question. But we do need to get the documents back. And we need to figure out how to do that.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Frank, let`s switch gears to another big development today. And that was the trial of one of the Oath Keepers a lot of interesting nuggets that came out of that including this revelation, dealing with the Secret Service, what do you make of all of today`s headlines out of that trial?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: It`s a curious development. And I know the secret service doesn`t need yet another kind of issue to deal with. So two things can be true at the same time, their statement they issued late tonight, which says, hey, it`s not unusual for us when we`re securing an event to talk to potential protesters, people who are going to participate, people who are going to serve as escorts and make sure we`re all on the same page. OK, OK.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Even a group as notorious as the Oath Keepers. I`m sure the Secret Service has a profile of who these individuals are.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So this is where I have a problem. Because yes, even I mean, when you`re securing that you`ll engage with even potentially violent protest groups because you want to -- you want to know what they`re thinking and what they`re planning.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re actually dealing with the Oath Keepers in a capacity where they`re going to be escorting and quote unquote, helping with security. I do have a problem with that.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:08]\par \par \uc2 The other thing, though, that sounds very typical for Stewart Rhodes is that for him to brag around people, Hey, I`ve got a Secret Service contract. I just got off the phone with him. So the truth may be somewhere in the middle. We`ve got to figure that out.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, it certainly was a secondhand report as well. And Chuck after the revelation about the Oath Keepers, as we mentioned, this Secret Service in the agency statement tonight, it`s not exactly a denial, could we be in for even more surprises on that -- on that front given everything we`ve been seeing the Secret Service deal with?\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: Surprises seem to be a constant, Ayman. So yes, I imagine we`ll be in for some more surprises. I just don`t know what they`ll be. But that said, you know, there is the possibility that Stewart Rhodes was bragging or making it up. And like Frank said, it could also be that he did have a contact of the Secret Service. And they were just trying to figure out the parameters of what you can bring into the District of Columbia at or near or in the event involving the president.\par \par \uc2 You know, so it`s not necessarily nefarious, need to figure out some more of the facts here. That`s why you do investigations. But from the Secret Service perspective, if it is more than that, if there is something nefarious, then they have to take a very hard look at their own people. And they have to figure out whether there`s some breach in their security. I`m not ready to say that yet.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROSENBERG: But it`s a possibility, and they need to figure it out.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Charlie, let me switch gears to another headline making development this evening. You know, it came out of the President`s mouth. The Justice Department is defending democracy. No doubt about that. Tonight, though, President Biden was warning of a potential nuclear Armageddon. He made those comments at a fundraiser speaking about Russia and Putin in particular, saying that the risk level has been or is at the highest it has been since the 1962 Cuban Missile Crisis.\par \par \uc2 What is this just President Biden being off the cuff? Or is this really the sentiment among the administration and sources you speak to in Washington?\par \par \uc2 SAVAGE: Well, what I hear more concern about is at this stage is the risk that Russia if it gets desperate that they`re really losing, and Putin needs to save face in order to save his own skin inside his country uses tactical nuclear weapons that is small nuclear weapons just to that you would fire out of an artillery shell or something like that. Take out not, you know, something on the battlefield or even just a demonstration, strike over the sea to threaten everyone and make them back off.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, that would be extraordinary frightening a terrible thing for the world if that taboo were breached, but it`s also not quite Armageddon, which is the word I associate with, you know, the extinction of the human species, this massive city destroying all around the world nukes that we were afraid of during the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, who knows exactly what was on the President`s mind, but hearing that record, it sounds a little bit extravagant to me.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, Charlie Savage Frank Figliuzzi, Chuck Rosenberg, Jim, and thanks to the three of you for your insights. Coming up, ignoring the facts, it has become an effective and quite honestly a lucrative strategy in American politics, fundraising off of scandals and lies and yes, even in the case of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and later while the President tries to keep a promise on pot, Saudi Arabia sides with Russia to raise oil prices, is it an attack on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The 11th Hour just getting underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:19]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have you reached out to any of the mothers of your children?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You ask why? Why not?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Why do I need to?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well because according to the article, one -- the woman who says that you paid for her to have an abortion is also the mother of one of your children. It seems like that`s an easy way to --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Because of the article I had more kids. That`s why I reached out to you didn`t want to because I said no. And that`s what I mean when I said no, I said is not correct. That`s a lie. And that`s what I mean. That`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: A bit of a head scratcher there that is \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  on the campaign trail in Georgia today confusingly denying the Daily Beast reporting that he paid for an ex-girlfriend`s abortion and had a child with the same women. Walker`s campaigns against abortion right. Abortion rights, excuse me, his Democratic opponent has mostly avoided talking about the scandal, but today, he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): I`ll let the pundits do the punditry. But here`s the thing, my what we are hearing about my opponent is disturbing. And I think the people of Georgia have a real choice about who they think is ready to represent them in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: We`re happy to welcome back Symone Sanders-Townsend, former chief spokesperson for vice president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and host of the show Symone on Peacock and right here on MSNBC and former Florida Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , who was a member of the Republican Conference for two years during the Trump presidency. It`s great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Symone, I`ll start with you. Is Warnock`s approach here the right one sounds to me like he`s trying to take a little bit of that high erode or should he be more critical about what has emerged and more explicitly Walker`s alleged hypocrisy on the issue of abortion?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:10]\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, FORMER CHIEF SPOKESPERSON FOR VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Look, Ayman, I think we are seeing classic campaign one on one here from Senator Warnock and Herschel Walker`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think Senator Warnock is doing what he should do there is you get down, you lay down in the mud with pigs, you`re going to get dirty, and Senator Warnock needs to stay above the fray, talking to the issues that Georgians care about, and let`s not forget, on the moral high ground sort of work as a pastor. He lead -- he leads and lead the church that \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . himself once pastored it and so he has good footing to stand on here.\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , to be very clear, his campaign apparatus has decided that they are not going to see the ground here. Sure, of course, duh. There is some there, there here. But they have made the calculation that instead of continuing to muddy the waters with some kind of explanation from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , he is better off saying it`s a lie. The people of Georgia will have to make a decision and I have to keep reminding folks, Ayman, there`s a debate October 14, and this is going to be a topic in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, no doubt about it. Carlos, let me play for you what \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  said about the Republican response earlier today, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Their hypocrisy is not limited to the hypocrisy of abortion, although it is draconian and unconscionable. The very party that has said that women cannot make their own choices they`re being absolutely silent. In fact, they`re rallying around someone who has done exactly what they`ve denied the right to other women to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So talk to me about this for a moment. Is the Republican lust for power so strong, that they`re willing to be this hypocritical about one of their candidates about their policy, something they`ve staked the last 50 years on, just so they can seize power once again?\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. CARLOS CURBELO (R-FL): Well, Ayman, the first thing I`ll say is that when \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  talks about candidate quality and the deficiencies Republicans have had \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is kind of the personification of that concern that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . I mean Republicans in a state like Georgia in a midterm election should have a pretty easy job flipping this seat, and it`s going to be very difficult because \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  just doesn`t make the grade by a longshot.\par \par \uc2 But yes, I mean, I think \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is fair to bring up this criticism immune Republicans are the pro-life party, it appears that this candidate of there`s a very prominent candidate, and an important race has a big issue here and no one wants to talk about it. I`m sure Republicans will fire back and say \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  have her own credibility issues on, you know, elections that accepting election results.\par \par \uc2 But abortion is a big issue for Republicans. For decades, the party worked to get a judges on courts that would roll back abortion rights, they succeeded. And now they have this contradiction in Georgia. It`s certainly uncomfortable for the party.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Symone, Walker is not only potentially lying about his own situation, but he has also now run a political ad falsely accusing Warnock of cutting police funding. Is there a democratic strategy to counter misleading messages like this? Or what do you think should be the way Democrats go about dealing with a false attack like this?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Look, Ayman, we -- I dealt with something like this when I worked for a number of candidates, but I`ll give you the example of President Biden`s campaign. He was getting hit on the issue of defund the police. And this is such a hot button issue, my earrings falling off. And on the issue of defunding police is not something that then candidate Biden supported, but it was resonating with people out in the country.\par \par \uc2 And so what did we do? What any campaign should do, you run focus groups you run, you get out there, you see what is sticking, and then you put out a message to counter it. That is what is going to be critical for not just Georgia, but every single one of these candidates that are on the docket for Senate, they`re being hit on this issue of crime. And it is not just on the campaigns to do this work. That is why the party committees exist, the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee, Chuck Schumer`s PAC that has injected a lot of money into some of these races.\par \par \uc2 That is some of the work that can be done and should be done over the next week or so, to put something up on air so that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not going out there and hitting Senator we`re not and not being counted. The money here matters. The ads can make a difference.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, I was going to say and it`s not just in Georgia, Carlos. You`ve got polls showing Republican attack ads, making racist tighter in places like Wisconsin, Pennsylvania and Nevada, do Republicans have more to offer than scaring voters about crime and the border?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, Ayman, we know that for Republicans to win what the leadership has told her members in the House and the Senate is they have to be talking about the economy, inflation, crime and immigration and certainly some of these candidates are doing everything they can to change the subject.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:09]\par \par \uc2 They saw the polling, they know what the abortion issue did to Republican candidates over the summer, this big red wave that had been predicted. Those predictions were scaled back as a result of democratic enthusiasm. So certainly Republicans are doing everything they can to try to change the subject.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, Symone, Carlos, stick with me. Coming up, the President keeps a campaign promise about justice and marijuana possession. What happens if gas prices go back up all of that when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:20]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, NATIONAL ACTION NETWORK PRESIDENT: I thought it was monumental. The President has in his meetings with civil rights leaders talked about this. But this is actually the first president to do it. And he went a step further by urging governors to do it. Because this is something that`s just fat, and just two people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  speaking today applauding the President`s decision to pardon thousands of people convicted of nonviolent federal marijuana possession charges. The White House says this is a step to fulfill a promise Biden made on the campaign trail. And some Republicans say it is just a desperate attempt to distract from the economy.\par \par \uc2 Symone Sanders-Townsend and \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  are still with us. And we want to point out Carlos has previously served as a strategic advisor for the Cannabis Trade Federation. Carlos, I`ll start with you in this round. You called this decision a no brainer. Recent polls show the President still needs to win over some young voters. Will this do the trick? What`s the motivation here in your estimation?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: So Ayman, I think this is good policy and good politics. And by the way, I`ve seen some praise from Republicans like South Carolina, Congresswoman Nancy Mace tweeted that this was just a smart move by the President. Obviously, marijuana has something that has become mainstream in the country. Most people accept that, most states have passed laws, including conservative states to legalize marijuana to varying degrees.\par \par \uc2 So in that sense, it`s good policy, it makes sense. People`s lives have been ruined because of these convictions just for simple possession of marijuana maybe 10, 20, 30 years ago. So that`s the policy side.\par \par \uc2 Politically, I do think it`s smart for the President to do this. We know that young voters tend not to turn out in midterm elections. We know this is an issue that motivates some young voters, especially Democratic voters, and they`re going to be more excited now to turn out because they`re seeing that government can do smart things that they happen to agree with. So I think this was a good move. It happens to be good politics, but more importantly, it`s just smart policy.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Symone, I`m going to give you the chance to play rebuttal here. If there is one. You may not see it that way. But some Republicans, they`ve been messaging heavily on crime and drugs. Is there a downside to this decision ahead of the midterms? Could pardoning people convicted of possession play into the hands of GOP messaging?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: You know, I don`t think so. In 2022, it is projected by some estimations that the cannabis industry will generate at least $100 billion in America. Juxtapose that with the fact that there are people who have records right now for simple possession of marijuana is something that right now, people who do not look like the people on this panel, on this show right now are making millions of dollars off of billions of dollars in the economy.\par \par \uc2 This is just smart. I think, Ayman, when we talk about crime, and I think sometimes we all have to check ourselves, right, because crime is an umbrella term. In Texas I talked to some moms in Texas the other day, some suburban moms. For them, the issue of crime is public safety. It is gun safety. For some folks, eat with some folks in North Carolina crime is one of the top three polling issues in the race in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  race.\par \par \uc2 Crime is particularly about public safety. Black folks want to be safe, too, right? So there is brown people want to be safe, white. Everyone wants to be able to walk through their community and be safe. And so this issue of crime, how you talk about it matters. And I do think that some Republicans have over torque on this particular issue. But I think this from the President today is a promise made and a promise kept.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And there are those, Symone, that follow this case, who follow this issue much more than I do much closer than I do. And they say, Yes, this was a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  campaign promise, he did promise to decriminalize pot. But does this in your opinion go far enough for some of the very voters he was hoping to energize? There were some who wanted to see him go even further with a decision like this.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Oh, this is a first step to be very clear, Ayman. And the President laid that out in his -- in his was a statement that was put out today. Marijuana is currently a schedule one drug. It is -- marijuana is right up there. It is worse than fentanyl on the schedule of drugs. Right. And we just talked about the billions of dollars that are projected to be generated this year.\par \par \uc2 So I do think you have to go further. The President also talked about expungements on the campaign trail, automatic expungement. What he did today does not in fact do that. So there`s more work to be done on the federal level but also working with governors which the President said he encouraged governors to follow his suit today on pardons. We need folks to talk about expungement. We need to clear these records completely.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:10]\par \par \uc2 And for so many people across the country, disproportionately black and brown folks, that will make a real difference.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Carlos, real quick one for you on politics. We just saw Trump back super PAC make its first ad buys in Ohio and Pennsylvania with a month to go. Will this MAGA ink spending spree make a difference?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: I think it has the potential and then of hurting these candidates. I mean, a lot of these Republicans who are struggling in states where they would usually be well positioned in a midterm election is because they are so closely tied to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  intervened in primaries, got the candidates he wanted. Those candidates have been struggling. So they`re kind of doubling down now on that strategy, and that has failed for them.\par \par \uc2 I really do think that this election cycle, the Trump Effect is going to end up hurting Republicans. So this is very risky, but some of these candidates are desperate, so I`m sure they`re welcoming this money. It`s a double edged sword.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Al; right, \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , Symone Sanders-Townsend, thank you so much to the both of you, as always, greatly appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Was \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cutting oil production a political attack on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? And what about Americans calls for revenge against the Saudis when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:07]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: It is being called hostile short sighted and a turning point. That is how some Democrats this evening are describing \uc2 OPEC\uc2  decision this week to cut oil output by 2 million barrels a day. The move sharply undercuts President Biden`s efforts to keep gas prices low here at home, particularly ahead of the crucial midterms. It also renews questions about the shaky relationship between the U.S. and Saudi Arabia, and where the Saudis loyalties really lie.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Aaron David Miller. He is a senior fellow at the \uc2 Carnegie Endowment for International Peace\uc2  specializing in U.S. foreign policy. He is of course a former State Department analyst, adviser and negotiator in both Republican and Democrat administrations.\par \par \uc2 Aaron, it`s great to see you again. Let`s start with the likely impact of this move, especially as we approach winter here, gas prices are already going back up in several states. What do you think was the motivation behind this?\par \par \uc2 AARON DAVID MILLER, SENIOR FELLOW, \uc2 CARNEGIE ENDOWMENT FOR INTERNATIONAL PEACE\uc2 : You know, Ayman, good to see you by the way. Pun intended here. Crude diplomacy is what I really think`s going on. The Saudis want to maintain \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cohesion, clearly has an authoritarian power, they have much more in common in many respects with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Russian oil is critically important to maintaining the sort of inflation -- inflationary prices they want when it comes to selling their oil product. They`re worried about the cap that the EU has in principle indoors, which is supposed to kick in by December and Russian oil they`re going to be embargoed in December as well.\par \par \uc2 So add to that the recession that they I suspect believe is coming. And all of this is designed, frankly, to maintain oil at significantly higher price than what it is now, per barrel. The gas prices here are going to go up considerably. But I suspect over time, I would worry not so much in the next four or five weeks, but worried by years and that there could be a significant hike in gas prices here and of course in Europe.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So I`m sure you saw all of the takes online and everywhere really following yesterday`s \uc2 OPEC\uc2  decision, we have been seeing plenty of references to the fist bump between the Saudi Crown Prince \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  and President Biden just over the summer.\par \par \uc2 You`ve tweeted that there is no clear sign that MBS is looking beyond Biden to the potential return of Trump or his Republican avatar. Is this global political strategy at play here? Do you believe the Saudis would prefer to have a different president than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  even though \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  went to Saudi Arabia and greeted MBS with that fist bump?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: He did. I mean, but he went very reluctantly. President really had to be persuaded by his advisors. And his initial reaction, of course, MBS in the wake of the murder and dismemberment Jamal Khashoggi, I think before the President really is here. This is not going to help the U.S.-Saudi relationship.\par \par \uc2 But I think the prevailing attitude in the administration, as far as I can determine on Congress is that unlike \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 , the U.S.-Saudi relationship is perceived in Washington to be too important and too big to fail.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, I think, \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2  at 37, going to rule Saudi Arabia for another 50 years is in fact looking way beyond \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who is going to turn 80 in November. I think he really would prefer Republican and whether it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or Republican avatar, I`m not sure he cares.\par \par \uc2 But there has been a reset in the U.S.-Saudi relations and it`s been done by the Saudis. And I think whether it was intended or not going to be a direct slap at Biden, it clearly was perceived as one.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 Remember you`ve got the deputy Russian Foreign Minister showing up in Vienna under U.S. sanctions to attend the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  plus. I mean, what more evidence do you need? The Saudis clearly are not interested in joining the we love and support Ukraine club.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. And the interesting thing is you have, Aaron, several Democrats floating, the idea of punishing Saudi Arabia, some suggesting pulling U.S. troops or defense agreements, defense systems, stop selling weapons to them, stop underwriting some of their military operations and wars in places like Yemen, you had Senate Majority Whip \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  going out further saying it is time for our foreign policy to imagine a world without this alliance with these royal backstabbers, his words, not mine. Is this a turning point for America and more narrowly the Democrat Party and the Saudis?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You know, having worked for Republicans and Democrats and a half a dozen administrations, I`ve heard talk of resetting the U.S.-Saudi relationship so many times. And the reality is it just -- it doesn`t compute. It doesn`t convey. The President has prerogatives according to constitution, he`ll direct foreign policy. The administrator -- Congress could in fact, pass legislation, but then you`ve got the problem of is this a critically important issue now? And is the President going to veto any serious effort to restrain or constrain the U.S.-Saudi relationship and probably, there are enough votes to override it?\par \par \uc2 So I think you may see a suspension of defensive military equipment of Saudi Arabia some expression of peak and opposition to what the Saudis have done, because it really is humiliating, I think, for the president in the wake of the fist bump around the world.\par \par \uc2 I just think in the end, I mean, I don`t believe in transformations. And I think we got to start looking at U.S.-Saudi relationship as a set of smart transactions. And if we`re going to -- if we describe them as allies, and I`m not sure why we do because there`s really no consistent coincidence of both interests and values across the board and Saudi Arabia, I think we have to turn this relationship into something much more transactional. They give, we give, they take. We make it unmistakably clear to them that we`re unhappy. We have leveraged the real question is, Ayman, are we going to use it.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Indeed. We`ll see how it all plays out. Aaron David Miller, always a pleasure, sir. Thank you so much for your time and your insights.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thanks, Ayman.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Coming up, the Simpsons they have a way of predicting the future so what does that have to do with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Oz when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:20]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Despite having a Mitch McConnell`s 10s of millions and Fox News, you know bearing down on us more than all the other candidates combined has allowed us to not only withstand that we`re actually above ahead. And just today we had a poll came out the head it`s up six points. And that`s a testament to our record and how we`ve been able to push back the lies and stand on the truth of our recipe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so the last thing before we go tonight, political predictions from Springfield. Pennsylvania`s lieutenant governor announced today that his campaign has raised a record breaking amount of cash and his Senate race against Republican doctor TV personality Mehmet Oz. John Fetterman`s campaign says it took in $22 million this past quarter for more than 330,000 unique donors and that might be due in part to Fetterman`s rather impressive social media presence. He routinely drags his opponent with accusations of lying and being out of touch.\par \par \uc2 And this week, he turned to a popular cartoon doctor to make his point. As Fetterman points out before there was Dr. Oz, there was Dr. Nick. They say the Simpsons always predict the future and once again, they nailed it. Attached to the tweet was this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi, everybody.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Hey everybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: With my diet you can eat all you want, anytime you want.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you lose weight?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You might it`s a free country.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I`ve got the number one miracle and Abeille to burn your fat. Lose fat without diet or exercise to burn stomach fat instantly disappears.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I recommend a slow, steady gorging process combined with assal horizontology.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Garcinia Cambogia Extract. Crystal sonic therapy. Sea buckthorn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Dr. Nick, this malpractice committee has received a few complaints against you.\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN ANCHOR: Dr. Oz being sued for advice he gave to viewers who struggle falling asleep.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Dr. Oz has been accused of promoting quack treatments by some top physicians.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you looking for a way to slash the cost of your medical expenses?\par \par \uc2 OZ: How much did you pay for a pill that takes your body back 10 years?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, 1, 600 (INAUDIBLE). The B is for bought again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: His empire and wealth have flourished.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The most rewarding part was when he gave me my money. Bye-bye, everybody.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [00:00:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Dr. Nick to help take us off the air tonight. And on that note, I wish you a good night from all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News. Thank you for staying up late with us.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221007eia600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,490 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: If that had happened, I would have said it, because there`s nothing to be ashamed of there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Walker defense keeps changing by the hour.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You said that if it did happen, there`s nothing to be ashamed of.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Wait, I never said it. I never said it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You said it this morning.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: New reporting that Walker`s accuser is the mother of his child. New concerns from Republicans about what they`re defending. And the real-world implications of the draconian laws \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wants to inflict on you.\par \par \uc2 Then, the head of the Oath Keepers may have been in contact with the Secret Service. What we learned today at the seditious conspiracy trial.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the under-the-radar Democrat who could pull off a North Carolina Senate stunner. And why today`s federal pardon for marijuana possession is a massive BFD.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Too many lives have been upended because of our failed approach to marijuana. It`s time that we write these wrongs.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. The \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  story keeps getting worse for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Earlier this week, of course, the Daily Beast first reported that Walker, the Trump- endorsed Republican nominee for Georgia Senate seat had paid for a woman`s abortion in 2009. And the report contained lots of contemporaneous evidence that has not been reviewed by us here at NBC News, but it included the literal receipt for the procedure, the check Walker sent the woman, and even the get-well-card Walker apparently mailed her, complete with what sure looks like his signature.\par \par \uc2 Now, as we`ve said, NBC News has not independently verified this story. This is a Daily Beast story. Walker vehemently denied the allegations when they first broke. He said he had no idea who the woman making the allegation could be. That seems a little difficult to believe though because according to a new report in The Daily Beast, the woman who says Walker paid for her abortion is also the mother of one of his children. Again, NBC News has also not verified this most recent report. In a statement, Walker said there was no truth to it or any other Daily Beast report.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be clear, there`s absolutely nothing wrong with a woman choosing to terminate a pregnancy nor is there anything wrong with that person`s partner offering to pay for the procedure. The problem comes when the partner in question, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , is running on one of the most extreme anti-abortion platforms in the country months after the Supreme Court got rid of Roe. Walker said he supports a national abortion ban without any exceptions, including for rape and incest. He has compared abortion to murder. And it is that crushing hypocrisy with real world consequences that inspired the mother of one of his children to come forward with this allegation.\par \par \uc2 She told The Daily Beast, "He seemed pretty pro-choice to me." He was pro- choice obviously, going on to say, "He didn`t accept responsibility for the kid we did have together and now he isn`t accepting responsibility for the one we didn`t have. That says so much about how he views the role of women and childbirth versus his own. And now he wants to take that choice away from other women and couples entirely."\par \par \uc2 Now, when this story initially broke, Walker put out a statement almost right away calling the story a defamatory lie, promising an imminent lawsuit against the Daily Beast for his reporting. Walker said the lawsuit would be filed tomorrow morning, meaning first thing Tuesday, now Thursday night, still no sign of any legal action. And by this morning, Walker`s toted changed.\par \par \uc2 His argument, if I`m tracking, and I`ll admit it`s a little hard to, is something along the lines of I didn`t do it, and if I did do it, would that really be so bad?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, TALK SHOW HOST: Is there anything you need to be forgiven for vis-a-vis a woman whose name we do not know? Do you know who this woman is? And do you need to forgiven?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: That`s what`s so funny. And I`m saying I`ve been forgiven because of all the things I did when I went to my -- the thing with my ex-wife and all that and things I did. I don`t know how many years (INAUDIBLE). I forgot. I wasn`t perfect. I had my problem with mental health, and I have - - I hate to tell that I`ve been born again, but I have a new life. And I`ve been moving forward and if that had happened, I would have -- I would have said it because there`s nothing to be ashamed of there. People have done that. But I know nothing about it. And if I knew about it, I`ll be honest and talk about it. But I know nothing about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Hugh is nodding his head there tracking better than I was because I`m having a little difficulty following. But Walker goes on an extended aside about past allegations of violent, threatening behavior towards his ex-wife, a different woman than the one in question, before finally saying, "if that had happened, I would have said there`s nothing to be ashamed of there. It`s something people do." That presumably meaning paid for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:12]\par \par \uc2 And again, as I keep saying, he`s right. There`s nothing to be ashamed of other than the fact that Walker wants to make abortion illegal across the country, wants to control whether women can get that procedure, control their bodies entirely. He did a press event today, and you`ll be shocked to hear it didn`t go that well.\par \par \uc2 Walker seemed to walk back his earlier statement he made this morning and again denied everything despite all the evidence to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You said that it did happen, there`s nothing to be ashamed of. How do you --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Wait, I never said it. I never said it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You said it this morning on Hugh Hewitt`s show.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No, what I said -- I was talking about something totally different if it did happen. I said, when my ex-wife, you know, my past, has nothing to do with what this woman says. I said this here, the abortion thing is false. It`s a lie. And that`s what I said. I said, anything happening with my ex-wife or what Christian was talking about, I don`t know. But as I said, if anything happened, there`s nothing to be ashamed of. And my ex- wife and I had been the best of friends with her husband and my wife. So, that`s the thing that I`ve said. And I said nothing about if it did happen because I said that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, we`re not going to go sentence by sentence here. You can decide for yourself if you think \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is telling the truth. It is undeniable he has had a real credibility problem and a track record for lying. He lied about his experience working with law enforcement and his supposed to work supervising hospitals, and about charitable donations his food company claimed to be making that never seem to materialize.\par \par \uc2 He even lied about the number of kids he has. And when the Daily Beast, in fact, the same reporter of these latest stories called him on that, Walker had to admit their reporting was accurate. Even as he now tries to claim every story the outlet has written about him as untrue. So again, you can make up your own mind about who you believe here, but I`ll just say, I don`t think it would be unreasonable if you concluded \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 But this pivot to Walker`s family history, the idea of forgiveness has been something of a recurring theme for the candidates since the allegations broke. He`s conflating the abortion story with allegations from his ex-wife and now his son that he behaved in a violent and threatening manner, including one incident when he held a gun to his ex-wife`s head and threatened to blow her brains out. An allegation that bears mentioning Walker does not deny.\par \par \uc2 Walker says he has been forgiven. He`s been saved, reborn since the struggle of mental illness that he claimed cause those violent incidences. And when he was asked about abortion, he pivots again to the religious rhetoric of personal growth without addressing the merits to the allegation which he sort of denies. The strategy is not working all that well. I mean, even some Georgia Republicans like the state`s sitting Republican lieutenant governor are clearly getting a little nervous.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. GEOFF DUNCAN (R-GA): Even the most-staunch Republicans I think are rattled at the continued flow of information. I think every Republican knew that there was baggage out there and -- but the weight of that baggage is starting to feel a little closer to unbearable at this point. Some of this stuff could have gotten cleaned up in the primary if we would have given an honest look and an honest primary. That would have looked hard at somebody`s leadership skills.\par \par \uc2 If we`re being intellectually honest, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  won the primary because he scored a bunch of touchdowns back in the 80s and he was Donald Trump`s friend. And now we`ve moved forward several months on the calendar and that`s no longer a recipe to win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now -- ouch. That man is not running for reelection, doesn`t have to share a ballot with Walker, which may be why he`s willing to speak so candidly. But behind the scenes, he`s definitely not the only one thinking that way I can tell you. Here`s just one example, one anonymous Republican strategist also laid the blame for Walker on the ex-president. "Since President Trump hasn`t spent a penny thus far to help his slate of flawed endorsed candidates, he could have at least been a few bucks to vet them first. It would have saved Trump from the embarrassment of what`s shaping up to be a very bad election night for his candidates."\par \par \uc2 It`s true \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is personally responsible for many of the terrible candidates Republicans are running in otherwise winnable races the cycle, but it`s too late to do anything about it now. The election is in 33 days. What are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 Greg Bluestein is a political reporter for the Atlanta Journal-Constitution who has been covering Herschel Walker`s Senate campaign. Tim Miller is a former \uc2 RNC\uc2  spokesman, author of Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road To Hell. And both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Greg, let me begin with you as an Atlanta-based reporter. I saw someone last night reporting from Atlanta who said the story wasn`t really breaking through on the local news. I don`t know if that changed. What`s your characterization of how much this is a present story in the Georgia media.\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, POLITICAL REPORTER, THE ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION: No, it`s an enormous story in the Georgia media. It`s all over the AJC website. It`s all over local news. There is probably 30 or 40 reporters out at the small town in East Georgia where \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  had his first major campaign event since The Daily Beast reporting. So, it`s a major story. The question is what kind of impact it will have? We don`t know yet. But we know as we heard from Lieutenant Governor Geoff Duncan, others who have spoken publicly and privately, Republicans are very nervous right now.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:11]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, Tim, we can talk about impact for a second first. I mean, look, this is going to be close race. And I don`t think anyone -- I`m certainly not expecting like he`s going to drop 10 points in the polls, but a race like this is going to be one at lost in the margins. And the question is, are there 5000 votes for people that don`t show up to vote that might have otherwise with this? What`s your assessment?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, FORMER SPOKESMAN, \uc2 RNC\uc2 : Look, absolutely, it`s going to make a difference, Chris. I think a lot of people have kind of a broken brain over the Access Hollywood situation and kind of believe that, LOL, nothing matters now because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But there are a lot of unique things that were happening in that race that made that workable for him.\par \par \uc2 One of them is -- of which was a lot of the voters just on the issue of abortion, for example, who are pro-choice voters thought -- kind of thought \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was pro-choice, right? He was able to do this thing where he appealed to Christian voters and to secular voters. So, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  doesn`t have that, right. He has this weight, as you said in the intro, of the abortion issue and of the hypocrisy of him pushing the most extreme anti-abortion views while allegedly paying for this abortion.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is I talked to a lot of voters in the state in these Atlanta suburbs. The ones who, when I was down there with Greg at an event a couple of weeks ago, the types of voters that put \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  over the top last time in what had been a red state, A lot of these people said they`re voting for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . This was before the most recent slate of allegations.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of them, you know, said to me that Walker`s candidacy was fundamentally insane, and that you couldn`t trust him, and that the ads that were running already about his ex-wife were just too far gone. This person can`t run a Senate office. These are -- these are real people, former Republican voters, people that crossed over for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and they could very well cross over again for \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . And the more the news talks about Herschel Walker`s lies is extremism, the more likely it is to nudge some of those voters across the line.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Greg, you did some reporting yourself of voters about how they`re sort of seeing this through the prism and found a range of views, including people sticking with him and those who are finding this intolerable.\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yes, there`s three broad general buckets. There`s the Republicans who don`t believe this report at all and don`t believe the "fake news." They don`t care. There`s the Republicans who believe it, but also kind of believe the ends justify the means and they`re willing to vote for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  despite these issues because they believe that he`ll vote with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and they want a Republican-controlled Senate.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s those voters that Tim was just talking about, those split ticket voters that were already present in the electorate even before these Daily Beast stories that might be more intense now. These are the voters that could swing the election. Because in Georgia two years ago, fewer than 12,000 voters decided that, that Brian -- that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the first -- the state`s first Democratic presidential nominee since 1992. And just a small fluctuation in voter patterns, just a small change could really affect this outcome.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, to your point, Tim, too. I mean, that line about fundamentally insane. I mean, I`ve tried to find the words to describe this because the whole thing when you step back is just so ludicrous. Like, I bear \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  no personal animus. And he was like someone who -- whose faith is extremely genuine and has had troubles and does feel like he`s come around. There`s just no reason for him to be U.S. Senator.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s thousands and thousands of people in the state of Georgia across ideological spectrum, across lines of race and class, whatever, who you could pull out of the hat to be the Senate nominee that aren`t \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  who is literally just there because as the lieutenant governor said, he was a famous football player and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was his friend. I mean, part of that is like, at a certain point, you might have to pay the piper for that.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And they should pay the piper. That Lieutenant Governor Geoff Duncan who did the right thing during Donald Trump`s attempt to steal the last election has been cast aside because of the GOP cults and the fact that everybody just wanted to go along with what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted. And they should have to pay the piper.\par \par \uc2 And yes, look, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  should be spending time in therapy not running for Senate. I can sense ways that you have to feel kind of bad for his family, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: His son is a far-right MAGA Tik Tok influencer. That is who`s speaking out. It`s not like a Ron Reagan Jr. liberal kids situation. They went on Fox News together to argue for Stop the Steal back in December of 2020. And so, you have to feel bad for this family. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  were reviewing secret kids. You know, that MAGA son is learning about secret siblings he didn`t know. Like, no wonder he`s mad. No wonder he`s angry at his father.\par \par \uc2 And he got pushed into this by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . You can`t let \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  off the hook and the other Republican leaders who didn`t want to have a real primary fight. And so, I don`t think it should be surprising that the very same voters that said no to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Georgia in 2020 are looking at this and saying, you know, come on, guys, give me a break.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I will also, Tim, just one follow-up. I was thinking about Tim Murphy from 2017 who is a Pennsylvania congressman who had a mistress whose abortion he had facilitated. She had sent him a text. "You have zero issue posting your pro-life stance all over the place when you have no issue asking me to abort our unborn child just last week when we thought that was one of the options." Or he had asked her to secure an abortion. He ends up resigning after that. So, it`s interesting to see five years later like where the line is.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:23]\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, we saw with \uc2 Roy Moore\uc2  that there is no line now, right? And this is the way (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. There`s no coming back from \uc2 Roy Moore\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MILLER: There`s no coming back. I mean, he was a pedophile, and everyone went along with it. The \uc2 RNC\uc2  did, the NRSC, right? And so, that is a lesson that the party has learned in the Trump era. And so, there`s nothing that can be done. And this is as Greg said, the ends justify the means situation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Greg Bluestein and Tim Miller, thank you, gentlemen, both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, as the Republican charade collapses with their support of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , there are real people across the country who have suffered from the party`s anti-abortion crusade, its successful crusade. Jessica Valenti has been an indispensable resource covering day in day out this beat of post-Dobbs America. She joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What`s your reaction to people so close to you, your son, a mother of your -- someone who says that she`s the mother of your kid saying that you`re lying. What`s your reaction to people so close to you saying don`t trust you?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Just like what I just said. Democrats are desperate for this seat. This seat is important.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: They`re your family.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: They`re very desperate for to seat. I love my family. I always love my family. I`m going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I know I`m going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is strange about this latest scandal with Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is that the substance of the accusation is not scandalous. The Daily Beast is reporting Walker paid for a girlfriend`s abortion in 2009. It`s something he has denied. Also, something NBC News has not independently verified.\par \par \uc2 But according to report, they had a relationship, she had an abortion, and he financially supported her through that. That is not scandalous behavior. In fact, it`s perfectly normal, something millions of Americans do. It would normally not be anyone`s business, except that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is trying to make abortion illegal and this is not just an abstract exercise.\par \par \uc2 Abortion bans and restrictions are already a lived reality for millions of people across nearly 20 states. As the woman whose abortion Walker reportedly paid for put it to the Daily Beast, "This was a decision I had to make twice about my future and a potential child`s future and I was able to make it both times. And Herschel was able to have a say. The fact that he now thinks it`s OK to just take that away, I just can`t understand