r seems pretty casual about it. Today, you have Kari Lake who`s again, running on a pro-life agenda who`s being like, yes, I`ve always wanted abortion to be, you know, safe and rare. And it`s like, wait a second, you guys waged a 40-year battle that just culminated a success in June. Like, what happened here?\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: I think it`s both that they are very confused and very scared, right? Like, you`re talking about a lot of people who actually don`t know that much about abortion at all, don`t know the medical details we know Republicans have said, like some of the most ridiculous things when it comes to women`s bodies. And then you`re dealing with a lot of candidates at the moment who can see the writing on the wall, they can see the polls, and they`re terrified.\par \par \uc2 They know that abortion bans are extremely, extremely unpopular, and that tying their names to those bans is a real mistake.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s exactly what it is. What is so evident to me is just the skin-deep commitment of many professional Republican politicians to this, which was, you`re a Republican, you`re running the primary, of course, you`re pro-life, you say I`m pro-life, and I`m proudly pro-life and that`s it. And I do think in the pre-Dobbs era it was a little more of a thought experiment. Not to say there weren`t a barrage of abortion restrictions being pushed at the state level particularly. But now, it`s like all on the table and they`re just all seem completely unprepared to -- like, they never even actually give it thought.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:28]\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Yeah, it`s pretty wild because they have had decades. This is --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Decades.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: -- what we`ve seen in the last days is decades of work. And this is the world they created and they have to own it. All of these stories that we`re seeing come out day after day, have raped children having to travel for abortions likely going to be forced to have children, in the future women being forced to carry dead and dying fetuses, burst ectopic pregnancies, these are happening every day and we`re only getting a tiny, tiny glimpse of the actual number of experiences that are happening because these are just the people who feel comfortable enough going to the media.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that is such an important point. And again, your \uc2 Substack\uc2  On Her Head has been really one -- probably the best place to aggregate all these stories. I`ve learned so much from it. But I think about that, too, when I read it and I think about it in the case of the woman who came forward in this in this case, right? It`s just not something that you want to go run and tell reporter. It`s not anyone`s business. It`s a personal decision you made. All of this is none of anyone`s business.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re only getting the tiny bit at the top and yet already, that is horrifying. And as we know, there`s just thousands and thousands of more stories.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: There`s thousands and thousands of more stories and Republicans are really depending on the idea that Americans and voters will just see these statistics or see these as anomalies, right? Like, they keep saying these are rare stories, these are anomalies. They don`t want voters to see these women as human beings who are really suffering every single day.\par \par \uc2 We all know someone who`s had an abortion. We all know someone who`s had a tragic story. These are not rarities. These are not anomalies. These are everyday experiences.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Jessica Valenti, author of many books and also \uc2 Substack\uc2  On Her Head where she does abortion today, and you should definitely check that out. Thank you, Jessica. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, shocking testimony today in the seditious conspiracy trial of the far-right gang who stormed the Capitol. A former member claims the group`s leader was in contact with a member of the Secret Service. The implications of that claim next.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER AIDE TO \uc2 MARK MEADOWS\uc2 : And he felt the mags were at fault for not letting everybody in. But another leading reason and likely the primary reason is because he wanted it full and he was angry that we weren`t letting people through the mags with weapons, what the Secret Service deemed as weapons and are -- are weapons.\par \par \uc2 But when we were in the offstage announce tent, I was part of a conversation -- I was in -- I was in the vicinity of a conversation where I overheard the President say something to the effect of, you know, I don`t effing care that they have weapons.\par \par \uc2 They`re not here to hurt me. Take that effing mags away. Let my people in. They can march to the Capitol from here. Let the people in. Take the effing mags away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was Trump White House aide Cassidy Hutchinson`s explosive testimony to the House January 6 Committee that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew that many of his supporters brought weapons to the Capitol and he did not care. Trump was even pressing the Secret Service to get rid of the mags, the magnetometers, that screen for weapons so that all his supporters, including those who were armed, could join the rally.\par \par \uc2 We know that on January 6, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  did not appear to trust the Secret Service and refuse to leave the Capitol with them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREG JACOB, FORMER WHITE HOUSE LAWYER TO FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: The Vice President had refused to get into the car. The head of his Secret Service detail, Tim, had said, I assure you we`re not going to drive out of the building without your permission. And the Vice President had said something to the effect of Tim, I know you, I trust you, but you`re not the one behind the wheel. He was determined that we would complete the work that we had set out to do that day that it was his constitutional duty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now we also know the Secret Service deleted their text messages from January 5th and 6th, the chief watchdog for the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  scrapped an investigation to recover said texts. And now, today, we got some explosive under oath testimony in the seditious conspiracy trial members of the far right Oath Keepers militia, that in the months leading up to the insurrection, the group`s founder, Elmer Stewart Rhodes III indicated he had contact with a member of the Secret Service.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Secret Service responded to that testimony releasing this statement. And get this. They don`t appear to deny these allegations. They said that they have been in contact with Oath Keepers in the past, and that it is not uncommon for groups to contact them about security restrictions for events.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by someone who was in the courtroom today for the Oath Keepers trial, Rachel Weiner. She`s legal affairs reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . Rachel, thank you for joining us. First, give us context - - give us context for this testimony today about this connection between Rhodes and the Secret Service. Who testified to that extent and what was the context for that testimony?\par \par \uc2 RACHEL WEINER, LEGAL AFFAIRS REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: The context is this is a former member of the Oath Keepers who`s testifying that when he was a member, he -- Rhodes talked about having a Secret Service contact, and that he heard Rhodes on the phone with someone talking about parameters for a rally which appeared to be -- could be a Secret Service agent.\par \par \uc2 So, that could be in line with, you know, what Secret Service is saying now which is basically listen, anytime anyone`s coming to DC planning to protest, we are interested in talking to them and basically letting them know what the rules are. This is someone who broke away from the group. They had three people testified today who broke away from the group because they no longer felt comfortable with the direction Rhodes was going in around the election, after the election talking about violent armed resistance, as they testified, to Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:53]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, my understanding is that the testimony was at a rally in North Carolina in September, if I`m not mistaken, where Rhodes was on the phone with this individual who identified as a Secret Service contact gaming out essentially where the security parameters are. Again, I didn`t know that the Secret Service -- that people like talk to Secret Service about stuff like that. So, their statement tonight is kind of news to me. I think that that sort of bears a little more investigating.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about the three individuals today that did testify and what -- and what that testimony -- what the basic contours of that were.\par \par \uc2 WEINER: Yes, so on the Secret Service, first of all, just to say, they are saying we do not know of any contact with him, if the contact happens, basically, it sounds like it was the sort of thing. Now, that doesn`t mean that something else couldn`t come out later but that`s sort of where the Secret Service is for now, and that this is a normal thing that they do even though it might not be advertised.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the rest of the testimony, they was sort of -- the day for at least some of the former Oath Keepers, there will probably be more -- I think the ones that will be most critical to potential conviction for the five Oath Keepers on trial are the ones who were with the group at the Capitol and has pleaded guilty to this seditious conspiracy and planning to testify that they were conspiring to stop Biden from being able to be certified as the president.\par \par \uc2 But the people who testified so far, what they`re trying to build is the case that Rhodes and the people close to him were actively calling for violence, and that violence was not offensive because the defendants are saying we were just preparing for Antifa. So, what we got from witnesses today, and what I`m sure we`ll get likely from more witnesses is people saying no, what disturbed us was that the conversation went beyond that. It was not just prepare yourself for leftists activist, it was saying, we want those people to come at us because we want violence, we`re going to respond with violence. We were sort of hoping to agitate and create a violent situation.\par \par \uc2 One of those people was concerned enough that he recorded this conversation and he shared it actually with authorities in the fall. But he was sort of scattershot. He said one of the people he reached out to was Senator Ted Stevens who has been dead for some time. So, no one really took it seriously or maybe no one even noticed it. Frankly, he just sent it in as a tip. And it was ignored, basically, until after the Capitol riot where he just sent it again. And then of course, it was of much greater interest and he immediately got a call from the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. So, we heard from the individual who actually made that recording and he was freaked out enough to make it and send it along. Final thing for you. Yes, Ted Stevens, that`s not going to go very far if you send it to him in the year 2020.\par \par \uc2 WEINER: Unfortunately, no.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The -- there was another plea today. This is the first Proud Boys leader to plead guilty to seditious conspiracy. Jeremy Bertino, 43, of Belmont, North Carolina became a potential key witness for the Justice Department against \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  and four other Proud Boy leaders, some of whom had ties to influential supporters of President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I had to remind myself today that`s a whole other case than the Oath Keeper seditious conspiracy case.\par \par \uc2 WEINER: It didn`t, yes, but it`s very similar and there are some connections between them which the most interesting of which is Roger Stone, who has come up on the very first day of this trial and DC that Stewart Rhodes was texting a group called Friends of Stone right around the election, a signal chat group, and talking about a violent resistance to election. Another person in that group was \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2 , the longtime chairman of the proud boys.\par \par \uc2 So, what Jeremy Bertino could say about the Stone-Tarrio relationship will be interesting. And what we hear in this Oath Keepers trial about the Rhodes-Stone relationship will be interesting. And prosecutors have already indicated some convergence between those two groups, and stone as being sort of a connector between them. So, there could be more overlap than we know about right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that`s fascinating. Thank you so much. Rachel Weiner, I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WEINER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:07]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, unleash the Dark Brandon memes. President Biden revolutionizes marijuana in the United States, or at least begins to, announcing the largest federal pardon in 45 years. The details next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, President Biden made an enormously consequential announcement issuing the largest federal pardon in more than 40 years.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:03]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: As I said when I ran for President, no one should be in jail just for using or possessing marijuana. I`m announcing a pardon for all prior federal offense -- federal offenses for the simple possession of marijuana. There are thousands of people who are convicted for marijuana possession who may be denied employment, housing, or educational opportunities as a result of that conviction. My pardon will remove this burden on them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The pardon will affect over 6500 people across the country, none of whom are currently in prison. The President called on governors to make similar action for state offenses. Now, the last time we saw anything like this was back in 1977 when \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  pardon the Vietnam War draft dodgers. In addition to the pardons, President Biden announced a new review into how marijuana is classified by the federal government which helps determine charges and penalties.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The Federal Government currently classifies marijuana as a schedule one substance, the same as heroin and LSD and more serious than fentanyl. It makes no sense. So, I`m asking the Secretary of Health and Human Services and the Attorney General to initiate a process to review how marijuana is scheduled under federal law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Rescheduling marijuana is something advocates have been calling for years. This would be the beginning of looking into that process, so it is truly a historic day as the president takes a big step towards making good on his campaign promise to decriminalize marijuana. It`s been a remarkable evolution, we should say for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who was very much drug warrior earlier in his career.\par \par \uc2 In 1989, he delivered the Democratic response to an address by President Bush on anti-drug policy and argued it didn`t go far enough.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think the President has to join us in making a significantly greater commitment to these six areas to stem the rising tide of violence in America. And that`s what it is violence. First, we have to join together to ensure that drug dealers are punished swiftly, surely, and severely in line with what the President is calling for. We have to hold every drug user accountable because if there were no drug users, there would be no appetite for drugs, there`d be no market for them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Wild, right? Biden came to his current position on marijuana quite slowly and gradually over the decades as the negative effects of the drug war have become more and more apparent. And importantly, as the politics around drugs have changed significantly. According to Gallup polling, the percentage of Americans who think marijuana use should be legal has exploded from 23 percent back in the mid-80s to 68 percent as of 2021.\par \par \uc2 And so, with just over a month to go before the midterms, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has achieved the rarest thing in politics, a win-win that is good for people, good on the merits, good policy, and also good politics.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: With just 33 days left until the midterm elections, we`ve got a good sense the key races that will likely determine the balance of power in the Senate. All eyes of course are on Pennsylvania where Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has a real shot at flipping a Republican seat if he could beat New Jersey resident Mehmet Oz. In Wisconsin, the polls show a tight race between incumbent Senator Ron Johnson and his Democratic challenger Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes. Ohio is not expected to be very competitive, but it is now a toss-up between Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Of course, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been getting a lot of attention in Georgia where he`s trying to oust the incumbent Democrat, Reverend \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . And rounding out what I think have been the top tier of competitive races is Arizona where Democratic Senator Mark Kelly is being challenged by far- right election denier \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But then there are two kind of sleeper races that are bleeding in opposite directions, weren`t on people`s radars in the same way, but basically tied. First, the Senate race in Nevada which right now looks like Republicans` best chance to flip a Democratic seat currently held by \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 . The other is the race to replay -- the race to replace retiring \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2  in North Carolina. The race political professionals have not been paying much attention to but now looks indisputably tied.\par \par \uc2 Trumpist Republican Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is neck and neck in polling with former Chief Justice of the North Carolina Supreme Court Democrat Cheri Beasley who now has a rare opportunity to flip a Republican seat in that state.\par \par \uc2 And former Justice Cheri Beasley joins me now. It`s great to have you on the program. Let me first ask if you are surprised or not by where things stand in this race right now.\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D-NC), SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, Hi, Chris. You know, we`re really excited about where we are. We said all along, even as we were starting this race that we were prepared to win regardless who are challenger would be. We now know that it`s Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , who has been very much embroiled in the pettiness of partisan politics in Washington. And we`re really excited as we travel across the state -- we`re pretty big state with 100 counties, listening to folks and talking about constructive issues and really feeling a sense of energy and engagement in this race. People are really very excited about where we are. And so, we are really thrilled.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congressman Budd is one of the co-sponsors for the House version of a national abortion ban. It would allow abortion pass 15 weeks. Would you support such a ban or oppose one?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: You know, Congressman Budd is leading the charge on this national abortion ban. And he and other politicians like him are fully out of step with where folks are here in North Carolina and across this country. And what we know about this absolute ban on abortion that Congressman Budd supports is that there are no exceptions for rape or incest or risk to a mother`s health.\par \par \uc2 And what that means is for women who are sexually assaulted, that they will have to carry these pregnancies to term. For women who have miscarriages, that their bodies weren`t released, or ectopic pregnancies, or septic uteruses, women will not be able to get the life-saving treatment that they need, which is an abortion, and women will die. And that is absolutely unacceptable. I will fully support and fight to make sure that Roe vs. Wade becomes the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, I think several months ago this summer, it seemed very unlikely the Democrats could possibly retain both the House and the Senate. I think -- I still think it`s not the most likely outcome. But it seems a possibility, which would mean the possibility of actual unified democratic governance again. If you were a senator, a Democratic senator, serving with a Senate majority for the Democrats and a Democratic president, maybe even a House, what legislation would be top of mind, top priority for you to pursue?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: You know, as I`m traveling across the state, what`s top of mind here in North Carolina is folks are making sure that they have the jobs they need to take care of their families, and they are deeply concerned about rising costs. They want to know that the next senator is going to fight hard to lower costs. And my opponent, Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , has had every opportunity in his service in the last six years in Congress to fight hard to do that. But he`d much rather complain about it than do something about it.\par \par \uc2 And what we know is that he voted against lowering prescription drug costs while also taking corporate PAC money from drug companies. And he voted against lowering the cost of gas while taking corporate PAC money from all companies. And he`s not at all been invested in trying to make sure that people can have some relief and lowering costs. He`s really more consumed with his own interest, corporate and special interest, and not the interest of people here in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 What I would ask is for your viewers, for more information, to go to cheribeasley.com to know more about my candidacy and the successful race that we`re running.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2 , voted against the Inflation Reduction Act, which passed on a party-line vote. Would you have voted for the Inflation Reduction Act if you were serving in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: Well, I certainly would have. I mean, we certainly know that it is legislation that would allow us to focus on our Made in America economy, to make sure that we are focusing on manufacturing and tech industries which need to bolster our Made in America economy and to make sure that good talented folks here in North Carolina can produce the goods that we need here in our everyday lives.\par \par \uc2 It will certainly help to bring the cost of goods down. And we certainly know that in so many ways, inflation has been caused by rising prices and the and the linkage to the supply chain problems. And so, it is important that Congress and the Senate take constructive action to really get a handle on this. And that`s what people in North Carolina really want.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You`re a justice on this State Supreme Court in North Carolina. It`s interesting, judging and electoral politics tend to be parallel pursuits. There`s not a ton of cross-pollination between the two. What did you learn as a judge and a justice on the State Supreme Court that qualifies you to go serve in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: Well, I have served as a judge and Chief Justice of the Supreme Court of North Carolina for over 20 years. And I`ve had a real respect for the rule of law and for upholding the Constitution. And I know it`s important to make sure that folks apply the rule of law and that we have congresspeople and senators who are working hard to make sure we pass legislation that works for folks here in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 The IRA also allows Medicare to negotiate drug prices down and it does for seniors and caps insulin, but so many folks here in North Carolina need that kind of relief. And Congressman Budd, as we well know, has taken drug money and not voted in favor of making sure we lower drug costs for folks here in this state.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Final question for you, Governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  is very popular in North Carolina. He`s a Democrat in very closely divided state. I think his approval rating is on 60 percent. What have you learned from his popularity?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: Well, I`m very thankful that he`s been supportive of our race. But I also know that I`ve been in tough races before and North Carolina judges run in contested statewide elections. I`ve been elected twice statewide. And I know that we can do this. We`re seeing a lot of energy and support. And we`re a diverse state. We are urban and rural and it`s been really important to be in every single one of our 100 counties, it`s exactly what I`ve done, to make sure that we`re listening to and really make a commitment to understanding who we are as North Carolinians and my commitment to fight for all of us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Cheri Beasley, former Chief Justice of the North Carolina Supreme Court, Democratic Nominee for Senate in that state, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY\uc2 : Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221007eia600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Yamiche Alcindor, Jonathan Lemire, Kayla Tausche,  Shaquille Brewster, Ken Dilanian, Frank Figliuzzi, Erin McLaughlin \par \uc2 8,605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Today, Republicans are doubling, even tripling down in their defense of Georgia`s Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , knowing that winning his race is key to reclaiming control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . But every day that`s looking more and more dicey. At this hour, Walker is holding an event in Wadley, Georgia after spending the last four days denying allegations connected to an alleged former girlfriend`s abortion, a claim that directly undercuts his position as an anti-abortion candidate.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has not been able to confirm The Daily Beast`s reporting. But overnight, they published a stunning update, claiming that the woman behind the allegations is also the mother of one of Walker`s children. The Daily Beast also explains why it withheld details about her. "The woman, a registered Democrat, whose years-long relationship with Walker continued after the abortion, told The Daily Beast that her chief concern with revealing her name was because she wanted to protect her family`s privacy as best she could." Walker is still denying this story and says he doesn`t even know who the woman could be.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, RADIO TALK SHOW HOST: The anonymous woman has now also alleged that she had a child by you. Your response?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: I say the same thing I said that, you know, I know this is untrue. I know it`s untrue. And they keep telling me things like that and it`s totally, totally untrue. And I`m not sure why that would be told. I know nothing about any woman having an abortion. And so, they can -- they can keep coming at me like that. And they`re doing it because they want to distract people.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For national Republicans, the response hasn`t been to run away from Walker but to jump to his defense. In the last 24 hours, Republicans like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , Ted Cruz, and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , all of them coming out insisting the stories are part of a political smear campaign. But it`s not a completely united front. And today, cracks are starting to show. Here`s what Georgia`s Lieutenant Governor Geoff Duncan said just last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. GEOFF DUNCAN, (R-GA): Even the most staunch Republicans I think are rattled at the continued flow of information. I think every Republican knew that there was baggage out there and -- but the weight of that baggage is starting to feel a little closer to unbearable at this point. And if we`re being intellectually honest, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  won the primary because he scored a bunch of touchdowns back in the 80s and he was Donald Trump`s friend. And now we`ve moved forward several months on the calendar and that`s no longer a recipe to win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But other Republicans accused Democrats of pushing a false narrative so they don`t have to talk about inflation. A new NPR Marist poll makes it crystal clear inflation and the economy, are voters` number one priorities. And on that front, Democrats should be worried. Gas prices are climbing again after \uc2 OPEC\uc2  said it would cut oil production. Mortgage rates are at a 16-year high. And global CEOs even the United Nations are now warning the world may be on the brink of recession.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to dig into what the Biden administration might be able to do about all that and what it means for you in just a moment, also, jury selection underway right now in New York City in the civil trial of Oscar- winning actor Kevin Spacey. He`s accused of sexual assault against a teenager back when he was in his 20s, what`s at stake for Spacey and the Me Too movement in just a few minutes?\par \par \uc2 But we want to start in Georgia. NBC News Washington correspondent Yamiche Alcindor is there in the town of Wadley. She is the anchor and moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . Jonathan Lemire is the White House bureau chief for Politico, host of MSNBC`s "WAY TOO EARLY," and an MSNBC political analyst. And Doug Heye is a Republican strategist and former communications director for the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 . Good to see all of you.\par \par \uc2 So, Yamiche, we learned on Wednesday that even while Walker is playing defense, he`s still raising money, half a million dollars in the three days after the story broke. Talk about the impact all this has had on Walker`s campaign and on the race itself.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, I`m here in Wadley, Georgia, a deeply red part of Georgia and it`s here where \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has decided to have a campaign event today. It was supposed to start a little bit earlier. It`s now supposed to start in the next few minutes possibly in the next few hours. But what we know is that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is expected to say that he is going to continue to deny these allegations.\par \par \uc2 He`s going to say we`re told that -- at NBC News that Democrats are making this up because they want to stop him from becoming a U.S. senator. Just behind me though on this stage, he`s expected to come to talk to workers here at the sawmill and it`s of course the drip, drip, drip of information that we`ve continued to get that is impacting this race it`s really upended this race in some ways with a lot of voters talking about it, but not many voters changing their minds.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you. I talked to people who don`t like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , they told me they didn`t like him because he had domestic abuse allegations and that this is just more of the same that shows that he doesn`t have the character to be a U.S. senator. But then you have \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  fans who tell me that they are sticking by \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , either they don`t believe the claims, they believe this woman is lying, or they say -- some of them, that they believe the claims and they`re sticking with them anyways because now \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is an opponent of abortion rights and doesn`t believe that abortion should happen even in cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 That being said, as you said, the politics here are getting interesting. You have the lieutenant governor saying that this baggage is possibly getting unbearable, his words, for some Republicans here. But of course, national Republicans are sticking with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . He`s also fundraising off of this allegation.\par \par \uc2 But the Daily Beast says this woman who says that she`s a mother to Herschel -- to one of Herschel Walker`s publicly acknowledged children that she`s sticking by her story. She says that Herschel Walker`s denials are "ridiculous." So hopefully, we`ll get a couple of questions in \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  once he shows up, but I`ll be watching the stage behind me until then, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, we`re hoping to hear from him directly and having him answer questions from reporters. But, Jonathan, in the meantime, Walker`s position seems like it comes directly out of the playbook of the man who is behind him, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , deny, deny, deny, blame the other side. What we don`t know is to what extent it might be working. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. This is classic Trump that when confronted with scandal and controversy as a candidate in 2016 as president and then on the 2020 election trail, he would deny, he would never admit wrongdoing, he certainly wouldn`t apologize. That`s what Trump`s done. That`s what a lot of Republicans do now as well. And Walker here, despite -- if these reports are true, pretty overwhelming evidence is still not going to cede any ground. And to this point, it`s working, at least in terms of keeping Republicans by his side.\par \par \uc2 Yes, you play that sound from the Georgia lieutenant governor, but his voice is in the minority to be sure. Most Republicans are still staying with him. They still believe that he should win. And that`s because they`re valuing just the idea of one and one thing only, victory.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And to the point, Doug --\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: They aim to try to change the word minority --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Sorry. Go ahead, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: I`d say minority, in Mitch McConnell`s title. Yes, just to finish the thought. That they just simply want control of the Senate and want the word minority in set -- in Senate Minority Leader Mitch McConnell`s title to turn to majority leader.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, again, I apologize for interrupting you. To his point about that -- all of that, I want to play -- and the Republican sticking with him, I want to play what Ted Cruz told our own Vaughn Hillyard about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Do you stand by \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): I believe \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to be the next senator. I`m proud to support \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: But his own son has said that his father has lied about the denials, and then he left his family, then he threatened his family.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: You know what -- and Raphael Warnock`s family has got nasty. The people of Georgia are really unhappy with the path we`re on. That`s why they`re going to elect a Republican and it`s not going to stop to meet that --\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And you want them to elect that Republican?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Hell, yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And yet, Doug, we heard again, from that Lieutenant Governor, that Walker`s baggage is starting to become unbearable. What are Republican tell -- Republicans telling you about this race?\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Two things. One, they`re going to back \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as far as they can, if they think he can win. And part of that template is the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  template of the Access Hollywood tape. You know, that Friday night when that tape came out, most people myself included thought, well, this race is over for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And it wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that we see is if there`s a chance of working -- I`m hearing if it looks like this is going to sink him, the timeframe is very similar to 2002 where you had a very unpopular Democratic Senator Bob Torricelli in New Jersey who got yanked from the ballot for no other reason that he couldn`t win replaced by Frank Lautenberg, a judge signed -- judge signed off and Lautenberg won. So there is still that possibility, though times ticking on that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I wonder, though, about the topic, right? Abortion is so much in the political conversation right now, Doug. And is it for Democrats, as long as they can talk about that, as long as they can remind particularly suburban women, voters, what`s at stake, those moderate voters who maybe might any other situation go, either way, they look at that as a win or is that overstating it?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: You know, Chris, you learn a lot about politics by watching college football on Saturdays. I`m watching the North Carolina-\uc2 Georgia Tech\uc2  football game on Saturday. I saw Sherry Beasley the Democratic nominee for Senate run an ad after ad about abortion. \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , the Republican run ad after ad about crime and inflation. The candidates will tell you what they want to talk about and what they want voters to respond on. What we`re seeing is the polling is showing that the economy is becoming even more important of an issue now than it was say three, or four weeks ago. And as we see this news with gas prices with \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , it looks like that may be more and more the case. Georgia is obviously a special case in this circumstance.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, Jonathan. I did show that poll before. It says inflation is the top priority for voters. But preserving democracy is number two. We saw \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  out in Arizona yesterday campaigning against Republican candidates. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): In Arizona today, you have a candidate for governor in Kari Lake, you have a candidate for Secretary of State in Mark Finchem, both of whom have said, this isn`t a surprise, it`s not a secret. They both said that they will only honor the results of an election if they agree with it. If you care about democracy and you care about the survival of our republic, then you need to understand -- we all have to understand that we cannot give people power who have told us that they will not honor elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan, she told that she was going to do this. I mean, she`s now naming names. What`s her impact on the conversation or on any of the individual candidates that she might be talking about, is there one, do you think?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Well, first of all, it`s just striking that she is doing this once again, choosing patriotism over party. And that`s pretty unique in today`s Washington. And certainly, she is one of only a couple of Republican voices. \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , another who have stood up to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his big lie, both serving on the January 6 committee which is about to have its next hearing next week. It has just been announced. And now she`s playing this role on the campaign trail where she`s going right at candidates, big lie candidates who have -- who are election deniers, who have refused to concede that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost in 2020, and frankly, have not vowed to correctly certify the winner in upcoming elections if they were to take office themselves.\par \par \uc2 So it is -- it is remarkable for Cheney that she`s doing this. It turns out his impact remains to be seen as she is someone without much of a constituency nationally and certainly among Republicans. Let`s recall, she got routed in her primary over the summer back in Wyoming. That said some of these races including Arizona, the polls show they`re so tight that even if she just pushes a little bit, moves the needle a little towards away from that -- away from that big lie candidate, she can play a big role.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Doug Heye, thank you. and Jonathan, you`re going to stick with me.\par \par \uc2 HEYE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We want to get a gut check on one of those issues that`s critically important to voters that`s gas prices. This week, we learned that starting next month \uc2 OPEC\uc2  nations will cut international oil exports by roughly two million barrels a day. And Gas Buddy says today that could raise the price of gas more than a quarter per gallon. It would essentially reverse what`s been a sharp downtrend in prices since the middle of summer when prices spiked to over $5 and then leveled off around $3.67 in September. In fact, already today, the average is $3.87. That`s up 20 cents in a month.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in CNBC senior White House correspondent Kayla Tausche. So, Kayla, the president called OPEC`s move short-sighted and I know you spoke with \uc2 Brian Deese\uc2 , who`s the director of the National Economic Council about this. What can they do about it?\par \par \uc2 KAYLA TAUSCHE, MSNBC SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, there are a few things that they can do with Congress`s help. But, of course, the future of those policies is uncertain. And also the timetable is not something that would allow them to deliver on those policies before the election. Those are things like perhaps pursuing permitting reform, which you said the president has been discussing with West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , or even pursuing so-called NOPEC legislation that would penalize oil-producing countries that are members of \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , for the cartel that the administration has been so critical of. But that would all be a long way off. And the support within Congress is still unclear.\par \par \uc2 But what they said that they are still looking at very strategically, is releasing more oil from the emergency reserves, these underground salt caverns located in Texas and Louisiana. They`ve already released or plan to release up to 180 million barrels. Republicans have been pointing to the fact that those reserves are at or near 40-year lows. They say it`s a national security issue that the U.S. needs to have more energy, but they said, it doesn`t matter, it`s a strategic resource, and they`ll still use it and utilize it and draw down more of it if they need to.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s one key piece of a much bigger puzzle that comes against the framework of the global economic issues and CEOs around the world are predicting some sort of recession. What could that mean for Americans who we know are already struggling with things like food and gas prices?\par \par \uc2 TAUSCHE: Well, it could mean a lot less security. I mean, a lot of Americans have been looking at the stock market and seeing it slowly move downward throughout the course of this year. They`re feeling uncertain about their 401Ks, their retirement, the interest rate that they see on their houses. But there`s one piece of the puzzle that if we go into a recession would change and that is the job security that many Americans feel that`s been a hallmark of this economic recovery and that the administration has pointed to time and time again is the singular statistic that means that the U.S. is not in a recession.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 But just this week, we saw that there are 1 million fewer job openings than there were the last time that the Labor Department reported that so-called JOLTS data. Just today, we learned that there are 29,000 more people applying for unemployment insurance. And tomorrow, we`ll get another read on how many jobs were created in the month of September. And that data is going to be critical about where exactly the economy stands, Chris. So many Americans have felt empowered to leave their jobs in search of new and better jobs and higher pay, and that security might go away if we go into a recession.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What a head -- mind-boggling change.\par \par \uc2 TAUSCHE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, it really makes you snap back when you see how quickly things have turned. Kayla Tausche, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also got some breaking news for you, something NBC News scooped and has been reporting is now official. The next January 6 committee hearing is a week from today, October 13, during this hour, 1:00 p.m. This is likely the committee`s last hearing. It has not released any information about possible themes or witnesses.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is in New York State this hour touring \uc2 IBM\uc2 , set to speak on job creation next hour. We`ll have that for you when it starts. But first, will Biden`s trip to storm-ravaged Florida translate with voters and where recovery efforts in Fort Myers Beach stand right now? Plus, dozens of children killed in a shooting at a daycare center in Thailand, the latest on the suspect, including why he appeared in court just hours before that rampage.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Breaking news and it`s just a horrific story out of Thailand where at least 38 people are confirmed dead, including 24 children and the pregnant teacher. Officials there say a former police officer went on a shooting and stabbing spree at a daycare center. The suspect, while fleeing the scene, drove straight into people and shot bystanders before returning home to kill his own wife and child. Then he took his own life. Thai police confirm he had appeared in court earlier in the day on drug charges.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, Hurricane Ian is now the deadliest to hit the state in close to 90 years. That storm killing 131 people, a number that`s been climbing every day. President Biden took in the damage firsthand in hard-hit Fort Myers. Looking at scenes like this from the sky and on the ground, he and a potential 2024 Republican rival, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , put on a united front, one with a shared goal of helping people whose lives have been destroyed. Biden comforted people there directly vowing to help them fully recover.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Shaquille Brewster is in Fort Myers Beach for us. Jonathan Lemire is back with me. Shaq, what`s the biggest challenge for folks there right now, and what are you hearing from them about the president`s visit?\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, many of them are telling me they don`t expect to see -- feel the impact of President Biden`s visit for months to come because right now they`re focused on the recovery and the cleanup. And we talked about the cleanup in the Fort Myers Beach area, just look at this. Marina, for example, I spoke to the owner of this -- of this marina -- the co-owner, he`s been here for about 20 years.\par \par \uc2 You see the destruction here, boats under docks, docks on top of boats, you have them completely what he called for in a spaghetti formation. That`s how he`s going to have to undo this. And the ducks are not insured. So this is going to have to come out of his own pocket. And that is his worry right now figuring out how to clean this up and what are those next steps. I want you to listen to some of my conversations with him about the experience that he`s been having.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT HANSON, SALTY SAM MARINA OWNER: It`s just gut-wrenching.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: What does this past week be like for you?\par \par \uc2 HANSON: Difficult. I tell people, I`m in my second hurricane now. Just trying to get through all the debris and it`s hard. And, you know, I`m trying to figure out how we`re going to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: He`s had to rebuild before and now is stuck trying to figure out how he needs to rebuild again. And it`s not just here at work in his business, but he also has damage at his home. He got emotional again when he was talking about the fact that his wife and his four kids are at home. And when he leaves here, he has to worry about the cleanup in that area as well.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Chris, we are seeing some glimmers of hope here. You know, he told me during the interview that he doesn`t expect to get electricity or have power anytime soon. But as I walked over to do this live shot, we saw a power company right in front of his parking lot -- right in the parking lot of this marina starting to put up some of those polls. We don`t know if that will be power is restored immediately. But you`re seeing those glimmers of hope across the island. But you still -- it`s so clear, so abundantly clear how much recovery work there is for people here to do.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan, it really does boggle the mind. And you know these are times when state governments, federal governments are expected to step up. That`s what Biden was doing there, right? That`s why presidents go to places like that, to assure people that they will. Two things struck me and I want your impressions having covered so many of these kinds of stories.\par \par \uc2 One was there was almost a sigh of relief when you can see that two political rivals can actually have a common purpose, right, and put things aside for a day. But the second thing was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in his element. We haven`t seen as much of that I feel like and certainly through the pandemic, but meeting with people who maybe didn`t even vote for him and making that connection, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Yes. First of all, the bar is so low in our politics right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: That it is sort of a remarkable thing when politicians of opposite parties work together and they collaborate. That`s what we saw yesterday. And certainly, Governor DeSantis had some pretty political things to say earlier in the week claiming that the media wished the storm would hit Tampa so as to damage his poll numbers. But yesterday he set that aside. We didn`t hear any politics from President Biden either.\par \par \uc2 The two men were complimentary of each other. They pledged that the state and federal governments would work together to rebuild from this truly devastating storm. And that`s what the priority here is, of course, helping those in need. But of -- but of course, this is going to be viewed through a political lens. We all remember the hug then-Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  of New Jersey gave \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  back in 2012 after superstorm Sandy and how that hug was held against Christie when he ran for our president four years later.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 But there was none of that yesterday. No snow politics. The two men dealt with each other politely and even warmly at times. And you`re right to President Biden, this plays into his strengths when he is -- this is obviously a tragedy, you know, he certainly doesn`t seek this space of -- unlike any public figure speaks on grief and loss because he`s had so much tragedy in his own life. And he`s able to provide comfort to people in need. And also shared a few laughs shared a few smiles. He is a retail politician. He`s a tactile politician. And even those people there, they told reporters later who didn`t vote for him. They said they rather liked him.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan, thank you so much. Shaquille Brewster, thank you. I will come back to you, Jonathan. If we get more news out of as we`re waiting, obviously to see if the press gets a chance to talk to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meantime, this seditious conspiracy trial for members of the Oath Keepers militia group is resuming today. We`ve got the latest from inside that courtroom. And the key piece of evidence newly revealed by prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:01]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In the last 30 minutes, testimony in the seditious conspiracy trial for five members of the Oath Keepers militia group, focus on some ideas for disguises on January 6th. A former Oath Keeper just testified that one of the defendants, the group`s leader, Stewart Rhodes, wanted people to dress up. He suggested someone dress as an elderly person or a parent pushing a baby carriage with weapons concealed inside in order to attack members of Antifa or \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC`s Ken Dilanian.\par \par \uc2 Ken, what`s the latest from inside that courthouse?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Chris. Well, they`re on a break for lunch right now, but the prosecution called its second witness today. As you mentioned, a former Oath Keeper named John Zimmerman. He`s a 27-year Army vet who lives in North Carolina, and his main purpose seemed to be to give the jury an idea of how the group worked from the inside.\par \par \uc2 But Zimmerman really caught our attention when he testified that he believed Oath Keeper Stewart Rhodes may have had a contact within the Secret Service. Zimmerman says he heard one side of a conversation between Rhodes and someone Rhodes claimed was a Secret Service agent back in September 2020 before a Trump rally in Fayetteville, North Carolina. He said they were working through the parameters of how and where the Oath Keepers would operate.\par \par \uc2 Now prosecutors didn`t disclose whether the \uc2 FBI\uc2  tracked down who Rhodes may have been talking to, but obviously it would be a big deal if that turned out to have been an actual Secret Service agent. Zimmerman followed two days of testimony by the lead witness, \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent Michael Palian. Palian was actually at the Capitol guarding senators on January 6th and he became one of the supervising agents in the Oath Keepers investigation.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors used him to introduce recordings and text messages showing just how the Oath Keepers planned to use violence to stop the lawful transfer of power, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In the last session, Ken, prosecutors showed text messages that were sent by one of the defendants, Thomas Caldwell, three days before the Capitol attack. He was suggesting the idea of using boats to ferry weapons across the Potomac River writing, quote, "If we had someone standing by at a dock ramp, one near the Pentagon for sure, we could have our quick response team with the heavy weapons standing by, quickly load them and ferry them across the river to our waiting arms."\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s a pretty disturbing image. How central is that to the prosecution?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: It`s really just one example, Chris, of how prosecutors already have presented very compelling evidence from the defendants` own words about how and why they intended to stop Congress from counting electoral votes on January 6th. And that includes a tape of Stewart Rhodes after the 6th saying his only regret was that the Capitol attackers did not bring rifles with them and it also includes a text message from Caldwell who you mentioned saying that if they did have guns, they would have killed a hundred politicians, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ken Dilanian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Frank Figliuzzi, former assistant director for counterintelligence at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and NBC News security analyst. He`s here with me in studio.\par \par \uc2 First, can I just get your reaction to what we`ve heard so far?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Yes, this trial even as early as it is in the proceedings has already given us evidence that should shake most Americans on how close this came to even worse violence on January 6th. You have the recorded voice of Stewart Rhodes, the Oath Keepers leader, saying, as Ken said, my only regret is we didn`t bring those staged rifles right into the Capitol with us.\par \par \uc2 And now the provocation plans we`re seeing about trying to provoke the insurrection. Remember, you can`t defend yourself by saying we were just waiting for the president to invoke the Insurrection Act when you`re actually planning to trigger that very insurrection.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right. So there`s another key piece of evidence. It`s the Oath Keepers leader Stewart Rhodes again before the 2020 election in which he said, quote, "We aren`t going through this without a civil war." So immediately online mentions in the civil war go up exponentially, right. And they really went crazy after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Donald Trump`s Mar-a- Lago home.\par \par \uc2 The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " wrote about this ominous trend, quote, "Posts on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that mentioned civil war soared nearly 3,000 percent in just a few hours as Mr. Trump`s supporters blasted the search as a provocation. And then they also report the pace peaked at 15,000 mentions, 15,000 tweets an hour later that evening. From a national security standpoint, what should we make of this?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So this is a textbook example of language as a reflection of radicalization. The study of the use of language and phrases is no longer some arcane academic thing but rather something that should point us towards predictive analysis. In fact what I`m noting is the change from the use of the phrase culture war to civil war. The change and the shift from we have differences of opinion and beliefs on how to govern versus we`re going to have to act out physically and kinetically to get what we want accomplished.\par \par \uc2 That`s scary and what`s even scarier, Chris, is the provocation of such language and by leaders including a former president.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Can we talk about where we are as a country in terms of focusing on these kinds of online whether threats, plans, even just mentions? Back when I was in the Obama White House, there was a big focus frankly on a lot of homegrown terrorists. But when you look at this kind of thing, how do they look at this and how do they balance it against what we always have the argument about, which is free speech claims?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So the answer to your question is with great difficulty. It is a monumental task just for sheer volume and numbers alone to try and filter all of the potentially violent indications that occur online, number one. Number two, as you mentioned, we are understandably constrained because we have freedom of speech, freedom of assembly, we don`t police ideology. We almost have to wait for the violence to be articulated before someone moves in.\par \par \uc2 But, at least when we`re filtering and screening phrases like civil war, that is helpful to indicate where and when we should be concerned. But it is a task. We still don`t have a domestic terrorism law, Chris, that would allow the law enforcement intelligence communities to reach in and go, that`s a problem. Let`s get on that right now before something happens.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, that brings us to January 6th and one of those injured in the insurrection who is \uc2 Michael Fanone\uc2 , the former D.C. police officer. He has written a new memoir, and he describes in horrific detail -- we`ve heard some of it from him exactly what happened to him. But he also talks about the way some Republican leaders in the Senate reacted when he told them the terrible things that had happened to him.\par \par \uc2 He says Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was too busy to meet with him. Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , he writes this, quote, "At one point as I was describing the assault in the tunnel, noting that we were vastly outnumbered, Graham interrupted, you guys should have shot them all in the head, Graham said. We gave you guys guns and you should have used them. I don`t understand why that didn`t happen. Around the room, jaws dropped. I said, well, Lindsey, I appreciate your enthusiasm but there are rules that dictate what we can and cannot do when it comes to the use of deadly force."\par \par \uc2 From a law enforcement standpoint, what do you even make of that exchange?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: My lord. So there`s a complete misunderstanding and dismissal of rules of engagement and the use of deadly force that govern law enforcement. And so, can you imagine if that had been done? The restraint shown by the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  and the district, the Metropolitan Police, was admirable, but if they had gone outside their operating policies and used deadly force against this crowd, we would have had a massacre and we would be right back at the civil war language that we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 That could have prompted an incredible fracture in our society. So I think Graham is out of place here. Look, when we look back at this period of time in our history, we`re going to call out the leaders who refused to say something about what happened to these police officers at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Frank Figliuzzi, it`s been how long since you`ve been in the studio?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Since New York closed down.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s so good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: Sure.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And moments ago the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  campaign event in Wadley, Georgia, we showed you that, we mentioned it at the top of the hour, it finally got under way. He is now speaking to voters. And if he takes questions from the reporters there, we will go to it live.\par \par \uc2 On the front lines, Ukrainian volunteer soldiers who liberated the Kharkiv region from Russia are now recounting harrowing stories of battle. You`re going to want to stay tuned for this, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: When you see this, how are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Angry. Yes, angry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:49]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, opening statements are starting in the trial of a man accused of intentionally plowing through a Christmas parade last year in Wisconsin. The defendant, Darrell Brooks, is representing himself and he`s back in the courtroom now after the judge kicked him out this week for being so disruptive with moments like these.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE JENNIFER DOROW, WAUKESHA COUNTY, WISCONSIN: Mr. Brooks, we`re having issues because of you.\par \par \uc2 DARRELL BROOKS, WAUKESHA PARADE SUSPECT: I`m not going to let you intimidate me --\par \par \uc2 DOROW: Mr. Brooks, I`m not intimidating you. I`m --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: Because you can`t, and you see that.\par \par \uc2 DOROW: Well, then don`t --\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: You clearly see that you can`t intimidate me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today he sat for a long time with his back to the judge. She had to ask him to turn around. He is accused of killing six people and injuring dozens more. And if convicted could spend life in prison.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, we have a rare look inside a village just 10 minutes from the Russian border where a group of regular citizens just fought back the Russian army. Only months ago they were gardeners, farmers, but as they told NBC`s Erin McLaughlin, they traded in their normal lives to defend their country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): In Ukraine, the road to Moscow is littered with the remains of Russia`s failed attack on Kharkiv. Miles from the border is the now destroyed village of Ruska Lozova. A Ukrainian commander who goes by the call sign Carnet (PH) takes us into the forest and shows us where the Russians were holed up.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): What was this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s Russian stolen --\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: They stole it and put the Z on it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): And eventually pushed out following an intense artillery battle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In one hour, 240, 250 ammunition come here.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: It`s difficult to believe all this was liberated with the help of a brigade of Ukrainian civilians. What started as a group of 35 untrained men, in two months Carnet (PH) helped transform into a battalion of more than 200.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have guys who only want to fight. They don`t want the money. They don`t want nothing because they want to kill Russian because they want to protect country.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (on-camera): So basically, you`ve taken a group of people, a group of men.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. Yes. Civilian.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: That have never --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Been on the battlefield before?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No. They take their rifle like their automatic machine gun. (INAUDIBLE) and said, what`s this?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:05]\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): Including a farmer who says he loves to grow sunflowers, and a masseuse who goes by the call sign Buga. Buga shows us the dark and damp makeshift bunker where they endured relentless bombardment. It was scary for everyone, he says. I understood that I might die. Many already have. Some in this very forest.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Two days ago in this area, die my friend.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (on-camera): My god, your friend died?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: How?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mine. Three guys died.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: When you see this, how are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Angry. Very angry. I want to kill Russian, everything for what they do with my country and with my people.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): Carnet (PH) says his men are unfazed by Putin`s move to mobilize hundreds of thousands of reservists and the tens of thousands dodging Russia`s draft.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): When you see the lines of cars leaving Russia, of Russian men running, what`s your message to them?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You good guy. Go away. And do something for your country. I hope you never come here. Because if you will come, you will die. We fight not like soldiers. We fight like free people who fight on freedom. We fight for our freedom.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voice-over): Erin McLaughlin, NBC News, Ruska Lozova, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Our thanks to her and to all our reporters who remain there.\par \par \uc2 Actor Kevin Spacey back in court nearly five years after being accused of sexual abuse. A report from the courthouse and the precedent this could set for future trials, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s go to Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  talking to the media.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: -- any of the mothers of your children?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Did you ask why? Why not?\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Why do I need to?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Well, because, according to the article, one -- the woman who says that you paid for her to have an abortion is also the mother of one of your children. It seems like that`s an easy way to --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Because of the article, I had more kids. That`s why I didn`t reach out to anyone because I said no, and that`s what I mean. When I said no, that`s a lie, and that`s what I mean, that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: But if the woman said that she --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, let me go to her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: Thank you. You said that if this did happen, there is nothing to be ashamed of. How do square that --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Right, I never said --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: You said it this morning on --\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No, what I said -- I was talking about something totally different. It didn`t happen. I said, you know, it was my ex-wife, in my past, and nothing to do with what this woman said. I said this here, the abortion thing is false, it`s a lie, and that`s why I said, I said anything to happened to my ex-wife or what Christian was talking about, I don`t know. But as I said, if anything happened, there`s nothing to be ashamed of, my ex-wife and I have been the best of friends, with her husband and my wife. So that`s the thing that I`ve said, and I said nothing about if it did happen, because I said that`s a lie. Let me go to somebody else.\par 