ity to try to explain themselves.\par \par \uc2 And most electorates probably baked in. Republicans will stay with Republicans. Democrats, not, but in races that are going to be this close, and this one is expected to be very close, there is that thin sliver of independents, of undecided, who might break one way or the other because of debate, those people who need to be watching both campaigns will be courted.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You`re right. We all have to remember that as much as we follow this day in and day out even minute to minute there are people out there doing other things and who may be watching this debate for figuring out exactly why they may vote the way they do and in a tight race that could be all the difference.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, thanks for sticking around for the hour. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 And that is going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Now we have another live event we`re waiting for. In just minutes, President Biden is expected to speak in Poughkeepsie, New York, about creating jobs at a time when we just learned in a new poll inflation, the economy, number one on voters` mines. "KATY TUR REPORTS" takes over our coverage next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221007eia600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for October 05, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Glenn Kirschner, Paul Butler \par \uc2 7,179 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:19]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 I learned about the latest turn in the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  story by listening to you report it just a few minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": And what a turn.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, I mean, there was that information -- I`m just thinking of the number of times on video I have seen men on Fox asked \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  if he knows who the accuser is and him saying I have no idea, and those men on Fox accepting it instantaneously and fully.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: At face value. And it turns out, Lawrence, that the woman who had the abortion was the mother of one of his children. It is a damning piece of information.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, we`re going to get --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: It counters the apparent lies that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been spouting.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`re going to get some of it later in this hour.\par \par \uc2 Senator Elizabeth Warren is joining us. This was not something that we plan to talk about it, but it is central to the abortion issue in the Senate campaigns. And one of the Senate campaigners, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , in Ohio, will also be joining us later in the hour.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: It`s a great show, Lawrence. See you later.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, it was another very good day for the United States of America. In the appropriately titled case of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  versus United States of America, the Justice Department asked the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals to expedite their appeal of the case; and in a one page order, the appeals court said, motion to expedite appeal is granted.\par \par \uc2 A three judge panel of the appeals court has already issued a temporary order while the full appeal is pending. In which they completely agreed with every word the justice department has said, about the need to exclude the classified documents, seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  at Donald Trump`s winter home, from examination by the special master in the case, who was appointed by federal district judge, Aileen Mercedes Cannon, who is the only judge involved in this case, who agreed with the Trump lawyers about anything.\par \par \uc2 Appeals like this usually takes several months or more just for all of the briefs to be filed in the case, before the appeals court actually done schedules a hearing and then often several weeks after that, or months, finally before they issue a decision in such a case. But the 11th Circuit gave the lawyers in this case a deadline of five weeks from now for summiting all of the written briefs from both sides, quote, with no extensions allowed.\par \par \uc2 This is an indication that the Appeals Court is very confident that the issues in this case can be decided quickly. As they were in the preliminary decisions issued by the three judge panel, exactly one day after the final brief in that appeal was filed in the appeals court. That 29-page decision written in less than 24 hours agreed with everything that federal prosecutors said in their appeal. And not one word of what Donald Trump`s lawyers said about the case.\par \par \uc2 In a separate action in the case, yesterday, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lawyers appealed the 11th circuit preliminary ruling against them directly to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . A Supreme Court then gave the Justice Department one week to file a written response to the Trump appeal.\par \par \uc2 Harvard Law Professor Laurence Tribe who is a federal appeals expert who has argued and won several cases in the United States have been quick will join our discussion tonight to detangle the two appeals routes that have now opened up in the case. And also to consider Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson`s debut this week on the bench, in the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have learned a more about what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  found in their search of Donald Trump`s home when a sealed documents filed in the case by the Justice Department was apparently temporarily available by mistake in the court dockets before it was then results. They sealed the filing was entered in the case by the Justice Department on August 30th, before Judge Cannon was involved in the case. The document explains to the courts, the procedures that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  used during the search to protect any documents that could be subject to attorney client privilege.\par \par \uc2 The filing says, during the search, the privilege review team agents took a broad view of potentially privileged information, to include any documents to, from, or even referencing an attorney, regardless of whether the documents appeared to capture communications to or from an attorney for the purpose of seeking legal advice and regardless of who the attorney represented.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:10]\par \par \uc2 Attachments to the filing includes a list of possibly privileged documents with descriptions like, substitution of council in E. Jean Carroll versus Trump from manila folder marked "legal". The list also includes references to pardons such as internal pardon package before "IR", and "JC". Internal pardon package for "MB". \uc2 Rod Blagojevich\uc2 , commutation internal analysis.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department filing noted many of these materials do not appear to be privileged. The criminal trial is underway in Washington in the seditious conspiracy case against Elmer Stewart Rhodes and four other defendants who call themselves Oath Keepers. They are charged and the seditious conspiracy to attack Congress on January 6, when the Electoral College votes were being counted. The prosecutors in the evidence in the case which Rhodes said after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the election, quote, we got to be in D.C., you`ve got to make sure that Trump knows that you are willing to die, to fight for this country.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight are two former federal prosecutor, Paul Butler, and Glenn Kirschner, Paul Butler is also a law professor at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Also joining us, Bradley Moss, a national security attorney.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Kirschner, let me begin with you, and I want to come to a little later in our discussion about what`s been happening in that Oath Keeper trial, you`ve been in the courtroom. But you are reading of what we are in this granting of an expedited by the 11th Circuit.\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, so there`s good news and bad news, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 The good news is it looks like the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals is a distinguishing itself to be on to the task. They are meeting the urgency of the moment and it looks like they are right on when it comes to the substance. You know, a unanimous three-judge panel, two judges have been appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , are basically smacking down every argument Trump tries to make, and importantly, they are not playing his game. His game is always to delay and run out the clock.\par \par \uc2 That`s the good news. The 11th Circuit seems to be up for the task. The bad news is, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is the one who has the supervisory responsibility over at the 11th Circuit. And to put it generously, I think he is a wild card and the calculation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He has given the Justice Department a week to respond, so we know there`s not nothing`s going to happen for the next week.\par \par \uc2 Paul Butler, what is your rating of where the case stands with the 11th Circuit at this point?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, Lawrence, the issue is whether prosecutors can right now use these 11,000 documents that they got from Mar-a-Lago search warrants. And Judge Cannon says they have to wait until the special master`s done, but now the department is appealing because they say these documents shed a light when sensitive materials were stored at Mar-a-Lago and who might have had access to them.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s the same old story that Trump has all of these documents that are vital to national security, and that are important to a criminal case against him. And he, as Glenn said, is just doing everything he can to slow-walk the investigation and prosecution.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Bradley Moss, because the Justice Department was successful in the first round up this appeal, the investigation is now allowed to proceed unencumbered, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY MOSS, NATIONAL SECURITY ATTORNEY: Only with respect to those documents that have the classification markings. And as Paul noted, those other thousands of records do have some relevance here. If there is ultimately going to be an indictment that`s will likely be not just in the fact that there were the documents with the classification marking, but how they were intermix and intertwined with these other records, where they were located.\par \par \uc2 So while, yes, they are continuing on that classification sites, they need access to those other documents. They need to be able to question people about how those other documents got there to make decisions on a full and encompassing potential prosecution decision.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Glenn Kirschner, as a former prosecutor, where would this investigation be going right now? Would they have witnesses in the grand jury where they be putting documents in front of them and asking them about what they know about those documents?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Oh, absolutely, and they would also be submitting documents for forensic analysis, for example, because one of the ways you can tell who had possessed documents is to fingerprint them.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 Paper retains fingerprints. And we can lift latent fingerprints off of documents.\par \par \uc2 We can also test documents for DNA because when you touch them, you actually leave your epithelial cells, your skin cells on those documents. So they would -- the Department of Justice would be dug into this investigation, not just with witnesses, but with forensic testing, and analysis, and Judge Cannon really sort of threw a monkey wrench into that by these orders that seem designed to cater to Trump, and slow things down. But hopefully, the 11th Circuit will keep things moving forward.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Paul Butler, with the reporting on "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " this week, about indicating that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  packed the boxes himself that were sent to Florida, and was involved in that packing himself, and then, even more importantly, personally packed the boxes that were first sent back to the National Archives, where he then was trying to suggest that that was everything he had, even though we now know that he had some documents right in his desk drawer.\par \par \uc2 That new information is personable and may already be known by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the investigators, because those are conversations you can have witnesses that have nothing to do with what is actually in the documents. Did you see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you know, putting papers in a box?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: That`s exactly right. And we literally have Donald Trump`s hands all over evidence. So one issue is always going to be his intent, and on that level, we learned also that this week, or we learned this week that he directed a lawyer to lie, to say that all the documents had been handed over to the National Archives. And that just wasn`t true.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a problem in duty for the lawyer, the lawyer affirms something that he knew wasn`t true, the lawyer could get a locked up, he could lose his license. But if that lawyer says he actually thought all the documents had been handed over, is it because he believed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Any lawyer should know better than that!\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. And, Bradley Moss, part of what we`re reading in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting and elsewhere, is that there is so much to this investigation that can`t be accomplished now, that does not require specific reference to specific documents.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Yeah, absolutely. You would hope and expect that what Paul and Glenn mentioned, that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was already investigating not only with the witnesses, but with the forensics, to track down where things were put, by whom, and particularly to what extent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was personally involved in it.\par \par \uc2 And you have some new reporting tonight, coming out from Jason Leopold, that Trump`s designee in 2021 had confirmed that everything being shipped off to Mar-a-Lago was government property. They already conceded this point, whether or not Trump realized it or not, they already put that into writing.\par \par \uc2 So that again ties their hands and shows how much that as much as Trump wants to keep playing this charade with Judge Cannon and 11th Circuit, maybe these are personal records. No, he`s already lost this game. He`s just trying to drag it out like Glenn noted, and eventually, the circus going to come to an end here.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn Kirschner, you`ve been in the courtroom during this is seditious conspiracy trial. This is the most January 6 trial yet. What can you tell us about how it has begun?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, thus far, the prosecution has had a series of good days. Now, mind you, they are early in their case and chief, the lead \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent, special agent, Michael Palian, has just about completed his testimony. So, there`s a lot of trial yet to come.\par \par \uc2 But even though during opening statement, there was a really good moment for the prosecution, and perhaps ironically, some of them came during the defense opening, because I really enjoyed when Elmer Stewart Rhodes` attorney got up, and said, you know, if any of the Oath Keepers went into the capitol that day, and we know they did, they were quote, off mission.\par \par \uc2 And then he added, you know, if Mr. Rhodes had gone into the Capitol, while we might be looking at a whole different problem for him. What did he do? The leader of the Oath Keepers, Stewart Rhodes, threw his fellow Oath Keepers under the bus by saying they were off mission, but I never went in to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 So that was a really good moment for the prosecution. But it came during the defense opening statement, and people should know -- every coconspirator is responsible for the crime up every other coconspirator once you`ve entered in to the criminal agreement, whether you actively participated in the crime, whether you were present at the crime, or not.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, this is -- really a strong case thus far. But we have a lot of trial left to come.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:01]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If you were the mastermind of the bank robbery who had all of the plans, architectural plans in the bank, but you didn`t go into the bank, you`re still guilty.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Kirschner, Paul Butler, Bradley Moss, thank you all very much for starting off our discussion tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Harvard Law Professor Laurence Tribe will join us, next to consider the debut of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  this week on the Supreme Court bench, and to give us his experts view of the appeals process in the case of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  versus the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 Professor Tribe is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:13]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The newest member of the Supreme Court taught the court a history lesson yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE KETANJI BROWN JACKSON, SUPREME COURT: The framers themselves adopted the Equal Protection Clause, the 14th Amendment, the 15th Amendment, in a race conscious way. That they were, in fact, trying to ensure that people who had been discriminated against, the freedman, and during the Reconstruction Period, we`re actually brought equal to all everyone else in society.\par \par \uc2 The legislator who introduced that amendment said that quote, unless the Constitution should restrain them, those states should all, I feel, keep up with this discrimination and crushed to death the hated freedmen.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Professor Laurence Tribe who taught constitutional law at Harvard Law School for five decades.\par \par \uc2 Professor Tribe, before we get your reaction to Justice Ketanji Brown Jackson`s debut on the Supreme Court, can we ask you the detangled the threads for us in the now two tracks of appeals in the case of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  versus United States of America, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appealing directly to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , while the Justice Department has an expedited appeal now moving in the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals.\par \par \uc2 LAURENCE TRIBE, HARVARD LAW SCHOOL PROFESSOR: Well, I couldn`t blame people for being a bit confused. So just to go back to the origin of all of this, it was a sergeant seizure who was pursuant to a search warrant that was issued by Judge Reinhart, and not a word and anything that has been filed in courts by the Trump lawyers suggest anything illegal about that search and seizure. Or that any of the things that were searched and seized can`t be used.\par \par \uc2 So all of the rest is sort of advancing around the edges and counting angels on the head of a pin. The Trump lawyers did manage to get this Judge Cannon, who`s left field, basically tossed a hand grenade into the proceedings, by issuing an order, an early September, saying that there would be a special master, go over all of these documents, and in the meantime, the documents that were seized could be used only to protect national security, but not to conduct a criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 That made no sense. And the 11th Circuit made fast work of it, in an opinion that basically said without actually ordering her to get off the case basically said that she has no business being there. That decision is being challenged only in limited part in the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , in a filing that was made on the eve of Yom Kippur, last night.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that the president now is trying to say, even though -- you know, he`s not asking the Supreme Court to undo the decision of the 11th circuit, that the criminal investigation can go on, that`s what he really wanted to stop is simply asking him to undo the part of their order. That said the classified documents shouldn`t be reviewed before claims of privilege by the special master, Judge Dearie.\par \par \uc2 But Judge Dearie has already said he doesn`t even need to see them because based on everything in his court, he knows that those documents belong to the government, they are not privileged. They are not protected. So, that`s just a sideshow.\par \par \uc2 The real dispute is the dispute that the 11th circuit, which as you pointed, out the 11th Circuit decided to hear on an extraordinary expedited basis. It turned down the attempt by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to slow things down, and in that court, the Department of Justice is appealing the entire intervention by Judge Cannon, saying she had no jurisdiction, no business interfering, no business appointing a special master. And the arguments for that are pretty close to slam dunk.\par \par \uc2 So it really looks like just as we have already seen, even judges appointed by Trump don`t buy his crazy arguments that looks like this is tempest in a teapot. And it looks like once this special master is removed from the case, which I think it`s going to happen sooner rather than later, they`ll be ready to indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for obstruction of justice, and for mishandling and in fact stealing, highly classified documents, and then keeping them in an insecure place, in a violation of among other things, the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:13]\par \par \uc2 Sort of seeing all of that coming down, he does what he usually does, and that is try to sort of kick up some dust and throw some sand in the gears. But in the end, these gears are moving forward, whether he likes it or not.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Should people be concerned that Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is the point person for the Supreme Court on this filing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made directly to the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Well, as it happens, he is the circuit justice for the 11th Circuit. But whatever else you may think of him, he simply doesn`t have the power, as a solo justice, to interfere with this criminal investigation. If he is inclined, I mean, so far, he`s insisting that the briefing go quickly, and setting a deadline next Tuesday, at 5:00 p.m. Or the response brave, he`s acting in a perfectly judicious way.\par \par \uc2 Before he actually did anything, to favor \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he would surely have to go into the full court. And I see no reason to think that the court is going to do Trump any special favors there.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: His arguments are just -- are just empty.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And that full court now includes Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . What was your impression of her debut on the bench this week?\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Well, I was frankly really thrilled. I wasn`t surprised because I`ve known her for years, and thought she was really brilliant and articulate and historically sophisticated.\par \par \uc2 But she used the opportunity of the oral arguments to conduct what is really a master class in the original meeting of the 14th Amendment. You could just see her addressing the conservatives on the courts. You want originalism, she read them the original history. The 14th Amendment was not passed in order to make the constitution color blind, but to provide a basis for legislative action that would take race into account in order to undo the lingering affects of slavery and racism.\par \par \uc2 To people that wrote it, she explained to both her colleagues and to the nation, that was listening, the people who wrote it, she read from the reports, the people who wrote it were in favor of freedmen`s bureau applied not only to former slaves, but to African Americans, to give them an equal opportunity.\par \par \uc2 Rather than just going around the edges the way some of the justices did, and even the U.S. solicitor general, she went to the absolute heart of the case. And the way these oral arguments are conducted, in a modern court, the justices are really talking not so much to the lawyers, but to one another. She staked out her position, made it`s a very powerful. And it`s going to make it much harder for the court to do what I think is unfortunately predetermined to do, and that is to still cut down even further the effective use of the Voting Rights Act, to undo racial gerrymandering without even -- without even giving the minorities a full equal chance to be represented.\par \par \uc2 But she is making a record for history, and basically teaching the country and in the process, making it much harder for the courts to pursue its agenda of dismantling the process of desegregating America.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Laurence Tribe, thank you very much for sharing your expertise with us again tonight. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Ad next, we turn to former Harvard law professor, Senator Elizabeth Warren, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:33:41]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don`t understand the connection between opinions that people disagree with and the legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: How about the connection between conflicts of interest and the legitimacy of the court? Every federal judge must comply with the judicial code of ethics, except the nine members of the most important court in the land, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has not said a word about the most glaring public conflicts of interest the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  has ever had in its entire history.\par \par \uc2 And that is Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  ruling on a case that could involve his wife. When \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was the only member of the Supreme Court to disagree with the court`s decision that the Trump White House communications had to be handed over to the January 6th Committee, we did not yet know that those communications could include text messages from Clarence Thomas`s wife to then White House chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since that decision, we had discovered that Virginia Thomas was texting the White House, urging on, any attempt to overthrow the presidential election, long after there were any legal options left for contesting that election.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:58]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And early in the process, Virginia Thomas texted \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  saying, "The Biden crime family and ballot fraud co-conspirators are being arrested and detained for ballots fraud right now, and over coming days, and will be living in barges off Gitmo to face military tribunals for sedition."\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Thomas is a lawyer and she actually wrote those things. Since those text have become public, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has not said one word about the obvious conflict of interest that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has in any case involving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In fact, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has never said a word about how to deal with conflict of interest at the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Our next guest has a plan for that. Joining us now is Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts. She`s a member of the Senate Finance Committee, the Senate Banking Committee, and is a co-sponsor of the Judicial Ethics and Anti-Corruption Act.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Senator Warren.\par \par \uc2 What would you say to the chief justice about the connection between legitimacy of the court and conflict of interest.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): That the court`s legitimacy is derived from the belief of the people of the United States, that this court will make decisions based on the rule of law, and not on personal entanglements. That`s why we have judicial ethics. And that`s why it is that conflicts of interest and even the appearance of impropriety causes a judge to recuse himself or herself from any decision.\par \par \uc2 Look, I want to pass the Judicial Ethics Law. But to do that we got an election coming up. And that means we got to have a majority in the House, we`ve got to have a majority in the House. We`ve got to have a majority 0big enough in the Senate to rollback the filibuster, to make the changes we need to make.\par \par \uc2 So every time you feel frustrated about what`s happening in the Supreme Court, every time you want to shake your fist, every time over the next 34 days that you want to throw things at the television, or tear up whatever your news feed is. -- stop and say, what can I do to influence the outcome of the election in November?\par \par \uc2 Because what happens in that election in November is going to profoundly affect not just what happens in the Supreme Court, but how much power the Supreme Court has, to determine our lives in so many other ways.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And you need more Democratic senators than just the 50 you have in the Supreme Court now.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You`re going to be followed in these program by \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  who in the latest poll is running three points ahead of his Republican opponent J.D. Vance --\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Go get them Tim.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia tonight, we have a Senate race that has been hit with something we`ve never seen before. And I wasn`t planning to ask you about this, but it`s been breaking news of the night that the Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been denying that he knows who the woman is, who emerged this week, saying that he paid for her abortion. She has revealed tonight that she is actually the mother of one of his children. And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has not yet responded to that.\par \par \uc2 But what we are seeing here is a Republican candidate who is involved in a story like this, while at the very same time, saying that all abortions in Georgia and America, all of them should be banned.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: So think of it this way again. This is about who the two candidates are in Georgia. This is about what the two parties are right now all across this country.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have made clear that if they can get enough 