e latest poll. Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:43:30]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In the all important campaign for control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  that Elizabeth Warren was just talking about, Democrats have some good news. In the latest poll of the Ohio Senate race, our next guest, Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is polling 3 points ahead of Donald Trump`s candidate J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won Ohio in the last election by eight points, which means \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has to convert some Republican voters to win.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 , (D-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: In the last two congresses, I`ve been running in the top 10 percent of most bipartisan members of congress. That`s the reality and J.D. is an extremist, you know. I mean he is that extreme wing of the extremists. And that is frustrating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  of Ohio. He`s a candidate for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  there. Congressman thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I hope you just heard about what Elizabeth Warren was saying about the importance of all the Senate campaigns and in particular yours. It`s a statistical tie there. You`re running three points ahead in the latest poll. There were recent polls showing, you know, Vance one point ahead of you. It`s all very tight there.\par \par \uc2 What are you expecting and what is your campaign plan in these closing weeks?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, people are recognizing, Lawrence -- and thanks for having me -- people are recognizing that J.D. Vance is an absolute extremist. He wants a national abortion ban. He`s called rape inconvenient. He`s told women who are in violent marriages that they should stay in those marriages for the sake of family values. I mean this is somebody who is so extreme and doesn`t connect with Ohioans.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:49]\par \par \uc2 RYAN: And what`s going to happen in the next four and a half weeks, we are on the ground. We`ve hit all 88 counties in the state. We are going to rural areas, working hard in red counties. And we`re getting a tremendous response.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a lot of two-time Trump voters that are coming our way. We`ve got a lot of Republicans coming our way. Rob Portman`s former chief of staff, a statewide official Jim Pietro (ph) Republican, all on board with a big contingent of Republicans saying we`ve got to go with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I mean this is the moderate wing of the old Republicans that you remember. That`s who`s here in Ohio, and they`re on board with us which is why you`re seeing those poll numbers, and why we are going to close this thing out and we are going to win the seat and shock the world with this race.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What are you telling Ohio voters about what the Biden administration and the Democratic Congress, Democratic Senate has delivered for Ohio?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, you are feeling a lot of what I have been advocating for my entire career on the ground here. The infrastructure bill is going to create 600,000 jobs here in Ohio. It`s going to make sure we get broadband, clean water, new bridges. That Brent Spence Bridge in Cincinnati, you know.\par \par \uc2 We are going to get these things funded, and J.D. was against this even though Rob Portman, the moderate Republican Senator in Ohio, helped put all this together.\par \par \uc2 The Chips Act landed the Intel project, going to be $100 billion investment by Intel and other companies. Average wage there $135,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 We are building electric trucks, electric cars, electric tractors, battery plants, solar panels. The announcements right after the Inflation Reduction Act have been unbelievable and that`s the next generation of manufacturing that we are doing right here in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 And people are going to vote their future, not the past and that`s where J.D. is. So we are pumped about it, I think people are really excited and they want someone`s who`s going to fight for them in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . They know that`s \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is your reaction when you see your opponent J.D. Vance get publicly humiliated by the guy who chose him to run, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says to Ohio voters that J.D. Vance kisses my, you know -- says that publicly about the candidate that he picked to run in Ohio. And J.D. Vance just takes it. I`ve never seen anything like that in a Senate candidate before.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thought Ted Cruz was the biggest you know what kisser in politics but it`s clearly J.D. Vance. I don`t know anybody I grew up with, Lawrence, you know, in Ohio that wouldn`t be that humiliated in front of the entire world, be called an ass-kisser and then ten minutes later get back up on stage and grip and grin with the guy who just humiliated you.\par \par \uc2 That`s not how Ohioans are built. We want -- we want an ass kicker from Ohio, and that`s what I`m going to do.\par \par \uc2 And look, we are building a grassroots effort, because people can smell the phoniness from the sky. We`ve got 350,000 low-dollar donors. We got this guy on the run. He won`t even be seen in Ohio. He`s afraid to meet with people in Ohio, one because he`s kind of lazy, but also he knows he`s so extreme that he can`t connect with Ohio voters.\par \par \uc2 So we are going all over the state. He`s not anywhere. He`s even afraid of letting some of the people who voted for Trump because he was so against Trump, like this guy`s all over the board.\par \par \uc2 We are asking people to help us win this race. This race just got called, move from lean Republican to toss-up. We`ve asking people to help us the last four weeks. Go to TimforOH.com. we are going to surprise the world here in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 And it`s going to be a great thing for the Democratic Party when \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  are shoulder to shoulder on behalf of working class people from the great state of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be an exciting time, and I think a whole new version of the Democratic Party is going to come to fruition because of what we are 0doing here in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: How did \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  statewide in Ohio -- a state that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins by eight points? And what can you learn from the \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  path to statewide victory?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Well, I worked with Sherrod very early on when I was a young member of Congress and he was in Congress. This day and the economic issues have to be the focus, right. It`s the trade agreements, it`s making sure we have fair trade, not just free trade.\par \par \uc2 It`s making sure that corporations can`t just get away with offshoring jobs. It`s not just about tax cuts for the top 1 percent, it`s about tax cuts for working people and small businesses.\par \par \uc2 And those bread and butter economic issues are the issues in Ohio, and Sherrod figured that out. I understand that in my bones because of where I grew up and how I grew up.\par \par \uc2 It`s the price of drugs, it`s the prescription cost, it`s insulin, it`s the opiate epidemic, it`s health care costs, schools for our kids, robust joint (ph) vocational.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:57]\par \par \uc2 RYAN: It`s about protecting unions and making sure that they have the right to organize and lift everybody up. Sherrod figured this out and we are running a very similar campaign. I`m a different guy than Sherrod but a very similar campaign.\par \par \uc2 And when you run an economic campaign, you know, even like Obama did against \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , right. If you run and economic campaign in Ohio, you win Ohio. And that`s why we`re going to win.\par \par \uc2 J.D. Is running around with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and the extremists, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and calling for a national ban on abortion. And I`m talking about jobs and wages, pensions and health care, and making sure that our kids have a good future, and actually building things in Ohio again.\par \par \uc2 That`s such a distinct difference and that`s why you`re seeing the poll numbers that you`re seeing. And that`s why ultimately we`re going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , three points ahead in Ohio. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. We always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Thanks Lawrence. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:59]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I won`t be here tomorrow night. My friend Ali Velshi will be here in my place. I will be celebrating the birth of Saturday Night Live legend Jim Downey, one of the show`s longest serving writers who appeared on this show some years ago with his friend \uc2 Bill Murray\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I will be back here on Friday night at 10:00 p.m. for Friday`s edition of THE LAST WORD. So I`ll see you here Friday at 10:00.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221006eia500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for October 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez \par \uc2 8,088 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: I won`t be here tomorrow night. My friend, Ali Velshi will be here in my place. I will be celebrating the birth of Saturday Night Live legend Jim Downey, one of the show`s longest serving writers who appeared on this show some years ago with his friend \uc2 Bill Murray\uc2 . I will be back here on Friday night at 10 p.m. for Friday`s edition of THE LAST WORD. So, I`ll see you here Friday at 10. THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the former president loses in the federal appeals court again in the Mar-a-Lago documents investigation. And new reports about how those classified documents ended up with the former president.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the new worry for Election Day as dangerous rhetoric rises online and her talk of civil war spikes with the former President`s actions.\par \par \uc2 Then new reporting about Georgia`s Republican Senate candidate and the woman who says he paid for her abortion. The very important detail could force him to change his story again, as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, I`m Alicia Menendez in for Stephanie Ruhle. There has been yet another legal setback for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as he tries to stall that widening Justice Department investigation into his handling of classified records, some of which were seized during the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 Today, an appeals court said it would speed up consideration of the Justice Department`s requests to stop an outside review of the documents found at the Florida club. The former President pushed for that review, which was signed off on by a judge he appointed. Today`s order comes the day after Trump asked the Supreme Court to get involved in this case, as he faces the possibility of federal charges, Trump has yet to explain why he held on to the documents. And today he remained defiant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Everyone knows we`ve done nothing wrong. They are targeting me because they want to silence me, silence you and silence are amazing Make America Great Again movement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Trump had insisted that one reason classified records were found at Mar-a-Lago was because government workers packed up boxes taken from the White House after his 2020 defeat. But emails obtained by Bloomberg News indicate those boxes are packed before federal workers arrived at the White House. As the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has reported about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it said he and others put briefing books, gifts, news clippings, and other possessions into boxes.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the current President and First Lady Jill Biden spent much of today in southwest Florida, getting a closer look at the devastation left by Hurricane Ian. And the trip brought the President face to face with one of his most vocal critics and potential 2024 opponent, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: We were very fortunate to have good coordination with White House and with FEMA. One of the things that you`re seeing in this response, we are cutting through the bureaucracy. We are cutting through the red tape, and that`s from local government, state government all the way up to the President.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: I think he`s done a good job. He`s -- look, I called him, I think, even before he called me, when I heard this storm was on its way. We`ve worked hand-in-glove.\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to leave. We`re going to see you through this entire process and it`s going to take a hell of long time. And I think the one thing this has finally ended is a discussion about whether or not there`s climate change and we should do something about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The President could soon have yet another crisis on his hands today the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  plus energy cartel announced its biggest production cuts in more than two years in hopes of raising oil prices. \uc2 OPEC\uc2  says it will slash production by 2 million barrels a day. The price of crude is already going back up and that could mean higher prices at the pump, just as voters are about to head to the polls on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s bring in our leadoff panel. NBC News Correspondent Carol Lee, Luke Broadwater, Pulitzer Prize-Winning Congressional Reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , he`s working on an edition of the January 6 reports, going to be released later this month. And former U.S. Attorney Joyce Vance who spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor. She`s also a law professor at the University of Alabama. It is good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, I want to start with you, was the 11th Circuit Court`s quick decision in favor of the DOJ a surprise here?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: It wasn`t much of a surprise and it also wasn`t all that quick. You`ll remember the first time that they encountered this case when they were asked to pass on an earlier motion. They actually filed an order late on a Saturday evening, this time they waited a little bit longer before they ruled but ultimately, they decided to expedite the case along the lines that DOJ had requested and this is a very clear case where the issue should be expedited. There is no factual development, no extensive record that needs to be reviewed. The parties have already briefed the legal issues in the lower court.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:12]\par \par \uc2 And Alicia, it`s important to remember the context here, which is that this is an ongoing criminal investigation, which has now been slowed down because the former president has filed this very unusual civil lawsuit entirely appropriate and not very surprising for the 11th Circuit to expedite it.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, Joyce, let me ask you a question about timeline here because the Wall Street Journal spoke to a former DOJ Mueller investigator who said, the latest order could, "result in the DOJ more quickly obtaining the 1000s of seized documents that are critical to their case." So, how long could it be before federal lawyers and get their hands on these records?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It`s difficult to say here, because you have the two different matters proceeding one now in the 11th Circuit, one in the Supreme Court. To some extent they`re intertwined. There is perhaps the suggestion that the Supreme Court could wait and see how the 11th Circuit dispenses with this issue, and then decide that Trump`s petition to them has been mooted and declined to hear it any further. That would mean that the calendar in the 11th Circuit would be controlling.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll just say having been an appellate chief in that circuit and litigated a number of cases, they can move very quickly, when they want to. They can expedite oral argument. They can turn a decision around very quickly. This is a circuit that considers a lot of capital murder case appeals. So, they are very used to acting promptly when there is a need to, but they can also act very slowly, and sometimes they`re not in a hurry to turn around an opinion. We`ll have to see where they land here.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Luke, there`s all of this and then there`s the reporting knocking down Trump`s claims about who packed the documents that went to Mar-a-Lago. What is the significance of this new information?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Sorry, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  came out today with some good reporting about the GSA and that the GSA says on the record, that they had nothing to do with packing any boxes that went to Mar-a-Lago, that they were involved in shipping boxes, but not at all packing. And so, this undercuts one of the claims that former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and one of his allies, Kash Patel was making.\par \par \uc2 And it`s just the latest claim that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made about the Mar-a- Lago boxes that have been either undercut or he`s since backed off of you`ll remember that he at first said that, well, President \uc2 Obama\uc2  mishandled classified documents to and then the archives shot that down. He said that the documents may have been planted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He`s since stopped saying that he first claimed that the judge was biased against him. He`s stopped saying that as well. So, you know, one claim after another, keeps getting shot down. And this is just the latest one.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yeah, I mean, just we`re accustomed to him sort of workshopping rationales for his actions. But I have to ask you, as someone who`s actually tried cases like this, what are prosecutors thinking now that they have this information, that the President had his hands on these documents?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: This increasingly becomes a very compelling case for prosecution. Not every case where someone leaves government and retains classified materials as a case for prosecution. Usually, there has to be a plus factor, someone who tried to destroy these items, someone who handled them so carelessly, that they became available to others, or even directly sold them to others, there has to be that plus factor.\par \par \uc2 And with this ongoing effort to obstruct the investigation, and with increasingly what can only be, I think, fairly characterized as the former president lying about what happened here, the pressure will mount on DOJ to act consistently and to treat Trump like they would anyone else in this sort of a situation and let this result in a prosecution.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: You know, Carol, I am sort of struck watching all of the images out of Florida because there is so much devastation, there is so much tragedy, you see the president Governor DeSantis, sort of acknowledging that we`re in a different moment here. This is not the time for politics, and yet the politics of this for them both are complicated.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Very complicated. They`re obviously big rivals, they have a number of disagreements on any number of issues, whether it`s migrants, the COVID vaccine. You know, DeSantis is somebody who`s been aligned with former President Trump. All of that was pushed aside. They had to acknowledge it because it was hanging out there and they were asked questions about it. But you saw both of them praising each other, thanking each others, that you heard the President say they`re working hand in glove, you heard Governor DeSantis say that this is something that he was grateful for all the help and so it was a team effort and it was a very interesting moment because the President was supposed to be in Florida last week, rallying Democrats, including DeSantis` opponent, you know, former Governor Crist.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:17]\par \par \uc2 And so, they`re -- but they are trying to navigate this landscape here. And it`ll be interesting to see what I found interesting is that Governor DeSantis seems to be able to do, something that President Trump couldn`t do. People liken him to Trump that they have similar demeanors, similar policy positions and all those things. But President Trump really had a difficult time to put it mildly, putting politics aside in moments like this. And you know, he was always seeing somebody who`s who was up, who`s down and what it was in it for him. And you didn`t see that, at least in this moment. How these ages will be very interesting.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Absolutely, how it ages. The big question, Luke, we`re about a month out from the election, you`ve been reporting on threats to members of Congress. What is happening?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yeah, the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  are preparing for a surge in threats. We`ve seen a steady increase in threats against lawmakers, really since the time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came on the scene nationally over the past five or six years. And last year, they were up to about 10,000 threats a year against members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 And if you talk to these congressional offices, it`s just horror story after horror story. I mean, you have people bringing a gun to a congresswoman`s house, you have someone smashing in senator`s window. You know, the environment right now is very dangerous. And every time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  or someone else goes out publicly, and says something like death, like they must have a death wish or, you know, Democrats are conspiring to murder Republicans and use this kind of heightened rhetoric, we often see an uptick in these threats. So, we expect that to perhaps intensify as the midterms get closer, and a lot of people are on very high alert right now.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: We`re going to talk in the next block with \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  about some of those threats and what is really fueling them, because I want to ask you about a different element going into midterms, and that is gas prices, which today is not good news for the administration. Honestly, not good news for the country. You know, the latest news that the administration is considering easing sanctions with Venezuela`s part of their efforts to curb this, which is a politically complicated thing for the White House to be considering, what are you hearing from them about where they go on this?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Like, they are very upset about this. They didn`t mince words today in their statements about it, saying that this is \uc2 OPEC\uc2  acted in their own self-interest, that this was a mistake, misguided, short sighted pick the word they use all of them. This is worse than they expected. And it comes after a lot of intense lobbying to try to keep this from happening. It`s raising questions about what President Biden got out of his trip to Saudi Arabia that he was so criticized for.\par \par \uc2 And look, the bigger problem is what you point out, for the President, politically, this comes right before the midterms. But he`s also decided to own gas prices. He`s taking credit for when they`re going down. So is he going to then have to take credit for when they`re going back up. And that`s something that the White House is going to have to contend to, they`re saying they`ll go to Congress to try to neuter some of OPEC`s control over oil prices, they`re going to release some more strategic petroleum from the strategic petroleum reserves, things like that. But this is -- this is not very good. And what they were saying, administration official I talked to you tonight is, look, it could have been worse. Well, that`s true, but it`s pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yeah. And you also have members of Congress now pressuring them to consider reducing military to the region?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yeah, all kinds of fallout in terms of how the president engages with foreign countries, how -- what the relationships are, there`s already that he`s under such pressure about the U.S. relationship with Saudi Arabia, this just adds to that pressure, and other countries. And, look, they`re going into a winter where oil prices, energy prices are going to be a real concern in Europe and elsewhere. And this is something that they don`t need, particularly the timing right before the midterms, the only timing that could be worse as maybe if it was before the 2024 election when President was on the ballot. But you know, they`re going to try to do what they can and like I said, they essentially have decided to own gas prices and say that the President can make them go up and go down. And so, he`s going to have to contend with that.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Joyce, well, we follow the legal back and forth in the classified documents investigation, the former president is getting the Supreme Court involved. His request went to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who`s given the DOJ a week to respond, what are you watching for next?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, there`s nothing unusual to read into this situation. Justice Thomas is our Circuit Justice in the 11th Circuit. The Supreme Court allocates one justice to each of the circuits and they try to keep them in the area that they come from. Justice Thomas is from Savannah Georgia which is in the 11th Circuit, which is why he looks at our cases as they come up.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:09]\par \par \uc2 One suspects that the likely outcome here is that the former president`s motion will be handled on the shadow docket. It would be very unusual if Justice Thomas handled this on his own, an issued an in chambers opinion, far more likely that this goes to the full court and we get some form of a shadow docket decision. But one suspects as well that the Supreme Court is not in a hurry here and could even wait to see the outcome of the 11th Circuit matter before they decide to act.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Look, it strikes me given everything that we have talked about that there is so much in the atmosphere right now. There are the former president`s legal troubles. There are gas prices and economic concerns, there is this rising level of threat against lawmakers. When you look forward to the midterms, which of those atmospherics resonates most with voters?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yeah, I think we`re going to see a sort of competing priorities. The Republicans are very interested in trying to drive this election about the state of the economy and about inflation and gas prices. So, this action, you know, today could actually, you know, a lot of Democrats see sort of a conspiracy here, where the foreign nations are in league with the Republicans to hurt their chances at the midterm and hurt President Biden. You know, I think Democrats though, abortion, is going to be just a huge issue, probably the biggest issue out of all of them. And I think that is going to drive voters to the polls for the Democrats. It still does look like from all the polling that Republicans have the edge in the House, and but that the Democrats have right now the slimmest of leads into holding the Senate. And there`s still some time to go. So, we`ll see which of those issues, voters care about more.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: There is still some time to go but we are closing in here. Carol Lee, Luke Broadwater, Joyce Vance, thank you all so much for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, how serious is the dramatic spike in online chatter about another civil war here in America? Homeland Security Expert is standing by. And later, the abortion story for the Georgia Senate Republican candidate got even more complicated tonight. The new report about a woman who says \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for her abortion. New details as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on a Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:22:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON VAN TATENHOVE, FORMER OATH KEEPER SPOKESMAN: I do fear for this next election cycle because who knows what that might bring, if a president that`s willing to try to instill and encourage to whip up a civil war amongst his followers, using lies and deceit and snake oil.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Here`s the thing, the possibility of another American Civil War used to be a fringe talking point among online right-wing extremists like the Oath Keepers he wants represented. But the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  puts a huge jump in that kind of talk following the completely illegal search of the former president`s Florida club. "Posts on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that mentioned "civil war" had soared nearly 3,000% In just a few hours as Trump supporters blasted the action as a provocation. Similar spikes followed, including on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , Reddit, Telegram Parler, Gab and Truth Social, Trump`s social media platform."\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , a West Point graduate, Army veteran, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent and a distinguished research fellow at the Foreign Policy Research Institute. Clint, can you just put some context around this for us. Exactly, what are they saying when they`re calling for a civil war?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Yeah, I think, Alicia, there`s some categories that you can put this into, you know, there`s a spectrum, much like we saw on January 6, there`s a spectrum of groups and individuals that will show up to any event, they maybe don`t know each other before they get there. But they come from the same online spheres. And I think that`s where we see a lot of this activity.\par \par \uc2 With the militia violent extremists, we will see them talk of revolt, they`ll see them bring up Civil War, that`s been a pretty consistent banner. But I wouldn`t have to say that the organization around that is pretty light. What is more concerning that thing is a lot of the younger generation, particularly known as accelerationist, their whole goal, than the idea of acceleration is to bring out or stoke a race base, or a class based civil war and to provoke that conflict. And I think that`s where the real danger comes in, is, the greater the frequency you see civil war being discussed. The larger the audience, you see gravitate towards that kind of rhetoric, that means going from the extreme to the mainstream, that goes from ideological extremist leaders to politicians saying those sorts of things, then you`re going to have a higher chance of what we would call stochastic terrorism, where the target is known, the perpetrator is not. And it`s very difficult to defend against. I think that`s the stage that we`re really in right now is it`s hard to anticipate where those come from. But, you know, we`ll come from somewhere.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Clint, you said a bunch of things, though, that I that I want to know more about one, which is why are you seeing that age divide? Why is it the youngest members of this group who seems are the most committed to radical acts? So, let`s start there.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:03]\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yeah, it`s a couple of components and what`s consistent about it is isolation of young men in the online environment. We see this through a variety of the mass shooter attacks, and attempted attacks we`ve seen over the last three to four years. There`s been a strong uptick in it, at different times, it`s usually based around political rhetoric. And I have to say, in election years, that`s when the boiling point kind of comes up, because you hear that rhetoric pick up separately with the militia groups. But you know, when they do show up, they often go right up to the line of what is protected free speech and assembly, or use of weapons, meaning that they dawn or show weapons, but they don`t really engage in what you might think of as a civil war.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s strangers that seem while they`re more visible in your face, they actually don`t really go over that line as much as the lone shooters that we`ve seen show up in many mass casualty situations.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The other thing you said that I want to tease out is that these are not just civilians, you have politicians who are using this language, you have people like former Trump National Security Adviser \uc2 Mike Flynn\uc2 , saying last week at a Republican fundraiser in Arizona, this. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL FLYNN\uc2 , FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: States` rights, did you know that a governor can declare war -- governor can declare war, OK. And we were going to probably, we`re going to probably see that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So, Clint, I want you to -- two things there, you can fact check the governor. And then you can talk to me about just how dangerous that rhetoric is coming from someone who has his type of platform.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yeah, it`s just particularly disappointing for me. I knew who General Flynn, was Colonel Flynn back then in the army. And to hear that sort of talk is just very upsetting. I think separately, governors don`t have the right to declare war, and this entire notion that each state would go to war with each other. It`s just nonsensical.\par \par \uc2 I think the danger in that is, General Flynn knows that`s nonsensical. I actually don`t think he probably believes that a lot of it, but that rhetoric is believed -- it is believed in the online environment. Groups like QAnon, I think that`s the other thing is, a year ago, I would have thought QAnon is mostly dead. But part of the embrace of some of these politicians, President Trump being one. General Flynn, being another figure is really started to embrace QAnon again. And this is where you`re seeing that dustup can, you`re seeing talk of civil war and QAnon type circles. And it brings this sort of fever pitch back around.\par \par \uc2 The problem is in those circles, as we saw on January 6, when things don`t unfold, and they believe the lie violence unfolds, I think that`s the real tragedy in all of this is it does mobilize people to violence. And it may only be a few people. In the case of January 6, it was many. But think if you`re at a state capitol, coming into the midterms, I think that`s where I`m most concerned, state local governments, polling places, and state capitals, headed into November, that`s the real vulnerable part. They don`t have a force to call to respond to something like we saw on January 6 in the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So, just to connect the dots here, how much of all this violent talk can be blamed on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I think to a large degree it can, but do remember that he has been removed from a lot of the social media platforms, he`s been pushed to the fringe platforms essentially. And I think at this point, it`s that the embrace of Trump`s rhetoric by many politicians and key figures, without size voices, is really taken over. Two years ago, you could set it was about one, and it is about that one individual, persistently over time. But there are many and I mean, many candidates running for election, from anything from local office, all the way up to state positions, that are saying the same thing, that are advocating the same thing. Literally pursuing power in a political system that they seem to want to overthrow. I think that`s where the real danger is, at this point is, it is become more diffuse. And if those people are elected into positions, particularly in state governments where they can control the vote, or control like a National Guard group, I think we got big problems heading into 2022, 2023 and particularly in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Clint, I think our audience understands this problem pretty well. I think the big question is, is where we go from here?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yeah, it really takes I think, the movement or losing, to be honest, it takes losing at the ballots. And I think that you`re seeing a lot of that trend, both in terms of Republican primaries, and in terms of general elections. You`re seeing a lot of those very fringe candidates are advocating for violence or not winning broad-based support, and they`re starting to struggle.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, I do think the momentum has come out of a lot of this movement, and that the idea of civil war or after January 6, there`s a reality horizon that has struck. The problem is there`s always the ones that cling to the very, very end of a movement. And when their movement is being pushed away and oftentimes is when they get back down to a corner and they become more violent.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:07]\par \par \uc2 So even when you have success, really pushing back this disinformation, pushing back the idea of these falsehoods which are mobilized and violence, those that stay committed to the very end are also the ones that are willing to fight and die for that movement, even if it`s on false pretenses. So, I think that`s where he gets worrisome, particularly for law enforcement as they try and really suppress this sort of violence mobilization in the coming weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , as always, thank you so much for your expertise and for your time.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a new development and that story we`ve been following, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has just responded to a new report that adds a new important detail about a woman who claims that the anti-abortion rights candidate paid for her abortion, the new report and his response when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:28]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m voting for Herschel. I mean, he made a mistake. But right now, I`m really more interested in what somebody`s going to do for me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  would never impact the way that I vote, like he would never even be a consideration. The only reason \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is on his ballot right now is because he supports Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I prefer people who are principled, who do the things they`re advocating. So -- but I don`t think it`ll impact my vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Georgia voters are reacting to reporting that Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  who is against abortion rights once paid for a girlfriend`s abortion. Walker has denied the report, calling it "A flat Out Lie."\par \par \uc2 Tonight, The Daily Beast is reporting Walker actually has a child with the same woman. NBC News has not confirmed the Daily Beast reporting show Walker just released a statement that says, "As I`ve already said, there`s no truth to this or any other Daily Beast report."\par \par \uc2 The Walker campaign says he`s raised over half a million dollars since the original story came out. With us for more, Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News Correspondent and Moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2 , and David Jolly, a former Republican Congressman from Florida, is now Chair of the Serve America Movement, also an MSNBC Political Contributor.\par \par \uc2 Friends, it is good to see you both. Yamiche, whoa, that sound there from voters sign that second woman up as a political analyst. She is sharp. You`ve been talking to these Georgia voters all day, is tonight`s development actually going to change anybody`s mind?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, PBS WASHINGTON WEEK MODERATOR: Alicia, it`s the critical question here. I`ve been in Georgia all day, this deep red state that has been shifting blue, talking to voters about this critical Senate race. Because the backstory, the big overarching thing here is that this is a Senate seat that Republicans absolutely want to and likely need to win in order to take back control of the Senate. They want to do that. And these voters I`ve been talking to you, they were passionate. They`re all sorts of emotions.\par \par \uc2 That women that you talked to, you just talked about the African American woman, she told me that she would never vote for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , because even before this claim that he paid for a woman who was carrying his child to have an abortion, he also had domestic abuse allegations against them. She also says that he just simply not fit to be in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But I also heard from a number of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  fans who said that this news just does not impact the way that they see this race. They told me either they, one, do not believe this woman who was asked to remain anonymous for privacy concerns according to The Daily Beast, or they told me that they do -- that if these are real or this is true, that they forgive \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  because everyone makes mistakes. A number of voters told me now that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is someone who is an opponent of abortion rights and says that there shouldn`t be abortion access, even in cases of rape or incest. He`s now on the right track. So, there`s a lot of developments here. But overall, my sense is that voters are not changing their votes, because if you didn`t like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , you don`t like him even more now. And if you were voting for him, you`re -- this is not really changing your view of things.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I mean, David, this is the truth of a lot of fans, which is that some of these dynamics are just baked. What I find fascinating is that it`s not just that these voters who support Walker are willing to forgive him as Yamiche said, is that you actually have seen this sort of outpouring of support, your reaction to the Walker campaign, saying they`ve raised over half a million dollars since this story broke?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, SERVE AMERICA MOVEMENT CHAIRMAN: Yeah, you know, the interesting thing, the outpouring of support, not because he said, yes, it`s true. And perhaps I would do things differently today. And I realized there`s hypocrisy in my but not because he owned it, right? He denied it. And somehow then he created this outpouring of support because he declared himself the victim.\par \par \uc2 The interesting thing I think, Yamiche is exactly right. Our politics are tribal. So, we won`t expect to see too many voters move. But you only need to see 2% to 3% of Georgia voters move and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  as a United States senator for the next six years, and that`s the critical piece here because this is very much like in the Senate runoffs coming out of the 2020 election where God bless the Democrats, they did everything right in Georgia, but the Republicans also completely screwed up and left a lot of Georgia voters saying what a cluster, the GOP is in the state of Georgia, and now they`re getting a reverb of that in this \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  race. But \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was never a candidate who would be elected on his qualifications. He was a vessel for national Republicans to prosecute a political case against Biden and Warnock. They can`t do that if they`re having to defend the trust and the hypocrisy allegations against Walker in these last five weeks. This is a troubling moment for his campaign.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:06]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, to that point, Yamiche, about his being a vessel, I want to play something Eddie Glaude said earlier today on MSNBC. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GLAUDE, AFRICAN AMERICAN STUDIES DEPT. CHAIR AT \uc2 PRINCETON UNIVERSITY\uc2 : \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is only here because of his name and that he`s a black man running against \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . That`s the only reason why he`s here. They`re playing on race, so that these people can vote for someone who will implement negative policy that will negatively impact poor people in vulnerable people, but they don`t have to feel like they`re being racist because they`re voting for a black man.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I just wonder how that tracks with what you`re hearing from black voters?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Alicia, I heard something very similar, especially from that African American woman that we played. She told me that part of this, she sees is really discriminatory. Because \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in her mind, this is her mind is saying that she does not believe that he really represents African Americans, and that he was really only put there because they wanted to run another African American against \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, there is some sense there that he might be and again, this is quoting a voter directly, someone called him a quote token, saying that he didn`t have the qualifications to run for the seat. Of course, I should tell you that I also talked to a young African American man, he voted for Trump in 2020. He`s voting for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  now likely he told me and he told me that he does not see these abortion allegations, or really anything wrapped in Herschel Walker`s background as problematic here. So that tells you that even as the African American woman who is really still a large part of the of the Democratic base, and I will say African American women out vote African American men. There are some at least one African American men that I`ve talked to in probably more out in here in the state who look at \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and say, there are a lot of men like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , they have problems. But, you know, what \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is saying, he`s a redeemed sinner and I take him at his word.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: David, I`ve only got about 30 seconds left, but you`ve made faces that tell me you have more to say.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yeah, look, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not fit to be a United States Senator, not because he paid for a former mistress or perhaps a wife to have an abortion. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not fit to be a United States Senator, because he`s incompetent on federal policy, and he cannot be trusted. That is why -- and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wants him for those two reasons. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wants an incompetent untrustworthy senator that he can manipulate. The problem is Georgia voters don`t want that. And that`s the opportunity for Democrats to prosecute that case in the weeks before November 8.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yamiche Alcindor, thank you so much for bringing us your reporting. David Jolly as always, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, immigrants just like the ones that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  put on a plane from Texas to Martha`s Vineyard, now heading back to Florida to look for work in the dangerous storm recovery effort. That`s next from THE 11TH HOUR continuous.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:26]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, VETERAN DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Forget about Martha`s Vineyard, hire a bunch of busses, go to El Paso to (inaudible), to Torito, to (inaudible), to Mexicali and put a sign up and says we need workers, no questions asked, high wages, right? Stop all the stuff that you`re doing. You`re going to need immigrants and a lot of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: That was Democratic Strategist James Carville last night on this program and today report in USA Today says immigrants from Venezuela, Honduras and other countries are often at the center of the multibillion- dollar recovery industry. And many are already trying to make their way to Florida looking for work.\par \par \uc2 Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is still facing criticism over the political stunt of flying migrants from Texas to Martha`s Vineyard, met with President Biden today.\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight, Maria Teresa Kumar, the President and CEO of Voto Latino, she is also an MSNBC Contributor.\par \par \uc2 Maria, here`s the thing, migrants mean much more to this country than just the labor they provide. I know you and I agree on that. But Carville is getting out what I think is an undeniable truth here, which is that Republicans can claim to not want migrants, not welcome migrants into this country. But we have an economy that is unsustainable without them, and they can`t get out from under that truth.\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, VOTO LATINO PRESIDENT & CEO: I think the truth is that they want migrant labor, they want people living in the shadows, they don`t want people who have rights. They don`t want enfranchised community that could actually help define the rest of this country and all echelons. The Republicans are comfortable with a second-class citizenry. And we know, Alicia, we`ve already been there. We know what that looks like. And now`s the time for Congress that is controlled by the Democrats to do something about it.\par \par \uc2 In the lame duck session, it is an imperative to make sure that Democrats send something to the White House, for Biden to sign. We`re not going to have another time in recent history where we`re one at full employment, people are looking for jobs, for workers and two, the fastest way to stop this Maga Republicans is to take the carpet from underneath the rug from underneath them and say, you know what, we want a democracy. We`re going to have these 11 million people who are living in the shadows come out and be full and franchise citizens. And if there are patriots in the Republican Party, we`re tired of this and this we recognize this opportunity for us to actually finally fix immigration.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, and your argument feels particularly timely given the DACA ruling that came out today, was sort of a mixed bag, allows people who already have status to continue to re up. But you have an entire group of young people who don`t have access and just reminder that they are in limbo.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:12]\par \par \uc2 So, I want to ask you about the politics of this. According to a new NBC poll 73% of conservative Latinos say they prefer GOP control of Congress, why that is interesting is because that`s a 33% increase in only a decade, of course, not enough to erase Democrats lead among Latino voters. You`ve seen all the same polls I`ve seen. And my read on it is Democrats in numbers are soft, softer than they want, but Republicans cannot seal the deal. So, wonder what you think is happening and the role that Democrats potentially passing some type of immigration reform would play in correcting those numbers for them?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Well, I first of all, what`s happening right now with DACA is abysmal. You have individuals that have for over a decade been living a political football between Congress and the judicial -- judiciary system, we have to stop that. Right now, there`s a young girl that`s probably 16, 17 years old, who has been working her heart out, trying to say I can become a DACA recipient, who all of a sudden, is going to bed tonight being incredibly uncertain, and her heartbroken and her dreams broken. And we as a country will not be able to fully benefit from her talent until we get our act together, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that when you`re asking about where -- why are Republicans -- why are the Democrats thought, they were not communicating to the Latino community that their vote worked? One of the reasons why we are at full employment, one of the reasons why people have their children back in classrooms is because the Democrats delivered.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons why all of a sudden in the Latino community, they`ve almost freed up all their student debt was because of the $20,000 debt relief that Biden passed. He`s delivering on his promises, but he has to remind the Latino community, what that is and what that means for their lives. But just as importantly, they need to talk to us all the time.\par \par \uc2 Alicia, you and I talked about this all the time. It`s, you know, two weeks out of the election. And all of a sudden people remember that the Latino community are the ones that propelled them to power. That`s not good enough anymore. And we have to make sure that we make those demands and as far as immigration is concerned, yes, 16 million Americans who live in mixed status families, there`s a loved one that goes to bed that wakes up in the morning that helps feed and manufacture everything that we need in this country. And yet they don`t feel safe and neither do their children and for us to do by right by them and by our democratic values is to ensure that during the lame duck session, they get the grid, Congress sets the grid to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Maria Teresa Kumar, there is no one I would rather talk about this with, thank you so much for staying up late and being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a preview of my interview with America 00:02:50 FAIR data on why Latino representation in media matters when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:39]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JOAQUIN CASTRO, (D) TEXAS: Latinos make up nearly 20% of our nation, and our contributions have shaped this country`s development and prosperity for centuries. But despite our numbers and growing power, our stories have been systematically excluded from Hollywood and the American media, which are the dominant image creating a narrative defining institutions in our nation, and therefore in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The last thing before we go tonight, the power of being seen, the power of having power. A new study from the \uc2 Government Accountability Office\uc2  shows that Latino representation in TV, film and media grew by only 1% in the past decade. The GAO found that, "Hispanic workers are underrepresented in the media industry compared to the representation in the rest of the U.S. workforce."\par \par \uc2 Little change occurred from 2010 through 2019. Actress producer, director, America Ferrera, was at the White House today with her organization, Poderistas, a community built to celebrate Latino culture and harness Latina power for an event celebrating Hispanic Heritage Month. I had the chance to sit down with Ferrera and ask her about this persistent gap.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMERICA FERRERA, PODERISTAS CO-FOUNDER: To really change the stories that get told and who gets to tell them and what kind of representations of Latinos exist in the media, we need the decision makers to change. We need to have Latinos who share and understand the Latino experience, invest in Latino creatives and really shift power to our community to be able to tell our stories, and that has to change behind the camera up and down the line. And so many things need to happen. But ultimately, we need to acknowledge that those in power to make the most important decisions, to not look like our community and there`s a reason why we`re so underrepresented and that needs to change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: If you want to see more of my interview with America Ferrera, Suchiq (ph) Gomez and Chloe Bridges, you can tune into American Voices this Saturday at 6 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. We talk about representation. We also talk about the upcoming midterms, the role that Latino voters are poised to play and what both parties get wrong when it comes to communicating with this critical voting bloc.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you a very good night and all of our colleagues across the networks have NBC News, thank you for staying up late.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020221006eia500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for October 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Michael Steele, Claire McCaskill \par \uc2 8,116 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: One last thing before we go. Do not forget to plan your vote. You can go to nbcnews.com/planyourvote for information on registration, on deadlines, on early voting, and more. It is so important. It couldn`t be more important. Please vote.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s "REIDOUT." ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight on All IN.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL: You`re saying that you weren`t the perfect dad or the perfect spouse, but you`ve been redeemed. Is that what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Oh, that`s exactly what I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Herschel Walker`s denial involves into a defense as the fox push to Hannities the Senate continues apace.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL: Claimed went to your hometown. Is it true you have a field named after you in that town?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I do. I have a field name after me. I have a street named after me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, what we`re learning about the Trump lawyer who refuse to lie about classified documents and the ex-President`s role in packing the boxes himself. Plus --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think the one thing this is finally ended is a discussion about whether or not there`s climate change and we should do something about it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The current president rallies the recovery in Florida as the current governor loses his boots.\par \par \uc2 And 34 days from the election, why the Saudi decision to cut oil production could have massive impact for Democrats and democracy when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. On Monday evening, one of the biggest stories of the Midterm elections dropped. According to the Daily Beast, Trump endorsed Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  reportedly paid for a woman to have an abortion in 2009. Now, Walker is a guy who is running on one of the most extreme anti-abortion platforms in the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, NBC News has not verified this story. Walker immediately denied the allegation -- a little more about that. But barely two hours after the story dropped, there he was, the big guest on the Fox News 9:00 p.m. show getting as is known in the business, Hannitized.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: A get-well card, it looks like it`s included with your signature on -- in the article. Have you seen it? And is that your signature?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I haven`t seen it. But you know I can tell you. I sent out so many get-will, send out so much anything. But I can tell you right now, I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never pay for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What about to $700 check. What -- is there anybody you can remember sending that much money to?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, I send money to a lot of people and that`s what`s so funny.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Who can keep track, really. Unfortunately for Walker, the first appearance on Fox News didn`t go so well. His initial response to the allegations right after the publish, bluster, denial, threats of a defamation lawsuit. In fact, he said tomorrow morning, I`m going to file a defamation lawsuit. It`s two days later, still no lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Daily Beast reporting seemed pretty solid. They had obtained a check from Walker reporting to pay for the abortion, along with a literal receipt from the abortion provider, and even included the pictures of the get well card Walker apparently sent to the woman after she had the abortion. Given that, Walker had to, well, change tactics. Where did he got a test-run his new defense? Back to Fox News this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: People see someone`s sitting here in front of you right now that has been redeemed. And I want America to know I`m living proof that you can make mistakes and get up and keep going forward. But you can only do it in this country right here. And you can only do it if we get this election correct this come November. Because we vote for the people on the left like the guy I`m running against, Senator Warnock, you`re not going to have a chance to be redeemed. He`s a minister and he don`t -- he don`t believe in redemption.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, OK, OK. You will note this is a very different tone than the tone released by his campaign after it published. This is defamatory. It`s so defamatory I will sue tomorrow morning. Today, it`s I`ve made mistakes and I`m redeemed. A little bit of tension there. There`s something pretty telling about Walker immediately running to Fox News to try out approach, one approach two, kind of mutually inconsistent to launder his image, because that`s what Republicans do now when they are trying to spin a negative story about them, especially if they`re running for the Senate this cycle.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another example, for instance. Also on Monday, a story broke about medical research on live animals, including dogs done under the name of Pennsylvania Republican Senate Candidate Mehmet Oz while he was conducting research at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2 . Now, NBC News has not verified that reporting. We didn`t even cover it on the show, but it made quite the stir online. And Oz`s opponent Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  jumped on the story.\par \par \uc2 And wouldn`t you know it, same night, Oz appeared on Fox News. Now, host Laura Ingraham did not ask Oz about the dog story, even though it`s probably the biggest development in his race that day. She did give him plenty of chances to attack his opponent. Ingraham`s show even labeled the candidate as Dr. Mehmet Oz Republican running against \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [20:05:10]\par \par \uc2 Now, to be clear, there`s nothing wrong with talking about politics, interviewing candidates for office. We`ve covered the midterms quite a bit on the show, but I just want to be clear about something. Fox`s coverage differs both in tone and just the sheer amount of it. At this point, Fox News is essentially an arm of the 24-hour public relations channel for Republican Senate candidates interrupted periodically by ads from My Pillow. There`s really no other way to put it.\par \par \uc2 According to new report from \uc2 Media Matters for America\uc2 , in the four weeks since Labor Day, Fox News` primetime opinion shows have covered the midterm races twice as often as this network and CNN combined. "\uc2 Fox\uc2  functions as a communications arm of the GOP. Its biggest stars are working to ensure Republicans take over the Senate and have an opportunity to implement a hard-right agenda. The network`s powerful right-wing propagandists are bombarding their audiences with a steady stream of attacks on Democratic nominees while lavishing positive airtime on the Republican ones.\par \par \uc2 You can see that all over Fox`s coverage, in case you don`t trust me, hyperbolic graphics depicting Democratic Senate candidates as losers, dangerous radicals, hell-bent on destroying this country. And I got to say, tip your hat where appropriate, the undisputed king of this stuff is Fox anchor \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 . And he`s sort of a unique figure here. He`s openly partisan in a way. He stands out even among his colleagues. Appeared at a Trump rally during 2020. Notably, he doesn`t even claim to be a journalist which is true. He steadfastly is not one. Though it prompts the question, what exactly is Sean Hannity`s job? Well, as far as we can tell, it`s getting Republicans elected.\par \par \uc2 Here`s \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  with a graphic labeling Herschel Walker`s opponent, Georgia Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  as Radical \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . Here`s Hannity labeling \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  as John the Socialist, even photoshopping him in front of some communist iconography. Here`s a scrolling graphic titled Rafael Warnock`s extremism exposed but just like a ton of stuff just going by the screen, and one called Fetterman`s extremism exposed with like the same thing.\par \par \uc2 Hannity once also did a segment with a banner reading, "In Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, and other key states, Democratic Senate candidates on defensive over extreme record? And here`s the thing. All those examples are just from one show. Hannity and his colleagues have been doing all of that five nights a week for weeks now.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be clear, it`s a free country. And as long as there has been media in this country going back to the beloved Founders, there has been partisan media, partisan media that use the media platforms as a way to sway elections. To me, the problem is not just partisan media. It`s the weird circumstances we find ourselves in. Because you have the combination of partisan media with the MAGA takeover of the Republican Party. And really, this started with Trump, right?\par \par \uc2 In the beginning, \uc2 Fox\uc2  was kind of hard on Trump a little bit, but it didn`t work. And he was a ratings bonanza. And when Trump became the nominee, Fox just close ranks around him. They defended Trump, helped him get elected, even though he should have been nowhere near the levers of power in this country. And lots of people knew it, and I imagine a lot of people inside \uc2 Fox\uc2  knew it. And now, they just do it with everyone. They`re doing it with all Trump`s acolytes because Trump elevates candidates who like him are unfit for office. There`s a kind of affinity there.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes he elevates like genuinely dangerous candidates who share his authoritarian sympathies, like \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania, or Kari Lake in Arizona. Sometimes he elevates candidates were wildly incompetent and or just completely untrustworthy like Mehmet Oz, TV grifter and snake oil salesman who does not even live in the state he`s running to represent. And \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who has been among other things accused of threatening his ex-wife but the gun, something he doesn`t even deny. He just says he doesn`t remember it. Now, he`s redeemed.\par \par \uc2 Fox\uc2  elevates these candidates, runs cover for them, works overtime to get them elected, along with the rest of the institutional Republican Party from \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . They`re all rowing in the same direction. It doesn`t matter how extreme it doesn`t matter how unfit. Once \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reaches in and picks who that person is going to be that`s going to be the nominee, they all get on board no matter how far they should obviously be kept from wielding power. And that is a huge part of the story. It might be the story of how we found ourselves in our current democratic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Michael Steele is a former chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  and served as lieutenant governor of Maryland. Claire McCaskill is a former Democratic Senator from Missouri. And both join me now.\par \par \uc2 You know, Michael, what I -- what I keep thinking about is there was a lot of dissent in Republican circles in Georgia among political operatives about Walker. They didn`t want him to run. They wanted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to butt out. They`re like, please don`t endorse this guy. But the point is that once Trump makes his selection the base follows and then the entire establishment gets in line, and it does not matter. They will do whatever it takes to push them across the finish line.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:17]\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, that`s the game plan as it has evolved since 2016. And understand what Fox`s role is here. \uc2 Fox\uc2  is not talking to the three of us. They don`t care about us. Who they`re -- who they`re talking to is that base. They`re driving that base because their internal numbers are showing them a very difficult campaign than they would like to see.\par \par \uc2 It is something \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  recognized a long time ago which is why he was very concerned about the kind of -- kind of candidates that were being put forth to the Senate, because he knew there was an opportunity there to take the Senate. And particularly when you have the race coming off the heels of the 20 election cycle in Georgia, that presented a real opportunity. The numbers show that Georgia is at its worst, a sort of light-red pinkish state. And at its best, it`s still a solid Republican base.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s what a lot of this is geared to doing is reminding that base, A, these are Trump`s candidates, and if they`re Trump`s candidates, they`re your candidates and you`ve got to get out there and support them. For the candidates like Walker, this is an opportunity for them to figure out how best to create the narrative that that base can get behind the easiest, which is what you see the trial and error shots on Fox.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s the funniest part of it. Yes. I mean, to go from well, you deny it, don`t you, to well, are you saying you`re redeemed, saved by grace. He just got a new ad today saved by grace. It talks about writing a book. Of course, this happened one year after that book came out about how we saved by grace, just to be clear.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Of course. And again, Claire, I mean, you have -- you have experienced running statewide races and statewide races and tough races in tough -- in tough states. When I think that, you know, in your races, I think the nature of polarization being less development that candidate quality mattered more, I mean that the walker race right now really seems like the ultimate test of it. Because if she gets through this, like Lord knows he can get through anything.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. I mean, if you think back 10 years ago, what Todd Akin said, they have a ticker tape parade for him today --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: -- for what he said. And what he said didn`t beat Todd Akin about legitimate race. What beat Todd Akin were all the Republicans deciding he was not qualified to be in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . The Republican Party beat Todd Akin.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. They ran away.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: They rejected him. They all rejected him. Now, compare and contrast that to today where you not only have the Republicans circling the wagons around this clearly flawed candidate who was taken to lying like his mentor, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you have Fox News deciding they`ve got to rehabilitate him.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: And Mike is right. This election is going to boil down to turnout. And what \uc2 Fox\uc2  is doing, they`re taking the most vulnerable candidates that the Republicans have, and they`re trying to get them well, and they`re trying to get turnout for these very, very flawed candidates.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s a great point and I had forgotten that part of it that there had been this kind of domino effect with Akin you`d seen. But you`d seen it with other candidates. I mean, you saw it a little bit with \uc2 George Allen\uc2  in Virginia with the -- with his infamous Macaca comment. I mean, something would happen in a race and people would distance themselves.\par \par \uc2 Now, you know, it`s a little bit of this. I think -- I think there`s a \uc2 Roger Ailes\uc2  line about, you know, if there had -- if the media had been different, Nixon never would have had to resign. I think that may be apocryphal, but I`m recalling this from memory. Like, there is just this effort. And you can see it, Michael, when you talked about it, as a broadcaster, it`s like I can watch the strenuousness of throwing of Hannity, throwing his back into this on the day. It`s like, well, this broke and we just have to do whatever it takes. It is -- it`s almost like watching a campaign mailer on air.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Let me give you a little bit of the inside of how we develop messaging in a moment like this inside the GOP. It is a matter of getting elements that resonate almost immediately with your base. So, the first thing is going to be OK, own the libs kind of language.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, right. The Democrats are doing this.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Secondly, if that -- if that doesn`t have the big draw, then you play that evangelical religious card baby. You roll out how much you`ve been redeemed and how much you love Jesus, and then you shut -- you let the world know I`m a sinner and I`ve sinned. So, when he says that I`ve -- today, I`ve made mistakes, so was that mistake paying for that abortion? Because that`s the natural follow-up. Is that the mistake you`re owning to?\par \par \uc2 [20:15:04]\par \par \uc2 So, you can begin to see how this becomes sort of a dual track for narratives. One in which they try to, again draw that base into these narratives around religion, faith, redemption, and then of course, you know, all those mean nasty liberals, they`re the ones who are making all this up. \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  asking him about, oh, let`s go to the childhood home thing. You got a street named after you, right? OK, yes, what does that do -- have to do with the receipt for the abortion?\par \par \uc2 Now, if the receipt is somehow tied to the naming of your street, then OK, but that`s not the point. It`s how they sort of cut it up, repackage it, and put it out there for them to just eat like manna from heaven.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And Claire, there`s a substantive issue here too, which is also I don`t care. I said this yesterday, I don`t care that he did this. I don`t care. You want to make it illegal. Like, it just gives rise to the substantive issue here, which is that women in Georgia can`t access abortion right now.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Yes. What I really wish some journalist -- it`s obviously not going to happen on Fox, but I just love him to get the hypothetical. If someone did this, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , your party believes that you should go to prison.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: So, if you did this, you belong in jail according to the policies you support. It`s accuracy that these guys are comfortable with. They got really comfortable with lying with Trump. And now, lying and being hypocrites kind of is the ethos of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Michael Steele, Claire McCaskill, thank you both. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We did a story a few weeks ago when a man named Don Bolduc won the Republican primary in the New Hampshire Senator race, maybe most memorable for the prop shield with the prop arrows he held during his victory speech. He is also the MAGA candidate who insisted the 2020 election was stolen before announcing the big lie was a myth after he won his primary. Well, it turns out the Republican candidate for Senate in New Hampshire has once again evolved on American democracy. You`ll never guess where he landed. That story next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Last month we introduced you to a guy named Don Bolduc. He is the Republican candidate for Senate in New Hampshire. He is also an election- denying MAGA Republican. On election night, he came out to address his supporters carrying a shield with arrows in it just like the Spartan shields used in the 300 movie that a lot of people in the right like so much.\par \par \uc2 Bolduc spent his primary campaign spreading election lies. But after he won the nomination in a swing state, he tried a different line.\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R-NH), SENATE CANDIDATE: I signed a letter with 120 other generals and admirals saying that Trump won the election and dammit, I stand by my letter.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CHANNEL: Do you stand by that today?\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: So, you know, we -- you know, live and learn, right? And I`ve done a lot of research on this. And I`ve spent the past couple of weeks talking to Granite Staters all over the state from, you know, every party. And I have come to the conclusion and I want to be definitive on this. The election was not stolen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Definitive, live and learned, talked to Granite Staters to recap in the primary. Bolduc said the election was stolen outright, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won, then pivoted to being pro-democracy of the general election, not stone definitive, except you`ll never guess what, he`s now flipped again. According to the Associated Press on Monday, Bolduc was asked at a town hall meeting whether the election was stolen. And he said, "I can`t say that it was stolen or not. I don`t have enough information, Bolduc said in response. But what I can say is that we have irregularity. We have to be able to say whether we agree with it or not a constitutional process was followed to put Biden in the White House, so we can then hold him accountable," Bolduc said. I don`t really know what that means.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the incumbent senator running against that guy, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . She`s Democrat of New Hampshire. Senator, it`s great to have you on. Are you clear on where your opponent stands on who won the 2020 election?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): Chris, thanks for having me on. And Don Bolduc is an election denier. He is now trying to mislead the people of New Hampshire about his true position. He is the most extreme \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  nominee that New Hampshire has had in decades on this, and on supporting a national abortion ban and on eliminating Social Security, for example. And he`s really working to mislead the people of New Hampshire about his extremism.\par \par \uc2 The problem we have is that the Republicans are all rallying around him. And they are spending $60 million against me to support him to try to mislead Granite Staters as well. We can`t have another election denier in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . But the Republicans are doing their best and spending a lot to put him in there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There was some talk about your race being a top tier race. And I know there was back and forth among Republicans about that primary race. The idea being that well, if it was Bolduc, he would have because of how extreme he was -- and this is what Republicans thought. I`m just sort of characterizing their views -- that he would not have a shot. But they are spending consistently and polling has the race very close. What do you think the defining issues are for the voters in your state?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Look, people are really worried about making ends meet. Inflation is a concern. That`s why I`ve been talking to people about my record of reducing the cost. We can reduce right now while working on the long-term drivers of inflation as we did and things like the Chips and Science Act and the Infrastructure Bill. But people are also really concerned about the efforts -- the Dobbs decision, and now the ongoing efforts of Republicans to impose the national abortion ban, something that my opponents record shows he would support.\par \par \uc2 And I think the last thing is, they just really would like us to stop being so divisive and come together and actually address a lot of the challenges we have affording housing, childcare, making sure that our kids are OK, addressing their mental health needs, continuing to address climate change. Those are all things I hear about from my constituents.\par \par \uc2 Don Bolduc is way out of step with their beliefs and their concerns. But to come back to the point here, the Republicans are spending enormous amounts of money. We`re seeing the race tighten. We always knew -- tightening -- and we always knew it would be a really, really close race. But people really need to get engaged in this race right now if we`re going to prevail and keep a very extreme election denier from becoming a United States Senator.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, it`s interesting when you talk about the cost of living and inflation. Obviously, polling supports that. Also, anyone with a pair of eyes and who lives in the world understands that. And it strikes me that it`s a difficult thing for an incumbent politician to talk about, right, because you`re held responsible. I don`t think the inflation that we`re seeing is because of what the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  has done. It`s a global phenomenon. But how do you -- how do you tell a voter who says the cost of living is too high, inflation is too high, I`ve seen my real wage power declined, that you can do something or have done something about it.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Well, first of all, I`ll keep pushing for the suspension of a gas tax. I`ll keep pushing to make sure we get funding to support people who are trying to afford their heating bills here in New Hampshire this year. I`ll talk about the work we have done to begin to lower prescription drug costs and to begin to lower energy costs and the work we need to do to stand up to big oil to hold them accountable for price gouging and market manipulation.\par \par \uc2 But we also know that we need to continue to come together and address long-term issues like the Chips and Science Act. And I`ll be pointing out to my constituents that Don Bolduc opposes Medicare negotiation for prescription drugs, opposes standing up to big oil to help people lower their energy costs and pivot to a clean energy economy. Don Bolduc supports eliminating Social Security and cutting Medicare drastically at a time when seniors are working so hard to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s important to acknowledge that this is a real problem, point to the things that we are doing, but also remind people that we can come together as we have begun to do and really take concrete steps to address this. But again, what I`m concerned about right now is I will be making my case to the voters of New Hampshire, which we do all the time in New Hampshire. That`s the beauty of my state. You have to earn every vote every election cycle, right?\par \par \uc2 But in order to do that, I have to have resources to combat $60 million that the Republicans are spending to shore up Don Bolduc. And I`m hoping people will get engaged now in the campaign and they can do that at maggiehassan.com.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  of New Hampshire, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Thanks so much, Chris. Take care.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the former president tries to make lemonade out of a federal investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You probably read and heard about the document hoax. Has anyone heard about the document hoax? Helicopters flying over Mar-a-Lago? Well, they`ve given us about $5 billion worth of free publicity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Mounting evidence \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the person most responsible for his latest legal predicament next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They spend all their time on nonsense, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on horrible things, and raiding Mar-a-Lago. Let`s raid Mar-a-Lago, but leave the cartels alone. How about that? Think of this. Think of this. I`m just thinking as I`m speaking. You don`t mind if I go a little off script. No, but I think they raid Mar-a-Lago, but the cartels, they have their own Mar-a-Lagos and those are fine. Leave them alone. Let them continue to destroy our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Every time I hear him now, it sounds like someone doing a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  impersonation. It has been, what is it, two months since Donald Trump`s retirement home was searched by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And since then, we just keep learning more about just how involved the ex-President was in not just keeping the highly classified documents he took from the White House in his possession, but how he actually personally interacted with them on multiple occasions.\par \par \uc2 Back in the end of August, remember, we found out the Trump "went through the boxes himself in late 2021 before returning 15 boxes to the natural archives." About a week later, federal prosecutors revealed in a court filing that when agents search Trump`s office, they found "a desk drawer that contained classified documents and governmental records commingled with other documents, including the ex-president`s passports, one of the most personal items that anyone owns."\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re finding out in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , and I quote here, "Trump himself eventually packed the boxes that were returned in January." So, after he went through the boxes, familiar size -- familiarized himself with the contents, he packed and sent some of them back to the archives and kept the others at his home.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:12]\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Danya Perry, former federal prosecutor and New York State Deputy Attorney General. Danya, it has always been a question in terms of the President`s criminal exposure, how much he did this and whether he did it knowingly. Those are two of the main issues. The latest reporting has him going through the boxes, packing them themselves, and presumably retaining the stuff that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  ends up finding when they search the place. That doesn`t seem great for his defense that it wasn`t him.\par \par \uc2 DANYA PERRY, FORMER DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL, NEW YORK: It doesn`t seem great. We`ve heard some circumstantial evidence in the reporting that the former President was aware that he was involved in some of the meetings about this, and that some of the documents in question were interspersed with his personal documents. But this, of course, would be direct evidence that he knew that there were remained classified documents at Mar-a-Lago and that he knowingly and intentionally conceal them and withheld them, and then, according to this new report, instructed one of his lawyers to lie on his behalf.\par \par \uc2 So, it goes both through his intention and potentially also to obstruction of justice. It is serious.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, this is -- yes, you just mentioned the next detail I wanted to do. It might be the craziest detail here. This is from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting. Trump`s layer refused his requests in February to say all documents returned. I quote from the piece here, Trump asked one of his lawyers to tell the \uc2 National Archives and Records Administration\uc2  in early 22 -- 2022 that Trump had returned all materials requested by the agency, but the lawyer declined because he was not sure the statement was true according to people familiar with the matter. The lawyer told others he was not sure if other documents were still at the club and would be uncomfortable making such a claim.\par \par \uc2 Smart lawyer, but again, a bad fact, as they say in the law for President 