, pepper spray, baseball bats, and flagpoles to attack police. Some of them even carried guns on them or had firearms nearby in their vehicles. But even with all of that on the record, there are still people including members who were inside the Capitol that day trying to wave it all away, downplay the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Take Ron Johnson, and actually the United States Senator which today, today is still ignoring all the evidence and defending the vicious rioters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): I asked the question of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent who did a hearing on this. I said how many firearms were confiscated either in the Capitol or on Capitol grounds? I don`t know the answer. For all I know, it was going to be thousand. No, zero, zero. Now, some of the protesters did teach us all how you can use flagpoles, that kind of stuff as weapons. But to call what happened on January 6th an armed insurrection, I just think is not accurate. You saw the pictures inside the Capitol, I saw that day. The armed insurrectionist stayed through the rope lines in the Rotunda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, hopefully, Senator Johnson was paying attention today on day two of the trial when we learned about just how violent the Oath Keepers wanted the day to be. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, in the second full day of the Oath Keeper seditious conspiracy trial, prosecutors went through the extensive communications between members of the far-right group, particularly their leader, Yale Law School graduate and dress-up military gang leader Elmer Stewart Rhodes III. Rhodes said, "Trump has one last chance right now to stand. But he will need us and our rifles too. But will he finally act." Adding, so will you step up and push Trump to finally take decisive action? That`s what we must do now. And then, if you still refuse to do his duty, we will still have to do ours and we will.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Reilly is a justice reporter at NBC News, has been in the courtroom reporting on this trial. And he joins me now. Ryan, great to have you back again. What did we learn today in day two of this trial?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, NBC NEWS JUSTICE REPORTER: You know, I think the biggest thing to me was that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  missed the ball on this one because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had a tip about this actually before any of this took place. After that initial November meeting that they had on November 9, they actually got a tip. Apparently, the recording was available to them from someone who was concerned about this, but there`s no evidence that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  reviewed that until far after the January 6 attack.\par \par \uc2 And it`s pretty -- it`s pretty, you know, damning evidence that they`ve been able to put forward in terms of what they`re discussing. I mean, Stewart Rhodes in this meeting very early on in November is talking about this entire idea that the QRF, the Quick Reaction Force outside of DC is only going to be on standby and will only come into DC on the President`s orders. And he says, that`s what gives them legal cover. He literally says it`s legal cover in this -- in this recorded phone call.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:20]\par \par \uc2 He also had mused about how he thought the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was already listening in to them already. But you know, he -- I think he was over-asserting their aggressiveness necessary or somewhat, because that wasn`t the case. They weren`t as on top of this potentially as they should have been in retrospect.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I just want to drill down on this. There`s a meet -- there`s a phone call he convenes. When does it -- pretty early in November, right? Pretty soon after the election?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: That`s right. Yes, November 9, the go-to-meeting, 103 people on the call pretty early on. It sort of lays this out. Now, the --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me stop you there. So, someone -- our understanding of this is that there was a third -- there`s someone who recorded the phone call that early, November 9th and sends it to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as a tip being like, hey, guys, bunch of people plotting an insurrection, maybe armed. You might want to check this out, right? I mean, that`s what we learned today.\par \par \uc2 REILLY: Yes, yes. And no -- and no indication that was taken -- you know, taken out. We don`t -- we don`t know fully what happened behind the scenes at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , whether that was something that was protect -- they thought that, you know, was perhaps protected, but there`s no indication that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took any action off of that.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s sort of the takeaway. You know, the agent who was testifying first pick this up in March of the next year, so several months after, or two months after the January 6 attack had already taken place when they`re starting to shape this entire plot together. And, you know, there -- there`s a lot of former law enforcement folks in Oath Keepers. That`s what they`re sort of all about.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not entirely surprising that someone might have, you know, grew a little backbone, I suppose, and said, hey, this is not looking great even if they lined with them politically to a certain extent. But, you know, that`s -- we haven`t seen any indication of action thus far even though a lot of Oath Keeper folks defense attorneys have said, you know, that there are these -- there are confidential human sources. But I think what that more means is that not necessarily that they had someone in, you know, necessarily embedded at the time that they had someone subsequently to basically start turn state`s witness so to speak and are now cooperating with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we expect to hear some testimony from some of those cooperating witnesses coming up, including an individual who says that he heard Stewart Rhodes trying to get in touch with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the night of January 6. That`s one thing I`m really looking forward to and really be interested in, along with, of course, Rhodes` testimony in his own defense which he is expected to take the stand, probably one of the first witnesses once it comes time for the defense to take up their case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, this is -- this is Rhodes in November 2020. "We`re in an era now where everything you say is being monitored. By now, this phone call is being recorded by the NSA -- Excuse me -- \uc2 FBI\uc2  and CIA, I`m sure. And everything you say can and will be used against you. So, you guys need to have discipline. Don`t make it easy for them to pop you with a conspiracy charge."\par \par \uc2 I mean, the awareness -- the two things that come out here, A, the awareness they`re walking a very fine line that looks like conspiracy to commit sedition, and B, that so early on, a huge part of the plot looks pretty baked in and so alarming that someone tips off the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REILLY: That`s right. Yes, I mean, I think that is. They are basically speaking in coded language. So, to a certain -- he basically lays that out. He says, hey, you know, this is our legal cover for this. But there -- you know, there are a lot of intervening events right there. Then there`s the - - there wasn`t that plot that`s really centered upon January 6 yet. It was more just sort of this broad idea of opposing the peaceful transfer of power, But they hadn`t really focus in on January 6 yet, because that was so early. And I think that that`s what prosecutors are sort of dealing with here, because there wasn`t the central focus, this date that they said, hey, we`re going to go to the Capitol. It was more ambiguous in that in the beginning stage, it was pretty -- as the plot began to take shape -- take shape. So, that`s what the defense is essentially pressing on and say, hey, none of this is about really the Capitol, this is more sort of --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: General, yes.\par \par \uc2 REILLY: Yes. So, I think that`s basically the conflict we`ve seen between the defense and the prosecution of thus far.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Ryan Reilly, thank you very much. Up next, the newest Supreme Court Justice jumped right in on the first week of the new term.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I don`t think that the historical record establishes that the founders believed that race neutrality or race blindness was required.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: How Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  use the conservative favorite play against them next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [02:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is the first week of the Supreme Court`s new session, the first week its newest member Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is on the bench. Because the court decided to continue a practice starting during the pandemic of live streaming the audio of its hearings, we are getting to hear the justices in oral arguments.\par \par \uc2 Today, we got to hear Justice Jackson use a favorite tool of conservatives, the judicial approach called originalism against them. Originalism is the idea we should interpret the Constitution strictly by what the authors of the document at the time intended what is public meaning was at the time. So, it`s fun to wonder what the originalism the court were thinking when our country`s first ever Black woman justice used originalism to argue against a conservative interpretation of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 The case is from Alabama, where Black people make up about 27 percent of the population but the state adopted a congressional map where only one of the seven districts, about 14 percent is majority Black. When Alabama`s lawyer argued the Constitution requires congressional maps to be "race- neutral," Justice Jackson said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I understood that we looked at the history and traditions of the Constitution at what the Framers and the Founders thought about. And when I drill down to that level of analysis, it became clear to me that the framers themselves adopted the Equal Protection Clause, the 14th Amendment, the 15th Amendment in a race-conscious way, that they were in fact trying to ensure that people who had been discriminated against the freedmen during the reconstruction period were actually brought equal to everyone else in the society.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:31]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Joining me now is one of the people who argued the case today before the Supreme Court, Deuell Ross, who spent the last nine years working at the \uc2 NAACP Legal Defense and Educational Fund\uc2  focusing on voting rights cases in Alabama. Deuell, let me first start -- if you could just explain - - this is a Voting Rights Act case, section two of the Voting Rights Act? What is the -- what is the claim that you folks are making about these congressional maps?\par \par \uc2 DEUELL ROSS, SENIOR COUNSEL, NAACP LEGAL DEFENSE FUND: The claim is that even though Black voters in Alabama make up about 27 percent of the population and White voters only make up about 63 percent of the population, White voters vote in such a way to never vote for the same candidates as Black voters. And so, White voters control about 85 percent of Alabama`s congressional districts, six of the seven and Black voters only can have an opportunity to election one district.\par \par \uc2 So, what we`re really asking for is for Alabama to stop discriminating against this very specific Black community made up of about 18 counties called the Black Belt, and to draw it in such a way that would give black voters an opportunity in a second congressional district.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, the Black Belt is an area where specific kinds of soil meant that you had very high levels of plantations and enslave folks back in the day that is carried through to high levels of Black population today. And you`re basically saying, look, the Voting Rights Act requires, right -- I mean, requires the state to give black folks a shot at this level of representation and the state of Alabama is in violation of it.\par \par \uc2 ROSS: Exactly, exactly. And this is something that, you know, Alabama is arguing that, look, this is the way that we`ve always done it. We`ve been, you know, breaking up the Black Belt since at least 1972. But if you go back to 1972, George Wallace was the governor of Alabama. Alabama was still passing literacy tests and Black voters were just becoming registered to vote in large numbers. And that`s when Alabama decided to start making decisions that made it even more difficult for Black voters to have representation in Congress.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What are the ramifications? We have seen various portions of the Voting Rights Act whittled away, gutted by the conservatives on this court. What are the implications here for this case, for this section two of the Voting Rights Act? What would it mean if the plaintiffs here were to lose?\par \par \uc2 ROSS: You know, section two of the Voting Rights Act is, you know, one of the key provisions of the act. It`s what has given us representation in Congress for people of color, not only in Congress, but in city councils and school boards all across the country. And what Alabama is arguing is that anytime you use or even think about race and any kind of redistricting, then that itself is unconstitutional. And so, it really make it extremely difficult if not impossible to have districts in which Black, Latinx, Asian any people of color have representation in Congress or any other level of government.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, there are just two people -- folks are clear here. The representation that we tend to have is born of the Voting Rights Act, section two, which creates majority districts. Because if you have 25 percent of the population, that`s African American state like Alabama, you could just carve up districts such that they`re not a majority in any of them, and then you just get all white representatives.\par \par \uc2 There was a revolution in American politics that produced a world in which we have non-White representatives due to this section of the Act. Am I -- am I misstating the history here?\par \par \uc2 ROSS: No, that`s exactly right. You know, Black people and other people fought and died. You know, \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  was beaten on the bridge in Selma, Alabama in order for us to get these rights, this opportunity to four states in some instances to draw districts in which Black and other voters have an opportunity to elect their candidates of choice. And, you know, those -- Alabama is essentially sort of trying to turn back the clock to a time where, you know, you can`t even draw those districts.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It just seems impossible to argue that under section two of the Voting Rights Act, you can`t be race-conscious when that`s obviously -- like, what am I missing about their argument here?\par \par \uc2 ROSS: You know, I mean, it`s not only obvious part of the Voting Rights Act that it sort of requires this remedial action, but it`s an obvious part of the 15th Amendment. The 15th amendment is the only part of the Constitution that mentions race, and it prohibits racial discrimination in voting. And that`s because Congress -- and it gives Congress the power to enforce that. That`s because, obviously, we fought a civil war and then had a civil rights movement 100 years later because Black voters were being disenfranchised and their voices were not being heard.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  I thought pointed out very well today. Deuell Ross who was before the Supreme Court early this morning and stayed up be with us tonight, I appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROSS: Thank you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221005eia400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for October 4, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ryan Reilly, Charles Coleman, Yamiche Alcindor,  Shaq Brewster, Sam Stein, Steve Kovach \par \uc2 8,278 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I'm Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Right now, multiple court battles are unfolding, cases that go directly to the heart of this country's character and what it stands for. In Washington, the trial of five members of the anti-government group, the Oath Keepers, began with the prosecution using the defendants' own words against them, as they allegedly tried to concoct a rebellion to keep President Biden from taking power. In the Supreme Court, arguments have begun over a congressional voting map in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NAACP\uc2  claiming it's a tool of racial discrimination designed specifically to diminish the black vote. In fact, a lower court agreed with that, but with the High Court yet to weigh in, the disputed map is still official. Meaning it is the one that will be used in next month's midterm elections. And, finally, huge parts of Florida and the Southeast still struggling to get back on their feet after hurricane Ian, no doubt it will take months if not years, in some places.\par \par \uc2 Check out these pictures from just yesterday in Orlando. You can see how much of that city remains flooded. At last count, 107 people have been confirmed dead. More than 2300 have had to be rescued. For many of the survivors, a big problem is lack of electricity, more than 400,000 Floridians still without power nearly a week after the storm.\par \par \uc2 We start today with that dramatic Oath Keepers trial. I want to bring in NBC justice reporter Ryan Wiley -- Reilly, who is outside the U.S. District Court in DC. Cody Keenan was the chief White House speechwriter for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . His new book is called "Grace: President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and 10 days in the battle for America." And Charles Coleman is a civil rights attorney, former Brooklyn prosecutor, and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Ryan, take us inside that courtroom. How is the prosecution making its case?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, MSNBC JUSTICE REPORTER: Sure. So essentially, what you have right now is testimony happening from an \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent who was there on January 6, actually, and helped escort senators back to the Senate on that evening. But essentially, you also have this breakdown of this GoTo Meeting meeting, essentially a virtual meeting that happened in November of 2020, directly after the 2020 election in which this plot, according to prosecutors started to take form. There are 103 Oath Keepers on that call - - from that initial call. And Stewart Rhodes is there, talking about what they need to do, and actually mentions, ironically enough that he thinks the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is probably listening into the call. They, as it happened, didn't get that call in so much later.\par \par \uc2 But they -- he talks about essentially, they have to be careful about their words, they have to, you know, be very deliberate about what they're saying because they don't want to get themselves in legal jeopardy. And he says, in one of the -- in this call that, you know, the insurrection act is going to be essentially their cover. This claim that they're going to make, the - - and that Trump is going to invoke the Insurrection Act, and then all of their actions are going to be legal is what was sorts of the basis for that. But in one of these calls, he essentially says that's just legal cover for what they have to do.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, in other calls that the government has produced -- in other recordings that the government has produced, Stewart Rhodes is essentially saying that this plan is going forward with or without \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , depending on they -- you know, they want to have him the -- they want to give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the ability to step in and invoke the Insurrection Act and stop the peaceful transfer of power. But they're going to go ahead with this, even if he doesn't do anything, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan, thank you so much for that reporting. I know you'll update us as things are happening inside the courtroom. But, Charles, just from that reporting, it sounds like they contradicted their own defense and they've got it on tape.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Absolutely. I think that prosecutors, in this case, are doing exactly what you're supposed to do with a case like this. Because they weren't able to complete the act, their notion of intent reigns paramount. The most important thing in trying to establish an attempt is the intent. And using their words directly to actually show what it is that they were trying to do and what it is that they intended to do is exactly what prosecutors have to do in order to get a conviction in a case like this.\par \par \uc2 And for the defense, you kind of sort of hurt yourself when you've already laid out what it is that you're trying to do, but then try to use their defense as legal cover when you've already been on tape saying, well, that's just legal cover. So, the fact that they've laid that out and put their words, the actual defendants' words behind these actions really makes it apparent that this is what they intended to do and this is why they're looking at a conviction.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, there was this encrypted message that the prosecutors used in the opening argument, and it's Stewart Rhodes, who is saying this. "We aren't getting through this without a civil war." He said days after the insurrection that "my only regret is that they should have brought rifles. We could have fixed it right there and then." How does that play into a case for seditious conspiracy?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, again, it shows the fact that they were willing to go as far as enacting violence to actually bring this about. It doesn't necessarily change what the severity of the charges will be because those are already out there. But in terms of establishing the necessary element of intent around this attempt and exactly establishing what it is that they wanted to do and wanting to affect.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 That makes it crystal clear. They were doing anything and everything but violence -- enacting violence, but they were prepared to do so and they regretted not doing so. Hence his argument or his comments about, we should have bought rifles, which actually undercuts his defense, in part. He noted -- his attorneys noted during their opening statement that he went to great lengths to obey DC's strict gun laws. But when you listen to that tape and you hear his words, it actually makes it very clear that that wasn't a concern of his at all.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And watching this trial began to unfold, Cody. I immediately went back to the beginning of your book because there was something that you wrote about when you were crafting the speech for the 50th anniversary of Selma. And we actually saw in your book, some of the handwriting from \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , and it said this. He wrote.\par \par \uc2 There are places moments in America where the nation's destiny has been decided, many are sites of war, concord in Lexington, Appomattox, and Gettysburg. Selma is such a place. And then below it, he wrote. It was not a clash of armies but a clash of wills, a contest to determine the true meaning of America.\par \par \uc2 He had in 20 words really written sort of the thesis for your speech. And I wonder how you think about that when you look at what's happening today.\par \par \uc2 CODY KEENAN, FORMER DIRECTOR OF SPEECHWRITING FOR PRESIDENT OBAMA: Sure. I mean, that's the -- that's the thesis of my book too, you nailed it. And that's really been the story of America all along. It's that politics is a clash of wills. And it's we're always engaged in this contest to determine what we want America to be.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're going through a period where it's getting more violent, and you've got actual sitting senators encouraging this violence. So it's dark, but the entire history of America is the story of progress and backlash. And even though we're living through a period of backlash, there are ways out of it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, speaking of sitting senators, \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  this morning said this. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON, (R-WI): Now, some protesters did teach us all how you can use flag poles, that kind of stuff as weapons. But to call what happened on January 6th an armed insurrection, I just think is not accurate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Charles scoffed at that. You're shaking your head. It does seem like further than the number of years since you were in the White House if that makes sense. I mean, administrations change, things change, but it seems like we've come more than one and a half administrations.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN: Yes, things are getting pretty dark, you know. And we all saw the videos, we know what happened there, there were plenty of weapons that weren't just flagpoles. It does -- it does make one feel cynical about where we're going. But there are plenty of people out there working hard, doing a good work, trying to, you know, change things for the better. And, you know, that's one thing I hope this book does is to convince people that these are cyclical, right, as long as everybody's engaged in revolt.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But one of the things that does really well is it shows you -- and I was making the comparison this morning to my team when you go to the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame, and you see how like the Beatles rewrote some of the great songs that all of us know, you help us to understand how those things were crafted. It seems like the Democrats and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  are trying to craft a message around democracy, around the fight for democracy, the importance of what's happening in the Supreme Court, the importance of what's happening at these trials. Which would that message be? That's what you do. You craft messages.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN: Yes, they're all related, you know. And all of 2016, President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's messages were about the fragility of democracy, and that it's only as strong as we are as we get up and get involved into it. And I think what President Biden has been trying to do by explicitly labeling MAGA politicians as the people that are in charge, the people that need to be voted out of office is a pretty shrewd thing to do if he's trying to create a permission structure for, you know, your average Republican who may be turned off by this kind of behavior to actually say, maybe I'll give somebody else a shot.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let's talk a little bit, Charles, about everyone talks about the fact that there hasn't been a trial like this in what, 80 years?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Sure, right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Seditious conspiracy is something very difficult to prove. Obviously, they think they have a case. How important is the outcome for how this is viewed? What happened?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, Chris, it's extremely important. I think that you talking about a case that not only will be precedent-setting in terms of the narrative around January 6 and how we remember it, both legally, but then also, just in terms of what we historically come to, and how we resolve this conversation around what happened on January 6. I think many of us know, but for these people not to be convicted, what will ultimately happen if that were the case is that it leaves too much room for the narrative to be left to debate. And I think that we have to be very clear about the direction of this country if we really want to make a point and be clear about the fact that we do not support what happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 This has to result in a conviction because much more than the law, we're talking about a fight for the soul of this nation. And when you're talking about something of that gratitude -- of that grandiose nature, it has to be punctuated with the fact that the law -- the law that governs this land does not tolerate this sort of behavior under the notion of patriotism or anything else for that matter.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: When you use words like grandiose and when you talk about the critical importance, how important is it again, when you're crafting a message, Cody? And not everybody is writing a speech, right? But they're going out in there talking to constituents. How important is it to frame it in a way to help people understand can you help people understand the moment where we are in this moment, what's at stake?\par \par \uc2 KEENAN: At democracy itself. And everybody needs to be very clear about that, you know, there's no room for being mealy-mouthed or saying, oh, I didn't see it, or I'm not sure. It's --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Maybe it was an insurrection, maybe it wasn't didn't look like it to many.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN: Yes, and you always see people say, you know, well, I don't know, it's up to other people. You don't have to ask him. No, it's very clear. We can all see it. We've seen the videos. We know what happened. This was an insurrection. Democracy is at stake. And anyone who's running for office right now trying to fill roles explicitly saying that they will overturn the next election is somebody that needs to be voted down right away.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: You know, Chris, really quickly, I agree with that wholeheartedly. And I think it's important that we pay attention to how we got here. This has not happened overnight. This was a progression of false equivalencies and allowing certain ideas and ideologies to get daylight when they should not have been. This was a matter of us questioning media and news and getting to a point where we couldn't trust or felt that we couldn't trust what it was that we were learning. And that only polarized our discussion even further. And it's so important to understand that this progression had been going on for a number of years and brought us to this place because if we don't, we'll end up here again.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cody and Charles, you're both sticking with us. We have a lot more to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, a couple of other stories we want to get you caught up on. In Michigan, there's a trial involving a separate homegrown militia group that's begun part of what's been described as the largest domestic terrorism case in a generation. This one involves three men believed to be part of the paramilitary group known as the Wolverine Watchmen, accused of assisting in a plot to kidnap Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 . The militia group was said to be angry over Whitmer's locked-down policies during the early part of the pandemic. Jury selection began just on Monday and could wrap up as early as today.\par \par \uc2 Well, President Biden hasn't made it official but NBC News has learned. He told MSNBC host \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  he is going to run for reelection. Sharpton, who is also the head of the National Action Network apparently told his staff that during a private conversation at the White House last month, Biden was discussing his 2020 bid, and then told Sharpton "I'm going to do it again."\par \par \uc2 Coming up, critical arguments, the fight in the Supreme Court today that cuts to the very heart of voting rights in our country. Plus, Hurricane Ian's death toll climbs as historic search and rescue operations continue in Florida. We've got the latest live from Fort Myers ahead. And taking on \uc2 Apple\uc2 , the big changes that could be coming to help millions of people charge their phones and frankly a ton of other electronic devices. You're watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A critical decision impacting the future of voting rights in America is on the line today as the Supreme Court hears arguments involving charges of racial gerrymandering. The issue before the court is a disputed Alabama congressional map. It was drawn by the state's Republicans. Critics say it's an example of illegal racial gerrymandering because only one of the state's seven districts would be majority black, even though Alabama's population is 27 percent black. Challengers say the redrawn congressional map dilutes the black vote and argue the state should have a second majority-black district.\par \par \uc2 For more, let's bring in NBC's Yamiche Alcindor, Alaina Beverly, a former aide in the Obama White House who helped lead African American outreach in the 2008 campaign, and Cody Keenan is back with us. So, Yamiche, give us a little background on this case and tell us what you're hearing from people on each side.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, it's a big day here at the Supreme Court because oral arguments are now complete in this landmark case, Merrill v. Milligan. Now, on the plaintiff's side, they're really saying that African Americans, their voting power is being diluted in Alabama because they should have more than one majority black congressional district. They're saying that they want to break it up, which is now a district being represented by \uc2 Terri Sewell\uc2 , and they should have two congressional members in Congress but, of course, Alabama is saying -- the opposite saying no, the congressional districts are drawn fairly, that they are constitutional.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to both people on this side. I want to play for you some sounds of the Alabama secretary of state as well as former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  who has been focused on redistricting since leaving the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN MERRILL\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE, ALABAMA: People in our state have the opportunity to choose where they live. They can locate or relocate wherever they want to.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: That's absurd. That's offensive. Gerrymandering negatively impacts the nation as a whole but disproportionately negatively impacts people of color.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: So, there you have the Alabama Secretary of State \uc2 John Merrill\uc2 , who was mentioned in this landmark case Merrill v. Milligan. Um, he's saying that people, if they don't like the district that they're in if they don't like the representation, that they should either they can move or that they can have different people be elected to the Alabama State Legislature. But \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  is saying that that's absurd, that people should not have to move in order to get equal representation.\par \par \uc2 I tell -- I can tell you that I talked to both sides after the oral argument. The Alabama Attorney General, who was in the court today, he told me that he feels confident that Alabama was able to make his case. But I also talked to Evan Milligan, who is one of the plaintiffs who says he's holding out hope that even in a majority conservative court that they will be able to win this case.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What do you see, Alaina, is the heart of this case?\par \par \uc2 ALAINA BEVERLEY, FORMER NATIONAL DEPUTY DIRECTOR, AFRICAN AMERICAN ENGAGEMENT, OBAMA '08: Well, let's remember that this is the first redistricting that we've seen since Section 5 of the Voting Rights Act was struck down.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 And Section 5 was responsible for helping to prevent some of these voting changes that would dilute minority voting strength and have those changes submitted to the Department of Justice. So at the heart of this case, is what is left of the Voting Rights Act. It's what the strength of Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act is, which basically says that rules, practices, and procedures cannot deny or abridge the right to vote on the basis of race. And Supreme Court precedent, there's a case called Thornburg v. Gingles that lays out the criteria for when minority voters are having their voting strength diluted by practices like this.\par \par \uc2 And so what I'm concerned about is that we have --now have a overwhelmingly conservative court that does not see the value in judicial precedent, see Roe v. Wade being overturned, and the possibility that this precedent in the voting rights context could be overturned and then -- or greatly diluted, which would result in diluting the strength of minority voters. \uc2 Merrill\uc2  is outlandish in his claim that black voters should move in order to be -- to be able to have voting power or to have representation. That it's traced directly to the same reason why the Voting Rights Act was enacted in the first place.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to play, Alaina, some of what we heard this morning. Our reporters were out at a rally in support of more -- majority black districts in the state. Here's what they told us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY SHELTON, POWER COALITION: I'm not going to make my peace with being a second-class citizen.\par \par \uc2 MARTINA YVETTE ALLEN, YOUTH COMMUNITY ORGANIZER: We're moving from guessing how many jellybeans are in a jar to our young people guessing if they are even eligible to vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wondering if they're even eligible to vote, Alaina. In January, a lower court found Alabama's map to be discriminatory. That map though is still going to be used in this year's congressional election whatever happens in the Supreme Court. What does this mean? I mean, sure, you use -- address the long-term implications, but short term, there are implications too, right?\par \par \uc2 BEVERLY: Absolutely. Short term, this plan that would protect 27 percent of the state's African American -- African American voters of Alabama will be packed into one district, meaning that when it comes to the midterm elections, they will only be eligible to have a candidate, one candidate of choice. But it's not just this case, that these redistricting efforts have swept the country. So, we're also going to see this type -- this ruling will have implications for redistricting in North Carolina and other critical states for both whether or not Democrats are able to have a majority in the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans are now using this redistricting gerrymandering as one other tool in their arsenal to try to take to tip -- to take -- try to take the House and to try to take the Senate. Long term, this is about voting power for minority voters overall but in the short term, it also has raised political consequences for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cody, there was a Wall Street Journal editorial board editorial today, it said the controversy looks less like a battle over minority voting rights and more like a typical partisan dust-up over district lines and voting rights. The Voting Rights Act was intended as to shield to protect minority voting rights, but it has evolved into a sword to advance the interests of the established political parties.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's like this huge chasm on one side you have it's a dustup and on the other, the right to vote critical to our democracy is at stake. And you write specifically about Selma, about \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , about the fight for voting rights, and I wonder if it shocks you where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 KEENAN: It shocks me where we are right now but we've also been here before, several times throughout our history. The prologue of the book talks about the Selma speech where President \uc2 Obama\uc2  laid out that thesis we were talking about. He went down there on the 50th anniversary of people who were poor and powerless, and overwhelmingly black, getting their heads beaten. And just because they did or did March for the right to vote. And what could be more American than that, you know?\par \par \uc2 Instead of talking about this side says this. This side said that you say, the right to vote is sacred for everybody. And there's one thing that ties together everything we've been talking about today, whether it's the so- called Oath Keepers trying to prevent the presidential count from happening, whether it's the Wolverines in Michigan trying to kidnap a Democratic governor, whether it's this case, their -- MAGA Republicans are actively trying to suppress people who would vote for Democrats and just consolidate power on their own so that they can do whatever they want.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We only have a minute left but there -- this book really is about an intense 10-day period. And I mean it was intense, including when you were awaiting Supreme Court decisions on same-sex marriage and ObamaCare. And you asked yourself a few key questions. What if, in just days, the Supreme Court set the movements for universal health care and LGBTQ equality back years? What would those decisions tell the world about who we were? What were those decisions tell America about who we were? What would I write if that happened? What would you write now?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 KEENAN: I'd read a lot of the same thing I wrote before. I mean the -- all the events in this book that happen in those 10 days whether it was the racist shooting in Charleston or the Supreme Court deciding whether or not gay Americans are second-class citizens and they're you know married right, like the rest of us, whether or not working Americans deserve health care. The whole thing about this country is it comes back to this contest to determine the true meaning of America. Does everybody matter? Are we all equal? Who gets to decide this? And right now you've got people taking it into their own hands to try to decide it. What really matters is everybody showing up at the ballot box every single election and really just voting overwhelmingly to say that these things are wrong, and our ideals are right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I was covering the Obama White House during those 10 days. It's a great book, Cody, congratulations. It really was a riveting read and also just very revealing behind the scenes you open the curtain a little bit. So thank you for being here, Cody Keenan. Yamiche Alcindor, and Alaina Beverly, thank you as well. And be sure to check out Cody's new book. It's out today. It's called "Grace: President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and 10 days in the battle for America."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the CDC just ended its country-by-country program that warned American travelers about the COVID risks when they went on the road. It will now only issue warnings if there's an extreme concern. The agency said the change comes because fewer countries are actually testing and reporting the numbers of cases, so that makes it tough to assess the actual risk level.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Ian's death toll is now in triple-digit, we're live in hard-hit Fort Myers, plus how climate change made that storm much worse. A climate expert will explain next. You're watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:30]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Search and rescue teams are still frantically trying to find missing people across Florida six days after Hurricane Ian devastated the southeastern United States. There are now at least 107 confirmed deaths tied to the storm. In hard-Hit Lee County, where officials have been criticized for not ordering evacuations sooner than they did, 55 people have been confirmed dead.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC's Shaq Brewster, who is in hard-hit Fort Myers Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 And I understand, Shaq, there are also more than 200,000 people just in that county without power. What's the range of challenges you're seeing on the ground right now?\par \par \uc2 SHAQ BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, power remains to be a problem, especially in the neighborhood that I'm in right now. We stepped away from -- three miles from Fort Myers Beach, two miles from the closest river, and despite that, look at the damage that you're seeing. I mean, this is a neighborhood where in this house that you're looking at, they told me that they stayed inside, a family of three, and the water got up about four and a half feet. They went up to the attic as the storm was coming through.\par \par \uc2 But, look, every single house on this street in this entire neighborhood has all the entire contents of the home out on the curb. You're seeing refrigerators, you're seeing dressers, you're seeing personal items. And you know, I came around and I spoke to one of the families here. Her name is Sue. She's an elderly couple. She was spending the past couple -- she's an elderly lady, I should say. She spent the past couple of days pulling her stuff out of the house with help from her family.\par \par \uc2 Look at what it looked like inside her home and how high that water mark was compared to her height.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SUE, FORT MYERS RESIDENT: There's nothing you can do about it. You've got to do what you've got to do, you know.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: How emotional does it get?\par \par \uc2 SUE: Bad. This is everything we have worked our whole life for. And it's gone. In a flash.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: I don't know if you can tell from the initial shot there, but that was a freshly remodeled kitchen. She said that she was planning to stay and ride out the storm in her house and then her friend gave her a call and said you need to leave right now. And that's when her and her husband went ahead and evacuated. But so many people in this area have a lot of recovering to do.\par \par \uc2 We did just see a FEMA team come through and do their initial assessments, taking pictures, checking and making sure there were no emergencies in any of these homes. And one thing you're also seeing, neighbor helping neighbor, people coming through, providing food, water, whatever folks need here as they have the long recovery ahead.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That is absolutely heartbreaking. Story after story after story.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for bringing it to us, Shaq. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in \uc2 University of Pennsylvania\uc2  presidential distinguished professor and director of the Penn Center for Science Sustainability and the Media, Michael Mann, who is also the author of "The New Climate War."\par \par \uc2 It's good to see you, Michael. Look, there was this new study. It was very quickly conducted after Hurricane Ian made landfall. It showed climate change added at least 10 percent more rain to Hurricane Ian. And this research wasn't peer reviewed, but in your latest piece for "The Guardian," you say Ian is no anomaly. So I guess do you think the 10 percent sounds reasonable? And is this the new normal?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MANN, UNIVERSITY OF PENNSYLVANIA PRESIDENTIAL DISTINGUISHED PROFESSOR: Yes, thanks, Chris. It's good to be with you. And it's actually worse than a new normal. A new normal makes it sound like, OK, we've arrived in this new situation. We just got to figure out how to deal with it. But this will all continue to get worse as we continue to warm up the planet through carbon pollution. It's a moving baseline and every moving baseline of more intense storms, of storms that bring more flooding rains and more damage.\par \par \uc2 This latest study that you mentioned, it's a solid study by some leading researchers. We sometimes call this an ad hoc detection study because it's sort of done in real time. It hasn't gone through peer review. And so it's a preliminary analysis. What it does show is that in the climate change made this storm more powerful and it made it more of a flooding event. That's something sort of we know from basic physics. You warm up the planet, there's more energy in these storms. So they intensify more.\par \par \uc2 You put more moisture into the atmosphere, so you can get more rainfall. And this study shows that by looking at two different sorts of simulations. One, where you run a climate model in a pre-industrial climate before we had increased the carbon pollution in the atmosphere, another where you account for the human increase in carbon emissions. And when you see in the latter case that an event like this happens much more often than it does in the other case, then you can attribute climate change.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 You can say climate change made this event much more likely. It made it -- this storm stronger. It put more rainfall.\par \par \uc2 One last point here. If anything, this study is conservative. This claim that climate change increased the flooding by 10 percent, that's probably a lower bound because the model that's used in this case has to make approximations, for example, in how you model the way that a stronger storm pulls more moisture into the storm. That affect isn't really that well captured in this model. And we know that that effect does contribute to the flooding rains with these storms.\par \par \uc2 It contributed to the huge flooding with Ian and the flooding that we saw a year ago with Ada as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I have to is you this really quickly but I want to get it in. When we're looking for solutions, we're seeing a lot of new strategies pop up. We're dealing with the effects, right, things like roof shingles that are resistant to high winds. But if we're talking about structural advances, are they essentially band-aids when the patient, meaning the planet, is bleeding out?\par \par \uc2 MANN: Yes, you're exactly right. Some of this stuff is sort of deck chairs on the Titanic. Look, if we don't get the source of this problem -- don't get at the source of the problem which is our continued burning of fossil fuels, the warming of the planet and the more energy there is to intensity these storms, if we don't attack that problem, then there's no amount of adaptation that is going to keep these impacts within our adaptive capacity, within our resilience.\par \par \uc2 So yes, we need to take measures to make ourselves more resilient given the impacts we're already seeing, but we have to prevent it from getting worse. And that requires policy action. That requires an even faster decarbonization of your climate and legislation that will help us accomplish that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Michael Mann, always great to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MANN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Coming up, the new reporting on Mar-a-Lago's document case that suggests former President Trump was notified of missing classified documents but kept hundreds of them anyway.\par \par \uc2 But first, saying good-bye to the queen of country music. Loretta Lynn's family confirming she died peacefully in her sleep this morning. She was 90 years old. Lynn introduced herself to the world through songs like "Coal Miner's Daughter." Born in a holler in Kentucky's Appalachian Mountains, she rose not just to stardom, but became a titan of the music industry. She had 51 top 10 hits, four Grammy Awards and a career that broke down doors to make things just a little bit easier for the next generation of women in country music. Four years ago, our Jenna Bush Hagar asked her what she's grateful for when she looks back on her life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LORETTA LYNN, COUNTRY MUSIC SINGER: My kids. My family. That's what I feel grateful for. And them making it because I have taken care of my family by doing this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:07]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now to those revealing new details in the case of former President Trump and the materials he brought to Mar-a-Lago from the White House. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports that at the beginning of this year, Trump asked one of his lawyers to tell the National Archives that he had turned over all the requested materials by the agency for Mar-a-Lago. Well, his lawyer refused unsure if that fact was in fact true, according to people familiar with the matter. And of course we now know there were more documents there.\par \par \uc2 And NBC News reports that all the way back in May of 2021, the National Archives told Trump's lawyers that some of the presidential records it was missing included his correspondence with North Korean leader Kim Jong-un and a letter President \uc2 Obama\uc2  left for Trump.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in "Politico" White House editor Sam Stein, back with us civil rights attorney, former Brooklyn prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst, Charles Coleman.\par \par \uc2 Charlies, how legally tough of a position did the former president put his lawyer in?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Extremely, Chris. You know, this is a guy who does not care about the precarious positions that he puts his attorneys in and whether he is ultimately threatening their livelihood and their licensure. Had this attorney made that representation and been wrong or been something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew was not correct, he could potentially have been disciplined and lost his license. That's not a concern for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before. An attorney, it has to be of paramount importance.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Sam, the NBC reporting is interesting on these two documents, Kim Jong-un and \uc2 Obama\uc2  letters. Given what we know about Trump, I mean, should we be surprised that those are among the things he apparently wanted to keep?\par \par \uc2 SAM STEIN, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE EDITOR: I'm completely not surprised that those are some of the memento that he wants to keep. Trump is a classic hoarder of those types of things. If you've been to his office, if you've ever stepped foot in Trump Tower, we know that his walls are adorned with magazine covers, letters, memorabilia, items like that. Those are the types of --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Including magazine covers that didn't actually exist.\par \par \uc2 STEIN: Yes. There are occasionally the "TIME" magazine covers that are nonexistent. And to a degree, obviously, this is driven by self-interest. I think where he runs into trouble obviously is that these documents fall under the Presidential Records Act, but also beyond that, it's not just that these are personal mementos, but some of them had classified markings, markings that said they were some of the most important secrets that the government could obtain intelligence wise.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, yes, it's all fun and games I guess a little bit that he kept his letters to Kim Jong-un, but there are serious national security implications to the documents that he brought with him to Mar-a-Lago, too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, I mean, you've got to separate those from a legal standpoint, right, Charles? There are things that even if you're not supposed to have them, you can understand, wow, I would like to keep that if I were him.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I understand we want to keep very different thing legally. Is it very different thing legally if it was classified?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: It is, but at the end of the day, it's important to understand, Chris, there's a process for all of that. There is a process for I would like to hold on to these mementos, and he ignored it entirely. Not to mention from a legal standpoint, this completely undercuts any argument that he either did not know or was not aware of these documents being in his position. It also undercuts this narrative that, you know, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  stormed in and I had no idea what this was about.\par \par \uc2 You had prior notice in every opportunity to return these documents that did not belong to you beforehand literally for months. And you blew them off. You ignored it and then ultimately this was the result.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So I guess, Sam, the question then becomes -- you know, I was going to say why did he do it, but he's been in a position -- no, he's been in a position for much of his life where he could simply say no or decide that he wanted to do something else and not really have a legal consequence follow him in any significant way, right?\par \par \uc2 STEIN: Yes, and this is not to excuse the action because Charles is absolutely right, there's an actual process by which you're supposed to follow. And certainly, it's notable that Trump, who came into office, criticizing \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's handling of records, is himself embroiled in a records classification and handling issue.\par \par \uc2 But, to your point, Chris, I think, you know, Trump obviously hailed from a domain in which he was the master. He ran a family-run business. He was always the boss. He did what he wanted and his lawyers cleaned up the mess after him. And I think that mentality did not change once he entered the White House. The laws that governed how you operate and what you do in the intel and the information that goes by your desk simply were immaterial to him.\par \par \uc2 And so you get anecdotal reports of aides basically sweeping documents into boxes and carting them off without any care for what the law actually said. This is a mentality that, you know, endears Trump to a lot of people, but ultimately it's one that runs afoul with the law and he's suffering the consequences for that right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Sam Stein, Charles Coleman, thanks for sticking around. Appreciate you guys very much.\par \par \uc2 A new development in the Georgia Senate race that could have major implications about which party gains control of the Senate. So yesterday the "Daily Beast" published a story alleging that GOP Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who has campaigned on his anti-abortion views, encouraged a woman to have an abortion after he got her pregnant in 2009. According to "The Daily Beast," the woman, who asked not to be identified for privacy concerns, provided a receipt from the abortion clinic, a bank deposit that includes an image of Walker's check reimbursing her for the cost of the procedure and what the woman said was a get well card signed by Walker.\par \par \uc2 The paper says it corroborated the details with a close friend of the woman, who was someone she told at the time and who according to the woman and her friend took care of her in the days after the procedure.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has not yet verified the woman's allegations or independently reviewed the documents. When reached for comment, Walker's campaign pointed to his responses on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , where he called the charges a flat-out lie. However, Walker's son soon took to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to call out his father, quote, "How dare you lie and act as though you're some moral Christian upright man. You've lived a life of destroying other people's lives." Walker replied, "I love my son no matter what."\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who was chair of the committee working to elect GOP senators, supported Walker saying that the NRSC and Republicans stand with him.\par \par \uc2 And if you're tired of traveling with multiple charges to keep all of your electronics alive, boy, are you not alone. A major new move that could make some serious adjustments to how people charge their devices. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:45]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK, so here it is. I know you've got one. A bag, a box of chargers. A pile of them, right? This one's been in my desk for years. I almost never seem to have the right charger anyway. But today, European lawmakers overwhelmingly approved a new law that would require a common charger for virtually all electronic devices. We're talking mobile phones, tablets, cameras, e-readers, ear buds sold in the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By the end of 2024 they must be equipped with a USB type C charging port. This massive overhaul would impact companies like \uc2 Apple\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in CNBC's technology correspondent, Steve Kovach.\par \par \uc2 Steve, given this pile of cords and I think pretty much everybody's got one, it feels revolutionary. Could it be in Europe?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KOVACH, CNBC TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it's already OK for people, Chris, who have Android phones because they're already using that USB type C. This law is really going to target \uc2 Apple\uc2  customers especially iPhone customers which has been using that smaller plug, that lightning plug for about a decade now. So what's going to have to happen is \uc2 Apple\uc2  needs to comply with that law by 2024 and start making new iPhones with that USB-C connector.\par \par \uc2 Now this shouldn't be too hard for \uc2 Apple\uc2  to do because it already puts USB- C in some of its devices. The newer -- if you go out and buy an iPad today, for example, it's already going to have that connector. So it might be an awkward transition if you buy a new iPhone two or three years from now and you have to replace all your old \uc2 Apple\uc2  chargers with new ones, that's going to be a pain of course. But if you're an Android user, you get to use your phone just as you have been using it for the last five or six years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Apple\uc2  didn't want this, though, right. They say it's going to stifle innovation, the exact opposite of a headline I saw which says it's a great day for consumers. What's the real story here?\par \par \uc2 KOVACH: Yes. It depends on if you're on E.U. regulator or if you're \uc2 Apple\uc2  trying to sell more iPhones I guess. What \uc2 Apple\uc2 's argument here is that what if someone comes up with a better way to charge your phone or plug it into your computer that's faster or better or charges it in a better way. This keeps people boxed in or manufacturers like \uc2 Apple\uc2  or \uc2 Samsung\uc2  boxed in from inventing something new or better. \uc2 Apple\uc2  is also pushing towards removing ports from the phone.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 You might remember a couple of years ago, they took away that headphone jack and everyone had a panic attack because you can no longer plug in headphones. Well, they've been slowly making moves to take away the charging port as well so this could damage their plans to make the iPhone just completely no charging ports and go full wireless. So \uc2 Apple\uc2  is not happy with this decision.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We only have 30 seconds left, but what are the chances something like this might half in the U.S.?\par \par \uc2 KOVACH: Well, that's the thing. These are global companies, Chris, so when \uc2 Samsung\uc2  or \uc2 Apple\uc2  makes a phone, they usually sell the same device across the world. So E.U. is a significant market, so if that change happens on an iPhone in the E.U., it's likely going to happen in the U.S. and around the world, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I don't know if it's going to help me with my mess, but we shall see.\par \par \uc2 KOVACH: Good luck.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Steve Kovach, thank you so much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KOVACH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That's going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to joins us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. Stay tuned. Much more news ahead. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good to be with you. I'm Katy Tur. We got a new piece of evidence in the investigation into the classified documents held at Mar-a-Lago. This one pointing right at the former president.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221005eia400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for September 30, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Liz McLaughlin, David Jolly, Jessica Layton, Melissa  Murray \par \uc2 6,907 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:10]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Recovery mode.\par \par \uc2 Right now, much of the southeast is either reeling or recovering from Hurricane Ian. President Biden issuing a federal disaster declaration today for North Carolina, as even Northeastern States prepared to deal with Ian`s path as a downgraded tropical cyclone. The historically destructive storm made landfall for the second time yesterday, this time in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 More than 180,000 residents lost power almost immediately. That number reported to have grown to nearly half a million as the storm has carved its way through the Carolinas. Of course, nowhere has been more devastated than Florida, where Ian`s wrath after touching down Wednesday has 77 people confirmed dead, homes literally washed away, entire communities recovering from being nearly submerged.\par \par \uc2 The President planning to visit the State to thank its first responders, said yesterday that Ian`s destruction in Florida is almost unmatched for our nation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The situation in Florida is far more devastating. We`re just beginning to see the scale of that destruction. It is likely to rank among the worst of the nation`s -- in the worst of the nation`s history. You will have all seen the -- seen on television, homes and property wiped out. It`s going to take months, years to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We`ll bring you the very latest on Hurricane Ian throughout the hour, including, of course, the Federal response.\par \par \uc2 But before we go any further, I just wanted to add that my heart is with those who have need of help and need it most right now. I myself watched the devastation from my travels this week, back and forth to Washington, which included some time at the Congressional Black Caucus Annual Conference as head of National Action Network, participating and speaking and taking the pulse of Black political America barely five weeks before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 That pulse was quickened this weekend by the formal swearing in of Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  to the Supreme Court, which enters its next session in just two days.\par \par \uc2 What`s at stake in this session? That`s coming up later in the show.\par \par \uc2 But first, let`s start with correspondent Liz McLaughlin, who is near Fort Myers, Florida in a town called Matlacha. I hope I`m saying it right.\par \par \uc2 What`s the latest on recovery --\par \par \uc2 LIZ MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What`s the latest on recovery and the road ahead for the people of Florida that you can tell us?\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: So, this is historic Matlacha, it`s sort of tricky word based on how it`s spelled, but you can see here, I mean, this is just crumbles of concrete, the main artery to the heart of Pine Island and the only way to get there. So, it looks more like an earthquake perhaps than a hurricane. And now, this island behind me just completely shut off from civilization.\par \par \uc2 They`re having to go by boat to deliver supplies, to rescue people who are now trapped in their waterlogged homes after surviving this monstrous storm that`s left you know, nearly nothing behind here. It looks like a blender, you know, chewed up this whole community and spit it back out again.\par \par \uc2 Power lines just tangled in with little bits of everyone`s lives. A Peloton, a toilet, a refrigerator, and then some homes just completely wiped off the map. They`ve been here for decades, and now, it looks like they never were.\par \par \uc2 The death toll continues to rise, now 77, but so does the number of people saved, more than a thousand now as Coast Guard crews, we see a boat going by right now, going to and from Pine Island to try to get people who are still trapped there.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with someone -- a couple and their dog, Benson -- who just got off the boat about how they are feeling. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE STILLSON, RESIDENT OF PINE ISLAND, FLORIDA: It is apocalyptic in St James area.\par \par \uc2 Our friend helped a couple. They spent the night under a table. Their roof blew off. We said goodbye to our neighbors, John and Mary and --\par \par \uc2 KIM STILLSON, RESIDENT OF PINE ISLAND, FLORIDA: Oh, I know.\par \par \uc2 J. STILLSON: She was like --\par \par \uc2 K. STILLSON: They don`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 J. STILLSON: They have nowhere to go and they`re afraid to leave. I mean, they`ve got --\par \par \uc2 K. STILLSON: It`s hard -- it`s hard to come to that reality of where things are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: This couple of saying that this community is so close-knit, it was hard for them to leave. They had neighbors who were over 80 years old, who hid under a table when their roof blew off.\par \par \uc2 And so some are scared and just grappling with what to do. The road to rebuild will be a long one, and in some cases, may be impossible, but they`re starting to work that direction. Florida Governor here saying that he needs support from the Biden administration, asking for that, though 10 years ago as a Congressman opposed aid for victims of Hurricane Sandy -- Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you, Liz for this great reporting out of the west coast of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now as Marcus Coleman, a Director of the Department of Homeland Security Center for Faith Based and Community Partnerships.\par \par \uc2 Director Coleman, thank you for being with us tonight. Let me start with saying President Biden has issued a Federal Disaster Declarations, I would say in plural, in Florida and for both Carolinas for the devastation of the kind we`ve seen in Florida. Where do Federal recovery efforts stand now and in the Carolinas, where I`d imagine you`re still trying to assess the damage done in the last day?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS COLEMAN, DIRECTOR OF THE DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY CENTER FOR FAITH BASED AND COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIPS: Reverend Sharpton, thank you for having me on this program. And you`re right, there is a substantial amount of damage in multiple states. I just want to start by giving my sincere prayers and condolences to those that have lost loved ones, lost homes, and lost families.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has enacted FEMA and the entire Federal family to be in full support of the people of Florida, and we continue to coordinate and support closely those that are impacted by the Hurricane Ian from South Carolina, North Carolina, and of course, Georgia as well.\par \par \uc2 We are working across a broad coalition, not just Federal partners, but many of our non-government organizations as well, because this is traumatic for a lot of people and it is something that we know we`re going to be dealing with and working through for a long time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, of course, in addition to restoring power and clearing damage, you still have people missing or trying to get to safety. What are the biggest challenges facing the thousands of Federal responders, FEMA has actively engaged in rescue efforts across the southeast right now, Director Coleman?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, thankfully, we`ve been able to be coordinated closely with all of our State counterparts by having people and commodities pre- positioned in conjunction with the State. I`d say some of the largest challenges that we`re facing, and that we know that survivors are facing. One is mental health and emotional and spiritual care.\par \par \uc2 I was able to be on the line with Dr. Neil Prem (ph) from the All Healers Mental Health Alliance and we were talking about just the sheer shock and trauma that people are experiencing right now.\par \par \uc2 So, we want to make sure that people can get access to the mental health that they need, but also that people know the ways that they can jumpstart their recovery. And the way that you can do that from FEMA is if you call 1-800-621-3362. Once again, that is 1-800-621-3362. For those disaster survivors in Florida, that begins your process to start the long road to recovery.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Director Coleman, I have to ask this. Obviously, some communities are better situated than others to deal with the devastation caused by Ian. I wonder what care is being taken by FEMA to ensure that vulnerable communities, communities of color, get the resources they need in these earliest stages of recovery, because that has not always been the case when we face disasters in the past.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: I appreciate your question, Reverend Sharpton, and one of the things that we have done is we`ve brought more organizations in. So, we are in contact with the coalition of partners, including the \uc2 National Urban League\uc2 , the \uc2 NAACP\uc2 , and the Institute for Diversity and Inclusion in Emergency Management to ensure those folks that are migrants, farmworkers, domestic workers and the people that keep economies running, are getting the help that we need and that as we are delivering our recovery programs, that we are keeping people first, not just in how we disseminate information, but how we walk with people through the mini bureaucratic process that often come up in times of disasters.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, speaking from your role the Director of Homeland Security Center for Faith Based and Community Partnership, and I know of some of the work there with our people as President of the National Action Network, Reverend Nelson Rivers and others.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: How is the Federal response coordinating with churches, shelters, food kitchens, to deliver aid on the ground right now? What can our viewers do to help with those efforts?\par \par \uc2 [17:10:25]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: So, there are a few options. One, and I`ll say with the African Methodist Episcopal Church, I joined several of their Bishops and Pastors for a coordinating call that they were having. There is an organization, the National Voluntary Organizations Active in Disasters across multiple States, they`re holding daily coordinating calls.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, we want people to support those organizations that they know and trust on the ground locally.\par \par \uc2 From the members of the National Panhellenic Council to the National Baptist Convention to National Action Network, we know that there are many affiliate chapters in communities that may -- that are providing immediate support right now. So, to the extent that people can, in terms of donating anything, cash-related or any other resources, I would say go to your local trusted organizations that you know in Florida, and if you don`t know a national organization in Florida, start with some of those national organizations that you know, and trust that may have presence in Florida.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On that last point about charity, Director, we`ve already seen warnings from State officials in Florida and the Carolinas warning against charity scams related to Hurricane Ian. How can well-meaning people make sure their donations are getting to organizations that are actually doing the work?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: So, we want to make sure that people are going again to organizations that they know and trust already. I`ll say at FEMA. We do have a FEMA volunteer and donate page.\par \par \uc2 We also have an organization {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.NVOAD.org"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.NVOAD.org}}}\uc2 . That takes you to more than 70 national trusted members from the National Baptist Convention to Salvation Army to Catholic charities and others.\par \par \uc2 But as I said, again, if you`re a member of the Lynx, if you`re a member of the National Panhellenic Council organization, it`s likely that your organization has a trusted Disaster Relief Fund as well.\par \par \uc2 And so we want to make sure that people lean in to the organizations that they know and trust that are going to be there for the people of Florida while we also on the Federal level continue to support the people in Florida and all impacted States as well.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Director Marcus Coleman, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Coming up: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  seems to have a change of heart when it comes to the Biden administration. I`ll talk about his about-face with one of his former congressional colleagues, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:16:49]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Let`s discuss today`s biggest topics with my political panel.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Tom Perez\uc2 , former DNC Chair and former Labor Secretary under President Obama; and David Jolly, former Republican Congressman of Florida and now a member of the Forward Party.\par \par \uc2 David, I want to first start off in your Home State of Florida by asking you about the recovery efforts after Hurricane Ian, you and your family were fortunately able to evacuate. The devastation left behind by this storm is a stark reminder of Hurricane Sandy, nearly a decade ago in the New York region.\par \par \uc2 Now then, a decade ago New York region, then Congressman \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , was one of two House members from Florida to oppose the Sandy Package. Now, that he is the Governor of Florida, he is making the appeal to the Biden administration for Federal assistance to help rebuild.\par \par \uc2 Now, you served with Governor DeSantis in Congress. What do you make of DeSantis about-face on disaster funding now that he is Florida`s governor?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. It is an example of where you stand is based on where you sit, I suppose. And you`re right, look, Florida was well-prepared for this storm. There`s an infrastructure of storm preparation, but we`re still in the stages of rescue of human life and preservation of human life, and the State is responding very well.\par \par \uc2 But that the resulting economic loss is disastrous. They would wreck the Florida economy if the State had to absorb it. It already has the Florida insurance -- homeowners insurance market, tinkering on the brink of collapse as well. And so you find in \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Governor who now has to do something that is antithetical to his core ideological convictions and has to explain that to his Republican followers who have championed this, no government approach.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  must be a big government Republican, and here is the critical thing, Rev, there is nobody that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  needs more tonight than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because the way the funding of recovery happens is the Biden administration, not \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  will have to make an emergency appeal to Congress to pass an emergency spending bill likely by mid- December, and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  needs Joe Biden`s advocacy for that. It`s an interesting chapter for the Governor from Florida.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, it`s funny, my earpiece went in and out. You said, if I heard you right, there is no one \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  need more than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , did I get that right?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, I thought I heard you right.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  needs \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Listen, he also needs \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , a hard spot for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, keep it keeping going, because he does. I mean, if this wasn`t so grave, I would prolong it, but I just want people to understand that reality has a way of making people come off their rhetoric and deal with really governing where people`s lives are at stake.\par \par \uc2 Tom, let`s turn to the January 6 Committee investigation. Ginni Thomas, conservative activist and wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  voluntarily spoke with the panel earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In an opening statement obtained by NBC News, Thomas told the panel she still believed the 2020 election was stolen, though she denies discussing any efforts she made to challenge the results with either White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , or her own husband.\par \par \uc2 Now, do you think these ongoing revelations about what happened on January 6th are going to resonate with voters as they head to the polls in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 You were a great DNC Chair. You have your hand on the pulse of voters better than most?\par \par \uc2 TOM PEREZ\uc2 , FORMER DNC CHAIR AND FORMER LABOR SECRETARY UNDER PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, I think it`s a stark reminder that our democracy still hangs in the balance. I mean, she is -- her statements are remarkable, but they are not remarkable in the sense that so many people have said this, Rev.\par \par \uc2 And we have to understand that there are so many things on the ballot right now -- healthcare, disaster relief, and, frankly, democracy as we know it. And these races and I -- you and I spent a lot of time together when I was DNC Chairman and when I was enforcing the Civil Rights Act, and labor laws. And we know that it`s really important who the Governors are.\par \par \uc2 It is really important who controls the Senate; it`s really important who controls the House. It`s really important who Secretaries of State are. In this November election so many consequences are going to follow from it for the 2024 presidential because so many of the people who are about to get elected, are going to play a really important role.\par \par \uc2 And when you have Ginni Thomas saying that and I find it just truly remarkable that she never discussed this with the Chief of Staff and her husband for that matter, but the stakes couldn`t be higher and I hope everybody gets to the polls.\par \par \uc2 If people can`t be motivated now to get out there and vote, I don`t know when they`ll be motivated.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is set to hold a rally tonight in Michigan. Yesterday, he unleashed a tirade at \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  on his Truth Social platform, in which he said the Senate minority leader had a: "Death wish" and called his Asian wife, "Coco Chow." In another post, he referred to "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " journalist, Maggie Haberman as a maggot.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans have been able to tune out Trump since he has been banned from platforms such as \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , but are you concerned the former President`s rhetoric is getting more dangerous now that he is only talking to people who agree with him?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, look. The statement where words of somebody who appears to be in trouble personally, emotionally, and psychologically, I have no idea how to explain it other than its racist, xenophobic, hateful, and dangerous and we have to call it what it is.\par \par \uc2 He still commands at least half of the Republican Party and nobody has demonstrated the ability to topple him. And until somebody does, it is important that we call out what it was -- racist, xenophobic, hateful, and dangerous. Those were the words of the former President.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tom, you you`re the former Chair, as I said of the DNC. So, I want to get you to weigh in on the split developing inside the Democratic Party about how to approach the midterms.\par \par \uc2 According to "The Hill": "Democrats disagree on the best midterm message -- pro-Biden or anti-Trump. One side wants to push against the MAGA ideology; another side wants to focus on Democrats` policies to keep them in power, and a third group wants to do both."\par \par \uc2 Which strategy do you think is the most effective? And is it possible to balance between the two?\par \par \uc2 PEREZ: I think you can draw a balance and I would start with making sure we remind voters of all the points that we`re putting on the board. Look at what President Biden has done for seniors, capping drug costs at $2,000.00. Medicare Part B premiums are going to go down next year, the first time in a decade.\par \par \uc2 Look at all the infrastructure investments, and by the way, Governor DeSantis opposes infrastructure investments, but wants to show up at all the ribbon cuttings for those infrastructure projects, and he and so many other Republicans are doing the same thing.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think it`s really important for us to make sure we are outlining that we have people`s backs and we`re working on the issues that matter most. Ordinarily, at this point, Rev, this is a referendum on the President, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is inserting himself and I think he is doing us a favor and all of these Extreme candidates are doing us a favor by reminding Independent voters that I voted against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2020 for a reason.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:10]\par \par \uc2 PEREZ: And now the issue of abortion is a huge issue across America. The Republicans are the -- they`re the dog that caught the car. Now, they have to own this. And people -- you saw it in Kansas, you saw it and that New York special, women and others are motivated to get out there and vote.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t think it`s an either or. I think we can -- I think we can highlight our accomplishments and in the meantime, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reminds us that he is extreme and all the candidates he is exposing and supporting are extreme.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you very much, David Jolly and \uc2 Tom Perez\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION after the break.\par \par \uc2 But first, today`s top stories with my colleague, Jessica Layton.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LAYTON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Thanks, Rev.\par \par \uc2 And stories that we`re watching for all of you at this hour right now: The confirmed fatalities from Hurricane Ian are now at 77. That`s according to the Florida Medical Examiner`s Commission.\par \par \uc2 As the remnants of Ian make their way through the Carolinas, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  approved an Emergency Declaration for North Carolina, ordering Federal assistance for that state.\par \par \uc2 Russian forces withdrew from the key eastern Ukrainian City of Lyman on Saturday marking a significant setback for Kremlin operations. And by the way, this was just a day after Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  had declared that that city was now part of Russia.\par \par \uc2 And the \uc2 Danish Energy Agency\uc2  says the \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  pipeline appears to have stopped leaking natural gas. The rupture of the pipeline, which is one of the two running under the Baltic Sea from Russia to Germany has led to what is now likely the biggest single release of the greenhouse gas methane ever recorded.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court begins its new term on Monday. Yesterday, the Court held a historic ceremony to welcome the Court`s first African-American female Justice.\par \par \uc2 Justice Jackson will join the liberal minority, but it is the six to three majority of conservative Justices who will be steering the Court. Last session, the High Court made a series of controversial rulings, most notably the reversal of Roe vs. Wade, and in the next nine months, it will wade into questions about race, LGBTQ rights, and Federal elections.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Melissa Murray, an attorney, Professor of Law at \uc2 New York University\uc2 , and former \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  Clerk for Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 . Thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Miss Murray, let me go right to it and let`s start with Justice Jackson.\par \par \uc2 What role was she played on the Court in this upcoming session, and in the years to come?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, ATTORNEY, PROFESSOR OF LAW AT \uc2 NEW YORK UNIVERSITY\uc2 , AND FORMER \uc2 US SUPREME COURT\uc2  CLERK FOR JUSTICE SONIA SOTOMAYOR: What`s been said any time a new Justice joins the Court, the introduction of that Justice really changes the dynamics. So at least for the short term, Justice Jackson is going to have that kind of disruptive effect as the rest of the Court sort of recalibrates to accommodate her new position on that Court.\par \par \uc2 But as you say, she will be part of a three-Justice liberal minority. There is not going to be a lot of room there on the cases that really matter. She is likely to be in dissent in most of those cases, but she may have the opportunity to form some unlikely coalitions of people like Justice Gorsuch, for example on criminal justice issues, but I think she is going to be a lot like \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was at the beginning of his career, really playing a long game, and hoping that in time the Court`s dynamics and personnel will change, and she`ll find herself part of majority eventually.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, America`s confidence in the judicial system is at historic lows. The new Gallup Poll finds less than half of respondents have a "Great deal or fair amount of trust in the judicial system." That`s a 20- percentage-point drop from just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 You follow these Justices closely. How are they responding to this widespread dissatisfaction with their work?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, for a group of people who say they don`t read the tea leaves and don`t read the polls, the justices have been at great pains to say that they`re not concerned about these claims of illegitimacy, but that only suggests that they actually are quite concerned about these claims of illegitimacy.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen the Chief Justice talk about this -- Justice Alito, Justice Kagan, even Justice Breyer in retirement -- so this definitely something that they`re thinking about, and they should be because the Court has found itself in something of a sticky wicket.\par \par \uc2 After many years of reaffirming Roe vs. Wade, most recently in 2020, this Court just last June, overturned that decision, and there is nothing else to explain it. But the fact that there is a new conservative six to three super majority on the Court, the law has not changed, but the Court has, and people know that and they have questions about that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, many of the issues to be considered by the Supreme Court will have profound implications for communities of color. On Tuesday, oral arguments will begin on Milligan vs. Merrill, a case that will decide whether the Alabama Republicans violated the law with a redistricting plan that included just one majority Black district in a state over 25 percent Black.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court already blocked a Lower Court Order that made -- that would have at least allowed for two majority Black districts for the upcoming midterms.\par \par \uc2 What`s your take on this case? Because it seems like the future of voting rights, what is left of it is at stake in this case?\par \par \uc2 [17:35:03]\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I think that`s exactly right, Rev.\par \par \uc2 So in 2019, the Court in a case called Rucho versus Common Cause held that the Court -- the Federal Courts can be no place to remedy the impact of partisan gerrymandering. So, it reserved that to the States. But there still remained the opportunity to remedy racial gerrymandering under Section II of the Voting Rights Act, and that`s what`s at stake in this case that`s going to be argued on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, the Court already entered a stay that allowed those maps that had been viewed to be racially suppressive to go into effect for this midterm election. So, the damage has already been done to some degree in Alabama. This is the map that`s going to be used for this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 But going forward, the question in this case is whether or not Section II of the Voting Rights Act, a law that this Court has already hobbled beyond recognition in Shelby County versus Holder, whether it will continue to be a potent means of remedying suppressive voter laws and redistricting plans.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I`m glad you broke that down, because I don`t think a lot of voters understand what`s left of the Voting Rights Act could be wiped away with this particular case that is going to be heard on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Some of the most important issues this Court could end up considering, I`ll put it that way, are the ones that are impossible to predict. I`m talking about possible cases brought forward by our highly litigious former President Trump -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- who has numerous legal disputes he could try to push all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 There is also the matter of his followers, some of whom are running for office on platforms of election denialism and are likely to dispute the outcome of their own races if they end up losing.\par \par \uc2 How likely is it that this Court could end up taking on highly politicized cases related to the basic functions of our democracy? And how might they rule on them if they take them all?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I think it`s more than likely, I think, it`s an inevitability.\par \par \uc2 One of the cases that the court will take up this year is a question about this Independent State Legislature Theory, this idea that only State Legislatures as opposed to State Courts or Boards of Elections, or any of those local election officials can make policy to run Federal elections in each State, and that`s going to have a massive impact on the legislative landscape and on the electoral landscape going forward.\par \par \uc2 And this was a theory that was floated in Bush versus Gore back in 2000, only three Justices seemed interested in it. But again, we have a completely different Court, and it`s very likely that we will find five Justices to endorse this theory that`s been floating out there for some time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, in late June, the Supreme Court gutted the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 `s options for limiting greenhouse gas emissions from existing power plants, and as Hurricane Ian hit landfall and created a path of destruction last week, climate scientists suspect disasters like Hurricane Ian stem from climate change.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe the Supreme Court is out of step with this issue?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I think the question isn`t, is the Supreme Court out of step on climate change? This decision, obviously, is out of step with where the science is on climate change. I think the real question is, why is the Supreme Court so hostile to the prospect of regulated industries? Because that really is the question underlying all of this. Can the government step in to regulate either through the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  or other administrative agencies, these corporate interests that have such an impact on our lives? And that really seems to be what this six to three conservative supermajority is hostile to, not just climate change, but the prospect of any kind of regulation being brought to bear on these corporate interests.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Melissa Murray, thank you so much for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 After the break, we`re headed to the battleground State of Wisconsin. The State`s Attorney General joins us to talk about efforts to protect the integrity of the vote ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:52]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 The State of Wisconsin has been the target of many of former President Trump`s baseless claims of fraud in the 2020 election. Residents of the state cite concerns about the future of our democracy as the number one issue in the midterms in a recent survey conducted by the "Milwaukee Journal Sentinel."\par \par \uc2 As the state`s top law enforcement official, my next guest has played a key role in protecting the integrity of Wisconsin`s elections, and he faces a tough reelection bid himself.\par \par \uc2 With me now is Wisconsin Attorney General \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Kaul, thanks for joining us, first of all, and let me say you`ve had a front row seat to former President Trump`s bid to overturn the 2020 election, including efforts to hold a partial recount that involved only two counties where most black Wisconsinites live.\par \par \uc2 Talk to me about the important role that a State Attorney General`s Office plays in defending our democracy.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KAUL\uc2 , WISCONSIN ATTORNEY GENERAL: Absolutely. And first, thanks for having me. I mean, I can talk about the role that we`ve played in Wisconsin since I`ve been Attorney General. Now, prior to the 2020 election, there was an effort to purge tens of thousands of voters from our voter rolls. We defended against that, and won and kept voters in the rolls.\par \par \uc2 There was also this effort to slow down the mail where we joined other States brought suit and ensured that ballots will be received on time. And then after the 2020 election, we all remember \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies filed suits in States across the country.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, there were several suits that were filed. Again, we defended the results of the election. And ultimately, we won every one of those cases, but one of them was four to three in our State Supreme Court. And, you know, I was there and we were advocating that -- we were making the point that our elections are free and fair and that the results reflect the will of the voters.\par \par \uc2 But if you have somebody in there who is an election denier or who just falsely claims that laws were violated or that there was widespread fraud that could throw our system into chaos and we had this post-election investigation by \uc2 Michael Gableman\uc2  that has now finally ended, but AG`s have been critical in protecting our democracy.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let`s move to another important issue now. On Tuesday, you released -- you released on Tuesday your whole basis of dealing with these kinds of issues that have plagued a lot of what has been decided upon in this race in the midterms, in terms of voting and in terms of things of that nature.\par \par \uc2 You released your 2023 budget through 2025. Requesting the Department of Justice for the State, increased law enforcement funding and grant program recruitment, retention, and wellness. Your plan directly contradicts Republican claims that Democrats are soft on crime and defund the police are giving increased resources to law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 Explain to me what you released and how it counters the propaganda by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 KAUL: Well, I`m a former Federal prosecutor and as Attorney General, I`ve overseen a department that investigates and prosecutes some of the most serious crimes in the State of Wisconsin and we have been trying to get further investment in public safety in our State.\par \par \uc2 And now, the reality is that it`s our Republicans in the legislature, who for decades have prevented resources from getting to our communities that can make people safer. So, we have proposed investments, as you said, in law enforcement officer recruitment and retention and wellness; also in community policing, as well as victim services, mental health crisis response and drug treatment programs so that we can address some of the underlying causes of addiction.\par \par \uc2 So, we are advocating for more resources to make our communities safer and that is what I`ve done throughout my time as Attorney General, and that`s what I`m going to keep doing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, you filed the lawsuit this summer challenging Wisconsin`s abortion ban. For our viewers who don`t know, this is 173-year-old statute written before women even had the right to vote, which went back into effect after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 In three days, Wisconsin lawmakers will head back to Madison for a Special Session called by Governor Evers to consider whether voters should repeal the ban. What`s the latest on the case? And what is the future of abortion rights in your State?\par \par \uc2 KAUL: Four days after the Supreme Court overruled Roe v. Wade, we filed the suit. The Governor and I joined together and we announced that lawsuit. That case is making its way through the Court process right now, but what happened in Wisconsin is that as you said, we have this ban that goes back to the middle of the 19th Century before the Civil War, decades before women won the right to vote and it is incredibly restrictive.\par \par \uc2 It has no exceptions for cases of rape or incest, and the only time a doctor can intervene to even protect the health of the mother is if it`s necessary to save the life of the mother, and we`ve begun to hear some really awful stories about the impacts that this is having.\par \par \uc2 There is one woman in Wisconsin who had a partial miscarriage and was left to bleed for 10 days before the doctor was able to intervene. I heard from an OB-GYN who said that she has appointments with people who have planned pregnancies, what used to be joyous occasions, now, sometimes people come in and they`re terrified about what`s going to happen if there`s a complication.\par \par \uc2 So we are working to restore access to safe and legal abortion. The Governor has also called a Special Session that would allow for a pathway to restoring access, and we`re going to keep fighting to restore women`s equality, women`s freedom, and to protect women`s health in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Last question, brief answer please. The Center for Politics calls your race one of the five most competitive Attorney General Elections in 2022. According to their analysis, your fate will be closely tied to that of your fellow Wisconsin Democrats, including \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  running for reelection as Governor and Mandela Barnes seeking to unseat Senator Ron Johnson.\par \par \uc2 We`ve reached out to your Republican opponent, Eric Toney, to invite him on the program, but as you campaign in your closely divided State, what is the mood of voters as the midterm elections draw closer?\par \par \uc2 KAUL: I think people are really engaged. I was just at a parade this morning in La Crosse, which is in Western Wisconsin. That`s one of the swing districts and it`s going to have a very competitive congressional race this year. But I think Democrats are really motivated to get out and vote. People know the stakes when it comes to reproductive freedom or democracy, public safety and other issues.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:09]\par \par \uc2 KAUL: So we`re going to keep working hard. It`s going to be really close. I won by 0.65 percentage points in 2018, so I`m used to close races, so is our Governor. But I think that if we keep getting the message out there and talking about all the results we`ve gotten for folks over the last four years, we`re going to prevail on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Attorney General \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2  of Wisconsin, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 My final (thought) is next stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we focus this week on the damage left behind by Hurricane Ian, I want to highlight some of the other stories that caught my attention this week.\par \par \uc2 Autopsy results for Elijah McClain, a young Black man who died during a police encounter in 2019 shows that he actually died from a lethal dose of ketamine, a sedative given to him by paramedics. The autopsy report concluded that the paramedics gave him "higher dose than recommended for his weight." Three officers and two paramedics are currently facing charges for his death.\par \par \uc2 A former police officer in Texas has been acquitted on murder charges following the shooting of Jonathan Price two years ago. Civil Rights attorney Lee Merritt who represents the Price family says he will appeal to the DOJ to bring Federal criminal charges against the officers.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And the White House held a Conference on Hunger, Nutrition, and Health for the first time in over five decades. President Biden revealed his national plan to end hunger and increase healthy eating and physical activity by the year of 2030.\par \par \uc2 Monday is my birthday. But my real attention will be Tuesday, when the Supreme Court hears this voting case. We must really look and see if this conservative Court will take race out of gerrymandering, which will, in my opinion, take away what is left of voting rights.\par \par \uc2 If we do not have voting rights. This country cannot practice democracy for those of us that have been locked out of equal voting rights until 1965.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "AMERICAN VOICES" with guest host, Julian Castro starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:01]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221002eia100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for September 30, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,073 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: She was denigrating White History by playing a flute but not a single soul on the right knew existed. They didn`t know that flute existed, even when between beaten his slaves, James Madison was playing it himself. They didn`t even know.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, PROFESSOR, \uc2 NYU\uc2 : Can I change my answer. Can I change my answer, Joy?\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes, you can.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I want to overrule myself like the court did.\par \par \uc2 REID: Amen.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Carla Hayden, the Librarian of Congress won the week.\par \par \uc2 REID: Halleluiah. Play that flute anytime you want.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ERRIN HAINES, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: (INAUDIBLE) Black woman is running the \uc2 Library of Congress\uc2  as well, I mean --\par \par \uc2 REID: Chris -- we all in Chris Hayes` show, so we`re going to stop now. I got to stop -- we`re not going to stop. Errin Haines, as well. This is where we love you guys both. Have a great weekend. That`s tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , HOST, FOX NEWS: Why did anyone want to -- why did you approve a special master that sign one of the FISA warrants? That was surprising.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Well, the lawyers had a lot of -- I didn`t know any of the people involved.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As Trump`s judge steps in for Trump`s defense, tonight, Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  on the corruption of justice in the wake of Mar-a-Lago and what his committee learned from Ginny Thomas. Then --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It`s not just a crisis for Florida. This is an American crisis. We`re all in this together.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What the shared responsibility for rebuilding mean for the governor and people of Florida. Plus, my interview with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , and why he turned the latest Dr. Oz attack into a campaign slogan.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: When he dresses like that, it`s not an accident. He`s kicking authority in the balls.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Lady Justice author Dahlia Lithwick on a new day for the Supreme Court with Justice Ketanj Brown Jackson, when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Weeks after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  executed that now infamous search warrant at Donald Trump`s home in Florida seeking classified documents he removed from the White House, the ex- President filed his first legal response in court. And if you recall, it`s basically an attempt to stall the \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation. He asked the court to appoint a special master to sort through his baseless claims of executive privilege over the documents the \uc2 FBI\uc2  found at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 Now, part of what was striking about that move is that Trump and his lawyers went out of their way to bring the matter before a new judge, not the federal magistrate judge that was already handling the case. No, they filed a motion as a new legal action that was assigned to a different federal judge who, as luck would have it, turned out to be someone that he, Trump, appointed. That`s Judge Aileen Cannon. Trump announced his intent to nominate Cannon in April of 2020. She was confirmed by the Senate in the lame duck session that year. That`s after Trump lost the election while he was trying to contest it and ultimately overthrow American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Now, federal judges are randomly assigned to cases, but we know that Trump has tried to get before Judge Cannon previously. In fact, earlier this year when he sued \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and former \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials over the Russia investigation which was subsequently thrown out, he filed the suit in South Florida where Mar-a-Lago is. But as the Daily Beast reports, when his attorneys formerly filed the paperwork, they selected a tiny courthouse in the sprawling Federal Court district`s furthest northeast corner, a satellite location that`s 70 miles from Mar-a-Lago. They ignore the West Palm Beach federal courthouse that`s a 12-minute drive away.\par \par \uc2 Now, the scheme failed. Trump`s case was assigned to a Clinton-era judge who called him out for basically forum shopping and a footnote, "I note the plaintiff filed this lawsuit in the Fort Pierce division of this district where only one federal judge sits, Judge Aileen Cannon, who plaintiff appointed in 2020."\par \par \uc2 Of course, we should be clear, there are plenty of judges throughout the federal judiciary who were appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who, when faced with cases that he had an interest, have done the right thing, refused to do his bidding and follow the law. That`s largely what happened in the aftermath of the election when several Trump-appointed judges shot down his bogus challenges. But I got to say Judge Aileen Cannon has really distinguished herself as a chilling example of what it looks like when the judiciary is just completely in the tank for a politician.\par \par \uc2 On Labor Day, she issued this order granting Donald Trump`s request for a special master, an order that was roundly, nearly unanimously criticized by legal expert experts, including some of Trump`s most staunch defenders.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The opinion I think was wrong and I think the government should appeal it. It`s deeply flawed in a number of ways. I don`t think the appointment of a special master is going to hold up, but even if it does, I don`t see it fundamentally changing the trajectory. In other words, I don`t think it changes the ballgame so much as maybe we`ll have a rain-delayed for a couple of innings.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, Bill Barr`s prediction came true just a few days later. Last weekend, an appeals court did strike down key parts of Judge Canon`s ruling. The three-judge panel, two of whom were appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , unanimously ruled she was wrong to prevent federal prosecutors from using the classified documents in their investigation while the special master reviewed them.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:06]\par \par \uc2 They said the ex-President "Has not even attempted to show he is in need to know the information contained in the classified documents. And then there`s a special master himself who Trump`s lawyers suggested and that Judge Cannon shows. He has not been quite what I think the Trump folks were likely hoping for. Judge Raymond Dearie, a long-serving federal judge with a very, very good reputation has taken a no-nonsense approach to the case.\par \par \uc2 In a filing last week, he essentially called Trump`s bluff. Just as he did after the election, the ex-president has been running around making outlandish claims in public, this time alleging the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted evidence during their search at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The problem that you have is they go into rooms, they won`t let anybody near that -- they wouldn`t even let them in the same building. Did they drop anything into those piles or did they do it later? There`s no chain of custody here with them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just asking questions. Did they plant evidence. Now, you know, evidence being planted has happened, we should be clear, by law enforcement officials all over the place, OK. But if you`re going to make that accusation, you need to present evidence. So, Judge Dearie ordered Trump and his lawyers to state whether they believe anything on the official inventory list of items the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seized is wrong, basically challenging Trump to show him where the planted evidence is in a court filing which would be subject to sanctions that they were lying.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, guess what? Judge Aileen Cannon jumped back in to protect Trump and his lawyers from the embarrassing proposition of having to state the plain truth that no, of course, nothing was planted. Judge Cannon also blocked the swift timetable Judge Dearie had set to resolve the review of the documents, slowing the matter down.\par \par \uc2 With each action she takes, it is harder and harder to conclude anything other than that Judge Cannon is fully biased in favor of the man that got her job, 