gnitaries was a pure formality, because Justice Jackson was sworn into the court back in July.\par \par \uc2 But after the ceremony, the full Supreme Court posed together for the first time, that`s what they look like, just three days before the new term begins Monday.\par \par \uc2 This term in the court will hear cases involving voting rights and elections as well as more Second Amendment. They could have momentous, even calamitous consequences for our democracy. At a time when polls show public approval of the court is at historic lows.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Dahlia Lithwick, senior editor for Slate, where she has written her column Supreme Court Dispatches since 1998. And I`ve been reading them the whole time.\par \par \uc2 She is author of the new book, Lady Justice: Women, the Law, and the Battle to Save America, which is getting rave reviews everywhere I look. Congratulations to the book, it`s great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 DAHLIA LITHWICK, SENIOR EDITOR, SLATE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Everything is sort of before and after dots. It feels like. I mean, obviously there`s things, you know, Dobbs was not at a rapid disjuncture, the result, the seeds were there.\par \par \uc2 This is the first -- Monday will be the first Monday post Dobbs. How are you thinking about this court convening on Monday for the first time after Dobbs?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: My instinct says if you thought last term was bad, buckle in. That everything --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that`s bleak.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: I mean, I`m here to be bleak. You know, I`m not -- I`m not hopeful. I think that a lot of the issues that didn`t make it onto the docket last year, as he said, are going to be on the docket this year. There`s two seminal voting rights cases. There`s an Indian Child Welfare Act case. Last term, we had the Clean Air Act, this tip term, we have the Clean Water Act.\par \par \uc2 You know, there`s not an affirmative action, I think, is on the chopping block among other things.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that, you know, there`s two stories to tell. One story is that a lot of you know, conservative legal movement, folks are saying, oh, last year was an aberration everything. Temperatures down, everything`s cool, cool, cool. Nothing to see here.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:04]\par \par \uc2 I think that what you`re actually seeing is that a lot of the stuff particularly involving race, and I think voting that we didn`t see last term is coming.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The broad context here is the court has seen its polling numbers plummet, which, you know, does that matter or not, they`re not elected, but I think it does matter, I think they know when that happens.\par \par \uc2 And then, some interesting words sort of exchanged from some of the justices about the court and its legitimacy. I want to play \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  sort of defending them. And then a few days later, Justice Kagan talking in not that veiled terms about perception of the court, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN\uc2 , SUPREME COURT ASSOCIATE JUSTICE: When courts become extensions of the political process, when people see them as extensions of the political process, when people see them as trying just to impose personal preferences on a society, irrespective of the law. That`s when there`s a problem and that`s when there ought to be a problem.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , SUPREME COURT CHIEF JUSTICE: I don`t understand the connection between opinions that people disagree with and the legitimacy of the court. If the court doesn`t retain its legitimate function of interpreting the constitution, I`m not sure who would.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And then on top of that you have Alito in the wake of Justice Kagan`s comments saying were saying or implying that the court is becoming an illegitimate institution or questioning our integrity crosses an important line.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Yes, I mean, it`s so problematic when you have, you know, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  holding the seat open. Scalia seat for almost 10 months.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: And then, bragging about it. Seating \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  amid, certainly, I think material claims that needed to be investigated that we`re not.\par \par \uc2 And then, seating \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 , after voting had already started in the 2020 election, in violation of the pretextual reason that they weren`t going to seat \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And then they overturned Dobbs. And then Justice Alito flies to Rome and spikes the football and cracks wise about it.\par \par \uc2 And then, we have the Chief Justice saying, I don`t understand why people think there`s legitimacy problems.\par \par \uc2 So, I sort of think this is classic gas lighting, where they`re doing the thing they`re doing, they`re actually kind of proud of it. And then they`re very upset and alarmed when \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2  says a version of what she wrote in the Dobbs dissent, which is look at our polling numbers, people think that we`re very bad.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This point, I think, is so important, because I do think like the gas lighting and it`s always driven me crazy right about the way that conservatives have talked about the court, the balls and strikes umpire metaphor of \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The notion that like, well, how dare you say, you know how \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  is going to rule on abortion. It`s like, I know how he (INAUDIBLE) the rule on abortion. Like, I`m not an idiot. Neither are you. We all know what`s going on here.\par \par \uc2 I do think there`s some promise in that declining public perception. But I wonder whether you think that as well, like, people seeing what`s up?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: I think there`s promise. And you know, it`s worth saying, I mean, that`s a 20 point drop in two years in public view of the legitimacy. 