ll? Like does it reach them? Give us the lay of the land.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right. I mean, part of the reason I went there is because it`s such a perfect microcosm of the race where you have this super far-right Republican candidate, somebody who aspires to join the squad of \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 . Those were the candidates -- there are those with the Congressman he named when I asked who he most admires and wants to work with in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And then you have this kind of working-class younger woman who is running on abortion rights and being a small business owner. So, in a way, I think both of these candidates are plausible futures for their party. But yes, I think one of the things that Marie Gluesenkamp Perez has to do, the Democratic candidate, who is the underdog, and it is a Republican district, although not a super right-wing one in the past, is I think, convey to people who Joe Kent is, which is a little bit difficult because like Kari Lake -- I mean, he doesn`t have the media experience with a Kari Lake, but he`s affable. He doesn`t have the sort of wild-eyed energy of \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  or \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He says these kinds of outrageous things but says them in a kind of laid- back comments as if they were the most -- as if they were kind of homespun common sense.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And he is -- he has been really out there. I mean, he has -- your column opens with him trying to pull off this thing of like distancing himself from these really nasty white supremacist, you know, figures he`s associated with, but not totally distancing himself. He went to Nick Fuentes` show. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:02]\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: No, he didn`t go into Nick Fuentes` show. So, he -- so, a Nick Fuentes is like a very high profile white supremacist. He had a conference call with Nick Fuentes early in his campaign where they apparently discuss social media strategy. There was a few other areas of overlap and his association with Nick Fuentes became a problem in the primary. So, he disavowed Nick Fuentes. But then the far-right got really mad at him for betraying them. So, he went on this other white nationalist show, maybe I would say, a tick less overtly white supremacist, but still pretty out there. He went on the show to kind of explain and defend himself.\par \par \uc2 And somebody on the show asks him at one point. They say, you know, do you believe that Chinese anchor baby citizens should have the same rights as -- I can`t remember the exact language -- but like natural American stock who fought in the revolution? And he just says, you know, no. And then he talks about how he wants to see an immigration moratorium. And he talks about how we need to preserve the demographics of the United States.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He also -- he says of January 6 that it reeks of an intelligence operation once all the political prisoners arrested for invading the Capitol released. They also reported -- the AP reported paid a member of the Proud Boys one of the groups that led the January 6 attack almost $11,000 for consulting services, describe the Proud Boys as a drinking group.\par \par \uc2 Like, he`s pretty -- he`s like real adjacent to like the furthest right sort of most dangerous pro coup elements of American politics.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. And so, if someone like that can win in a district like this, it sends a message that there is no limit, right? There is no kind of limiting principle on how far Republicans can go. If somebody like that loses in a district like this, it says to the Republican Party that, you know, kind of this extreme Trumpism is a -- is an electoral dead end in a lot of the country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and this is what`s key to set the scene here. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  is in a plus-25 Republican district. I mean, it`s like, you know, anyone who`s got the R next to their surname is going to win that. \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , same thing. I mean, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . Like these are just like gerrymandered districts that aren`t competitive. This is a plus -- Trump won this district by four points against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020. This is --\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: This is a district right next to Portland, Oregon.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. This is a Republican district but it`s not one -- it`s not a Marjorie Taylor Green district.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, we`ll find out, I guess. I mean, it`s not -- it`s not that lopsided, but we`ll find out if \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  candidate can win in a district like that. And if it does, it pretends something I think pretty alarming for what the next Congress is going to look like.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What are you -- what was your sense of the state of the race being there?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG" You know, it`s obviously hard to say. I would say there`s a huge amount of enthusiasm for Marie Gluesenkamp Perez. Like you know, I went to one of her campaign events at a brewery where not only was the brewery packed, but there was, you know, people -- there was an overflow crowd outside listening through the windows. Jaime Herrera Beutler`s biggest bundler is now supporting Marie Gluesenkamp Perez. So, there`s -- you know, I think -- I think everybody feels like she`s an underdog, but people do feel like with the right support, she has a shot.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, one of the big questions here, right, is about -- you know, we always talk about how it`s a MAGA party, this Trump`s party. That`s true, right? But again, you see it time and time, 20, 25, 30 percent of Republicans, somewhere in there, like don`t -- didn`t support the coup, would wish \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would go away. \uc2 Herrera Beutler\uc2  was the kind of person representing a district that there must be some of those voters in those -- in that district, right? And the question of which they go.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. (INAUDIBLE) in the primary.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: I mean, it was a pretty narrow loss in the primary. So, there is a chunk of voters in that district that voted for Jamie Herrera Beutler. And the question is, do they now just kind of out of partisan loyalty vote for Joe Kent or out of kind of democratic loyalty do they vote against Joe Kent?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this is sort of the question for the country, right, as like you try to like construct this popular front to like, vouch safe American democracy against its foes. You know, the kind of like \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  to Noam Chomsky popular front of people that like believe in the project of American representative government can unite people around that issue at a time when again, I don`t want to, you know, throw this at voters. Like, there`s a lot going on. People are not that tuned in and also upset about other stuff that may be, you know, crossing their plate.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: And I think that, again, is what Marie Gluesenkamp Perez is trying to do. I mean, she`s -- you know, like I said, she`s a small business owner. She owns an auto shop. She talks a lot about inflation and the struggles people are facing. But at the same time, her campaign has, according to their internal polling, the issue that voters say they care about most in this race is the preservation of democracy. And so, that is something she talks about a lot on the stump.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, it`s really interesting because Pat Ryan who won that surprising win in that special election in upstate New York somewhat similar district to this in terms of where it sits, said that there was a lot of enthusiasm -- abortion and democracy were both issues that really, really resonated among voters there. And again, this was a swing district, similar dynamic it seems like here in terms of the issue topics.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Yes, absolutely. And so -- I mean, I guess, you know, the dynamics of the special election are not necessarily the dynamics of November, but this is -- this is the big question that November is going to turn on, right, whether those two things are enough to offset Republican advantages and other areas.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Michelle Goldberg, it`s a great column. Thanks for coming in to talk --\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- tell us about it. That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220928ei9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for September 26, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Ken Dilanian, Aaron Gilchrist \par \uc2 7,669 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Antony Blinken gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:13]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Tonight, the January 6 committee is days from a new hearing. And we have new video of a Trump ally calling for violence. He says it was only a joke. Plus, an abortion ban from the 1800s is now the law of the land in Arizona. The chaos and confusion has doctors unsure of how to care for their patients.\par \par \uc2 Then, in a matter of hours, the head of the radical Oath Keepers is in court facing seditious conspiracy charges. His former spokesman is here with a warning about the danger from extremist groups in this country as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. The Breaking News Tonight, new video of Trump ally \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  that is likely to be on display during the January 6 committee`s hearing on Wednesday. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports the panel plans to show video of Stone from a Danish made documentary. In one specific clip, Stone speaks out about violence before the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROGER STONE\uc2 , TRUMP ALLY: Excellent. Violent, promoting let`s get right to violence. Shoots kill, CNN and Antifa shoot to kill. Done with this.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports stone went on to say he was kidding and renounced his violence completely. The 1/6 Committee only obtained that video after the filmmakers complied with a subpoena. Stone responded to the video in a statement saying in part this, I challenged the accuracy and authenticity of these videos and believe they have been manipulated and selectively edited.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday`s hearing will be the committee`s first in more than two months. And it could be the final one before lawmakers issued their report.\par \par \uc2 Now, a former committee advisor Denver Riggleman has written a book about his work for the panel. It includes new information about a call placed from the White House to a rioter who was at the Capitol on Jan. 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DENVER RIGGLEMAN, FORMER JANUARY 6 COMMITTEE ADVISER: You get a real aha moment when you see that the White House switchboard had connected to a writer`s phone while it`s happening. That`s a big, pretty big aha moment. You get to aha wait a bit.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Someone in the White House was calling one of the rioters while the riot was going on?\par \par \uc2 RIGGLEMAN: On January 6. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Riggleman talked about that discovery earlier on this network.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUGGLEMAN: How in the world in any way would you have somebody on a White House desk calling a rioter on January 6. The committee is pursuing this. They wanted to see the White House numbers they couldn`t get them. There`s hundreds of them. So there`s so much more here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jan. 6 committee member Jamie Raskin says they are aware of that call. Today they issued this statement quote, Mr. Riggleman had limited knowledge of the committee`s investigation. He departed from the staff in April prior to our hearings, and much of our important investigative work. Since his departure, the committee has rundown all the leads and digested and analyzed all the information that arose from his work.\par \par \uc2 With that let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight. Luke Broadwater, Pulitzer Prize winning congressional reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former senior member of the Mueller probe. Now he is a professor at \uc2 NYU\uc2 , and A.B. Stoddard joins us, veteran Washington journalist and associate editor and columnist for Real Clear Politics.\par \par \uc2 Andrew, since I know how much you love visiting the crew over at last word, I`m going to let you start us off this evening. Maybe getting the pole position will make you like it here as much. Let`s talk about Denver Riggleman had a lot to say about \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  earlier tonight watch a little bit of this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RIGGLEMAN: \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  is one of the most important factors of what happened on January 6, it`s just -- it`s not just in what he has said himself, but it`s really in the link connections in the data. And in the people that work for him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You are very, very familiar with the way \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  operates. Anything surprised you here?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, FORMER SENIOR MEMBER OF MUELLER PROBE: Yes. So you know, it`s really hard not to look at this through the lens of the Mueller investigation, because \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , if people remember was charged with seven counts. He was convicted of all seven counts, and what was it for? He was the connective tissue between Julian Assange and the hacked e-mails from the Hillary Clinton campaign and the Trump campaign and releasing those in October.\par \par \uc2 And he at the time, by the way, was said oh, when he was caught, said, Oh, it was it`s just a joke. I was just, you know, bloviating while we said you know what take that to a jury and the jury obviously thought that he wasn`t just joking and convicted him. He was then pardoned.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:11]\par \par \uc2 Well, here, I think the one thing that I agree with Riggleman is that, you know, \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  could provide very interesting connective tissue because although you have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , fomenting the riots and insurrection on January 6, you wanted to see was there any connection with the actual groups with the Oath Keepers, for instance, and, you know, if you remember, Cassidy Hutchinson said that on January 5th, she heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tell \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to call \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , and \uc2 Mike Flynn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So if you have \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  involved with the Oath Keepers, and communicating with them, that is the connective tissue. And you know, it is finally I`d say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is smart to rely on \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  as that intermediary, because he knows he does not flip. He is a guy who is willing to go to trial, be convicted, and he keeps the oath of America. So if you`re looking for that intermediary to pull something like this off, \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  is the go to guy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He`s also believed to be the architect behind stop the steel. Luke, how helpful do you believe this information about stone can be?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Right. Well, the January 6 committee sent some of their top investigators to Denmark to get this footage. It`s about 170 hours of documentary footage of Roger stones actions, and we do expect some of it to be played Wednesday. I knew the filmmakers are coming down from New York for the hearing.\par \par \uc2 You know, as Andrew rightly points out, you know, \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  had the Oath Keepers as his bodyguards on January 6, several of the Oath Keepers are charged criminally with seditious conspiracy. So you don`t really have to look that hard to see that he is a link between Trump. You know, he`s Trump`s longest serving political adviser, and the extremist groups.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s what Denver Riggleman was really trying to investigate here with his time on the committee. That said, you know, the committee has is displeased with some of the things Denver Riggleman is doing. They think he`s putting out some evidence before it`s fully run to ground fully, you know, fully cooked and ready for public consumption. So, you know, we`ll wait to see on Wednesday what they have, but the, you know, this -- the \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  stuff is a very high importance. I agree.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A.B., what do you think about that? Right, this committee has spent thousands and thousands of hours researching, investigating, doing work, all in the name of protecting our democracy. Denver Riggleman, who left his work there back in April, has now turned around and written a book, but he`s making money off of how does that look in the world of politics and democracy?\par \par \uc2 A.B. STODDARD, REAL CLEAR POLITICS ASSOC. EDITOR AND COLUMNIST: Well, I mean, in listening to Denver Riggleman interviews, he repeatedly says he`s not going after the committee, he applauds their work. He got to work on this book before the hearings were underway. He`s very pleased with, you know, how they`ve tackled all of the organization of the presentation of the evidence that we`ve seen in the hearings. He`s very intent on not criticizing the committee. And he focuses a lot, obviously, on how much data there is.\par \par \uc2 And I saw an interview did months ago, where he said, we have enough data that if we put our noses down, essentially, in so many words will take us a year to get through it. So he, we know, wanted more of a budget to get through all this data. And he got some money, but not what he wanted.\par \par \uc2 And he seems in all these interviews, including the one site per se, is to circle back repeatedly, to how all of these leads that need to be tracked down. And as you said, they have a million roads they have to put down and details they`re pursuing.\par \par \uc2 And that, you know, I think there`s some subtle disagreements on how they`ve gone about that between him and the committee. But again, he`s making many repeats that he`s not trying to criticize them and they`re doing a great job. And it`s the case that they do have a deadline. They cannot go through everything they have and we know that. They cannot track down recalcitrant witnesses. They cannot go through all the data. They have a deadline of November 8 when should the Republicans take back the House majority that committee will be dissolved.\par \par \uc2 So we`ll see what, you know, Tthey said they pursued what Riggleman is talking about but I don`t know that it`s a huge conflict in terms of what they`re going to uncover and, and what they`re not pursuing.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Andrew, back to \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , a problematic figure, you bring it up. He was involved back in the Muller investigation here. He is now potentially being the connective tissue. But he`s also done like a fox, what do you think about stone adding just kidding about the violence?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Well, there`s no way that \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  is going to flip. I mean, that`s something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can be sure of. So in terms of providing the connective tissue, it`s not going to come from \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  unless, you know, health reasons over. So it really has to do with what evidence they have, what hard evidence they have of text messages and emails.\par \par \uc2 The I`m just kidding, you know, as I mentioned, you know, that`s something that he tried it the last time he was criminally charged. And that didn`t fly, you know. I used to when I was in the government call that a jury issue, meaning, you know, we`re still going to charge you and you can tell the jury that, you know, you didn`t really mean it, when you threatened a witness. And, you know, that`s exactly what he said in the trial. He said he was just joking. And the jury found beyond reasonable doubt that that wasn`t true.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, to me, it`s really going to be his connections to this violent group. And then whatever the committee has, and whatever the department justice has to connect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to him, you know, clearly, if they can get inside the meeting that happened on January 5 at the Willard Hotel, and they get a witness to talk about what happened there, that would be a huge step forward.\par \par \uc2 But if the committee doesn`t do that, that is what the Department of Justice has to take up. And they have grand jury subpoenas. They have a lot of other tools that that Congress doesn`t have.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s my next question. Do you think \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  and I know you have limited information, Andrew, we all do. But do you think he could be criminally charged? Because I`m pretty sure the person living in the White House now won`t pardon him?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: It`s true. I mean, I hate to give this answer. But it`s just too early to tell. You know, we haven`t seen the evidence that would have him be aider or abettor or a conspirator with the Oath Keepers. But you know, tomorrow, that case, which I really have to applaud the Department of Justice, because that is a terrific domestic terrorism case against the leaders of the Oath Keepers is going to trial in DC. And between that and the hearing on Wednesday, we may learn more evidence that could answer your question, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Luke, what should we expect from the hearing on Wednesday? It could be the last meeting we have or that we see.\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Right, you know, the committee has been keeping their cards close to the vest on this one. But we have heard that it will be perhaps more sprawling than some of the other hearings that they`ll talk a lot about the entirety of the scheme to overturn the election. And we`ve been promised that they`ll fill in some key details and a key piece of the scheme they haven`t really focused on yet.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, we know there`s going to be some video played of the attack. We understand there`s new video from some of the depositions and also the video from the Danish filmmakers.\par \par \uc2 So, the other thing I was told today is that there will also be an emphasis on continuing threats to democracy that so not just stopping at January sixth, but the threats to democracy that are ongoing. So, you know, I think it might be a little forward looking. I`m doing a little speculating here, because I don`t really know. But I`m excited like everybody else to see what this new information is. They`ll present on Wednesday, and they do have a track record of delivering new revelations at each hearing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A.B., we are six weeks away from the midterms. Is there anything the committee could do could reveal that`s going to change voter`s minds or get people excited to turn out?\par \par \uc2 STODDARD: Well, Stephanie, the centrality of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the midterm campaign is already an energizing factor for Democrats because of independents who can`t stand him who were not pleased with the revelations from the January 6 hearings thus far. And Democrats are motivated the more Trump is in the picture.\par \par \uc2 He was being banged up by the revelations from the hearings, which is why you saw some Democratic -- Republicans in polling begin to shift away from him. The Mar-a-Lago scandal has brought the faithful back into the fold and embolden him he might be, you know, announcing a third campaign for president before the midterm elections should be a huge problem for Republicans. So, he remains a huge factor and the hearings and what the hearings devolves between now and November 8 will be salient as well.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Andrew, should we assume that the both the DOJ and the committee know everything already that we could have learned from regulations book.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:08]\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: No. I am very much in the Adam Schiff, Claire McCaskill camp, that when it comes to the January 6 investigation that the January 6 committee is far, far ahead of the Department of Justice. I think that you`re seeing a lot of activity now by the department on the January 6 investigation, but I think, you know, the reason you saw a flurry of I think 40 Grand Jury subpoenas just a couple of weeks ago is a sign that they`re starting to rev up not that they`re towards the end.\par \par \uc2 So I think that there`s a lot of work that the department has to do, if it`s going to bring a January 6 case. I think it`s different with Mar-a- Lago because that`s just a very simple case. I mean, that`s one where, in my view, if that can`t be done by the end of the year, then they need to change horses in terms of who`s stuffing that because that is just that. It`s a very basic case.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Andrew with the reminder, the Mar-a-Lago investigation is different from Jan. 6 and he hasn`t even mentioned the investigation into use of PAC money. Extraordinary. All right guys, thank you so much. LB, AV and AW, starting us off on this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Now we have to turn to the latest on the Mississippi welfare scandal involving NFL Hall of Famer Brett Favre you knew I was getting back to this story. We have been covering it for weeks.\par \par \uc2 Newly released text messages reveal more about how the nation`s poor state spent welfare money on a volleyball Stadium at a school where Brett Favre daughter played the sport, our own Ken Dilanian has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): NFL Hall of Famer Brett Favre is under growing pressure tonight. Sirius Radio putting his weekly football show on pause as a new court filing says the former football star ways to campaign to aggressively lobby for millions of dollars from the state welfare agency to finish building a volleyball facility and his alma mater, the \uc2 University of Southern Mississippi\uc2  where his daughter played the sport and also a football practice facility.\par \par \uc2 New Tech show Favre in contact with then Governor Phil Bryant and other state officials as he pushed to secure funding meant for people in poverty. I need your influence somehow to get donations and or sponsorships, Favre texted Bryant in April 2017. Core record show he secured $5 million but needed more.\par \par \uc2 Even after state auditors began to raise questions, Favre kept lobbying. In September 2019, he texted Bryant that he wanted to name the volleyball complex after the governor. We obviously need your help big time and time is working against us, Favre texted Bryant replied, we`re going to get there but we have to follow the law. I`m too old for federal prison.\par \par \uc2 The former governor says he did not know officials use millions from the federal welfare program to construct the sports complex. Bryant`s former welfare director pleaded guilty to state and federal crimes last week in the growing scandal. He also agreed to cooperate with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  which has been investigating the welfare misspending for two years.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN (on camera): Tonight, Favre`s lawyer tells me his client behaved honorably and did not know the state grants he saw it came from federal welfare funds. He noted that former Governor Bryant is not accusing farm of violating any laws. Favre has not been charged.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Favre has not been charged but he is welcome to join us on this show any night of the week. Our special thanks to Ken Dilanian for that report. And for staying on this important story.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a restrictive abortion band that is over 100 years old. It has just been reinstated in Arizona, Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2  on what that means for patients and the confusion it`s causing for doctors. It is a kind of thing that some Republicans want across the nation.\par \par \uc2 And later, former Oath Keeper spokesman Jason Van is here on the day before his former bosses January 6 trial begins. We`ll speak to him about the dangers of extremism and why he decided he must leave the group and Ron DeSantis`s stunt to send migrants to Massachusetts may not have been legal, but \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  says it`s something much, much worse. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a busy Monday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:57]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Arizona has become the latest state to impose restrictive and confusing abortion laws. A judge ruled a near total abortion ban that was passed in 1864 could now be enforced. But the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports on a disagreement among Republicans within the state. Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  has said that the 15-week ban he signed back in March would supersede the century old ban, but Attorney General \uc2 Mark Brnovich\uc2  has argued that the older ban should take precedence.\par \par \uc2 The confusion around abortion laws is across the nation. And it`s causing serious problems for doctors as states pass different restrictions. Joining us now to discuss Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2 , clinical physician and former Senior Policy Director during the Obama administration. She is also one of our public health experts.\par \par \uc2 Kavita, I`m so glad you`re here because this ban predates Arizona statehood. What are the medical consequences of this law?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , CLINICAL PHYSICIAN: Yes, Stephanie. The medical consequences of this law and the repercussions in the states around Arizona are incredible.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:04]\par \par \uc2 Just to be clear, a near total ban is essentially a total ban, because the exception they make is for the life of the mother. But when you look and unpack what was passed in 1864, enacted in 1901, Stephanie, just five years after we even had X-ray technology, you see that if I, as a physician wanted to transfer somebody to an abortion provider, because they had an ectopic pregnancy or any number of conditions, that would require that level of special skill, I could face criminal penalties and get put into jail.\par \par \uc2 And so you`re putting in between that you`re putting the criminal system, the justice system in between a patient and their health care team. That is absolutely the wrong place you want when it`s your life at stake.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What does that mean for patients privacy?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, so this is an area that I think has been active discussion, not just in Arizona. But as you know, it`s almost easier, Stephanie, to point out the states where we have protections 13 states in the United States, where we at least have protections for reproductive services, all the other states, including Arizona, for patients, this means that they`re not even sure if they can make any sort of query to get an appointment to even talk to a doctor about what they should do, or what they could do.\par \par \uc2 So this has created all sorts of concerns about privacy. Healthcare institutions have reinforced that they absolutely protect the patient`s privacy, but Stephanie, many states have on the books, laws that require notification, in order to get those exceptions, literally a physician in Arizona, for example, would have to be able to -- can basically consult with law enforcement, and be able to discuss why they think this is somebody that determines the exception, and have a committee of people or an individual.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to tell me that that promises privacy, or for that matter, any sort of dignity for anybody in that process, I beg you to show me that, it`s unconscionable. And honestly, it actually goes beyond reproductive care. There`s so many things that now could be brought up as suspect to, you know, any sort of judicial involvement, and it`s caused doctors, we are not risk.\par \par \uc2 I went into medicine, because it is evidence based scientific. There are facts. And what you have done is now created an entire culture amongst a profession, where we have to fear something about our science, our facts, and what we`ve been trained in, before we can act in the best interest of a patient that is going in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How dangerous is that? Right now you`re going to have doctors and patients confused about the law.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right. When and where there is confusion there are numerous errors. We know that medicine is a profession where we take pride in understanding what we do wrong, when we have so much confusion, and you have so many processes in place. You know, I need to consult a lawyer, I need to talk to our administration at a hospital. I need to find out what the law is today, when even judges do not agree on the interpretation of a law that delays the care that somebody needs, not just in an emergency, Stephanie, but in any process of what your body is doing.\par \par \uc2 You need to be able to have timely access to care. This absolutely prevents it. And it`s a redefining people are literally redefining the very textbooks that we have learned from and actually defining abortion, inaccurately. And that`s even more concerning for patients.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Could this impact the future of medicine? I`m thinking could it limit what medical schools are teaching?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes, so there are requirements for medical schools and residencies and all these training programs. Not only will it be difficult for someone, for example, Arizona or Texas or Louisiana to find these opportunities for education. But if you`ve never seen something, Stephanie, you won`t recognize it when it comes in front of you.\par \par \uc2 So even in the event, that someone does have something that could be threatening their life, you might be dealing with a medical team that has never seen that condition because the entire state, half the country, about two-thirds of the country no longer is able to teach that in person in real time. And that`s just devastating.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just Obstetricians and Gynecologists, we have a number of medical specialties where we rely on recognizing that these conditions early, often and being able to treat them accurately and all of that is in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2 , thank you for joining us tonight. You always make us smarter, you always make us safer. I appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, well, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  put the people of Florida ahead of political stunts as Hurricane Ian is getting closer and closer to Florida`s west coast. He might have to ask the Biden White House for help when the 11th hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:34:26]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R) FLORIDA: Folks should be prepared if you`re in this region, that there is going to be an interruption of power. So just plan on that. Understand that that that will happen. Even if the storm, the eye of the storm doesn`t hit your region, you`re going to have really significant winds. It`s going to knock over trees, it`s going to cause interruptions and so that`s just the name of the game.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Hurricane and could also prove to be a big political test for Florida`s controversial governor. He`s already under fire for flying on 50 migrants from Texas to Massachusetts using Florida taxpayer money and resources.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:04]\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  was asked about that very move.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: Obviously, there are issues with the border and with migration. But these are the kinds of stunts you see from people who don`t have a solution. Where have you recently Governor DeSantis was in Congress? Where was he when they were debating immigration reform? What have any of these people done to be part of the solution?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s discuss with Pulitzer Prize winning journalist \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , associate editor for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . But you know him here as anchor of the Sunday show on MSNBC and our dear friend, Tim Miller, contributor to The Bulwark and former communications director for Jeb Bush, author of the new book "Why We Did It."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Capehart, a natural disaster like a hurricane could have DeSantis asking Joe Biden`s White House for help. How`s that going to go?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , THE WASHINGTON POST ASSOCIATE EDITOR: Well, we shall see. The last Florida governor, I remember Republican governor, I remember asking a Democratic president for help, and also giving that Democratic president a hug, ended up being run out of the party. And I`m talking to you and I`m talking about now democratic congressman and candidate for governor. \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So the pickle that Governor DeSantis is in is he`s playing around with the lives of human beings with migrants, in defiance of human decency, and the administration. But then it`s going to ask that same administration for help when the citizens of Florida get hit by Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeSantis we now know has no shame. So I don`t think it`ll bother him one bit to ask the President of the United States for help.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Shamelessness is one thing, but he might himself have himself in a legal bind. Tim, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported that DeSantis once complained that the governor of Texas had the good political fortune to share a border with Mexico, he could dig into all those migrant issues. Is what DeSantis did this flight to Martha`s Vineyard beyond it being inhumane? Could he be, you know, bordering on Bridge Gate (ph) territory and possibly breaking the law?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: Well, certainly I think that if at non-governor if it was just a private citizen had tricked 50 people into putting them onto a plane to troll them. You`re dealing with interstate issues during the human trafficking type issues. He had no reason to move these folks.\par \par \uc2 And I appreciate the brunt of that Bridge Gate (ph). I thought that`s what Jonathan was going when he`s talking about a Republican governor embracing or they didn`t quite hug a Democratic president. And I was thinking about \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  with Hurricane Sandy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Hurricane Sandy (INAUDIBLE) hug.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, was there a hug? Yes. There was at least a beach walk.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Huggish, a bro hug. But I think that there`s a lot of similarities to Bridge Gate. Right. It was the type of thing that was a very petty political maneuver. At first, you know, it might have seemed that it wasn`t going to be a big deal. You know, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  was a rising star in the party at the time. But then there`s this drip, drip, drip, you start learning about emails, this there`s this \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  story over the weekend, where they`re interviewing the migrants. There was one magazine that really stood out to me, his name is Jose he was talking about how he was terrified and frightened how he was lied to.\par \par \uc2 There`s the Miami Herald story about how they`re going to be more flights, looks like one to Delaware to try to troll the President Biden, the person he`s about to ask for help for hurricane Ian. But that kind of didn`t happen, I think, which is maybe signaling that they realized that they were in a little bit of hot water down there in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t know, I think there`s a lot of these performative Republican guys who think that because Trump got away with everything, they could get away with it. And that really just isn`t how it works. And I think that it`s possible that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is getting himself in both legal and political trouble here.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, I need you to explain how this one works. I want to ask you about Arizona Democratic senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . She left Arizona, flew to Kentucky today, where she spoke at an event with none other than \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . At that event, McConnell called her the most effective first term Senator he has seen during his 37 years in the Senate. Jonathan to you first, can you explain this woman`s political strategy?\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Sinema`s political strategy? I have no idea that I do understand why Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  invited her to Kentucky one to troll -- to try to well I don`t know if he`s trying to own the libs, but he`s certainly trolling the libs, but also to call her the most effective first term senator.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s kind of -- it wasn`t damning with faint praise, but of course he thinks she`s the most effective first term senator.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:02]\par \par \uc2 She blocked a lot of the stuff that he couldn`t do as minority leader by insisting that the filibuster not be reformed, let`s say voting rights can happen -- voting rights reform can happen that other bills could be passed to codifying Roe standing in the way of the agenda of the Democratic president.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes. I get why \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is waking up to this.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Go ahead. Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But why would \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  be, Tim?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: She -- I think she`s the most inscrutable politician around for me right now. The best answer I have to that is that she thinks that she`s doing kind of this John McCain Maverick imitation in reverse. But there`s a lot of ways that that`s not working.\par \par \uc2 Number one, \uc2 John McCain\uc2  had some very clear issues that he was different with the party on. He was pretty loyal to the party and a lot of stuff and then he had a few things that matter to him torture, campaign finance reform immigration that he bucks the party on. That was his mantra and the voters understood. That`s where he was.\par \par \uc2 What exactly is Kysten Sinema`s position besides just wanting to be obstructionist, wanting to make \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  happy, like having some very weird, you know, particular complaints about the way that the Senate works, wanted to help out the hedge fund guys. Like it`s not a maverick. It`s not like a populist maverick style that I think has any appeal.\par \par \uc2 And so, look, I`ve been the number one Joe Manchin stand on MSNBC. I think what he`s doing makes a lot of sense. He`s in a deep red state. It makes sense for him to buck the party on certain things. \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  isn`t a state \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won. And she hasn`t made clear what her policy agenda is or what her vision is and why she would like the party. So I think that she thinks she`s being a maverick, but it really ain`t working.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I want to ask Joe Manchin, hey, you`re going to do this. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  really wants you to change parties. Joe Manchin laughed at me and said, Why on earth would I do that and reminded me the political power he has in his position.\par \par \uc2 What I wonder about \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  is who exactly is her base. Not too many private equity executives or hedge fund managers vote in the state of Arizona. \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , Tim Miller, always great to have you both here. When we come back with the leader of the Oath Keepers about to stand trial, his former spokesperson is here to update his warning to the January 6 committee about the very possibility of a civil war. That`s when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:03]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON VAN TATENHOVE, FORMER OATH KEEPERS SPOKESMAN: I think we saw a glimpse of what the vision of the Oath Keepers is on January 6. It doesn`t necessarily include the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO LCIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The former spokesman for the Oath Keepers delivered that testimony barely two months ago. Tomorrow, Oath Keepers founder Stewart Rhodes will stand trial alongside for other members of his militia group. They are the first January 6 rioters to face rare seditious conspiracy charges.\par \par \uc2 Then on Wednesday, the Jan. 6 Committee convenes for what may be its final public hearing.\par \par \uc2 With me now former Oath Keeper spokesperson, Jason Van Tatenhove. His book "The Perils of Extremism, How I Left the Oath Keepers and Why We Should be Concerned about a Future Civil War" due out early next year. Your former boss, I`m so glad you`re back with us. His trial begins tomorrow. This guy was once a Yale Law student. How did he go from there to here?\par \par \uc2 VAN TATENHOVE: You know, I think it has to do with disenfranchisement. I think if we look at some of his co-workers back when he was working for the Arizona Supreme Court, they talk about how he really kind of became more angry with his rhetoric and how he was talking just with fellow clerks. You know, I think it has all of this has to do with people being disenfranchised with where their lives are at the moment. Things may not be going so well. And they latch on to movements like this.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The disenfranchised Yale law alumni, a sad, sad group. We`re also hearing more about \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  his ties to the group. Andrew Giuliani possibly texting with members of the group. Does any of that surprise you? That doesn`t sound like a fringe group. That sounds like they are right in the middle of it all.\par \par \uc2 VAN TATENHOVE: Yes, no, I think they -- it does not surprise me at all. And I think we`re going to see a lot of revelations coming out during the trial about connections with specifically \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 . Giuliani, I`m sure. But I think it was really kind of Stone that was probably the one that was taking lead and opening lines of communication between in the campaign. And then the administration and leaders of these different militia groups.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Seditious conspiracy, those are serious charges. Do you think any of this is sort of making current members of the Oath Keepers sort of wake up to how serious this is and the trouble they could all be in?\par \par \uc2 VAN TATENHOVE: I sure your hope so but I think it really depends on whether or not there`s any account held, whether or not there are repercussions for the events we saw the violent insurrection that happened on January 6, because thus far, there has not really been any real account held to these leaders.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 There has been and sometimes served but cases dismissed and just the only people that have paid any sort of price for any of these actions from Bundy Ranch (ph) moving forward has been lower level people that were used, really and then abandoned by Stewart.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What happens if Stewart and some of these other top guys are in real trouble? Is there a group right beneath them ready to take the helm?\par \par \uc2 VAN TATENHOVE: I don`t think so now. I think that that will happen. I mean, certainly Stewart has given a blueprint to future leaders of these types of organizations. He showed them how to, you know, have a national organization, how to break that down into state chapters, and hyper local county chapters, and be autonomous.\par \par \uc2 So, unfortunately, he`s given out a blueprint that has been proven to work, and we`ll see who moves forward with it. And we got lucky with Stewart because he was always telling that legal line to a large degree, you know, he talked real tough. But when it came time to enter the Capitol, he was, you know, he was back where he didn`t actually enter. And we saw that at Bundy Ranch, we saw that up in the Pacific Northwest.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, if a leader were to come up that`s willing to take the, you know, lead that charge, well, then that that`s going to be a scarier place than we are now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s what I want to ask you about before we go when you spoke to the Jan. 6 panel, you said we were very, very close to a civil war. Has that view changed?\par \par \uc2 VAN TATENHOVE: No, it hasn`t. I think we are close to it. I think I think you know, the words that are out there, you know, those words go on to become actions. And I think we`re so divided as a country right now. We really need to focus on protecting our democracy first and foremost, and really trying to find ways to rebuild some of the unity that we`ve lost as a nation to breach these divides.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Every day, we have a chance to do that. All of us can work to bring us together. Jason, thank you for joining us. And your new book out early next year. The Perils of Extremism, please come back when it`s out.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, stay with MSNBC for special coverage of the next January 6 hearing on Wednesday led in the afternoon by Andrea Mitchell, Katie Tur, and Hallie Jackson. Then join us at MSNBC at night, a two-hour recap special beginning at 8:00 pm. I will be joining my colleagues Rachel Maddow, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , Nicolle Wallace, Chris Hayes, Lawrence O`Donnell, Ari Melber, and Alex Wagner breaking out down all elements, every word that we hear in that hearing on Wednesday, you do not want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 But up next, the American military organization that just started marching to a hip new beat, don`t go to bed yet. You`re going to want to hear this when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:22]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Aren`t you glad to set up for that. The last thing before we go tonight, Rappers Delight. Rap music has been part of American culture since the late 1970s from the Sugar Hill gang to Kendrick Lamar. These uniquely American artists have entertained us with a rhythmic takes on current American culture.\par \par \uc2 So you could say it`s about damn time, if not way past time that the U.S. military get hip to the trend. I was going to say hip hop but then I`d have to walk myself out. Our own Aaron Gilchrist reports on these two new resident rappers now performing with U.S. Army Field Band.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 AARON GILCHRIST, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voiceover): The United States Army Field Band is no stranger to the world stage. The Field Band has been playing concerts and connecting the public to the army for more than 75 years, honoring soldiers and veterans through music.\par \par \uc2 The notable Boogie Woogie Bugle Boy has never sounded like this. Rapper is Nicholas Feemster and Lamar Riddick, just over a year ago answered a job posting by the Army Field Band looking to add hip hop artists to its ranks.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST (on camera): How does it feel to be the first?\par \par \uc2 STAFF SGT. NICHOLAS FEEMSTER, U.S. ARMY FIELD BAND: To be here and to be able to be a part of such a great organization that was forward thinking enough to even bring on rappers is a -- it`s a great blessing.\par \par \uc2 LAMAR RIDDICK, U.S. ARMY FIELD BAND: I think people get caught up in the first and lose the quality. So we`re going to be focused on making sure we`re doing a good job with that title.\par \par \uc2 FEEMSTER: You can talk about a multitude of different things with hip hop music.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST (voiceover): After months of basic training, Feemster and Riddick are now full time members of the army and its field band. making their debut in front of a world audience in Scotland at the Edinburgh Military Tattoo last month.\par \par \uc2 FEEMSTER: When we hit that that walkway, me and him kind of just gave each other like a little. We`re just like, it`s showtime.\par \par \uc2 RIDDICK: That`s stuff that I pray for when I was younger. So I was glad to be there.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST: The Army striking a new chord with these Trailblazers leading the way.\par \par \uc2 FEEMSTER: And sometimes things just take time but it`s here now and we`re here to stay.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST: Aaron Gilchrist, NBC News, Fort Meade, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We are here to stay they say and from everybody THE 11TH HOUR we are really glad to hear it.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:04]\par \par \uc2 On that note, I wish you all at home a very, very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220927ei9q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION September 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Susan del Percio, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:50]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Tipping point.\par \par \uc2 Right now on this first weekend of fall, strong winds are blowing in Donald Trump`s world and it appeared this week that at least one of the investigations encircling Trump has reached a critical mass, that of course being New York Attorney General Tish James` probe of Trump real estate businesses, alleging at a minimum that Trump`s overvaluation of his properties constituted a massive decade`s long fraud that defined his organization.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the revelations were met mostly with silence from congressional Republicans, as the House GOP was more concerned this week with unveiling its 2023 agenda ahead of midterm elections in just six weeks. That agenda unsurprisingly focused on crime and the border.\par \par \uc2 Both issues providing a backdrop for the ongoing drama over the forced relocation of Hispanic migrants to liberal cities by red state Republican governors, one of those cities being the one I`m reporting to you from, New York City. And in a few moments, I`ll talk to the City`s Mayor, Eric Adams, about steps he is taking to house stranded migrants and push back on the GOP campaign stunts.\par \par \uc2 All of that tonight on POLITICSNATION, including my "Got-cha." My "Got-cha" moment will be on the one State that has banned more books than any other State in the country.\par \par \uc2 But we start with politics, Joining me now is Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , Democrat of California.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I have to begin with perhaps this week`s biggest news, and that, of course, is New York Attorney General Tish James filing a suit against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , including three of his children, alleging that Trump overvalued his properties for over a decade and that his children and the top \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  officials were in on it all along.\par \par \uc2 What if anything, surprises you about these charges?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): Reverend Sharpton, I`m not surprised because we had oversight hearings about this very topic years ago and \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and other people in Trump`s organization, basically under oath told the Committee that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is overvaluing these properties and is engaged in financial fraud and he admitted that under oath.\par \par \uc2 So, I give the Attorney General James credit for following the law and following the evidence that has been out there.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy laid out the House Republicans 2023 agenda just yesterday, and President Biden in turn blasted it as light on details and not consistent with what many Republicans have been saying on the campaign trail, especially as it pertains to supporting law enforcement groups like the \uc2 FBI\uc2  on abortion.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this proposed Republican platform especially when it does -- when it contrasts with what they`ve really been campaigning on, on the ground?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Reverend Sharpton, I know you`re one for straight talk, so let me just be blunt. I don`t care what it says in that document. I`ll tell you what will happen if the Republicans take control of Congress. They`re going to be talking about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  the whole time. That`s what happened last week.\par \par \uc2 There was a hearing on oversight about how to get people`s lives to be better, and all they wanted to do is talk about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , all they want to do is have investigations into this presidency and if we give them the power, it is going to be two years of obstructionism and if the Democrats are there we`re going to do more to actually lower prices for the middle class, to create more manufacturing jobs.\par \par \uc2 So, they`ve put in a bunch of stuff on crime and border, but that`s not what they intend to do if they win.\par \par \uc2 [17:18:22]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Talking about if they take over, let`s talk about right now. You`re the chair of the House Oversight Subcommittee on the Environment. You`ve been working with President Biden to refashion his climate agenda. You said that America should win the green energy race and analyzing it you`ve really made it an analogy to the space race.\par \par \uc2 Now, we see this devastation in Puerto Rico. Why is it still struggling with catastrophic flooding, and widespread power outages after Hurricane Fiona, despite billions, literally billions being allocated after Hurricane Maria? Would statehood help them out, maybe?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, we should give them the right to choose whether that`s statehood or self-determination and the House Democrats have passed that bill, and it should pass at Congress to give them the right to choose their own governance. Right now, they don`t have either statehood or their own governance.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, as you know, has said that the Federal government will pick up one month`s of costs of what`s happening in Puerto Rico, but we need even more heavy investment to rebuild that infrastructure and I just want to say, President Biden deserves an enormous amount of credit for the Inflation Reduction agenda. It is the largest investment in climate that anyone has ever made in any country. And, you know, our grandchildren will be reading about that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, last question, as you say, I`d like to talk straight, plain and simple, but just about six to seven weeks until midterms, what else can Congress do on voting rights issues that we`re seeing across the country?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Reverend Sharpton, we had Reverend Barber -- I had Reverend Barber and 50 clergy, and they had a simple point, keep voting on the Voting Rights Act, keep voting on making sure that the Child Tax Credit is permanent, because that`s the difference between someone being evicted or being able to stay in their apartment, keep voting to make sure that we actually are giving folks healthcare and a livable wage.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Republicans vote 70 to 80 times to take away people`s healthcare. We figure we just vote once and then we`re done with it. No. Let`s go on the record and vote and show the American people what the Republicans actually stand for and what we stand for, really to engage clergy far more. I mean, I am sick and tired of them doing that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me -- I said that was the last question, but I have to ask one more. I noticed you in the Iowa. You thinking about running for president, yourself, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I`m thinking of supporting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , fully a hundred percent and that`s not a political answer. He is the person who can beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think Trump`s going to run again and I`m looking forward to working as hard as I can across this country to reelect \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He has done a lot and he doesn`t get enough credit for what he`s done, and as someone who supported Bernie Sanders last time saying that we`ve got to rally around our President.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You are a big Bernie Sanders man, so that I`m sure is something the Biden people are hearing you say.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us, Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joining me now is Kelley Robinson, the new President of the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2 . Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Miss Robinson, you recently became the first Black queer woman to lead the largest LGBTQ advocacy group in the country, the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For our audience, tell us a little more about your background and your plans for the organization.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY ROBINSON, PRESIDENT, \uc2 HUMAN RIGHTS CAMPAIGN\uc2 : Absolutely. And thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 Look, the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  is the largest LGBTQ+ advocacy organization in the country. We are fighting for freedom and liberation without exception. And as a Black queer woman, as a mother and a wife, it is so clear to me that for me to sit here, celebrated and respected and these identities I hold, it is because of the work of organizations like the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2 , it`s because of so many generations of folks who have fought for the kind of change that allows me to live the life of my dreams.\par \par \uc2 I`m coming to this work in this movement from spending decades in the fight for reproductive health and rights and abortion access, and it is so clear to me that we are in a moment of crisis. We`re in a moment of urgency.\par \par \uc2 So I`m coming into this role with a mandate, a mandate to get to change for our people, to codify our rights into law and build the kind of political power to get victories today and this November, but also to strengthen our democracy in the long term.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, your previous job was with \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2  and obviously that work was on abortion and abortion is on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I know your predecessor there at Human Rights, more than anybody I know had done a lot of intersectional work, got a lot of us and Civil Rights groups together and you are now there. Will you continue this intersectional work? Because according to the \uc2 HRC\uc2 , citing data from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 , lesbian and bisexual women who have been pregnant are more likely than heterosexual women to have had an abortion.\par \par \uc2 Thinking about that with midterms in mind, do you get the sense that Democrats have engaged effectively with queer women on the issue of abortion?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Thank you for that question. The first thing I have to say is, the only way for us to move forward is intersectionally, because our people do not live single issue lives. As a Black queer woman, as a mother and a wife, I know that. And I also know that because I`ve heard the stories of our supporters all across the country, from trans kids in the Midwest, to married folks in the south, to folks who are new to the workplace looking for their first jobs in the LGBTQ+ community.\par \par \uc2 These things are front of mind, and the issues that impact us have a broad range, and it is all about making sure that we can experience respect and dignity in every aspect of our lives. The only way to do that is intersectionally.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that`s clear to me is that our opposition gets intersectionality because the same people that are coming after abortion rights are coming after our right to vote. They`re the same folks that are attacking my people in the LGBTQ+ community. We have got to get a broad range of folks together and fight. That`s the only way that we`re going to win.\par \par \uc2 We know that the numbers are with us. Over 70 percent of this country supports marriage equality, 80 percent of this country supports the access to abortion. We are on the winning side, we are on the morally just side.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I think that the outreach that`s happening in our communities is phenomenal and it has got to get stronger and bigger for us to build the kind of transformative movement that we need. And as a grassroots community organizer at my core, I know that more than anyone.\par \par \uc2 So, the way that we are going to win this November is person-to-person through communicating, through talking to one another and through making sure that folks know that we are powerful, we just have to vote like the majority that we are.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now a lot of the attention this election cycle is focused on the Supreme Court`s overturn of Roe, but unless something unexpected happens, this Court will remain dominated by conservative Justices possibly for decades.\par \par \uc2 What other rights are you worried could be in jeopardy in the years to come?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: We have to be clear that any of our rights that are vested in the Supreme Court, they must be codified into law. At the fall of Roe, it made it clear for me, for so many communities, especially the LGBTQ+ community, that we are one Supreme Court decision away from losing so many of our hard fought for Civil Rights, not just over the last 50 years, but over the last 400 years.\par \par \uc2 So for that reason, we`ve got to continue the fight because \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , in his concurring dissent, said, hey, next, we`re going to come for your right to privacy. Next, we`re going to come for your right to marriage equality.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to understand that they have told us their agenda, they double down on that agenda and that so-called Commitment to America Agenda that was just released and all that I saw was a commitment to attacking our rights, attacking our basic freedoms.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got to be clear that this and everything else is on the line this November and vote like it. That`s our test.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Miss Robinson, the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  condemned the House Republicans midterm agenda just released, saying, and I`m quoting, "The so- called Commitment to America, in reality is a commitment to keep attacking our rights and our democracy." Can you expand on that for us?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: That`s what it is. That agenda is so out of touch with where this American country is right now. They`re coming for LGBTQ rights in that agenda. They are coming for abortion rights in that agenda. They are telling us exactly who they are.\par \par \uc2 I think what we`ve got to remember is that we are the majority. They are out of touch with where the folks in this country are. Look, we are in a moment right now where folks care about the price of gas, folks are caring about inflation, folks are caring about protecting their families, folks are caring about the fact that we`ve gotten more -- we`ve had the deadliest record on year for trans lives. That`s what`s at the center of what people are experiencing right now.\par \par \uc2 And what they`re focused on is a political agenda run by extremists. This is our chance to tell them that as an American public, we are not going to stand for it. We are going to fight back and we are going to hold them accountable no just this November, but every election cycle moving forward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Kelley Robinson, thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this week`s "Got-cha," I`m taking Texas to school over its obsession with banning books, and New York City taking new steps to deal with migrants being bused to the city by Republican Governors. Mayor Eric Adams joins me later on POLITICSNATION. But first, my colleague Richard Lui, with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:32]\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Fiona, now a post tropical cyclone hit Canada`s East Coast this morning knocking out power for hundreds of thousands of Canadians. Officials warn Fiona could be a historic extreme event with sustained winds of 85 miles an hour, the power of a Category 1 Hurricane.\par \par \uc2 Florida is bracing for Tropical Storm Ian, meanwhile, as it strengthens over the Caribbean. Ian could be as strong as Cat 3 Hurricane when it makes expected landfall in Florida this Tuesday. The Governor there extended a State of Emergency for all Florida counties.\par \par \uc2 Now because of Ian, \uc2 NASA\uc2  postponed the Artemis moon rocket launch. It was scheduled for Tuesday. This is the third delay of the unmanned test flight. A new launch date has not yet been announced.\par \par \uc2 And Russia is holding what Western officials call sham referendums in Ukraine to annex territory, then defend it as Russian land. Ukrainian residents described voting in the presence of soldiers in ski masks, carrying rifles.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:38]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The new school year is underway, but as students in Texas hit the books, they have far fewer titles to choose from than their peers. That`s because the Lone Star State leads the country in books banned from school libraries.\par \par \uc2 According to a new report from Penn America, a nonprofit dedicated to free expression in literature, the study found more than 800 books were yanked from the shelves, many of them dealing with topics such as race, gender, sexuality, and abortion.\par \par \uc2 Some of the banned titles included Toni Morrison`s, "The Bluest Eyes," "Gender Queer" by Maia Kobe, and George M. Johnson`s "All Boys Aren`t Blue."\par \par \uc2 It goes without saying that these bans are an affront to the fundamental American value of free speech, but they also reveal a troubling backwards set of priorities on the part of those who run Texas schools. That`s because while they are rooting around in library books looking for things to be offended by, they aren`t doing nearly enough to prepare students for success.\par \par \uc2 Texas ranks 43rd in the country in educational attainment according to WalletHub, which the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  says can`t lead to higher rates of unemployment and lower average wages that lasts a lifetime that`s what it will lead to.\par \par \uc2 One in five Texas teachers were hired without certification last year, as the State struggles to attract and retain qualified educators.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of book bans often claim they are trying to protect children from indoctrination, and yet many appear to be victims of indoctrination themselves, showing up at School Board meetings, spouting bizarre online conspiracies.\par \par \uc2 MIT reports teachers are increasingly being targeted online with harassment and threats of violence when they run afoul of these conservative activists.\par \par \uc2 If these modern day book banners had been paying attention in History class, they`d know that censorship almost never leads in a positive direction. In a democracy, school are meant to be safe places for education and growth, not political battlefields.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:27:25]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 New polling suggests Americans aren`t buying Republicans` latest political stunt on immigration. A new survey finds just 29 percent of Americans approve of Republican Governors busing and flying migrants to Democratic States. Forty percent of respondents disapprove including nearly half of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The City of New York is dealing with the influx of thousands of migrants and asylum seekers, but coming by their own choice some and others at the urging of Republican officials.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to talk about what is being done to accommodate them is the Mayor of New York, Mayor Eric Adams.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Adams, first of all, thank you for joining us and let me get right to the immigration challenge New York City is facing right now. You recently estimated more than 13,000 migrants have arrived in New York City since the spring, but more than 9,500 of them seeking housing in the city shelter system and that number continues to grow every day.\par \par \uc2 Put that in perspective for our viewers. What kind of strain is that placing on the city`s resources?\\\par \par \uc2 MAYOR ERIC ADAMS (D), NEW YORK CITY: No, it`s amazing. The problem is when you look at the numbers, 10,300 are still in our shelter system. Over 13,600 were brought here to New York City, some wanting to go to different destinations, but they were duped into believing that they can only go to New York City. They were incentivized to do so.\par \par \uc2 But when you look at it, it is more than shelter, Reverend Sharpton. It`s all about education. You have 3,200 school-aged children we are finding and incorporating into our school system. It`s about healthcare. It is about making sure they have the basic needs that is required. That is food, shelter, clothing, and when you look at the already -- the problem we were facing coming out of COVID, it was just an inhumane, uncooperative action on the part of particularly the Governor of Texas.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, with that in mind, you announced this week, the city is responding to the influx of migrants by building two Humanitarian Emergency Response and Relief Centers, which are essentially tent cities designed to shelter migrants temporarily. On the other hand, your administration is responding to a desperate need, but on the other hand, immigration advocates have many concerns as to whether their facilities are legally adequate or whether a more permanent solution is needed, especially as we head into the winter.\par \par \uc2 What`s your response to these concerns?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: No one could have predicted over 13,000 refugee asylum seekers, migrants coming to New York City. We will always fulfill our moral and legal obligation to house New Yorkers and those who come to this city.\par \par \uc2 This is not a homeless issue. This is a migrant refugee asylum crisis that we`re facing and this is humanitarian action that was created by human hands. And so those who are evaluating this situation based on the traditional models that have been set in place over 40 years ago, that those who seek housing in New York City have get that housing.\par \par \uc2 We fulfill that all the time and we have done with over 13,000 people who have come to New York. That is not the same issue, and they need to look at it through the proper prism and not try to state that we`re not meeting our obligations both legally and morally.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Since August, Texas Governor Greg Abbott has sent roughly 2,700 migrants to New York City aboard more than 50 buses. In a recent interview for "The Texas Tribune Festival," you said, your team reached out to Governor Abbott`s administration asking to coordinate with them in their busing efforts, and they refused.\par \par \uc2 Why is the Texas Governor refusing to make even the slightest effort to make sure these transfers of human being from one place to another are at least handle in a more orderly and humane manner?\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: And crisis really causes us to have cooperation and coordination and people make a comparison to my communication with the El Paso Mayor, in comparison to the Governor of Texas. This is not a Democrat or Republican issue.\par \par \uc2 When people came to America, they were not trying to come to any particular State. They were trying to come to a place where we state dreams come alive, the American Dream. We were able to speak with the El Paso Mayor and stated "Let`s communicate." We`re not asking to have migrants or asylum seekers to come here in large numbers, but at least we should have some type of communication.\par \par \uc2 We did the same early with the Governor of Texas. They committed to communicating with us, they did just the opposite. They compelled people to get on the buses, they tagged some of them, 45-hour ride without food, proper food, water, restroom facilities, and medical care. Some arrived here with COVID.\par \par \uc2 It was clearly a subterfuge that they use, I believe, Reverend Sharpton to take attention away from what they have been doing over the last few months in his country, taking away the woman`s right to choose, taking away our protection by some of the gun laws that have come out of the Supreme Court decisions. We really need to focus on -- and this was just a political stunt and as you indicated in the poll, I think the stunt backfired.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I`ve got to go a little deeper, Mr. Mayor.\par \par \uc2 You and I know each other over 30 years, and you were one of the original members of National Action Network when we started. I`ve got to bring up a question of race. I`m bringing this up. Because not only are these Republican governors showing a callous disregard for these migrants and asylum seekers, most of whom are coming from Latin American countries, but they also seem to be specifically targeting cities with Black Mayors such as \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , in Chicago; Muriel Bowser in Washington, DC; and of course, you as Mayor of New York, even Martha`s Vineyard is well-known as a summer home for former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and many successful Blacks.\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts? Because I`m saying there`s a racial element to this and the President and others need to come in and help Black Mayors who already dealing with challenges. It`s not like you don`t have sanctuary cities with White Mayors.\par \par \uc2 It seems like there`s a racial element to this. I`ve been saying it and I`m going to keep saying because I don`t believe these are coincidences.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Well, Reverend Sharpton, we need to be clear on the facts. The facts are clear. Three Black Mayors -- Chicago, Washington, and New York City -- we were targeted and very clear, the Governor of Texas clearly stated that he was targeting these areas. It was clear that Martha`s Vineyard, a place where African-Americans have been coming for years including as you stated, the former President, President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [17:35:06]\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: I believe this is a clear attempt to undermine our cities. We are already dealing with the over proliferation of guns that are coming from the Southern border. We are coming out of COVID. New York was ground zero for the COVID crisis. We are experiencing historical crises already.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the specific targeting, out of all the States in America, we looked at the cities, cities where you had three Black mayors, where we were -- where they specifically sent buses to they to. They pass through a lot of cities to get to New York with destinations to get into New York.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: A lot of sanctuary cities they pass through by the way. They pass through a lot of sanctuary cities as well.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, I have to ask you about our fellow Americans in Puerto Rico. The island was devastated last weekend by Hurricane Fiona and some areas of still without power. You tweeted in solidarity with Puerto Rico earlier this week, pointing out the deep connections between this city and the island territory. Can you tell me more about what you and the city are doing to help them out? And I know you`ve announced I believe you`re going tomorrow to Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: These are our fellow Americans. I don`t subscribe to the theories of the former President that these are not current Americans. The brothers and sisters of New York has one of the largest Puerto Rican population outside the island of Puerto Rico. I will be leaving tonight to join a team that I`ve already sent to Puerto Rico, they are on the ground, three members of my administration, two emergency management officials and one police officer, we will be joining them and we are going to also look to go to the Dominican Republic as well.\par \par \uc2 We have an obligation, no matter how difficult things are, we have to continuously look after our brothers and sisters who are experiencing difficult times. That is what happened when we experienced some horrific issues like 9/11 and other natural disasters, people came to our aid. We are responsible for our brothers and sisters and we`re going to do as much as we can there in Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: New York Mayor Eric Adams, as always, thank you for being with us. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, next week will be the last chance for the January 6 Committee to show American voters what`s at stake this midterm.\par \par \uc2 My political panel joins me to discuss what we are expecting to see.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:41:44]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 With just 45 days until the midterms, let`s turn to my political panel to discuss what`s at stake this November. Joining me now Susan del Percio, Republican strategist and MSNBC contributor and former Democratic Governor, Steve Bullock of Montana, he is now the co-chair of American Bridge 21st Century.\par \par \uc2 Susan, let me go to you first. House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy rolled out a legislative agenda for the midterms on Friday. It covers in it, the economy, security, and public safety, freedom and government accountability. McCarthy said the first bill a Republican controlled House would pass would be to repeal the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 President Biden criticized the plan saying this. Let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: After opposing and obstructing everything we tried to do to stop progress for the last two years, the House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy, went to Pennsylvania and unveiled what he calls a Commitment to America.\par \par \uc2 That`s a thin series of policy goals with little or no detail that he says Republicans are going to pursue if they regain control of the Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, do you agree with the President?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I do agree with the President especially when he called it very thin on policy details.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, the most substantial piece of policy we`ve actually seen come from Republicans is from Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida, who wants to get rid of Medicare in five years. So, that`s what this country is looking towards, either very bad policy or very thin and not worthy of mention policy, frankly.\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake about it, if the Republicans get control, they will do -- it will not be any type of policy that they pursue, but rather an agenda of disruption and going against the President.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Governor, former President Trump once again played music that has been widely described as nearly identical to a song associated with the QAnon conspiracy. He did this at his last rally last night, in fact, in Wilmington, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Trump has been pushing the incident of an 18-year-old man who he claims was killed for being a Republican in North Dakota. Trump seems to be making an effort to lean more heavily into the fringe elements of his base and discussion of political violence ahead of the midterms. This comes as the January 6 Committee will hold its next hearing on this coming Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 This is perhaps the last chance the panel can probably talk about the threat Trump poses before voters head to the polls for the midterms. How concerned are you about the threat Trump poses to our democracy at this moment?\par \par \uc2 STEVE BULLOCK (D), FORMER MONTANA GOVERNOR: No, Rev, we should all be concerned. Let us not forget, here is the guy who said "Stand back and standby" to the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:05]\par \par \uc2 BULLOCK: Look at what he was doing last night as you note that, you know, more or less salutes either QAnon or almost look like Nazism. And as opposed to going against that, he is encouraging it.\par \par \uc2 Look, Americans, I think are finally tuning in and have tuned in to the threat to our democracy that January 6 and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  caused and continues to cause, and Democrats and Republicans say that, in this short and 46-year experiment called Representative Democracy, we can`t let this sort of just hate and baiting divide us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, I want to play you a political ad from Iowa`s Republican Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 . It got my attention this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): Defunding the police has to happen.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KIM REYNOLDS\uc2  (R-IO): Watching the news, you wonder: Has the rest of the country lost its mind?\par \par \uc2 Attacks on police, open borders, paying people not to work.\par \par \uc2 Aren`t you glad you live in Iowa?\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REYNOLDS: Here we still have commonsense. We kept businesses open and kids in school. We cut taxes and support our police, because in Iowa, America still works.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, of course the Black woman in that ad isn`t her opponent, Deidre DeJear, it is Missouri Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 . It is part of a nationwide trend of Republicans attacking Democrats for being soft on crime, using a combination of race tropes and outright lies.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, Dr. Oz is attacking his opponent, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  for allowing two African-American brothers to work on his campaign after serving 27 years in prison for second degree murder, even though they had their sentences commuted by the Governor last year and insists they were wrongly convicted.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have used this tactic time and time again. Why does this fear strategy resonate with the GOP base?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Well, it frankly registers because it`s what they fear. It is the others that have been frankly been blown up out of control through \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It doesn`t go much further than when you ask the Governor about what will happen with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and democracy. He knows how to call up people, whether it`s the Proud Boys or Q, or \uc2 David Duke\uc2 , for that matter.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact that Republicans are using this fear tactic, and you`re right, it has been something that has been done for a long time, they do it. I`m not saying it`s right, but they do it because it works and it does play to the base.\par \par \uc2 What is missing, though, is that what worked in 1992 will not necessarily work in 2022 and that is because we don`t just live in a small ecosystem of our congressional district or our country. People in Iowa are smarter than that. They see what is going around the country and the world.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that it is a tactic that will not work as well as the Governor hopes only because it`s a much different world.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Governor, the air charter company that assisted Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` latest stunt in flying dozens of Venezuelan migrants to Martha`s Vineyard, Vertol Systems Company has close political ties to the Republican Party, which has contributed thousands of dollars to GOP Super PACs, and was once legally represented by a close ally of the Governor.\par \par \uc2 The company was paid nearly $2 million by Florida for work it usually doesn`t do. But State officials have not said why it got the job.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the migrants being used as political props, DeSantis is using taxpayer funds to serve his allies. Your organization has been following this incident. What`s your reaction to this situation?\par \par \uc2 BULLOCK: Yes. No, Rev. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is trying every day to become more and more of a mini Trump and this just shows both DeSantis is a grifter and apparent sympathizer with Russia.\par \par \uc2 As a grifter, as you say $1.5 million to accompany that is run by a big Republican donor and had a close association with DeSantis` public safety czar. And when it comes to sympathizing with Russia, American Bridge Research recently sort of dug into this Vertol Systems, found that -- look, until two weeks ago on their website, they were bragging about being the only American civil company using Russian helicopters.\par \par \uc2 Just a few years ago, they paid over $48 million to a Russian company that the Treasury Department has banned because it is supporting actually Russians` transgressions against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:10]\par \par \uc2 BULLOCK: So here, he is really both being the grifter giving those huge contracts, illegal contracts -- they haven`t even been disclosed yet to close friends, and then also look at this company that now is taking its website and everything else down because they don`t want to see that just recently they`re bragging about their connections to Russia.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. Steve Bullock and Susan del Percio, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 After the break my final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We must do all we can to aid our American -- fellow Americans in Puerto Rico as they face the horrific disasters being caused by this hurricane. We have seen over the last several years is that this country, this island, I should say that has been hit with so many hurricanes because of years of neglect, some of which caused by not having a proper relationship and underpinnings economically from the United States.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:21]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I hope our government does all it can to support the people in Puerto Rico in this time of need, and all of us -- churches, nonprofits -- I will be talking with Ruben Diaz, Jr., the former Bronx Borough President, who is on the board of National Action Network, to see what we can do to help those in Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 We, at this time, have a moral obligation to stand up for those that need our help, especially since they are also Americans that are in the midst of a crisis.\par \par \uc2 I think with Mayor Adams going and others, it should say to this country, that when we see something happening in Puerto Rico, let`s stand up.\par \par \uc2 I remember when there were Navy bombings in Puerto Rico, I and others went down and protested about it. So, we`ve stood up and done this in other areas, let`s do it as we deal with this hurricane.\par \par \uc2 I went to jail three months for leading that protest. I`m not new to standing up for Puerto Rico. I`m not new to this. I`m true to this.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on POLITICSNATION, a brand new initiative by the \uc2 EPA\uc2  taking action on environmental justice and Civil Rights. Administrator Michael Regan will be here to tell us all about it. That`s Sunday, 5:00 PM Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:26]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220925ei9o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for September 23, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Jonathan Allen, Jake Ward, Liz Plank \par \uc2 8,468 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:21]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, as the former guy is back ranting about law enforcement, the feds are once again allowed to comb through the classified documents recovered from Mar-a-Lago, as the deadline looms for Team Trump to try to back up his wild claims.\par \par \uc2 Then the disturbing new ship, conspiracy theories are even more a part of MAGA mainstream, as Trump openly embraces their messaging. Plus, it has been a week dominated by major headlines, and we`re going to mix it up with a new team to break them all down, as the 11th Hour gets underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. The former president ends his week in even deeper legal jeopardy as he tries to cling to political power and control of the GOP. Tonight, the investigation into the classified documents seized at his club and Florida is heating up. Intel officials tell NBC News they have reviewed -- they have resumed their review of the top-secret records as they tried to figure out the potential damage to national security. This comes just days after appeals court judges gave the green light for the feds to access the evidence.\par \par \uc2 Remember, it has now been nearly seven weeks since the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search and Trump has yet to explain what he was doing with the documents in the first place. But his former personal lawyer suggests Trump`s motives may have something to do with the many investigations that are all unfolding around him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ATTORNEY: Obviously he took personal momento so in that way he can show off to people when he would go thereafter to maybe Saudi Arabia or he`d show hey, look, I got this letter from Putin. Or I got this from love letter. I mean, who gets love letters, right? From Kim Jong- un. I also believe he took the top-secret documents for the sole purpose of using it as a Get Out of Jail Free card. You want to indict me and my family, you want to incarcerate me, I`m going to turn these documents over to our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Meanwhile, there`s new reporting that Trump`s current lawyers who are cameras caught at a D.C. Federal Court yesterday are in a secret battle with the Justice Department. They reportedly are trying to prevent witnesses from testifying to a grand jury about his actions ahead of January 6.\par \par \uc2 Trump spent this evening holding a rally for North Carolina Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 . And earlier today, several Trump allies launched a new super PAC called MAGA Inc. Politico reports it will spend heavily to support Trump endorsed candidates in the midterms and beyond.\par \par \uc2 House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  were also focused on messaging ahead of the November election. McCarthy began this morning laying out what he called his party`s, "Commitment to America."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): On that very first day that we`re sworn in, you`ll see that it all changes. We can secure our border. We could become energy independent, where your price of gas is lower. We can build an education system that has a parent`s Bill of Rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: In a rebuttal of sorts that followed, President Biden was focused on what he says is a current threat to American democracy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: MAGA Republicans in a few decades have refused to accept the results of the 2020 election, has become a litmus test in their party to play his loyalty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by buying into the big lie. You can`t let the integrity of our elections be undermined. Democracy can`t survive it cannot survive not a joke, can`t survive when one side believes there`s only two outcomes than an election is a win, or they were cheated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tonight, the Biden`s hosted Trump`s favorite artists Elton John on the White House lawn. You might remember Trump tried but failed to get Elton to perform at his 2016 inauguration. But tonight, Sir Elton John gave another concert to a bipartisan audience in the Biden`s backyard.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And when the concert ended, the President and First Lady surprise the singer by presenting him with the National Humanities Medal for his work in fighting HIV/AIDS.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter this evening with the help of our lead off a Friday night panel. Barb McQuade is here, veteran federal prosecutor and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan, Stuart Stevens joins us, a veteran of the \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  Presidential campaigns. He`s now with the Lincoln Project, and my friend and colleague \uc2 Jon Allen\uc2 , Senior National Politics Reporter here at NBC News.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:13]\par \par \uc2 Barb, I got to go to you first, because Trump has had a lot of legal setbacks on a lot of fronts this week. And now the intel community is going back through those classified documents. I`m guessing that cannot be good for Trump, why else would he have been fighting against it so hard?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Yeah, and Stephanie, also, I think one thing that`s really important is that this -- the 11th Circuit also ruled that they may look at it for that intelligence risk assessment as well as criminal investigation, because those two tasks are so inextricably intertwined. And so, I imagine some of what they`re doing is trying to assess who had access to those documents. Why were there empty file folders, for example. And those kinds of things could point to criminal responsibility for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I also think that \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  clip that you played there is really fascinating. You know, I don`t know whether his speculation is accurate. But there is this concept in government when it comes to classified documents known as Graymail. And that is this idea that a person who has access to classified information can threaten to disclose them if they are prosecuted criminally. And so, you know, that that argument that he was holding them as leverage in case he ever gotten into trouble. \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is certainly speculating. But there is some merit to that strategy that we`ve seen occur in past cases.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s a scary speculation. Barb, we also saw headlines today sort of suggesting that the special master is testing Trump`s lawyers. What could be going on there?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yeah, I think that`s right, Stephanie. You know, we`ve heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say in the public, in the media, that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted evidence when they seize those documents at Mar-a-Lago that included classified documents. You know, of course, his defense has been changed. No, it was declassified. No, they weren`t there, all kinds of different defenses. But one of the things that Judge Dearie has done is he`s really pinning down the lawyers. The lawyers have never repeated these claims in court, because in court lies have consequences. Lawyers could face sanctions, disbarment, even criminal prosecution for making false statements in court.\par \par \uc2 And so, what Judge Dearie has said is, put your money where your mouth is, if you are claiming that there was evidence planted here than you need to say so and so he has given them a deadline of one week from today, to go through their full inventory of documents, and name the document that you think was planted there by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And it`ll be stunning to me if they actually name one, because this is where there are consequences for telling falsehoods.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mr. Allen, you wrote about Tish James` case and how it`s different from the other investigations against Trump. You said this one is personal. How so? And how does that change things? Because one would guess that I don`t know, going through every inch of Mar-a-Lago seems kind of personal to?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER: There were certainly some frustration about that on the Trump side the \uc2 FBI\uc2  going through everything at Mar-a-Lago. What`s different here is that Tish James is going straight at the heart of Donald Trump`s brand of his persona, and also straight in his family. If she`s able to win here, he will not be able to do business in New York, where he built this global brand, where he became the real estate baron, launched his television career to your fired moments and of course, launched his campaign for the presidency. And in addition to that, she`s named \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , Donald Jr. and \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  and basically said they were parties to what she described as a fraud against insurers, lenders. And the taxman, which means the public.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Humiliating. Stuart, Trump was back at it tonight at this rally in North Carolina. I want to share a bit of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We got more votes than any sitting president in the history of our country by far. And we may just have to do it again, right? We may have to do it again. We may have to do it again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Stuart, I`m not even going to weight stuff for fact`s sake on that, like enough already. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got the most votes, like this is absurd. But I do want to ask about this Super PAC that Trump allies just launched, is this about the midterms coming up? Or is it really about Trump in `24?\par \par \uc2 STUART STEVENS, THE LINCOLN PROJECT SENIOR ADVISER: Look, I think this is about a bunch of guys who are trying to make money. It was I think accurately called MAGA Inc. That`s really kind of putting it out there. I mean, there`s an industrial political complex in America and I think these guys see that they can make a bunch of money using the MAGA Inc. name. You know, by law, they`re not allowed to coordinate with Trump supposedly.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I think politics is one of the weirdest businesses because every year despite all the economic factors, we spend more money on campaigns than the previous year. So, I think this is some guys backing up the truck to the mega store and seeing what they can get.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:06]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But how about Trump himself, he already has a big, big super PAC. He didn`t seem to be spending much of that on any of these Trump endorsed candidates, why doesn`t he pony up some dough?\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Yeah, you know, from the very beginning, if you knew anything about campaigns, it was clear that the entire Trump campaign going back to 2015 was really a large criminal enterprise. And when they won, they took over the \uc2 RNC\uc2 . And the \uc2 RNC\uc2  became really a way that the Trump family was able to make money, launder money, and this continues. And you know, it`s one of the most shameful things and it`s being exposed. You have Bannon now as part of this grift, who`s now been indicted twice. There`s nobody to pardon him now. And it`s really an abuse of people give money to this campaign, and to Trump really thinking that they`re going to be doing good. And we see it that this money was exposed in the January 6 hearings, that what were they`re calling a recount fund or something, never went to that.\par \par \uc2 It`s just not surprising at all. If you know, the history of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it`s being exposed down was hanging in New York now. But it is that the Republican Party has gone along with this. And like an episode out of The Sopranos, they`ve allowed themselves to be taken over by this Trump corruption. And no one thought back and now that Trump corruption pretty much is the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, you do have a point there, but to anybody who`s still writing him a check, all the facts are out there, if you want to write a check to this, that`s kind of on you.\par \par \uc2 Barb, give us a bit of a civics lesson because tonight Trump is going after Tish James, saying that she should go after killers and drug dealers, gang members, instead of little old him. Can you explain to us that`s not even what the New York AG does?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yeah, you know, this is one of the defenses. He`s already laid out the defensive of, these were big banks, if they had just done their due diligence, then I couldn`t have been successful in defrauding them. You know, that`s victim blaming. And now this is -- she should be using her resources to go after file crime and not after me, as you say, that is not the purview of the Attorney General. That is the purview of district attorneys throughout the state of New York. The Attorney General is there to safeguard the state of New York. And this is a fraud against the people of the state of New York to the tune of $250 million. That is a very significant fraud.\par \par \uc2 And when you think about who are the victims here? One, it is the lenders who gave more favorable terms to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than he otherwise would have received, and who ultimately pays the cost there. All of the rest of us who do our banking and borrowing from these same banks. The tax authorities are also to blame -- victims here. And who are the victims when it`s tax authorities, the taxpayers, the rest of us. And so, some people steal money with guns, and that`s what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants to focus on. But other people steal money with lies. And that`s what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has allegedly done here and is absolutely the job of the Attorney General to discourage that ill- gotten game.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Jon Allen\uc2 , for years and years, Trump said, I can`t show you my taxes because they`re under audit. Let`s pretend they are under audit, if they were, wouldn`t he be in a mountain of trouble? Only Tish James would discover this, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  wouldn`t?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Well, Tish James has referred this case to the \uc2 IRS\uc2  because I think she believes that if in fact he was under audit, he would have been in a heap of trouble, as you said. What we`ve seen from his taxes before, what we`ve seen, from statements of his wealth, and his assets, his properties, all suggests that he`s been -- that he`s been, you know, I guess inflating the value of what he`s worth for a long time. And we`ve heard him make these claims publicly. Now, we`re seeing Tish James lay out evidence of these inflated claims which affect as Barbara was just saying, affect the lenders, the insurers and, and the tax collectors, which for the most part means us.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Man, so either the \uc2 IRS\uc2  is asleep at the wheel or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is lying, or some combination of the two.\par \par \uc2 Stuart, Kevin McCarthy`s Commitment to America sounds an awful lot like Newt Gingrich`s Contract with America from the Clinton years. It just seemed like though, pretty late in the game for the GOP to come up with an agenda. We`ve got less than six weeks to go, what is this?\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Yeah, today was a fascinating day in American politics, because you had both the Biden presidency and what`s really the Democratic campaign, and McCarthy out there. And campaigns are ultimately a battle of agenda who`s winning the agenda battle? And what this was, was basically an admission by Republicans that they weren`t running on anything. And we`re almost in October, you know, there has been this cultural war, you know, what I call the bathrooms and bedrooms campaign that someone like DeSantis is running on.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:10]\par \par \uc2 And they realize that they`re losing, and people are not -- they don`t have any reason to vote for Republicans. So now, they roll this thing out, if you read it, it`s just kind of a bunch of bromides that you really could have said, anytime over the last, you know, 40 years in American politics, like fix the national debt. But the reality is, you know, when Republicans controlled government, they had a chance to do stuff, like, you know, the infrastructure bill, and they never got it done. I mean, if you had been a kid born the first week that they talked about the infrastructure bill, you would have been like in the first grade, but the time Democrats finally passed it.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that when Biden is out there talking about democracy, they are pushing an agenda that is resonating with people and the -- whenever -- in history, you give people who are anti-democratic, more power, they don`t become more democratic, they become more anti-democratic. And that`s really what`s at stake here. And I`m just so glad to see the President out there pushing it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We should remind our audience, the GOP did try to roll out a plan months ago \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  but in it, it involves raising taxes and taking a hatchet to Social Security and they quickly realize that`s not what voters are looking for.\par \par \uc2 Barb McQuade, Stuart Stevens, \uc2 Jon Allen\uc2 , thank you for starting us off on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, as pressure grows on the former president, he is pulling fringe and baseless conspiracy theories out of the shadows and right onto the main stage.\par \par \uc2 And later, it has been a another very eventful week. In a deeply divided nation. You will not want to miss our all-star panel digging into it all. The 11th Hour just getting underway on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:21:35]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We are a nation that has lost its way. But we are not going to let this continue. Two years ago, we were a great nation. And we will soon be a great nation again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is hard not to notice the change and it is disturbing. In recent days \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has gone from just winking at QAnon to very openly embracing it. You saw it again today on Truth Social, and lately at his rallies tonight for the second time in just two weeks, Trump wrapped his rally in North Carolina with music that you heard right there that sounds an awful lot like the QAnon anthem playing in the background.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Tech Correspondent Jake Ward spent years studying what behavioral science says about the rise of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and conspiracy theories. He wrote the book, "The LOOP: How Technology is Creating a World Without Choices and How to Fight Back."\par \par \uc2 Jake, I`m so glad you`re here because so much is changing quickly. For quite some time, Trump danced around QAnon, kind of winked at it. And suddenly he is openly embracing the crazy why now?\par \par \uc2 JAKE WARD, NBC NEWS TECH CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think, Stephanie, that you`re right, winking is the relationship that he had with this conspiracy theory. But it`s also important to understand that he really relied on it for a strategy and support for a long time. I mean QAnon adherence, right, the people who believe in this really outrageous conspiracy theory that a cabal of powerful people is, you know, abusing children and running the world, and that somehow \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to save us all from that, you know, that belief system has been this extraordinary organizing engine that has served his purposes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the guy has been retweeting QAnon, sympathizing accounts of since 2016 -- 2017, when it first came online, you know, so he has really depended on it in many ways. It`s a machine for selling merchandise. It`s a machine for activating people in the midterms as he did in 2018. It`s a way in a weird way of sanitizing some of his most extreme backers, alright, people that say terrible racist, and anti-Semitic things, when their memes get picked up by QAnon people to get sanitized in this way that let them travel a little bit farther.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s always been able to rely on QAnon. But this is now a very, very open embrace of it. I have to assume it has something to do with wanting the most rock-solid base he can find right now. And there`s nobody more rock solid than people who believe in Q.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Jake, this is not just about marketing and money making this isn`t the -- they`re just become as you are. They`re not, you know, coastal elites. This is real America. The U.S. has labeled QAnon as a domestic terror threat. Can you talk about the real dangers here?\par \par \uc2 WARD: Well, this is the reason right that QAnon has been deep platformed off all of the major platforms because it is the kind of weird conspiracy theory and the adherence of it have really encouraged violence. There is this simmering undertone of threat to the QAnon community and so that`s right, it has been taken down. You know, it really is meant -- it was in many ways fractured by his loss in 2020 because they kept believing over and over again that he would somehow rise and arrest \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and all this stuff was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:05]\par \par \uc2 So, you are right, it is all of those things, these are -- you know, and this is why I think when we see the rally today, and we see people throwing up the one finger, which is supposed to signify this where we go one, we go all belief that is sort of one of the logos, the mottos of the QAnon conspiracy theorists. You know, when we see that stuff happening out in the open, it`s pretty malevolent, it`s pretty scary to see. And so, it is sort of strange to imagine, you know, that this kind of stuff, that we`re seeing it on camera, right? That people are actually coming out in this stuff, where they have just been winking at each other online before.\par \par \uc2 I think it has to do with how desperate the former president is for a certain rock-solid base of support. And he seems to be willing to take the scrutiny that`s going to come with it, you know, that, like you say that this thing has been basically described as a terror organization. The fact that he`s openly talking about it now is very disturbing, and I think speaks to how desperate he is for support going into the midterms. And then going into 2024, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jake, there are those in the group, some leaders who are Neo-Nazis, who are anti-immigrant, who are openly racist, but is that all of them? Or are there people who have been swept up into this movement and now just fed all these conspiracy theories?\par \par \uc2 WARD: Well, the people who study this stuff, and there is a fantastic new book out of Harvard called Meme Wars that really talks about the relationship very, very well. And I think one of the things that they articulated --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The author is on last night.\par \par \uc2 WARD: -- so many academics have articulated, you know all about it, right? So, these folks are extraordinary at pointing out the ways in which social media as an ecosystem, has really facilitated the growth of this kind of thing. It was originally intended to bring together people with disparate ideas and, you know, create a little community and electoral community. Well, now it turns out to have facilitated this weird, specific thing in which people believe themselves to be investigating the truth. That`s what QAnon people think that they are doing their own research, doing their own reporting.\par \par \uc2 What they are, in fact, only doing is going and finding the greatest pockets of popularity for the most extreme ideas that they happen to believe in. It is really a sort of a mutation of what the internet was designed to do. And it has unfortunately created this monstrous, monstrous problem that we have right now. One that has gotten away from the social media companies. And now that the form