interesting, because we`re expecting him to lay out a sharper vision of his party`s midterm message and push back against the Republican agenda. That was unveiled by Kevin McCarthy just this morning.\par \par \uc2 And both party strategies are facing the uncertainty of just what shoe might drop next in the myriad of investigations into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his associates and January 6. We know today that the clock is ticking for the former president and his legal team. They`ve now got just one week to produce evidence to backup Donald Trump`s repeated attacks on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , including allegations that agents planted records at his Mar-a-Lago home. He said it again this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The problem that you have is they go into rooms they won`t let anybody near the -- they wouldn`t even let them in the same building. Did they drop anything into those piles? Or did they do it later? There`s no chain of custody here with them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But now a Special Master Raymond Dearie has ordered Trump`s lawyers late Thursday, he did this just last night to account for in court, those unsubstantiated claims that Trump himself keeps making on T.V. And that`s not all Judge Dearie is asking for. He also wants Trump`s attorneys to say whether they think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was mistaken about what evidence was taken or where it was found.\par \par \uc2 And he`s giving them one last chance to say whether they want to claim the \uc2 FBI\uc2  took anything that`s not on the list. And if they don`t, the judge said, unequivocally, "This submission shall be plaintiff`s final opportunity to raise any factual disputes as to the completeness and accuracy of the detailed property inventory."\par \par \uc2 Now what remains to be seen is whether what happens next week will actually change public perception, though there`s some evidence it may already be happening. A new poll by Marquette show 67 percent of Americans believe the president had classified documents in his home, and that includes nearly four out of every 10 Republicans. I want to bring in our panel. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is at a Trump rally in Wilmington, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Charles Coleman is with me here in studio. Former prosecutor in Brooklyn, New York, and an MSNBC legal analyst. And Josh Dawsey, investigative reporter with \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . So Josh, is this what we`re seeing from Judge Dearie, a classic case of be careful what you wish for, because, after all, when Trump`s team picked Judge Dearie as a special master, I`m guessing they weren`t expecting him to call them out on their claims like this.\par \par \uc2 JOSH DAWSEY, INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, what he`s essentially said is if you`re going to say all these things in the public domain that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted evidence, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  behaved unethically that there are things there that you have privilege that you declassified certain things, that you have to be willing to say that under oath, and you cannot just make these claims to, you know, a permanent minority of your supporters or base that doesn`t really leave you.\par \par \uc2 But you have to make them under oath to the entire court. And so far, I guess we`ll see if they do that or not. What we`ve seen in the past is that when forced to go under oath or to say things in court, the former president`s legal team has not been willing to backup some of the more as - - I guess ridiculous assertions for lack of a better word that he`s made. For example, that you could just think that something was declassified and all of a sudden it was declassified, or that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted evidence.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he`s saying all these things publicly to his base, but whether his legal team will go along with that and it`s unclear to me and I think it`s very unlikely they go along with the idea that you can just think that something is declassified. The other thing that he said that they`ve not been wanting to say under oath is that he did declassify a lot of these documents and he had a standing declassification order.\par \par \uc2 What a former White House officials are told us that that`s just not so and they don`t know what he`s talking about. So we`ll see if his legal team was willing to say any of this under oath, but this judge who is respected basically on both sides and surprised a lot of folks that Trump`s team even picked him or was willing to pick him is really going further than Aileen Cannon, the judge in Florida, by making them say these things to the court.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This is so interesting to me, Charles, because I -- look, it`s not like a special master gets appointed every day.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This seems like to be going really far. And, you know, as Josh has pointed out, there were a number of things that Trump`s attorneys did not want to go on the record about for example, including whether or not he actually had classified documents there.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:08]\par \par \uc2 The judge is saying, I don`t care what you do or don`t want to do. These are the questions you will answer. Here`s the time by which you will answer them. If they can`t prove any of this stuff that he`s been saying, or they have been claiming, what does it do to their case? Do they have one?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, they don`t. And Chris, one of the things that I predicted with this judge was that he was going to set a very different tone than what we had already been seeing out of Florida at the district level. This special master is not playing around. He`s making it very clear to them that he is going to do everything that he can to get this train back on the tracks, even though it`s left the station already.\par \par \uc2 What he is, let them know is I am going to narrow down all of the issues that are in dispute, so that I can swiftly and methodically deal with anything that may be in question. And so these sort of narrative points that had been floating out in the atmosphere and in the media is speculation about, well, maybe these documents were planted, or maybe they were declassified. He is going to get them to commit one way or the other because he understands that they know that there is a penalty for lying on the record for not telling the truth or being less than honest on the record.\par \par \uc2 And so now he`s eliminating this space of ambiguity to make it very clear for them. We`ve got to narrow down the issues, move forward, make decisions and get this thing over with.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So I just want to make sure that we`re clear about what sort of -- what the responsibility is here. If they respond to him in writing, is that essentially the equivalent of being under oath?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes. Because you swear and affirm when you are filing a pleading with the court, that you know these things to be true. And you usually write as an attorney, under penalty of perjury. And so you understand that the same penalty that you would face it if you took the stand, put your hand on a bible and said that I swear and affirm under penalty of perjury, you`re being held to that same standard when you file a pleading in court.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You said one word that clearly is applicable here, which is swift.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: One of the things we`ve seen from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for decades, but we`ve also seen in this case is delay, delay, delay. It doesn`t look like the special master is going to have any of that.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Not at all. Judge Dearie is very clear about trying to move this along.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So what does that do with the DOJ case?\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, I think it only helps the DOJ case. When you think about the Eleventh Circuit and that decision that came down in terms of looking at what the district court decided and allowing them to proceed more swiftly with their criminal investigation, getting the special master to make decisions quickly and swiftly in terms of whatever it is that they can use, whatever it is that they should be able to use is only going to enable them to do what they need to do in terms of pursuing a potential indictment and prosecution against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others.\par \par \uc2 Without that, it only delays, you know, the overall process. And so, I think this is going to be a move that ultimately bites Team Trump in the backside because they are the ones who asked for this. And ultimately -- and it doesn`t look like it`s going to play in their favor.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. So Vaughn, you`ve been out there with Trump`s people a lot. If Trump fails to backup all of this in court, does it hurt him politically in any way? What are you hearing from folks you`re talking to?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS POLITICAL REPORTER: I think Chris there`s a reality that there is, number one, a court of public opinion. And then there`s a court of law who as we`ve seen over the last five years, the court of law has oftentimes had a little impact over those who have made up the base of loyal support to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And what you have seen was in 2020, election, ultimately, among the court of public opinion when the general election voting base is voting, that it costs him.\par \par \uc2 In the likes of Wisconsin narrowly losing Michigan, Georgia, Arizona. But then there`s a second reality that can be true at the same time, and is that is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is able to legitimately claim that most of his Trump-backed candidates won the GOP primaries, and that he continues to hold that significant powerful sway among Republican voters here and he is campaigning --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. I need to interrupt you if I can, Vaughn. I`m sorry, Vaughn, I have to interrupt you because we are going to go to President Biden speaking at a DNC event and saying we`re 46 days to the midterm. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You paid into. Holy mackerel. Anyway, but there`s a lot -- really, truly, there`s a lot in the ballot. There`s a -- my dad used to have an expression. He`d say, Joey, don`t compare me to the almighty. Compare me to the alternative. And we have a real alternative here that -- this is not a -- this is not going to be a -- an election that isn`t of significant consequence.\par \par \uc2 Gun safety for our kids and gun violence are on the ballot. The idea that you start school this year and kids in many parts of the country learn how to duck and cover rather than -- no, I`m serious, think about it. Rather than talking about reading, writing, and arithmetic is a very different circumstance. It`s not right. It`s not who we are. It`s not who we should be.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, look, the -- of survival our planet is on the ballot. And that sounds like hyperbole, but it genuinely is.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:08]\par \par \uc2 Well, I tell you what, the thing that we found out and everybody found out. There`s not a lot of total climate deniers anymore after they`ve seen what happened this year. But guess what? We got a lot to do.\par \par \uc2 You got to say hi to me. We go back a long way. She was 12. I was 30. But anyway, this woman helped me get an awful lot done. Anyway.\par \par \uc2 But right now -- and it`s not hyperbole to suggest right now democracy itself is on the ballot as well.\par \par \uc2 Folks, I believe America is at an inflection point. And I apologize to -- I`m a labor guy. I`ve spent a lot of time with all of you -- not all of you, but with labor movements since I got elected and my whole career. But -- and I apologize if I repeat some things.\par \par \uc2 But I think we`re really at an inflection point. It occurs every three, four, or five generations. It doesn`t occur every election.\par \par \uc2 And by inflection point I mean that these moments -- these are going to determine the shape of everything that`s going to come after, what we do now. What we do in the next several years is going to determine what this country looks like in 25 and 30 years. It`s that consequential.\par \par \uc2 Now, 46 days to choose, 46 days. And the path offered by Democrats is contrasted with the one offered by the MAGA Republicans. And by the way, not all Republicans are MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So I want to make that clear. And for years and years, I had a reputation - - I remember I got beat up in the campaign by saying that I wanted to unify the country and unify the parties. You used to be able to do that. But things have changed a whole bunch.\par \par \uc2 The MAGA Republicans control the Republican Party right now, and that`s self-evident. That`s self-evident. So there`s a lot at stake here. And I`m going to talk a little bit about that right now.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, after opposing and obstructing everything we tried to do to stop progress for the last two years, the House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy went to Pennsylvania and unveiled what he calls a Commitment to America. That`s a -- that`s a thin series of policy goals, with little or no detail that he says Republicans are going to pursue if they regain control of the Congress.\par \par \uc2 In the course of nearly an hour, here`s a few of the things we didn`t hear. We didn`t hear him mention the right to choose. We didn`t hear him mention Medicare. We didn`t hear him mention Social Security.\par \par \uc2 So let`s take a look at what Kevin said today. He said Republicans want to, "preserve our constitutional freedom." That sounds great. I`m for doing that as well. We all are. But look at what they`ve actually done.\par \par \uc2 The MAGA Republicans just cheered and embraced the first Supreme Court decision in our entire history -- the first one in our entire history that just didn`t fail to preserve a constitutional freedom. It actually took away a fundamental right that had been granted by the same court to so many Americans. The constitutional right to choose. And now nearly half the states in the United States of America have either passed a ban on abortion or will shortly. And in many states, abortion is already banned even in cases of rape and incest, a fundamental change.\par \par \uc2 Already, 166 House Republicans have signed on to a bill that would ban abortion nationwide. And the senior senator from South Carolina, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , has proposed a national ban on abortion with criminal penalties put on doctors and put them in jail if they, in fact, violate the ban.\par \par \uc2 So, in 46 days, America is going to choose. Republicans win control of the Congress, abortion will be banned. And, by the way, it will be initially banned, but if they win Congress, I will veto it.\par \par \uc2 But think what`s happening. They`re banning in the case of -- in the case of rape and incest in many states. No exceptions. And they`re going to be - - they`re trying to criminalize it nationwide.\par \par \uc2 But if you give me two more Democratic senators in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , I promise you -- I promise you we`re going to codify Roe. We`ll once again make Roe the law of the land. And we`ll once again protect a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 And the power to get this done is in the hands of the American people, especially the women of America.\par \par \uc2 No. It really is. Justice Alito said that women can decide the outcome of this election, some quote in the -- in the actual decision. Well, he isn`t seen nothing yet.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:01]\par \par \uc2 I don`t believe that MAGA Republicans have a clue about the power of American women. Let me tell you something. They`re about to find out.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, Kevin McCarthy also talked about, "longer, healthier lives for Americans." Again, it sounds great. Who`s not for that? Well, just look at what the Republicans are doing. Already, 158 House Republicans have signed on to a Republican budget that will cut Medicare and Social Security already.\par \par \uc2 The man in charge of electing Republicans in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida, has proposed a plan to put Social Security on the chopping block every five years. De novo.\par \par \uc2 No, I`m not -- I know this sounds like -- but I really mean it. Look, go on the Internet and check it out. They`ve laid out the platform.\par \par \uc2 It means every five years, according to -- if they succeed in the Senate, the Congress will vote to either cut, reduce, or completely eliminate Social Security, or bring it back exactly as it is. What do you think the prospects of it coming back exactly as it is?\par \par \uc2 You`ve been paying into Social Security ever since you got your first paycheck. Well, then there`s Senator Ron Johnson of Wisconsin as my mother would say, God bless his soul.\par \par \uc2 Look, he thinks waiting five years is too long. He wants to put -- no, he wants to put Social Security on the chopping block every single year. If Congress doesn`t vote to keep it, it goes away. An affirmative requirement to vote to keep it. Now, think of that. It`s not just Social Security he wants to put up, if you want Medicare, Medicaid benefits, every federal budget -- every federal benefit gets up and has to be voted on every year. De novo. Just from the -- from scratch.\par \par \uc2 So, in 46 days, America is going to face a choice. If Republicans control the Congress, Social Security will be on the chopping block. But if you support the Democrats, I promise you this -- Social Security will be protected. Period. And I won`t let any MAGA Republican take it away.\par \par \uc2 Leader McCarthy talked about waiting -- wanting a nation -- a nation that`s safe. Well, let`s look at what Republicans have done to make it safe. They voted against virtually everything we`ve done to reduce crime and keep communities safe.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan, it was the -- when we first got into office, we passed it. You know what I was dying to do? An awful lot of mayors and governors and county executives, they don`t have -- they didn`t have the tax base, because I was left with an economy that was in distress.\par \par \uc2 So we said, here you go. We`re going to give you this money so you can make sure you can keep your schools open, you can make sure you keep police departments open. Make sure you keep your hospitals open. Make sure you keep the fire departments open. And guess what? Every single Republican in the Congress voted against that. Every single Republican voted against it.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, we`re in a situation where now the MAGA Republicans are calling for defunding. They`re defunding. Defunding the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Now, let me tell you something. I don`t think -- and I know some of you weren`t happy with me, I didn`t think we should defund the police. I never believed that and said so at the time. But I sure in hell don`t think we should be defunding the \uc2 FBI\uc2  either.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s the -- then there`s the day we`ll never forget on January 6th. What happened on the 6th? Law enforcement was attacked, assaulted, brutalized, and stomped on, and later, several died. Some lost their lives because of that day. The MAGA Republicans didn`t side with law enforcement, they sided with the insurrectionists. And they still do. Don`t tell me you support law enforcement if you can`t condemn what happened on January the 6th.Don`t tell me that.\par \par \uc2 You can`t -- you can`t be pro-law enforcement and pro-insurrection. You can`t claim to be a party of law and order and call the people who attacked the police on January 6th patriots. That`s what they`re called.\par \par \uc2 And you can`t claim to be for a safe America if you take orders from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look, we just passed the most meaningful gun safety law in 30 years in this country, but most Republicans voted against it. Some, thank God, joined us and we passed it, and sided -- but most sided with the \uc2 NRA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After Buffalo, Uvalde, Newtown, El Paso, Parkland, Charleston, Las Vegas, Orlando, I went to every one of those places. I spent hours and hours and hours with those families, not a joke. Not a joke. Hours with them. I know what it`s like under different circumstances to lose children. But lose children like they lost their children? Enough. We`ve had enough. It`s time to pass the assault weapons ban.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:08]\par \par \uc2 Look, I support the Second Amendment. I have two shotguns, and I`m not -- I -- the only thing I really do is really target practice. I haven`t done that in a long time.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. There`s no justification for a weapon of war. None. The speed of that bullet is five times that that comes out of the muzzle of most weapons. It can penetrate your vests.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, as I pointed out when I was -- got a pass the first time as a senator in Delaware, we`re a big gun-owning state. A lot of duck hunters, a lot of hunters. And I`d be going -- and I literally went up and down the streams campaigning and going to the people who were part -- who belonged to the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and others. And I`d say, how many -- how many deer out here are wearing a Kevlar vests? You know?\par \par \uc2 No, but I`m -- but I`m -- seriously, think about it. What in God`s name do you need an assault weapon for? It`s an assault weapon designed to kill people, to defend America, to defend people.\par \par \uc2 But, folks, look, it`s just -- we`re just -- we`re just -- it`s way out of hand.\par \par \uc2 I`ve taken on the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and I beat them before, and I plan on doing it again.\par \par \uc2 And, folks, we can do it again. Our children -- our children should be in a situation of learning how, as I said, to read and write, not duck and cover.\par \par \uc2 So, in 46 days, if you want a safer America, America faces a choice. Republicans will once again side with the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and put American families and children at risk. And, I mean, some Democrats as well, and there are some Republicans who support us. It`s not down the line but it`s part of their platform. Democrats will ban assault weapons once and for all, God willing.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy also talks about their being for a strong economy.\par \par \uc2 But look what the Republicans have done. When I came to office, the economy was flat on its back. Millions out of work. Food lines that stretched -- remember seeing all those food lines? People in nice-looking automobiles waiting for an hour, hour and a half just to have a bucket of -- a box of food put in their trunk. In the United States of America, in the United States of America, that was happening, all across America.\par \par \uc2 The most anemic job performance since Herbert Hoover was in place. And that`s not hyperbole, that`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 So we acted quickly. We passed the American Rescue Plan, which lifted this nation from economic crisis to economic recovery. And every single Republican voted for it.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 10 million more jobs have been created since I`ve been president, the highest number of jobs in that period of time of any President of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 We have a 3.7 percent unemployment rate, the lowest in 50 -- more than 50 years. A record number of new -- record number of new small businesses created and over 668,000 new manufacturing jobs in America.\par \par \uc2 The American Rescue Plan helped keep teachers, police officers, firefighters, first responders on the job. It provided the money needed to get schools open. And it was a big reason we have been able to vaccinate over 220 million Americans. More to do. With the Infrastructure Law we passed, we did get some Republican help for that. Well, that Infrastructure Law is the most significant investment in America since President Eisenhower`s Interstate Highway System.\par \par \uc2 Now, some Republicans voted for it, and some were critical. But the truth is there`s a lot more Republicans out there taking credit for the new bridges and those (INAUDIBLE) than actually voted for it. I love going by and they`re, you know, and this is great thing. I voted against it, but this is a great thing.\par \par \uc2 But, folks, in addition to inheriting a failed MAGA Republican economy, we also confronted a global pandemic and Putin`s war in Ukraine, and that`s driven the global inflation we see today. That`s why it`s so important that we pass the Inflation Reduction Act, which I proposed from the beginning but didn`t get passed until this summer.\par \par \uc2 This law will cut costs for families, help reduce inflation at the kitchen table. The way we always measured inflation -- and my dad was a graceful, hardworking guy. He worked -- he worked a lot, his whole life. And my dad used to say -- he used to talk about whether inflation or -- the situation of a family to have breathing room is at the end of the month, when you`ve paid all your bills, did you have a little bit left? Just a little bit left?\par \par \uc2 So there`s more than one way, more than one way to deal with the inflation to a family to lower the price of the things they have to deal with, because that`s what folks at home think about. How much of their monthly bills -- how much do they have to pay for their necessities?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:09]\par \par \uc2 This law is going to give them just a little more breathing room, as my dad would say. We pay more for our prescription drugs than any nation in the world. Well, for years, so many of us for a long time, and many of the unions in here, every one of the unions here have been trying to fix this problem.\par \par \uc2 For years, Big Pharma has blocked us in Medicare from negotiating drug prices. But not this year. We beat Big Pharma. We finally beat them. First time Big Pharma lost. And, folks, now Medicare will have the power to do what so many Democrats and many Republicans in the past talked about doing. Lowering prescription drug prices. They pay billions of dollars. They can negotiate lower prices like other countries do.\par \par \uc2 And the seniors -- we passed a law saying, beginning in January, no senior, no matter what the cost of prescription drugs, cancer drugs, whatever drugs they have and some of them can be $60, $70, $80,000 a year. No senior will have to pay, no matter what the cost of their drugs, more than $2,000 a year. Beginning in January.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re -- if you`re a Med- -- if you`re on Medicare and you have diabetes, the cost of your insulin instead of being 30 to 40 to 50 times more than the cost to make the insulin, it costs 10 bucks to make that insulin. 10 and package it. But now you cannot charge any senior -- and we should make it for everybody. They voted down the other piece. But every senior will not have to pay more than $35.00 a month for the cost of their insulin.\par \par \uc2 We wanted to cut the cost of insulin for everyone, for everyone, including the hundreds of thousands of children who have Type 1 diabetes.\par \par \uc2 But look, folks, imagine being the parent of a child who had Type 1 diabetes. You had no insurance and you had no coverage. You have to look at that child and know -- and know that if he or she does not have access to that insulin, it will fundamentally affect their lives in ways that are going to be damaging for the rest of their lives. Imagine the feeling.\par \par \uc2 My dad used to say, Joey, a job is about a lot more than a paycheck. It`s about your dignity. It`s about respect. And he`d always end by saying, it`s about being able to look your kid in the eye and say, honey, it`s going to be OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, guess what? The Republicans blocked limiting that cost to 35 bucks for kids. As I said, it only costs 10 bucks for that prescription that he made and packaged. Drug companies are charging families more than 30 times as much for that drug on a monthly basis. And a lot of people just can`t do it.\par \par \uc2 Imagine, as I said, being that mom and dad needing that insulin. It has to be -- I was down in Virginia at a function. I met a mom who said they have to ration the insulin, be able -- I mean, it`s wrong. It`s not who we are. We`re going to fix that as well, I promise you.\par \par \uc2 Folks, the bottom line. This law is going to lower energy costs as well. Gas prices -- I know I got criticized for going into the stockpile. But guess what? Gas prices are down $1.30 a gallon. And in 41 states plus the District of Columbia, the average gasoline price is less than $2.99.\par \par \uc2 Look, the law makes the biggest investment ever. We invested in climate crisis. $369 billion to deal with the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 And it makes the tax code fairer. In 2020, 55 of the biggest corporations in the country paid zero dollars in federal income taxes.\par \par \uc2 And they made $40 billion. It`s not right. So we put a stop to it by enacting a corporate minimum tax of only -- but 15 percent. Today, the days of big, profitable corporations paying nothing in federal income tax is over.\par \par \uc2 And guess what? Every single Republican voted against the Inflation Reduction Act. When it actually comes to actually doing something about inflation, Republicans said no. And they said, time and again, that the first chance they get, they`re going to repeal the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 And here`s what else Republicans are in terms of their fiscal recklessness.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans had no problem -- had no problem enacting in his four years $2 trillion tax cut that overwhelmingly benefited the top 1 percent. And they didn`t pay for penny of it. And it massively increased the federal deficit.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, you know, we always say that Democrats are the biggest defenders? We brought down the deficit.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: $350 billion the first year and nearly $1.5 trillion this year.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And the Inflation Reduction Act is going to reduce it another $300 billion because over the next 10 years, because Medicare can reduce -- can pay less for the drugs they have to buy for the seniors. So I don`t want to hear it from Republicans. They`re the ones who blew up the deficit. They are the ones -- and we`re the ones bringing it down. And now thanks to the historic deficit reduction now we can afford to cancel $10,000 in student debt and $20,000 if you`re on a Pell Grant.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: For tens of millions of Americans making under $125,000, it`s a game changer. And Republicans are already looking for their hand-picked judges to deny relief for those hard working Americans. They will not succeed in my view, but that`s what they are looking for. And finally, with a straight face, Kevin McCarthy says that MAGA Republicans are going to restore faith in our elections.\par \par \uc2 As we say in my faith, bless me, Father, for I have sinned.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Restore faith in our elections? MAGA Republicans refused to accept the results of the 2020 election. And the will of the people. 81 million people voted Democrat for president last time.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Even though they lost court case after court case after court case after court case after court case. Even in front of Trump-appointed judges. And recount after recount proved the results were accurate. It`s become a litmus test in their party to pledge loyalty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by buying into the big lie.\par \par \uc2 Now I`m serious, you all see it on television. Look, the fact is that a lot of them, I have had six Republican senators who I have known for some time come to me and I gave them my word I`d never mention names and I never will, separately telling me that they agree with this. The other thing that I was proposing, but if I voted for it, it would cost them their election because they`d lose a primary.\par \par \uc2 But your see what`s happening. You see the MAGA Republican program being adapted by all these folks in the primary and then once they get out of the primary, they`re trying like hell to figure out how to get out from under that.\par \par \uc2 No, no, I`m serious. You can`t let the integrity of our elections be undermined. Democracy can`t survive. It cannot survive. Not a joke. Can`t survive when one side believes there`s only two outcomes to an election. Either they win or they were cheated. That`s not democracy. And that`s who the vast majority of MAGA Republicans are today. They don`t understand what every patriotic American knows. You can`t love your country only when you win. Only when you win.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So, folks, just 46 days, democracy will be on the ballot. Americans will have to choose between the MAGA Republican platform who have embraced extremism and the big lie, Democrats, independents, mainstream Republicans will believe in the rule of law. Rejecting the will of the people and accepting the results of a fair and free election is what we`re about, not what they`re about.\par \par \uc2 Let me close with this. As I said earlier, my dad used to say don`t compare me to the Almighty, compare me to the alternative. This November you have to choose. To be a nation of hope, unity and optimism, or a nation of fear, division and darkness.\par \par \uc2 I believe America will move forward to the future. The future with possibilities. The future in which we can build, dream and hope. I know we can because I know this nation like you do. I know the American people. I know their courage. I know their hearts. I know our history. That`s why, and I mean this from the bottom of my heart, and those of you who know me, no one ever doubts I mean when I say the problem is I sometimes say all that I mean.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But all kidding aside, I have never been more optimistic. We`re the only country in the world that`s come out of every crisis stronger than we went into it.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 It`s not just about building back to what it was before. We have a chance to build back in a way to make it better for everybody. Everybody.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And I want to get back to the time when I`m debating Republicans who are conservative as heck, but believe in the institutions. Believe in the institutions. Have a fair and square fight about what we`re doing. Think the court and the Congress and the presidency. Independent branches of government to be treated with respect.\par \par \uc2 That`s what I`m looking for. You just have to remember who in God`s name we are. We are the Unites States of America.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And there`s nothing, there`s nothing beyond our capacity, not a single thing we can`t do when we do it together. And the way to do that is start with the unions that do it together. Together, together.\par \par \uc2 And God bless you. May God protect our troops. And I should have started off by saying, excuse my back when I`m speaking. I apologize. Thank you all very much. God bless you all. Let`s keep this going. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: President Biden laying out his party`s case and delivering a pretty sharp rebuke to Republicans today as both parties are rolling out midterm messaging strategies. The new focus on messaging comes as a new poll just out today gives the president his highest approval rating of the year.\par \par \uc2 This is from Emerson College. 45 percent of likely voters give the president a positive grade. Up five points from July, seven points since May. And a lot of that improvement driven by women. 49 percent now approving of Biden`s performance, 10 points higher than July, which is probably why we heard the president just there talking about the Republicans launching what they call their commitment to America, but Biden pointing out that Kevin McCarthy doing that today did not mention the right to choose.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in NBC`s Mike Memoli, who of course covers \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for us, and NBC senior political reporter Natasha Korecki.\par \par \uc2 Good to see both of you. Mike, look, this speech was seen as setting up midterm messaging. There are plenty of specifics to talk about from abortion to Medicare to drug prices and guns. But he sounded like a candidate in a way I haven`t heard him. He even was wandering with the mic. He could have stood at the podium. There are a couple of microphones on it. What`s your take.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, you know, when I`ve been talking to White House advisers, other allies of the president about what`s in his political future in 2024, they acknowledge of course that his age is a major factor. A lot of questions among Democrats about whether he`s up for another campaign, another term. And as one adviser put it, there`s really only one way to answer those questions. And that`s for the president to show rather than tell.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we`ve seen the president on the stump of late really just like we`ve seen here but even more so today trying to show he still has that fire, wandering around the stage really engaging with the audience, with a union audience. The president loves speaking to union audiences for a reason. They`re enthusiastic and he feeds off that enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 And Chris, what we just saw I think was really fascinating political role reversal. We`re used to a major speech, a State of the Union address, a White House address, in which it`s the president speaking and the out-of- power party responding, Republicans. Today it`s the opposite. Kevin McCarthy, the potential future speaker of the House, laid out what he was calling the commitment to America if Republicans take back control of the Congress.\par \par \uc2 The White House really welcomed this opportunity. You heard the president say don`t judge me against the Almighty, just me against the alternative. And so the president really seizing the opportunity to respond to a leader in the Congress and make those sharp contrasts clear for voters with less than 50 days away. Talking about, for instance, an area where Democrats are on offense. Abortion, talking about the ability -- if he gets two more senators in the Senate to be able to codify the protections of Roe vs. Wade, to really signal for Democrats how to deal with the Republican attacks on crime, on law and order, by saying you can`t be pro-law enforcement and pro-insurrection. But then really sharpening I think the larger questions about the integrity of our democracy itself and calling out --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me stop you there if I can, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Because I think that`s where he got really fired up. So let me play a little clip from that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What happened on the 6th? Law enforcement was attacked, assaulted, brutalized and stomped on, and later several died. Some lost their lives because of that day. The MAGA Republicans didn`t side with law enforcement. They sided with the insurrectionists. And they still do. Don`t tell me you support law enforcement if you can`t condemn what happened on January 6th. Don`t tell me that.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:00]\par \par \uc2 You can`t be pro-law enforcement and pro-insurrection. You can`t claim to be a party of law and order and call the people who attacked the police on January 6th patriots.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So look, he ended by saying democracy is on the ballot, Mike, and I know that`s one of the messages. I think one of the conversations I`ve had with a lot of Democrats is, do you stick to one message? Do you talk about accomplishments, do you talk about the contrast? Are there too many messages out there? That`s often been, right, the criticism against Democrats. That they don`t have one focused message. What`s the strategy here?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, the White House has really defined their midterm messaging by looking at two different pillars. And you heard both in that speech from the president just now. The first is the economy bucket. They know that so many Americans are looking at the election choice through the lens of inflation, of higher costs, of what`s, you know, my bottom line. And that`s why the president made a full throated defense of his economic record, talking about how he inherited an economy in disarray from the former president and through the rescue plan and through the Inflation Reduction Act has taken steps to try to ease those burdens.\par \par \uc2 But the second category is a really important one. And it`s this idea that democracy and of our fundamental rights being at risk. And that`s where he leaned in hardest at the end there. Not just abortion which was an earlier part of the speech, but the idea that there are election denying Republicans on the ballot. He said you can`t only accept election result when you win. And that`s a really important message for the president, and one that has been, according to polls, a top concern, especially of Democratic voters.\par \par \uc2 So that`s what the president is trying to do is fire up their base. Midterm elections are often base elections. Fire up Democrats on the issues that matter most to them and he laid them all out pretty clearly here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And on the other side of this, Natasha, look, I think Mike pointed this out, very few people have as much at stake politically as Kevin McCarthy, who like laid out the Republican agenda, the Republican argument, messaging, whatever you want to call it, because he very much would like to be the next speaker of the House. Do Republicans you talk to feel like Democrats have their momentum? What do Democrats tell you about momentum and how they capitalize on it? Give us your sense of where it stands right now and how the two parties are viewing it.\par \par \uc2 NATASHA KORECKI, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: So Republicans particularly in battleground states where I talked to people a lot and I travel, they really are looking at the economy. And again as Mike mentioned, Biden talked about the economy. One thing he didn`t really mention was the grocery store, what are your grocery prices looking like now. And that`s what really on the phones, at the doors, that what we`re hearing from both parties that they`re hearing a lot about.\par \par \uc2 Like, my bills are still really high, even though gas prices are coming down. That`s one of the big concerns. And obviously Biden`s approval ratings are ticking up. The momentum is way different for Democrats than it was months ago. They are in a very different place. Biden is in much different place. But you again have to go to the battleground states and what`s his polling there and what are the biggest issues there.\par \par \uc2 Some places like Nevada and Wisconsin and Pennsylvania, you`re looking at economy. You`re looking at crime. We talked about crime. He had a very, very compelling argument. But what people are getting bombarded with in some of these states is local crime. What`s happening. Milwaukee, crime is up 40 percent. They are hammering Mandela Barnes, the Senate candidate there, on crime. So Biden coming back at that was very important for Democrats today and I think they`re probably very happy about that.\par \par \uc2 What I think -- I mean, there`s so many messages that have been, you know, Democrats are getting out the, you know, abortion, democracy, again and again and again. We hear however from Biden this is a choice. And what Democrats have been able to do is probably the best position that Democrats can be in right now is not make this a referendum on the presidency. You don`t want this a referendum on Biden and picking apart every decision he`s made, good or bad, and concentrating on gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Yes, they are down, but they are still high. Grocery prices not great. You want to make this a referendum on people like Kevin McCarthy and on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Natasha Korecki, Mike Memoli, such a great conversation and one we will continue to have in the days and weeks to come. Thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, there`s an increasingly desperate situation that`s unfolding for Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his war against Ukraine. Huge numbers of his citizens are protesting and even fleeing the country, fighting back against the war and a new draft. Take a look at some emotional video out of Russia. Men having to say good-bye to loved ones as they board buses and head off to training. Putin calling 300,000 civilians to the frontlines.\par \par \uc2 And not everybody is complying with this forced enlistment. In spite of Putin`s denials that it`s happening, there are traffic jams near the Georgian border and packed airports that suggest many would rather flee than fight.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 And more than 1300 people have been arrested over the past couple of days in the largest public protest since the war began. Meanwhile, voting begins today in what is being called a sham referendum in four Russian-occupied regions of Ukraine, calling for the annexation of 15 percent of Ukrainian territory.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Evelyn Farkas, former deputy assistant secretary of defense for Russia, Ukraine and Eurasia.\par \par \uc2 Lot to talk about, Evelyn. Look, in the seven months since the invasion, Putin has tried to I guess kind of shield the Russian people from the war. But this mobilization clearly is making that impossible. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " interviewed a 23-year-old Russian man. He fled to Istanbul. He told the paper, "I was sitting and thinking about what I could die for and I didn`t see any reason to die for the country."\par \par \uc2 And Aleksandr, an executive manager from Moscow, said he started packing even before Mr. Putin had finished his announcement on Wednesday. Minutes later he was on his way to the airport looking for available tickets and route. Tickets to his preferred destinations -- Istanbul, Almaty, Kazakhstan had already sold out so he settled on Namangan, Uzbekistan, a city he had never even heard of.\par \par \uc2 I`ve never heard of and I`m not sure, to be honest with you, Evelyn, I pronounced it right. But would you -- when you look at this in total, what does it mean for Putin?\par \par \uc2 EVELYN FARKAS, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, THE MCCAIN INSTITUTE: Yes, I mean, it`s really interesting, Chris, because you`re right. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has shielded the Russian population writ large from this war. He`s largely recruited Russians from the east offering them, you know, bonuses, and all kinds of money incentives, places where people were relatively poor. Places also where they`re not ethnic Russians, Dagestan, Chechnya, again in the east. And he`s also gone into penal colonies, so prisons basically, and recruited people from there as well.\par \par \uc2 The only kind of non, I guess, regular forces that he had in there were the conscripts, who were very poorly trained. But somewhere I read about a month or so ago that the total fatalities in the war from Moscow and St. Petersburg combined were 11. So that tells you a lot. Now he`s sending a signal to the Russian people that this is serious. He`s also sending a signal of course to the nationalists fighting in Ukraine and also the Russian, you know, nationalists inside the Russian body of politic that Russia is going to stick with you.\par \par \uc2 I think he`s also testing the Russia people. You know, will they put up with this? Yes, people are fleeing the country and that this is adding to an exodus that already started when they first launched this new invasion in February. And so he is looking to see, can he get away with this so- called partial mobilization? Because if he can, he may move to a full mobilization. And that`s clearly what people are afraid of. And he is looking to see whether he can control the population and the power centers in the military.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So we only have 30 seconds left, I`m afraid, because of President Biden`s speech, but do you see his actions this week as a sign of desperation?\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: I do, Chris. I mean, I definitely see him buying time first of all. Putin is really good at buying time. And he`s trying to placate the nationalists who were really up upset obviously because Russia is doing poorly militarily. And it doesn`t look like that`s going to change any time soon. By the way, this callup of course isn`t going to change that reality any time soon. So it`s mostly signaling. And I say that because to get 300,000 people called up, then equipped and then trained at least a little bit on those arms, will take time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Even a little bit.\par \par \uc2 Evelyn Farkas, it`s always great to see you. Thank you so much. Appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 FARKAS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Again, with just 46 days until the midterms, we`re now seeing Latinos in what historically Democratic stronghold maybe shifting to the right. What is driving that shift? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS, only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:21]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We keep telling it`s so close. 46 days until the midterms. And some key races will tell us a lot about the Latino vote, especially after that rightward shift we saw among Latino voters toward President Trump back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Here with me now is Paola Ramos, MSNBC contributor and host of "Field Report," which is dropping a new episode tonight, digging into exactly what`s driving this shift. This is politically important, if you like politics, it`s really fascinating. You did a deep dive on one race. Tell us about it and what it tells us.\par \par \uc2 PAOLA RAMOS, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: So we want to the 34th Congressional District in the Rio Grande Valley and we went there to understand the rise of Mayra Flores. So Mayra Flores this summer because the first Republican to win Hidalgo County in over 100 years. And we wanted to know what was at the heart of that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The first Republican of any kind.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: In 100 years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s wow.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: And she`s a Latina Republican, she`s the daughter of immigrants, she was born in Mexico, in Tamaulipas. And why was the question. The first thing is that her message resonated. Her message was simple, it was God, family and country. And I think she tapped into this socially conservative vein that a lot of Latinos, we have in them, perhaps they were closeted Republicans, but through that message, they came out.\par \par \uc2 The other thing was this political strategy that Republicans understood. They went into Latino evangelical churches and now they`re mobilizing those congregations, and I want to show you a clip from a Republican organizer who told me what`s behind this strategy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABRAHAM ENRIQUEZ, BIENVENIDO U.S. FOUNDER AND PRESIDENT: Eight out of 10 times, if a Latino is being outreached through the church, more than likely they believe in some of the conservative platforms, so it is much easier for them to get out and vote Republican. So Republicans don`t start strategizing the Latino pastors in churches like the Democrats have done for years with the black church, there will nobody victory for conservative candidates across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: But that`s interesting, right? What they`re saying essentially is why don`t we replicate the motto that Democrats are using in black churches, and the same way that many Democrats are mobilizing their base, why don`t we do the same thing. Now you can argue, are Republican Latino evangelicals using those space as hubs to discuss social justice, right, and racial equity or have they become these, you know, hubs for political indoctrination? That`s a question.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right. It`s a question, but, you know, when you talk about what might work or what might not work, I go back 10 years to Reince Priebus when he did an autopsy on the Republican Party and said we have got to be more inclusive and talk a lot about the Latino community. Are they finally doing that, and when I say doing that, that always means to me in real political terms, are national Republicans putting money into some of these races?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Yes. Why? Because Democrats are allowing them to.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 Take the case study of Mayra Flores in the 34th Congressional District, just the numbers there tell you the story. Republicans gave her over a million dollars to spend on TV ads, compared to her opponent, where Democrats gave him a little bit over $130,000.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Had money like that ever been spent there, I wonder. A million?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: So that`s the thing, right?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s significant.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Republicans are seeing a window there, and that is because of this void that has been created. Now Democrats -- win that district in the midterms. However, no matter how you see it, they created a Republican incumbent. They are taking for granted the Latino vote and I think that is the question of the midterm for me at least.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re almost out of time but is she alone? Are there other Latinas who are trying to do the same thing?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: There`s at least two more just like her. There one running in McCallum, Monica dela Cruz, another one in Laredo, Cassy Garcia, very similar stories. Conservatives. Daughters of immigrants. Grew up in working class neighborhoods. They`re trying to change the face of the party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That`s really fascinating. Paola, thank you for sharing it us with.\par \par \uc2 And you can see a lot more, tune in to "Field Report" with Paola Ramos tonight, 10:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC. It`s also streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS, every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. So I`ll see you on Monday. In the meantime, Yasmin Vossoughian is in the chair for "KATY TUR REPORTS" next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC ANCHOR: Hey, everybody. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220924ei9n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for September 22, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayman Mohyeldin, Paola Ramos, Michael Beschloss, Paul Butler, Jill  Wine-Banks, Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 7,395 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:50]\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST: Alex, it`s great to see you. I was going to say really quickly, I don`t think Republicans have any more red lines when they`re talking about how they field candidates. So, anything goes these days with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "AWT": They vanished a while ago. Nonetheless, J.R. Majewski doesn`t have access to their coffers anymore and Democrats in Ohio I am sure is celebrating.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yeah, we`ll see how that turns out.\par \par \uc2 Alex, it`s good to see, friend. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we`re going to talk about democracy tonight because the arc of history is long, but it bends towards justice, and it`s good to remember that quote tonight because there have been many days in recent memory that it has not felt like we are bending towards justice. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wins an election, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans just can`t claim he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 Trump incites an insurrection at the Capitol. Five weeks later, he`s acquitted by Republican senators.\par \par \uc2 In May, it is reported that the wife of the longest-serving Supreme Court justice is encouraging Republicans to overthrow the election. And nothing happens.\par \par \uc2 Next month, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  votes to take away constitutional right, the right-wing project of the last decades, it`s achieved, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  walks off from what he brought to one of his golf courses. It doesn`t feel so just. It`s frustrating.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, the Justice Department has the classified documents seized from Donald Trump`s phone, and the criminal investigation is ongoing after a panel of three federal judges for appeals court including to Trump appointed judges are having a full spectrum of the Trump`s lawyers case.\par \par \uc2 Today, special master Raymond Dearie is asking Trump`s lawyers to state on the record what they contend the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted in Trump`s home?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: The problem that you have is they go into rooms, they wouldn`t let anybody near it, they wouldn`t even allow them in the same building. Did they drop anything into those piles? Or did they do it later? There`s no chain of custody here with them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So Trump is actually learning that American courtrooms, they are not like Fox programs. He can`t just flood the zone with lies and in the window and nonsense and expect to get away with it.\par \par \uc2 In the order, Judge Dearie gives Trump`s lawyers until next Friday to provide evidence for those false claims made that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 In the January 6th committee whose hearings have been watched by tens of millions of people have secured an interview with Justice Thomas`s wife Ginni about her reported involvement in efforts to overturn our election. The committee has sought an interview with her since June. Specifically about her interactions with Trump allies \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and John Eastman.\par \par \uc2 And there will be another January 6 hearing next week ahead of the committee`s full report, and possibly a criminal referral for the former president. The guy who thought it was cool to dress like Hitler and verbally assault Capitol Hill police officers, well, he`s now headed had for four years in a federal prison also by a Trump appointed judge.\par \par \uc2 I don`t mention Trump judges here to say that makes them somehow more virtuous. No, it shows the system is actually working, the institutions of this country are working. They took oaths to uphold the law, to fulfill the constitutional vision of America, and they`re doing that even when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has and still is at every turn indicating that he thinks laws and truth, an honor, are for suckers.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  praises \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  for his strength and mirage created by state-run media, Potemkin elections and security thugs. But tonight, in Russia, Russians are saying, we don`t want to die in your war on Ukraine. Now, it is easy to scare people into submission, until they think you will kill them or kill their children anyway. And then they say, enough. What do I have to lose?\par \par \uc2 In Iran, a woman died after being arrested by the morality police for what she was wearing, and tonight, Iranians are saying enough. As we look at these pictures from Russia, and Iran, as we think about the ideals that Ukrainians are fighting for, being tortured and killed for, we know how much we have in our democracy, however imperfect, and how much it`s worth fighting to protect every day.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:19]\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Michael Beschloss, NBC News presidential historian, Jill Wine-Banks who served as assistant Watergate prosecutor, and Paul Butler, law professor at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  and a former federal prosecutor. They are both MSNBC legal analysts.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have all three of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Michael, I think you`re the perfect person to start on with this because as I was, you`re the historian here. You give us a sense of how this arc of history is bending. Can you tell us in your assessment of how we are doing on that arc of history tonight?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, NBC NEWS PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: Absolutely, I would be happy to. I hope you don`t mind my starting, Ayman, by noting my friends Jill and Paul, the three of us are all from Chicago. So, tonight, we`re hearing the voices of mid America, I think my friends will agree.\par \par \uc2 And having that said, democracy I think is more in danger than it has been since 1860, at the beginning of the civil war, late 1930s when this country was in danger of fascist leaders and politicians, and fascism abroad. Hitler, and Mussolini, in Japanese. But at the same time, I totally agree with what you said at the beginning, Ayman.\par \par \uc2 You know, let`s take a victory lap tonight. This is an evening that we can take one breath before I go to the flight of democracy and why it`s necessary. But we have to have faith that there`s a majority that loves democracy and loves the rule of law, and loves real free, and fair elections, will not be denied that by an angry minority ever.\par \par \uc2 And we are seeing that in a certain way in Russia, we are seeing that in Iran, as you mentioned, and we are also seeing it in the United States. Sometimes, it takes our system awhile to rally, but look at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , for instance. You mention. Not a great 24 hours for him, which I would like to celebrate personally tonight. This is someone who not only has this lawsuit in New York by the New York attorney general, listen to the offense as she listed those are astounding, I never heard of any president in the United States being accused as of something as bad as that.\par \par \uc2 And that is just won the election. He will be liable for legal actions most probably in connection with 9/11 and classified documents case, and in Georgia, a state that you know well, Ayman, and a couple of other cases.\par \par \uc2 And all I`m saying is, those who do not want Trump to run in 2024, which, I think would safely say includes the four of us here, would say that that is not going to happen because his base is going to be disgusted by the stuff. They are not discussed by anything. But, if you have a man in not great health like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who now has to face perhaps a four or five front legal war, I don`t see how someone can do that and run for president.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Paul, to Michael`s point, the success of this democracy depends on independent judges. And the judges in Trump`s case, other than Judge Cannon, who uphold the laws in the institution, seem to have no time for Donald Trump`s games. I mean, what do you make of the way these judges have been ruling and speaking out about the actions of the former president?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yeah, so, first, Ayman, shout out to my home boy, Michael, and my home girl, Jill. Chi-town in the house.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Same here, thank you. Go Cubs and Sox.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has had a terrible, no, good bad week and the rule of law is having a really good week. The same special master that Trump`s elected to review the Mar-a-Lago documents ruled against him, and to judges the Trump appointed took down his arguments expeditiously and forcefully. So, these judges are doing what judges are supposed to do.\par \par \uc2 I kind of think of that Chris Rock joke about when men get credit for taking care of their children, Chris Rock says, you`re supposed to take care of your children. And, Ayman, if you`re a federal judge, you`re supposed to uphold the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, most judges have actually been doing that in the Supreme Court decision, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was the only justice to vote that Trump shouldn`t have to turn over certain documents to investigators. Even justices that Trump appointed disagreed with that.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 And the many cases that, throughout the insurrection, and the fake elector scheme, judges who Trump appointed have consistently ruled against him.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Jill, you`ve had a front row seat at the Nixon administration. And often Trump`s compared to Nixon. But I think he`s far more egregious in terms of what he`s done to the country, to democracy, and the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 What was the mixing tipping point, and have we reached up with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? I think a lot of people are watching this with a sense of frustration that over the last several years, all of the legal machinations that have come against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for what people rightfully believed to have been violations of the law have not materialized in a meaningful, punitive way.\par \par \uc2 JILL WINE-BANKS, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: I think people are upset about that, and want to see some accountability and won`t settle for anything less than that. The differences, now we live in a world with alternative facts, and alternative reality. During Watergate, all of the networks at the state facts. Everyone agreed on it. It was bipartisan shift.\par \par \uc2 Those things are very much missing now. So you do have a group of people who were continue to believe the falsehoods that are being spewed by the Republican Party. Not just by Trump, but by all of his colleagues. And that is a danger to democracy that I don`t think we ever faced during the Nixon administration. That`s what I am worried about.\par \par \uc2 And I`m worried about also what I`ve seen most judges have upheld the rule of law, and by not historians, I`ve lived through historic times. I remember when judges were always viewed as independent, neutral arbiters of facts and law. And now we have a Supreme Court where no one, I should say no one, but many people do not believe that they are neutral arbiters. That they have determined the outcomes they want, and are issuing decisions that go along with that.\par \par \uc2 And there is one decision that is coming up in the next term that I want to point out because the independent state legislature theory, and that is very scary to me. That is the end of what we know as democracy if that case is decided in a certain way. It means that states can do anything they want, and there`s no review of it. And that they can say, well we want this candidate for president. I don`t care what the popular vote was.\par \par \uc2 People should be really paying attention to the Supreme Court, and to that particular case, I am very concerned about it.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: I think it was the cornerstone of the John Eastman argument which is, that the state legislators decide who wins the elections and forget the people in the votes.\par \par \uc2 Michael, let me pick up on something that Jill talked about which was this sense of bipartisanship because the facts were so apparent in the Nixon era that Republican said to Nixon, it`s time for you to go.\par \par \uc2 Is there a political tipping point in modern America? When will Republicans decide to stop paying Trump`s legal bills and move the party away from him or is that not likely to happen in this current climate?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: It hasn`t happened yet. And it just breaks my heart to think that a lot of people, many with very good intentions, gave money to Donald Trump`s funds with the idea that this would go to his political works.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, from recent reporting, it`s been used according to one report to pay his wife`s clothing designer $18,000 a month. That`s money from hardworking people, and hollowed out industrial towns, for instance, in the Midwest. The three of us know about extremely well. So there`s a tolerance if there never was for Nixon.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, I`m saying the obvious, there`s a median viral meant now that if you are someone who can think that Donald Trump`s wonderful and has done nothing wrong, and that he really won the 2020 election, and all sorts of other things that are not true, you can spend the whole day watching cable channels that will tell you that, and looking at websites that do the same.\par \par \uc2 And I would say that if \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  had committed the same offenses that Jill and her colleagues went after him justifiably so in `73 and `74, I think Republicans would not be against him in Congress, and he would`ve beaten the wrap and served eight years and that is a horrifying fault.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yeah, it certainly is, and certainly, you have the propaganda network like Fox operating on behalf of the president, the platform they gave him last night.\par \par \uc2 Paul, I want to ask you really quickly about something that the New York Attorney General Letitia James said yesterday about her lawsuits against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his children and business.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:01]\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES (D), NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: We believe the conduct alleged in this action also violates federal criminal law, including issuing false statements to financial institutions and bank fraud.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: What do you make of that, Paul? What do you make of Letitia James` move to essentially refer Trump to the SDNY and the \uc2 IRS\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: I think it`s important, although I think they would`ve had access to a lot of the same information that the New York attorney general has. She has an easier case to make. It`s civil litigation. So there`s a lower standard of proof.\par \par \uc2 And there`s another thing that federal prosecutors have to decide is that these many criminal investigations that are swirling around the former president, the insurrection, the fake elector scheme, the classified documents at Mar-a-Lago, where does this new information come in?\par \par \uc2 Since it mainly involves Trump`s conduct before he was president, I think that it`s likely in terms of a criminal prosecution, to take up backburner to his attempt to overturn our democracy. And we still don`t know what in the world he was doing with those classified documents. Of course, he hasn`t offered any proof that he what he was doing was illegal.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yeah, let me ask you about that, really quickly, general, because not that the appeals court has overturned Judge Cannon`s ruling in favor of Trump and I guess some would argue that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  confessed to having these classified documents in that bizarre interview he gave Fox News yesterday and effectively undermining his argument that they are classified -- what are the next steps in the special master`s examination of the classified documents?\par \par \uc2 WINE-BANKS: I think it`s remarkable that both the 11th circuit which acted with great speed, and the special master, are taking no baloney from the Trump team. They have made it very clear the facts are facts. And I think what is going to happen next is there is going to be a quick turnaround on the special master, and that no one is going to accept that they can`t say whether he declassified anything.\par \par \uc2 But even if he did, the court has made it clear, it doesn`t matter. Because the crimes he`s accused of don`t require that they be classified. They have national security information in them. That alone makes it enough. So he can`t have his cake and needed to, as the court basically said. He has to come forth with evidence, or it will be soon that they are classified information, and he has no right to them.\par \par \uc2 So, the Department of Justice is not going forward with both a criminal case while the intelligence communities going forward with a risk assessment, of what harm might have come from these. I just keep hoping that even the Republicans who support him will start to see the dangers he poses to this country. Not just our democracy, but our security. And that they too will get to the point that Republicans got to with Nixon, as you pointed out, it was Republicans who went to him and said, we see the special prosecutor`s evidence, you are guilty, and you will be convicted in an impeachment trial if you don`t resign. And he resigned.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our Chi-Town all-star panel, thank you for starting us off tonight, Michael Beschloss, Jill Wine-Banks, Paul Butler, thanks to the three of you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, 90 House Republicans are sponsoring a federal law that will ban all abortions in every state, and, yes that means deep blue states as well. Our next guest is running against one of those Republicans. Cheri Beasley, North Carolina`s Democratic candidate for Senate, joins us live, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:28]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: A version of the federal law that would ban abortion at 15 weeks, the one the one that was introduced to send it by \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , now has 90 cosponsors in the U.S. House, all of them Republicans. And this law flies in the face of the GOP`s mantra for states rights in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 So much so that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was against his own bill just 37 days before he introduced it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I`ve been consistent. I think states to decide issues of marriage, and states should decide issue of abortion. I have respect for South Carolina. South Carolina voters here I trust to define marriage and to deal with the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 I think we should have a law at the federal level that will say, after 15 weeks, no abortion on demand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Now, among the 90 House Republican sponsoring the national abortion is Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , who is now running for North Carolina`s open Senate seat. His Democratic opponent is Cheri Beasley who joins us live. She`s the former chief justice of the North Carolina Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for joining us. So, your opponent is generally against abortion access, he even called abortions in cases of rape or incest, and these are his words, not, mine a second tragedy turn already tough situation. How central has the issue of abortion become to this specific race?\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D), NORTH CAROLINA U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, Ayman, it is central. I mean, Congressman Budd has aligned himself to the most extreme faction of his party, and is completely out of step with where people are in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:08]\par \par \uc2 And he doesn`t believe in exceptions for rape or incest or risk to a mother`s health. And what that means is women who have ectopic pregnancies, who have septate uteruses, who can pass the miscarriages, won`t be able to get the treatment they need. And it also means that women will die.\par \par \uc2 This is completely unacceptable. I`ve been a judge for over two decades. I`ve served as chief justice of the Supreme Court of North Carolina. And what I know is women have had the reproductive constitutional right to make these decisions without government interference and with their doctors, but not with \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  interfering in this very personal decision.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Ms. Beasley, other Republicans are having a tough time navigating this issue. In fact, earlier this week, I talked to former Democratic Congressman Max Rose who is running for Congress again. I will play for you what he told me about this. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAX ROSE (D), NEW YORK U.S. HOUSE CANDIDATE: If you look at my election on Staten Island, my opponent, Congresswoman \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2 , has been unwilling to state her position on the \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  nationwide abortion ban. At first she claimed that she wasn`t aware of the bill. She said I want to have it delivered to my office.\par \par \uc2 So a constituent delivered to her office. And she still has not expressed her position on it. And unfortunately, she and so many other members of Congress on the Republican Party are unwilling to do the courageous thing, and side with the majority.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Why is the Republican Party, you, thanks so rigid on this?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: I don`t have an answer for that, but what I do know is this position with \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is completely out of step with the majority of North Carolinians are and the majority of North Carolinians -- of Americans. And what we know is honestly this position is really dangerous. And we know the women will die.\par \par \uc2 If elected to the Senate, I will fight hard to make Roe versus. Wade the law. It is exactly what we`ve been for the last 50 years and that`s exactly where we need to be.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: NBC News, Ms. Beasley, is reporting that an abortion ban like the one that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has proposed would likely force many patients to undergo invasive trans vaginal ultrasounds before terminating pregnancies, according to doctors, doctors who would be on the hook to as patients about the timing of their pregnancies and perform or cause to perform such medical examinations and tests as needed to make an accurate determination of gestational age.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to that and what this means to women`s health if this law is passed?\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY: I think this is a violation -- I know it`s a violation of reproductive freedom. And I also know that there`s absolutely no way for any politician to include \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  knows the specifics of any woman`s particular situation. That`s why it`s important that only the woman and a doctor, and not Congressman \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , be in the examination room as these very important and complicated decisions are made.\par \par \uc2 And I would ask your viewers, for more information on my race to please go to cheribeasley.com for more information.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right. Cheri Beasley, thank you so much for time for joining us tonight. I greatly appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right. As we mentioned earlier, Russian citizens are turning on Putin after he announced plans to mobilize 300,000 Russians for his faltering war. We`re going to tell you about that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:33:15]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: The very international order that we have gathered here to uphold is being shredded before our eyes. We cannot -- we will not -- allow President Putin to get away with it. Wherever the Russian tide recedes, we discover the horror that is left in its wake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST: All right. So that was Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  today at a meeting of the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2 . Russian foreign minister \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  was reportedly 90 minutes late for that very meeting. He was not seated with the Russian delegation as the secretary spoke. And during the meeting, Russia faced universal condemnation from the U.S. and its allies.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg News reports that Lavrov gave his speech accusing the West of forcing Russia to invade to protect itself and then he walked out.\par \par \uc2 British foreign minister James Cleverly spoke immediately after Lavrov. Here is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CLEVERLY, BRITISH FOREIGN MINISTER: And today, I have listened to further installments of Russia`s catalogs of distortions, dishonesty, and disinformation.\par \par \uc2 He has left the chamber. I am not surprised. I don`t think Mr. Lavrov wants to hear the collective condemnation of this Council.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Meanwhile in Moscow, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  facing yet another setback in his war. The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that since Putin`s announcement on Wednesday of a new troop call up, some Russian men who had once thought they were safe from the front lines, have actually fled the country.\par \par \uc2 They have done so in a rush, lining up at the borders and paying rising prices to try and catch fights to countries that allow them to enter without visas.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:56]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Joining us now Simon Shuster, senior correspondent for "Time Magazine". He recently visited Kyiv and is writing a book about the war in Ukraine and President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Simon, it`s great to see you again, thank you so much for joining us. So, you know --\par \par \uc2 SIMON SHUSTER, SENIOR CORRESPONDENT, "TIME MAGAZINE": Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: -- after months of fighting, not the days that he promised initially, those quick days that he thought it was going to happen -- Putin is actually facing protests, officials openly calling out his war. Men are fleeing Russia to avoid being drafted.\par \par \uc2 How are Ukrainian officials reacting to all these developments?\par \par \uc2 SHUSTER: I mean broadly, I have been reporting on Putin and his career for about 15 years now. I don`t think I have ever seen him as weak and as weakened as I have this week. And just in the last few days was the new draft. And I think it does show in the reaction of Ukrainian officials. I mean, you remember, you just alluded to this, that Ukraine was supposed to be the country that Russia could conquer and dominate in a matter of days. That was the prediction of the American intelligence services. It was certainly what the Russian intelligence services were telling President Putin.\par \par \uc2 And now, what he is hearing from Ukrainian officials is basically, you know, come at me. Their reaction to this announcement from Putin that he is going to draft 300,000 Russian conscripts into the military in order to continue waging this war -- the Ukrainians have shrugged it off.\par \par \uc2 I have been amazed, in my conversations with aides to President Zelenskyy, just how calm they are about it, including in the military. They say this is not going to change, fundamentally, the situation on the ground. The best it will allow Putin to do is try to cling on to the territories that he has occupied in the last months and then slow down the kinds of counteroffensives that we have seen Ukrainians pursue in the last weeks.\par \par \uc2 M2: Let me pick up on what you said about the weakness of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , based on what you have been reporting, and as you mentioned you have reported on him for 15 years.\par \par \uc2 You know, you can clearly see at least at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., Russia is isolated. Putin has cemented his status as a persona non grata on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 And now you -- as you are saying he is weakened but he has also made these veiled nuclear threats, which are rightfully being condemned by \uc2 NATO\uc2  and everyone else in the world. But what are your sources telling you about his possible next moves? And based on your reporting, given that he is so weakened, what might that mean?\par \par \uc2 SHUSTER: My reporting most recently has been on the Ukrainian side. My most recent trip was to Kyiv and I stay in close touch with President Zelenskyy and his team. And their reaction to the nuclear threats is also fairly mild.\par \par \uc2 First of all, they don`t believe that he will go there. But their response is, if he does, it will also not change the dynamic. Because Ukraine will continue to fight, as one very senior military official told me, to the last drop of blood if necessary.\par \par \uc2 And even the use of a tactical nuclear weapon in Ukraine, if Putin goes there, will not stop Ukraine from continuing to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 What it will do is, most likely, cause an even more serious reaction from the west. I mean it`s hard to game out exactly the steps that the U.S. and \uc2 NATO\uc2  will take. It depends on how much of that kind of attack affects \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory, for example, Poland.\par \par \uc2 These are the realistic kinds of scenarios that we have to think about when Putin is making these threats.\par \par \uc2 But in Kyiv, their policy and their stance is basically, keep calm and carry on. They are continuing to pursue counteroffensives. One, the defense minister, just the other day told me we are going to continue reaching them in Crimea, we are not going to stop, we are not going to lay off. And they are not taking Putin`s threats as a sign that they need to curtail their military actions.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Simon Shuster, it`s always a pleasure. Thank so much for your reporting. Thank you so much for joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, "Jin, Jiyan, Azadi" or "Women, Life, Freedom" -- that chant can be heard across Iran tonight as thousands protest the death of a 22-year- old Iranian Kurdish woman in the custody of Iran`s morality police. Iranian American journalist Negar Mortazavi joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:25]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Jin, Jiyan, Azadi.\par \par \uc2 Jin, Jiyan, Azadi.\par \par \uc2 Jin, Jiyan, Azadi.\par \par \uc2 Jin, Jiyan, Azadi\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So the chants that you just heard there -- Jin, Jiyan, Azadi -- or women, life, freedom is a phrase born out of the Kurdish Liberation Movement. It has now become a rallying cry across Iran after the death of Mahsa Amini, a 22-year-old Iranian Kurdish woman who died after being detained by Iran`s so-called morality police.\par \par \uc2 She was said to be in violation of the country`s strict dress code by wearing her hijab or head scarf improperly. The news of Mahsa Amini`s death last week immediately ignited protests across Iran and women have been at the forefront of this movement.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:52]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: In fact, many women in Iran are protesting by also cutting off their hair and burning their compulsory head scarfs in opposition to Iran`s law that has been put in place for over four decades.\par \par \uc2 Amnesty International\uc2  is reporting that they have collected evidence of Iranian security forces` unlawful use of bird shot and other metal pellets, tear gas, water cannon, and beatings with batons to disperse protesters. At least nine people have been killed since these demonstrations spread.\par \par \uc2 As footage of Iranian women burning their hijabs go viral, Iran is experiencing a near total Internet blackout, \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and WhatsApp, which protesters have relied heavily on to get their word out to the world, have been blocked by the Iranian regime.\par \par \uc2 With the violent crackdown against demonstrators ongoing, Iranian President \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  addressed the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 . But he never addressed the protests inside his country. Instead, he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EBRAHIM RAISI, IRANIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): The Islamic revolution of Iran was the realization of the movement of the people of Iran towards justice and fairness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: How ironic, the death of Mahsa Amini was a direct result of laws that were put into place after the Islamic revolution Iran. Where is the justice for Mahsa`s family.\par \par \uc2 The courage of the women leading these protests cannot be overstated. See, we find ourselves at an inflection point in human history. In India, women are fighting against a state`s ban on women wearing hijabs. In Iran, women are protesting the forced wearing of that very same head scarf. And here in the U.S., Well, we know what Republicans are doing to women.\par \par \uc2 See from Iran, to India, to right here in America, women globally are fighting for their rights in the midst of this rising oppression. They are fighting for control of their bodies from governments, ideologues and religious extremists.\par \par \uc2 And while that struggle takes on many different forms, we must recognize the fight for women`s rights is universal. And we must stand up for these rights here at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Negar Mortazavi, an Iranian American journalist and political analyst. She`s host of "The Iran Podcast". Negar, it`s great to have you. Thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Mahsa Amini`s father told \uc2 BBC News\uc2  that Iran is lying when they say Mahsa Amini`s health problems are contributed to her death, saying in part, quote, "She never had any medical conditions, she never had surgery."\par \par \uc2 What more can you tell us about the circumstances that we know or the family`s alleging about Mahsa Amini`s death?\par \par \uc2 NEGAR MORTAZAVI, IRANIAN AMERICAN JOURNALIST: Ayman, we know that Mahsa Amini was stopped when visiting Tehran when she got off the metro, arrested or detained by the morality police and then taken to a station. There is a video -- edited video was published later by the police forces, essentially alleging that they did not do -- commit any violence.\par \par \uc2 But what angers people is that they have time and again seen the violence of the morality police in past instances, video after video, images of women being violently and brutally arrested, thrown into police vans and taken to detention. So, even though we may not know the exact circumstances of what led to that exact moment of her death, the fact is that she died in the custody of a force that has been violent to women many times before.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s anger of women that has been building up, because they see themselves in Mahsa Amini. Men see their own sisters in Mahsa Amini.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people are coming out saying, this could be us, this could be any of us, and this could have happened to any of us. I think that is the kind of outpouring and the f anger that we are seeing in the sympathy and the solidarity by the protesters.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Let me ask you about where this goes from here. Because during a news conference today here in New York, President \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  warned that acts of chaos are unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 And we all remember what happened three years ago. 300 people were killed in Iran`s last major protest movement. How are Iranians responding to that rhetoric? Where do you think this movement goes from here?\par \par \uc2 MORTAZAVI: Well, Iranian women, what`s incredible is that you see a lot of women leading these protests, very symbolic images of essentially women coming out to ask for their basic rights and dignity and to be treated like equal citizens.\par \par \uc2 It`s living images of bravery and courage of these young women. And it is incredible because at the same time as you said, we have seen in past years that the state is willing to use violence to crack down on protests. In 2019 they essentially brought down an iron fist to crack down and as they call it wrap up the protests.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:51]\par \par \uc2 MORTAZAVI: So with Internet disruption, with the violent images that we`re seeing, I am afraid we`re going down that path. So I wouldn`t be surprised to see even more violence on the streets as they are trying to minimize the protests across Iran.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Absolutely. A scary thought, given what we have seen in the past, and what this regime has shown it is capable of.\par \par \uc2 All right. Negar Mortazavi, thank you so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 MORTAZAVI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Coming up, what is motivating the Latino votes here at home? Paola Ramos went to Texas to investigate. She joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In your special election campaign, do you think you were also able to garner the support of Democrats?\par \par \uc2 REP. MAYRA FLORES (R-TX): UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would not have one if I would not have had support from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right. So that`s Mayra Flores, now I should say, Congresswoman Mayra Flores who flipped a Democratic seat in a special election back in June, it was in Texas.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:50]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And I`m probably wondering if we`ve gotten your attention yet, because Congresswoman Flores was born in Mexico. Her parents were migrant farm workers who came to the United States when she was just six years old. She became a U.S. citizen at the age of 14 and her political story is this.\par \par \uc2 Her parents were Democrats. She supported President Obama as did most voters in her district that runs up from Brownsville on the U.S.-Mexico border all the way to east of San Antonio. And now, she loves \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . She has blamed the January 6th attack on Antifa and she has dabbled in QAnon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- out there that included hashtags for QAnon. And I`m just wondering where you come down on that whole QAnon thing.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: I will say it over and over. It was a hashtag. Washington wants us to focus on nonsense. I`m focused on the issues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: But Congresswoman Mayra Flores is not the story here. The story are the people, many of them Hispanic voters in this once reliably blue district in Texas, who actually turned out and voted for her.\par \par \uc2 And that is what my colleague, Paola Ramos, went to investigate. She joins us now, Paola Ramos, Vice News host and MSNBC contributor. Her special report, "THE FIELD REPORT WITH PAOLA RAMOS" airs tomorrow night at 10:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have you. It`s great to see you in person, Paola. I want to start with, you know the Obama campaign very well. So you know the machinations behind what these voters were thinking in 2008 and then they flipped and voted for Trump in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about that mindset and what you learned about the so-called Obama- Trump voter.\par \par \uc2 PAOLA RAMOS, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, I think the big picture is that in 2020, we have to remember that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won seven out of the 14 counties along the U.S. Mexico border. He was able to flip two of them and, as you saw, he made significant inroads in a place like the Rio Grande Valley.\par \par \uc2 Here is someone like Mayra Flores. She bets on this idea that Latinos, at our core, and in our hearts, and in our, souls, that Latinos are naturally more aligned with the Republican Party. That we are conservative at heart and that`s why her campaign slogan was simple. It was God, family, and country. And she bet on that, on that idea, and it worked.\par \par \uc2 It worked also because it wasn`t a white old man telling Latinos to vote for them. It was a Latina, an immigrant, someone that could speak in Apanish. And when you combine her message and the messenger, it worked.\par \par \uc2 She won a district that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by four points. She won by almost twice as much.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Ok. So, her slogan was about God, country. Let`s -- I want to play you this clip from your program about faith and then I will ask you about where religion fits in all this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Eight out of ten times if a Latino is being outreached through the church, more than likely believe in some of the conservative platforms. So it`s much easier for them to get out and vote Republican.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans don`t start strategizing the Latino pastors and churches like the Democrats have done for years with a black church. there will be no victory for conservative candidates across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so first, is this more a traditional Catholic Church? I think most people would think of it as the traditional Catholic church. Or are we talking evangelical churches?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: We`re talking evangelicals. The big picture is that Latinos are the fastest growing group of evangelicals in the United States, right. In this moment there are more Latino pastors opening churches than at any other time. More Latinos are leading with Catholicism -- and so, Republicans are politicizing that movement, right.\par \par \uc2 They are understanding that if you go to these evangelical churches and you do, in their eyes, and in their view, what Democrats have been doing in black churches, that is a motto that`s not only can work in the Rio Grande Valley but it can work across the country.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: We`ll see what happens on that front. But let me ask you really quickly. And we have talked a lot about disinformation on this program and elsewhere. Spanish language disinformation during COVID was ripe. I think a lot of people saw it.\par \par \uc2 Do you see based on your reporting similar streams of disinformation in the Spanish language when it comes to American politics?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Always. I mean it`s always there. I always remember this. If we think that big tech does a bad job at flagging mis and disinformation in English, imagine what it is in Spanish. I think there`s multiple studies out there that prove that \uc2 Facebook\uc2  flags around 30 percent of misinformation in Spanish compared to 70 percent in English.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Right. We saw those effects in a place like Florida with the communism narrative. You can see same narrative in a place like Texas. So, mis and disinformation is everywhere. It`s seeped into this.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Do you see any efforts to try to tackle it?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: I mean, yes. I think there`s a lot of lessons learned from 2016 in 2020. You see a lot more outreach in Spanish but it is not enough.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, Paola Ramos, thank you so much. The report, I should note, airs tomorrow night at 10:00 p.m. Make sure to catch that right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:47]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the Mar-a-Lago investigation special master tells the Trump team to show proof of his outlandish conspiracies about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . So far, they have said nothing.\par \par \uc2 Then, the enormous financial pressure on the former president, his family, and his company. Trump`s argument? It is up to you to prove he is not lying.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the fleecing of America.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220923ei9m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for September 22, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Rehema Ellis, Gabe Gutierrez \par \uc2 8,318 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Trump`s argument, it`s up to you to prove he`s not lying. Plus, the fleecing of America. Billions of tax dollars meant to help poor people and to feed children squandered on luxury cars and real estate, staggering fraud and scandal as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  getting hammered again. His latest legal dilemma comes just one day after a resounding defeat in federal appeals court over the classified records that were seized at Mar-a-Lago and after New York`s Attorney General filed a massive lawsuit against him.\par \par \uc2 Today, Trump`s lawyers heard from Raymond Dearie, he is the special master reviewing classified documents taken from the former guy`s Florida club. Judge Dearie demanded the lawyers offer actual proof for Trump`s repeated claim that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted evidence during their search. Dearie also wants the legal team to formally state whether they believe the \uc2 FBI\uc2  lied about the documents that were seized.\par \par \uc2 And Dearie goes on to say he might ask for testimony about the search and the documents from quote, witnesses with knowledge of the relevant facts. One more sign that the special master he means business. He is pushing for another judge to help review the thousands of items that were found in Trump`s home.\par \par \uc2 The former president has claimed he declassified sensitive records that he took from the White House by just thinking about it. Last night, he told \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  that he is able to do that just by thinking apparently, that was news to some Senate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): I think there`s a process for declassifying documents. And I think it ought to be adhered to and followed. And I think that should apply to anybody who has access to or deals with classified information.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC): I believe, as I understand the executive branch requirements, there is a process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That process is more than just a thought. And today, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  weighed in with their version of our facts sake writing this, the 2009 executive order directs the head of the department or agency that originally deemed information classified to oversee declassification reviews. The executive branch has regulations, laying out the process that should be followed.\par \par \uc2 While all of this is going on, the January 6 Committee is busy preparing for its upcoming interview with conservative activist Ginni Thomas, who, of course, is the wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Tonight, the panel`s chairman explained why they are eager to hear from her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): For the committee, we think because she was involved in helping organize and promote January 6, and individuals coming to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Could we possibly expect pieces of her testimony to be referenced or mentioned in next week`s hearing or?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (R-MD): It`s possible, but we haven`t made any final determinations yet, but I have no doubt that she will be a significant part of the report and I hope that at least some of what we got from her will end up in this hearing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So sit back, get a drink and let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel. We`ve got a lot to get to. Jeff Mason, White House correspondent for Reuters. Katie Benner, Pulitzer Prize winning Justice Department reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , and Professor Melissa Murray of NYU Law School. She was a law clerk for \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  on the federal bench before her nomination to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Melissa, we have got to start with this special master. This situation might not be working out the way Trump expected. Is that surprising to you?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, NYU LAW PROFESSOR: I`m not surprised, Judge Dearie has a very strong reputation in New York. He was on the Eastern District of New York for many years. And he`s known as a straight shooter. I think I said on your show earlier, that I was actually surprised that he was one of the names that was floated by the Trump team for the position as special master. He seemed to kind of play it down the middle kind of person and wasn`t exactly what I was expecting from them.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s we can see now, why the Justice Department was quick to take them up on this offer. I mean, he`s really made it clear that they either have to put up or they have to shut up, but they can`t keep making these claims without actually providing evidence to back them up.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Melissa, does it feel like he`s almost doing his own investigation here, beyond just reviewing the documents?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I mean, a document review is in some nature investigatory because you have to actually go through and determine what is or what is not privileged and to sort through it. So there is a investigative function to it.\par \par \uc2 But it seems like he`s what he`s actually doing is maybe doing some of the work that Judge Cannon might have done in that initial ruling is sort of trying to figure out what needs to go to the Department of Justice and what can be returned or what stays with the president or the former president rather and what keeps go and what gets returned to the government itself.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:10]\par \par \uc2 So to that end, it seems like he`s stepping in to fill a void that`s been there for some time.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s not good for Trump. Is it possible for Trump to back out of this, he`s the one that wanted the special master.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I don`t know if you can unring this bell. But I mean, I think if there are bills to be unrung, there have been quite a lot of bells peeling for the Trump team over the last 24 hours. This has really been Donald Trump`s no good, very bad, terrible day all in retrospect. So lots to go here. Lots to see.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, the 11th Circuit decision yesterday, it was a huge win for the Justice Department. Katie, so tell us where the case stands now.\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, THE NEW YORK TIMES JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER: So for the Justice Department, they can now see look at significant documents. They can go forward with the part of their investigation that they think is most important. Keep in mind, the justice department wanted to know, just a couple of things going into this matter before it became a huge court battle.\par \par \uc2 Did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have records that belong to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 ? Were some of those records classified? And then was there any sort of harm done to U.S. national security interests to have that classified information out in the wild?\par \par \uc2 In addition, they wanted to know whether or not Trump`s legal team was completely forthcoming when asked whether or not the President still had records at Mar-a-Lago. So we know from what`s out there in the public already, the Justice Department has reason to believe that no, Trump`s lawyers were not forthcoming, and that there were presidential records that should have been returned to the government. And there was a ton of classified information.\par \par \uc2 So in order to determine exactly how damaging all of that is, they need to be able to look at the records that the 11th Circuit just said they could look at, it`s going to help them go further with their investigation and take further investigative steps, including using the documents themselves to ask more questions, potentially, of other witnesses and other people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jeff, we heard from just two Republican senators today pretty clearly breaking with Trump on the whole, I can declassify by just thinking about it explanation. But what about the rest of the GOP? It was clear what Trump is saying is absolute nonsense.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, REUTERS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean, what about the rest of the GOP? That`s a question that has been asked for years, really, based on the multiple types of assertions that President Trump has made that have been outlandish. This was just the latest one, and the two that were the clips were played. How do you not distance yourself from that? I think that`s a question that Republican leaders are absolutely confronted with in this particular case.\par \par \uc2 I also think more broadly, Steph, it`s interesting this discussion about the special master and the athlete (ph) put up or shut up moment. It is -- it is a -- from a macro level, an example of something that the President - - the former president has been asked to do or forced to do on multiple different occasions. And in this case, he has to do it by the person who his team had supported.\par \par \uc2 After his allegations that the election was fraudulent, he was asked to prove it. They went to court a zillion times and weren`t able to prove it because it was a false allegation. Right now, there, again, they`re dropping things or he`s dropping comments saying, suggesting that maybe the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted evidence. Well, now he`s being called out on it. And we`ll see if they say anything, but as you said in your intro, so far, nothing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Melissa, that`s a former federal prosecutor last night on this show, told us that the New York AG`s case against Trump is currently civil, but it should be a criminal case. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, Attorney General James did note in her complaint that there was evidence that criminal laws had been broken, which begs the question why she didn`t continue with a criminal prosecution. Why this is a civil prosecutor, why this is a civil case. And I think the reason for that is primarily it comes down to the question of the burden of proof in a criminal case, it is much harder for the government, the standard of proof is beyond a reasonable doubt, which is a quite high standard.\par \par \uc2 In a civil case, it is a preponderance of evidence -- of the evidence. So a much lower standard much easier for the government to prove. Obviously, it doesn`t come with any incarceration or penalties like that. But it does come with significant penalties like the discouragement of the ill-gotten profits, as well as the possibility that the Trump team will be forbidden from doing business in the new in New York State and in from holding offices, like corporate board positions in New York state, so it`s quite sizable, and it seems like it`s an easier way for the government to go if they want to hold the Trump team accountable.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  of the Manhattan DA seems to now be saying his investigation is ongoing. Can you clear that up? Because the last we checked, it wasn`t and a few people had resigned in the DA`s office when he announced months ago that they weren`t pursuing criminal investigation?\par \par \uc2 [23:10:07]\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, there did seem to be some major defections in the Manhattan DA`s office around this, in part because there seemed to be a reluctance to go forward with the prosecution. But we really don`t know what`s been going on in the Manhattan DA`s office, whether or not they`ve actually continued this investigation. And whether they have more information.\par \par \uc2 We know that these two investigations by the AG and the Manhattan DA were running in parallel, and it`s likely that they were sharing information with each other. Now that the -- now the AG`s investigation seems to have borne fruit and has resulted in this lawsuit filed against the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , it`s likely that some of the evidence that was supporting that particular complaint might now be used with the Manhattan DA.\par \par \uc2 So, we just really don`t know what`s been going on behind the scenes. But yes, so a few months ago, it seemed like this was DOA, but now it seems to have been resuscitated.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Katie, the January 6 Committee is planning to talk to Ginni Thomas, how big a deal, is it? Yes, they`re going to talk to her, but we have no idea what she`s going to say.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: So pulling back, it`s a big deal, because they`re questioning the wife of the Supreme Court justice. And it`s the kind of thing that people just didn`t think was going to happen, particularly with \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , in such a position of power. She was reluctant to do so. And here she is doing it.\par \par \uc2 But I mean, in terms of whether or not this is going to be a big deal for the investigation and for the public. That`s to be seen. It depends on what questions are asked, and what Ginni Thomas said. So I don`t think that we can say this is some sort of knockout punch for the committee because we just don`t know what kind of information will yield.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: There`s another story that we`re following tonight conspiracy theorist Alex Jones. He is also under scrutiny from the January 6 Committee. He testified today in the defamation Connecticut trial over his Sandy Hook hoax lies. And Rehema Ellis has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEX JONES, CONSPIRACY THEORIST: I`ve already apologized to the parents. I don`t apologize to you. I don`t apologize to you.\par \par \uc2 REHEMA ELLIS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Alex Jones in a heated exchange with the attorney for the family suing him in a defamation trial. After he challenged 