e sure that message is loud and clear. And Democrats are have been critical and clearly painting a picture and a contrast of what they can expect with Republicans not only on abortion, but also on the active threat to that we`re facing with democracy. That`s something that the poll you mentioned earlier, Stephanie, did show as a bright spot because even though Trump`s favorability has not gone down, like one would expect with crime after crime being revealed. The reality is that 54 percent of those same respondents said that he actively presented a threat to democracy, and that was an issue for them.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats have been on hitting that nail on the head repeatedly under the leadership of President Biden who continues to lay out and make the case of the fact that our democracy is on the ballot in November. And that`s something Democrats absolutely needs to continue to do.\par \par \uc2 And one more thing, I can`t put the Inflation Reduction Act aside because it`s yet another example of Democrats fighting for people and eventually getting them what they need to help them. So that is another argument that Democrats have going into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right then, Juanita Tolliver, Susan Del Percio, great, great, great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Fleecing of America. The stunning scale of pandemic unemployment fraud is starting to come into focus. And it`s disgusting. How crooks stole billions of dollars meant to help those in need in the early days of the pandemic. They may be going to hell, but in the meantime, they`re driving sport scars.\par \par \uc2 The NBC News investigation is next when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:55]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We are learning more tonight about the shocking amount of alleged pandemic unemployment fraud according to a federal watchdog. More than 45 billion, billion with a B dollars from unemployment insurance meant to help people during the pandemic may have been stolen. That is way higher than the 16 billion in potential fraud that was identified last year.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ken Dilanian has been following all of this and joins us now. First, we need to give special thanks to Ken. I know you have been on TV since 7:00 am. I begged you to come on tonight because this is so important. I fear I`m going to be shouting during this segment because it`s just so upsetting. Walk us through this huge scale about unemployment fraud. Who are these people selling the money how they do it?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: So the worst news about this Stephanie is that this $46 billion estimate that came out today from the Labor Department IG is probably a vast undercount. When we looked at this about a year ago, and we were talking to private companies like \uc2 IDEMIA\uc2  and \uc2 LexisNexis\uc2 , that have contracts with the states, which really actually run the unemployment programs, the highest estimate we heard was $400 billion.\par \par \uc2 Now that -- now some people criticize that is too high, but maybe say let`s say it`s 200 billion. It`s an enormous sum of money for just as for comparison, so people understand. The annual budget of the Department of Education of the United States government is $68 billion.\par \par \uc2 So this is just an incredible sum of money. And what we found was that about half of the unemployment fraud was carried out by foreign criminal groups. They just preyed on this program because they realized that nobody was checking. All you needed was a fake identity of an American, a social security number or driver`s license, which is easily obtainable on the dark web, and Nigerian criminal gangs, Chinese hackers, Russian fraudsters, they just started bombarding these antiquated state computer systems with applications.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, so hold on. So, they`ve identified that they did this, are they all going to get away with it? We`re not going to get any of this money back. Nobody`s going to pay the price.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: You know, the sad fact is we`re most of the money is gone. The Secret Service, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the Department of Justice are doing what they can to clawback what they can. And they`ve recovered a few billion dollars, and they`re prosecuting as many people as they can.\par \par \uc2 But the math just doesn`t add up. I mean, there aren`t enough \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents and prosecutors in the country, even if they dropped everything they were doing and just worked on COVID fraud to catch even a 10th of the people that did this. I mean, they`re really -- they`re sort of having a triage and only going after the most obvious cases. We just covered one in Minneapolis where 47 people were charged in the biggest COVID fraud scheme that they`ve charged so far. $250 million stolen in this one city in Minneapolis with money that was supposed to go to hungry children, and people were just realizing that no one was checking again. So all they had to do was say they were providing these meals fill out the applications and the money went pouring in from the Agriculture Department.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:04]\par \par \uc2 The fundamental issue here, Stephanie, is that, you know, for some good reasons and I think some bad reasons, the government loosened the rules on all this COVID aid because they wanted to get the money out the door. And then they had very little oversight because people weren`t working, then people weren`t coming to the office inspectors weren`t going to visit the sites.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You and I reported on this real time. We talked about it all the time. We said, Where`s the oversight? When the CARES Act passed we said, Who`s going to run oversight? Who`s going to be on this committee? Right? So how is the government defending the fact that oh, we had to loosen the rules to get the money out. But you didn`t need to leave the barn door open and say, you know, it`s here for the taken?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Oh, well, I`m with you. This is an epic scandal. Because we know that private companies like \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , they figured out how to identify their customer and do large volumes of transactions while policing fraud.\par \par \uc2 And so the government could have done it, too. They didn`t do it. Some bureaucrats and some people this happened in the Trump administration made some terrible, terrible decisions, and we`re all paying for it now. And it is a bit frustrating, because even now, when you talk to people in the Justice Department, they will say, well, they just needed to get the money out. You know, there were there wasn`t much we could do we expected fraud. I think that`s a cop out.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It sure is.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: They need to be this way, Stephanie. And nobody`s you know, as far as I know, nobody`s lost their job over a badly designed COVID Relief Program. There haven`t been that many congressional hearings. You know, because both parties are complicit. Neither party is really seized on it and push this as the scandal that it is. So I`m glad you`re covering it. Because, you know, the American public should know how bad it is.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s absolute garbage. It`s why people don`t trust government full stop. You`re also reporting on the Mississippi welfare scandal. We`ve been covering it here a lot. Today, a former prosecutor of Mississippi`s welfare agency, right. The person who oversaw the welfare agency pleaded guilty to fraud charges. Walk us through this. What does it mean for the investigation because this is the one Brett Favre is involved in?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: That`s right. So this is a big development. This is because this this man is John Davis, he was the administrator of the welfare agency that handed out all this money to places it shouldn`t have gone. And he was -- he pleaded guilty today and agreed to cooperate with federal prosecutors. This is really the first federal action after years of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  looking into this, but not really doing much. Now they`ve got this guilty plea from this key figure.\par \par \uc2 And you know, it looks like they flipped him. And when you flip somebody in the investigation, you ask yourself, well, well, who`s next? Who`s the big fish they`re trying to get? Well, it`s -- the only person who outranks John Davis is the former governor of Mississippi, the Republican governor, so who he has claimed that he didn`t know that this money was welfare, but he was deeply involved in in helping Brett Favre obtain, for example, $5 million to build a volleyball facility that his daughter benefited from.\par \par \uc2 This was federal welfare money that was supposed to go to poor women and children. Instead, it built a volleyball facility. So, this is an outrageous scandal and you haven`t heard the last of it here, Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: In one of the poorest states in the entire country. Brett Favre, you know what else we haven`t heard from the \uc2 NFL\uc2  or \uc2 Sirius\uc2 , where he has a radio show. Ken Dilanian, thank you so much for staying up this late. And more importantly, thank you for this reporting is really important and people deserve to hear about it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, most of Puerto Rico still without power. They are in the dark and pleading with their American compatriots for help after Hurricane Fiona when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:37]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: To people of Puerto Rico who are still hurting from Hurricane Maria five years later, I know that we`re -- they should know that we are with you. We`re not going to walk away. We mean it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight disaster in Puerto Rico. Hurricane Fiona is the strongest storm on the planet right now. The category for Monster is making its way north to Bermuda and potentially on its way to make landfall in Canada. Fiona has already left a trail of destruction across the Caribbean. In Puerto Rico most people are still without power tonight, and parts of the island had been completely devastated. Our own Gabe Gutierrez has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This what`s Fiona`s theory in Turks and Caicos while Bermuda braces for a close call. In the Dominican Republic, there are still hundreds of thousands of people without water or power.\par \par \uc2 Here in Puerto Rico, new landslides overnight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So we`re still pretty much in this emergency response phase.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Satellite images show washed out bridges and flooded fields across Puerto Rico before and after the storm. These Satellite images show how much of the island is in the dark each night. This is before Fiona and this is after. 38 percent of customers now have power up from 27 percent yesterday.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has signed a major disaster declaration and at a FEMA briefing today he spoke with Puerto Rico`s Governor.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We will do everything, everything we can to meet the urgent needs you have.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: With the heat index hovering around 100 degrees. This is Jose Alvarez`s sweltering existence. When he tells us he wrote out the hurricane in remote Western Puerto Rico the river rushing under his home. Today for the first time local authorities brought him the bottled water he so desperately needed.\par \par \uc2 Now neighbors yell across the river to notify each other at the first glimpse of any supplies. During Hurricane Maria five years ago this mountainside got seven inches of rain. Finoa got 24. You feel forgotten this woman says. Tonight they have at least some water here but no power and ongoing disaster that is all too familiar.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Our thoughts are with the people of Puerto Rico and everyone affected by this devastating storm tonight.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:03]\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a good and safe night from all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News. Thanks for staying up late. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220923ei9m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for September 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Marc Caputo \par \uc2 8,254 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: NBC has a website for you. It`s called Plan Your Vote with a state-by-state guide to registration deadlines, voting rules, and mail-in, and in person voting. It`s at nbcnews.com/planyourvote. And very importantly, it is available both in English and in Spanish, so you have no excuse. Do it. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 ANDY WEISSMANN, FORMER LEAD PROSECUTOR, \uc2 ROBERT MUELLER\uc2  SPECIAL COUNSEL INVESTIGATION: He may be able to decide all of this without actually even seeing the classified documents.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The first special master hearing not looking good for the disgraced ex-president. Tonight, Trump`s absurd argument for keeping classified documents and just where he was getting his legal advice.\par \par \uc2 TOM FITTON\uc2 , PRESIDENT, JUDICIAL WATCH: As far as I`m concerned, Trump should get every single record they took back, not just his passport.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, the lawsuit and criminal investigation into the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  stunt.\par \par \uc2 JAVIER SALAZAR, SHERIFF, BEXAR COUNTY, TEXAS: Somebody saw fit to come from another state, hunt them down, prey upon them, and then take advantage of their desperate situation just for the sake of political theater.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  doubles down on a losing political issue and the state of play in the Midterms with my guest Bernie Sanders when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Donald Trump`s lawyers were in court today for their first hearing before the newly appointed special master who is tasked with reviewing the classified documents the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seized from Trump`s retirement home in Florida. And in that courtroom, his lawyers were essentially tasked with defending the indefensible.\par \par \uc2 The ex-President claimed that he had a right to keep the roughly 11,000 documents removed from Mar-a-Lago, and that he has some privilege that confers ownership on him with respect to the 300 documents found with classified markings. That position that his lawyers argued today does not make any sense. Someone pointed out, it all boils down to Donald Trump`s belief that he is still as of today, here in September of 2022, President of the United States, that he still has the powers of the executive branch, that those documents, the vast majority of which are clearly covered by the Presidential Records Act should be government property, that those documents belong to him to do with whatever you wants.That`s the core point from which this whole dispute flows.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Trump isn`t the president because he lost the election in 2020 fair and square no matter what he says, no matter how much he whines about it. He is no longer president and he no longer has any of the powers that go along with that office. But Trump doesn`t care about that. He kept the documents anyway even though, this is key, his own lawyers warned him not to do it over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported earlier this summer, Trump`s deputy White House Counsel Pat Philbin tried to help the National Archives retrieved the material that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took. But the former president repeatedly resisted entreaties from his advisors saying, "It`s not theirs, it`s mine." An email obtained by The Times last month shows that White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  also determined the record should have been turned over to the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, we learned that another Trump White House lawyer, Eric Herschmann "sought to impress upon Trump the seriousness of the issue, the potential for investigations and legal exposure if he did not return the documents. That was last year. It wasn`t one warning, it was multiple warnings from multiple lawyers about the same thing saying the same thing. The last one being this gentleman you see there, Mr. Herschmann. You may remember him from his role in the first impeachment trial when he was trying to convince all of us that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had done nothing wrong and could be trusted.\par \par \uc2 He also delivered a compelling testimony to the January 6 Committee including this story about the advice that he gave coup memo author, John Eastman, after January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC HERSCHMANN, FORMER WHITE HOUSE LAWYER: I said, I only want to hear two words coming out of your mouth for now on, orderly transition. And I screamed. I said, I don`t want to hear any other effing words coming out of your mouth no matter what other than orderly transition. Repeat those words to me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What did he say?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHMANN: Eventually, he said orderly transition. I said, good job. Now, I`m going to give you the best free legal advice you`re ever getting in your life. Get a great effing criminal defense lawyer. You`re going to need it. And then I hung up on him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We don`t have Herschmann is quite so tough when he`s actually in the midst of a conflicted situation. But you can imagine how the baseball bat of justice might have delivered the message to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that he was violating the law and had to get the documents back. But it was surely a familiar scene for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Throughout the ex-president`s life and career, his impulse is always, always to do sketchy unlawful things, to cut corners to get away with stuff while the lawyers around him tell him he cannot do those things.\par \par \uc2 What is sort of remarkable, honestly, is that in most cases, Trump actually has kind of a fine-tuned antenna for just how close he can get to the line without crossing into outright easily provable crimes. That has saved him many times before. But this time, it appears that he stepped right over that line, the crystal clear line that his lawyers told him we`re there. And to understand why that finally happened, you have to look at who he`s actually listening to because he was ignoring his highly qualified White House lawyers Pat Philbin, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , Eric Herschmann. Instead, he was reportedly taking the advice of this guy.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:42]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FITTON: We want to know about the deep state effort to overthrow the President of the United States. You know, in many ways, it`s remarkable the President is a crime victim. He was targeted illegally by the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 If they doesn`t want to fire Mueller directly, maybe just pardon everyone. Everyone caught up in it either directly or indirectly, who`s the subject, the target, you name it, just pardon it all.\par \par \uc2 I think the raid was a fraud, an abuse, and a sham. There was no good faith basis to target Trump here with this outrageous array. And all those records he has are his records. And they should stop pretending that just because they think they`re presidential records and they think it`s classified, you know, that`s the -- that`s the final answer, and it isn`t.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, that`s a guy named \uc2 Tom Fitton\uc2 . He`s like a second, third tier conservative activist, head of the right-wing group of \uc2 Judicial Watch\uc2 . They`ve been around for forever. He`s somewhat ludicrous figure, honestly. I put them in the like, Genni Thomas category of the American right. Imagine a more senior James O`Keefe on steroids.\par \par \uc2 As Politico puts it, Fitton has made a career of suing the federal government over suspected bureaucratic corruption, irritating every president since \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . That`s true. In fact, fitness organization has tormented the Clintons for nearly 20 years, uncovering documents on their scandals du jour from Whitewater, to Travelgate, to the suicide of Vince Foster.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s been at this a long time. Basically, a right-wing rabble rouser, but he found an enthusiastic booster in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Surprise, surprise. Now this is faded as it were considering Fitton has been boosting Trump for years. He actually takes credit for being one of the first public figures to criticize the Mueller investigation as corrupt and unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 Now, I shouldn`t know here, \uc2 Tom Fitton\uc2  is not a lawyer. He`s just a dude with some big money right-wing donors, I guess. But he`s been giving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a lot of legal advice that the ex-president is actually listening to. Since as early as February, Fitton has been reportedly been calling Trump, telling him it was a mistake to get the records from Mar-a-Lago to the National Archives, that his team should have never left the archives, "strong-arm him into returning them."\par \par \uc2 Fitton said the records belong to Trump, citing a 2012 court case involving his organization that he said gave the former president authority to do what he wanted with records from his own term in office. He suggested to Trump that if the Archives came back, he should not give up any additional records. Trump "began obsessing over Fitton`s arguments, complaining to aides about the 15 boxes that were handed over, and even as Fitton at one point to brief his attorneys. I`m sure they love that one.\par \par \uc2 As one person close to the ex-President put it, "the moment Tom got in the boss`s ear, it was downhill from there." You know, it`s like the Overstock guy or the Pillow Guy. So \uc2 Tom Fitton\uc2 , professional right-wing scandal monger appears to have facilitated the kind of black and white commission of an easily provable crime that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on his own had so far managed to avoid. It`s really an amazing feat of all people, \uc2 Tom Fitton\uc2 , who ended up putting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in arguably the most acute legal power he has ever been in. Well played.\par \par \uc2 And that legal peril was on full display today at the Brooklyn courthouse where Judge Raymond Dearie, the newly appointed special master, heard the arguments from the ex-President`s lawyers who repeated Trump`s claim that he declassified the documents. Judge Dearie said the government gave him -- government gave him clear evidence these are classified documents, but as far as he`s concerned, that`s the end of it. Trump`s lawyers responded by basically saying they do not yet want to make their case about why the documents are not classified. Judge Dearie told them, he will go ahead and continue the business of the court. "You can`t have your cake and eat it."\par \par \uc2 Andrew Weissmann spent years working in the Department of Justice, most recently served as a lead prosecutor in \uc2 Robert Mueller\uc2  Special Counsel Investigation. He was also in that Brooklyn courtroom today for the first hearing with a special master. And he joins me now here at the table. It`s great to have you.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Give us a rundown of your impressions of the hearing today.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, the first impression was it was like landing back on planet Earth. It just felt so normal And having been in front of Judge Dearie, he was just quintessential Judge Dearie, low-key, firm, people who confuse his being polite with his not being firm, make a big mistake. And then he ran a really tight ship. He was supposed to start at 2:00. At 2:00, he`s on the bench. In 40 minutes, it was over. He put both parties through their paces. So that`s sort of the atmosphere. He was in full control.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:26]\par \par \uc2 And from the outset, as you noted, he was not having any sort of BS from either side. He made it really clear to I think the public that this is a civil case, meaning that the plaintiff in the case has the burden. This is not a criminal case where the government needs to prove something beyond a reasonable doubt. And then he pointedly said, if you don`t present any evidence to me, Trump, then I am left with the records that I have.\par \par \uc2 I have evidence that these documents are classified. If you decide for whatever reason you do not want to present any evidence, that`s fine, you don`t have to. But he then went ahead and said, then this case becomes very easy. And I think that just means he`s signalling, I will then have a record in front of me where there is no dispute that these are classified. And he`s certainly not going to turn classified documents over -- the remedy, which is that he wants the documents to be turned over to him back to Mar-a-Lago is clearly not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. So, he says, as I understand it, look, we`ve got these documents which Judge Cannon who has created my position seems to think are disputed, right? So, the question is, OK, well, what`s the dispute?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, he comes before the party you sit at says, OK, one side, the government says these are our documents as is clearly identifiable by the fact they are marked classified.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: That would be -- that would be as the judge said, prima facie evidence.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He calls it prima facie evidence, right. If something`s marked classified, that is prima facie evidence, the government owns it.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, other side in a dispute, right, like --\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: What do you got?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What do you have? What do you say? Why is it yours? And they said --\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, one of the things they said, Judge, you know, it might be premature for us to actually have to come up with evidence. And the judge snidely, but politely said, and yet you managed to bring a lawsuit. Meaning, it doesn`t work that way. You bring a lawsuit when you have a claim, not because you were searching for a claim.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, the judge clearly is not going to have any of that. He`s going to say, look, you have the burden. Are you going to refute this or not? If you don`t refute it, the judge`s job is to decide a conflict. If there is no conflict, that`s the end of the story. And I thought one of the more telling things is, he said, you know, I may not need to actually see the actual contents of the classified documents. That becomes sort of irrelevant. Whether it`s Top Secret, Compartmentalized.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: And none of the parties need to -- obviously, the government can see it, but Trump doesn`t need to see it. If you`re not going to dispute that they are classified, then how do you get the remedy which is that these documents go back to you. So, that`s clearly the conundrum here for the Trump team.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As I was reading -- I was actually reading your notes on this, which we were all sent today because you were -- you were there and they were extremely helpful. As I was reading it, I couldn`t wonder -- and obviously, this is speculative so you don`t have to weigh in on this. It`s like, did Judge Cannon create this situation so that she wouldn`t have to be the one to say what Dearie is saying, right? She`s basically outsourcing to this guy to just be like, so what`s the dispute here, because she could have said that herself. WEISSMANN: She could be doing all of this. There is no reason for her to have another federal judge. Remember, Judge Dearie is a sitting federal judge to do something that she could do, but the government lucked out that she decided to not have her fingerprints on this. This makes it a lot easier for her to sort of have plausible deniability and just to approve what happened.\par \par \uc2 Chris, the other thing that I thought was really interesting is that the judge made it clear that his timeline is actually faster than either of the parties. He started by saying that there should be sort of responsible dispatch. That was his quote. And he put out a sort of proposal to the parties that we haven`t seen yet, that it would be done in four weeks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: And that`s faster than either side wanted. Obviously, a lot faster than the Trump side, but it was even faster than the government was anticipating. And I think that`s because Judge Dearie being so experienced is saying, this is not complicated. Like, let`s move along and let`s like get from A to Z and get it done.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is the next step here, then? I mean, I guess the question is, like, can they throw a monkey wrench into the procedural works here without raising an actual claim or dispute on a specific document or set of them?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, I think that the next thing that`s going to happen is we`re going to get a schedule. The judge is going to hear from both sides in very short order and he`s going to issue an order that sets out exactly what each side has to do. And at some point in that order, I anticipate it`s going to say to the Trump team, you need to make a motion.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:20]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: There is no motion right now to return property. You need to make that. And pointedly, one of the things he seemed to have said is why should I even hear it or Judge Cannon? Why isn`t that going back to the magistrate judge who decided this matter? So, I think that`s really the next step. But I think it`s going to all happen very, very quickly. The people who thought this was kind of going to be a speed bump, I think that seems right in terms of where we`re going to be two months from now. We`ll sort of look back on this and say, you know, thank goodness for Judge Dearie sort of moving this along.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Dearie said, the Justice Department have presented evidence several documents were classified, noting they are marked as such, pressed Trump`s lawyer, James Trusty, to explain why he should question the government`s determination. They are prima fascia classified. What business is it of the court? I guess my larger question here, there`s a piece of political today with all the kind of like conservative legal scholars and unitary executive people being like, well, actually, under our theory, you don`t get to touch this. I guess the question is, is classification reviewable thing by Article 3 branches?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, you know, the government very politely, they basically didn`t do a lot today. And frankly, when you`re -- when you`re ahead, that`s the right strategy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Don`t appeal favorable rulings?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Exactly. You can only -- it will only can go bad. So, they did make the point to the judge, though, that their view is that it is the executive decides what is classified and what they can do with it. So -- and Judge Dearie said, I totally understand that`s your view. I think if there was a dispute as to whether these were classified or not, then he would have a different role. But that`s why --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You have to make an affirmative case.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Exactly. And just remember, Trump is the plaintiff here. So, that is going to be his burden. And so, Judge Dearie was very loudly saying, you know, put up or shut up, and he did it in a judicial way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Andrew Weissmann, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wanted to show he could be mean to immigrants, and now that could be blowing up in his face.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SALAZAR: Here we have 48 people that are already on hard times, right? They are here legally in our country at that point. They have every right to be where they are. And I believe that they were preyed upon. Somebody came from out of state, preyed upon these people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Some strong words from the law enforcement in the county of Texas where those folks were recruited. The latest on the new investigation into the cruel stunt next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SALAZAR: As we understand it, 48 migrants were lured -- I will use the word lured -- under false pretenses. They were taken to Martha`s Vineyard from what -- from what we can gather for nothing -- for little more than a photo op video up. And then they were unceremoniously stranded.\par \par \uc2 When you`re playing with human lives of people that are already in a desperate situation, people that, again, had every right to be where they were, but were lured under false pretenses, that does tend to bother me quite a bit. Suffice it to say, we will be opening up a case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was the Sheriff of Bexar County, Texas yesterday announcing his office would open an investigation into the stunt pulled last week by Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . That`s when agents of the state of Florida appeared to lure approximately 50 migrants from Texas to board a plane that briefly stopped in Florida and then dropped them off in an island and Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 Now here`s the thing. There are a bunch of outstanding questions about just what happened that the folks in Bexar county will most definitely be digging into. Chief among them, as Josh Marshall with Talking Points Memo writes, is who is Perla? Perla is the woman that migrants arriving in Massachusetts said, recruited and paid them $200 for the flight.\par \par \uc2 So, who hired her? Who paid her? Where did she come from? Did anyone talk to her? How exactly did she get these 48 people on that plane and what did she tell them?\par \par \uc2 Marc Caputo is a national political reporter at \uc2 NBC News Digital\uc2 . He`s been reporting on the migrants lured from Texas by way of Florida, and left in Martha`s Vineyard, and he joins me now. So, Marc, I feel like the initial reaction here, there was a lot of talk about like the optics of it and it`s done and all the stuff like that. But it does appear there`s like some pretty big, outstanding questions about just the facts at issue here.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis goes on last night, and he refers to the vendor, OK, the vendor that the state contracted with? Like, it`s like, you know, they`re ordering food for the cafeteria at the capital. Like, who`s the vendor? Who do you \uc2 Google\uc2  at the state of Florida that would be like, we need the vendor that goes and tells migrants to get on a plane to go to Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 MARC CAPUTO, NBC NEWS DIGITAL, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, we`ve asked that question. We`ve asked for a copy of the contract. We`ve asked for names. We`ve gotten none of it. Florida has a general pretty broad and generous public records law, but so far, the governor`s office has not given the public records up.\par \par \uc2 Incidentally, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , his Democratic opponent for governor, has filed a public records request. That hasn`t been responded to either.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, I mean, again, Florida law -- most -- I`ve never been a reporter in Florida, but I do know it has very good Sunshine laws. Like, you can`t generally -- you know, the bureaucracy of state government is that it`s not that easy to just like throw money around for like, an off-books operation. Like, you got -- you know, you got to get bids, and you got to get contractors, and there`s all these rules. I mean, presumably, we will find out, right, at some point, like, who was Perla, who -- how much did she get paid and what was she told?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:22]\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Yes. And, oddly, it looks like the law enforcement route might be the fastest way to do it. The Bexar County Sheriff who you just referenced. If he does investigate, according to the reports we`ve seen from the migrants who landed in Martha`s Vineyard, they were given cell phone numbers, phone numbers of Perla or these other people who said, hey, contact me if you have any problems. And then of course, these folks arrived in Martha`s Vineyard, and suddenly the numbers didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s phone numbers there to find for the phone company and ways to track people down. So, yes, information is going to be discovered. The question is, what are we going to find out and when. I don`t want to bet on that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, one of the items that came across too is this brochure. I`m sure you`ve seen that was given to these migrants. Now, again, what sort of gross here is that? What clearly happened is someone printed off some section of the Massachusetts State refugee information about things available to refugees, but these people weren`t refugees. It`s a different legal category, and none of the stuff on the brochure applied to them, right?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Right. I wrote about this yesterday. Actually, it`s the Office of Immigration and Refugees in Massachusetts. They finally answered my question today, the Massachusetts office. They said, look, this brochure didn`t come from us. So some agents of the state or a vendor, to use DeSantis word, decided to pull this information apparently online and put it in a brochure.\par \par \uc2 And to your point, a migrants seeking asylum is part of a process. A refugee is an immigration status. An asylum-seeking migrant, an asylum seeker is not a refugee and therefore doesn`t qualify for the benefits that`s listed in the brochure which is stuff like housing assistance, cash assistance, the job assistance and the like. And that`s kind of the core of the complaint from the various lawyers who are representing the various migrants in the suit is they`re saying, look, this is sort of the smoking gun document showing that people were misled.\par \par \uc2 Now, the governor does say that people weren`t misled. He had said that these flights were humane. People were found homeless. They were fed. They were housed for a day at the expense of the state, and then flown again, from San Antonio, Texas, ultimately to Martha`s Vineyard, and they were taken care of here. That`s his position.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. He says it was all consented too. There -- you just mentioned the lawsuit, which I should mention, there is now a lawsuit, class action suit brought by three of these individuals, defendants and their unidentified accomplices designed and executed a premeditated fraudulent and illegal scheme centered on exploiting this vulnerability of the migrants for the sole purpose of advancing their own personal, financial and political interest.\par \par \uc2 I do wonder -- I mean, the weirdest thing about this to me, and I have sort of struggled tonight to not take the obvious bait that this entire spectacle was. Greg Abbott and \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2  are border state governors. They have people showing up in Arizona and Texas. And they are dealing with that and in some places, I think, you know, being quite sort of trollee in the way they`re busing them to Chicago and not actually letting city officials know. But those folks are actually showing up in those states.\par \par \uc2 These people had no connection to Florida, right? Am I missing something here? Like, there`s some -- like, what does Florida have to do with any of this?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: I`m going to -- I`m going to answer just from what DeSantis said because I don`t want to venture a guess on my own. His claim is since -- and most of these folks were from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: That the state research showed that as much as 40 percent of those who are coming across the border from Cuba, Venezuela, and Nicaragua ultimately will wind up in Florida. Therefore, we should kind of proactively go and get them. Again, that`s the state`s argument. Now, you`re talking about how does the governor get this money? There`s a line item in the state budget. It`s called proviso because it`s not just a line item, there`s language with it, that appropriates $12 million in interest accrued from unspent COVID relief money, which set up the "unauthorized aliens program," which enabled the governor to do this.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you -- if you accept the idea that the migrants were asylum seekers from Venezuela, they technically weren`t unauthorized aliens. Again, this is a term that`s used in the state budget. My former coworker and friend Gary Fineout who works with Politico, he wrote yesterday that during a budget debate, unrelated language, one of the state senators, Republican state senator had said, look, Venezuelans aren`t on authorized aliens.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: So, it turns out, not only were these folks according to the budget, supposed to be sent "from the state, which is Florida, and therefore not Texas, but they were not considered unauthorized aliens if they happen to be from Venezuela or asylum seekers from these other countries.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:12]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Jesus said, come with me and I will make you fishers of men, but he did not mean it in this way. Marc Caputo, thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, why Republican attempts to restrict abortion are so unpopular even Republican voters can`t get on board. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:35:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): The only thing I got going for me is every pro- life group in America and about 70 percent of the American people. So, I don`t apologize for being pro-life. I think the pro-life movement has found a position that most Americans would agree upon. There`ll be plenty of pro- choice people will say at 15 weeks, we should ban the late-term abortions, exceptions for rape, incest, life of the mother. The people are with me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Those aren`t late-term abortions, Senator. That was Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina digging in continuing to defend a bill he introduced that would ban abortions nationwide after the 15th week of pregnancy. It`s a bill that even Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and many other Republican senators have refused to support not because they oppose abortion bans, obviously they support them, but because they know proposing a federal abortion ban for the first time in this country`s history right now, just weeks before the Midterm Election, almost certainly would further galvanize voters who support abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Case in point, back in August, we saw what happened when Kansans tried to take this right away through a ballot measure and how it mobilized voters. In November, Michiganders will vote on a ballot measure that would enshrine abortion rights into the state constitution. And take a look at this new poll out of Michigan showing the ballot measure has the support of 56 percent of voters, 23 opposed, 21 undecided.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the really revealing numbers. Republicans are split down the middle on this measure in Michigan, 36 percent supporting, 36 percent in opposition. You can see why many Republican candidates are trying to soften their positions on abortion or just not talk about it like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  before the Midterms, or even better, avoid the topic altogether.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Cecile Richards, former president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , co- chair of the super PAC American Bridge 21st Century. Cecile, I wanted to talk to you tonight because you have watched this play out over the course of your political career, and get your sense of where things are right now post-Dobbs and how they`re different than they might have been, say, a year or two ago or five years ago.\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, FORMER PRESIDENT, PLANNED PARENTHOOD: Sure. Well, I think the important thing, Chris, is that the Republicans have now frankly, they`re the dog that caught the bus. I mean, they have been trying to ban abortion. It`s been in their platform. And now, of course, they`ve been able to jam enough people under the Supreme Court to do this. And the consequences are becoming real.\par \par \uc2 And it`s interesting. I just saw a poll from last week. 97 percent of voters in this country know that Roe was overturned. That`s more important than any other issue that I can imagine in my lifetime. And the Republicans have frankly handled this so poorly. You see someone like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  saying this is a state`s issue, then introducing a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Every single candidate, you know, in the most competitive states, Michigan, Pennsylvania, Arizona, all for abortion bans on those states, they`re wildly unpopular. And you showed that in Michigan, right, we`re seeing right now that Republicans are split evenly on the ballot initiative there. And in the state of Kansas, you know, the reports, they`ve just now done the analysis on who voted in that important test case, really, there in a state where by almost -- by almost 20 points, we won a ballot initiative, legalizing abortion, and 51 percent of the voters at were Republicans. So, thousands and thousands of Republicans voted to protect safe and legal abortion in the state of Kansas.\par \par \uc2 I was so struck by Graham in that -- in that sound that we played where he said I`m proudly pro-life and I don`t make no apologies for it. He reminded me of what \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  said and that VP debate in 2020 when he was pressed on this. And you can see they all fall back on this term, like, I`m pro- life, and we`ll -- because that polls well, I think, probably. I mean, who doesn`t like life? And it also just doesn`t point to anything specific or concrete about like, are you going to prosecute people and what if a woman has a fetal abnormality that shows up at 16 or 17 weeks? What`s she supposed to do then?\par \par \uc2 All that stuff just wiped away. But they can`t run away from those specifics by just repeating their prolife over and over? Unless -- I mean, I guess that`s what they`re going to try.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Well, no, I totally agree. And of course, a woman stood up and asked Senator Graham that exact same thing. And we are seeing this now, the reality of what it`s like to live in a state or a country that bans abortion. Now, as we have 13 states that have banned abortion, the stories are coming out. I think everyone now has heard the story of the 10-year-old girl in Ohio who literally had to go to Indiana. The 16-year-old in Florida who was denied access to illegal abortion, because the judge said she wasn`t mature enough to make that decision, yet somehow mature enough to be required to continue an unintended pregnancy. And the horror epic stories of women who are forced to leave their state to access safe and legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 Of course, under Lindsey Graham`s legislation, a federal abortion ban supported by the Republicans, women would literally have to leave the country. This is not what the American people want. It is incredibly unpopular. And it really goes to the very heart of freedom in this country, the freedom to make your own decisions about your pregnancy. That`s something that people do not believe politicians should be deciding, and particularly someone like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  who, frankly, will never be pregnant and will never know the consequences.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:37]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And it`s striking too -- the public opinion is shifting so much in reaction to how concrete this all is. Graham is trying to get back to what they think is a good polling base, which is 15 weeks. And I thought this was interesting that the -- a poll conducted in March found that most Americans favored laws banning abortions after 15 weeks. A poll in August found the opposite, right? So, there are -- you already see movement. Once the thing just isn`t an abstraction, it`s concrete. They`re trying to run back and say, well, how about -- how about this 15 week deal? And people are like, no, we look -- we know what abortion bans look like and we don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to see this continue to play out in the next seven weeks and after that, Cecile Richards, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, even though history is not in their favor, Democrats have a real chance to make actual gains in the Senate to gain senators in the Midterms. Senator Bernie Sanders joins me on that after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Back in 2021, Virginia Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin ran what at least on the surface looked like an old-school Republican campaign. The kind common during say the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  era. He campaigned as a genial businessman and a sweater vest with a focus on so-called kitchen table issues. He presented himself as something of an anti-Trump. That was all just surface level stuff.\par \par \uc2 Trump endorsed him. Youngkin in turn paid lip service to Trump`s big lie of election fraud, calling for an audit of the results. And today, that is just the reality for a lot of Republicans, especially those running in a blue state like Youngkin. They tried to have it both ways, though that position is ultimately untenable. Eventually, you`re going to have to choose a side.\par \par \uc2 Now to be clear, there are some very successful blue state Republican governors who rejected Trump. \uc2 Charlie Baker\uc2  of Massachusetts, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  of Maryland, \uc2 Phil Scott\uc2  of Vermont are among the most popular governors in the entire country. But they are basically Never Trump Republicans, which means they have no future in national politics, at least as far as we can see from here.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Youngkin wants a future in national politics immediately. He clearly has ambitions to run for president one day. And right now, that means embracing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, despite his apparent initial reluctance to be a Trump supporter, Virginia Governor Youngkin has gone all in on Team Maga.\par \par \uc2 Case in point, next month, Youngkin is traveling down to Arizona to campaign for Republican gubernatorial nominee Kari Lake. He`s using his image as a supposed moderate to lend legitimacy to one of the most extreme pro-coup candidates running for office this cycle, the kind of person who says just wildly untrue stuff like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`ve called \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  an illegitimate president. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-AZ), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: He lost the election and he shouldn`t be in the White House. We had a corrupt election. The reason we have inflation, high -- sky-high inflation, we can`t afford our gasoline, we can`t afford our groceries is because we had a rigged stolen election. The facts are there. The forensic audit proves it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The forensic audit proves no such thing. To the extent it proved anything, it was that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the state of Arizona. Now, it`s sad to say but in our current state of affairs, you have to assume all Republican politicians are essentially pro-coup until proven otherwise. Glenn Youngkin is simply the latest to choose fealty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over loyalty to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There are a whole bunch of competitive Senate seats this year, some of which are within a few points that are going to determine control of the chamber. According to FiveThirtyEight, Democrats right now have a 71 percent chance of holding the Senate. Their model appears to show Democrats could pick up as many as seven seats. So, that would be a winning the lottery kind of night, right?\par \par \uc2 Now, this isn`t a year where even just six months ago, it sure looks like Democrats were screwed, no hope. But now the Democrats have a shot what would it look like to have a majority of Democratic senators who could, for instance, override the filibuster move on popular legislative priorities like guaranteeing the right to an abortion? What possibilities are there if Democrats buck history in the Midterm elections?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Senator Bernie Sanders, Independent from Vermont, Chair of the Senate Democratic Outreach Committee. Senator, first let me take your temperature on how you thinks things -- how you think things are looking for retaining control of the Senate, these tough contested races in an environment that historically has been very difficult for the party that controls the White House?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (I-VT): Well, I would say that things are better than they were a few months ago. But as you indicated, there are many, many seats out there that within the margin of error, so we don`t know how these things will end up -- end up. But to my mind, Chris, to answer your question, if Democrats are going to do well in 2022, in my view, they`ve got to stand up very firmly for working families, make it clear that we are seeing unprecedented levels of corporate greed, unprecedented levels of concentration of ownership in this country all the while working families are struggling, in many instances seeing a decline and their standard of living.\par \par \uc2 So, I think now is the time if you want to win an election to say you know what, I`m on the side of the vast majority of Americans, Black, White, Latino. I`m prepared to take on greedy, powerful corporate interests who are enjoying record-breaking profits while you Americans can`t afford health care, can`t afford to send your kids to college, and are working for starvation wages. That to my mind is how you go forward and win.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:19]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the big accomplishments of the unified Democratic governance was the announcement of the President`s cancellation $10,000 of student debt, 20,000 for those who are getting Pell Grants. You know, you can chart a clear trajectory from your presidential campaign that champion that issue along with Elizabeth Warren to it actually happening. And there was a lot of back and forth, is this going to cause a backlash? Are people going to be resentful? Because my -- you know, I had to pay my loans.\par \par \uc2 What is your sense of the outcome of this in terms of what it`s meant for how -- what it`s meant for people`s lives, and also what it`s meant politically?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: You know, I have the radical idea that good policy is good politics. And it is good policy to cancel student debt in this country. I cannot tell you, Chris, how many young and middle-aged people I have talked to who have hesitated having children, can`t afford to buy a house, whose lives are in economic trouble, because they`re paying off these huge student debts.\par \par \uc2 So, what Biden did was the right thing. I would have gone further. It`s what the people want. I`m not going to tell you it`s 100 percent popular, but it is what the people want. And you know what? If you do what the people want, and not what the corporate world wants or billionaire campaign contributors want, you win elections. If you raise the minimum wage to a living wage, you win elections. If you expand Medicare to include dental and hearing and vision, which recently in a poll had only 87 percent support, you win elections. If you build housing and create jobs, you win elections. If you give tax breaks to the rich, you`re probably not going to win.\par \par \uc2 So, my own view is there is an agenda out there and the cancellation of student debt is one part of it. But we`ve got to go further and show working families we`re on their side.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There are people on the other side of it. Ted Cruz has been plotting with folks to try to challenge it in court. Republican legal challenges are expected to start once the application start rolling out. Legal experts say it`s possible the administration`s plan could be frozen by a federal judge. What do you think about this plot to try to find someone that is standing to block this in court?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, I think it sounds to me very much what happened with the Supreme Court ruling on abortion. It turns out that the overwhelming majority of the American people think it is women who have the right to control their own lives, not the Republican Party. A strong majority of the American people think we should cancel student debt. And if Senator Cruz and others want to challenge that, I think that`s going to hurt them politically. It`s the wrong thing to do and I think it hurts them politically.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the most striking features of the last two years, which had been strange in many ways, obviously, coming out of the worst parts of the COVID pandemic -- pandemic is still with us, inflation high, labor market is very tight, has been this raft of union organizing, unlike anything that we`ve really set -- seen in I think it`s fair to say decades. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  showing the union elections one by mid-year, you can see that spike there and 2022. It`s been about 20 years since we`ve seen this. What what do you think`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: It isn`t hard to tell you to figure out. Workers are working, in many instances, longer hours for low wages. And with inflation, they`re falling further and further behind. You want to know what`s going on in the last 49 years? Real wages today are lower than they were 49 years ago despite an increase in productivity. Almost all of the new income and wealth is going to the people on top.\par \par \uc2 And while working people are saying, excuse me, I can`t afford to buy a house, I can`t afford to send my kids to college, and yet, during the pandemic, the billionaire class saw a $2 trillion increase in their wealth. That`s what`s going on. People are standing up, fighting back. We`ve been involved in many of these strikes. We`re involved in this railroad situation right now where you`re seeing the most ugly type of corporate greed imaginable.\par \par \uc2 Railroads -- five major railroads made 20 billion in profits last year. Meanwhile, over the last six years, they laid off a third of their workforce. And when workers get sick, and they have to stay home, they are off in a position to lose their jobs. So, what you`re seeing right now our work is saying enough is enough. You know, you guys on top, you can`t have it all. We need an economy that works for all of us. Unions are one vehicle that help people get decent wages and working conditions.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, I have to say, as someone who, you know, covers this kind of stuff, it came as news to me that railroad workers don`t get sick days. I think it came as news to the President of the United States. Hopefully, that`s going to change. We`re going to see if they`re going to ratify this new contract but that appears to be one of the changes.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bernie Sanders, thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220921ei9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for September 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Shaquille Brewster, Steve Kornacki, David Jolly,  George Solis, Mike Memoli, Tom Winter, Charles Coleman, Richard Engel \par \uc2 9,491 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good day, you`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. I`m Peter Alexander, in today for my friend, Chris Jansing.\par \par \uc2 We are expecting President Biden to come out at any time now to push the DISCLOSE Act, a bill that would require Super PACs and so-called dark money groups to disclose donors who give more than $10,000. While we wait for that, we`re also just about an hour or so away from that first hearing by the so-called Special Master, Raymond Dearie, that court-ordered independent third party regarding documents found at former President Trump`s home in Florida. We do not know a lot about what will transpire in that hearing but we do know that Mr. Trump`s team is already taking issue with some of the judge`s directives claiming they do not want to tip their hand over any potential legal defense if the former president might be indicted.\par \par \uc2 More on that and the Trump team`s latest filing and the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals in just a minute, plus, Hurricane Fiona has now strengthened into a Category 4 storm potentially on track to become a Category 5. There is the map you can see Turks & Caicos getting pounded. It has been crushing Caribbean islands for days now, leaving catastrophic destruction in its wake. Coming up, the latest on the devastation and the potential impact for folks up and down the east coast. We`ll have that.\par \par \uc2 Finally, my conversation with the Deputy Secretary of State, \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2 , how the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  that kicks off today and President Biden`s scheduled speech tomorrow. This morning, the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . chief started things off by telling the assembly that the world is "gridlocked and colossal global dysfunction." So with war and floods and food scarcity, energy issues all competing for time on the international agenda, what can the \uc2 UN\uc2  do to help?\par \par \uc2 But we do want to begin right now with the politics. And the midterm elections now exactly seven weeks away, the race for control of Congress could not be any closer. Our latest NBC News poll shows it`s effectively a dead heat. So today, both parties are putting their focus on increasing their ranks, marking national registration day with events nationwide aimed at registering and motivating voters ahead of those all-important midterms. I want to bring in NBC`s Shaquille Brewster. He`s in Milwaukee. NBC News, national political correspondent Steve Kornacki, fortunately, for us, is dressed and ready at the big board.\par \par \uc2 Shaq, let`s get to you. In writing about the poll results, our NBC News political team said there are two completely different campaigns that are going on right now.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Is that what you`re finding when you speak and you listen to voters there?\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: That is absolutely the case, Peter. And by the way, one of those national voter registration events is happening here in Milwaukee, right behind me. They`ll might be kicking off any minute now going and doing a car parade to encourage voters to come out and encourage voters to register to vote on this day. But in my conversations with folks across the state of Wisconsin, you hear two issues that come up more than others, one, inflation and the economy, and two, abortion. But our NBC poll showed that different parties have the advantage with those issues.\par \par \uc2 You see, Republicans have the advantage when you`re talking about immigration, economy, and crime. Democrats have the advantage with abortion, health care, and education. And that advantage is trickling down to how voters are perceiving the candidates in this battleground state. I want you to listen to some of those conversations and see how they break through that nuance there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE KLUTH, WISCONSIN VOTER: Inflation to me is not -- I mean, it`s just kind of consequential of the world that we live in. If we can find ways to, like fix that great. But things that are way more important are like more of the social issues for me. So, yes, things like women`s rights and health and so forth, improving that landscape.\par \par \uc2 TORI KENPKEN, WISCONSIN VOTER: The cost of living affects everybody. And what`s happening? And I`m not going to say that like abortion rights doesn`t affect everybody, it affects a lot of people but the cost of living, you know, if you improve cost of living that may affect women who feel like they have to go the route of abortion because they can`t take care of their child financially.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: And that is why our politics team has called this the tale of two different campaigns. It`s not as if voters don`t care about other issues out there. They`re not looking into other issues. But in terms of what is most animating, what they bring up the most, what they`re concerned about the most. Those are the two issues. And you see there`s a partisan advantage for Republicans on one and Democrats on the other.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Shaq, thank you. Steve, let me ask you the difference between winning and losing. And these midterms, and we`ve said this pretty much every time, right? It`s going to come down to those voters in the middle that have yet to make up their minds. So help us sort of sort out who these people are. What do we know about these independent-minded voters?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it`s interesting. I think we got a little bit of data, a little bit of insight into that. Chuck Todd and our political team looked at a recent poll here, our most recent NBC poll, and found out first, a question of where the third persuadable, at least potentially persuadable voters are most likely to be here. You see a sort of different urban core, in a city, in a suburb, in a rural area.\par \par \uc2 You see, actually, it`s the outer suburbs. It`s the excerpts where you`re most likely to find the voters who are sort of up for grabs in this election. You can see 43 percent of them in outer suburbs. How do you define voters who are up for grabs? Well, it`s voters who are sort of sending mixed signals on some of the key questions we asked in the polls.\par \par \uc2 Let me take you through again, looking at these outer suburbs in particular, it`s -- a couple of things that would favor the Democrats, at least, on paper here. These are voters who by a slight majority say they want the Trump investigations to continue. They`re with the Democrats on that. They disapprove of the Dobbs decision, the Supreme Court ruling on abortion. They`re with, by and large, the Democrats on that.\par \par \uc2 But they`re not top to bottom with the Democrats because there are some issues here where they`re with the Republicans. They favor the idea of a Republican-controlled Congress. They disapprove by a wide margin of Joe Biden`s handling of the economy. So it`s sort of a clash of, they`re with the Democrats a little bit, they`re with the Republicans a little bit, which of those is going to sort of win out at the end of the day. In the kinds of districts, we`re talking about here.\par \par \uc2 When you say outer suburbs, give you an example here, one that we`ll be looking at closely on election night. This is the seventh district of Michigan. \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , she`s a Democratic incumbent. She was elected in that blue wave of 2018. The district has changed a little bit because of redistricting. But under his current lines, you take a look at this here, Biden won under these lines by a single point in 2020. It had been Trump by four points in 2016.\par \par \uc2 So you saw that movement of Trump plus four to Biden plus one. This is the kind of district that incorporates exactly what we`re talking about here, outer suburbs, sort of conflicted voters. This is a classic definition, I think on election night of a bellwether district.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: All right, Steve Kornacki, we appreciate that. Hard to believe, I think a lot of folks who are engaged in the daily sort of news grind that there`s some whose minds remain unmade up. That`s why we keep a close eye on them. Steve, Shaquille, thanks to both of you. I want to bring in Illinois Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Cheri Bustos\uc2  and former Florida Republican Congressman David Jolly, also an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 David, let me ask you first. You saw the way the issues break down and your mind is the overriding factor here we`re going to come down to whether people care more about those economic issues or their cultural issues, or is that sort of a too simplistic view?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. Well, I don`t think it`s too simplistic in terms of the top issues of the economy and a woman`s right to choose. Clearly, at a post-Dobbs world, we have seen the numbers shift more favorably to Democrats. I think what I`m fascinated to see in this cycle is you know you can break down election cycles into turnout races or persuasion races. And truthfully, they`re almost always just turn out races. Can I turn out enough people on my side based on enthusiasm or anger?\par \par \uc2 But what the Dobbs decision did is it handed Democrats a persuadable constituency on the issue of a woman`s right to choose. The nuance, Peter, though is the persuadable voters for Democrats this cycle are not traditional Democratic performing voters, and they probably have never identified as pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: But they`re learning that maybe they were pro-Roe all along. That message doesn`t fit neatly within the Democratic messaging of the past but is there for their taking. And I think that`s why you`re seeing democratic numbers bump up a little bit going into November.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Congresswoman, let me ask you about this. We know that the Fed is kicking off a big meeting today. The economy obviously remains on everybody`s mind. The president said on Sunday that he thinks the worst is over but an article and Kiplinger yesterday said the following. This is a quote, it said. "It`s very likely that the downturn we have been experiencing will get much worse in the bear market is likely not over." If the economy gets much worse, as they warn, how problematic is that for Democrats? Can the Democrats have to demonstrate continued progress to try to keep this issue from becoming a defining one for so many voters?\par \par \uc2 REP. CHERI BUSTOS, (D-IL): Yes, it`s not good if the economy continues to go in the wrong direction. It`s not good for Democrats. However, we have to look at what we have done and we have to keep talking about that. I mean look what just happened for Medicare patients, we are capping their yearly expenses at $2,000. If a senior has to be on insulin, that is capped at $35 a month. Nobody else has gotten that done.\par \par \uc2 We are -- the president just relieved $10,000 in student loan debt or $20,000 if you -- if you went to school on Pell Grants. So we`ve got a story to tell, but we just have to keep telling it. I could not agree with David more though about this is going to get down to doing a good job which I believe the Biden administration is and I believe House Democrats are as well and taking credit for it.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So we have to talk about that and then we have to make sure that we have the best Get Out The Vote plan in the history of our party. We`ve got anger going on. I come from a district that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won. In fact, I`m the only woman serving in the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2  who won every cycle when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in office. And so I understand those swing voters, I`m a Democrat in a Trump district. And as it pertains to choice, this is a hot-button issue. And it has changed dramatically in these 10 years that I`ve been in office.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: It went from an issue that you just didn`t talk about to an issue now that people are saying, I don`t want Uncle Sam in my -- in my room with the doctor helping me make a decision. This is my decision to make.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: Just like when we`re making Sunday dinner, we want to decide for ourselves whether we`re going to have fried chicken or baked chicken. We don`t want anybody else telling us what to do.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, those personal stories as it relates to women`s experiences with abortions in the past or the ability to have that freedom if they need to, at any point going forward have really I think, affected a lot of people making their decisions now. David, I want to switch to the Republican side. NBC`s first read included a number from the poll that struck a lot of folks. It said just 33 percent of Republicans and Republican-leaning voters say they consider themselves loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the Republican Party. That`s the lowest level since NBC started asking this question.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: 58 percent, nearly six in 10 say they`re primarily supporters of the Republican Party. So given that, how big of a factor is Mr. Trump in these midterms?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The presumption is that as the more we talk about him, the more this legal proceeding continues with the DOJ and his lawyers over Mar-a- Lago and the like, the better it is for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Absolutely. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is bad for Republicans. The Republicans want to prosecute a case against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the midterms. That is the playbook of every party outside of power is not unique this cycle. They do not want to be defending \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And to your point on those numbers, Peter, they also mirror that Donald Trump`s favor -- on favors down at about the 30 percent level as well.\par \par \uc2 But I would flip the script a little bit on this and suggest to you this. It actually indicates that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the one Republican leader today uniquely capable of dividing the party, right? Those numbers on DeSantis or Abbott or Haley or others are probably in the single digits. Those are just loyal Republicans. So what that number tells us is if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself removes himself from the scene, and particularly if he steps out of the `24 cycle, Republicans will unite behind DeSantis or some other. But if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  decides to make a play for party leadership to keep the reigns, that will continue to fracture the party, divide the party and play in favor of Democrats, not just in November, but in `24, as well.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Congresswoman, last question to you. What do the Democrats need to do seven more weeks to the midterms, you talked about Get Out The Vote, that effort there is what you think needs to be in place -- in place right now beyond just talking about their successes to this point?\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: Well, the MAGA Republicans, to the point David made about the Trump loyalists, we have to talk about them. They have begun to take over the Republican Party, which is -- which was a real shame. I -- look, I can look back and say I have many, many friends in the Republican Party but the hardcore MAGA Republicans, that`s a whole different story. And so I think what we have to do is talk about the hardcore nature of the MAGA Republicans that they want to criminalize women`s choices. They want to send Uncle Sam to the doctor`s office with us. They want to send women to jail if they are doing a search for a medical provider.\par \par \uc2 This is extreme and we need to talk about that. Anger is a motivator. And then we`ve got to have a -- the best -- not just to get out the vote plan but also continuing to register voters in every state, in every congressional district where we`ve got a shot.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSTOS: And so I -- it -- what I would do if I`m running a campaign is I would take some of that money that is being used. No offense to the airwaves, but it is being in -- the airwaves are being inundated. Let`s use some of that money to make sure we`re registering and then getting out the voter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, that`s a good point to make. Congresswoman \uc2 Cheri Bustos\uc2 , we appreciate your time. Former Congressman David Jolly, always good to have you for your perspective, as well.\par \par \uc2 By the way, on this national voter registration day, our "PLAN YOUR VOTE" tool is ready to give you the key information you need to register to vote. You see that Q.R. code right there on your screen, well, you can scan that code to be taken directly to the plan your vote site.\par \par \uc2 And soon right here, President Biden is set to speak on a new election transparency bill from the White House. When that begins, we`re going to take you there. The president, as we`ve noted, will head to New York next for the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 . He is speaking there tomorrow morning. What`s at stake in this high-level conference with a war on Ukraine set to come front and center? We`ll address it with the Deputy Secretary of State, \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2 . She`s going to join me ahead.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 But first, a tragic scene unfolding in Puerto Rico, we`re going to get the very latest on Hurricane Fiona`s path as millions in the Caribbean remain without power. You`re watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`re back. You`re watching MSNBC. Hurricane Fiona right now is a Category 3 storm barreling through the Atlantic pushing north towards Turks & Caicos. In its wake, a path of clear destruction, both the Dominican Republic and Puerto Rico are under a state of emergency adding insult to injury in Puerto Rico. Today marks five years since Hurricane Maria devastated the island territory. Fiona left millions without power after striking Puerto Rico and people are still struggling for access to food and the running water. Search and rescue missions there remain underway, complicated by the destruction. The island and its people are again forced to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC correspondent George Solis where -- he is in Ponce, Puerto Rico right now. George, a threat from this thing you know is far from over. What`s the biggest thing that folks on the ground are telling you about what they need right now?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Peter, right now, they want to know how soon federal aid will get here to help them get back on the power grid. Here we are five years after Hurricane Maria. And instead of talking about how much progress has been made, we`re talking about an island dealing with the effects of a Category 1 hurricane. And this is what a lot of crews are dealing with right now.\par \par \uc2 You see this right here behind me, a snapped power line. And this is just a small sample of the damage and destruction that`s rampant across the island. Not even four -- a couple of feet from me, you can see another downed power line in the street. Luma officials working to assess this now that the weather has cooperated, but we should note that there are still parts of this island that are still getting some rain as the hurricane still moves away from the island. And already, this island has seen 25, 30 inches of rain in some parts. So this, as you said is adding insult to injury here for the people.\par \par \uc2 And with those without power, you also now have the heat to contend with. So what`s happening is that people are stuck in their homes in areas where those search and rescue groups are still trying to get to them but they can`t because of these floodwaters that are still rising in some parts of the island. And resources are starting to come towards the aisle. We know that the FEMA Administrator is expected to touch down and bring with them some resources, some power generators, some much-needed food, some meals.\par \par \uc2 Governor Pierluisi saying this is a catastrophe for the island, and rightly so. But he is saying that it`s not quite fair to call this a disaster to the power grid because they expect their response to getting people back on line with the power to be much better than their response to Hurricane Maria. But people that I have talked to say they are frustrated, they are irritated, and on this day of all days to have to relive this experience with this storm that barreled through this island, it is just a painful reminder of the setback that they now have to face with working to rebuild, working to recover those losses. And they`re wondering how long it will take them to get back on that power grid. The governor is saying this might take days.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: But some wonder if it`s not going to be weeks, maybe even months in those areas that are still being affected by this storm, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, there`s so much for one island territory to have to deal with now again. George Solis there on the ground in Puerto Rico, George, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There are some other new developments to tell you about in the Mar-a-Lago probe with that special master conference starting in Brooklyn in just about 30 minutes or so from now between the former president`s legal team and the Department of Justice, what we can expect to go down in that hearing. It`s next here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Breaking news right now. You see the president in the Roosevelt Room speaking about the DISCLOSE Act that would require Super PACs and so- called dark money groups to disclose donors giving over $10,000. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Before I want to speak with you very briefly about a vote in Congress this week that addresses a serious problem facing our democracy in my view. I`ve proposed much earlier on that we should just publicly fund all of our elections, but I`m not -- I don`t have the support for that position. But there`s a second position that is very good. And that is the role of money in politics and how we can begin to solve that problem.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 There`s something I`ve always -- it`s something I`ve always cared about. And at its best, our democracy serves all people equally, no matter wealth or privilege. But here`s the deal. There`s much too much money that flows in the shadows to influence our elections. It`s called dark money. It`s hidden.\par \par \uc2 Right now, advocacy groups can run ads on issues, attacking or supporting a candidate right until Election Day without disclosing who`s paying for that ad. Too often powerful special interests use front groups to run these ads to win at any cost. Even foreign entities that aren`t allowed to contribute to political campaigns use dark money loopholes to try to influence our elections.\par \par \uc2 And here`s this one recent example. A conservative activist who spent as was his right decades working to put enough conservative justice on the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade now has access to $1.6 billion in dark money to do more damage, and from our perspective, and restrict more freedoms. As far as we know, that`s one of the biggest dark money transfers in our history.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the deal. The public only found out about the $1.6 billion transfer because someone tipped off some of your reporters, otherwise, we still wouldn`t know about it. But now we know and there`s something we can do about it. This week, the Senate is going to vote on the Disclosure Act. House Democrats already approved this act, and Senator Sheldon Whitehouse of the -- of Rhode Island helped write this bill as well.\par \par \uc2 Here`s some of the things that would do. advocacy group advocacy groups that run ads to influence elections would have to disclose any contribution above $10,000 that they receive. And foreign entities would be banned from making any contributions for these ads, period. Republicans should join Democrats to pass the DISCLOSURE Act and get it on my desk right away. And dark money has become so common in our politics. I believe sunlight is the best disinfectant. And I acknowledge it`s an issue for both parties.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the key difference. Democrats in the Congress support more openness and accountability, Republicans in Congress, so far, don`t -- so far don`t. I hope they`ll come around. They should follow the lead of Republican governors and state legislatures in Tennessee and Wyoming that have passed disclosure laws. Let`s remember, getting dark money out of our politics has been a bipartisan issue in the past.\par \par \uc2 My deceased friend, \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , spent a lot of time fighting for campaign finance reform. For him, it was a matter of fundamental fairness. And he was 100 percent right about that. Ultimately, this comes down to public trust. Dark money erodes public trust. We need to protect public trust. And I`m determined to do that.\par \par \uc2 So I want to thank you all very much and hope that Congress, the Senate will act on this in a favorable way so I can sign that law -- into law as rapidly as possible. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President, why are the borders more overwhelmed under your watch?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think it`s an hour later, I`m sorry. (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On the border, why is the border more overwhelmed under your watch, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because there are three countries that are never have it. There are fewer -- there are fewer immigrants coming from Central America and from Mexico. This is a totally different circumstance. What`s on my watch now is Venezuela, Cuba, and Nicaragua. And the ability to send them back to those states is not rational. You could send them back and have them while we`re working with Mexico and other countries to see if we can stop the flow. But that`s the difference. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What exactly is happening why is anyone sending these much migrants to Delaware? Do you have any comment or respond to that, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He should come visit. We have a beautiful shoreline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: He should come visit, we have a beautiful shoreline. That appeared to be present Biden`s response to the Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  having recently sent some migrants to Martha`s Vineyard, Texas Governor Abbott sending some of those migrants to Washington, DC where they were dropped off outside the vice president`s residence. There had been reporting today that there was another plane of migrants headed to the president`s home state of Delaware. You heard the president`s rebuttal there.\par \par \uc2 Mike Memoli, my colleague, is joining us now outside the White House. And, Mike, the intention from this event today before the president heads to the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  was to make news on what`s called the DISCLOSE Act ahead of the midterms to try to crack down on that dark money, the money that comes from anonymous sources. Instead, he really waded into this issue about immigration that`s been amplified by those Republican governors in the recent political stunts.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Peter. First of all, I think the value of the President`s del -- over Rehoboth Beach home just went up a little bit with that free ad for the Rehoboth Beach tourism bureau but the president making light of what is a very serious situation at the moment. I`ve been in touch with the governor`s office in Delaware who says that there are officials working with advocacy groups as well, preparing for the potential arrival of these migrant flights.\par \par \uc2 And you also heard the president try to make a distinction between this border issue as a consequence of what Republicans argue has been an open border policy of Democratic administrations, including Biden at this moment, and saying that we`re dealing with a different situation right now. There are many people fleeing the political situations, and you heard him mentioned those countries, Cuba, Venezuela and Nicaragua.\par \par \uc2 And one of the challenges facing this administration is that they don`t have relationships with these countries that would allow them to work as they have with countries like Mexico on a "Remain in Mexico" policy or to undo the consequences of the Trump administration`s actions there. So that is part of the challenge now. And the president repeating what we have heard repeatedly from the White House podium from \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , the press secretary, that these Republican governors are engaged in political stunts using struggling individuals as pawns.\par \par \uc2 Now as to the subject of the president`s remarks there, the Disclose Act, now this is an important issue, the idea of campaign finance, but not one that has been really front and center in our politics today. But I thought, Peter, it was really interesting to hear how the president tried to link this issue of campaign finance to what is one of the main pillars of the Biden team Democratic midterm election strategy, which is to talk about how these dark money groups have been able to, for instance, lead to the overturning of Roe vs. Wade, using the Supreme Court as an issue.\par \par \uc2 And so that is the president trying to use an issue that is important to a number of Democrats on Capitol Hill. We are likely to see this vote go down. It needs 60 votes that it doesn`t have. But to bring it back to what has been a key focus for this White House, two pillars. One, the economy, but second, this idea that voting rights, reproductive rights, other core freedoms are at risk. And in Biden`s case today pointing to the influence of dark money in eroding them.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. So it`s a good question. Why would this go down, why does it lack the votes necessary with Republicans I think across the board opposed to this Disclose Act? Among them some Republicans like Ted Cruz have argued that companies have the right to express themselves through anonymous donations. That is the sort of obstacle that`s existed in this effort as the president indicated there to try to get the Disclose Act pushed forward through Congress.\par \par \uc2 Mike Memoli, with that breaking news on a topic we didn`t anticipate would come up there, the topic of immigration, obviously. Mike, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Another major headline we`re watching right now is the Mar-a-Lago case and it returns to center stage today as the newly appointed special master, that independent third party, kicks off his first hearing. It should happen about 30 minutes from now. The Trump and DOJ legal teams are scheduled to make that first appearance before Judge Raymond Dearie at his Brooklyn courthouse. This comes after a new filing by Mr. Trump`s lawyers that call the case a, quote, "document storage dispute that spiraled out of control."\par \par \uc2 In an earlier filing, they refused to provide specifics about which of the seized documents Mr. Trump claims were declassified before he left office. Within the last hour, Republican attorneys general for 11 states have filed what`s called an amicus brief accusing the Biden administration of, in their words, ransacking the home of a political rival and attempting to, quote, "trade on the reputation of the Department of Justice and the intelligence community to thwart the appointment of a neutral special master."\par \par \uc2 There`s also new reporting from the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " that suggests that the former president was warned late last year of the legal perils of holding on to classified material. It`s a lot to try to make sense of. And for more to help us do that is NBC`s Tom Winter. Also Charles Coleman, a former Brooklyn prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Tom, tell us more about that new filing by the Trump team right now. What fundamentally is their argument here?\par \par \uc2 TOM WINTER, NBC NEWS INVESTIGATIONS CORRESPONDENT: Well, they`ve got two filings in two cases and fundamentally in the appeals court, Peter, they`re arguing that essentially the Justice Department should just sit back and wait and they should not get what they are asking the appellate court for. They should sit back and wait until the special master has done their job. They made it clear that a judge in Florida, one appointed by the former president at West Palm Beach has made a determination that a special master is the best way to go. That it provides the appropriate oversight, they say.\par \par \uc2 And so as a result, it`s real simple. Let`s just wait until that process is done. That`s their argument. Federal prosecutors say, look, between us and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , we need to get moving here. We might have a national defense or national security issue that we need to look into. The Office of the Director of National Intelligence wants to conduct a study and a review on these documents that were found at the property. Some of which were looking at presumably on screen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 And also we still need to conduct a federal investigation. So let`s put the special master issue aside and let`s get to work. We don`t know when the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals is going to make a decision on this whether or not they`re going to have some sort of oral argument or whether they`re going to issue some sort of paper opinion or ruling on this. So that`s just something that we`re going to have to follow.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 The other filing is the one that you talked about. That`s before the special master essentially saying hey, we don`t want to disclose whether or not we declassify or the former president declassified on his way out of White House certain documents. We might want to preserve that if we challenge the search warrant in and of itself. We might want to preserve that for a defense, if there`s any indictment in this investigation. So those are the thrust of the two main arguments they`re making in totally separate courts today but of course all over the same issue.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, no doubt. We`re glad to have you to help explain it to us.\par \par \uc2 Charles, let me ask you about the latter that Tom was talking about here. What`s happening before that special master and this argument that`s being made on behalf of Mr. Trump`s lawyers basically that they don`t want to have to make any argument. They don`t even want to wade into the conversation about what is or is not declassified. To be clear there`s no evidence that any of those documents were declassified to this point. But it does appear clear even though Raymond Dearie, this special master, was one of those individuals suggested as a potential special master by Mr. Trump`s own attorneys, that he has sort of put them on their heels a bit here.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, CIVIL RIGHT ATTORNEY: Well, I do expect that we`re going to see a very different tone out of Judge Dearie than we`ve seen thus far particularly out of Florida. And I think one of the reasons for that is when you looked at it objectively from 30,000-foot view, stepping away from it, you realize that a lot of what has happened thus far has been around the length and sort of these obscure tactics in terms of trying to get this distraction off track in terms of dealing with the actual issue.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that Judge Dearie is going to go for that. I think part of what we can expect to see today is a very different tone. I think that he`s going to be very clear about really outlining the issues from both parties, making clear what he believes is not an issue, what he agrees is an issue and then what he will allow both parties to argue even further in brief as to what the issues are.\par \par \uc2 Of course, as Tom sort of intimated, the strategy for team Trump is basically going to be make the issues as wide as possible, make this process go as long as possible. Try to drag it out without being committal so that they preserve as many options moving forward as they might. Whereas the DOJ is going to seek to make it as narrow as possible to eliminate any issues in question or as many as they can to make it so that the judge does not have very much that he has to decide.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, in addition to the concerns about national security, in addition to concerns about delaying the actual investigation, there`s also the issue that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has actually already gone through these documents. And so from the integrity standpoint of what is needed to make this investigation move forward, the DOJ already knows what`s there. So they also have to sort of navigate that with a very thin needle in terms of how they thread with respect to this judge.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Charles, let me ask you. The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports that the former president was warned that holding on to classified material when he left office could put him in legal jeopardy. The "Times" writes that former Trump lawyer Eric Herschman sought to impress upon Mr. Trump the seriousness of the issue and the potential for investigations and legal exposure if he did not return the documents, particularly any classified material.\par \par \uc2 So how does that affect a potential criminal case against the former president? Obviously, you know, you can`t say that you didn`t know if there`s now evidence that you were told just that.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Well, I think it speaks directly to the question of intent because you`ve been informed about the criminality of your actions and yet you continued in those actions, even after you had knowledge. I also think that one of the things that it does is that it really casts a strong amount of doubt on this notion of declassification because if in fact you are claiming that you declassified certain documents, at the point that you have been told that these documents need to be returned or at the point that you have been told that you are potentially in violation of the law to hold on to these documents, that for most people would have been the ideal time to step forward and say we have actually declassified documents.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a list of the documents we declassified. And therefore, we are not in violation of the law. That did not happen. And so when you look at the timeline, it certainly does not suggest or support what team Trump is trying to argue now in totality looking at how everything played out.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tom, help us, here with some other news that we just learned about over the last couple of hours, that the Republican attorneys general, and think it`s 11 states, West Virginia, Kentucky, I think Mississippi among them.\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Texas.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Have filed this amicus brief accusing the Biden administration of ransacking the home of a political rival. They say it was an effort to trade on the reputation of the Department of Justice to thwart the appointment of a neutral special master. Can you walk us through the significance of that? What if any impact that potentially has?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: Well, legally, and I think from an impact perspective, it probably has very little. I mean, the court is going to decide this case on the merits. What the Department of Justice attorneys have presented to the court, what Trump`s own attorneys have presented to them, look at the decision that was made in the Southern District of Florida, applying these sort of case law or precedent to that or create new precedent if they think that some should exist here, though I think it`s probably a little bit more straightforward than that.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:12]\par \par \uc2 The amicus curiae or friend of the court filings, Peter, service a way for interested parties to put something on the record and sometimes in hopes of swaying the judiciary`s opinion. But I`m still waiting for the first time in a political sense where I see that come to fruition. Sometimes amicus curiaes that are filed by potential victims, filed by the press, filed by others have sometimes an impact on the judiciary`s decision.\par \par \uc2 I just don`t know here if that`s going to be the case. And so just in, you know, reading that filing and seeing how these cases go in the past, I don`t see the impact of it being particularly high. I would say that if a group of Democratic attorney generals also wrote to the court, I just don`t see this making much of a difference as far as this instant ruling.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tom, at the end of the day, it`s a lot of back and forth. For a lot of people trying to pay attention, when does this thing resolve itself? What sort of timeframe are we playing with here?\par \par \uc2 WINTER: You know, just before coming on with you, Peter, I looked back at the most recent search warrant affidavit that we have that was unsealed and unredacted. So the most unredacted version of this still has approximately 54 percent that is redacted. If you look at the paragraphs that are redacted in full or in part. That`s a long way of saying we don`t still know a lot about this investigation.\par \par \uc2 I don`t anticipate anything happening until the end of the midterms. Certainly until the special master wraps up, if that`s the path that ends up being taken here. So bear minimum, I don`t expect any sort of decisions to be made until at least the end of November.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes. Democrats like folks talking about Trump. Mr. Trump likes talking about Mr. Trump, but Republicans haven`t really enjoyed this conversation. It`s obviously taken attention away from other issues they want to focus on.\par \par \uc2 Tom Winter and Charles Coleman, we appreciate both of your expertise.\par \par \uc2 Just a few hours from now, the president as we`ve noted is going to head to New York. He`s going to be addressing the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  there tomorrow. So what do we expect to hear from him as it relates to the war in Ukraine, global inflation and climate change? Deputy Secretary of State \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:46:29]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Just a few hours from now, President Biden is going to head back to New York where he is preparing to address the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  on Wednesday. Ukraine of course expected to be front and center there at the meeting of world leaders as the Russian invasion nears its seventh month. But also focus on the global economy as we fight record high inflation here at home and overseas. Climate change as the summer reflected some of the most catastrophic extreme weather events we`ve ever seen is certain to come up as well.\par \par \uc2 And I want to bring in the Deputy Secretary of State \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  right now. We`ve got a full plate of topics.\par \par \uc2 Madame Secretary, we appreciate you being here. President Biden, as you know, just vowed that the U.S. is going to stay with Ukraine as long as they need help. He said that commitment is ironclad. How critical is this moment for the U.S. to shore up international support for Ukraine`s counteroffensive?\par \par \uc2 WENDY SHERMAN\uc2 , DEPUTY SECRETARY OF STATE: Great to be with you, Peter. You know, I have been here at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . now for about a day and a half. And the global support for Ukraine is profound. I met with what`s called the B-9 today, which are the eastern flank states. These are states that were part of the old Soviet Union. They understand what Russia is all about. They are talking to colleagues all over the world here at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and I think President Biden will find that the world understands that this is critical.\par \par \uc2 This is about the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . charter, sovereignty, territorial integrity, the right of countries to make their own political choices. These are the principles of the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . charter and countries all over the world are committed to that charterer. He`s going to be speaking to that tomorrow. And I think he`s going to have a ready audience.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Secretary Sherman, let me ask you. President Biden warned \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  during the interview he did with "60 Minutes" not to use chemical or tactical nuclear weapons. . He said don`t. He repeated it multiple times. What is your level of concern that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will take drastic actions as Russian troops there are on increasingly it appears on the ropes?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, I agree with you that Russian troops appear on the ropes. We see Putin talking about mobilizing more people in his country. We see him putting forward the sham referenda in some of the parts, regions of Ukraine. A sham referendum that he thinks he`s going to conduct in three days. All of this is desperation by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And he is on the ropes. It of course increases our concern that he will use kinds of weapons of war that he should not.\par \par \uc2 He`s already weaponized food. He`s already weaponized energy. People all over the world are suffering from Putin`s war. And so I think we should be concerned. But hopefully he understands what the president just conveyed. Don`t, don`t, don`t. Russia, the world will be changed forever. And none of it will be good for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  or for the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You refer to that partial mobilization. We heard from the National Security adviser. Your colleague Jake Sullivan was speaking at the White House even as we were on the air here. He said that Russia may be resorting to this effort to bring more troops, Russian troops, into the mix. That might require as the U.S. suggests conscripts, basically, a new draft there.\par \par \uc2 Talk about why that is so significant right now, the potential that Russia may try to fuel its fight with more individuals, which, again, you know, the U.S. has spent billions of dollars already? This does have the potential to extend what is already just a dramatic and awful war.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:07]\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: It does have the potential to extend, but it also is going to send a signal to the people in Russia that their leader is desperate. That he needs to draft people. That he has to bring in conscript. I think Peter we cannot end this interview without talking about the Yale Observatory report today about Izium, the part of Ukraine where we are finding hundreds of bodies that are buried.\par \par \uc2 This follows the horrifying circumstances of Bucha that was destroyed by Russia. We see that the Ukrainians have moved on Kharkiv in a very affirmative offensive. So everyone wants to get to peace. Everyone does not want this war to go on. The people of Ukraine most of all but they also believe that they must defend their country, their democracy and the values that we all hold dear.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And as we speak about Russia, and thank you for raising that, Russia has yet to offer a serious counterproposal according to multiple officials to the U.S.`s proposed prisoner exchange for Paul Whalen and Brittney Griner, the \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star. So then how does the U.S. get these Americans back? \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  will be there. Is this likely to be discussed on the sidelines in New York?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, look. The United States has put a really good proposal on the table to bring home Brittney Griner and Paul Whalen. Each one of these situations is quite difficult and you`ve just seen that Mark Frerichs has returned from Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: We work on these cases day and night because our first priority is protecting American citizens at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Let me ask you if I can about some of the news the president made on the issue of Taiwan. He said in the interview that China -- he said we know that China is watching the invasion through the prism of Taiwan right now while the U.S. policy on Taiwan has not changed. This is what President Biden said about Taiwan over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY, \uc2 CBS\uc2  "60 MINUTES" CORRESPONDENT: Would U.S. forces defend the island?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes, if in fact there was an unprecedented attack.\par \par \uc2 PELLEY: So unlike Ukraine, to be clear, sir, U.S. forces, U.S. men and women, would defend Taiwan in the event of a Chinese invasion?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: So this isn`t one-off. As you know well, the White House has walked back in the past the president`s comments on a series of occasions on Taiwan and the potential future U.S. involvement. What signal does that send to the rest of the world that`s watching?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Well, let us be really clear. Our policy has not changed. The president is responding to hypotheticals. Our policy remains exactly what it has always been. Our One China policy remains as it has been.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Let me ask you about one other issue, the policy as it relates to Iran right now. You`ve said that we`re in a stalemate over nuclear talks. Any potential for a direct meeting between the U.S. and Iran on the sidelines of \uc2 UNGA\uc2  this week, the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: I don`t expect that at all, Peter. But let me be clear. The Europeans, the E-3, really negotiating as the front of the negotiation on the Joint Conference of Planet Action has put forward to Iran an excellent proposal that ensures compliance for compliance, and a return to the JCPOA, and now things are in Iran`s court. They need to make a decision about whether this is in their interest.\par \par \uc2 I believe that it is. I believe that the president of the United States is committed to ensuring that Iran never gets a nuclear weapon. The JCPOA is a critical step in that regard to ensure that they never do get such a nuclear weapon because they`d be able to project astonishing power into the Middle East and around the rest of the world so I hope Iran understands that this is really important for them, for us, for the world.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: \uc2 Wendy Sherman\uc2  is the U.S. deputy secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Sherman, thank you for your time and being with us.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Thank you, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: New details as we just noted are coming of Izium where rescue workers are pulling out bodies from mass gravesites found at the outskirts of that town just freed from Russian occupation.\par \par \uc2 NBC senior international correspondent Richard Engel now with more. And we should warn you that some of the images in Richard`s report here are disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: The Russian military has suffered stunning reversals here in eastern Ukraine. They`ve lost thousands of square miles of territory, but what they have left behind as the Russian military receded is evidence of what Ukrainians say is war crimes. And here, they are excavating bodies. We are on the outskirts of Izium, we`re in the forest here. And just today, these rescue workers have pulled out about 90 bodies.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 They`ve already filled up one refrigerated truck and it looks like they are going to fill up a second. There is a combination of Ukrainian soldiers here. One mass grave was filled with Ukrainian soldiers. Others have been filled with civilians and some of these rescue workers say that they are finding ample evidence of abuse that they`re seeing bodies that had broken bones, that had their hands tied behind their backs.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, not far from here, we went to a Russian headquarters that was used as an interrogation and torture center, and when the interrogations and the torture weren`t enough, there was already two executions and the body ended up here. And many Ukrainians are wondering why. Why the Russians were so brutal. Why they were carrying out such atrocities not just here. We saw a similar pattern outside of Kyiv when Russian troops were forced out of that area.\par \par \uc2 There were mass graves discovered there. There was evidence of torture. There were torture rooms. And Ukrainians are wondering why does this continue to happen. And the best answer they can come up with is that the Russians are overwhelmed. They`re not performing properly. They`re getting frustrated and that they`ve dehumanized the Ukrainians with all the propaganda, all the hate, all the anger that you see on Russian television, that you hear from Russian officials.\par \par \uc2 This is the result. The hate policy and you send soldiers off into battle and you reward the very soldiers who do these kinds of things. You create a cycle of violence. A cycle of atrocities and more mass graves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Richard Engel there reporting. It`s hard to watch but it`s critical that we don`t turn away.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. My colleague Hallie Jackson is in the chair for "KATY TUR REPORTS." That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, MSNBC ANCHOR: I`m Hallie Jackson, in for Katy Tur this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 And as we are coming on the air, you`ve got lawyers for the Department of Justice and for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just now sitting down together at a federal courthouse in Brooklyn. We`ve just seen attorneys for the Trump team walk in. I want to show you the live picture of it. You can see who is outside.\par \par \uc2 Both parties asked to be there by Judge Raymond Dearie, that special master or independent third party in the Mar-a-Lago investigation. Here`s the focus, right.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220921ei9k00001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd